{w + u} = 2^{v(w + u)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n3. \\( (2^w)^{u + v} = 2^{w(u + v)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nTherefore, equating exponents for base 2 and base 3:\n\nFor equation 1:\n\nThe left-hand side has only base 2, but the right-hand side has both 2 and 3. Therefore, this suggests that my substitution might not capture the 3 exponents. Wait a second, this is a problem.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. Earlier, I set \\( a = \\log_2 x \\), etc., but the right-hand sides of the original equations have factors of 3, which are not powers of 2. Therefore, taking logarithm base 2 of both sides would only handle the exponents of 2, but the 3 factors complicate things.\n\nTherefore, my previous approach of taking log base 2 and separating variables into a, b, c (which are log₂x, log₂y, log₂z) only captures the exponents of 2, but the presence of 3^4, 3^6, etc., means that the equations involve both 2 and 3, so we need to consider logarithms in a different base, or perhaps take logarithms with a common base that includes both 2 and 3?\n\nAlternatively, let me take natural logarithm on both sides of the equations.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nTaking natural logarithm of equation 1:\n\n\\( \\ln \\left( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\right) = \\ln \\left( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\right) \\)\n\nUsing logarithm power rule:\n\n\\( \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\ln x = 8 \\ln 2 + 4 \\ln 3 \\)\n\nSimilarly, express \\( \\log_2(y z) = \\frac{\\ln(y z)}{\\ln 2} = \\frac{\\ln y + \\ln z}{\\ln 2} \\)\n\nThus, equation 1 becomes:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{\\ln y + \\ln z}{\\ln 2} \\right) \\cdot \\ln x = 8 \\ln 2 + 4 \\ln 3 \\quad (1') \\)\n\nSimilarly, equation 2:\n\n\\( \\log_2(z x) \\cdot \\ln y = \\ln \\left( 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\right) \\)\n\nWhich becomes:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{\\ln z + \\ln x}{\\ln 2} \\right) \\cdot \\ln y = 9 \\ln 2 + 6 \\ln 3 \\quad (2') \\)\n\nEquation 3:\n\n\\( \\log_2(x y) \\cdot \\ln z = \\ln \\left( 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\right) \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{\\ln x + \\ln y}{\\ln 2} \\right) 