ere k from 1 to 1000 ⇒ m from 0.5 to 999.5.\n\nTherefore, overlapping m values are from 1.5 to 999.5. For these overlapping m, both the positive and negative terms include floor(2025/m) and floor(-2025/m), respectively.\n\nTherefore, the total combined sum over all m from 0.5 to 1000.5 is:\n\nterm(m=0.5) + sum_{m=1.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/m) + floor(-2025/m) ] + term(m=1000.5).\n\nTherefore, the entire sum S (excluding the j=0 term) is:\n\nsum_{m=0.5}^{1000.5} term(m) = [ -4050 ] + sum_{m=1.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/m) + floor(-2025/m) ] + 2.\n\nTherefore, S_total = 4050 + [ -4050 + sum_{m=1.5}^{999.5} (floor(2025/m) + floor(-2025/m)) + 2 ].\n\nWait, because S_total is 4050 (the j=0 term) plus the combined sum over m=0.5 to 1000.5 (which includes the negative and positive terms). Wait, no, actually:\n\nWait, the combined sum over m=0.5 to 1000.5 is exactly the sum over j=-1000 to 1000 except j=0. Because:\n\n- For j=-1000: m = -1000 +0.5 = -999.5? Wait no, hold on:\n\nWait a second, confusion here. Wait, when I did the substitution earlier, for the negative terms, j = -k, so m = k - 0.5, but actually, the substitution might have been m = j + 0.5 for positive terms, but for negative terms, j +0.5 = -k + 0.5, which is different.\n\nWait, perhaps my earlier substitution is causing confusion. Let me re-express:\n\nLet me instead think of the entire sum S (excluding j=0 term) as:\n\nsum_{j=-1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(j +0.5)) + sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j +0.5)).\n\nLet me make substitution for the negative j terms:\n\nLet j = -n, where n ranges from 1 to 1000.\n\nThus, the negative sum becomes sum_{n=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(-n + 0.5)) = sum_{n=1}^{1000} floor( -2025/(n - 0.5) ).\n\nTherefore, the entire S (excluding j=0) is:\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} [ floor(2025/(n +0.5)) + floor( -2025/(n - 0.5) ) ]\n\nTherefore, S_total = 4050 + sum_{n=1}^{1000} [ floor(2025/(n +0.5)) + floor( -2025/(n - 0.5) ) ].\n\nNow, let me analyze the term inside the sum: for each n from 1 to 1000,\n\nfloor(2025/(n +0.5)) + floor( -2025/(n -0.5) ).\n\nLet me denote for each n, let’s set a = n - 0.5 and b = n + 0.5. Then, note that b = a + 1.\n\nTherefore, the term becomes floor(2025/(a +1)) + floor( -2025/a ).\n\nBut a = n - 0.5, so a ranges from 0.5 to 999.5 in steps of 1 (since n is integer from 1 to 1000).\n\nTherefore, for each a from 0.5 to 999.5 (step 1), the term is floor(2025/(a +1)) + floor( -2025/a ).\n\nSo, let me write this as:\n\nfloor(2025/(a +1)) + floor( -2025/a ) = floor(2025/(a +1)) - ceil(2025/a ).\n\nBecause floor(-x) = -ceil(x) when x is not integer.\n\nTherefore, the term becomes floor(2025/(a +1)) - ceil(2025/a ).\n\nTherefore, the entire sum over n=1 to 1000 is equal to sum_{a=0.5}^{999.5 step 1} [ floor(2025/(a +1)) - ceil(2025/a) ].\n\nHmm, perhaps this telescopes?\n\nLet me see:\n\nLet me write the sum as:\n\nsum_{a=0.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/(a +1)) - ceil(2025/a) ].\n\nThis is a telescoping sum? Let me see:\n\nLet me denote for each term, when a increases by 1, the next term will have a new a', and so on. Let me see if the terms cancel out.\n\nLet me consider the terms:\n\nLet me write the sum as:\n\n[ floor(2025/(0.5 +1)) - ceil(2025/0.5) ] +\n\n[ floor(2025/(1.5 +1)) - ceil(2025/1.5) ] +\n\n[ floor(2025/(2.5 +1)) - ceil(2025/2.5) ] +\n\n... +\n\n[ floor(2025/(999.5 +1)) - ceil(2025/999.5) ]\n\nWait, but the last term is when a = 999.5:\n\nfloor(2025/(999.5 +1)) - ceil(2025/999.5) = floor(2025/1000.5) - ceil(2025/999.5).\n\nSo the sum is:\n\nsum_{a=0.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/(a +1)) - ceil(2025/a) ].\n\nLet me separate the sum into two parts:\n\nsum_{a=0.5}^{999.5} floor(2025/(a +1)) - sum_{a=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/a).\n\nNow, let me shift the index in the first sum:\n\nLet me set b = a +1. When a =0.5, then b =1.5; when a=999.5, then b=1000.5.\n\nThus, the first sum becomes sum_{b=1.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/b).\n\nThe second sum is sum_{a=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/a).\n\nTherefore, the entire expression becomes:\n\n[ sum_{b=1.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/b) ] - [ sum_{a=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/a) ]\n\nNow, notice that the first sum is over b from 1.5 to 1000.5, and the second sum is over a from 0.5 to 999.5.\n\nIf we reindex the second sum by letting a = c, then it's sum_{c=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/c).\n\nTherefore, the difference between the first sum (over b) and the second sum (over a) can be written as:\n\n[ sum_{b=1.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/b) ] - [ sum_{c=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/c) ]\n\nLet me write this as:\n\n[ sum_{b=1.5}^{999.5} floor(2025/b) + floor(2025/1000.5) ] - [ sum_{c=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/c) ]\n\nTherefore, this becomes:\n\nfloor(2025/1000.5) + [ sum_{b=1.5}^{999.5} floor(2025/b) - sum_{c=1.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/c) ] - ceil(2025/0.5).\n\nBecause the second sum includes an extra term at c=0.5, which is ceil(2025/0.5) = ceil(4050) = 4050.\n\nTherefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\nfloor(2025/1000.5) - ceil(2025/0.5) + sum_{b=1.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/b) - ceil(2025/b) ].\n\nNow, let's analyze each part:\n\nFirst term: floor(2025/1000.5) ≈ floor(2.02397) = 2,\n\nSecond term: -ceil(2025/0.5) = -ceil(4050) = -4050,\n\nThird term: sum_{b=1.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/b) - ceil(2025/b) ].\n\nNow, for any real number x, floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1 if x is not an integer, and 0 if x is an integer.\n\nBecause if x is integer, floor(x)=ceil(x)=x, so their difference is 0.\n\nIf x is not integer, then ceil(x) = floor(x) +1, so floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1.\n\nTherefore, the third term is equal to:\n\nsum_{b=1.5}^{999.5} [ -1 if 2025/b is not integer, else 0 ].\n\nTherefore, this sum is equal to - (number of non-integer values of 2025/b for b in [1.5, 999.5] step 1).\n\nWait, but b takes half-integer values: 1.5, 2.5, ..., 999.5.\n\nTherefore, the number of terms in the sum is the number of b's from 1.5 to 999.5 step 1, which is the same as the number of integers n from 1 to 999, since b = n + 0.5 where n from 1 to 999:\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nb starts at 1.5 (which is n=1: 1 + 0.5) up to 999.5 (which is n=999: 999 +0.5). So there are 999 terms in the sum.\n\nTherefore, the third term is - (number of non-integer 2025/b terms for b = 1.5, 2.5, ..., 999.5).\n\nBut note that 2025/b is integer only when b divides 2025 exactly, i.e., when b is a divisor of 2025. However, b here is a half-integer (since b = n +0.5 for integer n), so 2025 divided by a half-integer must be an integer.\n\nWait, similar to earlier analysis.\n\nLet me set b = m / 2, where m is an odd integer, since b = n +0.5 = (2n +1)/2. So m = 2n +1, which is odd.\n\nTherefore, 2025 divided by b = 2025 / (m/2) = 4050 / m.\n\nTherefore, 2025/b is integer if and only if m divides 4050.\n\nTherefore, 2025/b is integer ⇨ m divides 4050, where m = 2n +1, and n ranges from 1 to 999, so m ranges from 3 (when n=1) to 1999 (when n=999).\n\nTherefore, the number of integers b in [1.5, 999.5] (i.e., n from 1 to 999) for which 2025/b is integer is equal to the number of odd integers m in [3, 1999] that divide 4050.\n\nWait, this is exactly the same M we computed earlier! Because m = 2n +1, so m ranges from 3 to 1999 (odd numbers), and m must divide 4050. Therefore, the number of such m is exactly M=13.\n\nTherefore, the number of non-integer terms is total terms (999) minus the number of integer terms (M=13), so 999 -13 = 986.\n\nTherefore, the third term is - (999 -13) = -986.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nThe entire expression for the sum over n=1 to 1000 of [floor(2025/(n +0.5)) + floor(-2025/(n -0.5))] is equal to:\n\nFirst term: 2,\n\nSecond term: -4050,\n\nThird term: -986,\n\nSo total: 2 -4050 -986 = (2 - 4050) - 986 = (-4048) -986 = -5034.\n\nTherefore, the total sum S_total is:\n\n4050 (from j=0) + (-5034) = 4050 -5034 = -984.\n\nWhich matches our earlier result! Yay! So this confirms that S_total = -984.\n\nTherefore, after going through two different methods and verifying with substitution and telescoping sums, I can be confident that the answer is -984.\n\nBut just to be absolutely thorough, let me check if M was correctly computed as 13.\n\nEarlier, we listed the odd divisors of 4050 (excluding 1 and 2025) as:\n\n3,5,9,15,25,27,45,75,81,135,225,405,675.\n\nWait, that's 13 divisors. Let me count again:\n\n1. 3\n\n2. 5\n\n3. 9\n\n4. 15\n\n5. 25\n\n6. 27\n\n7. 45\n\n8. 75\n\n9. 81\n\n10. 135\n\n11. 225\n\n12. 405\n\n13. 675\n\nYes, 13 divisors. The next one would be 2025, which we excluded because it's over 1999. So yes, M=13.\n\nTherefore, all steps check out.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\boxed{-984}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{-984}\n</think>\n\nTo compute the sum \\(\\sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{j + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor\\), we analyze the terms by considering symmetry and properties of the floor function.\n\n1. **Symmetry Analysis**:\n   - For positive \\(j = k\\), the term is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{k + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor\\).\n   - For negative \\(j = - (k + 1)\\), the term is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{-(k + 0.5)} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor -\\frac{2025}{k + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor\\).\n   - If \\(x = \\frac{2025}{k + 0.5}\\) is an integer, the pair sums to 0. Otherwise, the pair sums to -1.\n\n2. **Key Observations**:\n   - The term at \\(j = 0\\) is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{0.5} \\right\\rfloor = 4050\\).\n   - The term at \\(j = 1000\\) is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{1000.5} \\right\\rfloor = 2\\).\n   - The term at \\(j = -1\\) is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{-0.5} \\right\\rfloor = -4050\\).\n\n3. **Counting Integer Divisors**:\n   - We need the number of odd divisors \\(m\\) of \\(4050\\) (since \\(2025/(k + 0.5)\\) must be an integer when \\(m = 2k + 1\\) divides \\(4050\\)).\n   - Prime factorization of \\(4050\\) is \\(2 \\times 3^4 \\times 5^2\\). The odd divisors are \\(3^b \\times 5^c\\) for \\(0 \\leq b \\leq 4\\) and \\(0 \\leq c \\leq 2\\), totaling 15. Excluding 1 and 2025 (since \\(2025 > 1999\\)), we have 13 valid divisors.\n\n4. **Sum Calculation**:\n   - Let \\(M = 13\\) (number of valid divisors).\n   - The paired terms contribute \\(- (999 - M)\\) since each non-integer pair contributes -1.\n   - Total sum: \\( - (999 - 13) + 2 = -986 + 2 = -984 \\).\n\n5. **Verification**:\n   - Using telescoping sums and reindexing confirmed the result, leading to the same final value.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{-984}\\).","num_tokens":17580,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"-984","full_generation":" Alright, so I need to compute this sum from j = -1000 to j = 1000 of the floor of (2025 divided by (j + 0.5)). Let me write that down to make sure I have it right:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{j + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor\n$$\n\nHmm, okay. The floor function here means that for each term in the sum, we're taking the greatest integer less than or equal to the value of 2025 divided by (j + 0.5). Since j ranges from -1000 to 1000, inclusive, that's a lot of terms—2001 terms to be precise (since we include both endpoints). Computing each term individually seems tedious, especially since for each j we'd have to do this division and then take the floor. There must be a smarter way to approach this problem without computing each term one by one. Let me think about how floor functions behave, and see if I can find some symmetry or a pattern here.\n\nFirst, note that the expression inside the floor function is 2025 divided by (j + 0.5). Let me denote the term inside the floor function as:\n\n$$\nf(j) = \\frac{2025}{j + 0.5}\n$$\n\nSo we have to compute the floor of f(j) for each j from -1000 to 1000, and then sum them all up.\n\nFirst, let me consider the domain of j. Since j goes from -1000 to 1000, the denominator j + 0.5 will range from:\n\nWhen j = -1000: denominator = -1000 + 0.5 = -999.5\n\nWhen j = 1000: denominator = 1000 + 0.5 = 1000.5\n\nSo the denominator ranges from -999.5 to 1000.5. Since the denominator is a real number, and we are dividing 2025 by these real numbers, so f(j) can be positive or negative, depending on the denominator's sign.\n\nSpecifically, when the denominator is positive, which occurs when j + 0.5 > 0, i.e., j > -0.5, so for j >= 0 (since j is an integer), then the denominator is positive, so f(j) is positive. When the denominator is negative, which occurs when j + 0.5 < 0, i.e., j < -0.5, so for j <= -1 (since j is an integer), then the denominator is negative, so f(j) is negative.\n\nTherefore, for j from -1000 up to -1, inclusive, f(j) is negative, and for j from 0 up to 1000, inclusive, f(j) is positive. Wait, but actually when j = -0.5, the denominator is zero, but j is an integer, so j can't be -0.5. The closest integers are j = -1 and j = 0. So for j = -1, denominator is -1 + 0.5 = -0.5, so f(-1) = 2025 / (-0.5) = -4050. For j = 0, denominator is 0 + 0.5 = 0.5, so f(0) = 2025 / 0.5 = 4050. So indeed, for j from -1000 to -1, the denominator is negative, so f(j) is negative, and for j from 0 to 1000, the denominator is positive, so f(j) is positive. However, what about when j = -1000? The denominator is -999.5, so f(j) is negative. Similarly, j = 1000 gives denominator 1000.5, so f(j) is positive.\n\nTherefore, the floor function of a negative number is the next lower integer. For example, floor(-1.2) = -2, floor(-4050) = -4050, since -4050 is an integer. Similarly, floor(4050) = 4050. So, in the negative terms, since f(j) is negative, the floor of a negative number is going to be more negative than the number itself if the number is not an integer. But if f(j) is an integer, then the floor is just f(j) itself.\n\nSo maybe I can split the sum into two parts: the sum over j from -1000 to -1, and the sum over j from 0 to 1000. Let me denote:\n\nLet S = sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} floor(2025 / (j + 0.5)) = S_neg + S_pos,\n\nwhere S_neg = sum_{j=-1000}^{-1} floor(2025 / (j + 0.5)), and S_pos = sum_{j=0}^{1000} floor(2025 / (j + 0.5)).\n\nSince S_neg consists of terms where f(j) is negative, and S_pos consists of terms where f(j) is positive.\n\nFirst, let's analyze 