\nMultiply both sides by (j + 0.5). But since (j + 0.5) is negative (because j <= -1), the inequality flips:\n\n2025 > -5*(j + 0.5)\n\nSo,\n\n2025 > -5j - 2.5\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n2025 + 5j + 2.5 > 0 → 5j > -2027.5 → j > -2027.5 / 5 → j > -405.5\n\nTherefore, the inequality f(j) < -5 holds when j > -405.5.\n\nBut j is an integer, so j >= -405.\n\nWait, this is confusing.\n\nLet me try plugging in j = -405:\n\nj = -405: denominator = -404.5\n\nf(-405) = 2025 / (-404.5) ≈ 2025 / 404.5 ≈ 5.0 (since 404.5 ×5 = 2022.5), so 2025 /404.5 ≈ 5.0 + 2.5/404.5 ≈ 5.006 → so f(-405) ≈ -5.006, so floor is -6.\n\nAh! There we go.\n\nSo j = -405:\n\nf(-405) = 2025 / (-404.5) ≈ -5.006 → floor is -6.\n\nSimilarly, j = -404:\n\ndenominator = -403.5\n\nf(-404) = 2025 / (-403.5) ≈ 2025 / 403.5 ≈ 5.015 → so f(j) ≈ -5.015 → floor = -6\n\nWait, so starting at j = -405, the floor becomes -6.\n\nTherefore, the floor values decrease by 1 each time the denominator crosses a point where f(j) crosses an integer boundary.\n\nThis is getting quite complex to track manually, but the key point is that the terms in S_neg (the negative terms) range from approximately -3 up to -4050, but not every integer in between is present. The exact count would require knowing how many times each integer is achieved, but perhaps the earlier approach using the formula is still valid because we didn't need to know the individual terms, just the count of when 2025/d is integer.\n\nWait, but in the formula derivation, we considered N_int as the number of denominators d = m/2 (where m is odd from 1 to 1999) such that 2025/d is integer. Which translates to m divides 4050 (since 2025/d = 4050/m), so m must be an odd divisor of 4050. Since m ranges up to 1999, we exclude m = 2025 (the only odd divisor exceeding 1999). Thus N_int = 14.\n\nTherefore, according to the formula, S = -1000 +14 +2 = -984.\n\nBut given that in the small example the formula worked, and given that the steps followed logically, even though m