Hmm, but how would that translate to a polynomial?\n\nAlternatively, if P(y) is a polynomial in y such that P(5 + 2√6) = P(5 - 2√6). Since 5 + 2√6 and 5 - 2√6 are conjugates over Q, similar to how α and β were conjugates. So, if we can make sure that P(y) is invariant under the automorphism that sends √6 to -√6, then that would force P(5 + 2√6) = P(5 - 2√6). Since the minimal polynomial of 5 + 2√6 over Q is (y - 5)^2 - (2√6)^2 = y² - 10y + 25 - 24 = y² - 10y + 1. Wait, that's the same as the minimal polynomial of α! Interesting.\n\nWait, let me check that. Let me compute the minimal polynomial of γ = 5 + 2√6. Let me set γ = 5 + 2√6. Then, γ - 5 = 2√6, so squaring both sides: (γ - 5)^2 = (2√6)^2 ⇒ γ² - 10γ + 25 = 24 ⇒ γ² - 10γ + 1 = 0. So indeed, the minimal polynomial of γ over Q is y² - 10y + 1. Therefore, similar to how α had minimal polynomial x⁴ - 10x² + 1, this γ has minimal polynomial y² - 10y + 1.\n\nTherefore, if we consider that to have P(γ) = P(γ'), where γ' is the conjugate of γ over Q, which would be 5 - 2√6, then similarly, we can require that P(y) is invariant under the automorphism that sends √6 to -√6. So, if P(y) is a polynomial with integer coefficients, then P(γ) must equal P(γ'), so P(y) must satisfy P(γ) = P(γ'), which would mean that P(y) is a polynomial in (y - 5)^2 - (2√6)^2? Wait, no, but (y - 5)^2 - (2√6)^2 is y² - 10y + 25 - 24 = y² - 10y + 1, which is the minimal polynomial of γ. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if we want P(y) to satisfy P(γ) = P(γ'), where γ and γ' are conjugates, then P(y) must be a polynomial in the minimal polynomial of γ, which is y² - 10y + 1. So, perhaps P(y) can be expressed as a polynomial in (y² - 10y + 1). But if that's the case, then P(y) would be a polynomial in (y² - 10y + 1). But then, since we want P(x) to be a polynomial in x (not in x²), maybe this approach is getting me confused.\n\nWait, let me backtrack. The problem is that we need P(α) = P(β), where α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. Since α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial x⁴ - 10x² + 1, then perhaps any polynomial that is invariant under the Galois group action that swaps α and β would satisfy P(α) = P(β). Since the Galois group of the extension Q(√3, √2) over Q is the Klein four-group, which has three elements: the identity, the automorphism that sends √3 to -√3 and √2 to √2, the automorphism that sends √3 to √3 and √2 to -√2, and the automorphism that sends both √3 and √2 to their negatives. However, the automorphism that swaps α and β is specifically the one that sends √2 to -√2, while leaving √3 fixed. Because:\n\nLet me denote the automorphisms:\n\nLet σ be the automorphism that sends √3 to -√3 and √2 to √2.\n\nLet τ be the automorphism that sends √3 to √3 and √2 to -√2.\n\nLet ρ be the automorphism that sends √3 to -√3 and √2 to -√2.\n\nThen, the composition στ would be the automorphism that sends √3 to -√3 and √2 to -√2, which is ρ.\n\nNow, the element α = √3 + √2 is mapped as follows:\n\nUnder τ: τ(α) = τ(√3 + √2) = τ(√3) + τ(√2) = √3 + (-√2) = √3 - √2 = β.\n\nUnder σ: σ(α) = σ(√3 + √2) = -√3 + √2.\n\nUnder ρ: ρ(α) = -√3 - √2.\n\nTherefore, the automorphism τ swaps α and β, while σ sends α to √3 - √2, which is actually β? Wait, hold on: Wait, σ(α) is σ(√3) + σ(√2) = -√3 + √2. But β is √3 - √2, so that's different. So σ(α) is actually equal to - (√3 - √2) = -β. Wait, no:\n\nWait, √3 - √2 is β, so -β is -√3 + √2, which is exactly σ(α). Therefore, σ(α) = -β. Similarly, τ(α) = β, and τ²(α) = α, since τ is an involution.\n\nTherefore, the automorphism τ swaps α and β, but also applies a sign change? Wait, τ swaps α and β, but τ(α) = β and τ(β) = α, because τ(β) = τ(√3 - √2) = τ(√3) - τ(√2) = √3 - (-√2) = √3 + √2 = α. Wait, hold on: τ(√3) is √3, τ(√2) is -√2, so τ(√3 - √2) = τ(√3) - τ(√2) = √3 - (-√2) = √3 + √2 = α. Therefore, τ swaps α and β. So τ(α) = β and τ(β) = α. So τ is the automorphism that swaps α and β. Therefore, if a polynomial is invariant under τ, then P(α) = P(τ(α)) = P(β), so that would satisfy the condition. Similarly, invariance under τ would ensure P(α) = P(β).\n\nTherefore, the set S consists of all monic polynomials with integer coefficients that are invariant under the automorphism τ. Therefore, such polynomials must satisfy P(τ(x)) = P(x) for all x. Wait, no, not for all x, but specifically, since τ is an automorphism of the field extension, then for any polynomial with coefficients in Q, if we apply τ to the argument, then P(τ(x)) must equal P(x) if P is τ-invariant. Hmm, but actually, the condition is only that P(α) = P(β), but perhaps more generally, if we want the polynomial to be invariant under the automorphism τ, then yes, it would satisfy P(τ(x)) = P(x) for all x in the field, but we just need it to satisfy P(α) = P(β). Maybe it's enough for the polynomial to be invariant under τ on the specific elements α and β, but perhaps not necessarily for all x? Hmm, maybe I need to be precise here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal polynomial Q that we are looking for is the minimal polynomial such that Q(α) = Q(β). Since α and β are conjugates, then if we can find a polynomial of minimal degree such that when evaluated at α, it gives the same value as when evaluated at β, then perhaps that polynomial must be the minimal polynomial of some element that is fixed by the automorphism τ? Wait, maybe not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the set {P(α) | P ∈ S} as being equal to {P(β) | P ∈ S}, so perhaps the values of such polynomials at α and β are the same. To have the minimal degree polynomial Q in S, perhaps Q must be such that Q(α) = Q(β) and Q is minimal in degree, so it can't be factored into lower-degree polynomials that also satisfy the condition? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal such polynomial is the minimal polynomial of some element that is in the fixed field of τ? The fixed field of τ is Q(√3), since τ fixes √3 and sends √2 to -√2, so the fixed field is Q(√3). Wait, let me think: The fixed field of an automorphism is the set of elements fixed by that automorphism. Since τ sends √2 to -√2 and fixes √3, then an element in the fixed field must be fixed by τ, so if x is in the fixed field, then τ(x) = x. So, if x is in Q(√3, √2), then τ(x) = x implies that x must be in Q(√3), because τ acts by flipping the sign of √2, so to be fixed, the coefficient of √2 must be zero. Therefore, the fixed field of τ is Q(√3).\n\nTherefore, if we want a polynomial Q such that Q(α) = Q(β), perhaps Q must take values in the fixed field Q(√3)? Since Q(α) and Q(β) must be equal and lie in Q(√3), but Q is a polynomial with integer coefficients, so Q(α) and Q(β) would actually be in Q(√3, √2), but since they are equal, they must lie in the fixed field Q(√3). Hmm, perhaps that's a path.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I need to construct such a polynomial Q by considering that since α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial x⁴ - 10x² + 1, then perhaps Q(x) must be a multiple of some polynomial that relates to this minimal polynomial, but in a way that enforces the equality at α and β. Wait, but how?\n\nAlternatively, suppose that Q(x) - c is divisible by the minimal polynomial of α and β, but since they are roots of x⁴ - 10x² + 1, then Q(x) - c must be divisible by x⁴ - 10x² + 1. But then Q(x) would have degree at least 4, but maybe that's not necessarily the minimal? Wait, but if Q(x) - c is divisible by x⁴ - 10x² + 1, then Q(x) would be equal to (x⁴ - 10x² + 1)*something + c, but since we want Q to be monic, then the leading term would have to be x⁴ times the leading coefficient of \"something\", so to make Q monic, \"something\" would have to be monic of degree n - 4, so Q would have degree at least 4. But maybe the minimal degree is 4? But the problem states that Q is in S and is of minimal degree. Wait, but the minimal polynomial of α is degree 4, so perhaps the minimal degree is 4? But let me see.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2)\". So, the minimal degree could be less than 4? Hmm, maybe not. Let me see.\n\nSuppose I try a linear polynomial: Let P(x) = x + c, where c is integer. Then P(α) = α + c and P(β) = β + c. For these to be equal, we need α + c = β + c ⇒ α = β, which is false. So linear is impossible.\n\nQuadratic: Let P(x) = x² + a x + b, with a, b integers. Then compute P(α) and P(β) and set them equal.\n\nCompute P(α) = (α)² + a α + b.\n\nWe already know that α² = 5 + 2√6, so substituting:\n\nP(α) = (5 + 2√6) + a(√3 + √2) + b = (5 + b) + a√3 + a√2 + 2√6.\n\nSimilarly, P(β) = (β)² + a β + b = (5 - 2√6) + a(√3 - √2) + b = (5 + b) + a√3 - a√2 - 2√6.\n\nSet P(α) = P(β):\n\n(5 + b) + a√3 + a√2 + 2√6 = (5 + b) + a√3 - a√2 - 2√6.\n\nSubtracting the common terms (5 + b) + a√3 from both sides, we get:\n\na√2 + 2√6 = -a√2 - 2√6.\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\na√2 + 2√6 + a√2 + 2√6 = 0 ⇒ 2a√2 + 4√6 = 0.\n\nFactor out 2:\n\n2(a√2 + 2√6) = 0 ⇒ a√2 + 2√6 = 0.\n\nSo, a√2 = -2√6 ⇒ a = -2√6 / √2 = -2√3.\n\nBut a must be an integer, and √3 is irrational, so this is impossible. Therefore, no quadratic polynomial works.\n\nOkay, so quadratic is impossible.\n\nCubic: Let me try cubic. Let P(x) = x³ + a x² + b x + c, with a, b, c integers.\n\nCompute P(α) and P(β). This might get a bit messy, but let me try.\n\nFirst, compute α³. Since α = √3 + √2, let me compute α³ step by step.\n\nFirst, α² = 5 + 2√6 as before.\n\nThen, α³ = α * α² = (√3 + √2)(5 + 2√6).\n\nLet me compute that:\n\nMultiply √3 by (5 + 2√6): 5√3 + 2√3√6.\n\nMultiply √2 by (5 + 2√6): 5√2 + 2√2√6.\n\nTherefore, α³ = [5√3 + 2√18] + [5√2 + 2√12].\n\nSimplify radicals:\n\n√18 = 3√2, so 2√18 = 6√2,\n\n√12 = 2√3, so 2√12 = 4√3.\n\nTherefore, substituting back:\n\nα³ = 5√3 + 6√2 + 5√2 + 4√3\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFor √3 terms: 5√3 + 4√3 = 9√3,\n\nFor √2 terms: 6√2 + 5√2 = 11√2.\n\nTherefore, α³ = 9√3 + 11√2.\n\nSimilarly, compute β³. Since β = √3 - √2,\n\nFirst, β² = 5 - 2√6,\n\nThen β³ = β * β² = (√3 - √2)(5 - 2√6).\n\nCompute that:\n\nMultiply √3 by (5 - 2√6): 5√3 - 2√18,\n\nMultiply -√2 by (5 - 2√6): -5√2 + 2√12.\n\nSimplify radicals:\n\n√18 = 3√2, so -2√18 = -6√2,\n\n√12 = 2√3, so 2√12 = 4√3.\n\nTherefore, substituting back:\n\nβ³ = [5√3 - 6√2] + [-5√2 + 4√3] = (5√3 + 4√3) + (-6√2 -5√2) = 9√3 - 11√2.\n\nTherefore, β³ = 9√3 - 11√2.\n\nNow, let's compute P(α) and P(β):\n\nP(α) = α³ + a α² + b α + c\n\nWe have α³ = 9√3 + 11√2,\n\nα² = 5 + 2√6,\n\nα = √3 + √2,\n\nSo substituting:\n\nP(α) = (9√3 + 11√2) + a*(5 + 2√6) + b*(√3 + √2) + c\n\nLet me expand each term:\n\nFirst term: 9√3 + 11√2,\n\nSecond term: 5a + 2a√6,\n\nThird term: b√3 + b√2,\n\nFourth term: c.\n\nCombine all terms:\n\nConstant terms: 5a + c,\n\nTerms with √3: 9√3 + b√3,\n\nTerms with √2: 11√2 + b√2,\n\nTerms with √6: 2a√6,\n\nSo altogether:\n\nP(α) = (5a + c) + (9 + b)√3 + (11 + b)√2 + 2a√6.\n\nSimilarly, compute P(β):\n\nP(β) = β³ + a β² + b β + c\n\nWe have β³ = 9√3 - 11√2,\n\nβ² = 5 - 2√6,\n\nβ = √3 - √2,\n\nSo substituting:\n\nP(β) = (9√3 - 11√2) + a*(5 - 2√6) + b*(√3 - √2) + c\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: 9√3 - 11√2,\n\nSecond term: 5a - 2a√6,\n\nThird term: b√3 - b√2,\n\nFourth term: c.\n\nCombine all terms:\n\nConstant terms: 5a + c,\n\nTerms with √3: 9√3 + b√3,\n\nTerms with √2: -11√2 - b√2,\n\nTerms with √6: -2a√6,\n\nSo altogether:\n\nP(β) = (5a + c) + (9 + b)√3 + (-11 - b)√2 - 2a√6.\n\nNow, set P(α) = P(β):\n\nSo,\n\n(5a + c) + (9 + b)√3 + (11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 = (5a + c) + (9 + b)√3 + (-11 - b)√2 - 2a√6.\n\nSubtract the common terms (5a + c) + (9 + b)√3 from both sides:\n\nLeft side remaining: (11 + b)√2 + 2a√6,\n\nRight side remaining: (-11 - b)√2 - 2a√6.\n\nTherefore, moving all terms to the left side:\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 - (-11 - b)√2 - (-2a√6) = 0,\n\nWait, actually, better to set the remaining parts equal:\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 = (-11 - b)√2 - 2a√6.\n\nBring all terms to the left side:\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 + 11√2 + b√2 + 2a√6 = 0.\n\nWait, no, actually, let me do it step by step.\n\nStarting from:\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 = (-11 - b)√2 - 2a√6.\n\nBring all terms to the left side:\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 + 11√2 + b√2 + 2a√6 = 0.\n\nWait, that's not quite. Let me subtract the right side from both sides:\n\n[(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6] - [(-11 - b)√2 - 2a√6] = 0.\n\nCompute term by term:\n\nFor √2 terms: (11 + b)√2 - (-11 - b)√2 = [11 + b + 11 + b]√2 = (22 + 2b)√2,\n\nFor √6 terms: 2a√6 - (-2a√6) = 2a√6 + 2a√6 = 4a√6,\n\nTherefore, overall equation:\n\n(22 + 2b)√2 + 4a√6 = 0.\n\nSo, this equation must hold, with a and b integers. Let me factor out 2:\n\n2*(11 + b)√2 + 4a√6 = 0 ⇒ 2*(11 + b)√2 + 4a√6 = 0.\n\nWe can factor out 2:\n\n2[ (11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 ] = 0 ⇒ (11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 = 0.\n\nSo, we have:\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 = 0.\n\nLet me write √6 as √2 * √3, so √6 = √2 * √3. Therefore,\n\n(11 + b)√2 + 2a√2 * √3 = 0.\n\nFactor out √2:\n\n√2 [ (11 + b) + 2a√3 ] = 0.\n\nSince √2 ≠ 0, we have:\n\n(11 + b) + 2a√3 = 0.\n\nSo, this equation must hold. However, the left side is a sum of a rational number (11 + b) and an irrational number (2a√3), which can only be zero if both the rational part and the irrational part are zero. That is:\n\n11 + b = 0,\n\nand\n\n2a√3 = 0.\n\nFrom the second equation: 2a√3 = 0 ⇒ a = 0 (since √3 ≠ 0).\n\nThen from the first equation: 11 + b = 0 ⇒ b = -11.\n\nSo, the only solution is a = 0 and b = -11.\n\nTherefore, for the cubic polynomial, we can have such a polynomial if a = 0 and b = -11, and c can be any integer? Wait, hold on. Wait, in the above derivation, when we set P(α) = P(β), we ended up with an equation that only involved a and b, and c canceled out. Let me check that again.\n\nLooking back at the expressions for P(α) and P(β):\n\nThe constant terms were both (5a + c), so when we subtracted, those canceled. The coefficients for √3 and the constants involving a and b also canceled except for the terms with √2 and √6. Therefore, c does not appear in the equation we derived. Therefore, c can be any integer, right? Because in the equation (11 + b)√2 + 2a√6 = 0, c didn't come into play. Therefore, for any integer c, if we set a = 0 and b = -11, then the equation holds, so P(α) = P(β).\n\nTherefore, the cubic polynomials of the form P(x) = x³ + 0 x² - 11 x + c, i.e., P(x) = x³ - 11x + c, with c any integer, satisfy P(α) = P(β). Since the problem requires monic polynomials with integer coefficients, nonconstant, so these are valid for any integer c. However, the problem is asking for the minimal degree element in S. Since we have found a cubic polynomial that works, and we saw that quadratic and linear don't work, then the minimal degree would be 3? Wait, but hold on.\n\nWait, but the problem specifies that Q is an element of S with minimal degree. So, if cubic works, then 3 is the minimal degree. But let me confirm that such a cubic polynomial is indeed in S, i.e., satisfies P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2). Since we just derived that with a = 0 and b = -11, and any c, that works. Therefore, for example, take c = 0 for simplicity, then Q(x) = x³ - 11x. Let me verify that Q(α) = Q(β).\n\nCompute Q(α) = α³ - 11α.\n\nWe already computed α³ = 9√3 + 11√2,\n\nand α = √3 + √2,\n\nso Q(α) = (9√3 + 11√2) - 11*(√3 + √2) = 9√3 + 11√2 - 11√3 - 11√2 = (9√3 - 11√3) + (11√2 - 11√2) = (-2√3) + 0 = -2√3.\n\nSimilarly, compute Q(β) = β³ - 11β.\n\nWe had β³ = 9√3 - 11√2,\n\nand β = √3 - √2,\n\nso Q(β) = (9√3 - 11√2) - 11*(√3 - √2) = 9√3 - 11√2 - 11√3 + 11√2 = (9√3 - 11√3) + (-11√2 + 11√2) = (-2√3) + 0 = -2√3.\n\nTherefore, indeed, Q(α) = Q(β) = -2√3. So that works. So, Q(x) = x³ - 11x is in S, and it's a cubic polynomial. Since we saw that quadratic and linear are impossible, then 3 is indeed the minimal degree. Therefore, the minimal degree is 3, and the polynomial is Q(x) = x³ - 11x. Therefore, now we need to compute Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nFirst, compute Q(10): Q(10) = 10³ - 11*10 = 1000 - 110 = 890.\n\nThen, compute Q(0): Q(0) = 0³ - 11*0 = 0 - 0 = 0.\n\nTherefore, Q(10) - Q(0) = 890 - 0 = 890.\n\nWait, but hold on. The problem says \"the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0)\", implying that even though there are multiple polynomials in S of minimal degree (which we just found to be 3), perhaps they all give the same value for Q(10) - Q(0)? Let me check that.\n\nSuppose that Q(x) is any monic cubic polynomial in S, so Q(x) = x³ + a x² + b x + c with a = 0 and b = -11, and c arbitrary integer. Then Q(x) = x³ - 11x + c. Then Q(10) = 10³ - 11*10 + c = 1000 - 110 + c = 890 + c. Q(0) = 0 - 0 + c = c. Therefore, Q(10) - Q(0) = (890 + c) - c = 890. So regardless of the value of c, the difference Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890. Therefore, the only possible value is 890. Therefore, the answer should be 890. Wait, but let me confirm once more.\n\nWait, in our analysis above for the cubic case, we found that in order for P(α) = P(β), we needed a = 0 and b = -11, and c can be any integer. Therefore, all such polynomials are of the form Q(x) = x³ - 11x + c. Since they are monic with integer coefficients, nonconstant (since cubic), so they are all in S. Therefore, for each such Q, Q(10) - Q(0) = [1000 - 110 + c] - [c] = 890. So yes, indeed, regardless of c, the difference is always 890. Therefore, the only possible value is 890. Therefore, the answer is 890.\n\nBut hold on, let me check with another example. Let me take c = 5. Then Q(x) = x³ - 11x + 5.\n\nThen Q(10) = 1000 - 110 + 5 = 895,\n\nQ(0) = 0 - 0 + 5 = 5,\n\nso Q(10) - Q(0) = 895 - 5 = 890. Yep, same result. Similarly, if c = -3, then Q(10) = 1000 - 110 - 3 = 887, and Q(0) = -3, so 887 - (-3) = 890. So indeed, the c terms cancel out. Therefore, no matter what c is, the difference is always 890. Therefore, the only possible value is 890.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0)\", so since all minimal degree polynomials in S give the same value for this difference, then 890 is the answer.\n\nBut hold on a second. Wait, the problem says \"nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients\". The minimal degree we found is 3, and all 