aybe in the current problem, it's also 1/B?\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nSuppose that in the current problem, \\( B^{N} \\equiv B^{2} \\mod N \\). Then, then r = (B^{2} -1) mod N, so then r/N would be (B^2 -1)/N. But in the small example, that fraction was (25-1)/120=24/120=1/5=1/B. So if in the current problem, (B^2 -1)/N=1/B, then that would hold. Let me check:\n\nSuppose \\( (B^2 -1)/N =1/B \\), then cross-multiplying gives B(B^2 -1)=N, so N=B^3 - B. But in the small example, N=120 and B=5, then B^3 - B=125 -5=120, which matches! So in the small example, N=B^3 - B.\n\nWait a minute, that's fascinating. In the small example, N=5! =120, and B^3 - B =125 -5=120. So N=B^3 - B.\n\nTherefore, in that case, \\( B^{N} \\equiv B^{B^3 - B} \\mod N \\). Wait, but how does that help?\n\nWait, in the small example, N=B^3 - B. Then, we can write exponent N = B^3 - B = B(B² -1)=B(B-1)(B+1). So exponent N is a multiple of B-1, B, and B+1. Hmm, perhaps in the small example, the exponent N is a multiple of the order of B modulo N? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since in the small example, N=B^3 - B, so B^3 ≡ B mod N. Therefore, B^3 ≡B mod N ⇒ B^3 - B ≡0 mod N ⇒ B(B² -1)≡0 mod N. But since N=B(B² -1), so yes, that's exact.\n\nTherefore, B^3 ≡ B mod N. So, in the small example, exponent N=B^3 - B is a multiple of the exponent needed to get back to B mod N. Therefore, perhaps in general, if N=B^3 - B, then B^{N} ≡ B^{B(B² -1)} ≡ (B^3)^{B(B-1)/3} ??? Not sure.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nIf B^3 ≡ B mod N, then B^3 - B ≡0 mod N ⇒ B(B² -1)≡0 mod N. Since N=B(B² -1), then this is exact. So B^3 ≡ B mod N.\n\nTherefore, B^3 ≡ B mod N ⇒ B^3 - B ≡0 mod N ⇒ B(B² -1) ≡0 mod N, which is true because N=B(B² -1). Therefore, B^3 ≡ B mod N.\n\nTherefore, in the small example, since B^3 ≡ B mod N, then B^{k} mod N cycles every 2 exponents? Wait, let's see:\n\nLet me compute B^{k} mod N:\n\nSince B^3 ≡ B mod N,\n\nThen, B^4 = B^3 * B ≡ B * B = B² mod N,\n\nB^5 = B^4 * B ≡ B² * B = B^3 ≡ B mod N,\n\nB^6 ≡ B^5 * B ≡ B * B = B² mod N,\n\nSo the cycle is B, B², B, B²,... every exponent.\n\nTherefore, the cycle length is 2.\n\nTherefore, for any exponent k:\n\nIf k is odd: B^{k} ≡ B mod N,\n\nIf k is even: B^{k} ≡ B² mod N.\n\nTherefore, in the small example, exponent N=120 (which is even), so B^{N} ≡ B² mod N, which is exactly what happened (25 mod120).\n\nTherefore, in the small example, because N=B^3 - B, which allowed us to write B^3 ≡ B mod N, leading to a cycle of length 2, so that even exponents give B² mod N.\n\nTherefore, if in the current problem, we can find a similar relation where B^{something} ≡ B mod N, then we could have a cycle and reduce the exponent accordingly.\n\nHowever, in the current problem, N=2025! and B=2017. Is there a similar relationship where B^k ≡ B mod N for some k?\n\nSpecifically, can we find k such that B^k ≡ B mod N?\n\nThen, that would imply B^{k-1} ≡1 mod (N / gcd(B,N)).\n\nBut since N=2025! and B=2017 divides N, so gcd(B,N)=B. Therefore, N/gcd(B,N)=N/B =m, which is an integer. Therefore, the congruence B^{k-1}≡1 mod m.\n\nTherefore, if we can find k such that B^{k-1} ≡1 mod m, then B^k ≡ B mod N.\n\nTherefore, the minimal such k would be the multiplicative order of B modulo m, plus 1.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute the multiplicative order of B modulo m, then set k = order +1, then B^k ≡ B mod N.\n\nTherefore, if we can find such a k that divides the exponent N=2025!.\n\nWait, but the exponent in our problem is N=2025!.\n\nSo if k divides N, then B^{N} ≡ B^{N mod k} mod N. But this seems too vague.\n\nAlternatively, if we can find that N is a multiple of the multiplicative order of B modulo m, then B^{N} ≡1^{something}≡1 mod m, and then combined with mod B, we can get something.\n\nWait, let's try to formalize this:\n\nWe want to compute B^{N} mod N, where N=B*m and gcd(B,m)=1.\n\nWe can split into mod B and mod m:\n\n- mod B: B^{N} ≡0 mod B,\n\n- mod m: Since gcd(B,m)=1, B^{φ(m)}≡1 mod m. Therefore, B^{N} ≡ B^{N mod φ(m)} mod m.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute N mod φ(m), then we can compute B^{N} mod m.\n\nThen, once we have B^{N} mod m and mod B, we can use Chinese Remainder Theorem to find B^{N} mod N.\n\nBut the problem is that φ(m) is enormous, and m itself is enormous, so this approach is not computationally feasible.\n\nHowever, in the small example, we had N=B*m where m=24, and φ(m)=φ(24)=8. Then N=120, so N mod φ(m)=120 mod8=0. Therefore, B^{N} mod m= B^{0}=1 mod m. Therefore, in that case, B^{N}≡0 mod B and 1 mod m, leading to 25 mod120.\n\nIn the current problem, if we can show that N ≡0 mod φ(m), then B^{N}≡1 mod m, and then the result would be similar to the small example.\n\nTherefore, if N ≡0 mod φ(m), then B^{N} ≡1 mod m, so then B^{N} mod N is the solution to x≡0 mod B and x≡1 mod m, which as before is x=B*inv(B,m) mod N. But unless inv(B,m) is known, we can’t compute the exact value.\n\nBut perhaps in the current problem, N is a multiple of φ(m), so that B^{N} ≡1 mod m. If so, then:\n\nx ≡0 mod B,\n\nx ≡1 mod m,\n\nso x ≡1 mod m,\n\nand x ≡0 mod B.\n\nThus, x is the unique solution mod N=B*m to these congruences.\n\nTo find x, we can write x = m*k +1, and this must be ≡0 mod B. So m*k +1 ≡0 mod B ⇒ m*k ≡-1 mod B ⇒ k ≡ (-1)/m mod B.\n\nTherefore, k ≡ -m^{-1} mod B.\n\nThus, k = B*t - m^{-1} for some integer t. Therefore, x= m*(B*t - m^{-1}) +1 = B*m*t - m*(m^{-1}) +1 = B*m*t -1 +1 = B*m*t.\n\nWait, that would imply x ≡0 mod N, but that can’t be, because x≡1 mod m and 0 mod B, but if x≡0 mod N, then x≡0 mod m as well, contradicting x≡1 mod m. Hmm, perhaps miscalculation.\n\nWait, let me do it step by step.\n\nWe have x ≡0 mod B,\n\nx ≡1 mod m.\n\nLet me write x = B * a, for some integer a.\n\nThen, substituting into the second congruence:\n\nB*a ≡1 mod m ⇒ a ≡ B^{-1} mod m.\n\nTherefore, a = m*b + B^{-1} for some integer b.\n\nTherefore, x = B*(m*b + B^{-1}) = B*m*b + B*(B^{-1}) mod N.\n\nSince we are working mod N=B*m, the term B*m*b ≡0 mod N, so x ≡ B*(B^{-1}) mod N ⇒ x ≡1 mod N?\n\nWait, that can’t be, because x must be ≡0 mod B and ≡1 mod m.\n\nWait, if x ≡1 mod m and x≡0 mod B, then x ≡1 mod m and x≡0 mod B. The solution is not necessarily 1 mod N. Wait, in the small example, we had x=25 mod120, which is 25≡1 mod24 and 25≡1 mod5? Wait no, 25 mod5=0. Wait, in the small example, x=25 mod120.\n\nWait, in the small example:\n\nWe had x≡0 mod5 and x≡1 mod24. Then solving x=24k +1, and 24k +1≡0 mod5 ⇒24k≡-1 mod5 ⇒24≡4 mod5 ⇒4k≡-1≡4 mod5 ⇒k≡1 mod5. So k=5t +1, so x=24*(5t +1)+1=120t +25. Thus, x≡25 mod120. So in that case, x≡25 mod120, which is 0 mod5 and 1 mod24.\n\nTherefore, in general, the solution is x= m*(B^{-1} mod m) mod N?\n\nWait, in the small example, B=5, m=24. Then B^{-1} mod m is 5^{-1} mod24=5, since 5×5=25≡1 mod24. Therefore, x= m*(B^{-1}) mod N=24×5 mod120=120 mod120=0, which is not correct. Wait, that gives zero, but we needed 25.\n\nHmm, perhaps my formula is off. Let me see:\n\nWait, in the small example, we had x = B*a, where a ≡ B^{-1} mod m. So a= B^{-1} mod m, so a=5 mod24. Therefore, x=5*5=25 mod120. So yes, x= B*(B^{-1} mod m) mod N.\n\nAh, right, so in general, x= B*(B^{-1} mod m) mod N.\n\nIn the small example, that worked: B*(B^{-1} mod m)=5*(5 mod24)=5*5=25 mod120.\n\nIn the current problem, if we can compute B^{-1} mod m, then x= B*(B^{-1} mod m) mod N would give the solution. However, without knowing B^{-1} mod m, we can’t compute this.\n\nBut perhaps in the current problem, similar to the small example, B^{-1} mod m is equal to B mod m?\n\nIn the small example, B=5, m=24, and B^{-1} mod24=5, which is equal to B mod24=5. So yes, in that case, B^{-1} ≡B mod m.\n\nIs this generally true?\n\nSuppose that B^{-1} ≡ B mod m. Then B*B ≡1 mod m ⇒ B² ≡1 mod m. So if B² ≡1 mod m, then B^{-1}≡B mod m.\n\nTherefore, in the small example, since m=24 and B=5, we had 5²=25≡1 mod24, so indeed 5^{-1}≡5 mod24.\n\nSo in the current problem, if we can show that B² ≡1 mod m, then B^{-1}≡B mod m, so x= B*B mod N= B² mod N, then r= B² -1 mod N, so r/N=(B² -1)/N.\n\nBut in the small example, that gave us (25-1)/120=24/120=1/5=1/B, which worked.\n\nTherefore, if in the current problem, B² ≡1 mod m, then (B² -1)/N would be (B² -1)/(B*m)= (B/m - 1/(B*m)), but unless m divides B² -1, this fraction might not simplify nicely. Wait, but in the small example, m=24, and B² -1=24, so 24 divides 24, so (B² -1)/N =24/120=1/5.\n\nAh! In the small example, m divides B² -1, since m=24 and B² -1=24, so 24 divides 24. Therefore, (B² -1)/N = (m)/ (B*m) )=1/B.\n\nTherefore, if in the current problem, m divides B² -1, then (B² -1)/N = (B² -1)/(B*m)= (m)/ (B*m)=1/B, so the result would be 1/B.\n\nTherefore, in the current problem, if m divides B² -1, then the answer is 1/B=1/2017.\n\nTherefore, the key question is: Does m divide B² -1, where m=N/B=2025!/2017?\n\nGiven that m=2025!/2017, which is a huge number, and B² -1=2017² -1=(2017 -1)(2017 +1)=2016×2018.\n\nTherefore, m divides (2016×2018) ?\n\nBut m=2025!/2017, which includes all the prime factors of 2025! except one factor of 2017.\n\nSince 2016×2018 factors into primes as follows:\n\n2016=2^5 * 3^2 * 7,\n\n2018=2×1009,\n\nso 2016×2018=2^6 * 3^2 *7 *1009.\n\nNow, m=2025!/2017. Since 2025! includes all the prime factors up to 2025, so it includes primes like 2,3,5,7,..., up to 2025. In particular, m contains primes like 2,3,5,7, etc., but crucially, it also contains primes larger than 2018, such as 2017 itself (but only once, since we divided out one 2017), and primes like 2019, 2020, etc., up to 2025.\n\nTherefore, m has prime factors that are not present in 2016×2018, such as 5, 11, 13, etc., which are factors of 2025! but not of 2016×2018. Therefore, m cannot divide 2016×2018 because m has prime factors that 2016×2018 does not have. Therefore, m does not divide B² -1.\n\nTherefore, in the current problem, the condition that m divides B² -1 is not satisfied, so the previous approach doesn't work. Therefore, the result won't be simply 1/B.\n\nHmm, so maybe my previous line of reasoning is not applicable here.\n\nAlternative approach: Since B=2017 and N=2025!,\n\nWe need to compute \\( B^{N} \\mod N \\).\n\nNote that N =2025! is divisible by B=2017, so let's write N=B*K where K=N/B=2025!/2017.\n\nNow, we can write \\( B^{N} = B^{B*K} \\).\n\nLet me consider the exponent B*K. Since B and K may not be coprime? Wait, K=2025!/2017, and B=2017. Since 2017 is a prime factor of 2025! only once, then K=2025!/2017 is an integer not divisible by 2017, so gcd(B,K)=1.\n\nTherefore, B and K are coprime.\n\nTherefore, we can apply Euler's theorem to B modulo K.\n\nSince gcd(B,K)=1, Euler's theorem says that \\( B^{\\phi(K)} \\equiv 1 \\mod K \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( B^{B*K} = (B^{\\phi(K)})^{B*K / \\phi(K)} } \\equiv 1^{B*K / \\phi(K)} } \\equiv 1 \\mod K \\), provided that B*K is a multiple of φ(K).\n\nTherefore, if B*K ≡0 mod φ(K), then \\( B^{B*K} ≡1 mod K \\).\n\nTherefore, if we can show that B*K is a multiple of φ(K), then \\( B^{N} ≡1 mod K \\), and since \\( B^{N} ≡0 mod B \\), then by Chinese Remainder Theorem, \\( B^{N} ≡ some number mod N \\).\n\nBut this requires knowledge of φ(K), which is difficult to compute.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps φ(K) divides B*K? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the small example, K=24, and φ(K)=φ(24)=8. Then B*K=5*24=120, which is a multiple of φ(K)=8, since 120/8=15. So in that case, it worked.\n\nIn the current problem, if B*K is a multiple of φ(K), then \\( B^{B*K} ≡1 mod K \\), so then \\( B^{N} ≡1 mod K \\), and \\( B^{N} ≡0 mod B \\), so then Chinese Remainder Theorem gives a unique solution mod N=B*K.\n\nThe solution would be the number congruent to 0 mod B and 1 mod K. Let’s denote this solution as x. Then x ≡0 mod B, so x=B*m for some m, and x≡1 mod K ⇒ B*m≡1 mod K ⇒ m≡B^{-1} mod K. Thus, m=K*n + B^{-1} for some n, so x=B*(K*n + B^{-1})=B*K*n + B*B^{-1}=B*K*n +1. Thus, x≡1 mod K and x≡0 mod B, so x≡1 mod K and x≡0 mod B, so the solution is x≡1 mod K and 0 mod B. The minimal positive solution is x= B*B^{-1} mod N? Wait, no, that would be 1 mod K and 0 mod B only if B*B^{-1} ≡1 mod K and 0 mod B. But B*B^{-1} is 1 mod K and B*B^{-1} mod B is 0 mod B only if B^{-1} ≡0 mod B, which is not true.\n\nWait, in any case, the solution is x=1 + t*K, and also x≡0 mod B ⇒1 + t*K ≡0 mod B ⇒t*K ≡-1 mod B ⇒t≡ (-1)/K mod B. Since K and B are coprime (gcd(K,B)=1), K has an inverse mod B. Let’s denote inv(K,B) as the inverse of K modulo B. Then t≡-inv(K,B) mod B. Therefore, the minimal positive solution is t= -inv(K,B) mod B, so x=1 + K*(-inv(K,B)) mod N. This is getting too abstract without knowing the actual values.\n\nHowever, in the small example, this worked out nicely because B^{-1} mod K was equal to B, leading to x= B*B mod N= B² mod N, which then gave us the correct remainder.\n\nBut in the current problem, unless B^{-1} mod K is something manageable, we can’t proceed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key insight is that in the small example, the exponent N was equal to B^3 - B, which allowed us to write B^3 ≡ B mod N, and thus the cycle length was 2, leading to B^{even}≡B² mod N.\n\nIn the current problem, if we can find an exponent relationship like B^k ≡ B mod N for some k dividing N, then we could reduce the exponent.\n\nSuppose that there exists some integer k such that B^k ≡ B mod N. Then, since N=B*K, this congruence implies that B^{k-1}≡1 mod K. Therefore, the order of B modulo K divides k-1.\n\nIf we can find such a k that divides N=B*K, then B^{N} ≡ B^{N mod k} mod N.\n\nBut without knowing k or the order, this is not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal such k is φ(K), so that B^{φ(K)}≡1 mod K, so then B^{φ(K)}≡1 mod K and B^{φ(K)}≡B mod B, so by Chinese Remainder Theorem, B^{φ(K)}≡ some value mod N.\n\nBut this seems too vague.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that in the small example, the result r/N=1/B, and given the problem's structure, the answer might still be 1/2017, even if the earlier reasoning about m dividing B²−1 doesn’t hold. Maybe there's a different reason.\n\nWait, another thought: In the problem statement, they specify that 2017 is prime. Maybe we can use the fact that 2017 is prime to apply Fermat's Little Theorem in some way, even though 2017 and N=2025! are not coprime.\n\nWait, Fermat's Little Theorem says that if p is prime and a not divisible by p, then a^{p-1}≡1 mod p. But here, a=2017 and p=2025! is not prime. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering the exponent N=2025!.\n\nNote that N=2025! is divisible by 2017, so 2017^{2017} ≡0 mod2017, but mod higher powers?\n\nAlternatively, since we are working mod2025!, and 2017 divides 2025!, so 2017 is a factor of the modulus. Therefore, 2017^{anything} ≡0 mod2017, but mod higher multiples?\n\nWait, perhaps using the concept of the Carmichael function, which gives the smallest exponent λ(n) such that a^{λ(n)}≡1 mod n for all a coprime to n. But again, since 2017 and N are not coprime, this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, since N=2025! is divisible by 2017, then in the modulus N, the residue of 2017 is just 2017, so 2017 ≡2017 mod2025!.\n\nTherefore, 2017^{2025!} mod2025! is the same as (2017)^{2025!} mod2025!.\n\nBut 2017 and 2025! share a common factor of 2017, so perhaps we can factor out 2017 from both the base and the modulus?\n\nWait, let me try to write 2017^{2025!} as 2017^{2025!} and modulus 2025!.\n\nLet me consider that 2025! = 2017 * m, as before, with m=2025!/2017.\n\nThen, 2017^{2025!} = 2017^{2017 * m} = (2017^{m})^{2017}.\n\nNow, consider that 2017^{m} mod m. Wait, but m=2025!/2017, which is still a huge number. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that since m is divisible by all primes less than 2025 except for one factor of 2017, but I don't see how that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering that 2017^{2025!} is divisible by 2017^{2025!}, but since the modulus is 2025!, which is divisible by 2017 only once (since 2017 appears only once in the prime factorization of 2025!), then 2017^{2025!} is divisible by 2017^{1}, but not necessarily higher powers. Wait, but 2025! has only one factor of 2017, so the exponent of 2017 in 2025! is 1. Therefore, 2017^{2025!} has exponent of 2017 equal to 2025!.\n\nTherefore, when we take 2017^{2025!} mod2025!, the exponent of 2017 in the modulus is 1, so the result will be congruent to 0 mod2017, but modulo higher powers of other primes?\n\nWait, perhaps breaking down the modulus into prime power components.\n\nLet me recall that to compute \\( a^b \\mod n \\), when n is composite, it's often useful to compute it modulo each prime power dividing n, then use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to combine the results.\n\nGiven that 2025! is the product of all primes up to 2025 raised to their respective exponents in 2025!.\n\nTherefore, to compute \\( 2017^{2025!} \\mod 2025! \\), we can compute it modulo each prime power \\( p^k \\) dividing 2025!, then combine the results via the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\nHowever, this is an enormous task because there are many prime powers in 2025!.\n\nHowever, perhaps for primes other than 2017, the computation simplifies?\n\nLet me consider a prime p ≠2017, p ≤2025.\n\nWe need to compute \\( 2017^{2025!} \\mod p^k \\), where p^k is the highest power of p dividing 2025!.\n\nSince 2017 and p are distinct primes (p ≠2017), so 2017 and p are coprime. Therefore, by Euler's theorem, since φ(p^k)=p^k - p^{k-1}, we have:\n\n2017^{φ(p^k)} ≡1 mod p^k.\n\nTherefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 2017^{2025! mod φ(p^k)} mod p^k.\n\nBut calculating 2025! mod φ(p^k) for each prime power p^k dividing 2025! is still a huge task.\n\nHowever, note that 2025! is a multiple of φ(p^k) for many primes p. In fact, for primes p ≤2025, φ(p^k) divides p^k - p^{k-1} which is much smaller than 2025! for large primes, so 2025! is a multiple of φ(p^k). Therefore, 2025! ≡0 mod φ(p^k), so 2017^{2025!} ≡1 mod p^k.\n\nWait, let me verify that.\n\nSuppose that for a prime power p^k dividing 2025!,\n\nSince φ(p^k) = p^k - p^{k-1} = p^{k-1}(p -1).\n\nNow, 2025! is a multiple of p^{k} because p^k divides 2025!.\n\nBut does 2025! also a multiple of φ(p^k)?\n\nNot necessarily. For example, take p=2, k= something. Let me see.\n\nTake p=2, then the highest power of 2 in 2025! is floor(2025/2)+floor(2025/4)+floor(2025/8)+... which is a large exponent, say K.\n\nThen φ(2^K)=2^K - 2^{K-1}=2^{K-1}.\n\nNow, 2025! is divisible by 2^K, so certainly divisible by 2^{K-1}=φ(2^K). So yes, in this case, 2025! is divisible by φ(p^k).\n\nSimilarly, for any prime p and its highest power p^k in 2025!, φ(p^k)=p^{k-1}(p-1). Since p^k divides 2025!, then p^{k} ≤2025!,\n\nand p^{k-1}(p-1) ≤ p^{k} ≤2025!.\n\nTherefore, φ(p^k) divides 2025!.\n\nWait, not exactly divides, but 2025! is a multiple of φ(p^k)?\n\nLet me see:\n\nSince p^k divides 2025!, then p^{k} ≤2025!.\n\nAlso, φ(p^k)=p^{k-1}(p-1).\n\nSince p-1 < p, so φ(p^k) < p^k ≤2025!.\n\nTherefore, φ(p^k) is less than p^k, which is less than or equal to 2025!.\n\nBut 2025! is a multiple of p^k, but not necessarily a multiple of φ(p^k).\n\nWait, for example, take p=3, k=1. Then φ(3^1)=2, and 2025! is certainly divisible by 2, so yes.\n\nTake p=3, k=2: φ(9)=6. 2025! is divisible by 6, since 6 divides 2025!.\n\nSimilarly, for p=5, k=1: φ(5)=4 divides 2025!.\n\nWait, in general, since p-1 is an integer less than p, and φ(p^k)=p^{k-1}(p-1). Since p^k divides 2025!, then p^{k-1} divides 2025! as well, and (p-1) is an integer less than p, so since p divides 2025! (as p ≤2025), then p-1 is less than p, so p-1 divides 2025! as well? Not necessarily. For example, take p=7, then p-1=6, which divides 2025!.\n\nWait, actually, since 2025! is the product of all numbers up to 2025, it contains all numbers from 1 to 2025, so any number less than or equal to 2025 divides 2025!.\n\nTherefore, since φ(p^k)=p^{k-1}(p-1), and p^{k-1} divides 2025! (because p^k divides 2025! implies p^{k-1} divides 2025!), and (p-1) is less than p, which is ≤2025, so (p-1) divides 2025!.\n\nTherefore, φ(p^k) divides 2025!.\n\nTherefore, φ(p^k) is a divisor of 2025!.\n\nTherefore, 2025! is a multiple of φ(p^k). Therefore, 2025! ≡0 mod φ(p^k).\n\nTherefore, for any prime power p^k dividing 2025!, since φ(p^k) divides 2025!, then 2025! ≡0 mod φ(p^k).\n\nTherefore, by Euler's theorem, since 2017 and p are coprime (as p≠2017), we have:\n\n2017^{φ(p^k)} ≡1 mod p^k.\n\nTherefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡2017^{0} ≡1 mod p^k.\n\nTherefore, for every prime power p^k dividing 2025! where p≠2017, we have 2017^{2025!} ≡1 mod p^k.\n\nNow, what about the prime power p=2017?\n\nThe highest power of 2017 in 2025! is 1, since 2017 is a prime and appears once in the factorial.\n\nTherefore, the prime power component for p=2017 is just 2017^1.\n\nTherefore, we need to compute 2017^{2025!} mod 2017.\n\nBut 2017^{anything} mod2017 is 0, since 2017 divides the base.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\nWe have computed that for every prime power p^k dividing 2025! except for p=2017, we have 2017^{2025!} ≡1 mod p^k.\n\nFor the prime power p=2017, we have 2017^{2025!} ≡0 mod2017.\n\nTherefore, by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, the number x=2017^{2025!} mod2025! must satisfy:\n\nx ≡0 mod2017,\n\nand\n\nx ≡1 mod p^k for every other prime power p^k dividing 2025!.\n\nTherefore, x is the unique number modulo2025! that is congruent to 1 modulo all prime powers except for 2017, and congruent to 0 modulo2017.\n\nTherefore, x ≡1 mod (2025! / 2017), since the modulus 2025! is the product of all prime powers, and removing the 2017 component gives 2025!/2017.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nLet me denote M =2025! /2017, which is the product of all prime powers in 2025! except for the single factor of 2017.\n\nThen, M is coprime to 2017, since we've removed the only factor of 2017.\n\nTherefore, the conditions are:\n\nx ≡0 mod2017,\n\nx ≡1 mod M.\n\nTherefore, we need to solve for x ≡0 mod2017 and x≡1 mod M.\n\nLet me write x=2017*y. Then, substituting into the second congruence:\n\n2017*y ≡1 mod M.\n\nWe need to solve for y: 2017*y ≡1 mod M.\n\nSince gcd(2017, M)=1 (because M=2025!/2017 and 2017 is a prime not dividing M), then 2017 has an inverse modulo M.\n\nLet me denote inv = inverse of 2017 mod M. Then y≡ inv mod M.\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive solution is y= inv, so x=2017*inv mod (2017*M)= mod2025!.\n\nTherefore, x=2017*inv mod2025!.\n\nTherefore, the remainder r= x -1, since we have to compute \\(2017^{2025!} -1 \\mod2025!\\), so r= x -1.\n\nTherefore, r=2017*inv -1 mod2025!.\n\nBut we need to compute r/2025! which would be (2017*inv -1)/2025!.\n\nBut this seems complicated unless inv is congruent to something specific.\n\nHowever, recall that in the small example with p=5 and N=120=5*24,\n\nM=N/p=24,\n\nand we needed to solve x≡0 mod5 and x≡1 mod24,\n\nwhich led to x=25 mod120,\n\nso r=25-1=24,\n\nand r/N=24/120=1/5=1/p.\n\nIn that case, inv was the inverse of p mod M,\n\nwhere inv=5^{-1} mod24=5,\n\nso x=5*5=25 mod120,\n\nand r=25-1=24,\n\nso r/N=(24)/120=1/5=1/p.\n\nTherefore, in the small example, r/N=1/p.\n\nIn the current problem, following the same logic, since x=2017*inv mod2025!,\n\nthen r= x -1=2017*inv -1,\n\nso r/2025! = (2017*inv -1)/2025!.\n\nBut in the small example, this became (5*5 -1)/120=24/120=1/5.\n\nNotice that in the small example, inv=p mod M,\n\nsince inv=5 mod24=p mod M,\n\nso inv=p mod M,\n\nso then x=p*p mod N,\n\nso r= p² -1 mod N,\n\nso r/N=(p² -1)/N,\n\nbut in that case, since M=N/p,\n\nthen (p² -1)/N= (p² -1)/(p*M)= (p/M - 1/(p*M)),\n\nbut in the small example, M=24, p=5,\n\nso (25-1)/120=24/120=1/5=1/p.\n\nAh! Because in the small example, p² -1=24= M,\n\nso (p² -1)/N= M/(p*M)=1/p.\n\nTherefore, in the current problem, if we can show that p² -1 is divisible by M=2025!/p,\n\nthen r/N=(p² -1)/N= (p² -1)/(p*M)= (p/M - 1/(p*M)),\n\nbut only if p² -1 is divisible by M, which would make the first term an integer and the second term negligible? Wait, but fractions are involved.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nSuppose in the current problem that p² -1 is divisible by M=2025!/p,\n\nthen (p² -1)/M is an integer,\n\nand then (p² -1)/N = (p² -1)/(p*M)= [ (p² -1)/M ] / p,\n\nwhich would be an integer divided by p,\n\nso r/N= integer / p,\n\nbut unless that integer is a multiple of p, which I don't know.\n\nHowever, in the small example, p² -1=24=M,\n\nso (p² -1)/M=1,\n\nso r/N=1/p.\n\nTherefore, if in the current problem, p² -1 is divisible by M=2025!/p,\n\nthen r/N=(p² -1)/N=1/p,\n\nso the answer would be 1/2017.\n\nBut is p² -1 divisible by M=2025!/p?\n\nIn the small example, yes, because M=p² -1.\n\nBut in the current problem, M=2025!/p,\n\nand p² -1=2017² -1=(2017 -1)(2017 +1)=2016×2018.\n\nSo, is 2016×2018 divisible by M=2025!/2017?\n\nThat is, is (2016×2018) divisible by (2025!/2017)?\n\nBut 2025! is a gigantic number, way larger than 2016×2018,\n\nso 2025!/2017 is also gigantic, and certainly much larger than 2016×2018,\n\nso 2016×2018 cannot be divisible by M=2025!/2017.\n\nTherefore, the condition that p² -1 is divisible by M is not met in the current problem.\n\nTherefore, in the current problem, we cannot apply the small example's logic directly.\n\nHmm, so what now?\n\nWait, but in the small example, we had that M=p² -1,\n\nbut in the current problem, M=2025!/p,\n\nwhich is not related to p² -1 except in the specific case where p! =p*(p-1)*(p+1), but 2025! is way larger.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a different relationship here.\n\nWait, recall in the small example, the solution x was equal to p² mod N,\n\nand thus r = p² -1 mod N,\n\nleading to r/N = (p² -1)/N =1/p,\n\nbecause N = p*(p² -1),\n\nso (p² -1)/N =1/p.\n\nTherefore, in that case, N = p*(p² -1),\n\nwhich was exactly the case because N=p! and p! =p*(p-1)*(p+1)*(other terms) but in the small example with p=5,\n\n5! =120=5*24=5*(5² -1),\n\nsince 5² -1=24,\n\nso indeed, 5! =5*(5² -1).\n\nWait a second! For p=5,\n\np! =5! =120,\n\nand p*(p² -1)=5*(25-1)=5*24=120.\n\nSo in that case, N=p! =p*(p² -1).\n\nTherefore, in that case, the relationship N=p*(p² -1) held exactly.\n\nTherefore, in that case, the result r/N=(p² -1)/N=1/p.\n\nBut in our current problem, is N=2025! equal to p*(p² -1)?\n\nNo, because p=2017,\n\np*(p² -1)=2017*(2017² -1)=2017*(2016×2018).\n\nBut 2025! is vastly larger than that, since 2025! includes all the numbers from 1 to 2025 multiplied together, which is way more than 2017×2016×2018.\n\nTherefore, the relationship N=p*(p² -1) only holds for p=5 (and perhaps p=2,3? Let's check p=3:\n\nFor p=3, p! =6,\n\np*(p² -1)=3*(9-1)=3×8=24,\n\nwhich is not equal to 6. So only p=5 satisfies N=p! =p*(p² -1).\n\nTherefore, the small example was a special case where N=p! equaled p*(p² -1). Therefore, the result r/N=1/p only holds in that specific case.\n\nTherefore, in our current problem, since N=2025! is not equal to p*(p² -1), the same reasoning does not apply.\n\nHmm, so what can we do?\n\nWait, but in the small example, the key was that N=p*(p² -1), so that p² ≡1 mod N/p,\n\nbecause N/p= p² -1,\n\nso p² ≡1 mod(N/p),\n\nwhich allowed us to have the inverse of p mod N/p equal to p,\n\nthereby giving x=p² mod N,\n\nleading to the result.\n\nIn our current problem, if we can find that p² ≡1 mod M,\n\nwhere M=N/p=2025!/2017,\n\nthen we would have inv=p mod M,\n\nleading to x=p*p mod N,\n\nand then r=p² -1 mod N,\n\nso r/N=(p² -1)/N,\n\nbut unless (p² -1)/N simplifies to 1/p,\n\nwhich would require N=p*(p² -1),\n\nwhich it is not,\n\nthen this approach won't work.\n\nHowever, perhaps in the current problem, even though M is not equal to p² -1,\n\np² ≡1 mod M,\n\nwhich would allow inv=p mod M,\n\nleading to x=p² mod N,\n\nand r=p² -1 mod N,\n\nso r/N=(p² -1)/N.\n\nBut unless (p² -1)/N is equal to 1/p,\n\nwhich would require N=p*(p² -1),\n\nwhich it isn't,\n\nthen this fraction won't simplify to 1/p.\n\nHowever, perhaps in the current problem,\n\n(p² -1) is divisible by M=N/p,\n\nwhich would make (p² -1)/M an integer,\n\nand then r/N=(p² -1)/N=(p² -1)/(p*M)=( (p² -1)/M ) / p,\n\nso if (p² -1)/M is an integer,\n\nthen r/N is an integer divided by p,\n\nbut unless that integer is a multiple of p,\n\nthen r/N would be a fraction with denominator p,\n\nbut not necessarily exactly 1/p.\n\nHowever, in the small example,\n\n(p² -1)/M=(p² -1)/(N/p)= (p² -1)/( (p*(p² -1))/p )= (p² -1)/(p² -1)=1,\n\nso it worked because (p² -1)/M=1,\n\nso then r/N=1/p.\n\nBut in the current problem,\n\n(p² -1)/M=(p² -1)/(2025!/2017).\n\nSince 2025! is divisible by p² -1?\n\nWait, is 2025! divisible by p² -1=2016×2018?\n\nWell, 2025! includes all factors up to 2025,\n\nso certainly includes 2016 and 2018 as factors,\n\nsince 2016=2^5*3^2*7 and 2018=2*1009,\n\nand both 2016 and 2018 are less than 2025,\n\nso yes,\n\n2016 divides 2025!,\n\n2018 divides 2025!,\n\ntherefore,\n\ntheir product 2016×2018 divides 2025!.\n\nTherefore,\n\n2025! = (2016×2018)*k,\n\nfor some integer k,\n\nso M=2025!/2017= (2016×2018*k)/2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\n(p² -1)/M=(2016×2018)/[ (2016×2018*k)/2017 ]=2017/k.\n\nHmm,\n\nWait,\n\nWait, let me write this step by step:\n\nLet me denote D = p² -1 =2016×2018,\n\nand N =2025!,\n\nso M=N/p=2025!/2017.\n\nThen,\n\nD/M = (2016×2018)/(2025!/2017) = (2016×2018×2017)/2025!.\n\nBut 2025! =2025×2024×…×2018×…×2017×…×1,\n\nso 2025! = (2025×2024×…×2019) × (2018×…×2017×…×1).\n\nTherefore,\n\n2025! = [Product from n=2018 to 2025 of n] × [Product from n=1 to 2017 of n].\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe denominator 2025! includes the term 2018×…×2017×…×1,\n\nso the numerator 2016×2018×2017 is part of that.\n\nBut this seems messy.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nD/M = (2016×2018×2017)/2025!.\n\nBut 2025! includes 2016×2017×2018 as factors,\n\nso D/M is a fraction where numerator is a part of the denominator.\n\nSpecifically,\n\n2025! = (2016×2017×2018) × [the rest of the product from 1 to 2025 excluding those three terms].\n\nTherefore,\n\nD/M = (2016×2018×2017)/ [ (2016×2017×2018) × (rest) ) ] = 1/(rest).\n\nTherefore,\n\nD/M =1/(rest),\n\nwhere \"rest\" is the product of all integers from 1 to