017² ⇒ M ≡0 mod2017.\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, 2025! =2017*M,\n\nso 2025! ≡0 mod2017² only if M is divisible by2017,\n\nbut M=2025!/2017,\n\nand since the exponent of2017 in2025! is1,\n\nthen M is not divisible by2017,\n\nso M mod2017 is not zero.\n\nWait, let's compute M mod2017:\n\nM=2025!/2017,\n\nand since 2025! =2017 * M,\n\nthen 2025! ≡0 mod2017,\n\nbut M mod2017 = (2025! /2017) mod2017.\n\nLet me write 2025! =2017*M,\n\nso dividing both sides by2017,\n\nM =2025! /2017,\n\nso M mod2017 = (2025! /2017) mod2017.\n\nBut 2025! ≡0 mod2017,\n\nso let's write 2025! =2017*k,\n\nthen M=k,\n\nso M mod2017 =k mod2017,\n\nbut k=2025!/2017,\n\nand since 2025! =2017*k,\n\nthen k=2025! /2017,\n\nso k mod2017 = (2025! /2017) mod2017.\n\nBut 2025! =2017*k,\n\nso 2025! ≡0 mod2017² if and only if k is divisible by2017,\n\nbut since the exponent of2017 in2025! is1,\n\nthen k is not divisible by2017,\n\nso k mod2017 is not zero.\n\nTherefore, we can’t simplify this further without more information.\n\nTherefore, returning to the equation:\n\n(2025!/2017)*k ≡-1 mod2017,\n\nwhich is M*k ≡-1 mod2017,\n\nwhere M=2025!/2017,\n\nso M*k ≡-1 mod2017,\n\nbut M mod2017 is some number, say m,\n\nso m*k ≡-1 mod2017,\n\nso k≡-m^{-1} mod2017,\n\ntherefore, k is determined mod2017,\n\nbut this doesn’t give us much.\n\nThis seems too involved.\n\nGiven that I'm stuck here, and given that in the small example with analogous parameters (prime p and modulus N=p!),\n\nthe answer was 1/p,\n\nand the problem here has modulus 2025! which is larger than p=2017,\n\nbut the structure is similar except for the size,\n\nmaybe the answer is still 1/2017,\n\nand the question is testing recognition of that pattern,\n\neven if the rigorous proof requires deeper number theory than I can muster right now.\n\nAlternatively, since in the small example with p=5 and N=5!,\n\nthe remainder r= (p^{N} -1) mod N =24= (5-1)!,\n\nand then r/N=1/p,\n\nmaybe in general,\n\nfor a prime p and modulus N=p!,\n\nthe remainder r=(p-1)!