odulus is not p! but larger,\n\nbut perhaps the same formula holds?\n\nWait, in the small example with p=5 and N=5!,\n\nit worked,\n\nbut in the current problem with p=2017 and N=2025!,\n\nwhich is larger than p!,\n\ndoes the same formula hold?\n\nLet me see:\n\nSuppose we have a prime p and modulus N,\n\nwhere N is a multiple of p,\n\nthen perhaps,\n\nif N is a multiple of p,\n\nand the exponent is N,\n\nthen p^{N} mod N = N/p mod N?\n\nWait,\n\nIn the small example,\n\np=5,\n\nN=120=5×24,\n\nand p^N mod N=25 mod120=25= N/p mod N,\n\nsince N/p=24,\n\nbut 25≠24 mod120,\n\nso that doesn't hold.\n\nWait,\n\nWait in the small example,\n\np^N mod N=25,\n\nand N/p=24,\n\nso 25= N/p +1,\n\nso not quite.\n\nHmm,\n\nWait, but r= p^N mod N -1=24,\n\nwhich equals N/p,\n\nso p^N mod N = N/p +1,\n\nso that r=N/p,\n\nso p^N ≡N/p +1 mod N,\n\nso p^N ≡1 mod N/p,\n\nand 0 mod p,\n\nso combining,\n\np^N ≡1 mod (N/p),\n\nand 0 mod p,\n\nso the solution is x= N/p +1 mod N,\n\nWait,\n\nWait in the small example,\n\nN/p=24,\n\nso x=24+1=25 mod120,\n\nso that works,\n\nso x= N/p +1 mod N,\n\nso p^N ≡ N/p +1 mod N,\n\nso r= p^N -1 ≡ N/p mod N,\n\nso r=N/p,\n\nso r/N=1/p.\n\nTherefore, generalizing,\n\nif we have a prime p and modulus N,\n\nwhere N is a multiple of p,\n\nand we compute p^N mod N,\n\nthen the remainder r= p^N -1 mod N is equal to N/p,\n\nso r/N=1/p.\n\nBut why would this be the case?\n\nIn the small example,\n\nit worked because N=p*(p² -1),\n\nso that N/p= p² -1,\n\nand p^N mod N= p² mod N,\n\nso p^N -1= p² -1 mod N,\n\nwhich is N/p,\n\nso r=N/p.\n\nBut in the current problem,\n\nif N is a multiple of p,\n\nbut not necessarily N=p*(p² -1),\n\nthen why would p^N ≡ N/p +1 mod N?\n\nPerhaps there's a different reason.\n\nWait,\n\nSuppose that N is any multiple of p,\n\nthen,\n\np^{N} mod N,\n\nif we can show that p^{N} ≡1 mod (N/p),\n\nand p^{N}≡0 mod p,\n\nthen by Chinese Remainder Theorem,\n\np^{N} ≡ something mod N,\n\nand if that something is N/p +1,\n\nthen r=N/p.\n\nBut