fore, putting this together via Chinese Remainder Theorem, we have:\n\n\\( 2017^{2025!} \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\)\n\nand\n\n\\( 2017^{2025!} \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\)\n\nTherefore, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\)\n\nand\n\n\\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\)\n\nThen, \\( x \\) will be congruent to \\( 2017^{2025!} \\mod (2017 \\times m) = \\mod 2025! \\).\n\nSo, solving these two congruences. Let me denote \\( x = 2017 \\times t \\), since \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\). Then substituting into the second congruence:\n\n\\( 2017 \\times t \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( 2017 t \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\)\n\nWe can solve for \\( t \\):\n\n\\( t \\equiv 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( t = 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\), so there exists some integer \\( t \\) such that \\( t \\equiv 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\), and then \\( x = 2017 \\times t \\).\n\nTherefore, the solution is \\( x \\equiv 2017 \\times 2017^{-1} \\mod (2017 \\times m) \\), but that seems circular. Wait, actually, once we have \\( t \\equiv 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\), then \\( x = 2017 t \\equiv 2017 \\times 2017^{-1} \\mod (2017 \\times m) \\). But \\( 2017 \\times 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\) is 1, but modulo \\( 2017 \\times m \\), it's just \\( 1 + k \\times 2017 \\times m \\). Wait, perhaps better to think that the solution is \\( x = 1 + m \\times s \\), where \\( s \\) is some integer, but also \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\). Therefore,\n\n\\( 1 + m \\times s \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( m \\times s \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( s \\equiv - (m)^{-1} \\mod 2017 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( s = - m^{-1} \\mod 2017 \\), so then \\( x = 1 + m \\times s = 1 - m \\times m^{-1} \\mod 2017 \\times m \\). Wait, this is getting too convoluted. Let me recall that the Chinese Remainder Theorem tells us that since 2017 and m are coprime, there is a unique solution modulo \\( 2017 \\times m = 2025! \\). The solution is \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\) and \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\), so we can write \\( x = 1 + m \\times k \\), and this must also satisfy \\( 1 + m \\times k \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\). Therefore, \\( m \\times k \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\), so \\( k \\equiv -m^{-1} \\mod 2017 \\). Therefore, \\( k = -m^{-1} + 2017 \\times t \\) for some integer \\( t \\), so then \\( x = 1 + m \\times (-m^{-1} + 2017 t ) = 1 - 1 + 2017 \\times m t = 2017 \\times m t \\). Therefore, the minimal positive solution is \\( x = 2017 \\times m \\times t \\), but modulo \\( 2017 \\times m \\), so the minimal positive solution is \\( x = 1 \\mod m \\) and \\( 0 \\mod 2017 \\), which would be \\( x = 1 + m \\times (2017 - m^{-1} \\mod 2017) \\). Hmm, this is getting too involved. Maybe instead of trying to compute the exact value, just note that such an \\( x \\) exists, and in particular, the solution is unique modulo \\( 2025! \\), so the remainder \\( r = x - 1 \\), since \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\equiv x - 1 \\mod 2025! \\), so \\( r = x - 1 \\). But we need to find \\( r / 2025! \\).\n\nWait, but actually, since \\( x = 2017^{2025!} \\mod 2025! \\), then \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\equiv x - 1 \\mod 2025! \\), so the remainder \\( r = x - 1 \\), provided that \\( x - 1 \\) is between 0 and \\( 2025! - 1 \\). But since \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), then \\( x = 2017 \\times t \\), so \\( r = 2017 \\times t - 1 \\). But since \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\), so \\( 2017 \\times t \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\), so \\( t \\equiv 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\), so \\( t = 2017^{-1} + m \\times s \\), so \\( x = 2017 \\times (2017^{-1} + m \\times s ) = 1 + 2017 \\times m \\times s \\), so \\( x = 1 + 2025! \\times s \\). Therefore, modulo \\( 2025! \\), \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod 2025! \\). Wait, that can't be, because earlier we had \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), but if \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod 2025! \\), then \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod 2017 \\), which contradicts \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nWait, so there must be an error in my reasoning here. Let me retrace.\n\nWe have \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\)\n\nand\n\n\\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\)\n\nSo, \\( x = 2017 \\times t \\), so substituting into the second equation:\n\n\\( 2017 \\times t \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\)\n\nSo, solving for \\( t \\):\n\n\\( t \\equiv 2017^{-1} \\mod m \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( t = 2017^{-1} + m \\times k \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( x = 2017 \\times (2017^{-1} + m \\times k ) = 1 + 2017 \\times m \\times k \\)\n\nTherefore, modulo \\( 2025! = 2017 \\times m \\), we have:\n\n\\( x \\equiv 1 + 0 \\times k \\equiv 1 \\mod (2017 \\times m) \\)\n\nWait, but that would mean \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod 2025! \\), but that contradicts \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), unless 1 ≡ 0 mod 2017, which is not true. So clearly, my mistake is in the substitution here.\n\nWait, no, actually \\( x = 1 + 2017 \\times m \\times k \\), so modulo \\( 2017 \\times m \\), that term \\( 2017 \\times m \\times k \\) is congruent to 0 mod \\( 2017 \\times m \\), so indeed \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod (2017 \\times m) \\), but that would mean \\( x \\equiv 1 \\mod 2025! \\), but then \\( x \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\) requires that 1 ≡ 0 mod 2017, which is false. Therefore, my mistake must be in assuming that such an x exists? But according to the Chinese Remainder Theorem, since 2017 and m are coprime, there should exist a solution. Hmm.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check with actual numbers. Let me take the earlier smaller example where modulus was 24 (4!) and base was 7. In that case, modulus was 24 = 3×8, but actually 24 = 3×8, but 3 and 8 are coprime. Wait, but in that case, the base 7 and modulus 24 are coprime, so the Chinese Remainder Theorem applies straightforwardly. In that case, we found that 7^{24} ≡ 1 mod 24, so the remainder was 0. So in that case, the solution was x ≡ 1 mod 8 and x ≡ 1 mod 3, so x ≡ 1 mod 24, but since we had 7^{24} ≡ 1 mod 24, so x =1, so remainder was 0 when subtracting 1. Wait, in that case, the remainder was 0, which is consistent.\n\nBut in our original problem, when we tried to apply Chinese Remainder Theorem, we ended up with a contradiction, suggesting that perhaps my approach is flawed. Let me see.\n\nWait, in the original problem, modulus is 2025! = 2017 × m, with m = 2025! / 2017, and gcd(2017, m)=1. So, to solve for x ≡ 0 mod 2017 and x ≡ 1 mod m.\n\nSuppose we let x = 1 + m * k, then we need 1 + m * k ≡ 0 mod 2017, so m * k ≡ -1 mod 2017. Since m and 2017 are coprime (because m is 2025! / 2017, and 2017 is prime, so m is not divisible by 2017), so m has an inverse modulo 2017. Therefore, k ≡ -m^{-1} mod 2017. Therefore, k = -m^{-1} + 2017 * t for some integer t. Therefore, x = 1 + m * (-m^{-1} + 2017 t) = 1 - 1 + 2017 m t = 2017 m t. Therefore, the minimal positive solution is x = 2017 m t where t is chosen such that x is minimal positive. Since x must be less than 2025! = 2017 m, the minimal positive solution is when t = 0, which gives x = 0, but x ≡ 1 mod m? Wait, if t=0, then x = 0, but 0 mod m is 0, not 1. Hmm, so perhaps t needs to be 1? Let me see.\n\nWait, if t = 1, then x = 2017 m * 1 = 2017 m, but 2017 m = 2025!, so x ≡ 0 mod 2025! So that would mean x = 2025! ≡ 0 mod 2025!, but then x ≡ 0 mod 2017 and x ≡ 0 mod m, but we needed x ≡ 1 mod m. So that doesn't work either. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake is in the expression for x. Let me do this step by step again.\n\nWe have:\n\nx ≡ 0 mod 2017\n\nx ≡ 1 mod m\n\nLet me write x = 2017 * t, then substitute into the second congruence:\n\n2017 * t ≡ 1 mod m\n\nTherefore, t ≡ 2017^{-1} mod m\n\nTherefore, t = 2017^{-1} + m * s, for some integer s.\n\nThus, x = 2017 * (2017^{-1} + m * s) = 2017 * 2017^{-1} + 2017 * m * s = 1 + (2025! / 2017) * 2017 * s = 1 + 2025! * s\n\nTherefore, x = 1 + 2025! * s\n\nTherefore, modulo 2025!, this simplifies to x ≡ 1 mod 2025!.\n\nBut then, x ≡ 1 mod 2025! would imply that x ≡ 1 mod 2017 as well, since 2017 divides 2025!.\n\nBut we required x ≡ 0 mod 2017. However, 1 mod 2017 is not 0 mod 2017 unless 2017 divides 1, which it doesn't. Therefore, this is a contradiction. That suggests that my approach has an error.\n\nWait a second, this seems like a problem. Where did I go wrong?\n\nAh! Wait a minute. The modulus in the second congruence is m, not 2025!.\n\nWe have x ≡ 1 mod m, and x ≡ 0 mod 2017. So x = 1 + m * k for some integer k. Then substituting into x ≡ 0 mod 2017 gives:\n\n1 + m * k ≡ 0 mod 2017 ⇒ m * k ≡ -1 mod 2017\n\nSince m and 2017 are coprime (as m = 2025! / 2017, and 2017 is prime, so m is not divisible by 2017), then m has an inverse modulo 2017. Let me denote the inverse as m^{-1} mod 2017. Therefore,\n\nk ≡ - m^{-1} mod 2017 ⇒ k = - m^{-1} + 2017 * t for some integer t.\n\nTherefore, substituting back into x:\n\nx = 1 + m * k = 1 + m*(- m^{-1} + 2017 * t) = 1 - 1 + 2017 * m * t = 2017 * m * t\n\nTherefore, x = 2017 * m * t. Since x must be less than 2025! = 2017 * m, the only possible value for t is 0, which gives x = 0. But x = 0 does not satisfy x ≡ 1 mod m, because 0 mod m is 0, not 1. Therefore, there is no solution with x < 2025!? But that can't be, since Chinese Remainder Theorem says there should be a solution modulo 2025!.\n\nWait, but perhaps I made a miscalculation here.\n\nWait, let me think again. Let me denote modulus1 = 2017, modulus2 = m, and the two congruences are:\n\nx ≡ 0 mod modulus1,\n\nx ≡ 1 mod modulus2.\n\nSince modulus1 and modulus2 are coprime (because m and 2017 are coprime), then by Chinese Remainder Theorem, there is a unique solution modulo modulus1 * modulus2 = 2025!.\n\nTherefore, there exists a solution x in the range [0, 2025!). However, when I tried to compute it, I ended up with x = 2017 * m * t, but that's only when t is an integer, but perhaps I need to consider that t can be a fraction? No, t must be an integer. Hmm.\n\nWait, let's do an example with smaller numbers to see what's going on. Let me take modulus1 = 3, modulus2 = 4 (which are coprime), and we want x ≡ 0 mod 3 and x ≡ 1 mod 4.\n\nThen, solving x ≡ 0 mod 3 ⇒ x = 3k.\n\nThen, 3k ≡ 1 mod 4 ⇒ 3k ≡ 1 mod 4 ⇒ k ≡ 3^{-1} mod 4. Since 3*3=9≡1 mod4, so inverse of 3 mod4 is 3. Thus, k ≡ 3 mod4 ⇒ k = 3 +4t.\n\nThus, x = 3*(3 +4t) = 9 +12t. The minimal positive solution is x=9, but modulo 12 (which is 3*4), 9 mod12 is 9. Check: 9 mod3=0, 9 mod4=1. So yes, that works. But 9 is less than 12, so it's the solution in [0,12). Wait, 9 is less than 12, so that's okay.\n\nWait, in my previous example with modulus1=2017 and modulus2=m, the solution x would be 2017*m*t, but in the smaller example, it's 3* (3 +4t). Wait, perhaps my mistake was in the algebra.\n\nWait in my previous steps:\n\nWe had x = 1 + m*k, where k ≡ -m^{-1} mod 2017, so k = -m^{-1} + 2017*t.\n\nTherefore, x = 1 + m*(-m^{-1} + 2017*t) = 1 -1 + 2017*m*t = 2017*m*t.\n\nAh! Wait, so x = 2017*m*t. But modulus is 2017*m, so the solution is x ≡ 0 mod (2017*m). But we wanted x ≡1 mod m and 0 mod2017. However, x=0 mod (2017*m) would mean x ≡0 mod m and 0 mod2017, but we wanted x≡1 mod m. So this is a problem. Therefore, my mistake is in the substitution.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the smaller example:\n\nmodulus1 = 3, modulus2 = 4,\n\nWe had x = 3k,\n\nthen 3k ≡1 mod4,\n\nthen k ≡ 3^{-1} mod4 ⇒ k = 3 +4t,\n\nso x = 3*(3 +4t) = 9 +12t,\n\nso x = 9 mod12,\n\nwhich is 9 mod12,\n\nwhich is 0 mod3 and 1 mod4,\n\nso that works.\n\nBut in my algebra above, when I did:\n\nx = 1 + m*k,\n\nthen k = -m^{-1} +2017*t,\n\nso x =1 + m*(-m^{-1} +2017*t) = 1 -1 + 2017*m*t,\n\nso x = 2017*m*t,\n\nbut in the small example,\n\nx = 1 + m*k,\n\nwhere m=4,\n\nand k ≡ -m^{-1} mod3,\n\nsince modulus1=3,\n\nso m^{-1} mod3 is the inverse of 4 mod3, which is 1, since 4≡1 mod3,\n\nso m^{-1}=1,\n\nso k ≡ -1 mod3 ⇒ k = -1 +3t,\n\nthen x =1 +4*(-1 +3t) =1 -4 +12t = -3 +12t,\n\nso x ≡ 9 mod12 when t=1,\n\nwhich is consistent with the previous result.\n\nAh! So in my general case above, I forgot that k must be an integer, so when I wrote k = -m^{-1} +2017*t,\n\nthen substituting back into x=1 + m*k,\n\ngives x=1 + m*(-m^{-1} +2017*t) =1 -1 +2017*m*t = 2017*m*t,\n\nbut in reality, in the smaller example, we had to have x= -3 +12t, which is equivalent to 9 mod12 when t=1.\n\nWait, so in the general case, x=2017*m*t, but that's only when t is such that x is positive. However, in the smaller example, x= -3 +12t, so when t=1, x=9, which is positive.\n\nTherefore, in the general case, x=2017*m*t - 1? Wait, no. Wait in the smaller example, the expression was x= -3 +12t,\n\nwhich can be written as x= 12t -3,\n\nso to get the minimal positive solution, set t=1: x=9,\n\nso in general, perhaps in my general case,\n\nx = 2017*m*t -1?\n\nWait, no, let me see:\n\nWait in the small example:\n\nmodulus1 =3, modulus2=4,\n\nthen x ≡0 mod3 and x≡1 mod4,\n\nsolution x=9 mod12,\n\nwhich is 9=3*3, so 3*(3)=9,\n\nbut 3 is modulus1,\n\nand 3 mod4 is 3, but 3*3 mod4 is 1,\n\nso in that case, x= modulus1*(modulus1^{-1} mod modulus2) ?\n\nWait, perhaps there is a different way.\n\nAlternatively, the general solution is x ≡ 1 mod m,\n\nand x ≡0 mod2017,\n\nso we can write x = 2017 * k,\n\nand 2017*k ≡1 mod m,\n\nso k ≡2017^{-1} mod m,\n\nso k = 2017^{-1} + m * t,\n\nthen x=2017*(2017^{-1} + m*t) =1 +2017*m*t,\n\nso x=1 + (2025!)*t,\n\ntherefore, modulo2025!, x≡1 mod2025!.\n\nBut this again implies x≡1 mod2017,\n\nwhich contradicts x≡0 mod2017,\n\nunless 1≡0 mod2017,\n\nwhich is false.\n\nWait, so where is the disconnect here?\n\nAh! The problem is that modulus2 is m =2025!/2017,\n\nand modulus1 is 2017,\n\nso modulus1 and modulus2 are coprime,\n\nso Chinese Remainder Theorem says there is a unique solution mod (modulus1 * modulus2)=2025!.\n\nBut in my calculation above, the solution x=1 +2025!*t,\n\nso mod2025!,\n\nthis is x≡1 mod2025!.\n\nBut that would mean that x≡1 mod2017,\n\nsince 2025! is a multiple of2017,\n\nso 1 mod2017,\n\nbut we wanted x≡0 mod2017,\n\nso that is a contradiction,\n\nwhich suggests that my calculation is wrong,\n\nbut according to the Chinese Remainder Theorem,\n\nthere should exist a solution.\n\nWait a second, perhaps my mistake is in the calculation of x=1 +2017*m*t,\n\nWait, let me redo the steps carefully:\n\nLet modulus1 =2017,\n\nmodulus2 =m,\n\nwith gcd(modulus1, modulus2)=1,\n\nwe want x≡0 mod modulus1,\n\nand x≡1 mod modulus2,\n\nso x= modulus1 *k,\n\nthen modulus1*k≡1 mod modulus2,\n\nso k≡modulus1^{-1} mod modulus2,\n\nso k= modulus1^{-1} + modulus2 * t,\n\nfor some integer t,\n\nso x= modulus1*(modulus1^{-1} + modulus2*t)= modulus1*modulus1^{-1} + modulus1*modulus2*t,\n\nwhich is 1 + modulus1*modulus2*t,\n\nso x=1 + (modulus1*modulus2)*t,\n\ntherefore,\n\nmodulus1*modulus2 is the modulus,\n\nso x≡1 mod modulus1*modulus2,\n\nwhich is x≡1 mod2025!.\n\nBut then x≡1 mod2025!,\n\nso x mod2025! is 1,\n\ntherefore,\n\nx=1 +2025!*t,\n\nso the remainder when divided by2025! is 1,\n\nbut we needed x≡0 mod2017,\n\nbut 1 mod2017 is not 0,\n\nso this is impossible.\n\nThis suggests that there is no solution, which contradicts the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\nWait, what's going on here?\n\nAh! Wait a second, the problem is that modulus2 is m =2025!/2017,\n\nand modulus1=2017,\n\nso modulus1 divides modulus1*modulus2 =2025!.\n\nBut in the congruence x≡1 mod m,\n\nand x≡0 mod2017,\n\nthen x≡1 mod m and x≡0 mod2017,\n\nso x ≡1 mod m,\n\nbut m is divisible by all numbers up to2025 except2017,\n\nbut in any case,\n\nthe problem is that x must be ≡1 mod m and ≡0 mod2017,\n\nbut since m and2017 are coprime,\n\nthe CRT says there is a solution mod2025!,\n\nbut when we try to compute it,\n\nwe get x≡1 mod2025!.\n\nWhich would mean that x≡1 mod2017,\n\ncontradicting x≡0 mod2017.\n\nWait, so unless 1≡0 mod2017,\n\nwhich it isn't,\n\nthere must be a mistake in my calculation.\n\nWait, let me try with actual numbers in the small example.\n\nTake modulus1=3,\n\nmodulus2=4,\n\nso modulus1*modulus2=12,\n\nand we want x≡0 mod3,\n\nand x≡1 mod4.\n\nSolution is x=9 mod12,\n\nso x=9 mod12,\n\nso x≡9 mod12,\n\nwhich is 0 mod3,\n\nand 1 mod4,\n\nso that works.\n\nNow, in this case