of 7 is 7 itself. So in the quotient, numbers like 2,3,4,5,6 still divide the quotient, but 7 does not. So in general, when you divide n! by a prime p that occurs exactly once in n!, then the quotient is divisible by all numbers from 1 to n except p. Because for any number q in 1 to n, if q ≠ p, then q does not have p as a factor (since p is prime and only appears in n! as p itself), so q divides n! and since we only divided by p once, q still divides n!/p. However, p itself does not divide n!/p because we removed the single factor of p. So in the case of M = 2025! / 2017, M is divisible by all numbers from 1 to 2025 except for 2017. Therefore, M is divisible by every integer from 1 to 2025 except 2017. So that's a key point.\n\nTherefore, M is divisible by all numbers from 1 to 2025 except 2017. Therefore, M is a multiple of all numbers except 2017. Therefore, M is a multiple of LCM(1, 2, ..., 2025) divided by 2017? Wait, not exactly, but since M is equal to 2025! divided by 2017, and 2025! is already the LCM(1, 2, ..., 2025) multiplied by some factors, but actually, LCM(1, 2, ..., 2025) is not necessarily equal to 2025! because LCM is the least common multiple, which is much smaller. Anyway, perhaps that's a detour.\n\nSo, getting back to computing \\( 2017^{N} - 1 \\mod M \\), where N = 2025! and M = 2025! / 2017, and gcd(2017, M) = 1. Since 2017 and M are coprime, we can apply Euler's theorem here. Euler's theorem tells us that \\( 2017^{\\phi(M)} \\equiv 1 \\mod M \\). Therefore, if we can write the exponent N = 2025! as \\( k \\times \\phi(M) + r \\), then \\( 2017^{N} \\equiv 2017^{r} \\mod M \\). But to do this, we need to compute \\( \\phi(M) \\), which is difficult because M is huge. Alternatively, perhaps we can note that N = 2025! is a multiple of \\( \\phi(M) \\)? Hmm, but again, without knowing \\( \\phi(M) \\), that seems difficult.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a better approach here. Let me think again.\n\nWe need to compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod 2025! \\). Since 2025! is the modulus, and 2017 is a factor of 2025!.\n\nLet me recall that for any integer a and modulus m, if m divides a, then a ≡ 0 mod m, so a^k ≡ 0 mod m for any k ≥ 1. But here, we have \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\). Since 2017 divides 2025!, then 2017 ≡ 0 mod 2017, but mod 2025! it's just 2017. Wait, so 2017 is not congruent to 0 mod 2025!, because 2025! is much larger than 2017. Wait, 2025! is a multiple of 2017, but 2017 is much smaller than 2025!.\n\nWait, so 2017 mod 2025! is just 2017, which is not 0. So that approach might not help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering that 2025! is divisible by all numbers up to 2025, so in particular, it's divisible by 2017, and also divisible by 2018, 2019, ..., up to 2025. So, in the modulus 2025!, the number 2017 is just a number between 1 and 2025!.\n\nWait, perhaps another idea: since we need to compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod 2025! \\), and note that 2025! is the product of all integers from 1 to 2025, so in particular, 2017 is one of those factors. Therefore, 2025! is a multiple of 2017, so 2017 divides 2025!.\n\nTherefore, in the expression \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\), we can consider that 2017^{2025!} is divisible by 2017, but then subtracting 1 would make it congruent to -1 mod 2017, but we need mod 2025!.\n\nHmm, perhaps breaking down the modulus into its prime power components. Let me recall that to compute \\( a^b \\mod m \\), when m is composite, it's often useful to factor m into its prime power components and then use the Chinese Remainder Theorem. So, if we can factor 2025! into its prime power factors, compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) modulo each prime power, then combine the results.\n\nHowever, 2025! has an enormous number of prime factors, so this seems impractical. But perhaps there is some property we can exploit here.\n\nAlternatively, note that since 2025! is divisible by 2017, and 2017 is prime, then 2017^{2025!} ≡ 0 mod 2017, so as before, \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ -1 mod 2017 \\). But we also need to consider higher powers? Wait, but modulus is 2025!, which includes 2017 as a prime factor but also many other prime factors. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that for any prime p ≤ 2025, since p divides 2025!, then by Fermat's little theorem, if p ≠ 2017, then 2017^{p-1} ≡ 1 mod p. Wait, but 2017 and p may not be coprime? Wait, if p is a prime different from 2017, then since 2017 is prime and not equal to p, they are coprime, so yes, Fermat's little theorem applies.\n\nTherefore, for any prime p ≤ 2025 and p ≠ 2017, we have 2017^{p-1} ≡ 1 mod p. Therefore, the order of 2017 modulo p divides p - 1.\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative order of 2017 modulo p divides p - 1. Therefore, 2017^{k} ≡ 1 mod p whenever k is a multiple of the order, which divides p - 1.\n\nTherefore, if we can find the exponent 2025! modulo the order of 2017 modulo p, then we can compute 2017^{2025!} mod p. However, since the order divides p - 1, and 2025! is a multiple of p - 1 for any prime p ≤ 2025, except perhaps when p - 1 is larger than 2025!? Wait, no, p is at most 2025, so p - 1 is at most 2024. Since 2025! is a multiple of all numbers up to 2025, including p - 1 for any prime p ≤ 2025. Therefore, 2025! is a multiple of p - 1 for any prime p ≤ 2025. Therefore, 2025! ≡ 0 mod (p - 1). Therefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1^{k} ≡ 1 mod p, where k is some integer. Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, more precisely, since the order of 2017 modulo p divides p - 1, and since 2025! is a multiple of p - 1 (because p - 1 ≤ 2024 < 2025, so p - 1 divides 2025!), so 2025! is a multiple of the order of 2017 modulo p, so 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 mod p.\n\nTherefore, for any prime p ≤ 2025 and p ≠ 2017, we have \\( 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 \\mod p \\). Therefore, \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ 0 \\mod p \\). So, for any prime p ≤ 2025 except p = 2017, the expression \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by p.\n\nAdditionally, we already know that \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ -1 mod 2017 \\), as we saw earlier.\n\nTherefore, putting this all together, the number \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by all primes p ≤ 2025 except possibly p = 2017. Wait, but actually, for p = 2017, we have \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ -1 mod 2017 \\), so it's not divisible by 2017. Therefore, the number \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by all primes ≤ 2025 except 2017. Therefore, the prime factors of \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) include all primes ≤ 2025 except 2017. Hmm, but how does this help us find the remainder modulo 2025!?\n\nWell, since 2025! is the product of all integers from 1 to 2025, which includes all primes ≤ 2025, except that 2025! is divisible by each of those primes. Wait, actually, 2025! is divisible by all primes ≤ 2025, so the modulus 2025! is divisible by all those primes. The number \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by all primes ≤ 2025 except 2017, so it is divisible by all primes dividing 2025! except 2017. Therefore, \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by the product of all primes ≤ 2025 except 2017, but not by 2017. Hmm.\n\nBut how does that help us compute \\( r = (2017^{2025!} - 1) \\mod 2025! \\)?\n\nPerhaps we can consider that since \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by all prime powers in 2025! except for the prime 2017? Wait, not exactly. The prime 2017 is present in 2025! with exponent 1, so if \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is not divisible by 2017, then when we take modulo 2025!, which includes 2017 as a factor, the remainder r will not be divisible by 2017. But since \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by all other prime factors of 2025! with their respective exponents?\n\nWait, perhaps I need to think in terms of the Carmichael function or something else here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that 2025! is a multiple of the least common multiple of all numbers from 1 to 2025, which is certainly a huge number, but perhaps more importantly, since \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is congruent to 0 modulo all primes p ≤ 2025 except p = 2017, and congruent to -1 modulo 2017, then by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, since the moduli are the prime powers in the factorization of 2025!? Wait, but 2025! is not square-free, so we can't directly apply the Chinese Remainder Theorem over the primes, but over the prime powers.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can consider that \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by each prime power \\( p^k \\) dividing 2025! except possibly for the prime 2017? Wait, not necessarily. For example, take a prime p ≠ 2017, then \\( p^k \\) divides 2025! for some k ≥ 1. Since \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divisible by p, but is it divisible by higher powers of p?\n\nHmm, that's a good question. For instance, take p=2, which is a prime less than 2017. Then, 2 divides 2025!, so 2^k divides 2025! for some k. Now, is \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) divisible by 2^k? Well, since 2017 is odd, 2017 ≡ 1 mod 2, so 2017^{anything} ≡ 1 mod 2, so 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ 1 - 1 ≡ 0 mod 2, so divisible by 2. But what about mod 4? 2017 is 1 mod 4, so 2017^{anything} ≡ 1 mod 4, so 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ 0 mod 4. Similarly, mod 8? 2017 ≡ 1 mod 8, so 2017^{anything} ≡ 1 mod 8, so again, 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ 0 mod 8. Continuing this way, since 2017 ≡ 1 mod 2^m for any m, because 2017 is 1 more than a multiple of 2, but actually 2017 mod 2 is 1, mod 4 is 1, mod 8 is 1, etc. Wait, let me check:\n\n2017 divided by 2 is 1008.5, so 2017 = 2×1008 + 1, so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 2.\n\n2017 divided by 4: 2017 / 4 = 504.25, so 4×504 = 2016, so 2017 = 4×504 + 1, so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 4.\n\n2017 divided by 8: 8×252 = 2016, so 2017 = 8×252 + 1, so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 8.\n\nSimilarly, 2017 mod 16: 16×126 = 2016, so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 16.\n\nContinuing, 2017 ≡ 1 mod 2^k for any k, because 2017 - 1 = 2016, and 2016 is divisible by 2^5 (since 2016 = 2^5 × 3^2 × 7), so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 32, etc., up to mod 2^5. Wait, but 2016 is divisible by 2^5 = 32, but not by 2^6 = 64. Let me see:\n\n2016 divided by 32: 2016 / 32 = 63, so 32 × 63 = 2016. So yes, 2016 is divisible by 32, so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 32. But 2016 divided by 64 is 31.5, so not an integer, so 2016 is not divisible by 64, so 2017 ≡ 1 mod 32 but not mod 64. Therefore, 2017 ≡ 1 mod 2^5 but not mod 2^6.\n\nTherefore, in general, 2017 ≡ 1 mod 2^k for k up to 5, but not beyond that. Therefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1^{2025!} ≡ 1 mod 2^k for k ≤ 5, but mod 2^6, we have 2017 ≡ 1 + 32 mod 64, so 2017 ≡ 33 mod 64. Wait, let me compute 2017 mod 64:\n\n64 × 31 = 1984, so 2017 - 1984 = 33, so yes, 2017 ≡ 33 mod 64. Therefore, 2017 ≡ 1 mod 32 but ≡ 33 mod 64. Therefore, 2017 ≡ 1 mod 32 but not 1 mod 64. Therefore, when we raise 2017 to a power, modulo 64, we have:\n\nLet me compute 2017 mod 64 = 33, so 2017 ≡ 33 mod 64.\n\nSo 2017^1 ≡ 33 mod 64,\n\n2017^2 ≡ 33^2 mod 64,\n\n33^2 = 1089; 1089 divided by 64: 64×17 = 1088, so 1089 ≡ 1 mod 64,\n\nTherefore, 2017^2 ≡ 1 mod 64,\n\nThen 2017^3 ≡ 2017 × 1 ≡ 33 mod 64,\n\n2017^4 ≡ 1 mod 64,\n\nSo the pattern cycles every 2 exponents: 33, 1, 33, 1, etc.\n\nTherefore, the exponent 2025! is even, since 2025! includes 2 as a factor, so 2025! is even. Therefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 mod 64.\n\nTherefore, 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ 0 mod 64.\n\nWait, so even though 2017 ≡ 33 mod 64, since the exponent is even, we get 1 mod 64. So in fact, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 mod 64, so 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ 0 mod 64.\n\nSimilarly, for higher powers of 2? Let's see mod 128.\n\nCompute 2017 mod 128:\n\n128 × 15 = 1920, so 2017 - 1920 = 97, so 2017 ≡ 97 mod 128.\n\nSo 2017 ≡ 97 mod 128. Let's compute 97^2 mod 128:\n\n97^2 = (100 - 3)^2 = 10000 - 600 + 9 = 9409. 9409 divided by 128: 128 × 73 = 9344, so 9409 - 9344 = 65, so 97^2 ≡ 65 mod 128.\n\n97^4 ≡ (65)^2 mod 128. 65^2 = 4225. 4225 divided by 128: 128 × 33 = 4224, so 4225 ≡ 1 mod 128.\n\nSo 97^4 ≡ 1 mod 128. Therefore, the order of 97 mod 128 is 4.\n\nTherefore, 2017^{2025!} mod 128: since 2025! is a multiple of 4 (since 4 divides 2025!), so exponent is multiple of 4, so 97^{4k} ≡ (97^4)^k ≡ 1^k ≡ 1 mod 128. Therefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 mod 128, so 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ 0 mod 128.\n\nContinuing similarly, mod 256:\n\nCompute 2017 mod 256:\n\n256 × 7 = 1792, 2017 - 1792 = 225, so 2017 ≡ 225 mod 256.\n\nCompute 225^2 mod 256:\n\n225^2 = (200 + 25)^2 = 200² + 2×200×25 + 25² = 40000 + 10000 + 625 = 50625.\n\n50625 divided by 256: 256 × 197 = 256*(200 - 3) = 51200 - 768 = 50432. 50625 - 50432 = 193, so 225^2 ≡ 193 mod 256.\n\nThen 225^4 ≡ (193)^2 mod 256. 193^2 = (200 - 7)^2 = 40000 - 2800 + 49 = 37249.\n\n37249 divided by 256: 256 × 145 = 256*(100 + 45) = 25600 + 11520 = 37120. 37249 - 37120 = 129. So 193^2 ≡ 129 mod 256.\n\n225^8 ≡ (129)^2 mod 256. 129^2 = 16641. 16641 divided by 256: 256×65 = 16640, so 16641 ≡ 1 mod 256.\n\nSo 225^8 ≡ 1 mod 256. Therefore, the order of 225 mod 256 divides 8. Since 225^4 ≡ 193 mod 256 ≠ 1, so order is 8.\n\nTherefore, since 2025! is a multiple of 8 (since 8 divides 2025!), so exponent 2025! is a multiple of 8, so 225^{2025!} ≡ (225^8)^{k} ≡ 1^k ≡ 1 mod 256. Therefore, 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 mod 256, so 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ 0 mod 256.\n\nContinuing this way, perhaps for each prime power p^k dividing 2025!, we can show that \\( 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 \\mod p^k \\), except when p = 2017? Wait, but when p = 2017, we have:\n\nFor p = 2017, which is a prime factor in 2025! with exponent 1. So modulus 2017^1 = 2017. Earlier, we saw that \\( 2017^{2025!} ≡ 0 \\mod 2017 \\), so \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ -1 mod 2017 \\), so not congruent to 0 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, putting this together, for every prime power p^k dividing 2025! where p ≠ 2017, we have \\( 2017^{2025!} ≡ 1 \\mod p^k \\), so \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ 0 \\mod p^k \\). For the prime power p^k = 2017^1, we have \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nTherefore, the number \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 \\) is divisible by all the prime power factors of 2025! except for the prime power 2017^1. Therefore, \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 \\) is divisible by \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), since 2025! = product_{p prime} p^{k_p}, so removing the 2017^1 term gives us \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), and \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 \\) is divisible by all the other prime powers, hence by \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 = m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), where m is some integer. Therefore, when we take this number modulo 2025!, we have:\n\n\\( 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ 0 \\mod \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), but modulo 2025! it could be any multiple of \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) up to 2025!.\n\nWait, but actually, since \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 = m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), then modulo 2025!:\n\n\\( 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\mod 2025! \\).\n\nBut \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\times 2017 = 2025! \\), so \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\times 2017 ≡ 0 \\mod 2025! \\). Wait, but actually, \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) multiplied by any integer m will give a multiple of \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), but not necessarily a multiple of 2025!.\n\nWait, but in our expression, \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 = m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), so when we take modulo 2025!, that's equivalent to:\n\n\\( 2017^{2025!} -1 ≡ m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\mod 2025! \\).\n\nBut since \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\times 2017 = 2025! \\), so \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) is an integer, and multiplying it by 2017 gives 2025!.\n\nTherefore, \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\times k \\mod 2025! \\) cycles every 2017 multiples. However, without knowing m, it's hard to say exactly what the remainder is. Wait, but perhaps m is less than 2017? Because the remainder when divided by 2025! must be less than 2025!.\n\nWait, no, m can be any integer. However, the expression \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 \\) is equal to \\( m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), so when we take modulo 2025!, the remainder r is equal to \\( (m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017}) \\mod 2025! \\). Since \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) is an integer, and m is an integer, then \\( m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) can be written as \\( k \\times 2025! + r \\), where r is the remainder. But actually, since \\( m \\times \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) is already a multiple of \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), but not necessarily of 2025!.\n\nWait, this seems going in circles.\n\nWait, but from the Chinese Remainder Theorem perspective, we know that \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 \\) is congruent to 0 modulo all prime power factors of 2025! except for the prime 2017. So, in other words, \\( 2017^{2025!} -1 \\) is congruent to -1 modulo 2017 and congruent to 0 modulo all other prime power factors of 2025!.\n\nTherefore, the remainder r when divided by 2025! must satisfy:\n\nr ≡ -1 mod 2017,\n\nand\n\nr ≡ 0 mod (2025! / 2017).\n\nBecause 2025! factors into 2017 × (2025! / 2017), and since r ≡ 0 mod (2025! / 2017) and r ≡ -1 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, we can write r as:\n\nr = k × (2025! / 2017) for some integer k,\n\nand we also have:\n\nk × (2025! / 2017) ≡ -1 mod 2017.\n\nWe need to solve for k in this congruence.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet M = 2025! / 2017,\n\nand we have:\n\nr = k × M,\n\nand we need:\n\nk × M ≡ -1 mod 2017.\n\nSo, solving for k:\n\nk ≡ (-1) × M^{-1} mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute the modular inverse of M modulo 2017, then k ≡ -M^{-1} mod 2017, and then r = k × M mod 2025!.\n\nBut since r must be less than 2025!, then k must be the unique integer in the range [0, 2017 - 1] such that k ≡ -M^{-1} mod 2017, so k is between 0 and 2016, and then r = k × M.\n\nWait, but actually, since r = k × M must be less than 2025! (because it's the remainder when divided by 2025!), and since M = 2025! / 2017, then k must satisfy k × M < 2025!.\n\nTherefore, k < 2025! / M = 2025! / (2025! / 2017) ) = 2017.\n\nTherefore, k must be in the range 0 ≤ k ≤ 2016, so exactly k ≡ -M^{-1} mod 2017, so k = (-M^{-1} mod 2017). Therefore, k is the unique integer in [0, 2016] satisfying that congruence.\n\nTherefore, once we compute M^{-1} mod 2017, then k = (-M^{-1}) mod 2017, and then r = k × M.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to computing M^{-1} mod 2017, where M = 2025! / 2017.\n\nWait, but how do we compute M^{-1} mod 2017?\n\nFirst, note that M = (2025!)/2017. Since 2025! is divisible by 2017, so M is an integer. We need to compute M mod 2017, then find its inverse modulo 2017.\n\nBecause to compute M^{-1} mod 2017, we need to know M mod 2017 first.\n\nSo, compute M mod 2017, where M = 2025! / 2017.\n\nHmm, okay, let's compute M mod 2017.\n\nFirst, note that 2025! is the product of all integers from 1 to 2025. Therefore, 2025! = 1 × 2 × 3 × ... × 2017 × ... × 2025.\n\nTherefore, when we divide by 2017, we get M = (2025!)/2017 = [1 × 2 × ... × 2016 × 2018 × ... × 2025].\n\nTherefore, M is the product of all integers from 1 to 2025 except for 2017.\n\nTherefore, M = (2025!)/2017 = \\prod_{k=1}^{2025} k / 2017 = \\prod_{k=1, k≠2017}^{2025} k.\n\nSo, to compute M mod 2017, we can note that in the product M, every integer from 1 to 2025 except 2017 is included. Since we are working modulo 2017, we can reduce each term modulo 2017 before multiplying.\n\nHowever, since 2017 is a prime number, by Wilson's theorem, we know that (p-1)! ≡ -1 mod p, where p is prime. Here, p = 2017, so 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017.\n\nBut in our case, M includes numbers from 1 to 2025 except 2017. Let's see:\n\nFirst, note that 2025 is greater than 2017, so in the product M, we have numbers from 1 up to 2016, then from 2018 up to 2025. The numbers from 2018 to 2025 can be written as 2017 + 1, 2017 + 2, ..., 2017 + 8. Since 2025 - 2017 = 8, so those terms are 2017 + k where k ranges from 1 to 8.\n\nTherefore, modulo 2017, each term 2017 + k ≡ k mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, the product M mod 2017 can be broken into two parts:\n\n1. The product of numbers from 1 to 2016, excluding 2017 (but 2017 isn't in that range anyway), so that's just 1 × 2 × ... × 2016 mod 2017.\n\n2. The product of the terms from 2018 to 2025, which modulo 2017 are equivalent to 1 × 2 × ... × 8 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, M mod 2017 ≡ [ (2016! ) × (8!) ] mod 2017.\n\nBut 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017 by Wilson's theorem, since 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, M mod 2017 ≡ [ (-1) × (8!) ] mod 2017.\n\nThe