nce r must be less than M = 2025!, which is a multiple of 2017, the value of k can be found as the unique solution modulo 2017, but scaled appropriately?\n\nWait, actually, since r = k * P must be less than M = 2025!, and since P is already a product of all primes except 2017 up to 2025, which is a huge number, but 2025! is even larger, so k can be 0, 1, 2, ..., up to floor(M / P). But since we need r ≡ -1 mod 2017, so the smallest positive k that satisfies \\( k \\times P ≡ -1 \\mod 2017 \\) will give us the minimal r, but since r must be less than M, which is a multiple of 2017, perhaps the solution is unique modulo 2017 * something? Hmm, perhaps this is getting too abstract.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps since we can write r = (some multiple of P) such that r ≡ -1 mod 2017, and since P is co-prime to 2017? Wait, is P coprime to 2017?\n\nSince P is the product of all primes up to 2025 except 2017, and 2017 is a prime not included in P, so yes, P and 2017 are coprime. Therefore, P has an inverse modulo 2017.\n\nTherefore, the equation \\( k \\times P \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\) has a unique solution modulo 2017. Let me denote \\( k_0 \\) as the solution in the range \\( 0 \\leq k_0 < 2017 \\). Therefore, the general solution for k is \\( k = k_0 + t \\times 2017 \\), for integer t. Then, substituting back into r:\n\n\\( r = k \\times P = (k_0 + t \\times 2017) \\times P \\)\n\nBut since r must be less than \\( M = 2025! \\), we need to find t such that \\( (k_0 + t \\times 2017) \\times P < 2025! \\). Since \\( P \\) is a product of primes less than 2017 (except 2017 itself, but 2017 is excluded), so P is less than 2025! but still, the value of t can be 0 or 1? Because even with t=1, \\( (k_0 + 2017) \\times P \\) might already exceed 2025!.\n\nWait, but P is the product of all primes up to 2025 except 2017. Let's see, 2025! is the product of all integers from 1 to 2025, so it's much larger than P, which is just the product of primes up to 2025 except 2017. Therefore, the ratio \\( \\frac{2025!}{P} \\) is a huge number, so there are many multiples of P less than 2025!.\n\nBut we need the smallest non-negative solution for k such that \\( r = k \\times P < 2025! \\), but also satisfying \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\). Since the solutions for k are of the form \\( k = k_0 + t \\times 2017 \\), for integer t ≥ 0, so the minimal solution is \\( k_0 \\), but we have to check if \\( k_0 \\times P < 2025! \\). Since \\( k_0 < 2017 \\), and \\( P \\) is a product of primes up to 2025 except 2017, so P is at least as big as the product of all primes less than 2017, which is still enormous, but likely much smaller than 2025!.\n\nWait, but actually, \\( 2025! \\) includes all the factors of P, so \\( 2025! = P \\times Q \\), where Q is the product of all the composite numbers and the prime 2017 from 1 to 2025. Therefore, \\( Q = \\frac{2025!}{P} \\), which is an integer. Therefore, since \\( Q \\) is an integer greater than 1 (because 2025! includes more factors than just the primes in P), then \\( P \\times Q = 2025! \\), so \\( P \\times (Q) = 2025! \\), so the maximum multiple of P less than 2025! is \\( P \\times (Q - 1) \\). Therefore, the possible values of k are integers from 0 up to \\( Q - 1 \\). Therefore, since k must satisfy \\( k \\equiv k_0 \\mod 2017 \\), the minimal positive solution is k = k_0, provided that \\( k_0 \\leq Q - 1 \\). Since \\( Q = \\frac{2025!}{P} \\), which is certainly greater than 2017 (since 2025! is much larger than 2017), so k_0, which is less than 2017, is less than Q, so k = k_0 is acceptable, giving r = k_0 * P, which is less than Q * P = 2025!.\n\nTherefore, the unique solution is r = k_0 * P, where k_0 is the solution to \\( k_0 * P \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nTherefore, to compute r / 2025! = (k_0 * P) / (2025! ) = (k_0 / Q ) since Q = 2025! / P, so this simplifies to \\( \\frac{k_0}{Q} \\).\n\nBut Q is a huge number, so this fraction is going to be a very small number, but perhaps it can be exp