fore, Q = (2025! ) / P.\n\nBut Q is an integer, as established earlier. However, unless there is some relation between k₀ and Q, I don't see an immediate simplification.\n\nWait a second, perhaps notice that r ≡ -1 mod 2017, so r + 1 is divisible by 2017. Also, since r is divisible by all primes except 2017, then r + 1 is congruent to 1 mod p for each prime p ≠ 2017 dividing M (since r ≡ 0 mod p, so r + 1 ≡ 1 mod p). Therefore, r + 1 is congruent to 1 modulo all primes p ≠ 2017, and congruent to 0 modulo 2017. Therefore, r + 1 is divisible by 2017 and co-prime to all other primes? Wait, not necessarily co-prime, but r + 1 ≡ 1 mod p for each p ≠ 2017, so r + 1 is congruent to 1 modulo those primes, so it is not divisible by any of them, except possibly 2017. Therefore, r + 1 is equal to 2017 multiplied by some integer that is co-prime to all primes except possibly 2017. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, since r + 1 is divisible by 2017, and r is divisible by all other primes, then r + 1 is of the form 2017 × m, where m is an integer not divisible by any prime less than or equal to 2025 except possibly 2017. Wait, but m could have other prime factors?\n\nActually, since r + 1 = 2017 × m, and r is divisible by all primes p ≠ 2017 up to 2025, so m must be such that 2017 × m ≡ 1 mod p for each prime p ≠ 2017. Since r ≡ 0 mod p, so r + 1 ≡ 1 mod p, so 2017 × m ≡ 1 mod p, so m ≡ (1 / 2017) mod p for each prime p ≠ 2017.\n\nTherefore, m must be congruent to the inverse of 2017 modulo each prime p ≠ 2017. Therefore, m is the solution to the system of congruences:\n\nm ≡ (2017^{-1}) mod p for each prime p ≠ 2017 dividing M.\n\nTherefore, m is the solution modulo the product of all primes p ≠ 2017, which is exactly P. Therefore, m ≡ some value mod P, so m = m₀ + t * P, for some integer t. Therefore, r + 1 = 2017*(m₀ + t*P), so r = 2017*m₀ + 2017*t*P - 1. Wait, but this seems getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, since m must satisfy m ≡ (2017^{-1}) mod p for each prime p ≠ 2017, then