 the problem's uniqueness is justified.\n\nBut to find the exact value of k, perhaps we can assume that k is a rational number, or some fraction that makes the equation manageable?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the following substitution:\n\nLet me let’s set t = sqrt(k - 4),\n\nsince k >4,\n\nthen, k = t² +4,\n\nthen,\n\na² = (k/2 -1) = (t² +4)/2 -1 = (t²)/2 + 2 -1 = t²/2 +1,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nb² = (k/3 -1) = (t² +4)/3 -1 = t²/3 +4/3 -1 = t²/3 +1/3,\n\nc² = (k/4 -1) = (t² +4)/4 -1 = t²/4 +1 -1 = t²/4,\n\nTherefore,\n\na = sqrt( t²/2 +1 ),\n\nb = sqrt( t²/3 +1/3 ),\n\nc = sqrt( t²/4 ) = t/2,\n\nsince c>0,\n\nTherefore, c = t/2,\n\nNow, the expression ab + bc + ca =1 can be written in terms of t:\n\nFirst, compute a, b in terms of t:\n\na = sqrt( (t²)/2 +1 ),\n\nb = sqrt( (t²)/3 + 1/3 ) = sqrt( (t² +1)/3 ),\n\nc = t/2,\n\nTherefore,\n\nab = sqrt( (t²/2 +1 ) ) * sqrt( (t² +1)/3 ),\n\nbc = sqrt( (t² +1)/3 ) * (t/2),\n\nca = (t/2) * sqrt( t²/2 +1 ),\n\nSo, the equation ab + bc + ca =1 becomes:\n\nsqrt( (t²/2 +1 ) * (t² +1)/3 ) + (t/2)*sqrt( (t² +1)/3 ) + (t/2)*sqrt( t²/2 +1 ) =1.\n\nHmm, still complicated, but perhaps we can factor terms or make a substitution here?\n\nLet me denote s = t²,\n\nthen t = sqrt(s),\n\nand since t>0,\n\nthen,\n\nFirst term inside sqrt:\n\n(t²/2 +1) = (s/2 +1),\n\n(t² +1)/3 = (s +1)/3,\n\nThus,\n\nFirst term: sqrt( (s/2 +1)*(s +1)/3 ),\n\nSecond term: (sqrt(s)/2)*sqrt( (s +1)/3 ),\n\nThird term: (sqrt(s)/2)*sqrt( s/2 +1 ),\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\nsqrt( (s/2 +1)(s +1)/3 ) + (sqrt(s)/2)*sqrt( (s +1)/3 ) + (sqrt(s)/2)*sqrt( s/2 +1 ) =1.\n\nHmm, still complex. Perhaps set u = sqrt(s), so that s = u²,\n\nthen t = u,\n\nso,\n\nFirst term: sqrt( (u²/2 +1)(u² +1)/3 ),\n\nSecond term: (u/2)*sqrt( (u² +1)/3 ),\n\nThird term: (u/2)*sqrt( u²/2 +1 ),\n\nHmm, so equation becomes:\n\nsqrt( ( (u²/2 +1)(u² +1) ) /3 ) + (u/2)*sqrt( (u² +1)/3 ) + (u/2)*sqrt( u²/2 +1 ) =1,\n\nHmm, still not obviously helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps try to assume tha