ms of k,\n\nso perhaps write everything in terms of k,\n\nthen set the equation equal to 1 and solve for k numerically?\n\nBut since this is a competition problem, likely an exact solution exists, so maybe there is a substitution that can be made.\n\nWait, let me think of the following:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me set p = sqrt(x y),\n\nq = sqrt(y z),\n\nr = sqrt(z x),\n\nthen, the equation is p + q + r =1,\n\nand we have:\n\np = sqrt( x y ) = sqrt( (k/2 -1)(k/3 -1) ),\n\nq = sqrt( y z ) = sqrt( (k/3 -1)(k/4 -1) ),\n\nr = sqrt( z x ) = sqrt( (k/4 -1)(k/2 -1) ),\n\nAlso, note that p q r = sqrt( x y ) sqrt( y z ) sqrt( z x ) = sqrt( x^2 y^2 z^2 ) = x y z,\n\nTherefore, p q r = x y z,\n\nBut not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider ratios between p, q, r?\n\nLet me compute the ratios:\n\np / q = sqrt( (k/2 -1)(k/3 -1) ) / sqrt( (k/3 -1)(k/4 -1) ) = sqrt( (k/2 -1)/(k/4 -1) ),\n\nSimilarly,\n\np / r = sqrt( (k/2 -1)(k/3 -1) ) / sqrt( (k/4 -1)(k/2 -1) ) = sqrt( (k/3 -1)/(k/4 -1) ),\n\nHmm, perhaps this can lead to some proportionality?\n\nAlternatively, maybe set t = k/12, so that k =12 t,\n\nthen,\n\nk/2 =6t,\n\nk/3 =4t,\n\nk/4 =3t,\n\nTherefore,\n\nx =6t -1,\n\ny=4t -1,\n\nz=3t -1,\n\nTherefore, the expressions become:\n\nsqrt( (6t -1)(4t -1) ) + sqrt( (4t -1)(3t -1) ) + sqrt( (3t -1)(6t -1) ) =1,\n\nand we need to solve for t,\n\nwith constraints that 6t -1 >0 ⇒ t>1/6,\n\n4t -1>0 ⇒ t>1/4,\n\n3t -1>0 ⇒t>1/3,\n\nso overall t>1/3.\n\nHmm, maybe this substitution simplifies things?\n\nLet me set t =1/3 + s, where s>0,\n\nthen,\n\n6t -1=6*(1/3 +s) -1=2 +6s -1=1 +6s,\n\n4t -1=4*(1/3 +s) -1=4/3 +4s -1=1/3 +4s,\n\n3t -1=3*(1/3 +s) -1=1 +3s -1=3s,\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equation,\n\nsqrt( (1 +6s)(1/3 +4s) ) + sqrt( (1/3 +4s)(3s) ) + sqrt( (3s)(1 +6s) ) =1.\n\nHmm, still complicated, but maybe with s small?\n\nSince in the approximate solution earlier, when k≈4.1742,\n\nthen t =k/12≈4.1742/12≈0.348,\n\nso s = t -1/3≈0.348 -0.333≈0.015,\n\nso s is small.\n\nTherefore, perhaps expand in terms of s as a small parameter?\n\nLet me try that.\n\nLet me let s be small, so s≈0.015,\n\nthen,\n\nFirst term inside first sqrt:\n\n(1 +6s)(1/3 +4s)=1*(1/3) +1*(4s) +6s*(1/3) +6s*4s,\n\n=1/3 +4s +2s +24s²,\n\n=1/3 +6s +24s²,\n\nSecond term inside second sqrt:\n\n(1/3 +4s)(3s)= (1/3)(3s) +4s*(3s)=s +12s²,\n\nThird term inside third sqrt:\n\n(3s)(1 +6s)=3s +18s²,\n\nTherefore,\n\nFirst sqrt term: sqrt(1/3 +6s +24s²),\n\nSecond sqrt term: sqrt( s +12s² ),\n\nThird sqrt term: sqrt(3s +18s²),\n\nNow, since s is small, approximate each sqrt term using Taylor series:\n\nFirst term:\n\nsqrt(1/3 +6s +24s²) ≈ sqrt(1/3) + (6s +24s²)/(2 sqrt(1/3)) - ( (6s)^2 )/(8 (1/3)^(3/2) ) + ...,\n\nWait, maybe better to write as sqrt(a + b) ≈ sqrt(a) + b/(2 sqrt(a)) - b²/(8 a^(3/2)) + ...,\n\nLet me denote for the first term:\n\na =1/3,\n\nb=6s +24s²,\n\nso sqrt(a + b) ≈ sqrt(a) + (b)/(2 sqrt(a)) - (b²)/(8 a^(3/2)),\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nFirst term:\n\nsqrt(1/3 +6s +24s²) ≈ sqrt(1/3) + (6s +24s²)/(2 sqrt(1/3)) - ( (6s)^2 )/(8 (1/3)^(3/2) ),\n\nWait, actually, the second term in the expansion is (b)/(2 sqrt(a)), but b itself is 6s +24s²,\n\nso,\n\nFirst term ≈ sqrt(1/3) + (6s +24s²)/(2 sqrt(1/3)) - ( (6s)^2 )/(8 (1/3)^(3/2) ) + ...,\n\nWait, but actually, the expansion is:\n\nsqrt(a + b) = sqrt(a) + (b)/(2 sqrt(a)) - (b²)/(8 a^(3/2)) + ...,\n\nwhere b is the small perturbation. But here, b is 6s +24s², which is of order s and s²,\n\nso let's compute up to first order in s,\n\nFirst term ≈ sqrt(1/3) + (6s)/(2 sqrt(1/3)),\n\n= sqrt(1/3) + (3s)/sqrt(1/3),\n\n= (1/√3) + 3s √3,\n\nSimilarly, second term sqrt(s +12s²) ≈ sqrt(s) + (12s²)/(2 sqrt(s)),\n\nWait, but s is small, so sqrt(s +12s²) ≈ sqrt(s) + (12s²)/(2 sqrt(s)),\n\nbut this is sqrt(s) +6s^(3/2),\n\nBut since s is small, but the leading term is sqrt(s), which is of order s^{1/2}, which is larger than s,\n\nHmm, so perhaps the expansion isn't converging here because s is not that small?\n\nWait, in our case, s≈0.015,\n\nso sqrt(s)≈0.122, which is not that small compared to the other terms?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this approach is not the best.\n\nAlternatively, given that in the approximate solution, a + b + c ≈1.877,\n\nand since the problem is likely expecting an exact answer, perhaps the answer is sqrt(7), which is approximately 2.645, no, too big. Or sqrt( (something) ).\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is 2, but my approximation was 1.877,\n\nWait, 1.877 is close to sqrt(3.5) ≈1.8708,\n\nsqrt(3.5)=sqrt(7/2)= (√14)/2≈1.8708,\n\nHmm, which is very close to my approximate value of 1.877.\n\nPerhaps the exact value is sqrt(7/2), which is sqrt(14)/2?\n\nLet me check with k such that a + b + c = sqrt(14)/2≈1.8708,\n\nBut in my approximate calculation with k≈4.1742, I had a + b + c≈1.877,\n\nwhich is close but not exact. Maybe due to approximation errors?\n\nAlternatively, let me compute sqrt(14)/2≈1.8708,\n\nand my approximate a + b + c was 1.877,\n\ndifference is about 0.006, which might be due to the approximated k value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the exact value is sqrt(14)/2?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if with a + b + c = sqrt(14)/2,\n\nthen squaring both sides,\n\n(a + b + c)^2 = (14)/4 = 3.5,\n\nBut expanding,\n\na² + b² + c² + 2(ab + bc + ca) =3.5,\n\nWe know that ab + bc + ca=1,\n\nso,\n\na² + b² + c² + 2(1)=3.5 ⇒a² + b² + c²=1.5,\n\nNow, compute a² + b² + c² from the expressions in terms of k:\n\na² =k/2 -1,\n\nb² =k/3 -1,\n\nc² =k/4 -1,\n\nTherefore,\n\na² + b² + c² = (k/2 -1)+(k/3 -1)+(k/4 -1)=k/2 +k/3 +k/4 -3,\n\nCompute k/2 +k/3 +k/4,\n\ncommon denominator 12,\n\n=6k/12 +4k/12 +3k/12=13k/12,\n\nTherefore,\n\na² + b² + c²=13k/12 -3,\n\nSet this equal to 1.5,\n\nso,\n\n13k/12 -3 = 1.5 ⇒13k/12=4.5 ⇒k= (4.5 *12)/13=54/13≈4.1538,\n\nHmm, so if k=54/13≈4.1538,\n\nthen,\n\na + b + c would be sqrt(14)/2≈1.8708,\n\nBut let me check whether with k=54/13≈4.1538,\n\nwhether ab + bc + ca=1,\n\nLet me compute a, b, c:\n\na = sqrt(k/2 -1)=sqrt( (54/1