substituting into the first set of equations,\n\n2(a² +1)=2*( (p²)/2 +1 )= p² +2,\n\n3(b² +1)=3*( (q²)/3 +1 )= q² +3,\n\n4(c² +1)=4*( (r²)/4 +1 )= r² +4,\n\nTherefore, setting these equal to k,\n\nwe have:\n\np² +2 =k,\n\nq² +3=k,\n\nr² +4=k,\n\nSo, p² =k -2,\n\nq² =k -3,\n\nr² =k -4,\n\nTherefore, p = sqrt(k -2),\n\nq = sqrt(k -3),\n\nr = sqrt(k -4),\n\nNow, the second equation is ab + bc + ca =1,\n\nexpressed in terms of p, q, r:\n\nab + bc + ca = (p/√2)(q/√3) + (q/√3)(r/2) + (r/2)(p/√2) =1,\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: (p q)/(√6),\n\nSecond term: (q r)/(2√3),\n\nThird term: (r p)/(2√2),\n\nTherefore,\n\n(p q)/√6 + (q r)/(2√3) + (r p)/(2√2) =1,\n\nNow, substituting p= sqrt(k-2),\n\nq= sqrt(k-3),\n\nr= sqrt(k-4),\n\nThus,\n\n[ sqrt(k-2) sqrt(k-3) ] / sqrt(6) +\n\n[ sqrt(k-3) sqrt(k-4) ] / (2 sqrt(3)) +\n\n[ sqrt(k-4) sqrt(k-2) ] / (2 sqrt(2)) =1,\n\nHmm, still complicated, but maybe this form is more manageable?\n\nLet me see if we can factor something out or find a common term.\n\nLet me factor out 1/sqrt(6) from the first term,\n\nand see the other terms:\n\nFirst term: sqrt( (k-2)(k-3) ) / sqrt(6),\n\nSecond term: sqrt( (k-3)(k-4) ) / (2 sqrt(3)) = sqrt( (k-3)(k-4) ) / (2 sqrt(3)),\n\nThird term: sqrt( (k-4)(k-2) ) / (2 sqrt(2)),\n\nHmm, perhaps express all terms with denominator sqrt(6):\n\nFirst term: sqrt( (k-2)(k-3) ) / sqrt(6),\n\nSecond term: [ sqrt( (k-3)(k-4) ) / (2 sqrt(3)) ] = [ sqrt( (k-3)(k-4) ) / (2 sqrt(3)) ] * [ sqrt(2)/sqrt(2) ] = sqrt( 2 (k-3)(k-4) ) / (2 sqrt(6)),\n\nThird term: [ sqrt( (k-4)(k-2) ) / (2 sqrt(2)) ] = [ sqrt( (k-4)(k-2) ) / (2 sqrt(2)) ] * [ sqrt(3)/sqrt(3) ] = sqrt( 3 (k-4)(k-2) ) / (2 sqrt(6)),\n\nTherefore, combining all terms over a common denominator of sqrt(6):\n\nFirst term: sqrt( (k-2)(k-3) ),\n\nSecond term: sqrt( 2(k-3)(k-4) ) / 2,\n\nThird term: sqrt( 3(k-4)(k-2) ) / 2,\n\nAll divided by sqrt(6), and the sum equals 1,\n\nTherefore,\n\n[ sqrt( (k-2)(k-3) ) + ( sqrt( 2(k-3)(k-4) ) + sqrt( 3(k-4)(k-2) ) ) 