= 3 = 2*1 +1?\n\nN_4 = 5 = 2*N_3 - 1 = 2*3 -1=5\n\nN_5 = 11 = 2*N_4 + 3? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nLooking at the differences between numerators:\n\nFrom N_1 to N_2: 0\n\nN_2 to N_3: +2\n\nN_3 to N_4: +2\n\nN_4 to N_5: +6 = 3*2\n\nN_5 to N_6: +12 = 6*2\n\nN_6 to N_7: +24 = 12*2\n\nN_7 to N_8: +46 = 24*2 - 2\n\nN_8 to N_9: +94 = 46*2 + 2\n\nN_9 to N_10: +186 = 94*2 - 2\n\nN_10 to N_11: +372 = 186*2\n\nN_11 to N_12: +744 = 372*2\n\nN_12 to N_13: +1488 = 744*2\n\nN_13 to N_14: +2976 = 1488*2\n\nN_14 to N_15: +5954 = 2976*2 - 2\n\nN_15 to N_16: +11908 = 5954*2\n\nN_16 to N_17: +23814 = 11908*2 - 2\n\nAh! Now this seems like a clearer pattern.\n\nStarting from N_4:\n\nThe differences follow the pattern of doubling the previous difference, but every third difference (starting from N_4 to N_5) is adjusted by subtracting or adding 2 alternately:\n\nFrom N_4 to N_5: difference is +6 = 3*2 (but not sure)\n\nWait, let's see starting from N_7:\n\nThe differences after N_7 are:\n\nN_7 to N_8: +46 = 2*(24) - 2\n\nN_8 to N_9: +94 = 2*(46) + 2\n\nN_9 to N_10: +186 = 2*(94) - 2\n\nN_10 to N_11: +372 = 2*(186)\n\nN_11 to N_12: +744 = 2*(372)\n\nN_12 to N_13: +1488 = 2*(744)\n\nN_13 to N_14: +2976 = 2*(1488)\n\nN_14 to N_15: +5954 = 2*(2976) - 2\n\nN_15 to N_16: +11908 = 2*(5954)\n\nN_16 to N_17: +23814 = 2*(11908) - 2\n\nSo the pattern here is every third difference (starting from N_7 to N_8) alternates between subtracting 2 and adding 2 when doubling:\n\n- After doubling, if the step count is 1 mod 3, subtract 2; if 2 mod 3, add 2; then next is exact?\n\nWait:\n\nLooking at the differences after N_7:\n\nTerm (n):\n\nN_7 to N_8: +46 = 2*24 - 2 (24 was the previous difference before this?)\n\nWait, perhaps better to index the differences:\n\nLet me index the differences as D_k = N_{k+1} - N_k.\n\nStarting from D_1 = N_2 - N_1 = 0\n\nD_2 = N_3 - N_2 = 2\n\nD_3 = N_4 - N_3 = 2\n\nD_4 = N_5 - N_4 = 6\n\nD_5 = N_6 - N_5 = 12\n\nD_6 = N_7 - N_6 = 24\n\nD_7 = N_8 - N_7 = 46\n\nD_8 = N_9 - N_8 = 94\n\nD_9 = N_{10} - N_9 = 186\n\nD_{10} = N_{11} - N_{10} = 372\n\nD_{11} = N_{12} - N_{11} = 744\n\nD_{12} = N_{13} - N_{12} = 1488\n\nD_{13} = N_{14} - N_{13} = 2976\n\nD_{14} = N_{15} - N_{14} = 5954\n\nD_{15} = N_{16} - N_{15} = 11908\n\nD_{16} = N_{17} - N_{16} = 23814\n\nNow, starting from D_4 =6:\n\nD_4 =6\n\nD_5 =12 = D_4 *2\n\nD_6 =24 = D_5 *2\n\nD_7 =46 = D_6 *2 - 2\n\nD_8 =94 = D_7 *2 + 2\n\nD_9 =186 = D_8 *2 - 2\n\nD_{10} =372 = D_9 *2\n\nD_{11}=744 = D_{10}*2\n\nD_{12}=1488 = D_{11}*2\n\nD_{13}=2976 = D_{12}*2\n\nD_{14}=5954 = D_{13}*2 - 2\n\nD_{15}=11908 = D_{14}*2\n\nD_{16}=23814 = D_{15}*2 - 2\n\nAh, now the pattern is clear:\n\nStarting from D_4 =6:\n\nEvery three differences, the pattern is:\n\n- Double the previous difference,\n\n- Double again,\n\n- Then double and subtract 2,\n\n- Then double and add 2,\n\n- Then double,\n\n- Then double,\n\n- Then double and subtract 2,\n\n- Etc.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nFrom D_4=6:\n\nD_5 = 6*2 =12\n\nD_6 =12*2=24\n\nD_7 =24*2 -2=46\n\nD_8 =46*2 +2=94\n\nD_9=94*2 -2=186\n\nD_{10}=186*2=372\n\nD_{11}=372*2=744\n\nD_{12}=744*2=1488\n\nD_{13}=1488*2=2976\n\nD_{14}=2976*2 -2=5954\n\nD_{15}=5954*2=11908\n\nD_{16}=11908*2 -2=23814\n\nD_{17}=23814*2 +2=47630? (But we didn't compute D_{17} yet)\n\nYes, so the pattern is every third difference (starting from D_4) follows:\n\nD_{3k+1} = D_{3k}*2 - 2\n\nD_{3k+2} = D_{3k+1}*2 + 2\n\nD_{3k+3} = D_{3k+2}*2\n\nThen repeats.\n\nWait:\n\nLooking at indices:\n\nD_4 (k=1): 6\n\nD_7 (3*1+4=7? Hmm, perhaps better to see in terms of triplets:\n\nStarting from D_4:\n\nTriplet 1: D_4=6, D_5=12, D_6=24\n\nTriplet 2: D_7=46, D_8=94, D_9=186\n\nTriplet 3: D_{10}=372, D_{11}=744, D_{12}=1488\n\nTriplet 4: D_{13}=2976, D_{14}=5954, D_{15}=11908\n\nTriplet 5: D_{16}=23814, D_{17}=47630, D_{18}=95260,...\n\nEach triplet follows:\n\nFirst element: previous triplet's last element *2 -2,\n\nSecond element: first element *2 +2,\n\nThird element: second element *2,\n\nThen next triplet starts with third element *2 -2,\n\nWait, actually:\n\nTriplet 1: 6,12,24\n\nTriplet 2: 6*2 -2=10? No, D_7=46 which is 24*2 -2=46,\n\nAh, yes:\n\nTriplet 2 first element: D_7 = D_6*2 -2=24*2 -2=46,\n\nSecond element: D_8= D_7*2 +2=46*2 +2=94,\n\nThird element: D_9= D_8*2 -2=94*2 -2=186,\n\nWait no, D_9 was 186, which is 94*2 -2=186,\n\nWait, but then D_9 is the third element of the second triplet,\n\nThen Triplet 3:\n\nD_{10}= D_9*2=186*2=372,\n\nD_{11}= D_{10}*2=372*2=744,\n\nD_{12}= D_{11}*2=744*2=1488,\n\nTriplet 4:\n\nD_{13}= D_{12}*2 -2=1488*2 -2=2976 -2? Wait no: 1488*2=2976, so D_{13}=2976,\n\nWait, no, D_{13}=1488*2=2976,\n\nD_{14}= D_{13}*2 -2=2976*2 -2=5952-2=5950? But actual D_{14}=5954. Hmm, discrepancy.\n\nWait, no, according to our earlier calculation D_{14}=5954,\n\nWait let's recast:\n\nTriplet 3:\n\nD_{10}=372,\n\nD_{11}=744,\n\nD_{12}=1488,\n\nTriplet 4:\n\nFirst element D_{13}= D_{12}*2 -2=1488*2 -2=2976-2=2974? But actual D_{13}=2976.\n\nWait, this contradicts. Hmm, perhaps my assumption is incorrect.\n\nWait according to earlier calculations:\n\nD_{13}=2976,\n\nD_{14}=5954,\n\nD_{15}=11908,\n\nD_{16}=23814,\n\nSo D_{13}=2976 = D_{12}*2 =1488*2=2976,\n\nD_{14}=5954 = D_{13}*2 -2=2976*2 -2=5952-2=5950? But actual is 5954,\n\nWait inconsistency here.\n\nHmm, perhaps my earlier manual calculations were wrong? Let me recheck the numerator differences:\n\nFrom S_13 = 2977/8192,\n\nS_14 = 2977/8192 - 1/16384 = (2977*2 -1)/16384 = (5954 -1)/16384 = 5953/16384. So numerator N_{14}=5953.\n\nWait a second, so D_{14}=N_{14} - N_{13}=5953 -2977=2976.\n\nWait, so D_{14}=2976,\n\nThen S_15 = 5953/16384 + 1/32768 = (5953*2 +1)/32768 = (11906 +1)/32768 = 11907/32768,\n\nThus N_{15}=11907,\n\nD_{15}=N_{15} - N_{14}=11907 -5953=5954,\n\nD_{16}=N_{16} - N_{15}=23815 -11907=11908,\n\nD_{17}=47629 -23815=23814,\n\nAh! So the differences are:\n\nD_4 =6 (N5-N4=11-5=6)\n\nD_5=12 (N6 -N5=23-11=12)\n\nD_6=24 (N7 -N6=47-23=24)\n\nD_7=46 (N8 -N7=93-47=46)\n\nD_8=94 (N9 -N8=187-93=94)\n\nD_9=186 (N10 -N9=373-187=186)\n\nD_{10}=372 (N11 -N10=745-373=372)\n\nD_{11}=744 (N12 -N11=1489-745=744)\n\nD_{12}=1488 (N13 -N12=2977-1489=1488)\n\nD_{13}=2976 (N14 -N13=5953-2977=2976)\n\nD_{14}=5954 (N15 -N14=11907-5953=5954)\n\nD_{15}=11908 (N16 -N15=23815-11907=11908)\n\nD_{16}=23814 (N17 -N16=47629-23815=23814)\n\nSo now the differences are:\n\nD4=6,\n\nD5=12,\n\nD6=24,\n\nD7=46,\n\nD8=94,\n\nD9=186,\n\nD10=372,\n\nD11=744,\n\nD12=1488,\n\nD13=2976,\n\nD14=5954,\n\nD15=11908,\n\nD16=23814,...\n\nAh! Now the pattern is clearer:\n\nStarting from D4=6,\n\nEach D_k for k>=4 follows:\n\nD_{k} = D_{k-3} * 4 - 2,\n\nWait let's see:\n\nD4=6,\n\nD7=46 = D4*8 - 2? 6*8=48 -2=46,\n\nD10=372 = D7*8 - 2? 46*8=368 -2=366≠372,\n\nHmm not quite.\n\nAlternatively, D_{k} = 2*D_{k-1} + something.\n\nLooking at D7=46 = 2*D6 + 2*D5 - something?\n\nD6=24, D5=12: 2*24 + 2*12 = 72, which is more than 46.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nLooking at D4=6,\n\nD5=12=6*2,\n\nD6=24=12*2,\n\nD7=46=24*2 - 2,\n\nD8=94=46*2 + 2,\n\nD9=186=94*2 - 2,\n\nD10=372=186*2,\n\nD11=744=372*2,\n\nD12=1488=744*2,\n\nD13=2976=1488*2,\n\nD14=5954=2976*2 - 2,\n\nD15=11908=5954*2,\n\nD16=23814=11908*2 - 2,\n\nAh! Now it's clear:\n\nThe pattern is:\n\nStarting from D4=6,\n\nEvery three terms, the pattern is:\n\n- Double the previous difference,\n\n- Double again,\n\n- Then double and subtract 2,\n\nThen repeat the cycle:\n\n- Double the previous difference (which was the result of the subtraction),\n\n- Double again,\n\n- Then double and add 2,\n\nWait no, let me see the cycle:\n\nFrom D4=6,\n\nD5=12 (double),\n\nD6=24 (double),\n\nD7=46 (double D6 minus 2),\n\nD8=94 (double D7 plus 2),\n\nD9=186 (double D8 minus 2),\n\nD10=372 (double D9),\n\nD11=744 (double D10),\n\nD12=1488 (double D11),\n\nD13=2976 (double D12),\n\nD14=5954 (double D13 minus 2),\n\nD15=11908 (double D14),\n\nD16=23814 (double D15 minus 2),\n\nSo the cycle is every three differences after D4:\n\n- First, double twice,\n\n- Then double and subtract 2,\n\n- Then double and add 2,\n\n- Then double twice,\n\n- Then repeat?\n\nWait, not exactly, but the pattern is every third difference starting from D7:\n\nD7 = 2*D6 - 2,\n\nD8 = 2*D7 + 2,\n\nD9 = 2*D8 - 2,\n\nD10 = 2*D9,\n\nD11 = 2*D10,\n\nD12 = 2*D11,\n\nD13 = 2*D12,\n\nD14 = 2*D13 - 2,\n\nD15 = 2*D14,\n\nD16 = 2*D15 - 2,\n\nSo the pattern is:\n\nAfter D4, every set of three differences follows:\n\nD_{3k+4} = 2*D_{3k+3} - 2,\n\nD_{3k+5} = 2*D_{3k+4} + 2,\n\nD_{3k+6} = 2*D_{3k+5},\n\nThen the next triplet starts with D_{3k+7} = 2*D_{3k+6} - 2,\n\nEtc.\n\nThis seems consistent with the observed differences:\n\nFor k=0:\n\nD7=2*D6 - 2,\n\nD8=2*D7 + 2,\n\nD9=2*D8 - 2,\n\nThen D10=2*D9,\n\nD11=2*D10,\n\nD12=2*D11,\n\nThen D13=2*D12 - 2,\n\nD14=2*D13,\n\nD15=2*D14,\n\nD16=2*D15 - 2,\n\nWait D14=2*D13,\n\nD15=2*D14,\n\nD16=2*D15 -2,\n\nYes, so every three terms after the initial three (D4,D5,D6), the pattern is:\n\n- Subtract 2 after two doublings,\n\n- Then continue doubling for two steps,\n\n- Then repeat.\n\nThis seems like a consistent pattern every three differences after D4.\n\nAssuming this pattern continues indefinitely, we can model the differences D_k for large k.\n\nHowever, since the series we are summing converges, the differences N_n (numerators) grow exponentially but the terms b_n = N_n / 2^n decay exponentially, so the series converges.\n\nHowever, the problem is to find the exact sum, which likely requires recognizing that the partial sums approach a specific fraction.\n\nLooking at the partial sums:\n\nS_1 = 1/2 = 0.5\n\nS_2 = 1/4 = 0.25\n\nS_3 = 3/8 = 0.375\n\nS_4 = 5/16 ≈0.3125\n\nS_5 = 11/32 ≈0.34375\n\nS_6 = 23/64 ≈0.359375\n\nS_7 = 47/128 ≈0.3671875\n\nS_8 = 93/256 ≈0.36328125\n\nS_9 = 187/512 ≈0.365234375\n\nS_10 = 373/1024 ≈0.3642578125\n\nS_11 = 745/2048 ≈0.36376953125\n\nS_12 = 1489/4096 ≈0.363525390625\n\nS_13 = 2977/8192 ≈0.3634033203125\n\nS_14 = 5953/16384 ≈0.36334228515625\n\nS_15 = 11907/32768 ≈0.363372802734375\n\nS_16 = 238