he s_n = sgn(sin(2^n mod 2π)) will take on both positive and negative values infinitely often,\n\nbut without any periodicity.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe series is an infinite sum of terms that alternate in a non-periodic way,\n\nbut with magnitude decreasing as 1/2^n,\n\nso the total sum converges,\n\nbut to what?\n\nSince I can't find an exact pattern,\n\nmaybe the sum is simply 1/2,\n\nbecause the first term is 1/2,\n\nand the rest of the terms sum to zero?\n\nBut that can't be,\n\nsince the rest of the terms are non-zero and their sum is about 0.363 - 0.5 = -0.136,\n\nso no.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nmaybe the sum is 1/2 multiplied by something?\n\nAlternatively,\n\nmaybe the sum telescopes when considering the relation between terms,\n\nbut I couldn't find such a relation.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nmaybe the answer is 1/2,\n\nand the partial sums approach 1/2 from below,\n\nbut my calculation up to n=10 only got to ~0.364,\n\nwhich is still far from 0.5,\n\nso that's unlikely.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nmaybe I made a mistake in calculating the signs?\n\nLet me verify a few of the signs again,\n\nstarting with n=1 to n=5,\n\nsince those are the first few terms where I might have made an error.\n\nn=1: 2 radians.\n\n2 radians is between π/2 (~1.5708) and π (~3.1416),\n\nso in the second quadrant,\n\nso sine is positive,\n\nso s_1=+1,\n\ncorrect.\n\nn=2: 4 radians.\n\n4 radians is between π (~3.1416) and 3π/2 (~4.7124),\n\nso third quadrant,\n\nsine negative,\n\nso s_2=-1,\n\ncorrect.\n\nn=3: 8 radians.\n\n8 radians minus 2π (~6.2832) is 1.7168 radians,\n\nwhich is between π/2 (~1.5708) and π (~3.1416),\n\nso second quadrant,\n\nsine positive,\n\nso s_3=+1,\n\ncorrect.\n\nn=4: 16 radians.\n\n16 - 2π*2 = 16 - 12.5664 ≈ 3.4336 radians,\n\nwhich is between π and 3π/2 (~4.7124),\n\nso third quadrant,\n\nsine negative,\n\nso s_4=-1,\n\ncorrect.\n\nn=5: 32 radians.\n\n32 - 10π ≈ 32 - 31.4159 ≈ 0.5841 radians,\n\nwhich is in the first quadrant,\n\nso sine positive,\n\ns_5=+1,\n\ncorrect.\n\n