\n\nWith d_1 = 1 (since product from k=1 to 0 is empty product =1).\n\nTherefore, the sequence d_n is:\n\nd_1 = 1,\n\nd_2 = d_1 * (c_1/2),\n\nd_3 = d_2 * (c_2/2),\n\nand so on.\n\nTherefore, S_N = (1/2) * sum_{n=1}^N d_n.\n\nBut this still doesn't help unless we can find a closed-form expression for the sum of d_n.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps recognize that this is similar to a binary expansion where each term is multiplied by a factor that depends on the previous sign.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that the infinite product of c_k converges to some value, but since each c_k is ±1, the infinite product would be ±1, but the partial products oscillate.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the terms d_n are related to the partial products s_n divided by 2^{n-1}:\n\nSince s_n = product_{k=1}^{n-1} c_k,\n\nthen d_n = s_n / 2^{n-1}.\n\nTherefore,\n\nS = sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n = sum_{n=1}^\\infty s_n / 2^n = sum_{n=1}^\\infty (s_n / 2^{n-1}) * (1/2) = (1/2) sum_{n=1}^\\infty d_n,\n\nwhere d_n = s_n / 2^{n-1}.\n\nBut d_n = s_n / 2^{n-1} = (s_{n-1} * c_{n-1}) / 2^{n-1} = (s_{n-1} / 2^{n-2}) ) * (c_{n-1}/2) = d_{n-1} * (c_{n-1}/2).\n\nSo again, the same recursion.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of this as a random walk where each step is multiplied by a random ±1 factor, but scaled by 1/2 each time. The expected value of such a walk would be zero, but the actual sum here is converging to a positive value because the initial terms are positive and the negative terms are less frequent or smaller?\n\nWait, but the partial sums seem to be approaching a positive value, not zero. The terms alternate in sign but the later terms are small and the partial sums are converging to around 0.3634.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sum can be expressed as the limit of S_N = (1/2) * sum_{n=0}^{N-1} d_n, where d_n follows the recursion d_n = d_{n-1} * c_{n-1}/2, with d_0 = 1.\n\nBut unless we can find a closed-form for this recursive sequence, this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that the infinite sum S can be written as a continued fraction or some other expression?\n\nAlternatively, given that manually computing terms up to n=32 gives a partial sum of approximately 0.3634292486, and considering that each subsequent term is getting smaller by a factor of 1/2 each time, the remaining terms after n=32 contribute less than 1e-9, so the value is very close to approximately 0.3634292486.\n\nLooking up this value in the OEIS or a calculator, but since I can't access external resources, I need to think differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the exact value is 1/2 - 1/4 + 1/8 - 1/16 + 1/32 + 1/64 + 1/128 - 1/256 + ... and so on, but the pattern isn't obvious.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the partial sum after n=1 is 1/2,\n\nafter n=2: 1/4,\n\nafter n=3: 3/8,\n\nafter n=4: 5/16,\n\nafter n=5: 11/32,\n\nafter n=6: 23/64,\n\nafter n=7: 47/128,\n\nafter n=8: 95/256,\n\nafter n=9: 191/512,\n\nafter n=10: 383/1024,\n\nafter n=11: 767/2048,\n\nafter n=12: 1535/4096,\n\nafter n=13: 3071/8192,\n\nafter n=14: 6143/16384,\n\nafter n=15: 12287/32768,\n\nafter n=16: 24575/65536,\n\nafter n=17: 49151/131072,\n\nafter n=18: 98303/262144,\n\nafter n=19: 196607/524288,\n\nafter n=20: 393215/1048576,\n\nafter n=21: 786431/2097152,\n\nafter n=22: 1572863/4194304,\n\nafter n=23: 3145727/8388608,\n\nafter n=24: 6291455/16777216,\n\nafter n=25: 12582911/33554432,\n\nafter n=26: 25165823/67108864,\n\nafter n=27: 50331647/134217728,\n\nafter n=28: 100663295/268435456,\n\nafter n=29: 201326591/536870912,\n\nafter n=30: 402653183/1073741824,\n\nafter n=31: 805306367/2147483648,\n\nafter n=32: 1610612735/4294967296 ≈0.3748...\n\nWait a second, hold on, this contradicts my previous decimal approximations. Wait, let me recalculate the partial sums using fractions to see where the mistake was.\n\nWait, perhaps my earlier decimal approximations were inaccurate due to rounding errors.\n\nLet me try to compute the partial sums more precisely using fractions:\n\nStarting over with fractions:\n\nTerm 1: +1/2 = 1/2 ≈0.5\n\nPartial Sum 1: 1/2\n\nTerm 2: -1/4 = -1/4\n\nPartial Sum 2: 1/2 - 1/4 = 1/4 = 0.25\n\nTerm 3: +1/8 = +1/8\n\nPartial Sum 3: 1/4 + 1/8 = 3/8 = 0.375\n\nTerm 4: -1/16 = -1/16\n\nPartial Sum 4: 3/8 - 1/16 = 6/16 - 1/16 = 5/16 ≈0.3125\n\nTerm 5: +1/32 = +1/32\n\nPartial Sum 5: 5/16 + 1/32 = 10/32 + 1/32 = 11/32 ≈0.34375\n\nTerm 6: +1/64 = +1/64\n\nPartial Sum 6: 11/32 + 1/64 = 22/64 + 1/64 = 23/64 ≈0.359375\n\nTerm 7: +1/128 = +1/128\n\nPartial Sum 7: 23/64 + 1/128 = 46/128 + 1/128 = 47/128 ≈0.3671875\n\nTerm 8: -1/256 = -1/256\n\nPartial Sum 8: 47/128 - 1/256 = 94/256 - 1/256 = 93/256 ≈0.36328125\n\nTerm 9: +1/512 = +1/512\n\nPartial Sum 9: 93/256 + 1/512 = 186/512 + 1/512 = 187/512 ≈0.365234375\n\nTerm 10: -1/1024 = -1/1024\n\nPartial Sum 10: 187/512 - 1/1024 = 374/1024 - 1/1024 = 373/1024 ≈0.3642578125\n\nTerm 11: -1/2048 = -1/2048\n\nPartial Sum 11: 373/1024 - 1/2048 = 746/2048 - 1/2048 = 745/2048 ≈0.36376953125\n\nTerm 12: -1/4096 = -1/4096\n\nPartial Sum 12: 745/2048 - 1/4096 = 1490/4096 - 1/4096 = 1489/4096 ≈0.363525390625\n\nTerm 13: -1/8192 = -1/8192\n\nPartial Sum 13: 1489/4096 - 1/8192 = 2978/8192 - 1/8192 = 2977/8192 ≈0.3634033203125\n\nTerm 14: +1/16384 = +1/16384\n\nPartial Sum 14: 2977/8192 + 1/16384 = 5954/16384 + 1/16384 = 5955/16384 ≈0.36346435546875\n\nTerm 15: -1/32768 = -1/32768\n\nPartial Sum 15: 5955/16384 - 1/32768 = 11910/32768 - 1/32768 = 11909/32768 ≈0.363433837890625\n\nTerm 16: -1/65536 = -1/65536\n\nPartial Sum 16: 11909/32768 - 1/65536 = 23818/65536 - 1/65536 = 23817/65536 ≈0.3634185791015625\n\nTerm 17: +1/131072 = +1/131072\n\nPartial Sum 17: 23817/65536 + 1/131072 = 47634/131072 + 1/131072 = 47635/131072 ≈0.3634300231933594\n\nTerm 18: -1/262144 = -1/262144\n\nPartial Sum 18: 47635/131072 - 1/262144 = 95270/262144 - 1/262144 = 95269/262144 ≈0.3634290695190429\n\nTerm 19: -1/524288 = -1/524288\n\nPartial Sum 19: 95269/262144 - 1/524288 = 190538/524288 - 1/524288 = 190537/524288 ≈0.3634281158447266\n\n