ints? That sounds like Lagrange interpolation.\n\nYes, exactly. Since f(x) interpolates the values at n = 1, 2, ..., 2027, with f(n) = 1 if n is a perfect square, else 0, then f(x) can be expressed as a linear combination of the Lagrange basis polynomials, each of which is 1 at one of the points and 0 at the others.\n\nSpecifically, for each n from 1 to 2027, let L_n(x) be the Lagrange basis polynomial defined by:\n\nL_n(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{1 \\leq m \\leq 2027 \\\\ m \\neq n}} \\frac{x - m}{n - m}\n\nThen, since f(n) = 1 if n is a perfect square and 0 otherwise, we can write f(x) as the sum over all perfect squares k^2 in 1 to 2027 of L_{k^2}(x). So,\n\nf(x) = \\sum_{\\substack{k^2 \\leq 2027 \\\\ k \\geq 1}} L_{k^2}(x)\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) is the sum over all k^2 in 1 to 2027 of the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_{k^2}(x).\n\nTherefore, if I can compute, for each perfect square k^2 in 1 to 2027, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_{k^2}(x), and then sum them all together, that will give me the desired coefficient c of x^{2025} in f(x).\n\nSo, first, let's figure out how many perfect squares there are between 1 and 2027. Since 2027 is prime, but the squares are just integers k^2 where k^2 ≤ 2027. The largest integer k such that k^2 ≤ 2027 is floor(sqrt(2027)).\n\nLet me compute sqrt(2027). Since 45^2 = 2025, so sqrt(2025) = 45, so sqrt(2027) is just a bit more than 45, so floor(sqrt(2027)) = 45. Therefore, the perfect squares in 1 to 2027 are k^2 where k ranges from 1 to 45, since 45^2 = 2025 ≤ 2027 and 46^2 = 2116 > 2027. Therefore, there are 45 perfect squares between 1 and 2027.\n\nTherefore, f(x) is the sum over k = 1 to 45 of L_{k^2}(x).\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) is the sum over k=1 to 45 of [coefficient of x^{2025} in L_{k^2}(x)].\n\nSo, now, I need to find, for each k from 1 to 45, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_{k^2}(x). Let me recall that Lagrange basis polynomials are of degree at most 2026, since ther