d d ≠0,\n\n0, otherwise.\n\n}\n\nTherefore,\n\nS(m + p -2) = S(m -1 mod (p-1)).\n\nLet’s denote d_m = m -1 mod (p-1).\n\nThen,\n\nS(m + p -2) =\n\n- p, if d_m =0 and m -1 ≡0 mod (p-1) and m -1=0,\n\n- p-1, if d_m=0 and m -1 ≠0,\n\n- 0, otherwise.\n\nWait, but d_m is m -1 mod (p-1), so d_m=0 ⇒ m -1 ≡0 mod (p-1) ⇒ m ≡1 mod (p-1).\n\nSince m ranges from0 to p-1,\n\nm ≡1 mod (p-1) ⇒ m=1 or m=1 +k(p-1). But since m ≤p-1, the only solution is m=1.\n\nTherefore,\n\nFor each m:\n\nIf m=1: d_m=0 ⇒ S(m + p -2)=S(p-1)=S( (p-1) +0 )=S(p-1). But S(p-1)= sum_{α} α^{p-1}=sum_{α}1 (since α^{p-1}=1 for α≠0) plus 0^{p-1}=0. Therefore S(p-1)=p-1 +0=p-1.\n\nWait, but according to the formula:\n\nd_m = m-1 mod(p-1)=0 ⇒ S(d_m)=p-1 (since d_m=0 mod(p-1) and d_m ≠0 because m=1 ⇒ m-1=0 ⇒ d_m=0, but m-1=0 ⇒ d_m=0, but m-1=0 ⇒ d_m=0, but d_m= m-1=0 ⇒ d_m=0 ⇒ S(d_m)=p-1 (since d_m≠0? Wait, m=1 ⇒ m-1=0 ⇒ d_m=0 ⇒ S(d_m)=p-1? Wait no:\n\nWait when d_m=0 (mod(p-1)), but the actual value of d_m is m-1. For m=1, d_m=0 ⇒ S(d_m)=p-1 (since d_m=0 but m-1=0 ⇒ d_m=0, but d_m=0 corresponds to exponent d=0, but in S(d), when d=0, S(0)=p. Wait, confusion again.\n\nWait, S(d) is defined as sum_{α in GF(p)} α^d. When d=0, S(0)=p. When d ≡0 mod(p-1) but d≠0, then S(d)=p-1. When d not ≡0 mod(p-1), S(d)=0.\n\nTherefore, for d_m=0 (i.e., m=1):\n\nS(d_m)=S(0)=p.\n\nWait, but in our case, d_m = m -1 mod(p-1), but the actual exponent is m + p -2, which is congruent to d_m mod(p-1). However, the value of S(m + p -2) depends on the actual exponent m + p -2, not just its residue mod(p-1).\n\nWait, this is a critical mistake. The formula for S(d) depends on the actual exponent d, not just d mod(p-1). Therefore, even if two exponents are congruent mod(p-1), their S(d) values can differ if one of them is zero.\n\nTherefore, my prior approach is flawed because S(d) depends on d being exactly zero or a multiple of p-1, not just its residue.\n\nTherefore, to correctly compute S(m + p -2), we need to evaluate it as the sum over α in GF(p) of α^{m + p -2}, which is equal to:\n\nIf m + p -2 ≡0 mod(p-1):\n\nThen, α^{m + p -2} = α^{k(p-1)} = (α^{p-1})^k =1^k=1 for α≠0, and 0^{m+p-2}=0 if m+p-2>0.\n\nTherefore, S(m + p -2)=sum_{α} α^{m+p-2}= (p-1) +0= p-1 if m + p -2 ≡0 mod(p-1) and m+p-2 ≠0,\n\nor S= p if m+p-2=0.\n\nElse, if m + p -2 ≡ non-zero mod(p-1), then S(m + p -2)=0.\n\nTherefore, to compute S(m + p -2):\n\nCase 1: m + p -2 =0 ⇒ m=2 -p. But m ranges from0 to p-1, so this is only possible if p=2, which is not our case (p is odd prime ≥3). So this case is irrelevant.\n\nCase 2: m + p -2 ≡0 mod(p-1) ⇒ m ≡ - (p-2) mod(p-1).\n\nSince p ≡1 mod(p-1), so p -2 ≡-1 mod(p-1),\n\nThus, m ≡1 mod(p-1).\n\nSince m is between0 and p-1, the only solution is m=1.\n\nTherefore, for m=1:\n\nS(m + p -2)=S(p-1)= sum_{α} α^{p-1}=sum_{α≠0}1 +0^{p-1}= (p-1)+0=p-1.\n\nFor other m:\n\nIf m ≡1 mod(p-1) ⇒ m=1,\n\nElse,\n\nIf m + p -2 ≡0 mod(p-1) ⇒ already covered,\n\nElse,\n\nIf m + p -2 ≡ non-zero mod(p-1),\n\nthen S(m + p -2)=0.\n\nTherefore, summarizing:\n\nFor m from0 to p-1,\n\nS(m + p -2) =\n\n- p-1, if m=1,\n\n- 0, otherwise.\n\nTherefore,\n\nS_{p-2} = sum_{m=0}^{p-1} a_m S(m + p -2) = a_1*(p-1) + sum_{m≠1} a_m *0 = a_1*(p-1).\n\nBut we also have S_{p-2} = sum_{α in GF(p)} f(α) α^{p-2}.\n\nNow, what is S_{p-2}?\n\nS_{p-2} = sum_{α in GF(p)} f(α) α^{p-2}.\n\nBut f(α) is 1 if α is a non-zero quadratic residue, else 0.\n\nTherefore,\n\nS_{p-2} = sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α^{p-2} + sum_{α not in QR_{non-zero}} 0 * α^{p-2} = sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α^{p-2}.\n\nNow, note that for any non-zero α in GF(p), α^{p-1}=1, so α^{p-2}= α^{-1}.\n\nTherefore, S_{p-2} = sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α^{-1}.\n\nBut the sum over the inverses of the quadratic residues.\n\nNow, the set of quadratic residues forms a multiplicative subgroup of index 2 in the multiplicative group. Since the multiplicative group is cyclic of order p-1, which is even (since p is odd), the quadratic residues form a subgroup of index 2, hence cyclic of order (p-1)/2.\n\nLet g be a primitive root mod p. Then the quadratic residues are the elements g^{2k} for k=0,..., (p-3)/2.\n\nThe inverse of a quadratic residue α =g^{2k} is α^{-1}=g^{-2k}=g^{(p-1 -2k)}.\n\nTherefore, the sum over quadratic residues of α^{-1} is the same as the sum over k of g^{(p-1 -2k)}.\n\nBut this sum is the same as the sum over all quadratic residues of α, since the mapping α → α^{-1} is a bijection on the multiplicative group, and the quadratic residues are closed under inverses (since if α is a quadratic residue, so is α^{-1}).\n\nTherefore, the sum over α in QR_{non-zero} of α^{-1} is equal to the sum over α in QR_{non-zero} of α.\n\nTherefore, S_{p-2}=sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α.\n\nNow, what is this sum?\n\nIn a cyclic group of order n (here n=(p-1)/2), the sum of all elements of a subgroup of index m?\n\nWait, the quadratic residues form a subgroup of the multiplicative group of order n=(p-1)/2.\n\nIn a cyclic group of order n, the sum of all elements of a subgroup of order d (where d divides n) is 0 unless d=1, in which case it's the single element.\n\nBut here, the subgroup is the entire quadratic residues group, which has order n=(p-1)/2. The sum over all elements of a cyclic subgroup of order n in the additive group? Wait no, we're in the multiplicative group, but the sum is in the additive group.\n\nWait, the sum S = sum_{α in QR} α is an element of GF(p). We need to compute this sum.\n\nIn a cyclic group of order n, generated by g, the elements of the subgroup QR are g^{2k} for k=0,...,n-1 (since n=(p-1)/2). The sum S = sum_{k=0}^{n-1} g^{2k}.\n\nThis is a geometric series with ratio r=g^2, so:\n\nS = (1 - r^n)/(1 - r) = (1 - (g^2)^n)/(1 - g^2).\n\nBut since g has order p-1, then g^{p-1}=1 ⇒ g^{2n}=g^{2*(p-1)/2}=g^{p-1}=1. Therefore, r^n = (g^2)^n =g^{2n}=1.\n\nTherefore,\n\nS = (1 -1)/(1 - g^2)=0/(1 -g^2)=0.\n\nTherefore, the sum of all quadratic residues (excluding 0) is 0 in GF(p).\n\nWait, that's a crucial result!\n\nTherefore, S_{p-2}=0.\n\nTherefore, going back to the equation:\n\nS_{p-2} = a_1*(p-1) =0 ⇒ a_1*(p-1)=0 ⇒ a_1=0, since p-1 ≠0 in GF(p).\n\nWait, but in our p=3 example, we had a_1=2, and p-1=2, so 2*2=4≡1 mod3, but S_{p-2}=1, which is not zero. Contradiction. So clearly something is wrong.\n\nWait, in the p=3 case, according to this general formula:\n\nS_{p-2}=sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α^{-1}=sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α= sum of quadratic residues.\n\nIn p=3, the non-zero quadratic residues are just {1}, so sum is1. Thus S_{p-2}=1.\n\nBut according to the formula above using the cyclic group:\n\nn=(p-1)/2=1, so the subgroup QR has order1, generated by g^2 where g is a primitive root. Let's take g=2 in GF(3), since 2 is a primitive root mod3 (order 2). Then QR consists of g^{2*0}=1. So sum is1, which matches.\n\nThe formula S=(1 - r^n)/(1 -r). Here, r=g^2= (2)^2=1 mod3. So r=1. Then S=(1 -1^1)/(1 -1)=0/0, which is undefined. So the formula doesn't apply when r=1, which occurs when g^2=1 ⇒ g=±1. In GF(3), g=2, so g^2=1, so indeed r=1. Hence the geometric series sum becomes sum_{k=0}^{n-1}1= n=1, which matches.\n\nTherefore, in cases where the common ratio r=1 (i.e., when the subgroup is the entire multiplicative group, but here n=(p-1)/2, so only when (p-1)/2=1 ⇒ p=3, then r=1), the sum is n=1.\n\nIn general, for p>3, when n=(p-1)/2 >1, then r=g^2 ≠1, so the sum S= (1 -1)/(1 -r)=0.\n\nWait, but when r≠1, then S=0. Therefore, for p>3:\n\nsum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α =0.\n\nFor p=3, it's1.\n\nTherefore, in general:\n\nS_{p-2}= {\n\n0, if p>3,\n\n1, if p=3.\n\n}\n\nBut in our case, p=2027, which is much larger than3, so S_{p-2}=0.\n\nTherefore, returning to the equation:\n\nS_{p-2}=a_1*(p-1)=0 ⇒ a_1=0, since p-1≠0.\n\nWait, but in the p=3 case, we had a_1=2, and S_{p-2}=1= a_1*(p-1)=2*2=4≡1 mod3, which holds.\n\nIn general, for prime p≠3:\n\nS_{p-2}=0 ⇒ a_1*(p-1)=0 ⇒ a_1=0.\n\nWait, but in our p=3 case, we had a_1=2, which is not zero, but in that case S_{p-2}=1, and a_1*(p-1)=2*2=4≡1 mod3, so it holds.\n\nTherefore, for general prime p:\n\nS_{p-2}=a_1*(p-1) mod p.\n\nBut S_{p-2}=sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α^{-1}=sum_{α in QR_{non-zero}} α=0 modp for p>3, and 1 modp for p=3.\n\nTherefore, for p>3:\n\n0 ≡a_1*(p-1) modp ⇒ since p-1 ≡-1 modp, so:\n\n0≡a_1*(-1) modp ⇒ a_1≡0 modp.\n\nBut a_1 is a rational number, but in the polynomial f(x) with integer coefficients, the coefficient a_1 must be an integer. Therefore, a_1 must be divisible by p.\n\nWait, but in the p=3 case, a_1=2, which is not divisible by3, but that's because p=3 is a special case where S_{p-2}=1 mod3, so the equation was 1 ≡a_1*2 mod3 ⇒ 2a_1≡1 mod3 ⇒ a_1≡2 mod3, which is consistent with a_1=2.\n\nBut for p>3:\n\nWe have a_1*(p-1)≡0 modp ⇒ a_1≡0 modp, since p-1 and p are coprime (because p is prime and p-1 <p). Therefore, p does not divide p-1, so the only solution is a_1≡0 modp.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient a_{p-2}=a_1 is divisible by p.\n\nBut the problem states that the polynomial f(x) has degree at most2026, and is uniquely determined by its values at2027 points. Therefore, its coefficients are rational numbers, but when reduced to lowest terms a/b, the denominator b must divide the leading coefficient's denominator?\n\nWait, actually, the coefficients of the interpolating polynomial can be expressed as fractions with denominators dividing the product of the denominators from the Lagrange basis polynomials. For example, in the p=3 case, the coefficient a_1 was 2, which is integer, so denominator 1.\n\nIn general, for prime p, the Lagrange basis polynomials L_k(x) have denominators that are products of (k - m) for m≠k. Since we're working over integers, the denominators are integers, and the overall polynomial f(x) will have coefficients that are rational numbers with denominators dividing the product of all these denominators.\n\nHowever, in our case, we need the coefficient a_{p-2}=a_1 of x^{p-2} in f(x), which we just concluded must be divisible by p for p>3. Therefore, a_1= p * c