ient of x^{2025} in f(x) mod 2027, then that will give me r directly. So, perhaps this is easier?\n\nTherefore, perhaps working over GF(2027) is the way to go here. Let me see.\n\nSo, in GF(2027)[x], f(x) is a polynomial of degree at most 2026 such that f(k) = 1 for each square k in {1, 4, 9, ..., 2025} and f(0) = 0. Wait, but actually, f(x) mod 2027 must also satisfy f(2027) ≡ f(0) ≡ 0 mod 2027, which it does, as per above. So, in GF(2027)[x], the polynomial f(x) mod 2027 is the unique interpolating polynomial for these 2027 points. Now, perhaps in GF(2027)[x], there is a simpler expression for f(x)?\n\nAlternatively, notice that in GF(2027), the function f(x) is 1 at the squares and 0 elsewhere, except at 0 where it is 0. Wait, but 0 is not in the domain of the original f(n), except that n=2027 maps to 0. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of f(x) mod 2027 as the polynomial which is 1 at the squares and 0 at non-squares in GF(2027). Wait, but GF(2027) has 2027 elements, and the squares in GF(2027) are the quadratic residues. Since 2027 is prime, the number of quadratic residues mod 2027 is (2027 + 1)/2 = 1014, because in a finite field of odd order p, the number of quadratic residues is (p-1)/2 + 1 = (p+1)/2. Wait, actually, the multiplicative group is cyclic of order p-1, so the number of quadratic residues is (p-1)/2, and including 0, the total number of squares is (p+1)/2. Wait, actually, 0 is also a square (0 = 0²), so in GF(p), the number of squares is (p+1)/2. Since p = 2027 is prime, so the number of squares in GF(2027) is (2027 + 1)/2 = 1014. However, in our case, the squares we are considering are only those from 1² to 45², which is 45 squares, which is much less than 1014. Wait a second, so actually in GF(2027), there are way more squares than the 45 squares we have in the original problem. Therefore, my previous thought was incorrect.\n\nWait, actually, in the problem, f(n) is defined only on the integers from 1 to 2027, and it's 1 only at the perfect