x) / D_m, where D_m = ∏_{j ∈ {1, 2, ..., 2027} \\ {m}} (m - j).\n\nThen, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_m(x) is [ - (sum_{j ∈ {1, ..., 2027} \\ {m}} j) ] / D_m.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) mod 2027 is:\n\nSum_{m ∈ S} [ - (sum_{j ≠ m} j) / D_m ] mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, to compute this, we need for each m in S, compute two things:\n\n1. The sum of all j from 1 to 2027 except m, i.e., Sum_{j ≠ m} j.\n\n2. The denominator D_m = ∏_{j ≠ m} (m - j).\n\nThen, compute each term as [ - (Sum_{j ≠ m} j) / D_m ] mod 2027, and sum all these terms over m in S.\n\nThis seems complicated, but maybe there are simplifications.\n\nFirst, let me compute Sum_{j ≠ m} j. The sum from j=1 to 2027 of j is (2027)(2028)/2. Therefore, Sum_{j ≠ m} j = (2027)(2028)/2 - m.\n\nSo that part is straightforward.\n\nNow, the denominator D_m = ∏_{j ≠ m} (m - j). Let me note that this product is equal to ∏_{j=1}^{2027} (m - j) divided by (m - m) = 0, but actually, since j ≠ m, so it's the product over all j from 1 to 2027 except j = m of (m - j). Wait, but (m - j) for j from 1 to 2027 except j = m is the same as the product over k = 1 to 2027 of (m - k) divided by (m - m) = 0, but since we exclude j = m, it's actually equal to (-1)^{2026} times the product from k=1 to 2027 of (k - m), because (m - j) = - (j - m). So, the product over j ≠ m of (m - j) = (-1)^{2026} times the product over j ≠ m of (j - m) = (-1)^{2026} times [ product_{j=1}^{2027} (j - m) / (m - m) ) ] but again, since we exclude j = m, it's actually equal to (-1)^{2026} times [ product_{j=1}^{2027} (j - m) / (m - m) ) ] but division by zero isn't helpful. Alternatively, just note that product_{j ≠ m} (m - j) = product_{j=1}^{m-1} (m - j) * product_{j=m+1}^{2027} (m - j) = [product_{k=1}^{m-1} k] * [product_{k=1}^{2027 - m} (-k)] = [ (m - 1)! ] * [ (-1)^{2027 - m} (2027 - m)! ) ].\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nLet me write the product as two parts: j from 1 to m - 1 and j from m + 1 to 2027.\n\nFor j from 1 to m - 1: (m - j) = (m - 1) - (j - 1) = ... well, actually, just (m - j) where j ranges from 1 to m - 1, so that's (m - 1)(m - 2)...(1) = (m - 1)!.\n\nFor j from m + 1 to 2027: (m - j) = - (j - m). Here, j - m ranges from 1 to 2027 - m. So, the product over j = m + 1 to 2027 of (m - j) = product_{k=1}^{2027 - m} (-k) = (-1)^{2027 - m} * (2027 - m)!.\n\nTherefore, combining both parts:\n\nD_m = product_{j ≠ m} (m - j) = (m - 1)! * (-1)^{2027 - m} * (2027 - m)!.\n\nTherefore,\n\nD_m = (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! * (2027 - m)!.\n\nTherefore, D_m = (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! * (2027 - m)!.\n\nTherefore, putting this together, the term for each m in S is:\n\nTerm_m = [ - (Sum_{j ≠ m} j) / D_m ] = [ - ( (2027*2028/2) - m ) / ( (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! * (2027 - m)! ) ) ]\n\nHmm, this is getting quite involved, but let's try to simplify step by step.\n\nFirst, let me denote S_total = Sum_{j=1}^{2027} j = 2027 * 2028 / 2.\n\nTherefore, Sum_{j ≠ m} j = S_total - m.\n\nTherefore, Term_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] / D_m.\n\nAnd D_m = (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! * (2027 - m)!.\n\nTherefore,\n\nTerm_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] / [ (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! * (2027 - m)! ) ]\n\nLet me rewrite this as:\n\nTerm_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] * [ (-1)^{m - 2027} / ( (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ]\n\nBecause 1 / (-1)^{2027 - m} = (-1)^{m - 2027} = (-1)^{m} * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{2027} since (-1)^{-1} = -1. Wait, actually, (-1)^{m - 2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^m * [ (-1)^{2027} ]^{-1}. But since 2027 is odd, (-1)^{2027} = -1, so [ (-1)^{2027} ]^{-1} = (-1)^{-1} = -1. Therefore,\n\n(-1)^{m - 2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{ -1} * (-1)^{2026} = (-1)^m * (-1) * 1 = - (-1)^m.\n\nWait, maybe better to compute directly:\n\n(-1)^{m - 2027} = (-1)^{m} * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^m * [ (-1)^{2027} ]^{-1} = (-1)^m * [ -1 ]^{-1} = (-1)^m * (-1) = - (-1)^m.\n\nTherefore, Term_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] * [ - (-1)^m / ( (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ) ]\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nTerm_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] / [ (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ]\n\nLet me write this as:\n\nTerm_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] * [ 1 / ( (-1)^{2027 - m} * (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ) ]\n\n= [ - (S_total - m) ] * [ (-1)^{m - 2027} / ( (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ) ]\n\nThen, as above, (-1)^{m - 2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{ - (2027) } = (-1)^m * (-1)^{ -1 } because 2027 is odd, so exponent -2027 is equivalent to -1 mod 2? Wait, actually exponentiation with negative exponents:\n\n(-1)^{-k} = [ (-1)^k ]^{-1} = (-1)^k since (-1)^k is ±1. Therefore, (-1)^{-2027} = [ (-1)^{2027} ]^{-1} = (-1)^{-1} = -1.\n\nTherefore, (-1)^{m - 2027} = (-1)^m * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^m * (-1) = - (-1)^m.\n\nTherefore, Term_m = [ - (S_total - m) ] * [ - (-1)^m / ( (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ) ]\n\nMultiply the two negatives: - * - = +, so:\n\nTerm_m = (S_total - m) * [ (-1)^m / ( (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) )\n\nTherefore, Term_m = (S_total - m) * (-1)^m / [ (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) mod 2027 is:\n\nSum_{m ∈ S} Term_m = Sum_{m ∈ S} [ (S_total - m) * (-1)^m / ( (m - 1)! (2027 - m)! ) ) ] mod 2027.\n\nHmm, this is still quite complicated, but perhaps we can find a way to compute this sum modulo 2027.\n\nNote that 2027 is a prime, so we can work in the field GF(2027). Let me denote p = 2027 for simplicity.\n\nSo p = 2027, which is prime. The set S is {1², 2², ..., 45²}, since 45² = 2025 < p, and 46² = 2116 > p. So S = {1, 4, 9, ..., 2025}.\n\nOur sum is over m in S of [ (S_total - m) * (-1)^m / ( (m - 1)! (p - m)! ) ) ] mod p.\n\nFirst, let's note that S_total = p(p + 1)/2. Since p is odd (2027 is odd), so p + 1 is even, so S_total is an integer.\n\nCompute S_total mod p:\n\nS_total = p(p + 1)/2 mod p.\n\nBut p ≡ 0 mod p, so p(p + 1)/2 ≡ 0*(p + 1)/2 ≡ 0 mod p.\n\nTherefore, S_total ≡ 0 mod p.\n\nTherefore, S_total - m ≡ -m mod p.\n\nTherefore, (S_total - m) ≡ -m mod p.\n\nTherefore, the term (S_total - m) can be replaced by -m mod p in the numerator.\n\nTherefore, the entire