 - j) = product_{j ≠ k} [ - (j - k) ] = (-1)^{2026} product_{j ≠ k} (j - k) = product_{j ≠ k} (j - k) because (-1)^{2026}=1. Therefore, D_k = [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! ] * [ (2027 - k)! ]\n\nWait, hold on: Wait, product_{j ≠ k} (j - k) = [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! ] * [ (2027 - k)! ]\n\nTherefore, D_k = product_{j ≠ k} (k - j) = product_{j ≠ k} [ - (j - k) ] = (-1)^{2026} product_{j ≠ k} (j - k) = product_{j ≠ k} (j - k). Since (-1)^{2026}=1, so D_k = product_{j ≠ k} (j - k) = [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! ] * [ (2027 - k)! ]\n\nTherefore, D_k = [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! ] * [ (2027 - k)! ]\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_k(x) is [ - (S - k) ] / D_k, where S = sum_{j=1}^{2027} j = (2027)(2028)/2.\n\nTherefore, substituting D_k:\n\nCoefficient = [ - (S - k) ] / [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nWait, hold on, let me write that again:\n\nD_k = [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! ] * [ (2027 - k)! ]\n\nTherefore, 1 / D_k = 1 / [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_k(x) is [ - (S - k) ] divided by D_k, so:\n\nCoefficient_{L_k} = [ - (S - k) ] / [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nLet me simplify this expression:\n\nFirst, note that (-1)^{k - 1} in the denominator can be written as (-1)^{k - 1} = (-1)^{k} * (-1)^{-1} = (-1)^{k} * (-1) = - (-1)^k. Wait, but perhaps better to just manipulate the signs:\n\nLet me write the denominator as (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! )\n\nSo, 1 / D_k = 1 / [ (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = (-1)^{-(k - 1)} / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nTherefore,\n\nCoefficient_{L_k} = [ - (S - k) ] * [ (-1)^{-(k - 1)} / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\n= [ - (S - k) ] * [ (-1)^{ - (k - 1) } ] / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nNote that (-1)^{ - (k - 1) } = (-1)^{k - 1}, since (-1)^{-n} = [ (-1)^n ]^{-1} = (-1)^n because (-1)^n is ±1, so inverse is same as itself. Therefore,\n\nCoefficient_{L_k} = [ - (S - k) ] * [ (-1)^{k - 1} ] / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nTherefore