2025}. Because in a monic polynomial with roots r_1, r_2, ..., r_n, the coefficient of x^{n - 1} is - (r_1 + r_2 + ... + r_n). Wait, that's from Vieta's formula.\n\nYes! Vieta's formula tells us that for a monic polynomial P(x) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a_0, then the coefficient a_{n - 1} is equal to - (sum of the roots). So in our case, for P_k(x), which is a monic polynomial of degree 2026 with roots at all integers from 1 to 2027 except k. Therefore, the sum of the roots of P_k(x) is equal to [sum_{j=1}^{2027} j] - k.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in P_k(x) is equal to - [sum_{j=1}^{2027} j - k].\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in P_k(x) is - [S - k], where S is the sum from j=1 to 2027 of j.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_k(x) is [ - (S - k) ] divided by Q_k, where Q_k is the denominator, which is the product over j ≠ k of (k - j). Wait, let me confirm.\n\nWait, Vieta's formula says that for a monic polynomial P(x) = Π_{i=1}^n (x - r_i) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a_0, then a_{n - 1} = - Σ r_i. So in our case, P_k(x) = Π_{j ≠ k} (x - j) = x^{2026} - (sum_{j ≠ k} j) x^{2025} + ... So the coefficient of x^{2025} is indeed - (sum_{j ≠ k} j). Therefore, that is correct.\n\nTherefore, coefficient of x^{2025} in P_k(x) is - [sum_{j=1}^{2027} j - k], since sum_{j ≠ k} j = sum_{j=1}^{2027} j - k.\n\nTherefore, coefficient of x^{2025} in L_k(x) is [ - (S - k) ] / Q_k, where S = sum_{j=1}^{2027} j, and Q_k = Π_{j ≠ k} (k - j).\n\nTherefore, the total coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) is the sum over k = 1 to 45 of [ - (S - k) / Q_k ].\n\nTherefore, c = a/b = [ - Σ_{k=1}^{45} (S - k)/Q_k ].\n\nHmm, so now we need to compute this sum. Let's note that Q_k is the product over j ≠ k of (k - j). Let's see what Q_k is. Let me think about Q_k.\n\nQ_k = Π_{j=1, j ≠ k}^{2027} (k - j) = Π_{j=1}^{k - 1} (k - j) * Π_{j=k + 1}^{2027} (k - j)\n\nFirst, for j from 1 to k - 1, the terms are (k - 1), (k - 2), ..., 1. So that product is (k - 1)!.\n\nSecond, for j from k + 1 to 2027, the terms are (k - (k + 1)) = -1, (k - (k + 2)) = -2, ..., up to (k - 2027) = (k - 2027). Therefore, the second product is Π_{m=1}^{2027 - k} (-m) = (-1)^{2027 - k} (2027 - k)!.\n\nTherefore, putting these together:\n\nQ_k = (k - 1)! * (-1)^{2027 - k} (2027 - k)!.\n\nTherefore, Q_k = (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)!.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_k(x) is:\n\n[ - (S - k) ] / [ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nLet me write that as:\n\n[ - (S - k) ] / [ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = [ - (S - k) ] * [ (-1)^{ - (2027 - k) } / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nBecause 1 / (-1)^{n} = (-1)^{-n}.\n\nTherefore, this is equal to:\n\n[ - (S - k) ] * [ (-1)^{k - 2027} / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nSimplify exponents:\n\nNote that (-1)^{k - 2027} = (-1)^{k} * (-1)^{-2027} = (-1)^k * (-1)^{2027} because (-1)^{-n} = (-1)^n.\n\nSince 2027 is odd, (-1)^{2027} = -1. Therefore,\n\n(-1)^{k - 2027} = (-1)^k * (-1)^{2027} = (-1)^k * (-1) = - (-1)^k.\n\nTherefore, substituting back:\n\n[ - (S - k) ] * [ - (-1)^k / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ) ]\n\nMultiply the two negatives:\n\n[ - (S - k) ] * [ - (-1)^k / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ) ] = [ (S - k) ] * [ (-1)^k / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_k(x) simplifies to:\n\n(S - k) * (-1)^k / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the total coefficient c of x^{2025} in f(x) is:\n\nc = Σ_{k=1}^{45} [ (S - k) * (-1)^k / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nHmm, okay, so now we have an expression for c in terms of a sum over k from 1 to 45 of terms involving (S - k), (-1)^k, factorials, etc.\n\nFirst, let's compute S, the sum from j=1 to 2027 of j. That's straightforward: S = (2027)(2027 + 1)/2 = (2027)(2028)/2.\n\nLet me compute that:\n\n2027 * 2028 / 2. Since 2028 / 2 = 1014, so S = 2027 * 1014.\n\nLet me compute 2027 * 1014. Let's break it down:\n\n2027 * 1000 = 2,027,000\n\n2027 * 14 = ?\n\n2027 * 10 = 20,270\n\n2027 * 4 = 8,108\n\nSo 20,270 + 8,108 = 28,378\n\nTherefore, total S = 2,027,000 + 28,378 = 2,055,378.\n\nWait, let me verify that multiplication again:\n\n2027 * 1014:\n\nFirst, note that 1014 = 1000 + 14.\n\nTherefore, 2027 * 1000 = 2,027,000\n\n2027 * 14:\n\nCalculate 2000 * 14 = 28,000\n\n27 * 14 = 378\n\nTherefore, 28,000 + 378 = 28,378\n\nThus, 2,027,000 + 28,378 = 2,055,378. Yes, so S = 2,055,378.\n\nTherefore, S - k = 2,055,378 - k.\n\nTherefore, substituting back into c:\n\nc = Σ_{k=1}^{45} [ (2,055,378 - k) * (-1)^k / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ]\n\nHmm, okay. So now, this is a sum from k=1 to k=45 of terms each involving (-1)^k, (2,055,378 - k), and divided by (k - 1)! (2027 - k)!.\n\nThis seems quite complex, but perhaps there is some symmetry or telescoping or combinatorial interpretation here?\n\nAlternatively, note that 2027 is a prime number, given in the problem statement. Since 2027 is prime, perhaps we can work modulo 2027 from the start, since we only need the value of a - r*b ≡ 0 mod 2027, so perhaps we can compute c mod 2027, then since c = a/b, then a ≡ c * b mod 2027, but since we need a - r b ≡ 0 mod 2027, so r ≡ a / b mod 2027, but since fractions modulo primes can be handled via inverses.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute the entire sum c modulo 2027 directly, since we only care about c mod 2027, and then since c = a/b, then a/b ≡ r mod 2027, so r ≡ a * b^{-1} mod 2027, but since we need a - r b ≡ 0 mod 2027, so r ≡ a / b mod 2027, so r is the representative of a/b mod 2027 in the range 0 to 2026.\n\nHowever, to compute c mod 2027, perhaps we can compute each term of the sum modulo 2027 and then add them up modulo 2027.\n\nGiven that 2027 is prime, we can work in the field Z/2027Z. Let me note that 2027 is prime, so all the factorials (k - 1)! and (2027 - k)! can be considered modulo 2027, but note that for k from 1 to 45, (2027 - k)! is (2026)! down to (2027 - 45)! = (1982)!.\n\nHowever, since 