 - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ].\n\nBut 2027 is prime, and perhaps we can relate this sum to some combinatorial identity modulo 2027?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that since we need c mod 2027, and c is expressed as a sum over k=1 to 45 of terms involving (S - k) * (-1)^k divided by ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ), perhaps we can rewrite this expression in terms of binomial coefficients?\n\nNote that 1 / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = 1 / [ (2027 - 1)! / ( (2027 - k)! (k - 1)! ) ) ] * 1 / (2026)! )\n\nWait, let me think. Let me recall that the binomial coefficient C(n, k) = n! / (k! (n - k)! ), so perhaps we can express this term as something involving binomial coefficients.\n\nLet me see:\n\nLet me note that (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! = (2027 - 1)! / [ C(2026, k - 1) * something ]? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, note that:\n\n1 / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = [2027! / ( (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] * 1 / 2027! ) = C(2027, k - 1) / 2027! ?\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nC(2027, k - 1) = 2027! / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - (k - 1))! ) ] = 2027! / [ (k - 1)! (2028 - k)! ) ]\n\nHmm, not quite matching. Alternatively, perhaps C(2027, k) ?\n\nC(2027, k) = 2027! / [k! (2027 - k)! ]\n\nSo if we have 1 / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = [k / (2027! / (2027 - k)! (k)! ) ] * something? Hmm, maybe not directly helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps write the term as:\n\n[ (S - k) * (-1)^k ] / [ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = [ (S - k) * (-1)^k * 2027! ] / [ 2027! (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! ) ] = [ (S - k) * (-1)^k * C(2027, k - 1) * (k) ] / [ 2027! ] ?\n\nWait, perhaps this is overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, since we are working modulo 2027, and 2027 is prime, then 2027! ≡ -1 mod 2027 by Wilson's theorem. Wait, Wilson's theorem says (p - 1)! ≡ -1 mod p, so 2026! ≡ -1 mod 2027, but 2027! = 2027 * 2026! ≡ 0 * (-1) ≡ 0 mod 2027. Wait, actually 2027! is divisible by 2027, so 2027! ≡ 0 mod 2027. Hmm, that complicates things because denominators in the terms involve factorials in the denominator, but modulo 2027, if the denominator has 