ic equation \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x - 10 = 0 \\).\n\nTry rational roots using Rational Root Theorem. Possible rational roots are ±1, ±2, ±5, ±10.\n\nTesting x=1: 1 + 3 - 3 -10 = -9 ≠ 0.\n\nx=2: 8 + 12 -6 -10 = 4 ≠ 0.\n\nx= -1: -1 + 3 + 3 -10 = -5 ≠ 0.\n\nx= -2: -8 + 12 + 6 -10 = 0. Wait: -8 +12 is 4, 4 +6 is 10, 10 -10 = 0. Yes! x = -2 is a root.\n\nTherefore, factor out (x + 2):\n\nUsing polynomial division or synthetic division:\n\nDivide \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x -10 \\) by (x + 2):\n\nCoefficients: 1 | 3 | -3 | -10\n\nBring down 1.\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient: 3 + (-2) = 1.\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient: -3 + (-2) = -5.\n\nMultiply by -2: -5*(-2) = 10. Add to last coefficient: -10 +10 = 0.\n\nSo, the quotient polynomial is \\( x^2 + x -5 \\).\n\nTherefore, the cubic factors as \\( (x + 2)(x^2 + x -5) = 0 \\).\n\nThus, roots are \\( x = -2 \\), and roots of \\( x^2 + x -5 = 0 \\), which are \\( x = [-1 ± sqrt(1 + 20)] / 2 = [-1 ± sqrt(21)] / 2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the roots are \\( -2 \\), \\( \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), and \\( \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nNow, let's check if these roots satisfy the original system \\( a^2 = b +6 \\), etc.\n\nLet me denote the roots as \\( a = -2 \\), \\( b = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), and \\( c = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), but need to check if they are assigned correctly such that each squared equals the next plus 6.\n\nWait, but we need to assign which root is a, which is b, and which is c such that the cyclic conditions hold.\n\nSuppose we set \\( a = -2 \\). Then, according to Equation (1): \\( a^2 = (-2)^2 = 4 \\), so this should equal \\( b + 6 \\). Therefore, \\( b = 4 - 6 = -2 \\).\n\nBut then \\( b = -2 \\), which is same as \\( a \\), but the problem states that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are pairwise distinct. Therefore, this is invalid.\n\nHmm, so that's a problem. So, if we take \\( a = -2 \\), then \\( b = -2 \\), which contradicts the distinctness.\n\nAlternatively, maybe assign \\( a \\) to be one of the other roots?\n\nLet me try \\( a = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nThen, \\( a^2 = \\left( \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1 - 2\\sqrt{21} + 21}{4} = \\frac{22 - 2\\sqrt{21}}{4} = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{21}}{2} \\).\n\nAccording to Equation (1), this should equal \\( b + 6 \\), so:\n\n\\( b = a^2 - 6 = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{21}}{2} - 6 = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{21} - 12}{2} = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nSo, \\( b = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nThen, compute \\( b^2 \\):\n\n\\( b^2 = \\left( \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1 + 2\\sqrt{21} + 21}{4} = \\frac{22 + 2\\sqrt{21}}{4} = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21}}{2} \\).\n\nAccording to Equation (2), this should equal \\( c + 6 \\), so:\n\n\\( c = b^2 - 6 = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21}}{2} - 6 = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21} - 12}{2} = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nWait, so \\( c = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), which is the same as the original \\( a \\). But \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) would then be equal, which is not allowed since they must be pairwise distinct.\n\nHmm, so that's a problem too.\n\nAlternatively, let me try assigning \\( a \\) to the third root, \\( c \\).\n\nWait, perhaps the assignments need to be cyclic. Let me see.\n\nSuppose we set \\( a = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), then \\( b = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), and then \\( c = a \\), which is bad. Alternatively, maybe I need to cycle through differently?\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nLet me denote the three roots of the cubic equation as \\( r_1 = -2 \\), \\( r_2 = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), and \\( r_3 = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nSuppose we assign:\n\n\\( a = r_2 \\),\n\nthen \\( b = a^2 - 6 = r_3 \\),\n\nthen \\( c = b^2 - 6 = (r_3)^2 - 6 \\).\n\nWait, let's compute \\( c \\):\n\nFirst, \\( r_3 = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\).\n\nCompute \\( r_3^2 \\):\n\n\\( r_3^2 = \\left( \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1 + 2\\sqrt{21} + 21}{4} = \\frac{22 + 2\\sqrt{21}}{4} = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21}}{2} \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( c = r_3^2 - 6 = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21}}{2} - 6 = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21} - 12}{2} = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} = r_2 \\).\n\nThus, \\( c = r_2 = a \\), which again is a repetition.\n\nHmm, so this is not working.\n\nAlternatively, if we assign \\( a = r_3 \\), then \\( b = a^2 - 6 \\).\n\nCompute \\( a = r_3 \\), so \\( a^2 = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21}}{2} \\), so \\( b = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{21}}{2} - 6 = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} = r_2 \\).\n\nThen \\( c = b^2 - 6 = (r_2)^2 -6 \\).\n\nCompute \\( r_2^2 \\):\n\n\\( r_2 = \\frac{ -1 + \\sqrt{21} }{2} \\), so squared is \\( \\frac{1 - 2\\sqrt{21} + 21}{4} = \\frac{22 - 2\\sqrt{21}}{4} = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{21}}{2} \\).\n\nThen \\( c = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{21}}{2} - 6 = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{21} - 12}{2} = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{21} }{2} = r_3 = a \\). Again, same problem.\n\nFinally, if we take \\( a = r_1 = -2 \\), then \\( b = a^2 - 6 = 4 -6 = -2 = a \\), which is same as \\( a \\), so not allowed.\n\nTherefore, the cubic equation with \\( z = 10 \\) and \\( S = -3 \\) does not give us three distinct roots satisfying the original equations. Hmm, so maybe that solution is extraneous?\n\nAlternatively, let's check the other cubic equation for \\( z = -9 \\).\n\nSo, for \\( S = -3 \\), \\( z = -9 \\):\n\nThe cubic equation is \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 9 = 0 \\).\n\nAgain, apply the Rational Root Theorem. Possible roots: ±1, ±3, ±9.\n\nTest x = -3:\n\n\\( (-3)^3 + 3*(-3)^2 -3*(-3) + 9 = -27 + 27 + 9 + 9 = 18 ≠ 0 \\).\n\nx = -1:\n\n\\( -1 + 3 + 3 + 9 = 14 ≠ 0 \\).\n\nx = 1:\n\n1 + 3 - 3 + 9 = 10 ≠ 0.\n\nx = 3:\n\n27 + 27 -9 +9 = 54 ≠ 0.\n\nx = -9:\n\nToo large, probably not.\n\nHmm, maybe it doesn't have rational roots. Let me try to factor it another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe factor by grouping:\n\n\\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x + 9 = (x^3 + 3x^2) + (-3x + 9) = x^2(x + 3) -3(x - 3) \\). Hmm, not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps synthetic division?\n\nAlternatively, maybe this cubic has one real root and two complex roots? Since the original problem allows complex numbers, but they must be distinct.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute the discriminant of the cubic equation \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 9 = 0 \\).\n\nThe discriminant \\( D \\) of a cubic equation \\( x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\( D = 18abc - 4a^3c + a^2b^2 - 4b^3 - 27c^2 \\).\n\nPlugging in \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), \\( c = 9 \\):\n\nFirst term: \\( 18 * 3 * (-3) * 9 = 18 * (-81) = -1458 \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( -4a^3c = -4*(27)*(9) = -4*243 = -972 \\).\n\nThird term: \\( a^2b^2 = (9)*(9) = 81 \\).\n\nFourth term: \\( -4b^3 = -4*(-27) = 108 \\).\n\nFifth term: \\( -27c^2 = -27*(81) = -2187 \\).\n\nSo,\n\n\\( D = (-1458) + (-972) + 81 + 108 + (-2187) \\).\n\nCompute step by step:\n\nStart with -1458 - 972 = -2430\n\nThen, -2430 + 81 = -2349\n\n-2349 + 108 = -2241\n\n-2241 - 2187 = -4428.\n\nSo discriminant D = -4428, which is negative. Therefore, this cubic has one real root and two complex conjugate roots. Therefore, there are three distinct roots (since complex roots come in pairs, and one real), so they are distinct as long as the complex ones are not real. Since discriminant is negative, yes, two complex and one real, so all three are distinct.\n\nTherefore, perhaps this cubic does give us three distinct roots satisfying the original equations? Let me try to see.\n\nLet me denote the cubic equation as \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 9 = 0 \\).\n\nLet me attempt to find its roots numerically or symbolically.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use substitution to depress the cubic.\n\nLet me perform a substitution \\( x = y - d \\), to eliminate the quadratic term. The standard substitution for a cubic \\( x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is to let \\( x = y - a/3 \\).\n\nHere, \\( a = 3 \\), so let \\( x = y - 1 \\).\n\nThen,\n\n\\( (y - 1)^3 + 3(y - 1)^2 - 3(y - 1) + 9 = 0 \\).\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( (y^3 - 3y^2 + 3y - 1) \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( 3(y^2 - 2y + 1) = 3y^2 - 6y + 3 \\).\n\nThird term: \\( -3(y - 1) = -3y + 3 \\).\n\nFourth term: +9.\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n\\( y^3 - 3y^2 + 3y -1 + 3y^2 -6y +3 -3y +3 +9 = 0 \\).\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n- \\( y^3 \\)\n\n- \\( -3y^2 + 3y^2 = 0 \\)\n\n- \\( 3y -6y -3y = -6y \\)\n\n- Constants: -1 +3 +3 +9 = 14\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\( y^3 - 6y + 14 = 0 \\).\n\nHmm, so depressed cubic: \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\).\n\nLet me see if this has any real roots. Let me plug in y = -3: (-27) - (-18) +14 = -27 +18 +14 = 5 ≠0.\n\ny = -2: (-8) - (-12) +14 = -8 +12 +14 = 18 ≠0.\n\ny = -4: (-64) - (-24) +14 = -64 +24 +14 = -26 ≠0.\n\ny = -5: (-125) - (-30) +14 = -125 +30 +14 = -81 ≠0.\n\nHmm, maybe there is no real root? Wait, but the discriminant was negative, so there should be one real root and two complex roots. Maybe the real root is irrational?\n\nAlternatively, let me compute the discriminant of the depressed cubic \\( y^3 + py + q = 0 \\), which in this case is \\( p = -6 \\), \\( q = 14 \\).\n\nThe discriminant is \\( -4p^3 -27q^2 \\).\n\nWait, actually, for the depressed cubic \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\), the discriminant is \\( -4p^3 - 27q^2 \\).\n\nSo here, \\( p = -6 \\), \\( q = 14 \\).\n\nThus discriminant D = -4*(-6)^3 -27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) -27*(196) = 864 - 5292 = -4428, which matches the earlier discriminant. Since D < 0, there is one real root and two complex conjugate roots.\n\nTherefore, the real root can be found using trigonometric method for depressed cubic with three real roots? Wait no, actually when discriminant is negative, it has one real and two complex roots, but trigonometric method is for when discriminant is negative and we have three real roots? Wait, actually, I might be mixing things up.\n\nWait, actually, for the depressed cubic \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\):\n\n- If \\( D > 0 \\): one real root and two complex conjugate roots.\n\n- If \\( D = 0 \\): multiple root and all roots real.\n\n- If \\( D < 0 \\): three distinct real roots.\n\nWait, now I'm confused because different sources might define it differently. Wait, let me check:\n\nActually, the discriminant for the cubic equation \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\) is given by \\( \\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2 \\).\n\n- If \\( \\Delta > 0 \\): one real root and two complex conjugate roots.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta = 0 \\): multiple root(s) and all roots real.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta < 0 \\): three distinct real roots.\n\nWait, so in our case, \\( \\Delta = -4428 < 0 \\), so there should be three distinct real roots? But that contradicts the earlier substitution which led to \\( D = -4428 \\), but in the original equation with \\( S = -3 \\), the cubic equation had one real root and two complex roots?\n\nWait, no, actually, when we depressed the cubic, we did a substitution \\( x = y - 1 \\), so the real roots of the depressed cubic correspond to real roots of the original equation. Since the depressed cubic has three real roots (because discriminant is negative), then the original cubic also has three real roots? But earlier when we computed the discriminant of the original cubic \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\), we found discriminant \\( D = -4428 \\), which according to the formula above, since \\( \\Delta <0 \\), it should have three distinct real roots. But when I tried plugging in values, I didn't find any real roots?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in calculation?\n\nLet me recalculate the discriminant for the original cubic \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 = 0 \\):\n\nUsing the formula for discriminant of cubic \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\):\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18abcd -4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\).\n\nWait, maybe I used the wrong formula earlier.\n\nWait, let me confirm the discriminant formula.\n\nThe discriminant of a cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) is:\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\).\n\nSo for our equation \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\), so \\( a=1 \\), \\( b=3 \\), \\( c=-3 \\), \\( d=9 \\).\n\nPlugging into the formula:\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18*1*3*(-3)*9 - 4*(3)^3*(9) + (3)^2*(-3)^2 - 4*1*(-3)^3 - 27*(1)^2*(9)^2 \\).\n\nCompute each term step by step:\n\nFirst term: \\( 18 * 1 * 3 * (-3) * 9 \\).\n\nCalculate inside: 18 * 3 = 54; 54 * (-3) = -162; -162 *9 = -1458.\n\nSecond term: \\( -4 * (27) * 9 \\).\n\nBecause \\( b^3 = 3^3 =27 \\), so: -4 * 27 *9 = -4*243 = -972.\n\nThird term: \\( (3)^2*(-3)^2 = 9 * 9 = 81 \\).\n\nFourth term: \\( -4 * 1 * (-3)^3 \\).\n\n\\( (-3)^3 = -27 \\), so: -4 * 1 * (-27) = +108.\n\nFifth term: \\( -27 * 1 * (81) = -27*81 = -2187 \\).\n\nNow, adding all terms:\n\nFirst term: -1458\n\nSecond term: -972 → total so far: -1458 -972 = -2430\n\nThird term: +81 → -2430 +81 = -2349\n\nFourth term: +108 → -2349 +108 = -2241\n\nFifth term: -2187 → -2241 -2187 = -4428.\n\nSo, indeed, discriminant is -4428, which is less than 0, so the equation has three distinct real roots. But when I tried plugging in integer values, I didn't find any roots. That suggests that the real roots are irrational or maybe between certain values?\n\nLet me try to find approximate roots.\n\nConsider the function \\( f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 9 \\).\n\nCompute \\( f(-4) = (-64) + 48 - (-12) +9 = (-64 +48)= -16; (-16 +12)= -4; (-4 +9)=5.\n\nWait, no, wait: \\( f(-4) = (-4)^3 + 3*(-4)^2 -3*(-4) +9 = -64 + 3*16 +12 +9 = -64 +48 +12 +9 = (-64 +48) = -16; (-16 +12)= -4; (-4 +9)=5. So f(-4)=5.\n\nf(-5)= (-125) + 3*25 -3*(-5)+9 = -125 +75 +15 +9 = (-125 +75)= -50; (-50 +15)= -35; (-35 +9)= -26. So f(-5)= -26.\n\nSo between x=-5 and x=-4, the function goes from -26 to 5, so crosses zero there.\n\nSimilarly, f(-3)= (-27) + 27 - (-9) +9 = (-27+27)=0; (0 +9)=9; (9 +9)=18. So f(-3)=18.\n\nf(-4)=5, f(-5)=-26, so a root between -5 and -4.\n\nThen f(-2)= (-8) + 12 - (-6) +9 = (-8+12)=4; (4 +6)=10; (10+9)=19. f(-2)=19.\n\nf(-1)= (-1)+3 - (-3)+9= (-1+3)=2; (2+3)=5; (5+9)=14. f(-1)=14.\n\nf(0)=0 +0 -0 +9=9.\n\nf(1)=1+3-3+9=10.\n\nSo the function is always positive for x >= -4 except between -5 and -4 where it crosses from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, only one real root between -5 and -4, and two complex roots? But discriminant says three real roots. Contradiction?\n\nWait a second, perhaps my mistake is in the discriminant interpretation. Wait, according to the formula, when discriminant is negative, the equation has three distinct real roots. But in our case, the function only crosses the x-axis once, so that suggests only one real root? There's a contradiction here. Hmm, so perhaps my calculation of the discriminant was wrong?\n\nWait, let me double-check the discriminant formula. Maybe I misapplied it.\n\nLooking up the discriminant of a cubic equation:\n\nThe discriminant of \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\) is:\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\).\n\nWait, yes, that's what I used. So with the numbers plugged in correctly, we get -4428. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an arithmetic mistake in calculating f(-5)?\n\nWait, let me recalculate f(-5):\n\n\\( f(-5) = (-5)^3 + 3*(-5)^2 -3*(-5) + 9 \\).\n\nCompute each term:\n\n- \\( (-5)^3 = -125 \\)\n\n- \\( 3*(-5)^2 = 3*25 = 75 \\)\n\n- \\( -3*(-5) = 15 \\)\n\n- \\( +9 \\)\n\nAdding them together: -125 + 75 = -50; -50 +15 = -35; -35 +9 = -26. So that's correct.\n\nf(-4)= (-4)^3 +3*(-4)^2 -3*(-4)+9 = -64 + 48 +12 +9 = (-64 +48)= -16; (-16 +12)= -4; (-4 +9)=5. Correct.\n\nf(-3)= (-27) + 3*(9) -3*(-3) +9 = -27 +27 +9 +9 = 18. Correct.\n\nHmm, so the function is indeed only crossing the x-axis once between -5 and -4. So only one real root? But discriminant says three real roots. Contradiction.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the discriminant formula? Let me check with another source.\n\nWait, according to the standard formula, for cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\), discriminant is:\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\).\n\nAlternatively, another source says:\n\nThe discriminant of a cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) is:\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\).\n\nSo that is the same as what I used. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I messed up the sign somewhere?\n\nWait, let me recalculate the discriminant for the cubic equation \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x + 9 =0 \\):\n\na=1, b=3, c=-3, d=9.\n\nFirst term: 18*a*b*c*d = 18*1*3*(-3)*9 = 18 * 3*(-3)*9 = 18*(-81) = -1458.\n\nSecond term: -4*b^3*d = -4*(3)^3*(9) = -4*27*9 = -972.\n\nThird term: b^2*c^2 = (3)^2*(-3)^2 = 9*9=81.\n\nFourth term: -4*a*c^3 = -4*1*(-3)^3 = -4*(-27) = +108.\n\nFifth term: -27*a^2*d^2 = -27*(1)^2*(9)^2 = -27*81 = -2187.\n\nNow adding all terms:\n\n-1458 (first term)\n\n-1458 -972 = -2430 (after second term)\n\n-2430 +81 = -2349 (third term)\n\n-2349 +108 = -2241 (fourth term)\n\n-2241 -2187 = -4428 (fifth term). Correct.\n\nHmm, so discriminant is indeed negative. But the graph only crosses once? That contradicts the theory. Wait, maybe my graph is wrong?\n\nWait, perhaps I need to check another point. Let me try x = -6:\n\nf(-6) = (-216) + 3*(36) -3*(-6) +9 = -216 +108 +18 +9 = (-216 +108) = -108; (-108 +18)= -90; (-90 +9)= -81.\n\nf(-6)= -81.\n\nf(-5)= -26, f(-4)=5, so between -6 and -5 it goes from -81 to -26, still negative.\n\nBetween -5 and -4, it goes from -26 to +5, so crosses zero once there.\n\nBetween -4 and -3, from 5 to 18, stays positive.\n\nFrom -3 onwards, it keeps increasing? f(-2)=19, f(-1)=14, f(0)=9, f(1)=10, f(2)= 8 + 12 -6 +9 = 23, etc.\n\nSo only one real root between -5 and -4. So where are the other two real roots?\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake is in assuming the depressed cubic has three real roots when discriminant is negative? Wait no, let me check with the depressed cubic.\n\nWait, the depressed cubic was \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\). Let me compute its discriminant.\n\nFor depressed cubic \\( t^3 + pt + q =0 \\), discriminant is \\( -4p^3 -27q^2 \\).\n\nHere, p = -6, q=14.\n\nDiscriminant D = -4*(-6)^3 -27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) -27*(196) = 864 - 5292 = -4428, same as before.\n\nSince D <0, then depressed cubic has three real roots. But when we made the substitution \\( x = y -1 \\), so the real roots of the depressed cubic correspond to real roots of the original cubic. Therefore, the original cubic should have three real roots, but our analysis of f(x) shows only one real root. This inconsistency must be resolved.\n\nWait, perhaps my substitution was wrong?\n\nWait, let me recheck the substitution.\n\nOriginal cubic: \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\).\n\nLet \\( x = y - d \\). To eliminate the quadratic term, we set d = b/(3a) = 3/(3*1)=1. So substitution is \\( x = y -1 \\).\n\nThen,\n\n\\( (y -1)^3 + 3(y -1)^2 -3(y -1) +9 =0 \\).\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( (y^3 - 3y^2 + 3y -1) \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( 3*(y^2 - 2y +1) = 3y^2 -6y +3 \\).\n\nThird term: \\( -3*(y -1) = -3y +3 \\).\n\nFourth term: +9.\n\nNow, add all terms together:\n\ny^3 -3y^2 +3y -1 + 3y^2 -6y +3 -3y +3 +9.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\ny^3: y^3.\n\ny^2 terms: -3y^2 + 3y^2 = 0.\n\ny terms: 3y -6y -3y = -6y.\n\nConstants: -1 +3 +3 +9 = 14.\n\nThus, equation becomes: y^3 -6y +14 =0. That is correct.\n\nNow, the depressed cubic is \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\). Let me compute its value at various y:\n\ny = -4: (-64) -6*(-4) +14 = -64 +24 +14 = -26.\n\ny = -3: (-27) -6*(-3) +14 = -27 +18 +14 = 5.\n\ny = -2: (-8) -6*(-2) +14 = -8 +12 +14 = 18.\n\ny = -1: (-1) -6*(-1) +14 = -1 +6 +14 = 19.\n\ny=0: 0 -0 +14=14.\n\ny=1:1 -6 +14=9.\n\ny=2:8 -12 +14=10.\n\ny=3:27 -18 +14=23.\n\nHmm, so the depressed cubic also only crosses the x-axis once between y=-4 and y=-3 (since at y=-4, f(y)=-26; at y=-3, f(y)=5). So only one real root there, and the other two roots are complex? But discriminant says three real roots. Wait, this is conflicting.\n\nWait, the discriminant of the depressed cubic was D = -4428 <0, which should imply three real roots. But the graph shows only one real root. What is happening?\n\nWait, maybe my calculation of the discriminant for the depressed cubic is wrong?\n\nWait, for depressed cubic \\( y^3 + py + q =0 \\), discriminant is \\( D = -4p^3 - 27q^2 \\).\n\nHere, p = -6, q =14.\n\nThus,\n\nD = -4*(-6)^3 - 27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) - 27*(196) = 864 - 5292 = -4428.\n\nSame as before, so D <0 implies one real root and two complex conjugate roots. Wait, so my earlier understanding was wrong? I thought that D <0 implies three real roots, but actually, no, it's the other way around?\n\nWait, let me double-check the discriminant conditions for the cubic.\n\nAccording to standard references:\n\nFor the cubic equation \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\):\n\n- If \\( D > 0 \\): one real root and two complex conjugate roots.\n\n- If \\( D = 0 \\): multiple root(s) and all roots real.\n\n- If \\( D < 0 \\): three distinct real roots.\n\nWait, now I'm confused because different sources might have different conventions, but according to this, if D <0, there are three distinct real roots. But in our case, the depressed cubic has D=-4428 <0, so three real roots, but our analysis shows only one real root?\n\nThere must be an error in my analysis.\n\nWait, let me graph the depressed cubic \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\).\n\nCompute values at more points:\n\nAt y = -5:\n\ny = -5: (-125) -6*(-5) +14 = -125 +30 +14 = -81.\n\nAt y = -4: -64 +24 +14 = -26.\n\nAt y = -3: -27 +18 +14 =5.\n\nAt y = -2: -8 +12 +14 = 18.\n\nAt y = -1: -1 +6 +14 =19.\n\nAt y=0:14.\n\nAt y=1:1 -6 +14=9.\n\nAt y=2:8 -12 +14=10.\n\nAt y=3:27 -18 +14=23.\n\nWait a second, the function is increasing from y = -infty to some point, then decreasing, then increasing again? Wait, let me compute the derivative.\n\nThe derivative of \\( y^3 -6y +14 \\) is \\( 3y^2 -6 \\).\n\nSetting to zero: \\( 3y^2 -6 =0 \\Rightarrow y^2 =2 \\Rightarrow y= \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414 \\) and \\( y = -\\sqrt{2} \\approx -1.414 \\).\n\nThus, the function has a local maximum at \\( y = -\\sqrt{2} \\approx -1.414 \\) and a local minimum at \\( y = \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414 \\).\n\nCompute the function at \\( y = -\\sqrt{2} \\approx -1.414 \\):\n\nLet me compute \\( f(-\\sqrt{2}) \\):\n\nFirst, \\( y^3 = (-\\sqrt{2})^3 = - (2)^{3/2} = -2.828 \\).\n\nThen, \\( -6y = -6*(-\\sqrt{2}) = 6\\sqrt{2} ≈8.485 \\).\n\nAdding the constant 14:\n\nTotal ≈ -2.828 +8.485 +14 ≈ (-2.828 +8.485)=5.657 +14≈19.657.\n\nSimilarly at \\( y = \\sqrt{2} ≈1.414 \\):\n\n\\( y^3 ≈ (1.414)^3 ≈2.828 \\).\n\n\\( -6y ≈-6*(1.414)≈-8.485 \\).\n\nThus, total ≈2.828 -8.485 +14 ≈ (2.828 -8.485)= -5.657 +14≈8.343.\n\nSo, the local maximum at \\( y ≈-1.414 \\) is about 19.657, and the local minimum at \\( y≈1.414 \\) is about 8.343.\n\nTherefore, the function only crosses the x-axis once, at y between -5 and -4, where it goes from -81 to -26 at y=-5 to y=-4, but wait, at y=-4 it is -26 and at y=-3 it is 5, so crosses from negative to positive between y=-4 and y=-3. After that, it keeps increasing to the local maximum at y≈-1.414 of ~19.657, then decreases to the local minimum at y≈1.414 of ~8.343, then increases again. Since the local minimum at y≈1.414 is still positive (~8.343), the function never crosses the x-axis again. Therefore, only one real root.\n\nThis contradicts the discriminant condition. Therefore, I must have made a mistake in the discriminant formula for the depressed cubic.\n\nWait, let me check another source for the discriminant of the depressed cubic.\n\nAccording to Wikipedia: For the depressed cubic \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\), the discriminant is \\( \\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2 \\).\n\n- If \\( \\Delta > 0 \\): one real root and two non-real complex conjugate roots.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta = 0 \\): all roots are real, with at least two equal.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta < 0 \\): three distinct real roots.\n\nWait, so according to this, when \\( \\Delta <0 \\), there are three distinct real roots. But in our case, the depressed cubic has \\( \\Delta = -4428 <0 \\), so it should have three real roots, but our analysis shows only one real root. There must be an error in my calculations.\n\nWait, wait, in our case, the depressed cubic is \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\), so p = -6, q =14.\n\nTherefore, discriminant D = -4*(-6)^3 -27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) -27*196 = 864 - 5292 = -4428 <0.\n\nAccording to the Wikipedia, that should mean three distinct real roots, but our analysis shows only one real root. Contradiction.\n\nWait, but in the graph, the function only crosses the x-axis once. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the substitution?\n\nWait, let me re-express the depressed cubic equation \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\).\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the substitution step?\n\nLet me redo the substitution:\n\nOriginal equation: \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\).\n\nLet \\( x = y - d \\), where d = coefficient of x² divided by 3a, so d= 3/(3*1)=1.\n\nTherefore, x = y -1.\n\nSubstitute into equation:\n\n\\( (y -1)^3 + 3(y -1)^2 -3(y -1) +9 =0 \\).\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( (y -1)^3 = y^3 - 3y^2 + 3y -1 \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( 3(y -1)^2 = 3(y² - 2y +1) = 3y² -6y +3 \\).\n\nThird term: \\( -3(y -1) = -3y +3 \\).\n\nFourth term: +9.\n\nNow, adding all together:\n\nFirst: y³ -3y² +3y -1,\n\nSecond: +3y² -6y +3,\n\nThird: -3y +3,\n\nFourth: +9.\n\nCombine term by term:\n\ny³: y³,\n\ny² terms: -3y² +3y² =0,\n\ny terms: 3y -6y -3y = (3y -6y) = -3y; (-3y -3y)= -6y,\n\nConstants: -1 +3 +3 +9 = (-1 +3)=2; (2 +3)=5; (5 +9)=14.\n\nThus, the equation becomes \\( y³ -6y +14 =0 \\). Correct.\n\nHmm, so the substitution is correct. Then why does the graph only show one real root?\n\nWait, perhaps my calculation of the local maximum and minimum was wrong?\n\nWait, derivative is \\( 3y² -6 \\), so critical points at y=±√2 ≈ ±1.414.\n\nAt y= -√2 ≈-1.414:\n\nCompute f(-√2):\n\nFirst, y³ = (-√2)^3 = - (√2)^3 = - (2.828) ≈ -2.828,\n\nThen, -6y = -6*(-√2) = 6*1.414 ≈8.485,\n\nSo total: -2.828 +8.485 +14 ≈ (-2.828 +8.485)=5.657 +14≈19.657,\n\nWhich is positive.\n\nAt y=√2 ≈1.414:\n\ny³≈ (1.414)^3≈2.828,\n\n-6y≈-6*1.414≈-8.485,\n\nSo total: 2.828 -8.485 +14≈ (2.828 -8.485)= -5.657 +14≈8.343,\n\nAlso positive.\n\nSo the function has a local maximum at y≈-1.414 of ~19.657 and a local minimum at y≈1.414 of ~8.343, both above zero. Therefore, the function only crosses the x-axis once on the left side where y is less than -√2, but since after that it goes to -infty as y approaches -infty?\n\nWait, wait a second. As y approaches -infty, the term y^3 dominates, so \\( y^3 -6y +14 \\) approaches -infty. Therefore, since at y=-5, f(y)= -81, and at y=-4, f(y)=-26, then at y approaching -infty, it goes to -infty, so between y=-infty and y=-5, the function goes from -infty to -81, so no crossing there. Between y=-5 and y=-4, it goes from -81 to -26, still negative. Then between y=-4 and y=-3, it goes from -26 to +5, crossing zero once there. Then from y=-3 onwards, it keeps increasing to the local maximum at y≈-1.414, then decreases to the local minimum at y≈1.414 (still positive), then increases to infinity. So only one real root between y=-4 and y=-3. Thus, only one real root for the depressed cubic, implying only one real root for the original cubic. But according to discriminant, it should have three real roots. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in my understanding of the discriminant for the depressed cubic? Let me check another source.\n\nAccording to the Wikipedia page on cubic functions, under the section \"Discriminant\", for the depressed cubic \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\):\n\nThe discriminant is \\( \\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2 \\).\n\n- If \\( \\Delta > 0 \\): one real root and two non-real complex conjugate roots.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta = 0 \\): multiple root(s) and all roots real.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta < 0 \\): three distinct real roots.\n\nWait, so if \\( \\Delta <0 \\), three distinct real roots. But in our case, the depressed cubic has \\( \\Delta = -4428 <0 \\), so should have three distinct real roots, but our analysis shows only one real root. This inconsistency must be due to an error in calculations?\n\nWait, but the function \\( f(y) = y^3 -6y +14 \\) evaluated at y = -5 is -81, at y = -4 is -26, at y = -3 is 5, so crosses from negative to positive between y=-4 and y=-3: that's one real root.\n\nAt y approaching -infty, it goes to -infty, so between y=-infty and y=-5, it goes from -infty to -81, so no crossing there.\n\nBetween y=-3 and y=+infty, the function never goes below zero, since the local minimum at y≈1.414 is ~8.343, so no other crossings.\n\nTherefore, only one real root. But discriminant says three real roots. Contradiction. So, I must have messed up the discriminant calculation?\n\nWait, let me compute discriminant again:\n\nDepressed cubic: \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\).\n\nSo p = -6, q =14.\n\nDiscriminant D = -4*(-6)^3 -27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) -27*(196).\n\nCompute each term:\n\n-4*(-216) = 864,\n\n-27*(196): 27*200 =5400; 27*4=108; so 27*196=27*(200-4)=5400-108=5292.\n\nThus, D =864 -5292 = -4428, which is correct.\n\nHmm, so what's going on? Maybe the problem is that the discriminant formula for the depressed cubic is different?\n\nWait, no, the formula is standard. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the substitution?\n\nWait, original cubic: \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\).\n\nAfter substitution x = y -1, we get \\( y^3 -6y +14 =0 \\).\n\nLet me plug in y = -4. Let me see:\n\ny = -4: (-64) -6*(-4) +14 = -64 +24 +14 = -26. Correct.\n\ny = -3: (-27) -6*(-3) +14 = -27 +18 +14=5. Correct.\n\nWait, so between y=-4 and y=-3, f(y) crosses from -26 to +5, so crosses zero once there.\n\nBetween y=-5 and y=-4: f(-5)= -81 to f(-4)= -26: stays negative.\n\nBetween y=-infty and y=-5: f approaches -infty and at y=-5 is -81, so no crossing.\n\nBetween y=-3 and y=+infty: the function never goes below zero, since the local minimum is at y≈1.414 with f(y)=~8.343.\n\nThus only one real root. So why does discriminant say three real roots?\n\nThis is a paradox. Perhaps the discriminant formula is different?\n\nWait, perhaps I mixed up the sign in the discriminant formula.\n\nWait, according to the Wikipedia page, for the cubic equation \\( t^3 + pt + q = 0 \\), the discriminant is \\( \\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2 \\).\n\nBut in our case, p = -6, q =14.\n\nSo,\n\nΔ = -4*(-6)^3 -27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) -27*(196) = 864 - 5292 = -4428 <0,\n\nwhich according to Wikipedia implies three distinct real roots. But the graph shows only one real root.\n\nWait, unless I'm miscalculating the graph?\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake is in assuming that the local minimum is at y=√2 ≈1.414? Let me recalculate the critical points.\n\nThe derivative is \\( 3y² -6 \\), set to zero:\n\n\\( 3y² =6 \\Rightarrow y² =2 ⇒ y= ±√2 ≈ ±1.414 \\).\n\nThus, critical points at y≈±1.414.\n\nAt y = -√2 ≈-1.414:\n\nf(y)= (-√2)^3 -6*(-√2) +14 ≈ (-2.828) +8.485 +14 ≈19.657.\n\nAt y=√2 ≈1.414:\n\nf(y)= (√2)^3 -6*(√2) +14 ≈2.828 -8.485 +14≈8.343.\n\nThus, the function has a local maximum at y≈-1.414 of ~19.657 and a local minimum at y≈1.414 of ~8.343.\n\nTherefore, since the local minimum is still above zero, the function only crosses the x-axis once on the far left.\n\nTherefore, this suggests only one real root, contradicting the discriminant's implication of three real roots.\n\nThis must mean that there is an error in the discriminant formula for the depressed cubic. Wait, let me check another source.\n\nAccording to the book \"Abstract Algebra\" by Herstein, the discriminant of the cubic equation \\( x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c =0 \\) is \\( \\Delta = 18abc -4a^3c + a^2b^2 -4b^3 -27c^2 \\). Which is what I used earlier. So for the depressed cubic \\( y^3 + py + q =0 \\), substituting a=0, b=p, c=q, the discriminant would be:\n\n\\( \\Delta = 18*0*p*q -4*0^3*q +0^2*p^2 -4*p^3 -27*q^2 = -4p^3 -27q^2 \\). Which matches the formula above. So the discriminant is correct.\n\nTherefore, there is a contradiction here. It must be that my graph analysis is wrong, but I can't see how. Alternatively, perhaps there's a mistake in the substitution?\n\nWait, let me try plugging in a value less than -5, say y = -6:\n\nf(-6) = (-6)^3 -6*(-6) +14 = -216 +36 +14 = -166.\n\nAt y = -7:\n\nf(-7)= -343 -6*(-7)+14 = -343 +42 +14= -287.\n\nSo it's decreasing further to -infty as y approaches -infty.\n\nWait a second, but between y=-infty and y=-5, it goes from -infty to -81 at y=-5, so it's monotonically increasing? Wait, the derivative is 3y² -6. For y < -√2 ≈-1.414, the derivative is positive (since y² >2 ⇒ 3y² -6 >0). Thus, for y < -√2, the function is increasing.\n\nTherefore, from y=-infty to y=-√2, the function is increasing from -infty to f(-√2)=~19.657.\n\nTherefore, between y=-infty and y=-√2 (~-1.414), the function increases from -infty to ~19.657, crossing the x-axis exactly once in that interval.\n\nThen, from y=-√2 to y=√2 (~1.414), the function decreases from ~19.657 to ~8.343.\n\nThen, from y=√2 to infinity, the function increases from ~8.343 to infinity.\n\nTherefore, the function crosses the x-axis only once, in the interval y < -√2 (~-1.414). So that's one real root, and the other two roots are complex? But discriminant says three real roots.\n\nWait, but according to the derivative, between y=-infty to y=-√2, the function is increasing from -infty to ~19.657, so it must cross the x-axis exactly once there. Then from y=-√2 to y=√2, it decreases from ~19.657 to ~8.343, which is still above zero, so no crossing. Then from y=√2 onwards, it increases from ~8.343 to infinity, so no crossing. Therefore, only one real root. So the discriminant must be wrong?\n\nBut according to the formula, discriminant D=-4428 <0 implies three real roots. But the graph shows only one real root. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, unless there's a miscalculation in the discriminant?\n\nWait, let me recalculate discriminant for depressed cubic \\( y^3 -6y +14=0 \\):\n\np = coefficient of y term = -6,\n\nq = constant term =14.\n\nDiscriminant D = -4*(-6)^3 -27*(14)^2 = -4*(-216) -27*(196) = 864 - 5292 = -4428.\n\nYes, that's correct. So if D <0, three real roots. But graph shows only one. I must be missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in my assumption that the cubic has three real roots when discriminant is negative? Wait, according to Wikipedia:\n\n\"For the general cubic equation, the discriminant is given by \\( \\Delta = 18abcd -4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\). The equation has:\n\n- Three distinct real roots if \\( \\Delta > 0 \\),\n\n- A multiple root and two other real roots if \\( \\Delta = 0 \\),\n\n- One real root and two non-real complex conjugate roots if \\( \\Delta < 0 \\).\"\n\nWait a second! Wait, that contradicts what I thought earlier. According to this, if \\( \\Delta <0 \\), there is one real root and two complex roots. If \\( \\Delta >0 \\), three real roots. If \\( \\Delta =0 \\), multiple root and others real.\n\nSo my mistake was in the previous assertion from Wikipedia about the depressed cubic. The general cubic discriminant says:\n\n- If \\( \\Delta >0 \\): three distinct real roots.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta =0 \\): multiple root and all roots real.\n\n- If \\( \\Delta <0 \\): one real root and two non-real complex conjugate roots.\n\nAh! So that's the correct interpretation. Therefore, for the depressed cubic with \\( \\Delta =-4428 <0 \\), it has one real root and two complex conjugate roots. So my initial analysis was correct, and the confusion was due to a misquote from another source.\n\nTherefore, the cubic equation \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\) has one real root and two complex roots. Thus, the case \\( S = -3 \\) does not give us three distinct real numbers \\( a, b, c \\), but rather one real and two complex. Since the problem states that \\( a, b, c \\) are pairwise distinct complex numbers, which includes complex numbers (not necessarily real), so this is acceptable? Wait, but the problem didn't specify that they are real, so complex numbers are allowed.\n\nBut the question is to compute the two possible values of \\( a + b + c \\). So perhaps both cases when \\( z =10 \\) and \\( z = -9 \\) are valid, leading to two different values of \\( S \\).\n\nWait, but when \\( S = -3 \\), the cubic has one real and two complex roots. Since the problem allows complex numbers, that is acceptable. However, in the case of \\( z =10 \\), we saw that the roots included a real number and two complex numbers which when cycled through didn't satisfy the original equations because of duplication, but maybe with complex roots it works?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to consider both cases for \\( z \\) when \\( S = -3 \\).\n\nWait, when \\( S = -3 \\), we have two possibilities for \\( z \\): 10 and -9.\n\nThe cubic equation with \\( z =10 \\) had roots that didn't satisfy the original equations because of duplication, but perhaps the other cubic equation with \\( z =-9 \\) does?\n\nWait, let me check the cubic equation with \\( z =-9 \\):\n\nCubic equation: \\( x^3 + 3x^2 - 3x +9 =0 \\). Wait, no, no, wait:\n\nWait, when \\( S = -3 \\), and \\( z = -9 \\), the cubic equation is \\( x^3 + 3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\), which we just analyzed has one real root and two complex roots.\n\nSuppose we assign the real root as \\( a \\), then compute \\( b = a^2 -6 \\), then \\( c = b^2 -6 \\), then check if \\( c^2 -6 = a \\).\n\nGiven that the roots are one real and two complex, let's denote the real root as \\( a \\), and the complex roots as \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), but since they are complex conjugates, let's say \\( b = p + qi \\), \\( c = p - qi \\).\n\nThen, let's see if \\( a^2 = b +6 \\), \\( b^2 = c +6 \\), \\( c^2 = a +6 \\).\n\nThis might hold in complex numbers.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider that even though the roots aren't real, they still satisfy the cyclic equations in the complex plane.\n\nSince the problem allows complex numbers, perhaps both cases \\( z =10 \\) and \\( z = -9 \\) correspond to valid solutions with complex \\( a, b, c \\), but when I tried the real roots, they didn't work, but the complex roots might?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \\( S = -3 \\) is one possible value, and there is another value of \\( S \\) from a different equation?\n\nWait, earlier when I tried \\( S = -3 \\), we obtained two possibilities for \\( z \\), but the cubic equations didn't yield valid roots when considering real assignments. But maybe there are other values of \\( S \\) that also satisfy the equation \\( z^2 - z - 3S^2 - 33S -162 =0 \\), leading to different \\( S \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there are two possible values of \\( S \\), and when I considered \\( S = -3 \\), it's one of them, and there's another \\( S \\).\n\nWait, the equation for \\( S \\) is a quadratic in \\( z \\), but we need another relation to find \\( S \\). Alternatively, maybe there are two possible values of \\( S \\) that satisfy some equation.\n\nWait, let me consider that we have two equations:\n\n1. \\( y = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\).\n\n2. \\( z^2 - z - 3S^2 - 33S -162 =0 \\).\n\nAdditionally, we can consider that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of the cubic equation \\( x^3 - Sx^2 + yx - z =0 \\), and since each of them satisfies their respective equation \\( a^2 = b +6 \\), etc., perhaps we can form another equation by combining these?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that the sum \\( a + b + c = S \\), and also the sum \\( a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S + 18 \\).\n\nAdditionally, the sum \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 \\) can be expressed in terms of \\( S \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\).\n\nRecall that \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = (a + b + c)^3 - 3(a + b + c)(ab + bc + ca) + 3abc \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = S^3 - 3S y + 3z \\).\n\nBut also, from the original equations:\n\nFrom Equation (1): \\( a^2 = b +6 \\Rightarrow a^3 = a b +6a \\).\n\nSimilarly, \\( b^3 = b c +6b \\),\n\n\\( c^3 = c a +6c \\).\n\nThus, summing these:\n\n\\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = a b + b c + c a +6(a + b + c) \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = y + 6S \\).\n\nTherefore, equate the two expressions for \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 \\):\n\n\\( S^3 - 3S y + 3z = y + 6S \\).\n\nNow, substitute \\( y = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\):\n\nFirst, compute each term:\n\nLeft side:\n\n\\( S^3 - 3S \\cdot \\left( \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\right) + 3z \\).\n\nRight side:\n\n\\( \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} + 6S \\).\n\nLet me compute left side:\n\nFirst term: \\( S^3 \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( -3S \\cdot \\frac{S^2 - S -18}{2} = -\\frac{3S(S^2 - S -18)}{2} \\).\n\nThird term: \\( 3z \\).\n\nRight side:\n\nFirst term: \\( \\frac{S^2 - S -18}{2} \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( 6S = \\frac{12S}{2} \\).\n\nThus, right side: \\( \\frac{S^2 - S -18 + 12S}{2} = \\frac{S^2 +11S -18}{2} \\).\n\nSo, putting together:\n\nLeft side:\n\n\\( S^3 - \\frac{3S(S^2 - S -18)}{2} + 3z \\).\n\nRight side:\n\n\\( \\frac{S^2 +11S -18}{2} \\).\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 to eliminate denominators:\n\nLeft side:\n\n\\( 2S^3 - 3S(S^2 - S -18) + 6z \\).\n\nRight side:\n\n\\( S^2 +11S -18 \\).\n\nExpand the left side's second term:\n\n\\( -3S(S^2 - S -18) = -3S^3 +3S^2 +54S \\).\n\nThus, left side becomes:\n\n\\( 2S^3 + (-3S^3 +3S^2 +54S) +6z \\).\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\( (2S^3 -3S^3) + 3S^2 +54S +6z \\).\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 3S^2 +54S +6z \\).\n\nThus, equation becomes:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 3S^2 +54S +6z = S^2 +11S -18 \\).\n\nBring all terms to the left side:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 3S^2 +54S +6z - S^2 -11S +18 =0 \\).\n\nSimplify:\n\n- \\( -S^3 \\)\n\n- \\( 3S^2 - S^2 = 2S^2 \\)\n\n- \\( 54S -11S =43S \\)\n\n- \\( +18 \\)\n\n- \\( +6z \\)\n\nThus:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 2S^2 +43S +18 +6z =0 \\).\n\nRearranged:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 2S^2 +43S +18 +6z =0 \\).\n\nNow, recall from Equation (7): \\( z^2 - z -3S^2 -33S -162 =0 \\).\n\nWe can solve Equation (7) for \\( z^2 \\):\n\n\\( z^2 = z + 3S^2 +33S +162 \\).\n\nBut we need to relate this to the current equation involving \\( z \\).\n\nLet me see if we can express \\( z \\) from the current equation and substitute.\n\nThe current equation is:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 2S^2 +43S +18 +6z =0 \\).\n\nSolve for \\( z \\):\n\n\\( 6z = S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18 \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( z = \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18}{6} \\).\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\( z \\) into Equation (7):\n\n\\( z^2 - z -3S^2 -33S -162 =0 \\).\n\nThis will give an equation solely in terms of \\( S \\).\n\nThis will be a high-degree equation, but let's proceed step by step.\n\nLet me denote \\( z = \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18}{6} \\).\n\nThen,\n\n\\( z^2 = \\left( \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18}{6} \\right)^2 \\).\n\nThe equation becomes:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18}{6} \\right)^2 - \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18}{6} -3S^2 -33S -162 =0 \\).\n\nThis looks very complicated, but let's multiply both sides by \\( 36 \\) to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( 36 z^2 - 6 z - 36*(3S^2 +33S +162) =0 \\).\n\nWait, actually, better to do term by term:\n\nMultiply each term by 36:\n\nFirst term: \\( 36 z^2 = 36 * \\left( \\frac{(S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18)^2}{36} \\right) = (S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18)^2 \\).\n\nSecond term: \\( -6 z = -6 * \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18}{6} = - (S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18) \\).\n\nThird term: \\( -36*(3S^2 +33S +162) = -108S^2 - 1188S -5832 \\).\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\( (S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18)^2 - (S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18) -108S^2 -1188S -5832 =0 \\).\n\nThis is a massive equation, but perhaps we can factor or find roots by substitution.\n\nLet me denote \\( T = S^3 - 2S^2 -43S -18 \\).\n\nThen, the equation is:\n\n\\( T^2 - T -108S^2 -1188S -5832 =0 \\).\n\nBut this substitution might not help much. Alternatively, perhaps try plugging in possible integer values of \\( S \\).\n\nWe already tried \\( S = -3 \\), which gave us a cubic with one real root, but maybe that is one solution, and there is another integer value?\n\nAlternatively, let me try \\( S = 3 \\):\n\nCompute \\( z = \\frac{27 - 18 - 129 -18}{6} = \\frac{27 -18=9; 9 -129= -120; -120 -18= -138}{6} = -138/6 = -23 \\).\n\nThen, plug into Equation (7): \\( z^2 - z -3S^2 -33S -162 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( (-23)^2 - (-23) -3*(9) -33*3 -162 = 529 +23 -27 -99 -162 = (529+23)=552; (552-27)=525; (525-99)=426; (426-162)=264 ≠0. Not zero.\n\nTry \\( S = 0 \\):\n\n\\( z = \\frac{0 -0 -0 -18}{6} = -18/6 = -3 \\).\n\nEquation (7): \\( (-3)^2 - (-3) -0 -0 -162 =9 +3 -162 = -150 ≠0.\n\nTry \\( S = -6 \\):\n\nCompute \\( z = [ (-216) - 2*(36) -43*(-6) -18 ] /6.\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n-216 -72 +258 -18 = (-216-72)= -288; (-288 +258)= -30; (-30 -18)= -48.\n\nThus, z = -48 /6 = -8.\n\nPlug into Equation (7):\n\n\\( (-8)^2 - (-8) -3*(36) -33*(-6) -162 =64 +8 -108 +198 -162.\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\n64 +8 =72;\n\n72 -108 = -36;\n\n-36 +198 =162;\n\n162 -162 =0.\n\nHey! It works! So when \\( S = -6 \\), we get \\( z = -8 \\), and Equation (7) is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, \\( S = -6 \\) is a solution.\n\nTherefore, this is another possible value for \\( S \\).\n\nSo, now we have two possible values for \\( S \\): \\( S = -3 \\) and \\( S = -6 \\).\n\nWait, let me verify with \\( S = -6 \\).\n\nSo, if \\( S = -6 \\), then:\n\nFrom Equation (5):\n\n\\( y = \\frac{S^2 - S -18}{2} = \\frac{36 - (-6) -18}{2} = \\frac{36 +6 -18}{2} = \\frac{24}{2}=12 \\).\n\nFrom Equation (7): \\( z^2 - z -3*(-6)^2 -33*(-6) -162 = z^2 - z -108 +198 -162 = z^2 - z -72 =0 \\).\n\nSolving for z:\n\n\\( z = [1 ± sqrt(1 + 288)] /2 = [1 ± sqrt(289)] /2 = [1 ±17]/2 \\).\n\nThus, \\( z = (1 +17)/2 = 9 \\) or \\( z = (1 -17)/2 = -8 \\).\n\nSo when \\( S = -6 \\), possible \\( z =9 \\) or \\( z =-8 \\).\n\nWait, but we computed \\( z = -8 \\), so that's one possibility. The other is \\( z =9 \\).\n\nTherefore, for \\( S = -6 \\), we have two possibilities for \\( z \\): 9 and -8.\n\nLet me check the cubic equation for \\( z =-8 \\):\n\nCubic equation: \\( x^3 - Sx^2 + yx - z = x^3 - (-6)x^2 +12x - (-8) =x^3 +6x^2 +12x +8 =0 \\).\n\nFactor this cubic equation:\n\nTry rational roots: possible roots ±1, ±2, ±4, ±8.\n\nTest x = -2:\n\n\\( (-8) +6*(4) +12*(-2) +8 = -8 +24 -24 +8 =0. Yes! x = -2 is a root.\n\nFactor out (x + 2):\n\nUsing synthetic division:\n\nCoefficients: 1 | 6 | 12 |8\n\nDivide by x = -2:\n\nBring down 1.\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient:6 + (-2)=4.\n\nMultiply by -2:4*(-2)= -8. Add to next coefficient:12 + (-8)=4.\n\nMultiply by -2:4*(-2)= -8. Add to last term:8 + (-8)=0.\n\nThus, quotient polynomial: \\(x^2 +4x +4 \\).\n\nTherefore, the cubic factors as \\( (x +2)(x^2 +4x +4) = (x+2)(x+2)^2 = (x+2)^3 \\).\n\nThus, the cubic equation has a triple root at x = -2. But the problem states that \\( a, b, c \\) must be pairwise distinct. Therefore, this solution is invalid since all roots are the same.\n\nTherefore, the other possibility for \\( z =9 \\):\n\nCubic equation: \\(x^3 +6x^2 +12x -9 =0 \\).\n\nTry rational roots: possible roots ±1, ±3, ±9.\n\nTest x=1: 1 +6 +12 -9 =10 ≠0.\n\nx= -3: (-27) + 54 -36 -9 = (-27+54)=27; (27-36)= -9; (-9 -9)= -18 ≠0.\n\nx=3: 27 +54 +36 -9= 108 ≠0.\n\nx= -1: -1 +6 -12 -9= -16 ≠0.\n\nx= -9: too big, likely not.\n\nThus, no rational roots. Let me check discriminant.\n\nFor cubic \\( x^3 +6x^2 +12x -9 =0 \\).\n\nUsing discriminant formula:\n\n\\( a=1, b=6, c=12, d=-9 \\).\n\nΔ = 18*1*6*12*(-9) -4*6^3*(-9) +6^2*12^2 -4*1*12^3 -27*1^2*(-9)^2.\n\nThis will take time, but let me compute term by term:\n\nFirst term: 18*1*6*12*(-9) = 18*6*12*(-9).\n\n18*6 = 108; 108*12=1296; 1296*(-9)= -11664.\n\nSecond term: -4*b^3*d = -4*(216)*(-9) = -4*(-1944)= +7776.\n\nThird term: b^2*c^2 = 36*144 = 5184.\n\nFourth term: -4*a*c^3 = -4*1*(1728) = -6912.\n\nFifth term: -27*a^2*d^2 = -27*1*81 = -2187.\n\nNow, sum all terms:\n\nFirst: -11664\n\nSecond: +7776 → total: -11664 +7776 = -3888\n\nThird: +5184 → total: -3888 +5184 = 1296\n\nFourth: -6912 → total: 1296 -6912 = -5616\n\nFifth: -2187 → total: -5616 -2187 = -7803.\n\nThus discriminant Δ = -7803 <0, so one real root and two complex roots.\n\nTherefore, this cubic has one real root and two complex roots, which are distinct from each other (since discriminant <0 implies distinct roots). Thus, this would satisfy the condition of pairwise distinct complex numbers.\n\nTherefore, \\( S = -6 \\) is another valid solution.\n\nSo now we have two possible values for \\( S = a + b + c \\): \\( S = -3 \\) and \\( S = -6 \\).\n\nWait, but when \\( S = -3 \\), the cubic equation had one real root and two complex roots, but when I tried assigning the real root as \\( a \\), it led to duplication. However, perhaps in the complex case, it works?\n\nLet me consider \\( S = -6 \\):\n\nThe cubic equation with \\( z =9 \\) has one real root and two complex roots. Let me denote the real root as \\( a \\), and the complex roots as \\( b \\) and \\( c \\).\n\nThen, according to the original equations:\n\n\\( a^2 = b +6 \\),\n\n\\( b^2 = c +6 \\),\n\n\\( c^2 = a +6 \\).\n\nSince \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) are complex conjugates (assuming the cubic has real coefficients, which it does), let me denote \\( b = p + qi \\), \\( c = p - qi \\).\n\nThen, \\( a^2 = (p + qi) +6 \\).\n\n\\( b^2 = (p + qi)^2 = p^2 - q^2 + 2p qi = (p^2 - q^2) + 2p qi = c +6 = (p - qi) +6 \\).\n\nTherefore, equating real and imaginary parts:\n\nReal part of \\( b^2 \\): \\( p^2 - q^2 = p +6 \\).\n\nImaginary part of \\( b^2 \\): \\( 2p q = -q \\).\n\nFrom the imaginary part equation:\n\n\\( 2p q = -q \\).\n\nAssuming \\( q ≠0 \\) (since otherwise \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) would be real), we can divide both sides by \\( q \\):\n\n\\( 2p = -1 ⇒ p = -1/2 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( p = -1/2 \\) into the real part equation:\n\n\\( (-1/2)^2 - q^2 = (-1/2) +6 \\).\n\nCompute left side: \\( 1/4 - q^2 \\).\n\nRight side: \\( -1/2 +6 = 11/2 \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( 1/4 - q^2 = 11/2 \\).\n\nSolving for \\( q^2 \\):\n\n\\( -q^2 = 11/2 -1/4 = (22/4 -1/4)=21/4 ⇒ q^2 = -21/4 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( q = ± i \\sqrt{21}/2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the complex roots are \\( b = -1/2 + i \\sqrt{21}/2 \\), and \\( c = -1/2 - i \\sqrt{21}/2 \\).\n\nNow, let's compute \\( a \\):\n\nFrom \\( a^2 = b +6 = (-1/2 + i \\sqrt{21}/2) +6 = (11/2) + i \\sqrt{21}/2 \\).\n\nThus, \\( a = \\sqrt{ \\frac{11}{2} + i \\sqrt{21}/2 } \\).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated, but perhaps we can compute \\( a \\) explicitly.\n\nLet me denote \\( a = x + yi \\), then \\( a^2 = (x^2 - y^2) + 2xyi = \\frac{11}{2} + i \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2} \\).\n\nEquating real and imaginary parts:\n\n1. \\( x^2 - y^2 = 11/2 \\).\n\n2. \\( 2xy = \\sqrt{21}/2 \\).\n\nLet me solve these equations.\n\nFrom equation 2: \\( xy = \\sqrt{21}/4 \\).\n\nLet me denote \\( x = \\sqrt{21}/(4y) \\).\n\nSubstitute into equation 1:\n\n\\( (\\sqrt{21}/(4y))^2 - y^2 = 11/2 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( (21)/(16 y^2) - y^2 = 11/2 \\).\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( 16 y^2 \\):\n\n21 - 16 y^4 = 88 y^2.\n\nRearranged:\n\n\\( -16 y^4 -88 y^2 +21 =0 \\).\n\nLet me set \\( u = y^2 \\), then equation becomes:\n\n\\( -16 u^2 -88 u +21 =0 \\).\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n\\( 16u^2 +88u -21 =0 \\).\n\nSolve for \\( u \\):\n\n\\( u = [ -88 ± sqrt(88^2 -4*16*(-21)) ] / (2*16) \\).\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n\\( 7744 + 1344 = 9088 \\).\n\nsqrt(9088): Let me see, 95^2=9025, 96^2=9216, so between 95 and 96.\n\nBut exact value might not be necessary. Anyway,\n\n\\( u = [ -88 ± sqrt(9088) ] /32 \\).\n\nSince \\( u = y^2 \\geq0 \\), we take the positive root:\n\n\\( u = [ -88 + sqrt(9088) ] /32 \\).\n\nBut this is getting too messy. Perhaps instead of computing \\( a \\), we can just accept that such complex numbers exist, since the equations are satisfied in the complex plane.\n\nTherefore, \\( S = -6 \\) is valid.\n\nNow, let's revisit \\( S = -3 \\).\n\nEarlier, when \\( S = -3 \\), we had two possibilities for \\( z \\): 10 and -9.\n\nFor \\( z =10 \\), the cubic equation had roots -2, and two complex roots which when assigned led to duplication, but perhaps in the complex case it works?\n\nLet me consider the cubic equation with \\( z =10 \\): \\( x^3 +3x^2 -3x -10 =0 \\).\n\nWe found that it has a real root at x = -2 and two complex roots.\n\nLet me denote the real root as \\( a = -2 \\), then \\( b = a^2 -6 = 4 -6 = -2 \\), which is same as \\( a \\), so duplicate. Not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, suppose the real root is not \\( a \\), but one of the complex roots is \\( a \\), then the other complex root is \\( c \\), and the real root is \\( b \\).\n\nLet me denote the roots as \\( a = p + qi \\), \\( b = r \\) (real), \\( c = p - qi \\).\n\nThen, according to the equations:\n\n\\( a^2 = b +6 \\),\n\n\\( b^2 = c +6 \\),\n\n\\( c^2 = a +6 \\).\n\nLet me try to see if this can hold.\n\nFirst, compute \\( b^2 = c +6 \\).\n\nSince \\( c = p - qi \\), then \\( b^2 = (p - qi) +6 \\).\n\nBut \\( b \\) is real, so \\( b^2 \\) is real. Therefore, the imaginary part of \\( c +6 \\) must be zero. Since \\( c \\) has imaginary part \\( -qi \\), so \\( -q =0 \\Rightarrow q=0 \\). But then \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) would be real, contradicting that they are complex. Therefore, this is impossible.\n\nThus, the case \\( z =10 \\) leads to inconsistency, so only \\( z = -9 \\) is valid for \\( S =-3 \\), but we saw that cubic equation with \\( z =-9 \\) has one real root and two complex roots, so similar to the \\( S =-6 \\) case, perhaps it works?\n\nLet me consider the cubic equation with \\( S =-3 \\), \\( z =-9 \\): \\( x^3 +3x^2 -3x +9 =0 \\).\n\nThis cubic has one real root and two complex roots. Let me denote the real root as \\( a \\), and the complex roots as \\( b \\) and \\( c \\).\n\nThen, according to the equations:\n\n\\( a^2 = b +6 \\),\n\n\\( b^2 = c +6 \\),\n\n\\( c^2 = a +6 \\).\n\nSince \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) are complex conjugates (because coefficients are real), let \\( b = p + qi \\), \\( c = p - qi \\).\n\nThen,\n\n\\( a^2 = (p + qi) +6 \\),\n\n\\( b^2 = (p + qi)^2 = p^2 - q^2 + 2p qi = (p - qi) +6 = c +6 \\).\n\nThus, equate real and imaginary parts of \\( b^2 = c +6 \\):\n\nReal part: \\( p^2 - q^2 = p +6 \\),\n\nImaginary part: \\( 2p q = -q \\).\n\nAssuming \\( q ≠0 \\), divide by \\( q \\):\n\n\\( 2p = -1 ⇒ p = -1/2 \\).\n\nSubstitute into real part equation:\n\n\\( (-1/2)^2 - q^2 = (-1/2) +6 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( 1/4 - q^2 = 11/2 \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( -q^2 =