s of \\( f \\) are roots of \\( P(x) \\). Therefore, \\( P(x) \\) is divisible by \\( f(x) - x = x^2 - x - 6 \\). Let me verify that.\n\nLet me compute \\( P(x) = f(f(f(x))) - x \\). Since \\( f(f(f(x))) = f(f(y)) \\) where \\( y = f(x) \\), but maybe it's easier to do polynomial division.\n\nAlternatively, since \\( P(x) = f(f(f(x))) - x \\), and since \\( f(x) - x \\) divides \\( P(x) \\), because if \\( f(x) = x \\), then \\( P(x) = f(f(f(x))) - x = f(f(x)) - x = f(x) - x = 0 \\). Therefore, \\( f(x) - x \\) divides \\( P(x) \\). Therefore, \\( P(x) = (x^2 - x - 6) \\cdot Q(x) \\), where \\( Q(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 6.\n\nTherefore, perhaps if I factor out \\( x^2 - x - 6 \\) from \\( P(x) \\), then the remaining polynomial \\( Q(x) \\) of degree 6 would have the roots corresponding to the 3-cycles and other cycles? Maybe. But factoring an eighth-degree polynomial is going to be tedious. Alternatively, perhaps there is a better way.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are in a cycle, then each of them satisfies \\( f(f(f(a))) = a \\), so they are roots of \\( P(x) = 0 \\), but not roots of \\( f(x) - x = 0 \\). Therefore, the roots we are interested in are the roots of \\( Q(x) = 0 \\), where \\( Q(x) = P(x)/(x^2 - x - 6) \\). So, if we can compute \\( Q(x) \\), then we can perhaps analyze it for its roots and find the sum of the roots in each 3-cycle.\n\nHowever, computing \\( Q(x) \\) requires polynomial division, which is going to be quite involved. Maybe there's a smarter substitution or another trick.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that if \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are in a 3-cycle, then:\n\n\\( a \\to b \\to c \\to a \\), under the mapping \\( f \\).\n\nTherefore, the mapping cycles through \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), so each is the image of the previous under \\( f \\).\n\nTherefore, if we consider the sum \\( S = a + b + c \\), perhaps we can find a relation for \\( S \\).\n\nLet me see. Let