layer is \\( [u^2 - 6]^2 - 6 = (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 - 6 \\)\n\nTherefore, the entire equation is:\n\n\\( y = (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 - 6 \\)\n\nTherefore, moving all terms to the left-hand side:\n\n\\( (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 - 6 - y = 0 \\)\n\nLet me expand \\( (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 \\):\n\nFirst, let me denote \\( A = y^4 \\), \\( B = -12y^2 \\), \\( C = 30 \\).\n\nThen, \\( (A + B + C)^2 = A^2 + B^2 + C^2 + 2AB + 2AC + 2BC \\).\n\nCompute each term:\n\n- \\( A^2 = (y^4)^2 = y^8 \\)\n- \\( B^2 = (-12y^2)^2 = 144y^4 \\)\n- \\( C^2 = 30^2 = 900 \\)\n- \\( 2AB = 2(y^4)(-12y^2) = -24y^6 \\)\n- \\( 2AC = 2(y^4)(30) = 60y^4 \\)\n- \\( 2BC = 2(-12y^2)(30) = -720y^2 \\)\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\n\\( (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 = y^8 - 24y^6 + (144y^4 + 60y^4) + (-720y^2) + 900 \\)\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n- \\( y^8 \\)\n- \\( -24y^6 \\)\n- \\( 144y^4 + 60y^4 = 204y^4 \\)\n- \\( -720y^2 \\)\n- \\( +900 \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 = y^8 - 24y^6 + 204y^4 - 720y^2 + 900 \\)\n\nTherefore, the equation \\( (y^4 - 12y^2 + 30)^2 - 6 - y = 0 \\) becomes:\n\n\\( y^8 - 24y^6 + 204y^4 - 720y^2 + 900 - 6 - y = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify constants: \\( 900 - 6 = 894 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( y^8 - 24y^6 + 204y^4 - 720y^2 - y + 894 = 0 \\)\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\n\\( y^8 - 24y^6 + 204y^4 - 720y^2 - y + 894 = 0 \\)\n\nHmm, so this is the eighth-degree equation that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and the other roots satisfy. Since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are roots of this equation, and they form a 3-cycle, then perhaps this polynomial factors into a product of lower-degree polynomials, one of which is a cubic with roots \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and others?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a quadratic factor corresponding to the fixed points, which we know are 3 and -2, so \\( (y - 3)(y + 2) = y^2 - y - 6 \\), which is the fixed point polynomial. Therefore, perhaps we can factor that out from the eighth-degree polynomial?\n\nLet me attempt polynomial division: divide \\( P(y) = y^8 - 24y^6 + 204y^4 - 720y^2 - y + 894 \\) by \\( D(y) = y^2 - y - 6 \\), and see if it divides evenly, which it should, since we know the fixed points are roots.\n\nLet me perform polynomial long division.\n\nDivide \\( P(y) \\) by \\( D(y) = y^2 - y - 6 \\).\n\nFirst, write both polynomials in descending powers, including all degrees even if coefficients are zero.\n\n\\( P(y) = y^8 + 0y^7 - 24y^6 + 0y^5 + 204y^4 + 0y^3 - 720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\n\\( D(y) = y^2 - y - 6 \\)\n\nWe need to divide \\( P(y) \\) by \\( D(y) \\).\n\nLet me set up the long division.\n\nDividend: \\( y^8 + 0y^7 -24y^6 + 0y^5 + 204y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nDivisor: \\( y^2 - y - 6 \\)\n\nWe start by dividing the leading term of the dividend \\( y^8 \\) by the leading term of the divisor \\( y^2 \\), which gives \\( y^{6} \\). So the first term of the quotient is \\( y^6 \\).\n\nMultiply the entire divisor by \\( y^6 \\):\n\n\\( y^6 \\cdot (y^2 - y - 6) = y^8 - y^7 - 6y^6 \\)\n\nSubtract this from the dividend:\n\n\\( (y^8 + 0y^7 -24y^6 + ... ) - (y^8 - y^7 -6y^6) \\)\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n- \\( y^8 - y^8 = 0 \\)\n- \\( 0y^7 - (-y^7) = + y^7 \\)\n- \\( -24y^6 - (-6y^6) = -24y^6 + 6y^6 = -18y^6 \\)\n- The remaining terms: \\( + 204y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nSo, the new polynomial after subtraction is:\n\n\\( y^7 - 18y^6 + 204y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nNext, divide the leading term \\( y^7 \\) by \\( y^2 \\), getting \\( y^5 \\). So, the next term in the quotient is \\( + y^5 \\).\n\nMultiply divisor by \\( y^5 \\):\n\n\\( y^5 \\cdot (y^2 - y -6) = y^7 - y^6 -6y^5 \\)\n\nSubtract this from the current dividend:\n\n\\( (y^7 - 18y^6 + 204y^4 + ... ) - (y^7 - y^6 -6y^5) \\)\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n- \\( y^7 - y^7 = 0 \\)\n- \\( -18y^6 - (-y^6) = -18y^6 + y^6 = -17y^6 \\)\n- \\( 0y^5 - (-6y^5) = +6y^5 \\)\n- The remaining terms: \\( +204y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nSo, the new polynomial is:\n\n\\( -17y^6 + 6y^5 + 204y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nNext, divide leading term \\( -17y^6 \\) by \\( y^2 \\), giving \\( -17y^4 \\). So, next term in quotient is \\( -17y^4 \\).\n\nMultiply divisor by \\( -17y^4 \\):\n\n\\( -17y^4 \\cdot (y^2 - y -6) = -17y^6 + 17y^5 + 102y^4 \\)\n\nSubtract this from the current dividend:\n\n\\( (-17y^6 + 6y^5 + 204y^4 + ...) - (-17y^6 + 17y^5 + 102y^4) \\)\n\nTerm by term:\n\n- \\( -17y^6 - (-17y^6) = 0 \\)\n- \\( 6y^5 - 17y^5 = -11y^5 \\)\n- \\( 204y^4 - 102y^4 = 102y^4 \\)\n- The remaining terms: \\( 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nSo now the polynomial is:\n\n\\( -11y^5 + 102y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nNext, divide leading term \\( -11y^5 \\) by \\( y^2 \\), getting \\( -11y^3 \\). Add this to the quotient.\n\nMultiply divisor by \\( -11y^3 \\):\n\n\\( -11y^3 \\cdot (y^2 - y -6) = -11y^5 + 11y^4 + 66y^3 \\)\n\nSubtract this from the current dividend:\n\n\\( (-11y^5 + 102y^4 + 0y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894) - (-11y^5 + 11y^4 + 66y^3) \\)\n\nTerm by term:\n\n- \\( -11y^5 - (-11y^5) = 0 \\)\n- \\( 102y^4 - 11y^4 = 91y^4 \\)\n- \\( 0y^3 - 66y^3 = -66y^3 \\)\n- \\( -720y^2 remains \\)\n- \\( -y remains \\)\n- \\( +894 remains \\)\n\nThus, the new polynomial is:\n\n\\( 91y^4 - 66y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nNext, divide leading term \\( 91y^4 \\) by \\( y^2 \\), getting \\( 91y^2 \\). Add to quotient.\n\nMultiply divisor by \\( 91y^2 \\):\n\n\\( 91y^2 \\cdot (y^2 - y -6) = 91y^4 - 91y^3 - 546y^2 \\)\n\nSubtract this from the current dividend:\n\n\\( (91y^4 - 66y^3 -720y^2 - y + 894) - (91y^4 - 91y^3 - 546y^2) \\)\n\nTerm by term:\n\n- \\( 91y^4 - 91y^4 = 0 \\)\n- \\( -66y^3 - (-91y^3) = 25y^3 \\)\n- \\( -720y^2 - (-546y^2) = -720y^2 + 546y^2 = -174y^2 \\)\n- \\( -y remains \\)\n- \\( +894 remains \\)\n\nSo now, the polynomial is:\n\n\\( 25y^3 - 174y^2 - y + 894 \\)\n\nNext, divide leading term \\( 25y^3 \\) by \\( y^2 \\), getting \\( 25y \\). Add to quotient.\n\nMultiply divisor by \\( 25y \\):\n\n\\( 25y \\cdot (y^2 - y -6) = 25y^3 - 25y^2 - 150y \\)\n\nSubtract this from the current dividend:\n\n\\( (25y^3 - 174y^2 - y + 894) - (25y^3 - 25y^2 - 150y) \\)\n\nTerm by term:\n\n- \\( 25y^3 -25y^3 = 0 \\)\n- \\( -174y^2 - (-25y^2) = -149y^2 \\)\n- \\( -y - (-150y) = 149y \\)\n- \\( +894 remains \\)\n\nThus, the polynomial becomes:\n\n\\( -149y^2 + 149y + 894 \\)\n\nNext, divide leading term \\( -149y^2 \\) by \\( y^2 \\), giving \\( -149 \\). Add this to the quotient.\n\nMultiply divisor by \\( -149 \\):\n\n\\( -149 \\cdot (y^2 - y -6) = -149y^2 + 149y + 894 \\)\n\nSubtract this from the current dividend:\n\n\\( (-149y^2 + 149y + 894) - (-149y^2 + 149y + 894) = 0 \\)\n\nPerfect, no remainder! So the division works out.\n\nTherefore, the quotient polynomial is:\n\n\\( y^6 + y^5 -17y^4 -11y^3 +91y^2 +25y -149 \\)\n\nWait, let me recall the terms of the quotient step by step.\n\nThe quotient terms we obtained, in order, were:\n\n1. \\( y^6 \\)\n2. \\( + y^5 \\)\n3. \\( -17y^4 \\)\n4. \\( -11y^3 \\)\n5. \\( +91y^2 \\)\n6. \\( +25y \\)\n7. \\( -149 \\)\n\nTherefore, combining these terms, the quotient polynomial is:\n\n\\( Q(y) = y^6 + y^5 -17y^4 -11y^3 +91y^2 +25y -149 \\)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n\\( P(y) = D(y) \\cdot Q(y) \\)\n\nwhere \\( D(y) = y^2 - y -6 \\), and \\( Q(y) \\) is the sixth-degree polynomial above.\n\nTherefore, the roots of \\( P(y) = 0 \\) are the roots of \\( D(y) = 0 \\) (i.e., \\( y = 3 \\), \\( y = -2 \\)), and the roots of \\( Q(y) = 0 \\).\n\nSince we are interested in the roots that form a 3-cycle (and are distinct from the fixed points), we can focus on the roots of \\( Q(y) = 0 \\).\n\nNow, if \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of \\( Q(y) = 0 \\) and form a 3-cycle, then they must be three distinct roots of \\( Q(y) \\), and the other three roots of \\( Q(y) \\) might form another 3-cycle or perhaps a 2-cycle or something else. However, since the problem states that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are pairwise distinct, but doesn't specify anything about the other roots, so we can assume that \\( Q(y) \\) factors into two cubics, each corresponding to a 3-cycle? Or maybe one cubic with the 3-cycle and another cubic with another 3-cycle, or perhaps a quadratic and a quartic?\n\nAlternatively, maybe \\( Q(y) \\) factors into a product of two cubics?\n\nLet me check if \\( Q(y) \\) can be factored into two cubics.\n\nSuppose \\( Q(y) = (y^3 + p y^2 + q y + r)(y^3 + s y^2 + t y + u) \\)\n\nMultiplying out:\n\n\\( y^6 + (p + s)y^5 + (q + t + ps)y^4 + (r + u + pt + qs)y^3 + (pu + qt + rs)y^2 + (qu + rt)y + ru \\)\n\nSet equal to \\( Q(y) = y^6 + y^5 -17y^4 -11y^3 +91y^2 +25y -149 \\)\n\nTherefore, equate coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of \\( y^6 \\): 1 = 1*1 ⇒ okay.\n\n2. Coefficient of \\( y^5 \\): \\( p + s = 1 \\)\n\n3. Coefficient of \\( y^4 \\): \\( q + t + p s = -17 \\)\n\n4. Coefficient of \\( y^3 \\): \\( r + u + p t + q s = -11 \\)\n\n5. Coefficient of \\( y^2 \\): \\( p u + q t + r s = 91 \\)\n\n6. Coefficient of \\( y \\): \\( q u + r t = 25 \\)\n\n7. Constant term: \\( r u = -149 \\)\n\nHmm, so we have a system of equations here with variables \\( p, q, r, s, t, u \\). This might be a bit involved, but perhaps we can guess some integer roots or find factors.\n\nFirst, let me note that the constant term is \\( r u = -149 \\). Since 149 is a prime number (I think; let me check: 149 divided by primes up to sqrt(149) ~12.2. 2, 3, 5, 7, 11. 149/2 no, 149/3 no, 149/5 no, 149/7 ~21.28, not integer, 149/11 ~13.54. So yes, 149 is prime. Therefore, possible integer pairs for \\( (r, u) \\) are (1, -149), (-1, 149), (149, -1), (-149, 1). Since we are dealing with integers? Maybe.\n\nLet me try \\( r = 149 \\), \\( u = -1 \\). Then, \\( r u = 149*(-1) = -149 \\). Let's see if that works.\n\nAlternatively, \\( r = -149 \\), \\( u = 1 \\). Let me try \\( r = -149 \\), \\( u = 1 \\).\n\nLet me try \\( r = -149 \\), \\( u = 1 \\).\n\nThen, moving to equation 7: done.\n\nEquation 6: \\( q u + r t = 25 \\). Plugging in \\( u = 1 \\), \\( r = -149 \\):\n\n\\( q(1) + (-149) t = 25 \\implies q - 149 t = 25 \\). Hmm, this may be difficult unless t is small.\n\nEquation 5: \\( p u + q t + r s = 91 \\). Plugging in \\( u = 1 \\), \\( r = -149 \\):\n\n\\( p(1) + q t + (-149)s = 91 \\implies p + q t -149 s = 91 \\)\n\nEquation 4: \\( r + u + p t + q s = -11 \\). Plugging in \\( r = -149 \\), \\( u =1 \\):\n\n\\( -149 + 1 + p t + q s = -11 \\implies p t + q s = -11 + 148 = 137 \\)\n\nEquation 3: \\( q + t + p s = -17 \\)\n\nEquation 2: \\( p + s = 1 \\implies s = 1 - p \\)\n\nEquation 1: already used.\n\nThis seems quite involved, but let me see if I can make some assumptions.\n\nSuppose that \\( p \\) and \\( s \\) are integers. Since \\( s = 1 - p \\), let me let \\( p \\) be an integer, then \\( s = 1 - p \\).\n\nLet me try small integer values for \\( p \\), maybe.\n\nLet me also consider equation 6: \\( q - 149 t = 25 \\). Let me suppose that \\( t \\) is an integer, then \\( q = 25 + 149 t \\). So, \\( q \\) is expressed in terms of \\( t \\).\n\nThen, equation 3: \\( q + t + p s = -17 \\). Substitute \\( q = 25 + 149 t \\), and \\( s = 1 - p \\):\n\n\\( (25 + 149 t) + t + p (1 - p) = -17 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( 25 + 150 t + p - p^2 = -17 \\)\n\nRearranged:\n\n\\( -p^2 + p + 150 t + 25 + 17 = 0 \\implies -p^2 + p + 150 t + 42 = 0 \\implies p^2 - p - 150 t - 42 = 0 \\)\n\nHmm, so \\( p^2 - p - 42 = 150 t \\). Since the left side must be divisible by 150, and t is an integer. Since 150 factors into 2*3*5^2, so 150 t must be an integer. Let me see possible t such that \\( p^2 - p -42 \\) is divisible by 150.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps trying small integer values for t.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this is getting too convoluted. Maybe instead of assuming integer coefficients, perhaps the cubic factors have non-integer coefficients? But that complicates things further.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a better approach.\n\nWait, going back to the original problem: we need only the sum \\( a + b + c \\). If \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of a cubic equation, then by Vieta's formula, the sum \\( S = a + b + c \\) is equal to the negative of the coefficient of \\( y^2 \\) divided by the coefficient of \\( y^3 \\). So, if we can find a cubic equation that has \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) as roots, then we can get \\( S \\) directly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can construct such a cubic equation by using the original relations.\n\nLet me consider that each of \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) satisfy the equation \\( x = f(f(f(x))) \\), but not the fixed point equation. Since they are part of a 3-cycle, then perhaps applying \\( f \\) three times brings you back to the original variable, but applying it once or twice does not. Therefore, each of them satisfies \\( f(f(f(x))) - x = 0 \\), but \\( f(x) - x \\neq 0 \\), \\( f(f(x)) - x \\neq 0 \\).\n\nTherefore, perhaps the minimal polynomial for \\( a \\) (and similarly for \\( b \\), \\( c \\)) is a factor of \\( P(x) = f(f(f(x))) - x \\), but not a factor of \\( f(x) - x \\) or \\( f(f(x)) - x \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal polynomial for \\( a \\) is a cubic, which would divide \\( P(x) \\), but not divide \\( f(x) - x \\) or \\( f(f(x)) - x \\). If so, then that cubic would have roots \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and thus we could use Vieta on that cubic to find \\( S \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of dealing with the eighth-degree equation, let me consider that since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) satisfy the cyclic relations, we can write a system of equations and try to find \\( S = a + b + c \\).\n\nLet me recall that we had earlier:\n\nFrom the original equations:\n\n1. \\( a^2 = b + 6 \\)\n2. \\( b^2 = c + 6 \\)\n3. \\( c^2 = a + 6 \\)\n\nLet me try to express everything in terms of \\( S \\).\n\nFirst, let me note that:\n\n\\( a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S + 18 \\), as we had before.\n\nAlso, we can compute \\( a^3 \\), \\( b^3 \\), \\( c^3 \\) in terms of other variables?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps multiplying each equation by the next variable.\n\nFor instance, multiply Equation 1 by \\( a \\):\n\n\\( a^3 = a b + 6a \\)\n\nSimilarly, multiply Equation 2 by \\( b \\):\n\n\\( b^3 = b c + 6b \\)\n\nMultiply Equation 3 by \\( c \\):\n\n\\( c^3 = c a + 6c \\)\n\nSo now, we have:\n\n1. \\( a^3 = a b + 6a \\) — (1a)\n2. \\( b^3 = b c + 6b \\) — (2a)\n3. \\( c^3 = c a + 6c \\) — (3a)\n\nNow, let me sum these three equations:\n\n\\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = a b + b c + c a + 6(a + b + c) \\)\n\nWe know that \\( a + b + c = S \\), so:\n\n\\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = (ab + bc + ca) + 6S \\)\n\nNow, recall that \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 \\) can be expressed in terms of \\( S \\), \\( ab + bc + ca \\), and \\( abc \\). The identity is:\n\n\\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = (a + b + c)^3 - 3(a + b + c)(ab + bc + ca) + 3abc \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( (S)^3 - 3 S (ab + bc + ca) + 3 abc = (ab + bc + ca) + 6 S \\)\n\nLet me rearrange this equation:\n\n\\( S^3 - 3 S (ab + bc + ca) + 3 abc - (ab + bc + ca) - 6 S = 0 \\)\n\nFactor terms with \\( ab + bc + ca \\):\n\n\\( S^3 - 6 S - [3 S (ab + bc + ca) + (ab + bc + ca)] + 3 abc = 0 \\)\n\nFactor \\( ab + bc + ca \\):\n\n\\( S^3 - 6 S - (ab + bc + ca)(3 S + 1) + 3 abc = 0 \\)\n\nHmm, okay. Let me denote \\( ab + bc + ca = M \\), and \\( abc = N \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\n\\( S^3 - 6 S - M(3 S + 1) + 3 N = 0 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( S^3 - 6 S - 3 S M - M + 3 N = 0 \\)\n\nEquation (5): \\( S^3 - 6 S - 3 S M - M + 3 N = 0 \\)\n\nNow, if I can find expressions for \\( M \\) and \\( N \\) in terms of \\( S \\), then I can substitute into this equation and solve for \\( S \\).\n\nWe already have from earlier:\n\nFrom Equation 4: \\( M = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\)\n\nSo, \\( M = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\)\n\nWe need an expression for \\( N = abc \\).\n\nHmm, how can I find \\( abc \\)?\n\nLet me see if I can find \\( abc \\) in terms of \\( S \\) or other known terms.\n\nLet me consider multiplying all three original equations together:\n\n\\( a^2 \\cdot b^2 \\cdot c^2 = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) \\)\n\nLeft-hand side: \\( (abc)^2 = N^2 \\)\n\nRight-hand side: Let me expand \\( (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) \\)\n\nFirst, expand two terms:\n\n\\( (b + 6)(c + 6) = bc + 6b + 6c + 36 \\)\n\nThen multiply by \\( (a + 6) \\):\n\n\\( [bc + 6b + 6c + 36][a + 6] \\)\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\n= \\( bc \\cdot a + bc \\cdot 6 + 6b \\cdot a + 6b \\cdot 6 + 6c \\cdot a + 6c \\cdot 6 + 36 \\cdot a + 36 \\cdot 6 \\)\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n= \\( abc + 6 bc + 6ab + 36b + 6ac + 36c + 36a + 216 \\)\n\nTherefore, the entire right-hand side is:\n\n\\( abc + 6 bc + 6ab + 36b + 6ac + 36c + 36a + 216 \\)\n\nTherefore, setting equal to left-hand side:\n\n\\( N^2 = abc + 6(bc + ab + ac) + 36(a + b + c) + 216 \\)\n\nNote that \\( bc + ab + ac = M \\), and \\( a + b + c = S \\). Therefore,\n\n\\( N^2 = N + 6M + 36S + 216 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( N^2 - N - 6M - 36S - 216 = 0 \\) — (Equation 6)\n\nNow, we have expressions for \\( M \\) in terms of \\( S \\), so let's substitute that into Equation 6.\n\nRecall \\( M = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\), so:\n\n\\( N^2 - N - 6 \\left( \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\right) - 36 S - 216 = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\nFirst, compute \\( -6 \\times \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} = -3(S^2 - S - 18) = -3S^2 + 3S + 54 \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting back:\n\n\\( N^2 - N - 3S^2 + 3S + 54 - 36S - 216 = 0 \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n- Terms with \\( S^2 \\): \\( -3S^2 \\)\n- Terms with \\( S \\): \\( 3S - 36S = -33S \\)\n- Constants: \\( 54 - 216 = -162 \\)\n- The rest: \\( N^2 - N \\)\n\nSo, equation becomes:\n\n\\( N^2 - N - 3S^2 - 33S - 162 = 0 \\)\n\nLet me write this as:\n\n\\( N^2 - N = 3S^2 + 33S + 162 \\) — (Equation 7)\n\nNow, going back to Equation 5:\n\n\\( S^3 - 6 S - 3 S M - M + 3 N = 0 \\)\n\nWe can substitute \\( M = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\) into this equation.\n\nFirst, compute \\( 3 S M \\):\n\n\\( 3 S \\cdot \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} = \\frac{3S^3 - 3S^2 - 54S}{2} \\)\n\nThen, compute \\( - M \\):\n\n\\( - \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting into Equation 5:\n\n\\( S^3 - 6 S - \\frac{3S^3 - 3S^2 - 54S}{2} - \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} + 3 N = 0 \\)\n\nTo combine the terms, let me get a common denominator of 2:\n\nMultiply the entire equation by 2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( 2S^3 - 12 S - (3S^3 - 3S^2 - 54S) - (S^2 - S - 18) + 6N = 0 \\)\n\nNow, expand each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( 2S^3 - 12 S \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( -3S^3 + 3S^2 + 54S \\)\n\nThird term: \\( -S^2 + S + 18 \\)\n\nFourth term: \\( +6N \\)\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n- \\( 2S^3 - 3S^3 = -S^3 \\)\n- \\( 3S^2 - S^2 = 2S^2 \\)\n- \\( -12S + 54S + S = 43S \\)\n- \\( +18 \\)\n- \\( +6N \\)\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n\\( -S^3 + 2S^2 + 43S + 18 + 6N = 0 \\)\n\nLet me rearrange:\n\n\\( 6N = S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18 \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( N = \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6} \\) — (Equation 8)\n\nNow, we have expressions for \\( N \\) (Equation 8) and \\( M \\) (Equation 4) in terms of \\( S \\). Let me recall Equation 7:\n\n\\( N^2 - N = 3S^2 + 33S + 162 \\)\n\nSo, substituting \\( N \\) from Equation 8 into this equation will give an equation solely in terms of \\( S \\). This will be a high-degree equation, but maybe it simplifies.\n\nLet me denote \\( N = \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( N^2 = \\left( \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6} \\right)^2 \\)\n\nSo, substituting into Equation 7:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6} \\right)^2 - \\left( \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6} \\right) = 3S^2 + 33S + 162 \\)\n\nThis looks very complicated, but let me try to compute step by step.\n\nLet me denote \\( N = \\frac{P}{6} \\), where \\( P = S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18 \\). Then Equation 7 becomes:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{P}{6} \\right)^2 - \\frac{P}{6} = 3S^2 + 33S + 162 \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 36 to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( P^2 - 6P = 36(3S^2 + 33S + 162) \\)\n\nCompute RHS: \\( 36 \\times 3 S^2 = 108 S^2 \\), \\( 36 \\times 33 S = 1188 S \\), \\( 36 \\times 162 = 5832 \\). Therefore,\n\n\\( P^2 - 6P = 108 S^2 + 1188 S + 5832 \\)\n\nNow, substitute back \\( P = S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18 \\):\n\nFirst compute \\( P^2 \\):\n\n\\( P^2 = (S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18)^2 \\)\n\nThis will be a degree 6 polynomial in \\( S \\).\n\nThen, \\( -6P = -6(S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18) \\)\n\nThus, the entire left-hand side (LHS):\n\n\\( (S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18)^2 - 6(S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18) \\)\n\nAnd the right-hand side (RHS) is \\( 108 S^2 + 1188 S + 5832 \\)\n\nTherefore, bringing everything to the left-hand side:\n\n\\( (S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18)^2 - 6(S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18) - 108 S^2 - 1188 S - 5832 = 0 \\)\n\nThis is going to be a very high-degree equation, but perhaps it simplifies nicely?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a better way. Alternatively, maybe we can guess possible values of \\( S \\).\n\nGiven that the problem states there are two possible values for \\( a + b + c \\), so likely the equation for \\( S \\) is quadratic or quartic with two real solutions? Maybe.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the equation for \\( S \\) factors into a quadratic and another polynomial, and we can solve the quadratic.\n\nAlternatively, let me try plugging in possible integer values for \\( S \\).\n\nLet me see if I can guess possible values for \\( S \\).\n\nLet me consider that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are complex numbers, but perhaps the sum \\( S \\) is a real number? Not necessarily, but maybe the problem is expecting real solutions? Wait, the problem says \"complex numbers\", so \\( S \\) could be complex, but the question says \"compute the two possible values of \\( a + b + c \\)\", and asks for the answer in a comma-separated list of two valid LaTeX expressions. Since they are complex numbers, but perhaps they are conjugates or something, leading to two real values? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe the two values are real.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the possible values of \\( S \\) are roots of a quadratic equation, so let me see if I can find such an equation.\n\nLet me see if I can find \\( S \\) by assuming that \\( S \\) satisfies a quadratic equation. Let me suppose that \\( S \\) is a solution to \\( S^2 + p S + q = 0 \\), but without knowing p and q, this is vague.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that perhaps the sum \\( S \\) is related to the fixed points.\n\nWait, the fixed points are 3 and -2, but those are excluded. But perhaps the sum \\( S \\) could be related to those?\n\nAlternatively, maybe if I consider that the equations are symmetric, perhaps the sum \\( S \\) must satisfy a quadratic equation.\n\nAlternatively, let me try substituting \\( S = 3 \\). Wait, but 3 is a fixed point, so if \\( S = 3 \\), then all variables would be 3, but they have to be distinct, so that's invalid. Similarly, \\( S = -2 \\) would give all variables as -2, which is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \\( S = 0 \\)? Let me test \\( S = 0 \\).\n\nIf \\( S = 0 \\), then \\( a + b + c = 0 \\).\n\nLet me see if that's possible.\n\nSuppose \\( a + b + c = 0 \\).\n\nFrom Equation 1: \\( a^2 = b + 6 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 2: \\( b^2 = c + 6 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 3: \\( c^2 = a + 6 \\)\n\nIf \\( a + b + c = 0 \\), then \\( c = -a - b \\). Substitute into Equation 2:\n\n\\( b^2 = c + 6 = (-a - b) + 6 \\implies b^2 + a + b - 6 = 0 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 1: \\( a^2 = b + 6 \\implies b = a^2 - 6 \\). Substitute this into the above equation:\n\n\\( (a^2 - 6)^2 + a + (a^2 - 6) - 6 = 0 \\)\n\nExpand \\( (a^2 - 6)^2 \\):\n\n\\( a^4 - 12a^2 + 36 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( a^4 - 12a^2 + 36 + a + a^2 - 6 - 6 = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 = 0 \\)\n\nWait, this is exactly the same equation we had earlier for \\( S \\) when we expressed \\( S = a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 \\), but here we have \\( a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 = 0 \\). Wait, but if \\( S = 0 \\), then according to the expression we had earlier, \\( S = a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 \\), so setting \\( S = 0 \\implies a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 = 0 \\). But this equation is exactly the same as the one we obtained when we expressed \\( S \\) in terms of \\( a \\), but here we're assuming \\( S = 0 \\). Therefore, if there exists a solution where \\( a + b + c = 0 \\), then \\( a \\) must satisfy this quartic equation. However, this quartic might factor?\n\nLet me try to factor \\( a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 \\).\n\nLet me attempt rational roots. The possible rational roots are factors of 24 over factors of 1, so ±1, ±2, ±3, ±4, ±6, ±8, ±12, ±24.\n\nTesting \\( a = 1 \\):\n\n\\( 1 - 11 + 1 + 24 = 15 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = -1 \\):\n\n\\( 1 - 11 - 1 + 24 = 13 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = 2 \\):\n\n\\( 16 - 44 + 2 + 24 = (16 + 2 + 24) - 44 = 42 - 44 = -2 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = -2 \\):\n\n\\( 16 - 44 - 2 + 24 = (16 - 2) + (-44 + 24) = 14 - 20 = -6 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = 3 \\):\n\n\\( 81 - 99 + 3 + 24 = (81 + 3 + 24) - 99 = 108 - 99 = 9 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = -3 \\):\n\n\\( 81 - 99 - 3 + 24 = (81 + 24) - (99 + 3) = 105 - 102 = 3 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = 4 \\):\n\n\\( 256 - 176 + 4 + 24 = 256 - 176 = 80; 80 + 4 + 24 = 108 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = -4 \\):\n\n\\( 256 - 176 -4 + 24 = 256 - 176 = 80; 80 -4 +24 = 100 ≠ 0 \\)\n\n\\( a = 6 \\):\n\nToo big, likely not zero.\n\nHmm, so no rational roots. Maybe it factors into quadratics?\n\nAssume \\( a^4 - 11a^2 + a + 24 = (a^2 + p a + q)(a^2 + r a + s) \\)\n\nMultiply out:\n\n\\( a^4 + (p + r)a^3 + (q + s + p r)a^2 + (p s + q r)a + q s \\)\n\nSet equal to \\( a^4 -11a^2 + a +24 \\)\n\nTherefore, equate coefficients:\n\n1. \\( p + r = 0 \\) (coefficient of \\( a^3 \\))\n\n2. \\( q + s + p r = -11 \\) (coefficient of \\( a^2 \\))\n\n3. \\( p s + q r = 1 \\) (coefficient of \\( a \\))\n\n4. \\( q s = 24 \\) (constant term)\n\nFrom equation 1: \\( r = -p \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( r = -p \\) into other equations.\n\nEquation 2: \\( q + s + p (-p) = q + s - p^2 = -11 \\)\n\nEquation 3: \\( p s + q (-p) = p(s - q) = 1 \\)\n\nEquation 4: \\( q s = 24 \\)\n\nLet me let \\( q \\) and \\( s \\) be integers such that their product is 24. Let me list the possible pairs (q, s):\n\n(1,24), (2,12), (3,8), (4,6), (-1,-24), (-2,-12), (-3,-8), (-4,-6), and the reverses.\n\nLet me try some pairs.\n\nFirst, let me try positive factors first.\n\nTry (q, s) = (3,8):\n\nThen equation 4 is satisfied: 3*8=24.\n\nThen equation 3: \\( p(s - q) = p(8 - 3) = 5p = 1 \\implies p = 1/5 \\), not integer, so discard.\n\nNext, (q,s) = (4,6):\n\nThen equation 4: 4*6=24.\n\nEquation 3: \\( p(6 - 4) = 2p =1 \\implies p=1/2 \\), not integer.\n\nNext, (q,s)=(2,12):\n\nEquation 4: 2*12=24.\n\nEquation 3: \\( p(12 - 2)=10p =1 \\implies p=1/10 \\), nope.\n\n(q,s)=(1,24):\n\nEquation 3: p(24 -1)=23p=1 ⇒ p=1/23, nope.\n\nNow try negative pairs:\n\n(q,s)=(-3,-8):\n\nEquation 4: (-3)(-8)=24, good.\n\nEquation 3: \\( p(-8 - (-3)) = p(-5) =1 ⇒ p = -1/5 \\), not integer.\n\n(q,s)=(-4,-6):\n\nEquation 4: (-4)(-6)=24.\n\nEquation 3: \\( p(-6 - (-4)) = p(-2) =1 ⇒ p = -1/2 \\), nope.\n\n(q,s)=(-2,-12):\n\nEquation 4: (-2)(-12)=24.\n\nEquation 3: \\( p(-12 - (-2)) = p(-10) =1 ⇒ p = -1/10 \\), nope.\n\n(q,s)=(-1,-24):\n\nEquation 4: (-1)(-24)=24.\n\nEquation 3: \\( p(-24 - (-1)) = p(-23) =1 ⇒ p = -1/23 \\), nope.\n\nHmm, none of the integer pairs for (q,s) give integer p. Maybe non-integer coefficients? Then this approach might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this quartic is irreducible, so \\( S = 0 \\) is not a solution? Or maybe \\( S = 0 \\) is a solution but the quartic doesn't factor, so we need another approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \\( S = 3 \\) or \\( S = -2 \\), but those are excluded.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the possible values of \\( S \\) are 3 and -2, but since those lead to all variables equal, which is invalid, so maybe other values?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sum \\( S \\) satisfies a quadratic equation, so let me assume that and try to find it.\n\nLet me recall that we had expressions for \\( N \\) and \\( M \\) in terms of \\( S \\), so perhaps substituting into Equation 7 and Equation 8 can lead to an equation in \\( S \\).\n\nAlternatively, going back to Equation 8: \\( N = \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6} \\)\n\nAnd Equation 7: \\( N^2 - N = 3S^2 + 33S + 162 \\)\n\nSo substituting \\( N \\) from Equation 8 into Equation 7 gives an equation in \\( S \\). Let me denote \\( N = \\frac{P}{6} \\), where \\( P = S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18 \\).\n\nThen Equation 7 becomes:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{P}{6} \\right)^2 - \\frac{P}{6} = 3S^2 + 33S + 162 \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 36:\n\n\\( P^2 - 6P = 108 S^2 + 1188 S + 5832 \\)\n\nBut \\( P = S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18 \\), so substitute that in:\n\n\\( (S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18)^2 - 6(S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18) - 108 S^2 - 1188 S - 5832 = 0 \\)\n\nThis is going to be a degree 6 equation, but let me try expanding it step by step.\n\nFirst, compute \\( (S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18)^2 \\):\n\nLet me denote \\( A = S^3 \\), \\( B = -2S^2 \\), \\( C = -43S \\), \\( D = -18 \\)\n\nThen, \\( (A + B + C + D)^2 = A^2 + B^2 + C^2 + D^2 + 2AB + 2AC + 2AD + 2BC + 2BD + 2CD \\)\n\nCompute each term:\n\n- \\( A^2 = S^6 \\)\n- \\( B^2 = 4S^4 \\)\n- \\( C^2 = 1849 S^2 \\) (since (-43)^2 = 1849)\n- \\( D^2 = 324 \\)\n- \\( 2AB = 2(S^3)(-2S^2) = -4 S^5 \\)\n- \\( 2AC = 2(S^3)(-43S) = -86 S^4 \\)\n- \\( 2AD = 2(S^3)(-18) = -36 S^3 \\)\n- \\( 2BC = 2(-2S^2)(-43S) = 172 S^3 \\)\n- \\( 2BD = 2(-2S^2)(-18) = 72 S^2 \\)\n- \\( 2CD = 2(-43S)(-18) = 1548 S \\)\n\nNow, combine all terms:\n\n- \\( S^6 \\)\n- \\( -4 S^5 \\)\n- \\( 4S^4 - 86 S^4 = -82 S^4 \\)\n- \\( -36 S^3 + 172 S^3 = 136 S^3 \\)\n- \\( 1849 S^2 + 72 S^2 = 1921 S^2 \\)\n- \\( 1548 S \\)\n- \\( 324 \\)\n\nTherefore, the square term is:\n\n\\( S^6 - 4 S^5 - 82 S^4 + 136 S^3 + 1921 S^2 + 1548 S + 324 \\)\n\nNow, subtract \\( 6P \\), where \\( P = S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18 \\):\n\nCompute \\( 6P = 6S^3 - 12 S^2 - 258 S - 108 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( -6P = -6S^3 + 12 S^2 + 258 S + 108 \\)\n\nNow, subtract \\( 108 S^2 + 1188 S + 5832 \\):\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\nThe entire left-hand side expression is:\n\n[ \\( S^6 - 4 S^5 - 82 S^4 + 136 S^3 + 1921 S^2 + 1548 S + 324 \\) ]\n\nplus\n\n[ \\( -6S^3 + 12 S^2 + 258 S + 108 \\) ]\n\nminus\n\n[ \\( 108 S^2 + 1188 S + 5832 \\) ]\n\nTherefore, term by term:\n\n1. \\( S^6 \\)\n\n2. \\( -4 S^5 \\)\n\n3. \\( -82 S^4 \\)\n\n4. \\( 136 S^3 - 6 S^3 = 130 S^3 \\)\n\n5. \\( 1921 S^2 + 12 S^2 - 108 S^2 = (1921 + 12 - 108) S^2 = 1825 S^2 \\)\n\n6. \\( 1548 S + 258 S - 1188 S = (1548 + 258 - 1188) S = (1806 - 1188) S = 618 S \\)\n\n7. \\( 324 + 108 - 5832 = (324 + 108) - 5832 = 432 - 5832 = -5400 \\)\n\nSo, the entire expression simplifies to:\n\n\\( S^6 - 4 S^5 - 82 S^4 + 130 S^3 + 1825 S^2 + 618 S - 5400 = 0 \\)\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\n\\( S^6 - 4 S^5 - 82 S^4 + 130 S^3 + 1825 S^2 + 618 S - 5400 = 0 \\)\n\nWow, that's a sixth-degree equation. This seems really complicated. Maybe there is a factor here?\n\nLet me try to factor this equation. Let me see if there are any rational roots. By the Rational Root Theorem, possible rational roots are factors of 5400 divided by factors of 1, so ±1, ±2, ±3, ..., ±5400. That's a lot, but maybe some small integers.\n\nLet me try S = 5:\n\nCompute \\( 5^6 -4*5^5 -82*5^4 +130*5^3 +1825*5^2 +618*5 -5400 \\)\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\n5^6 = 15625\n\n4*5^5 = 4*3125 = 12500\n\n82*5^4 = 82*625 = 51250\n\n130*5^3 = 130*125 = 16250\n\n1825*5^2 = 1825*25 = 45625\n\n618*5 = 3090\n\nSo,\n\n15625 - 12500 - 51250 + 16250 + 45625 + 3090 - 5400\n\nCompute term by term:\n\nStart with 15625 - 12500 = 3125\n\n3125 - 51250 = -48125\n\n-48125 + 16250 = -31875\n\n-31875 + 45625 = 13750\n\n13750 + 3090 = 16840\n\n16840 - 5400 = 11440 ≠ 0\n\nNot a root.\n\nTry S = 3:\n\n3^6 = 729\n\n4*3^5 = 4*243 = 972\n\n82*3^4 = 82*81 = 6642\n\n130*3^3 = 130*27 = 3510\n\n1825*3^2 = 1825*9 = 16425\n\n618*3 = 1854\n\nSo,\n\n729 - 972 - 6642 + 3510 + 16425 + 1854 - 5400\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n729 - 972 = -243\n\n-243 - 6642 = -6885\n\n-6885 + 3510 = -3375\n\n-3375 + 16425 = 13050\n\n13050 + 1854 = 14904\n\n14904 - 5400 = 9504 ≠ 0\n\nNot a root.\n\nTry S = -3:\n\n(-3)^6 = 729\n\n-4*(-3)^5 = -4*(-243) = +9