uation B)\n\n3. \\( (a - c)(a + c) = b - a \\) — (Equation C)\n\nPerhaps we can manipulate these equations to find a relationship involving \\( S \\).\n\nLet me see if I can express \\( b - c \\), \\( c - a \\), and \\( b - a \\) in terms of other variables.\n\nFrom Equation A: \\( (a - b)(a + b) = b - c \\)\n\nLet me solve for \\( b - c \\):\n\n\\( b - c = (a - b)(a + b) \\)\n\nSimilarly, from Equation B: \\( (b - c)(b + c) = c - a \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( b - c \\) from above into this:\n\n\\( [ (a - b)(a + b) ] (b + c) = c - a \\)\n\nSimilarly, from Equation C: \\( (a - c)(a + c) = b - a \\)\n\nLet me note that \\( b - a = - (a - b) \\), so:\n\n\\( (a - c)(a + c) = - (a - b) \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( d = a - b \\), \\( e = b - c \\), \\( f = c - a \\). Note that \\( d + e + f = 0 \\).\n\nBut perhaps this substitution complicates things further.\n\nAlternatively, let me express everything in terms of \\( S \\).\n\nLet me recall that \\( S = a + b + c \\). Let me also denote \\( P = ab + bc + ca \\), and \\( Q = abc \\), which we've used before.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the differences:\n\nFrom Equation A: \\( (a - b)(a + b) = b - c \\)\n\nExpress \\( b - c = -(c - b) \\), so:\n\n\\( (a - b)(a + b) = - (c - b) \\)\n\nSimilarly, from Equation B: \\( (b - c)(b + c) = c - a \\)\n\nExpress \\( c - a = -(a - c) \\), so:\n\n\\( (b - c)(b + c) = - (a - c) \\)\n\nFrom Equation C: \\( (a - c)(a + c) = b - a \\)\n\nWhich is \\( (a - c)(a + c) = - (a - b) \\)\n\nHmm, not sure yet.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to divide Equation A by Equation B.\n\nTake Equation A: \\( (a - b)(a + b) = b - c \\)\n\nEquation B: \\( (b - c)(b + c) = c - a \\)\n\nDivide A by B:\n\n\\( \\frac{(a - b)(a + b)}{(b - c)(b + c)} = \\frac{b - c}{c - a} \\)\n\nSimplify RHS: \\( \\frac{b - c}{c - a} = \\frac{ -(c - b) }{ c - a } \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{(a - b)(a + b)}{(b - c)(b + c)} = \\frac{ - (c - b) }{ c - a } \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( (b - c)(b + c)(c - a) \\):\n\nLeft side: \\( 