clude those.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all these S values are possible except for some? Let's see.\n\nLet me first consider S = -6.\n\nSuppose S = -6.\n\nLet me see if there exists a solution with a, b, c distinct.\n\nLet me attempt to find such a solution.\n\nLet me denote S = a + b + c = -6,\n\nand from Equation 4: a² + b² + c² = S + 18 = -6 + 18 = 12.\n\nAlso, from the original equations:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nLet me see if we can find such a, b, c.\n\nLet me suppose that a, b, c satisfy these equations and sum to -6.\n\nLet me try to solve this system.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nFrom the first equation: b = a² - 6,\n\nSecond equation: c = b² - 6 = (a² - 6)^2 - 6,\n\nThird equation: a = c² - 6 = [ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ]^2 - 6.\n\nSo, as before, this gives the equation:\n\na = [ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ]^2 - 6,\n\nwhich is the eighth-degree equation we had before. So, solving this equation for a when S = -6 would require that a is a root of that equation, and that the corresponding b and c computed from a are distinct from a and each other.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a solution with S = -6 where a, b, c are distinct?\n\nAlternatively, maybe S = -6 corresponds to a case where a, b, c form a cycle of period 3, hence distinct?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider that since we obtained these S values through legitimate algebraic manipulations, and the problem allows complex numbers (so even if for some S values the variables are not distinct, but since the problem specifies that a, b, c are pairwise distinct, then those S values must correspond to solutions where they are distinct, so perhaps all S values except those that force a = b = c are valid? But in this case, the equations a² = b + 6 etc., prevent a = b = c because if a = b = c, then a² = a + 6 ⇒ a² - a -6 =0 ⇒ a = [1 ± sqrt(1 +24)]/2 = [1 ±5]/2 ⇒ a = 3 or a = -2. Then, if a = b = c = 3, then a² = 9, but b +6 = 3 +6 =9, so that works, but they are not distinct. Similarly for a = b = c = -2: (-2)^2 = 4, and b +6 = -2 +6=4, so that also works, but again, not distinct. So S = 3*3 =9, and S = 3*(-2) = -6.\n\nAh! Wait a second. If a = b = c = 3, then S = 9,\n\nand if a = b = c = -2, then S = -6.\n\nTherefore, the S = 9 and S = -6 correspond to the cases where all three variables are equal, which are invalid because they must be pairwise distinct. Therefore, these solutions (S =9 and S =-6) must be excluded.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solutions are the other roots: S = [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] / 2 and S = 1 ± sqrt(26).\n\nBut the problem states \"Compute the two possible values of a + b + c\". Hmm, so maybe there are only two possible values? But we have four roots here: two from the quadratics.\n\nWait, but the problem might be that even though the equations for S give four roots, some of them might correspond to solutions where a, b, c are not all distinct, but we have to check.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when S = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2 and S = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2, these correspond to valid solutions with distinct a, b, c,\n\nand similarly S = 1 + sqrt(26) and S = 1 - sqrt(26) also correspond to solutions with distinct a, b, c,\n\nso that there are four possible values of S? But the problem says \"compute the two possible values\".\n\nHmm, so perhaps I made a mistake here. Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so perhaps even though there are four roots for S, some of them are actually the same or perhaps some of the S values lead to contradictions?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the quartic equation's roots are actually complex? Wait no, sqrt(17) and sqrt(26) are real numbers, so all roots are real. So all four roots are real numbers.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"complex numbers\", which includes real numbers, so they are acceptable. But the problem asks for \"the two possible values of a + b + c\". Hmm, so perhaps there are only two possible values? That suggests that maybe two of the roots are extraneous, introduced during the algebraic manipulations?\n\nAlternatively, maybe each quadratic in S corresponds to two solutions for S, but they are actually the same in some way?\n\nWait, let me check the quartic factors:\n\nThe quartic factored into (S^2 + S -4)(S^2 - 2 S -25). The roots of the first quadratic are [ -1 ± sqrt(1 + 16) ] /2 = [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] /2,\n\nand the roots of the second quadratic are [ 2 ± sqrt(4 + 100) ] /2 = [2 ± sqrt(104)] /2 = [2 ± 2*sqrt(26)] /2 = 1 ± sqrt(26).\n\nTherefore, four roots: two from each quadratic.\n\nSo that's four roots. But the problem states \"two possible values of a + b + c\".\n\nHmm. Perhaps some of these roots correspond to the same triplet (a,b,c) in a different order? Since the equations are cyclic, permuting a, b, c would give the same S but different assignments.\n\nAlternatively, maybe for each S from the quartic, there are two distinct triplets (a,b,c) and (c,b,a), but the sum S is the same. Therefore, each S value from the quartic corresponds to a unique S, so there are four possible S values? But the problem says two possible values. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when we considered S = -6 and S = 9, those were the only real ones, but the problem allows complex numbers, so maybe the other roots are complex?\n\nWait, no. All roots are real. Wait sqrt(17) and sqrt(26) are real, so all roots are real. Therefore, all four roots are real numbers. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is from a competition where only two of them are valid? Or maybe I made an error in the factoring?\n\nWait let me verify the quartic factorization again.\n\nWe had the quartic S^4 - S^3 -31 S^2 -17 S + 100,\n\nand we factored it as (S^2 + S -4)(S^2 - 2 S -25).\n\nLet me multiply those factors again to confirm:\n\nFirst, (S^2 + S -4)(S^2 - 2 S -25):\n\nMultiply S^2 by each term in the second quadratic: S^2 * S^2 = S^4, S^2*(-2S) = -2 S^3, S^2*(-25) = -25 S^2,\n\nThen S times each term: S * S^2 = S^3, S*(-2 S) = -2 S^2, S*(-25) = -25 S,\n\nThen -4 times each term: -4*S^2 = -4 S^2, -4*(-2 S) = +8 S, -4*(-25) = +100.\n\nNow, collect like terms:\n\nS^4,\n\nS^3 terms: -2 S^3 + S^3 = -S^3,\n\nS^2 terms: -25 S^2 -2 S^2 -4 S^2 = (-25 -2 -4) S^2 = -31 S^2,\n\nS terms: -25 S +8 S = -17 S,\n\nConstants: +100.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the factorization is correct.\n\nTherefore, the quartic indeed has four real roots. Thus, in total, the sixth-degree equation has six real roots: S = -6, 9, and the four from the quartic.\n\nBut since S = -6 and S =9 correspond to the cases where a = b = c, which are invalid, then we discard those two, leaving four possible values for S: the four roots from the quartic. But the problem says \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c\".\n\nHmm, so this suggests that perhaps there is an error in my reasoning somewhere. Alternatively, maybe the problem is from a source where only two of the quartic roots are valid?\n\nWait, let me think differently. Perhaps when we set up the equations, we assumed that a, b, c are distinct, so maybe some of the quartic roots correspond to cases where two variables are equal even if they are not all three equal?\n\nFor instance, suppose S is such that two variables are equal but the third is different. Then that solution would be invalid, so we must exclude those S values as well.\n\nTherefore, we need to check whether for the quartic roots S = [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] / 2 and S = 1 ± sqrt(26), whether the corresponding a, b, c are all distinct.\n\nLet me first consider S = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2,\n\nLet me denote this as S1 = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2,\n\nSimilarly S2 = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2,\n\nS3 = 1 + sqrt(26),\n\nS4 = 1 - sqrt(26).\n\nWe need to check for each of these S values whether the corresponding a, b, c are all distinct.\n\nLet me pick one of them, say S = S3 = 1 + sqrt(26),\n\nand try to see if the corresponding a, b, c are distinct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to note that since the equations are cyclic and symmetric in a certain way, if S is a solution, then so is another solution related by cycling the variables, but since we are summing them, the sum remains the same.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps each S from the quartic corresponds to two distinct triplets (a,b,c) and (c,b,a) or something, but the sum S is the same. So each S value from the quartic is valid, giving two possible values? Wait, no, there are four S values from the quartic.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting only two solutions because when considering the original equations, there's some symmetry or constraint I missed?\n\nWait, going back to the original problem: it says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so as long as a, b, c are distinct, regardless of being real or complex. Since all the roots we found for S are real, but a, b, c could still be complex? Wait no, if S is real and the equations are with real coefficients, then if a is complex, then its conjugate might also be a solution? Hmm, but the problem doesn't specify that a, b, c are real, just complex.\n\nWait a second! Wait, hold on. The equations are given with real coefficients? Let me check:\n\nThe equations are a² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nThese are equations over the complex numbers, so coefficients are real, but variables can be complex. However, the expressions for S = a + b + c could still be real or complex. But in our analysis, we derived that S must satisfy a polynomial equation with real coefficients, so complex roots would come in conjugate pairs. However, in our case, all roots of the polynomial are real, so S must be real.\n\nTherefore, even though a, b, c could be complex, their sum S is real. Interesting.\n\nTherefore, perhaps even if a, b, c are complex numbers, their sum is real.\n\nNow, returning to the problem: the question says \"Compute the two possible values of a + b + c\". Since we have four roots from the quartic, but the problem says two possible values, maybe I made a mistake in the factorization?\n\nWait, let me check the original sixth-degree polynomial:\n\nWe had the sixth-degree polynomial: S^6 -4 S^5 -82 S^4 + 130 S^3 + 1825 S^2 + 618 S -5400 = 0,\n\nand factored it as (S +6)(S -9)(S^2 + S -4)(S^2 -2 S -25).\n\nBut let me verify the factorization once more:\n\n(S +6)(S -9) = S² - 3 S -54,\n\nMultiply that by (S² + S -4):\n\nFirst, (S² -3 S -54)(S² + S -4).\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nMultiply S²*(S² + S -4) = S^4 + S^3 -4 S²,\n\nMultiply -3 S*(S² + S -4) = -3 S^3 -3 S² +12 S,\n\nMultiply -54*(S² + S -4) = -54 S² -54 S +216.\n\nAdd them all together:\n\nS^4 + S^3 -4 S² -3 S^3 -3 S² +12 S -54 S² -54 S +216.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nS^4,\n\nS^3 terms: 1 S^3 -3 S^3 = -2 S^3,\n\nS² terms: -4 S² -3 S² -54 S² = (-4 -3 -54) S² = -61 S²,\n\nS terms: 12 S -54 S = -42 S,\n\nConstants: +216.\n\nSo the product is S^4 -2 S^3 -61 S² -42 S +216.\n\nThen multiply this by the other quadratic (S² -2 S -25):\n\nMultiply (S^4 -2 S^3 -61 S² -42 S +216) by (S² -2 S -25).\n\nThis will be tedious, but let me try:\n\nFirst, S^4*(S² -2 S -25) = S^6 -2 S^5 -25 S^4,\n\n-2 S^3*(S² -2 S -25) = -2 S^5 +4 S^4 +50 S^3,\n\n-61 S²*(S² -2 S -25) = -61 S^4 +122 S^3 +1525 S²,\n\n-42 S*(S² -2 S -25) = -42 S^3 +84 S^2 +1050 S,\n\n216*(S² -2 S -25) =216 S² -432 S -5400.\n\nNow, adding all these terms together:\n\nStart with S^6,\n\nS^5 terms: -2 S^5 -2 S^5 = -4 S^5,\n\nS^4 terms: -25 S^4 +4 S^4 -61 S^4 = (-25 +4 -61) S^4 = -82 S^4,\n\nS^3 terms:50 S^3 +122 S^3 -42 S^3 = (50 +122 -42) S^3 = 130 S^3,\n\nS^2 terms:1525 S^2 +84 S^2 +216 S^2 = (1525 +84 +216) S^2 = 1825 S^2,\n\nS terms:1050 S -432 S = 618 S,\n\nConstants: -5400.\n\nTherefore, the product is S^6 -4 S^5 -82 S^4 +130 S^3 +1825 S^2 +618 S -5400,\n\nwhich matches the original polynomial. Therefore, the factorization is correct.\n\nThus, the roots are indeed as found. So there are six roots: S = -6, 9, and the four from the quartic. But since S = -6 and S =9 correspond to the cases where a = b = c, which are invalid, we are left with four possible values for S: the four roots from the quartic.\n\nHowever, the problem states \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c\". This suggests that perhaps there are only two valid S values, so maybe I made a miscalculation in assuming all four roots from the quartic are valid?\n\nWait, perhaps for two of the quartic roots, the corresponding a, b, c are not distinct? Let me check for one of them.\n\nLet me pick S = 1 + sqrt(26). Let's call this S3 = 1 + sqrt(26).\n\nSuppose S = S3. Let me try to see if there exists a solution with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAssume that a, b, c are distinct complex numbers satisfying the equations and sum to S3.\n\nAlternatively, since the equations are cyclic, perhaps each S from the quartic corresponds to two different triplets (a,b,c) and (c,b,a), but the sum S remains the same. Therefore, each S value from the quartic is valid and corresponds to two different triplets, but the sum S is the same. Therefore, there are four distinct S values, but the problem says two possible values. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps the quartic equation's roots come in pairs that are related?\n\nLooking at the quartic's roots:\n\nFirst quadratic: [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] / 2,\n\nSecond quadratic: 1 ± sqrt(26).\n\nHmm, these don't seem to be related in a way that would pair them into two values. Alternatively, maybe the problem expects only the real solutions? But they are all real.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the sign when factoring?\n\nWait, let me recall that when we derived the equation for S, we used substitutions that might have introduced extraneous roots?\n\nSpecifically, when we multiplied equations or squared terms, sometimes extraneous solutions can appear. However, in this case, since we were working with substitutions and algebraic manipulations without squaring both sides (except implicitly when substituting variables), so the roots of the polynomial should correspond to possible S values, but we have to check if they actually correspond to solutions with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, maybe for two of the quartic roots, the variables end up being equal even if not all three?\n\nLet me test with one of them. Let me pick S = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2,\n\nLet me denote this as S1.\n\nSuppose S1 is a valid sum where a, b, c are distinct.\n\nTo check this, perhaps we can attempt to solve for a in the equation a = [ (a² -6)^2 -6 ]^2 -6, with the constraint that a + b + c = S1.\n\nHowever, this might be complicated without knowing the exact value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that if a, b, c are distinct, then the cycle a → b → c → a must not repeat until three steps, so they form a 3-cycle, which is possible only if the function f(x) = x² -6 has a 3-cycle. In such a case, the sum S would be the sum of the three elements in the cycle.\n\nSimilarly for the other roots. Since we have four possible S values, maybe there are two distinct 3-cycles leading to two distinct sums, and the other two roots correspond to something else? But I don't know.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is from an Olympiad where the answer is two values, so maybe the quartic factors further or something else.\n\nWait, let me compute the numerical values of the quartic roots to see if any of them are equal or if they can be paired.\n\nCompute S1 = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2,\n\nsqrt(17) ≈ 4.123,\n\nso S1 ≈ ( -1 + 4.123 ) / 2 ≈ 3.123 / 2 ≈ 1.5615,\n\nS2 = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2 ≈ ( -1 -4.123 ) / 2 ≈ -5.123 / 2 ≈ -2.5615,\n\nS3 = 1 + sqrt(26) ≈ 1 + 5.099 ≈ 6.099,\n\nS4 = 1 - sqrt(26) ≈ 1 -5.099 ≈ -4.099.\n\nSo the four roots are approximately 1.56, -2.56, 6.10, -4.10.\n\nNow, perhaps some of these correspond to the same triplet when considering the cyclic permutations?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem considers that S and -S or something else are related, but they are all distinct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is expecting only the two real roots that are not the fixed points? But there are four real roots.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when forming the equations, there is a symmetry that causes some of the roots to be duplicates when considering the cyclicity?\n\nWait, the equations are cyclic, so perhaps the values S1 and S2 are related via some symmetry, as are S3 and S4?\n\nLet me see:\n\nS1 ≈1.56,\n\nS2 ≈-2.56,\n\nS3≈6.10,\n\nS4≈-4.10.\n\nHmm, not obvious. Alternatively, perhaps the problem assumes that a, b, c are roots of a cubic equation, so their sum S is related to the coefficients, but given the cyclic nature, the cubic would have coefficients related to the constants in the equations.\n\nAlternatively, let me think back to the original equations:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nLet me consider adding 6 to both sides of each equation:\n\na² - b = 6,\n\nb² - c = 6,\n\nc² - a = 6.\n\nLet me think of this as a system of equations:\n\na² - b = 6,\n\nb² - c = 6,\n\nc² - a = 6.\n\nIf I let x = a, y = b, z = c,\n\nthen:\n\nx² - y = 6,\n\ny² - z = 6,\n\nz² - x = 6.\n\nThis is a system of three equations in three variables.\n\nWe can attempt to solve this system.\n\nFrom the first equation: y = x² - 6,\n\nFrom the second: z = y² - 6 = (x² - 6)^2 - 6,\n\nFrom the third: x = z² - 6 = [ (x² - 6)^2 - 6 ]^2 - 6.\n\nSo, as before, this reduces to the equation:\n\nx = [ (x² - 6)^2 - 6 ]^2 - 6,\n\nwhich is an eighth-degree equation.\n\nHowever, perhaps we can find solutions by assuming that the variables are part of a symmetric cycle.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that a, b, c are the roots of the cubic equation t³ - S t² + P t - Q = 0,\n\nwhere S = a + b + c,\n\nP = ab + bc + ca,\n\nQ = abc.\n\nWe had earlier established relationships between S, P, and Q.\n\nFrom Equation 6: P = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nFrom Equation 12: Q = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6,\n\nAdditionally, from the identity for cubic equations: a³ + b³ + c³ = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q.\n\nBut also, from the original equations:\n\na² = b + 6 ⇒ a² - b = 6,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nb² - c = 6,\n\nc² - a = 6.\n\nIf I sum these three equations:\n\n(a² + b² + c²) - (a + b + c) = 18,\n\nWhich we already used to get a² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand since a² + b² + c² = S² - 2P,\n\nthen S² - 2P = S + 18 ⇒ P = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nwhich matches Equation 6.\n\nAdditionally, perhaps we can find another relation using the original equations.\n\nLet me compute a³ + b³ + c³.\n\nFrom the original equations,\n\na² = b + 6 ⇒ a³ = a b + 6 a,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nb³ = b c + 6 b,\n\nc³ = c a + 6 c,\n\nTherefore, a³ + b³ + c³ = a b + b c + c a + 6(a + b + c) = P + 6 S.\n\nBut also, from the cubic identity:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q.\n\nTherefore,\n\nP + 6 S = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q,\n\nRearranged:\n\nS³ - 3 S P - P - 6 S + 3 Q = 0,\n\nFactor terms with P:\n\nS³ - P(3 S + 1) -6 S + 3 Q = 0,\n\nNow substitute P and Q from earlier expressions:\n\nP = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S - 18)/6,\n\nSo,\n\nS³ - [ (S² - S - 18)/2 ]*(3 S + 1) -6 S + 3*[ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S - 18)/6 ] = 0.\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nFirst term: S³,\n\nSecond term: - [ (S² - S - 18)/2 ]*(3 S + 1),\n\nThird term: -6 S,\n\nFourth term: 3*[ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S - 18)/6 ] = [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S - 18)/2 ].\n\nSo, combining all terms:\n\nS³ - [ (S² - S - 18)(3 S + 1)/2 ] -6 S + [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S - 18)/2 ] = 0.\n\nMultiply every term by 2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n2 S³ - (S² - S - 18)(3 S + 1) -12 S + (S³ - 2 S² -43 S - 18) = 0.\n\nNow, expand the term (S² - S - 18)(3 S + 1):\n\nFirst, S²*(3 S + 1) = 3 S³ + S²,\n\n- S*(3 S +1) = -3 S² - S,\n\n-18*(3 S +1) = -54 S -18,\n\nSo altogether:\n\n3 S³ + S² -3 S² - S -54 S -18 = 3 S³ -2 S² -55 S -18.\n\nTherefore, substituting back into the equation:\n\n2 S³ - [3 S³ -2 S² -55 S -18] -12 S + [S³ -2 S² -43 S -18] = 0,\n\nNow distribute the negative sign:\n\n2 S³ -3 S³ +2 S² +55 S +18 -12 S + S³ -2 S² -43 S -18 = 0.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nS³ terms: 2 S³ -3 S³ + S³ = 0,\n\nS² terms: 2 S² -2 S² = 0,\n\nS terms:55 S -12 S -43 S = 0,\n\nConstants:18 -18 =0,\n\nSo everything cancels out, resulting in 0 =0.\n\nHmm, so this doesn't give us a new equation—it just confirms consistency, but doesn't help us solve for S.\n\nThis suggests that our earlier approach is correct, but we still need to resolve the discrepancy with the problem stating \"two possible values\".\n\nPerhaps the key lies in the fact that although the quartic has four roots, two of them are actually the same when considering the cyclic nature?\n\nAlternatively, maybe each pair of roots from the quartic corresponds to the same triplet but in different order, hence the same S?\n\nWait, but S is the sum, which is invariant under permutation of a, b, c. Therefore, each distinct triplet gives a unique S, but different triplets can give the same S. However, since the equations are cyclic, a solution (a,b,c) can be cycled to (b,c,a) or (c,a,b), which would give the same S but different assignments. However, the sum S remains the same. Therefore, each S from the quartic corresponds to multiple triplets, but the S value itself is distinct for each root of the quartic.\n\nTherefore, unless there's a symmetry where two different S values are actually the same when considering complex conjugates, but all roots are real.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem assumes that a, b, c are real numbers? If so, then even though the equations allow complex solutions, the problem might be expecting real solutions only. In that case, all roots are real, so four possible S values. But the problem states \"complex numbers\", so that can't be.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the beginning by assuming that all roots of the equation for S are valid, but actually, only two of them correspond to solutions where a, b, c are distinct.\n\nLet me consider S = 1 + sqrt(26) ≈6.099,\n\nSuppose we attempt to find a, b, c such that they satisfy the equations and sum to this S.\n\nLet me attempt to find such a numerically.\n\nLet me start with an initial guess for a, then compute b, c, and iterate until it converges or cycles.\n\nAlternatively, since this is time-consuming, perhaps I can consider that since the problem is from a competition, the answer is likely to be the two values from the quadratic factors that are more \"nice\", but in our case, the quartic factors into two quadratics with irrational roots, so perhaps the problem expects the two real roots from the quartic that are not the ones corresponding to the fixed points?\n\nWait, but we already discarded the fixed points S = -6 and S =9.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem has a trick where a + b + c must satisfy a quadratic equation, hence only two possible values.\n\nLooking back at the equations:\n\nWe had earlier:\n\nFrom Equation 4: a² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand from the identity:\n\na² + b² + c² = S² - 2(ab + bc + ca).\n\nSo, S² - 2(ab + bc + ca) = S + 18,\n\nand from Equation 6: ab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nTherefore, substituting that into the above,\n\nS² - 2*( (S² - S -18)/2 ) = S + 18,\n\nSimplify left-hand side:\n\nS² - (S² - S -18) = S + 18,\n\nWhich gives:\n\nS² - S² + S + 18 = S + 18,\n\nSimplify:\n\nS + 18 = S + 18,\n\nWhich is an identity, so it doesn't provide new information.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps combining other equations.\n\nWe had Equation 11: (abc)^2 - abc - 3S² - 33 S - 162 = 0,\n\nand Equation 12: abc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6,\n\nSubstitute Equation 12 into Equation 11:\n\nLet me denote Q = abc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6,\n\nThen Equation 11 becomes:\n\nQ² - Q - 3S² -33 S -162 =0,\n\nSubstituting Q:\n\n[ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ]^2 - [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ] -3 S² -33 S -162 =0,\n\nThis is exactly the equation we had before, leading to the sixth-degree polynomial. Therefore, no new info.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed such that the two possible values are the roots of one of the quadratics, say the first quadratic S² + S -4=0, giving S = [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] /2,\n\nand the other quadratic S² - 2 S -25=0 gives S =1 ± sqrt(26),\n\nbut the problem says \"two possible values\", so maybe only the ones from one quadratic are valid?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the two quadratics each give rise to one valid S value when considering distinctness?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when S = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] /2 ≈1.56, then the variables a, b, c are distinct,\n\nwhereas when S = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] /2 ≈-2.56, the variables might not be distinct?\n\nLet me test with S ≈-2.56,\n\nSuppose S ≈-2.56,\n\nthen a + b + c ≈-2.56,\n\nbut let's see if such a solution exists with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the quartic's roots come in pairs where one pair corresponds to solutions with distinct a, b, c and the other pair also do, but the problem counts them as two distinct values? For example, maybe the two quadratics each contribute one valid S value?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is from an exam where only two of the quartic roots are acceptable due to the problem's constraints not explicitly mentioned.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to re-express the quartic equation in terms of S and find that it factors into two quadratics with discriminants that allow for two distinct S values when considering the problem's conditions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that when you have the system of equations:\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nthen adding all three gives a² + b² + c² = a + b + c + 18,\n\nwhich we already used,\n\nbut also, if we consider the product abc,\n\nfrom the earlier multiplied equations: (abc)^2 = (a +6)(b +6)(c +6),\n\nand we also have expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc in terms of S,\n\nbut this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to think of this as a function iteration.\n\nDefine the function f(x) = x² -6,\n\nthen the equations are:\n\nb = f(a),\n\nc = f(b),\n\na = f(c),\n\nso we have a = f(f(f(a))).\n\nThus, the solutions for a are the fixed points of the function f composed three times, f^3(x) = x.\n\nTherefore, the solutions for a are the periodic points of period 3 for the function f(x) = x² -6,\n\nor fixed points of f^3(x).\n\nThe fixed points of f^3(x) include the fixed points of f(x) (period 1), period 2 points, and period 3 points.\n\nWe already saw that the fixed points of f(x) are x = 3 and x = -2, which give S = 9 and S = -6 respectively.\n\nPeriod 2 points would satisfy f(f(x)) = x but f(x) ≠x,\n\nso solving f(f(x)) = x,\n\nwhich is x² -6 = f(x) ⇒ f(f(x)) = f(x² -6) = (x² -6)^2 -6,\n\nsetting equal to x:\n\n(x² -6)^2 -6 = x,\n\nwhich is x^4 -12x² +36 -6 -x = x^4 -12x² -x +30 =0.\n\nThis quartic might have solutions that are period 2 points,\n\nand period 3 points would be solutions to f^3(x)=x but not of lower period.\n\nTherefore, the solutions for a in our problem are the period 3 points,\n\nso their sum S would correspond to the sum of the three elements in the 3-cycle,\n\nand since there can be multiple 3-cycles,\n\neach 3-cycle would give a distinct S,\n\nbut in our case, the equation f^3(x) -x =0 has roots at the period 1 points (S=9 and S=-6),\n\nperiod 2 points,\n\nand period 3 points.\n\nThe number of period 3 points can be calculated via the degree,\n\nsince f^3(x) -x is an eighth-degree polynomial,\n\nso total roots: 8,\n\nsubtract the 2 period 1 points,\n\nand possibly some period 2 points,\n\nleaving the rest as period 3 points.\n\nHowever, without calculating the exact number,\n\nbut since we are to find the possible S values for period 3 cycles,\n\nwhich would correspond to the quartic roots (since we factored out the period 1 roots),\n\nthen those quartic roots are the S values for the period 3 cycles,\n\nand since each 3-cycle corresponds to a unique S (the sum of its elements),\n\nand since there can be multiple 3-cycles,\n\neach giving a distinct S,\n\nbut in our case the quartic factors into two quadratics,\n\neach quadratic giving two roots,\n\nso four S values,\n\nso four different 3-cycles,\n\neach with their own S.\n\nBut the problem says \"compute the two possible values\",\n\nsuggesting that maybe there are only two distinct S values from the quartic,\n\nbut since the quartic has four real roots,\n\nthis is conflicting.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed so that the two values are the solutions to the quadratic equation obtained by assuming that the quartic's roots satisfy a quadratic in S,\n\nbut that seems unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a miscalculation in the factorization.\n\nWait, let me re-express the quartic equation:\n\nS^4 - S^3 -31 S^2 -17 S + 100 =0.\n\nSuppose we let T = S + k,\n\nfor some constant k to eliminate the cubic term,\n\nbut that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the quartic can be factored into two quadratics with integer coefficients that I missed?\n\nWait, we already factored it into (S^2 + S -4)(S^2 -2 S -25),\n\nwhich are both quadratics with integer coefficients,\n\nso that's the complete factorization.\n\nTherefore, the roots are indeed as found.\n\nGiven that the problem states \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", and since all roots are real,\n\nthen all four roots are valid unless they force a, b, c to be equal,\n\nbut we already saw that S = -6 and 9 correspond to all variables equal,\n\nbut the other four roots correspond to cycles where a, b, c are distinct.\n\nTherefore, the problem might have a typo, or perhaps I misread it.\n\nWait, the original problem says: \"Compute the two possible values of a + b + c.\"\n\nPerhaps in the problem's context, considering that the equations are symmetric and cyclic, there are only two distinct sums possible,\n\neven though algebraically there are four,\n\nbecause the other two are duplicates or related via symmetry?\n\nWait, looking at the quartic roots:\n\nS1 = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2 ≈1.56,\n\nS2 = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2 ≈-2.56,\n\nS3 =1 + sqrt(26)≈6.10,\n\nS4=1 - sqrt(26)≈-4.10.\n\nThese are four distinct real numbers.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem assumes that a, b, c are roots of a cubic equation with real coefficients,\n\nand hence their sum S must satisfy certain conditions,\n\nbut since all roots are real,\n\nthen all four S values are possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is from a specific competition where the answer is two values,\n\nand in the process of solving,\n\nonly two of the quartic roots survive some additional constraint,\n\nbut I don't see it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user who submitted the problem made a mistake in the problem statement,\n\nand the equations are meant to be a^2 = b + 6, b^2 = c + 6, c^2 = a + 6,\n\nwith a, b, c being real numbers,\n\nand in that case,\n\nmaybe only two of the quartic roots yield real a, b, c?\n\nWait, but if a, b, c are real,\n\nthen the equations require that each step's right-hand side is real,\n\nso if a is real,\n\nthen b = a² -6 is real,\n\nthen c = b² -6 is real,\n\nand then a = c² -6 must also be real,\n\nso all are real,\n\nso all roots are valid,\n\nso four real S values.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is in the initial step where I added the three equations,\n\ngetting a² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand from the identity,\n\na² + b² + c² = S² - 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nso,\n\nS² - 2(ab + bc + ca) = S + 18,\n\nthen,\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nbut if we also consider that the product abc can be expressed in terms of S,\n\nfrom the earlier equations,\n\nand then using the cubic equation's relations,\n\nbut this leads us back to the sixth-degree equation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is designed such that when you consider that a, b, c are roots of the cubic equation x³ - Sx² + Px - Q =0,\n\nthen Vieta's formula gives the relationships,\n\nand combining with the original equations,\n\nyou can find that S must satisfy a quadratic equation,\n\nleading to two solutions.\n\nLet me try this approach.\n\nAssume that a, b, c are roots of the cubic equation:\n\nx³ - S x² + P x - Q = 0,\n\nwhere S = a + b + c,\n\nP = ab + bc + ca,\n\nQ = abc.\n\nFrom the original equations,\n\nwe have:\n\na² = b + 6 ⇒ b = a² -6,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nc = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nand a = c² -6 = [ (a² -6)^2 -6 ]^2 -6.\n\nBut perhaps instead of following this path,\n\nlet me use the original equations to express b, c in terms of a,\n\nthen express everything in terms of a,\n\nand then find a relation for S.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that since a, b, c satisfy the cyclic equations,\n\nthen each of them satisfies the equation x² - y -6 =0,\n\nwhere y is the next variable,\n\nso composing these,\n\nwe get the eighth-degree equation,\n\nbut if we consider that the sum S = a + b + c,\n\nthen perhaps we can find a relation between S and the equations.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that from the three equations,\n\nwe can write:\n\na² - b = 6,\n\nb² - c = 6,\n\nc² - a = 6.\n\nAdding them gives a² + b² + c² - (a + b + c) = 18,\n\nwhich we've used.\n\nLet me also consider multiplying all three equations:\n\n(a² - b)(b² - c)(c² - a) = 6*6*6 = 216.\n\nBut expanding the left side seems complicated,\n\nbut perhaps we can find a relation using S, P, Q.\n\nAlternatively, note that:\n\n(a² - b) =6 ⇒ a² = b +6,\n\nsimilarly,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nso,\n\n(a² -6)(b² -6)(c² -6) = b c a,\n\nsince:\n\n(a² -6)=b,\n\n(b² -6)=c,\n\n(c² -6)=a,\n\nso,\n\n(b)(c)(a) = abc,\n\ntherefore,\n\n(a² -6)(b² -6)(c² -6) = abc,\n\nThus,\n\nabc = (a² -6)(b² -6)(c² -6).\n\nBut also from the original multiplied equations:\n\n(abc)^2 = (a +6)(b +6)(c +6),\n\nSo,\n\nabc = sqrt[ (a +6)(b +6)(c +6) ],\n\nbut this may not help.\n\nAlternatively, combining the two equations:\n\nabc = (a² -6)(b² -6)(c² -6),\n\nand\n\n(abc)^2 = (a +6)(b +6)(c +6).\n\nTherefore,\n\n[ (a² -6)(b² -6)(c² -6) ]^2 = (a +6)(b +6)(c +6).\n\nThis seems too complex.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps substituting expressions for a², b², c² from the original equations into this equation,\n\nbut it might not lead anywhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed such that the two possible values of S are the roots of the quadratic equation obtained by assuming that the quartic factors into two quadratics with certain symmetries,\n\nbut since we already factored it into two quadratics with real coefficients,\n\nand each quadratic has two real roots,\n\nthen there are four real S values,\n\nso the problem's mention of \"two possible values\" might be an error,\n\nor perhaps I missed a constraint.\n\nWait, going back to the original problem statement:\n\n\"Let a, b, and c be pairwise distinct complex numbers such that\n\na² = b + 6, b² = c + 6, and c² = a + 6.\n\nCompute the two possible values of a + b + c.\"\n\nThe key might be in \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\".\n\nIf we consider that the system might have solutions where a, b, c are roots of a cubic equation with certain properties,\n\nand the sum S must satisfy a quadratic equation,\n\nleading to two solutions.\n\nLet me suppose that the quartic equation can be reduced to a quadratic in terms of S² or something,\n\nbut looking at the quartic:\n\nS^4 - S^3 -31 S^2 -17 S + 100 =0.\n\nIt doesn't look reducible to a quadratic in S²,\n\nbut perhaps through substitution.\n\nLet me let T = S + k/S or something,\n\nbut that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the quartic can be written as (S^2 + p S + q)(S^2 + r S + s),\n\nwhich we already did,\n\nso unless there's another way,\n\nperhaps the problem expects the two values from the quadratic factors,\n\neach quadratic having two roots,\n\nbut since they are quadratics,\n\nmaybe the problem considers the two quadratics as the two possible cases,\n\nleading to two possible values for S,\n\nbut that doesn't make sense since each quadratic has two roots.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in a context where only positive values are considered,\n\nbut S can be positive or negative.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two possible values are the ones from the first quadratic,\n\n[ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] /2,\n\nand the other quadratic's roots are extraneous,\n\nbut why?\n\nAlternatively, maybe when solving the system,\n\nonly the first quadratic's roots lead to distinct a, b, c,\n\nwhereas the other quadratic's roots lead to a = b or something.\n\nLet me test with S =1 + sqrt(26) ≈6.10,\n\nSuppose we try to find a solution with this S,\n\nstart with an initial guess for a,\n\nsay a = 3,\n\nthen b = a² -6 = 9 -6 = 3,\n\nthen c = b² -6 = 9 -6 =3,\n\nthen a = c² -6 =9 -6=3,\n\nso a = b = c =3,\n\nwhich is the fixed point we already know,\n\nbut this corresponds to S=9,\n\nso not our case.\n\nAlternatively, pick a different starting value,\n\nsay a = 4,\n\nthen b = 16 -6 =10,\n\nc =100 -6=94,\n\nthen a should be c² -6 = 94² -6,\n\nwhich is way larger,\n\nso it diverges.\n\nAlternatively, pick a smaller a,\n\nsay a = 2,\n\nthen b =4 -6 = -2,\n\nc = (-2)^2 -6 =4 -6 =-2,\n\nthen a should be (-2)^2 -6 =4-6=-2,\n\nso a = -2,\n\nthen b = (-2)^2 -6 =4-6=-2,\n\nso again a = b = c =-2,\n\nthe other fixed point.\n\nHmm,\n\nso perhaps for the roots of the first quadratic,\n\nS = [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] /2,\n\nthere exist solutions with distinct a, b, c,\n\nwhereas for the other quadratic's roots,\n\nthe solutions collapse to the fixed points?\n\nWait, but the fixed points are already excluded.\n\nAlternatively, maybe for S =1 + sqrt(26),\n\nthe variables a, b, c form a cycle that doesn't repeat until three steps,\n\nso they are distinct,\n\nbut for S =1 - sqrt(26),\n\nwhich is negative,\n\nmaybe the cycle involves complex numbers?\n\nWait, but sqrt(26) is real,\n\nso S =1 - sqrt(26) is real,\n\nbut a, b, c could still be complex?\n\nWait, no,\n\nif we start with a real number,\n\nthen b = a² -6 is real,\n\nthen c = b² -6 is real,\n\nthen a = c² -6 is real,\n\nso all variables would be real,\n\nso for real S,\n\nthe variables are real,\n\nso the solutions with S =1 ± sqrt(26),\n\nand S = [ -1 ± sqrt(17) ] /2 are all real,\n\nand correspond to real a, b, c,\n\nwhich may or may not be distinct.\n\nPerhaps for S =1 - sqrt(26) ≈-4.1,\n\nthe variables a, b, c are real but not all distinct?\n\nLet me try to see.\n\nLet me attempt to find a solution with S =1 - sqrt(26) ≈-4.1,\n\nLet me denote S = K ≈-4.1.\n\nLet me pick an initial guess for a,\n\nsay a =0,\n\nthen b =0 -6 =-6,\n\nc = (-6)^2 -6 =36 -6=30,\n\nthen a should be 30² -6 =900 -6=894,\n\nwhich is nowhere near K,\n\nso diverges.\n\nAlternatively, start with a =-3,\n\nthen b =9 -6=3,\n\nc =9 -6=3,\n\nthen a =9 -6=3,\n\nso a=3, b=3, c=3,\n\nwhich is the fixed point again.\n\nHmm,\n\nanother attempt,\n\npick a =-4,\n\nthen b=16-6=10,\n\nc=100-6=94,\n\nthen a=94² -6,\n\nwhich is huge,\n\nso diverges.\n\nAlternatively, pick a =-5,\n\nthen b=25 -6=19,\n\nc=361-6=355,\n\nthen a=355² -6,\n\ndiverging.\n\nHmm,\n\nmaybe starting from a negative a,\n\nthis approach isn't working.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the solution requires complex numbers?\n\nWait, but the problem allows complex numbers,\n\nso perhaps a, b, c are complex,\n\nand thus S can be real or complex,\n\nbut our polynomial for S had only real roots,\n\nso S must be real.\n\nTherefore, even if a, b, c are complex,\n\ntheir sum S is real.\n\nTherefore,\n\nfor S =1 - sqrt(26),\n\nthere must exist complex a, b, c such that they sum to that real number,\n\nand satisfy the cyclic equations,\n\nbut they are not all real,\n\nso they are distinct complex numbers.\n\nTherefore,\n\nall four roots are valid,\n\nso the problem might have an error,\n\nor perhaps I misunderstood the question.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nthe problem might be from a specific context where only the quadratic S^2 + S -4 =0 is considered,\n\nleading to two solutions,\n\nbut why?\n\nWait,\n\ngoing back to the original problem,\n\nperhaps there's a different approach.\n\nLet me consider that the equations are cyclic,\n\nso we can write:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² -c =6,\n\nc² -a =6.\n\nLet me subtract the first equation from the second:\n\n(b² - c) - (a² - b) =0,\n\nwhich is b² -c -a² +b =0,\n\nb² -a² +b -c =0,\n\nFactor b² -a² as (b -a)(b +a),\n\nso,\n\n(b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) =0,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nsubtract second equation from third:\n\n(c² -a) - (b² -c) =0,\n\nc² -a -b² +c =0,\n\nc² - b² +c -a =0,\n\n(c -b)(c +b) + (c -a) =0,\n\nAnd subtract third equation from first:\n\n(a² - b) - (c² -a) =0,\n\na² -b -c² +a =0,\n\na² -c² +a -b =0,\n\n(a -c)(a +c) + (a -b)=0.\n\nThis gives three new equations:\n\n1. (b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) =0,\n\n2. (c -b)(c +b) + (c -a) =0,\n\n3. (a -c)(a +c) + (a -b)=0.\n\nThese might be useful.\n\nLet me denote the differences:\n\nLet me set d1 = b -a,\n\nd2 = c -b,\n\nd3 = a -c.\n\nNote that d1 + d2 + d3 =0,\n\nsince (b -a) + (c -b) + (a -c) =0.\n\nNow,\n\nFrom equation 1:\n\n(b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) = d1*(b +a) + d2 =0,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nequation 2:\n\n(c -b)(c +b) + (c -a) = d2*(c +b) + d3 =0,\n\nequation 3:\n\n(a -c)(a +c) + (a -b) = d3*(a +c) + d1 =0.\n\nThus,\n\nwe have the system:\n\n1. d1*(b + a) + d2 =0,\n\n2. d2*(c + b) + d3 =0,\n\n3. d3*(a + c) + d1 =0,\n\nwith d1 + d2 + d3 =0.\n\nThis system might be solvable in terms of d1, d2, d3.\n\nLet me express d3 = -d1 -d2,\n\nfrom the first equation,\n\nd1*(b +a) + d2 =0 ⇒ d2 = -d1*(b +a).\n\nSubstitute into the second equation:\n\nd2*(c + b) + d3 =0,\n\n=> (-d1*(b +a))*(c + b) + (-d1 -d2) =0,\n\nWait, this might get too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nfrom equation 1: d2 = -d1*(b +a),\n\nfrom equation 2: d3 = -d2*(c + b),\n\nfrom equation 3: d1 = -d3*(a +c),\n\nNow substitute d2 from equation 1 into equation 2:\n\nd3 = - [ -d1*(b +a) ]*(c + b) = d1*(b +a)*(c + b),\n\nThen substitute d3 into equation 3:\n\nd1 = - [ d1*(b +a)*(c + b) ]*(a +c),\n\nAssuming d1 ≠0,\n\nwe can divide both sides by d1:\n\n1 = - (b +a)*(c + b)*(a +c),\n\nThus,\n\n(b +a)(c + b)(a +c) = -1.\n\nThis is a key equation.\n\nAdditionally, we know that a + b + c = S,\n\nso,\n\na + b = S - c,\n\nb + c = S - a,\n\na + c = S - b,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(b +a)(c + b)(a +c) = (S - c)(S - a)(S - b) = -1.\n\nBut (S - a)(S - b)(S - c) is the value of the cubic polynomial x³ - Sx² + Px - Q evaluated at x = S,\n\nwhich is S³ - S*S² + P S - Q = - (P S - Q),\n\nsince x³ - Sx² + Px - Q evaluated at x=S is S³ - S³ + P S - Q = P S - Q,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(S - a)(S - b)(S - c) = P S - Q = -1,\n\nfrom the above equation.\n\nBut from Equation 12,\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6,\n\nand from Equation 6,\n\nP = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nTherefore,\n\nP S - Q = [ (S² - S -18)/2 ] * S - [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ].\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nFirst term: [S³ - S² -18 S]/2,\n\nSecond term: - [S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18]/6,\n\nCombine the two terms over a common denominator of 6:\n\n= [3(S³ - S² -18 S) - (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)] /6,\n\nExpand numerator:\n\n3S³ -3S² -54 S -S³ +2 S² +43 S +18,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(3S³ -S³) + (-3S² +2 S²) + (-54 S +43 S) +18,\n\n= 2S³ - S² -11 S +18,\n\nThus,\n\nP S - Q = (2S³ - S² -11 S +18)/6,\n\nand we have from above that this equals -1,\n\nso:\n\n(2S³ - S² -11 S +18)/6 = -1,\n\nMultiply both sides by 6:\n\n2S³ - S² -11 S +18 = -6,\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n2S³ - S² -11 S +24 =0,\n\nThus,\n\n2S³ - S² -11 S +24 =0,\n\nNow, this is a cubic equation in S,\n\nwhich we can attempt to factor.\n\nLet me try rational roots,\n\npossible roots are ±1, ±2, ±3, ±4, ±6, ±8, ±12, ±24, divided by 1,2.\n\nTesting S=2:\n\n2*(8) -4 -22 +24 =16 -4 -22 +24=14 ≠0,\n\nS=3:\n\n2*27 -9 -33 +24=54 -9 -33 +24= 36 ≠0,\n\nS= -3:\n\n2*(-27) -9 -(-33) +24= -54 -9 +33 +24= -6 ≠0,\n\nS=4:\n\n2*64 -16 -44 +24=128-16-44+24=92≠0,\n\nS= -2:\n\n2*(-8) -4 -(-22)+24= -16-4+22+24=26≠0,\n\nS= 1:\n\n2 -1 -11 +24=14≠0,\n\nS= 4/ something?\n\nWait, try S= 3/2:\n\n2*(27/8) - (9/4) -11*(3/2) +24,\n\n= 27/4 - 9/4 - 33/2 +24,\n\n= (27-9)/4 - 33/2 +24,\n\n= 18/4 -33/2 +24,\n\n= 9/2 -33/2 +24,\n\n= (-24/2) +24 = -12 +24 =12≠0,\n\nS= -4:\n\n2*(-64) -16 -(-44) +24 = -128 -16 +44 +24 = -76≠0,\n\nS= 8:\n\nToo big, probably not.\n\nS= 24/2=12: too big.\n\nHmm, not finding a rational root.\n\nMaybe factor by grouping?\n\n2S³ - S² -11 S +24,\n\ngroup as (2S³ - S²) + (-11 S +24),\n\nFactor S² from first two terms: S²(2S -1),\n\nSecond group: -11 S +24,\n\nNo common factor.\n\nAlternatively,\n\ntry to factor as (2S³ + a