have a = b = c,\n\nBut then a² = a +6 ⇒ a² -a -6 =0 ⇒ a=(1 ±√25)/2=(1 ±5)/2 ⇒ a=3 or a=-2.\n\nBut in this case, a=b=c=3 or a=b=c=-2,\n\nBut the problem states that a, b, c are pairwise distinct, so these solutions are excluded. So the values S=3*3=9 or S=3*(-2)=-6 would correspond to equal triples, but since they are excluded, maybe S=9 and S=-6 are the ones that come from the equal triples, so they might be excluded?\n\nWait, if a=b=c=3, then S=9, and similarly a=b=c=-2 gives S=-6. Since those are the only solutions with a=b=c, then the problem requires a, b, c to be pairwise distinct, so those two S values (9 and -6) are invalid? But wait, maybe there are other solutions with S=9 or S=-6 where a, b, c are distinct?\n\nWait, perhaps not. Maybe when S=9, the only solutions are the triples where a=b=c=3, so there are no distinct triples with S=9. Similarly, for S=-6, the only solutions are a=b=c=-2, so no distinct triples there. Therefore, those two S values are invalid, so we must exclude them.\n\nTherefore, the remaining S values are the other four: approximately 1.56, -2.56, 6.10, -4.10. But the problem says there are two possible values. Hmm, so maybe two of these four are actually the same or something?\n\nWait, the four roots from the quadratics:\n\nS = [ -1 ±√17 ] /2,\n\nand S =1 ±√26,\n\nThese are four distinct real numbers. However, the problem says \"complex numbers\", so maybe some of these roots are complex?\n\nWait, √17 and √26 are real, so all four roots are real. So S can take four real values besides -6 and 9.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so even if they are real, they are complex (since real numbers are a subset of complex numbers). So all six roots are possible in the complex plane, but the problem wants the two possible values of a + b + c where a, b, c are pairwise distinct. Since S=-6 and S=9 only correspond to the cases where a=b=c, which are excluded, so those are invalid. The other four S values are candidates.\n\nBut the problem says \"two possible values\". Hmm, so maybe two of these four are actually the same? But they are all distinct.\n\nWait, let me calculate the exact values:\n\nFirst quadratic roots:\n\nS₁ = [ -1 + √17 ] / 2,\n\nS₂ = [ -1 - √17 ] / 2,\n\nSecond quadratic roots:\n\nS₃ =1 + √26,\n\nS₄ =1 - √26,\n\nThese are four distinct real numbers. So that would give four possible values for S, but the problem says two. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when considering the original eighth-degree equation in a, each S corresponds to multiple a's, but perhaps some S values lead to the same a's but with different permutations, but the sum S is the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is set such that despite the quartic having four roots, only two of them actually correspond to valid triples with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when we solved for S, we have to consider that the system might have symmetries or that some S values are duplicates under permutation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my mistake was in assuming all roots except S=9 and S=-6 are valid, but actually some of the other S values also lead to a, b, c not being distinct?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when S is one of the four values, the system may have solutions where two of a, b, c are equal?\n\nLet me pick one of the S values and see if it can lead to a solution with distinct a, b, c.\n\nLet me try S =1 +√26 ≈6.099.\n\nLet me suppose S=1 +√26,\n\nThen, from the expressions we had earlier:\n\nQ = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nand P = (S³ -2S² -43S -18)/6,\n\nBut perhaps it's easier to attempt to find a solution for a given S.\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that for each S, there is at least one solution with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is designed so that the only possible S values are 9 and -6, but since those correspond to equal triples which are excluded, then perhaps there are no solutions? But the problem states that a, b, c are pairwise distinct complex numbers satisfying the equations, so there must be solutions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my factorization is correct, but I need to check if the quartic's roots lead to valid triples with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is expecting us to consider that even though the eighth-degree equation has eight roots for a, but due to the cyclic nature, each S corresponds to two roots for a (since each a can generate a unique b and c), and so the possible S values are the six roots, but with some multiplicities or symmetries?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is from a competition where the answer is two values, likely integers, so perhaps S=9 and S=-6 are the answers, but since they correspond to equal triples which are excluded, but maybe the problem didn't specify that a, b, c must be distinct from each other, just that they are pairwise distinct? Wait, the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so they must be distinct. Therefore, S=9 and S=-6 are invalid.\n\nHmm. This is getting confusing.\n\nWait, let me think differently.\n\nSuppose that we have the eighth-degree equation in a, which we factored as (a +6)(a -9)(quartic)=0,\n\nWait, actually, no—the eighth-degree equation in a was reduced to a sixth-degree equation in S, but the equation in a itself was degree eight.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to consider that each root S corresponds to multiple roots a, but when considering the entire system, each solution for a gives a unique S, but some S values may correspond to multiple a's but still the same S.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the quartic in S (the four roots) correspond to two values of S when considering the cycles?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is an error in my approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a smarter substitution.\n\nLet me try to consider that since the system is cyclic, maybe assume that a, b, c are roots of the equation x³ - Sx² + Qx - P =0,\n\nAnd from the earlier equations, we have relationships between S, Q, P,\n\nWe had:\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nSo, perhaps we can write:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² - c =6,\n\nc² -a =6,\n\nLet me write these as:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² - c =6,\n\nc² -a =6.\n\nIf I add all three equations:\n\na² + b² + c² - (a + b + c) = 18,\n\nWhich is the same as before, so a² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nAdditionally, perhaps subtract equations?\n\nFor instance, subtract the first equation from the second:\n\n(b² - c) - (a² - b) =0,\n\nSo, b² -c -a² + b =0,\n\nWhich is b² -a² + b -c =0,\n\nFactor b² -a² = (b -a)(b +a),\n\nThus,\n\n(b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) =0,\n\nHmm, not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express each variable in terms of the next and substitute into the cubic equation.\n\nAlternatively, since a, b, c satisfy the cubic equation x³ - Sx² + Qx - P =0,\n\nThen each of a, b, c satisfies their own cubic equation.\n\nSo, for example,\n\na³ - S a² + Q a - P =0,\n\nBut from the original equation, a² = b +6,\n\nSo, a³ = a * a² = a(b +6) = ab +6a,\n\nTherefore, substituting into the cubic equation:\n\n(ab +6a) - S a² + Q a - P =0,\n\nBut a² = b +6,\n\nSo,\n\nab +6a - S(b +6) + Q a - P =0,\n\nLet me collect terms:\n\nab +6a + Q a - S b -6S - P =0,\n\nGroup terms with b:\n\nb(a - S) + a(6 + Q) -6S - P =0,\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to express everything in terms of S.\n\nWe had expressions for Q and P in terms of S:\n\nQ = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nP = (S³ - 2S² -43S -18)/6,\n\nTherefore, substituting into the cubic equation,\n\nx³ - S x² + [(S² - S - 18)/2] x - [(S³ - 2S² -43S -18)/6] =0,\n\nBut since a, b, c are roots of this equation, perhaps plugging in one of the variables?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this is getting too convoluted.\n\nWait, going back to the start, perhaps the problem is designed so that a + b + c is either 9 or -6, but those correspond to the equal triples which are excluded, but maybe the actual solutions for distinct triples have sums that are the other roots?\n\nWait, the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so maybe even though S=9 and S=-6 correspond to triples where all three are equal (which are excluded), but there might be other triples with the same sum S=9 or S=-6 where the numbers are distinct?\n\nWait, for example, suppose S=9, can there exist a solution where a, b, c are distinct but sum to 9?\n\nSuppose so. Let me assume S=9 and see if there exists a solution with a, b, c distinct.\n\nLet me suppose S=9,\n\nThen, from the equations:\n\na + b + c =9,\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² =a +6,\n\nLet me try to solve this system.\n\nFrom a + b + c =9,\n\nWe can write c =9 -a -b,\n\nThen, substitute into the other equations.\n\nFirst equation: a² = b +6 ⇒ b = a² -6,\n\nSecond equation: b² = c +6 ⇒ c = b² -6,\n\nBut c is also 9 -a -b,\n\nThus,\n\nb² -6 =9 -a -b,\n\nSo,\n\nb² + b + a -15 =0,\n\nBut since b =a² -6,\n\nSubstitute b =a² -6 into the above equation:\n\n(a² -6)^2 + (a² -6) +a -15 =0,\n\nExpand (a² -6)^2:\n\na^4 -12a² +36,\n\nThus,\n\na^4 -12a² +36 + a² -6 +a -15 =0,\n\nSimplify:\n\na^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nSo we have quartic equation: a^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nHmm, let me see if this factors.\n\nTry possible rational roots: ±1, ±3, ±5, ±15.\n\nTest a=1: 1 -11 +1 +15 =6 ≠0,\n\na=-1: 1 -11 -1 +15=4 ≠0,\n\na=3: 81 - 99 +3 +15=0,\n\n81-99= -18, -18+3= -15, -15+15=0,\n\nHey! a=3 is a root.\n\nTherefore, (a -3) is a factor.\n\nDivide the quartic by (a -3):\n\nUsing synthetic division,\n\nCoefficients: 1 (a^4), 0 (a^3), -11(a²),1(a),15(constant).\n\nDivide by (a -3), so root at 3.\n\nBring down 1,\n\nMultiply 1 by3=3, add to next coefficient: 0+3=3,\n\nMultiply 3 by3=9, add to next coefficient: -11+9=-2,\n\nMultiply -2 by3= -6, add to next coefficient:1 + (-6)= -5,\n\nMultiply -5 by3= -15, add to last term:15 + (-15)=0,\n\nThus, the quartic factors as:\n\n(a -3)(a^3 +3a² -2a -5).\n\nNow, factor the cubic: a^3 +3a² -2a -5.\n\nTry rational roots: ±1, ±5.\n\nTest a=1:1 +3 -2 -5= -3≠0,\n\na=-1: -1 +3 +2 -5= -1≠0,\n\na=5:125 +75 -10 -5=185≠0,\n\na=-5: -125 +75 +10 -5= -45≠0,\n\nThus, no rational roots. So the cubic is irreducible over rationals. Therefore, the quartic factors as (a -3)(cubic), so real roots at a=3 and the roots of the cubic.\n\nIf a=3, then b =a² -6=9-6=3,\n\nThen c=9 -a -b=9 -3 -3=3,\n\nSo a=b=c=3, which is the equal triple, which is excluded.\n\nThe other roots of the quartic (from the cubic) would give other a's leading to distinct a, b, c?\n\nSuppose a is a root of the cubic a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0,\n\nThen b =a² -6,\n\nc=9 -a -b =9 -a -(a² -6)=15 -a -a²,\n\nThen, need to check if c² =a +6,\n\nCompute c²:\n\nc =15 -a -a²,\n\nc² = (15 -a -a²)^2,\n\nExpand that:\n\n=225 -30a -30a² +a² + 2a³ +a^4,\n\nWait, step by step:\n\nLet me compute ( -a² -a +15 )²,\n\n= ( -a² -a +15 )*( -a² -a +15 )\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\nFirst, -a²*(-a²) = a^4,\n\n- a²*(-a) = a^3,\n\n- a²*(15) = -15a²,\n\nThen, -a*(-a²)=a^3,\n\n- a*(-a)=a²,\n\n- a*(15)= -15a,\n\nThen,15*(-a²)= -15a²,\n\n15*(-a)= -15a,\n\n15*15=225,\n\nNow, combine all terms:\n\na^4,\n\na^3 +a^3 =2a^3,\n\n-15a² +a² -15a² = (-15 +1 -15)a² = (-29a²),\n\n-15a -15a =-30a,\n\n+225,\n\nTherefore,\n\nc² =a^4 +2a^3 -29a² -30a +225,\n\nNow, set this equal to a +6,\n\nThus,\n\na^4 +2a^3 -29a² -30a +225 =a +6,\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\na^4 +2a^3 -29a² -31a +219 =0,\n\nBut since a is a root of the cubic a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0,\n\nWe can use this to reduce the quartic equation.\n\nLet me denote the cubic equation as:\n\na^3 = -3a² +2a +5,\n\nThus, a^3 = -3a² +2a +5,\n\nThen, a^4 = a*(a^3) =a*(-3a² +2a +5)= -3a^3 +2a² +5a,\n\nBut substitute a^3 again:\n\n= -3*(-3a² +2a +5) +2a² +5a,\n\n=9a² -6a -15 +2a² +5a,\n\n=11a² -a -15,\n\nThus, a^4 =11a² -a -15,\n\nNow, substitute a^4 and a^3 into the quartic equation:\n\na^4 +2a^3 -29a² -31a +219,\n\nReplace a^4 with 11a² -a -15,\n\nand a^3 with -3a² +2a +5,\n\nThus,\n\n(11a² -a -15) +2*(-3a² +2a +5) -29a² -31a +219,\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n11a² -a -15,\n\n+2*(-3a²)= -6a², +2*(2a)=4a, +2*5=10,\n\nSo,\n\n11a² -a -15 -6a² +4a +10,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(11a² -6a²)=5a²,\n\n(-a +4a)=3a,\n\n(-15 +10)= -5,\n\nThen,\n\n5a² +3a -5 -29a² -31a +219,\n\nCombine terms:\n\n5a² -29a²= -24a²,\n\n3a -31a= -28a,\n\n-5 +219=214,\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\n-24a² -28a +214,\n\nSet equal to zero:\n\n-24a² -28a +214 =0,\n\nDivide both sides by -2:\n\n12a² +14a -107=0,\n\nNow, solving this quadratic equation for a:\n\na = [-14 ±√(14² -4*12*(-107))]/(2*12),\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n196 + 4*12*107,\n\n4*12=48,\n\n48*107=5136,\n\nThus discriminant=196 +5136=5332,\n\n√5332,\n\nHmm, 5332 divided by 4 is 1333, which is not a perfect square. So irrational roots.\n\nThus, the equation 12a² +14a -107=0 has solutions, but they are not nice, but real.\n\nTherefore, for each real root a of the cubic, we can compute b and c, and they will satisfy c² =a +6, but since a is real and not 3, then b and c are real numbers different from a?\n\nWait, but since the cubic equation a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0 has one real root and two complex roots?\n\nWait, the cubic equation with real coefficients must have at least one real root. Let me check approximate values.\n\nLet me compute f(a)=a^3 +3a² -2a -5,\n\nAt a=1:1 +3 -2 -5= -3,\n\na=2:8 +12 -4 -5=11,\n\nSo there's a real root between 1 and 2.\n\nAt a=1.5: 3.375 +6.75 -3 -5=2.125,\n\nStill positive,\n\nAt a=1.3: 2.197 + 5.07 -2.6 -5≈-0.333,\n\nSo between 1.3 and 1.5,\n\nAt a=1.4: 2.744 + 5.88 -2.8 -5≈1.824,\n\nHmm, wait, that can't be. Wait:\n\nWait, compute f(1.3):\n\n1.3³ +3*(1.3)^2 -2*(1.3) -5,\n\n1.3³ = 2.197,\n\n3*(1.69)=5.07,\n\n-2*1.3= -2.6,\n\nSo total:2.197 +5.07=7.267; 7.267 -2.6=4.667; 4.667 -5= -0.333,\n\nAt a=1.4:\n\n1.4³ = 2.744,\n\n3*(1.96)=5.88,\n\n-2*(1.4)= -2.8,\n\nTotal:2.744 +5.88=8.624; 8.624 -2.8=5.824; 5.824 -5=0.824,\n\nSo between a=1.3 and 1.4, f(a) crosses zero.\n\nThus, there is a real root between 1.3 and 1.4,\n\nSimilarly, as a approaches -infty, f(a) approaches -infty,\n\nAt a=-4: (-64) + 48 - (-8) -5= -64+48+8-5= -13,\n\nAt a=-3: -27 +27 -(-6) -5= -27+27+6-5=1,\n\nSo between a=-4 and a=-3, f(a) crosses from -13 to 1, so another real root between -4 and -3,\n\nAnd since it's a cubic, there must be three real roots or one real and two complex.\n\nWait, but f(-5)= -125 +75 - (-10) -5= -125+75+10-5= -45,\n\nf(-4)= -64 +48 -(-8) -5= -64+48+8-5= -13,\n\nf(-3)= -27+27 -(-6) -5= -27+27+6-5=1,\n\nf(-2)= -8 +12 -(-4) -5= -8+12+4-5=3,\n\nf(-1)= -1 +3 -(-2) -5= -1+3+2-5= -1,\n\nf(0)=0 +0 -0 -5= -5,\n\nf(1)= -3,\n\nf(2)=11,\n\nSo, the function crosses zero between a=-4 and -3,\n\nbetween a=-3 and -2 (since f(-3)=1, f(-2)=3),\n\nWait, no, between a=-4 and -3: f(-4)=-13, f(-3)=1,\n\nso one real root there,\n\nBetween a=-3 and a=-2: f(-3)=1, f(-2)=3, so no crossing,\n\nBetween a=-2 and a=-1: f(-2)=3, f(-1)=-1,\n\nSo another root between a=-2 and -1,\n\nBetween a=-1 and a=0: f(-1)=-1, f(0)=-5,\n\nNo crossing,\n\nBetween a=0 and a=1: f(0)=-5, f(1)=-3,\n\nNo crossing,\n\nBetween a=1 and a=2: f(1)=-3, f(2)=11,\n\nThird real root between 1 and 2,\n\nThus, three real roots. So all roots of the cubic are real.\n\nTherefore, for each real root a of the cubic, we can compute b and c,\n\nand since a is not 3, then b and c will be different from a?\n\nLet me take an approximate root, say a≈1.35,\n\nThen b =a² -6≈1.8225 -6≈-4.1775,\n\nc=9 -a -b≈9 -1.35 -(-4.1775)=9 -1.35 +4.1775≈11.8275,\n\nThen check c² ≈ (11.8275)^2≈140. So c²≈140,\n\na +6≈1.35+6=7.35,\n\nWait, that's not equal. Hmm, discrepancy here. Wait, perhaps my approximation is too rough.\n\nWait, actually, if a is a root of the cubic, then c should satisfy c² =a +6,\n\nWait, but in our substitution above, we had that if a is a root of the cubic, then c is defined as 9 -a -b,\n\nand b =a² -6,\n\nso c =9 -a -(a² -6)=15 -a -a²,\n\nThen, c² should equal a +6,\n\nBut when I computed with approximate a≈1.35,\n\nc≈15 -1.35 - (1.35)^2≈15 -1.35 -1.8225≈11.8275,\n\nthen c²≈(11.8275)^2≈140.0,\n\nand a +6≈7.35,\n\nwhich is not equal. So my mistake must be in the substitution.\n\nWait, actually, when we set S=9, and derived the equation for a, we had:\n\nStarting from S=9,\n\nWe set c=9 -a -b,\n\nThen, substituted into the second equation to get an equation in a,\n\nwhich led to the quartic equation,\n\nand then found that a=3 is a root leading to the equal triple,\n\nbut for other roots a of the quartic,\n\nthey should satisfy c² =a +6,\n\nBut my calculation shows inconsistency, which suggests an error in derivation?\n\nWait, let me check the substitution steps again.\n\nStarting with S=9,\n\na + b + c =9,\n\nFrom first equation: b =a² -6,\n\nSecond equation: c =b² -6,\n\nThird equation: c² =a +6,\n\nSo, substituting b and c in terms of a,\n\nc = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nThen, from the sum equation,\n\na + (a² -6) + [(a² -6)^2 -6] =9,\n\nCompute this:\n\na + a² -6 + (a^4 -12a² +36) -6 =9,\n\nSimplify:\n\na +a² -6 +a^4 -12a² +36 -6,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\na^4,\n\na² -12a² = -11a²,\n\na,\n\nConstants: -6 +36 -6=24,\n\nThus,\n\na^4 -11a² +a +24 =9,\n\nSo,\n\na^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nAh! Wait, here's the mistake. Earlier, I had written the quartic as a^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nBut in my previous calculation when I substituted, I thought I had a^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nBut when I later tried to compute with an approximate root, I must have miscalculated.\n\nWait, so the quartic equation is a^4 -11a² +a +15=0,\n\nThen, when I factored it as (a-3)(a^3 +3a² -2a -5)=0,\n\nWait let's confirm that:\n\nMultiply (a -3)(a^3 +3a² -2a -5):\n\n= a*(a^3 +3a² -2a -5) -3*(a^3 +3a² -2a -5),\n\n= a^4 +3a^3 -2a² -5a -3a^3 -9a² +6a +15,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\na^4,\n\n3a^3 -3a^3 =0,\n\n-2a² -9a² =-11a²,\n\n-5a +6a =a,\n\n+15,\n\nThus, gives a^4 -11a² +a +15,\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo the quartic is correctly factored.\n\nTherefore, the cubic factor is a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0,\n\nNow, if a is a root of this cubic,\n\nthen c = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nand then c² should equal a +6,\n\nLet me take an approximate root a≈1.35,\n\nCompute b =a² -6≈(1.8225) -6≈-4.1775,\n\nc=(b)^2 -6≈(-4.1775)^2 -6≈17.45 -6≈11.45,\n\nThen c²≈(11.45)^2≈131.1,\n\nBut a +6≈1.35 +6≈7.35,\n\nHmm, not equal. So discrepancy.\n\nWait, but according to the equations, if a is a root of the quartic, then c is defined as 9 -a -b,\n\nWait, but in the original system, c is defined as b² -6,\n\nso there are two different expressions for c,\n\nWait a minute! There's a contradiction here.\n\nWait, no, actually, when we set S=9,\n\nwe have three equations:\n\n1. a + b + c =9,\n\n2. b =a² -6,\n\n3. c =b² -6,\n\nTherefore, c is both equal to b² -6 and to 9 -a -b,\n\nso the equation c =b² -6 =9 -a -b,\n\nwhich is how we arrived at the quartic equation.\n\nTherefore, if a is a root of the quartic (other than a=3),\n\nthen c must satisfy both c =b² -6 and c =9 -a -b,\n\nand also c² =a +6,\n\nTherefore, if a is a root of the quartic, then by construction, c =b² -6 and c =9 -a -b,\n\nso those are consistent,\n\nbut then the third equation c² =a +6 must also hold,\n\nwhich is why we had to derive the quartic equation,\n\nso in reality, the quartic equation is the result of enforcing all three original equations with S=9,\n\nso any root a of the quartic (other than a=3) should satisfy c² =a +6,\n\nbut in my numerical example, it didn't,\n\nlikely due to the approximation error.\n\nLet me take a better approximation.\n\nLet me find a better approximation for the real root of the cubic a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0 between 1.3 and 1.4,\n\nLet me use Newton-Raphson method.\n\nLet f(a)=a³ +3a² -2a -5,\n\nf(1.3)= (2.197) + 3*(1.69) -2*(1.3) -5 ≈2.197 +5.07 -2.6 -5≈-0.333,\n\nf(1.35)= (1.35)^3 +3*(1.35)^2 -2*(1.35) -5,\n\n1.35^3=2.460,\n\n3*(1.8225)=5.4675,\n\n-2*(1.35)= -2.7,\n\nSo total:2.460 +5.4675=7.9275; 7.9275 -2.7=5.2275; 5.2275 -5=0.2275,\n\nThus f(1.35)=≈0.2275,\n\nf(1.3)=≈-0.333,\n\nSo between a=1.3 and 1.35,\n\nWe can approximate:\n\nLet me take a0=1.3,\n\nf(a0)= -0.333,\n\nf’(a)=3a² +6a -2,\n\nf’(1.3)=3*(1.69)+6*(1.3) -2≈5.07+7.8-2≈10.87,\n\nNext iteration:\n\na1 = a0 - f(a0)/f’(a0)=1.3 - (-0.333)/10.87≈1.3 +0.0306≈1.3306,\n\nCompute f(1.3306):\n\na=1.3306,\n\na³≈(1.33)^3≈2.352,\n\n3a²≈3*(1.7689)=5.3067,\n\n-2a≈-2.6612,\n\nTotal:2.352 +5.3067=7.6587; 7.6587 -2.6612≈4.9975; 4.9975 -5≈-0.0025,\n\nWow, very close.\n\nSo f(1.3306)≈-0.0025,\n\nf’(1.3306)=3*(1.3306)^2 +6*(1.3306) -2,\n\nCompute 1.3306²≈1.771,\n\n3*1.771≈5.313,\n\n6*1.3306≈7.9836,\n\nThus f’≈5.313 +7.9836 -2≈11.2966,\n\nNext iteration:\n\na2 = a1 - f(a1)/f’(a1)=1.3306 - (-0.0025)/11.2966≈1.3306 +0.00022≈1.3308,\n\nCompute f(1.3308):\n\na=1.3308,\n\na³≈(1.3308)^3≈1.3308*1.771≈2.356,\n\n3a²≈3*(1.771)=5.313,\n\n-2a≈-2.6616,\n\nTotal:2.356 +5.313≈7.669; 7.669 -2.6616≈5.0074; 5.0074 -5≈0.0074,\n\nWait, that's moving away. Hmm, maybe calculation error.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps better to accept that a≈1.3306,\n\nThus, with a≈1.3306,\n\nthen b =a² -6≈(1.771) -6≈-4.229,\n\nthen c=9 -a -b≈9 -1.3306 -(-4.229)=9 -1.3306 +4.229≈11.8984,\n\nthen c²≈(11.8984)^2≈141.57,\n\nand a +6≈1.3306 +6≈7.3306,\n\nWait, that's not equal. Hmm, so something is wrong.\n\nWait a second, this inconsistency suggests that even though a satisfies the quartic equation derived under the assumption S=9,\n\nit does not satisfy the third equation c² =a +6,\n\nwhich means my derivation might have an error.\n\nWait, let's go back.\n\nWhen I set S=9,\n\nI used the three equations:\n\n1. a + b + c =9,\n\n2. b =a² -6,\n\n3. c =b² -6,\n\nand substituted into each other to get a quartic equation,\n\nbut the third original equation is c² =a +6,\n\nwhich should be automatically satisfied if a satisfies the quartic?\n\nWait, let's see.\n\nWe have:\n\nFrom the quartic equation derived, we have:\n\na^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nBut the third equation is c² =a +6,\n\nBut c =b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nThus,\n\nc =a^4 -12a² +36 -6 =a^4 -12a² +30,\n\nThus,\n\nc² = (a^4 -12a² +30)^2,\n\nWhich should equal a +6,\n\nThus,\n\n(a^4 -12a² +30)^2 =a +6,\n\nBut the quartic equation is a^4 -11a² +a +15 =0,\n\nThese are different equations.\n\nWait a second! I think I see the mistake.\n\nWhen I derived the quartic equation for S=9,\n\nI used the first two equations and the sum equation,\n\nbut didn't include the third equation c² =a +6,\n\nThus the quartic equation only ensures that a, b, c sum to 9 and satisfy the first two equations,\n\nbut does not guarantee that the third equation holds.\n\nTherefore, to fully satisfy all three equations, we need to also impose c² =a +6,\n\nwhich gives an additional equation,\n\nhence the need for the eighth-degree equation.\n\nTherefore, when I set S=9 and derived the quartic, it only ensures that a, b, c sum to 9 and satisfy the first two equations, but not the third,\n\nso the solutions of the quartic are not necessarily solutions of the original system.\n\nTherefore, my mistake was in assuming that solving for S=9 with the first two equations and the sum would suffice, but we also need the third equation to hold.\n\nTherefore, the only solution with S=9 is the trivial one with a=b=c=3,\n\nwhich is excluded,\n\nand the other roots of the quartic do not satisfy the third equation,\n\nso they are extraneous,\n\ntherefore S=9 cannot be achieved with distinct a, b, c.\n\nSimilarly, for S=-6,\n\nthe only solution is the triple (-2, -2, -2),\n\nwhich is excluded,\n\nso S=-6 is invalid.\n\nTherefore, the remaining possible S values are the four from the quartic in S: namely,\n\nS = [ -1 ±√17 ] /2,\n\nand S =1 ±√26,\n\nbut since the problem says \"two possible values\", perhaps two of these four are actually the same when considering complex numbers?\n\nWait, but they are all distinct real numbers,\n\nWait, perhaps when considering complex solutions, some of these S values might not actually be achievable because the system might require certain conditions?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed such that despite the quartic having four real roots, only two of them correspond to valid triples with distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is from a competition where the answer is the two integer values 9 and -6, but since those are excluded, but the problem didn't specify that the numbers have to be real, so maybe even though the real solutions for S=9 and S=-6 only give the equal triples, there are complex solutions with those S values where a, b, c are distinct?\n\nHmm, that's possible.\n\nLet me consider S=9 again,\n\nSuppose we allow complex numbers,\n\nthen the cubic equation a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0 has three roots (one real and two complex),\n\nfor each complex root a,\n\nwe can compute b and c,\n\nand then check if c² =a +6,\n\nbut since we derived the quartic equation under the assumption that c² =a +6,\n\nthen those roots should satisfy it,\n\nbut my numerical approximation failed because I used a real root which didn't satisfy the third equation,\n\nbut perhaps the complex roots do?\n\nLet me attempt to compute symbolically.\n\nLet me suppose a is a complex root of the cubic a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0,\n\nthen,\n\nc = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nand we need to check whether c² =a +6,\n\nLet me compute c² - (a +6) and see if it's zero,\n\ngiven that a satisfies the cubic equation.\n\nCompute c = (a² -6)^2 -6 =a^4 -12a² +30,\n\nThen,\n\nc² = (a^4 -12a² +30)^2,\n\nWe need to show this equals a +6,\n\nBut given that a^3 = -3a² +2a +5,\n\nwe can reduce higher powers of a.\n\nFirst compute a^4 =a *a^3 =a*(-3a² +2a +5)= -3a^3 +2a² +5a,\n\nBut substitute a^3 =-3a² +2a +5,\n\nThus,\n\na^4 =-3*(-3a² +2a +5) +2a² +5a =9a² -6a -15 +2a² +5a =11a² -a -15,\n\nThus, a^4 =11a² -a -15,\n\nNow compute a^4 -12a² +30,\n\n= (11a² -a -15) -12a² +30 = -a² -a +15,\n\nTherefore, c = -a² -a +15,\n\nThus, c = -a² -a +15,\n\nNow compute c²:\n\nc² = (-a² -a +15)^2,\n\nExpand:\n\n= ( -a² -a +15 )^2,\n\n= (a² +a -15)^2,\n\n= a^4 + 2a^3 -29a² -30a +225,\n\nWait, let me expand step by step:\n\nFirst, (-a² -a +15)^2,\n\n= [ (-a²) + (-a) +15 ]^2,\n\n= (-a²)^2 + (-a)^2 +15^2 + 2*(-a²)(-a) + 2*(-a²)*15 + 2*(-a)*15,\n\n= a^4 +a² +225 + 2a^3 -30a² -30a,\n\nThus,\n\n= a^4 +2a^3 -29a² -30a +225,\n\nNow, substitute a^4=11a² -a -15,\n\nand a^3= -3a² +2a +5,\n\nThus,\n\na^4 +2a^3 -29a² -30a +225,\n\n= (11a² -a -15) + 2*(-3a² +2a +5) -29a² -30a +225,\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n11a² -a -15,\n\n+2*(-3a²)= -6a²,\n\n+2*(2a)=4a,\n\n+2*5=10,\n\nSo,\n\n11a² -a -15 -6a² +4a +10,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n11a² -6a² =5a²,\n\n- a +4a =3a,\n\n-15 +10 =-5,\n\nThus,\n\n5a² +3a -5,\n\nThen,\n\n-29a² -30a +225,\n\nAdding the remaining terms:\n\n5a² +3a -5 -29a² -30a +225,\n\nCombine terms:\n\n5a² -29a² =-24a²,\n\n3a -30a =-27a,\n\n-5 +225=220,\n\nThus,\n\n-24a² -27a +220,\n\nTherefore,\n\nc² = -24a² -27a +220,\n\nNow, set this equal to a +6,\n\nThus,\n\n-24a² -27a +220 =a +6,\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n-24a² -27a +220 -a -6=0,\n\nSimplify:\n\n-24a² -28a +214=0,\n\nMultiply by -1:\n\n24a² +28a -214=0,\n\nDivide by 2:\n\n12a² +14a -107=0,\n\nNow, solving for a,\n\na = [-14 ±√(14² -4*12*(-107))]/(2*12),\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n196 + 5136=5332,\n\n√5332= let's see, 73²=5329, so √5332≈73.02,\n\nThus,\n\na≈[-14 ±73.02]/24,\n\nThus,\n\na≈(59.02)/24≈2.46,\n\nor a≈(-87.02)/24≈-3.62,\n\nWait, but a was supposed to be a root of the cubic equation a^3 +3a² -2a -5=0,\n\nwhich has roots approximately at 1.33, and two others,\n\nso this suggests inconsistency,\n\nmeaning that even with complex a,\n\nthe equation c² =a +6 does not hold,\n\nwhich implies that my initial assumption that S=9 is possible is wrong,\n\nexcept for the triple solution,\n\nTherefore, the only valid S values are the other four,\n\nbut since the problem states there are two possible values,\n\nperhaps the quartic in S had two real roots and two complex roots,\n\nbut since the problem allows complex numbers,\n\nthen all four roots are valid,\n\nbut the question says \"compute the two possible values\",\n\nperhaps I made a mistake in the factorization?\n\nWait, going back to the sixth-degree equation in S,\n\nwe had:\n\n(T +6)(T -9)(T² + T -4)(T² -2T -25)=0,\n\nwhere T=S,\n\nThus, the roots are T=-6,9, and roots of T²+T-4=0 and T²-2T-25=0,\n\nThe quadratic T² +T -4=0 has roots,\n\nT=(-1 ±√(1 +16))/2=(-1±√17)/2,\n\nand T²-2T-25=0 has roots,\n\nT=(2±√(4 +100))/2=(2±√104)/2=1±(√104)/2=1±(2√26)/2=1±√26,\n\nThus, the four roots from the quadratics are real,\n\nso all six roots of the sixth-degree equation are real,\n\nso the four non-excluded S values are real,\n\nso why does the problem say \"two possible values\"?\n\nMaybe because when considering the system's symmetry,\n\neach S value corresponds to two different triples (a, b, c) which are cyclic permutations,\n\nbut the sum S remains the same,\n\nso the distinct S values are the four,\n\nbut the problem might have a typo,\n\nor perhaps I made a mistake in the earlier steps.\n\nWait, let me recall the original problem statement:\n\n\"Compute the two possible values of a + b + c.\"\n\nGiven that a, b, c are pairwise distinct complex numbers satisfying those equations.\n\nPerhaps in the complex plane, even though there are four real S values,\n\nbut some of them are complex conjugates or something,\n\nbut no, all four are real.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is from an Olympiad where only integer answers are expected,\n\nand among the four real roots,\n\ntwo are integers,\n\nbut none of them are integers except possibly S=9 and S=-6,\n\nwhich are excluded,\n\nso this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my initial approach was wrong,\n\nand there is a simpler way.\n\nLet me try to assume that a + b + c = k,\n\nand try to find k,\n\nusing the three equations:\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nLet me add them:\n\na² + b² + c² = a + b + c +18,\n\nSo,\n\na² + b² + c² =k +18,\n\nAlso,\n\nWe can compute (a + b + c)^2 =k² =a² +b² +c² +2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nThus,\n\nk² = (k +18) + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nTherefore,\n\nab + bc + ca=(k² -k -18)/2,\n\nNow,\n\nLet me multiply the three original equations:\n\n(a²)(b²)(c²)=(b +6)(c +6)(a +6),\n\nWhich is (abc)^2=(a +6)(b +6)(c +6),\n\nLet me denote abc = m,\n\nthen,\n\nm²=(a +6)(b +6)(c +6),\n\nExpand the right-hand side:\n\n(a +6)(b +6)(c +6)=abc +6(ab + bc + ca)+36(a + b + c)+216,\n\nThus,\n\nm² = m +6*( (k² -k -18)/2 ) +36k +216,\n\nSimplify:\n\nm² =m +3(k² -k -18) +36k +216,\n\n= m +3k² -3k -54 +36k +216,\n\n= m +3k² +33k +162,\n\nThus,\n\nm² -m -3k² -33k -162=0,\n\nNow, we need another equation involving m and k,\n\nPerhaps we can find m in terms of k.\n\nLet me consider multiplying the three original equations:\n\na² =b +6 ⇒ b =a² -6,\n\nb² =c +6 ⇒ c =b² -6,\n\nc² =a +6 ⇒ a =c² -6,\n\nThus,\n\na = (b² -6)^2 -6,\n\nBut b =a² -6,\n\nThus,\n\na = [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 -6,\n\nWhich is the eighth-degree equation,\n\nBut this seems not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, express m =abc,\n\nExpress in terms of a,\n\nb =a² -6,\n\nc = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nThus,\n\nm =a*(a² -6)*[(a² -6)^2 -6],\n\nThis is a function of a,\n\nBut unless we can relate it to k,\n\nit might not help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express m in terms of k,\n\nbut I don't see a straightforward way.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that the system has a symmetry such that a, b, c are roots of the equation x³ -kx² + px - q =0,\n\nand using the relationships between the coefficients,\n\nbut this might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the equations are cyclic,\n\nso perhaps assume that a, b, c satisfy the same quadratic relation,\n\nbut since they are linked cyclically,\n\nmaybe consider that a, b, c are roots of x³ -kx² + qx -p =0,\n\nand from the equations,\n\na² -b =6,\n\nb² -c =6,\n\nc² -a =6,\n\nSo,\n\na² =b +6,\n\nb² =c +6,\n\nc² =a +6,\n\nLet me subtract the first equation from the second,\n\nb² -a² =c -b,\n\nFactor left side: (b -a)(b +a) =c -b,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nFrom second and third,\n\nc² -b² =a -c,\n\nFactor: (c -b)(c +b)=a -c,\n\nFrom third and first,\n\na² -c² =b -a,\n\nFactor: (a -c)(a +c)=b -a,\n\nHmm, so we have three equations:\n\n1. (b -a)(b +a) =c -b,\n\n2. (c -b)(c +b)=a -c,\n\n3. (a -c)(a +c)=b -a,\n\nLet me denote the differences:\n\nLet me set x = b - a,\n\ny = c - b,\n\nz = a - c,\n\nNote that x + y + z =0,\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\nx*(b +a) = y,\n\nSecond equation,\n\ny*(c + b)= z,\n\nThird equation,\n\nz*(a + c)=x,\n\nBut since x + y + z=0,\n\nwe can write z= -x -y,\n\nSubstitute into third equation,\n\n(-x -y)*(a + c)=x,\n\nBut this seems complicated.\n\nAlternatively, express each equation in terms of x, y, z,\n\nBut this might not lead anywhere.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider dividing the first equation by the second equation,\n\n[(b -a)(b +a)] / [(c -b)(c +b)] = (c -b)/(a -c),\n\nWait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, from equation 1:\n\n(b -a)(b +a) =c -b ⇒ (b -a)(b +a) + (b -c)=0,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nFrom equation 2:\n\n(c -b)(c +b) + (c -a)=0,\n\nFrom equation 3:\n\n(a -c)(a +c) + (a -b)=0,\n\nThis might not help.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that all differences are non-zero because a,b,c are distinct,\n\nso we can divide by them,\n\nFrom equation 1:\n\n(b -a)(b +a) =c -b ⇒ (b +a) = (c -b)/(b -a),\n\nSimilarly,\n\nFrom equation 2,\n\n(c +b)= (a -c)/(c -b),\n\nFrom equation 3,\n\n(a +c)= (b -a)/(a -c),\n\nMultiply all three equations together,\n\n(b +a)(c +b)(a +c) = [(c -b)/(b -a)] * [(a -c)/(c -b)] * [(b -a)/(a -c)],\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\nThe numerators: (c -b)*(a -c)*(b -a),\n\nDenominators: (b -a)*(c -b)*(a -c),\n\nThus,\n\nThe RHS simplifies to [ (c -b)(a -c)(b -a) ] / [ (b -a)(c -b)(a -c) ) ] =1,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(b +a)(c +b)(a +c)=1,\n\nInteresting!\n\nSo we have,\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(c + a)=1,\n\nThis is a key equation.\n\nAdditionally, we have the earlier equations:\n\na² + b² + c² =k +18,\n\nand\n\n(a + b + c)^2 =k² = (k +18) + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nAlso, we have:\n\nFrom the product above,\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(c + a)=1,\n\nExpand this product,\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(c + a),\n\nFirst, expand two terms:\n\n(a + b)(b + c) =ab +a c +b² +b c,\n\nThen multiply by (c +a):\n\n= [ab +ac +b² + bc]*(c +a),\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\nab*c +ab*a +ac*c +ac*a +b²*c +b²*a + bc*c + bc*a,\n\n=abc +a²b +a c² +a²c +b²c +a b² +b c² +