and ca individually?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express ab + bc + ca in terms of S and other expressions?\n\nWait, let me see if we can find another relation. Let's think about multiplying the equations together?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express each variable in terms of the next and then multiply them?\n\nWait, let me think step by step.\n\nWe have the three original equations:\n\n1) a² = b + 6,\n\n2) b² = c + 6,\n\n3) c² = a + 6.\n\nLet me try to express each variable in terms of the next variable as I did before:\n\nFrom 1): b = a² - 6,\n\nFrom 2): c = b² - 6,\n\nFrom 3): a = c² - 6.\n\nSo substituting each into the next, we can get a in terms of a, but as I saw earlier, this leads to a very high-degree equation. Maybe instead of going all the way, let me see if I can find a relationship between S and P?\n\nAlternatively, let's compute ab + bc + ca.\n\nLet me see: ab + bc + ca. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider multiplying the three original equations together?\n\nLeft-hand sides: a² * b² * c² = (a b c)^2,\n\nRight-hand sides: (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6).\n\nHmm, so (a b c)^2 = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6).\n\nHmm, that might be a bit complicated, but maybe we can write it as:\n\nLet me denote abc = k, so then (k)^2 = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6). Hmm, but unless we can express (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) in terms of S and P or something?\n\nAlternatively, expand the right-hand side:\n\n(b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) = (b c a) + 6(b c + b a + c a) + 36(b + c + a) + 216.\n\nWait, let me check that expansion step by step.\n\nLet me denote (b + 6)(c + 6) first:\n\n(b + 6)(c + 6) = b c + 6b + 6c + 36.\n\nThen, multiply that by (a + 6):\n\n[ b c + 6b + 6c + 36 ] * (a + 6) =\n\n= b c * a + b c * 6 + 6b * a + 6b * 6 + 6c * a + 6c * 6 + 36 * a + 36 * 6\n\n= a b c + 6 b c + 6 a b + 36 b + 6 a c + 36 c + 36 a + 216.\n\nNow, let me group like terms:\n\nTerms with a b c: a b c,\n\nTerms with a b: 6 a b,\n\nTerms with a c: 6 a c,\n\nTerms with b c: 6 b c,\n\nTerms with a: 36 a,\n\nTerms with b: 36 b,\n\nTerms with c: 36 c,\n\nConstant term: 216.\n\nTherefore, altogether:\n\n(b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) = a b c + 6(a b + a c + b c) + 36(a + b + c) + 216.\n\nTherefore, going back to the earlier equation:\n\n(abc)^2 = [ (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) ]\n\nTherefore,\n\n(abc)^2 = abc + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36(a + b + c) + 216.\n\nHmm, so that's an equation involving abc and ab + bc + ca and a + b + c.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s let S = a + b + c,\n\nP = ab + bc + ca,\n\nQ = abc.\n\nThen the equation becomes:\n\nQ² = Q + 6P + 36S + 216.\n\nSo that's one equation involving Q, P, and S.\n\nEarlier, from the sum of the original equations, we had:\n\na² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand since a² + b² + c² = S² - 2P,\n\nso S² - 2P = S + 18,\n\nso rearranged: S² - S - 2P - 18 = 0,\n\nso that's equation (1): S² - S - 2P = 18.\n\nAnd now, from the product of the three equations, we have equation (2): Q² - Q - 6P - 36S - 216 = 0.\n\nHmm, so we have equation (1): S² - S - 2P = 18,\n\nand equation (2): Q² - Q - 6P - 36S = 216,\n\nbut we need a third equation to relate these variables S, P, Q.\n\nPerhaps we can find another relation involving S, P, Q?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can express Q in terms of S or something else?\n\nAlternatively, let's see if we can find expressions for Q = abc in terms of S and P?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the original equations again?\n\nWait, let me think. Let's see if we can find expressions for a, b, c individually?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the original equations to express ab + bc + ca?\n\nAlternatively, let's consider the original equations again:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nLet me try to compute ab + bc + ca.\n\nHmm. Let me see if I can express ab, bc, and ca in terms of a, b, c?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express each product in terms of the equations.\n\nFor example, from the first equation, b = a² - 6,\n\nso ab = a(a² - 6) = a³ - 6a,\n\nSimilarly, from the second equation, c = b² - 6,\n\nso bc = b(b² - 6) = b³ - 6b,\n\nand from the third equation, a = c² - 6,\n\nso ca = c(c² - 6) = c³ - 6c,\n\nTherefore, ab + bc + ca = (a³ - 6a) + (b³ - 6b) + (c³ - 6c)\n\n= (a³ + b³ + c³) - 6(a + b + c)\n\n= (a³ + b³ + c³) - 6S.\n\nHmm, okay, so ab + bc + ca = (a³ + b³ + c³) - 6S.\n\nBut now, we need to relate a³ + b³ + c³ to S, P, Q.\n\nRecall that there's a formula for a³ + b³ + c³:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = (a + b + c)^3 - 3(a + b + c)(ab + bc + ca) + 3abc.\n\nYes, that's a standard identity:\n\nx³ + y³ + z³ - 3xyz = (x + y + z)(x² + y² + z² - xy - yz - zx).\n\nSo rearranged, we have:\n\nx³ + y³ + z³ = 3xyz + (x + y + z)(x² + y² + z² - xy - yz - zx).\n\nAlternatively, another way to write it is:\n\nx³ + y³ + z³ = (x + y + z)^3 - 3(x + y + z)(xy + yz + zx) + 3xyz.\n\nYes, that's the identity.\n\nTherefore, in our case:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q.\n\nTherefore, substituting back into the expression for ab + bc + ca:\n\nab + bc + ca = [S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q] - 6 S\n\n= S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q - 6 S.\n\nSo, P = ab + bc + ca = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q - 6 S.\n\nHmm, so we can rearrange this equation to solve for P?\n\nWait, let me write that again:\n\nWe have:\n\nP = (a³ + b³ + c³) - 6S,\n\nand since a³ + b³ + c³ = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q,\n\nthen substituting:\n\nP = (S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q) - 6 S.\n\nTherefore,\n\nP = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q - 6 S.\n\nLet me bring all terms to one side:\n\nP - S³ + 3 S P - 3 Q + 6 S = 0,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(-S³) + (P + 3 S P) + 6 S - 3 Q = 0,\n\nFactor P terms:\n\n- S³ + P(1 + 3 S) + 6 S - 3 Q = 0.\n\nHmm, this seems a bit messy. Maybe instead, let's just rearrange the equation step by step.\n\nStarting again:\n\nP = S³ - 3 S P + 3 Q - 6 S.\n\nLet me move all terms to the left side except the terms involving P:\n\nP + 3 S P = S³ + 3 Q - 6 S,\n\nFactor P on the left:\n\nP (1 + 3 S) = S³ + 3 Q - 6 S.\n\nTherefore,\n\nP = [S³ + 3 Q - 6 S] / (1 + 3 S).\n\nHmm, okay, so now we have an expression for P in terms of S and Q.\n\nNow, recall from equation (1):\n\nS² - S - 2 P = 18,\n\nso we can write equation (1) as:\n\n2 P = S² - S - 18,\n\nso,\n\nP = (S² - S - 18)/2.\n\nTherefore, now we have two expressions for P:\n\nFirst expression: P = [S³ + 3 Q - 6 S] / (1 + 3 S),\n\nSecond expression: P = (S² - S - 18)/2.\n\nTherefore, we can set them equal:\n\n(S² - S - 18)/2 = [S³ + 3 Q - 6 S] / (1 + 3 S).\n\nHmm, so now we can solve for Q in terms of S?\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me write:\n\nMultiply both sides by 2(1 + 3 S) to eliminate denominators:\n\n(S² - S - 18)(1 + 3 S) = 2 [S³ + 3 Q - 6 S].\n\nLet me expand the left-hand side first:\n\n(S² - S - 18)(1 + 3 S).\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\nFirst, S² * 1 = S²,\n\nS² * 3 S = 3 S³,\n\n- S * 1 = - S,\n\n- S * 3 S = - 3 S²,\n\n-18 * 1 = -18,\n\n-18 * 3 S = -54 S.\n\nTherefore, adding all these terms together:\n\n3 S³ + S² - 3 S² - S - 54 S - 18.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n3 S³ + (S² - 3 S²) + (-S - 54 S) - 18\n\n= 3 S³ - 2 S² - 55 S - 18.\n\nSo the left-hand side is 3 S³ - 2 S² - 55 S - 18.\n\nThe right-hand side is 2 [S³ + 3 Q - 6 S] = 2 S³ + 6 Q - 12 S.\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n3 S³ - 2 S² - 55 S - 18 = 2 S³ + 6 Q - 12 S.\n\nLet me bring all terms to the left side:\n\n3 S³ - 2 S² - 55 S - 18 - 2 S³ - 6 Q + 12 S = 0.\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n(3 S³ - 2 S³) + (-2 S²) + (-55 S + 12 S) + (-18) + (-6 Q) = 0,\n\nWhich is:\n\nS³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18 - 6 Q = 0.\n\nTherefore,\n\nS³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18 - 6 Q = 0,\n\nLet me solve for Q:\n\n-6 Q = - S³ + 2 S² + 43 S + 18,\n\nMultiply both sides by (-1):\n\n6 Q = S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18,\n\nTherefore,\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6.\n\nHmm, so now we have Q expressed in terms of S.\n\nEarlier, from the product equation, equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q - 6 P - 36 S = 216.\n\nWait, let me recall equation (2):\n\nEarlier, we had:\n\nQ² = Q + 6 P + 36 S + 216,\n\nso rearranged:\n\nQ² - Q - 6 P - 36 S - 216 = 0.\n\nTherefore, equation (2): Q² - Q - 6 P - 36 S = 216.\n\nSo, we can substitute P and Q in terms of S into this equation.\n\nWe have:\n\nP = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nand\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6.\n\nSo, let's substitute these expressions into equation (2):\n\n[Q]^2 - Q - 6 [P] - 36 S = 216.\n\nTherefore,\n\nFirst, compute Q²:\n\nQ² = [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ]²,\n\nthen subtract Q: minus [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ],\n\nthen subtract 6P: minus 6*( (S² - S - 18)/2 ) = minus 3*(S² - S - 18),\n\nthen subtract 36 S,\n\nand set equal to 216.\n\nThis is going to be very algebra intensive, but let me try to proceed step by step.\n\nLet me denote Q as Q = [S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18]/6 for simplicity.\n\nFirst, compute Q²:\n\nQ² = [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 ] / 36.\n\nThen, the term -Q is - [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ].\n\nThe term -6P is -6 * [ (S² - S - 18)/2 ] = -3*(S² - S - 18).\n\nThen, -36 S is just -36 S.\n\nTherefore, putting all together:\n\n[ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 / 36 ] - [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ] - 3*(S² - S - 18) - 36 S = 216.\n\nThis equation is in terms of S only, but it's going to be a very high-degree equation. Let me see if I can multiply through by 36 to eliminate denominators.\n\nMultiply every term by 36:\n\nFirst term: [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 / 36 ] * 36 = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2,\n\nSecond term: - [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ] * 36 = -6*(S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18),\n\nThird term: -3*(S² - S - 18)*36 = -108*(S² - S - 18),\n\nFourth term: -36 S * 36 = -1296 S,\n\nRight-hand side: 216 * 36 = 7776.\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n(S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 - 6*(S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18) - 108*(S² - S - 18) - 1296 S = 7776.\n\nWow, okay. That's a massive equation, but let me try to simplify step by step.\n\nFirst, let me denote T = S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18, to make notation simpler.\n\nThen the equation is:\n\nT² - 6 T - 108*(S² - S - 18) - 1296 S = 7776.\n\nHmm, but even with substitution, it might not help much. Alternatively, perhaps I can expand T² and then collect like terms?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a better way. Since we are looking for possible values of S = a + b + c, which are two in number (as per the problem statement), perhaps S satisfies a quadratic equation? Since the problem says \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c\", implying that there are two real or complex numbers S that satisfy the conditions. Perhaps S is a root of a quadratic equation? That would make sense if the higher-degree terms somehow cancel out, leaving a quadratic in S.\n\nAlternatively, maybe after all this substitution, the equation reduces to a quadratic in S? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can assume that S is a root of some quadratic equation and work backwards?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a smarter substitution or approach altogether?\n\nWait a second. Let me think differently.\n\nSuppose we let S = a + b + c.\n\nWe have the equations:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nLet me try to compute a + b + c in terms of itself.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the system as a cyclic system. Since each variable is defined in terms of the next, perhaps we can consider that after three steps, we come back to the original variable?\n\nIndeed, starting with a, we have:\n\na → b = a² - 6,\n\nthen c = b² - 6,\n\nthen a = c² - 6.\n\nSo after three iterations of the function f(x) = x² - 6, we get back to a. Therefore, a is a periodic point of period 3 for the function f(x) = x² - 6, or possibly a fixed point (if period 1), but since the problem states that a, b, c are pairwise distinct, so it can't be a fixed point, so it must be a point of period 3.\n\nTherefore, the values of a, b, c are part of a 3-cycle of the function f(x) = x² - 6. So, to find such points, we can look for solutions to f(f(f(a))) = a, which is exactly the equation I had earlier: a = f(f(f(a))).\n\nTherefore, the equation to solve is f(f(f(a))) - a = 0, which, as I thought before, is a degree 64 polynomial equation. However, since we are only interested in the sum S = a + b + c, which is a + f(a) + f(f(a)), perhaps we can find S without solving for a, b, c individually?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps S satisfies a quadratic equation? Let me see.\n\nWait, maybe there's a trick here. Let me suppose that S = a + b + c is a root of a quadratic equation. Let me try to see if I can find such an equation.\n\nEarlier, I had:\n\nFrom equation (1): S² - S - 2P = 18,\n\nand from above, we had expressions for P and Q in terms of S.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can combine equations (1) and (2) with the expression for Q in terms of S.\n\nWait, equation (2): Q² - Q - 6 P - 36 S = 216,\n\nand we have expressions for Q and P in terms of S:\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6,\n\nand P = (S² - S - 18)/2.\n\nSo perhaps plug these into equation (2):\n\nLet me write equation (2) again:\n\nQ² - Q - 6 P - 36 S = 216.\n\nLet me substitute Q and P:\n\nFirst, compute Q²:\n\nQ² = [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ]²,\n\nthen subtract Q: minus [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ],\n\nthen subtract 6P: minus 6*( (S² - S - 18)/2 ) = minus 3*(S² - S - 18),\n\nthen subtract 36 S,\n\nand set equal to 216.\n\nSo, putting all together:\n\n[ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 ] / 36 - [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ] - 3(S² - S - 18) - 36 S = 216.\n\nThis is exactly the same equation as before when multiplied by 36. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps let me make a substitution for S. Let me suppose that S is a solution to a quadratic equation, say S² + m S + n = 0, then perhaps plugging that into the equation might lead to some cancellation?\n\nAlternatively, maybe try small integer values for S and see if they satisfy the equation? Since the problem says there are two possible values, maybe they are integers or simple fractions?\n\nLet me try to guess possible S values.\n\nSuppose S is an integer. Let me see if I can find integer values for S that might satisfy the equations.\n\nFirst, let me recall that from equation (1):\n\nS² - S - 2P = 18,\n\nand since P = ab + bc + ca,\n\nand since a, b, c are complex numbers, but perhaps S could still be an integer?\n\nAlternatively, let's see if S can be 6? Let me try S = 6.\n\nIf S = 6,\n\nthen from equation (1):\n\n6² - 6 - 2P = 18 ⇒ 36 - 6 - 2P = 18 ⇒ 30 - 2P = 18 ⇒ -2P = -12 ⇒ P = 6.\n\nThen, from the expression for Q:\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6,\n\nplugging S = 6:\n\nQ = (216 - 2*36 - 43*6 - 18)/6,\n\nCalculate numerator:\n\n216 - 72 - 258 - 18 = 216 - 72 = 144; 144 - 258 = -114; -114 -18 = -132,\n\nSo Q = -132 / 6 = -22.\n\nNow, let's check equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q - 6 P - 36 S = ?\n\nQ² = (-22)^2 = 484,\n\n-Q = -(-22) = 22,\n\n-6 P = -6*6 = -36,\n\n-36 S = -36*6 = -216,\n\nAdding all together: 484 + 22 - 36 - 216 = (484 + 22) = 506; (506 - 36) = 470; (470 - 216) = 254.\n\nBut equation (2) requires this to equal 216. 254 ≠ 216. So S = 6 is not a solution.\n\nHmm, okay, next guess. How about S = 0?\n\nLet me try S = 0:\n\nEquation (1): 0 - 0 - 2P = 18 ⇒ -2P = 18 ⇒ P = -9.\n\nThen Q = (0 - 0 - 0 - 18)/6 = (-18)/6 = -3.\n\nThen check equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q - 6 P - 36 S = (-3)^2 - (-3) -6*(-9) - 0 = 9 + 3 + 54 = 66, which is not 216. Not good.\n\nHmm, S = 3?\n\nLet me try S = 3.\n\nEquation (1): 9 - 3 - 2P = 18 ⇒ 6 - 2P = 18 ⇒ -2P = 12 ⇒ P = -6.\n\nQ = (27 - 18 - 129 - 18)/6. Wait, let me compute numerator:\n\nS³ = 27,\n\n-2 S² = -2*(9) = -18,\n\n-43 S = -43*3 = -129,\n\n-18,\n\nso total numerator: 27 - 18 - 129 -18 = (27 - 18) = 9; (9 - 129) = -120; (-120 -18) = -138,\n\nThus Q = -138 / 6 = -23.\n\nThen equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q -6 P -36 S = (-23)^2 - (-23) -6*(-6) -36*(3)\n\n= 529 +23 +36 - 108\n\n= (529 +23) = 552; (552 +36)=588; (588 - 108)=480. Not 216. Nope.\n\nHmm. S = -3?\n\nTry S = -3.\n\nEquation (1): (-3)^2 - (-3) -2P = 18 ⇒ 9 +3 -2P = 18 ⇒ 12 -2P =18 ⇒ -2P =6 ⇒ P = -3.\n\nThen Q = [ (-27) - 2*(9) -43*(-3) -18 ] /6,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n-27 - 18 + 129 -18,\n\n-27 -18 = -45; -45 +129 =84; 84 -18=66,\n\nSo Q = 66 /6 = 11.\n\nNow check equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q -6 P -36 S = 121 -11 -6*(-3) -36*(-3)\n\n= 110 +18 +108 = 110+18=128; 128 +108=236. Not 216. Close but no.\n\nHmm. Maybe S = 4?\n\nLet me try S = 4:\n\nEquation (1): 16 -4 -2P =18 ⇒12 -2P =18 ⇒ -2P =6 ⇒ P = -3.\n\nQ = (64 - 32 - 172 -18)/6,\n\nCompute numerator: 64 -32 =32; 32 -172 =-140; -140 -18 =-158,\n\nThus Q = -158 /6 ≈ -26.333... Not integer. Let's see equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q -6*(-3) -36*4,\n\nWait, let me compute exactly:\n\nQ = -158/6 = -79/3,\n\nSo Q² = (79/3)^2 = 6241/9,\n\n-Q = 79/3,\n\n-6 P = -6*(-3)=18,\n\n-36 S = -36*4 = -144,\n\nSo total:\n\n6241/9 + 79/3 + 18 -144.\n\nConvert all to ninths:\n\n6241/9 + (79/3)*(3/3)=237/9 + (18)*(9/9)=162/9 + (-144)*(9/9)= -1296/9,\n\nSo total: [6241 + 237 + 162 - 1296]/9,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n6241 + 237 = 6478,\n\n6478 + 162 = 6640,\n\n6640 - 1296 = 5344,\n\nSo 5344 /9 ≈ 594.222..., which is not 216. Not good.\n\nHmm, maybe S = 9?\n\nS = 9:\n\nEquation (1): 81 -9 -2P =18 ⇒72 -2P=18 ⇒ -2P= -54 ⇒ P=27.\n\nQ = (729 - 2*81 -43*9 -18)/6,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n729 -162 - 387 -18,\n\n729 -162 = 567,\n\n567 -387 =180,\n\n180 -18 =162,\n\nThus Q = 162 /6 =27.\n\nNow equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q -6 P -36 S = 27² -27 -6*27 -36*9,\n\n= 729 -27 -162 -324,\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n729 -27 =702,\n\n702 -162 =540,\n\n540 -324 =216.\n\nHey! That works! So when S = 9,\n\nEquation (2) gives 216 = 216, which is correct.\n\nSo S = 9 is a solution.\n\nAlright, that's one value. Let me check equation (1) to confirm.\n\nWith S =9, equation (1):\n\nS² - S -2P =18,\n\nWe had P =27,\n\nSo 81 -9 -54 = 18,\n\n81 -9 =72; 72 -54 =18. Correct.\n\nGood. So S=9 is a valid solution.\n\nNow, let's see if there is another solution. Let me try S = something else. Maybe S = -6?\n\nLet me try S = -6.\n\nEquation (1): (-6)^2 - (-6) -2P = 18 ⇒36 +6 -2P=18 ⇒42 -2P=18 ⇒-2P= -24 ⇒ P=12.\n\nThen Q = [ (-6)^3 - 2*(-6)^2 -43*(-6) -18 ] /6,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n-216 - 2*(36) - (-258) -18,\n\n= -216 -72 +258 -18,\n\n-216 -72 = -288,\n\n-288 +258 = -30,\n\n-30 -18 = -48,\n\nThus Q = -48 /6 = -8.\n\nNow check equation (2):\n\nQ² - Q -6 P -36 S = (-8)^2 - (-8) -6*(12) -36*(-6),\n\n=64 +8 -72 +216,\n\n64 +8 =72; 72 -72 =0; 0 +216=216. Perfect! So S = -6 also satisfies equation (2).\n\nTherefore, S = -6 is another solution.\n\nTherefore, the two possible values for S = a + b + c are 9 and -6.\n\nWait, so that's it? Let me confirm.\n\nWe tried S = 9 and S = -6, and both satisfy equation (2). Also, they satisfy equation (1) as we checked for S =9 and S = -6.\n\nTherefore, since the problem states that a, b, c are pairwise distinct complex numbers, so we need to ensure that these solutions for S correspond to such triples (a, b, c) where a, b, c are distinct.\n\nTherefore, we need to check whether for S=9 and S=-6, the corresponding a, b, c are indeed distinct.\n\nAlternatively, since the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so perhaps both solutions S=9 and S=-6 are valid, each corresponding to a set of distinct a, b, c.\n\nTherefore, the two possible values of a + b + c are 9 and -6.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{9} and \\boxed{-6}, but in the problem statement it says \"list the two values in a comma-separated list of two valid \\LaTeX expressions\", so probably inside a single box as \\boxed{9}, \\boxed{-6} or maybe \\boxed{9} and \\boxed{-6}, but the standard way is to have them in one box separated by commas.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"Compute the two possible values of a+b+c. In your answer, list the two values in a comma-separated list of two valid \\LaTeX expressions.\"\n\nHmm, so probably they want the answer as \\boxed{9}, \\boxed{-6} but in one box? Wait, maybe like \\boxed{9} and \\boxed{-6} but the instructions say \"a comma-separated list of two valid \\LaTeX expressions\". So perhaps inside a single box: \\boxed{9, -6}\n\nYes, that seems likely.\n\nLet me confirm that S = 9 and S = -6 are indeed possible, and that the corresponding systems have distinct a, b, c.\n\nFirst, let me consider S = 9.\n\nIf S = 9, then from equation (1):\n\nP = (S² - S - 18)/2 = (81 - 9 - 18)/2 = (54)/2 = 27.\n\nSo ab + bc + ca = 27.\n\nAnd Q = abc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 = (729 - 2*81 -43*9 -18)/6,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n729 - 162 - 387 - 18,\n\n729 - 162 = 567,\n\n567 - 387 = 180,\n\n180 - 18 = 162,\n\nSo Q = 162 /6 = 27.\n\nSo abc = 27.\n\nTherefore, we have S = 9, P =27, Q=27.\n\nTherefore, the cubic equation with roots a, b, c is x³ - S x² + P x - Q = 0,\n\nSo x³ -9 x² +27 x -27 =0.\n\nLet me factor this cubic equation.\n\nTry rational roots: possible roots are factors of 27 over 1, so ±1, ±3, ±9, ±27.\n\nTesting x=3:\n\n3³ -9*(3)^2 +27*3 -27 = 27 -81 +81 -27 = 0. Yes, x=3 is a root.\n\nTherefore, factor as (x - 3)(x² -6x +9) = (x - 3)(x - 3)^2 = (x - 3)^3.\n\nTherefore, the equation has a triple root at x=3. But the problem states that a, b, c are pairwise distinct. Therefore, this would not be acceptable, since all three roots are the same. Hmm, that's a problem.\n\nWait, so if S=9 leads to a triple root at 3, which would mean a = b = c = 3, but the problem requires them to be pairwise distinct. Therefore, S=9 might not be a valid solution? Contradiction?\n\nHmm, so that suggests that even though S=9 satisfies the equations, it does not correspond to a valid solution with distinct a, b, c. Therefore, S=9 must be rejected?\n\nWait, but let me think again. The cubic equation x³ -9x² +27x -27 =0 factors as (x - 3)^3 =0, so indeed, all roots are 3. But in the problem statement, a, b, c must be pairwise distinct. Therefore, this solution is invalid. So even though algebraically S=9 satisfies the equations, it does not correspond to a valid solution with distinct a, b, c. Therefore, S=9 is extraneous?\n\nHmm, that complicates things. So then maybe S=-6 is the only valid solution?\n\nWait, let me check S=-6.\n\nCompute for S = -6:\n\nFirst, equation (1):\n\nP = (S² - S -18)/2 = (36 - (-6) -18)/2 = (36 +6 -18)/2 = (24)/2 =12.\n\nQ = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6,\n\nPlugging S=-6:\n\n(-216) - 2*(36) -43*(-6) -18,\n\n= -216 -72 +258 -18,\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n-216 -72 = -288,\n\n-288 +258 = -30,\n\n-30 -18 = -48,\n\nThus Q = -48 /6 = -8.\n\nTherefore, the cubic equation with roots a, b, c is x³ - S x² + P x - Q = x³ - (-6)x² +12x - (-8) = x³ +6x² +12x +8 =0.\n\nLet me factor this cubic equation.\n\nTry possible rational roots: ±1, ±2, ±4, ±8.\n\nTest x = -2:\n\n(-2)^3 +6*(-2)^2 +12*(-2) +8 = -8 + 24 -24 +8 = 0. Yes, x=-2 is a root.\n\nTherefore, factor as (x + 2)(x² +4x +4) = (x + 2)(x + 2)^2 = (x + 2)^3.\n\nWait, so again, the cubic factors as (x + 2)^3, so triple root at x = -2. Again, all roots are the same, which would mean a = b = c = -2, but they need to be pairwise distinct. So that's also invalid?\n\nWait, this is a problem. Both S=9 and S=-6 lead to triple roots, which would mean that a, b, c are all equal, but the problem states they must be pairwise distinct. So neither of these solutions is valid?\n\nHmm, so that suggests that perhaps there are other solutions for S which are not roots of the quadratic equation we derived? Wait, but we derived that S must satisfy that big equation, which reduced to when we plugged in S=9 and S=-6, but those led to triple roots.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there are other roots for S which are not integers? Maybe quadratic solutions?\n\nWait a minute. Let me think. The problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\". So even though in the cases of S=9 and S=-6, the variables collapse to a triple root, but in complex numbers, maybe there are other solutions where the roots are distinct?\n\nWait, but when we derived the equation for S, we used the conditions given by the problem, which included that a, b, c are distinct. Wait, but in the algebra above, when we derived the equation for S, we didn't use the distinctness condition. Therefore, the equation for S includes solutions where a, b, c may not be distinct, so we have to discard those solutions where a, b, c are not distinct.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the equation for S is of higher degree, and S=9 and S=-6 are roots, but there are other roots which correspond to distinct a, b, c?\n\nWait, but when I plugged in S=9 and S=-6 into the big equation (the one after multiplying by 36), they worked, but those corresponded to triple roots. Therefore, perhaps the other roots of that big equation correspond to cases where a, b, c are distinct?\n\nTherefore, perhaps the equation for S is of degree higher than 2, and the quadratic factor (S -9)(S +6) is part of it, but there are other factors?\n\nWait, but when I assumed S was an integer and found S=9 and S=-6, but perhaps the full equation for S is actually a quadratic? Wait, but when we had substituted everything, we ended up with an equation that, when S=9 and S=-6 satisfy it, but perhaps the equation is quadratic? Wait, but when I tried S=9 and S=-6, both worked, but in reality, the equation was of higher degree. Let me see.\n\nWait, let's recall that when we set up the equations, we had:\n\nAfter substituting everything, we ended up with an equation that S must satisfy, which when multiplied out was a degree 6 equation? Wait, no, let me think:\n\nWait, let me recall that Q was expressed in terms of S, and then plugged into equation (2), which involved Q squared. Since Q is a cubic in S, Q squared is a sixth-degree polynomial in S, so the entire equation is a sixth-degree polynomial equation in S. Therefore, the equation we obtained is of degree 6, so there are six roots (counting multiplicity) for S. However, when we tested S=9 and S=-6, they were roots, but each led to triple roots in a,b,c, so those correspond to multiple roots in the S equation? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the equation factors into (S -9)(S +6)(quartic) =0, where the quartic may have roots corresponding to distinct a,b,c?\n\nTherefore, perhaps the two values we need are 9 and -6, but since they correspond to triples with repeated roots, which are invalid, so maybe the actual valid solutions are the other roots of the equation?\n\nBut then, the problem says \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c\", implying that there are exactly two, but maybe in the complex numbers, even with the distinctness condition, there are only two possible sums? Or perhaps despite the triple roots, the problem allows considering them as valid? Wait, the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so triple roots are invalid. Therefore, maybe the only valid solutions are the other roots of that sixth-degree equation?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in assuming that S must satisfy that equation? Wait, let me recap.\n\nWe started with the three original equations, and through substitutions and using symmetric sums, we derived that S must satisfy that big equation, which when simplified, after substituting expressions for P and Q in terms of S, led to an equation that S must satisfy. However, in the process, we might have introduced extraneous solutions where a, b, c are not distinct. So even though S=9 and S=-6 satisfy the equation, they don't correspond to solutions with distinct a, b, c, so we must discard them and look for other roots.\n\nBut the problem states \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so we have to find S such that there exists distinct a,b,c satisfying the original equations and summing to S. Therefore, perhaps the only possible S values are the ones from the quartic factor?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another approach to this problem that avoids getting into such a high-degree equation.\n\nWait, let me think again. Suppose we let a, b, c be distinct complex numbers satisfying those three equations. Then, from the equations:\n\na² - b = 6,\n\nb² - c = 6,\n\nc² - a = 6.\n\nLet me write this as a system:\n\na² - b = 6,\n\nb² - c = 6,\n\nc² - a = 6.\n\nLet me try to consider this as a cyclic system and try to find a relationship.\n\nLet me subtract the first equation from the second equation:\n\n(b² - c) - (a² - b) = 0,\n\nSo b² - c - a² + b =0,\n\nRearranged: b² - a² + b - c =0,\n\nFactor b² - a²: (b - a)(b + a) + (b - c) =0.\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe express each variable in terms of the previous and substitute cyclically.\n\nWe have:\n\nFrom first equation: b = a² -6,\n\nSecond equation: c = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nThird equation: a = c² -6 = [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 -6.\n\nTherefore, the equation is a = f(f(f(a))), where f(x) = x² -6.\n\nTherefore, the solutions for a are the fixed points of the third iterate of f, excluding fixed points of f itself (since if a were a fixed point, then b = a, c = a, so they wouldn't be distinct). Therefore, the solutions we are seeking are the 3-cycles of the function f(x) = x² -6.\n\nNow, in complex dynamics, a 3-cycle would correspond to solutions where applying f three times brings you back to the original value, but not before. So, the equation f(f(f(a))) = a would include fixed points (period 1) and period 3 points, as well as points with periods dividing 3, like period 1 or 3.\n\nTherefore, to get the period-3 points, we need to factor out the fixed points.\n\nFirst, find the fixed points of f(x): f(x) = x,\n\nSo x² -6 =x ⇒x² -x -6 =0 ⇒x = [1 ±√(1 +24)]/2 = [1 ±5]/2 ⇒x=3 or x=-2.\n\nTherefore, the fixed points are x=3 and x=-2. These correspond to the solutions we found earlier where S=9 and S=-6, which led to all variables being 3 or all being -2. Since those are fixed points, they don't give us distinct a, b, c.\n\nTherefore, the other solutions of f(f(f(a))) =a correspond to period-3 cycles, which would give us three distinct values a, b, c (each being f of the previous), and then the sum S = a + b + c would be the sum of the three elements in the cycle.\n\nTherefore, the equation f(f(f(a))) =a is of degree 8 (since each application of f is degree 2, so f^3 is degree 8), so the equation f(f(f(a))) -a =0 is an eighth-degree equation, which would have 8 roots (counting multiplicity), including the two fixed points (each with multiplicity 1?), and the six period-3 points (each cycle contributing three roots, but since they form a cycle, they come in groups of three? Wait, no, actually each period-3 cycle has three distinct points, so the equation would have three roots for each cycle, but since cycles are disjoint, the total number would be 3 times the number of distinct cycles plus the fixed points.\n\nWait, perhaps better to think that the equation f^3(a) =a has degree 8, so 8 roots in total (in the complex plane), counting multiplicities. Two of them are the fixed points (3 and -2), so the remaining 6 roots must form two period-3 cycles (each cycle has 3 distinct points, so two cycles would account for 6 roots). Therefore, the two possible sums S would be the sums of each of the two period-3 cycles.\n\nTherefore, the two possible values of a + b + c correspond to the sums of the two distinct period-3 cycles. Therefore, those sums are the two values we need to find.\n\nTherefore, to find these sums, perhaps we can factor the equation f^3(a) -a =0 as (f(a) -a)(something) =0, and then the \"something\" would be a degree 6 polynomial whose roots are the period-3 points, and then the sum of the roots of that degree 6 polynomial would be related to the coefficients.\n\nWait, but actually, the sum of the roots of the entire equation f^3(a) -a =0 is equal to the negative coefficient of a^7 divided by the leading coefficient.\n\nLet me compute the equation f^3(a) - a =0, and find its coefficients to get the sum of all roots.\n\nFirst, let's compute f(x) =x² -6.\n\nCompute f(f(x)): f(f(x)) = f(x² -6) = (x² -6)^2 -6 = x⁴ -12x² +36 -6 = x⁴ -12x² +30.\n\nThen f(f(f(x))) = f(f(f(x))) = f(x⁴ -12x² +30) = [ (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 ] -6.\n\nLet me compute that:\n\nLet me denote y = x⁴ -12x² +30,\n\nthen f(y) = y² -6,\n\nso f(f(f(x))) = (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 -6.\n\nTherefore, the equation f^3(a) -a =0 is:\n\n(x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 -6 -x =0.\n\nLet me expand (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2:\n\nFirst, (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 = (x⁴)^2 + (-12x²)^2 + (30)^2 + 2*(x⁴*(-12x²) + x⁴*30 + (-12x²)*30)\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n(x⁴)^2 = x^8,\n\n(-12x²)^2 = 144x^4,\n\n(30)^2 = 900,\n\nCross terms:\n\n2*(x⁴*(-12x²)) = 2*(-12)x^{6} = -24x^6,\n\n2*(x⁴*30) = 2*30 x^4 = 60x^4,\n\n2*(-12x²*30) = 2*(-360)x² = -720x².\n\nTherefore, altogether:\n\n(x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 = x^8 -24x^6 + (144x^4 +60x^4) + (-720x²) +900,\n\nSimplify:\n\nx^8 -24x^6 + 204x^4 -720x² +900.\n\nTherefore, f^3(x) -x = [x^8 -24x^6 + 204x^4 -720x² +900] -x = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x +900.\n\nThus, the equation is:\n\nx^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x +900 =0.\n\nThis is an eighth-degree polynomial equation.\n\nWe know that x=3 and x=-2 are roots (the fixed points), so let's factor those out.\n\nLet me perform polynomial division or use synthetic division to factor out (x -3) and (x +2).\n\nFirst, let's factor out (x - 3). Let me use synthetic division for x=3.\n\nThe polynomial is:\n\nx^8 + 0x^7 -24x^6 +0x^5 +204x^4 +0x^3 -720x^2 -x +900.\n\nWait, writing all coefficients explicitly:\n\nDegree 8: 1,\n\nDegree 7: 0,\n\nDegree 6: -24,\n\nDegree 5: 0,\n\nDegree 4: 204,\n\nDegree 3: 0,\n\nDegree 2: -720,\n\nDegree 1: -1,\n\nDegree 0: 900.\n\nSo the coefficients are: [1, 0, -24, 0, 204, 0, -720, -1, 900].\n\nDivide this polynomial by (x - 3). Using synthetic division with root 3:\n\nSet up coefficients: 1, 0, -24, 0, 204, 0, -720, -1, 900.\n\nBring down the 1.\n\nMultiply by 3: 1*3=3. Add to next coefficient: 0 +3=3.\n\nMultiply by 3: 3*3=9. Add to next coefficient: -24 +9= -15.\n\nMultiply by 3: -15*3= -45. Add to next coefficient: 0 + (-45)= -45.\n\nMultiply by 3: -45*3= -135. Add to next coefficient: 204 + (-135)=69.\n\nMultiply by 3: 69*3=207. Add to next coefficient: 0 +207=207.\n\nMultiply by 3: 207*3=621. Add to next coefficient: -720 +621= -99.\n\nMultiply by 3: -99*3= -297. Add to next coefficient: -1 + (-297)= -298.\n\nMultiply by 3: -298*3= -894. Add to last coefficient: 900 + (-894)=6.\n\nSo the remainder is 6, not zero. Wait, that's a problem. Wait, but we know that x=3 is a root, so remainder should be zero. Did I make a mistake in calculation?\n\nLet me check the synthetic division steps again.\n\nStarting with coefficients: 1, 0, -24, 0, 204, 0, -720, -1, 900.\n\nRoot: 3.\n\nStart with 1.\n\nMultiply by 3: 1*3 = 3. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 +3 =3.\n\nMultiply by 3: 3*3=9. Add to next coefficient (-24): -24 +9 = -15.\n\nMultiply by 3: -15*3 = -45. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 + (-45)= -45.\n\nMultiply by 3: -45*3= -135. Add to next coefficient (204): 204 + (-135)= 69.\n\nMultiply by 3: 69*3=207. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 +207=207.\n\nMultiply by 3: 207*3=621. Add to next coefficient (-720): -720 +621= -99.\n\nMultiply by 3: -99*3= -297. Add to next coefficient (-1): -1 + (-297)= -298.\n\nMultiply by 3: -298*3= -894. Add to last coefficient (900): 900 + (-894)=6.\n\nHmm, the remainder is 6, not zero. That suggests that x=3 is not a root? But we know from before that when S=9, which came from a=3, b=3, c=3, which would satisfy the original equations, but they are not distinct. Wait, but in the equation f^3(a) -a=0, substituting a=3:\n\nf(f(f(3))) -3.\n\nCompute f(3)=3² -6=9-6=3,\n\nthen f(f(3))=f(3)=3,\n\nthen f(f(f(3)))=3,\n\nso 3 -3=0. So a=3 is indeed a root. Therefore, I must have made a mistake in the synthetic division.\n\nWait, let me check the coefficients again. The polynomial is f^3(x) -x, which we computed as x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x +900.\n\nWait, let me confirm the expansion of (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2:\n\nFirst term: (x⁴)^2 = x^8,\n\nSecond term: 2*(x⁴)*(-12x²) = -24x^6,\n\nThird term: 2*(x⁴)*(30) + (-12x²)^2 = 60x^4 + 144x^4 = 204x^4,\n\nFourth term: 2*(-12x²)*(30) + ... Wait, actually, when expanding (A + B + C)^2 where A =x⁴, B= -12x², C=30,\n\nit's A² + B² + C² + 2AB + 2AC + 2BC,\n\nso:\n\nA² =x^8,\n\nB²= ( -12x² )²=144x^4,\n\nC²=900,\n\n2AB=2*(x⁴)(-12x²)= -24x^6,\n\n2AC=2*(x⁴)(30)=60x^4,\n\n2BC=2*(-12x²)(30)= -720x²,\n\nTherefore, altogether:\n\nx^8 -24x^6 + (144x^4 +60x^4) + (-720x²) +900,\n\nso x^8 -24x^6 + 204x^4 -720x² +900,\n\nthen f^3(x) -x = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900 -x,\n\nso the coefficients are correct: x^8 term:1, x^7:0, x^6:-24, x^5:0, x^4:204, x^3:0, x^2:-720, x^1:-1, constant term:900.\n\nTherefore, the coefficients are correct.\n\nHmm, so why does synthetic division with x=3 give remainder 6?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an arithmetic error in the synthetic division steps.\n\nLet me do the synthetic division again step by step.\n\nCoefficients: 1 (x^8), 0 (x^7), -24 (x^6), 0 (x^5), 204 (x^4), 0 (x^3), -720 (x^2), -1 (x), 900 (constant).\n\nRoot: 3.\n\nStart with 1.\n\nBring down the 1.\n\nMultiply 1 by 3: 3. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 + 3 = 3.\n\nMultiply 3 by 3: 9. Add to next coefficient (-24): -24 + 9 = -15.\n\nMultiply -15 by 3: -45. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 + (-45) = -45.\n\nMultiply -45 by 3: -135. Add to next coefficient (204): 204 + (-135) = 69.\n\nMultiply 69 by 3: 207. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 + 207 = 207.\n\nMultiply 207 by 3: 621. Add to next coefficient (-720): -720 + 621 = -99.\n\nMultiply -99 by 3: -297. Add to next coefficient (-1): -1 + (-297) = -298.\n\nMultiply -298 by 3: -894. Add to next coefficient (900): 900 + (-894) = 6.\n\nSame result. Hmm. That's odd because x=3 is supposed to be a root.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in forming the polynomial f^3(x) -x?\n\nWait, let me compute f^3(3) - 3:\n\nf(3) = 3² -6 = 9 -6 = 3,\n\nf(f(3)) = f(3) = 3,\n\nf(f(f(3))) = f(3) = 3,\n\nso f^3(3) -3 = 3 -3 =0. So x=3 is a root. Therefore, there must be an error in the polynomial expression.\n\nWait, let me recalculate f^3(x):\n\nFirst, f(x) =x² -6,\n\nf(f(x)) = (x² -6)^2 -6 = x⁴ -12x² +36 -6 =x⁴ -12x² +30,\n\nthen f(f(f(x))) = f(x⁴ -12x² +30) = (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 -6,\n\nthen f^3(x) -x = (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 -6 -x.\n\nWait, so when I expanded (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2, I got x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900,\n\nthen subtract 6 and subtract x,\n\nso that would be x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900 -6 -x,\n\nwhich is x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x + 894.\n\nWait a second! I think I made a mistake here. The expansion of (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 is x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900,\n\nthen f(f(f(x))) = that expression minus 6,\n\nso f^3(x) = (x⁴ -12x² +30)^2 -6 = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900 -6 = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +894,\n\nthen f^3(x) -x = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +894 -x,\n\ntherefore the constant term is 894, not 900.\n\nAh! There's the mistake! I forgot to subtract the 6 when forming f^3(x). Therefore, the constant term should be 900 -6 = 894, not 900.\n\nSimilarly, the coefficient for x is -1 (from the -x term). So the correct polynomial is:\n\nx^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x +894.\n\nTherefore, the coefficients are:\n\nx^8: 1,\n\nx^7: 0,\n\nx^6: -24,\n\nx^5:0,\n\nx^4:204,\n\nx^3:0,\n\nx^2:-720,\n\nx^1: -1,\n\nconstant term:894.\n\nOkay, so now redo the synthetic division with this corrected polynomial.\n\nCoefficients: [1, 0, -24, 0, 204, 0, -720, -1, 894].\n\nDivide by (x -3):\n\nStart with 1.\n\nMultiply by 3: 1*3=3. Add to next coefficient (0): 0+3=3.\n\nMultiply by3: 3*3=9. Add to next coefficient (-24): -24 +9= -15.\n\nMultiply by3: -15*3= -45. Add to next coefficient (0): 0 + (-45)= -45.\n\nMultiply by3: -45*3= -135. Add to next coefficient (204): 204 + (-135)=69.\n\nMultiply by3:69*3=207. Add to next coefficient (0):0+207=207.\n\nMultiply by3:207*3=621. Add to next coefficient (-720): -720 +621= -99.\n\nMultiply by3: -99*3= -297. Add to next coefficient (-1): -1 + (-297)= -298.\n\nMultiply by3: -298*3= -894. Add to last coefficient (894): 894 + (-894)=0. Perfect, no remainder.\n\nSo the result of the division is:\n\nThe quotient polynomial is:\n\nx^7 + 3x^6 -15x^5 -45x^4 +69x^3 +207x^2 -99x -298,\n\nand the remainder is 0.\n\nTherefore, (x -3) is a factor.\n\nNow, we can factor out (x -3) from the eighth-degree polynomial, leaving a seventh-degree polynomial. Now, let's check if x = -2 is also a root of the seventh-degree polynomial.\n\nLet me plug x = -2 into the quotient polynomial:\n\nLet me denote the quotient polynomial as Q(x) = x^7 + 3x^6 -15x^5 -45x^4 +69x^3 +207x^2 -99x -298.\n\nCompute Q(-2):\n\n(-2)^7 +3*(-2)^6 -15*(-2)^5 -45*(-2)^4 +69*(-2)^3 +207*(-2)^2 -99*(-2) -298.\n\nCalculate term by term:\n\n(-2)^7 = -128,\n\n3*(-2)^6 = 3*(64) = 192,\n\n-15*(-2)^5 = -15*(-32) = +480,\n\n-45*(-2)^4 = -45*(16) = -720,\n\n69*(-2)^3 = 69*(-8) = -552,\n\n207*(-2)^2 = 207*(4) = 828,\n\n-99*(-2) = +198,\n\n-298 remains.\n\nNow, add them all up:\n\nStart with -128 +192 = 64,\n\n64 +480 = 544,\n\n544 -720 = -176,\n\n-176 -552 = -728,\n\n-728 +828 = 100,\n\n100 +198 = 298,\n\n298 -298 =0.\n\nSo Q(-2)=0, so (x +2) is also a factor.\n\nTherefore, we can factor (x +2) from the quotient polynomial Q(x).\n\nLet me perform synthetic division on Q(x) with root x = -2.\n\nThe coefficients of Q(x): [1, 3, -15, -45, 69, 207, -99, -298].\n\nDivide by (x +2), which is root x = -2.\n\nSet up synthetic division:\n\nCoefficients: 1, 3, -15, -45, 69, 207, -99, -298.\n\nRoot: -2.\n\nBring down the 1.\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient: 3 + (-2) =1.\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient: -15 + (-2) = -17.\n\nMultiply by -2: -17*(-2)=34. Add to next coefficient: -45 +34 = -11.\n\nMultiply by -2: -11*(-2)=22. Add to next coefficient: 69 +22=91.\n\nMultiply by -2:91*(-2)= -182. Add to next coefficient:207 + (-182)=25.\n\nMultiply by -2:25*(-2)= -50. Add to next coefficient: -99 + (-50)= -149.\n\nMultiply by -2: -149*(-2)=298. Add to last coefficient: -298 +298=0.\n\nTherefore, the quotient polynomial after dividing by (x +2) is:\n\nx^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nTherefore, the original eighth-degree polynomial factors as:\n\n(x -3)(x +2)(x^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149) =0.\n\nNow, we need to factor the sixth-degree polynomial further. Since we are looking for period-3 cycles, which correspond to roots that are not fixed points, the sixth-degree polynomial should factor into two cubic polynomials, each corresponding to a period-3 cycle.\n\nAssuming that, let me attempt to factor the sixth-degree polynomial into two cubics.\n\nLet me denote the sixth-degree polynomial as P(x) =x^6 +x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nSuppose it factors as (x^3 + a x^2 +b x +c)(x^3 +d x^2 +e x +f).\n\nMultiplying these out:\n\n= x^6 + (a + d)x^5 + (b + e + a d)x^4 + (c + a e + b d)x^3 + (a f + b e + c d)x^2 + (b f + c e)x + c f.\n\nSet equal to P(x):\n\nx^6 +x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nTherefore, equate coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of x^6: 1 =1, okay.\n\n2. Coefficient of x^5: a + d =1.\n\n3. Coefficient of x^4: b + e + a d = -17.\n\n4. Coefficient of x^3: c + a e + b d = -11.\n\n5. Coefficient of x^2: a f + b e + c d =91.\n\n6. Coefficient of x: b f + c e =25.\n\n7. Constant term: c f = -149.\n\nWe need to solve this system for integers a, b, c, d, e, f.\n\nGiven that the constant term c*f = -149. Since 149 is a prime number (yes, 149 is prime), so the possible integer pairs (c,f) are:\n\n(1, -149), (-1, 149), (149, -1), (-149, 1).\n\nLet me try these possibilities.\n\nFirst try c=1, f=-149:\n\nThen, equation 7: 1*(-149)= -149, which works.\n\nNow, equation 6: b*(-149) +1*e =25 ⇒ -149 b + e =25 ⇒ e =25 +149 b.\n\nEquation 2: a + d =1 ⇒ d=1 -a.\n\nEquation 3: b + e + a d = -17.\n\nSubstitute e=25 +149 b, d=1 -a:\n\nb + (25 +149 b) + a*(1 -a) = -17,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n( b +149 b ) +25 +a -a² = -17,\n\n150 b +25 +a -a² = -17,\n\nRearranged:\n\n- a² +a +150 b +25 +17 =0,\n\n- a² +a +150 b +42 =0,\n\nEquation 4: c + a e + b d = -11,\n\nc=1, e=25+149b, d=1 -a,\n\nso 1 + a*(25 +149b) + b*(1 -a) = -11,\n\nExpand:\n\n1 +25a +149a b +b -a b = -11,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n1 +25a + (149a b -a b) + b = -11,\n\n1 +25a +148a b +b = -11,\n\nRearranged:\n\n148a b +25a +b +1 +11 =0,\n\n148a b +25a +b +12 =0,\n\nEquation 5: a f + b e + c d =91,\n\na*(-149) + b*(25 +149b) +1*(1 -a) =91,\n\n-149a +25b +149b² +1 -a =91,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-150a +25b +149b² +1 =91,\n\n-150a +25b +149b² =90,\n\nSo now we have equations:\n\nFrom equation 3 rearranged: -a² +a +150 b +42 =0,\n\nFrom equation 4 rearranged:148a b +25a +b +12 =0,\n\nFrom equation 5 rearranged: -150a +25b +149b² =90,\n\nThis seems complicated, but maybe we can try small integer values for b.\n\nSince b is likely to be small, given the other coefficients.\n\nLet me suppose b=0,\n\nThen equation 3: -a² +a +0 +42 =0 ⇒ -a² +a +42=0 ⇒ a² -a -42=0 ⇒ discriminant=1 +168=169 ⇒a=(1±13)/2 ⇒a=7 or a=-6.\n\nCheck equation 4 with b=0:\n\n148*0*a +25a +0 +12 =25a +12=0 ⇒25a = -12 ⇒a= -12/25, not integer. Reject.\n\nNext, b=1:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*1 +42= -a² +a +192=0 ⇒a² -a -192=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +768=769, not a square. Not integer.\n\nb= -1:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*(-1) +42= -a² +a -150 +42= -a² +a -108=0 ⇒a² -a +108=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 -432= -431 <0, no real roots.\n\nb=2:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*2 +42= -a² +a +300 +42= -a² +a +342=0 ⇒a² -a -342=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +1368=1369=37²,\n\nThus a=(1 ±37)/2 ⇒ a=(38)/2=19 or a=(-36)/2=-18.\n\nCheck equation4 with b=2:\n\n148a*2 +25a +2 +12= (296a) +25a +14= 321a +14=0 ⇒321a =-14 ⇒a= -14/321, not integer.\n\nb= -2:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*(-2) +42= -a² +a -300 +42= -a² +a -258=0 ⇒a² -a +258=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 -1032= -1031 <0. No.\n\nb=3:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*3 +42= -a² +a +450 +42= -a² +a +492=0 ⇒a² -a -492=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +1968=1969, not a square (44²=1936, 45²=2025). No.\n\nb= -3:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*(-3)+42= -a² +a -450 +42= -a² +a -408=0 ⇒a² -a +408=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 -1632= negative.\n\nb=4:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*4 +42= -a² +a +600 +42= -a² +a +642=0 ⇒a² -a -642=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +2568=2569, not square (50²=2500, 51²=2601). No.\n\nb=5:\n\nEquation3: -a² +a +150*5 +42= -a² +a +750 +42= -a² +a +792=0 ⇒a² -a -792=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +3168=3169, which is 56.3… not square.\n\nHmm, this is not working. Let me try another pair for c and f.\n\nNext possible pair: c= -1, f=149.\n\nThen equation7: (-1)(149)= -149, which is okay.\n\nEquation6: b*(149) + (-1)*e =25 ⇒149b -e =25 ⇒e=149b -25.\n\nEquation2: a +d=1 ⇒d=1 -a.\n\nEquation3: b + e +a d = -17,\n\nSubstitute e=149b -25 and d=1 -a,\n\nso:\n\nb + (149b -25) + a*(1 -a) = -17,\n\nCombine terms:\n\n(1 +149)b -25 +a -a² = -17,\n\n150b -25 +a -a² = -17,\n\nRearranged:\n\n- a² +a +150b -25 +17=0,\n\n- a² +a +150b -8=0 ⇒ a² -a -150b +8=0.\n\nEquation4: c +a e +b d = -11,\n\nc=-1, e=149b -25, d=1 -a,\n\nso:\n\n-1 +a*(149b -25) +b*(1 -a) = -11,\n\nExpand:\n\n-1 +149a b -25a +b -a b = -11,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-1 -25a + (149a b -a b) +b = -11,\n\n-1 -25a +148a b +b = -11,\n\nRearranged:\n\n148a b -25a +b -1 +11=0,\n\n148a b -25a +b +10=0.\n\nEquation5: a f +b e +c d =91,\n\na*(149) +b*(149b -25) + (-1)*(1 -a) =91,\n\n149a +149b² -25b -1 +a =91,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(149a +a) +149b² -25b -1 =91,\n\n150a +149b² -25b -1 -91=0,\n\n150a +149b² -25b -92=0.\n\nNow, we have equations:\n\nFrom equation3 rearranged: a² -a -150b +8=0,\n\nFrom equation4 rearranged:148a b -25a +b +10=0,\n\nFrom equation5 rearranged:150a +149b² -25b -92=0.\n\nThis still looks complicated, but let's try small integer values for b.\n\nStart with b=0:\n\nEquation3: a² -a +8=0 ⇒ discriminant=1-32= -31. No real solution.\n\nb=1:\n\nEquation3: a² -a -150*1 +8= a² -a -142=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +568=569, not square.\n\nb= -1:\n\nEquation3: a² -a -150*(-1)+8= a² -a +150 +8= a² -a +158=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 -632= negative.\n\nb=2:\n\nEquation3: a² -a -300 +8= a² -a -292=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +1168=1169, not square (34²=1156, 35²=1225).\n\nb=3:\n\nEquation3: a² -a -450 +8= a² -a -442=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +1768=1769, not square.\n\nb=4:\n\nEquation3: a² -a -600 +8= a² -a -592=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +2368=2369, not square.\n\nb=5:\n\nEquation3: a² -a -750 +8= a² -a -742=0,\n\nDiscriminant=1 +2968=2969, not square.\n\nHmm, not promising. Let's try the next possible c,f pair.\n\nNext pair: c=149, f=-1.\n\nEquation7:149*(-1)= -149, okay.\n\nEquation6: b*(-1) +149*e =25 ⇒-b +149 e =25 ⇒149 e =25 +b ⇒e=(25 +b)/149.\n\nSince e must be integer (assuming integer coefficients), 149 divides (25 +b). Since 149 is prime, 25 +b must be multiple of 149. Let b =149k -25 for some integer k.\n\nPossible small k:\n\nk=1: b=149 -25=124,\n\nk=0: b= -25,\n\nk=-1: b= -149 -25= -174,\n\netc. These are large numbers, likely leading to large coefficients. Not promising.\n\nLast pair: c=-149, f=1.\n\nEquation7: (-149)(1)= -149, okay.\n\nEquation6: b*1 + (-149)*e =25 ⇒b -149 e =25 ⇒b=25 +149 e.\n\nEquation2: a +d=1 ⇒d=1 -a.\n\nEquation3: b + e +a d = -17,\n\nSubstitute b=25 +149 e and d=1 -a,\n\nso:\n\n25 +149 e + e +a*(1 -a)= -17,\n\n25 +150 e +a -a² = -17,\n\nRearranged:\n\n- a² +a +150 e +25 +17 =0,\n\n- a² +a +150 e +42=0 ⇒a² -a -150 e -42=0.\n\nEquation4: c +a e +b d = -11,\n\nc=-149, e=e, b=25+149 e, d=1 -a,\n\nso:\n\n-149 +a e + (25 +149 e)(1 -a) = -11,\n\nExpand the product:\n\n(25 +149 e)(1 -a) =25(1 -a) +149 e (1 -a)=25 -25a +149 e -149 a e,\n\nTherefore, the entire equation becomes:\n\n-149 +a e +25 -25a +149 e -149 a e = -11,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(-149 +25) + (-25a) + (a e +149 e -149 a e) + ... Wait, let me do step by step:\n\nTerms without a or e:\n\n-149 +25 = -124,\n\nTerms with a:\n\n-25a,\n\nTerms with e:\n\na e +149 e,\n\nTerms with a e:\n\n-149 a e,\n\nWait, actually:\n\nWait, let me re-express all terms:\n\n-149 + a e +25 -25a +149 e -149 a e,\n\nCombine constants: -149 +25 = -124,\n\nTerms with a: -25a,\n\nTerms with e: a e +149 e,\n\nTerms with a e: -149 a e,\n\nSo altogether:\n\n-124 -25a + (a e +149 e) -149 a e,\n\nFactor e terms:\n\n-124 -25a + e(a +149) -149 a e,\n\nHmm, this is getting too convoluted. Perhaps this trial and error isn't the best approach.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the complexity of factoring the sixth-degree polynomial, perhaps there is a better approach.\n\nAlternatively, since we are only interested in the sum S = a + b + c for the period-3 cycles, and since each period-3 cycle has three roots a, b, c such that a → b → c → a, then the sum S = a + b + c is the sum of the three roots of a cubic factor of the sixth-degree polynomial. Since the sixth-degree polynomial factors into two cubics, each cubic corresponding to a period-3 cycle, the sum of the roots of each cubic would be the negative coefficient of x² divided by the coefficient of x³ (by Vieta's formula).\n\nTherefore, if we can factor the sixth-degree polynomial into two cubics, say P(x) = (x³ + p x² + q x + r)(x³ + s x² + t x + u), then the sum of the roots of the first cubic is -p, and the sum of the roots of the second cubic is -s. Therefore, the possible values of S are -p and -s.\n\nTherefore, if we can find the coefficients p and s, then we can get the two possible sums.\n\nAlternatively, even without factoring, we can use Vieta's formula on the sixth-degree polynomial.\n\nLet me denote the sixth-degree polynomial as P(x) =x^6 +x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nLet the roots of P(x) be the six roots corresponding to the period-3 cycles (three roots for each cycle). Let the roots be r1, r2, r3, s1, s2, s3, where {r1, r2, r3} form one cycle and {s1, s2, s3} form the other.\n\nThen, the sum of all roots of P(x) is equal to - coefficient of x^5 / coefficient of x^6 = -1/1 = -1.\n\nTherefore, (r1 + r2 + r3) + (s1 + s2 + s3) = -1.\n\nTherefore, the two sums S1 = r1 + r2 + r3 and S2 = s1 + s2 + s3 satisfy S1 + S2 = -1.\n\nAdditionally, the sum of the products of roots two at a time for P(x) is coefficient of x^4 / coefficient of x^6 = -17/1 = -17.\n\nBut the sum of products two at a time for all six roots is:\n\n(r1 r2 + r1 r3 + r2 r3) + (s1 s2 + s1 s3 + s2 s3) + (r1 s1 + r1 s2 + r1 s3 + r2 s1 + r2 s2 + r2 s3 + r3 s1 + r3 s2 + r3 s3) = -17.\n\nHmm, this seems complicated.\n\nAlternatively, using Vieta's formula for the two cubic factors.\n\nSuppose P(x) = (x³ + a x² + b x + c)(x³ + d x² + e x + f),\n\nthen the sum of roots of first cubic is -a,\n\nsum of roots of second cubic is -d,\n\nso S1 = -a, S2 = -d,\n\nand we have S1 + S2 = - (a + d).\n\nBut from the original polynomial P(x), the coefficient of x^5 is 1, which is equal to (a + d).\n\nTherefore, S1 + S2 = - 