hat position 3 must be one of {3,4,5,6}.\n\nBetween position 3 and 4: the numbers there must satisfy the product ≤20.\n\nBetween position 4 and 5: same.\n\nBetween position 5 and 6: same.\n\nBetween position 6 and 7 (which is 2): the number in position 6 must be adjacent to 2. Since 2 can be adjacent to any number except itself, but since 2 is already placed in position 7, the number in position 6 can be any of the remaining numbers except 2, but actually, the number in position 6 must be adjacent to 2 in terms of the product condition. Wait, actually, the adjacency condition between position 6 and 7 (which is 2) requires that the number in position 6 multiplied by 2 is ≤20.\n\nWait, hold on! Wait a second! I made a mistake here. Earlier, I thought that adjacency only required the product condition, but the adjacency between two numbers must have their product ≤20. So, for example, between position 6 and position 7 (which is 2), the number in position 6 must satisfy (number in position 6)*2 ≤20.\n\nSimilarly, between position 2 (which is 1) and position 3, since 1 can be adjacent to any number, so the number in position 3 can be anything, but actually, the product must be ≤20, but 1 times anything is that anything, which is ≤20 since all numbers are ≤7, so that's okay.\n\nBut for position 6 and 7: since position 7 is 2, the number in position 6 must satisfy (number in position 6)*2 ≤20. Since the number in position 6 is one of {3,4,5,6} (since we already placed 1 and 2), so let's see:\n\nIf position 6 is 3: 3*2=6 ≤20: okay.\n\nIf position 6 is 4: 4*2=8 ≤20: okay.\n\nIf position 6 is 5: 5*2=10 ≤20: okay.\n\nIf position 6 is 6: 6*2=12 ≤20: okay.\n\nTherefore, all remaining numbers {3,4,5,6} are okay for position 6. So no additional constraints from that side.\n\nHowever, moving on, the critical constraints will be between the internal positions (positions 3-4, 4-5, 5-6). Let me note the adjacency constraints for each adjacent pair:\n\nFirst, let me list the adjacency constraints between numbers in the remaining set {3,4,5,6}:\n\nFor numbers 3,4,5,6, their allowed adjacents (products ≤20) are as follows:\n\n- 3 can be adjacent to: 1,2,4,5,6 (from earlier adjacency list). But since in this case, we are dealing with the numbers 3,4,5,6, and the adjacent numbers must also be from this set? Wait no, actually, the adjacency constraints are between the numbers in the sequence. For example, between position 3 and 4, the two numbers must have a product ≤20. So, for example, if position 3 is 3 and position 4 is 6: 3*6=18 ≤20: okay. If position 3 is 4 and position 4 is 5: 4*5=20 ≤20: okay. If position 3 is 5 and position 4 is 6: 5*6=30 >20: not okay. So, that would be a problem.\n\nTherefore, we need to consider the adjacency constraints between the numbers in positions 3,4,5,6 as they are arranged in the sequence, with the numbers 3,4,5,6, and the product of each adjacent pair must be ≤20.\n\nAdditionally, the number in position 3 must be adjacent to 1 (position 2), but since 1 can be adjacent to anything, that doesn't impose a restriction on the number in position 3 (other than being one of 3,4,5,6). Similarly, the number in position 6 must be adjacent to 2 (position 7), but as we saw, all remaining numbers are okay with that.\n\nTherefore, the main constraints are between positions 3-4, 4-5, and 5-6.\n\nLet me think of this as a path from position 2 (which is 1) to position 7 (which is 2), passing through positions 3,4,5,6 with numbers 3,4,5,6 arranged such that each consecutive pair in the path has a product ≤20.\n\nWait, actually, since the numbers 3,4,5,6 are to be arranged in positions 3,4,5,6, forming a sequence of four numbers where each adjacent pair in the sequence must multiply to ≤20, and also the first number (position 3) can be any of 3,4,5,6, and the last number (position 6) can be any of 3,4,5,6, but with the added constraints that the entire sequence must use each number exactly once (since we have to place 3,4,5,6 exactly once each in those positions). So it's a permutation of [3,4,5,6] with the adjacency constraints on consecutive elements.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of permutations of [3,4,5,6] such that for every i from 1 to 3 (since there are 4 elements, so 3 adjacent pairs), the product of the i-th and (i+1)-th elements is ≤20.\n\nAdditionally, since the entire sequence is part of a larger cycle, but in this case, since we fixed positions 2 and 7, we just need the internal constraints.\n\nSo, let me consider all permutations of [3,4,5,6] and count those that satisfy the adjacency product constraints.\n\nThere are 4! = 24 permutations. Let me list them and check which ones satisfy the conditions. Alternatively, maybe we can do it more systematically.\n\nFirst, let me note the adjacency constraints between the numbers 3,4,5,6:\n\nPossible adjacent pairs and their products:\n\n3 & 4: 12 ≤20: okay.\n\n3 & 5: 15 ≤20: okay.\n\n3 & 6: 18 ≤20: okay.\n\n4 & 3: same as above.\n\n4 & 5: 20 ≤20: okay.\n\n4 & 6: 24 >20: not okay.\n\n5 & 3: same as 3 & 5: okay.\n\n5 & 4: same as 4 & 5: okay.\n\n5 & 6: 30 >20: not okay.\n\n6 & 3: same as 3 & 6: okay.\n\n6 & 4: same as 4 & 6: not okay.\n\n6 & 5: same as 5 & 6: not okay.\n\nTherefore, the forbidden adjacent pairs are:\n\n4 & 6,\n\n5 & 6,\n\n6 & 4,\n\n6 & 5.\n\nIn other words, 6 cannot be adjacent to 4 or 5, and 5 cannot be adjacent to 6, and 4 cannot be adjacent to 6.\n\nWait, more precisely:\n\n- 4 and 6 cannot be adjacent.\n\n- 5 and 6 cannot be adjacent.\n\nAdditionally, 6 cannot be adjacent to 4 or 5, and 5 cannot be adjacent to 6, and 4 cannot be adjacent to 6.\n\nSo, the forbidden adjacents are 4-6, 5-6, 6-4, 6-5. Since adjacency is undirected, we can just say that 6 cannot be adjacent to 4 or 5, and 5 cannot be adjacent to 6, and 4 cannot be adjacent to 6. So, essentially, 6 cannot be next to 4 or 5, and 5 cannot be next to 6. Wait, actually, since adjacency is undirected, if 4 and 6 can't be adjacent, that's the same as 6 and 4 can't be adjacent. Similarly, 5 and 6 can't be adjacent.\n\nTherefore, in the permutation of [3,4,5,6], the number 6 cannot be adjacent to 4 or 5, and the number 5 cannot be adjacent to 6. Wait, but actually, 5 and 6 also can't be adjacent, so that's covered by the 6-5 adjacency.\n\nSo, in summary, in the permutation of [3,4,5,6], the number 6 must be adjacent only to 3, and numbers 4 and 5 must not be adjacent to 6. Additionally, 4 and 5 can be adjacent to each other (since 4*5=20 is okay), but 4 and 5 cannot be adjacent to 6.\n\nTherefore, to count the number of permutations of [3,4,5,6] where 6 is only adjacent to 3, and 4 and 5 are not adjacent to 6.\n\nWait, but 6 can also be adjacent to 3, which is allowed, since 3*6=18 ≤20. So, 6 can be next to 3, but cannot be next to 4 or 5.\n\nTherefore, in the permutation, wherever 6 is placed, its neighbors must be 3. Since 6 is in a permutation of four elements, it has two neighbors (unless it's at the end, but in a permutation of four elements in a sequence, each element except the first and last has two neighbors, but in our case, the entire sequence is a linear arrangement from position 3 to 6, so it's a straight line, not a circle. Wait, actually, in our case, the positions 3,4,5,6 form a linear sequence, not a circle, because the circle is already closed by the fixed positions 2 and 7. So, the sequence from position 3 to 6 is linear, with position 3 adjacent only to 4, position 4 adjacent to 3 and 5, position 5 adjacent to 4 and 6, and position 6 adjacent only to 5. Wait no:\n\nWait, positions 3,4,5,6 are in a straight line: position 3 is next to position 4, position 4 is next to 3 and 5, position 5 is next to 4 and 6, position 6 is next to 5. So, in terms of the permutation of [3,4,5,6] in these positions, the adjacency is as a linear chain, so the first element (position 3) has a right neighbor (position 4), the last element (position 6) has a left neighbor (position 5), and the middle elements have two neighbors.\n\nTherefore, the constraints are:\n\n- The element at position 3 (first in the sequence) must not be 4 or 5 if its right neighbor is 6? Wait no, actually, the constraints are on the products of adjacent elements. Let me rephrase:\n\nFor the permutation [a,b,c,d] where a is position 3, b is position 4, c is position 5, d is position 6:\n\n- a and b must satisfy a*b ≤20\n\n- b and c must satisfy b*c ≤20\n\n- c and d must satisfy c*d ≤20\n\nAdditionally, since d is the last element (position 6), it must also satisfy d*2 ≤20 (because position 7 is 2). Wait, but earlier we saw that d (position 6) can be 3,4,5,6 and all of them multiplied by 2 are ≤20, so that's okay. So that constraint is already satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the only constraints are on the internal adjacencies: a*b, b*c, c*d must all be ≤20.\n\nSo, given that, we can model this as permutations of [3,4,5,6] with the constraints that:\n\n- The product of the first and second element ≤20,\n\n- The product of the second and third element ≤20,\n\n- The product of the third and fourth element ≤20.\n\nAdditionally, since all elements are used exactly once, we have to consider all permutations and eliminate those that violate any of the three adjacency product conditions.\n\nGiven that, let me list all 24 permutations of [3,4,5,6] and check which ones satisfy the three adjacency product conditions. That might be tedious, but with four elements, it's manageable.\n\nFirst, let me list all permutations:\n\n1. 3,4,5,6\n\n2. 3,4,6,5\n\n3. 3,5,4,6\n\n4. 3,5,6,4\n\n5. 3,6,4,5\n\n6. 3,6,5,4\n\n7. 4,3,5,6\n\n8. 4,3,6,5\n\n9. 4,5,3,6\n\n10. 4,5,6,3\n\n11. 4,6,3,5\n\n12. 4,6,5,3\n\n13. 5,3,4,6\n\n14. 5,3,6,4\n\n15. 5,4,3,6\n\n16. 5,4,6,3\n\n17. 5,6,3,4\n\n18. 5,6,4,3\n\n19. 6,3,4,5\n\n20. 6,3,5,4\n\n21. 6,4,3,5\n\n22. 6,4,5,3\n\n23. 6,5,3,4\n\n24. 6,5,4,3\n\nNow, let's go through each permutation and check the three adjacent products.\n\n1. 3,4,5,6:\n\nCheck 3*4=12 ≤20: okay.\n\n4*5=20 ≤20: okay.\n\n5*6=30 >20: Not okay. So this permutation is invalid.\n\n2. 3,4,6,5:\n\n3*4=12: okay.\n\n4*6=24 >20: Not okay. So invalid.\n\n3. 3,5,4,6:\n\n3*5=15: okay.\n\n5*4=20: okay.\n\n4*6=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n4. 3,5,6,4:\n\n3*5=15: okay.\n\n5*6=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n5. 3,6,4,5:\n\n3*6=18: okay.\n\n6*4=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n6. 3,6,5,4:\n\n3*6=18: okay.\n\n6*5=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n7. 4,3,5,6:\n\n4*3=12: okay.\n\n3*5=15: okay.\n\n5*6=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n8. 4,3,6,5:\n\n4*3=12: okay.\n\n3*6=18: okay.\n\n6*5=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n9. 4,5,3,6:\n\n4*5=20: okay.\n\n5*3=15: okay.\n\n3*6=18: okay. All okay. This permutation is valid.\n\n10. 4,5,6,3:\n\n4*5=20: okay.\n\n5*6=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n11. 4,6,3,5:\n\n4*6=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n12. 4,6,5,3:\n\n4*6=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n13. 5,3,4,6:\n\n5*3=15: okay.\n\n3*4=12: okay.\n\n4*6=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n14. 5,3,6,4:\n\n5*3=15: okay.\n\n3*6=18: okay.\n\n6*4=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n15. 5,4,3,6:\n\n5*4=20: okay.\n\n4*3=12: okay.\n\n3*6=18: okay. All okay. Valid.\n\n16. 5,4,6,3:\n\n5*4=20: okay.\n\n4*6=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n17. 5,6,3,4:\n\n5*6=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n18. 5,6,4,3:\n\n5*6=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n19. 6,3,4,5:\n\n6*3=18: okay.\n\n3*4=12: okay.\n\n4*5=20: okay. All okay. Valid.\n\n20. 6,3,5,4:\n\n6*3=18: okay.\n\n3*5=15: okay.\n\n5*4=20: okay. All okay. Valid.\n\n21. 6,4,3,5:\n\n6*4=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n22. 6,4,5,3:\n\n6*4=24 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n23. 6,5,3,4:\n\n6*5=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\n24. 6,5,4,3:\n\n6*5=30 >20: Not okay. Invalid.\n\nSo, out of the 24 permutations, the valid ones are permutations 9, 15, 19, and 20. Let's confirm:\n\nPermutation 9: 4,5,3,6\n\nCheck products:\n\n4*5=20, 5*3=15, 3*6=18: all ≤20: valid.\n\nPermutation 15: 5,4,3,6\n\nProducts: 5*4=20, 4*3=12, 3*6=18: all okay: valid.\n\nPermutation 19: 6,3,4,5\n\nProducts: 6*3=18, 3*4=12, 4*5=20: all okay: valid.\n\nPermutation 20: 6,3,5,4\n\nProducts: 6*3=18, 3*5=15, 5*4=20: all okay: valid.\n\nSo that's four valid permutations for the internal sequence positions 3-6 when we are in Case 1 (position 2=1, position7=2).\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, there are 4 possible arrangements for the middle four positions.\n\nNow, moving on to Case 2: Position 2 = 2 and Position 7 = 1.\n\nSimilarly, in this case, position 2 is 2 and position 7 is 1. The remaining numbers to arrange in positions 3,4,5,6 are {1,3,4,5,6} minus the ones already used? Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, original numbers to arrange are 1,2,3,4,5,6. Position 2 is 2, and position 7 is 1, so the remaining numbers are {3,4,5,6}. Wait, same as in Case 1? Wait no:\n\nWait, position 2 is 2 and position 7 is 1, so the numbers used in positions 2 and 7 are 2 and 1, so the remaining numbers to place in positions 3,4,5,6 are {3,4,5,6}. Wait, same as Case 1? That's interesting. So the remaining numbers are the same set {3,4,5,6}, but now the constraints might be different because the first element (position 3) is adjacent to position 2 (which is 2), and the last element (position 6) is adjacent to position 7 (which is 1). Let's see:\n\nIn this case:\n\n- Position 3 is adjacent to position 2 (which is 2). Therefore, the number in position 3 must satisfy (number in position 3)*2 ≤20.\n\n- Position 6 is adjacent to position 7 (which is 1). Since 1 can be adjacent to anything, so no constraint from that side except that the number in position 6 must be one of {3,4,5,6}, which it is.\n\nBut the key constraints here are:\n\n- Between position 3 and 4: their product must be ≤20.\n\n- Between position 4 and 5: same.\n\n- Between position 5 and 6: same.\n\nAdditionally, the number in position 3 must satisfy (position3)*2 ≤20.\n\nLet me see what numbers are allowed in position 3:\n\nThe number in position 3 must be one of {3,4,5,6}, and must satisfy (number)*2 ≤20. Since all of 3,4,5,6 when multiplied by 2 give 6, 8, 10, 12, all ≤20. So all numbers in {3,4,5,6} are okay for position 3.\n\nSimilarly, the number in position 6 is adjacent to position 7 (which is 1), so no additional constraints except it must be one of the remaining numbers, but since we are permuting {3,4,5,6} in positions 3-6, that's already satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the constraints are similar to Case 1 but with different endpoints.\n\nSpecifically, the sequence is from position 3 to 6, with numbers {3,4,5,6}, arranged such that:\n\n- The first element (position3) can be any of {3,4,5,6}, since multiplying by 2 is okay.\n\n- The last element (position6) can be any of {3,4,5,6}, since multiplying by 1 is okay.\n\n- The internal adjacencies (positions3-4, 4-5, 5-6) must satisfy the product ≤20, just like before.\n\nWait a second! So actually, the constraints on the internal adjacencies are exactly the same as in Case 1, because the adjacency between position3 and position4 is the same as in Case 1 (both are internal to the sequence), and similarly for the other adjacencies. The only difference is that in Case 1, the first element (position3) was adjacent to 1 (position2), but since 1 can be adjacent to anything, there was no additional constraint. Similarly, in Case 2, the last element (position6) is adjacent to 1 (position7), which is also okay. Therefore, the constraints on the internal adjacencies between positions3-4, 4-5, 5-6 are the same as in Case 1: namely, the products must be ≤20. However, the difference is that in Case 2, the first element (position3) is now adjacent to 2 (position2), but since 2 can be adjacent to any number except itself, and since position3 is one of {3,4,5,6}, which are all allowed to be adjacent to 2 (as 2 can be adjacent to anything except itself), so that doesn't add any new constraints. Similarly, the last element (position6) is adjacent to 1, which is fine.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, the constraints on the permutation of {3,4,5,6} in positions 3-6 are exactly the same as in Case 1: the three adjacent pairs must have products ≤20, and the permutation must be of the four numbers with those constraints. However, in Case 1, we had an additional constraint that the last element (position6) had to be 2, but in this case, the last element (position6) can be any of {3,4,5,6} since position7 is 1. Wait no, actually in Case 2, the last element (position6) is part of the permutation of {3,4,5,6}, so it can be any of those, but in Case 1, the last element (position6) was fixed as 2, but here it's part of the permutation.\n\nWait, actually in Case 2, position7 is fixed as 1, so the number in position6 is part of the permutation of {3,4,5,6}, so it can be any of those, and the constraint between position6 and position7 (which is 1) is automatically satisfied. So, the only constraints are the internal ones, just like in Case 1.\n\nTherefore, the number of valid permutations for Case 2 is also the same as in Case 1, which was 4.\n\nWait, but let me confirm by actually going through the same process.\n\nIn Case 2, the sequence is positions3-6: permutation of [3,4,5,6], with the constraints that:\n\n- The product of positions3 & 4 ≤20,\n\n- The product of positions4 & 5 ≤20,\n\n- The product of positions5 & 6 ≤20,\n\nand additionally, the number in position3 can be anything in {3,4,5,6} (since it's adjacent to 2, which is okay),\n\nand the number in position6 can be anything in {3,4,5,6} (since it's adjacent to 1, which is okay).\n\nTherefore, the constraints are exactly the same as in Case 1, so the valid permutations are the same four permutations: 9,15,19,20 as above.\n\nWait, but hold on, in Case 1, the last element (position6) had to be 2, but in this case, it's part of the permutation, so actually in Case 2, the permutations are of the four numbers, so the last element (position6) can be any of 3,4,5,6, but the constraints are still the same. Therefore, the count should be the same, 4 permutations.\n\nHowever, wait, in Case 1, the last element (position6) was fixed as 2, but in Case 2, the last element (position6) is part of the permutation, so actually, in Case 2, the permutations are of the four numbers {3,4,5,6}, so the number of valid permutations is indeed 4, same as Case 1.\n\nTherefore, Case 2 also has 4 arrangements.\n\nTherefore, total arrangements for both cases: 4 + 4 = 8.\n\nBut wait, hold on: in Case 1, we fixed position2 to 1 and position7 to 2, and found 4 permutations for the middle. In Case 2, fixed position2 to 2 and position7 to 1, and found 4 permutations for the middle. Therefore, total arrangements with 7 fixed in position1 are 8.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that in both cases, the numbers are all distinct and properly placed.\n\nWait, let me check one of the permutations in Case 1 to see if it's valid in the full arrangement.\n\nTake Case1 permutation 9: [4,5,3,6] in positions3-6.\n\nSo full arrangement with 7 fixed at position1:\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:1\n\n3:4\n\n4:5\n\n5:3\n\n6:6\n\n7:2\n\nNow, check all adjacent products:\n\n- 7 & 1: 7*1=7 ≤20: okay.\n\n- 1 & 4:1*4=4 ≤20: okay.\n\n- 4 &5:4*5=20 ≤20: okay.\n\n- 5 &3:5*3=15 ≤20: okay.\n\n- 3 &6:3*6=18 ≤20: okay.\n\n- 6 &2:6*2=12 ≤20: okay.\n\n- 2 &7:2*7=14 ≤20: okay.\n\nAll products are okay. Good.\n\nAnother permutation: Case1 permutation 15: [5,4,3,6]\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:1\n\n3:5\n\n4:4\n\n5:3\n\n6:6\n\n7:2\n\nCheck products:\n\n7&1:7 okay.\n\n1&5:5 okay.\n\n5&4:20 okay.\n\n4&3:12 okay.\n\n3&6:18 okay.\n\n6&2:12 okay.\n\n2&7:14 okay.\n\nGood.\n\nCase1 permutation19: [6,3,4,5]\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:1\n\n3:6\n\n4:3\n\n5:4\n\n6:5\n\n7:2\n\nCheck products:\n\n7&1:7 okay.\n\n1&6:6 okay.\n\n6&3:18 okay.\n\n3&4:12 okay.\n\n4&5:20 okay.\n\n5&2:10 okay.\n\n2&7:14 okay.\n\nAll good.\n\nCase1 permutation20: [6,3,5,4]\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:1\n\n3:6\n\n4:3\n\n5:5\n\n6:4\n\n7:2\n\nProducts:\n\n7&1:7 okay.\n\n1&6:6 okay.\n\n6&3:18 okay.\n\n3&5:15 okay.\n\n5&4:20 okay.\n\n4&2:8 okay.\n\n2&7:14 okay.\n\nPerfect.\n\nNow, check a permutation from Case2, say permutation9: [4,5,3,6] in positions3-6.\n\nWait, but in Case2, the permutation is in positions3-6, with position2=2 and position7=1.\n\nSo:\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:2\n\n3:4\n\n4:5\n\n5:3\n\n6:6\n\n7:1\n\nCheck products:\n\n7&2:14 ≤20: okay.\n\n2&4:8 ≤20: okay.\n\n4&5:20 ≤20: okay.\n\n5&3:15 ≤20: okay.\n\n3&6:18 ≤20: okay.\n\n6&1:6 ≤20: okay.\n\n1&7:7 ≤20: okay.\n\nAll good.\n\nAnother Case2 permutation, say permutation15: [5,4,3,6]\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:2\n\n3:5\n\n4:4\n\n5:3\n\n6:6\n\n7:1\n\nCheck products:\n\n7&2:14 okay.\n\n2&5:10 okay.\n\n5&4:20 okay.\n\n4&3:12 okay.\n\n3&6:18 okay.\n\n6&1:6 okay.\n\n1&7:7 okay.\n\nGood.\n\nPermutation19: [6,3,4,5]\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:2\n\n3:6\n\n4:3\n\n5:4\n\n6:5\n\n7:1\n\nProducts:\n\n7&2:14 okay.\n\n2&6:12 okay.\n\n6&3:18 okay.\n\n3&4:12 okay.\n\n4&5:20 okay.\n\n5&1:5 okay.\n\n1&7:7 okay.\n\nPerfect.\n\nPermutation20: [6,3,5,4]\n\nPositions:\n\n1:7\n\n2:2\n\n3:6\n\n4:3\n\n5:5\n\n6:4\n\n7:1\n\nCheck:\n\n7&2:14 okay.\n\n2&6:12 okay.\n\n6&3:18 okay.\n\n3&5:15 okay.\n\n5&4:20 okay.\n\n4&1:4 okay.\n\n1&7:7 okay.\n\nAll products are okay. Great.\n\nSo, both cases indeed give 4 valid permutations each, so total 8 arrangements with 7 fixed in position1.\n\nTherefore, since we fixed 7 in position1, and found 8 arrangements, then to get the total number of arrangements where 7 can be in any of the 7 positions, we would multiply by 7, giving 8*7=56.\n\nBut wait, hold on! Wait a second. Is that correct?\n\nWait, in the above, we fixed 7 in position1 and found 8 arrangements. Since in the problem statement, rotations are considered different, so each position of 7 is a different arrangement, so yes, if we fix 7 in position1 and find 8 arrangements, then rotating 7 to any of the 7 positions would give 7*8=56 total arrangements.\n\nHowever, hold on: is there any overcounting here? For example, is there an arrangement where 7 is in more than one position? No, because each arrangement has 7 in exactly one position. Since we are counting all arrangements where 7 is in each position exactly once, and the rest of the numbers are arranged accordingly, so multiplying by 7 is correct.\n\nBut wait, but in the above, when we fixed 7 in position1, and found 8 arrangements, each of those arrangements has 7 only in position1, so when we rotate them so that 7 is in another position, they become distinct arrangements. Since rotations are considered different, that's okay.\n\nTherefore, total arrangements would be 7 * 8 = 56.\n\nBut wait, hold on, hold on. Wait, let me think again. When we fixed 7 in position1 and found 8 arrangements, each of those arrangements is unique when considering 7 fixed at position1. So, if we were to rotate those arrangements so that 7 is in another position, they become different arrangements. Since there are 7 positions for 7, and for each position of 7, there are 8 arrangements (as calculated when fixing 7 there), then total arrangements are indeed 7×8=56.\n\nHowever, hold on a second: in Case 1 and Case 2 when we fixed 7 in position1, we considered two cases based on the neighbors of 7 (which had to be 1 or 2). But when we rotate the arrangement so that 7 is in a different position, say position k, then the neighbors of 7 in that new position would have to be 1 or 2 as well. However, in our calculation above, when we fixed 7 in position1, we already considered that its neighbors (positions 7 and 2) must be 1 or 2, and we counted only those arrangements where the adjacent positions to 7 (positions 2 and 7) are 1 or 2 appropriately. Therefore, when we rotate the arrangement so that 7 is in another position, say position k, the neighbors of position k (which is now 7) must still be 1 or 2, and the counting we did when fixing 7 in position1 already ensures that the neighbors are 1 or 2. Therefore, by multiplying by 7, we are indeed counting all possible arrangements where 7 is in any position with its two neighbors being 1 or 2 appropriately, and the rest of the arrangement following the constraints.\n\nTherefore, the total number should be 7×8=56.\n\nWait, but hold on, let me think again: when we fixed 7 in position1 and found 8 arrangements, each of those 8 arrangements has 7 in position1 with specific neighbors. When we rotate the circle so that 7 is in position2, that rotation would produce an arrangement where 7 is in position2, but the neighbors of 7 (positions7 and 3) must also be 1 or 2. However, in our count when we fixed 7 in position1, we didn't fix the specific neighbors of 7 beyond that they had to be 1 or 2, but in the specific arrangements we found, the neighbors of 7 (positions2 and 7) were set to specific numbers (either 1 and 2 or 2 and 1). Therefore, when we rotate such an arrangement so that 7 is in a different position, the neighbors of 7 in the new position may not necessarily be 1 or 2. Wait, hold on! This is a critical point.\n\nWait, actually, in our calculation above, when we fixed 7 in position1, we required that the neighbors of 7 (positions2 and position7) must be 1 or 2, and then arranged the rest accordingly. Therefore, in those 8 arrangements with 7 fixed in position1, the neighbors of 7 are always 1 and 2 (in some order). Therefore, when we rotate such an arrangement so that 7 is in a different position, say position k, then the neighbors of position k (which is 7) would be the previous neighbors rotated around. For example, if we have an arrangement with 7 at position1, with neighbors 1 (position2) and 2 (position7), then rotating it so that 7 is at position2 would make its neighbors position1 (which was previously position7, now position1) and position3 (which was previously position2). The neighbor that was previously position7 (now position1) is 2, and the other neighbor (position3) was previously position2, which was 1. So in the rotated arrangement, the neighbors of 7 (now at position2) would be 2 and 1, which are still allowed (since 7 can be adjacent to 1 or 2). Therefore, the neighbors of 7 in any rotated arrangement will still be 