der.\n\nSo the permutations are:\n\n2a. [B, C, A, D]\n\n2b. [C, B, A, D]\n\nNow, check the adjacencies:\n\nIn 2a: [B, C, A, D]\n\nAdjacencies:\n\nB (4) and C (5): 4×5=20 ≤20, okay.\n\nC (5) and A (3): 5×3=15 ≤20, okay.\n\nA (3) and D (6): 3×6=18 ≤20, okay.\n\nIn 2b: [C, B, A, D]\n\nAdjacencies:\n\nC (5) and B (4): 5×4=20 ≤20, okay.\n\nB (4) and A (3): 4×3=12 ≤20, okay.\n\nA (3) and D (6): same as above, okay.\n\nAlso, the adjacency between the first two elements in 2a: B and C is okay (20), and in 2b: C and B is okay (20). So both 2a and 2b are valid.\n\nTherefore, Case 2 yields 2 permutations.\n\nNow, are there any other cases? Since D cannot be in the middle positions (positions 2 or 3) because that would require D to be adjacent to two elements, both of which must be A, but there's only one A. Therefore, the only valid permutations are the four we've found so far: 1a, 1b, 2a, 2b.\n\nWait, but hold on, that seems too few. Four permutations? But we have four elements, so total permutations are 24. But with constraints, maybe it's indeed small.\n\nWait, but let me check if there are other possibilities where D is not at the ends but still satisfies the constraints. Wait, suppose we try to place D somewhere in the middle but with A on both sides. But since there's only one A, that's impossible. Alternatively, perhaps D is adjacent only to A on one side and something else on the other? But the other side would have to be a non-forbidden element, but the only non-forbidden element besides A is... Actually, the forbidden elements for D are B and C. So the only permissible neighbor for D is A. Therefore, D cannot be adjacent to anything else except A, so it must be at the end with A next to it.\n\nTherefore, indeed, only the four permutations above are possible?\n\nWait, hold on, let me see if there are permutations where D is not at the ends but still only adjacent to A. For example:\n\nSuppose the permutation is [A, D, B, C]. Here, D is in the second position. Its neighbors are A (first) and B (third). But B is forbidden to be adjacent to D, since 4×6=24>20. So that's invalid.\n\nSimilarly, [A, D, C, B]: D is adjacent to C, which is forbidden.\n\n[D, B, A, C]: D is first, adjacent to B. But D and B cannot be adjacent, since 6×4=24>20. So invalid.\n\n[D, C, A, B]: D adjacent to C: 6×5=30>20, invalid.\n\n[B, D, A, C]: D is second, adjacent to B and A. Adjacent to B is invalid.\n\n[C, D, A, B]: D adjacent to C, invalid.\n\n[A, B, D, C]: D is third, adjacent to B and C. Both forbidden.\n\n[A, C, D, B]: D adjacent to C and B, both forbidden.\n\n[B, A, D, C]: D adjacent to A and C. Adjacent to C is forbidden.\n\n[C, A, D, B]: D adjacent to A and B. Adjacent to B is forbidden.\n\n[D, A, B, C] and [D, A, C, B] are already counted in Case 1.\n\n[A, D, B, C] etc. are invalid.\n\nSo indeed, only the four permutations are valid: the two with D first followed by A and then B/C, and the two with A followed by D at the end preceded by B/C.\n\nTherefore, total of 4 permutations.\n\nWait, but hold on, in the above, when I considered Case 1 and Case 2, I got 2 + 2 = 4 permutations. However, in each case, the middle elements B and C can be arranged in 2 ways, so that's 2×2=4.\n\nBut let me list them explicitly:\n\nCase 1: D first, then A, then B then C: [6,3,4,5]\n\nCase 1b: [6,3,5,4]\n\nCase 2a: [4,5,3,6]\n\nCase 2b: [5,4,3,6]\n\nSo these four permutations:\n\n1. [6,3,4,5]\n\n2. [6,3,5,4]\n\n3. [4,5,3,6]\n\n4. [5,4,3,6]\n\nNow, let's verify each of these permutations for the adjacency constraints:\n\n1. [6,3,4,5]:\n\nCheck adjacent products:\n\n6×3 = 18 ≤20\n\n3×4 = 12 ≤20\n\n4×5 = 20 ≤20\n\nAll good.\n\n2. [6,3,5,4]:\n\n6×3 = 18\n\n3×5 = 15\n\n5×4 = 20\n\nAll good.\n\n3. [4,5,3,6]:\n\n4×5 = 20\n\n5×3 = 15\n\n3×6 = 18\n\nAll good.\n\n4. [5,4,3,6]:\n\n5×4 = 20\n\n4×3 = 12\n\n3×6 = 18\n\nAll good.\n\nPerfect, so all four permutations are valid.\n\nTherefore, there are 4 such paths from 2 to 1 through the nodes 3,4,5,6 with the given constraints.\n\nHowever, wait a second. In our earlier analysis, we considered the path starting at 2, going to D (6) first, but in the permutation sequences above, the first element after 2 is either 6 or 3,4,5. Wait, actually in the permutations we considered, the first element after 2 is v1, which in the first case was 6, and in the second case was 4 or 5. Wait, but hold on, in the permutation sequences we found, the first element after 2 can be 6, 4, or 5, but in the permutations above, when D is first, the first element after 2 is D=6, and when D is last, the first element after 2 can be 4 or 5.\n\nWait, actually in the Case 1 permutations, the first element after 2 is D=6, then A=3, then B or C. In the Case 2 permutations, the first element after 2 is B or C (4 or 5), followed by A=3, then D=6.\n\nTherefore, in total, the four permutations correspond to:\n\nStarting with 6 or starting with 4 or 5 (but constrained by the adjacency rules). Anyway, we found four permutations.\n\nTherefore, the number of paths P is 4.\n\nHowever, wait a moment. In our formulation, the path is from 2 to 1 via the four nodes 3,4,5,6, and we found four such paths. Each such path corresponds to a unique undirected Hamiltonian cycle in the graph G.\n\nBut wait, in the undirected cycle, the direction doesn't matter, but in our counting above, we fixed the direction by starting at 2 and going to 1 via the specific permutations. However, in an undirected cycle, the same cycle can be traversed in the reverse direction. Therefore, does each undirected cycle correspond to two directed paths (one in each direction)?\n\nWait, in our problem, since we are counting undirected Hamiltonian cycles and then multiplying by 14 (2n) to get the directed arrangements, but let's see.\n\nWait, actually, in our analysis above, we considered only the paths from 2 to 1 in a specific direction (i.e., following the permutation sequences we found), but in an undirected cycle, the path from 2 to 1 can also go the other way around the cycle, which would correspond to a different permutation.\n\nWait, but in our counting of the permutations, we only considered paths that go through the four nodes in the specific order dictated by the permutations. However, in the undirected cycle, the path from 2 to 1 can be in either of the two possible directions around the cycle. Therefore, each undirected cycle is counted twice in our path counting: once for each direction.\n\nWait, for example, take the permutation [6,3,4,5]. This corresponds to the path 2-6-3-4-5-1. The reverse path would be 2-5-4-3-6-1, which would correspond to the permutation [5,4,3,6], which is one of our Case 2 permutations. Similarly, the permutation [6,3,5,4] corresponds to the path 2-6-3-5-4-1, and its reverse would be 2-4-5-3-6-1, which is the permutation [4,5,3,6], which is also in our list. Similarly, the permutation [4,5,3,6] is the reverse of [6,3,5,4], and [5,4,3,6] is the reverse of [6,3,4,5].\n\nTherefore, actually, the four permutations we found correspond to two undirected cycles, each counted twice (once in each direction). For example:\n\n- The cycle formed by the path 2-6-3-4-5-1 is the same as the cycle formed by 2-5-4-3-6-1 when considered undirected.\n\nSimilarly, the cycle from 2-6-3-5-4-1 is the same as 2-4-5-3-6-1.\n\nTherefore, the four permutations actually correspond to two distinct undirected cycles.\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nFirst, consider the cycle defined by the sequence 2-6-3-4-5-1. The undirected cycle is the same as 2-5-4-3-6-1, because you can traverse it backwards. Similarly, the cycle 2-6-3-5-4-1 is the same as 2-4-5-3-6-1 when reversed.\n\nTherefore, there are two distinct undirected Hamiltonian cycles in the graph G.\n\nEach of these two cycles can be traversed in two directions, but since we are counting undirected cycles, they are considered the same. However, in our path counting above, we counted both directions as separate paths. Therefore, the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles C is