5, the only available neighbor not yet visited is... the nodes visited so far are 1,2,3,4,5,7. So remaining node is 6. But 5 is not connected to 6. Therefore, stuck. So this path cannot be extended to a Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 1a (going 3→4) leads to a dead end.\n\nSubcase 1b: From 3, go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-3-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. The visited nodes are 1,2,3,5,7. So available neighbors from 5 are 4 (since 1,2,3 are already visited). So go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-3-5-4. From 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. All except 6 are already visited? Wait, visited nodes are 1,2,3,4,5,7. Remaining node is 6. But 4 is not connected to 6. So from 4, can't go to 6. So stuck again. Dead end.\n\nSubcase 1c: From 3, go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-3-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. All except 4,5,7 are already in the path? Wait, visited nodes are 1,2,3,6,7. So remaining nodes are 4 and 5. But 6 is connected only to 1,2,3, so cannot go to 4 or 5. Therefore, stuck. Dead end.\n\nTherefore, Case 1 (starting with 1-7-2-3) leads to dead ends in all subcases. Therefore, no Hamiltonian cycle in this case.\n\nCase 2: From 2, go to 4.\n\nSo path: 1-7-2-4. From 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Already visited nodes: 1,2,4,7. So available neighbors from 4 are 3 and 5.\n\nSubcase 2a: From 4, go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-3. From 3, neighbors are 4,5,6. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,7. Available neighbors from 3: 5,6.\n\nSubsubcase 2a1: From 3, go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-3-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,5,7. Remaining node: 6. 5 is not connected to 6, so can't proceed. Dead end.\n\nSubsubcase 2a2: From 3, go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-3-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. All except 5 and 7 are already visited? Wait, visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,6,7. Remaining node is 5. But 6 is not connected to 5. So stuck. Dead end.\n\nSubcase 2b: From 4, go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Visited nodes: 1,2,4,5,7. Available neighbors from 5: 3.\n\nSo go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-5-3. From 3, neighbors are 4,5,6. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,5,7. Remaining node: 6. From 3, can go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6. Now, from 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. All except 7 are already visited? Wait, 7 is already in the path. Wait, the nodes visited so far: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7. All nodes are visited! Now, we need to close the cycle back to 1. But from 6, can we go back to 1? Let's see: 6 is connected to 1, yes. So the path is 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1. That's a cycle! Let's check all adjacent products:\n\n1-7: 7 ≤20 ✔️\n\n7-2: 14 ≤20 ✔️\n\n2-4: 8 ≤20 ✔️\n\n4-5: 20 ≤20 ✔️\n\n5-3: 15 ≤20 ✔️\n\n3-6: 18 ≤20 ✔️\n\n6-1: 6 ≤20 ✔️\n\nPerfect, all products are ≤20. So that's one Hamiltonian cycle: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1.\n\nNow, let's see if there are other paths in this case.\n\nAfter reaching 6, we have to go back to 1 to complete the cycle, and since 6 is connected to 1, that works. So this is a valid cycle.\n\nNow, let's check if there are other possibilities in Subcase 2b.\n\nAfter going to 5-3-6, we have to go to 1, which is the only option. So that's the only path in that subsubcase.\n\nSo Subcase 2b yields one Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nNow, are there other subcases?\n\nSubcase 2c: From 4, go to... wait, we considered both neighbors of 4 when at 4: 3 and 5. So those are the only options. So only Subcases 2a and 2b. And only Subcase 2b gives a valid cycle.\n\nTherefore, Case 2 yields one Hamiltonian cycle: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1.\n\nCase 3: From 2, go to 5.\n\nSo path: 1-7-2-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Visited nodes: 1,2,5,7. So available neighbors from 5: 3,4.\n\nSubcase 3a: From 5, go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-3. From 3, neighbors are 4,5,6. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,5,7. Available neighbors: 4,6.\n\nSubsubcase 3a1: From 3, go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-3-4. From 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. All except 6 are visited? Visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,5,7. Remaining node: 6. From 4, can't go to 6 (since 4 is not connected to 6). Dead end.\n\nSubsubcase 3a2: From 3, go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-3-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,5,6,7. Remaining node: 4. From 6, can't go to 4. Dead end.\n\nSubcase 3b: From 5, go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4. From 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Visited nodes: 1,2,4,5,7. Available neighbors: 3.\n\nGo to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4-3. From 3, neighbors are 4,5,6. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,5,7. Remaining node: 6. From 3, go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. All except 7 are visited? Wait, 7 is already visited. So need to go back to 1? From 6, can go to 1. So path: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1. Check the products:\n\n1-7: 7 ✔️\n\n7-2:14 ✔️\n\n2-5:10 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-3:12 ✔️\n\n3-6:18 ✔️\n\n6-1:6 ✔️\n\nAll products are ≤20. So that's another Hamiltonian cycle: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1.\n\nSo Subcase 3b yields another valid cycle.\n\nTherefore, Case 3 yields one Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nCase 4: From 2, go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Visited nodes: 1,2,6,7. So available neighbors from 6: 3.\n\nGo to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3. From 3, neighbors are 4,5,6. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,6,7. Available neighbors: 4,5.\n\nSubcase 4a: From 3, go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3-4. From 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,6,7. Remaining node: 5. From 4, can go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. All except 6 and 7 are visited? Wait, visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7. All nodes except none? Wait, all nodes are visited. Now, need to close the cycle back to 1. From 5, can we go to 1? 5 is connected to 1, yes. So path: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1.\n\nCheck products:\n\n1-7:7 ✔️\n\n7-2:14 ✔️\n\n2-6:12 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-4:12 ✔️\n\n4-5:20 ✔️\n\n5-1:5 ✔️\n\nAll products are ≤20. So that's another Hamiltonian cycle: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1.\n\nSubsubcase 4a1: Wait, after going to 5, we have to go back to 1. Since it's a cycle, the last step is from 5 to 1, which is allowed. So that works.\n\nSubcase 4b: From 3, go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Visited nodes: 1,2,3,5,6,7. Remaining node:4. From 5, can go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4. From 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. All except 6 and 7 are visited? Wait, visited nodes: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7. All nodes except none. Need to close the cycle back to 1. From 4, can go to 1? 4 is connected to 1, yes. So path: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1.\n\nCheck products:\n\n1-7:7 ✔️\n\n7-2:14 ✔️\n\n2-6:12 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-5:15 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-1:4 ✔️\n\nAll products are okay. So that's another Hamiltonian cycle: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 4b also yields a valid cycle.\n\nSo in Case 4, starting with 1-7-2-6, we have two possible cycles depending on whether we go from 3 to 4 or to 5.\n\nTherefore, Case 4 yields two Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\nIn Subcase 4a, going 3→4→5→1 gives one cycle.\n\nIn Subcase 4b, going 3→5→4→1 gives another cycle.\n\nYes, so two cycles from Case 4.\n\nTherefore, summarizing the cases:\n\nCase 1: 0 cycles\n\nCase 2: 1 cycle\n\nCase 3: 1 cycle\n\nCase 4: 2 cycles\n\nSo total undirected Hamiltonian cycles with the starting point fixed at 1 and the path starting with 1-7-2 are 0+1+1+2=4 cycles.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Wait, in each case, we fixed the starting point at 1 and the next node as 7, then the next as 2. So this is just one particular \"segment\" of the cycle. Since in an undirected cycle, we can start at any node and go in either direction, so by fixing the starting point and the first two edges, we might be undercounting or overcounting?\n\nWait, actually, in our approach, we fixed the starting node as 1, then the next node as 7, then the next as 2. Since in an undirected cycle, any node can be the start, and the direction can be reversed. Therefore, by fixing the starting point and the direction (specifically, the path starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2), we are counting each cycle exactly once for each time it can be rotated to start at 1 and have 7 next. Since in a cycle of 7 nodes, each cycle has 7 starting points and 2 directions, so the number of such \"fixed\" paths (starting at 1, then 7, then 2) would correspond to 2 cycles (since direction can be reversed?), but actually, in our case, we fixed both the starting point and the direction (since we went 1→7→2), so each undirected cycle that includes the edge 1-7 and 2-7 and the path from 1 to 7 to 2 is counted once here, but depending on the cycle's structure, it might have multiple such embeddings?\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think that by fixing the starting point at 1 and the next two nodes as 7 and 2, we are effectively counting each undirected cycle exactly once for each occurrence of the sequence 1-7-2 in the cycle. Since in a cycle, the sequence 1-7-2 can appear in two different directions: either 1-7-2 or 1-2-7. However, in our fixed path, we specifically took the 1-7-2 direction, so we are only counting the cycles that have the edge 1-7 and then 7-2 in that order. Since the undirected cycle doesn't have a direction, the sequence 1-7-2 is part of the cycle in one direction, and the reverse would be another direction. But since we fixed the direction as going from 1 to 7 to 2, we are only counting each cycle once if it contains the subpath 1-7-2 in that order. However, in an undirected cycle, the subpath 1-7-2 can only occur in one direction or the other, but since we've fixed the direction, we are counting each cycle exactly once if it has the edges 1-7 and 7-2, which all cycles must have, since 7 is adjacent to both 1 and 2. Wait, actually, every Hamiltonian cycle must include both edges 1-7 and 7-2, because 7 must be between 1 and 2. Therefore, every Hamiltonian cycle will have the subpath 1-7-2 or 2-7-1 in some part of the cycle, but since we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction as 1-7-2, then each cycle is counted exactly once in this enumeration. Because in the cycle, the segment from 1 to 7 to 2 is unique once the cycle is fixed in direction.\n\nWait, perhaps an example: take the cycle we found in Case 2: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1. If we rotate this cycle so that it starts at 1 and goes through 7 then 2, that's exactly the path we considered in Case 2. Similarly, the cycle from Case 3: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1, same starting point and direction. The cycles from Case 4: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1 and 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1, also start with 1-7-2. So each of the 4 cycles we found are distinct and only counted once each in this enumeration.\n\nHowever, there might be other Hamiltonian cycles that do not start with 1-7-2, but instead start with a different sequence? Wait, but since we fixed the starting point at 1 and the next two nodes as 7 and 2, we are only counting those cycles where the neighbors of 1 are 7 and ... well, actually, in the cycle, 1 is adjacent to two nodes: 7 and another node. Wait a second, hold on! Wait, in our earlier reasoning, we assumed that 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, but actually, in the cycle, each node has two neighbors. For node 1, its two neighbors must be among its neighbors in the graph, which are 2,3,4,5,6,7. But we previously concluded that 7 must be adjacent to 1 and 2, but actually, node 1 could be adjacent to another node besides 7 and 2?\n\nWait, hold on, this is a critical mistake I might have made earlier.\n\nEarlier, I thought that since node 7 only has two neighbors (1 and 2), then in any Hamiltonian cycle, node 7 must be between 1 and 2. Therefore, node 1 must be adjacent to 7 and to another node, and node 2 must be adjacent to 7 and to another node.\n\nBut in the cycle, node 1 has two neighbors: one is 7, and the other is some other node, say x. Similarly, node 2 has neighbors 7 and some other node y. However, node x (the other neighbor of 1) can be any of 3,4,5,6, but with the constraint that the entire cycle must close properly.\n\nBut in our earlier approach, we fixed node 1's neighbors as 7 and then proceeded to 2 via 7, but actually, node 1 could have another neighbor besides 7 and 2. Wait, but actually, node 2 is not necessarily adjacent to node 1. Wait, hold on: node 1 and node 2 are connected (since 1 is connected to 2), so in any cycle, they could be adjacent or not?\n\nWait, in the cycle, nodes are arranged in a circle, so each node is adjacent to two others. Node 1 is connected to two nodes in the cycle. Since node 1 is connected to node 2 in the graph, it is possible for node 1 and node 2 to be adjacent in the cycle, or not. However, node 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, as we concluded before, because 7 can only be adjacent to 1 and 2. Therefore, in the cycle, node 7 is between 1 and 2, but nodes 1 and 2 may or may not be adjacent to each other? Wait, but if node 1 and node 2 are not adjacent in the cycle, then their other neighbors must be connected appropriately.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nSince node 7 is only connected to 1 and 2, in the cycle, node 7 must be between 1 and 2. Therefore, in the cycle, the order must be ...-1-7-2-... or ...-2-7-1-..., so that 7 is between 1 and 2. Therefore, in the cycle, 1 and 2 are not adjacent to each other directly; they are separated by 7. Therefore, the neighbors of node 1 are 7 and some other node (let's call it A), and the neighbors of node 2 are 7 and some other node (B), where A and B are different from each other and from 7 and 1 and 2.\n\nTherefore, in the cycle, the sequence around node 1 is either [A-1-7-2-B-...] or [...-7-1-A-...], but since the cycle is closed, the exact placement matters.\n\nHowever, in our earlier approach, we fixed the starting point at 1 and then went to 7, then to 2, which is one specific embedding of the cycle where node 1 is followed by 7 and then 2. But node 1 could also be preceded by another node (A) and followed by 7, but in that case, the sequence would be A-1-7-2-... So depending on where we start, the immediate neighbors of 1 can vary. However, since we fixed the starting point at 1 and forced the next node to be 7, we are only counting those cycles where 1 is followed by 7. However, in an undirected cycle, the direction can be reversed, so a cycle that has ...-A-1-7-2-... can also be traversed as ...-2-7-1-A-..., so if we consider all possible starting points and directions, each cycle is counted multiple times in this fixed enumeration.\n\nBut since we are trying to count undirected Hamiltonian cycles, each cycle is counted once for each of its possible starting points and directions. Since we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction as going to 7, we are only counting each cycle once if it has the edge 1-7 and the edge 7-2 in that specific order. However, in an undirected cycle, the edges are undirected, so the direction doesn't matter. Therefore, the path 1-7-2 is the same as 2-7-1 in the undirected cycle. Therefore, by fixing the starting point and the first step to 7, we are effectively counting each undirected cycle exactly once, because for each undirected cycle, there is exactly one way to start at 1 and proceed to 7 (since the edge 1-7 is unique), and then from 7, you must go to 2 (since that's the only other neighbor of 7 besides 1). Therefore, every undirected Hamiltonian cycle that includes the nodes 1, 2, and 7 must have the subpath 1-7-2 or 2-7-1, but since we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction to 7, we are only counting those cycles where after starting at 1, you go to 7 then to 2, which is exactly half of the possible directions for that subpath. Wait, no, actually, in an undirected cycle, the edge between 1 and 7 is the same as between 7 and 1, so once you fix the starting point at 1 and the first step to 7, the next step from 7 must be to 2, because 7's only other neighbor is 2. Therefore, actually, every undirected Hamiltonian cycle that includes 1, 2, and 7 must have the subpath 1-7-2 in one direction or the other, but since we fixed the starting point and the direction to go from 1 to 7 to 2, we are only counting each such cycle once. Because in the undirected cycle, the path from 1 to 7 to 2 is unique once you fix the starting point and direction. Therefore, our enumeration above, by fixing starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2, and then proceeding, should count each undirected Hamiltonian cycle exactly once.\n\nTherefore, the four cycles we found in the above cases (Cases 2, 3, and 4 yielding 1,1,2 cycles respectively) are all the undirected Hamiltonian cycles that include the edge 1-7 and 7-2 in that specific order. Since every Hamiltonian cycle must include the edges 1-7 and 7-2 (because 7 must be between 1 and 2), then these four cycles are in fact all the undirected Hamiltonian cycles in the graph.\n\nWait, but let me verify this.\n\nSuppose there exists another Hamiltonian cycle that doesn't follow this path? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we try to make a cycle where node 1 is not adjacent to 7? Wait, but node 7 can only be adjacent to 1 and 2, so in the cycle, node 7 must be between 1 and 2, so node 1 must be adjacent to 7. Therefore, every Hamiltonian cycle must include the edge 1-7 and 7-2 (or 2-7-1). Therefore, every Hamiltonian cycle must have 1 adjacent to 7 and 2 adjacent to 7. Therefore, the four cycles we found are indeed all the undirected Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nWait, but let me think if there could be another cycle where, say, after 1-7-2, instead of going to 3,4,5,6, we go somewhere else? Wait, but from node 2, the available neighbors after 1 and 7 are 3,4,5,6. We considered all four possibilities (going to 3,4,5,6) in Cases 1 through 4, and only Cases 2,3,4 yielded cycles, with Case 1 failing. So that seems comprehensive.\n\nTherefore, total undirected Hamiltonian cycles: 4.\n\nBut let me double-check for possible other cycles that we might have missed.\n\nSuppose we consider a different path where after 1-7-2, we go to 5 first, but we did consider that in Case 3. Let me see:\n\nWait, in Case 3, when from 2 we went to 5, that was covered. Similarly, going to 4 or 6. So all possibilities from node 2 after 1-7-2 were covered in Cases 1 through 4, with Cases 1 (going to 3) leading to dead ends, and the others leading to some cycles.\n\nTherefore, I think we have accounted for all possibilities, and there are indeed 4 undirected Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nTherefore, the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles is 4.\n\nTherefore, the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 2 * 7 * 4 = 56? Wait, hold on, let's recall:\n\nIn graph theory, the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in an undirected graph is equal to the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles multiplied by 2n, where n is the number of nodes. Because for each undirected cycle, you can traverse it in two directions, and for each direction, you can start at any of the n nodes. Therefore, each undirected cycle corresponds to 2n directed cycles.\n\nTherefore, if there are H undirected Hamiltonian cycles, then the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 2nH.\n\nHere, n=7, H=4, so directed cycles would be 2×7×4 = 56.\n\nTherefore, the total number of arrangements is 56?\n\nWait, but let me confirm with an example.\n\nTake one of the undirected cycles we found, say the first one: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1.\n\nIf we consider directed cycles, starting at 1 and going clockwise: 1→7→2→4→5→3→6→1.\n\nAnother directed cycle would be the reverse: 1→6→3→5→4→2→7→1.\n\nAdditionally, starting at 7: 7→1→6→3→5→4→2→7, etc.\n\nEach undirected cycle gives 14 directed cycles (7 starting points × 2 directions). Since we have 4 undirected cycles, total directed cycles would be 4×14=56.\n\nTherefore, if this is correct, then the answer is 56.\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. Let me think again: in our enumeration above, we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction such that we go from 1 to 7 to 2. Each undirected cycle is counted exactly once in that fixed enumeration. Since each undirected cycle can be started at any of the 7 nodes and in two directions, the total number of directed cycles is indeed 14 per undirected cycle, so 4×14=56.\n\nHowever, in our earlier manual enumeration, when we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction to go to 7 then to 2, we found 4 different paths, each corresponding to a unique undirected cycle. Therefore, those 4 correspond to 4 undirected cycles, each of which would then have 14 directed versions, so 56 total.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 56?\n\nWait, but let me make sure that in our enumeration we didn't miss any undirected cycles.\n\nLet me try to see if there's another undirected cycle not covered by our Cases 2,3,4.\n\nSuppose we try constructing a different cycle.\n\nSuppose starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2, then to 6, then to 3, then to 5, then to 4, then to 1. Wait, that's the same as one of our Case 4 cycles: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1. Yes, that's the second one in Case 4.\n\nAlternatively, starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2, then to 4, then to 5, then to 3, then to 6, then back to 1: that's the first Case 2 cycle.\n\nAnother one: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1, which is Case 3.\n\nIs there another possible path?\n\nSuppose starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2, then to 3, but we saw that leads to a dead end unless we take a different route. Wait, in Case 1, going to 3 led to a dead end, so that path doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, is there a cycle that doesn't go through 4 or 5 early on?\n\nWait, let's try another approach. Let me list all four undirected cycles we found:\n\nCycle 1 (from Case 2):\n\n1 - 7 - 2 - 4 - 5 - 3 - 6 - 1\n\nCycle 2 (from Case 3):\n\n1 - 7 - 2 - 5 - 4 - 3 - 6 - 1\n\nCycle 3 (from Case 4a):\n\n1 - 7 - 2 - 6 - 3 - 4 - 5 - 1\n\nCycle 4 (from Case 4b):\n\n1 - 7 - 2 - 6 - 3 - 5 - 4 - 1\n\nAre there any others?\n\nSuppose we try a different route from node 2 after 1-7-2.\n\nWait, we considered going to 3,4,5,6. All possibilities covered in Cases 1-4.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we start at a different node? Wait, but we fixed the starting point at 1. However, since we concluded that every Hamiltonian cycle must have 1 adjacent to 7 and 2 adjacent to 7, then every cycle can be rotated so that it starts with 1-7-2, so all cycles are accounted for in our enumeration. Therefore, there are only 4 undirected cycles.\n\nTherefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 4×14=56.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 56? Hmm.\n\nWait, but let me check for another possible cycle.\n\nSuppose we have the cycle: 1-7-2-6-1? Wait, no, that's too short. Needs to include all 7 nodes.\n\nAlternatively, let's try constructing a different cycle manually.\n\nStart at 1, go to 7, then to 2, then to 6, then to 3, then to 5, then to 4, then to 1. Wait, that's the same as Cycle 3 or 4?\n\nWait, 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1: that's Cycle 4.\n\nAlternatively, 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1: that's Cycle 3.\n\nAlternatively, 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1: that's Cycle 2.\n\nAnd 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1: that's Cycle 1.\n\nSo those are the four.\n\nIs there a fifth cycle?\n\nSuppose starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2, then to 5, then to 3, then to 6, then to 4, then to 1?\n\nWait, let's see: 1-7-2-5-3-6-4-1.\n\nCheck the products:\n\n1-7:7 ✔️\n\n7-2:14 ✔️\n\n2-5:10 ✔️\n\n5-3:15 ✔️\n\n3-6:18 ✔️\n\n6-4:24 >20 ❌\n\nOh, 6 and 4 are not connected. 6 is connected to 1,2,3. So 6-4 is invalid. So that path is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, 1-7-2-5-3-4-6-1:\n\nFrom 3 to 4: okay, 3-4 is allowed.\n\nThen 4-6: but 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5. 4-6 is not an edge. So invalid.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother try: 1-7-2-4-3-6-5-1.\n\nCheck the connections:\n\n1-7: ok\n\n7-2: ok\n\n2-4: ok\n\n4-3: ok\n\n3-6: ok\n\n6-5: 6 is not connected to 5. 6's connections are 1,2,3. So 6-5 is invalid.\n\nNo good.\n\nHow about 1-7-2-5-4-6-3-1?\n\nCheck:\n\n1-7: ok\n\n7-2: ok\n\n2-5: ok\n\n5-4: ok\n\n4-6: invalid\n\nNope.\n\nAlternatively, 1-7-2-5-3-4-6-1:\n\nWait, tried that, 4-6 invalid.\n\nAlternatively, 1-7-2-5-4-6-3-... but 4-6 invalid.\n\nHmm. It seems difficult to find another cycle. Let me try starting differently.\n\nSuppose starting at 2 instead of 1? But since we fixed starting at 1, but in undirected cycles, all are accounted for via rotation.\n\nAlternatively, suppose a cycle that goes 1-3-... but node 1 is connected to 3, but in our previous analysis, node 7 must be adjacent to 1 and 2, so 1 must be between 7 and another node. If we try to have 1 adjacent to 3 instead of 7, that would violate the necessity for 7 to be adjacent to 1 and 2. Wait, no, actually, node 1 could be adjacent to 3 and 7, as long as 7 is also adjacent to 2. So 1 could have neighbors 3 and 7, but then 7 must still be adjacent to 2. So the cycle could have ...-3-1-7-2-..., which is a valid configuration.\n\nWait a second, in our earlier analysis, we assumed that the cycle must have 1-7-2 in sequence, but actually, 1 could be between 7 and another node (like 3), as long as 7 is also between 1 and 2. Wait, but in a cycle, each node has two neighbors. So if 1 is adjacent to 7 and 3, then the sequence could be ...-3-1-7-2-... or ...-7-1-3-..., but in that case, 7 is adjacent to 1 and 2, which is okay, but 1 is adjacent to 3 and 7. So such a cycle is possible?\n\nWait, but in that case, the cycle would have the segment 3-1-7-2, so starting at 3, going to 1, then to 7, then to 2. However, in our earlier enumeration, when we fixed the starting point at 1 and went to 7, we didn't consider the possibility that 1's other neighbor is not 2 but another node. Wait, but in the cycle, node 1 must be adjacent to two nodes: one is 7 (as established), and the other can be any of its other neighbors except 2? Wait no, node 1 can be adjacent to 2 as well.\n\nWait, hold on, this is a critical mistake I made earlier.\n\nEarlier, I thought that since node 7 must be between 1 and 2, then node 1 must be adjacent to 7 and to another node, and node 2 must be adjacent to 7 and to another node, but nodes 1 and 2 themselves can be adjacent or not.\n\nWait, actually, node 1 and node 2 are connected by an edge (since 1 is connected to 2 in the graph). Therefore, in the cycle, it is possible for nodes 1 and 2 to be adjacent. However, node 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, so node 7 cannot be between 1 and 2 if 1 and 2 are adjacent. Wait, no: if 1 and 2 are adjacent, then in the cycle, they are next to each other, and node 7 has to be adjacent to both 1 and 2, which would require that node 7 is adjacent to two nodes that are already adjacent to each other, forming a triangle: 1-2-7-1? But in a cycle, you can't have a triangle unless it's a 3-cycle, but we have 7 nodes. Wait, no, actually, in a larger cycle, you can have a triangle as part of the cycle? Wait, no, in a cycle, each node has exactly two neighbors. So if nodes 1 and 2 are adjacent, then node 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, meaning that node 7 is adjacent to two nodes that are already adjacent, forming a \"triangle\" locally, but in the context of the larger cycle, it would look like ...-1-2-7-... or ...-7-2-1-..., but in this case, node 7 is between 2 and some other node, and node 1 is between 2 and another node? Wait, this is getting confusing.\n\nLet me try to draw this mentally.\n\nSuppose nodes 1, 2, and 7 form a triangle: 1-2-7-1. But in a 7-node cycle, you can't have a 3-cycle as part of it because the entire graph must be a single cycle. So actually, the nodes 1, 2, and 7 cannot form a triangle because that would create a smaller cycle, which is not allowed in a single Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nTherefore, nodes 1, 2, and 7 cannot be mutually adjacent in a way that forms a triangle. Specifically, since 1 and 2 are adjacent, and both are adjacent to 7, then the sequence must be either ...-1-2-7-... or ...-7-2-1-..., but in such a case, node 7 is adjacent to 2 and another node, and node 1 is adjacent to 2 and another node. Therefore, the segment would be like ...-X-1-2-7-Y-..., where X is the other neighbor of 1, and Y is the other neighbor of 7 (which must be 2 and X? Wait no.\n\nWait, node 7 has only two neighbors: 1 and 2. Therefore, in the cycle, node 7 must be between 1 and 2. Therefore, the sequence must be ...-1-7-2-... or ...-2-7-1-..., meaning that 1 and 2 are not adjacent to each other in the cycle; they are separated by 7. Therefore, my initial conclusion was correct: nodes 1 and 2 cannot be adjacent in the cycle because they are both connected to 7, and 7 can only be between them. Therefore, in the cycle, 1 and 2 must have 7 between them, so they are not adjacent. Therefore, the neighbors of 1 are 7 and another node (not 2), and the neighbors of 2 are 7 and another node (not 1).\n\nTherefore, my initial enumeration was correct in that when we fixed the starting point at 1 and went to 7, then from 7 we had to go to 2, and the other neighbor of 1 is some other node (not 2). Therefore, the cycle must proceed from 1 to 7 to 2, and then from 2 to another node (not 1), etc.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the earlier enumeration is valid, and there are indeed only 4 undirected Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nThus, proceeding with that, the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute the number of Hamiltonian cycles by considering the structure of the graph.\n\nLooking at the graph:\n\nNodes:\n\n1: connected to 2,3,4,5,6,7 (degree 6)\n\n2: connected to 3,4,5,6,7 (degree 5)\n\n3: connected to 4,5,6 (degree 3)\n\n4: connected to 1,2,3,5 (degree 4)\n\n5: connected to 1,2,3,4 (degree 4)\n\n6: connected to 1,2,3 (degree 3)\n\n7: connected to 1,2 (degree 2)\n\nGiven the high degree of node 1 and node 2, and the low degree of node 7, it's likely that the only Hamiltonian cycles are those that snake through the graph in specific ways.\n\nAlternatively, since node 7 is so restricted (only connected to 1 and 2), the entire cycle must pass through 7 right after 1 and before 2 (or vice versa), so the structure is somewhat constrained.\n\nGiven that, and our earlier enumeration yielding 4 undirected cycles, and the logic that all must pass through 1-7-2 or 2-7-1, and fixing the starting point at 1 and proceeding to 7 then 2 gives all possibilities, then 4 undirected cycles is likely correct.\n\nTherefore, proceeding with that, the total number of directed cycles is 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nHowever, before finalizing this answer, let me see if I can find any online resource or another way to verify.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can calculate the number of Hamiltonian cycles step by step using recursion or backtracking.\n\nLet me attempt a recursive approach.\n\nWe can think of building the cycle one node at a time, keeping track of the current position and the set of visited nodes.\n\nHowever, since this is time-consuming manually, perhaps we can use the following approach:\n\nGiven the graph's structure, we can note that node 7 is a \"leaf\" node in the sense that it only connects to 1 and 2. Therefore, in any Hamiltonian cycle, the path must go through 1-7-2 or 2-7-1. Without loss of generality, let's fix the path as starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2. Then, we need to count the number of paths from 2 that visit all remaining nodes (3,4,5,6) and return to 1, forming a cycle.\n\nThis is similar to what I did earlier.\n\nFrom node 2, after 1-7-2, the next node can be 3,4,5, or 6.\n\nWe can model this as a tree of possibilities.\n\nLet me define the problem as: after the segment 1-7-2, we need to traverse the remaining nodes {3,4,5,6} and return to 1, without repeating nodes, and using only the allowed edges.\n\nThis is equivalent to finding a path from 2 to 1 that visits all nodes {3,4,5,6} exactly once, and uses only edges present in the graph.\n\nWait, but since it's a cycle, the path must start at 2, visit all remaining nodes (3,4,5,6), and end at a node adjacent to 1 (since it must return to 1). The only nodes adjacent to 1 are 2,3,4,5,6,7, but 2,7 are already visited (except 7 is already in the path). Wait, actually, the path must start at 2, visit 3,4,5,6 in some order, and end at a node adjacent to 1 (other than 2 or 7, since those are already used). The available nodes to end at are 3,4,5,6, but the ending node must be adjacent to 1. Since 1 is connected to 3,4,5,6, so any of those nodes can be the last node before returning to 1.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of paths from 2 to any of {3,4,5,6} that visit all of {3,4,5,6} exactly once, using the allowed edges.\n\nThis is essentially counting the number of Hamiltonian paths in the subgraph induced by nodes {2,3,4,5,6}, starting at 2 and ending at one of {3,4,5,6}, with the constraint that the path must traverse all four nodes.\n\nWait, actually, the nodes to traverse are {3,4,5,6}, starting at 2 (which is outside this set but connected to them) and ending at a node in {3,4,5,6} adjacent to 1.\n\nWait, perhaps better to consider the entire remaining graph after fixing the path 1-7-2.\n\nAfter visiting 1,7,2, the remaining nodes are 3,4,5,6. The current position is at 2, and we need to visit 3,4,5,6 in some order, returning to 1 at the end.\n\nThe allowed moves from 2 are to 3,4,5,6 (since 2 is connected to those). From each of those nodes, we have to continue moving to unvisited nodes, etc., until we reach a node adjacent to 1 (i.e., 3,4,5, or 6), then go to 1.\n\nTherefore, we can model this as a path starting at 2, visiting all of 3,4,5,6, and ending at one of them, then connecting back to 1.\n\nTherefore, the number of such paths is equal to the number of Hamiltonian paths in the subgraph induced by nodes {2,3,4,5,6} starting at 2 and ending at any of {3,4,5,6}, multiplied by 1 (since the last step to 1 is fixed once the path ends at the appropriate node).\n\nHowever, the subgraph induced by {2,3,4,5,6} includes the edges between these nodes as per the original graph.\n\nLet me list the edges within this subgraph:\n\nEdges from 2: connected to 3,4,5,6.\n\nEdges from 3: connected to 4,5,6.\n\nEdges from 4: connected to 2,3,5.\n\nWait, in the original graph, node 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5. But in the subgraph excluding 1 and 7, node 4 is connected to 2,3,5.\n\nEdges from 5: connected to 2,3,4.\n\nEdges from 6: connected to 2,3.\n\nSo the subgraph induced by {2,3,4,5,6} has the following edges:\n\nBetween 2 and 3,4,5,6.\n\nBetween 3 and 4,5,6.\n\nBetween 4 and 3,5.\n\nBetween 5 and 2,3,4.\n\nBetween 6 and 2,3.\n\nTherefore, this is a connected subgraph, and we need the number of Hamiltonian paths starting at 2 and visiting all nodes 3,4,5,6 exactly once, ending at any node in {3,4,5,6}, such that the last node is adjacent to 1 (which it all are, since all of 3,4,5,6 are connected to 1). So actually, any Hamiltonian path from 2 to any of 3,4,5,6 that covers all nodes is acceptable.\n\nTherefore, the number of such paths is equal to the number of Hamiltonian paths in this subgraph starting at 2 and ending at any node in {3,4,5,6}.\n\nCalculating this manually might be feasible.\n\nLet me denote the nodes as 2,3,4,5,6. We need paths starting at 2, visiting all five nodes, ending at one of the other four nodes. Wait, actually, since we have to visit all four nodes {3,4,5,6} plus the starting node 2? Wait no: the path starts at 2, and must visit all nodes in {3,4,5,6}, so it's a path of length 4 (visiting 5 nodes: 2 plus the four others), ending at one of the four nodes.\n\nTherefore, the number of Hamiltonian paths starting at 2 in the subgraph {2,3,4,5,6}.\n\nTo compute this, let's use recursion.\n\nLet me define f(current, visited) as the number of paths starting from 'current' node, having visited the set 'visited', and needing to visit the remaining nodes.\n\nWe need to compute f(2, {2}) = total number of Hamiltonian paths starting at 2.\n\nThe base case is when all nodes are visited, then if we're at a node adjacent to 1 (which they all are), then it's a valid path.\n\nWait, actually, since we must end at a node adjacent to 1, but all nodes 3,4,5,6 are adjacent to 1, so any Hamiltonian path that starts at 2 and visits all four nodes will end at some node in {3,4,5,6}, which is acceptable.\n\nTherefore, the total number of such paths is equal to the number of Hamiltonian paths starting at 2 in the subgraph {2,3,4,5,6}.\n\nLet me compute this step by step.\n\nWe can use a recursive approach with memoization, but since it's small, let's try enumerating.\n\nLet me consider the nodes as 2,3,4,5,6.\n\nWe need paths starting at 2, visiting all nodes, ending anywhere except 2.\n\nLet me denote the nodes as A=2, B=3, C=4, D=5, E=6 for simplicity.\n\nEdges:\n\nA connected to B,C,D,E\n\nB connected to A,C,D,E\n\nC connected to A,B,D\n\nD connected to A,B,C\n\nE connected to A,B\n\nWait, let me re-express edges based on earlier analysis:\n\nWait in the subgraph {2,3,4,5,6}:\n\nEdges from 2 (A): to 3 (B),4 (C),5 (D),6 (E)\n\nEdges from 3 (B): to 4 (C),5 (D),6 (E)\n\nEdges from 4 (C): to 2 (A),3 (B),5 (D)\n\nEdges from 5 (D): to 2 (A),3 (B),4 (C)\n\nEdges from 6 (E): to 2 (A),3 (B)\n\nSo edges:\n\nA: B,C,D,E\n\nB: A,C,D,E\n\nC: A,B,D\n\nD: A,B,C\n\nE: A,B\n\nWe need all paths starting at A, visiting all nodes B,C,D,E, ending at any node except A.\n\nLet me try to enumerate all such paths.\n\nThis might take a while, but let's proceed.\n\nStarting at A (node 2).\n\nFirst step from A can go to B, C, D, or E.\n\nCase 1: A → B (3)\n\nNow, current node is B, visited = {A,B}, remaining nodes: C, D, E.\n\nFrom B, can go to A (visited), C, D, E. But cannot go back to A, so options: C, D, E.\n\nSubcase 1a: A→B→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, visited={A,B,C}, remaining: D, E.\n\nFrom C, connected to A (visited), B (visited), D. So only can go to D.\n\nSubsubcase 1a1: A→B→C→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited={A,B,C,D}, remaining: E.\n\nFrom D, connected to A (visited), B (visited), C (visited). Wait, D is connected to A,B,C. All visited except E? Wait, no, remaining node is E.\n\nBut D is not connected to E. D is connected to A,B,C. So cannot go to E from D. Dead end.\n\nSubsubcase 1a2: Hmm, no other options from D. So this path is stuck.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 1a (A-B-C) leads to dead end.\n\nSubcase 1b: A→B→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited={A,B,D}, remaining: C, E.\n\nFrom D, connected to A (visited), B (visited), C. So can go to C.\n\nSubsubcase 1b1: A→B→D→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, visited={A,B,D,C}, remaining: E.\n\nFrom C, connected to A (visited), B (visited), D (visited). No unvisited neighbors. Cannot proceed to E. Dead end.\n\nAlternatively, from D, could we go to E? D is not connected to E. D's connections are A,B,C. So no.\n\nSubsubcase 1b2: From D, no other options. So dead end.\n\nSubcase 1c: A→B→E (6)\n\nCurrent: E, visited={A,B,E}, remaining: C, D.\n\nFrom E, connected to A (visited), B (visited). No other connections. Dead end. Cannot proceed to C or D.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 1c is dead end.\n\nTherefore, Case 1 (A→B) leads to no valid paths.\n\nCase 2: A→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, visited={A,C}, remaining: B, D, E.\n\nFrom C, connected to A (visited), B, D.\n\nOptions: B or D.\n\nSubcase 2a: A→C→B (3)\n\nCurrent: B, visited={A,C,B}, remaining: D, E.\n\nFrom B, connected to A (visited), C (visited), D, E.\n\nOptions: D or E.\n\nSubsubcase 2a1: A→C→B→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited={A,B,C,D}, remaining: E.\n\nFrom D, connected to A (visited), B (visited), C (visited). No connection to E. Dead end.\n\nSubsubcase 2a2: A→C→B→E (6)\n\nCurrent: E, visited={A,B,C,E}, remaining: D.\n\nFrom E, connected to A (visited), B (visited). No connection to D. Dead end.\n\nSubcase 2b: A→C→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited={A,C,D}, remaining: B, E.\n\nFrom D, connected to A (visited), B, C (visited). So can go to B.\n\nSubsubcase 2b1: A→C→D→B (3)\n\nCurrent: B, visited={A,C,D,B}, remaining: E.\n\nFrom B, connected to A (visited), C (visited), D (visited), E. So can go to E.\n\nSubsubsubcase 2b1a: A→C→D→B→E (6)\n\nCurrent: E, visited all nodes except? Wait, visited nodes are A,C,D,B,E. All nodes except... all nodes are visited. The path is A-C-D-B-E. Now, we need to end at E, which is connected to A. So the path is A-C-D-B-E, and then we can go back to A? But in the context of the Hamiltonian path, we need to end at E, which is adjacent to A, so this is a valid path: A-C-D-B-E. This is a valid Hamiltonian path from A to E.\n\nSimilarly, Subsubsubcase 2b1a: that's one path.\n\nSubsubsubcase 2b1b: From B, after A-C-D-B, going to E is the only option. So that's the only path here.\n\nTherefore, Subsubcase 2b1 yields one path: A-C-D-B-E.\n\nSubsubcase 2b2: From D, can we go to E? D is not connected to E. D is connected to A,B,C. So no.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 2b yields one path.\n\nSubcase 2c: From C, after A-C, can we go to E? C is not connected to E. C's connections are A,B,D. So no.\n\nTherefore, only Subcase 2b yields a path.\n\nThus, Case 2 (A→C) yields one path: A-C-D-B-E.\n\nCase 3: A→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited={A,D}, remaining: B, C, E.\n\nFrom D, connected to A (visited), B, C.\n\nOptions: B or C.\n\nSubcase 3a: A→D→B (3)\n\nCurrent: B, visited={A,D,B}, remaining: C, E.\n\nFrom B, connected to A (visited), C, D (visited), E.\n\nOptions: C or E.\n\nSubsubcase 3a1: A→D→B→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, visited={A,B,D,C}, remaining: E.\n\nFrom C, connected to A (visited), B (visited), D (visited). No connection to E. Dead end.\n\nSubsubcase 3a2: A→D→B→E (6)\n\nCurrent: E, visited={A,B,D,E}, remaining: C.\n\nFrom E, connected to A (visited), B (visited). No connection to C. Dead end.\n\nSubcase 3b: A→D→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, visited={A,D,C}, remaining: B, E.\n\nFrom C, connected to A (visited), B, D (visited). So can go to B.\n\nSubsubcase 3b1: A→D→C→B (3)\n\nCurrent: B, visited={A,D,C,B}, remaining: E.\n\nFrom B, connected to A (visited), C (visited), D (visited), E.\n\nSo go to E.\n\nSubsubsubcase 3b1a: A→D→C→B→E (6)\n\nCurrent: E, all nodes visited. This is a valid path: A-D-C-B-E.\n\nTherefore, this is another path.\n\nSubsubcase 3b2: From B, after A-D-C-B, can we go to E? Yes, as above. So only one path here.\n\nSubcase 3c: From D, can we go to E? D is not connected to E. So no.\n\nThus, Subcase 3b yields one path: A-D-C-B-E.\n\nTherefore, Case 3 (A→D) yields two paths: one from Subcase 3a (but that led to dead end) and one from Subcase 3b: A-D-C-B-E.\n\nWait, actually Subcase 3a led to dead ends, only Subcase 3b gives a path.\n\nTherefore, Case 3 yields one path.\n\nCase 4: A→E (6)\n\nCurrent: E, visited={A,E}, remaining: B, C, D.\n\nFrom E, connected to A (visited), B.\n\nSo only can go to B.\n\nSubcase 4a: A→E→B (3)\n\nCurrent: B, visited={A,E,B}, remaining: C, D.\n\nFrom B, connected to A (visited), C, D, E (visited). So options: C or D.\n\nSubsubcase 4a1: A→E→B→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, visited={A,B,C,E}, remaining: D.\n\nFrom C, connected to A (visited), B (visited), D.\n\nGo to D.\n\nSubsubsubcase 4a1a: A→E→B→C→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited all nodes. Path: A-E-B-C-D. This is a valid path ending at D, which is adjacent to A. So yes, this is a valid Hamiltonian path.\n\nSubsubcase 4a2: From B, go to D instead.\n\nSubsubcase 4a2: A→E→B→D (5)\n\nCurrent: D, visited={A,B,D,E}, remaining: C.\n\nFrom D, connected to A (visited), B (visited), C.\n\nGo to C.\n\nSubsubsubcase 4a2a: A→E→B→D→C (4)\n\nCurrent: C, all nodes visited. Path: A-E-B-D-C. Ends at C, which is adjacent to A. Valid.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 4a yields two paths:\n\n1. A-E-B-C-D\n\n2. A-E-B-D-C\n\nSubcase 4b: From B, after A-E-B, are there other options? No, only C and D.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 4a yields two paths.\n\nSubcase 4c: From E, after A-E, can we go to anywhere else? No, only B.\n\nTherefore, Case 4 yields two paths.\n\nTotal paths from all cases:\n\nCase 1: 0\n\nCase 2: 1\n\nCase 3: 1\n\nCase 4: 2\n\nTotal: 0 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 paths.\n\nTherefore, there are 4 Hamiltonian paths starting at A (node 2) in the subgraph, visiting all nodes {3,4,5,6}, ending at any of the nodes.\n\nEach of these paths corresponds to a unique Hamiltonian cycle when connected back to 1 via the ending node.\n\nFor example:\n\nPath 1 from Case 2: A-C-D-B-E → then from E back to 1: so the full cycle is 1-7-2-A-C-D-B-E-1, which is the same as Cycle 4: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1 (if we map A=2, B=3, C=4, D=5, E=6). Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, in the path A-C-D-B-E, translating back to original nodes:\n\nA=2, C=4, D=5, B=3, E=6.\n\nSo the path is 2-4-5-3-6. Then back to 1: so the full cycle is 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1, which is Cycle 1.\n\nSimilarly, the path from Case 3: A-D-C-B-E translates to 2-5-4-3-6, so the cycle is 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1, which is Cycle 2.\n\nThe two paths from Case 4:\n\nFirst path: A-E-B-C-D → 2-6-3-4-5 → cycle: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1 (Cycle 3).\n\nSecond path: A-E-B-D-C → 2-6-3-5-4 → cycle: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1 (Cycle 4).\n\nTherefore, the four paths correspond exactly to the four undirected cycles we found earlier. Therefore, this confirms that there are 4 undirected Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nThus, returning to the original problem, the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nHowever, wait a moment. In our recursive calculation above, we found 4 Hamiltonian paths in the subgraph starting at 2, each corresponding to a unique undirected cycle. Since each undirected cycle can be traversed in two directions, but in our fixed starting point and direction (starting at 1, going to 7, then to 2), we only counted each undirected cycle once. Therefore, the 4 undirected cycles correspond to 4 directed cycles in the fixed embedding. However, when considering all possible directed cycles (i.e., all possible starting points and directions), each undirected cycle gives 14 directed cycles (7 starting points × 2 directions). Thus, 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nAlternatively, since we found 4 undirected cycles, and each can be traversed in 2 directions, giving 8 directed cycles, but considering that rotations (starting points) also count as different, so for each undirected cycle, there are 7 starting points and 2 directions, so 14 directed cycles per undirected cycle.\n\nYes, that's correct. Therefore, 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 56.\n\nBut hold on, wait, in our recursive calculation above, we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction as going to 7 then to 2, and found 4 paths, each corresponding to one undirected cycle. Therefore, those 4 correspond to 4 directed cycles in that specific embedding. However, in reality, each undirected cycle can be rotated to start at any of the 7 nodes, and can be traversed in two directions, so each undirected cycle corresponds to 14 directed cycles. Since we found 4 undirected cycles, that gives 56 directed cycles.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 56.\n\nBut let me think again if there's any chance I missed an undirected cycle.\n\nSuppose someone constructs a different cycle, say:\n\n1-3-4-5-2-7-6-1\n\nWait, let's check the products:\n\n1-3: 3 ✔️\n\n3-4:12 ✔️\n\n4-5:20 ✔️\n\n5-2:10 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-6:42 ❌\n\nOh, 7-6 is 42 which is over 20. So invalid.\n\nAnother attempt:\n\n1-3-6-2-7-4-5-1\n\nCheck products:\n\n1-3:3 ✔️\n\n3-6:18 ✔️\n\n6-2:12 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-4:28 ❌\n\nNo good.\n\nHow about 1-3-5-4-2-7-6-1:\n\n1-3:3 ✔️\n\n3-5:15 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-2:8 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-6:42 ❌\n\nNope.\n\nAnother one: 1-4-5-2-7-3-6-1\n\nCheck:\n\n1-4:4 ✔️\n\n4-5:20 ✔️\n\n5-2:10 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-3:21 ❌\n\nNope.\n\nHmm, seems difficult to find another cycle.\n\nAlternatively, 1-5-4-3-6-2-7-1:\n\n1-5:5 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-3:12 ✔️\n\n3-6:18 ✔️\n\n6-2:12 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-1:7 ✔️\n\nAll products are ≤20! Wait a minute! This seems like a valid cycle.\n\nWait, let me list the nodes in order: 1,5,4,3,6,2,7,1.\n\nCheck each adjacent pair:\n\n1-5:5 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-3:12 ✔️\n\n3-6:18 ✔️\n\n6-2:12 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-1:7 ✔️\n\nYes! All products are ≤20. So this is another Hamiltonian cycle: 1-5-4-3-6-2-7-1.\n\nWait a second, this is different from the four we found earlier.\n\nWait, let me write this cycle in the standard order: 1-5-4-3-6-2-7-1.\n\nIs this cycle equivalent to one of the four we found?\n\nLet me see:\n\nOur earlier cycles were:\n\nCycle 1: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1\n\nCycle 2: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1\n\nCycle 3: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1\n\nCycle 4: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nThe new cycle is 1-5-4-3-6-2-7-1. Let me see if this can be rotated or reflected to match one of the existing cycles.\n\nIf we rotate the new cycle to start at 1, but the new cycle's sequence is 1-5-4-3-6-2-7-1. Let's see if this is a rotation of Cycle 3 or 4.\n\nCycle 3 is 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1. If we rotate this to start at 1, it's the same. If we rotate it to start at 5: 5-4-3-6-2-7-1-5, which is different.\n\nAlternatively, reflecting the new cycle: the reverse would be 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1, which is Cycle 3. Wait, let's reverse the new cycle:\n\nOriginal new cycle: 1-5-4-3-6-2-7-1\n\nReversed: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1, which is exactly Cycle 3.\n\nAh! So the new cycle is the reverse of Cycle 3. Since undirected cycles don't consider direction, they are the same cycle. Therefore, this new cycle is actually Cycle 3 traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, it's not a new undirected cycle, just the reverse of an existing one.\n\nTherefore, it doesn't add to the count of undirected cycles.\n\nSimilarly, if I reverse any of our four cycles, I get the same undirected cycle.\n\nTherefore, this attempt didn't find a new cycle.\n\nAnother try: 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1\n\nCheck products:\n\n1-6:6 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-5:15 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-2:8 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-1:7 ✔️\n\nAll good. This is the cycle 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1.\n\nIs this a new undirected cycle?\n\nLet's see: written in order: 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1.\n\nIf we rotate this to start at 1, it's the same as the sequence above. Let's see if this is equivalent to one of the existing cycles.\n\nCompare to Cycle 4: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1.\n\nIf we rotate Cycle 4 to start at 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1, let's see:\n\nCycle 4 is 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1. If we rotate it to start at 6: 6-3-5-4-1-7-2-6. Not the same.\n\nAlternatively, reverse Cycle 4: 1-4-5-3-6-2-7-1, which is different.\n\nAlternatively, the new cycle 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1.\n\nLet me see if this can be rearranged into one of the existing cycles via rotation or reflection.\n\nSuppose we take the new cycle and rotate it so that 6 is first: 6-3-5-4-2-7-1-6. Compare to Cycle 3: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1. Not the same.\n\nAlternatively, reflect the new cycle: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1, which is Cycle 1.\n\nWait, reflecting the new cycle:\n\nOriginal new cycle: 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1.\n\nReverse the direction: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1, which is Cycle 1.\n\nTherefore, this new cycle is the reverse of Cycle 1, hence the same undirected cycle.\n\nTherefore, it doesn't count as a new one.\n\nThus, it seems that all possible cycles are accounted for in the four we found.\n\nTherefore, I think we can be confident that there are indeed four undirected Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nTherefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nHowever, hold on! In our recursive approach above, we found 4 paths starting at node 2, each leading to a unique undirected cycle. However, when we calculated the number of undirected cycles as 4, that included all possible cycles regardless of where they start. Therefore, the four undirected cycles are indeed distinct and account for all possibilities.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 56.\n\nBut wait, let me recall that in the problem statement, it says \"arrange the numbers 1,2,3,4,5,6, and 7 around a circle such that the product of every pair of adjacent numbers on the circle is at most 20. (Rotations and reflections count as different arrangements.)\"\n\nTherefore, each distinct circular arrangement, considering rotations and reflections as different, is counted separately. In graph theory terms, this is exactly the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in the graph, because each directed cycle corresponds to a unique arrangement where the starting point and direction matter.\n\nSince we have 4 undirected Hamiltonian cycles, each giving rise to 14 directed cycles (7 starting points × 2 directions), then total is 4×14=56.\n\nTherefore, I believe the answer is 56.\n\nHowever, to be absolutely thorough, let me consider that in our enumeration of undirected cycles, we might have missed a cycle that can be started elsewhere, but given the earlier attempts to find another cycle failed, and the recursive count confirmed only 4 paths from node 2 leading to 4 undirected cycl