n\nFrom 3, adjacent nodes:1,2,4,5,6. Already visited:2,7,1,6. Next can be 4 or 5.\n\nSubsubcase B4a: 2-7-1-6-3-4\n\nFrom 3 to 4.\n\nSequence: 2-7-1-6-3-4\n\nFrom 4, adjacent nodes:1,2,3,5. Already visited:2,7,1,6,3. Next can be 5.\n\nSequence: 2-7-1-6-3-4-5\n\nFrom 5, adjacent nodes:1,2,3,4. Already visited:2,7,1,6,3,4. Next must connect back to 2? 5-2: 10 ≤20, yes. So the cycle is 2-7-1-6-3-4-5-2.\n\nCheck adjacencies:\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-1:7 ✔️\n\n1-6:6 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-4:12 ✔️\n\n4-5:20 ✔️\n\n5-2:10 ✔️\n\nAll good. So that's a valid cycle: 2-7-1-6-3-4-5-2.\n\nSubsubcase B4a yields one valid cycle.\n\nSubsubcase B4b: 2-7-1-6-3-5\n\nFrom 3 to 5.\n\nSequence: 2-7-1-6-3-5\n\nFrom 5, adjacent nodes:1,2,3,4. Already visited:2,7,1,6,3. Next can be 4.\n\nSequence: 2-7-1-6-3-5-4\n\nFrom 4, adjacent nodes:1,2,3,5. Already visited:2,7,1,6,3,5. Next must connect back to 2? 4-2: 8 ≤20. So the cycle is 2-7-1-6-3-5-4-2.\n\nCheck adjacencies:\n\n2-7:14 ✔️\n\n7-1:7 ✔️\n\n1-6:6 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-5:15 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-2:8 ✔️\n\nAll products okay. So another valid cycle: 2-7-1-6-3-5-4-2.\n\nTherefore, Subsubcase B4b yields one valid cycle.\n\nThus, Subcase B4 yields two valid cycles.\n\nTherefore, summarizing Case B:\n\nSubcase B1 (next after 1 is 3): 0\n\nSubcase B2 (next after 1 is 4): 1 (from B2b)\n\nSubcase B3 (next after 1 is 5): 1 (from B3b)\n\nSubcase B4 (next after 1 is 6): 2 (from B4a and B4b)\n\nTotal for Case B: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 valid cycles.\n\nTherefore, total number of valid cycles in the graph is Case A (4) + Case B (4) = 8.\n\nHowever, wait a second. Hold on. In Case A and Case B, we fixed the starting point as 1 or 2 respectively, but in reality, in circular arrangements, the starting point can be anywhere. However, in our analysis above, we fixed the sequence starting with 1 or 2 to account for the position of 7. However, in reality, the entire cycle can be rotated such that 7 can be in any position. Wait, but in our analysis, we considered both cases where 7 is between 1 and 2 in either order, but since rotations are considered different arrangements, we don't need to adjust for rotational symmetry. Wait, but in our analysis, we fixed the starting point as 1 or 2 to make the counting manageable, but since rotations are considered different, each of these cycles we found can be rotated to start at any position, but we have already counted all possible arrangements by considering all possible starting points in the cycle.\n\nWait, actually, no. When we fixed the sequence to start with 1-7-2 or 2-7-1, we were effectively considering all cyclic permutations where 7 is between 1 and 2, but in the graph, there are multiple cycles that can be rotated such that 7 is in any position. Wait, but in our analysis above, when we fixed the starting point as 1 or 2, we might have undercounted or overcounted?\n\nWait, perhaps not. Let me think again.\n\nIn Case A, we considered all cycles that contain the segment 1-7-2 (in that order). But in reality, the segment could be anywhere in the cycle. However, by fixing the starting point as 1, we are effectively counting all cycles that have 1 followed by 7 followed by 2 somewhere in the cycle, but considering that the cycle can start at any node. Wait, actually, no. When we fix the starting point as 1, we are only counting the cycles where 1 is the first element, but since rotations are considered different arrangements, each rotation of a cycle is a different arrangement. Therefore, by fixing the starting point, we might have only counted a fraction of the total arrangements.\n\nWait, this is a critical point. Let me clarify:\n\nIn graph theory, a Hamiltonian cycle is typically considered as an equivalence class under rotation and reflection. However, in this problem, rotations and reflections are considered different arrangements, so each distinct cyclic permutation (i.e., each rotation and reflection) is a distinct arrangement. Therefore, the total number of arrangements is equal to the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in the graph multiplied by 1? Wait, no. Wait, actually, each undirected Hamiltonian cycle corresponds to two directed cycles (clockwise and counterclockwise), and each directed cycle can be started at any of the 7 nodes. Therefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles (i.e., arrangements where direction and starting point matter) is equal to the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles multiplied by 14 (7 starting points × 2 directions). Wait, actually, for each undirected cycle, there are 7×2=14 directed cycles (since you can start at any of the 7 nodes and go in either of the 2 directions). Therefore, if we let C be the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles, then the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 14C. However, in our problem, we are to count all the directed Hamiltonian cycles (since rotations and reflections are considered different), so the answer would be 14C.\n\nBut in our earlier analysis, when we fixed the starting point and considered specific sequences like 1-7-2..., we were effectively counting the directed Hamiltonian cycles that start with 1-7-2 or 2-7-1, but only in one direction. Wait, perhaps my earlier approach was not accounting for all possibilities because I fixed the starting point.\n\nWait, let me re-express:\n\nSuppose that in the graph, there are C undirected Hamiltonian cycles. Each undirected cycle can be traversed in two directions, and each traversal can start at any of the 7 nodes. Therefore, each undirected cycle corresponds to 14 directed cycles (7 starting points × 2 directions). Therefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles (which is what we need for the problem, since rotations and reflections are different) is 14C.\n\nHowever, in my earlier analysis, when I fixed the starting point as 1 or 2 and considered specific paths, I might have only counted a subset of these directed cycles. Specifically, in Case A, I fixed the starting point as 1 and considered 