is that node 7 must be between 1 and 2, so any valid arrangement must have 1 and 2 adjacent to 7. Therefore, the rest of the nodes must form a path connecting the two \"ends\" of this 1-7-2 or 2-7-1 segment.\n\nHowever, in my analysis, I found only 8 such directed cycles. But considering that each of these can be rotated to start at any node, the total number should be 8 × 7 = 56? Because for each of the 8 directed cycles, there are 7 rotations. But wait, each directed cycle has exactly 7 rotations (since it's a cycle of length 7), so the total number of directed cycles would be 8 × 7? No, that can’t be.\n\nActually, each directed cycle has 7 rotational positions, but they are all considered distinct in the problem. Therefore, if there is one undirected cycle, it corresponds to 14 directed cycles (7 rotations × 2 directions). If I found 8 directed cycles, that suggests there are 8 / 14 = 4/7 of an undirected cycle, which is impossible. Therefore, my analysis must have an error.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I missed some cycles in my initial analysis.\n\nLet me try to construct another cycle manually.\n\nConsider the sequence: 1-2-4-5-3-6-7-1.\n\nCheck adjacencies:\n\n1-2: okay\n\n2-4: okay\n\n4-5: okay\n\n5-3: okay\n\n3-6: okay\n\n6-7: Not allowed (6×7=42>20). Invalid.\n\nAnother try: 1-3-4-5-2-7-6-1\n\nEdges:\n\n1-3: okay\n\n3-4: okay\n\n4-5: okay\n\n5-2: okay\n\n2-7: okay\n\n7-6: invalid.\n\nAnother: 1-5-4-2-7-1... can't repeat 1.\n\nAnother: 1-4-2-7-6-3-5-1\n\nEdges:\n\n1-4: okay\n\n4-2: okay\n\n2-7: okay\n\n7-6: invalid.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother: 1-6-3-4-5-2-7-1\n\nEdges:\n\n1-6: okay\n\n6-3: okay\n\n3-4: okay\n\n4-5: okay\n\n5-2: okay\n\n2-7: okay\n\n7-1: okay\n\nThis works! Let's verify all edges:\n\n1-6:6 ≤20 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ≤20 ✔️\n\n3-4:12 ≤20 ✔️\n\n4-5:20 ≤20 ✔️\n\n5-2:10 ≤20 ✔️\n\n2-7:14 ≤20 ✔️\n\n7-1:7 ≤20 ✔️\n\nYes! This is a valid cycle: 1-6-3-4-5-2-7-1.\n\nBut in my earlier analysis, this wasn't found because I fixed the starting point as 1 and considered the segment starting with 1-7-2 or 2-7-1. However, this cycle starts with 1-6, so it doesn't have the 1-7-2 segment at the beginning. But in this cycle, where is 7 located?\n\nIn this cycle: 1-6-3-4-5-2-7-1, the node 7 is between 2 and 1. So the segment is ...-2-7-1-..., which is the Case B segment. Therefore, this cycle is actually included in Case B!\n\nSpecifically, this cycle can be written as starting at 1: 1-6-3-4-5-2-7-1. To see if this is in my Case B analysis:\n\nCase B starts with 2-7-1. Let's rotate this cycle to start with 2:\n\nStarting at 2: 2-7-1-6-3-4-5-2. Which is exactly the third Case B cycle I found: 2-7-1-6-3-4-5-2.\n\nTherefore, this cycle is already counted in my Case B analysis.\n\nSimilarly, if I rotate this cycle to start at 6: 6-3-4-5-2-7-1-6, which is a rotation of the Case B cycle.\n\nTherefore, my initial analysis did include this cycle, just not starting at 1.\n\nTherefore, all valid cycles are indeed accounted for in the 8 directed cycles I found, each corresponding to a unique directed Hamiltonian cycle, and considering that rotations and reflections are different, the total number is indeed 8.\n\nWait, but in my Case B analysis, I found four cycles starting with 2-7-1, but this cycle starting with 2-7-1 is one of them: 2-7-1-6-3-4-5-2, which is the third one. So yes, it's included.\n\nSimilarly, the cycle I just found, when rotated to start at 1, is 1-6-3-4-5-2-7-1, which is not starting with 1-7-2 but is part of the Case B cycles when considering different starting points.\n\nTherefore, my initial count of 8 directed cycles is correct because they encompass all possible directed Hamiltonian cycles, considering that any cycle can be rotated to start with any node, and reflections are considered different. Wait, but reflections would be different arrangements as well.\n\nWait a second, in my analysis, I only considered the two directions (clockwise and counter-clockwise) for the segments starting with 1-7-2 or 2-7-1, but reflections would f