case 3a leads to dead ends.\n\nSubcase 3b: From 5, go to 4 instead of 3.\n\nPath: 2-5-4, remaining vertices: {1,3,6}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Excluding visited: 2,4,5. Available: 1,3.\n\nCannot go to 1 yet, so go to 3.\n\nSubcase 3b1: 2-5-4-3\n\nPath: 2-5-4-3, remaining vertices: {1,6}\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Excluding visited: 2,4,5,3. Available: 1 and 6.\n\nCannot go to 1 yet, so go to 6.\n\nSubcase 3b1a: 2-5-4-3-6\n\nPath: 2-5-4-3-6, remaining vertex: {1}\n\nFrom 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Excluding visited: 2,3,4,5,6. Available: 1. So go to 1.\n\nThus, the path is 2-5-4-3-6-1. That's valid.\n\nSubcase 3b1a is valid.\n\nSubcase 3b2: From 3, instead of going to 6, go to 1? But can't yet, since 6 is left. So no.\n\nTherefore, only one path in Subcase 3b: 2-5-4-3-6-1.\n\nSubcase 3c: From 5, after 2-5, can we go to 1? No, because we can't visit 1 until the end.\n\nTherefore, only Subcase 3b yields a valid path.\n\nCase 4: First move from 2 to 6.\n\nPath: 2-6, remaining vertices: {1,3,4,5}\n\nFrom 6, neighbors in G'' are 1,2,3. Excluding visited: 2,6. So available: 1 and 3.\n\nCannot go to 1 yet, so must go to 3.\n\nSubcase 4a: 2-6-3\n\nPath: 2-6-3, remaining vertices: {1,4,5}\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Excluding visited: 2,3,6. Available: 1,4,5.\n\nCannot go to 1 yet, so go to 4 or 5.\n\nSubcase 4a1: 2-6-3-4\n\nPath: 2-6-3-4, remaining vertices: {1,5}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Excluding visited: 2,3,4. Available: 1 and 5.\n\nCannot go to 1 yet, so go to 5.\n\nSubcase 4a1a: 2-6-3-4-5\n\nPath: 2-6-3-4-5, remaining vertex: {1}\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Excluding visited: 2,3,4,5. Available: 1. So go to 1.\n\nThus, the path is 2-6-3-4-5-1. That's valid.\n\nSubcase 4a1a is valid.\n\nSubcase 4a2: From 4, after 2-6-3-4, instead of going to 5, go to 1? Can't yet, since 5 is left. So no.\n\nSubcase 4a3: From 3, after 2-6-3, instead of going to 4, go to 5.\n\nPath: 2-6-3-5\n\nRemaining vertices: {1,4}\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Excluding visited: 2,3,5. Available: 1 and 4.\n\nCannot go to 1 yet, so go to 4.\n\nSubcase 4a3a: 2-6-3-5-4\n\nPath: 2-6-3-5-4, remaining vertex: {1}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Excluding visited: 2,3,4,5. Available: 1. So go to 1.\n\nThus, path is 2-6-3-5-4-1. That's valid.\n\nSubcase 4a3a is valid.\n\nSubcase 4a4: From 3, after 2-6-3, going to 1? Not allowed yet.\n\nTherefore, Subcases 4a1 and 4a3 yield two valid paths.\n\nSubcase 4b: From 6, after 2-6, instead of going to 3, go to 1? But can't, since we can't visit 1 yet. So no.\n\nTherefore, Case 4 yields two valid paths: 2-6-3-4-5-1 and 2-6-3-5-4-1.\n\nNow, let's tally up all valid paths we found:\n\nCase 2 yielded 1 path: 2-4-5-3-6-1\n\nCase 3 yielded 1 path: 2-5-4-3-6-1\n\nCase 4 yielded 2 paths: 2-6-3-4-5-1 and 2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nCase 1 yielded 0 paths.\n\nTherefore, total N = 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 directed Hamiltonian paths from 2 to 1 in G''.\n\nWait, let me list them explicitly to confirm:\n\nFrom Case 2:\n\n1. 2-4-5-3-6-1\n\nFrom Case 3:\n\n2. 2-5-4-3-6-1\n\nFrom Case 4:\n\n3. 2-6-3-4-5-1\n\n4. 2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nYes, that's four paths.\n\nTherefore, N = 4.\n\nTherefore, the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles in G is H = N / 2 = 4 / 2 = 2.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, why divided by 2? Because each undirected cycle is counted twice in the directed paths (once in each direction). Since we fixed the starting point at 2 and ending at 1, each undirected cycle that includes the segment 1-7-2 will have two directed paths: one going clockwise and one counter-clockwise through the cycle. However, in our counting of N, we only counted paths that start at 2 and end at 1, which is a specific direction. Therefore, each undirected cycle corresponds to exactly one of these directed paths? Wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, in the undirected cycle, there are two possible directions. However, in our case, we fixed the starting point at 2 and the ending point at 1, so each undirected cycle that contains the edge between 2 and 1 (through 7) will have exactly one directed path from 2 to 1 in G''. Therefore, actually, H = N, since each undirected cycle is uniquely determined by the directed path from 2 to 1 in G''. Wait, but that can't be, because in an undirected cycle, the path from 2 to 1 can be traversed in two directions, but in our case, we fixed the starting point at 2 and the ending point at 1, so each undirected cycle corresponds to exactly one directed path in this count. Therefore, actually, H = N = 4?\n\nWait, confusion arises because in the undirected cycle, the path from 2 to 1 is just one direction, and the reverse path would start at 1 and end at 2, which is a different directed path but the same undirected cycle. Since we are counting only paths from 2 to 1, each undirected cycle is counted once in N. Therefore, H = N = 4.\n\nWait, but in our earlier reasoning, we thought that H = N / 2, but maybe that was a mistake.\n\nWait, let's think again. In the graph G, each undirected Hamiltonian cycle can be traversed in two directions. However, in our counting of N, we fixed the starting vertex at 2 and the ending vertex at 1, so each undirected cycle that includes the edge between 2 and 1 (via the segment 1-7-2) is represented exactly once in N. Since in an undirected cycle, the edge between 2 and 1 is present (through the segment 1-7-2), then each undirected cycle has exactly one directed path from 2 to 1 in G''. Therefore, the number of undirected cycles H is exactly equal to N, which is 4.\n\nWait, but let's verify with an example.\n\nTake one of the paths we found, say 2-4-5-3-6-1. If we add the segment 1-7-2 to the beginning, we get the cycle: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1. Is this a valid Hamiltonian cycle in G?\n\nLet's check the edges:\n\n1-7: allowed (product 7 ≤20)\n\n7-2: allowed (product 14 ≤20)\n\n2-4: allowed (8 ≤20)\n\n4-5: allowed (20 ≤20)\n\n5-3: allowed (15 ≤20)\n\n3-6: allowed (18 ≤20)\n\n6-1: allowed (6 ≤20)\n\nAnd then 1-7 again to close the cycle. Wait, but in the cycle, after 6-1, we need to go back to 1, but the last edge is 6-1, and then 1 is connected back to 7? Wait, no. Wait, in the cycle as constructed: starting at 1, going to 7, then 2, then 4,5,3,6, and then back to 1. So the edges are:\n\n1-7, 7-2, 2-4, 4-5, 5-3, 3-6, 6-1.\n\nYes, that's seven edges, forming a cycle, and all edges are present in G. Let me confirm each edge:\n\n1-7: yes\n\n7-2: yes\n\n2-4: yes\n\n4-5: yes\n\n5-3: yes\n\n3-6: yes\n\n6-1: yes\n\nAll edges are allowed. Good.\n\nSimilarly, take another path: 2-5-4-3-6-1. Adding 1-7-2 gives the cycle:\n\n1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1.\n\nEdges:\n\n1-7, 7-2, 2-5, 5-4, 4-3, 3-6, 6-1.\n\nCheck edges:\n\n2-5: yes (10 ≤20)\n\n5-4: yes (20 ≤20)\n\n4-3: yes (12 ≤20)\n\n3-6: yes (18 ≤20)\n\nAll edges valid. Good.\n\nThird path: 2-6-3-4-5-1. Adding 1-7-2:\n\nCycle: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1.\n\nEdges:\n\n1-7, 7-2, 2-6, 6-3, 3-4, 4-5, 5-1.\n\nCheck edges:\n\n2-6: yes (12 ≤20)\n\n6-3: yes (18 ≤20)\n\n3-4: yes (12 ≤20)\n\n4-5: yes (20 ≤20)\n\n5-1: yes (5 ≤20)\n\nAll edges valid.\n\nFourth path: 2-6-3-5-4-1. Adding 1-7-2:\n\nCycle: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1.\n\nEdges:\n\n1-7, 7-2, 2-6, 6-3, 3-5, 5-4, 4-1.\n\nCheck edges:\n\n2-6: yes\n\n6-3: yes\n\n3-5: yes (15 ≤20)\n\n5-4: yes\n\n4-1: yes (4 ≤20)\n\nAll edges valid.\n\nTherefore, all four paths correspond to distinct Hamiltonian cycles in G. Since we found four such paths, and each corresponds to a unique undirected cycle (since the direction is fixed by the path), then H = 4.\n\nTherefore, the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles in G is 4.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem: the number of arrangements is 14 × H = 14 × 4 = 56.\n\nWait, hold on. Earlier, we reasoned that each undirected Hamiltonian cycle corresponds to 14 directed arrangements (7 starting points × 2 directions). But in our case, since we fixed the segment 1-7-2 in the cycle, does that affect the count?\n\nWait, actually, in our analysis, we considered that each undirected Hamiltonian cycle in G can be traversed in two directions, and for each direction, there are 7 starting points, but since the cycle is fixed with the segment 1-7-2, perhaps the count is different?\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the graph G, the cycles we found don't necessarily have the segment 1-7-2 in a specific place. Wait, actually, in our construction, we fixed the segment 1-7-2 as part of the cycle because vertex 7 must be between 1 and 2. Therefore, every Hamiltonian cycle in G must include the edge 1-7 and 7-2, or 2-7 and 7-1, but since it's undirected, those are the same.\n\nWait a second, actually, in our construction above, we assumed that in any Hamiltonian cycle, the vertices 1,7,2 must form a path of length 2, i.e., either 1-7-2 or 2-7-1. Since the graph G has edges 1-7 and 2-7, so those are the only edges incident to 7, so indeed, in any cycle, 7 must be between 1 and 2. Therefore, every Hamiltonian cycle in G must include the subpath 1-7-2 or 2-7-1. Therefore, all Hamiltonian cycles in G are of the form [1-7-2] followed by a path from 2 to 1 covering all other vertices, or [2-7-1] followed by a path from 1 to 2 covering all other vertices. But since the cycle is undirected, these are the same cycle traversed in opposite directions. Therefore, each undirected Hamiltonian cycle is counted once in our count of H = 4. Therefore, each such cycle can be traversed in two directions, and for each direction, there are 7 starting points, but since the cycle is fixed with the 1-7-2 segment, perhaps the count is different.\n\nWait, no, actually, in our calculation of H, we found 4 undirected cycles. Each undirected cycle can be traversed in two directions, and for each direction, there are 7 starting points, but since the cycle is a closed loop, the number of distinct directed cycles (with labeled starting points) is 14 per undirected cycle. Therefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in G is 4 × 14 = 56. However, in our problem, the arrangements are exactly the directed Hamiltonian cycles, because each arrangement is a labeled circular arrangement where rotations and reflections are different. Therefore, the total number of arrangements is 56.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, in our calculation above, when we fixed the segment 1-7-2 in the cycle and counted the paths from 2 to 1 in G'', we found 4 undirected cycles. Each undirected cycle corresponds to two directed cycles (clockwise and counter-clockwise). For each directed cycle, there are 7 possible starting points, but since the cycle is a loop, starting at any point is equivalent under rotation. However, in our problem, rotations are considered different arrangements. Therefore, for each directed cycle (i.e., each cyclic order), there are 7 different arrangements corresponding to the 7 rotations. Additionally, each directed cycle can be traversed in two directions, so for each undirected cycle, there are 14 directed arrangements (7 rotations × 2 directions). Wait, actually, no: for a given directed cycle (i.e., a specific cyclic order), there are 7 rotations (each starting point) and each rotation is a different arrangement because the positions are labeled. However, the direction is already fixed in the directed cycle. For example, if we have a directed cycle A → B → C → A, then the arrangements are [A,B,C], [B,C,A], [C,A,B], and their reverses [A,C,B], [B,A,C], [C,B,A]. Wait, actually, no. Wait, if direction matters, then the reverse of the cycle is a different directed cycle. Therefore, for each undirected cycle, there are two directed cycles (clockwise and counter-clockwise), and each directed cycle has 7 starting points, so total arrangements per undirected cycle is 14.\n\nTherefore, since we have H = 4 undirected cycles, total arrangements would be 4 × 14 = 56.\n\nBut in our earlier calculation, when we counted the paths from 2 to 1 in G'' as N = 4, and each such path corresponds to a unique directed cycle when combined with the 1-7-2 segment, then the total number of directed cycles is exactly N × 1 (since we fixed the starting point at 1 and the direction towards 2 via 7), but no, actually each path from 2 to 1 in G'' gives a unique directed cycle when combined with the 1-7-2 segment. Wait, let's see:\n\nTake one of the paths in G'': say 2-4-5-3-6-1. When we add the segment 1-7-2 to the beginning, we get the directed cycle: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1. This is a specific directed cycle (clockwise, say). The reverse cycle would be 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1, which would correspond to a path from 1 to 6 in G''. But in our count of N, we only counted paths from 2 to 1, so the reverse cycle isn't counted in N. Therefore, each undirected cycle corresponds to exactly one path in N (the one going from 2 to 1), and its reverse corresponds to a path from 1 to 2 in G'', which we didn't count. Since we only counted N = 4 paths from 2 to 1, that gives us 4 directed cycles (each with a specific direction). Therefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in G is 4 (from N) plus 4 (their reverses), totaling 8? Wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, each undirected cycle has two directed versions. Since we found 4 undirected cycles, that would give 8 directed cycles. However, in our path counting in G'', we only found 4 directed paths from 2 to 1, which correspond to 4 directed cycles (each with the direction going from 2 to 1 via the 1-7 segment). The other 4 directed cycles would be the reverses, going from 1 to 2 via the 2-7 segment, but those were not counted in N because we fixed the start at 2. Therefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 8.\n\nBut then, if each directed cycle corresponds to 7 starting points, but actually, no: in the problem's terms, each arrangement is a labeling of the circle's positions, so each directed cycle can be started at any of the 7 positions, but since we consider rotations as different, each directed cycle (i.e., each cyclic order) gives rise to 7 different arrangements (rotations). Additionally, the direction can be reversed, giving another 7 arrangements. Therefore, for each undirected cycle, there are 14 arrangements. Since there are 4 undirected cycles, that gives 4 × 14 = 56 arrangements. Alternatively, since there are 8 directed cycles (4 undirected × 2 directions), each directed cycle has 7 rotations, so 8 × 7 = 56.\n\nYes, so either way, it's 56.\n\nBut in our earlier calculation, when we considered the paths in G'' from 2 to 1, we found 4 directed paths, each corresponding to a unique directed cycle when combined with the 1-7-2 segment. The reverses of these cycles would correspond to paths from 1 to 2 in G'' going through the other vertices, which we didn't count. Therefore, those reverse cycles are separate and should also be counted, leading to a total of 8 directed cycles, each giving 7 arrangements (rotations), totaling 56.\n\nTherefore, the total number of arrangements is 56.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me verify with an example. Take the first cycle we found: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1. If we rotate this arrangement so that it starts at 2, we get 2-4-5-3-6-1-7-2, which is a different arrangement because the starting position is different. Similarly, rotating to start at 4 gives 4-5-3-6-1-7-2-4, etc. Each rotation is a different arrangement. Additionally, the reverse cycle: 1-6-3-5-4-2-7-1 is another directed cycle, which when rotated gives another set of 7 arrangements. Therefore, each of the 4 undirected cycles gives 14 arrangements (7 rotations × 2 directions), so 4 × 14 = 56 total arrangements.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 56? But let me see if that makes sense.\n\nWait, but let me think again: in our count of N, the number of paths from 2 to 1 in G'' was 4. Each such path, when combined with the 1-7-2 segment, gives a directed cycle. The reverse cycle (going the other way around) would require a path from 1 to 2 in G'' that goes through the other vertices. Since G'' is the same graph, the number of such paths should also be 4, leading to 8 directed cycles. Hence, 8 directed cycles × 7 rotations? Wait no, rotations are already accounted for in the directed cycles.\n\nWait, actually, in graph theory terms, a directed Hamiltonian cycle is a specific cyclic order with a direction. So if you have a directed cycle, rotating it doesn't give a new directed cycle; it's the same directed cycle starting at a different vertex. Therefore, the number of distinct directed Hamiltonian cycles in a graph is (number of undirected cycles) × 2. Because for each undirected cycle, you can have two directions. Then, when considering labeled vertices (i.e., arrangements where rotations are different), each directed cycle corresponds to 7 different arrangements (rotations). Wait, no: actually, if you fix the direction, then rotating the cycle gives different arrangements because the starting point is different, but the direction remains the same. For example, the directed cycle 1→2→3→4→5→6→7→1 is different from 2→3→4→5→6→7→1→2 because the starting point is different, but the direction is the same. Therefore, for each directed cycle (i.e., each cyclic order with a specific direction), there are 7 different arrangements (rotations). Therefore, if there are D directed cycles (accounting for both directions), then the total number of arrangements is D × 7.\n\nIn our case, the number of undirected cycles H is 4, so the number of directed cycles D is 2 × H = 8. Therefore, total arrangements would be D × 7 = 8 × 7 = 56. So that matches.\n\nAlternatively, since each undirected cycle can be traversed in two directions, giving two directed cycles, and each directed cycle has 7 rotational arrangements, so total is 4 × 2 × 7 = 56.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 56.\n\nBut wait, let me check for possible mistakes in the counting of N.\n\nEarlier, when we counted the paths in G'' from 2 to 1, we found 4 paths:\n\n1. 2-4-5-3-6-1\n\n2. 2-5-4-3-6-1\n\n3. 2-6-3-4-5-1\n\n4. 2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nAre there any other paths that we might have missed?\n\nLet me go through the cases again quickly.\n\nCase 1: 2-3: led to dead ends.\n\