is a valid Hamiltonian cycle!\n\nGreat! So that's one cycle.\n\nNow, let's see if there are others in this subcase.\n\nContinuing from Subcase 1b2: After reaching 6, we close the cycle back to 1, which is valid. So that gives us one cycle.\n\nNow, are there other paths in Subcase 1b?\n\nAfter Subcase 1b2, we've found one cycle. Let's see if there are other branches.\n\nWait, after Subcase 1b2, we went from 3 to 6. Is there another choice at any point?\n\nLet me retrace:\n\nStarting from 1-7-2-4-5-3.\n\nAt node 3, the available moves were to 6 (since 5 was already used? Wait, no: at node 3, after coming from 5, the used nodes are {1,2,4,5,7,3}? Wait, let's check:\n\nWait in Subcase 1b2: Path is 1-7-2-4-5-3.\n\nUsed nodes: 1,7,2,4,5,3. So remaining node is 6. Therefore, from 3, only available node is 6, since 3 is connected to 6. So that path is forced. So only one path here.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 1b2 yields one cycle.\n\nNow, returning to Subcase 1b: from 4, going to 5 gave us one cycle. The other option from 4 was going to 3, which we saw led to a dead end. So only one cycle from Subcase 1b.\n\nSubcase 1c: From 2, go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5. Used: {1,2,5,7}\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Used: 1,2,5,7. Available: 3,4.\n\nSo from 5, can go to 3 or 4.\n\nSubcase 1c1: 1-7-2-5-3\n\nUsed: {1,2,3,5,7}\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Used: 1,2,3,5,7. Available: 4,6.\n\nGo to 4 or 6.\n\nSubcase 1c1a: 1-7-2-5-3-4\n\nUsed: {1,2,3,4,5,7}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. All used except... remaining node is 6. 4 is not connected to 6. Stuck.\n\nSubcase 1c1b: 1-7-2-5-3-6\n\nUsed: {1,2,3,5,6,7}\n\nFrom 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. All used except 4. 6 not connected to 4. Stuck.\n\nSubcase 1c2: From 5, go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4. Used: {1,2,4,5,7}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Used: 1,2,4,5,7. Available: 3.\n\nGo to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4-3. Used: {1,2,3,4,5,7}\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Used: 1,2,3,4,5,7. Available: 6.\n\nGo to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6. Used: all 