\n\nNow, let's tally up all the cycles found in Case 1 (starting with 1-7-2):\n\n- Subcase 1b: 1 cycle\n\n- Subcase 1c: 1 cycle\n\n- Subcase 1d: 2 cycles\n\nTotal in Case 1: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 cycles.\n\nBut wait, hold on: in Subcase 1b, we had one cycle: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1\n\nIn Subcase 1c, we had 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1\n\nIn Subcase 1d, we had two cycles:\n\nFirst: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1\n\nSecond: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nWait, but in Subcase 1d, the second path was 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1, right?\n\nYes. So those are distinct cycles.\n\nSo total of four cycles starting with 1-7-2.\n\nNow, we need to consider the other case where the triplet is 2-7-1.\n\nWait, but in an undirected cycle, the direction doesn't matter, so the cycle 1-7-2-... is the same as 2-7-1-... when considered undirected. However, since we are counting directed cycles later, we might need to account for directionality here. Wait, but in the above, we fixed the starting point as 1 and went clockwise as 1-7-2-..., but if we instead went the other way, starting at 1 and going to 2 first, then to 7, then to 1, but that would require 7 to be adjacent to 2 and 1, which it is, but we need to see if that can form a cycle.\n\nWait a second, actually in the above analysis, we fixed the starting point as 1 and went to 7 next. But in an undirected cycle, the direction can be reversed. However, since we are fixing the starting point as 1, we need to consider both directions from 1.\n\nWait, hold on. When we fixed the starting point as 1, the first step could be to either neighbor of 1, which are all nodes except none—actually, 1 is connected to everyone, so from 1, we can go to any of the other 6 nodes. However, in our previous analysis, we constrained the path to go to 7 first, but actually, we should consider all possibilities for the first step.\n\nWait a minute, I think I made a mistake here. Earlier, I assumed that because 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, we have to have the triplet 1-7-2 or 2-7-1 in the cycle. However, when building