 include all nodes. Therefore, this path is invalid.\n\nSubcase 2b: From 2, go to another node, say 3.\n\nPath: 1-2-3. Used: {1,2,3}.\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Used: 1,2,3. Available: 4,5,6.\n\nProceeding from 3, we can go to 4,5, or 6.\n\nWe need to eventually include 7 adjacent to both 1 and 2. Since we've left 1 and 2, we need to have a path that connects back to 1 through 7 and connects 7 to 2.\n\nThis seems complicated. Let me see if such a cycle is possible.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's impossible to have a Hamiltonian cycle starting with 1-2-... because 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, but if we start at 1-2, then 7 has to be adjacent to 2 and 1, but to include 7 in the cycle without getting stuck, we might need to insert 7 between 2 and some other node, but then how do we get back to 1?\n\nAlternatively, let's try:\n\nStart at 1-2-7. But then from 7, can only go back to 2 or 1, both already visited. So that doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, start at 1-2-3-... and eventually loop back to 1 via 7.\n\nSuppose we go 1-2-3-4-5-6-7-1. Let's check the adjacencies and the products.\n\nCheck the products:\n\n1-2: 2 ✔️\n\n2-3: 6 ✔️\n\n3-4: 12 ✔️\n\n4-5: 20 ✔️\n\n5-6: 30 >20 ❌\n\nOh, 5×6=30 which is over 20. So that doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, different path.\n\nStart at 1-2-7 is invalid.\n\nStart at 1-2-3-7? But from 3, can we go to 7? 3 and 7: 3×7=21 >20. Not allowed.\n\n3 and 7 are not adjacent in the graph, since 3's neighbors are up to 6, and 7's neighbors are only 1 and 2. So 3 and 7 are not adjacent. Therefore, cannot go from 3 to 7.\n\nSimilarly, from 2, going to 4:\n\nPath: 1-2-4. Then from 4, can go to 1,2,3,5.\n\nBut 1 and 2 are used, so go to 3 or 5.\n\nSuppose 1-2-4-5. Then from 5, can go to 1,2,3,4. Used: 1,2,4,5. Available: 3,6,7.\n\nFrom 5, can go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-2-4-5-3. From 3, go to 6 or 7? 3 is connected to 6.\n\n3 and 7: not connected. 3 and 6: yes.\n\nGo to 6: 1-2-4-5-3-6. From 6, can go to 1,2,3. Used: 1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7.\n\nBut 6 is not connected to 7. Dead end.\n\nAlternatively, from 3 go to 7? Not possible.\n\nAlternatively, from 5 go to 7? 5 and 7: 5×7=35>20. Not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, from 4 go to 3 instead of 5.\n\nPath: 1-2-4-3. From 3, go to 5 or 6.\n\nIf go to 5: 1-2-4-3-5. From 5, go to 1,2,3,4. Used: 1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nFrom 5, can go to 6? 5 and 6: 5×6=30>20. Not allowed. So can't go to 6. Can't go to 7 either. Dead end.\n\nIf from 3 go to 6: 1-2-4-3-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,6. Available:5,7.\n\nFrom 6 can't go to 5 or 7. Dead end.\n\nHmm. This is tricky. Maybe starting with 1-2 is not feasible?\n\nAlternatively, let's try another path:\n\nStart at 1-2-5.\n\nPath: 1-2-5. From 5, can go to 1,2,3,4.\n\nUsed:1,2,5. Available:3,4,6,7.\n\nGo to 3: 1-2-5-3. From 3, go to 4,6.\n\nIf to 4: 1-2-5-3-4. From 4, go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nFrom 4, can go to 6? 4 and 6: 4×6=24>20. Not allowed. Can't go to 7 either. Dead end.\n\nIf from 3 go to 6: 1-2-5-3-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,5,6. Available:4,7.\n\nFrom 6 can't go to 4 or 7. Dead end.\n\nFrom 5 go to 4 instead: 1-2-5-4. From 4, go to 3 or 1,2,5. Used:1,2,4,5. Available:3,6,7.\n\nGo to 3: 1-2-5-4-3. From 3 go to 6 or 7? 3 and 7 not connected. 3 and 6: yes.\n\n1-2-5-4-3-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nAlternatively, from 3 go to 7? Not possible.\n\nAlternatively, from 4 go to 7? 4 and 7: 4×7=28>20. Not allowed.\n\nHmm. Not working.\n\nAlternatively, start with 1-2-6.\n\nPath: 1-2-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Used:1,2,6. Available:3,4,5,7.\n\nGo to 3: 1-2-6-3. From 3, go to 4,5.\n\nIf go to 4: 1-2-6-3-4. From 4, go to 5 or 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,6. Available:5,7.\n\nFrom 4 to 5: 1-2-6-3-4-5. From 5, go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nFrom 5 can't go to 7.\n\nAlternatively, from 4 go to 7? 4×7=28>20. No.\n\nFrom 3 go to 5: 1-2-6-3-5. From 5, go to 4 or 1,2,3.\n\nIf go to 4: 1-2-6-3-5-4. From 4, go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nIf from 5 go to 1: already used.\n\nAlternatively, from 5 go to 7? 5×7=35>20. No.\n\nAlternatively, from 6 go to 7? 6 and 7: 6×7=42>20. Not allowed.\n\nHmm, this is not working either.\n\nMaybe starting with 1-2 is impossible? Because we can't get 7 into the cycle without violating the adjacency or product conditions.\n\nAlternatively, let's try a different approach: perhaps there are no Hamiltonian cycles that start with 1-2. Let's see.\n\nSuppose we try to include 7 in the cycle when starting with 1-2.\n\nThe node 7 must be adjacent to both 1 and 2, so in the cycle, 7 must be between 1 and 2 or between 2 and 1. But if we start at 1-2, then to include 7 between them, we'd have to have 1-7-2 or 2-7-1, but since we started at 1-2, we can't go back to 1 from 2 via 7 because that would require revisiting 1 prematurely.\n\nAlternatively, the 7 could be elsewhere in the cycle, but then it wouldn't be adjacent to both 1 and 2. Since 7 can only be adjacent to 1 and 2, it must be between them in the cycle. Therefore, if we start at 1-2, then the only way to have 7 adjacent to both is to have the cycle go ...-1-7-2-..., but since we started at 1-2, we can't insert 7 between them without breaking the path.\n\nTherefore, it seems impossible to have a Hamiltonian cycle that starts with 1-2 and includes 7 adjacent to both 1 and 2. Therefore, starting with 1-2 cannot lead to a valid Hamiltonian cycle. Therefore, Case 2 (starting with 1-2) yields 0 cycles.\n\nCase 3: Starting with 1-3.\n\nNow, we start at 1, go to 3. Now, we need to ensure that 7 is adjacent to both 1 and 2 somewhere in the cycle. Since we've left 1 to go to 3, the other neighbor of 1 must be 7 (to satisfy 7's requirement of being adjacent to 1). Therefore, eventually, the cycle must loop back to 1 through 7, and also include 2 adjacent to 7.\n\nSo, the path must include ...-7-1-3-... and also have 7 adjacent to 2. So 7 must be connected to both 1 and 2, so 2 must be somewhere in the cycle adjacent to 7.\n\nLet me try to build this.\n\nStart at 1-3. Used: {1,3}\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Used:1, so available:2,4,5,6.\n\nSubcase 3a: From 3 go to 2.\n\nPath: 1-3-2. Now, 2 is adjacent to 3 and must be adjacent to 7. So from 2, we can go to 7 or other nodes.\n\nFrom 2, neighbors are 1,3,4,5,6,7. Used:1,2,3. Available:4,5,6,7.\n\nTo satisfy 7 needing to be adjacent to 2, we should go to 7 next.\n\nSubcase 3a1: From 2 go to 7.\n\nPath: 1-3-2-7. Now, 7 is adjacent to 2 and needs to be adjacent to 1. The other neighbor of 7 must be 1, but 1 is already two steps back. Since it's a cycle, we need to form a loop that includes 1 connected to 7.\n\nFrom 7, neighbors are 1 and 2, both already used. So we can't proceed further from 7 without repeating nodes. Thus, this path is stuck unless we can loop back, but we have remaining nodes: 4,5,6.\n\nTherefore, this path is invalid.\n\nSubcase 3a2: From 2, instead of going to 7, go to another node, say 4.\n\nPath: 1-3-2-4. Used: {1,2,3,4}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4. Available:5,6,7.\n\nGo to 5 or 7 or others? 4 can go to 5.\n\nSubcase 3a2a: 1-3-2-4-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nGo to 6: 1-3-2-4-5-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nGo to 7 from 5? 5 and 7: 5×7=35>20. Not allowed.\n\nSubcase 3a2b: From 4 go to 7.\n\nPath: 1-3-2-4-7. From 7, neighbors are 1 and 2, both used. Stuck. Remaining nodes:5,6. Can't proceed.\n\nSubcase 3a3: From 2 go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-3-2-5. From 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,5. Available:4,6,7.\n\nGo to 4: 1-3-2-5-4. From 4, go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nGo to 6: 1-3-2-5-4-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nGo to 7 from 4? 4×7=28>20. No.\n\nSubcase 3a4: From 2 go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-3-2-6. From 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,6. Available:4,5,7.\n\nGo to 4: 1-3-2-6-4. From 4, go to 5 or 1,2,3.\n\nGo to 5: 1-3-2-6-4-5. From 5, go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nGo to 7 from 4? 4×7=28>20. No.\n\nAlternatively, from 6 go to 7? 6 and 7: 6×7=42>20. Not allowed.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 3a (starting with 1-3-2) seems to lead to dead ends.\n\nSubcase 3b: From 3, go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-3-4. Used: {1,3,4}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Used:1,3,4. Available:2,5.\n\nGo to 2 or 5.\n\nSubcase 3b1: From 4 go to 2.\n\nPath: 1-3-4-2. Now, 2 must be adjacent to 7. From 2, neighbors are 1,3,4,5,6,7. Used:1,2,3,4. Available:5,6,7.\n\nGo to 7: 1-3-4-2-7. From 7, neighbors are 1 and 2, both used. Stuck. Remaining nodes:5,6. Can't reach them.\n\nGo to 5 or 6 first:\n\nSubcase 3b1a: From 2 go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-3-4-2-5. From 5, go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nGo to 6: 1-3-4-2-5-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nSubcase 3b1b: From 2 go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-3-4-2-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,6. Available:5,7.\n\nGo to 5: 1-3-4-2-6-5. From 5, go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nSubcase 3b1c: From 2 go to 7. As above, leads to dead end.\n\nSubcase 3b2: From 4 go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-3-4-5. Used: {1,3,4,5}\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Used:1,3,4,5. Available:2,6,7.\n\nGo to 2: 1-3-4-5-2. From 2, go to 6,7, or others.\n\nSubcase 3b2a: From 2 go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-3-4-5-2-6. From 6, go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nSubcase 3b2b: From 2 go to 7.\n\nPath: 1-3-4-5-2-7. From 7, stuck. Remaining nodes:6. Can't reach.\n\nSubcase 3b2c: From 5 go to 7? 5×7=35>20. Not allowed.\n\nSubcase 3b3: From 4 go to 5 then... Not helpful.\n\nThis is getting too time-consuming and it seems like starting with 1-3 may not yield any valid cycles either. Perhaps this entire approach is too tedious. Maybe I should consider that only the cycles we found earlier in Case 1 (starting with 1-7-2) are valid, and that starting with other first steps doesn't yield any cycles because of the constraints on 7.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only Hamiltonian cycles are the four we found in Case 1. Let me check whether there are other cycles starting with 1-7.\n\nEarlier in Case 1, we found four cycles:\n\n1. 1-7-2-4-5-3-6-1\n\n2. 1-7-2-5-4-3-6-1\n\n3. 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1\n\n4. 1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nWait, but in the third and fourth cycles, let me verify the adjacency products again to be sure.\n\nThird cycle: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5-1\n\nCheck products:\n\n1-7:7 ✔️\n\n7-2:14 ✔️\n\n2-6:12 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-4:12 ✔️\n\n4-5:20 ✔️\n\n5-1:5 ✔️\n\nAll good.\n\nFourth cycle:1-7-2-6-3-5-4-1\n\nProducts:\n\n1-7:7 ✔️\n\n7-2:14 ✔️\n\n2-6:12 ✔️\n\n6-3:18 ✔️\n\n3-5:15 ✔️\n\n5-4:20 ✔️\n\n4-1:4 ✔️\n\nAll good.\n\nSo those four are valid.\n\nNow, are there any other cycles starting with 1-7?\n\nIn the initial analysis of Case 1 (starting with 1-7-2), we found four cycles. But let's see if there are other paths when starting with 1-7.\n\nWait, in our initial analysis for Case 1, after 1-7-2, we considered going to 3,4,5, or 6 next. We exhausted those possibilities and found four cycles. But let me verify if there were any other possibilities when choosing different paths.\n\nWait, in Subcase 1b, when we went from 2 to 4, then to 5 or 3, etc., we found one cycle. In Subcase 1c, going from 2 to 5, then to 4 or 3, etc., found another. In Subcase 1d, going from 2 to 6, then to 3, then to 4 or 5, found two cycles. So total of four.\n\nAre there any other subcases when starting with 1-7-2?\n\nFor instance, after 1-7-2, going to 3, but then perhaps different routes?\n\nWait, in Subcase 1a, going from 2 to 3 led to dead ends, but perhaps I missed something?\n\nSubcase 1a: 1-7-2-3\n\nFrom 3, could we go to 6? Let's see:\n\nWait in Subcase 1a, after 1-7-2-3, from 3, neighbors are 1,2,4,5,6. Used nodes:1,2,3,7. So available:4,5,6.\n\nWe went to 4,5,6 but all led to dead ends. Wait, if from 3 we go to 6:\n\nPath:1-7-2-3-6. Then from 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. All used except none, but remaining nodes are 4 and 5. Can't reach them. So indeed dead end.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nTherefore, indeed only four cycles in Case 1.\n\nNow, what about other starting points? For example, starting with 1-7- something else?\n\nWait, no, once we fix the starting point as 1, the first step is to one of its neighbors, which are all nodes except none. But we considered starting with 1-7, 1-2, 1-3, etc. We saw that starting with 1-2 or 1-3 didn't yield any cycles, but maybe starting with 1-4, 1-5, or 1-6?\n\nLet me quickly check starting with 1-6.\n\nCase 4: Starting with 1-6.\n\nPath:1-6. Used: {1,6}\n\nFrom 6, neighbors are 1,2,3. Used:1, so available:2,3.\n\nSubcase 4a: From 6 go to 2.\n\nPath:1-6-2. Now, 2 must be adjacent to 7. From 2, go to 7.\n\nSubcase 4a1:1-6-2-7. From 7, neighbors are 1 and 2, both used. Stuck. Remaining nodes:3,4,5. Can't reach.\n\nSubcase 4a2: From 2 go to 3 or 4 or 5 or 7.\n\nIf from 2 go to 3: 1-6-2-3. From 3, go to 4,5, etc.\n\nProceeding:\n\nPath:1-6-2-3. From 3, go to 4,5.\n\nIf to 4: 1-6-2-3-4. From 4, go to 5 or 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,6. Available:5,7.\n\nGo to 5: 1-6-2-3-4-5. From 5, go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach 7.\n\nGo to 7 from 4? 4×7=28>20. No.\n\nIf from 3 go to 5: 1-6-2-3-5. From 5, go to 4 or others. 1-6-2-3-5-4. From 4, go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nAlternatively, from 5 go to 7? 5×7=35>20. No.\n\nSubcase 4a3: From 2 go to 4.\n\nPath:1-6-2-4. From 4, go to 3,5.\n\nIf to 3: 1-6-2-4-3. From 3 go to 5 or 6, but 6 is used. 1-6-2-4-3-5. From 5 go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nIf from 4 go to 5:1-6-2-4-5. From 5 go to 3 or others. 1-6-2-4-5-3. From 3 go to 7? No. 3 and 7 not connected. Can't reach 7.\n\nSubcase 4a4: From 2 go to 5.\n\nPath:1-6-2-5. From 5, go to 3,4.\n\nIf to 3:1-6-2-5-3. From 3 go to 4 or 6. 6 is used. 1-6-2-5-3-4. From 4 go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nIf from 5 go to 4:1-6-2-5-4. From 4 go to 3 or others. 1-6-2-5-4-3. From 3 go to 7? No. Can't reach 7.\n\nSubcase 4a5: From 2 go to 7.\n\nPath:1-6-2-7. Stuck, as before.\n\nTherefore, Case 4 (starting with 1-6) yields no valid cycles.\n\nCase 5: Starting with 1-5.\n\nPath:1-5. Used: {1,5}\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are 1,2,3,4. Available:2,3,4.\n\nSubcase 5a: From 5 go to 2.\n\nPath:1-5-2. Now, 2 must be adjacent to 7. From 2 go to 7.\n\nSubcase 5a1:1-5-2-7. From 7 stuck. Remaining nodes:3,4,6. Can't reach.\n\nSubcase 5a2: From 2 go to 3,4, or 6.\n\nIf to 3:1-5-2-3. From 3 go to 4,6.\n\nProceeding:\n\n1-5-2-3-4. From 4 go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nGo to 6:1-5-2-3-4-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nGo to 7 from 4? 4×7=28>20. No.\n\nIf from 3 go to 6:1-5-2-3-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,5,6. Available:4,7.\n\nGo to 4:1-5-2-3-6-4. From 4 go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nGo to 7 from 4? No.\n\nSubcase 5a3: From 2 go to 4.\n\nPath:1-5-2-4. From 4 go to 3 or 5,1,2.\n\nUsed:1,2,4,5. Available:3,6,7.\n\nGo to 3:1-5-2-4-3. From 3 go to 6 or others.\n\n1-5-2-4-3-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nGo to 7 from 3? No.\n\nGo to 7 from 4? No.\n\nSubcase 5a4: From 2 go to 6.\n\nPath:1-5-2-6. From 6 go to 3.\n\n1-5-2-6-3. From 3 go to 4 or 5, etc.\n\n1-5-2-6-3-4. From 4 go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nSimilarly, other paths lead to dead ends.\n\nSubcase 5b: From 5 go to 3.\n\nPath:1-5-3. From 3 go to 2,4,6.\n\nSubcase 5b1: From 3 go to 2.\n\nPath:1-5-3-2. From 2 go to 7.\n\nSubcase 5b1a:1-5-3-2-7. Stuck. Remaining nodes:4,6.\n\nCan't reach them.\n\nSubcase 5b1b: From 2 go to 4 or 6.\n\nIf to 4:1-5-3-2-4. From 4 go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nGo to 6:1-5-3-2-4-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nSubcase 5b2: From 3 go to 4.\n\nPath:1-5-3-4. From 4 go to 2 or others.\n\nUsed:1,3,4,5. Available:2,6,7.\n\nGo to 2:1-5-3-4-2. From 2 go to 6 or 7.\n\nIf to 6:1-5-3-4-2-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nIf to 7:1-5-3-4-2-7. Stuck. Remaining nodes:6.\n\nSubcase 5b3: From 3 go to 6.\n\nPath:1-5-3-6. From 6 go to 2 or 1,3.\n\nGo to 2:1-5-3-6-2. From 2 go to 4 or 7.\n\nIf to 4:1-5-3-6-2-4. From 4 go to 1,2,3,5. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nIf to 7:1-5-3-6-2-7. Stuck. Remaining nodes:4.\n\nSubcase 5c: From 5 go to 4.\n\nPath:1-5-4. From 4 go to 2,3.\n\nSubcase 5c1: From 4 go to 2.\n\nPath:1-5-4-2. From 2 go to 3,6,7.\n\nIf to 3:1-5-4-2-3. From 3 go to 6.\n\n1-5-4-2-3-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nIf from 2 go to 6:1-5-4-2-6. From 6 go to 3. 1-5-4-2-6-3. From 3 go to 7? No. Can't reach.\n\nIf from 2 go to 7:1-5-4-2-7. Stuck. Remaining nodes:3,6.\n\nSubcase 5c2: From 4 go to 3.\n\nPath:1-5-4-3. From 3 go to 2,6.\n\nIf to 2:1-5-4-3-2. From 2 go to 6 or 7.\n\nIf to 6:1-5-4-3-2-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nIf to 7:1-5-4-3-2-7. Stuck. Remaining nodes:6.\n\nTherefore, Case 5 (starting with 1-5) yields no valid cycles.\n\nCase 6: Starting with 1-4.\n\nPath:1-4. Used: {1,4}\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are 1,2,3,5. Available:2,3,5.\n\nSubcase 6a: From 4 go to 2.\n\nPath:1-4-2. From 2 go to 3,5,6,7.\n\nSubcase 6a1: From 2 go to 7.\n\nPath:1-4-2-7. From 7 stuck. Remaining nodes:3,5,6.\n\nCan't reach.\n\nSubcase 6a2: From 2 go to 3.\n\nPath:1-4-2-3. From 3 go to 4,5,6.\n\nUsed:1,2,3,4. Available:5,6,7.\n\nGo to 5:1-4-2-3-5. From 5 go to 1,2,3,4. Used:1,2,3,4,5. Available:6,7.\n\nGo to 6:1-4-2-3-5-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nGo to 7 from 5? 5×7=35>20. No.\n\nGo to 7 from 3? 3×7=21>20. No.\n\nSubcase 6a3: From 2 go to 5.\n\nPath:1-4-2-5. From 5 go to 3 or others.\n\nUsed:1,2,4,5. Available:3,6,7.\n\nGo to 3:1-4-2-5-3. From 3 go to 6.\n\n1-4-2-5-3-6. From 6 go to 1,2,3. Used:1,2,3,4,5,6. Available:7. Can't reach.\n\nGo to 7 from 3? No.\n\nSubcase 6a4: From 2 g