revious analysis, but starting from 7.\n\nProceeding similarly, we might end up with similar cycles but starting at 7.\n\nHowever, due to the time constraints and complexity, perhaps it's better to realize that each undirected Hamiltonian cycle corresponds to 2n directed cycles (n starting points, 2 directions). Since we found 4 directed cycles starting at 1, and assuming symmetry where each node has the same number of cycles starting from it (which might not hold, but let's check with node 7).\n\nSuppose the four cycles we found starting at 1 correspond to two undirected cycles, each traversed in two directions, and starting at seven different nodes. Wait, no.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps each of the four directed cycles starting at 1 corresponds to a unique undirected cycle, and since the graph is not symmetric, each undirected cycle can be started at any node in two directions, so total directed cycles would be 4 × 7 × 2 / something?\n\nWait, I need to think differently.\n\nLet me suppose that the four directed cycles we found starting at node 1 are all the possible directed cycles in the graph. That seems unlikely because there are other starting points.\n\nAlternatively, since we fixed the starting point as 1 and found 4 cycles, and the graph is not symmetric, but node 7 is unique, perhaps the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 4 × 7? No, that might overcount.\n\nAlternatively, in our analysis, we found 4 distinct directed cycles starting at node 1. Each of these cycles can be rotated to start at any of the 7 nodes, and each can be traversed in the reverse direction, giving 4 × 7 × 2 directed cycles. But that would be 56, which seems high.\n\nWait, no. Actually, each directed cycle has a specific starting point and direction. If we have a directed cycle, say C, then rotating it so that it starts at a different node gives a different directed cycle, and reversing it gives another directed cycle.\n\nTherefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is equal to the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles multiplied by 2n (n starting points, 2 directions). In our case, if we found 4 directed cycles starting at node 1, and assuming that these are all the directed cycles starting at node 1, then the total number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles would be 4 / (2) = 2, since each undirected cycle is counted twice in the directed count (once in each direction). Therefore, if there are 2 undirected Hamiltonian cycles, then the total number of directed cycles would be 2 × 2 × 7 = 28? Wait, no:\n\nWait, formula: For each undirected cycle, there are 2 directions and n starting points, so total directed cycles per undirected cycle: 2n.\n\nTherefore, if there are C undirected Hamiltonian cycles, then total directed Hamiltonian cycles are 2nC.\n\nIn our case, if we found 4 directed cycles starting at node 1, then the number of undirected cycles is 4 / (2) = 2 (since each undirected cycle is counted twice in the directed count when considering all starting points). Wait, no, actually, for a single undirected cycle, the number of directed cycles it contributes is 2n (each starting point and each direction). Therefore, if we have counted the number of directed cycles starting at a particular node (node 1), say D, then the total number of directed cycles is n × D, but this assumes that every node has the same number of directed cycles starting from it, which may not be true.\n\nIn our case, we found D=4 directed cycles starting at node 1. If node 7 has a different number, say D', then total directed cycles would be the sum over all nodes of D_i, where D_i is the number starting at node i. However, in an undirected graph, for each undirected cycle, it contributes exactly 2n directed cycles (n starting points × 2 directions). Therefore, if the total number of directed cycles is T, then T = 2nC where C is the number of undirected cycles.\n\nIn our case, if we found that starting at node 1, there are 4 directed cycles, then if the graph is symmetric enough that every node has the same number of directed cycles starting from it, then T = 7×4 = 28, so 28 = 2×7×C ⇒ C = 2. So there are 2 undirected Hamiltonian cycles. Then the total directed cycles would be 28.\n\nAlternatively, if node 7 has a different number, say starting at node 7, there are fewer cycles, then the total might be different. But given the symmetry of the problem except for node 7's restricted degree, it's possible that the number of directed cycles starting at node 1 is the same as starting at any other node except node 7.\n\nWait, node 7 has degree 2, so it's less connected, so likely fewer cycles start at node 7.\n\nSuppose starting at node 7, how many directed Hamiltonian cycles are there?\n\nLet me try to count them.\n\nStarting at 7, next node must be 1 or 2.\n\nSubcase B1: 7-1-...\n\nFrom 1, can go to any node except 7: 2,3,4,5,6.\n\nSubcase B1a: 7-1-2-...\n\nFrom 2, can go to 3,4,5,6,7. But 7 is already visited, so 3,4,5,6.\n\nSubcase B1a1: 7-1-2-3-...\n\nThis is similar to our previous analysis. Following through similar steps as before, we might end up with the same cycles but starting from 7.\n\nAlternatively, following the same logic as before, the number of directed cycles starting at 7 might be 2, leading to total directed cycles: starting at 1:4, starting at 7:2, and others?\n\nThis is getting too time-consuming without a clear pattern.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the potential for error in manual enumeration, perhaps I should consider that the four cycles we found starting at 1, and since the problem counts all rotations and reflections as different, the total number is 4 × 7 (starting positions) × 2 (directions) divided by something? Wait, no.\n\nAlternatively, since we fixed the starting point as 1 and found 4 directed cycles, each of these cycles can be rotated to start at any of the 7 nodes, resulting in 7 × 4 = 28 directed cycles. Additionally, each cycle can be traversed in the reverse direction, doubling the count to 56. But this would be true if all cycles are distinct under rotation and reflection.\n\nWait, actually, no. Each directed cycle has a specific direction and starting point. So if you have a directed cycle, rotating it changes the starting point but keeps the direction the same. Reversing the direction gives a different directed cycle.\n\nTherefore, if we have a single undirected cycle, it corresponds to 2n directed cycles (n starting points × 2 directions). Therefore, if we found that starting at node 1 there are 4 directed cycles, then the total number of directed cycles would be (4 / 2) × 2 × 7 = 4 × 7 = 28? Wait, no.\n\nLet me think of it this way: suppose there is one undirected cycle. Then it has 2 directions and can be started at any of the 7 nodes, so total directed cycles: 7 × 2 = 14.\n\nIf there are two undirected cycles, then total directed cycles would be 2 × 14 = 28.\n\nSince we found starting at node 1 there are 4 directed cycles, and if there are two undirected cycles, then the total is 28. That would mean that starting at node 1, we have 4 directed cycles, which is 2 undirected cycles × 2 directions = 4, which matches. Therefore, likely the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 28.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 28.\n\nBut wait, in our earlier analysis, starting at node 1, we found 4 directed cycles. If there are two undirected cycles, each contributing 2 directions × 7 starting points, but no, per undirected cycle, it's 2 directions × 7 starting points? Wait no.\n\nWait, for one undirected cycle, the number of directed cycles is 2 × 7 (since for each of the 7 starting points, you can go in two directions). Wait, no, actually, for a cycle of length n, the number of directed cycles is 2n, because you can start at any of the n nodes and go in two directions. Wait, no, that would be overcounting.\n\nActually, in a cycle of n nodes, the number of distinct directed cycles is 2n because you can start at any of the n nodes and go in