n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of paths from 2 to some node X (which will be adjacent to 1) that visit all four nodes 3,4,5,6 exactly once, with the adjacency constraints. Then, since X can be any of the four nodes, but must be connected back to 1, which is always allowed, so the count is the number of such paths multiplied by 1 (since X can be any node, but the path must end at X which is then adjacent to 1). Wait, actually, the path must start at 2, end at some node which is adjacent to 1, but since all nodes are allowed to be adjacent to 1, the end node can be any of the four, so actually, the number of such paths is equal to the number of Hamiltonian paths from 2 to any node in the set {3,4,5,6} that traverse all four nodes with the adjacency constraints.\n\nAlternatively, the total number of such paths is equal to the number of permutations of [3,4,5,6] starting at 2? Wait no, starting at 2?\n\nWait, in the fixed segment 1-7-2, the next node after 2 must be one of the remaining nodes (3,4,5,6), and the path must continue through all four nodes and end at a node that connects back to 1. Since the connection back to 1 is allowed regardless, the only constraints are on the path from 2 through the four nodes.\n\nWait, this is similar to our prior approach where we had a linear arrangement of the four nodes with specific start and end points.\n\nWait, in our prior calculation, when we fixed 7 between 1 and 2, we considered the sequence from 1 to 2 via the four nodes, but in reality, the path from 2 must go through all four nodes and end at some node which loops back to 1. Since the loop back to 1 is unconstrained (as 1 can be adjacent to any), the only constraints are on the path from 2 through the four nodes. Therefore, the number of such paths is equal to the number of permutations of [3,4,5,6] starting at some node and ending at some node, with the adjacency constraints. Wait, actually, the path starts at 2 and must include all four node