nect back to 1.\n\nTherefore, the constraints are only on the internal adjacencies between the permutation elements. Since the first element only needs to be adjacent to 2 (which it is), and the last element just needs to connect to 1 (which it can), the only constraints are on the three internal adjacencies in the permutation of [3,4,5,6].\n\nWait a second! This is exactly the same problem as before when we considered permutations of [3,4,5,6] with adjacency constraints, except now the permutation is of the four numbers, and the first element can be any of them (since 2 is already the starting point and adjacent to any), and the last element can be any of them (since it will connect to 1). Therefore, the number of such paths is exactly the number of permutations of [3,4,5,6] with the adjacency constraints on consecutive elements, which we previously calculated as 4 permutations for each direction?\n\nWait no, in our prior calculation, when we fixed 7 between 1 and 2, we considered the permutations of [3,4,5,6] in the middle positions with the adjacency constraints, and found 4 permutations for each case (Case1 and Case2). But in this current analysis, we're now considering permutations of [3,4,5,6] with the adjacency constraints on consecutive elements, which was exactly what we did before, resulting in 4 permutations. However, in that prior calculation, we had two cases because the first element (position3) was adjacent to 1 or 2, but here, since we're starting at 2, the first element (a) can be any of 3,4,5,6 because 2 can be adjacent to any of them. Therefore, the number of such paths is exactly the number of valid permutations of [3,4,5,6] with the adjacency constraints, which we found to be 4 permutations.\n\nWait, but in our prior calculation, when we considered the permutations of [3,4,5,6], we found 4 permutations that satisfied the adjacency constraints. Therefore, the number of paths from 2 through the four nodes is 4. But in our prior case analysis, when