nd since the grid is small, that's accurate. Let me list all six paths explicitly:\n\n1. Right, Right, Up, Up: nodes (3,1), (3,2), (3,3), (2,3), (1,3)\n2. Right, Up, Right, Up: nodes (3,1), (3,2), (2,2), (2,3), (1,3)\n3. Right, Up, Up, Right: nodes (3,1), (3,2), (2,2), (1,2), (1,3)\n4. Up, Up, Right, Right: nodes (3,1), (2,1), (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n5. Up, Right, Up, Right: nodes (3,1), (2,1), (2,2), (1,2), (1,3)\n6. Up, Right, Right, Up: nodes (3,1), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (1,3)\n\nYes, that's six distinct paths. Each has five nodes, and each omits four different nodes. \n\nFor example, path 1 omits (2,1), (1,1), (1,2), and (2,2). \n\nPath 2 omits (3,3), (1,1), (1,2), and (2,3). \n\nWait, actually, let's check path 2's omitted nodes:\n\nPath 2 nodes: (3,1), (3,2), (2,2), (2,3), (1,3). Therefore, omitted non-fixed nodes are: (1,1), (1,2), (3,3), and (2,1). \n\nSimilarly, path 3's nodes: (3,1), (3,2), (2,2), (1,2), (1,3). So omitted non-fixed nodes: (2,1), (1,1), (2,3), (3,3). \n\nPath 4: (3,1), (2,1), (1,1), (1,2), (1,3). Omitted non-fixed nodes: (3,2), (2,3), (2,2), (3,3). \n\nPath 5: (3,1), (2,1), (2,2), (1,2), (1,3). Omitted non-fixed nodes: (3,2), (1,1), (2,3), (3,3). \n\nPath 6: (3,1), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (1,3). Omitted non-fixed nodes: (3,2), (1,1), (1,2), (3,3). \n\nEach path omits a different combination of four nodes, and each combination corresponds to exactly one path. Therefore, the six subsets are distinct and there are no overlaps. \n\nTherefore, the count is accurate. \n\nTherefore, the number of ways to remove 4 lily pads such that the frog cannot get home is 35 - 6 = 29. \n\nBut hold on, the problem says \"compute the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads so that the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain, but Kelvin cannot get home.\" \n\nWait a second, in our calculation, we considered subsets S of 5 lily pads (including the two fixed ones) where the frog cannot get home, which is 29. Each such subset corresponds to a unique way of rem