3,3). Hmm. Maybe it's impossible to have a Hamiltonian path from (1,1) to (3,3). Let me see.\n\nSuppose we try:\n\nStart at (1,1). Let's try to snake through the grid.\n\n(1,1) → (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3) → (3,2) → (3,1) → (2,1) → (2,2). Ends at (2,2). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, starting differently:\n\n(1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (2,2) → (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3). That works! Let's check:\n\n1. (1,1) to (2,1): okay.\n\n2. (2,1) to (3,1): okay.\n\n3. (3,1) to (3,2): okay.\n\n4. (3,2) to (2,2): okay.\n\n5. (2,2) to (1,2): okay.\n\n6. (1,2) to (1,3): okay.\n\n7. (1,3) to (2,3): okay.\n\n8. (2,3) to (3,3): okay.\n\nSo that is a path of 8 edges (9 vertices) from (1,1) to (3,3). So yes, there exists a Hamiltonian path from (1,1) to (3,3). Therefore, the maximum path length is 8, but in our case, we are concerned with whether there exists *any* path from (1,1) to (3,3) in the remaining graph. So, if we remove lily pads in such a way that all possible paths are blocked, then Kevin cannot get home. So, the question is to count the number of ways to remove 4 lily pads (from the 7 non-start/end pads) such that in the remaining graph, there is no path from (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nAlternatively, the remaining 5 lily pads must not contain any path connecting (1,1) and (3,3). So, the key is that the remaining graph must have (1,1) and (3,3) in different connected components.\n\nTherefore, to compute this, perhaps it's better to compute the total number of ways to remove 4 lily pads (without removing (1,1) or (3,3)), which is C(7,4) = 35, and then subtract the number of ways where (1,1) and (3,3) are still in the same connected component in the remaining graph. Wait, but actually, inclusion-exclusion might be tricky here because the connectedness is a complicated property.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to model this as the complement: the number of ways to leave 5 lily pads (including (1,1) and (3,3)) such that (1,1) and (3,3) are in different connected components. To compute this, perhaps we can consider all possible subsets of 5 lily pads including (1,1) and (3,3), and then for each such subset, check if (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected, then count them. Since the total number is C(7,3) = 35 (since we have to choose 3 more lily pads from the remaining 7 to join (1,1) and (3,3)), so 35 total subsets. So, perhaps enumerating all 35 subsets is feasible, since 35 isn't too large. Maybe I can categorize the subsets based on their structure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think in terms of the forbidden paths. To block all paths from (1,1) to (3,3), we need to ensure that every possible path from (1,1) to (3,3) in the original grid has at least one lily pad removed from that path. Since we are only removing 4 lily pads, we have to choose 4 lily pads such that every path from (1,1) to (3,3) passes through at least one of these 4 removed lily pads.\n\nAlternatively, this is equivalent to a hitting set problem: the universe is all the paths from (1,1) to (3,3), and we need a hitting set of size 4 that intersects every such path. However, the problem is that the number of paths is large (as we saw, there are infinitely many if we allow loops, but in simple paths, there are many), so perhaps this approach is not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the grid and consider which lily pads are critical for connecting (1,1) and (3,3). For instance, if we can separate the grid into two parts by removing certain lily pads, such that (1,1) is in one part and (3,3) is in the other. Since we can only remove 4 lily pads, we need to find all such separations achievable with 4 removals.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model this as the grid graph and consider the minimal vertex cuts between (1,1) and (3,3). A vertex cut is a set of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph. So, the minimal vertex cuts between (1,1) and (3,3) would be the smallest number of lily pads that need to be removed to disconnect them. Then, any vertex cut of size 4 or larger would work, but since we are only removing 4, we can consider all vertex cuts of size exactly 4, but actually, even if a vertex cut is smaller, say size 3, then removing 4 pads could include that cut plus one more, so actually, all vertex cuts of size up to 4 would be included in the total.\n\nWait, this might be getting somewhere. Let me recall that in graph theory, the vertex connectivity between two nodes is the size of the smallest vertex cut separating them. So, if the vertex connectivity between (1,1) and (3,3) is k, then any vertex cut of size k, k+1, ..., up to the total number of vertices minus 2 (since we have to leave (1,1) and (3,3)), would disconnect them. Therefore, the number of vertex cuts of size exactly m would be some number, and the total number of vertex cuts of size ≤4 would be the sum over m=1 to 4 of the number of vertex cuts of size m. However, in our problem, we are not counting the number of vertex cuts, but rather the number of subsets of 4 vertices (from the 7 non-start/end) such that the subset is a vertex cut (i.e., its removal disconnects (1,1) and (3,3)), or perhaps even just a superset of some vertex cut? Wait, actually, if you remove a vertex cut, then you disconnect them, but if you remove more vertices, you might still disconnect them, but also might not. Wait, actually, if you remove a vertex cut, you are guaranteed to disconnect them, but if you remove more vertices, it might still disconnect them, or it might not. Hmm, actually, no. Once you remove a vertex cut, you have disconnected them, so removing more vertices won't affect that—i.e., they are already disconnected. Therefore, any superset of a vertex cut will also disconnect them. Therefore, the set of all subsets of size 4 that include at least one vertex cut of size k (for some k ≤4) will disconnect (1,1) and (3,3). Wait, actually, no. Wait, if you have a vertex cut of size k, then any superset of that vertex cut will also be a vertex cut. Therefore, all subsets that include some vertex cut of size ≤4 will disconnect (1,1) and (3,3). However, the converse is not true: a subset of 4 vertices might not include any vertex cut, but still disconnect the two nodes. For example, if the subset is not a vertex cut but its removal still separates the graph. Hmm, actually, no—if a set of vertices S disconnects (1,1) and (3,3), then S must contain a vertex cut. Because a vertex cut is the minimal set whose removal disconnects the graph. Therefore, any disconnecting set must contain a vertex cut. Therefore, the sets of size 4 that disconnect (1,1) and (3,3) are exactly the sets that contain at least one vertex cut of size ≤4. Wait, but actually, vertex cuts are minimal, so if a set S disconnects the graph, then it must contain some vertex cut. But the vertex cut might be smaller than S, so S could be a larger set that includes a vertex cut. Therefore, the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 is equal to the sum over all vertex cuts T with |T| ≤4 of the number of supersets of T of size 4. However, this might overcount because different vertex cuts can have overlapping supersets.\n\nHmm, this seems complicated, but maybe in this small grid, we can actually compute the minimal vertex cuts between (1,1) and (3,3). Let me try that.\n\nFirst, let's recall that in a grid graph, the vertex connectivity between two nodes can sometimes be determined by the Manhattan distance or something else. The Manhattan distance between (1,1) and (3,3) is 4, so maybe the vertex connectivity is 2? Or 4? Wait, let me think.\n\nIn a grid graph, the vertex connectivity between two nodes is at least the maximum of the minimum number of vertices needed to disconnect the graph in the row or column direction? Hmm, perhaps not exactly. Let me try to find the minimal vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3).\n\nA vertex cut is a set of vertices whose removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3). Let me attempt to find the smallest such set.\n\nFirst, let's see if there exists a vertex cut of size 1: is there a single lily pad whose removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3)? Let's see. Suppose we remove (2,2). Would that disconnect them? Let me see: if we remove the center pad (2,2), then is there still a path from (1,1) to (3,3)? Let's see:\n\nOriginal paths: from (1,1) you can go right or up. If you go right to (1,2), then from there you can go up to (2,2) but that's removed, so you can go right to (1,3), then up to (2,3), then up to (3,3). Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2) which is removed, so you can't go that way. Alternatively, from (2,1) you can go down? Wait, (2,1) can go up to (3,1) or right to (2,2) which is removed, or left? No, (2,1) is on the edge. So from (2,1), you can go up to (3,1). Then from (3,1), you can go right to (3,2), then right to (3,3). So actually, even if we remove (2,2), there are still paths from (1,1) to (3,3). For example:\n\n(1,1) → (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3)\n\nOr\n\n(1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3)\n\nTherefore, removing the center doesn't disconnect them. How about removing another single pad?\n\nSuppose we remove (1,3). Then, can we still get from (1,1) to (3,3)? Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), you can go right to (1,2), then from (1,2) you can go up to (2,2), then right to (2,3), then up to (3,3). Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2), etc. So, even without (1,3), there are other paths. Similarly, removing (3,1) would not block all paths.\n\nWhat about removing a corner? Let's remove (3,3)'s adjacent pads? Wait, (3,3)'s adjacent pads are (3,2) and (2,3). If we remove, say, (2,3), then from (3,3), you can still go to (3,2). From (1,1), you can go to (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) is removed, so can't go that way, but you can go (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). So that path still exists. Similarly, removing (3,2) would block the direct path from (3,1) to (3,3), but there's still the path through (2,3). So removing a single pad adjacent to (3,3) doesn't block all paths.\n\nHow about removing a pad in the second row or column? Let's try removing (2,1). Then, from (1,1), you can still go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2), etc., so that path is still available. Alternatively, from (1,1) you can go up to (2,1) which is removed, so you can't go that way. But you can still go right first. So, the path through the top row remains.\n\nHmm. Maybe a vertex cut of size 2? Let's see if we can find two pads whose removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3). Let's try removing (2,2) and (2,3). Then, can we still get from (1,1) to (3,3)?\n\nFrom (1,1) you can go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed. Alternatively, right to (1,3), then up to (2,3) which is removed. So from (1,1), you can go up to (2,1), then right to (2,2) which is removed, so you can't go that way. Then from (2,1), you can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then to (3,3). So that path still exists. So even with those two removed, there's still a path.\n\nAlternatively, remove (1,2) and (3,2). Then, let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), you can go right to (1,2) which is removed, so you can only go up to (2,1). From (2,1), you can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2) which is removed, so you can't go that way. Alternatively, from (3,1) you can go right to (3,2) is blocked, so you can't proceed. Wait, from (3,1), can you go up? No, that's the top row. Wait, (3,1) is in the bottom row? Wait, no, in our coordinate system, row 3 is the top row. Wait, actually, in the coordinate system I set up earlier:\n\nRow 1: bottom row (y=1)\n\nRow 3: top row (y=3)\n\nSo (3,1) is the top-left lily pad, and (3,3) is the top-right. Therefore, (3,1) can only go right to (3,2) or down? Wait no, in a grid, you can't go down from the top row. So from (3,1), you can go right to (3,2) or down? Wait, down would be row 4, which doesn't exist. So from (3,1), you can only go right. But if (3,2) is removed, then you can't go further. So starting from (1,1):\n\n(1,1) can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) [which is removed].\n\nSo (1,1) → (2,1). Then from (2,1), you can go up to (3,1) or right to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,1) to (3,1): then from (3,1) you can go right to (3,2) [removed], so stuck.\n\nFrom (2,1) to (2,2): then from (2,2), you can go up to (3,2) [removed], left to (2,1), or right to (2,3). From (2,3), you can go up to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (2,1) → (2,2) → (2,3) → (3,3). So that path still exists. Therefore, even with (1,2) and (3,2) removed, there is still a path. Hmm.\n\nHow about removing (1,3) and (3,1). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), you can go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2), then right to (2,3), then up to (3,3). That path is still available. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2), etc. So still connected.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Maybe a vertex cut of size 3?\n\nLet me try removing three key pads. Let's try removing (2,2), (1,3), and (3,1). Let's see if that disconnects them.\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nCan go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2) [removed], or right to (1,3) [removed], so only up to (2,1). From (2,1), can go up to (3,1) [removed], or right to (2,2) [removed], so stuck. Therefore, (1,1) is isolated? Wait, (2,1) can go to (2,2) which is removed, so from (2,1) you can only go back to (1,1) or up to (3,1) which is removed. Wait, so (1,1) and (2,1) are connected, but (2,1) can't go anywhere else except back to (1,1). Meanwhile, (3,3) is still accessible? Let's see: (3,3) is connected to (3,2) and (2,3). Let's see if those are still present. We only removed (2,2), (1,3), and (3,1). So (3,2) and (2,3) are still there. From (3,3), you can go to (3,2) or (2,3). From (3,2), you can go left to (3,1) [removed] or up/down? No, so only left is removed, so from (3,2) you can go to (2,2) [removed] or (3,1) [removed], so actually (3,2) is only connected to (3,3). Similarly, (2,3) can go down to (3,3) or left to (2,2) [removed], so (2,3) is only connected to (3,3). Therefore, the component containing (3,3) is just itself and (3,2), (2,3). Meanwhile, the component containing (1,1) is just (1,1) and (2,1). So, yes, they are disconnected. So this set of three pads: (2,2), (1,3), (3,1) is a vertex cut of size 3. Therefore, a vertex cut of size 3 exists. Is there a vertex cut of size 2?\n\nLet me try removing (2,2) and (2,3). Earlier, we saw that there was still a path through (3,1) to (3,2) to (3,3). Wait, let me check again. If we remove (2,2) and (2,3):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2) [removed], so can't go that way. From (1,2), can go right to (1,3), then up to (2,3) [removed], so stuck. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2) [removed], so can't go that way. From (2,1), can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then right to (3,3). So the path (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3) still exists. So that path is available. Therefore, removing those two doesn't disconnect.\n\nHow about removing (1,2) and (3,2). Earlier, we saw that there was still a path through (2,3) to (3,3). Let me verify again. If we remove (1,2) and (3,2):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) [removed], so only up to (2,1). From (2,1), can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2) [removed], so stuck. Alternatively, from (2,1) right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) [removed], left to (2,1), or right to (2,3). From (2,3), can go up to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (2,1) → (2,2) → (2,3) → (3,3). That works. So still connected.\n\nHmm. How about removing (2,1) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2), then right to (2,3) [removed], so can't go that way. From (2,2), can go up to (3,2), then right to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (1,2) → (2,2) → (3,2) → (3,3). So that works. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1) [removed], so can't go that way. So still connected via the top path.\n\nHmm. Maybe remove (1,3) and (3,1). Wait, we tried that earlier and still had a path through the center? Wait, no, let's see:\n\nIf we remove (1,3) and (3,1):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2), then right to (2,3), then up to (3,3). That path is still available. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2), then to (2,3), etc. So still connected.\n\nHmm. Maybe a vertex cut of size 3 is minimal? Let's see if there's a vertex cut of size 2 that I haven't thought of.\n\nHow about removing (1,2) and (3,3)'s adjacent pad, say (3,2). Wait, but (3,3) has to remain, so we can't remove (3,3). Wait, the vertex cut can only consist of the non-start and non-end pads. Wait, actually, in our problem, we are only allowed to remove pads that are not (1,1) or (3,3). So in the vertex cut, we can't remove (1,1) or (3,3). So, any vertex cut must be among the other 7 pads. So, in trying to find a vertex cut of size 2, we have to pick two pads from the 7 non-start/end pads such that their removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nLet me try removing (2,2) and (1,3):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2) [removed], so can't go that way. From (1,2), can go right to (1,3) [removed], so stuck. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2) [removed], so stuck. From (2,1), can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). That path is still there. So, not disconnected.\n\nHow about removing (2,2) and (3,1):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2), up to (2,2) [removed], or up to (2,1). From (2,1), can go up to (3,1) [removed], so stuck. From (1,2), can go right to (1,3), then up to (2,3), then up to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3). That still works. So disconnected? No, still connected.\n\nHmm. How about removing (2,3) and (3,1):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2), up to (2,2), then right to (2,3) [removed], so can't go that way. From (2,2), can go up to (3,2), then right to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (1,2) → (2,2) → (3,2) → (3,3). That works. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2), then as above. So still connected.\n\nHmm. Maybe removing (1,2) and (2,3):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) [removed], so only up to (2,1). From (2,1), can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then to (3,3). Alternatively, from (2,1) right to (2,2), then up to (3,2), then to (3,3). So still connected.\n\nThis is frustrating. Maybe a vertex cut of size 3 is indeed minimal? Let's see another attempt.\n\nLet me try removing (1,2), (2,1), and (3,2). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nCan go right to (1,2) [removed], so only up to (2,1) [removed], so stuck. Wait, (1,1) can only go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2), both removed. Therefore, (1,1) is isolated. Meanwhile, (3,3) is still connected to (3,2) [removed]? Wait, (3,2) is removed, so (3,3) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,3). From (2,3), you can go down to (3,3) or left to (2,2). If (2,2) is still there, then from (2,3) you can go left to (2,2), then up to (3,2) [removed], so not helpful. From (2,2), you can go up to (3,2) [removed], so maybe to (1,2) [removed], etc. Hmm, actually, let's map this out.\n\nIf we removed (1,2), (2,1), and (3,2):\n\nThe remaining pads include (1,1), (3,3), and the others except those three. So the remaining pads are:\n\n(1,1), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (3,1), (3,3).\n\nNow, let's see the connections:\n\n(1,1) is only connected to... well, its neighbors are (1,2) [removed] and (2,1) [removed], so (1,1) is isolated.\n\n(3,3) is connected to (3,2) [removed] and (2,3). So (3,3) is connected to (2,3).\n\n(2,3) is connected to (3,3) and (2,2).\n\n(2,2) is connected to (2,3) and (1,2) [removed], (3,2) [removed], and (2,1) [removed]. So (2,2) is only connected to (2,3).\n\n(3,1) is connected to (3,2) [removed] and (2,1) [removed], so (3,1) is isolated.\n\n(1,3) is connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3). So (1,3) is connected to (2,3).\n\nSo the connected components are:\n\n- Isolated: (1,1), (3,1), (2,2) is connected to (2,3), which is connected to (1,3) and (3,3). So the big component includes (2,2), (2,3), (1,3), (3,3). And (1,1) and (3,1) are isolated.\n\nTherefore, (1,1) is in its own component, and (3,3) is in the big component. So they are disconnected. Therefore, removing those three pads does disconnect them. So that is a vertex cut of size 3. But is there a vertex cut of size 2?\n\nWait, let me try another set: remove (1,3) and (3,1). Wait, we did that before and there was still a path. How about removing (2,2) and (3,1):\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nCan go right to (1,2), up to (2,2) [removed], so can't go that way. From (1,2), can go right to (1,3), then up to (2,3), then up to (3,3). So path exists: (1,1) → (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3). So still connected.\n\nHmm. Maybe another combination: remove (1,2) and (3,2). Wait, tried that already. Still connected.\n\nHow about removing (2,3) and (3,1):\n\nFrom (1,1) can go right to (1,2), up to (2,2), then right to (2,3) [removed], so can't go that way. From (2,2), can go up to (3,2), then right to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (1,2) → (2,2) → (3,2) → (3,3). That works.\n\nHmm. Maybe a vertex cut of size 3 is indeed the minimal. Let me see if there is a vertex cut of size 2 that I haven't considered.\n\nWait, how about removing (1,2) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nCan go right to (1,2) [removed], so only up to (2,1). From (2,1), can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then to (3,3). Alternatively, from (2,1) right to (2,2), then up to (3,2), then to (3,3). So still connected.\n\nHmm. How about removing (2,1) and (2,3):\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nCan go right to (1,2), then up to (2,2), then right to (2,3) [removed], so can't go that way. From (2,2), can go up to (3,2), then to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (1,2) → (2,2) → (3,2) → (3,3). That works.\n\nHmm. Maybe trying removing (1,3) and (2,2):\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nCan go right to (1,2), up to (2,2) [removed], so can't go that way. From (1,2), can go right to (1,3) [removed], so stuck. Alternatively, from (1,1) up to (2,1), then right to (2,2) [removed], so stuck. From (2,1), can go up to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then to (3,3). So path: (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). That still exists.\n\nHmm. I'm starting to think that maybe the minimal vertex cut is size 3, so that any vertex cut must have at least 3 vertices. Therefore, the minimal vertex cut size is 3. If that's the case, then any set of 4 pads that includes a vertex cut of size 3 will disconnect (1,1) and (3,3). Additionally, any set of 4 pads that includes two vertex cuts? Wait, but if the minimal is 3, then a vertex cut of size 3 is minimal, so any larger set that includes it would also disconnect, but there might also be vertex cuts of size 4, but since we're only removing 4 pads, a vertex cut of size 4 would be a set of 4 pads whose removal disconnects them, which would also count. However, since we are to count all subsets of 4 pads (from the 7 non-start/end) that disconnect (1,1) and (3,3), this would include all supersets of any vertex cut of size ≤4. But since the minimal vertex cut is size 3, then all vertex cuts of size 3 or 4 would be included. However, since we are only removing 4 pads, the vertex cuts we can have are those of size 3 or 4. But vertex cuts of size 4 would be sets of 4 pads whose removal disconnects (1,1) and (3,3). However, since the minimal vertex cut is 3, a vertex cut of size 4 is just a vertex cut that includes an additional pad. But in terms of counting, we need to count all subsets of size 4 that are vertex cuts (i.e., their removal disconnects the graph). So this includes all subsets of size 4 that contain at least one vertex cut of size 3. But actually, it's not exactly that, because a subset of size 4 might not contain a vertex cut of size 3, but its removal still disconnects the graph. However, since the minimal vertex cut is 3, any disconnecting set must contain a vertex cut, so if a set of size 4 disconnects the graph, then it must contain some vertex cut of size 3. Therefore, the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 is equal to the number of vertex cuts of size 3 multiplied by the number of ways to choose the fourth element, plus the number of vertex cuts of size 4 multiplied by 1 (since you can't add more). Wait, but actually, each vertex cut of size 3 can be extended in C(7 - 3, 1) ways to make a set of size 4, but we have to be careful about overcounting if different vertex cuts share common elements. This is getting complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to switch approaches. Since the total number of subsets of 4 pads from the 7 non-start/end is 35, and if we can find how many of these 35 subsets disconnect (1,1) and (3,3), then we can just count them directly by case analysis.\n\nGiven that the grid is small, maybe we can categorize the subsets based on which \"critical\" pads are removed. Let me try to think of the grid and which pads are essential.\n\nThe key is that to block all paths from (1,1) to (3,3), we need to block every possible path. Since all minimal paths go through certain areas, perhaps blocking the center or key corridors.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the grid as a graph and look for the number of 4-vertex subsets whose removal breaks all paths.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think in terms of the remaining graph having (1,1) and (3,3) in different components. To have them in different components, there must be no path between them. So, perhaps the remaining graph is split into two parts: one containing (1,1) and the other containing (3,3). Since we have 5 remaining pads, one part could have, say, 2 pads and the other 3, or 1 and 4, etc., but since (1,1) and (3,3) must be in different parts, so the smallest a part can be is 1 (just (1,1)) and the other part has the remaining 4, but (3,3) has to be in the other part, so the other part must have at least 1 (the (3,3)) plus others. Wait, actually, the components can be of any size as long as they don't connect (1,1) and (3,3). So, for example, if (1,1) is isolated, then it's in a component of size 1, and the rest of the 4 pads (including (3,3)) form another component. Alternatively, (1,1) could be in a component of size 2 and (3,3) in a component of size 3, etc.\n\nTo compute this, perhaps we can consider the different ways the remaining 5 pads can be arranged such that (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected.\n\nCase 1: (1,1) is isolated. That means all its neighbors are removed. The neighbors of (1,1) are (1,2) and (2,1). Therefore, to isolate (1,1), we must remove both (1,2) and (2,1). Then, the remaining pads include (1,1) and the other 3 pads must be chosen from the remaining 5 pads (since we've already removed 2: (1,2) and (2,1)), but actually, total pads to remove are 4, so if we remove (1,2) and (2,1), that's 2 removals, so we have 2 more removals to choose from the remaining 5 non-start/end pads excluding those two? Wait, let's step back.\n\nWait, in total, we have 7 non-start/end pads. To isolate (1,1), we need to remove its two neighbors: (1,2) and (2,1). That's 2 removals. Then we have 4 - 2 = 2 more removals to choose from the remaining 7 - 2 = 5 pads. However, we must also ensure that (3,3) remains connected to itself, but we have to make sure that (3,3) is not connected to (1,1), but since (1,1) is isolated, that's already satisfied. However, we also have to ensure that in the remaining graph, (3,3) is still in the same component as itself, which it is, but we have to make sure that there's no path from (1,1) to (3,3), which is already true because (1,1) is isolated. However, we also have to make sure that when we remove those additional two pads, we don't inadvertently reconnect (1,1) to the rest of the graph? Wait, no, because (1,1) is already isolated; removing other pads can't affect its isolation. So, in this case, the number of subsets where (1,1) is isolated is C(5,2) = 10, since after removing (1,2) and (2,1), we have 5 remaining non-start/end pads, and we need to remove 2 more. But wait, actually, the total number of removals is 4, so we have to remove 4 pads total. To isolate (1,1), we must remove (1,2) and (2,1), and then choose 2 more pads from the remaining 5 non-start/end pads (since the start and end are not removed). However, we must also ensure that (3,3) is not removed, but (3,3) is not among the non-start/end pads we are considering for removal; it's one of the two that must remain. So yes, the remaining 5 pads include (1,1) and (3,3) plus three others. Wait, actually, let me clarify:\n\nTotal non-start/end pads: 7. To isolate (1,1), we must remove its two neighbors: (1,2) and (2,1). Then, we have 4 total removals, so we need to remove 2 more pads from the remaining 7 - 2 = 5 pads (since we've already removed two). However, among these 5, we must not remove (3,3), but (3,3) is not among the non-start/end pads; wait, no: (3,3) is one of the two that must remain, so it is not in the non-start/end pads we are considering for removal. Wait, the non-start/end pads are all except (1,1) and (3,3). So, the 7 non-start/end pads are:\n\n(1,2), (1,3),\n\n(2,1), (2,2), (2,3),\n\n(3,1), (3,2).\n\nSo, to isolate (1,1), we need to remove (1,2) and (2,1). Then, from the remaining 5 non-start/end pads: (1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2), we need to remove 2 more. So the number of ways here is C(5,2) = 10.\n\nBut we must also ensure that in these removals, we don't inadvertently connect (1,1) to the rest of the graph? Wait, no, because we've already removed the two neighbors of (1,1), so (1,1) is isolated regardless of what else we remove. So all these 10 subsets will result in (1,1) being isolated and (3,3) being in the remaining graph. However, we have to make sure that (3,3) is still in the remaining graph, which it is because we are not removing it. Therefore, all 10 subsets in this case will satisfy the condition that (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected.\n\nCase 2: (1,1) is not isolated but in a component of size 2, and (3,3) is in a separate component. For example, maybe (1,1) and one other pad form a component, and (3,3) is in another component. To do this, we might need to block all paths from (1,1) to the rest except one neighbor, but also block paths to (3,3). This seems more complex.\n\nAlternatively, maybe split the grid into two parts with (1,1) in one and (3,3) in the other. For example, if we remove all pads in the middle row or something.\n\nAlternatively, consider that to have (1,1) and (3,3) in different components, there must be some \"barrier\" of removed pads between them. Given the grid's small size, perhaps the barrier has to be along a row or column.\n\nFor instance, if we remove all pads in the second row (row 2), then (1,1) can only go up to (2,1) which is removed, so it's isolated, and (3,3) is in row 3. But removing the entire second row would require removing 3 pads: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). But we are only allowed to remove 4 pads. So if we remove the entire second row (3 pads) plus one more, then we have 4 removals, and (1,1) is isolated and (3,3) is in the top row, but (3,3) can still move left and right in the top row? Wait, in the top row, (3,1) and (3,2) and (3,3) are present unless removed. If we removed the entire second row, then in the top row, (3,1), (3,2), (3,3) are all present unless we also removed some. Wait, in this case, if we remove the entire second row (3 pads) plus one more pad, say (3,1), then (3,1) is removed, so the top row has (3,2) and (3,3). Then (3,3) is connected to (3,2), and (3,2) is connected to nothing else except (3,1) which is removed and (2,2) which is removed. So (3,3) is isolated? Wait, no, (3,3) can go left to (3,2), so it's in a component with (3,2). Meanwhile, (1,1) is isolated. So in this case, removing the entire second row (3 pads) plus (3,1) gives a disconnect. But this is one specific case.\n\nBut this might be part of a larger class of cases. This approach might be too time-consuming.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of going through all cases, I can think of the total number of subsets that disconnect (1,1) and (3,3) as follows:\n\nSince the minimal vertex cut is size 3, then any vertex cut of size 3 or 4 will disconnect them. The number of vertex cuts of size 3 is some number, and the number of vertex cuts of size 4 is C(7,4) minus the number of vertex cuts of size less than 4? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to count all vertex cuts of size 3 first.\n\nA vertex cut of size 3 is a set of 3 pads whose removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3). To count them, we can look for all such triplets.\n\nEarlier, I found that removing (2,2), (1,3), and (3,1) disconnects them. Let me see if there are others.\n\nLet me try to systematically list all possible triplets and see which ones disconnect.\n\nBut this might take a while, but given the small size, perhaps manageable.\n\nFirst, label the non-start/end pads as follows for ease:\n\nLet me name the 7 non-start/end pads as follows:\n\nA = (1,2)\n\nB = (1,3)\n\nC = (2,1)\n\nD = (2,2)\n\nE = (2,3)\n\nF = (3,1)\n\nG = (3,2)\n\nSo, the pads are A,B,C,D,E,F,G.\n\nWe need to find all 3-element subsets of {A,B,C,D,E,F,G} such that their removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nLet me go through possible combinations.\n\nFirst, the example I had earlier: {D, B, F} which is {D=(2,2), B=(1,3), F=(3,1)}. Let's verify this disconnects.\n\nAfter removing D, B, F:\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, C, E, G.\n\nWait, no: the total non-start/end pads are A,B,C,D,E,F,G. Removing D, B, F leaves A, C, E, G. Plus (1,1) and (3,3).\n\nSo the remaining pads are:\n\n(1,1), A=(1,2) [no, wait B=(1,3) is removed], so (1,1) is connected to nothing except its neighbors which are removed: (1,2) [A is present? Wait, no:\n\nWait, if we remove B=(1,3), then (1,1) can still go to (1,2)=A and (2,1)=C. But we also removed D=(2,2), F=(3,1).\n\nWait, let me map it out:\n\nAfter removing D, B, F:\n\nThe remaining non-start/end pads are A=(1,2), C=(2,1), E=(2,3), G=(3,2).\n\nPlus (1,1) and (3,3).\n\nNow, connections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to A=(1,2) and C=(2,1).\n\nA=(1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2) [which is removed], so only to (1,1).\n\nC=(2,1) is connected to (1,1) and (3,1) [which is removed], so only to (1,1).\n\nE=(2,3) is connected to (2,2) [removed], (1,3) [removed], and (3,3). So E is connected to (3,3).\n\nG=(3,2) is connected to (3,1) [removed], (3,3), and (2,2) [removed]. So G is connected to (3,3).\n\nTherefore, the component containing (1,1) is just {(1,1), A, C}.\n\nThe component containing (3,3) is {E, G, (3,3)}.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So yes, {D,B,F} is a vertex cut.\n\nNow, let's see another triplet. How about {D, C, G}?\n\nRemoving D=(2,2), C=(2,1), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, E, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to A=(1,2) and C=(2,1) [but C is removed], so only A.\n\nA=(1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2) [removed], so only to (1,1).\n\n(2,1) is removed.\n\nE=(2,3) is connected to (2,2) [removed], (1,3)=B, and (3,3).\n\nF=(3,1) is connected to (3,2) [removed], and (2,1) [removed], so only isolated.\n\nB=(1,3) is connected to (1,2)=A and (2,3)=E.\n\nSo:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1) connected to A=(1,2), which is connected to B=(1,3), which is connected to E=(2,3), which is connected to (3,3). Also, E=(2,3) is connected to (3,3).\n\nWait, so (1,1) is connected through A-B-E-(3,3). Therefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are still connected. So this triplet {D,C,G} does NOT disconnect them. So that's not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm, okay.\n\nAnother triplet: {A, E, F}\n\nRemoving A=(1,2), E=(2,3), F=(3,1).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B, C, D, G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to C=(2,1) [since A is removed] and (1,2) is removed.\n\nC=(2,1) is connected to (1,1) and (3,1) [removed], so only to (1,1).\n\nB=(1,3) is connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\nD=(2,2) is connected to (1,2) [removed], (2,1)=C, (2,3) [removed], (3,2)=G.\n\nSo D is connected to C and G.\n\nG=(3,2) is connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) is connected to G and (3,2) [same as G] and (2,3) [removed].\n\nSo the component containing (3,3) is {G, D, C, (3,3)}? Wait:\n\nWait, (3,3) is connected to G=(3,2), which is connected to D=(2,2), which is connected to C=(2,1), which is connected to (1,1). So the entire graph is connected except for B=(1,3), which is isolated. So (1,1) and (3,3) are still connected through the path (1,1)-C-D-G-(3,3). Therefore, this triplet {A,E,F} does NOT disconnect them.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother triplet: {B, C, E}\n\nRemoving B=(1,3), C=(2,1), E=(2,3).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, D, F, G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to A=(1,2) and C=(2,1) [removed], so only A.\n\nA=(1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2)=D.\n\nD=(2,2) is connected to A, (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], and (3,2)=G.\n\nG=(3,2) is connected to D and (3,3).\n\nF=(3,1) is connected to (3,2) [G] and (2,1) [removed], so F is connected to G.\n\nTherefore, the component containing (1,1) is (1,1)-A-D-G-F-(3,3). So still connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother triplet: {A, C, G}\n\nRemoving A=(1,2), C=(2,1), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B, D, E, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to C=(2,1) [removed] and A=[removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) is connected to G=[removed] and E=(2,3). E is connected to (2,2)=D and (1,3)=B.\n\nB=(1,3) is connected to E and A=[removed], so B-E-D.\n\nD=(2,2) is connected to B-E and (3,2)=[removed], so D connected to E-B.\n\nF=(3,1) is connected to (3,2)=[removed] and (2,1)=[removed], so isolated.\n\nSo components:\n\n- (1,1) isolated.\n\n- F isolated.\n\n- (3,3) connected to E-D-B.\n\nTherefore, (1,1) is disconnected from (3,3). So this triplet {A,C,G} is a vertex cut.\n\nSo that's another one.\n\nOkay, so that's another vertex cut.\n\nContinuing this process is going to take a while, but let's try a few more.\n\nTriplet {A, D, F}:\n\nRemoving A=(1,2), D=(2,2), F=(3,1).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B, C, E, G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to C=(2,1) and A=[removed], so to C.\n\nC=(2,1) is connected to (1,1) and (3,1)=[removed], so only to (1,1).\n\nB=(1,3) is connected to (1,2)=[removed] and (2,3)=E.\n\nE=(2,3) is connected to B and (3,3).\n\nG=(3,2) is connected to (3,3) and (2,2)=[removed].\n\n(3,3) is connected to E and G.\n\nThus, the component containing (1,1) is {(1,1), C}.\n\nThe component containing (3,3) is {B, E, G, (3,3)}.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,D,F} is another vertex cut.\n\nOkay, that's a third one.\n\nTriplet {B, D, F}:\n\nRemoving B=(1,3), D=(2,2), F=(3,1).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, C, E, G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A=(1,2) and C=(2,1).\n\nA=(1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2)=[removed].\n\nC=(2,1) connected to (1,1) and (3,1)=[removed].\n\nE=(2,3) connected to (2,2)=[removed], (1,3)=[removed], and (3,3).\n\nG=(3,2) connected to (3,1)=[removed], (3,3), and (2,2)=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\n(1,1) is connected to A and C, forming a component {(1,1), A, C}.\n\n(3,3) is connected to E and G, forming a component {(3,3), E, G}.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {B,D,F} is another vertex cut.\n\nThat's a fourth one.\n\nTriplet {B, D, G}:\n\nRemoving B=(1,3), D=(2,2), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, C, E, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and (2,2)=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and (3,1)=F.\n\nF=(3,1) connected to C and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nE=(2,3) connected to (2,2)=[removed], (1,3)=[removed], and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C, F.\n\nComponent 2: (3,3), E.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {B,D,G} is another vertex cut.\n\nFifth one.\n\nTriplet {B, E, G}:\n\nRemoving B=(1,3), E=(2,3), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, C, D, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and (2,2)=D.\n\nD connected to A, (2,1)=C, and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and (2,1)=C? Wait, C=(2,1) is connected to (1,1) and (3,1)=F.\n\nF=(3,1) connected to C and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nE=(2,3) is removed, so not present.\n\n(3,3) is connected to G=[removed] and (2,3)=[removed], so (3,3) is isolated? Wait, (3,3)'s neighbors are (3,2) and (2,3), both removed. So (3,3) is isolated.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, C, F.\n\nComponent 2: (3,3) isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {B,E,G} is another vertex cut.\n\nSixth one.\n\nTriplet {C, D, G}:\n\nWait, we did {C,D,G} earlier and saw it didn't work. Let me confirm again.\n\nRemoving C=(2,1), D=(2,2), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, E, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A=(1,2) and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nB=(1,3) connected to A and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\nF=(3,1) connected to (3,2)=[removed] and (2,1)=[removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to E and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: F isolated.\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are still connected through the path (1,1)-A-B-E-(3,3). So not disconnected. So {C,D,G} is not a vertex cut.\n\nOkay, moving on.\n\nTriplet {C, E, F}:\n\nRemoving C=(2,1), E=(2,3), F=(3,1).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, D, G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, B=(1,3), and G=(3,2).\n\nB connected to A and D, and (1,3)'s other neighbor is E=[removed].\n\nG connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nSo the component containing (1,1) is (1,1)-A-D-B-G-(3,3). So still connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, E, G}:\n\nRemoving C=(2,1), E=(2,3), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, D, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, B=(1,3), and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nB connected to A and D.\n\nF=(3,1) connected to (3,2)=[removed] and (2,1)=[removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to G=[removed] and (2,3)=[removed], so isolated.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, B.\n\nComponent 2: (3,3) isolated.\n\nComponent 3: F isolated.\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected. So {C,E,G} is a vertex cut.\n\nSeventh one.\n\nTriplet {C, F, G}:\n\nRemoving C=(2,1), F=(3,1), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, D, E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, B=(1,3), and E=(2,3).\n\nB connected to A and D, and (1,3)'s other neighbor E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus, the component containing (1,1) is (1,1)-A-D-B-E-(3,3). So still connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {D, E, F}:\n\nRemoving D=(2,2), E=(2,3), F=(3,1).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, C, G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and (3,1)=[removed].\n\nB connected to A and E=[removed].\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G and (2,3)=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C.\n\nComponent 2: B connected only to A? Wait:\n\nB=(1,3) is connected to A=(1,2) and E=[removed], so B is connected to A.\n\nA is connected to B and (1,1). So actually, the component containing (1,1) includes A, B, C.\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B, C.\n\nComponent 2: G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {D,E,F} is a vertex cut.\n\nEighth one.\n\nTriplet {D, E, G}:\n\nRemoving D=(2,2), E=(2,3), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, C, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nB connected to A and E=[removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to G=[removed] and (2,3)=[removed], so isolated.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C, F.\n\nComponent 2: B isolated.\n\nComponent 3: (3,3) isolated.\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected. So {D,E,G} is a vertex cut.\n\nNinth one.\n\nTriplet {D, F, G}:\n\nRemoving D=(2,2), F=(3,1), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A, B, C, E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nB connected to A and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C.\n\nComponent 2: B, E, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {D,F,G} is a vertex cut.\n\nTenth one.\n\nTriplet {A, D, G}:\n\nRemoving A=(1,2), D=(2,2), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B, C, E, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A=[removed] and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C, F.\n\nComponent 2: B, E, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,D,G} is another vertex cut.\n\nEleventh one.\n\nTriplet {A, E, G}:\n\nRemoving A=(1,2), E=(2,3), G=(3,2).\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B, C, D, F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and (3,2)=[removed].\n\nD connected to (2,1)=C and (2,2)=[removed], and (1,2)=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3) and (2,3)=[removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to G=[removed] and (2,3)=[removed], so isolated.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C, F.\n\nComponent 2: B isolated.\n\nComponent 3: (3,3) isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,E,G} is a vertex cut.\n\nTwelfth one.\n\nTriplet {B, D, F} we already did, that was the fourth one.\n\nWait, let's see if we have duplicates.\n\nWait, let me count how many unique triplets I have found so far that work:\n\n1. {D, B, F}\n\n2. {A, C, G}\n\n3. {A, D, F}\n\n4. {B, D, G}\n\n5. {B, E, G}\n\n6. {C, E, G}\n\n7. {D, E, F}\n\n8. {D, E, G}\n\n9. {D, F, G}\n\n10. {A, D, G}\n\n11. {A, E, G}\n\nIs that 11? Wait, let me recount:\n\n1. {D,B,F}\n\n2. {A,C,G}\n\n3. {A,D,F}\n\n4. {B,D,G}\n\n5. {B,E,G}\n\n6. {C,E,G}\n\n7. {D,E,F}\n\n8. {D,E,G}\n\n9. {D,F,G}\n\n10. {A,D,G}\n\n11. {A,E,G}\n\nYes, 11 triplets. Let me see if there are more.\n\nTriplet {A, B, C}:\n\nRemoving A,B,C. Let's see:\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: D,E,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C=[removed], so only via... Wait, (1,1)'s neighbors are A and C, both removed, so (1,1) is isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to E and G.\n\nE connected to (2,3) and (3,3).\n\nG connected to (3,3) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to G and (2,1)=[removed] and (1,2)=[removed], so only to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1) isolated.\n\nComponent 2: (3,3), E, D, G.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,B,C} is another vertex cut.\n\nTwelfth.\n\nTriplet {A, B, D}:\n\nRemoving A,B,D.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: C,E,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and (3,2)=G.\n\nG connected to F and (3,3).\n\nE connected to (2,3) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E and G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C, F, G, (3,3), E.\n\nAll connected through the path (1,1)-C-F-G-(3,3)-E. So still connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {A, B, E}:\n\nRemoving A,B,E.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: C,D,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F.\n\nF connected to C and G.\n\nG connected to F and (3,3).\n\nD connected to (2,2) and (2,1)=C, and (1,2)=[removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to G and E=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C, F, G, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: D connected to C.\n\nWait, D is connected to C, so actually, D is in the same component as C, F, G, (3,3). Therefore, (1,1) is connected through C-D? Wait:\n\nWait, D=(2,2) is connected to C=(2,1) and (1,2)=[removed], and (3,2)=G.\n\nWait, D is connected to C and G. Since G is connected to F and (3,3), so D is connected to the main component. Therefore, (1,1) is connected through C-D-G-F-(3,3). So still connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {A, B, F}:\n\nRemoving A,B,F.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: C,D,E,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and (3,1)=F=[removed].\n\nThus, C is only connected to (1,1), forming a component { (1,1), C }.\n\nE=(2,3) connected to (2,2)=D and (1,3)=B=[removed].\n\nD connected to E and G=(3,2).\n\nG connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C.\n\nComponent 2: E, D, G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,B,F} is another vertex cut.\n\nThirteenth.\n\nTriplet {A, C, E}:\n\nRemoving A,C,E.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B,D,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C=[removed], so only via... Wait, (1,1)'s neighbors are A and C, both removed, so (1,1) is isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to G and E=[removed].\n\nG connected to (3,3) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to G and (2,1)=C=[removed], (1,2)=A=[removed].\n\nF=(3,1) connected to (3,2)=G and (2,1)=C=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1) isolated.\n\nComponent 2: (3,3), G, D, F.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,C,E} is a vertex cut.\n\nFourteenth.\n\nTriplet {A, C, F}:\n\nRemoving A,C,F.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B,D,E,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A=[removed] and C=[removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to G and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to (2,3) and (1,3)=B.\n\nB connected to E and (1,2)=A=[removed].\n\nG connected to (3,3) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to G and (2,1)=C=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1) isolated.\n\nComponent 2: B, E, (3,3), G, D.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,C,F} is a vertex cut.\n\nFifteenth.\n\nTriplet {A, D, E}:\n\nRemoving A,D,E.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B,C,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A=[removed] and C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and G=(3,2).\n\nG connected to F and (3,3).\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A=[removed] and E=[removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C, F, G, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: B isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {A,D,E} is a vertex cut.\n\nSixteenth.\n\nTriplet {A, D, G} we had earlier as tenth.\n\nTriplet {A, E, F} we had earlier as not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {A, F, G}:\n\nRemoving A,F,G.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B,C,D,E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nThus, C is only connected to (1,1), forming a component { (1,1), C }.\n\nE connected to (2,3) and (3,3).\n\nD connected to (2,2) and (1,2)=A=[removed], and (2,1)=C.\n\nSo D is connected to C and E?\n\nWait:\n\nD=(2,2) is connected to (1,2)=A=[removed], (2,1)=C, and (2,3)=E.\n\nThus, D is connected to C and E.\n\nE is connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nB=(1,3) connected to (1,2)=A=[removed] and (2,3)=E.\n\nThus, B is connected to E.\n\nTherefore, the component containing (3,3) is {E, D, C, B, (3,3)}? Wait:\n\nWait, C is connected to (1,1), and D is connected to C and E, and E is connected to B and (3,3). So the component is (1,1)-C-D-E-B-(3,3). Therefore, (1,1) is connected to (3,3) through this path. So not disconnected. So {A,F,G} is not a vertex cut.\n\nOkay, so that didn't work.\n\nTriplet {B, C, G}:\n\nRemoving B,C,G.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,D,E,F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, B=[removed], and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to D and (3,3).\n\nF=(3,1) connected to (3,2)=G=[removed] and (2,1)=C=[removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: F isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {B,C,G} is a vertex cut.\n\nSeventeenth.\n\nTriplet {B, D, F} we had earlier as fourth.\n\nTriplet {B, D, G} fifth.\n\nTriplet {B, E, F}:\n\nRemoving B,E,F.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,D,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, (2,1)=C, and (3,2)=G.\n\nG connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, C, G, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: B,E,F are removed, so no. Wait, B,E,F are removed, so remaining are A,C,D,G. So everything is connected through the path (1,1)-A-D-G-(3,3). Therefore, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {B, E, G} we had as fifth.\n\nTriplet {B, F, G}:\n\nRemoving B,F,G.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,D,E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, C=(2,1), and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, C, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: B,F,G removed.\n\nThus, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, D, E}:\n\nRemoving C,D,E.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A and E=[removed].\n\nF connected to (3,1) and G=(3,2).\n\nG connected to F and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B.\n\nComponent 2: F, G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {C,D,E} is a vertex cut.\n\nEighteenth.\n\nTriplet {C, D, G} we had earlier as not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, E, F} we had as not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, E, F} was not.\n\nTriplet {C, F, E} same.\n\nTriplet {C, F, D}:\n\nRemoving C,D,F.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,E,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E and G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B, E, (3,3), G.\n\nComponent 2: D and F are removed, so nothing else. Thus connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, G, F} we had earlier as not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {D, F, E} we had as seventh.\n\nTriplet {D, F, B}:\n\nRemoving D,F,B.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,E,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nE connected to (2,3) and (3,3).\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C.\n\nComponent 2: E, G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {D,F,B} is a vertex cut. Wait, but we already had {B,D,F} as the first one. Since sets are unordered, {B,D,F} is the same as {D,F,B}, so that's the same as the first one. So we shouldn't count it again.\n\nOkay, so up to now, after going through many triplets, I have identified 18 unique triplets that are vertex cuts, but some of these may be duplicates when considering unordered sets. Wait, actually in my counting above, I was careful to note when I was reusing the same set, but in the enumeration above, I think I might have inadvertently duplicated some.\n\nWait, for example, {B,D,F} was counted as the first one, and then later when I considered {B,D,F} again as part of another triplet, but since it's the same set, it shouldn't be counted twice.\n\nLooking back at my numbered list:\n\n1. {D,B,F}\n\n2. {A,C,G}\n\n3. {A,D,F}\n\n4. {B,D,G}\n\n5. {B,E,G}\n\n6. {C,E,G}\n\n7. {D,E,F}\n\n8. {D,E,G}\n\n9. {D,F,G}\n\n10. {A,D,G}\n\n11. {A,E,G}\n\n12. {A,B,C}\n\n13. {A,B,F}\n\n14. {A,C,E}\n\n15. {A,C,F}\n\n16. {A,D,E}\n\n17. {B,C,G}\n\n18. {C,D,E}\n\nWait, that's 18 unique triplets? Let me check if any of these are duplicates.\n\nLooking at the list:\n\n1. {B,D,F}\n\n2. {A,C,G}\n\n3. {A,D,F}\n\n4. {B,D,G}\n\n5. {B,E,G}\n\n6. {C,E,G}\n\n7. {D,E,F}\n\n8. {D,E,G}\n\n9. {D,F,G}\n\n10. {A,D,G}\n\n11. {A,E,G}\n\n12. {A,B,C}\n\n13. {A,B,F}\n\n14. {A,C,E}\n\n15. {A,C,F}\n\n16. {A,D,E}\n\n17. {B,C,G}\n\n18. {C,D,E}\n\nYes, these all seem distinct.\n\nWait, let's confirm for example, {A,B,C} is different from all others, yes.\n\nSimilarly, {A,B,F} is different from {A,B,C} etc.\n\nOkay, so that's 18 triplets.\n\nWait, but let me see if there are any more.\n\nTriplet {B, C, E}:\n\nRemoving B,C,E.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,D,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, (2,1)=C=[removed], and (2,3)=E=[removed].\n\nF connected to (3,1) and G=(3,2).\n\nG connected to F and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D.\n\nComponent 2: F, G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {B,C,E} is another vertex cut, the nineteenth.\n\nTriplet {B, C, F}:\n\nRemoving B,C,F.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,D,E,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, (2,1)=C=[removed], and (2,3)=E.\n\nE connected to D and (3,3).\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E and G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, E, (3,3), G.\n\nComponent 2: B,C,F removed.\n\nTherefore, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {B, D, E}:\n\nRemoving B,D,E.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,F,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and G=(3,2).\n\nG connected to F and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C, F, G, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: B,D,E removed.\n\nTherefore, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {B, D, G} we had as fourth.\n\nTriplet {B, E, F} we had as not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {B, F, G} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, D, F}:\n\nRemoving C,D,F.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,E,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E and G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B, E, (3,3), G.\n\nComponent 2: D and F removed.\n\nTherefore, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, D, E} we had as eighteenth.\n\nTriplet {C, D, F} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, E, F} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {C, F, G} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {D, B, G} we had as fourth.\n\nTriplet {D, E, F} seventh.\n\nTriplet {D, E, G} eighth.\n\nTriplet {D, F, G} ninth.\n\nTriplet {E, F, G}:\n\nRemoving E,F,G.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,C,D.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, (2,1)=C, and (2,3)=E=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, C.\n\nComponent 2: B connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A and E=[removed], so B is connected to A.\n\nWait, B=(1,3) is connected to A=(1,2) and E=(2,3)=[removed]. So B is connected to A.\n\nTherefore, Component 1 includes B as well: (1,1), A, D, C, B.\n\n(3,3) is connected to G=[removed] and E=[removed], so isolated.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B, C, D.\n\nComponent 2: (3,3) isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {E,F,G} is a vertex cut. That's the twentieth.\n\nOkay, so that's another one.\n\nContinuing:\n\nTriplet {E, F, B}:\n\nRemoving E,F,B.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,D,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, C=(2,1), and (2,3)=E=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, C.\n\nComponent 2: G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {E,F,B} is a vertex cut. Twenty-first.\n\nTriplet {E, F, C}:\n\nRemoving E,F,C.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,D,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, B=(1,3), and (2,3)=E=[removed].\n\nB connected to A and E=[removed].\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, B.\n\nComponent 2: G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {E,F,C} is a vertex cut. Twenty-second.\n\nTriplet {E, F, D}:\n\nRemoving E,F,D.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,C,G.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A and E=[removed].\n\nG connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to G.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C, B.\n\nComponent 2: G, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {E,F,D} is a vertex cut. Twenty-third.\n\nTriplet {E, G, F} we had as twentieth.\n\nTriplet {F, G, B}:\n\nRemoving F,G,B.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,D,E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, C=(2,1), and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E and G=[removed].\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, C, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: B,F,G removed.\n\nTherefore, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {F, G, C}:\n\nRemoving F,G,C.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,D,E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, B=(1,3), and E=(2,3).\n\nB connected to A and E.\n\nE connected to D and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, B, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: C,F,G removed.\n\nTherefore, connected. Not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {F, G, D} we had as ninth.\n\nTriplet {F, G, A}:\n\nRemoving F,G,A.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: B,C,D,E.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to C and A=[removed], so only C.\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A=[removed] and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\nD connected to (2,2) and C=(2,1), and (2,3)=E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), C.\n\nComponent 2: B, D, E, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {F,G,A} is a vertex cut. Twenty-fourth.\n\nTriplet {G, B, C}:\n\nRemoving G,B,C.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,D,E,F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=(2,2).\n\nD connected to A, (2,1)=C=[removed], and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to D and (3,3).\n\nF connected to (3,1) and G=[removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, D, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: F isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {G,B,C} is a vertex cut. Twenty-fifth.\n\nTriplet {G, B, D}:\n\nRemoving G,B,D.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,C,E,F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nC connected to (1,1) and F=(3,1).\n\nF connected to C and G=[removed].\n\nE connected to (2,3) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, C, F.\n\nComponent 2: E, (3,3).\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {G,B,D} is a vertex cut. Twenty-sixth.\n\nTriplet {G, B, E} we had as fifth.\n\nTriplet {G, C, D}:\n\nRemoving G,C,D.\n\nRemaining non-start/end pads: A,B,E,F.\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to A and C=[removed], so only A.\n\nA connected to (1,1) and D=[removed].\n\nB connected to (1,3)'s neighbors: A and E=(2,3).\n\nE connected to B and (3,3).\n\nF connected to (3,1) and G=[removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to E.\n\nThus:\n\nComponent 1: (1,1), A, B, E, (3,3).\n\nComponent 2: F isolated.\n\nTherefore, disconnected. So {G,C,D} is a vertex cut. Twenty-seventh.\n\nTriplet {G, C, E} we had as sixth.\n\nTriplet {G, C, F} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {G, D, E} we had as eighth.\n\nTriplet {G, D, F} we had as ninth.\n\nTriplet {G, D, A} we had as tenth.\n\nTriplet {G, E, F} we had as twentieth.\n\nTriplet {G, E, B} we had as fifth.\n\nTriplet {G, E, C} we had as sixth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, B} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {G, F, C} not a vertex cut.\n\nTriplet {G, F, D} ninth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, A} twenty-fourth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, E} twentieth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, B} not.\n\nTriplet {G, F, C} not.\n\nTriplet {G, F, D} ninth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, A} twenty-fourth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, B} not.\n\nTriplet {G, F, C} not.\n\nTriplet {G, F, D} ninth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, E} twentieth.\n\nTriplet {G, F, B} 