l number of vertex cuts of size 3 is 18, and each can be combined with 4 other pads, giving 18*4 = 72, but since the total number of subsets of size 4 is C(7,4)=35, which is much smaller than 72, this approach is flawed.\n\nWait a minute! Wait, this is a critical mistake. The total number of subsets of size 4 from 7 elements is C(7,4)=35. So how can we have 72? That's impossible. Therefore, my enumeration must have overcounted.\n\nAh, here's the problem: when I listed the triplets, I was considering all triplets from the 7 non-start/end pads, but actually, the vertex cuts of size 3 are subsets of size 3 from those 7, and the number of such subsets is C(7,3)=35. But I was trying to find which of those 35 triplets are vertex cuts. Earlier, I listed 18 triplets that are vertex cuts, but clearly, there are only 35 total triplets, so 18 is plausible, but when we want to count subsets of size 4 that include at least one vertex cut of size 3, then the number would be:\n\nFor each vertex cut of size 3, the number of supersets of size 4 that include it is C(7-3,1)=4, so total would be 18*4 = 72, but since the total number of subsets of size 4 is only 35, this is impossible. Therefore, my enumeration must have been flawed.\n\nAh, I see now. The mistake is that when I was enumerating the vertex cuts of size 3, I was considering all possible triplets from the 7 non-start/end pads, but in reality, some of these triplets might not actually disconnect (1,1) and (3,3), and some might be duplicates or I might have miscounted.\n\nWait, but I was careful to check each one. Alternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in considering the non-start/end pads.\n\nWait, let me recast:\n\nThe non-start/end pads are 7 in total:\n\nA=(1,2), B=(1,3),\n\nC=(2,1), D=(2,2), E=(2,3),\n\nF=(3,1), G=(3,2).\n\nTotal of 7.\n\nA vertex cut of size 3 is a subset of 3 of these 7 that, when removed, disconnects (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nI went through many triplets and found 18 that work, but since there are only C(