3, and there are more than just the row or column.\n\nHowever, the number of minimal vertex cuts might be calculated as follows:\n\nThe minimal vertex cut can be any set of 3 pads that \"separate\" the grid into two parts.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, the number of minimal vertex cuts between opposite corners might be symmetrical.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the number is 8 or 12.\n\nBut without precise calculation, it's hard.\n\nAlternatively, I recall that in a 3x3 grid, the number of minimal vertex cuts between two opposite corners is 8. For example, the four edges of the center and the four corners? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the grid as a graph and compute the vertex connectivity between (1,1) and (3,3).\n\nVertex connectivity k is the minimum number of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph.\n\nWe can use Menger's theorem, which states that the vertex connectivity is equal to the maximum number of vertex-disjoint paths.\n\nIn our case, the number of vertex-disjoint paths from (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nIn the original grid, how many vertex-disjoint paths are there?\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, from (1,1) to (3,3), the minimal path length is 4, and there are 6 paths of length 4.\n\nBut vertex-disjoint paths would require paths that don't share any vertices.\n\nGiven the small size, likely only one vertex-disjoint path exists, so the vertex connectivity is 1? But that can't be because we saw that removing one vertex doesn't disconnect them.\n\nWait, according to Menger's theorem, the vertex connectivity is the maximum number of vertex-disjoint paths. If there's only one vertex-disjoint path, then the connectivity is 1, but we saw that removing one vertex doesn't disconnect them, so that can't be.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake.\n\nWait, actually, in the 3x3 grid, between (1,1) and (3,3), how many vertex-disjoint paths are there?\n\nLet me try to find two vertex-disjoint paths.\n\nFirst path: right, right, up, up: (1,1) → (1,2) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3).\n\nSecond path: u