) [removed] or (3,2). So stuck at (3,3). So from (3,1), you can only reach (3,2) and (3,3), and from (3,1) to (2,1) to (1,1). But from (1,1), you can't go further right because (1,2) is removed, and from (3,3), you can't go up because (2,3) is removed. Meanwhile, (1,3) is connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so (1,3) is only connected to nothing? Wait, (1,3) has neighbors (1,2) and (2,3), both removed. Therefore, (1,3) is isolated? Wait, if we remove (1,2) and (2,3), then (1,3) has no neighbors left? So (1,3) is an isolated node. Meanwhile, (3,1) can reach (3,2) and (3,3) and (2,1) and (1,1), but (1,1) can't go anywhere except back to (2,1), and (3,2) and (3,3) can't go up. So (3,1) is in a connected component with (3,2), (3,3), (2,1), (1,1), but (1,3) is isolated. Therefore, (3,1) cannot reach (1,3) because (1,3) is in its own component. So in this case, removing (2,2), (1,2), and (2,3) disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). But wait, but in this case, we removed three nodes: (2,2), (1,2), (2,3). But we are supposed to remove four nodes. Hmm. So perhaps three nodes are sufficient? But the problem requires removing four nodes. So, in this case, if three nodes suffice to disconnect them, then any set of four nodes that includes those three (and one more) would also disconnect them, but also, there might be other ways to disconnect them with four nodes that don't include those three. Hmm.\n\nBut actually, the question is asking for the number of ways to remove four lily pads such that (3,1) and (1,3) remain but Kevin cannot get home. So, if three nodes can do the job, then any four-node removal that includes those three nodes will also do the job, but also, there might be other four-node removals that also disconnect the two points without necessarily including all three of those nodes. Therefore, we can't just count based on the minimal cut, but have to consider all possibilities.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the problem as: the set of all 4-node subsets of the 7 removable nodes, minus those subsets which do NOT disconnect (3,1) and (1,3). So, total subsets: C(7,4) = 35. Then, subtract the number of subsets where (3,1) and (1,3) remain connected. So, if we can compute the number of 4-node subsets that DO NOT disconnect (3,1) and (1,3), then the answer is 35 minus that number.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's manageable to compute the number of subsets that do disconnect them, directly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can categorize the nodes by their importance and see which removals are critical.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of trying to compute the complement, let me try to think of possible ways to block all paths.\n\nFirst, note that to block all paths from (3,1) to (1,3), we need to ensure that every possible path from (3,1) to (1,3) has at least one node removed from it. Since we can only remove 4 nodes, we have to choose 4 nodes such that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) contains at least one of the removed nodes.\n\nAlternatively, this is equivalent to saying that the set of removed nodes forms a vertex cover for all paths from (3,1) to (1,3). Wait, actually, a vertex cover is a set of vertices such that every edge is incident to at least one vertex in the set, but here we need a set of vertices such that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) includes at least one vertex from the set. That is called a vertex cut or a separating set. The minimal such set is called the vertex connectivity between the two nodes.\n\nBut in any case, since we have to remove exactly four nodes, perhaps we can find all such separating sets of size four.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the grid as layers. Let me consider the distance from (3,1) and (1,3). Let me see:\n\nThe grid is 3x3. Let me label the rows from top (row 1) to bottom (row 3), columns left (column 1) to right (column 3).\n\nSo starting from (3,1), which is the bottom-left corner. The top-right corner is (1,3). Let me see the distances.\n\nIn terms of Manhattan distance, the distance from (3,1) to (1,3) is |3-1| + |1-3| = 2 + 2 = 4. So the minimal path length is 4 edges, hence 5 vertices. So any path from (3,1) to (1,3) must have at least 5 vertices. So, the minimal number of nodes to remove to block all paths would be... hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can consider the grid as a graph and try to see which nodes are articulation points or something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider specific cases.\n\nFirst, note that in order to block all paths, we must block all possible routes. Let me try to identify critical nodes.\n\nThe center node (2,2) is very central. If we can block access to the center, that might help. Also, the nodes along the top row and the right column might be important.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the grid as consisting of the following \"rivers\" or \"corridors\":\n\n1. The bottom row: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n\n2. The left column: (1,1), (2,1), (3,1)\n\n3. The right column: (1,3), (2,3), (3,3)\n\n4. The top row: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\n5. The middle row: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)\n\n6. The middle column: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2)\n\nBut maybe that's complicating.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to consider that any path from (3,1) to (1,3) must pass through either the top row, the right column, or the center area.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to block the central area or the top row or the right column.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that in order to get from the bottom-left to the top-right, you have to go up and right. So, perhaps if we can block all the possible \"up\" moves or \"right\" moves in some critical locations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think in terms of rows. The frog has to move from row 3 to row 1, so must move up two times, and from column 1 to column 3, so must move right two times. So, in some sense, the frog must traverse two up moves and two right moves in some order, but the exact path depends on the grid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model this as a graph and think about which nodes are in every possible path. If there is a node that is common to all paths, then removing that node would disconnect the graph. But in this case, I don't think there is such a node. For example, the node (2,2) is on some paths but not all. For instance, the path going through (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3) doesn't go through (2,2). Similarly, the path going through (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) doesn't go through (2,2). So, no single node is on all paths.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, we need to remove nodes such that every possible path has at least one node removed.\n\nGiven that, perhaps the problem is similar to the classic vertex cover problem on the path graph, but here it's on a grid with multiple paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the dual: instead of removing nodes, we can think of the remaining nodes must form a graph where (3,1) and (1,3) are in different components. Since the grid is small, maybe we can enumerate the possibilities.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can consider that in order to prevent a path from existing, certain key areas must be blocked.\n\nLet me consider that in order for the frog to reach the top-right, it must pass through either the top row or the right column at some point. Wait, actually, the destination is on the top-right corner, so the frog must reach either (1,2) or (2,3) to get to (1,3). Alternatively, (1,3) itself is the destination, so to get there, the frog must arrive at either (1,2) or (2,3).\n\nTherefore, if we can block access to (1,2) and (2,3), then the frog cannot reach (1,3). However, note that (1,2) and (2,3) are not the start or end, so they are removable. But blocking both (1,2) and (2,3) would certainly prevent reaching (1,3), but maybe there are other paths. Wait, but if (1,2) is removed and (2,3) is removed, then the only way to reach (1,3) would be through other nodes? But (1,3)'s only neighbors are (1,2) and (2,3), so if both are removed, then (1