ved].\n\nSo from (2,1) to (1,1). From (1,1): can go to (1,2) or (2,1). From (1,1) to (1,2). From (1,2): can go to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed] or (1,1). So from (1,2) to (1,3). Thus, path: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). So that path still exists. So removing those two nodes isn't enough.\n\nHmm, how about removing (2,2) and (1,3)'s neighbor (2,3)? Wait, but (1,3) is fixed, so we can't remove it, but (2,3) is removable. So removing (2,2) and (2,3):\n\nWait, we did that before and found a path through the top row.\n\nAlternatively, removing (1,2) and (2,3):\n\nLet's see:\n\nAfter removing (1,2) and (2,3):\n\nStarting at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (3,2) or (2,1).\n\nFrom (3,2): can go to (3,3) or (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,1): can go to (1,1) or (3,1) or (2,2).\n\nFrom (3,3): can go to (2,3) [removed] or (3,2).\n\nFrom (1,1): can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,2): can go to (1,2) [removed], (2,1), (2,3) [removed], (3,2).\n\nSo let's see possible paths:\n\nOption 1: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(3,2) [loop], or (3,2)->(2,2). From (2,2), can go to (2,1) or (3,2). From (2,2)->(2,1)->(1,1). From (1,1) can't go further right because (1,2) is removed. From (2,2) can also go to (3,2)->(3,3), but that's a loop.\n\nAlternatively, from (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1). From (1,1) can't go right. From (1,1) can go back to (2,1). From (2,1) can go to (2,2). From (2,2) can go to (3,2) or (2,1). From (3,2) can go to (3,3). From (3,3) can go to (2,3) [removed], so stuck. So from (3,1), the frog can reach (3,2), (3,3), (2,2), (2,1), (1,1), but cannot reach (1,3) because to get to (1,3) you need to go through (1,2) or (2,3), both removed. Therefore, (1,3) is isolated. Thus, in this case, removing (1,2) and (2,3) disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). Wait, but we only removed two nodes here. Wait, but we need to remove four nodes. So if removing two nodes can disconnect them, then any set of four nodes that includes those two (and two others) would also disconnect them, but also there might be other four-node removals that disconnect them without necessarily including both (1,2) and (2,3).\n\nWait, in this case, removing (1,2) and (2,3) alone is enough to disconnect (3,1) from (1,3). But since we have to remove four nodes, perhaps we can consider that any four-node subset that includes these two critical nodes (1,2) and (2,3) will work, but also other four-node subsets that block other paths.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there are multiple minimal cuts of size two, three, or four.\n\nWait, let's verify again the case when removing (1,2) and (2,3):\n\nAfter removal, (1,3) has no neighbors left because its neighbors (1,2) and (2,3) are gone. So (1,3) is an isolated node, while (3,1) is still connected to a bunch of other nodes but cannot reach (1,3). Therefore, yes, that's a valid disconnection with just two node removals.\n\nSimilarly, if we remove (2,2) and (1,3)'s other neighbor (2,3), then (1,3) still has (1,2) as a neighbor, so (1,3) is not isolated, but maybe the path is blocked? Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, removing (2,2) and (2,3):\n\nThen, (1,3)'s neighbors are (1,2) and (2,3) [removed]. So (1,3) is connected only to (1,2). So (1,3) is still connected to (1,2). Then, can we get from (3,1) to (1,2)?\n\nYes: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2). So the path exists through (1,2), so (1,3) is reachable via (1,2). So in this case, removing (2,2) and (2,3) does not disconnect (3,1) from (1,3), because (1,3) is still reachable via (1,2). So that was my mistake earlier. So only when we remove both (1,2) and (2,3) does (1,3) become isolated, thus disconnected.\n\nSimilarly, if we remove (1,2) and (3,3):\n\nThen, (1,3)'s neighbors are (1,2) [removed] and (2,3). So (1,3) is still connected to (2,3). Then, can we get from (3,1) to (2,3)?\n\nYes: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3). So that path exists. So removing (1,2) and (3,3) doesn't disconnect them.\n\nAlternatively, removing (1,2) and (3,2):\n\nThen, (1,3)'s neighbors are (1,2) [removed] and (2,3). So (1,3) is connected to (2,3). Can we get from (3,1) to