3)->(2,3). That path exists. So from (3,1) to (3,2) to (3,3) to (2,3) to (1,3). So that path is still there. Therefore, (1,3) is reachable.\n\nTherefore, only when both (1,2) and (2,3) are removed does (1,3) become isolated. Alternatively, if we remove (1,2) and some other node that also blocks the path to (2,3), but I don't think so. For instance, if we remove (1,2) and (3,3):\n\nThen, as before, (1,3) is connected to (2,3), and (3,1) can reach (2,3) via (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3). So still connected.\n\nAlternatively, removing (1,2) and (3,2):\n\nThen, (1,3) is connected to (2,3). From (3,1), can we reach (2,3)? Yes: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3). So that path exists.\n\nTherefore, the only pair of nodes whose removal disconnects (3,1) from (1,3) is the pair (1,2) and (2,3). Any other pair does not suffice.\n\nWait, but what about removing (1,2), (2,3), and two others? That would also disconnect, but we are looking for the minimal cuts. Since a pair suffices, then any four-node set that includes that pair would also disconnect, but there are also other four-node sets that don't include both (1,2) and (2,3) but still disconnect the graph by blocking all paths in some other way.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the total number of disconnecting subsets is equal to:\n\n- All subsets of 4 nodes that include both (1,2) and (2,3), plus\n\n- All subsets of 4 nodes that don't include both (1,2) and (2,3) but still block all paths.\n\nTherefore, to compute the total, we need to compute both.\n\nFirst, compute the number of subsets that include both (1,2) and (2,3). Since we must choose 4 nodes from the 7 removable nodes, and two of them are fixed as (1,2) and (2,3), then the remaining two nodes to choose are from the remaining 5 removable nodes (since we've already chosen two: (1,2) and (2,3)), so C(5,2) = 10.\n\nWait, hold on: the removable nodes are 7 in total: (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3). So if we require that a subset includes (1,2) and (2,3), then the other two nodes are chosen from the remaining 5: (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3). So yes, C(5,2)=10.\n\nSecond, we need to compute the number of subsets that do NOT include both (1,2) and (2,3), but still manage to disconnect (3,1) from (1,3). These would be subsets that block all paths without relying on isolating (1,3) by removing both its neighbors. So, such subsets must block all possible paths through other means.\n\nTo compute this, we need to find all 4-node subsets that don't include both (1,2) and (2,3), but still have the property that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) passes through at least one removed node.\n\nThis seems complicated, but perhaps manageable by considering different cases.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's easier to calculate the total number of subsets that disconnect the two points (including those that include the critical pair and those that don't) and then subtract from the total to get the answer? Wait, no, actually, the question is to compute the number of subsets that DO disconnect them, so we need to compute both parts.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think of all the possible ways to block all paths without isolating (1,3). For instance, maybe by blocking access through the center or other key nodes.\n\nLet me consider that in order to block all paths without isolating (1,3), we must ensure that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) passes through at least one of the removed nodes, but (1,3) still has at least one neighbor remaining so it's not isolated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only way to disconnect without isolating (1,3) is to block all paths through the center or through the top row and right column.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of specific subsets.\n\nFirst, let me consider subsets that include (2,2). Since the center node is very central, removing it might help block many paths.\n\nSuppose we remove (2,2) and three other nodes. Let's see if such a subset can disconnect (3,1) from (1,3) without isolating (1,3).\n\nFor example, remove (2,2), (1,1), (2,1), and (3,3). Let's see if that disconnects them.\n\nAfter removing those four nodes: (2,2), (1,1), (2,1), (3,3).\n\nCheck if (3,1) can reach (1,3):\n\nStarting at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (3,2) or (2,1). But (2,1) is removed, so only (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2): can go to (3,3) [removed] or (2,2) [removed] or (3,1). So stuck at (3,2). So (3,1) can only reach (3,2). Meanwhile, (1,3)'s neighbors are (1,2) and (2,3). Let's see if those are accessible from the remaining nodes.\n\n(1,2) is still present? Wait, (1,1) was removed, but (1,2) is not removed unless we removed it. In this subset, we removed (2,2), (1,1), (2,1), (3,3). So (1,2) is still there. Similarly, (2,3) is still there.\n\nFrom (3,1)'s side, it's stuck at (3,2). From (1,3)'s side, (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (2,3). Let's see if there's a path from (1,3) to the rest of the graph.\n\nFrom (1,3) -> (1,2). From (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed], (1,3), or (2,2) [removed]. So from (1,2) can only go back to (1,3). From (1,3) -> (2,3). From (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2) [removed], or (3,3) [removed]. So (2,3) is also stuck. Thus, the component containing (1,3) is just (1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3), but those can't go anywhere else. Meanwhile, (3,1) is in a separate component with (3,2). So, actually, (3,1) and (1,3) are in separate components. Therefore, this subset does disconnect them, even though (1,3) is not isolated—it's in a component of size 3 (itself, (1,2), (2,3)), but disconnected from the other component containing (3,1). Therefore, this is another disconnecting subset that doesn't involve isolating (1,3).\n\nTherefore, such subsets exist beyond those that include both (1,2) and (2,3). Therefore, we have to consider these as well.\n\nThis is getting complex. Maybe a better approach is to model this as a graph and for each subset of 4 nodes (from the 7 removable), check if (3,1) and (1,3) are disconnected. Since there are only 35 subsets, perhaps we can categorize them based on certain properties.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think of the problem in terms of the necessary conditions for disconnection.\n\nFor the frog to be unable to reach home, the following must hold: there is no path from (3,1) to (1,3) in the remaining graph. This can happen in two main scenarios:\n\n1. (1,3) is isolated (i.e., all its neighbors are removed), which requires removing both (1,2) and (2,3).\n\n2. (1,3) is not isolated, but all paths to it from (3,1) pass through at least one removed node.\n\nTherefore, the total number of disconnecting subsets is equal to:\n\n- The number of subsets that remove both (1,2) and (2,3) plus\n\n- The number of subsets that remove neither (1,2) nor (2,3) but still block all paths.\n\nTherefore, let's compute these two parts.\n\nFirst part: subsets that remove both (1,2) and (2,3). As above, the number of such subsets is C(5,2) = 10. Because we must include (1,2) and (2,3), and choose 2 more from the remaining 5 removable nodes (since total removable nodes are 7, minus the 2 we've already chosen, leaves 5).\n\nSecond part: subsets that do NOT remove both (1,2) and (2,3), but still block all paths. To compute this, we need to count subsets of 4 nodes from the 7 removable nodes that do not include both (1,2) and (2,3), but still ensure that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) has at least one node removed.\n\nTo compute this, we can consider cases where either:\n\n- The subset includes (1,2) but not (2,3), and still blocks all paths.\n\n- The subset includes (2,3) but not (1,2), and still blocks all paths.\n\n- The subset includes neither (1,2) nor (2,3), and still blocks all paths.\n\nSo we can break this into three cases.\n\nCase 1: Subsets that include (1,2) but not (2,3).\n\nIn this case, since (1,2) is removed, the neighbor (1,2) of (1,3) is gone, so (1,3) can only be reached via (2,3). Therefore, to block the path to (1,3), we need to also block access to (2,3). Since (2,3) is not removed in this case, we must block all paths to (2,3) from (3,1). Additionally, we must ensure that there is no alternative path to (1,3) through other nodes.\n\nWait, but if (1,2) is removed, then (1,3)'s only neighbor remaining is (2,3) (unless we also remove (2,3), but in this case, we are in the case where we do not remove (2,3)). Therefore, to prevent reaching (1,3), we must ensure that there is no path from (3,1) to (2,3). So the requirement is that in the subset, we must remove enough nodes so that there is no path from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nAdditionally, since (1,2) is already removed, but we still have to remove one more node (since we are choosing 4 nodes total, and we've already chosen (1,2) and need three more, but excluding (2,3)), so let me clarify:\n\nIn Case 1: Subsets that include (1,2) but not (2,3). So we have to choose 3 more nodes from the remaining 5 removable nodes excluding (2,3). Wait, total removable nodes are 7: { (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3) }.\n\nIf we must include (1,2) and exclude (2,3), then the remaining nodes to choose are from the 5 nodes: { (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3) }, and we need to choose 3 of them. So the number of such subsets is C(5,3) = 10. However, not all of these subsets will block all paths. We need to count how many of these 10 subsets block all paths from (3,1) to (2,3), because if they do, then since (1,3) can only be reached via (2,3), then (1,3) would be unreachable.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the number of valid subsets in Case 1 is equal to the number of 3-node subsets from { (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3) } that, when combined with (1,2), block all paths from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nSimilarly, let's define the problem for this subcase: given the graph with (1,2) removed, how many 3-node subsets from the remaining nodes (excluding (2,3)) block all paths from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nAlternatively, in the modified graph where (1,2) is removed, we need to remove 3 more nodes (from the 5 listed above) such that there is no path from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nLet me consider the graph with (1,2) removed. The nodes are still all except (1,2) and the removed ones will be added. Wait, no: in this case, we are considering the subsets that include (1,2) and three others from the five, so the graph after removal has (1,2) and those three others removed, plus (2,3) is still present.\n\nWait, actually, to clarify:\n\nIn Case 1: The subset includes (1,2) and three others from { (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3) }, and does not include (2,3). Therefore, in the remaining graph, (2,3) is still present, as well as (1,3). The question is whether there is a path from (3,1) to (2,3), because if there is, then the frog can go from (3,1) to (2,3) and then to (1,3), so that would be a path. Therefore, to block this, we must ensure that there is no path from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: In the original grid with (1,2) removed, how many 3-node subsets (from the five nodes: (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3)) when removed, block all paths from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nAlternatively, equivalently, in the graph where (1,2) is already removed, we want to remove three more nodes such that (3,1) and (2,3) are disconnected.\n\nSo let's model this subgraph: original grid minus (1,2). So nodes present are all except (1,2). We need to remove three more nodes (from the five: (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3)) such that (3,1) and (2,3) are disconnected.\n\nLet me denote the nodes:\n\nNodes present: (1,1), (1,3), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3). Wait, but (1,2) is removed. So the nodes are all except (1,2). So (1,1), (1,3), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3).\n\nWe need to remove three of the five: (1,1), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3). Let's call these the \"candidate nodes\" for removal. The remaining nodes after removal will be the original minus (1,2) minus the three candidates.\n\nWe need the remaining graph (after removing (1,2) and the three candidates) to have no path from (3,1) to (2,3).\n\nSo, to compute this, perhaps it's 