e can use inclusion-exclusion or other combinatorial techniques, but let me see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider the structure of the grid and see which pads are \"critical\" or \"necessary\" for connectivity.\n\nFirst, note that the center pad (2,2) is very central. Removing it might significantly disrupt paths.\n\nSimilarly, the pads along the main diagonals or central rows and columns might be important.\n\nLet me consider that in order to block all paths from A=(1,1) to B=(3,3), we need to ensure that every possible path from A to B has at least one of its pads removed.\n\nSo, equivalently, the set of removed pads must form a hitting set for all paths from A to B. A hitting set is a set of elements that intersects every set in a collection. In this case, the collection is all paths from A to B, and we want a hitting set of size 4.\n\nTherefore, we need to count the number of hitting sets of size 4 for all paths from A to B in the original graph, excluding A and B themselves.\n\nBut hitting set problems are generally hard, but since the graph is small, maybe we can do it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps first characterize all the paths from A to B, then see which pads are on which paths, and then figure out which pads are in the most paths, so that removing them would block many paths, but we need to remove four pads that cover all paths.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think in terms of necessary pads: if a pad is on every path from A to B, then removing that pad would block all paths. But as we saw earlier, there might not be any such pad because there are multiple paths that don't share a common pad.\n\nWait, let me check: is there any pad that is on every path from A to B?\n\nTake pad (2,2). Is (2,2) on every path?\n\nWell, some paths go through (2,2), like the ones that go right, right, up, up or up, up, right, right, but others go around. For instance, the path (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3) does not go through (2,2). Similarly, the path (1,1)->(2,1)