om B.\n\nLet me try removing (2,2), (1,3), and (3,1).\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (1,2) or (2,1).\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\nCan go to (2,2) [removed] or (1,3) [removed], so stuck.\n\nFrom (2,1):\n\nCan go to (3,1) [removed] or (2,2) [removed], so stuck.\n\nTherefore, from A, the frog can't go anywhere. So he's stuck at (1,1). Therefore, he cannot reach B. So this is a vertex cut of size 3. Similarly, if we remove other triples, maybe?\n\nAnother triple: (2,2), (3,2), (1,3).\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nGo to (1,2) or (2,1).\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\nCan go to (1,3) [removed] or (2,2) [removed], so stuck.\n\nFrom (2,1):\n\nCan go to (3,1) or (2,2) [removed]. So from (2,1) can go to (3,1).\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\nCan go to (3,2) [removed], so stuck. So from (3,1) can't proceed. Thus, the frog can't reach B from A via that path, but is there another path?\n\nWait, from (2,1) to (3,1) is blocked by not being able to go further? Wait, (3,1) can go to (3,2) which is removed, so no. So the frog can't get to B via that route. And the other route via (1,2) is also blocked. So yes, this is also a vertex cut of size 3.\n\nSo indeed, vertex cuts of size 3 are possible. So the minimal vertex cut size is 3. Therefore, vertex cuts of size 4 would be all subsets of 4 vertices that include a vertex cut of size 3, or maybe some other configurations?\n\nWait, no. A vertex cut of size 4 is just a set of four vertices whose removal disconnects A from B. It doesn't have to include a minimal vertex cut. It just needs to be that the set disconnects them, regardless of whether it's minimal or not.\n\nTherefore, to count all such subsets of size 4 that disconnect A from B, we need to consider all subsets of four vertices (from the seven non-A/B pads) such that every path from A to B in the original graph passes through at least one vertex in the subset.\n\nSo, perhaps the way to compute this is:\n\nTotal subsets of 4 pads from 7: 35.\n\nMinus the subsets that do NOT disconnect A from B, i.e., subsets where there exists 