 problem as a graph and try to find all subsets of four nodes that form a vertex cut between A and B.\n\nSince the total number is manageable (35), maybe I can find a smart way to count them by considering different cases based on which critical pads are removed.\n\nFirst, note that the center pad (2,2) is critical in many paths, so maybe removing it is a good start.\n\nCase 1: The subset of four removed pads includes the center pad (Pad4).\n\nCase 2: The subset does not include the center pad.\n\nWe can compute these two cases separately.\n\nCase 1: Removing Pad4 (center) plus three other pads.\n\nWe need to ensure that even with Pad4 removed, the remaining three pads plus the removal of Pad4 block all paths from A to B.\n\nWait, actually, in this case, since we are removing four pads total, and one of them is Pad4, we have to remove three more pads from the remaining six pads (since total non-A/B pads are seven, minus Pad4 leaves six).\n\nBut in this case, we need the combination of Pad4 removal plus three others to block all paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think that in Case 1, we have already removed Pad4, so we need three more pads to be removed such that, along with Pad4, they block all paths.\n\nBut maybe this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to think of the grid and see what needs to be removed to block all paths.\n\nFirst, note that any path from A=(1,1) to B=(3,3) must traverse from the bottom-left to the top-right. Since the frog can move only up, down, left, right, the path must move generally towards the top-right.\n\nThere are two main directions: moving right then up, or up then right, but can also meander.\n\nHowever, to block all paths, we need to ensure that every possible route is blocked by at least one removed pad.\n\nLet me consider that the grid can be divided into two \"sides\": the left side and the right side, or top and bottom? Maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, the key is that to get from (1,1) to (3,3), you have to cross either the middle row or the middle column at some point.\n\nSpecifically, the middle row is row 2, and the middle column is column 2.\n\nThe frog must at some point enter either the middle row or the middle column to proceed towards the top-right.\n\nWait, actually, to go from row 1 to row 3, you have to go through row 2 at some point, unless you go around via the sides. Wait, but in a 3x3 grid, moving from row 1 to row 3 requires passing through row 2 unless you move along the columns.\n\nWait, actually, no. For example, from (1,1), you can go to (2,1) (entering row 2), or to (1,2) (staying in row 1). From (1,2), you can go to (1,3) (staying in row 1) or to (2,2) (entering row 2). From (1,3), you can go down to (2,3) (entering row 2). Similarly, from the bottom row (row 1), to get to the top row (row 3), you have to go through row 2 at some point, unless you go all the way right then up, but even then, from (1,3) you have to go down to (2,3) to get to (3,3). So actually, every path from A to B must pass through row 2 at some point, either in column 1, 2, or 3.\n\nSimilarly, to get from column 1 to column 3, you have to pass through column 2 at some point.\n\nTherefore, any path from A to B must traverse through either the middle row (row 2) or the middle column (column 2), or both.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, we need to ensure that every path is blocked by removing at least one pad in either the middle row or the middle column.\n\nWait, but actually, since paths can go around, maybe we need to block all possible entries into the middle row and middle column?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as needing to block all entry points into the \"critical\" area.\n\nAlternatively, another approach: to block all paths, we need to make sure that from A=(1,1), the frog cannot reach any pad in row 2 or column 2 without first passing through a removed pad.\n\nWait, not exactly. Alternatively, perhaps if we remove all pads in row 2 and column 2 except for the necessary ones? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the grid's structure as follows:\n\nThe starting point A=(1,1) is in the bottom-left corner. The destination B=(3,3) is in the top-right corner.\n\nTo move from A to B, the frog must eventually reach either the middle row (row 2) or the middle column (column 2). Let's consider the middle row (row 2):\n\nRow 2 has three pads: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3).\n\nSimilarly, column 2 has three pads: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2).\n\nThe center pad (2,2) is shared by both.\n\nIf we can block all paths through the middle row and middle column, then perhaps we can disconnect A from B.\n\nAlternatively, to block all paths through the middle row, we might need to remove all pads in row 2 except maybe one, but that might be too many.\n\nAlternatively, if we remove all pads in row 2, that would be three pads, but we can only remove four, so combined with another pad somewhere else.\n\nWait, but let me think step by step.\n\nSuppose we want to block all paths that go through the middle row (row 2). To do this, we can remove all three pads in row 2. But that would require removing three pads, and then we can remove one more pad somewhere else. However, even if we remove all three pads in row 2, then the frog cannot use the middle row at all. Let's see if that blocks all paths.\n\nIf we remove row 2 entirely: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). Then, from A=(1,1), the frog can go to (1,2) or (2,1). But (2,1) is removed, so only (1,2). From (1,2), can go to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed], so only (1,3). From (1,3), can go to (2,3) [removed], so only down to (2,3) is blocked. Wait, (1,3) can only go down to (2,3), which is removed, so from (1,3), the frog is stuck. Therefore, from A=(1,1), the frog can only go to (1,2), then to (1,3), then stuck. So cannot reach B. Therefore, removing all three pads in row 2 would block all paths. But that's three pads, so if we remove those three plus one more, that would be four pads. But actually, even removing just the three pads in row 2 would suffice to block all paths. But we are only required to remove four pads, so in this case, if we remove the three pads of row 2 plus any one other pad, then the frog cannot reach B. But actually, removing just the three pads of row 2 already blocks all paths, so any superset of that (i.e., removing those three plus another) would also block all paths. However, we need to count all subsets of four pads that block all paths, which includes not only those that include all three row 2 pads plus one more, but also other combinations that might block the paths without removing all of row 2.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that there are multiple ways to block the paths: either by removing key pads in row 2, column 2, or some combination.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to categorize the possible blocking strategies.\n\nStrategy 1: Remove all three pads in row 2. As above, this blocks all paths. Then, any subset that includes these three plus one more pad will also block all paths. The number of such subsets is C(remaining pads, 1). Since we have already removed the three row 2 pads, we can choose any one more pad from the remaining four non-A/B pads that are not in row 2. Wait, total non-A/B pads are seven. The row 2 pads are three: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). The other non-A/B pads are: (1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2). So four pads. Therefore, the number of subsets that include the three row 2 pads plus one of these four is 4. So four subsets in this category.\n\nStrategy 2: Remove all three pads in column 2. Column 2 has three pads: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2). Similarly, removing these three would block all paths through column 2. Let's verify:\n\nIf we remove column 2's three pads: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2).\n\nFrom A=(1,1), the frog can go to (2,1) or (1,2). But (1,2) is removed, so only (2,1). From (2,1), can go to (3,1) or (2,2) [removed], so only (3,1). From (3,1), can go to (3,2) [removed], so stuck. Thus, the frog cannot reach B. So removing the three column 2 pads blocks all paths. Then, the number of subsets that include these three plus one more pad from the remaining non-A/B pads not in column 2. The non-A/B pads not in column 2 are: (1,3), (2,3), (3,1), (2,1). Wait, column 2 is (1,2), (2,2), (3,2). So non-A/B pads not in column 2 are: (1,3), (2,3), (3,1), (2,1). That's four pads. So again, subsets would be C(4,1)=4. So another four subsets.\n\nStrategy 3: Remove two pads from row 2 and two from column 2. Wait, but this might overcount with the previous strategies. Alternatively, maybe other combinations.\n\nAlternatively, Strategy 4: Remove the center pad (2,2) and then three other pads that block the remaining paths.\n\nIf we remove (2,2), then we have to block the remaining paths that go around the center. For example, the path that goes along the top and right edges: (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3). To block this path, we need to remove either (1,2), (1,3), or (2,3).\n\nSimilarly, the path that goes down the left side: (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3). To block this, need to remove (2,1), (3,1), or (3,2).\n\nAdditionally, there are paths that go through the other side, like (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) [but (2,2) is removed], so that path is blocked. Wait, if we remove (2,2), then paths going through (2,2) are blocked, but other paths can still go around.\n\nSo to block all paths after removing (2,2), we need to block the top-right and bottom-left paths.\n\nSpecifically, to block the top-right path: must remove at least one of (1,2), (1,3), or (2,3).\n\nTo block the bottom-left path: must remove at least one of (2,1), (3,1), or (3,2).\n\nAdditionally, there might be other paths, like (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2) is blocked, but (1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3) is still possible unless we block (1,3) or (2,3). Similarly, (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3) is blocked if we remove (3,2), but if not, then that path is still open.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths after removing (2,2), we need to ensure that:\n\n- On the top path (through (1,2), (1,3), (2,3)), at least one of those three is removed.\n\n- On the bottom path (through (2,1), (3,1), (3,2)), at least one of those three is removed.\n\nAdditionally, there might be other paths that go through other combinations, but I think these two paths cover most of the possibilities.\n\nTherefore, if we remove (2,2) plus one pad from the top path trio and one pad from the bottom path trio, that might suffice, but we need to make sure that all other potential paths are also blocked.\n\nAlternatively, to guarantee blocking all paths when removing (2,2), we need to ensure that both the top path and the bottom path are blocked, which requires:\n\n- At least one of { (1,2), (1,3), (2,3) } is removed,\n\nAND\n\n- At least one of { (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) } is removed.\n\nAdditionally, since we are removing four pads in total, and one of them is (2,2), we need to choose three more pads such that the above two conditions are satisfied.\n\nThis seems a bit involved, but let's see.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet T = { (1,2), (1,3), (2,3) }, the top path pads.\n\nLet B = { (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) }, the bottom path pads.\n\nWe need to choose three more pads (since we already removed (2,2)), such that at least one from T and at least one from B are removed.\n\nThe remaining non-A/B pads excluding (2,2) are:\n\nTotal non-A/B pads: 7.\n\nMinus (2,2): 6 left.\n\nThese six pads are:\n\nFrom T: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3)\n\nFrom B: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2)\n\nAnd also the center row/column but not in T or B? Wait no, T and B cover all except the center and the other corners?\n\nWait, actually, the non-A/B pads excluding (2,2) are:\n\nPad1: (1,2) ∈ T,\n\nPad2: (1,3) ∈ T,\n\nPad3: (2,1) ∈ B,\n\nPad4: removed (center),\n\nPad5: (2,3) ∈ T,\n\nPad6: (3,1) ∈ B,\n\nPad7: (3,2) ∈ B.\n\nSo the remaining six pads are indeed T ∪ B, where T has three pads and B has three pads.\n\nTherefore, we have to choose three pads from these six, with the condition that at least one is from T and at least one is from B.\n\nThe total number of ways to choose three pads from six is C(6,3)=20.\n\nFrom these, we subtract the number of subsets that have no pads from T (all three from B) and the number that have no pads from B (all three from T).\n\nNumber with no pads from T: all three from B. There are C(3,3)=1 subset.\n\nNumber with no pads from B: all three from T. There are C(3,3)=1 subset.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets of three pads from the six that include at least one from T and at least one from B is 20 - 1 - 1 = 18.\n\nTherefore, in this case (Case 1 where we removed (2,2) plus three others chosen from the remaining six such that they block both T and B paths), there are 18 subsets.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Each of these subsets of three pads, when combined with the removal of (2,2), would block all paths? Is that necessarily true?\n\nWait, let me test with an example.\n\nSuppose we remove (2,2) plus the three pads from T: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). This is one of the subsets we excluded (the all-T subset). But we subtracted those, so they are excluded. So that's good, because if we remove all of T, then the top path is blocked, but the bottom path might still be open. Wait, actually, if we remove all of T, then the top path is blocked, but the bottom path could still be open unless we also block it.\n\nWait, in this case, if we remove (2,2) plus all of T: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3), then:\n\nFrom A=(1,1), can go to (2,1) or (1,2). But (1,2) is removed, so only (2,1). From (2,1), can go to (3,1) or (2,2) [removed], so to (3,1). From (3,1), can go to (3,2). From (3,2), can go to (3,3) or (2,2) [removed], so to (3,3). So path exists: (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3). So even with removing T and (2,2), the bottom path is still open. Therefore, this subset does NOT block all paths, which is why we subtracted it. Similarly, the all-B subset: removing (2,2) plus all of B: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). Then:\n\nFrom A=(1,1), can go to (1,2) or (2,1). (2,1) is removed, so only (1,2). From (1,2), can go to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed], so to (1,3). From (1,3), can go to (2,3). From (2,3), can go to (3,3) or (2,2) [removed], so to (3,3). So path exists: (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3). So that path is still open. Therefore, those subsets that take all of T or all of B do not block all paths, so we correctly subtracted them.\n\nHowever, what about a subset that has two from T and one from B?\n\nFor example, remove (2,2), plus (1,2), (1,3) [from T], and (3,1) [from B].\n\nThen, can the frog still find a path?\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nGo to (2,1) or (1,2). (1,2) is removed, so only (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (3,1) [removed] or (2,2) [removed], so stuck. So cannot proceed further. Therefore, this subset does block all paths? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, from (2,1), you can go to (3,1), but (3,1) is removed, so no. And (2,2) is removed. So from (2,1), you can only go back to (1,1). So the frog is stuck. Meanwhile, the other path through (1,2) is blocked because (1,2) is removed. So indeed, this subset blocks all paths. So that works.\n\nAnother example: remove (2,2), plus (1,3), (2,3) [both from T], and (3,2) [from B].\n\nThen, from A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (1,2) or (2,1).\n\nIf go to (1,2), then from there to (1,3) [removed], so stuck. If go to (2,1), then from (2,1) can go to (3,1) or (2,2) [removed]. So to (3,1). From (3,1), can go to (3,2) [removed], so stuck. Therefore, all paths are blocked. Good.\n\nAnother example: remove (2,2), plus (2,1) [from B], (3,1) [from B], and (2,3) [from T].\n\nThen:\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nGo to (1,2) or (2,1). (2,1) is removed, so only (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2), can go to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed], so to (1,3).\n\nFrom (1,3), can go to (2,3) [removed], so stuck. So the frog can't reach B. Alternatively, if from (1,2) you go to (2,2) which is removed, so stuck. So yes, blocked.\n\nTherefore, it seems that as long as in the three additional pads, we have at least one from T and at least one from B, then the paths are blocked. So the count of 18 in this case (Case 1 where we removed the center pad and three others with at least one from T and one from B) is correct.\n\nTherefore, Case 1 total: 18 subsets.\n\nCase 2: Subsets that do NOT include the center pad (Pad4). So we are removing four pads from the remaining six non-A/B pads excluding the center. Wait, total non-A/B pads are seven, so excluding the center leaves six. We need to choose four of these six, such that their removal disconnects A from B.\n\nIn this case, we don't have the center pad removed, so we have to block all paths without relying on the center pad being removed.\n\nSo we need to choose four pads from the six non-center non-A/B pads (i.e., the six pads: (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2)) such that their removal blocks all paths from A to B.\n\nNote that these six pads form the \"frame\" around the center.\n\nLet me denote these six pads as:\n\nPad1: (1,2) – top of left edge\n\nPad2: (1,3) – top of right edge\n\nPad3: (2,1) – left of middle row\n\nPad5: (2,3) – right of middle row\n\nPad6: (3,1) – bottom of left edge\n\nPad7: (3,2) – bottom of middle column\n\nWait, actually, in terms of the six non-center non-A/B pads:\n\nThey are:\n\nTop row (row 1): (1,2), (1,3)\n\nMiddle row (row 2): (2,1), (2,3) [excluding center]\n\nBottom row (row 3): (3,1), (3,2)\n\nSo arranged like a square frame around the center.\n\nTo block all paths from A to B without using the center, we need to block all possible routes around the frame.\n\nLet me consider the possible paths that don't go through the center.\n\nThere are two main \"sides\":\n\n1. The top and right side: going along the top row to (1,3), then down to (2,3), then right to (3,3). Wait, but (3,3) is B. Wait, actually, from (2,3) you can go down to (3,3). So that path is (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3). This path uses Pad1, Pad2, Pad5.\n\n2. The left and bottom side: going down the left edge to (3,1), then right to (3,2), then up to (3,3). Wait, but from (3,1) you can go right to (3,2), then up? No, (3,2) is in the bottom row. From (3,2), you can go to (3,3) directly. So the path is (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3). This path uses Pad3, Pad6, Pad7.\n\n3. Other paths that go around the outside: for example, going all the way around the perimeter: (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3), which is the first path.\n\nOr (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3), the second path.\n\nOr maybe a path that goes (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2) but we can't go through center, so that's blocked.\n\nAlternatively, a path that goes (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) is blocked because center is not removed, but we are in Case 2 where center is not removed. Wait, in this case, the center is still present, so the frog can go through the center if it's not removed. Wait a minute! Wait a second.\n\nHold on. In Case 2, we are NOT removing the center pad (Pad4). Therefore, the center pad is still present. Therefore, the frog can potentially use the center pad in its path.\n\nOh! This is a crucial point I missed earlier.\n\nIn Case 2, we are removing four pads from the six non-center non-A/B pads. The center pad (2,2) remains. Therefore, the frog can still traverse through the center pad if it's part of a path. Therefore, my earlier analysis for Case 1 was correct because in Case 1 we removed the center, but in Case 2, the center is still there, so paths can still go through it.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, we have to block all paths that go through the center as well as those that go around it.\n\nTherefore, this complicates things because now there are more paths to block.\n\nSo in Case 2, the frog can take paths that go through the center, so we have to block those too.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths in Case 2, we need to ensure that:\n\n1. All paths going through the center are blocked: i.e., any path that goes through (2,2) must have at least one of its pads removed.\n\n2. All paths that go around the center (the two main perimeter paths) are also blocked.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, we must block:\n\na) The two perimeter paths (top-right and bottom-left),\n\nAND\n\nb) All paths that go through the center.\n\nTo block paths through the center, we need to ensure that every path from A to B that goes through (2,2) has at least one of its pads removed.\n\nLet me analyze paths through the center.\n\nA path going through the center (2,2) can take various routes, but generally, it would go from A to some pad near the center, then to (2,2), then to some pad near B.\n\nFor example:\n\nFrom A=(1,1), go to (1,2) or (2,1), then to (2,2), then to (2,3) or (3,2), then to B=(3,3).\n\nSo the paths through the center can be categorized as:\n\n- From A to (1,2) to (2,2) to (2,3) to B.\n\n- From A to (2,1) to (2,2) to (3,2) to B.\n\n- From A to (1,2) to (2,2) to (3,2) to B.\n\n- From A to (2,1) to (2,2) to (2,3) to B.\n\n- Etc. essentially, any path that passes through (2,2) must have come from either the left or top side and exited to the right or bottom side.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, we need to ensure that for every possible entrance to (2,2) from A's side and every possible exit from (2,2) to B's side, at least one pad on that segment is removed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the critical pads near the center are the ones adjacent to it: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Because those are the pads adjacent to the center that could lead into or out of it.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, we need to ensure that at least one of the four \"arms\" leading into or out of the center is blocked.\n\nSpecifically, the four pads adjacent to the center are:\n\nNorth: (1,2),\n\nSouth: (3,2),\n\nWest: (2,1),\n\nEast: (2,3).\n\nSo, to block all paths through the center, we must ensure that for any path going through (2,2), at least one of the incoming or outgoing edges is blocked by a removed pad.\n\nThat is, for any path that goes from A to (2,2), then to B, the segment from A to (2,2) must have a removed pad, or the segment from (2,2) to B must have a removed pad.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, we need that:\n\nEither:\n\n- All paths from A to (2,2) are blocked, OR\n\n- All paths from (2,2) to B are blocked.\n\nAlternatively, more precisely, for every possible path from A to B passing through (2,2), at least one pad on that path is removed.\n\nThis might be complex, but perhaps we can think of it as needing to block access to the center from A and/or block exit from the center to B.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's simpler to require that either:\n\n1. All paths from A to the center are blocked, or\n\n2. All paths from the center to B are blocked.\n\nBecause if both are blocked, then all paths through the center are blocked. Alternatively, if either one is blocked, then any path through the center is blocked.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, we need to ensure that:\n\nEither:\n\na) Every path from A to (2,2) is blocked, OR\n\nb) Every path from (2,2) to B is blocked.\n\nTherefore, let's compute the number of ways to block all paths through the center by blocking all paths from A to center or all paths from center to B.\n\nFirst, compute the number of subsets of four pads (from the six non-center non-A/B pads) that block all paths from A to center.\n\nPaths from A=(1,1) to center=(2,2):\n\nThe possible paths are:\n\n1. (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (2,2)\n\n2. (1,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2)\n\nSo two distinct shortest paths. Longer paths might exist, but since we're only concerned with blocking all paths, we need to block all possible paths, so we must block all possible routes into the center.\n\nTo block all paths from A to center, we must block both incoming paths:\n\n- Block the path (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2): this requires removing either (1,2) or (2,2). But (2,2) is not removed in this case (Case 2), so we must remove (1,2).\n\n- Block the path (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2): this requires removing either (2,1) or (2,2). Again, (2,2) is not removed, so must remove (2,1).\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from A to center, we need to remove both (1,2) and (2,1).\n\nSimilarly, to block all paths from center to B:\n\nPaths from center=(2,2) to B=(3,3):\n\nPossible paths:\n\n1. (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (3,3)\n\n2. (2,2) -> (3,2) -> (3,3)\n\n3. (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (3,3) is same as first,\n\n4. (2,2) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) is second,\n\n5. Also, (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (3,3) is one path,\n\n6. (2,2) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) is another,\n\nand longer paths like (2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3) is direct, but also (2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So two distinct shortest paths.\n\nTo block all paths from center to B, we must block both paths:\n\n- Block (2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3): requires removing (2,3) or (3,3). But (3,3) is B and cannot be removed, so remove (2,3).\n\n- Block (2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3): requires removing (3,2) or (3,3). Again, (3,3) is not removable, so remove (3,2).\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from center to B, must remove both (2,3) and (3,2).\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, we can do either:\n\nOption 1: Remove both (1,2) and (2,1) [blocking entry to center], and then we can choose any two more pads from the remaining four pads (since we've already used two of the six non-center non-A/B pads). Wait, but we are limited to removing four pads total.\n\nWait, let me formalize:\n\nTo block all paths through the center, we can choose to:\n\nSubcase 2a: Block all entry paths to center by removing (1,2) and (2,1), and then we need to also block the exit paths from center to B by removing (2,3) and (3,2). But that would require removing four pads: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). That's four pads. Alternatively, we could block entry and then just block one exit path, but then the other exit path would still be open.\n\nWait, actually, to block all paths through the center, we need to block both entry and exit. Because if we only block one entry, then the other entry path is still open, allowing the frog to enter the center, and if the exit paths are not blocked, the frog can exit to B.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, we must block all entry paths AND all exit paths.\n\nTherefore, the minimal set to block all paths through the center is the four pads: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Removing these four pads would block all paths through the center.\n\nAlternatively, we could remove more pads, but since we are limited to four, this is the minimal set for that strategy.\n\nAlternatively, there might be other combinations where we remove fewer than four pads to block all entry and exit paths, but given that each entry path requires one pad and each exit path requires one pad, and they are separate, you need to remove at least two entry pads and two exit pads, totaling four. So that is the minimal.\n\nTherefore, in Subcase 2a: Removing those four specific pads: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). This set of four pads, when removed, blocks all paths through the center, and also may block the perimeter paths.\n\nWait, but we also have to ensure that the perimeter paths are blocked as well. Because even if we block all paths through the center, the frog might still take the perimeter paths around the outside.\n\nAh, right! So even if we block all paths through the center, we still need to block the two perimeter paths that go around the outside.\n\nTherefore, in Subcase 2a, where we remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), we also need to check whether the perimeter paths are blocked.\n\nThe two perimeter paths are:\n\n1. The top path: (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3). But (1,2) and (2,3) are removed, so this path is blocked.\n\n2. The bottom path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3). Here, (2,1) and (3,2) are removed, so this path is blocked.\n\nTherefore, removing those four pads indeed blocks both perimeter paths and all center paths. So this is a valid subset.\n\nSimilarly, are there other subsets of four pads that can block all paths through the center and the perimeters?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can achieve blocking all paths through other combinations.\n\nFor example, suppose we remove three of the four critical pads plus another pad.\n\nSuppose we remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and one more pad, say (1,3). Let's see:\n\nRemoving (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (1,3).\n\nThen:\n\n- The top path: (1,1)->(1,2) [removed] -> blocked.\n\n- The bottom path: (1,1)->(2,1) [removed] -> blocked.\n\n- Paths through the center: as before, blocked because entry is blocked.\n\nAdditionally, any other paths?\n\nFor example, a path like (1,1)->(1,2) is blocked, but what about (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)? (2,1) is removed, so blocked. And (1,1)->(1,3) is possible? From (1,1) to (1,3) directly? No, because they are two apart; you have to go through (1,2), which is removed. So you can't go directly. So the only way to (1,3) is through (1,2), which is removed. So the frog can't reach (1,3) or beyond.\n\nTherefore, this subset also blocks all paths.\n\nWait a second, so actually, any subset that includes three of the four critical pads plus any other pad would still block all paths?\n\nWait, let me test with removing three of the four critical pads and one non-critical.\n\nSuppose we remove (1,2), (2,1), (3,2), and (1,3). That's the same as above.\n\nAlternatively, remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,1). Let's see:\n\n- Top path blocked by (1,2) and (2,3).\n\n- Bottom path: (1,1)->(2,1) [removed] -> blocked. From (3,1), can go to (3,2) [still present? Wait, (3,2) is not removed in this case. Wait, in this subset we removed (3,1)? No, in this example, we removed (3,1) as the fourth pad? Wait no:\n\nWait, in this case, the four pads removed are:\n\n(1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,1). So (3,2) is still present.\n\nThen, the bottom path: (1,1)->(2,1) [removed] -> blocked. From (3,1), can go to (3,2) [present], then to (3,3). So the path (1,1)->(2,1) is blocked, but the frog can go from (1,1) to (1,2) [removed], so no. Alternatively, from (1,1) can it go to (2,1) which is removed, so no. So the only way out is blocked. However, from (3,1), can the frog reach (3,2) and then to B? Yes, but how does the frog get to (3,1)?\n\nThe frog can go from (1,1) to (2,1) [removed], so no. Or to (1,2) [removed]. So the frog can't reach (3,1) either. Therefore, the path through the bottom is blocked because the frog can't get to (3,1) or (3,2) from A.\n\nWait, but (3,1) is removed? No, in this subset, we removed (3,1). So (3,1) is gone. Therefore, the frog cannot reach (3,1), so the path that would go through (3,1) is blocked. Therefore, this subset also works.\n\nWait, so actually, any subset that includes at least three of the four critical pads (the ones surrounding the center) will block all paths through the center, and combined with blocking the perimeter paths, which require removing at least one pad from each perimeter path.\n\nWait, this is getting a bit tangled. Maybe it's better to think that to block all paths in Case 2 (without removing the center), we must remove at least three of the four critical pads (the ones adjacent to the center: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2)), because if you remove three of them, then the fourth one might still allow a path through the center?\n\nWait, suppose we remove three of the four critical pads but leave one. For example, remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), leaving (3,2). Then:\n\n- The top path is blocked because (1,2) and (2,3) are removed.\n\n- The bottom path is blocked because (2,1) is removed.\n\n- Paths through the center: to enter the center, you can still go via (3,2)? Wait no. To enter the center from below, you can go from (3,2) to (2,2), but to get to (3,2), you need to come from (3,1) or (3,3), but (3,3) is the end. From (3,1), can you get there?\n\nThe frog can go from (1,1) to (2,1) [removed], so no. Or to (1,2) [removed]. So cannot reach (3,1) or (3,2) from A. Therefore, even if (3,2) is present, the frog can't reach it. Thus, the center's exit path via (3,2) is irrelevant because the frog can't get there.\n\nWait, actually, if the frog can't reach the critical pads adjacent to the center, then even if the center is present, the frog can't get to it. So maybe removing three of the four critical pads is sufficient?\n\nWait, let's think step by step with an example where we remove three critical pads:\n\nSuppose we remove (1,2), (2,1), and (2,3), leaving (3,2). Then, can the frog reach B?\n\nFrom A=(1,1), the frog can go to (2,1) [removed] or (1,2) [removed], so stuck. Therefore, cannot reach anywhere. So even with leaving (3,2) in place, the frog can't get there. So actually, removing three critical pads is sufficient to block all paths.\n\nWait a minute, so if we remove any three of the four critical pads, then the frog cannot reach the center or the other critical pad, so all paths are blocked. Additionally, we have to remove one more pad (since we're removing four in total), but that fourth pad can be any of the remaining non-critical pads.\n\nWait, but the critical pads are four: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). The other two non-critical non-center non-A/B pads are (1,3) and (3,1).\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, to block all paths, we can choose any three of the four critical pads and any one of the two non-critical pads. But wait, does the non-critical pad matter?\n\nWait, actually, if we remove three critical pads, then regardless of the fourth pad, the frog is already blocked, because the frog can't reach the remaining critical pad or the center. Let me verify.\n\nSuppose we remove three critical pads: say (1,2), (2,1), (2,3). Then the fourth pad removed can be, say, (1,3). Then, as above, the frog is blocked. If the fourth pad is (3,1), same thing.\n\nAlternatively, if we remove three critical pads and the fourth pad is one of the non-critical ones, the frog is still blocked because the critical pads around the center are mostly removed, preventing access to the center and the perimeters.\n\nTherefore, in this scenario, any subset of four pads that includes at least three of the four critical pads will block all paths.\n\nAdditionally, there might be other subsets that don't include three critical pads but still block all paths by some other means.\n\nFor instance, suppose we remove two critical pads and two non-critical pads in such a way that all paths are blocked.\n\nIs that possible?\n\nLet me try an example:\n\nRemove (1,2) [critical], (3,2) [critical], and the two non-critical pads (1,3) and (3,1).\n\nSo the four removed pads are: (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), (3,1).\n\nNow, check if this blocks all paths.\n\nFirst, paths through the center:\n\nTo enter the center from A, the frog can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,1). If (2,1) is not removed, then the frog can go to (2,1). From (2,1), can go to (2,2) [center] or (3,1) [removed]. So (3,1) is removed, so from (2,1) the frog can go to the center.\n\nFrom the center, can the frog exit to B?\n\nFrom center=(2,2), can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [both are critical pads? (2,3) is not removed, (3,2) is removed. So from center, can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed]. So can go to (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3), can go to (3,3). So the path would be: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3). Is this path blocked?\n\nLet's see:\n\n- (2,1) is not removed? Wait, in this subset, we removed (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), (3,1). We did not remove (2,1). So (2,1) is still there.\n\nSo path exists: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3). Therefore, this path is not blocked. So this subset does NOT block all paths.\n\nTherefore, this subset is invalid.\n\nAnother example: remove two critical pads and two non-critical, but different.\n\nSuppose remove (1,2), (3,2) [critical], and (2,3), (3,1) [non-critical? Wait, (2,3) is critical. Let's say remove (1,2), (3,2), (2,3), and (3,1). This is similar to before, but let's see:\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) or (1,2). (1,2) is removed, so to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (3,1) [removed] or (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go to (2,3) [removed] or (3,2) [removed]. So stuck. Therefore, path blocked.\n\nThe other path: trying to go the other way:\n\nFrom A=(1,1)->(1,2) is blocked. Can't go to (1,3) directly. From (1,1) to (1,3) requires going through (1,2), which is removed. So no. From (1,1) to (2,1) is the only option, which then leads to a dead end. So this subset might work? Wait, let's check again.\n\nWait, from (2,1), can the frog go to (2,2) and then where? From (2,2), can it go to (3,2) which is removed, or (2,3) which is removed, so no. So yes, that path is blocked.\n\nWhat about another path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2) [removed], so no. So no path to B. The other potential path through the top: (1,1)->(1,2) is blocked. So this subset might work?\n\nWait, but in this subset we removed (1,2), (3,2), (2,3), and (3,1). The remaining pads include (2,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,3). Can the frog reach B via (1,3)?\n\nFrom (1,1) can't reach (1,3) because (1,2) is removed. From (2,1), can't reach (1,3). From (2,2), can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,3) [removed] or (3,2) [removed]. So no. So indeed, this subset blocks all paths.\n\nWait, so this subset with two critical pads removed? Wait no, in this case, we removed three critical pads: (1,2), (2,3), (3,2), and one non-critical (3,1). So three critical pads and one non-critical. So it is included in the previous category of three critical pads plus one non-critical.\n\nAh, so actually, in order to block all paths in Case 2, we need to remove at least three of the four critical pads. Because if you remove only two critical pads, there might still be a path through the remaining critical pad and the center.\n\nFor example, suppose we remove only two critical pads: say (1,2) and (3,2), and two non-critical pads: (1,3) and (3,1). Then, as in the earlier example, the frog can still go through (2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3) if (2,1) and (2,3) are not removed. Since in this case, we only removed (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), (3,1), so (2,1) and (2,3) are still present. Then the path (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3) is still open. Therefore, this subset doesn't block all paths.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths in Case 2, we must remove at least three of the four critical pads. The fourth pad can be any of the remaining two non-critical pads (since there are only two non-critical non-center non-A/B pads: (1,3) and (3,1)) or even another critical pad, but since we're already removing three critical pads, the fourth can be any of the remaining four pads (wait, total non-center non-A/B pads are six: four critical and two non-critical). So if we remove three critical pads, the fourth can be either one of the remaining critical pad or one of the two non-critical pads.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets in Case 2 that block all paths is equal to:\n\nNumber of ways to choose three critical pads out of four, multiplied by the number of ways to choose the fourth pad from the remaining three pads (since after choosing three critical pads, there are 4 - 3 = 1 critical pad left and 2 non-critical pads, totaling 3 remaining pads). Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, the four critical pads are: let's call them C1=(1,2), C2=(2,1), C3=(2,3), C4=(3,2).\n\nThe two non-critical non-A/B non-center pads are: NC1=(1,3), NC2=(3,1).\n\nTherefore, total non-center non-A/B pads: 4 critical + 2 non-critical = 6.\n\nIf we choose three critical pads out of the four, that can be done in C(4,3)=4 ways.\n\nFor each such choice of three critical pads, we need to choose one more pad from the remaining three pads: which are:\n\n- The one remaining critical pad not chosen, and the two non-critical pads.\n\nTherefore, for each of the 4 choices of three critical pads, there are 3 choices for the fourth pad (the remaining critical pad or either of the two non-critical pads). Thus, total subsets in this category: 4 * 3 = 12.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that these subsets indeed block all paths. As we saw in the earlier example, removing three critical pads plus any fourth pad does block all paths, because the frog cannot reach the remaining critical pad or the center. Let me confirm with another example where the fourth pad is the remaining critical pad.\n\nSuppose we remove three critical pads: C1, C2, C3 (i.e., (1,2), (2,1), (2,3)), and the fourth pad is the remaining critical pad C4=(3,2). So the four removed pads are: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2).\n\nAs analyzed earlier, this blocks all paths through the center and the perimeters. Similarly, if the fourth pad is one of the non-critical pads, say NC1=(1,3), then the frog can't reach (1,3) because (1,2) is removed, so that's also blocked.\n\nTherefore, all 12 subsets in this category do block all paths.\n\nAdditionally, are there other subsets in Case 2 that block all paths without removing three critical pads?\n\nSuppose we remove two critical pads and two non-critical pads in such a way that all paths are blocked. Is that possible?\n\nLet me try an example:\n\nRemove critical pads C1=(1,2) and C4=(3,2), and non-critical pads NC1=(1,3) and NC2=(3,1).\n\nSo removed pads: (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), (3,1).\n\nNow, can the frog find a path to B?\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) or (1,2) [removed]. So to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (3,1) [removed] or (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed].\n\nFrom (2,3), can go to (3,3).\n\nSo the path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3). Is this path blocked?\n\nCheck each pad:\n\n- (2,1) is present? Yes, not removed.\n\n- (2,2) is present? Yes.\n\n- (2,3) is present? Yes.\n\n- (3,2) is removed, but that's not on this path.\n\nTherefore, this path exists: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3). So the frog can reach B. Therefore, this subset does NOT block all paths.\n\nAnother try: remove critical pads C2=(2,1) and C3=(2,3), and non-critical pads NC1=(1,3) and NC2=(3,1).\n\nRemoved pads: (2,1), (2,3), (1,3), (3,1).\n\nCheck paths:\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (1,2) or (2,1) [removed]. So to (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2), can go to (1,3) [removed] or (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go to (2,3) [removed] or (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2), can go to (3,3).\n\nSo path: (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). This path exists because:\n\n- (1,2) is present,\n\n- (2,2) is present,\n\n- (3,2) is present (not removed in this subset),\n\n- (3,3) is B.\n\nTherefore, this path is open. So subset does not block all paths.\n\nAnother attempt: remove critical pads C1=(1,2) and C3=(2,3), and non-critical pads NC1=(1,3) and NC2=(3,1).\n\nRemoved pads: (1,2), (2,3), (1,3), (3,1).\n\nPath check:\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nGo to (2,1) or (1,2) [removed]. So to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (3,1) [removed] or (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go to (2,3) [removed] or (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2), can go to (3,3).\n\nThus path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). This path is open because:\n\n- (2,1) is present,\n\n- (2,2) is present,\n\n- (3,2) is present,\n\n- (3,3) is B.\n\nSo path exists. Hence, this subset doesn't block all paths.\n\nHmm, seems challenging to block all paths with only two critical pads removed. Let's try removing one critical pad and three non-critical pads.\n\nFor example, remove critical pad C1=(1,2), and non-critical pads NC1, NC2, and one more. Wait, but non-critical pads are only two: (1,3) and (3,1). So to remove three non-critical pads, we'd have to remove both non-critical pads and one more, but there are only two non-critical pads. So maximum two non-critical pads can be removed. So to remove three non-critical pads is impossible. Therefore, removing one critical pad and three non-critical pads is impossible.\n\nAlternatively, remove one critical pad and three non-critical pads: but since there are only two non-critical pads, we can only remove two. So maximum two non-critical.\n\nTherefore, removing one critical pad and two non-critical pads plus another critical pad? Wait, this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's impossible to block all paths in Case 2 without removing at least three critical pads. Since in all the attempted subsets with only two critical pads removed, there remained a path through the remaining critical pad and the center.\n\nTherefore, it seems that in Case 2, the only subsets that block all paths are those that include at least three of the four critical pads. Therefore, the number of such subsets is 12, as calculated earlier.\n\nTherefore, total for Case 2: 12 subsets.\n\nTherefore, total number of subsets that block all paths are Case 1 (18) + Case 2 (12) = 30.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Wait a second. Let me confirm the counts again.\n\nCase 1: Removing the center pad (Pad4) and three others from the remaining six pads (which include the four critical and two non-critical). Wait, no: in Case 1, when we remove the center pad (Pad4), we are left with six non-center non-A/B pads. Among these six, the critical pads are four: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). The other two are non-critical: (1,3), (3,1).\n\nIn Case 1, we need to choose three more pads from these six, with the condition that at least one is from the critical pads and at least one from the non-critical? Wait no, in Case 1, we already considered that when we removed the center, we needed to block the top and bottom paths by selecting at least one from T and one from B, where T and B were defined as the top and bottom path pads.\n\nWait, in my earlier analysis for Case 1, I considered that after removing the center, to block all paths, we needed to block both the top path (T) and the bottom path (B), which required selecting at least one from T and at least one from B in the three additional pads. The count was 18.\n\nHowever, in the current analysis for Case 2, I considered that removing three critical pads (which include both T and B pads?) Wait, actually, in the critical pads, T and B were defined as:\n\nT = { (1,2), (1,3), (2,3) }, but actually in the critical pads around the center, it's different.\n\nWait, there might be confusion in terminology. Let me recap:\n\nIn Case 1, when we removed the center pad, we defined T as the top path pads (excluding the center) and B as the bottom path pads (excluding the center). So T had three pads: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3), and B had three pads: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). Then, to block those paths, we needed at least one from T and one from B in the three additional pads. The count was 18.\n\nIn Case 2, when not removing the center, we considered the four critical pads surrounding the center: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). These correspond to the intersection points near the center. The non-critical non-A/B non-center pads are (1,3) and (3,1).\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, when we removed the center, we had to block the top and bottom paths (which included some of the critical pads and some non-critical pads). Whereas in Case 2, when not removing the center, we have to block access to the center by removing three of the four critical pads.\n\nNow, adding the two cases:\n\nCase 1: 18 subsets.\n\nCase 2: 12 subsets.\n\nTotal blocking subsets: 18 + 12 = 30.\n\nBut wait, the total number of subsets we're considering is C(7,4)=35. So 35 total, minus 30 blocking subsets would give 5 subsets that do NOT block all paths. But the question asks for the number of ways to remove 4 pads so that the frog cannot get home, i.e., the blocking subsets, which would be 30. But let me verify this because there might be an overlap between Case 1 and Case 2.\n\nWait, in Case 1, we removed the center pad (Pad4) plus three others. In Case 2, we did not remove the center pad, so there is no overlap between Case 1 and Case 2. Therefore, total blocking subsets are indeed 18 + 12 = 30.\n\nTherefore, the answer would be 30? But wait, let me confirm with an example.\n\nWait, but let's make sure that in Case 2, the 12 subsets are indeed all the subsets that don't include the center and block all paths, and in Case 1, the 18 subsets are those that do include the center and block all paths. Since these are disjoint, yes.\n\nBut wait, let me verify with a small example.\n\nSuppose in Case 2, we have a subset that removes three critical pads and one non-critical pad. For example, remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (1,3). This is one of the 12 subsets. As we saw earlier, this blocks all paths.\n\nAnother subset in Case 2: remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,1). Also blocks all paths.\n\nAnother: remove (1,2), (2,1), (3,2), and (1,3). This also blocks all paths.\n\nSimilarly, removing (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), and (1,3): blocks all paths.\n\nSimilarly for the other combinations. So 4 choices for the three critical pads, each with 3 choices for the fourth pad, so 12 total.\n\nCase 1: removing the center pad plus three others with at least one from T and one from B.\n\nRecall in Case 1, T was the top path pads (excluding center): (1,2), (1,3), (2,3).\n\nB was the bottom path pads (excluding center): (2,1), (3,1), (3,2).\n\nWhen we removed the center, to block the top path (which requires blocking at least one of (1,2), (1,3), (2,3)), and the bottom path (blocking at least one of (2,1), (3,1), (3,2)), we needed to choose three pads from the six non-center non-A/B pads, with at least one from T and at least one from B.\n\nThe calculation was C(6,3) - C(3,3) - C(3,3) = 20 -1 -1 =18.\n\nYes, so 18 subsets in Case 1.\n\nThus, total blocking subsets: 18 + 12 = 30.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 30? But wait, the problem states \"Compute the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads so that the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain, but Kelvin cannot get home.\"\n\nWait, but let me confirm once more because I might have made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, in Case 1: removing the center pad (Pad4) and three others from the remaining six pads (which include the four critical pads and two non-critical pads). The requirement was that among those three others, at least one is from T and at least one from B, where T and B were the top and bottom path pads.\n\nWait, actually, in Case 1, the T and B definitions were:\n\nT = { (1,2), (1,3), (2,3) } (the top path pads)\n\nB = { (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) } (the bottom path pads)\n\nThese are exactly the four critical pads except for (2,3) and (3,2) are in both T and B? Wait:\n\nWait, T includes (2,3), which is part of the critical pads, and B includes (3,2), which is also part of the critical pads. So T and B together include all four critical pads except that (1,2) and (2,1) are in T and B respectively, while (2,3) is in T and (3,2) is in B. So T and B together cover all four critical pads plus the non-critical pads (1,3) and (3,1) are in T and B respectively.\n\nWait, actually, (1,3) is in T, and (3,1) is in B.\n\nTherefore, when we removed the center and three others from the remaining six pads (which are T ∪ B), requiring that in those three, at least one is from T and at least one from B, that ensures that both the top and bottom paths are blocked.\n\nTherefore, the count of 18 is correct for Case 1.\n\nCase 2: removing four pads not including the center, and requiring at least three of the four critical pads, giving 12 subsets.\n\nTotal: 18 + 12 = 30.\n\nHowever, the total number of ways to remove 4 pads from the 7 non-A/B pads is C(7,4)=35. So 35 total, minus the 30 that block all paths, would give 5 subsets that do NOT block all paths, meaning the frog can still reach home. But the question asks for the number that DO block all paths, so 30.\n\nBut wait, before finalizing, let me see if my Case 2 count of 12 is correct.\n\nCase 2: non-center subsets that block all paths must include at least three of the four critical pads. The four critical pads are: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). The two non-critical are (1,3), (3,1).\n\nNumber of ways to choose three critical pads: C(4,3)=4.\n\nFor each such choice, you can choose the fourth pad from the remaining three pads: the one remaining critical pad or the two non-critical pads. So 3 choices.\n\nHence 4×3=12. That seems correct.\n\nCase 1: 18.\n\nTotal 30. So the answer is 30? Hmm.\n\nWait, but let me think of a specific example in Case 2 that should be counted but perhaps isn't.\n\nSuppose we remove three critical pads: (1,2), (2,1), (3,2), and the fourth pad is (1,3). This should be counted in Case 2.\n\nDoes this block all paths?\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [removed] or (1,2) [removed]. So stuck. Therefore, cannot reach B. So yes, blocked.\n\nAnother example: remove (2,3), (3,2), (1,2), and (3,1). This should block all paths.\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) or (1,2) [removed]. So to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (3,1) [removed] or (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go to (2,3) [removed] or (3,2) [removed]. So stuck. Thus, blocked.\n\nYes, works.\n\nAnother example: remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,1). This should block all paths.\n\nFrom A=(1,1):\n\nGo to (2,1) [removed] or (1,2) [removed]. Stuck. So blocked.\n\nYes.\n\nTherefore, the count seems correct.\n\nThus, I think the answer is 30.\n\nWait, but hold on a second. Let me verify with another approach.\n\nAlternative approach: total subsets: C(7,4)=35.\n\nSubtract the subsets that do NOT block all paths, i.e., subsets where there exists at least one path from A to B.\n\nWe need to compute the number of subsets that do not block all paths, then subtract from 35.\n\nTo compute the number of subsets that do not block all paths, we can think of it as the subsets where at least one path remains open.\n\nBut this might be complex due to overlapping paths, but maybe we can use inclusion-exclusion.\n\nAlternatively, note that in my previous analysis, I found 30 subsets that do block all paths, so 35-30=5 subsets that do not block all paths. Let's see if that makes sense.\n\nWhat are the 5 subsets that allow the frog to reach home?\n\nThese would be the subsets that don't remove enough critical pads.\n\nIn Case 2, the only subsets that don't block all paths are those that remove fewer than three critical pads. Since in Case 2, removing two critical pads allows a path, and removing one or zero also allows paths.\n\nSimilarly, in Case 1, are there subsets that don't block all paths?\n\nIn Case 1, when we removed the center pad and three others, we required that among those three, there was at least one from T and one from B to block the top and bottom paths. If a subset in Case 1 fails to have at least one from T or at least one from B, then it might allow a path through the remaining T or B.\n\nWait, in Case 1, the requirement was to have at least one from T and at least one from B in the three non-center pads. If a subset in Case 1 has, say, all three from T, then it would not block the bottom path, allowing the frog to go through the bottom path.\n\nSimilarly, all three from B would not block the top path.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, the subsets that don't block all paths are those that are all-T or all-B in their three chosen pads.\n\nNumber of all-T subsets in Case 1: choosing all three from T. Since T has three pads: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So C(3,3)=1 subset.\n\nSimilarly, all-B subsets: choosing all three from B. B has three pads: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). So C(3,3)=1 subset.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, there are 2 subsets that do NOT block all paths.\n\nThus, in Case 1: total subsets: C(6,3)=20. Wait, no: in Case 1, we are removing the center and three others from the six non-center non-A/B pads. The total number of subsets in Case 1 is C(6,3)=20. Out of these 20, 18 block all paths, and 2 don't (the all-T and all-B subsets).\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, there are 2 subsets that don't block all paths.\n\nIn Case 2, all 12 subsets block all paths, as we reasoned, so the non-blocking subsets are only the 2 from Case 1.\n\nWait, but hold on: in Case 2, are there any subsets that don't block all paths?\n\nEarlier, we considered that in Case 2, any subset with fewer than three critical pads allows a path. But how many subsets in Case 2 have fewer than three critical pads?\n\nTotal subsets in Case 2: C(6,4)=15. Wait a second, hold on: Case 2 is the number of subsets that do NOT include the center pad. So total non-center subsets of size 4: C(6,4)=15. Earlier, I considered that among these 15, 12 block all paths, leaving 3 that don't.\n\nWait, now I'm confused because my previous analysis said Case 2 had 12 blocking subsets, but if total Case 2 subsets are 15, then non-blocking would be 3.\n\nWhere did I go wrong?\n\nAh! Here's the mistake:\n\nEarlier, in Case 2, I considered that to block all paths, you need to remove at least three critical pads. The total number of subsets in Case 2 (removing four pads without the center) is C(6,4)=15.\n\nThe number of subsets in Case 2 that include at least three critical pads:\n\nNumber of subsets with exactly three critical pads: C(4,3)*C(2,1)=4*2=8.\n\nNumber of subsets with exactly four critical pads: C(4,4)*C(2,0)=1*1=1.\n\nThus, total subsets in Case 2 with at least three critical pads: 8+1=9.\n\nTherefore, blocking subsets in Case 2: 9.\n\nThe remaining subsets in Case 2 (15 - 9 = 6) have fewer than three critical pads (i.e., 0, 1, or 2 critical pads), and thus do not block all paths.\n\nBut earlier, I thought it was 12, but that was incorrect because I miscalculated the combinations.\n\nWait, let's recast:\n\nCase 2: removing four pads from the six non-center non-A/B pads (which include four critical and two non-critical).\n\nTo block all paths, need to remove at least three critical pads.\n\nNumber of subsets with exactly three critical pads:\n\nChoose 3 critical out of 4: C(4,3)=4.\n\nChoose 1 non-critical out of 2: C(2,1)=2.\n\nTotal: 4×2=8.\n\nNumber of subsets with exactly four critical pads:\n\nC(4,4)=1.\n\nTotal blocking subsets in Case 2: 8+1=9.\n\nTherefore, total blocking subsets:\n\nCase 1: 18 (which included the center pad)\n\nCase 2: 9 (without the center pad)\n\nTotal: 18+9=27.\n\nThen, the non-blocking subsets are total subsets (35) minus blocking subsets (27) = 8.\n\nBut this contradicts previous counts. Clearly, I messed up somewhere.\n\nLet me start over with Case 2:\n\nCase 2: subsets that do NOT include the center pad (Pad4). We need to count how many of these 15 subsets (C(6,4)=15) block all paths.\n\nTo block all paths in Case 2, the subset must:\n\n1. Block all paths through the center: which requires that every path from A to center or from center to B is blocked. As before, this requires blocking all entry paths to center or all exit paths from center.\n\nBut actually, more precisely, to block all paths through the center, the subset must block all entry paths to the center and all exit paths from the center.\n\nThe entry paths to the center are from A to center: which can go through (1,2) or (2,1). To block both entry paths, must remove both (1,2) and (2,1).\n\nThe exit paths from the center to B are through (2,3) or (3,2). To block both exit paths, must remove both (2,3) and (3,2).\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, you need to remove both (1,2) and (2,1) AND both (2,3) and (3,2). That is four pads: the four critical pads surrounding the center.\n\nAlternatively, if you remove all entry paths or all exit paths, but not both, then paths through the center can still use the remaining paths.\n\nWait, if you remove all entry paths (both (1,2) and (2,1)), then even if exit paths are open, the frog can't get to the center to exit. Similarly, if you remove all exit paths, the frog can't get out even if they get in.\n\nTherefore, to block all center-related paths, you must remove either:\n\n- All entry paths AND all exit paths, OR\n\n- Block all entry paths OR block all exit paths.\n\nWait, actually, to block all paths through the center, it's sufficient to block all entry paths OR block all exit paths.\n\nBecause if you block all entry paths, then no path can get to the center, so no path through the center exists. Similarly, if you block all exit paths, then any path that reaches the center can't proceed to B.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through the center, you need either:\n\nA) Remove both (1,2) and (2,1) [blocking entry to center], OR\n\nB) Remove both (2,3) and (3,2) [blocking exit from center].\n\nAdditionally, you also need to block the perimeter paths around the outside, which are the two main paths that don't go through the center: the top path and the bottom path.\n\nThe top path is (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3)->(2,3)->(3,3). To block this, need to remove at least one of (1,2), (1,3), or (2,3).\n\nThe bottom path is (1,1)->(2,1)->(3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3). To block this, need to remove at least one of (2,1), (3,1), or (3,2).\n\nTherefore, in total, to block all paths, we must satisfy:\n\nEither:\n\n1. Block all entry paths to center AND block all perimeter paths, OR\n\n2. Block all exit paths from center AND block all perimeter paths, OR\n\n3. Block entry paths AND exit paths (which is the four critical pads), AND block perimeter paths? Wait, this is getting too convoluted.\n\nPerhaps it's better to think that to block all paths, the subset must block:\n\n- All perimeter paths (top and bottom), and\n\n- All center-related paths (either block entry or exit).\n\nBut this is complex.\n\nAlternatively, since in Case 2 we are not removing the center, the frog can potentially use the center. Therefore, to block all paths, we must ensure that:\n\n- The two perimeter paths are blocked, AND\n\n- All paths through the center are blocked (i.e., either block entry or exit).\n\nTherefore, to compute the number of subsets in Case 2 that block all paths, we need:\n\nNumber of subsets that block both perimeter paths AND (block entry paths OR block exit paths).\n\nThis is getting very involved. Given the time I've spent and the potential for error, perhaps my initial approach was better, but I messed up in Case 2's count.\n\nAlternatively, let me try a different approach: instead of splitting into cases, let's enumerate all possibilities.\n\nThe seven non-A/B pads are:\n\nPad1: (1,2) [T entry]\n\nPad2: (1,3) [top right]\n\nPad3: (2,1) [bottom entry]\n\nPad4: (2,3) [right exit]\n\nPad5: (3,1) [bottom exit]\n\nPad6: (3,2) [middle bottom]\n\nPad7: (2,2) [center]\n\nWait, no, center is Pad4 in the original labeling? Wait, no, in the initial labeling, Pad4 was (2,2), the center. But in the non-A/B