e subset must satisfy:\n\n- Block the top path: remove at least one of {P1, P2, P4}.\n\n- Block the bottom path: remove at least one of {P3, P5, P6}.\n\nAND\n\n- Block all paths through the center: which requires that either:\n\n    i) Remove both P1 and P3 (blocking entry), OR\n\n    ii) Remove both P4 and P6 (blocking exit).\n\nTherefore, the total number of subsets is the number of four-element subsets of {P1,P2,P3,P4,P5,P6} that satisfy the above three conditions.\n\nThis is quite involved, but let's try to compute it.\n\nFirst, let's compute the total number of four-element subsets: C(6,4)=15.\n\nNow, we need to find how many of these 15 subsets meet the three conditions.\n\nLet me denote the conditions as:\n\nC1: At least one of {P1, P2, P4} is removed.\n\nC2: At least one of {P3, P5, P6} is removed.\n\nC3: Either (both P1 and P3 are removed) OR (both P4 and P6 are removed).\n\nWe need subsets that satisfy C1, C2, and C3.\n\nThis is complex, but perhaps we can break it down by considering C3 first.\n\nCase A: Subsets where both P1 and P3 are removed.\n\nCase B: Subsets where both P4 and P6 are removed.\n\nCase C: Subsets where neither P1&P3 nor P4&P6 are both removed, but still satisfy C3? Wait no, C3 requires that either both P1&P3 or both P4&P6 are removed. So only Cases A and B apply.\n\nTherefore, total subsets satisfying C3 are those in Case A plus those in Case B.\n\nNow, within each case, we also need to satisfy C1 and C2.\n\nLet's compute Case A: subsets that include both P1 and P3, and are four-element subsets.\n\nIn Case A, we have P1 and P3 removed. We need to choose two more pads from the remaining four pads: {P2, P4, P5, P6}.\n\nTotal subsets in Case A: C(4,2)=6.\n\nNow, within these 6 subsets, we must also satisfy C1 and C2.\n\nC1: At least one of {P1, P2, P4} is removed. But P1 is already removed, so C1 is satisfied.\n\nC2: At least one of {P3, P5, P6} is removed. P3 is already removed, so C2 is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, all 6 subsets in Case A s