>(1,2)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me try {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}. Let's see.\n\nRemove (1,2), (3,2), (2,3).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) removed or left/right. From (2,2), going left to (2,1) or right to (2,3) removed. From (2,2), going up is blocked. From (2,1), going up to (3,1). From (3,1), can go right to (3,2) removed or up—can't. So stuck. From (2,2), can go to (2,1) or back. From (1,1) can't reach (3,3). So yes, this triple disconnects.\n\nSo that's another one: {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}.\n\nSimilarly, {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)} we had earlier.\n\nOkay, so compiling all the disconnecting triples I have found so far:\n\n1. {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)} — vertical middle column\n\n2. {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)} — horizontal middle row\n\n3. {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)} — top-right, center, bottom-left\n\n4. {(1,3), (2,2), (3,2)} — top-right, center, bottom-middle\n\n5. {(1,2), (3,1), (2,2)} — top-middle, bottom-left, center\n\n6. {(1,3), (2,1), (2,2)} — top-right, middle-left, center\n\n7. {(1,2), (3,2), (2,1)} — top-middle, bottom-middle, middle-left\n\n8. {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)} — top-middle, bottom-middle, middle-right\n\nWait, let's verify the seventh one: {(1,2), (3,2), (2,1)}.\n\nRemove (1,2), (3,2), (2,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) removed or right to (1,2) removed. So stuck. Yes, that disconnects.\n\nEighth one: {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}.\n\nAs above, yes, that disconnects.\n\nIs there a ninth?\n\nHow about {(1,3), (2,3), (3,1)}? Let's see.\n\nRemove (1,3), (2,3), (3,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) or up to (2,1). Let's go right: (1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) removed. Stuck. From (1,2), can go up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nHow about {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)}?\n\nRemove (1,2), (3,1), (2,3).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nHmm. Let's try {(1,3), (2,1), (3,3)}. Wait, can't remove (3,3). So invalid.\n\nHow about {(1,2), (2,3), (3,1)}?\n\nRemove (1,2), (2,3), (3,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nAnother candidate: {(1,3), (2,2), (3,3)}. But can't remove (3,3).\n\nHmm. How about {(1,2), (2,1), (3,3)}. Again, can't remove (3,3).\n\nAlright, maybe I have found 8 disconnecting triples so far. Let me list them again with numbering:\n\n1. {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)} — vertical middle column\n\n2. {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)} — horizontal middle row\n\n3. {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)} — top-right, center, bottom-left\n\n4. {(1,3), (2,2), (3,2)} — top-right, center, bottom-middle\n\n5. {(1,2), (3,1), (2,2)} — top-middle, bottom-left, center\n\n6. {(1,3), (2,1), (2,2)} — top-right, middle-left, center\n\n7. {(1,2), (3,2), (2,1)} — top-middle, bottom-middle, middle-left\n\n8. {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)} — top-middle, bottom-middle, middle-right\n\nAre there any more?\n\nLet me try {(1,3), (2,1), (3,2)} — previously checked and didn't disconnect.\n\nHow about {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)} — checked, didn't disconnect.\n\nAnother possibility: {(1,3), (2,3), (3,1)} — checked, didn't disconnect.\n\nHmm. Let's see {(2,1), (3,2), (2,3)}. Let's see.\n\nRemove (2,1), (3,2), (2,3).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) or up to (2,1) removed. So only right to (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2), can go up to (2,2) or left/right. From (1,2), going up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) removed or left/right. From (2,2), going left to (2,1) removed or right to (2,3) removed. So stuck at (2,2). From (1,2), can go to (1,3). From (1,3), can go up to (2,3) removed, so stuck. From (1,1) can't reach (3,3). So this triple disconnects.\n\nSo that's another one: {(2,1), (3,2), (2,3)}.\n\nLet me verify:\n\nRemoving (2,1), (3,2), (2,3):\n\nFrom (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (2,2) is stuck because from (2,2), can't go up (to (3,2) removed), left (to (2,1) removed), right (to (2,3) removed). So yes, stuck. From (1,2) can't reach (1,3) because (1,3) can go up to (2,3) removed. So yes, this disconnects. So that's a ninth disconnecting triple.\n\nSimilarly, {(1,3), (2,3), (3,1)}? Wait, already checked that.\n\nWait, let's try {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)}. Wait, did that earlier, didn't disconnect.\n\nHmm. Another possibility: {(1,2), (2,3), (3,1)}. Let's see.\n\nRemove (1,2), (2,3), (3,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nHmm. Let me try {(1,3), (2,1), (3,3)} — can't remove (3,3).\n\nAlright, so perhaps I have 9 disconnecting triples now. Let me list them again with numbering:\n\n1. {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)}\n\n2. {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)}\n\n3. {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)}\n\n4. {(1,3), (2,2), (3,2)}\n\n5. {(1,2), (3,1), (2,2)}\n\n6. {(1,3), (2,1), (2,2)}\n\n7. {(1,2), (3,2), (2,1)}\n\n8. {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}\n\n9. {(2,1), (3,2), (2,3)}\n\nHmm. Let me check if there are any others.\n\nHow about {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)}. Let me see again.\n\nRemove (1,2), (3,1), (2,3).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nHow about {(1,3), (2,3), (3,1)} — already checked.\n\nHmm. Let me see {(1,2), (2,3), (3,1)}.\n\nRemove (1,2), (2,3), (3,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that works. Doesn't disconnect.\n\nAlright, maybe that's all. So I have 9 disconnecting triples.\n\nWait, let me check if the triple {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)} is a disconnecting triple? Earlier I thought it wasn't, but let me check again.\n\nWait, no, I think I did that one already. Let me confirm:\n\nTriple {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)}:\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) or left/right. From (2,2), going up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path exists. Therefore, not a disconnecting triple.\n\nAlright, so sticking with 9 disconnecting triples.\n\nNow, assuming these are all the minimal disconnecting sets (i.e., 3-vertex sets whose removal disconnects the graph), then the next step is to compute how many four-vertex subsets include at least one of these triples.\n\nTo do this, we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. For each of the 9 triples, the number of four-vertex subsets that include that triple is C(7 - 3, 1) = C(4,1) = 4, since we have to choose 1 more vertex from the remaining 7 - 3 = 4 non-corner vertices.\n\nHowever, this counts overlaps multiple times. For example, if a four-vertex subset includes two different triples, it would be counted twice. So we need to subtract the overlaps.\n\nFirst, let's compute the total overcount:\n\nTotal = 9 triples * 4 = 36.\n\nBut now we need to subtract the number of four-vertex subsets that include two triples, multiplied by the number of times they were overcounted.\n\nSo we need to compute for each pair of triples, how many four-vertex subsets include both triples. But this could be complex because some triples may share vertices, so their intersection may be more than one vertex, making it impossible for a four-vertex subset to include both.\n\nAlternatively, first, let's see if any of the triples overlap in such a way that their intersection is non-empty, meaning that a four-vertex subset could include both.\n\nLooking at the triples:\n\nLet me list the triples again with their vertices:\n\n1. T1: {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)}\n\n2. T2: {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)}\n\n3. T3: {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)}\n\n4. T4: {(1,3), (2,2), (3,2)}\n\n5. T5: {(1,2), (3,1), (2,2)}\n\n6. T6: {(1,3), (2,1), (2,2)}\n\n7. T7: {(1,2), (3,2), (2,1)}\n\n8. T8: {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}\n\n9. T9: {(2,1), (3,2), (2,3)}\n\nNow, let's see if any two triples share two or more vertices.\n\nFor example:\n\n- T1 and T4 both contain (2,2) and (3,2). Specifically, T1 has (1,2), (2,2), (3,2); T4 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,2). So they share (2,2) and (3,2). Therefore, the intersection of T1 and T4 is two vertices: {(2,2), (3,2)}.\n\nSimilarly, T1 and T8: T1 has (1,2), (2,2), (3,2); T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). They share (1,2) and (3,2).\n\nT1 and T7: T1 has (1,2), (2,2), (3,2); T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1). They share (1,2) and (3,2).\n\nT1 and T9: T1 has (1,2), (2,2), (3,2); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). They share (3,2) and (2,2) [Wait, no: T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). T1 has (2,2). So only (3,2) is shared. So intersection is just (3,2).]\n\nT2 and T6: T2 has (2,1), (2,2), (2,3); T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2). They share (2,1) and (2,2).\n\nT2 and T8: T2 has (2,1), (2,2), (2,3); T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). They share (2,3).\n\nT2 and T9: T2 has (2,1), (2,2), (2,3); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). They share (2,1) and (2,3).\n\nT3 and T5: T3 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,1); T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2). They share (2,2) and (3,1).\n\nT3 and T6: T3 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,1); T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2). They share (1,3) and (2,2).\n\nT3 and T9: T3 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,1); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). No common vertices except maybe (2,2) is in T3 but not in T9? Wait, T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). So no overlap with T3 except possibly (2,2) is not in T9. So no overlap.\n\nT4 and T5: T4 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,2); T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2). They share (2,2).\n\nT4 and T6: T4 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,2); T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2). They share (1,3) and (2,2).\n\nT4 and T7: T4 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,2); T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1). They share (3,2).\n\nT4 and T8: T4 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,2); T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). They share (1,3) and (3,2) only if (1,3) is in T8? No, T8 has (1,2), so only (3,2) is shared.\n\nT4 and T9: T4 has (1,3), (2,2), (3,2); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). They share (3,2).\n\nT5 and T6: T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2); T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2). They share (2,2).\n\nT5 and T7: T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2); T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1). They share (1,2) and (2,1)? Wait, T5 has (3,1) and T7 has (3,2), so they share (1,2) and (2,1) is in T7 but not in T5? Wait, T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2). T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1). So overlapping vertices are (1,2) and (2,1)? No, T5 doesn't have (2,1). T5 has (2,2), not (2,1). So overlapping vertices are only (1,2).\n\nT5 and T8: T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2); T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). They share (1,2).\n\nT5 and T9: T5 has (1,2), (3,1), (2,2); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). No common vertices except maybe (2,2) is in T5 but not in T9. So no.\n\nT6 and T7: T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2); T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1). They share (2,1).\n\nT6 and T8: T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2); T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). No common vertices except maybe (2,2) is in T6 but not in T8? T8 has (2,3). So no.\n\nT6 and T9: T6 has (1,3), (2,1), (2,2); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). They share (2,1).\n\nT7 and T8: T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1); T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). They share (1,2) and (3,2).\n\nT7 and T9: T7 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,1); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). They share (2,1) and (3,2).\n\nT8 and T9: T8 has (1,2), (3,2), (2,3); T9 has (2,1), (3,2), (2,3). They share (3,2) and (2,3).\n\nOkay, this is getting quite involved, but let's see if any two triples share two vertices. From above:\n\n- T1 & T4: share two vertices (2,2), (3,2)\n\n- T1 & T7: share two vertices (1,2), (3,2)\n\n- T1 & T8: share two vertices (1,2), (3,2)\n\n- T2 & T6: share two vertices (2,1), (2,2)\n\n- T2 & T9: share two vertices (2,1), (2,3)\n\n- T3 & T5: share two vertices (2,2), (3,1)\n\n- T3 & T6: share two vertices (1,3), (2,2)\n\n- T4 & T5: share one vertex (2,2)\n\n- T4 & T6: share two vertices (1,3), (2,2)\n\n- T4 & T7: share one vertex (3,2)\n\n- T4 & T8: share one vertex (3,2)\n\n- T5 & T6: share one vertex (2,2)\n\n- T5 & T7: share one vertex (1,2)\n\n- T5 & T8: share one vertex (1,2)\n\n- T6 & T7: share one vertex (2,1)\n\n- T6 & T9: share one vertex (2,1)\n\n- T7 & T8: share two vertices (1,2), (3,2)\n\n- T7 & T9: share two vertices (2,1), (3,2)\n\n- T8 & T9: share two vertices (3,2), (2,3)\n\nSo the pairs of triples that share two vertices are:\n\n1. T1 & T4\n\n2. T1 & T7\n\n3. T1 & T8\n\n4. T2 & T6\n\n5. T2 & T9\n\n6. T3 & T5\n\n7. T4 & T6\n\n8. T7 & T8\n\n9. T7 & T9\n\n10. T8 & T9\n\nAdditionally, T7 & T9 share two vertices, T8 & T9 share two vertices, etc.\n\nNow, for these overlapping pairs, when we consider a four-vertex subset that includes both triples, the intersection of the two triples must be a subset of the four vertices. Since each triple has three vertices, their intersection has two vertices, so the union of the two triples has 3 + 3 - 2 = 4 vertices. Therefore, the only four-vertex subsets that contain both triples are exactly the unions of these two triples. Because the union has exactly four vertices, so if you have a four-vertex subset containing both triples, it must be exactly the union of the two triples.\n\nTherefore, for each such pair of triples that share two vertices, there is exactly one four-vertex subset that is their union, which contains both triples. For pairs of triples that share only one vertex, their union would have 3 + 3 - 1 = 5 vertices, which is more than four, so no four-vertex subset can contain both.\n\nTherefore, only the pairs of triples that share two vertices contribute to overcounting in the inclusion-exclusion principle. Each such pair corresponds to exactly one four-vertex subset that includes both triples, and this subset has been counted twice in the initial count (once for each triple).\n\nThere are 10 such pairs (as listed above), each contributing one overcounted subset. Therefore, we need to subtract these 10 subsets once each to correct the overcount.\n\nHowever, wait, let me confirm with an example:\n\nTake the first overlapping pair: T1 and T4.\n\nT1: {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)}\n\nT4: {(1,3), (2,2), (3,2)}\n\nTheir union is {(1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (3,2)}, which is a four-vertex subset. This subset was counted once for T1 and once for T4, so total of two counts, but it should only be counted once in the total overcount. Therefore, we need to subtract 1 for each such pair.\n\nSimilarly for each of the 10 pairs, each such union is a four-vertex subset that was counted twice in the initial total of 36, so we need to subtract 1 for each of the 10 overlaps.\n\nTherefore, the inclusion-exclusion correction is -10.\n\nHowever, we also need to consider if there are any four-vertex subsets that include three or more of the triples. For example, is there a four-vertex subset that includes three of the triples? Given that each triple is three vertices, and the four-vertex subset can only contain four vertices, it's impossible for a four-vertex subset to include three triples, because each triple requires three vertices, and three triples would require at least 3*3 - overlaps vertices, but with overlaps, but even so, in four vertices, you can't have three triples unless they all share common vertices in a way that allows it, but given the triples we have, it's unlikely.\n\nLet me check an example: Take T1, T4, and T7. Their union would be:\n\nT1: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2)\n\nT4: (1,3), (2,2), (3,2)\n\nT7: (1,2), (3,2), (2,1)\n\nThe union would be (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2). That's five vertices, so can't be a four-vertex subset. Therefore, no four-vertex subset can include three triples.\n\nSimilarly, other combinations would require more than four vertices. Therefore, there are no four-vertex subsets that include three or more triples. Hence, the inclusion-exclusion stops at the second order.\n\nTherefore, applying inclusion-exclusion:\n\nTotal subsets that disconnect = (9 triples * 4) - (10 overlapping pairs * 1 overcount) = 36 - 10 = 26.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Wait, the initial total was 9 triples, each contributing 4 subsets, so 36. But these 36 subsets may include duplicates where a four-vertex subset is counted multiple times if it contains multiple triples. Each four-vertex subset that contains exactly two triples (and no more) is counted twice in the 36, so we need to subtract the number of such subsets once for each overcount. Since there are 10 such subsets (each corresponding to a pair of triples that share two vertices), each was counted twice, so we subtract 10 to get the correct count.\n\nTherefore, the total number of four-vertex subsets that disconnect the graph is 36 - 10 = 26.\n\nHowever, we must also consider that some four-vertex subsets might disconnect the graph in more than one way, but since we are just counting all four-vertex subsets that disconnect the graph, regardless of how many triples they contain, as long as they disconnect, we don't need to worry about over-subtracting further because inclusion-exclusion up to two overlaps should suffice.\n\nTherefore, according to this calculation, the number of four-vertex subsets that disconnect the graph is 26. Therefore, the number of four-vertex subsets that do NOT disconnect the graph (i.e., leave a path) is total subsets (35) minus 26 = 9.\n\nBut wait, hold on: total subsets are C(7,4)=35. If disconnecting subsets are 26, then non-disconnecting are 9. But that seems low. Let me verify with an example.\n\nWait, let me think of a four-vertex subset that doesn't disconnect. For example, removing four vertices that are all on one side, say, removing the four rightmost vertices: (1,3), (2,3), (3,3) is the home pad which can't be removed, so actually can't remove (3,3). Wait, non-corner vertices are the seven excluding (1,1) and (3,3). So the rightmost non-corner is (1,3), (2,3), and (3,2). Wait, (3,3) is the home pad.\n\nWait, actually, the non-corner vertices are:\n\nRow 1: (1,2), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,2)\n\nSo total seven.\n\nSuppose we remove four vertices that are all on the top row: but the top row non-corner vertices are only (3,1) and (3,2). So can't remove four from there.\n\nAlternatively, removing the four corners? But there are only two corners that can be removed: (1,1) and (3,3) can't be removed. The other corners are (1,3) and (3,1), which are non-corner. Wait, actually, in the problem statement, we are only removing from the non-corner pads, so the four to remove are among the seven non-corner pads.\n\nSuppose we remove four non-corner pads that are all on the top row? But the top row non-corner pads are only two: (3,1) and (3,2). So you can only remove two from the top row. Similarly, the bottom row non-corner pads are (1,2) and (1,3). The middle row has three.\n\nAlternatively, remove all three middle row non-corner pads plus one more. The middle row non-corner pads are (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). If we remove all three of them plus, say, (1,2), then the remaining non-removed non-corner pads are (1,3), (3,1), (3,2). Then the remaining graph has the two corners plus these three. Let's see if there's a path from (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) which was removed, so only right to (1,2) which was removed. So stuck. So actually, this subset would disconnect.\n\nHmm, so maybe my calculation of 26 disconnecting subsets is correct, but I need to see if my inclusion-exclusion was properly applied.\n\nWait, but let's see: if there are 35 total subsets, and according to this, 26 disconnect, 9 do not. But when I think of subsets that don't disconnect, they are those where there still exists a path from (1,1) to (3,3). Let me try to think of such a subset.\n\nFor example, suppose we remove four vertices that are all on one side, say, the right side. Let's try removing (1,3), (2,3), (3,2), and one more, say (2,1). Wait, but then let's see if there's still a path.\n\nWait, removing (1,3), (2,3), (3,2), and (2,1):\n\nRemaining non-corner pads: (1,2), (3,1), (2,2).\n\nSo the remaining graph has (1,1), (3,3), plus (1,2), (3,1), (2,2).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) or up to (2,1) which was removed. So only right to (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2), can go up to (2,2) or left/right. From (1,2), going up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) which was removed, left to (2,1) removed, or right to (2,3) removed. So stuck. From (1,2), can also go to (1,3) which was removed. So stuck. From (1,1) can't reach (3,3). So this subset actually disconnects.\n\nHmm, another example: remove four vertices that are all in the middle row. The middle row non-corner vertices are (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). If we remove all three of them plus one more, say (1,2):\n\nThen remaining non-corner pads: (1,3), (3,1), (3,2). The remaining graph includes (1,1), (3,3), plus these three.\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) removed or up to (2,1) removed. Stuck. So disconnects.\n\nHmm. How about removing four vertices that are not all in a central area?\n\nSuppose we remove (1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2). So the four removed are the top and bottom non-corner pads on the sides.\n\nRemaining non-corner pads: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3).\n\nSo the remaining graph has (1,1), (3,3), plus (2,1), (2,2), (2,3).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) or right to (1,2) removed. So only up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) removed or left/right. From (2,2), going up is blocked, so can go to (2,1) or (2,3). From (2,3), can go up to (3,3). So path: (1,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(3,3). So this path exists. Therefore, this subset does NOT disconnect the graph. So this is one of the 9 non-disconnecting subsets.\n\nAh, so this is a valid non-disconnecting subset. So such subsets do exist. Therefore, 9 seems plausible.\n\nLet me see how many such subsets there are.\n\nIn the above example, the subset removed is {(1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2)}. This leaves the middle row intact except for the corners, but actually, the middle row is all non-corner except the center? Wait, no, the middle row non-corner pads are (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). So when we removed (1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2), we left (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) in the middle row. So the middle row is still connected, allowing a path through the middle row.\n\nTherefore, this subset does not disconnect.\n\nSimilarly, another non-disconnecting subset: remove the four corners except the start and end? But the corners are (1,1) and (3,3), which can't be removed. The other corners are (1,3) and (3,1). So removing those two plus two others might leave a path.\n\nWait, if we remove (1,3), (3,1), and two others that don't block the center.\n\nFor example, remove (1,3), (3,1), (1,2), and (3,2). Then remaining non-corner pads are (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). As above, that leaves a path through the middle row.\n\nAlternatively, remove (1,3), (3,1), and two from the middle row, say (2,1) and (2,3). Then remaining non-corner pads: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) or up to (2,1) removed. So only right to (1,2). From (1,2), can go up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2). From (3,2), can go right to (3,3). So path exists: (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2)->(3,2)->(3,3). So that subset also doesn't disconnect.\n\nSo there are multiple non-disconnecting subsets.\n\nGiven that according to inclusion-exclusion we have 9 non-disconnecting subsets, and we found at least two examples, maybe it's correct.\n\nBut let me see if my inclusion-exclusion was right.\n\nWe had 9 triples, each contributing 4 subsets, so 36.\n\nThen, for overlapping pairs (sharing two vertices), there are 10 such pairs, each corresponding to one four-vertex subset that was counted twice. Therefore, we subtract 10 to get 26 disconnecting subsets.\n\nTherefore, the number of non-disconnecting subsets is 35 - 26 = 9.\n\nTherefore, the answer would be 9. But let me confirm with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of inclusion-exclusion, we can directly count the non-disconnecting subsets.\n\nA non-disconnecting subset is one where there exists a path from (1,1) to (3,3). To count these, perhaps we can consider that the remaining graph must contain a path from start to end.\n\nGiven that we are leaving 5 vertices: the two corners plus three others. The three others must form a graph that, together with the corners, allows a path from (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nAlternatively, the three remaining non-corner vertices must not block all possible paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the non-disconnecting subsets are those that do not include any of the disconnecting triples. Wait, but disconnecting subsets are those that do include at least one disconnecting triple. Therefore, non-disconnecting subsets are those that do not include any disconnecting triples. Wait, no: a subset might include parts of a disconnecting triple but not all three, so it might still disconnect or not.\n\nActually, no: a subset that does not include any entire disconnecting triple might still disconnect the graph if the removal of its four vertices blocks all paths even without 