ed vertex, but no three of them are a disconnecting set.\n\nIs this possible?\n\nLet me try an example.\n\nSuppose we remove (1,2), (2,3), (3,1), and (2,1).\n\nLet's see if this disconnects.\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) which is removed, or right to (1,2) removed. So stuck. So this subset disconnects.\n\nBut does this subset contain any disconnecting triple?\n\nThe removed vertices are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,1).\n\nLooking for a triple within these four that disconnects:\n\nTake any three:\n\n- (1,2), (2,1), (2,3): does this triple disconnect?\n\nYes, as we saw earlier (T7 or T something). Let's see:\n\nRemoving (1,2), (2,1), (2,3):\n\nFrom (1,1), can go up to (2,1) removed or right to (1,2) removed. So stuck. So yes, this triple disconnects. Therefore, this four-vertex subset includes a disconnecting triple, so it was already counted in the inclusion-exclusion.\n\nTherefore, perhaps all disconnecting subsets do contain at least one disconnecting triple, so the count via inclusion-exclusion is accurate.\n\nTherefore, if there are 9 non-disconnecting subsets, then the answer is 9.\n\nBut let's see if we can find all 9 non-disconnecting subsets.\n\nThe non-disconnecting subsets are those four-vertex removals where there remains a path from (1,1) to (3,3). To have such a path, the remaining three non-corner vertices must form a graph that, together with the corners, allows a path.\n\nOne obvious way is to leave the middle row intact. The middle row non-corner vertices are (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). If we remove four vertices that don't include any of these, but there are only four non-middle-row non-corner vertices: (1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2). So if we remove all four of these, then the remaining non-corner vertices are the entire middle row. This is the example I had earlier, which is one non-disconnecting subset.\n\nSimilarly, any subset that leaves at least one path through the middle row or another route.\n\nAlternatively, leaving the top row non-corner vertices (1