at do not include any of the 9 disconnecting triples. Since each disconnecting triple is a 3-vertex set, the number of 4-vertex subsets that include a particular triple is C(4,1)=4, as before. So total overcounts are 9*4=36.\n\nBut some subsets are counted multiple times. Each subset that includes two triples is counted twice, so we subtract those. As we found, there are 10 such subsets (each corresponding to a pair of triples sharing two vertices), each counted twice, so we subtract 10.\n\nThus total disconnecting subsets: 36 - 10 = 26.\n\nThus non-disconnecting: 35 - 26 = 9.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 9.\n\nBut let me see if that matches with my enumeration.\n\nEarlier, I found at least two non-disconnecting subsets:\n\n1. Removing the four \"side\" non-corner vertices: (1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2).\n\n2. Removing (1,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,3).\n\nIs there another one?\n\nLet's try removing (1,2), (3,1), (2,3), and (2,1). Wait, does this disconnect?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nRemoved vertices: (1,2), (3,1), (2,1), (2,3).\n\nRemaining non-corner: (1,3), (2,2), (3,2).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) removed or up to (2,1) removed. Stuck. So disconnects. So not non-disconnecting.\n\nAnother one: remove (1,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3).\n\nRemaining non-corner: (1,2), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nFrom (1,1) can go up to (2,1) removed or right to (1,2). From (1,2), can go up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) removed or left/right. From (2,2), going up is blocked, so can go to (2,1) removed or (2,3) removed. From (3,1), can go right to (3,2) removed. So path: (1,1)->(1,2)->(2,2)->(2,1) removed. No. Can't reach (3,3). So disconnects.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother non-disconnecting subset: remove (1,2), (3,2), (2,3), and (3,1).\n\nRemaining non-corner: (1,3), (2,1), (2,2).\n\nFrom (1,1) can go right to (1,2) removed or up to (2,1). From (2,1), can go up to (3,1) removed or right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go up to (3,2) removed or left/right. From (2,2), going up is blocked, so can go to (2