em, the vertex connectivity is at least 2. Since the minimum degree is 2, the vertex connectivity is exactly 2. Therefore, the minimal number of vertices needed to disconnect the graph is 2. So, in the original grid, you can disconnect start from home by removing just 2 vertices, provided they are chosen appropriately (i.e., placed on a cut vertex or on two different paths).\n\nTherefore, in our problem, since we are allowed to remove 4 vertices, which is more than the minimal 2, so there are many ways to disconnect the graph, but we need exactly to remove 4 vertices from the 7 non-start, non-home pads such that start and home are disconnected. So, perhaps the strategy is to consider all possible subsets of 4 vertices from the 7 non-start, non-home pads that form a disconnecting set (i.e., whose removal disconnects start from home), and count them.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's easier to compute the total number of subsets (35) minus the number of subsets that do NOT disconnect start from home, i.e., the number of subsets where start and home remain connected.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute the number of 4-element subsets of the 7 non-start, non-home pads that leave start and home in the same connected component, then subtract that from 35 to get the answer.\n\nHmm, so which is easier: counting directly the disconnecting sets of size 4, or counting the connected sets of size 4 (but actually, it's the subsets whose removal doesn't disconnect, which is equivalent to the remaining graph still having start and home connected). Hmm, perhaps the connectedness is a bit tricky.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's feasible to model this as a graph and perform some kind of recursive counting or use inclusion-exclusion, but that might be complicated.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small (3x3), perhaps we can enumerate the possibilities.\n\nFirst, let me label all the lily pads except the start (3,1) and home (1,3). The remaining lily pads are:\n\n(1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2), (2,