xist some path from (3,1) to (1,3) that doesn't go through any of the removed pads. Therefore, to have them disconnected, all possible paths must go through at least one removed pad.\n\nTherefore, the set of removed pads must form a vertex cut between start and home. So, in other words, the removed pads must include at least one vertex from every path from start to home. Since we are removing 4 pads, we need that every path from start to home passes through at least one of the 4 removed pads.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of 4-element subsets of the 7 non-start, non-home pads that form a vertex cover for all paths from start to home. Wait, actually, a vertex cover is a set of vertices that touches every edge, but here we need a set of vertices that intersects every path from start to home. That is called a vertex cut or a separating set.\n\nYes, exactly, a separating set or a disconnecting set. So, a disconnecting set S is a set of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph. So, in this case, we need disconnecting sets of size 4 from the 7 non-start, non-home vertices.\n\nAlternatively, since we are only allowed to remove 4 vertices, and we need to count all such disconnecting sets of size 4, given that we cannot remove the start or home.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the minimal disconnecting sets and then see how adding more vertices to them can give us disconnecting sets of size 4.\n\nSince the minimal vertex cut between start and home is 2 vertices (as per Menger's theorem, since there are two disjoint paths), so any disconnecting set must contain at least 2 vertices. Therefore, the disconnecting sets of size 4 can be formed by taking any disconnecting set of size 2 and adding 2 more vertices, or taking a disconnecting set of size 3 and adding 1 more, etc. But this might get complicated because there could be overlapping disconnecting sets.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can list all minimal disconnecting sets (i.e., all sets of size 2 whose removal disconnects the graph), and then for each such minimal set, count the number of ways to add 2 more vertices (from the remaining 5 non-minimal vertices) such that the resulting set of 4 still disconnects the graph. But this might overcount because some larger sets might disconnect the graph even without containing a minimal disconnecting set, but actually, since the minimal disconnecting sets are size 2, any disconnecting set must contain at least one minimal disconnecting set. Wait, actually, that might not necessarily be true. For example, there could be a disconnecting set of size 3 that doesn't contain any minimal disconnecting set of size 2. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to just try to enumerate all possible disconnecting sets of size 4. Given that the grid is small, maybe feasible.\n\nFirst, let me try to identify all minimal disconnecting sets (size 2) between start and home. Then, from there, see how adding more vertices can form disconnecting sets of size 4.\n\nFirst, let's find all minimal disconnecting sets (i.e., all pairs of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph). So, pairs of vertices S = {v, w} such that removing v and w disconnects the graph (i.e., start and home are in different components).\n\nTo find such pairs, let me consider the two disjoint paths between start and home that we identified earlier:\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nAdditionally, there are other paths that use different routes. For example:\n\nAnother path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nAnother path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nAnother path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) [same as path 2]\n\nWait, actually, path 2 is (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). Similarly, the path going through (2,2):\n\n(3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nOr (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nOr (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) [path 2]\n\nOr (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3) [path 1]\n\nSo, in fact, there are multiple paths. Let me see if I can characterize all minimal disconnecting pairs.\n\nA minimal disconnecting pair must intersect every path from start to home. Since there are two internally disjoint paths (paths that don't share any internal vertices), a minimal disconnecting set must contain at least one vertex from each of these two paths. Because if it didn't, then you could take a path that goes through the other path which isn't blocked.\n\nWait, in our case, since the two paths we mentioned earlier (Path 1 and Path 2) are vertex-disjoint except at the endpoints, then to block both paths, a minimal disconnecting set must contain at least one vertex from Path 1 and at least one vertex from Path 2.\n\nTherefore, any minimal disconnecting set must include at least one vertex from each of these two paths. Therefore, the minimal disconnecting sets are exactly the pairs consisting of one vertex from Path 1 and one vertex from Path 2.\n\nBut let's confirm that. Let me think: suppose we pick a vertex from Path 1 and a vertex from Path 2, then removing those two would block both paths, right? Because Path 1 has that vertex removed, so it can't be used, and Path 2 has that vertex removed, so it can't be used. Therefore, any such pair would disconnect the graph. Conversely, any minimal disconnecting set must contain at least one vertex from Path 1 and at least one from Path 2, because if it only contained vertices from Path 1, then the Path 2 would still be intact, so start and home would still be connected via Path 2. Similarly, if it only contained vertices from Path 2, then Path 1 would still be intact. Therefore, minimal disconnecting sets must contain at least one vertex from each path.\n\nTherefore, the minimal disconnecting sets are exactly the pairs formed by one vertex from Path 1 and one vertex from Path 2.\n\nTherefore, let's first identify the vertices along Path 1 and Path 2.\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nThe internal vertices (excluding start and home) on Path 1 are: (3,2), (3,3), (2,3)\n\nSimilarly, Path 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nThe internal vertices (excluding start and home) on Path 2 are: (2,1), (1,1), (1,2)\n\nTherefore, Path 1's internal vertices: { (3,2), (3,3), (2,3) }\n\nPath 2's internal vertices: { (2,1), (1,1), (1,2) }\n\nTherefore, a minimal disconnecting set must consist of one vertex from { (3,2), (3,3), (2,3) } and one vertex from { (2,1), (1,1), (1,2) }.\n\nTherefore, the number of minimal disconnecting sets (pairs) is 3 (choices from Path 1) multiplied by 3 (choices from Path 2), so 9 minimal disconnecting sets of size 2.\n\nEach such pair, when removed, would disconnect the graph.\n\nNow, to form a disconnecting set of size 4, we can take any minimal disconnecting set of size 2 and add 2 more vertices from the remaining 5 non-start, non-home lily pads (since we have 7 total non-start, non-home, and we've already used 2 in the minimal disconnecting set). However, we have to be careful because adding extra vertices might not necessarily keep the graph disconnected; in fact, adding vertices might actually reconnect the graph if those vertices are on alternative paths.\n\nWait, actually, if we have a disconnecting set S of size 2, and then add two more vertices, the resulting set S' of size 4 will still disconnect the graph only if those two added vertices do not provide an alternative path that bypasses the original disconnecting set. Hmm, this complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think that any set of 4 vertices that includes a minimal disconnecting set (of size 2) will also disconnect the graph, provided that the additional vertices don't create a new path around the original disconnecting set.\n\nWait, but actually, if you have a disconnecting set S of size 2, and you add two more vertices, then the new set S' = S ∪ {v, w} will disconnect the graph unless the added vertices v and w, along with possibly other vertices, form a path that reconnects the two components separated by S.\n\nTherefore, to ensure that S' is still a disconnecting set, we must ensure that the addition of v and w does not create a new path between start and home that doesn't go through S.\n\nTherefore, it's not straightforward. So maybe this approach is too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of trying to build up from minimal disconnecting sets, we can consider all subsets of size 4 from the 7 non-start, non-home pads and check for each subset whether it disconnects start from home. Since 7 choose 4 is 35, which is manageable with some careful analysis.\n\nGiven that the grid is small, perhaps we can categorize the subsets based on which parts of the grid they remove.\n\nFirst, let me note that the 7 non-start, non-home pads are:\n\nA: (1,1)\n\nB: (1,2)\n\nC: (2,1)\n\nD: (2,2)\n\nE: (2,3)\n\nF: (3,2)\n\nG: (3,3)\n\nSo let me list them again with their coordinates:\n\nA: (1,1)\n\nB: (1,2)\n\nC: (2,1)\n\nD: (2,2)\n\nE: (2,3)\n\nF: (3,2)\n\nG: (3,3)\n\nOur goal is to choose 4 of these 7 such that in the remaining grid (with start (3,1) and home (1,3)), there is no path from (3,1) to (1,3).\n\nTo do this, we can think about which pads, if removed, block all possible paths.\n\nFirst, let me consider the two main paths again:\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nAdditionally, there are other paths that go through the center (2,2):\n\nFor example:\n\nPath 3: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 4: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 5: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) [same as Path 2]\n\nPath 6: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 7: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nWait, actually, there are quite a few paths. Let me see if I can characterize all possible paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the required cuts.\n\nTo block all paths, we need to ensure that every possible path from (3,1) to (1,3) must pass through at least one removed pad.\n\nTherefore, the set of removed pads must form a vertex cover for all paths from start to home.\n\nGiven that the minimal vertex cover (in terms of vertex cut) is size 2, but here we need a vertex cover of size 4.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of which rows or columns must be blocked.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to consider the grid and see which pads, when removed, would block all paths.\n\nFirst, note that the start is at the bottom-left (3,1), and home is at the top-right (1,3). The center of the grid is (2,2), which is a key intersection point.\n\nLet me consider removing certain key pads that lie on all paths.\n\nFor instance, if we remove the center pad D (2,2), does that block all paths?\n\nLet me see: If D (2,2) is removed, then any path going through the center is blocked, but are there alternative paths?\n\nYes. For example, the path going along the bottom row then up the right side: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through D.\n\nSimilarly, the path going up the left side: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This also doesn't go through D.\n\nTherefore, removing D alone is insufficient to disconnect start from home.\n\nSimilarly, removing just F (3,2) or G (3,3) might not be enough.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider removing both (3,2) and (2,3). Let's see if that blocks all paths.\n\nIf we remove F (3,2) and E (2,3):\n\nThen, the bottom row from (3,1) can't go to (3,3) because (3,2) is removed, so the only way up from (3,1) is to (2,1). From (2,1), you can go up to (1,1) or right to (2,2). From (2,2), you can go up to (1,2) or left to (2,1) or right to (2,3) but (2,3) is also removed? Wait, in this case, we removed E (2,3) and F (3,2). So, from (2,2), you can go to (1,2) or (2,1) or (2,3) but (2,3) is removed, so only up or left. From (1,2), you can go to (1,3) if that's still there, which it is. Wait, but (1,2) to (1,3) is still open. So the path (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) is still possible, so removing F and E doesn't block all paths.\n\nAlternatively, removing F (3,2) and C (2,1):\n\nThen, from (3,1), you can't go right to (3,2) (since F is removed) and can't go up to (2,1) (since C is removed). Therefore, the start (3,1) would be isolated? Wait, (3,1) is still present, but all its neighbors are removed? Wait, (3,1)'s neighbors are (2,1) and (3,2). If both C (2,1) and F (3,2) are removed, then (3,1) has no neighbors left. Therefore, (3,1) is isolated, so it can't reach anywhere, including home. But the problem states that the home must remain, but the start must remain as well, but if the start is isolated, then technically, it can't reach home, but the problem says \"the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain\", which they do, but the frog cannot get home because he can't move anywhere. Is that considered a valid case?\n\nWait, the problem says \"Kevin cannot get home\". If Kevin is stuck on the start pad with no possible moves, then he certainly cannot get home, so that should be counted. However, in this case, removing C and F would isolate the start pad, which is allowed as long as we don't remove the start pad itself. However, in this case, we are only removing 4 pads from the 7 non-start, non-home pads, so removing C and F (two pads) plus two others. Wait, in the above example, if we remove C, F, and two others, then the start is isolated, so that's one way. But in the problem, we have to remove exactly 4 pads, so if we remove C and F plus two more, that's 4 pads. However, in the scenario where we remove C and F, plus two others, the start is already isolated, so removing any two more pads won't affect that; the start is still isolated. Therefore, such subsets would definitely disconnect start from home. So, that's one way.\n\nBut in my previous consideration, when I considered removing F and E, that didn't isolate the start, but still left some paths. So, to get a disconnecting set of size 4, there are multiple strategies: either isolate the start, or block all possible paths through the grid.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to categorize the disconnecting sets of size 4 into different cases:\n\nCase 1: The start (3,1) is isolated. To do this, we need to remove both of its adjacent pads: (2,1) and (3,2). So, to isolate the start, we must remove C (2,1) and F (3,2). Then, we need to remove two more pads from the remaining 5 non-start, non-home pads (excluding C and F, so remaining are A, B, D, E, G). However, once we've removed C and F, the start is already isolated, so any additional removals won't affect that. Therefore, the number of subsets in this case is the number of ways to choose 2 more pads from the remaining 5 (A, B, D, E, G). That is C(5,2) = 10.\n\nCase 2: The home (1,3) is isolated. Similarly, to isolate home, we need to remove both of its adjacent pads: (1,2) and (2,3). The home's neighbors are (1,2) and (2,3). So, to isolate home, we must remove B (1,2) and E (2,3). Then, we need to remove two more pads from the remaining 5 non-start, non-home pads (excluding B and E, so remaining are A, C, D, F, G). The number here is also C(5,2) = 10.\n\nCase 3: Neither the start nor the home is isolated, but all paths from start to home go through at least one removed pad. In this case, we need to remove pads such that every possible path from start to home must pass through at least one of the removed pads, without isolating either start or home.\n\nTherefore, total disconnecting sets of size 4 would be Case 1 + Case 2 + Case 3.\n\nHowever, we need to be careful about overlap between the cases. For instance, a set that both isolates the start and the home would be counted in both Case 1 and Case 2, but such a set would require removing C, F, B, E, which is 4 pads. So that specific subset {B, C, E, F} would be counted in both Case 1 and Case 2. Therefore, we need to use inclusion-exclusion.\n\nSo, let's formalize this:\n\nLet A be the set of disconnecting sets of size 4 that isolate the start.\n\nLet B be the set of disconnecting sets of size 4 that isolate the home.\n\nLet C be the set of disconnecting sets of size 4 that neither isolate the start nor the home but still disconnect them.\n\nThen, total disconnecting sets = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|.\n\nWait, actually, if we consider A and B as sets of subsets that isolate the start or the home respectively, then their intersection is the set of subsets that isolate both start and home. Therefore, by inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B|\n\nTherefore, the total disconnecting sets that isolate either the start or the home are |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B|. Then, the total disconnecting sets would be |A ∪ B| + |C|, where C is those that disconnect without isolating either.\n\nBut actually, in our problem, we need all disconnecting sets, whether they isolate start, isolate home, or do neither but still disconnect. So total is |A ∪ B| + |C|. But since |A ∪ B| already includes all disconnecting sets that isolate start or home, and |C| are the ones that disconnect without isolating either, so total is |A ∪ B| + |C| = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|.\n\nBut perhaps it's simpler to compute |A|, |B|, |A ∩ B|, and |C| separately and then add them up.\n\nFirst, compute |A|: the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 that isolate the start.\n\nTo isolate the start, we must remove both of its neighbors: C (2,1) and F (3,2). Then, we need to choose 2 more pads from the remaining 5 non-start, non-home pads (excluding C and F). However, we have to ensure that those two additional pads are not... Well, actually, once we've already isolated the start, any additional pads we remove won't affect the isolation of the start, but we have to make sure that we are only counting subsets of size 4. So, the number here is C(5,2) = 10, as I thought before. But wait, hold on: the total number of pads to remove is 4. To isolate the start, we must remove C and F (2 pads), and then choose any 2 more from the remaining 5 (A, B, D, E, G). However, some of those choices might also isolate the home or do something else, but for the purpose of counting |A|, which is all subsets that isolate the start regardless of what happens to the home, we just need to count all subsets that include C and F and any two others from the remaining five. So that's indeed C(5,2)=10.\n\nSimilarly, |B|: the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 that isolate the home.\n\nTo isolate the home, we need to remove both of its neighbors: B (1,2) and E (2,3). Then, we need to choose 2 more pads from the remaining 5 non-start, non-home pads, excluding B and E. The remaining pads are A, C, D, F, G. Therefore, again, C(5,2)=10.\n\nNow, |A ∩ B|: the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 that isolate both the start and the home.\n\nTo isolate both the start and the home, we need to remove the neighbors of the start (C and F) and the neighbors of the home (B and E). That is, remove C, F, B, E. That's 4 pads. So there is exactly one such subset: {B, C, E, F}. Therefore, |A ∩ B| = 1.\n\nNow, we need to compute |C|: the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 that do not isolate the start nor the home, but still disconnect them. That is, they remove 4 pads, not including both C and F (so the start has at least one neighbor left), not including both B and E (so the home has at least one neighbor left), but still the start and home are disconnected.\n\nThis is the trickiest part. Let's try to compute |C|.\n\nTo compute |C|, we need subsets of 4 pads from the 7 non-start, non-home pads, such that:\n\n1. The subset does not include both C and F (so the start is not isolated),\n\n2. The subset does not include both B and E (so the home is not isolated),\n\n3. However, the subset still disconnects start from home.\n\nTherefore, to count |C|, we can consider all subsets of size 4 from the 7 non-start, non-home pads, excluding those subsets that isolate the start or the home, and then subtract those that don't disconnect at all. Wait, no, actually:\n\nWait, actually, |C| is the number of subsets of size 4 that do not isolate start or home, but do disconnect them. Therefore, to compute |C|, we can take the total number of disconnecting sets of size 4 (which we are trying to find in the first place) minus |A| minus |B| plus |A ∩ B|, but that might not help. Alternatively, since we are trying to compute |C| as part of the total, maybe it's better to compute it directly.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute the total number of disconnecting sets of size 4 as:\n\nTotal = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|\n\nBut since we don't know Total yet, this might not help. Alternatively, perhaps we can compute |C| by considering all subsets of size 4 that don't isolate start or home and then check whether they disconnect the graph.\n\nFirst, let's compute the total number of subsets of size 4 that do not isolate the start or the home.\n\nTotal subsets of size 4: C(7,4)=35.\n\nSubtract the subsets that isolate the start: |A|=10.\n\nSubtract the subsets that isolate the home: |B|=10.\n\nBut wait, when we subtract |A| and |B|, we have subtracted twice the intersection |A ∩ B|, so to get the number of subsets that do not isolate start or home, it's Total - |A| - |B| + |A ∩ B| = 35 - 10 -10 +1=16.\n\nWait, that formula is inclusion-exclusion for the complement:\n\nNumber of subsets that do not isolate start AND do not isolate home = Total - |A| - |B| + |A ∩ B| = 35 - 10 -10 +1 = 16.\n\nTherefore, there are 16 subsets of size 4 that do not isolate the start nor the home. Among these 16 subsets, some may still disconnect the graph (i.e., |C|), and some may not (i.e., they leave a path from start to home). Therefore, to find |C|, we need to determine how many of these 16 subsets disconnect the graph.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute how many of these 16 subsets disconnect the graph, that will be |C|, and then the total disconnecting sets are |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C| = (10 + 10 -1) + |C| = 19 + |C|. But actually, we need to see:\n\nWait, actually, the total disconnecting sets are the subsets that either isolate start, isolate home, or disconnect without isolating either. So:\n\nTotal disconnecting sets = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|.\n\nBut since we have:\n\nTotal disconnecting sets = (subsets that isolate start) + (subsets that isolate home) - (subsets that isolate both) + (subsets that disconnect without isolating either)\n\n= |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|\n\nBut since we know |A|, |B|, |A ∩ B|, and we can compute |C| as the number among the 16 subsets that do disconnect, then we can compute Total.\n\nAlternatively, since we need the answer, which is the total number of disconnecting sets (i.e., Total disconnecting sets), which is what we're trying to compute. Since the problem asks for the number of ways to remove 4 pads so that start and home remain but Kevin cannot get home, which is exactly the total disconnecting sets of size 4 (since we are not removing start or home). So, the answer is Total = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C| = 10 +10 -1 + |C| = 19 + |C|.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute |C|, which is the number of disconnecting sets of size 4 that neither isolate start nor home, then we can get the answer.\n\nSo, let's focus on computing |C|.\n\nWe have 16 subsets of size 4 that do not isolate start nor home. Each of these subsets does not include both C and F, and does not include both B and E. Therefore, in these subsets, the start has at least one neighbor (either C or F or both) remaining, and the home has at least one neighbor (either B or E or both) remaining. However, despite that, the start and home are still disconnected.\n\nTo compute |C|, we need to count how many of these 16 subsets actually disconnect the graph.\n\nThis requires analyzing each of the 16 subsets, but 16 is manageable, albeit tedious.\n\nFirst, let me list all subsets of size 4 that do not isolate start or home. Since this is going to take time, perhaps we can find a smarter way.\n\nFirst, note that the 16 subsets are all 4-element subsets of the 7 non-start, non-home pads, excluding those that include both C and F, or both B and E, or both?\n\nWait, actually, the subsets that do not isolate the start are those that do not include both C and F. Similarly, the subsets that do not isolate the home are those that do not include both B and E. Therefore, the subsets we are considering (the 16) are those that:\n\n- Do not contain both C and F,\n\nAND\n\n- Do not contain both B and E.\n\nTherefore, to list them, perhaps it's better to think in terms of forbidden pairs.\n\nFirst, let me note that the forbidden subsets (to exclude) are:\n\n1. Those that include both C and F (which would isolate the start),\n\n2. Those that include both B and E (which would isolate the home),\n\n3. Additionally, subsets that include both C and F AND both B and E are already included in the above, so we don't have to worry about overcounting beyond inclusion-exclusion.\n\nBut in any case, since we already computed that the number of subsets not isolating start or home is 16, let's see if we can categorize these 16 subsets based on which of the \"critical\" pads they include or exclude.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the structure of the grid and see what pads, when removed along with others, can block all paths without isolating start or home.\n\nLet me consider that in order to disconnect start from home without isolating either, we must block all possible paths by removing key pads that are on multiple paths, but not removing both neighbors of start or home.\n\nGiven that, let's consider the center pad D (2,2). Removing D might be a good candidate, as it's on many paths.\n\nSuppose we remove D. Then, any path going through D is blocked, but there might still be paths going around.\n\nFor instance, as before:\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 3: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) [now blocked because D is removed]\n\nPath 4: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) [blocked]\n\nBut Path 1 and Path 2 are still open unless we remove more pads.\n\nTherefore, removing D alone is insufficient, but if we remove D plus some other pads, maybe we can block all paths.\n\nSuppose we remove D and, say, F (3,2). Then:\n\n- Path 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) is blocked (F removed), so Path 1 is blocked.\n\n- Path 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) is still open unless we remove something else on that path.\n\n- Path going through D is blocked because D is removed.\n\nBut Path 2 remains open. So to block Path 2, we need to remove, say, (2,1) or (1,1) or (1,2).\n\nSuppose we remove D, F, and also B (1,2). Then:\n\n- Path 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) is blocked because B (1,2) is removed, so the path can't proceed to (1,3). So Path 2 is blocked.\n\n- Path 1 is already blocked by removing F.\n\n- The only remaining path would be through the center? Wait, no, center D is removed. Are there any other paths?\n\nWait, from (3,1) you can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1), then to (1,2) is blocked, so from (1,1) you can go to (1,2) which is blocked, or to (2,1) which is already passed. Alternatively, from (1,1) can you go to (1,0)? No, that's off the grid. So actually, if we remove D, F, and B, then:\n\nStart at (3,1). Can go to (2,1). From (2,1), can go to (1,1). From (1,1), can go to (1,2) but that's removed, so stuck. Alternatively, from (1,1) can go down to (2,1), but that's already been there. So the only way is to go back. Therefore, the start is in a component with (3,1), (2,1), (1,1), and that's it. The home (1,3) is in another component. So yes, this would disconnect them. The removed pads here are D, F, B, and we need a fourth pad. Wait, in this case, we've only removed three pads: D, F, B. To make it a size 4 subset, we need to remove one more pad. Let's say we remove G (3,3) as the fourth pad. Then, does that further disconnect?\n\nWait, but in this case, with D, F, B, G removed, let's see:\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1). From (2,1) to (1,1). From (1,1) can't go to (1,2) because B is removed. From (1,1) can go down to (2,1) or left? No, left is off the grid. So stuck. The home (1,3) is still isolated because:\n\nFrom home (1,3), its neighbors are (1,2) [removed] and (2,3). If (2,3) is still present, then the home can go to (2,3). From (2,3), can go to (3,3) [which is removed] or to (2,2) [removed] or to (1,3). So from (2,3), you can only go back to home. Therefore, the home is only connected to (2,3), which is a dead end. Therefore, the home is in a component with (1,3) and (2,3), but (2,3) can't go anywhere else. The start is in its own component. So yes, this disconnects them. So this subset {B, D, F, G} would be a disconnecting set not isolating start or home.\n\nBut this is getting complicated. Maybe a better approach is to consider that in order to disconnect the graph without isolating start or home, we need to remove at least one pad from each of the two main paths (Path 1 and Path 2) and also block the alternative paths through the center.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the two main paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 3: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 4: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3)\n\nPath 5: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\nEtc.\n\nTo block all these paths, we need to ensure that for each path, at least one of its internal vertices is removed.\n\nGiven that the minimal vertex cut is 2 (one from each of the two main paths), but since we have to remove 4 vertices, we can think of covering all paths with 4 vertices.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to consider that any disconnecting set of size 4 not isolating start or home must contain at least one vertex from each of the two main paths (Path 1 and Path 2) and also some others to block the center paths.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to think of the 16 subsets and categorize them based on which critical pads they include.\n\nFirst, note that the 16 subsets are all 4-element subsets not containing both C and F, and not containing both B and E.\n\nLet me consider the pads involved in the two main paths:\n\nPath 1 internal pads: {F, G, E} (since Path 1 goes through (3,2)=F, (3,3)=G, (2,3)=E)\n\nWait, actually, Path 1's internal vertices (excluding start and home) are (3,2), (3,3), (2,3). So F, G, E.\n\nPath 2 internal vertices: {C, A, B} (since Path 2 goes through (2,1)=C, (1,1)=A, (1,2)=B)\n\nWait, actually:\n\nPath 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) [C] -> (1,1) [A] -> (1,2) [B] -> (1,3). So internal vertices: C, A, B.\n\nTherefore, Path 1's internal vertices: F, G, E.\n\nPath 2's internal vertices: C, A, B.\n\nAdditionally, the center pad D is part of other paths but not part of these two main paths.\n\nSo, to block all paths, a disconnecting set must intersect both Path 1's internal vertices and Path 2's internal vertices, but since we're not isolating start or home, we can't remove both F and C (which would isolate start) or both E and B (which would isolate home).\n\nTherefore, a disconnecting set that doesn't isolate start or home must include at least one vertex from Path 1's internal vertices (F, G, E) and at least one vertex from Path 2's internal vertices (C, A, B), but not both F and C, and not both E and B.\n\nAdditionally, it might need to include other pads like D to block the center paths.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that to block all paths, the set must hit every possible path. Since there are multiple paths, we need to cover all possible routes.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the grid's structure and identify which pads are \"articulation points\" or \"cut vertices\".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider that in order to disconnect start from home without isolating either, we must remove at least one vertex from each of the two main paths (Path 1 and Path 2) and also block the center connections.\n\nWait, but let's see an example.\n\nSuppose we remove F (from Path 1) and B (from Path 2). Then, we've removed one from each main path. Then, we need to remove two more pads. If we remove, say, D and G, does that disconnect?\n\nLet me see:\n\nRemoving F, B, D, G:\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C] or (3,2) [F removed]. So only can go up to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (1,1) [A] or (2,2) [D removed]. So to (1,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go to (1,2) [B removed] or (2,1). So stuck except going back.\n\nSo the start is in a component with (3,1), (2,1), (1,1). The home is at (1,3). Let's see if home is reachable from elsewhere:\n\nHome (1,3) is connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3) [E]. From (2,3), you can go to (3,3) [G removed] or (2,2) [D removed] or (1,3). So (2,3) is only connected to home. Therefore, the home is in a component with (1,3) and (2,3). The start is in its own component. So yes, this disconnects them. So the subset {B, D, F, G} is a disconnecting set not isolating start or home.\n\nTherefore, this is part of |C|.\n\nAnother example: remove A, C, D, E.\n\nLet's see:\n\nA is (1,1), C is (2,1), D is (2,2), E is (2,3).\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C removed] or (3,2) [F is still there? Wait, we removed A, C, D, E. F is not removed. So (3,2) is still present.\n\nSo from (3,1) can go to (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2), can go to (3,3) [G], (2,2) [D removed], or (3,1). So to (3,3) or back.\n\nFrom (3,3), can go to (2,3) [E removed] or (3,2). So only back.\n\nSo the start can reach (3,2) and (3,3), but can't proceed further up.\n\nThe home (1,3) is connected to (1,2) [B] and (2,3) [E removed]. So home can go to (1,2) [if B is present]. Since we didn't remove B, home can go to (1,2), then to (1,1) [A removed] or (2,2) [D removed]. So from (1,2), you can't go anywhere except back to home. So home is in a component with itself and (1,2). The start is in a component with (3,1), (3,2), (3,3). These are separate components. So this also disconnects them. So subset {A, C, D, E} is in |C|.\n\nHmm, this is going to take a while, but maybe we can find a pattern or formula.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that any disconnecting set not isolating start or home must contain at least one vertex from Path 1's internal vertices (F, G, E) and at least one from Path 2's internal vertices (C, A, B), and also may or may not include D.\n\nAdditionally, the fourth vertex can be any of the remaining non-start/non-home pads, provided they don't cause isolation.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that to block all paths, the set must \"hit\" all possible routes. Given the complexity, maybe the best way is to compute |C| by considering that the 16 subsets can be partitioned based on whether they include D or not, and then analyze accordingly.\n\nLet me split the 16 subsets into two categories:\n\nCategory 1: Subsets that include the center pad D.\n\nCategory 2: Subsets that do not include D.\n\nCompute |C| as the number in Category 1 that disconnect plus the number in Category 2 that disconnect.\n\nFirst, compute Category 1: subsets of size 4 that include D and do not isolate start or home.\n\nTo include D, we must choose D plus 3 more pads from the remaining 6 non-start/non-home pads (since we can't include both C and F, and can't include both B and E). Wait, actually, no: when we include D, we still have to ensure that the subset doesn't isolate start or home.\n\nWait, actually, the subsets in Category 1 are all 4-element subsets that include D and do not isolate start or home. Since D is included, but we still have to avoid isolating start or home, so:\n\nTo not isolate start: the subset cannot include both C and F.\n\nTo not isolate home: the subset cannot include both B and E.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets in Category 1 is equal to the number of 3-element subsets of the remaining 6 pads (excluding D) that do not include both C and F and do not include both B and E, and then combined with D to make a 4-element subset.\n\nWait, actually, since we're choosing 4 elements including D, the other three must be chosen from the remaining 6 (A, B, C, E, F, G), with the constraints:\n\n- Not both C and F,\n\n- Not both B and E.\n\nTherefore, the number of such subsets is equal to:\n\nTotal subsets of size 3 from {A, B, C, E, F, G} minus those that include both C and F minus those that include both B and E plus those that include both C, F, B, E (inclusion-exclusion).\n\nWait, let me compute it step by step.\n\nFirst, total number of 3-element subsets from 6 elements: C(6,3) = 20.\n\nSubtract the subsets that include both C and F.\n\nHow many subsets include both C and F? If we include both C and F, then the third element is chosen from the remaining 4 elements: {A, B, E, G}. So C(4,1)=4.\n\nSimilarly, subtract subsets that include both B and E: similarly, if we include both B and E, the third element is from the remaining 4 elements: {A, C, F, G}. So also 4.\n\nBut now, we have subtracted twice the subsets that include both C,F and also B,E. How many subsets include all four elements C,F,B,E? Wait, but we are choosing subsets of size 3, so it's impossible to have all four elements C,F,B,E in a 3-element subset. Therefore, the intersection is zero. Therefore, by inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber of subsets violating the constraints = 4 + 4 = 8.\n\nTherefore, the number of valid subsets is 20 - 8 = 12.\n\nTherefore, Category 1 has 12 subsets.\n\nNow, we need to determine how many of these 12 subsets actually disconnect the graph.\n\nSimilarly, for Category 2: subsets of size 4 that do not include D and do not isolate start or home.\n\nNumber of subsets in Category 2: total subsets of size 4 not isolating start or home and not including D.\n\nTotal subsets not isolating start or home: 16.\n\nMinus subsets that include D: which we just calculated as 12, so 16 - 12 = 4.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\nTotal subsets not isolating start or home: 16.\n\nSubsets that include D and don't isolate: 12.\n\nTherefore, subsets that don't include D and don't isolate: 16 - 12 = 4.\n\nTherefore, Category 2 has 4 subsets.\n\nNow, we need to determine how many of the 12 in Category 1 and the 4 in Category 2 actually disconnect the graph.\n\nStarting with Category 2: only 4 subsets. Let's list them.\n\nCategory 2 subsets: 4-element subsets not containing D, not isolating start or home.\n\nTo form such a subset, we must choose 4 pads from the 6 non-D pads (A, B, C, E, F, G), with the constraints:\n\n- Not both C and F,\n\n- Not both B and E,\n\nand also not including D.\n\nAdditionally, they must not isolate start or home, which is already enforced by the constraints above.\n\nSo, the subsets in Category 2 are the 4-element subsets from {A, B, C, E, F, G} with:\n\n- Not both C and F,\n\n- Not both B and E,\n\nand size 4.\n\nLet me list all such subsets:\n\nFirst, list all 4-element subsets of {A, B, C, E, F, G} that don't have both C & F and don't have both B & E.\n\nTotal 4-element subsets of 6 elements: C(6,4)=15.\n\nSubtract those that have both C and F: How many 4-element subsets include both C and F?\n\nIf we include both C and F, then we need to choose 2 more from the remaining 4 elements: {A, B, E, G}. So C(4,2)=6.\n\nSubtract those that have both B and E: Similarly, including both B and E, choose 2 more from {A, C, F, G}. So C(4,2)=6.\n\nBut now, we have subtracted twice the subsets that include both C,F,B,E. How many subsets include all four C,F,B,E? Since we are choosing 4 elements, including all four would require the subset to be exactly {C,F,B,E}, which is one subset. So we need to add back 1.\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber of invalid subsets = 6 + 6 - 1 = 11.\n\nTherefore, valid subsets = 15 - 11 = 4. Which matches our previous count.\n\nNow, let's list these 4 valid subsets:\n\nFirst, the invalid subsets due to having both C & F:\n\nThe subsets that include C, F, and two others from {A,B,E,G}. The two others can be:\n\n1. A and B: {C, F, A, B}\n\n2. A and E: {C, F, A, E}\n\n3. A and G: {C, F, A, G}\n\n4. B and E: {C, F, B, E} (this one also has B and E, so it's also invalid for the other condition)\n\nWait, actually, when we subtracted the subsets with both C & F, we didn't consider their overlap with subsets having both B & E. Similarly for the other direction.\n\nBut since we already did inclusion-exclusion, the four valid subsets are those that don't have both C&F and don't have both B&E.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to list them explicitly.\n\nThe four valid subsets are:\n\n1. {A, B, C, E} – does this have both B&E? Yes, B and E are both present. So this is invalid.\n\nWait, no. Wait, hold on, this is getting confusing.\n\nWait, let me think differently: the valid subsets are those that:\n\n- Do not contain both C and F,\n\nAND\n\n- Do not contain both B and E.\n\nTherefore, let's enumerate all 4-element subsets from {A,B,C,E,F,G} and pick those that satisfy both conditions.\n\nTotal subsets: 15.\n\nLet me list them:\n\n1. {A,B,C,E}\n\n2. {A,B,C,F}\n\n3. {A,B,C,G}\n\n4. {A,B,E,F}\n\n5. {A,B,E,G}\n\n6. {A,B,F,G}\n\n7. {A,C,E,F}\n\n8. {A,C,E,G}\n\n9. {A,C,F,G}\n\n10. {A,E,F,G}\n\n11. {B,C,E,F}\n\n12. {B,C,E,G}\n\n13. {B,C,F,G}\n\n14. {B,E,F,G}\n\n15. {C,E,F,G}\n\nNow, let's check each for the conditions:\n\n1. {A,B,C,E}: contains C and F? No. Contains B and E? Yes (B and E are both present). → Invalid.\n\n2. {A,B,C,F}: contains C and F? Yes → Invalid.\n\n3. {A,B,C,G}: contains C and F? No. Contains B and E? No. → Valid.\n\n4. {A,B,E,F}: contains B and E? Yes → Invalid.\n\n5. {A,B,E,G}: contains B and E? Yes → Invalid.\n\n6. {A,B,F,G}: contains C and F? No. Contains B and E? No. → Valid.\n\n7. {A,C,E,F}: contains C and F? Yes → Invalid.\n\n8. {A,C,E,G}: contains C and F? No. Contains B and E? No. → Valid.\n\n9. {A,C,F,G}: contains C and F? Yes → Invalid.\n\n10. {A,E,F,G}: contains C and F? No. Contains B and E? No. → Valid.\n\n11. {B,C,E,F}: contains B and E? Yes → Invalid.\n\n12. {B,C,E,G}: contains B and E? Yes → Invalid.\n\n13. {B,C,F,G}: contains C and F? Yes → Invalid.\n\n14. {B,E,F,G}: contains B and E? Yes → Invalid.\n\n15. {C,E,F,G}: contains C and F? Yes → Invalid.\n\nTherefore, the valid subsets are numbers 3, 6, 8, 10:\n\n3. {A,B,C,G}\n\n6. {A,B,F,G}\n\n8. {A,C,E,G}\n\n10. {A,E,F,G}\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\n3. {A,B,C,G}: has C but not F; has B and E? No, E is not present. So okay.\n\n6. {A,B,F,G}: has F but not C; has B and E? No E. Okay.\n\n8. {A,C,E,G}: has C but not F; has B and E? No B. Okay.\n\n10. {A,E,F,G}: has F but not C; has B and E? No B. Okay.\n\nSo these four subsets are valid. Now, for each of these four subsets, we need to determine if they disconnect the graph.\n\nLet's analyze each one:\n\nFirst subset: {A,B,C,G} = { (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (3,3) }\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nNeighbors are (2,1) [C is present] and (3,2) [F is not in the subset, so F is removed? Wait, no: the subset is the set of removed pads. Wait a second! Wait, I think I made a critical mistake here.\n\nHold on, in my earlier analysis, I was considering the subsets as the pads to be removed, right? Because the problem says \"the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads\", so the subsets we are considering are the sets of pads to be removed. So when I was listing the subsets like {A,B,C,G}, that means we are removing those pads. Therefore, in the grid, those pads are gone, and we need to see if start and home are disconnected.\n\nTherefore, in the first subset: {A,B,C,G} corresponds to removing pads A (1,1), B (1,2), C (2,1), and G (3,3).\n\nSo, the remaining pads are:\n\nStart (3,1) is still there.\n\nHome (1,3) is still there.\n\nThe removed pads are A, B, C, G.\n\nTherefore, let's see connectivity:\n\nFrom start (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C is removed] or (3,2) [F is not removed, so F is still there? Wait, F is (3,2), which is not in the removed set {A,B,C,G}. So (3,2) is still present. Wait, hold on: the removed pads are A, B, C, G. So (3,2) is not removed, so it's still there.\n\nTherefore, from (3,1) can go to (3,2) [F] or (2,1) [C removed].\n\nSo can go to (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2) [F], can go to (3,1), (3,3) [G removed], or (2,2) [D is not removed, so D is still there? Wait, D is (2,2), which is not in the removed set. So D is present.\n\nFrom (3,2) can go up to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2) [D], can go to (2,1) [C removed], (2,3) [E is not removed? E is (2,3), which is not in the removed set {A,B,C,G}, so E is present.\n\nFrom (2,2) can go to (2,3) [E].\n\nFrom (2,3) [E], can go to (1,3) [home] or (2,2) or (3,3) [G removed].\n\nSo from (2,3) can go to home.\n\nTherefore, path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). So this path exists. Therefore, the subset {A,B,C,G} does NOT disconnect start from home. Therefore, this subset is NOT in |C|.\n\nSecond subset: {A,B,F,G} corresponds to removing A (1,1), B (1,2), F (3,2), G (3,3).\n\nSo removed pads: A, B, F, G.\n\nRemaining pads include start (3,1), home (1,3), and others:\n\nFrom start (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C is not removed], or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nSo can go to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) [C], can go to (1,1) [A removed], (2,2) [D present], or (3,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2) [D], can go to (2,1), (2,3) [E present], (1,2) [B removed], or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nSo from (2,2) can go to (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3) [E], can go to (1,3) [home] or (2,2) or (3,3) [G removed].\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). This path exists. Therefore, this subset also does NOT disconnect. So not in |C|.\n\nThird subset: {A,C,E,G} corresponds to removing A (1,1), C (2,1), E (2,3), G (3,3).\n\nRemoved pads: A, C, E, G.\n\nRemaining pads: start (3,1), home (1,3), and others:\n\nFrom start (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C removed] or (3,2) [F present? F is (3,2), not removed].\n\nSo can go to (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2) [F], can go to (3,1), (3,3) [G removed], or (2,2) [D present].\n\nFrom (3,2) -> (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2) [D], can go to (2,1) [C removed], (2,3) [E removed], (1,2) [B present], or (3,2).\n\nFrom (2,2) -> (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2) [B], can go to (1,1) [A removed], (1,3) [home], or (2,2).\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). This path exists. Therefore, this subset also does NOT disconnect. Not in |C|.\n\nFourth subset: {A,E,F,G} corresponds to removing A (1,1), E (2,3), F (3,2), G (3,3).\n\nRemoved pads: A, E, F, G.\n\nRemaining pads: start (3,1), home (1,3), and others:\n\nFrom start (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C present] or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nSo can go to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) [C], can go to (1,1) [A removed], (2,2) [D present], or (3,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2) [D], can go to (2,1), (2,3) [E removed], (1,2) [B present], or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nFrom (2,2) -> (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2) [B], can go to (1,1) [A removed], (1,3) [home], or (2,2).\n\nPath: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). This path exists. So this subset also does NOT disconnect. Not in |C|.\n\nTherefore, all four subsets in Category 2 do NOT disconnect the graph. So |C| from Category 2 is 0.\n\nNow, moving to Category 1: subsets of size 4 that include D and do not isolate start or home. There are 12 such subsets. We need to determine how many of these 12 disconnect the graph.\n\nThis is going to be tedious, but let's try to find a pattern or find how many of these 12 subsets actually disconnect.\n\nFirst, note that since these subsets include D, which is the center, removing D can block some paths, but not all. To disconnect, they need to block all paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps many of these subsets do disconnect, but some don't.\n\nLet me consider that in Category 1, the subsets include D and three other pads from {A,B,C,E,F,G}, with the constraints that they don't have both C & F and don't have both B & E.\n\nGiven that D is removed, let's analyze how the removal of D affects the graph.\n\nRemoving D (2,2) breaks the center, so any path going through D is blocked. So paths that go through D are blocked, but other paths may still exist.\n\nFor example:\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3) still exists unless we remove one of these pads.\n\nPath 2: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) still exists unless we remove one of these pads.\n\nPath 3: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) [D removed] -> ... is blocked.\n\nPath 4: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) [D removed] -> ... is blocked.\n\nPath 5: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3) is blocked at D.\n\nPath 6: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) is blocked at D.\n\nSo, the remaining possible paths are Path 1 and Path 2, unless additional pads are removed.\n\nTherefore, to disconnect the graph with D removed, we need to additionally block Path 1 and Path 2.\n\nTherefore, in the subsets in Category 1 (which include D and three others), to disconnect, they must also block Path 1 and Path 2.\n\nTo block Path 1: need to remove at least one of its internal vertices: F, G, or E.\n\nTo block Path 2: need to remove at least one of its internal vertices: C, A, or B.\n\nTherefore, in addition to removing D, the three other removed pads must include at least one from {F, G, E} and at least one from {C, A, B}.\n\nHowever, the subsets in Category 1 cannot have both C and F, and cannot have both B and E. So we need to ensure that within the three additional pads (excluding D), we have at least one from Path 1's internal vertices (F, G, E) and at least one from Path 2's internal vertices (C, A, B), without having both C and F or both B and E.\n\nTherefore, the three additional pads must satisfy:\n\n- At least one from {F, G, E},\n\n- At least one from {C, A, B},\n\n- Not both C and F,\n\n- Not both B and E.\n\nTherefore, the number of such subsets is equal to the number of 3-element subsets from {A,B,C,E,F,G} that include at least one from {F,G,E} and at least one from {C,A,B}, and also not having both C and F or both B and E.\n\nThis is getting complex, but perhaps we can compute it as follows:\n\nTotal subsets in Category 1: 12.\n\nOut of these 12, how many have at least one from {F,G,E} and at least one from {C,A,B}?\n\nAlternatively, compute the number that fail to block Path 1 or Path 2, and subtract from 12.\n\nA subset fails to block Path 1 if it has none of F, G, E.\n\nSimilarly, it fails to block Path 2 if it has none of C, A, B.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets that do NOT disconnect is:\n\nNumber of subsets missing {F,G,E} OR missing {C,A,B}.\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber missing {F,G,E} = number of 3-element subsets with all elements in {C,A,B} (since excluding F,G,E). But {C,A,B} has 3 elements, so the only subset is {C,A,B}, but we need 3 elements. So if we are choosing 3 elements from {C,A,B}, which has size 3, there's only 1 subset: {C,A,B}. However, we must also ensure that the subset doesn't violate the constraints (i.e., not both C and F [but F is excluded], not both B and E [E is excluded]).\n\nWait, since we're in the subsets that are part of Category 1, which already satisfy not having both C and F and not having both B and E. So if we take a subset that excludes F,G,E, then it must be a subset of {C,A,B}, but must also not have both C and F (which is automatically satisfied since F isn't there) and not have both B and E (E isn't there). Therefore, any subset of {C,A,B} is acceptable as long as it doesn't have both B and E, but since E isn't present, it's okay. So the only subset missing {F,G,E} is {C,A,B}.\n\nSimilarly, the number missing {C,A,B} is the number of 3-element subsets from {F,G,E} (since we exclude C,A,B). {F,G,E} has 3 elements, so only one subset: {F,G,E}.\n\nNow, the intersection of these two sets (subsets missing both {F,G,E} and {C,A,B}) is empty, since you can't have a subset of size 3 that's entirely within {C,A,B} and also entirely within {F,G,E}.\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion, the number of subsets that fail to block at least one path is 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets that successfully block both paths (and thus disconnect) is total subsets in Category 1 (12) minus 2 = 10.\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, the total number of subsets in Category 1 is 12.\n\nNumber of subsets that fail to block Path 1 OR Path 2 is 2.\n\nTherefore, the number that successfully block both paths (and thus disconnect) is 12 - 2 = 10.\n\nBut wait, let me verify with an example.\n\nFirst, the subset that fails to block Path 1 is {C,A,B} combined with D. So the full subset is {C, A, B, D}. Let's see if this disconnects:\n\nRemoving C, A, B, D.\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C removed] or (3,2) [F present? F is not removed, so yes].\n\nFrom (3,1) -> (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2), can go to (3,1), (3,3) [G present? G is not removed], or (2,2) [D removed].\n\nFrom (3,2) -> (3,3).\n\nFrom (3,3), can go to (2,3) [E present? E is not removed], (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,3) -> (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3), can go to (1,3) [home] or (2,2) [D removed] or (3,3).\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). This path exists. So this subset does NOT disconnect. So that's one failure.\n\nSimilarly, the subset that fails to block Path 2 is {F,G,E} combined with D: {F, G, E, D}. Let's see:\n\nRemoving F, G, E, D.\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C present? C is not removed], or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nFrom (3,1) -> (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (1,1) [A present? A is not removed], (2,2) [D removed], or (3,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (1,1).\n\nFrom (1,1), can go to (1,2) [B present? B is not removed], (2,1).\n\nFrom (1,1) -> (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2), can go to (1,1), (1,3) [home], or (2,2) [D removed].\n\nPath: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). This path exists. So this subset also does NOT disconnect. So that's the second failure.\n\nTherefore, indeed, there are two subsets in Category 1 that do NOT disconnect the graph. Thus, the remaining 12 - 2 = 10 subsets in Category 1 DO disconnect the graph.\n\nTherefore, |C| = 10 (from Category 1) + 0 (from Category 2) = 10.\n\nTherefore, total disconnecting sets = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C| = 10 + 10 - 1 + 10 = 29.\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, let's recap:\n\nTotal disconnecting sets = subsets that isolate start + subsets that isolate home - subsets that isolate both + subsets that disconnect without isolating either.\n\nWhich is |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|.\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\n|A| = 10,\n\n|B| = 10,\n\n|A ∩ B| = 1,\n\n|C| = 10,\n\nTherefore total disconnecting sets = 10 + 10 - 1 + 10 = 29.\n\nBut wait, the total number of subsets is only 35, and we had earlier calculated that subsets that do NOT isolate start or home are 16, of which 10 disconnect and 6 do not (since 16 - 10 = 6). But according to this calculation, total disconnecting sets are 29. Let me see if that makes sense.\n\nWait, actually, the disconnecting sets include those that isolate start, isolate home, and those that disconnect without isolating. So:\n\n- Isolate start: 10 subsets,\n\n- Isolate home: 10 subsets,\n\n- Both: 1 subset,\n\n- Disconnect without isolating: 10 subsets,\n\nTotal: 10 + 10 - 1 + 10 = 29.\n\nBut let's verify with another angle:\n\nTotal subsets: 35.\n\nNumber of subsets that do NOT disconnect: those that leave a path from start to home.\n\nThese are:\n\n- Subsets that neither isolate start nor home but still leave a path: 16 total non-isolating subsets minus |C| (which is 10) = 6.\n\n- Subsets that isolate start but don't isolate home: |A| - |A ∩ B| = 10 - 1 = 9.\n\n- Subsets that isolate home but don't isolate start: |B| - |A ∩ B| = 10 - 1 = 9.\n\nTherefore, total non-disconnecting subsets: 9 + 9 + 6 = 24.\n\nTherefore, total disconnecting subsets = 35 - 24 = 11? Wait, this contradicts previous calculation.\n\nWait, this is confusing. There must be an error in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let's do it carefully:\n\nTotal subsets: 35.\n\nSubsets that disconnect: ?\n\nSubsets that do NOT disconnect: those that leave a path from start to home.\n\nSubsets that leave a path from start to home can be partitioned into:\n\n1. Subsets that isolate neither start nor home but still leave a path: let's call this X.\n\n2. Subsets that isolate start but leave a path from home: but if you isolate start, then start is disconnected from everyone, so home is also disconnected from start, but the question is about Kevin cannot get home, which is true even if he's isolated. Wait, actually, if you isolate the start, then Kevin cannot get home, so those subsets that isolate start are already disconnecting sets. Similarly for isolating home.\n\nWait, my mistake earlier: the subsets that isolate start are disconnecting sets, because start cannot reach home (since it's isolated). Similarly for isolating home. The subsets that isolate both are also disconnecting. The subsets that disconnect without isolating are also disconnecting. The subsets that do NOT disconnect are those that neither isolate start nor home AND still leave a path from start to home.\n\nTherefore:\n\nTotal disconnecting subsets = (isolating start) + (isolating home) - (isolating both) + (disconnect without isolating)\n\n= |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| + |C|\n\n= 10 + 10 - 1 + 10 = 29.\n\nTotal non-disconnecting subsets = total subsets - disconnecting subsets = 35 - 29 = 6.\n\nAlternatively, non-disconnecting subsets are those that do not isolate start or home AND still have a path from start to home. Which we calculated as X = 16 - |C| = 16 - 10 = 6.\n\nYes, so that matches: 6 non-disconnecting subsets.\n\nTherefore, the total number of disconnecting subsets is 29.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that we must keep the start and home pads, and remove 4 others. The answer is the number of such subsets, which is 29.\n\nBut let me sanity check with an example.\n\nEarlier, we saw that the subset {B, C, E, F} (which isolates both start and home) is counted in |A ∩ B| =1, and is indeed a disconnecting set.\n\nAlso, the subset {A,B,C,G} was in Category 2 but didn't disconnect, so it's part of the non-disconnecting sets.\n\nSimilarly, the subset {A,B,F,G} also didn't disconnect.\n\nWait, but according to the calculation, there are 6 non-disconnecting subsets, which include those in Category 2 (the four subsets) plus two more?\n\nWait, in Category 2, we had four subsets that didn't disconnect, and in Category 1, we had two subsets that didn't disconnect (the ones failing to block both paths). So total non-disconnecting subsets are 4 (Category 2) + 2 (Category 1) = 6, which matches.\n\nTherefore, the total disconnecting subsets are 35 - 6 = 29.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 29?\n\nBut let me cross-verify with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, recall that in the beginning, I considered that the minimal disconnecting sets are size 2, and we can build up to size 4.\n\nEach minimal disconnecting set of size 2 can be combined with any two other pads from the remaining 5, but we have to ensure that the larger set remains a disconnecting set.\n\nHowever, this approach might overcount because adding two pads might reconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, the total number of disconnecting sets of size 4 is equal to:\n\nFor each minimal disconnecting set of size 2 (there are 9 of them), the number of ways to add two more pads such that the resulting set still disconnects.\n\nFor each minimal disconnecting set S of size 2, the number of ways to choose 2 more pads from the remaining 5, but such that the addition doesn't reconnect the graph.\n\nHowever, this requires knowing, for each minimal disconnecting set S, how many supersets of size 4 maintain the disconnect.\n\nThis seems complex, but let's try with one example.\n\nTake a minimal disconnecting set S1 = {C, B} (removing C and B). This isolates the home? Wait no: S1 is a minimal disconnecting set, which should disconnect the graph without necessarily isolating start or home.\n\nWait, actually, earlier we had that minimal disconnecting sets are pairs with one from Path 1 and one from Path 2.\n\nFor example, take S = {F (from Path 1), B (from Path 2)}. This should disconnect the graph.\n\nNow, adding two more pads to this set: we have to choose 2 more from the remaining 5 pads: A, C, D, E, G.\n\nWe need to ensure that the new set {F, B, x, y} still disconnects the graph.\n\nDepending on which x and y we choose, it might or might not reconnect.\n\nFor instance, if we add D and A, then the new set is {F, B, D, A}. Does this disconnect?\n\nLet's see:\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C is present? C is not removed, so yes], or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nFrom (3,1) -> (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (1,1) [A removed? A is removed now], so can't go that way, or (2,2) [D removed? D is removed now], so can't go that way, or back to start.\n\nWait, start at (3,1):\n\nNeighbors: (2,1) and (3,2). (3,2) is removed (F is removed), (2,1) is present (C is not removed). So can go to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), neighbors: (1,1) [A removed], (2,2) [D removed], and (3,1). So stuck. So start is in a component with (3,1), (2,1).\n\nHome (1,3):\n\nNeighbors: (1,2) [B removed], (2,3) [E present? E is not removed].\n\nFrom home (1,3) can go to (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2) [D removed], or (3,3) [G present? G is not removed].\n\nFrom (2,3) -> (3,3).\n\nFrom (3,3) can go to (3,2) [F removed], (2,3), or (3,4) which doesn't exist.\n\nSo home is in a component with (1,3), (2,3), (3,3).\n\nThese are separate components from the start's component. So yes, this subset disconnects.\n\nAnother example: adding C and E to S={F,B}:\n\nNew set {F, B, C, E}.\n\nCheck connectivity:\n\nStart (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C removed? C is now removed], so can't go that way. Or (3,2) [F removed]. So start is isolated? Wait:\n\nWait, start (3,1)'s neighbors are (2,1) and (3,2). Both C and F are removed. Therefore, start is isolated. So this set isolates the start, hence it's a disconnecting set. But in this case, we added pads that caused isolation of the start, which is allowed in disconnecting sets. Since we're counting all disconnecting sets, including those that isolate start or home, this is acceptable.\n\nAnother example: adding D and G to S={F,B}:\n\nNew set {F, B, D, G}.\n\nCheck connectivity:\n\nStart (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C is present? C not removed], or (3,2) [F removed].\n\nSo can go to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (1,1) [A present? A not removed], (2,2) [D removed], or (3,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (1,1).\n\nFrom (1,1) can go to (1,2) [B removed], (2,1), or (1,0) invalid.\n\nStuck at (1,1) except back.\n\nHome (1,3):\n\nCan go to (1,2) [B removed] or (2,3) [E present? E not removed].\n\nFrom (1,3) -> (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3) -> (1,3), (2,2) [D removed], or (3,3) [G removed].\n\nSo home is in a component with (1,3), (2,3).\n\nThe start is in a component with (3,1), (2,1), (1,1). These are separate from the home component. So this disconnects.\n\nAnother example: adding A and C to S={F,B}:\n\nNew set {F, B, A, C}.\n\nCheck:\n\nStart (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C removed? C is removed now], so no. Or (3,2) [F removed]. So start is isolated. So this is a disconnecting set (isolating start).\n\nTherefore, in many cases, adding any two pads to a minimal disconnecting set will still result in a disconnecting set, except perhaps when adding pads that reconnect.\n\nWait, when would adding two pads reconnect?\n\nSuppose we have minimal disconnecting set S = {F, B} (removing F and B). Adding pads that provide an alternative path.\n\nFor instance, adding D and A:\n\nNew set {F, B, D, A}.\n\nDoes this reconnect?\n\nStart at (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) [C is present? C not removed], so (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (1,1) [A removed? A is removed now], so can't go that way. Or (2,2) [D removed? D is removed now]. So stuck.\n\nHome (1,3):\n\nCan go to (2,3) [E present? E not removed].\n\nFrom (2,3) -> (1,3), (2,2) [D removed], or (3,3) [G present? G not removed].\n\nFrom (3,3) -> (2,3), (3,2) [F removed], or (3,4) invalid.\n\nSo home's