tical for the paths. Let me try to visualize the grid and see which squares are on many paths.\n\nFirst, note that (3,1) and (1,3) are fixed, so we can't remove them. The other lily pads are the 7 in the middle. Let me label all the lily pads for clarity:\n\nLet me denote the lily pads as follows, with coordinates (row, column):\n\n1. (1,1) - top-left\n2. (1,2) - top-center\n3. (1,3) - top-right (Kevin's home)\n4. (2,1) - middle-left\n5. (2,2) - center\n6. (2,3) - middle-right\n7. (3,1) - bottom-left (Kevin's start)\n8. (3,2) - bottom-center\n9. (3,3) - bottom-right\n\nSo, we must keep lily pads 1, 3, 7, and we have to remove 4 of the remaining 7 (positions 2,4,5,6,8,9, and also positions 1 and 3 are kept, but wait, hold on: actually, positions 1, 3, and 7 must be kept? Wait, hold on. Wait, the problem says: \"Compute the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads so that the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain\". So the bottom-left is (3,1) which is position 7, and the top-right is (1,3) which is position 3. So positions 3 and 7 must remain, and we remove 4 of the other 7 (positions 1,2,4,5,6,8,9). Therefore, the remaining lily pads will be positions 3,7 plus three more from the other seven, so total of five lily pads.\n\nTherefore, we need to choose three lily pads from positions 1,2,4,5,6,8,9 to keep, such that in the induced subgraph with nodes {3,7} union those three, there is no path from 3 to 7? Wait, no: the frog is starting at (3,1) which is position 7, and trying to get to (1,3) which is position 3. So in the induced subgraph, there should be no path from position 7 to position 3. So we need to choose three more positions to keep (from the seven available) such that in the subgraph induced by {3,7} and those three, there is no path from 7 to 3.\n\nAlternatively, the remaining lily pads (positions 3,7 and three others) must form a graph where 7 and 3 are in different connected components. \n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: how many subsets 