atively, from (3,1) up to (2,1), then to (1,1) which is removed, so can't go that way. But the other path still works. So that's not a cut.\n\nAlternatively, remove (1,3)'s adjacent squares? Wait, but (1,3) must remain. Wait, we can't remove (1,3). Similarly, (3,1) must remain.\n\nAlternatively, try removing (2,2) and (1,2). Let's see:\n\nRemoving (2,2) and (1,2):\n\nFrom (3,1), go up to (2,1), then up to (1,1), then right to (1,2) which is removed, so blocked. Alternatively, from (2,1) right to (2,2) which is removed, so can't go that way. From (3,1) right to (3,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed, so blocked. So from (3,1), can you go anywhere? Let's see:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Up to (2,1) is okay, but from there you can't go further because (1,1) is accessible but then (1,2) is removed, and (2,2) is removed. From (2,1), you can go right to (2,2) which is removed, so no. So from (2,1), only up to (1,1) but then stuck. From (3,1) right to (3,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed, so stuck. From (3,1) right to (3,2), then right to (3,3), then up to (2,3), then up to (1,3). Wait, (3,3) is still there, right? Because we only removed (2,2) and (1,2). So (3,3) is still present. So path: (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3) → (2,3) → (1,3). That's a valid path. So that path still exists. Therefore, removing (2,2) and (1,2) doesn't block all paths.\n\nHmm. Maybe minimal vertex cut is 3?\n\nLet me try removing three vertices. Let's see if we can disconnect (3,1) from (1,3) by removing three vertices.\n\nLet me try removing the center (2,2), and the two middle edges: (1,2) and (3,2). So removing (1,2), (2,2), (3,2). Let's see if that disconnects (3,1) from (1,3):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1), then up to (1,1). From (1,1), can go right? (1,2) is removed, so can't go to (1,3). From (1,1), can't go down because it's on the top row. From (2,1), can go right to (2,2) which is removed. So from (3,1) you can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1), but from there, no way to (1,3). Alternatively, from (3,1) right to (3,2) which is removed. Then up to (2,2) which is removed. So all paths seem blocked? Wait, let me check again:\n\nIs there another path?\n\n(3,1) → (3,2) is blocked. (3,1) → (2,1) → (2,2) is blocked. (3,1) → (2,1) → (1,1). From (1,1), you can't go right to (1,2) because it's removed. From (1,1), can you go down? No, because it's on the top row. So (1,1) is a dead end. \n\nAlternatively, is there a path through (3,3)? From (3,1) can you go to (3,3)? That would require going through (3,2) which is removed, so no. So seems like all paths are blocked. So removing (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). So that is a vertex cut of size 3.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there are other vertex cuts of size 3. Let me see.\n\nSuppose instead we remove (2,1), (2,3), and (1,2). Let's see:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. So can't go that way. From (3,1) right to (3,2), then up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go right to (2,3) which is removed. So from (2,2), can go up to (1,2) which is removed. So from (3,1) you can go to (3,2), then to (2,2), but then stuck. Alternatively, from (3,2) right to (3,3), then up to (2,3) which is removed. So stuck again. So that path is blocked. Alternatively, from (3,2) up to (2,2), then left? No, (2,2) can go left to (2,1) which is removed. Hmm. So seems like that also blocks all paths. So that's another vertex cut of size 3.\n\nAlternatively, removing (1,1), (2,2), and (3,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1) which is removed. Alternatively, right to (3,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed. So stuck again. From (3,1), can you go to (3,3)? That would require going through (3,2) then (3,3), but (3,3) is removed? Wait, we removed (3,3). So (3,3) is gone. So from (3,2) you can't go to (3,3). So yes, that path is blocked. From (3,1) to (1,3):\n\nAlternatively, is there a path through the center? (3,1) to (2,1) to (2,2) is removed. (3,1) to (3,2) to (2,2) is removed. So no. So that also blocks all paths. So that's another vertex cut of size 3.\n\nSo it seems that there are multiple vertex cuts of size 3. Therefore, the minimal vertex cut size is 3. Therefore, any vertex cut of size 4 must consist of a minimal vertex cut plus an extra vertex. Therefore, perhaps we can compute the number of minimal vertex cuts of size 3, and then for each such minimal cut, count the number of ways to add one more vertex to it (without overlapping), and then also account for any other vertex cuts of size 4 that aren't just minimal cuts plus one.\n\nHowever, this might be complicated, but let's try.\n\nFirst, let me try to enumerate all minimal vertex cuts of size 3. Since the grid is small, maybe we can do this.\n\nFirst, let me consider the grid:\n\nPositions (using coordinates):\n\n(1,1) top-left\n\n(1,2) top-center\n\n(1,3) top-right (home)\n\n(2,1) middle-left\n\n(2,2) center\n\n(2,3) middle-right\n\n(3,1) bottom-left (start)\n\n(3,2) bottom-center\n\n(3,3) bottom-right\n\nWe need to find all sets of 3 vertices (excluding (3,1) and (1,3)) whose removal disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). \n\nFrom my earlier trials, I found a few:\n\n1. { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) } \n\n2. { (2,1), (2,3), (1,2) }\n\n3. { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }\n\nAre there others?\n\nLet me try another combination. Suppose we remove (1,1), (2,1), and (2,3):\n\nRemoving those three:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. So can't go that way. From (3,1) right to (3,2), then up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go right to (2,3) which is removed, or up to (1,2). From (1,2), can go right to (1,3). So path: (3,1) → (3,2) → (2,2) → (1,2) → (1,3). So that path still exists. Therefore, this isn't a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. How about removing (1,1), (3,2), and (2,3):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1) which is removed. Alternatively, right to (3,2) which is removed. So from (3,1), only path is up to (2,1), then stuck. From (2,1), can go right to (2,2). From (2,2), can go right to (2,3) which is removed, or up to (1,2). From (1,2), can go right to (1,3). So path: (3,1) → (2,1) → (2,2) → (1,2) → (1,3). So that path still exists. So that's not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm, another attempt: removing (2,2), (1,3)'s neighbor? Wait, but (1,3) must stay. How about removing (1,2), (2,2), and (3,1)'s neighbor? Wait, (3,1)'s neighbors are (2,1) and (3,2). Suppose we remove (2,1), (2,2), and (3,2). Let's see:\n\nRemoving (2,1), (2,2), (3,2):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. So can't go up. From (3,1) right to (3,2) which is removed. So only possible move is... none? Wait, (3,1) is only connected to (2,1) and (3,2), both removed. So (3,1) is isolated? But (3,1) must remain, but if all its neighbors are removed, then it's isolated from the rest. But (1,3) is still there, but can (1,3) reach anywhere? Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,3), it can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2) which is removed, or down to (2,3). From (1,2), can go down to (2,2) which is removed, or left to (1,1). From (2,3), can go down to (3,3). So (1,3) can go to (1,2) → (1,1) or (2,3) → (3,3). But (3,1) is isolated. So in this case, (3,1) is in its own component, and (1,3) is in another component, so they are disconnected. So this is also a vertex cut. Wait, so this is another vertex cut of size 3: { (2,1), (2,2), (3,2) }.\n\nWait, but isn't this the same as my first example? Wait, no, in my first example I had { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }, whereas this is { (2,1), (2,2), (3,2) }.\n\nYes, different sets. So that's another one.\n\nSimilarly, perhaps { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) }? Let's see:\n\nRemoving (1,2), (2,1), (2,3):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. So can't go up. Right to (3,2), then up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go right to (2,3) which is removed, or up to (1,2) which is removed. So from (2,2), stuck. From (3,2), can go to (2,2) or (3,3). From (3,3), can go up to (2,3) which is removed, so stuck. So the only path from (3,1) would be through (3,2) to (2,2), but that's a dead end. So (3,1) is only connected to (3,2), but (3,2) can't reach anywhere else except maybe (3,3), but (3,3) can't go anywhere. Wait, (3,3) can go up to (2,3) which is removed, so (3,3) is also isolated. Therefore, (3,1) is connected only to (3,2), which is connected to (3,3), but (3,3) is isolated, so (3,1) and (3,3) form a component, and (1,3) is in another component. So yes, disconnected. Therefore, { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) } is another vertex cut of size 3.\n\nHmm, so there are several such minimal vertex cuts. Let me see if I can find a systematic way.\n\nFirst, let's note that any vertex cut of size 3 must block all paths. Since the minimal vertex cut is 3, perhaps all minimal vertex cuts are of this form: blocking the central column or row?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the grid as a graph and see the possible cuts.\n\nAlternatively, note that the graph is symmetric, so maybe we can categorize the vertex cuts by their positions.\n\nLet me try to list all possible minimal vertex cuts of size 3:\n\n1. The first example I had: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) } – this is the central column (column 2) except the top and bottom? Wait, actually, column 2 has three nodes: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2). Removing all of column 2 would certainly disconnect the grid, but we only need to remove 3 nodes here, which are exactly column 2. Wait, but column 2 has three nodes: (1,2), (2,2), (3,2). So removing all three nodes of column 2 would disconnect the grid? Let's see:\n\nIf we remove column 2 entirely, then the grid is split into two separate parts: the left half and the right half. Because you can't go from left of column 2 to right of column 2, since column 2 is removed. So yes, removing column 2 (three nodes) disconnects the grid into two parts: left (columns 1) and right (columns 3). Since (3,1) is on the left and (1,3) is on the right, they are disconnected. So that is indeed a vertex cut of size 3. Similarly, removing row 2 entirely would also disconnect the grid? Let's see: removing row 2 (nodes (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)):\n\nFrom (3,1), you can go up to (1,1), then right to (1,3). So that path still exists. So removing row 2 doesn't disconnect (3,1) from (1,3). So row 2 isn't a cut.\n\nAlternatively, removing column 1? Column 1 has nodes (1,1), (2,1), (3,1). But we must keep (3,1), so can't remove that. Similarly, column 3 has (1,3), which must stay, so can't remove column 3 entirely. \n\nSimilarly, removing the center node (2,2) and the two nodes above and below it in column 2? Wait, that's column 2 again.\n\nAlternatively, another minimal cut is removing the \"cross\" of the center and the two middle edges? Wait, like (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) as we had.\n\nSimilarly, another minimal cut could be removing the left and right edges of the center column? Wait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that to disconnect the start and finish, we need to block all possible paths. Since the start is on the bottom-left and finish on top-right, one obvious way is to block the central column (column 2), which we saw is a cut of size 3. Alternatively, block the central row? But central row is row 2, which if we remove row 2, we still have a path through the top and bottom rows.\n\nAlternatively, block the two \"diagonal\" paths? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there are other minimal cuts besides column 2 and some others.\n\nAnother example: removing the nodes (1,1), (2,2), and (3,3). Let me check again:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1) which is removed. Alternatively, right to (3,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed. So stuck. From (3,1), can't reach anywhere else. Meanwhile, (1,3) can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2) which is removed, or down to (2,3). From (2,3) can go down to (3,3) which is removed. So (1,3) can go to (1,2), then to (1,1) which is removed, or to (2,3) then to (3,3) which is removed. Wait, actually, from (1,3), can it go down to (2,3)? Yes, (2,3) is still there unless removed. In this case, we only removed (1,1), (2,2), and (3,3). So (2,3) is still present. So from (1,3) you can go down to (2,3), then down to (3,3) which is removed, so can't go further. Alternatively, from (2,3) left to (2,2) which is removed. So (1,3) is connected to (2,3), but (2,3) can't go anywhere else except back. So (1,3) is in a component with (2,3), and (3,1) is isolated. Therefore, they are disconnected. So that is another minimal cut: { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }.\n\nSimilarly, by symmetry, we can have { (1,3), (2,2), (3,1) }, but we can't remove (1,3) or (3,1). So that's invalid.\n\nAnother symmetry: if we remove the diagonal from (1,1) to (3,3) except the corners, but since we can't remove the corners, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there are cuts that are not along a column or a diagonal.\n\nFor instance, removing (1,2), (2,1), and (2,3):\n\nEarlier, we saw that this disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). Let me confirm again:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. So can't go that way. Right to (3,2), then up to (2,2). From (2,2), can go right to (2,3) which is removed, or up to (1,2) which is removed. So stuck. So (3,1) can't reach anywhere else. Meanwhile, (1,3) can go left to (1,2) which is removed, or down to (2,3) which is removed. Wait, (1,3) can only go down to (2,3), which is removed, so (1,3) is isolated? Wait no, (1,3) can also go left to (1,2), but that's removed. So (1,3) is only connected to (2,3), which is removed. Wait, so (1,3) is also isolated? Then both (3,1) and (1,3) are isolated, but they're in separate components? Wait, actually, if we remove (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), then:\n\n- (3,1) is connected only to (3,2) and (2,1), but both (2,1) and (3,2)'s connections might be limited.\n\nWait, let's map the entire remaining graph:\n\nAfter removing (1,2), (2,1), (2,3):\n\nRemaining nodes:\n\n(1,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3).\n\nEdges:\n\n(1,1) is connected to (2,1) [removed], so only connected to nothing? Wait, (1,1) can go down to (2,1) which is removed, so (1,1) is isolated.\n\n(1,3) can go down to (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(2,2) is connected to (1,2) [removed], (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], and (3,2). So (2,2) is connected only to (3,2).\n\n(3,1) is connected to (3,2) and (2,1) [removed], so only to (3,2).\n\n(3,2) is connected to (3,1), (2,2), and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) is connected to (3,2) and (2,3) [removed], so only to (3,2).\n\nTherefore, the connected components are:\n\n- (1,1) alone.\n\n- (1,3) alone.\n\n- (2,2) connected to (3,2) and (3,3).\n\n- (3,1) connected to (3,2).\n\nTherefore, (3,1) is in the same component as (2,2), (3,2), (3,3), and (1,3) is alone. So (3,1) and (1,3) are in separate components. Therefore, yes, this is a vertex cut of size 3. So that's another one.\n\nSo far, I have found several minimal vertex cuts:\n\n1. Column 2: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }\n\n2. The diagonal corners and center: { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }\n\n3. The \"cross\" of the center and the two side nodes: { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) }\n\n4. Another symmetric one perhaps? For instance, removing (1,3)'s adjacent nodes? But (1,3) must stay. Alternatively, removing (3,1)'s adjacent nodes? But (3,1) must stay. Alternatively, removing (2,2), (1,1), and (3,3) as in the diagonal.\n\nWait, let me see if there are more. Let's try removing (1,1), (2,2), and (3,2):\n\nRemoving those three:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1) which is removed. Alternatively, right to (3,2) which is removed. So stuck. From (3,1) only path is blocked. \n\nFrom (1,3):\n\n- Can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2) which is removed, or down to (2,3). From (2,3), can go down to (3,3). So path: (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3). So (1,3) is connected to (2,3) and (3,3). Meanwhile, (3,1) is isolated. So they are disconnected. Therefore, { (1,1), (2,2), (3,2) } is another minimal vertex cut.\n\nSimilarly, by symmetry, removing { (1,3), (2,2), (3,1) } is invalid because we can't remove (3,1) or (1,3). But if we remove { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }, which we already have.\n\nAlternatively, removing (1,2), (2,2), and (3,1)'s neighbor (3,2):\n\nWait, that's the first example: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }.\n\nAlternatively, removing (2,1), (2,2), and (3,3):\n\nLet me check that:\n\nRemoving (2,1), (2,2), (3,3):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. Right to (3,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed. So stuck. \n\nFrom (1,3):\n\n- Can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2) which is removed, or down to (2,3). From (2,3), can go down to (3,3) which is removed, so stuck. So (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (2,3). But (1,2) can go down to (2,2) which is removed, so (1,2) is only connected to (1,3). (2,3) can go left to (2,2) which is removed or down to (3,3) which is removed. So (2,3) is only connected to (1,3). Therefore, (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (2,3), but those are only connected to (1,3). Meanwhile, (3,1) is isolated. So they are disconnected. Thus, { (2,1), (2,2), (3,3) } is another minimal vertex cut.\n\nSo this is getting a bit extensive, but perhaps we can categorize these minimal vertex cuts.\n\nIt seems that there are multiple types of minimal vertex cuts:\n\nType 1: The entire column 2: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }\n\nType 2: The diagonal from (1,1) to (3,3) through the center: { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }\n\nType 3: The \"cross\" involving the center and the two side nodes on the top and bottom: Wait, actually, the earlier example { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) } is different.\n\nAlternatively, Type 3: The \"L\" shape or something else.\n\nAlternatively, Type 4: Removing a corner, the center, and another node.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to list all minimal vertex cuts we can find.\n\nLet me attempt to list all possible minimal vertex cuts (size 3) by considering different cases:\n\nCase 1: Removing the entire column 2 (three nodes). This is one minimal cut.\n\nCase 2: Removing three nodes forming a diagonal from corner to corner through the center: { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }, and similarly { (1,3), (2,2), (3,1) } but the latter is invalid because (3,1) and (1,3) must stay. So only one such diagonal cut.\n\nCase 3: Removing the center node (2,2) plus two nodes that block the horizontal and vertical pathways. For instance:\n\n- Removing (2,2), (1,2), and (3,2) [which is column 2], already counted in Case 1.\n\n- Removing (2,2), (2,1), and (2,3) [middle row], but removing the middle row disconnects? Wait, let's check:\n\nRemoving (2,1), (2,2), (2,3):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. So can't go that way. Right to (3,2), then up to (2,2) which is removed. So stuck. From (3,1) can only go to (3,2), but from there can't proceed. Meanwhile, (1,3) can go down to (2,3) which is removed, so stuck. Wait, actually, (1,3) can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2) which is removed, or down to (2,3) which is removed. So (1,3) is isolated. (3,1) is also isolated? Wait, (3,1) can go to (3,2), which is still present. (3,2) can go up to (2,2) which is removed, so (3,2) is only connected to (3,1). So (3,1) and (3,2) form a component, and (1,3) is alone. So they are disconnected. Therefore, removing the middle row is also a vertex cut. So { (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) } is another minimal cut.\n\nAh, so that's another type: removing the entire middle row (three nodes). So that's another minimal cut.\n\nSimilarly, by symmetry, removing the middle column (column 2) is one, removing the middle row is another.\n\nSo Case 4: Removing the middle row: { (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) }\n\nSimilarly, removing the middle column: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) } as in Case 1.\n\nAdditionally, we had the diagonal cut: { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) } as Case 2.\n\nThen we had the \"cross\" cuts like { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) }, which is different.\n\nLet me see if that can be generalized. For instance, removing the center (2,2) and one node from the top row and one from the bottom row, but not in the same column.\n\nWait, { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) } removes the center and the top-center and the middle-left and middle-right? Wait no: (2,1) and (2,3) are on the middle row, not the top row. Wait, (1,2) is top-center, (2,1) is middle-left, (2,3) is middle-right. So this is removing the top-center, and the two middle-edge nodes. This seems to block all paths.\n\nAlternatively, another similar cut could be removing the bottom-center and the two middle-edge nodes? Let's see:\n\nRemoving (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). Let's check:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed. Right to (3,2) which is removed. So stuck. \n\nFrom (1,3):\n\n- Can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2). From (2,2), can go left to (2,1) which is removed or right to (2,3) which is removed. So (2,2) is only connected to (1,2) and (3,2), but those are removed except (1,2). Wait, (2,2) can go up to (1,2) and down to (3,2), both of which are removed except (1,2) is still there? Wait:\n\nWait, if we removed (3,2), (2,1), (2,3):\n\nThen remaining nodes:\n\n(1,1), (1,2), (1,3),\n\n(2,2),\n\n(3,1), (3,3).\n\nEdges:\n\n(1,1) connected to (2,1) [removed], so only connected to nothing? Wait, (1,1) can go down to (2,1) which is removed, so isolated.\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1) [isolated], (1,3), and (2,2).\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3) [removed], so only to (1,2).\n\n(2,2) connected to (1,2), (3,2) [removed], and (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed]. So (2,2) is only connected to (1,2).\n\n(3,1) connected to (2,1) [removed] and (3,2) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\nTherefore, components:\n\n- (1,1) isolated.\n\n- (1,2) connected to (1,3) and (2,2).\n\n- (3,1) isolated.\n\n- (3,3) isolated.\n\nSo (3,1) is isolated, and (1,3) is in the same component as (1,2) and (2,2). Therefore, disconnected. So { (3,2), (2,1), (2,3) } is also a minimal vertex cut.\n\nSimilarly, by symmetry, removing the top-center, middle-left, and middle-right: { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) }, which we already had, and removing the bottom-center, middle-left, middle-right: { (3,2), (2,1), (2,3) }, and similarly removing the top-center, middle-left, and middle-right? Wait, actually, the first one was top-center, middle-left, middle-right, and the second is bottom-center, middle-left, middle-right.\n\nTherefore, that gives us two more minimal cuts of this type.\n\nSimilarly, what about removing the top-left, center, and bottom-right? Wait, like { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }, which is the diagonal we already had.\n\nAlternatively, removing top-right, center, and bottom-left? But we can't remove the top-right or bottom-left since they're the start and finish.\n\nAlternatively, removing top-center, center, and bottom-center: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }, which is the column 2.\n\nSo summarizing, so far I have identified the following distinct minimal vertex cuts of size 3:\n\n1. Column 2: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }\n\n2. Middle row: { (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) }\n\n3. Diagonal from (1,1) to (3,3): { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }\n\n4. Cross of top-center, middle-left, middle-right: { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) }\n\n5. Cross of bottom-center, middle-left, middle-right: { (3,2), (2,1), (2,3) }\n\nAdditionally, are there others?\n\nLet me try removing (1,1), (2,3), and (3,2):\n\nRemoving those three:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1), then to (1,1) which is removed. Alternatively, right to (3,2) which is removed. So stuck.\n\nFrom (1,3):\n\n- Can go left to (1,2), then down to (2,2). From (2,2), can go right to (2,3) which is removed, or down to (3,2) which is removed. So (2,2) is only connected to (1,2). From (1,2), can go to (1,1) which is removed or (1,3). So (1,3) is connected to (1,2), which is connected to nothing else except (1,3). Meanwhile, (3,1) is stuck. So they are disconnected. So this is another minimal cut: { (1,1), (2,3), (3,2) }.\n\nSimilarly, by symmetry, removing (1,3), (2,1), (3,2) is invalid because we can't remove (1,3) or (3,1). But removing (1,3)'s counterpart: maybe { (1,3)'s left neighbor, center, and bottom-right? Not sure, but let's see another one.\n\nRemoving (1,1), (2,2), a