f subsets that leave at least one path intact.\n\nTo do this, perhaps we can identify all the minimal paths and ensure that our subset doesn't block all of them.\n\nBut again, this is tricky.\n\nAlternatively, since we know the minimal vertex cut is 3, then any vertex cut of size 4 must contain a minimal vertex cut. Therefore, the number of vertex cuts of size 4 is equal to the sum over all minimal vertex cuts C of size 3 of (number of supersets of C of size 4). However, this counts each vertex cut of size 4 exactly as many times as the number of minimal vertex cuts it contains. Therefore, if we let m be the number of minimal vertex cuts of size 3, and for each vertex cut D of size 4, let k_D be the number of minimal vertex cuts contained in D, then the total count would be sum_{D} k_D = sum_{C} (7 - 3) = m * 4. But this counts each D with multiplicity k_D. Therefore, to get the actual number of vertex cuts of size 4, we would need to divide by the average k_D, but this is not straightforward unless all D have the same k_D, which they likely don't.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to accept that this is too complex and instead try to enumerate all vertex cuts of size 4 by considering the different types of minimal cuts we identified earlier and their supersets.\n\nEarlier, I identified several types of minimal vertex cuts of size 3. Let me try to list them all systematically.\n\nFirst, let me list all minimal vertex cuts of size 3 that I can find:\n\n1. Column 2: { (1,2), (2,2), (3,2) }\n\n2. Middle row: { (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) }\n\n3. Diagonal from (1,1) to (3,3): { (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) }\n\n4. Cross of top-center, middle-left, middle-right: { (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) }\n\n5. Cross of bottom-center, middle-left, middle-right: { (3,2), (2,1), (2,3) }\n\n6. Cross of top-center, middle-left, bottom-right: { (1,2), (2,1), (3,3) }\n\nWait, let's check this last one:\n\n{ (1,2), (2,1), (3,3) }\n\nRemoving these three:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- Can go up to (2,1) which is removed.