rnatively, can we go from (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) [blocked] -> so that path is blocked.\n\nAlternatively, (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). Is that path blocked? Let's see: (3,2) is still present (we only removed (2,2) and (1,2)), so (3,2) is okay. Then (3,3) is present, (2,3) is present, and (1,3) is present. Therefore, that path is still open: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). So that path exists. Therefore, removing (2,2) and (1,2) is not enough.\n\nHmm, so that didn't work. Let me try another pair.\n\nSuppose we remove (2,1) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nThen, starting at (3,1), can we go up to (2,1)? No, because (2,1) is removed. So from (3,1), the only move is right to (3,2). Then from (3,2), you can go up to (2,2), then from (2,2) you can go left to (2,1) [blocked] or right to (2,3) [blocked], or up to (1,2). From (2,2) to (1,2) is allowed. Then from (1,2), you can go left to (1,1) or right to (1,3). So from (1,2), moving right to (1,3) is allowed. Therefore, the path is: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). So that path still exists. Therefore, removing (2,1) and (2,3) is insufficient.\n\nHmm. Let's try removing two central nodes. How about (2,2) and (1,1)?\n\nSo remove (2,2) and (1,1). Let's see if that disconnects (3,1) from (1,3).\n\nPath 1: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) [blocked] -> can't go that way.\n\nAlternative path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) [blocked] -> can't go that way.\n\nAlternative path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). Is that path blocked?\n\n(3,2) is still there, (3,3) is there, (2,3) is there, and (1,3) is there. So that path is still open. Therefore, that path exists. So removing (2,2) and (1,1) still leaves a path.\n\nHmm. Maybe another pair. How about (1,2) and (2,2)?\n\nRemoving (1,2) and (2,2). Let's see:\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\nCan go to (2,1) -> (1,1) -> then from (1,1), can go to (1,2) [blocked], or down to (2,1). So stuck there.\n\nAlternatively, (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) [blocked] -> so can't go that way.\n\nAlterna