f size 2?\n\nWait, let me check again:\n\nIf we remove (1,2) and (2,3), then:\n\nIs there any path from (3,1) to (1,3)?\n\nLet me try to trace:\n\nStarting at (3,1):\n\nMove right to (3,2). From (3,2), can go up to (2,2) or left to (3,1). Let's go up to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go left to (2,1), right to (2,3) [blocked], or up to (1,2) [blocked]. So from (2,2), can go left to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go up to (1,1) or down to (3,1) [already been there].\n\nFrom (1,1), can go right to (1,2) [blocked] or down to (2,1). So stuck there.\n\nAlternatively, from (3,2), go to (3,3). From (3,3), can go up to (2,3) [blocked] or left to (3,2). So stuck.\n\nAlternatively, from (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3). From (3,3), no way up because (2,3) is blocked. So no path to (1,3).\n\nTherefore, removing (1,2) and (2,3) does disconnect (3,1) from (1,3). So that is a vertex cut of size 2!\n\nWait a second! So I must have made a mistake earlier. Let me verify again.\n\nIf we remove nodes (1,2) and (2,3):\n\nPath from (3,1) to (1,3):\n\n- The direct paths that go through (1,2) or (2,3) are blocked. But are there any alternative paths?\n\nAlternative path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) [blocked] -> no.\n\nAnother alternative: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) [blocked] -> no.\n\nAnother alternative: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) [blocked] -> no.\n\nAnother alternative: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) [blocked] -> no.\n\nAlternatively, (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) [blocked]. All paths go through either (1,2) or (2,3), which are removed. Therefore, yes, removing those two nodes does disconnect (3,1) from (1,3). Therefore, the minimal vertex cut size is 2.\n\nWait, that contradicts my earlier thought that removing two nodes wasn't sufficient, but apparently it is sufficient if you remove (1,2) and (2,3). So the minimal vertex cut size is 2.\n\nTherefore, there exist vertex cuts of size 2. Therefore, to achieve disconnection, we can remove two nodes, but in our problem, we have to remove four nodes. So, in our problem, since we must remove four nodes, we can think of it as: the set of excluded nodes must include a vertex cut (of size 2) plus two other nodes, or perhaps include a larger vertex cut.\n\nBut actually, since we have to remove exactly four nodes, the excluded set must be a superset of some vertex cut, but not necessarily minimal. However, the key point is that in order for the induced subgraph to be disconnected between (3,1) and (1,3), the excluded set must intersect every path from (3,1) to (1,3). So, the excluded set must form a vertex cut (i.e., a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3)), and its size is exactly four.\n\nTherefore, the number N we are seeking (the number of subsets S where the induced subgraph has a path) is equal to the number of 5-node subsets containing (3,1) and (1,3) such that the excluded nodes (the four nodes not in S) form a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3). Wait, no, actually, the induced subgraph has a path from (3,1) to (1,3) if and only if the excluded nodes do NOT form a vertex cut between those two nodes. Because if the excluded nodes do form a vertex cut, then the induced subgraph has no path. Conversely, if the excluded nodes do NOT form a vertex cut, then there exists some path in the induced subgraph.\n\nTherefore, the number N (subsets where there IS a path) is equal to the number of subsets S (of size 5 containing (3,1) and (1,3)) such that the excluded nodes (the four nodes not in S) do NOT form a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3). Therefore, to compute N, we need to compute total subsets minus the number of subsets where the excluded nodes DO form a vertex cut. Wait, actually, let me rephrase:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet U be the set of all subsets S of size 5 containing (3,1) and (1,3). |U| = C(7,3) = 35.\n\nFor each such subset S, let E_S be the set of excluded nodes (the four nodes not in S). Then, the induced subgraph on S has a path from (3,1) to (1,3) if and only if E_S is NOT a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3). Therefore, the number N (subsets with a path) is equal to |U| minus the number of subsets S where E_S is a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3).\n\nTherefore, if we can compute the number of subsets S where the excluded nodes E_S form a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3), then N = 35 - that number.\n\nTherefore, our task reduces to computing the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (3,1) and (1,3), because E_S must be a vertex cut of size 4 (since E_S has size 4), and then the number of such subsets S is equal to the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (3,1) and (1,3). Because each vertex cut of size 4 corresponds to exactly one subset S (the complement of that vertex cut, plus the two required nodes). Wait, actually, no: a vertex cut of size k corresponds to a set E of size k that is a vertex cut. Since in our case, we need E_S (the excluded nodes) to be a vertex cut of size 4. So the number of such subsets S is exactly equal to the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (3,1) and (1,3). Because each vertex cut of size 4 is a unique set E of size 4 that is a vertex cut, and then S is the complement of E in the original 9 nodes, but since we must include (3,1) and (1,3), actually, the vertex cut E must not include (3,1) or (1,3), because those are required to be in S. Since vertex cuts are about separating the two nodes, they can't include the two nodes themselves, because if you include (3,1) in the excluded set, then (3,1) wouldn't be in S, which is required. Similarly for (1,3). Therefore, vertex cuts between (3,1) and (1,3) cannot include either of those two nodes. Therefore, all vertex cuts between (3,1) and (1,3) are subsets of the remaining 7 nodes. Therefore, the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (3,1) and (1,3) is exactly the number of subsets E of size 4 from the 7 nodes (excluding (3,1) and (1,3)) such that E is a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3). Therefore, the number we need is exactly that number, and then N = 35 - that number.\n\nTherefore, to solve the problem, we need to compute the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (3,1) and (1,3) in the original graph. Then subtract that number from 35 to get the answer.\n\nTherefore, now the problem reduces to: how many vertex cuts of size 4 are there between (3,1) and (1,3) in the 3x3 grid graph?\n\nTo compute this, perhaps we can enumerate all possible 4-node subsets from the 7 available nodes (excluding (3,1) and (1,3)), and check which of them are vertex cuts.\n\nSince there are C(7,4) = 35 such subsets, which is the same as the total number of subsets S, which is a bit circular, but perhaps manageable.\n\nAlternatively, since we know that a vertex cut of size 4 must contain all minimal vertex cuts? Not necessarily, but perhaps we can use inclusion.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to think in terms of the required conditions for a set E of four nodes (from the seven) to be a vertex cut between (3,1) and (1,3). That is, every path from (3,1) to (1,3) in the original grid must pass through at least one node in E.\n\nTherefore, we need to count all 4-element subsets E of the seven nodes (excluding (3,1) and (1,3)) such that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) in the original grid contains at least one node from E.\n\nSo, to find such E, we can consider the following approach:\n\nFirst, note that in the original grid, all paths from (3,1) to (1,3) must pass through certain critical nodes or regions.\n\nEarlier, we found that a vertex cut of size 2 exists, for example, the set { (1,2), (2,3) }, so any vertex cut must include at least one node from every path.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can characterize all such E.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the grid and identify which nodes are \"essential\" for connecting (3,1) and (1,3). For instance, if we remove certain nodes, we block all paths.\n\nGiven that a vertex cut of size 2 is possible, then any vertex cut of size 4 must include that size 2 cut plus two more nodes. But actually, not necessarily; a vertex cut of size 4 could be a different set that also blocks all paths.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the grid and see which nodes are on all paths.\n\nWait, but actually, in the grid, there are multiple paths, so no single node is on all paths. However, certain nodes are on many paths.\n\nFor instance, the center node (2,2) is on some paths, but not all. For example, the path going through the top row: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) does not go through (2,2). Similarly, the path going through the right column: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3) also does not go through (2,2). However, the path (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3) does go through (2,2). So (2,2) is on some paths but not all.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, the vertex cut must intersect every path, so it must include at least one node from each path.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can model this as a hitting set problem: find all 4-element hitting sets for the family of all paths from (3,1) to (1,3). Since the hitting set must hit every path, i.e., intersect every path.\n\nGiven that there are multiple paths, we need to find all 4-element subsets of the seven nodes that intersect every path.\n\nThis might be tedious, but since the grid is small, perhaps we can do it.\n\nFirst, let me list all the paths from (3,1) to (1,3). Since the grid is small, perhaps enumerating all paths is feasible.\n\nFirst, note that any path from (3,1) to (1,3) must consist of exactly two moves up and two moves right, in some order, but with the constraint of the grid boundaries.\n\nWait, actually, in terms of steps, since we need to go from row 3 to row 1 (a difference of 2) and from column 1 to column 3 (a difference of 2), so in total, 4 steps: two ups and two rights, in some order. However, the path must stay within the grid, so we can't go beyond the grid boundaries.\n\nTherefore, the number of minimal paths is equal to the number of ways to arrange 2 ups and 2 rights in a sequence of 4 moves, without going outside the grid. However, some sequences may go outside the grid, so we have to subtract those.\n\nAlternatively, since it's a small grid, let me enumerate all minimal paths:\n\nLet me denote a path as a sequence of nodes starting at (3,1) and ending at (1,3).\n\nEach step is either up (U) or right (R), but we have to stay within the grid.\n\nLet me attempt to enumerate all such paths.\n\nStarting at (3,1):\n\nFirst move options:\n\n1. Right to (3,2)\n\n2. Up to (2,1)\n\nCase 1: First move Right to (3,2)\n\nFrom (3,2):\n\nNext moves:\n\na. Right to (3,3)\n\nb. Up to (2,2)\n\nCase 1a: (3,2) -> (3,3)\n\nFrom (3,3):\n\nNext moves:\n\nc. Up to (2,3)\n\nd. Left to (3,2) [backtrack, but we can consider paths without backtracking?]\n\nAssuming we don't backtrack, so from (3,3):\n\nOnly possible move up to (2,3)\n\nFrom (2,3):\n\nNext moves:\n\ne. Up to (1,3) [success!]\n\nOr left to (2,2)\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). That's one path.\n\nAlternatively, from (2,3), instead of going up, go left to (2,2), then from (2,2):\n\nf. Up to (1,2)\n\ng. Left to (2,1)\n\nh. Right to (2,3) [backtrack]\n\nAssuming no backtracking:\n\nFrom (2,2):\n\nUp to (1,2):\n\nThen from (1,2):\n\ni. Right to (1,3)\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). That's another path.\n\nAlternatively, from (2,2) left to (2,1):\n\nThen from (2,1):\n\nj. Up to (1,1)\n\nk. Right to (2,2) [backtrack]\n\nAssuming no backtracking:\n\nFrom (2,1) -> (1,1):\n\nThen from (1,1):\n\nl. Right to (1,2)\n\nm. Down to (2,1) [backtrack]\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (2,2) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). That's a longer path, but still valid.\n\nBut since we are considering all paths, including non-minimal ones, this complicates things, because there are infinitely many paths if we allow backtracking, but since we are in a finite grid, actually, the number of paths is finite but quite a few.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider all possible paths, both minimal and non-minimal, but that might be too time-consuming. Alternatively, since we are dealing with vertex cuts, which only need to intersect every path, regardless of length, we need to ensure that every possible path, of any length, from (3,1) to (1,3) must pass through at least one node in E.\n\nGiven that, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the grid's structure and identify nodes that lie on all possible paths or something like that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of necessary nodes. For instance, certain nodes are necessary to block all paths.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that to block all paths from (3,1) to (1,3), the excluded set E must include at least one node from every possible path.\n\nTherefore, E must hit every path. So, equivalently, E is a hitting set for the family of all paths from (3,1) to (1,3).\n\nWe need to count all 4-element hitting sets for this family.\n\nGiven that the hitting set problem is NP-hard in general, but since the instance is small, perhaps manageable.\n\nFirst, let me try to characterize the paths.\n\nFirst, let me note that all paths must go from column 1 to column 3, so they must pass through columns 1, 2, and 3 in some order. Similarly, they must go from row 3 to row 1, so they must pass through rows 3, 2, and 1 in some order.\n\nTherefore, any path must traverse through the central column (column 2) or the central row (row 2), or both? Not necessarily, but let's see.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there are paths that avoid certain nodes.\n\nFor example, is there a path from (3,1) to (1,3) that avoids the center node (2,2)?\n\nYes, as we saw earlier: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path avoids (2,2).\n\nAnother path: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This also avoids (2,2).\n\nA path that goes through (2,2): (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). This goes through (2,2).\n\nTherefore, (2,2) is on some paths but not all.\n\nSimilarly, the node (1,2) is on some paths but not all. For example, the path (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3) does not go through (1,2).\n\nSimilarly, (2,3) is on some paths but not all: the path (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) does not go through (2,3).\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, the hitting set E must include at least one node from each \"branch\" of the paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can partition the paths into groups that share common nodes, and then ensure that for each group, at least one node is excluded.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to find all the minimal hitting sets and then build up.\n\nFirst, since we know that a minimal vertex cut of size 2 exists, like { (1,2), (2,3) }, then any hitting set of size 4 must include this set plus two more nodes, or other combinations.\n\nBut there may be other minimal hitting sets of size 2, such as { (2,2), (1,3) } but (1,3) can't be excluded. Wait, no, (1,3) must be included in S, so cannot be in E. Similarly, (3,1) must be included, so cannot be in E. So the minimal vertex cuts are subsets of the remaining 7 nodes.\n\nWe already found one minimal vertex cut of size 2: { (1,2), (2,3) }.\n\nAre there others?\n\nLet me see:\n\nAnother possible size 2 vertex cut: { (1,1), (2,3) }.\n\nCheck if this is a vertex cut:\n\nPaths:\n\n1. The path going through the top row: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path goes through (1,1), so if (1,1) is excluded, then this path is blocked.\n\n2. The path going through the right column: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path goes through (2,3), so if (2,3) is excluded, this path is blocked.\n\n3. The path through the center: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path does not go through (1,1) or (2,3), so it would still exist if those two are excluded. Therefore, the path (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3) is still present. Therefore, { (1,1), (2,3) } is not a vertex cut.\n\nSimilarly, trying { (2,1), (2,3) }:\n\nCheck if this blocks all paths.\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) is blocked, but alternative path: (3,1)->(3,2)->...->(1,3). For example, (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path goes through (2,3), which is excluded, so blocked. Another path: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (2,1) or (2,3). Therefore, it's still present. So { (2,1), (2,3) } is not a vertex cut.\n\nHow about { (3,2), (2,2) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2) is blocked, so can't go that way.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (3,2) or (2,2), so it's still open.\n\nThus, not a vertex cut.\n\nHow about { (1,2), (3,2) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2) is blocked, so can't go that way.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path goes through (1,2), which is excluded, so blocked.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). Also blocked at (1,2).\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path is blocked at (3,2).\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3). Blocked at (3,2).\n\nPath 6: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path goes through (2,3), which is not excluded, so it's still there. From (2,2) to (2,3) is allowed, then to (1,3). So this path exists. Therefore, { (1,2), (3,2) } is not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. How about { (1,2), (2,1) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) blocked, so can't go that way.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (1,2) or (2,1), so it's still open.\n\nTherefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nAnother candidate: { (2,2), (1,3) }, but (1,3) can't be excluded.\n\nHow about { (2,2), (3,3) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3) blocked at (3,3).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path goes through (2,2), which is excluded, so blocked.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (2,2) or (3,3), so it's still there. Therefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm, this is tedious. Let me think differently.\n\nWe already found one minimal vertex cut of size 2: { (1,2), (2,3) }.\n\nAre there others?\n\nLet me try { (1,1), (2,3) } as before, but saw that it didn't work.\n\nWait, what about { (1,1), (3,3) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3) blocked at (3,3).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). Blocked at (1,1).\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (1,1) or (3,3), so it's still there.\n\nTherefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHow about { (2,1), (1,3) } but (1,3) can't be excluded.\n\nWait, perhaps another size 2 vertex cut: { (2,2), (1,1) }.\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1) blocked at (1,1).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). Blocked at (2,2).\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (2,2) or (1,1), so it's still there. Therefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Maybe { (1,2), (2,2) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at (2,2).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). Blocked at (1,2).\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (1,2) or (2,2), so it's still there. Therefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Let me try { (3,2), (2,3) }.\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at (3,2).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (3,2) or (2,3), so it's still there.\n\nTherefore, nope.\n\nWait, how about { (2,3), (3,2) }?\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). Still open.\n\nTherefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Let's see another candidate: { (1,2), (2,1) }.\n\nWait, already tried that. Not a vertex cut.\n\nWait a minute, maybe the only size 2 vertex cut is { (1,2), (2,3) }?\n\nLet me see if there are others.\n\nHow about { (1,2), (3,3) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3) blocked at (3,3).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). Blocked at (1,2).\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). Blocked at (1,2).\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (1,2) or (3,3), so it's still there. Therefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. What about { (2,2), (3,3) }? Already checked, nope.\n\nHow about { (1,1), (3,3) }? Also checked, nope.\n\nHmm. Maybe { (2,2), (1,3) } but (1,3) is excluded, can't be.\n\nAlternatively, { (2,2), (3,2) }?\n\nCheck:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through (2,2) or (3,2), so it's still there.\n\nThus, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Maybe only one size 2 vertex cut exists: { (1,2), (2,3) }.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider { (1,2), (2,2) }.\n\nWait, already checked that path through (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3) is still open.\n\nHmm. Let me try { (1,2), (2,1) } again.\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) blocked.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). Open.\n\nTherefore, no.\n\nAlternatively, { (2,3), (3,2) }.\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). Open.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, perhaps there is another size 2 vertex cut: { (1,1), (2,3) } but we saw that path through (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3) is still open.\n\nAlternatively, maybe { (1,2), (2,3) } is the only size 2 vertex cut.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of another approach. Let's consider that in order to block all paths, we need to block all possible routes.\n\nThe two main routes are:\n\n1. The top route: going up along the left edge then right on the top row.\n\n2. The right route: going right along the bottom row then up on the rightmost column.\n\n3. The central route: going through the center.\n\nTo block all three, we need to block at least one node from each of these routes.\n\nThe top route: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3). To block this, we can block any of the nodes (2,1), (1,1), (1,2).\n\nThe right route: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3). To block this, we can block any of the nodes (3,2), (3,3), (2,3).\n\nThe central route: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3). To block this, we can block any of the nodes (3,2), (2,2), (1,2).\n\nTherefore, to block all three routes, we need to choose