ny paths.\n\nThe node (1,2) [B] is on several paths: the top route, the central route, and the path going through the top-right.\n\nThe node (2,3) [E] is on the right route and the central route.\n\nThe node (3,2) [F] is on the right route and the central route.\n\nThe node (2,2) [D] is on some paths but not all.\n\nThe nodes on the top route: A, B, C.\n\nThe nodes on the right route: F, G, E.\n\nThe nodes on the central route: F, D, B.\n\nAdditionally, there are longer paths that weave through the grid.\n\nTo block all paths, E must intersect each of these routes.\n\nFor instance, to block the top route (A-B), E must include A or B or some node on that path.\n\nSimilarly for the right route (F-G-E), and the central route (F-D-B).\n\nTherefore, to block all three main routes, E must have at least one node from each of these three routes.\n\nAdditionally, it must also block any \"diagonal\" paths that might bypass these.\n\nBut let's see:\n\nIf E contains one node from the top route (A or B or C), one node from the right route (F, G, E), and one node from the central route (F, D, B), then perhaps that would block all paths.\n\nBut since we need E to be size 4, perhaps we can require that E contains at least one node from each of the three main routes (top, right, central), and then the fourth node can be anywhere.\n\nAlternatively, let's see:\n\nFirst, the top route nodes: A, B, C.\n\nThe right route nodes: F, G, E.\n\nThe central route nodes: F, D, B.\n\nAdditionally, there are other nodes like D and G that are on some paths.\n\nLet me consider that to block all paths, E must have at least one node from the top route (A/B/C), at least one from the right route (F/G/E), and at least one from the central route (F/D/B).\n\nBut note that some nodes are shared between routes, like B is in both the top and central routes, and F is in both the right and central routes.\n\nTherefore, to cover all three routes, perhaps E needs to include:\n\n- At least one node from {A, B, C} (top),\n\n- At least one node from {F, G, E} (right),\n\n- At least one node from {F, D, B} (central),\n\nBut since some nodes are in multiple categories, this can be achieved with fewer nodes.\n\nFor example, if E includes B and E and F, that covers all three routes (B covers top and central, E covers right, F covers central and right). So that's three nodes, and the fourth can be any.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of it as needing to cover the three \"directions\": left-top, right-bottom, and central.\n\nBut perhaps it's better to proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's note that the minimal vertex cut of size 2 is {B, E} (which are nodes (1,2) and (2,3)). This set blocks:\n\n- The top route at B,\n\n- The right route at E,\n\n- The central route at B and E.\n\nHowever, as we saw earlier, there is still a path going through F and D: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) is blocked at B, but (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3) is blocked at E. Wait, actually, if we have {B, E}, then the path (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3) goes through E (which is excluded), so that path is blocked. Wait, (2,3) is included in the path, but E is (2,3). Wait, E is node (2,3), so if E is excluded, then (2,3) is not in the induced subgraph. Therefore, that path cannot go through (2,3). So the path (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3) is blocked at (2,3) (which is E, excluded). Therefore, actually, {B, E} does block all paths. Wait, earlier I thought there was a path through F and D, but if E is excluded, then (2,3) is gone, so that path cannot proceed to (1,3) from (2,3). Let me verify again:\n\nIf we have E = {B, E} (i.e., nodes (1,2) and (2,3) are excluded), then in the induced subgraph:\n\nNodes remaining: all except (1,2) and (2,3).\n\nCan we go from (3,1) to (1,3)?\n\nLet's see:\n\nFrom (3,1), possible moves: up to (2,1) or right to (3,2).\n\nOption 1: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) [excluded], so stuck.\n\nOption 2: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) [excluded], so stuck.\n\nOption 3: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) [excluded], stuck.\n\nOption 4: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) [excluded], stuck.\n\nAlternatively, (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) [excluded], so no.\n\nTherefore, indeed, {B, E} blocks all paths. So my earlier mistake was thinking there was a path through (2,2) to (2,3), but (2,3) is excluded. Therefore, {B, E} is indeed a vertex cut of size 2.\n\nTherefore, returning, to form a vertex cut of size 4, we need to add two more nodes to this minimal cut, or find other minimal cuts.\n\nAlternatively, any vertex cut of size 4 must include at least one node from every path, so it must include nodes that cover all possible paths.\n\nGiven that the minimal vertex cut is size 2, any larger vertex cut must include that minimal cut plus additional nodes, or other combinations that still cover all paths.\n\nTherefore, perhaps all vertex cuts of size 4 are supersets of some minimal vertex cut.\n\nAssuming that, then the number of vertex cuts of size 4 would be equal to the number of supersets of each minimal vertex cut of size 2, plus other vertex cuts not containing those minimal cuts.\n\nBut if the only minimal vertex cut is {B, E}, then all vertex cuts of size 4 must include {B, E} plus two more nodes, or other combinations that also form a vertex cut without including {B, E}.\n\nBut since we need to verify whether other minimal vertex cuts exist.\n\nSuppose there is another minimal vertex cut, say of size 2 not containing {B, E}. Let's try to find one.\n\nConsider the set {A, G} (nodes (1,1) and (3,3)).\n\nCheck if this is a vertex cut:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at A=(1,1).\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at G=(3,3).\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked by A or G.\n\nTherefore, this path remains, so {A, G} is not a vertex cut.\n\nHow about {C, G} (nodes (2,1) and (3,3)):\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) blocked at C.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3) blocked at G.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked.\n\nThus, not a vertex cut.\n\nHow about {D, F} (nodes (2,2) and (3,2)):\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at D.\n\nPath 6: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D and F (since F is (3,2), which is blocked).\n\nWait, (2,3) is still there. From (2,2) to (2,3) is allowed, then to (1,3). So path: (3,1)->(3,2) is blocked at F, so cannot go that way. Alternatively, (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) blocked at D, so cannot go that way. The only path left is (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3), which isn't blocked. So {D, F} is not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. How about {B, F} (nodes (1,2) and (3,2)):\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F (can't go to (3,2)), but can go from (2,2) to (2,3) then to (1,3). Since (2,3) is not blocked, this path exists: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) is blocked, but (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3) is possible. Wait:\n\nWait, (3,1) to (2,1) is allowed, then to (2,2), then to (2,3), then to (1,3). This path doesn't go through B or F. Therefore, {B, F} is not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm.\n\nHow about {B, D} (nodes (1,2) and (2,2)):\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) blocked at B and D.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): not blocked by B or D.\n\nTherefore, path 4 exists, so not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm.\n\nHow about {E, F} (nodes (2,3) and (3,2)):\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at F.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3) blocked at E.\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3) blocked at E.\n\nPath 6: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked.\n\nThus, paths 1 and 6 exist, so not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm.\n\nHow about {B, E, F} (nodes (1,2), (2,3), (3,2)):\n\nThis is a size 3 set. Does it block all paths?\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) blocked at B and F.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3) blocked at E.\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3) blocked at E.\n\nPath 6: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(2,2)->(2,3) blocked at E.\n\nPath 7: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) blocked at B.\n\nPath 8: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3) blocked at F and E.\n\nTherefore, all paths are blocked. So {B, E, F} is a vertex cut of size 3.\n\nSimilarly, adding another node to this set would give size 4 vertex cuts.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the only minimal vertex cuts are size 2 and size 3, and so on.\n\nGiven that, let's try to count all vertex cuts of size 4.\n\nApproach:\n\nWe can consider that any vertex cut of size 4 must include at least one of the minimal vertex cuts (size 2 or 3), or other combinations.\n\nBut perhaps it's better to consider that to form a vertex cut of size 4, we can start with the minimal vertex cut {B, E} and add any two more nodes from the remaining five nodes (since we have seven nodes total, minus the two in the minimal cut leaves five nodes: A, C, D, F, G).\n\nAlternatively, we can also have vertex cuts that don't include the minimal cut {B, E}, but still block all paths.\n\nHowever, since we couldn't find any other minimal vertex cuts of size 2, it's possible that all vertex cuts of size 4 must include the minimal cut {B, E} plus two more nodes, or other combinations that also block all paths without including {B, E}.\n\nBut since we couldn't find any other minimal vertex cuts of size 2, perhaps all vertex cuts of size 4 must include the minimal cut {B, E} plus two more nodes.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there are vertex cuts of size 4 that don't include {B, E} but still block all paths.\n\nLet me try to construct one.\n\nSuppose we take the set {A, C, E, G} (nodes (1,1), (2,1), (2,3), (3,3)).\n\nCheck if this blocks all paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) blocked at C.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3) blocked at G.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked by A, C, E, or G. So this path exists.\n\nThus, not a vertex cut.\n\nAnother try: {A, F, G, D} (nodes (1,1), (3,2), (3,3), (2,2)).\n\nCheck paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1) blocked at A.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F (can't go to (3,2)), but can go from (3,2) to (2,2) to (2,3) to (1,3). Wait:\n\nFrom (3,1)->(3,2) is blocked at F, so can't go that way.\n\nFrom (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked by A, F, G, D.\n\nThus, path exists. Not a vertex cut.\n\nAnother attempt: {C, E, F, G} (nodes (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3)).\n\nCheck paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) blocked at C.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): not blocked by C, E, F, G. So path exists through (2,2).\n\nTherefore, not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Let's try {B, D, F, G} (nodes (1,2), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3)).\n\nCheck paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F (can't go to (3,2)), but can go from (3,2) to (2,2) to (2,3) to (1,3). Wait:\n\nFrom (3,1)->(3,2) is blocked at F, so can't go that way.\n\nFrom (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nFrom (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nThe only remaining path is (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3), but (3,2) is blocked, so that path is blocked.\n\nWait, is there any other path?\n\n(3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D and B isn't on this path. Wait, (2,2) is blocked at D, so that path is blocked.\n\nSimilarly, (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) blocked at B.\n\nTherefore, all paths are blocked. So {B, D, F, G} is a vertex cut of size 4.\n\nThis set does not include the minimal cut {B, E}, so there are vertex cuts of size 4 that don't include the minimal size 2 cut.\n\nTherefore, there are vertex cuts of size 4 that are not supersets of the minimal size 2 cut.\n\nTherefore, we have to consider all possibilities.\n\nGiven that this is getting too time-consuming, perhaps we can instead note that the total number of vertex cuts of size 4 is equal to the number of 4-node subsets that include at least one node from every path.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps it's better to realize that this problem is likely expecting an answer that can be computed via complementary counting or symmetry, but I might have to proceed differently.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: since we need to block all paths from (3,1) to (1,3), we can consider that the frog's path must go through either the top row, the right column, or the central column/row.\n\nTo block all paths, we can block entry to the top row beyond (1,1), block the right column beyond (3,3), or something like that. But not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since this is a small grid, maybe the number of vertex cuts of size 4 can be calculated by considering that each vertex cut must include at least one node from each of the three main routes (top, right, central), and since we need four nodes, perhaps we can compute it combinatorially.\n\nLet me think of the three main routes:\n\n1. Top route: nodes (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). To block this route, we need to remove at least one node from { (2,1), (1,1), (1,2) }.\n\n2. Right route: nodes (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). To block this route, we need to remove at least one node from { (3,2), (3,3), (2,3) }.\n\n3. Central route: nodes (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). To block this route, we need to remove at least one node from { (3,2), (2,2), (1,2) }.\n\nAdditionally, there are other paths that weave through the grid, like going through (2,2) and (2,3), but if we block the three main routes, perhaps that's sufficient? Not sure, but let's assume for now that blocking the three main routes is sufficient.\n\nTherefore, to form a vertex cut of size 4, we need to choose nodes such that:\n\n- At least one from the top route's critical nodes: { (2,1), (1,1), (1,2) }.\n\n- At least one from the right route's critical nodes: { (3,2), (3,3), (2,3) }.\n\n- At least one from the central route's critical nodes: { (3,2), (2,2), (1,2) }.\n\nAdditionally, we have to choose four nodes in total from the seven nodes.\n\nLet me define the critical nodes for each route:\n\nTop route critical nodes: T = {C, A, B} (nodes (2,1), (1,1), (1,2)).\n\nRight route critical nodes: R = {F, G, E} (nodes (3,2), (3,3), (2,3)).\n\nCentral route critical nodes: CR = {F, D, B} (nodes (3,2), (2,2), (1,2)).\n\nNote that node F is in both R and CR, and node B is in both T and CR.\n\nOur goal is to choose four nodes such that:\n\n- At least one from T,\n\n- At least one from R,\n\n- At least one from CR,\n\nAnd the four nodes are selected from the seven nodes: A,B,C,D,E,F,G.\n\nAdditionally, since these sets overlap (e.g., B is in both T and CR, F is in R and CR), we have to account for overlaps.\n\nThis is a problem of counting the number of 4-element subsets of {A,B,C,D,E,F,G} that intersect each of T, R, and CR.\n\nThis is an inclusion-exclusion problem.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet U = {A,B,C,D,E,F,G} (7 elements).\n\nLet X = subsets of U of size 4 that intersect T, R, and CR.\n\nWe need |X|.\n\nTo compute |X|, we can use inclusion-exclusion over the complements.\n\nLet me define:\n\nFor each of the three sets T, R, CR, let’s define:\n\nA: subsets that do NOT intersect T.\n\nB: subsets that do NOT intersect R.\n\nC: subsets that do NOT intersect CR.\n\nWe want the total subsets of size 4 that do NOT have A or B or C, i.e., |U| - |A ∪ B ∪ C|.\n\nBy inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A ∩ B| - |A ∩ C| - |B ∩ C| + |A ∩ B ∩ C|.\n\nTherefore,\n\n|X| = |U| - [ |A| + |B| + |C| - |A ∩ B| - |A ∩ C| - |B ∩ C| + |A ∩ B ∩ C| ].\n\nFirst, compute |U| = C(7,4) = 35.\n\nNow compute |A|: subsets of size 4 that do NOT intersect T.\n\nT = {C, A, B} (nodes (2,1), (1,1), (1,2)).\n\nTherefore, subsets in A are those that contain none of {A,B,C}. Since T has three nodes, the subsets in A are subsets of the remaining nodes: U \\ T = {D, E, F, G} (four nodes).\n\nThus, |A| = C(4,4) = 1 (only the subset {D,E,F,G}).\n\nSimilarly, compute |B|: subsets of size 4 that do NOT intersect R.\n\nR = {F, G, E} (nodes (3,2), (3,3), (2,3)).\n\nThus, subsets in B must exclude R, so they are subsets of U \\ R = {A,B,C,D} (four nodes).\n\nTherefore, |B| = C(4,4) = 1 (only subset {A,B,C,D}).\n\nNext, |C|: subsets of size 4 that do NOT intersect CR.\n\nCR = {F, D, B} (nodes (3,2), (2,2), (1,2)).\n\nTherefore, subsets in C must exclude CR, so they are subsets of U \\ CR = {A,C,E,G} (four nodes: A,C,E,G).\n\nWait, U has seven nodes: A,B,C,D,E,F,G.\n\nU \\ CR = all nodes except B, D, F.\n\nThus, U \\ CR = {A, C, E, G} (four nodes).\n\nTherefore, |C| = C(4,4) = 1 (only subset {A,C,E,G}).\n\nNow compute |A ∩ B|: subsets that do not intersect T AND do not intersect R.\n\nThat is, subsets that are subsets of (U \\ T) ∩ (U \\ R) = (U \\ T) ∩ (U \\ R) = U \\ (T ∪ R).\n\nCompute T ∪ R = {A,B,C} ∪ {E,F,G} = {A,B,C,E,F,G} (six nodes).\n\nThus, U \\ (T ∪ R) = {D} (only node D).\n\nTherefore, subsets in A ∩ B must be subsets of {D}, but size 4. Impossible. Therefore |A ∩ B| = 0.\n\nSimilarly, |A ∩ C|: subsets that do not intersect T AND do not intersect CR.\n\nThat is, subsets of (U \\ T) ∩ (U \\ CR).\n\nU \\ T = {D,E,F,G}\n\nU \\ CR = {A,C,E,G}\n\nThus, intersection is {E,G} (nodes E and G).\n\nTherefore, subsets in A ∩ C must be subsets of {E,G} of size 4. Impossible. Thus |A ∩ C| = 0.\n\nNext, |B ∩ C|: subsets that do not intersect R AND do not intersect CR.\n\nThat is, subsets of (U \\ R) ∩ (U \\ CR).\n\nU \\ R = {A,B,C,D}\n\nU \\ CR = {A,C,E,G}\n\nThus, intersection is {A,C} (nodes A and C).\n\nSubsets of size 4 from {A,C} is impossible. Thus |B ∩ C| = 0.\n\nFinally, |A ∩ B ∩ C|: subsets that do not intersect T, R, or CR. These would have to be subsets of U \\ (T ∪ R ∪ CR).\n\nCompute T ∪ R ∪ CR = {A,B,C} ∪ {E,F,G} ∪ {B,D,F} = {A,B,C,D,E,F,G} (all nodes except none, since all are included). Wait:\n\nT = {A,B,C}\n\nR = {E,F,G}\n\nCR = {B,D,F}\n\nThus, union is:\n\nA,B,C from T,\n\nE,F,G from R,\n\nand B,D,F from CR.\n\nCombined, that's A,B,C,D,E,F,G. All seven nodes.\n\nTherefore, U \\ (T ∪ R ∪ CR) is empty. Therefore, subsets in A ∩ B ∩ C must be subsets of the empty set, which is impossible. Thus |A ∩ B ∩ C| = 0.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\n|A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A ∩ B| - |A ∩ C| - |B ∩ C| + |A ∩ B ∩ C| = 1 + 1 + 1 - 0 - 0 - 0 + 0 = 3.\n\nTherefore, |X| = |U| - |A ∪ B ∪ C| = 35 - 3 = 32.\n\nWait, this can't be right, because we know that there are some subsets that do block all paths, but according to this, 32 subsets are \"good\" (i.e., have a path), and 3 are \"bad\" (no path). But we know that at least the subset corresponding to the vertex cut {B, E} plus two other nodes is bad, and there are multiple such.\n\nBut according to this calculation, there are only 3 subsets that are bad (i.e., vertex cuts of size 4?), but that seems inconsistent with our earlier finding that {B, D, F, G} is a vertex cut of size 4, and {B, E, F, something} etc.\n\nThis suggests an error in the inclusion-exclusion approach.\n\nThe mistake likely arises because the conditions we imposed (intersecting T, R, and CR) are insufficient to ensure that all paths are blocked. In other words, blocking the three main routes might not be sufficient to block all possible paths, so the inclusion-exclusion counted only subsets that block those three routes but not necessarily all paths.\n\nTherefore, this approach undercounts the bad subsets because it only considers blocking the three main routes, but there are other paths that might still exist even if those three routes are blocked.\n\nTherefore, this method is invalid because the problem requires blocking all paths, not just the three main routes.\n\nTherefore, back to the drawing board.\n\nPerhaps the only feasible way is to enumerate all 35 subsets and count how many are vertex cuts.\n\nGiven that this is time-consuming but manageable, let's proceed.\n\nFirst, list all 4-node subsets from the seven nodes {A,B,C,D,E,F,G} and check each one.\n\nLabel the nodes as before:\n\nA: (1,1)\n\nB: (1,2)\n\nC: (2,1)\n\nD: (2,2)\n\nE: (2,3)\n\nF: (3,2)\n\nG: (3,3)\n\nWe need to consider all 4-node subsets of these seven nodes and determine if they form a vertex cut (i.e., every path from (3,1) to (1,3) in the original grid passes through at least one node in the subset).\n\nLet me proceed systematically.\n\nFirst, note that the node (3,1) is fixed to be included in S, and (1,3) is fixed to be included in S. The excluded nodes are the four nodes in the subset E.\n\nTo have no path from (3,1) to (1,3), every path in the original grid must pass through at least one node in E.\n\nLet me go through the subsets one by one.\n\nHowever, enumerating 35 subsets is tedious, but let's try to find a pattern or categorize them.\n\nFirst, note that any vertex cut must include at least one node from the following critical areas:\n\n- The top route's critical nodes: A, B, C.\n\n- The right route's critical nodes: E, F, G.\n\n- The central route's critical nodes: B, D, F.\n\nAdditionally, other nodes like D and G might be important.\n\nLet me categorize subsets based on whether they include certain key nodes.\n\nFirst, note that the minimal vertex cut {B, E} (nodes (1,2) and (2,3)) is a size 2 cut. Any superset of this plus two more nodes is a size 4 cut.\n\nThere are C(5,2) = 10 such subsets (since we have 7 nodes total, minus the two in the minimal cut leaves 5 nodes).\n\nAdditionally, there might be other vertex cuts that don't include both B and E but still block all paths.\n\nEarlier, we found {B, D, F, G} as another vertex cut of size 4. Let's verify this:\n\nNodes in E = {B, D, F, G}.\n\nCheck if all paths are blocked:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F and G (can't go to (3,2) or (3,3)), but actually, (3,3) is not excluded, so path can go to (3,3), then to (2,3), then to (1,3). However, (3,2) is excluded, so from (3,1) can't go right to (3,2). From (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) is blocked at B.\n\nWait, the path (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3) is blocked at B.\n\nPath (3,1)->(3,2) is blocked at F.\n\nPath (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) blocked at D and B.\n\nPath (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D and F? (2,3) is not excluded, but can go from (2,2) to (2,3), then to (1,3). However, (2,2) is excluded, so this path is blocked at D.\n\nPath (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D.\n\nPath (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at B.\n\nPath (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F (can't go to (3,2)), but can go from (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D.\n\nThus, all paths are blocked. So {B, D, F, G} is a vertex cut.\n\nSimilarly, let's see another subset: {B, E, F, something}.\n\nTake {B, E, F, A}:\n\nNodes excluded: B, E, F, A.\n\nCheck paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1) blocked at A.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) blocked at B and F.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F.\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at A and B.\n\nPath 6: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at B isn't on this path, but blocked at F? No, (2,2) is allowed. From (2,2) to (2,3) is allowed, then to (1,3). So path exists: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3). This path doesn't go through B, E, F, or A. Therefore, this subset {B, E, F, A} is not a vertex cut.\n\nTherefore, adding A to the minimal cut doesn't work.\n\nAnother subset: {B, E, F, D}:\n\nExcluded nodes: B, E, F, D.\n\nCheck paths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2) blocked at F and D.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at F.\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2) blocked at D.\n\nPath 6: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at B.\n\nPath 7: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D.\n\nPath 8: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at F and D and B.\n\nPath 9: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at D.\n\nAll paths seem blocked. Thus, {B, E, F, D} is a vertex cut.\n\nContinuing this way would take too long, but perhaps we can see a pattern.\n\nEach vertex cut of size 4 must include:\n\n- At least one node from the top route's critical nodes (A, B, C),\n\n- At least one node from the right route's critical nodes (E, F, G),\n\n- At least one node from the central route's critical nodes (B, D, F),\n\nAdditionally, since we have four nodes, and overlaps exist between these groups, we can categorize the subsets based on which critical nodes they include.\n\nAlternatively, since this is taking too long and I might not have enough time, perhaps I can recall that in such grid problems, the number of vertex cuts of size 4 might be 8 or something, but I need to be precise.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the total number of vertex cuts of size 4 is 8, so the answer is 35 - 8 = 27, but I need to verify.\n\nWait, let me think differently.\n\nSuppose we consider that any vertex cut of size 4 must include the minimal cut {B, E} plus two more nodes, but also other cuts that don't include B and E but still block all paths.\n\nEarlier we found {B, D, F, G} as a vertex cut not containing E.\n\nSimilarly, perhaps there are others like:\n\n- {B, D, E, G}\n\n- {B, D, E, F}\n\n- {B, C, E, G}\n\n- {B, C, E, F}\n\n- {B, C, D, G}\n\n- {B, C, D, F}\n\n- {C, E, F, G}\n\n- {C, E, F, D}\n\n- {C, E, G, D}\n\n- {E, F, G, D}\n\nWait, this is getting too ambiguous.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that the only vertex cuts of size 4 are those that include either:\n\n1. The minimal cut {B, E} plus any two nodes from the remaining five nodes (A, C, D, F, G). However, not all of these will block all paths.\n\nFor example, adding A and C to {B, E} gives {A, B, C, E}. Let's see if this blocks all paths:\n\nExcluded nodes: A, B, C, E.\n\nPaths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1) blocked at A.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3): blocked at E.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3): blocked at B and E.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): not blocked by excluded nodes (since A,B,C,E are excluded, but this path goes through (2,2) and (2,3), which are not excluded). Thus, this path exists. Therefore, {A,B,C,E} is not a vertex cut.\n\nTherefore, not all supersets of {B,E} are vertex cuts.\n\nSimilarly, adding D and F to {B,E} gives {B, D, E, F}, which we saw earlier is a vertex cut.\n\nAdding F and G to {B,E} gives {B, E, F, G}. Let's check:\n\nExcluded nodes: B, E, F, G.\n\nPaths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1)->(1,1)->(1,2) blocked at B.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) blocked at F.\n\nPath 3: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2) blocked at B and F.\n\nPath 4: (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,3) blocked at F and E.\n\nPath 5: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3): not blocked by excluded nodes (only B,E,F,G). So this path exists: (3,1)->(2,1)->(2,2)->(2,3)->(1,3). Thus, {B,E,F,G} is not a vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Only some of the supersets work.\n\nThis is very time-consuming, but let me try to count the ones I can find:\n\n1. {B, E, F, D} (found earlier)\n\n2. {B, E, F, G} (doesn't work)\n\n3. {B, E, F, A} (doesn't work)\n\n4. {B, E, F, C} (let's check):\n\nExcluded nodes: B, E, F, C.\n\nPaths:\n\nPath 1: (3,1)->(2,1) blocked at C.\n\nPath 2: (3,1)->(3,2) block