\n\nWait, confusion between choosing to keep or remove. \n\nLet me clarify: \n\nThere are 9 lily pads. \n\nWe need to remove 4, so keep 5. \n\nTwo specific lily pads (bottom-left and top-right) must be kept. \n\nTherefore, the number of ways to choose the 5 kept lily pads is: \n\nWe must include the two fixed ones, so we choose 3 more from the remaining 7. \n\nTherefore, C(7,3)=35. \n\nAlternatively, the number of ways to choose the 4 removed lily pads is: we cannot remove the two fixed ones, so we choose all 4 from the remaining 7. \n\nTherefore, C(7,4)=35. \n\nYes, that's consistent. Since C(7,3)=C(7,4)=35. Because C(n,k)=C(n,n−k). \n\nSo both ways give 35. \n\nTherefore, my earlier analysis was correct: there are 35 possible ways to remove 4 lily pads such that the two required ones remain. \n\nThen, among those 35, 6 of them allow the frog to reach home (the six path subsets), so the answer is 35-6=29. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is 29. \n\nBut wait, hold on! Wait, the problem says \"Compute the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads so that the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain, but Kelvin cannot get home.\" \n\nTherefore, the answer is 29. \n\nHowever, I just found a possible error: when I considered the six paths, each path corresponds to a unique subset of kept lily pads (the path itself). However, each such subset is obtained by keeping exactly the nodes of the path (including the two fixed ones). Therefore, the number of removal sets that allow the frog to reach home is exactly six. \n\nTherefore, the number of removal sets that do not allow the frog to reach home is 35 - 6 = 29. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 29. \n\nBut now I recall that in the problem statement, it specifies that Kelvin is on the bottom-left lily pad and wants to get to the top-right. The frog can only jump between horizontally or vertically adjacent lily pads. \n\nWait, but in our analysis, we considered paths moving only up and right, but in the grid, the frog can