ive vertices.\n\n- A 2-tile: occupies 2 consecutive vertices, but must be followed by a gap (at least one vertex) and then another 2-tile? No, that would be part of a Type II box.\n\nWait, this is really challenging. Maybe it's better to look for existing literature or similar problems.\n\nWait, actually, the problem is similar to a domino tiling but with different tile sizes and constraints. Alternatively, perhaps we can model this as a recurrence relation where we build the tiling from left to right.\n\nLet me try defining a recurrence.\n\nLet me denote f(n) as the number of ways to tile a path of n vertices with the allowed tiles (each tile is either a 4-tile or a 2+2-tile), where the tiles are distinguishable (i.e., the order matters). Then, the total number of labeled partitions would be f(16), since each tiling corresponds to a labeled partition where the order of the tiles matters (i.e., which box is first, etc.), but since the boxes are actually indistinct, we need to adjust for overcounting.\n\nHowever, the problem is that a Type II tile is not just a single 2-tile but a combination of two 2-tiles separated by a gap. So perhaps we need a more nuanced recurrence.\n\nLet me define f(n) as the number of tilings of a path of length n with tiles of size 4 or \"2+2\" tiles (which take up 4 vertices with a gap in between). Wait, but how do we account for the gap?\n\nAlternatively, think of a \"2+2\" tile as occupying 4 vertices but requiring that between its two 2-blocks there is at least one vertex which is part of another tile. Therefore, when placing a \"2+2\" tile starting at position i, it occupies positions i, i+1, then skips at least one position, then occupies positions i+3, i+4. Wait, no:\n\nWait, a \"2+2\" tile consists of two blocks of two consecutive vertices separated by at least one vertex. So, for example, if the first block is at positions i and i+1, the second block must be at positions j and j+1 where j ≥ i+3. Therefore, the entire \"2+2\" tile spans from i to j+1, but with a gap between i+1 and j-1. The total number of vertices covered by the \"2+2\" tile is (j+1) - i + 1 = j - i + 2 vertices. Since the two blocks are separated by at least one vertex, j ≥ i+3, so the minimal span is i to i+4, covering 5 vertices? Wait:\n\nWait, first block: positions i and i+1\n\nSecond block: positions j and j+1, with j ≥ i+3\n\nThen the span from i to j+1 is (j+1) - i + 1 = j - i + 2 vertices.\n\nSince j ≥ i+3, then j - i ≥ 3, so the span is at least 3 + 2 = 5 vertices? Wait, no:\n\nWait, j - i ≥ 3 ⇒ j - i + 2 ≥ 5, yes. So a \"2+2\" tile requires at least 5 vertices to place both blocks with a gap between them. But we have only 4 vertices per tile. This is a contradiction.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. Earlier, I thought a Type II box (which is a group of four vertices) can be two blocks of two with a gap between them. However, if those two blocks are part of the same group, then the gap between them must be filled by vertices from other groups. Therefore, the group itself only occupies the four vertices in the two blocks, but the gap between them is part of other groups. Therefore, in terms of tiling, the \"2+2\" tile (the group) does not occupy the gap; the gap is occupied by other tiles. Therefore, the \"2+2\" tile only occupies its four vertices (the two blocks), and the gap is just space between them which is part of other tiles. Therefore, when tiling, a \"2+2\" tile can be placed anywhere as long as its two blocks are separated by at least one vertex, which will be part of another tile.\n\nTherefore, when building the tiling from left to right, if I place a \"2+2\" tile starting at position i with blocks [i, i+1] and [j, j+1], then the positions between i+2 and j-1 must be covered by other tiles. However, this complicates the recurrence because placing a \"2+2\" tile affects the available space for subsequent tiles.\n\nThis seems too complex for a simple recurrence. Maybe instead, think of the entire path and consider that the only valid tilings are those that partition the path into four blocks of four, either as four single 4-blocks or some combination with 2-blocks separated by other blocks.\n\nWait, earlier we saw that when we have four Type I boxes, that's one way.\n\nWhen we have two Type I boxes and two Type II boxes, we need to see how many ways there are to place the two Type I boxes such that the remaining 8 vertices can be partitioned into two Type II boxes. Earlier, we found that in an 8-vertex segment, there are two distinct ways to partition into two Type II boxes. But depending on where the two Type I boxes are placed in the 16-vertex path, the remaining 8-vertex segment might be in different positions, possibly allowing those two configurations or not.\n\nFor example, suppose the two Type I boxes are placed at the very beginning and very end:\n\nType I boxes at positions 1-4 and 13-16. Then the middle 8 positions (5-12) form an 8-vertex segment. In this middle segment, we can partition it into two Type II boxes in two ways as we found earlier: {5,6,9,10} and {7,8,11,12} or something like that? Wait, let's map the earlier examples to this.\n\nIf the middle segment is positions 5-12 (8 vertices), then the two Type II box partitions would be analogous to the earlier examples:\n\nFirst partition: {5,6,9,10} and {7,8,11,12}\n\nSecond partition: {7,8,9,10} and {5,6,11,12}\n\nWait, but in the earlier examples, the first partition was {1,2,5,6} and {3,4,7,8} for an 8-vertex segment starting at 1. If we shift that to positions 5-12, then it would be {5,6,9,10} and {7,8,11,12}. Similarly, the second partition {3,4,5,6} and {1,2,7,8} shifted would be {7,8,9,10} and {5,6,11,12}? Wait, let me check:\n\nOriginal second partition on positions 1-8 was {3,4,5,6} and {1,2,7,8}. Shifting by 4 positions, it becomes {7,8,9,10} and {5,6,11,12}. Yes, that works.\n\nTherefore, in this configuration where the two Type I boxes are at the ends, there are two ways to partition the middle 8