9-16] (length 8). The [9-16] can be split into two 4-blocks, so total 1 (from [3-6]) + 2 (from [9-16]) = 3 blocks. Good.\n\n2. With b=11: two-2-block at [1,2] and [11,12], leaving segments [3-10] (length 8) and [13-16] (length 4). The [3-10] can be split into two 4-blocks, and [13-16] is one, totaling three. Good.\n\n3. With b=15: two-2-block at [1,2] and [15,16], leaving segment [3-14] (length 12), which can be split into three 4-blocks. Good.\n\nSimilarly, when a=5:\n\nThe two-2-block is placed with a=5 (so first 2-block at 5-6), and to get the after segment to be length 4, we needed b=11, resulting in the two-2-block at [5,6] and [11,12], leaving segments [1-4], [7-10], [13-16], each of length 4. That's the central placement we considered first.\n\nAdditionally, could a=5 allow other b's?\n\nIf a=5, then the before segment is length 4 (positions 1-4). The after segment must be length 4 ⇒ b=11 as before. If we choose b= something else, say b=9:\n\nThen the after segment length is 15 - 9 = 6, which is not divisible by 4, so can't be partitioned. The middle segment length is b - a - 2 = 9 -5 -2=2, which is too short. So that won't work.\n\nIf b=10: after segment length is 15 -10=5, which is not divisible by 4. Middle segment length=10-5-2=3, still bad.\n\nIf b=12: after segment length=15-12=3, which is not divisible by 4. Middle segment length=12-5-2=5, no good.\n\nIf b=13: after segment length=2, which is bad. Middle segment length=13-5-2=6, which can be split into one 4-block and a 2-block, but we can't have 2-blocks; we need all 4-blocks. So no.\n\nThus, only b=11 works when a=5.\n\nNow, what about a=6? Let's see:\n\nIf a=6, then the before segment length is a-1=5, which is not divisible by 4. Therefore, the before segment can't be a 4-block, so this placement would require that the before segment is combined with something else, but since it's a separate segment, it can't be. Therefore, invalid.\n\nSimilarly, a=4:\n\na=4 ⇒ before segment length=3, not divisible by 4 ⇒ invalid.\n\na=3 ⇒ before segment length=2 ⇒ invalid.\n\na=2 ⇒ before segment length=1 ⇒ invalid.\n\na=1 we already considered.\n\nWhat about a=0? Not possible, since the first 2-block can't start at position 0.\n\nSimilarly, a can go up to... Let's see, the first 2-block must start at a such that the second 2-block can fit. For example, if a is too large, then b would have to be even larger, possibly exceeding 16.\n\nFor example, if a=7, then the before segment length is 6, which isn't divisible by 4, so invalid.\n\nTherefore, the only valid a values are a=1 and a=5.\n\nWhen a=1, we have three possible b values: 7, 11, and 15.\n\nWhen a=5, we have one possible b value: 11.\n\nWait, when a=5 and b=11, that's the central placement.\n\nSo total placements for the two-2-block in this case (three 4-blocks and one two-2-block) are:\n\n- For a=1, b=7,\n\n- a=1, b=11,\n\n- a=1, b=15,\n\n- a=5, b=11.\n\nTherefore, four different placements?\n\nWait, let's verify:\n\n1. a=1, b=7: two-2-block at [1,2] and [7,8].\n\n2. a=1, b=11: two-2-block at [1,2] and [11,12].\n\n3. a=1, b=15: two-2-block at [1,2] and [15,16].\n\n4. a=5, b=11: two-2-block at [5,6] and [11,12].\n\nAre these all distinct placements?\n\nYes, each of these four placements results in a different set of subsets:\n\n1. The first case: the two-2-block is at the very beginning (positions 1-2 and 7-8), leaving the rest as three 4-blocks in [3-6], [9-12], [13-16].\n\nWait, no, in the first case with a=1, b=7:\n\n- The two-2-block is at [1,2] and [7,8].\n\n- The segments are:\n\n  - Before: none (since a=1),\n\n  - Middle: positions 3-6 (length 4),\n\n  - After: positions 9-16 (length 8), which is split into [9-12] and [13-16].\n\nTherefore, the subsets would be:\n\n- Two-2-block: {1,2,7,8},\n\n- 4-blocks: {3,4,5,6}, {9,10,11,12}, {13,14,15,16}.\n\nSecond case: a=1, b=11:\n\n- Two-2-block: {1,2,11,12},\n\n- Segments:\n\n  - Before: none,\n\n  - Middle: positions 3-10 (length 8), split into {3-6}, {7-10},\n\n  - After: {13-16},\n\nTherefore subsets:\n\n- {1,2,11,12},\n\n- {3-6}, {7-10}, {13-16}.\n\nThird case: a=1, b=15:\n\n- Two-2-block: {1,2,15,16},\n\n- Segments:\n\n  - Before: none,\n\n  - Middle: positions 3-14 (length 12), split into three 4-blocks: {3-6}, {7-10}, {11-14},\n\n  - After: none,\n\nTherefore subsets:\n\n- {1,2,15,16},\n\n- {3-6}, {7-10}, {11-14}.\n\nFourth case: a=5, b=11:\n\n- Two-2-block: {5,6,11,12},\n\n- Segments:\n\n  - Before: {1-4},\n\n  - Middle: {7-10},\n\n  - After: {13-16},\n\nTherefore subsets:\n\n- {5,6,11,12},\n\n- {1-4}, {7-10}, {13-16}.\n\nTherefore, these are four distinct partitions. However, we need to ensure that in each case, the two-2-block is indeed a valid two-2-block (i.e., the two pairs are separated by at least one vertex). In all four cases, the separation between the two 2-blocks is at least one vertex:\n\n- In case 1: between positions 2 and 7: positions 3-6 are in the middle, so separation is 5 vertices (positions 3-6), which is more than one.\n\n- In case 2: between positions 2 and 11: separation of positions 3-10, which is 8 vertices.\n\n- In case 3: between positions 2 and 15: separation of positions 3-14, which is 12 vertices.\n\n- In case 4: between positions 6 and 11: separation of position 7-10, which is 4 vertices.\n\nAll are valid.\n\nTherefore, there are four distinct partitions in Case 2.\n\nHowever, wait a second: the boxes are indistinct, so are these four partitions considered distinct?\n\nYes, because the actual sets of vertices in the boxes are different. For example, in the first case, the two-2-block includes vertices 1,2,7,8, whereas in the fourth case, it includes 5,6,11,12. Since the sets are different, these are distinct partitions. Similarly, the other cases have different subsets. Therefore, these four are indeed distinct.\n\nTherefore, Case 2 contributes 4 partitions.\n\nCase 3: Two 4-blocks and two two-2-blocks.\n\nNow, this case has two types of subsets: two 4-blocks and two two-2-blocks. We need t