rom Cases 1 and 2, totaling 5, but I need to verify.\n\nWait, let me consider that in Case 3, two two-2-blocks and two 4-blocks, perhaps there are 6 ways.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that the two two-2-blocks must be placed such that their gaps are separated by the 4-blocks.\n\nSuppose we have:\n\n- Two two-2-blocks placed at the very beginning and the very end, with two 4-blocks in the middle.\n\nFor example:\n\nFirst two-2-block: positions 1-2 and 6-7 (gap at 3-5),\n\nSecond two-2-block: positions 14-15 and 16-? Wait, 16 is the last vertex. So positions 14-15 and 16 can't form a pair. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nFirst two-2-block: positions 1-2 and 6-7 (gap 3-5),\n\nSecond two-2-block: positions 11-12 and 15-16 (gap 13-14),\n\nThen the remaining positions 3-5 (3 vertices), 8-10 (3 vertices), and 13-14 (2 vertices). Not enough for two 4-blocks.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nFirst two-2-block: positions 1-2 and 5-6 (gap 3-4),\n\nSecond two-2-block: positions 9-10 and 13-14 (gap 11-12),\n\nThen the remaining positions are 3-4 (2 vertices), 7-8 (2 vertices), 11-12 (2 vertices), 15-16 (2 vertices). Not enough for 4-blocks.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nFirst two-2-block: positions 1-2 and 7-8 (gap 3-6),\n\nSecond two-2-block: positions 10-11 and 15-16 (gap 12-14),\n\nRemaining positions: 3-6 (4 vertices), 9-10 (2 vertices), 12-14 (3 vertices). The 3-6 can be a 4-block, but 9-10 and 12-14 can't form 4-blocks.\n\nThis is not working.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the two two-2-blocks are placed such that their gaps are covered by a single 4-block each.\n\nSuppose:\n\nFirst two-2-block: positions 1-2 and 5-6 (gap 3-4),\n\nSecond two-2-block: positions 10-11 and 14-15 (gap 12-13),\n\nThen the gaps at 3-4 and 12-13 can be covered by two 4-blocks:\n\n- First gap (3-4) needs to be part of a 4-block. The earliest 4-block could be 3-6, but that would include the second pair of the first two-2-block at 5-6. Conflict.\n\nAlternatively, the 4-block could be 3-6, but then the two-2-block's second pair