ment that the remaining segments must be splittable into three 4-blocks, it seems only those four positions are possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the two-2-block can be placed symmetrically in the middle, but that's already accounted for in the a=5, b=11 case.\n\nTherefore, I think the total is indeed 5.\n\nHowever, let me think again about the central case (a=5, b=11). In this case, the two-2-block is in the center, splitting the path into three equal segments of 4 each. This is symmetric and might be considered the \"balanced\" partition.\n\nThe other three Case 2 partitions have the two-2-block either at the very start or near the end, with the remaining segments split into varying lengths.\n\nGiven that these are all distinct, I think the total is 5.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"the order in which the balls are placed does not matter,\" but since we're counting set partitions, the order of the subsets doesn't matter. However, in our count above, each configuration is a distinct set partition because the subsets are different.\n\nFor example, in the first Case 2 partition, one of the subsets is {1,2,7,8}, which is different from the central case's subset {5,6,11,12}, so they are distinct partitions.\n\nSimilarly, the other Case 2 partitions have different subsets, so they are indeed distinct.\n\nTherefore, the total number should be 5.\n\nBut I recall that in combinatorics, sometimes when you have symmetries, you might have overcounted or undercounted. Let me see if there's a symmetric counterpart to the a=1, b=15 case.\n\nThe a=1, b=15 case has the two-2-block at the very end: {1,2,15,16}. Is there a symmetric case with the two-2-block at the very beginning and another near the end?\n\nWait, the a=1, b=15 case is already at the beginning and the end. If we placed the two-2-block at the end instead of the beginning, that would be similar but mirrored, but since the path is linear from 1 to 16, placing it at the beginning or the end are distinct because the lab