iding walks from (x,y) to (3,3) without revisiting any vertices, then when computing f(x,y), we can look at all possible next moves from (x,y) to its neighbors (as per the allowed moves), and for each such neighbor (x', y'), we can add f(x', y') to f(x,y), provided that (x', y') is not yet visited in the path. However, since we are defining f(x,y) as the total number of such paths, the key point is that when moving from (x,y) to (x', y'), the path from (x,y) to (3,3) must not have visited (x', y') before arriving at (x', y'). But in the definition of f(x', y'), the paths counted in f(x', y') are those that start at (x', y') and go to (3,3) without revisiting any nodes, so if we go from (x,y) to (x', y'), then the path from (x,y) to (3,3) will include the segment from (x,y) to (x', y'), and then the path from (x', y') to (3,3). The only potential overlap is if the path from (x', y') to (3,3) revisits (x,y), but since we are moving forward from (x,y) to (x', y'), and the path from (x', y') to (3,3) is a separate path that doesn't include (x,y) unless it's allowed. Wait, actually, no, the path from (x', y') to (3,3) could potentially pass through (x,y), but in the overall path from (x,y) to (3,3), we would have gone from (x,y) to (x', y') and then from (x', y') to (3,3), which might pass through (x,y) again, which is not allowed because we can't revisit (x,y). Therefore, actually, the path from (x', y') to (3,3) must not pass through (x,y), but since we are moving in the opposite direction, how do we account for that?\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. Maybe the standard way to handle self-avoiding walks in dynamic programming is to represent the state as the current position and the set of visited nodes. Since the grid is small, perhaps we can do this with a recursive approach with memoization, tracking the visited nodes as a bitmask. Since there are 16 nodes, each node can be represented by a bit in a 16-bit integer. So the state is (current position, visited nodes), bu