hout revisiting any nodes.\n\nWe know that f(3,3) = 1.\n\nNow, let's compute f(x,y) for all other nodes, starting from those closest to (3,3).\n\nFirst, consider nodes adjacent to (3,3). What nodes can move to (3,3) in one step?\n\nTo reach (3,3) from a previous node (x,y), the move must be one of the four allowed moves:\n\n1. From (x, y) to (x+1, y) = (3,3) ⇒ x+1 = 3 and y = 3 ⇒ x=2, y=3.\n\n2. From (x, y) to (x, y+1) = (3,3) ⇒ x=3 and y+1 = 3 ⇒ y=2.\n\n3. From (x, y) to (x-1, y+1) = (3,3) ⇒ x-1 = 3 and y+1 = 3 ⇒ x=4, y=2, but x=4 is invalid.\n\n4. From (x, y) to (x+1, y-1) = (3,3) ⇒ x+1 = 3 and y-1 = 3 ⇒ x=2, y=4, which is invalid since y=4 > 3.\n\nTherefore, the only nodes that can move directly to (3,3) are:\n\n- (2,3): via move 1 (right)\n\n- (3,2): via move 2 (up)\n\nSo f(2,3) and f(3,2) can contribute to f(3,3). Specifically:\n\nThe paths to (3,3) are:\n\n- From (2,3): moving right to (3,3)\n\n- From (3,2): moving up to (3,3)\n\nAdditionally, from (3,3)'s perspective, those are the only immediate predecessors.\n\nTherefore, f(3,3) = f(2,3) + f(3,2) ?\n\nWait, no. Wait, actually, f(x,y) is the number of paths starting at (x,y) and going to (3,3). Therefore, f(3,3) = 1 by definition. The predecessors of (3,3) are (2,3) and (3,2). Therefore, the number of paths to (3,3) from those predecessors is f(2,3) + f(3,2), but since f(3,3) is 1, that must equal 1? That doesn't make sense. Wait, perhaps I have the definitions reversed.\n\nWait, actually, if we are computing f(x,y) as the number of paths from (x,y) to (3,3), then:\n\nf(3,3) = 1 (the path that stays at (3,3), but since we have to end at (3,3), maybe it's considered as 1 path of length 0? Or maybe the base case is f(3,3) = 1, and for other nodes, f(x,y) is the sum over the neighbors of f(neighbors), but with some restrictions.\n\nWait, perhaps a better way is:\n\nLet me redefine f(x,y) as the number of paths from (x,y) to (3,3) without revisiting any nodes. Then:\n\n- f(3,3) = 1 (the trivial path that does no