ode (0,1)'s move: +1,\n\n          from node (2,0)'s move: +1,\n\n          from node (0,2)'s move: +1,\n\n          from node (1,1)'s move: +2,\n\n          total: 1+1+1+1+2 = 6,\n\n- (0,2): contributions from node (0,1)'s move: +1,\n\n          from node (1,1)'s move: +2,\n\n          total: 1 + 2 = 3,\n\n- (2,1): contributions from node (2,0)'s move: +1,\n\n          from node (1,1)'s move: +2,\n\n          total: 1 + 2 = 3,\n\n- (1,2): contributions from node (0,2)'s move: +1,\n\n          from node (1,1)'s move: +2,\n\n          total: 1 + 2 = 3,\n\n- (0,3): contributions from node (0,2)'s move: +1,\n\n- (2,0) is already counted above,\n\n- (1,1) is counted,\n\n- (3,0) is counted,\n\n- (0,3) is new,\n\n- (1,2) is counted,\n\n- (2,1) is counted,\n\n- (0,2) is counted,\n\n- (1,0) is counted,\n\n- (0,1) is counted,\n\n- (2,0) is counted,\n\n- (3,0),\n\n- Also, any other nodes?\n\n- (3,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,2), etc.?\n\nWait, let me check all possible nodes at step 3:\n\nPossible nodes reachable in 3 steps from (0,0):\n\nStarting from (0,0), in three moves, possible coordinates:\n\nThe maximum x or y coordinate reachable in 3 steps is 3 (if moving right or up three times), but with diagonal moves, you can also reach (3,3) in fewer steps, but in three steps, you can't reach (3,3) because each diagonal move changes both coordinates by 1, so in three moves, the maximum net displacement is 3 in some direction.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nTo reach (3,3) in three steps would require a net displacement of (3,3), but each move can contribute at most +1 in x or y, so three moves can give a maximum displacement of (3,3) only if all moves are right and up, which would require 3 rights and 3 ups, totaling 6 moves. So in three moves, you can't reach (3,3). So step 3 nodes are up to (3,0), (0,3), etc.\n\nContinuing with the tally:\n\nNodes at step 3:\n\n- (0,3): 1,\n\n- (1,0): 1,\n\n- (0,1): 1,\n\n- (2,0): 3,\n\n- (3,0): 1,\n\n- (1,1): 6,\n\n- (0,2): 3,\n\n- (2,1): 3,\n\n- (1,2): 3,\n\n- (others: 0).\n\nLet me verify each:\n\n- (0,3): only from (0,2)'s move to (0,3): 1,\n\n- (1,0): only from (0,1)'s move to (1,0): 1,\n\n- (0,1): from (1,0)'s move: 1,\n\n- (2,0): 1 (from (1,0)) + 2 (from (1,1)) = 3,\n\n- (3,0): from (2,0): 1,\n\n- (1,1): contributions from (1,0):1, (0,1):1, (2,0):1, (0,2):1, and (1,1) itself's contribution? Wait no, contributions to (1,1) come from other nodes moving into it:\n\nWait, no, the contributions to (1,1) are from:\n\n- (1,0) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- (0,1) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- (2,0) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- (0,2) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- (1,1) itself can't contribute to its own count,\n\n- and from (1,1) moving to other nodes, but those contributions are to other nodes, not to (1,1).\n\nWait, no, sorry, the contributions to (1,1) are only from the moves of other nodes into it. The node (1,1) itself is the current node being processed, and its moves go out to other nodes, not into itself.\n\nTherefore, my earlier tally for (1,1) is correct: 1+1+1+1 = 4 from the other nodes, plus nothing from (1,1) itself, so total 4? Wait no, the contributions from (1,1) node are to its neighbors, not to itself. Wait, no, sorry, the node (1,1) with count 2 is being processed, and from it, we add 2 to each of its neighbors, but it doesn't contribute to itself.\n\nTherefore, the count for (1,1) at step 3 is the sum of contributions from other nodes moving into it:\n\n- From (1,0) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- From (0,1) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- From (2,0) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\n- From (0,2) moving to (1,1): +1,\n\nTotal: 1+1+1+1 = 4,\n\nWait, but above I had written 6? That was a mistake.\n\nWait, let's recalculate (1,1)'s count:\n\nLooking back:\n\nWhen processing node (1,0) with count 1:\n\n- Can go to (1,1), so adds 1 to (1,1).\n\nProcessing node (0,1) with count 1:\n\n- Can go to (1,1), so adds 1 to (1,1).\n\nProcessing node (2,0) with count 1:\n\n- Can go to (1,1), so adds 1 to (1,1).\n\nProcessing node (0,2) with count 1:\n\n- 