se x-coordinate can't be less than 0. Similarly, moving to (x + 1, y - 1) from some point might take her to a y-coordinate less than 0, which is also invalid. So, those moves are only allowed if they don't result in coordinates outside the 0 to 3 bounds for either x or y.\n\nAlso, she cannot revisit any points along her path. So, the path must be self-avoiding. So, in addition to the coordinate constraints, the path must not cross over itself.\n\nHmm. So, this seems like a problem that could be approached with recursion or dynamic programming, where we consider the number of paths from a given point to the destination, considering the constraints.\n\nLet me formalize this. Let me denote by f(x, y) the number of paths from (x, y) to (3, 3) without revisiting any points and staying within the bounds [0, 3] for both x and y coordinates. Our goal is to compute f(0, 0).\n\nTo compute f(x, y), we can use the principle of recursion with memoization: the number of paths from (x, y) to (3, 3) is the sum of the number of paths from each of the possible next positions to (3, 3), provided those next positions are valid and not yet visited. However, the problem is that the constraint of not revisiting any points complicates things, because the availability of paths from a position depends on the path taken to get there.\n\nWait a second, in standard path counting problems where you can revisit points, you can use dynamic programming where f(x, y) is the number of paths from (x, y) to the destination without any restrictions except the movement rules and coordinate bounds. But in this case, since the path cannot revisit any points, that introduces dependencies along the path, so the standard dynamic programming approach might not work straightforwardly because the number of paths from a point (x, y) to (3, 3) depends on which points have already been visited in the path leading up to (x, y). Since we can’t revisit any points, the state needs to include the set of visited points, 