ove those two, then the middle row's cells are disconnected from the rest? Wait, no. Each cell in the middle row is connected vertically to the row above and below. So if you remove the horizontal edges in the middle row, the middle row's cells are still connected vertically to the rows above and below. Therefore, the graph remains connected. Hmm, so that doesn't disconnect it.\n\nAlternatively, if you remove all vertical edges in the middle column. Similarly, the middle column's cells are still connected horizontally to their neighbors in the same row. So, removing vertical edges in the middle column doesn't disconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, to disconnect the graph, you might need to remove a set of edges that form a cut. For example, suppose we remove all edges adjacent to a particular node. Let's pick the center node in the 3x3 grid. It has four edges: up, down, left, right. If we remove those four edges, then the center node is disconnected, but the rest of the graph remains connected. So, that would require removing four edges to disconnect the graph. Alternatively, perhaps there is a smaller cut.\n\nSuppose we remove the four edges forming a \"square\" around the center. Wait, not sure. Alternatively, suppose we remove two vertical edges on the left side and two horizontal edges on the top. Maybe that could disconnect a corner? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the edge connectivity of a 3x3 grid is 4? Because to disconnect it, you might need to remove four edges. If that's the case, then in larger grids, the edge connectivity increases. For an m x n grid with m,n ≥ 2, the edge connectivity is 2*(min(m,n))? Not sure. Wait, actually according to some references I recall, the edge connectivity of an m × n grid graph is 2 if either m or n is 1, and 4 otherwise. Wait, if that's the case, then in our 11x11 grid, which is much larger, the edge connectivity is 4. Therefore, the grid graph is 4-edge-connected, meaning that you need to remove at least four edges to disconnect it. Therefore, if we remove 18 edges, which is way more than four, the graph will still be connected, right? Because edge connectivity is 4, so as long as we remove less than 4 edges, it stays connected, but we are removing 18 edges, which is way more than 4, so it's definitely still connected? Wait, no. Edge connectivity is the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to disconnect the graph. So if the edge connectivity is 4, that means that there exists a set of 4 edges whose removal disconnects the graph, but you can also disconnect the graph by removing more than 4 edges. So even though the minimal number is 4, removing 18 edges will certainly disconnect the graph? Wait, no, that's not necessarily the case. Just because the minimal number is 4, doesn't mean that removing more edges won't disconnect it. In fact, removing more edges will likely disconnect it further, but the point is that as long as we don't remove a minimal cut, the graph remains connected.\n\nWait, actually, no. The edge connectivity is the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to disconnect the graph. So, if you remove more than that number, the graph may or may not be disconnected. For example, in a 4-node cycle (edge connectivity 2), if you remove 3 edges, the graph is still connected (it becomes a single edge). If you remove all 4 edges, it's disconnected. So, in that case, removing more than the edge connectivity can still leave the graph connected, depending on which edges you remove.\n\nTherefore, in our case, since the grid graph has edge connectivity 4 (assuming that's true for 11x11 grid), then there exists a set of 4 edges whose removal disconnects the graph, but if we remove 18 edges that are not a minimal cut, the graph might still remain connected. Therefore, it is possible that by carefully choosing which edges to remove (i.e., not removing a minimal cut), we can keep the graph connected while removing 18 edges.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to think about this problem without getting bogged down in edge connectivity. Let's suppose that it is possible to remove 18 edges in such a way that the graph remains connected. Then, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse would be 220 - 18 = 202. Therefore, is 202 the answer?\n\nBut wait, hold on. Let me think differently. Maybe instead of trying to make the entire graph Eulerian, we can consider that the maximum closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of edges in a maximal matching of the odd-degree vertices? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is similar to the Chinese Postman Problem, where you have to find the minimal route that traverses every edge, possibly repeating some. But in our case, we can't repeat edges, so it's the opposite: we want the longest possible trail without repeating edges, which is just the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges that need to be excluded to make the graph Eulerian. So that would be 220 - 18 = 202, assuming that the graph can be made Eulerian by removing 18 edges without disconnecting it. Since the grid is so highly connected, I think this is plausible. Therefore, maybe 202 is the answer.\n\nBut let me check with a smaller grid to see if the reasoning holds. Let's take a small grid, say 3x3, and see what the maximum closed trail would be.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, how many edges are there? Let's compute:\n\nEach row has 3 cells, so 2 horizontal edges per row, 3 rows: 6 horizontal edges.\n\nEach column has 3 cells, so 2 vertical edges per column, 3 columns: 6 vertical edges.\n\nTotal edges: 12.\n\nTotal vertices: 9.\n\nDegrees:\n\nCorner vertices (4): each has degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices (each side has 1 non-corner vertex; 4 sides, so 4 vertices): each has degree 3.\n\nInterior vertex (1 center): degree 4.\n\nTherefore, odd-degree vertices: 4 (the edge non-corner ones). So to make the graph Eulerian, we need to remove edges so that all degrees are even. Since we have 4 odd-degree vertices, we need to remove 2 edges that each connect two odd-degree vertices.\n\nHow many edges connect two odd-degree vertices in a 3x3 grid?\n\nLet's see:\n\nThe edge non-corner vertices are the ones on the edges but not corners. In a 3x3 grid, the edge non-corner vertices are the four middle edges: for example, on the top row, the middle cell (position (1,2)), on the bottom row (3,2), on the left column (2,1), and on the right column (2,3). Each of these four vertices has degree 3.\n\nNow, edges connecting two odd-degree vertices:\n\nLooking at the top middle vertex (1,2): its neighbors are (1,1) [corner, degree 2], (1,3) [corner, degree 2], and (2,2) [interior, degree 4]. So the edges from (1,2) go to two corners and one interior. Therefore, none of its edges connect to another odd-degree vertex. Similarly, the bottom middle vertex (3,2) has neighbors (3,1) [corner], (3,3) [corner], and (2,2) [interior]. Again, no edges to other odd-degree vertices.\n\nThe left middle vertex (2,1): neighbors are (1,1) [corner], (3,1) [corner], and (2,2) [interior]. Similarly, no edges to other odd-degree vertices.\n\nThe right middle vertex (2,3): neighbors are (1,3) [corner], (3,3) [corner], and (2,2) [interior]. Again, no edges to other odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, none of the edges connect two odd-degree vertices! All the edges from the odd-degree vertices go to even-degree vertices (corners or interior). Therefore, in this case, we cannot remove any edge that connects two odd-degree vertices because there are none. Therefore, to make the graph Eulerian, we have to reduce the number of odd-degree vertices from 4 to 0 or 2. Since we can't remove edges between two odd-degree vertices, each edge removal that we do will affect one odd-degree vertex and one even-degree vertex, flipping the parity of both.\n\nSo, each edge removal will change the number of odd-degree vertices by -1 +1 = 0? Wait, let's see:\n\nSuppose we remove