 in our problem, we are dealing with a closed trail, which may have repeated vertices, but no repeated edges. So, it's a closed walk with no repeated edges. So, such a walk can be decomposed into a set of edge-disjoint cycles? Hmm, not necessarily, because the walk is connected, so actually, the entire trail must form a single connected component, which in this case, since the original graph is connected, the trail is connected, so it's a single closed trail which may have repeated vertices but not edges.\n\nHowever, in any case, in such a trail, the induced subgraph on the edges of the trail must be a graph where every vertex has even degree (since each time you enter a vertex you leave it, so the degree is even). Therefore, the trail corresponds to a collection of cycles, but since it's connected, it must be a single cycle? Wait, no. Wait, if you have a trail that is closed and has repeated vertices, but no repeated edges, then the induced subgraph on the edges is actually a graph where each vertex has even degree, but it might not be 2-regular. For example, you could have a vertex with degree 4 in the trail, meaning it was entered and exited twice. So, the trail could wind around through the graph, making loops but not repeating edges. But in any case, the key point is that the trail corresponds to an Eulerian circuit on some subgraph? Wait, no, because an Eulerian circuit requires that every vertex has even degree in the subgraph, which is exactly what we have here.\n\nWait, actually, if we consider the trail as a sequence of edges, then the trail forms a closed walk where each edge is used exactly once, so the trail is an Eulerian circuit on the trail's own induced subgraph? Wait, that might not make sense because the trail is a walk, not a subgraph. Wait, perhaps better to think of the trail as a subgraph where the edges are those traversed, and the vertices are those visited. Then, in this subgraph, every vertex has even degree, as each time you enter a vertex, you leave it, so the degree is even. Therefore, the trail corresponds to an Eulerian circuit on that subgraph? Wait, no. Wait, an Eulerian circuit is a closed walk that traverses each edge exactly once. So, if the trail is a closed walk that traverses each edge exactly once, then it is an Eulerian circuit on the original graph. However, in our case, the trail is just a closed walk that doesn't repeat edges, but it might not traverse all edges. So, the trail is an Eulerian circuit on some subgraph of the original graph, where the subgraph consists of the edges traversed in the trail, and that subgraph must have all vertices of even degree. Therefore, the trail is an Eulerian circuit on its own subgraph.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding a subgraph of the 11x11 grid graph which is Eulerian (i.e., all vertices have even degree) and has as many edges as possible. Then, the maximum number of doors (edges) Karthik can traverse is equal to the number of edges in such a maximum Eulerian subgraph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps even more precise: since the trail is a closed walk that doesn't repeat edges, the trail corresponds exactly to an Eulerian circuit in some subgraph of the grid graph, and we want the subgraph with the maximum number of edges that has an Eulerian circuit. Therefore, the problem reduces to finding the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian subgraph of the 11x11 grid graph.\n\nAlternatively, since an Eulerian subgraph must have all vertices of even degree, then the maximum Eulerian subgraph would be the largest subgraph (in terms of number of edges) with all degrees even. But actually, we can have a subgraph with as many edges as possible, provided all degrees are even. But the question is, how do we maximize the number of edges in such a subgraph?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as starting from the entire grid graph and then removing a minimal set of edges so that the resulting graph has all vertices of even degree. Then, the number of edges removed would be the difference between the total number of edges in the grid and the number of edges in the maximum Eulerian subgraph. So, perhaps that approach?\n\nWait, let me recall that in any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree is even. So, in the original grid graph, how many vertices have odd degree?\n\nEarlier, we saw that in an 11x11 grid graph, the corner cells have degree 2 (even), the edge cells (not corners) have degree 3 (odd), and the inner cells have degree 4 (even). So, let's compute the number of vertices with odd degree.\n\nFirst, the grid is 11x11, so there are 4 corner cells, each with degree 2 (even).\n\nThen, the edge cells: the edges of the grid have cells that are on the border but not corners. Each side of the grid has 11 cells, but subtracting the two corners, so 9 edge cells per side. There are four sides, so 4 * 9 = 36 edge cells (non-corner). Each of these has degree 3 (odd).\n\nThen, the inner cells: total cells is 121, subtract the 4 corners and 36 edge cells: 121 - 4 - 36 = 81 inner cells. Each inner cell has degree 4 (even).\n\nTherefore, total number of vertices with odd degree: the 36 edge cells each have degree 3 (odd), so 36 vertices of odd degree.\n\nSince the total number of vertices with odd degree must be even (as per the handshaking lemma), and 36 is even, that's consistent.\n\nSo, to make the entire graph Eulerian, we need to make all vertices have even degree. Therefore, we need to adjust the degrees of these 36 vertices from odd to even. Since each time we add or remove an edge incident to a vertex, we can change its degree by ±2. Wait, actually, to change the parity of a vertex's degree, we need to change its degree by an odd number. Since each edge is shared between two vertices, changing the degree of one vertex by 1 would require changing another vertex's degree by 1 as well. Wait, actually, perhaps it's better to think in terms of edge removals or additions.\n\nWait, actually, in the context of the problem, we can't add edges; we can only traverse existing edges, but without repeating any. So, the subgraph we are considering is a subgraph of the original grid graph, so we can only remove edges, not add them. Therefore, to make all vertices have even degree in the subgraph, we need to remove a set of edges such that the degrees of all vertices become even, and we want to remove as few edges as possible (so that the remaining subgraph has as many edges as possible).\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: what is the minimum number of edges we need to remove from the 11x11 grid graph so that all vertices have even degree? Then, the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian subgraph is equal to the total number of edges in the original graph minus the minimum number of edges to remove.\n\nTherefore, the question now is: what is the minimum number of edges to remove from the grid graph to make all degrees even?\n\nThis is a classic problem in graph theory: the minimum number of edges to remove to make all degrees even. I believe this is equivalent to finding a minimal edge set such that every vertex has even degree in the remaining graph. Alternatively, since removing an edge affects the degrees of two vertices, flipping their parities. So, we need to flip the parities of all the vertices with odd degree from odd to even. Since the original graph has 36 vertices of odd degree, we need to flip the parity of each of these 36 vertices. Each edge removal flips the parity of two vertices. Therefore, to flip the parity of 36 vertices, we need to perform edge removals such that each edge removal flips two parities. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is 36 / 2 = 18. Because each edge can fix two odd-degree vertices.\n\nWait, that seems promising. Let me think again.\n\nLet me formalize this:\n\nLet G be the original grid graph with vertices V and edges E.\n\nLet O be the set of vertices with odd degree in G. We know |O| = 36.\n\nWe want to find a subgraph H of G such that every vertex has even degree in H, and the number of edges in H is maximized, which is equivalent to minimizing the number of edges removed (i.e., |E| - |E(H)| is minimized).\n\nTo achieve this, we need to remove a set of edges F such that in G \\ F, all vertices have even degree. The set F must satisfy that for each vertex v in O, the degree of v in G \\ F is even. Since the degree of v in G \\ F is deg_G(v) - deg_F(v), where deg_F(v) is the degree of v in F (i.e., the number of edges incident to v that are removed). Therefore, we need deg_G(v) - deg_F(v) ≡ 0 mod 2, so deg_F(v) ≡ deg_G(v) mod 2. Since deg_G(v) is odd for v in O and even for v not in O, we require that for v in O, deg_F(v) is odd, and for v not in O, deg_F(v) is even.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding a set F of edges such that for each vertex v in O, the degree of v in F is odd, and for each vertex not in O, the degree of v in F is even. This is equivalent to finding a set of edges F that forms an edge set with all degrees even except for those in O, which have odd degrees. Such a set F is called an \"odd edge set\" or something, but more formally, it's a subset of edges such that the induced subgraph on F has all degrees even except for the vertices in O, which have odd degrees.\n\nHowever, in graph theory, such a set F is called an \"edge set with prescribed parities\" or something similar. Alternatively, this is equivalent to finding a subset F of E such that the symmetric difference of F and the edge set of some Eulerian subgraph? Hmm, maybe not directly.\n\nAlternatively, note that the problem is equivalent to finding a minimal set of edges F such that F forms a \"T-join\" where T is the set of vertices with odd degrees in G. Wait, yes! A T-join is a set of edges such that every vertex in T has odd degree in the subgraph induced by F, and every vertex not in T has even degree in the subgraph induced by F. Exactly! So in this case, T is the set of vertices with odd degrees in G, which is O, the 36 vertices on the edges (non-corner) of the grid. Therefore, the minimal set F we need to remove is a minimal T-join for T = O. Then, the minimal number of edges in such a T-join is equal to |T| / 2 if the graph is connected? Wait, actually, in general, the minimal size of a T-join in a connected graph is |T|/2 when |T| is even, which it is here (36 is even). Wait, is that a theorem?\n\nYes, I believe that in any connected graph, the minimal size of a T-join is |T|/2, provided that T is a subset of vertices with even cardinality, which it is here (36). Wait, actually, more precisely, in a connected graph, the minimal T-join has size |T|/2. Is that correct?\n\nLet me recall: A T-join is a set of edges such that every vertex in T has odd degree in the T-join and every vertex not in T has even degree in the T-join. The minimal T-join is called the \"minimum T-join\". For a connected graph, it is known that the minimum T-join has size exactly |T|/2 when |T| is even. Wait, is that the case?\n\nWait, actually, according to some references I recall, in any graph, the minimum size of a T-join is at least |T|/2, and this is achievable if the graph is connected. Since in our case the grid graph is connected, then the minimal T-join has size exactly |T|/2 = 18. Therefore, the minimal number of edges we need to remove is 18. Therefore, the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian subgraph (i.e., the subgraph after removing the T-join) is equal to the total number of edges in the original graph minus 18.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute the total number of edges in the original grid graph, then subtract 18, and that should give the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse.\n\nSo, let's compute the total number of edges in the 11x11 grid graph.\n\nIn a grid graph of m rows and n columns, the number of edges is calculated as follows: horizontal edges and vertical edges.\n\nFor horizontal edges: in each row, there are (n - 1) horizontal edges, and there are m rows. So total horizontal edges: m*(n - 1).\n\nSimilarly, vertical edges: in each column, there are (m - 1) vertical edges, and there are n columns. So total vertical edges: n*(m - 1).\n\nTherefore, total edges = m*(n - 1) + n*(m - 1) = 2*m*n - m - n.\n\nIn our case, m = 11, n = 11. Therefore,\n\nTotal edges = 2*11*11 - 11 - 11 = 2*121 - 22 = 242 - 22 = 220.\n\nSo the original grid graph has 220 edges.\n\nTherefore, if we remove a minimal T-join of size 18, then the remaining graph has 220 - 18 = 202 edges. Therefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 202?\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, the problem says Karthik is walking a path that starts and ends at the same cell, without repeating any door (edge). So, that path is a closed trail, which is an Eulerian circuit on the remaining subgraph (the one with 202 edges). Since the remaining subgraph has all vertices of even degree, it does have an Eulerian circuit, which traverses every edge exactly once. Therefore, the length of the Eulerian circuit is equal to the number of edges in the subgraph, which is 202. Therefore, the maximum number of doors he can go through is 202?\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. Wait, an Eulerian circuit in a graph with E edges has exactly E edges, right? So if the subgraph has 202 edges and is Eulerian, then the circuit will traverse all 202 edges, so the number of doors traversed is 202. Therefore, that would be the maximum, since you can't traverse more edges than exist in the graph without repeating.\n\nBut let me confirm again: the key idea here is that by removing the minimal number of edges (a T-join of size 18) to make the remaining graph Eulerian, then we can have an Eulerian circuit which uses all the remaining edges, hence 202 doors. Since we cannot have an Eulerian circuit on the original graph (because it's not Eulerian), so we have to remove edges until it becomes Eulerian, and the minimal number to remove is 18, so 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 202?\n\nBut let me think again. Is the minimal T-join indeed 18? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a mistake here. Let me think through the T-join concept again.\n\nIn a connected graph, the minimum T-join size is indeed |T| / 2 when |T| is even. Since our graph is connected (the grid graph is connected), and |T| = 36, which is even, so the minimal T-join has size 18. Therefore, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in some cases, even in a connected graph, you might need more? Wait, let me see with a small example.\n\nSuppose we have a simple graph that is a path graph with four vertices: A - B - C - D. The degrees are: A:1, B:2, C:2, D:1. So the set T of odd degree vertices is {A, D}, size 2. The minimal T-join should be of size 1. Indeed, the edge B-C is a T-join: if we remove edge B-C, then the degrees become A:1, B:1, C:1, D:1. Wait, that's worse. Wait, no, actually, a T-join is a set of edges such that the induced subgraph on T has all vertices of odd degree, and the rest even. Wait, actually, the T-join itself must have the property that the subgraph induced by the T-join has the degrees as required? Wait, maybe I got confused.\n\nWait, actually, in the T-join definition, the T-join is a subset of edges F such that in the subgraph induced by F, every vertex in T has odd degree and every vertex not in T has even degree. Wait, no: actually, in the T-join, the degrees in the entire graph? Wait, no, actually, no. Wait, confusion here.\n\nWait, let me check the exact definition. From Wikipedia: \"In graph theory, a T-join is a set of edges in a graph G such that every vertex in a subset T of the vertices has an odd degree in the subgraph induced by the edges of the T-join, and every vertex not in T has an even degree in the subgraph induced by the edges of the T-join.\"\n\nWait, so the T-join is a subset F of edges such that in the subgraph F, the induced degrees satisfy: for v in T, deg_F(v) is odd; for v not in T, deg_F(v) is even. So, the T-join is a set of edges whose induced subgraph has the prescribed parities.\n\nTherefore, in our problem, we need to remove a set F of edges such that in the remaining graph G \\ F, all vertices have even degree. Therefore, the set F must satisfy that in G \\ F, the degrees are even. Therefore, the degrees in G \\ F are equal to deg_G(v) - deg_F(v). Therefore, we require that deg_G(v) - deg_F(v) ≡ 0 mod 2 for all v. Therefore, deg_F(v) ≡ deg_G(v) mod 2 for all v. Therefore, for vertices in T (original odd degree), we need deg_F(v) ≡ 1 mod 2, and for others, deg_F(v) ≡ 0 mod 2. Therefore, the set F must be a T-join where T is the set of vertices with odd degree in G. Wait, exactly! So F must be a T-join where T is the set of original odd-degree vertices. Therefore, the minimal size of F is the minimal T-join, which, in a connected graph, is |T| / 2. Since |T| is even, this is an integer.\n\nTherefore, in our case, |T| = 36, so the minimal size is 18. Therefore, removing 18 edges, forming a T-join, will result in G \\ F having all even degrees, so it's Eulerian, and the number of edges remaining is 220 - 18 = 202, so the Eulerian circuit will have 202 edges, so Karthik can traverse 202 doors.\n\nTherefore, is 202 the correct answer?\n\nWait, but before I get too confident, let me think if there's a possible mistake here. For instance, is the grid graph really connected? Yes, it's a grid, so definitely connected.\n\nAnother possible issue: in the grid graph, are there any other constraints on the T-join? For instance, can we actually find a T-join of size 18 in the grid graph?\n\nAlternatively, maybe my assumption that the minimal T-join size is |T|/2 is incorrect in the case of the grid graph? Let me see.\n\nSuppose we have a grid graph. Since it's a bipartite graph, as we mentioned earlier, with partitions of size 66 and 55. The set T of vertices with odd degree are the 36 edge non-corner cells. Let me see: in the bipartite graph, the T-join must consist of a set of edges that flip the parity of exactly the vertices in T. Since the grid is bipartite, all edges go between the two partitions. Let me denote the two partitions as A and B, where A has 66 vertices and B has 55 vertices. The vertices in T (the 36 odd-degree vertices) are all in partition B? Wait, no, let's see.\n\nWait, in the grid graph, the corners are in, say, partition A (assuming the top-left corner is black). Let me think: in a chessboard coloring, the corners are all the same color. Since it's an 11x11 grid, which is odd-sized, all four corners will be the same color. Let me suppose the top-left corner is black. Then the top row will have black, white, black, ..., ending with black (since 11 is odd). Similarly, the first column will be black, white, black, ..., ending with black. Therefore, the four corners are all black. The other edge cells (non-corner) on the top and bottom rows (which are rows 1 and 11) and the first and last columns (columns 1 and 11) will alternate between black and white. Since the corners are black, the edge cells adjacent to them will be white, then black, etc.\n\nSpecifically, for the top row (row 1), starting at column 1 (black), then column 2 (white), column 3 (black), ..., column 11 (black). So the non-corner edge cells on the top row are columns 2 through 10, which are 9 cells. Each of these cells: starting at column 2 (white), then alternating. Since there are 9 cells, which is odd, so the colors will be white, black, white, ..., ending with black (since 9 is odd). Wait, starting at column 2 (white), then column 3 (black), ..., column 10 (black), column 11 is corner (black). Wait, actually column 10 would be column 10: since starting at column 1 (black), column 2 (white), column 3 (black), ..., so column 10: since 10 is even, column 10 would be white? Wait, let me compute:\n\nColumn 1: black\n\nColumn 2: white\n\nColumn 3: black\n\nColumn 4: white\n\n...\n\nColumn 10: since 10 is even, column 10 is white\n\nColumn 11: black (corner)\n\nTherefore, on the top row, the non-corner edge cells (columns 2-10) are columns 2 to 10, which is 9 cells. Starting at column 2 (white), alternating. So the colors are white, black, white, ..., ending at column 10 (white). Since 9 cells: positions 2 to 10: that's 9 cells. Since starting at white, and 9 is odd, the last one (column 10) is white. Wait, column 10 is even column number, so column 10 is white, yes. Therefore, on the top row non-corner edge cells, there are 5 white and 4 black cells? Wait, let's count:\n\nStarting at column 2 (white):\n\nPositions 2: white\n\n3: black\n\n4: white\n\n5: black\n\n6: white\n\n7: black\n\n8: white\n\n9: black\n\n10: white\n\nSo that's columns 2 to 10: 9 cells. Let's count the number of white and black:\n\nWhite at columns 2,4,6,8,10: that's 5 white cells.\n\nBlack at columns 3,5,7,9: that's 4 black cells.\n\nSimilarly, the bottom row (row 11) is the same as the top row, since it's also an odd row. So the non-corner edge cells on the bottom row (columns 2-10) will also have 5 white and 4 black cells.\n\nNow, the leftmost column (column 1) is all black, except for the non-corner cells. The non-corner cells in column 1 are rows 2 through 10. Since column 1 is all black, and each row alternates, so row 2 (second row) in column 1 is white? Wait, no: in column 1, the first cell (row 1, column 1) is black. Then row 2, column 1: since it's the next row, it would be white, then row 3, column 1: black, etc. So in column 1, non-corner cells (rows 2-10) are 9 cells, alternating starting with white. So similar to the top row, column 1's non-corner cells have 5 white and 4 black cells.\n\nSimilarly, the rightmost column (column 11) is all black, with non-corner cells (rows 2-10) also alternating starting with white, so 5 white and 4 black cells.\n\nTherefore, in total, for all four sides:\n\nTop row non-corner: 5 white, 4 black\n\nBottom row non-corner: 5 white, 4 black\n\nLeft column non-corner: 5 white, 4 black\n\nRight column non-corner: 5 white, 4 black\n\nTotal edge non-corner cells:\n\nEach side has 9 cells, 4 sides: 36 cells. Let's compute total white and black among these 36:\n\nTop and bottom rows: each has 5 white, so 10 white; each has 4 black, so 8 black.\n\nLeft and right columns: each has 5 white and 4 black, so total for left and right: 10 white and 8 black.\n\nTotal over all four sides: white: 10 + 10 = 20; black: 8 + 8 = 16.\n\nTherefore, among the 36 edge non-corner cells (the set T), there are 20 white and 16 black cells.\n\nRecall that the partitions A and B of the bipartite graph: partition A has 66 cells, partition B has 55 cells.\n\nSince the corners are all in partition A (black), and the rest of the grid alternates.\n\nTotal black cells (partition A): 66. The corners are 4 black cells, so the remaining black cells in the inner part: 66 - 4 = 62.\n\nTotal white cells (partition B): 55. The edge non-corner cells that are white: we just calculated 20 white edge non-corner cells. The remaining white cells are in the inner part: 55 - 20 = 35.\n\nTherefore, in the set T (the 36 edge non-corner cells), there are 20 white and 16 black cells.\n\nNow, in the T-join, which is a set of edges that will be removed, such that in the remaining graph, all degrees are even.\n\nSince the T-join must flip the parity of the vertices in T. Since the T-join is a set of edges, each edge in the T-join is between a white and a black vertex (because the graph is bipartite). Each edge in the T-join will contribute to the degree of one white and one black vertex. Therefore, each edge in the T-join flips the parity of one white and one black vertex in T.\n\nWait, actually, more precisely: in the original graph, the vertices in T (the 36 edge non-corner cells) have odd degrees. We need to remove edges so that their degrees become even. Each edge removed from a vertex in T will decrease its degree by 1, so flipping its parity. However, each edge is shared between two vertices, so removing an edge affects two vertices.\n\nBut in terms of the T-join, the set F (edges to remove) must be such that for each vertex in T, the number of edges incident to it in F is odd (so that deg_G(v) - deg_F(v) is even), and for vertices not in T, the number of edges incident to them in F is even.\n\nBut given the bipartition, let's consider the two partitions A and B. The vertices in T are all in the edge non-corner cells, which we've categorized as 20 white (in partition B) and 16 black (in partition A). Wait, hold on: partition A is the black cells, so the black edge non-corner cells are in partition A, and white edge non-corner cells are in partition B.\n\nTherefore, the set T has 16 vertices in partition A and 20 vertices in partition B.\n\nNow, the T-join must consist of edges such that:\n\n- For each vertex in T (i.e., the 16 black edge non-corner in A and 20 white edge non-corner in B), the number of edges in F incident to them must be odd.\n\n- For all other vertices (the remaining 66 - 16 = 50 in partition A and 55 - 20 = 35 in partition B), the number of edges in F incident to them must be even.\n\nBut since the graph is bipartite, all edges go between A and B. So, edges in F can only connect A to B.\n\nLet me think of the T-join as a set of edges where:\n\n- For the 16 vertices in T ∩ A (black edge non-corner), each must have an odd number of edges in F incident to them.\n\n- For the 20 vertices in T ∩ B (white edge non-corner), each must have an odd number of edges in F incident to them.\n\n- For the remaining 50 vertices in A \\ T and 35 vertices in B \\ T, each must have an even number of edges in F incident to them.\n\nNow, in a bipartite graph, the edges in F must satisfy these degree conditions. Since all edges go between A and B, the total number of edges in F must satisfy some parity conditions.\n\nLet me consider the total \"deficit\" in terms of odd degrees.\n\nEach vertex in T ∩ A needs an odd number of edges in F, so each contributes +1 mod 2 to the count of edges at that vertex. Similarly for T ∩ B. The other vertices need even, so 0 mod 2.\n\nThe total number of \"odd\" requirements is 16 + 20 = 36, which is even, so that's consistent.\n\nNow, in bipartite graphs, the sum over all vertices in A of their degrees in F must equal the sum over all vertices in B of their degrees in F, since each edge in F connects A to B. Therefore, the total number of edges in F must be equal to both the sum over A and the sum over B. Since the sum over A must be congruent mod 2 to the number of vertices in T ∩ A (since each contributes 1 mod 2, and others 0 mod 2). Similarly, the sum over B must be congruent mod 2 to the number of vertices in T ∩ B.\n\nTherefore, the total number of edges in F must be congruent mod 2 to both 16 and 20. Since 16 and 20 are both even, so the total number of edges in F must be even. Which is consistent with our previous conclusion that |F| = 18, which is even.\n\nBut is there any other constraint?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a flow problem or use some other method, but maybe it's more straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, since the minimal T-join in a connected bipartite graph can be constructed by pairing up the vertices in T and connecting them appropriately. Since the minimal size is |T|/2, which is 18, then such a T-join exists.\n\nTherefore, assuming that such a T-join can be constructed in the grid graph, then removing those 18 edges will result in an Eulerian graph with 202 edges, so the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 202.\n\nHowever, just to be thorough, let me consider a smaller grid to see if this reasoning holds.\n\nSuppose we have a 3x3 grid. Let's see what happens there.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, total cells = 9. The corners have degree 2 (even), the edge non-corner cells (there are 4 of them) have degree 3 (odd), and the center cell has degree 4 (even). So, the set T of vertices with odd degree has size 4. Therefore, minimal T-join should be size 2. Total edges in 3x3 grid: horizontal edges: 3 rows * 2 edges per row = 6; vertical edges: 3 columns * 2 edges per column = 6; total edges = 12.\n\nIf we remove a T-join of size 2, then remaining edges = 10. Then, the Eulerian circuit would have 10 edges. Let me see if that works.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to actually construct such a T-join in the 3x3 grid.\n\nThe grid:\n\nLet me label the cells as follows, with coordinates (row, column), 1-based:\n\nCorners: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) – all have degree 2 (even).\n\nEdge non-corner cells: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2) – each has degree 3 (odd). These are the T set.\n\nCenter cell (2,2) has degree 4 (even).\n\nWe need to remove a set of 2 edges such that the remaining graph has all even degrees.\n\nLet me try to remove two edges. Let's pick two edges that are incident to the T vertices appropriately.\n\nFor example, remove the edge between (1,2) and (1,1), and the edge between (2,1) and (1,1). Wait, but (1,1) is a corner with even degree. If we remove two edges incident to (1,1), which originally had degree 2, then its degree becomes 0, which is even. The vertex (1,2) had degree 3; removing one edge (to (1,1)) reduces its degree to 2, which is even. Similarly, (2,1) had degree 3; removing the edge to (1,1) reduces it to 2, which is even. However, the other edges connected to (1,2) and (2,1) remain. Wait, but then we might have other vertices with odd degrees.\n\nWait, let me be precise. Let me denote the edges:\n\nFirst, list all edges in the 3x3 grid:\n\nHorizontal edges:\n\nRow 1: between (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3)\n\nRow 2: between (2,1)-(2,2), (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nRow 3: between (3,1)-(3,2), (3,2)-(3,3)\n\nVertical edges:\n\nColumn 1: between (1,1)-(2,1), (2,1)-(3,1)\n\nColumn 2: between (1,2)-(2,2), (2,2)-(3,2)\n\nColumn 3: between (1,3)-(2,3), (2,3)-(3,3)\n\nSo total edges: 12 as expected.\n\nThe T set is the four edge non-corner cells: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2).\n\nEach of these has degree 3.\n\nLet me attempt to remove two edges to make all degrees even.\n\nSuppose I remove the edge (1,2)-(1,3) and the edge (2,1)-(3,1).\n\nThen, let's see the degrees:\n\nVertex (1,2): originally degree 3. It loses the edge to (1,3), so now degree 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,1): originally degree 3. It loses the edge to (3,1), so now degree 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,3): still has degree 3 (since we didn't remove any edges incident to it). Wait, that's a problem. Vertex (2,3) is in T and still has degree 3 (odd). Similarly, vertex (3,2): still has degree 3 (odd). The other T vertices: (1,2) and (2,1) are fixed, but (2,3) and (3,2) are still odd.\n\nSo that didn't work.\n\nAlternatively, try removing edges (1,2)-(2,2) and (3,2)-(2,2). Let's see:\n\nVertex (1,2): loses edge to (2,2), so degree becomes 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,2): loses two edges, so degree from 4 to 2 (even). But (2,2) wasn't in T, so that's okay.\n\nVertex (3,2): loses edge to (2,2), so degree becomes 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,3): still has degree 3 (odd). Hmm, so (2,3) is still odd.\n\nHmm, not good.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(1,1) and (2,3)-(3,3). Let's see:\n\nVertex (1,2): loses edge to (1,1), degree 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,3): loses edge to (3,3), degree 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,1): still degree 3 (odd).\n\nVertex (3,2): still degree 3 (odd).\n\nStill two vertices with odd degrees.\n\nHmm, tricky. Maybe remove edges (1,2)-(2,2) and (3,2)-(2,2). Wait, that was tried earlier.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(1,1) and (2,1)-(3,1), and also (2,3)-(3,3) and (3,2)-(3,1). Wait, but that's four edges, which would be too many. We need only two edges.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach. Let's try to make pairs.\n\nEach edge we remove affects two vertices. To fix the four odd-degree vertices, we need to pair them up and remove an edge between each pair? But they are not directly connected.\n\nAlternatively, since in bipartite graph, the T set has 16 in A and 20 in B in the original problem, but in this 3x3 case, the T set has 4 vertices: 2 in partition A and 2 in partition B?\n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid, partitions: since it's a chessboard coloring, the corners (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) are all the same color, say black (partition A). The edge non-corner cells: (1,2) is white (partition B), (2,1) is white (partition B), (2,3) is white (partition B), and (3,2) is white (partition B). Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, in a 3x3 grid, with (1,1) black, then:\n\n(1,1): black (A)\n\n(1,2): white (B)\n\n(1,3): black (A)\n\n(2,1): white (B)\n\n(2,2): black (A)\n\n(2,3): white (B)\n\n(3,1): black (A)\n\n(3,2): white (B)\n\n(3,3): black (A)\n\nTherefore, the T set (edge non-corner cells with odd degree) are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), all of which are in partition B (white). Therefore, all four vertices in T are in partition B. Therefore, in this case, the T-join must be a set of edges such that each of these four vertices has odd degree in F, and all other vertices have even degree in F.\n\nSince all T vertices are in B, and edges go from A to B, each edge in F must connect a vertex in A to a vertex in B. To make the four vertices in B have odd degrees in F, and the rest even.\n\nSince all four T vertices are in B, and the other vertices in B (there are total 5 white cells: since total cells 9, 5 in B: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), and (2,2)). Wait, no: (2,2) is black (A). Wait, partition B has the white cells: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), and (2,2)? Wait no, (2,2) is black. Wait, in a 3x3 grid, the center (2,2) is black. So partition B has four white cells: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). The fifth white cell? Wait no, total cells: 9. Partition A has 5 cells (black): (1,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,1), (3,3). Partition B has 4 cells (white): (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Wait, that adds up to 9. So in the 3x3 grid, partition B has only 4 cells. Therefore, the T set (the four edge non-corner cells which are all in B) are exactly the entire partition B. Therefore, in this case, T is the entire partition B.\n\nTherefore, to form a T-join where every vertex in B has odd degree in F, and every vertex not in T (i.e., partition A) has even degree in F.\n\nSince all vertices in B must have odd degree in F, and all vertices in A must have even degree in F.\n\nIn a bipartite graph, the sum of degrees in A must equal the sum of degrees in B (since each edge goes from A to B). Since each vertex in B has odd degree, and there are 4 vertices in B, the total sum over B is 4*(odd) = even. Therefore, the total sum over A must also be even, which is okay because each vertex in A must have even degree.\n\nTherefore, it is possible.\n\nNow, to construct such a T-join (a set F of edges where each vertex in B has odd degree and each in A has even degree). Since all of B must have odd degrees, and all of A must have even degrees.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, how can we do this with minimal edges?\n\nLet me try to pick two edges:\n\nSuppose we remove edges (1,2)-(2,2) and (3,2)-(2,2). Then:\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): loses one edge (to (2,2)), so degree becomes 2 (original degree 3) → odd minus 1 is even? Wait, no:\n\nWait, original degree of (1,2) is 3: connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). If we remove the edge to (2,2), then its degree becomes 2, which is even. But we needed it to be odd. Oops, that's the opposite.\n\nWait, actually, we need the degree in F to be odd for vertices in B. Wait no, confusion again.\n\nWait, sorry, let's clarify.\n\nThe set F is the set of edges we are removing. The requirement is that in the remaining graph G \\ F, all vertices have even degree. Therefore, the degrees in G \\ F are equal to original degrees minus the degrees in F. So to have deg_{G \\ F}(v) even, we need deg_G(v) - deg_F(v) ≡ 0 mod 2, so deg_F(v) ≡ deg_G(v) mod 2.\n\nTherefore, for vertices in T (B in this case), deg_F(v) must be odd (since deg_G(v) was odd). For vertices not in T (A), deg_F(v) must be even (since deg_G(v) was even).\n\nTherefore, in the set F (edges removed), the degrees in F for vertices in B must be odd, and for A must be even.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 example, we need to choose a set F of edges such that:\n\n- Each of the four B vertices (the white cells) has an odd number of edges in F.\n\n- Each of the five A vertices (black cells) has an even number of edges in F.\n\nWe need the minimal such F.\n\nLet me try with two edges:\n\nSuppose we remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (3,2) and (2,2). Then:\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): degree in F = 1 (odd) – good.\n\n(2,1): degree in F = 0 (even) – bad, needs to be odd.\n\n(2,3): degree in F = 0 (even) – bad.\n\n(3,2): degree in F = 1 (odd) – good.\n\nSo two B vertices are good, two are bad. Not sufficient.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(1,1) and (3,2)-(3,3):\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): degree in F = 1 (odd) – good.\n\n(2,1): 0 – bad.\n\n(2,3): 0 – bad.\n\n(3,2): 1 (odd) – good.\n\nStill same problem.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(2,1) and (3,2)-(2,3). Wait, but those edges don't exist. Wait, in the 3x3 grid, (1,2) is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). It is not connected to (2,1). Similarly, (3,2) is connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2). It is not connected to (2,3). So those edges don't exist. So can't remove those.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(2,2) and (2,1)-(3,1):\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): degree in F = 1 (odd) – good.\n\n(2,1): degree in F = 1 (odd) – good.\n\n(2,3): 0 – bad.\n\n(3,2): 0 – bad.\n\nStill two bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(2,2) and (2,3)-(2,2):\n\nThen:\n\n(1,2): 1 (good)\n\n(2,3): 1 (good)\n\n(2,1): 0 (bad)\n\n(3,2): 0 (bad)\n\nSame as before.\n\nAlternatively, remove three edges: but we need minimal.\n\nWait, perhaps three edges?\n\nSuppose we remove edges (1,2)-(2,2), (2,1)-(3,1), and (2,3)-(3,3):\n\nThen:\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): 1 (good)\n\n(2,1): 1 (good)\n\n(2,3): 1 (good)\n\n(3,2): 0 (bad)\n\nStill one bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(2,2), (2,1)-(3,1), (3,2)-(2,2), and (2,3)-(3,3). That's four edges. Then:\n\n(1,2): 1 (good)\n\n(2,1): 1 (good)\n\n(2,3): 1 (good)\n\n(3,2): 1 (good)\n\nAll B vertices have odd degrees in F. Now check A vertices:\n\n(1,1): connected to (1,2) and (2,1). In F, edges removed: none from (1,1), since we removed (1,2)-(2,2) and (2,1)-(3,1). So (1,1)'s degree in F is 0 (even) – good.\n\n(1,3): connected to (1,2) and (2,3). In F, removed edge (1,2)-(2,2) and (2,3)-(3,3). So (1,3)'s degree in F: only the edge to (1,2) is removed? Wait no: (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (2,3). The edge (1,3)-(1,2) is not removed; we only removed (1,2)-(2,2). Similarly, the edge (2,3)-(3,3) is removed, but that's from (2,3) to (3,3). So (1,3)'s edges in F: only if any of its edges are in F. The edge between (1,3) and (2,3) is not removed. The edge between (1,3) and (1,2) is not removed. So (1,3)'s degree in F is 0 – even, good.\n\n(2,2): connected to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). The edges removed involving (2,2) are (1,2)-(2,2) and (3,2)-(2,2). So degree in F: 2, which is even – good.\n\n(3,1): connected to (2,1) and (3,2). The edge (2,1)-(3,1) is removed, and (3,2) is connected to (3,1) via an edge? Wait, (3,1) is connected to (3,2) horizontally? Yes, in row 3, columns 1 and 2 are adjacent. So the edge between (3,1) and (3,2) is present. But in F, we removed (3,2)-(2,2), but not (3,1)-(3,2). So (3,1)'s degree in F: only the edge to (2,1) is removed, so degree in F is 1 (odd) – which is bad, because (3,1) is in A and needs even degree in F.\n\nAh, problem here. (3,1) is in partition A (black), and its degree in F must be even. But in this case, it has degree 1 in F, which is odd. So that's bad.\n\nSo this attempt also fails.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Maybe three edges isn't enough? Let's try with three edges.\n\nSuppose we remove edges: (1,2)-(2,2), (2,1)-(3,1), and (3,2)-(2,2). Then:\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): 1 (good)\n\n(2,1): 1 (good)\n\n(2,3): 0 (bad)\n\n(3,2): 1 (good)\n\nStill (2,3) is bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(2,2), (2,3)-(3,3), and (3,2)-(2,2):\n\nThen