ry removing edges:\n\n(1,2)-(2,2),\n\n(2,1)-(3,1),\n\n(2,3)-(3,3),\n\n(3,2)-(3,1).\n\nWait, but (3,2)-(3,1) is a horizontal edge in row 3. Let's see:\n\nThen:\n\nFor B vertices:\n\n(1,2): 1 (good)\n\n(2,1): 1 (good)\n\n(2,3): 1 (good)\n\n(3,2): 1 (good)\n\nFor A vertices:\n\n(3,1): connected to (3,2) and (2,1). Edges removed: (2,1)-(3,1) and (3,2)-(3,1). So degree in F: 2 (even) – good.\n\n(3,3): connected to (2,3) and (3,2). Edges removed: (2,3)-(3,3) and none on (3,2)-(3,3). So degree in F: 1 (odd) – bad.\n\nHmm, still one bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove edge (3,2)-(3,3) as well. That's five edges.\n\nNow, (3,3)'s degree in F: 1 (from (2,3)-(3,3)) and 1 (from (3,2)-(3,3))? Wait, if we remove (3,2)-(3,3), then (3,3)'s edges in F are (2,3)-(3,3) and (3,2)-(3,3), so degree 2 (even). Let's see:\n\nRemoving edges:\n\n(1,2)-(2,2),\n\n(2,1)-(3,1),\n\n(2,3)-(3,3),\n\n(3,2)-(2,2),\n\n(3,2)-(3,3).\n\nNow:\n\nB vertices all have degree 1 in F (good).\n\nA vertices:\n\n(3,1): degree in F: 1 (from (2,1)-(3,1)) – odd. Bad.\n\n(3,3): degree in F: 2 (from (2,3)-(3,3) and (3,2)-(3,3)) – even. Good.\n\n(2,2): degree in F: 2 (from (1,2)-(2,2) and (3,2)-(2,2)) – even. Good.\n\n(1,1): degree 0 – good.\n\n(1,3): degree 0 – good.\n\n(3,1) still has odd degree in F. So still bad.\n\nHmm. This is tricky. Maybe five edges? Remove one more edge involving (3,1). But (3,1) is connected to (2,1) and (3,2). We already removed (2,1)-(3,1). If we remove (3,1)-(3,2), then:\n\nAdding that edge removal:\n\nEdges removed:\n\n(1,2)-(2,2),\n\n(2,1)-(3,1),\n\n(2,3)-(3,3),\n\n(3,2)-(2,2),\n\n(3,2)-(3,3),\n\n(3,1)-(3,2).\n\nNow:\n\n(3,1)'s degree in F: edges (2,1)-(3,1) and (3,1)-(3,2), so degree 2 – even. Good.\n\nBut now (3,2)'s degree in F: edges to (2,2), (3,3), and (3,1). So degree 3 – odd. But (3,2) is in B, needs odd – that's okay. Wait, but (3,2) is in B, so odd is okay. Wait, but we're done with B vertices.\n\nWait, let me check all:\n\nB vertices:\n\n(1,2): 1 (good)\n\n(2,1): 1 (good)\n\n(2,3): 1 