in the subgraph, and others have even degrees.\n   - The minimal T-join size in a connected graph is \\( \\frac{|T|}{2} \\) when \\( |T| \\) is even.\n\n5. **Calculations**:\n   - Total edges in the grid: \\( 11 \\times 10 \\times 2 = 220 \\).\n   - Minimal T-join size for 36 odd-degree vertices: \\( \\frac{36}{2} = 18 \\).\n   - Remaining edges after removing the T-join: \\( 220 - 18 = 202 \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - The remaining graph after removing the minimal T-join is Eulerian, allowing an Eulerian circuit that traverses all 202 edges.\n\nThus, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is \\(\\boxed{202}\\).","num_tokens":21563,"correct":false,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"202","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here about an 11x11 grid of cells. Each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door. Karthik wants to walk a path in this grid, and he can start in any cell, but he must end in the same cell he started in. Also, he cannot go through any door more than once, not even in opposite directions. The question is asking for the maximum number of doors he can go through in such a path. Alright, let me try to unpack this step by step.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the grid and the doors correctly. An 11x11 grid means there are 121 cells in total, right? Each cell is a square, and each cell is adjacent to its edge-adjacent neighbors. So, for any given cell, if it's not on the edge of the grid, it will have four doors: one to the north, one to the south, one to the east, and one to the west. But if it's on the edge, then it will have fewer doors—for example, a corner cell only has two doors, and an edge cell that's not a corner has three doors.\n\nNow, Karthik is going to walk a path where he can start at any cell, but he has to end up back at the starting cell. Also, he cannot go through any door more than once. That means every time he goes through a door, he can't use that same door again in any direction. So, if he goes through a door from cell A to cell B, he can't later go back from cell B to cell A through that same door. Furthermore, he can't traverse the same door in any other way either—since each door is between two specific cells, and each door can only be traversed once in one direction or the other, but not both.\n\nSo, essentially, the path Karthik takes must form a closed loop starting and ending at the same cell, with the constraint that no door is used more than once. Since he can start anywhere, we need to find the closed loop that visits the most number of doors without repeating any door. The question is asking for the maximum number of doors that can be traversed in such a path.\n\nHmm, okay. So, first, perhaps I should think about what kind of closed loops can be formed on a grid with doors between adjacent cells, without reusing any door. Since each door connects two cells, and once you traverse a door from A to B, you can't go back from B to A through that door. So, effectively, each door can be thought of as a directed edge in a graph, where each undirected edge (door) can be traversed in one direction or the other, but not both. However, in this case, the problem is phrased such that you can't go through any door more than once, regardless of direction. So, it's like an undirected graph where each edge (door) can be traversed at most once in either direction, but since the path must be a closed loop, it must form an Eulerian circuit?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait a second, an Eulerian circuit is a closed path that traverses every edge exactly once. But in this problem, Karthik is not required to traverse every door, just the maximum number possible without repeating any door. So, perhaps the problem is asking for the longest possible closed trail (a trail that starts and ends at the same vertex) in this grid graph where no edge is traversed more than once. Since trails are paths that do not repeat edges, but vertices can be repeated. Wait, but in an Eulerian circuit, you traverse every edge exactly once. But here, we don't necessarily have to traverse all edges, just as many as possible without repeating any.\n\nHowever, an Eulerian circuit exists in a graph if and only if the graph is connected and every vertex has even degree. If the graph is not connected, then you can have Eulerian circuits in each connected component, but if there are vertices with odd degrees, you can't have an Eulerian circuit. But in this problem, since the grid is connected, so the graph is connected. However, in the grid graph, each interior vertex has degree 4, which is even, but each edge vertex (on the boundary of the grid) has degree 3, which is odd. Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, in an 11x11 grid of cells, the vertices? Wait, hold on, actually, hold on. Wait, is the grid of cells considered as vertices, or are the vertices the intersections of the grid lines?\n\nWait, the problem says: \"In an 11 × 11 grid of cells, each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door.\" So, perhaps the cells are the vertices? Or are the vertices the points where the grid lines intersect?\n\nHmm, this is a bit ambiguous. Let me read the problem again.\n\n\"In an 11 × 11 grid of cells, each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door.\"\n\nSo, in a grid of cells, the cells are adjacent if they share a common edge. So, each cell is a square, and two cells are edge-adjacent if they share an entire edge. So, in graph terms, the grid can be represented as a graph where each cell is a vertex, and edges connect adjacent cells. Therefore, the graph is the grid graph of an 11x11 grid of vertices, which would actually be a 12x12 grid of points, right?\n\nWait, hold on. Let me clarify.\n\nIf you have an 11x11 grid of cells, then that corresponds to a grid of points (vertices) that are arranged in 12 rows and 12 columns, because to have 11 cells in a row, you need 12 vertical lines, and similarly 12 horizontal lines. Therefore, the grid graph here is actually a 12x12 grid of vertices, where each vertex is a corner point of the cells, and each edge of the grid graph connects two adjacent vertices (i.e., two points that are a distance of one unit apart horizontally or vertically). Then, the cells themselves are the faces of the grid graph.\n\nBut in the problem statement, it says: \"each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door.\" So, the doors are between cells, not between vertices. Therefore, perhaps the graph we are considering is the dual graph of the grid? Wait, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is considering the grid cells as the vertices of the graph, and the doors as the edges connecting adjacent cells. So, in that case, the graph would be a grid graph where each vertex corresponds to a cell in the 11x11 grid, and edges connect vertices (cells) that are edge-adjacent. Therefore, in this graph, the degree of each vertex (cell) would be 2, 3, or 4, depending on its position: corner cells have degree 2, edge cells (non-corner) have degree 3, and interior cells have degree 4. Wait, but actually, in an 11x11 grid of cells, the corner cells would be the four corners of the entire grid, each of which is adjacent to only two other cells (right and down, for example). The edge cells (on the sides but not corners) would be adjacent to three cells, and the interior cells would be adjacent to four cells.\n\nTherefore, in this graph representation, each cell is a vertex, and edges represent adjacency between cells, with doors being these edges. Therefore, the problem is about traversing a closed trail (a circuit) in this grid graph, starting and ending at the same vertex (cell), with the constraint that no edge (door) is traversed more than once. The question is to find the maximum number of edges (doors) that can be traversed in such a circuit.\n\nSo, since this is a graph theory question, perhaps we can recall some concepts here. In graph theory, a trail is a walk where no edge is repeated. A circuit is a trail that starts and ends at the same vertex. An Eulerian circuit is a circuit that traverses every edge exactly once. So, if the graph is Eulerian, meaning it is connected and all vertices have even degree, then it has an Eulerian circuit, which would traverse all edges. Therefore, in that case, the maximum number of doors would be equal to the total number of doors in the grid.\n\nHowever, in our case, the grid graph of an 11x11 grid of cells has vertices (cells) with degrees 2, 3, and 4. Since the corner cells have degree 2 (even), the edge cells (non-corner) have degree 3 (odd), and the interior cells have degree 4 (even). Therefore, the graph is not Eulerian because there are vertices with odd degrees (the edge cells). Therefore, an Eulerian circuit does not exist. Therefore, we cannot traverse every door in a single closed trail.\n\nTherefore, the problem is asking for the maximum-length closed trail (circuit) in this graph. Now, in graph theory, the concept of the longest trail is a bit tricky because it's not always straightforward. However, perhaps we can think about how to maximize the number of edges in a closed trail without repeating edges.\n\nFirst, perhaps we can recall that in any graph, the maximum length of a trail is bounded by the number of edges, but since we can't traverse all edges because of the odd degree vertices, we have to leave some edges unused. The question is, how many edges can we traverse in such a trail?\n\nI remember that in a graph, if you have vertices of odd degree, then the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian trail (which is a trail that covers all edges) is not possible, but you can have trails that cover all but a certain number of edges. Specifically, the classic result is that the minimum number of trails needed to cover all edges of a graph is equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, actually, more precisely, the minimum number of trails needed to cover all edges of a graph is equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2, rounded up or something? Wait, actually, according to Euler's theorem, the number of vertices with odd degree must be even, and then the minimum number of trails required to cover all edges is equal to half the number of odd-degree vertices. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me recall: In any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree is even. Then, the minimum number of trails needed to traverse every edge exactly once (an Eulerian trail decomposition) is equal to half the number of odd-degree vertices. Wait, actually, no, it's equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2. Let me check.\n\nSuppose you have a graph with k vertices of odd degree. Then, the minimum number of trails needed to cover all the edges is (k - 1)/2 if k is odd? Wait, no, actually, the formula is that the minimum number of trails needed to cover every edge exactly once is equal to (number of odd-degree vertices)/2. Wait, but since the number of odd-degree vertices must be even, then it's an integer. So, if there are k odd-degree vertices, then the minimum number of trails required is k/2. Hmm, actually, I need to recall the exact theorem.\n\nAh, yes, the classical result is that in any graph, the edge set can be partitioned into a set of trails, and the minimum number of trails required is equal to the maximum number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, no, actually, it's that the minimum number of trails needed to cover all edges is equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2, rounded up to the nearest integer? Wait, let me think of an example.\n\nTake a simple graph: a single vertex with a loop. Then, that vertex has degree 2 (since the loop contributes 2 to the degree). Wait, actually, in graph theory, a loop contributes 2 to the degree of the vertex. So, if you have a single vertex with a loop, the degree is 2, which is even, so the number of odd-degree vertices is 0, so the minimum number of trails needed to cover all edges is 0? That doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, perhaps I should recall that in a graph with no isolated vertices, the minimum number of trails needed to cover all edges is equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. But in the case of a single vertex with a loop, the number of odd-degree vertices is 0, so you need 0 trails? That also doesn't make sense because you have an edge (the loop) that needs to be covered. Wait, actually, a loop can be considered as a trail of length 1 that starts and ends at the same vertex. So, in that case, you can cover the loop with one trail. Hmm, so maybe the formula is that the minimum number of trails needed is (number of odd-degree vertices)/2, but when there are loops, they contribute to the degree, so perhaps it's better to think in terms of the standard Eulerian trail concepts.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to recall that in any graph, the edge set can be decomposed into 1-factors (perfect matchings) if the graph is 2k-regular, but that might not be directly helpful here.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the problem in terms of the degrees of the vertices. Since we want a closed trail (a circuit) that uses as many edges as possible without repeating any, the key issue is the vertices with odd degrees. In an Eulerian circuit, all vertices must have even degrees, so that every time you enter a vertex, you can leave it again. But in our case, since we have vertices with odd degrees, we can't have an Eulerian circuit. However, we can have a closed trail that uses all edges except for a set of edges that form a matching on the odd-degree vertices.\n\nWait, here's a theorem: In any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges needed to make all vertices even degree. That is, if you remove a set of edges such that all remaining vertices have even degree, then the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit, and the closed trail can be the Eulerian circuit on the remaining graph. Therefore, the maximum number of edges in a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of edges in a minimal edge set whose removal makes all degrees even.\n\nAlternatively, in other words, the minimal number of edges to remove to make the graph Eulerian is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2. Wait, let me recall.\n\nYes, according to the concept of an Eulerian trail, if a graph has exactly two vertices of odd degree, then it has an Eulerian trail (which is not necessarily a circuit). To make a graph Eulerian, you can add a edge between the two odd-degree vertices, but if you want to remove edges instead, then perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove would be related.\n\nAlternatively, here's a theorem: In any graph, the minimum number of edges that must be removed so that the remaining graph has all vertices of even degree is equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, let me see.\n\nSuppose you have a graph with k vertices of odd degree. Then, to make all degrees even, you need to remove or add edges such that each odd-degree vertex has its degree reduced by an odd number (if you remove edges) or increased by an odd number (if you add edges). Since we are only allowed to remove edges here, then for each odd-degree vertex, we can remove edges incident to it to make its degree even. However, each edge removal affects two vertices. So, if you remove an edge between two vertices, each of those vertices' degrees is reduced by 1. Therefore, to fix the parity of the degrees, you need to pair up the odd-degree vertices and remove edges between them appropriately.\n\nSpecifically, since the number of odd-degree vertices must be even, you can partition them into pairs, and for each pair, remove a path between them or something? Hmm, not exactly. Wait, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2. Let me think of an example.\n\nSuppose you have a simple graph with four vertices: A, B, C, D. Suppose A and C have degree 1, and B and D have degree 1. Then, the total number of odd-degree vertices is 4. To make all degrees even, you could remove the edges incident to A and C, but that might not be minimal. Alternatively, you can remove edges in such a way that each removal fixes two odd degrees. For instance, if you have edges AB, BC, CD, DA (forming a square), but with each vertex degree 2 except A and C, but in this case, actually all vertices would have degree 2. Wait, maybe my example is not good.\n\nWait, let's take a graph with two vertices of degree 3 and two vertices of degree 1. Let's say vertices A, B, C, D with edges: A connected to B, B connected to C, C connected to D, D connected to A, and also A connected to C, and B connected to D. So, in this graph, each vertex has degree: A is connected to B, D, and C: so degree 3; B is connected to A, C, and D: degree 3; C is connected to B, D, and A: degree 3; D is connected to C, A, and B: degree 3. Wait, so all degrees are 3, which is odd. So, four vertices of odd degree. To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that each vertex's degree becomes even. Since each vertex has degree 3, which is odd, we need to remove an odd number of edges from each vertex. The minimal number of edges to remove would be such that the total number of removed edges times 2 (since each edge removal affects two vertices) equals the total deficit in degrees. Each vertex needs to lose 1 degree (since 3 - 1 = 2, which is even). So each vertex needs to lose 1 degree. So total deficit is 4 vertices × 1 = 4, so total edges to remove would be 4 / 2 = 2 edges. For example, if we remove edges AC and BD, then each of those edges is incident to two vertices, so removing AC reduces the degree of A and C by 1 each, and removing BD reduces the degree of B and D by 1 each. Then, each vertex would have degree 2, which is even. So, two edges