tween adjacent cells.\n\nTherefore, in this graph, the degree of each vertex (cell) is as follows: corner cells have degree 2, edge cells (not corners) have degree 3, and interior cells have degree 4.\n\nTherefore, let's compute the number of vertices (cells) with odd degree. The corner cells: there are 4 corners, each with degree 2 (even). The edge cells: on each side of the grid, the number of edge cells (excluding corners) is (11 - 2) = 9. Since there are four sides, that would be 4 * 9 = 36 edge cells. Each of these has degree 3 (odd). The interior cells: the total number of cells is 11x11 = 121. Subtract the 4 corners and the 36 edge cells, so interior cells are 121 - 4 - 36 = 81. Each interior cell has degree 4 (even).\n\nTherefore, the number of vertices (cells) with odd degree is 36 (the edge cells, non-corner). Since 36 is even, that's consistent with the handshaking lemma, which requires that the number of vertices with odd degree is even.\n\nTherefore, according to the earlier theorem, the minimal number of edges we need to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, so 36 / 2 = 18 edges. Therefore, if we remove 18 edges, the remaining graph will have all vertices of even degree, and thus will have an Eulerian circuit. The number of edges in that Eulerian circuit would be the original number of edges (220) minus the 18 edges removed, so 220 - 18 = 202 edges. Therefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse in such a path would be 202?\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait a second. Let me verify this reasoning again because this seems a bit straightforward but I might be missing something.\n\nFirst, in the grid graph of 11x11 cells (vertices), we have 4 corners with degree 2 (even), 36 edge cells (non-corner) with degree 3 (odd), and 81 interior cells with degree 4 (even). So, 36 vertices of odd degree. To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that each of these 36 vertices has its degree reduced by an odd number (since they are odd, subtracting an odd number makes them even). However, each edge removal affects two vertices. So, each edge we remove can reduce the degree of two vertices by 1 each. Therefore, to fix the degrees of the 36 odd-degree vertices, we need to perform edge removals such that the total number of \"odd reductions\" is 36. Since each edge removal contributes 2 to the total reduction (1 for each of the two vertices), we need to remove 36 / 2 = 18 edges. Therefore, that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, if we remove 18 edges, then the remaining graph has all even degrees and is connected? Wait, is the remaining graph still connected?\n\nWait, hold on. The original grid graph is connected. If we remove 18 edges, it might become disconnected, but the Eulerian circuit requires the graph to be connected. So, actually, the theorem I was recalling earlier might have assumed that the graph remains connected after edge removals? Hmm, this is a critical point.\n\nIn the theorem, when we talk about decomposing the edge set into trails, we need the graph to remain connected. If by removing edges we disconnect the graph, then the Eulerian circuit can't exist on the disconnected graph. Therefore, perhaps my earlier reasoning is incomplete because removing edges might disconnect the graph, so we need to ensure that the remaining graph is still connected.\n\nHmm, so perhaps I need to think differently. The problem is that when you remove edges to make all degrees even, you might end up disconnecting the graph, which would prevent the existence of an Eulerian circuit. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove isn't just 18, because we might have to remove more edges to keep the graph connected.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the grid graph, even after removing 18 edges, the graph remains connected? Maybe the grid is so highly connected that removing 18 edges is not enough to disconnect it?\n\nHmm, this is a bit more complicated. Let me think.\n\nFirst, let's note that the grid graph is a planar graph, and it's quite robust. Removing a few edges probably won't disconnect it, especially since it's a grid. However, to be precise, let's consider how edges are arranged.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula still holds in terms of the maximum trail length, regardless of connectivity? Wait, no, an Eulerian circuit requires the graph to be connected. Therefore, if we remove edges in such a way that the graph becomes disconnected, then we can't have an Eulerian circuit on the whole remaining graph. Therefore, perhaps we need to remove edges in a way that keeps the graph connected, and then the number of edges we can traverse is total edges minus the number of edges removed to make all degrees even, but only if such a removal keeps the graph connected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a different approach here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of trying to remove edges to make an Eulerian circuit, we can consider that the maximum trail will have to start and end at the same vertex, and in doing so, it can traverse all edges except for a set of edges that form a matching on the odd-degree vertices. But perhaps in this grid, because of its structure, we can find a trail that covers almost all edges except for a certain number.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a graph and try to compute the maximum trail.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think in terms of the degrees. Each time you traverse an edge into a vertex with odd degree, you have to leave it in a way that doesn't create an imbalance.\n\nAlternatively, here's another thought: in any graph, the length of the longest trail is at most the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, but I need to verify.\n\nWait, actually, in the classic problem, the maximum trail in a graph is limited by the number of odd-degree vertices. Since each time you enter a vertex, you have to leave it, except for the start and end vertices. But in a closed trail (circuit), you must enter and exit every vertex the same number of times, so all vertices must have even degrees in the trail. Wait, but the trail is not required to use all edges. Wait, perhaps this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, let me recall that in any graph, the maximum length of a trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of edges in a minimal edge set that makes all vertices have even degree in the remaining graph. But this minimal edge set is called a \"Eulerian orientation\" or something?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps an easier way is to consider that in order to have a closed trail covering as many edges as possible, you can start at some vertex, and as you traverse edges, you have to make sure that you don't get \"stuck\" because you can't leave a vertex with an odd number of remaining edges.\n\nWait, here's an approach from another angle: the maximum trail in a graph can be found by considering the degrees of the vertices. Each time you enter a vertex, you must leave it, except for the starting/ending vertex in an open trail, but in a closed trail, you must leave and enter the same number of times for every vertex, so all vertices must have even degrees in the trail. Therefore, the trail can only include edges that form a graph where all vertices have even degrees. Therefore, the trail is a subset of the edges of the original graph such that the induced subgraph has all even degrees, and is connected (so that an Eulerian circuit exists in that subgraph). Therefore, the problem reduces to finding the largest such subgraph (with all even degrees and connected) and then the number of edges in that subgraph is the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse.\n\nTherefore, to maximize the number of edges, we need the largest connected subgraph with all even degrees, which is equivalent to the original graph minus a minimal edge set whose removal makes all degrees even and the graph remains connected.\n\nTherefore, the question becomes: what is the minimal number of edges we need to remove from the original grid graph so that the remaining graph is connected and all vertices have even degree?\n\nThen, the maximum trail length would be total edges minus that minimal number.\n\nSo, in order to compute this, we need to find the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even while keeping the graph connected.\n\nEarlier, we thought that removing 18 edges (since 36 odd degree vertices / 2) would suffice, but we need to confirm that such a removal can be done in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a grid graph, such a removal is possible without disconnecting the graph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find a way to pair up the odd-degree vertices and remove edges between them in such a way that the graph remains connected.\n\nGiven that the grid graph is highly connected, maybe we can do this.\n\nFirst, note that all the odd-degree vertices are the edge cells (non-corner) on the border of the grid. Each of these has degree 3. Since they are on the edge of the grid, each such edge cell is adjacent to two other cells along the edge and one interior cell.\n\nTo make their degrees even, we need to remove one edge from each of these 36 vertices. Since each edge removal affects two vertices, we can pair up these 36 vertices and remove an edge that connects each pair. However, we need to ensure that these edges we remove are such that the graph remains connected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of pairing the odd-degree vertices arbitrarily, we can find a way to remove edges along the perimeter or something.\n\nAlternatively, here's an idea: since all the odd-degree vertices are on the boundary, perhaps if we remove a \"matching\" of edges along the boundary, such that each removed edge is adjacent to two odd-degree vertices, thereby reducing their degrees by one (making them even). Since the boundary consists of the outer edges of the grid, maybe we can traverse the perimeter and remove every other edge or something like that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that each edge on the perimeter (the outer edges of the grid) is adjacent to exactly one odd-degree vertex? Wait, actually, each edge on the perimeter is shared between a boundary cell (which is an odd-degree vertex) and an interior cell (which is even-degree). Wait, no, actually, the perimeter edges are the ones on the boundary of the grid. Each perimeter edge is part of a boundary cell (which has degree 3) and an interior cell? Wait no, actually, the perimeter edges are between a boundary cell and another cell. Wait, let me think.\n\nWait, the grid is 11x11 cells. Each cell on the edge (but not corner) has three neighbors: two along the edge and one inward. For example, take the top edge: the top row of cells. Each cell in the top row (except the corners) has neighbors to the left, right, and below. The top-left corner cell has neighbors to the right and below. Similarly, the top-right corner has neighbors to the left and below. The cells along the top edge (non-corner) have three neighbors: left, right, and below. The cells along the bottom edge have neighbors above, left, and right (except corners). Similarly for the left and right edges.\n\nTherefore, each edge on the perimeter of the entire grid is shared between a boundary cell and an interior cell. Wait, actually, no. Wait, the perimeter edges are the edges on the boundary of the entire grid. Each such edge is adjacent to only one cell, because the grid is finite. Wait, hold on, no. Wait, actually, in the grid of cells, each edge of the grid is part of the boundary. For example, the top edge of the top row of cells is not adjacent to any other cell; it's just the outer boundary. Therefore, actually, the edges on the perimeter of the entire grid are not shared between two cells; they are only part of one cell. Wait a second, this is a key point.\n\nHold on, perhaps I made a mistake earlier in considering the degrees. Let me clarify once again:\n\nIn the grid of cells (vertices as cells), each cell has edges (doors) to its adjacent cells. The cells on the edge of the entire grid have some of their edges on the boundary of the grid, meaning those edges are not connected to another cell. Therefore, those boundary edges are not doors between two cells; they are just edges of the grid. Wait, hold on, the problem says: \"each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door.\" So, only the edges between two cells are doors. The edges on the boundary of the grid (the perimeter) are not connected to any other cell, so they are not doors. Therefore, actually, the degrees of the cells are as follows:\n\nFor a corner cell: it has two adjacent cells (e.g., top-left corner has right and down neighbors), so degree 2.\n\nFor an edge cell (not corner): it has three adjacent cells (e.g., a top-edge non-corner cell has left, right, and down neighbors), so degree 3.\n\nFor an interior cell: it has four adjacent cells (up, down, left, right), so degree 4.\n\nHowever, the actual edges (doors) are only between two cells. The perimeter edges of the entire grid are not doors because they are not between two cells. Therefore, the total number of doors (edges in the graph) is indeed 11*10 (horizontal doors) + 10*11 (vertical doors) = 220, as I had before.\n\nBut the degrees of the cells (vertices) are as I mentioned: 4 corners with degree 2, 36 edge cells (non-corner) with degree 3, and 81 interior cells with degree 4.\n\nTherefore, returning to the problem: the graph has 36 vertices of odd degree (the edge cells). To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that each of these 36 vertices has an odd number of edges removed incident to it. Since each edge removal affects two vertices, we need to pair up these 36 vertices and remove an edge between each pair, thereby reducing their degrees by 1 (thereby making them even). Since 36 is even, this is possible.\n\nHowever, we need to do this in such a way that the remaining graph is still connected. The question is: can we find 18 edges (since 36/2 = 18) such that each removed edge connects two of the odd-degree vertices, and the removal of these edges does not disconnect the graph?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can remove edges along the perimeter in a way that \"fixes\" the degrees without disconnecting the graph.\n\nLet me try to visualize the grid. Let's consider the outermost layer of the grid. The outermost layer consists of the perimeter cells (the edge cells). Each of these has degree 3. To make their degrees even, we need to remove one edge from each. Since each perimeter cell is on the edge, one of its edges is on the boundary of the grid (i.e., not connected to another cell). Wait, hold on, actually, no: each perimeter cell (edge cell) has three edges connected to other cells, and one edge on the boundary. Wait, no, actually, each edge cell (non-corner) has three adjacent cells and one \"outside\" edge, but in our graph representation, the edges are only between cells, so the \"outside\" edge isn't part of the graph. Therefore, each edge cell has three edges (doors) connecting to other cells, and those are its only edges. Therefore, the degree is 3, as previously stated.\n\nTherefore, for each edge cell (non-corner), which has degree 3, we need to remove one of its three edges (doors) to make its degree 2. To do this for all 36 edge cells, we need to remove 18 edges, each removal affecting two edge cells? Wait, no. Wait, each edge is shared between two cells. If we remove an edge between two edge cells, then both of those cells will have their degrees reduced by 1. However, if we remove an edge between an edge cell and an interior cell, then only the edge cell's degree is reduced by 1, and the interior cell's degree is also reduced by 1. But the interior cell originally had degree 4, which is even, so reducing it to 3 would make it odd, which is bad. Therefore, we cannot remove edges that connect an edge cell to an interior cell, because that would turn the interior cell into an odd-degree vertex, which we don't want. Therefore, we must only remove edges that are between two edge cells.\n\nTherefore, to avoid creating new odd-degree vertices among the interior cells, all edge removals must be between two edge cells. Therefore, we need to find a set of 18 edges, each connecting two edge cells (non-corner), such that removing these edges reduces the degree of each involved edge cell by 1 (thereby making their degrees 2, which is even), and the removal of these edges does not disconnect the graph.\n\nSo, the problem reduces to: can we find 18 edges along the perimeter (or along the edges of the grid) that are between two edge cells, such that removing them doesn't disconnect the graph, and each such edge is between two edge cells?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can \"pair up\" the edge cells along the perimeter and remove the edges between them in a way that the perimeter remains connected.\n\nLet me consider the perimeter as a cycle. Wait, the outer perimeter of the grid is a cycle of edge cells? Let's see.\n\nThe entire grid's outer boundary is a rectangle. The perimeter cells form a cycle around the grid. Let me think of the top edge: the top row of cells, from left to right, excluding the corners, are edge cells. Similarly, the right edge (excluding corners) is the rightmost column, top to bottom, the bottom edge (excluding corners) is the bottom row, right to left, and the left edge (excluding corners) is the leftmost column, bottom to top.\n\nIf we traverse the perimeter of the grid, moving clockwise, we can imagine a cycle that goes around the outer edge of the grid, passing through all the edge cells. However, each edge cell is part of this perimeter cycle, but each edge cell is also connected inward to an interior cell.\n\nWait, actually, the perimeter cells form a cycle themselves? Let me see: starting at the top-left corner cell, moving right along the top edge to the top-right corner, then down the right edge to the bottom-right corner, then left along the bottom edge to the bottom-left corner, then up the left edge back to the top-left corner. Each of these moves along the perimeter of the grid, passing through the edge cells. However, each edge cell (non-corner) is part of this cycle. For example, the top edge non-corner cells are part of the top edge of the cycle, the right edge non-corner cells are part of the right edge of the cycle, etc.\n\nTherefore, the outer perimeter of the grid forms a cycle of length equal to the perimeter of the grid in terms of cells. Since it's an 11x11 grid, the perimeter in terms of cells would be 2*(11 + 11) - 4 = 44 - 4 = 40 cells? Wait, no. Wait, the number of edge cells (non-corner) is 36, as we had before, but the total number of perimeter cells (including corners) is 4*11 - 4 = 40? Wait, 11 on each side, but corners counted twice. So total perimeter cells: 4*(11) - 4 = 40. So 40 cells on the perimeter, 4 of which are corners, and 36 are edge cells (non-corner). Therefore, the perimeter forms a cycle of 40 cells? Wait, actually, the perimeter as a cycle would have as many edges as the number of perimeter cells? Wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, in terms of the grid graph, the perimeter cells form a cycle. Each perimeter cell (except corners) is connected to two adjacent perimeter cells and one interior cell. The corners are connected to two perimeter cells and one interior cell? Wait, no: the corner cells are only connected to two perimeter cells? Wait, no, the corner cells are connected to two adjacent perimeter cells and one interior cell? Wait, actually, let me take an example: the top-left corner cell is connected to the cell to its right (along the top edge) and the cell below it (along the left edge). Both of those are perimeter cells. So the corner cell has degree 2, connected only to two perimeter cells. Then, the next cell along the top edge (non-corner) is connected to the left corner cell, the right cell (another non-corner top edge cell), and the cell below it (which is an interior cell if we're not at the top row? Wait, no: the cell below a top-edge non-corner cell is in the second row, which is still on the top edge? Wait, no, the top row is the first row. So the cell below a top-edge non-corner cell is in the second row, which is an interior row? Wait, the second row is not the edge, except for the cells on the edges of that row. Wait, actually, in the second row, the cells in the second column (from the left) are interior unless they are on the side.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think in terms of coordinates.\n\nLet me assign coordinates to the cells: let the top-left corner be (1,1), and the bottom-right corner be (11,11). Then, a cell at (i,j) has neighbors:\n\n- (i-1,j) if i > 1,\n\n- (i+1,j) if i < 11,\n\n- (i,j-1) if j > 1,\n\n- (i,j+1) if j < 11.\n\nTherefore, the corner cells are (1,1), (1,11), (11,1), (11,11). Each corner cell has two neighbors:\n\n- (1,1): (1,2) and (2,1)\n\n- (1,11): (1,10) and (2,11)\n\n- (11,1): (10,1) and (11,2)\n\n- (11,11): (11,10) and (10,11)\n\nEach edge cell (non-corner) has three neighbors:\n\nFor example, take (1,2): neighbors are (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2).\n\nSimilarly, (2,1): neighbors are (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2).\n\nAn interior cell, say (2,2): neighbors are (1,2), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3).\n\nTherefore, the perimeter cells (the edge cells) form a cycle? Let's see: starting at (1,1), go to (1,2), then (1,3), ..., up to (1,11), then down to (2,11), then (3,11), ..., up to (11,11), then left to (11,10), ..., down to (11,1), then up to (10,1), ..., right to (2,1), then up to (1,1). Wait, actually, this forms a cycle that includes all the perimeter cells. Each perimeter cell is part of this outer cycle. However, each perimeter cell (except the corners) is also connected inward to an interior cell.\n\nTherefore, the outer perimeter forms a cycle of 40 cells (since there are 40 perimeter cells: 4 corners and 36 edge non-corner cells). Wait, actually, the number of edges on this outer cycle would be equal to the number of perimeter cells, since it's a cycle. Each perimeter cell is connected to two adjacent perimeter cells except for the corners? Wait, no, actually, each perimeter cell is connected to two perimeter neighbors except for the corners? Wait, no. Wait, each corner cell is connected to two perimeter cells and one interior cell. Wait, actually, in terms of the outer cycle, each perimeter cell is connected to its two neighbors along the perimeter. For example, (1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (1,3), both of which are perimeter cells. Similarly, (1,11) is connected to (1,10) and (2,11), both perimeter cells. The corner cell (1,1) is connected to (1,2) and (2,1), both perimeter cells. Therefore, the entire outer perimeter is indeed a cycle of 40 cells? Wait, but each edge between perimeter cells is an edge in the grid graph. Let's count the number of edges on the outer perimeter.\n\nStarting from (1,1), moving right along the top edge: from (1,1) to (1,2) to ... to (1,11): that's 10 edges on the top edge (since there are 11 cells, so 10 gaps between them). Then, from (1,11) down to (2,11): that's one edge. Then, along the right edge from (2,11) to (3,11) to ... to (11,11): that's 9 edges (since from row 2 to row 11 is 10 rows, so 9 gaps). Then, from (11,11) left along the bottom edge to (11,10) to ... to (11,1): that's 10 edges. Then, up the left edge from (11,1) to (10,1) to ... to (2,1): that's 9 edges. Then, from (2,1) back to (1,1): that's one edge. Wait, let me add these up:\n\nTop edge: 10 edges\n\nRight edge: 9 edges (from row 2 to 11, which is 10 cells, so 9 edges)\n\nBottom edge: 10 edges (from column 11 to 1, 11 cells, so 10 edges)\n\nLeft edge: 9 edges (from row 11 to 2, which is 10 cells, so 9 edges)\n\nAnd then the final edge from (2,1) back to (1,1): that's 1 edge.\n\nWait, so total outer perimeter edges: 10 + 9 + 10 + 9 + 1 = 39 edges? Hmm, that doesn't seem to add up to 40. Alternatively, perhaps my counting is off.\n\nWait, let's think differently. The number of edges on the outer perimeter should be equal to the number of perimeter cells, since it's a cycle. There are 40 perimeter cells, so there should be 40 edges in the outer cycle. Wait, but in a cycle with n nodes, there are n edges. So if there are 40 perimeter cells arranged in a cycle, then there should be 40 edges along the perimeter.\n\nBut when I counted above, I got 39. Let me check again.\n\nStarting at (1,1):\n\n1. (1,1) to (1,2) – top edge, first edge.\n\n2. (1,2) to (1,3) – second edge.\n\n...\n\n10. (1,10) to (1,11) – tenth edge on the top.\n\nThen, from (1,11) down to (2,11): that's the 11th edge.\n\nThen, moving down the right edge:\n\n12. (2,11) to (3,11)\n\n13. (3,11) to (4,11)\n\n...\n\n20. (10,11) to (11,11) – that's 9 edges (from row 2 to 11 is 10 rows, so 9 edges). So edges 11 through 20: 10 edges.\n\nThen, moving left along the bottom edge from (11,11) to (11,10) to ... to (11,1):\n\n21. (11,11) to (11,10)\n\n22. (11,10) to (11,9)\n\n...\n\n30. (11,2) to (11,1) – that's 10 edges (from column 11 to 1 is 11 columns, so 10 edges). So edges 21 through 30: 10 edges.\n\nThen, moving up the left edge from (11,1) to (10,1) to ... to (2,1):\n\n31. (11,1) to (10,1)\n\n32. (10,1) to (9,1)\n\n...\n\n40. (3,1) to (2,1) – that's 9 edges (from row 11 to 2 is 10 rows, so 9 edges). So edges 31 through 40: 10 edges? Wait, 31 to 40 is 10 edges, but from row 11 to row 2 is 9 steps. Wait, perhaps my counting is inconsistent.\n\nWait, starting at (11,1), moving up to (10,1): that's edge 31.\n\nThen (10,1) to (9,1): edge 32,\n\n(9,1) to (8,1): edge 33,\n\n...\n\n(3,1) to (2,1): that's edge 39 (since 11 - 2 = 9 steps, so 9 edges). Then, from (2,1) back to (1,1): that's edge 40.\n\nAh, right! So after reaching (2,1) at edge 39, the final edge is from (2,1) to (1,1), which is edge 40. Therefore, total edges on the outer perimeter: 40 edges. So that checks out. Each of the 40 perimeter cells is connected to two other perimeter cells via these 40 edges, forming a cycle.\n\nTherefore, the outer perimeter is a 40-node cycle graph. Now, each of these perimeter nodes (cells) has degree 3 in the original grid graph: two edges along the perimeter cycle and one edge inward to an interior cell. For example, the cell (1,2) is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). The edge to (2,2) is the inward edge.\n\nTherefore, to make all perimeter cells have even degree (i.e., 2), we need to remove one edge from each perimeter cell. However, since each perimeter cell already has two edges on the perimeter cycle and one inward edge, removing the inward edge would make its degree 2 (even), but that would disconnect it from the interior, but the problem is that we can't have disconnected graph? Wait, no, actually, if we remove the inward edge from a perimeter cell, then that perimeter cell is only connected to its two perimeter neighbors, so it's still part of the perimeter cycle, but the interior cell it was connected to is now only connected to other interior cells? Hmm, but the interior cells originally had degree 4, so if we remove an edge from an interior cell to a perimeter cell, that interior cell now has degree 3, which is odd, which we don't want.\n\nTherefore, we cannot remove the inward edges, because that would create new odd-degree vertices among the interior cells. Therefore, the only edges we can remove are the perimeter edges, the edges along the outer cycle. Each perimeter edge is shared between two perimeter cells. If we remove a perimeter edge, say between cell A and cell B (both perimeter cells), then both cell A and cell B lose one degree, so their degrees go from 3 to 2, which is even. Additionally, the interior cells connected to those perimeter cells remain unaffected, since we're only removing perimeter edges.\n\nTherefore, if we remove a set of perimeter edges such that every perimeter cell loses exactly one edge (so that their degrees become 2), then we can do this by selecting a perfect matching on the perimeter cycle. Since the perimeter is a cycle of 40 nodes, a perfect matching would consist of 20 edges, but we only need to remove 18 edges? Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, each perimeter cell needs to lose one edge. Since each edge removal affects two perimeter cells, the number of edges to remove is equal to the number of perimeter cells divided by 2, which is 40 / 2 = 20. But we only have 36 odd-degree cells (the perimeter cells), not 40. Wait, hold on, confusion here.\n\nWait a second, the perimeter cells include the corner cells, which have degree 2 (even). The 36 odd-degree cells are only the non-corner perimeter cells. The four corner cells have degree 2, which is even, so they don't need any edges removed. Therefore, only the 36 non-corner perimeter cells need to have one edge removed each. Since each edge we remove can fix two non-corner perimeter cells (if we remove an edge between two non-corner perimeter cells), then we need to remove 36 / 2 = 18 edges.\n\nTherefore, we need to select 18 edges along the perimeter cycle that connect pairs of non-corner perimeter cells, such that each non-corner perimeter cell is incident to exactly one removed edge. This is equivalent to finding a matching in the subgraph induced by the non-corner perimeter cells, but since the perimeter is a cycle, we can do this by selecting every other edge or something.\n\nWait, the non-corner perimeter cells form a cycle of 36 nodes? Wait, no. The entire perimeter cycle has 40 nodes (including corners), but the non-corner perimeter cells are 36, so they form a cycle of 36 nodes? Wait, no, the non-corner perimeter cells are arranged along the perimeter cycle, but between the corners. Let me think.\n\nActually, the perimeter cycle has 40 nodes (cells). Of those 40, 4 are corners (each with degree 2), and 36 are non-corner (each with degree 3). The non-corner perimeter cells are all the other 36. These 36 non-corner perimeter cells are arranged along the perimeter cycle, between the corners. For example, between the top-left corner (1,1) and the top-right corner (1,11), there are 9 non-corner top-edge cells (from (1,2) to (1,10)). Similarly, between the top-right corner and the bottom-right corner, there are 9 non-corner right-edge cells (from (2,11) to (10,11)), and so on.\n\nTherefore, the 36 non-corner perimeter cells form a cycle of 36 nodes? Wait, no, they are part of the larger 40-node cycle. Specifically, the 36 non-corner perimeter cells are spaced between the corners. So, the entire perimeter cycle is: corner, non-corner, non-corner, ..., non-corner, corner, non-corner, non-corner, ..., etc.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner perimeter cells are interleaved with the corner cells in the 40-node cycle. Since there are 4 corners and 36 non-corners, the non-corner cells are arranged as 9 between each pair of corners on each side.\n\nTherefore, to form a matching among the non-corner perimeter cells, we need to select edges in the perimeter cycle that connect two non-corner perimeter cells, such that every non-corner cell is in exactly one removed edge.\n\nGiven that the non-corner perimeter cells are every other node in the 40-node cycle (since corners are every 10th node?), let's see.\n\nWait, let's think of the perimeter cycle as a sequence of nodes: starting at corner A1, then non-corner N1, non-corner N2, ..., non-corner N9, corner A2, non-corner N10, ..., non-corner N18, corner A3, non-corner N19, ..., non-corner N27, corner A4, non-corner N28, ..., non-corner N36, and back to A1.\n\nWait, actually, between each pair of corners on a side, there are 9 non-corner cells. Since there are four sides, that's 4*9 = 36 non-corner cells. Therefore, the perimeter cycle can be divided into four segments, each segment being a side of the grid, consisting of 10 nodes: corner, 9 non-corner, corner. Wait, no: each side has 11 cells, so between two corners on a side, there are 9 non-corner cells. Therefore, each side contributes 11 nodes to the perimeter cycle: corner, 9 non-corner, corner. But since the corners are shared between sides, the total is 4*(11) - 4 = 40 nodes.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner perimeter cells are arranged in groups of 9 between each pair of corners. To form a matching among the non-corner cells, we can do the following:\n\nOn each side of the grid (top, right, bottom, left), which has 9 non-corner cells, we can pair them up. Since 9 is odd, we can't pair all of them, but perhaps we can leave one unpaired? Wait, but we need to pair all 36 non-corner cells into 18 edges. Since 36 is even, that works.\n\nWait, actually, each side has 9 non-corner cells. Since 9 is odd, we can't pair all of them within a single side. Therefore, we have to consider the entire cycle.\n\nAlternatively, since the entire perimeter cycle has 40 nodes, with 4 corners and 36 non-corners, and the non-corners are every node except the corners. So, if we traverse the perimeter cycle, the nodes go: corner, non-corner, non-corner, ..., non-corner, corner, non-corner, non-corner, ..., etc.\n\nTo form a matching of the non-corner nodes, we can select edges that skip the corners. For instance, in the perimeter cycle, between two corners, there are 9 non-corner nodes. If we number the nodes around the perimeter from 1 to 40, with node 1 being a corner, node 2 to 10 being the non-corner cells on the top edge, node 11 being the next corner (top-right), nodes 12 to 20 being the non-corner cells on the right edge, node 21 being the bottom-right corner, nodes 22 to 30 being non-corner on the bottom edge, node 31 being the bottom-left corner, nodes 32 to 40 being non-corner on the left edge, and node 40 connecting back to node 1.\n\nIn this numbering, the non-corner nodes are 2-10, 12-20, 22-30, 32-40. Each of these groups has 9 nodes.\n\nTo form a matching among the non-corner nodes, we can select edges that connect non-corner nodes without involving the corners. For example, on the top edge segment (nodes 2-10), which are 9 non-corner nodes, we can pair them as follows: connect node 2 to node 4, node 3 to node 5, etc., but this might not work because they need to be adjacent in the perimeter cycle.\n\nWait, actually, the perimeter cycle's edges are only between consecutive nodes. For example, node 1 (corner) is connected to node 2 and node 40. Node 2 is connected to node 1 and node 3, etc. Therefore, the edges between non-corner nodes are the edges that connect node i to node i+1 for i from 2 to 39, except where node i is a corner.\n\nWait, actually, in the perimeter cycle, every edge is between two consecutive nodes in the cycle. Since the corners are every 10th node, the edges between non-corner nodes are all the edges except those connecting a corner to the next node.\n\nTherefore, to form a matching of the non-corner nodes, we need to select a set of edges that are between non-corner nodes, such that each non-corner node is in exactly one selected edge.\n\nThis is equivalent to finding a perfect matching in the graph induced by the non-corner perimeter nodes, where edges are the perimeter edges between non-corner nodes.\n\nHowever, the non-corner perimeter nodes form a cycle of 36 nodes? Wait, no, they are part of a 40-node cycle, but with the corners removed. Since we have 36 non-corner nodes, arranged in the cycle with the corners removed, they form a cycle of 36 nodes? Wait, if you remove the 4 corner nodes from the 40-node cycle, you get a cycle of 36 nodes? No, actually, removing 4 nodes from a cycle of 40 nodes breaks the cycle into a path of 36 nodes? Wait no, removing a node from a cycle breaks it into a path, but removing multiple nodes can break it into multiple paths.\n\nWait, actually, if you have a cycle and remove k nodes, you get a graph that is a collection of paths. Specifically, if the removed nodes are not all consecutive, then you get several paths.\n\nIn our case, the 4 corner nodes are spaced evenly around the cycle. Each corner is separated by 10 nodes (since 40 nodes total, 4 corners, so between each pair of corners are 10 nodes: 9 non-corners plus the next corner). Wait, between two adjacent corners, there are 10 nodes: the next corner is 10 steps away? Let me see with the numbering:\n\nBetween node 1 (corner) and node 11 (next corner): nodes 2 through 10 (9 nodes) plus node 11 is the corner. So between two corners, there are 9 non-corner nodes and the next corner. Therefore, the distance between two corners is 10 steps along the cycle (including the next corner). So, if you remove the 4 corner nodes, then the remaining 36 non-corner nodes form a single cycle? No, because removing the corners breaks the cycle into four separate paths.\n\nFor example, removing node 1 (corner) breaks the cycle between node 40 and node 2. Similarly, removing node 11 breaks the cycle between node 10 and node 12, etc. So after removing all four corners, the remaining 36 non-corner nodes form four separate paths, each corresponding to a side of the grid.\n\nEach of these paths has 9 non-corner nodes. For example, the top edge path (originally between node 1 and node 11) now is from node 2 to node 10 (9 nodes), which is a path of 9 nodes. Similarly, the right edge path (originally between node 11 and node 21) is now nodes 12 to 20 (9 nodes), etc. Each of these four paths is a straight line of 9 nodes, not a cycle.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner perimeter nodes form four separate paths, each of length 9 nodes. To find a perfect matching among all 36 nodes, we need to match nodes within these paths. However, each path has an odd number of nodes (9), so we cannot have a perfect matching within each path (since perfect matching requires even number of nodes). Therefore, we cannot have a perfect matching entirely within the non-corner nodes, because the four paths each have an odd number of nodes, making it impossible to have a perfect matching for each path individually.\n\nHmm, this complicates things. Therefore, perhaps we need to allow some edges in the matching to go across the \"breaks\" where the corners were removed? But the corners are removed nodes, so the edges that were connected to the corners are now disconnected.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider the entire perimeter cycle (including the corners) and find a matching that includes some edges adjacent to the corners but in a way that still pairs up all non-corner nodes.\n\nWait, but the corners themselves have even degree (2), so we don't need to touch their edges. We only need to remove edges that are between non-corner nodes. Because if we remove an edge that is between a corner and a non-corner node, then the corner would lose a degree, making its degree 1, which is odd, which we don't want. Therefore, we cannot remove any edges incident to the corners.\n\nTherefore, all edges we can remove must be between two non-corner perimeter nodes. However, as we saw, the non-corner nodes are partitioned into four paths of 9 nodes each (after removing the corners), and each path has an odd number of nodes, so we cannot have a perfect matching within each path. Therefore, it's impossible to have a perfect matching among the non-corner nodes using only the edges between them.\n\nTherefore, this approach may not work. Hmm, so perhaps we need to remove some edges that are incident to corners? But that would affect the corner's degree.\n\nWait, the corners have degree 2, which is even. If we remove one edge from a corner, its degree becomes 1, which is odd, which is bad. If we remove two edges from a corner, its degree becomes 0, which is even, but that would disconnect it from the graph. Since we can't have degree 0 (as the corner would then be isolated), that's not acceptable either.\n\nTherefore, we cannot remove any edges incident to the corners. Therefore, we are stuck with the four paths of 9 non-corner nodes each, and we cannot form a perfect matching among them because each path has an odd number of nodes. Therefore, it's impossible to remove 18 edges (which is 36/2) to fix all the non-corner perimeter nodes' degrees without leaving some nodes unmatched.\n\nHmm, so this suggests that perhaps it's impossible to make all degrees even by only removing perimeter edges? Or maybe we need to remove some interior edges as well?\n\nWait, but earlier we thought that removing interior edges would create new odd-degree vertices among the interior cells. Let me see: suppose we remove an edge between an interior cell and a perimeter cell. Then the perimeter cell loses a degree (good, making it even), but the interior cell loses a degree as well. The interior cell originally had degree 4, so after removal it has degree 3, which is odd. Therefore, we now have an additional odd-degree vertex in the interior. To fix that, we would need to remove another edge incident to that interior cell, but then that would affect another vertex, potentially causing a chain reaction.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can remove pairs of edges: one perimeter edge and one interior edge in such a way that the net effect is two degree reductions on perimeter cells and two on interior cells, but keeping the interior cells' degrees even?\n\nWait, let's think of this as a system of equations. Each perimeter cell needs to lose one degree, so total required degree reductions on perimeter cells: 36. Each interior cell currently has degree 4, which is even. If we remove edges, each edge removal affects two vertices. If we remove an interior edge (between two interior cells), then both interior cells lose a degree, turning them from even to odd, which is bad. If we remove an edge between an interior cell and a perimeter cell, then the perimeter cell loses a degree (good) and the interior cell loses a degree (bad). If we remove an edge between two perimeter cells, then both perimeter cells lose a degree (good), and no interior cells are affected. Therefore, the only way to achieve the required degree reductions without creating new odd-degree interior cells is to only remove edges between two perimeter cells.\n\nHowever, as we saw, this leads to an impossibility because the non-corner perimeter nodes form four paths of 9 nodes each, and 9 is odd, so we can't have a perfect matching.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we need to remove some edges that are between a perimeter cell and an interior cell, but compensate for the resulting odd-degree interior cells by also removing another edge incident to them?\n\nFor example, suppose we remove an edge between a perimeter cell P and an interior cell I. This makes P's degree even (good) but I's degree odd (bad). To fix I's degree, we need to remove another edge incident to I. If we remove another edge from I to another perimeter cell P', then P' also loses a degree (good), but now I has degree 2 (even) and P and P' each have their degrees reduced by 1 (so if they were non-corner perimeter cells, they go from 3 to 2, which is good). However, this requires that we remove two edges: one between P and I, and another between P' and I. But then the interior cell I has two edges removed, going from degree 4 to 2, which is even. Meanwhile, perimeter cells P and P' each lose one edge, going from 3 to 2. So this could work.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can do this in pairs: for each interior cell that we want to \"serve\" as a hub for two perimeter cells, we remove two edges from it to two different perimeter cells, thereby fixing both perimeter cells and keeping the interior cell's degree even.\n\nHowever, this would require that we have enough interior cells to \"absorb\" the extra degree reductions. Each such operation (removing two edges from an interior cell) allows us to fix two perimeter cells. Since we need to fix 36 perimeter cells, we would need 18 such interior cells (each handling two perimeter cells). But the interior cells are 81 in total, so that's feasible.\n\nTherefore, the plan could be:\n\n1. Select 18 interior cells.\n\n2. For each selected interior cell, connect it to two different perimeter cells via edges.\n\n3. Remove those two edges for each interior cell.\n\nThis way, each selected interior cell loses two edges (from degree 4 to 2, which is even), and each of the two perimeter cells lose one edge (from degree 3 to 2, which is even). Therefore, all perimeter cells are fixed, and all interior cells remain with even degrees (either 4 - 2 = 2 or 4 - 0 = 4, depending on whether they were selected or not). Wait, actually, the unselected interior cells remain at degree 4, and the selected interior cells are at degree 2. Since 2 is even, that's okay.\n\nHowever, we have to make sure that the edges we remove are actually present. That is, for each selected interior cell, we need to choose two perimeter cells that are adjacent to it, and remove the edges between them and the interior cell.\n\nBut in the grid, each interior cell is adjacent to four other cells: up, down, left, right. Among these four, some may be perimeter cells and some may be interior cells. Specifically, an interior cell not on the edge of the grid will have all four neighbors as interior cells. A perimeter-adjacent interior cell (i.e., a cell adjacent to the perimeter but not on the perimeter itself) will have one neighbor on the perimeter and three interior neighbors. Wait, actually, let's clarify:\n\nAn interior cell is defined as a cell not on the perimeter. Therefore, an interior cell is at least one cell away from all sides. Therefore, such a cell has all four neighbors also as interior cells. Wait, no: if the grid is 11x11, then the interior cells would be from row 2 to 10 and column 2 to 10, forming a 9x9 grid of interior cells. Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, the grid has 11 rows and 11 columns. The perimeter cells are those in the first and last rows and first and last columns. Therefore, the interior cells are those not in the first or last row or column, so rows 2 through 10 and columns 2 through 10, which is 9x9 = 81 cells, as previously stated.\n\nTherefore, an interior cell is surrounded by other interior cells on all four sides. Therefore, an interior cell cannot be adjacent to any perimeter cell. Wait a second! That's a critical realization.\n\nIf all interior cells are completely surrounded by other interior cells, then none of the edges from an interior cell go to a perimeter cell. Therefore, every edge incident to an interior cell is between two interior cells. Therefore, we cannot remove an edge between an interior cell and a perimeter cell, because such an edge does not exist!\n\nOh no! This changes everything. I made a mistake earlier. In the grid graph where vertices are cells, an interior cell (not on the perimeter) is only adjacent to other interior cells. Therefore, all edges incident to an interior cell are between two interior cells. Therefore, you cannot remove an edge between an interior cell and a perimeter cell because there is no such edge. The perimeter cells are only adjacent to other perimeter cells and to interior cells? Wait, hold on, no:\n\nWait, let's take an example. Consider a cell on the top edge, say (1,2). Its neighbors are (1,1) [a corner, perimeter], (1,3) [a non-corner perimeter], and (2,2) [an interior cell]. Therefore, the cell (1,2) is adjacent to one interior cell (2,2) and two perimeter cells (1,1) and (1,3). Therefore, edges from a perimeter cell can go to interior cells.\n\nBut an interior cell, say (2,2), is adjacent to (1,2) [perimeter], (3,2) [interior], (2,1) [perimeter], and (2,3) [interior]. Wait, no! Wait, (2,1) is on the left edge, so it's a perimeter cell. Therefore, the cell (2,2) is adjacent to two perimeter cells: (1,2) and (2,1), and two interior cells: (3,2) and (2,3). Therefore, an interior cell is adjacent to two perimeter cells and two interior cells.\n\nAh! So, my mistake earlier was thinking that interior cells are only adjacent to other interior cells, but actually, an interior cell adjacent to the perimeter (i.e., one cell in from the edge) is adjacent to two perimeter cells and two interior cells. Whereas an interior cell that is two cells in from all edges (i.e., not adjacent to any perimeter cells) would be surrounded by four interior cells. Wait, but in an 11x11 grid, the cells adjacent to the perimeter are the ones in the second and tenth rows and columns. For example, row 2 and row 10 are adjacent to the top and bottom edges, respectively, and columns 2 and 10 are adjacent to the left and right edges. Therefore, cells in rows 2-10 and columns 2-10 are the interior cells, but some of them are adjacent to the perimeter, specifically those in rows 2 and 10, and columns 2 and 10.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- Cells in row 2, columns 2-10: these are adjacent to the top edge (row 1), so their top neighbor is a perimeter cell.\n\n- Cells in row 10, columns 2-10: adjacent to the bottom edge (row 11), so their bottom neighbor is a perimeter cell.\n\n- Cells in column 2, rows 2-10: adjacent to the left edge (column 1), so their left neighbor is a perimeter cell.\n\n- Cells in column 10, rows 2-10: adjacent to the right edge (column 11), so their right neighbor is a perimeter cell.\n\nTherefore, the interior cells that are in the \"second layer\" (adjacent to the perimeter) have two perimeter neighbors, while the interior cells that are further in (not adjacent to any perimeter) have no perimeter neighbors. Wait, actually, no. Let me see:\n\nTake a cell in row 3, column 3: this is three rows down from the top and three columns right from the left, so it's not adjacent to any perimeter cells. Its neighbors are all interior cells.\n\nA cell in row 2, column 3: this is adjacent to the top perimeter cell (row 1, column 3), and its left neighbor is row 2, column 2 (which is also an interior cell if column 2 is not on the edge). Wait, column 2 is adjacent to the left edge (column 1), so row 2, column 2 is adjacent to the left perimeter cell (row 2, column 1). Therefore, cell (2,3) has neighbors: (1,3) [perimeter], (3,3) [interior], (2,2) [interior], and (2,4) [interior]. So, only one perimeter neighbor.\n\nWait, actually, for any interior cell not on the \"second layer\", meaning not in row 2, 10 or column 2, 10, then they are not adjacent to any perimeter cells. Wait, no. For example, take cell (3,3): its neighbors are (2,3), (4,3), (3,2), (3,4). None of these are perimeter cells, since (2,3) is in row 2 (adjacent to top edge) but column 3 is not on the side, so (2,3) is an interior cell? Wait no: (2,3) is in row 2, which is adjacent to the top edge, but column 3 is not on the left or right edge. Therefore, (2,3) is an interior cell (since it's not on the perimeter), and its neighbors are:\n\n- (1,3): which is a perimeter cell (top edge),\n\n- (3,3): interior,\n\n- (2,2): interior (since column 2 is adjacent to the left edge, but row 2, column 2 is still an interior cell because it's not on the perimeter? Wait, column 2 is adjacent to the left edge (column 1), so cell (2,2) is in column 2, which is on the left edge's adjacent column, so it is a perimeter cell? Wait no.\n\nHold on, let's recall the definition of perimeter cells. The perimeter cells are those in the first row, last row, first column, or last column. Therefore:\n\n- First row: all cells (1, j) for j=1 to 11,\n\n- Last row: all cells (11, j) for j=1 to 11,\n\n- First column: all cells (i, 1) for i=1 to 11,\n\n- Last column: all cells (i, 11) for i=1 to 11.\n\nTherefore, a cell is a perimeter cell if it is in any of those four sets. Therefore, cell (2,2) is in column 2, which is not the first or last column (unless 2 = 1 or 11, which it's not), and row 2 is not the first or last row (since 2 ≠ 1 or 11). Therefore, cell (2,2) is an interior cell. Its neighbors:\n\n- (1,2): which is in the first row, so perimeter cell,\n\n- (3,2): interior,\n\n- (2,1): which is in the first column, so perimeter cell,\n\n- (2,3): interior.\n\nTherefore, cell (2,2) is adjacent to two perimeter cells: (1,2) and (2,1). Therefore, even cells that are one layer in from the edge can be adjacent to two perimeter cells.\n\nTherefore, in general, any interior cell that is on the \"edge\" of the interior (i.e., adjacent to the perimeter) will have two perimeter neighbors, while interior cells deeper inside have none. Specifically:\n\n- Cells in row 2 or row 10: each such cell has a neighbor in row 1 or row 11 (perimeter),\n\n- Cells in column 2 or column 10: each such cell has a neighbor in column 1 or column 11 (perimeter),\n\n- Cells in both row 2 or 10 and column 2 or 10: these have two perimeter neighbors,\n\n- Cells in row 2 or 10 but not in column 2 or 10: have one perimeter neighbor,\n\n- Cells in column 2 or 10 but not in row 2 or 10: have one perimeter neighbor,\n\n- Cells not in row 2, 10 or column 2, 10: have no perimeter neighbors.\n\nTherefore, the interior cells can have 0, 1, or 2 perimeter neighbors.\n\nThis means that some interior cells are adjacent to perimeter cells, and some are not. Therefore, it is possible to remove edges between an interior cell and a perimeter cell, as those edges exist.\n\nHowever, as previously noted, removing such an edge would reduce the interior cell's degree by 1, turning it from even to odd (since interior cells originally have degree 4), which is bad. Therefore, to fix this, we would need to also remove another edge from that interior cell to another perimeter cell (if possible), thereby reducing its degree by 2, keeping it even, while also reducing the degrees of two perimeter cells by 1 each, making them even.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that we can perform such paired removals:\n\nFor each interior cell that we decide to \"use\" as a hub, we can remove two of its edges that connect to perimeter cells, thereby:\n\n- Reducing the interior cell's degree by 2 (from 4 to 2),\n\n- Reducing the degree of two different perimeter cells by 1 each (from 3 to 2),\n\nThus, fixing two perimeter cells' degrees without introducing new odd-degree vertices.\n\nHowever, this requires that the interior cell has at least two edges connecting to perimeter cells. That is, the interior cell must be adjacent to at least two perimeter cells. Which is true for interior cells that are on the \"edge\" of the interior, i.e., those in row 2, 10 or column 2, 10.\n\nSpecifically, interior cells in the \"second layer\" (adjacent to the perimeter) can have two perimeter neighbors, so we can remove both edges from such a cell to the perimeter, thereby fixing two perimeter cells and keeping the interior cell's degree even.\n\nInterior cells that are only adjacent to one perimeter cell cannot be used this way, since we can only remove one edge from them, which would make their degree odd, which is bad. Therefore, we must only use interior cells that are adjacent to two perimeter cells, i.e., those in the \"corner\" of the interior, like cell (2,2), (2,10), (10,2), (10,10), etc.\n\nWait, actually, any interior cell that is in row 2 or 10 and column 2 or 10 will have two perimeter neighbors. For example:\n\n- Cell (2,2): adjacent to (1,2) and (2,1),\n\n- Cell (2,10): adjacent to (1,10) and (2,11),\n\n- Cell (10,2): adjacent to (11,2) and (9,2),\n\n- Cell (10,10): adjacent to (11,10) and (10,11),\n\nand so on for other cells in the intersections of row 2/10 and column 2/10.\n\nIn addition, cells like (2,3) are adjacent to only one perimeter cell (the one above them), so they cannot be used as hubs.\n\nTherefore, the interior cells that can serve as hubs are those in the \"corner\" positions of the interior, specifically those at the intersections of row 2/10 and column 2/10. How many such cells are there?\n\nIn rows 2 and 10, and columns 2 and 10:\n\n- Row 2, column 2: one cell,\n\n- Row 2, column 10: one cell,\n\n- Row 10, column 2: one cell,\n\n- Row 10, column 10: one cell,\n\n- Additionally, in row 2, columns 3-9: these are in row 2 but not column 2 or 10, so they are adjacent to only one perimeter cell (the one above),\n\nSimilarly, row 10, columns 3-9: same,\n\nColumns 2, rows 3-9: adjacent to the left perimeter cell but not the top or bottom,\n\nColumns 10, rows 3-9: same,\n\nTherefore, the only interior cells with two perimeter neighbors are the four cells at the \"corners\" of the interior: (2,2), (2,10), (10,2), (10,10). Each of these four cells has two perimeter neighbors.\n\nAdditionally, are there any other interior cells with two perimeter neighbors? For example, consider a cell in row 2, column 2: that's already counted. What about a cell in row 2, column 11? Wait, column 11 is the last column, so cell (2,11) is a perimeter cell, not interior. Similarly, row 11 is the last row, so cells there are perimeter. Therefore, the only interior cells with two perimeter neighbors are those four cells: (2,2), (2,10), (10,2), (10,10).\n\nWait, let's confirm with another example: cell (2,3). Its neighbors are:\n\n- (1,3): perimeter (top edge),\n\n- (3,3): interior,\n\n- (2,2): interior,\n\n- (2,4): interior.\n\nSo only one perimeter neighbor.\n\nSimilarly, cell (3,2):\n\n- (2,2): interior,\n\n- (4,2): interior,\n\n- (3,1): perimeter (left edge),\n\n- (3,3): interior.\n\nOnly one perimeter neighbor.\n\nTherefore, only the four corner interior cells (2,2), (2,10), (10,2), (10,10) have two perimeter neighbors. Each of these four cells can have two edges removed to perimeter cells, thereby fixing two perimeter cells each.\n\nEach such operation would fix two perimeter cells and use up two edges from the hub interior cell. Since each hub interior cell can contribute to fixing two perimeter cells, and we have four hub interior cells, this would fix 4 × 2 = 8 perimeter cells. However, we need to fix all 36 non-corner perimeter cells. Therefore, this approach alone is insufficient.\n\nHmm, so perhaps we need to combine this with removing perimeter-to-perimeter edges?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use some of the hub interior cells to fix multiple perimeter cells, and then use perimeter-perimeter edge removals for the remaining perimeter cells.\n\nLet me think of this as a two-step process:\n\n1. Use the four hub interior cells to fix as many perimeter cells as possible.\n\n2. For the remaining perimeter cells, remove perimeter-perimeter edges to fix the remaining perimeter cells' degrees.\n\nFirst, step 1: each hub interior cell (there are four of them) can be used to fix two perimeter cells by removing two edges from the hub to the perimeter. Each such removal also reduces the hub's degree by 2 (keeping it even) and each perimeter cell's degree by 1 (fixing it to even). Therefore, using all four hubs can fix 4 × 2 = 8 perimeter cells. However, each hub is a single interior cell, so each can only be used once? Or can we use the same hub multiple times? No, because once you remove edges from a hub, its degree is reduced, but you can't remove more edges from it than it has.\n\nEach hub interior cell has two edges to perimeter cells. If you remove both, then its degree becomes 4 - 2 = 2, which is even, so you can't remove any more edges from it. Therefore, each hub can be used to fix two perimeter cells, but only once. Therefore, total perimeter cells fixed this way: 8.\n\nRemaining perimeter cells to fix: 36 - 8 = 28.\n\nThese 28 perimeter cells are all non-corner, non-hub-adjacent perimeter cells. They each need to have one edge removed to fix their degree.\n\nHowever, to fix these remaining 28 perimeter cells, we need to remove 28 edges, each incident to one of these perimeter cells. But each edge we remove must be between two perimeter cells (since we can't remove edges from perimeter to interior anymore, as that would require using a hub which we've already used up, or would create odd degrees in interior cells).\n\nWait, but these remaining perimeter cells are all non-corner and not adjacent to any hub interior cells? Or are they?\n\nWait, actually, the non-corner perimeter cells include all perimeter cells except the four corners. The hub interior cells are at positions like (2,2), which is adjacent to perimeter cells (1,2) and (2,1). Therefore, the perimeter cells adjacent to the hubs are specific ones: for example, (1,2) and (2,1) are perimeter cells adjacent to hub (2,2). These perimeter cells would have been fixed in step 1.\n\nThe remaining 28 perimeter cells are the other non-corner perimeter cells not adjacent to any hub. Let me see how many perimeter cells are adjacent to the hubs.\n\nEach hub interior cell is adjacent to two perimeter cells:\n\n- (2,2) is adjacent to (1,2) and (2,1),\n\n- (2,10) is adjacent to (1,10) and (2,11),\n\n- (10,2) is adjacent to (11,2) and (9,2),\n\n- (10,10) is adjacent to (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nTherefore, these four hub interior cells are each adjacent to two unique perimeter cells. Specifically, the perimeter cells fixed in step 1 are:\n\nFrom (2,2): (1,2) and (2,1),\n\nFrom (2,10): (1,10) and (2,11),\n\nFrom (10,2): (11,2) and (9,2),\n\nFrom (10,10): (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nTherefore, these are 8 specific perimeter cells that have been fixed by step 1.\n\nThe remaining non-corner perimeter cells are 36 - 8 = 28.\n\nThese 28 perimeter cells are all the other non-corner perimeter cells not listed above. These include:\n\n- On the top edge (row 1, columns 3-10): cells (1,3) through (1,10),\n\n- On the right edge (column 11, rows 2-10): cells (2,11) through (10,11),\n\n- On the bottom edge (row 11, columns 2-9): cells (11,2) through (11,9),\n\n- On the left edge (column 1, rows 3-10): cells (3,1) through (10,1),\n\nplus the remaining ones on the top and right/bottom/left edges not yet accounted for.\n\nWait, let me count:\n\nTop edge non-corner cells: columns 2-10 of row 1: that's 9 cells. Out of these, (1,2) was fixed by the hub at (2,2), so remaining: 8 cells.\n\nRight edge non-corner cells: rows 2-10 of column 11: that's 9 cells. Out of these, (2,11) and (10,11) were fixed by the hubs at (2,10) and (10,10), so remaining: 7 cells.\n\nBottom edge non-corner cells: rows 2-10 of column 11? No, bottom edge is row 11, columns 2-10: rows 2-10 of column 11? Wait, no:\n\nWait, bottom edge is row 11, columns 1 to 11. Non-corner cells are columns 2-10 of row 11: 9 cells. Out of these, (11,2) and (11,10) were fixed by hubs at (10,2) and (10,10), so remaining: 7 cells.\n\nLeft edge non-corner cells: column 1, rows 2-10: 9 cells. Out of these, (2,1) and (10,1) were fixed by hubs at (2,2) and (10,2), so remaining: 7 cells.\n\nAdditionally, the four corners are already excluded.\n\nWait, but let's verify the total:\n\nTop edge non-corner: 9, minus 1 fixed: 8,\n\nRight edge non-corner: 9, minus 2 fixed: 7,\n\nBottom edge non-corner: 9, minus 2 fixed: 7,\n\nLeft edge non-corner: 9, minus 2 fixed: 7,\n\nTotal remaining: 8 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 29? Wait, that's 29, but we expected 28. Hmm, discrepancy here.\n\nWait, let's recount:\n\nTop edge non-corner cells: columns 2-10 (9 cells). Fixed: (1,2). Remaining: 8.\n\nRight edge non-corner cells: rows 2-10 (9 cells). Fixed: (2,11) and (10,11). Remaining: 9 - 2 = 7.\n\nBottom edge non-corner cells: rows 2-10 (9 cells). Fixed: (11,2) and (11,10). Remaining: 7.\n\nLeft edge non-corner cells: rows 2-10 (9 cells). Fixed: (2,1) and (10,1). Remaining: 7.\n\nTotal remaining: 8 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 29. Hmm, so that's one too many. Where is the extra?\n\nAh, perhaps the cell (10,11) is on the bottom edge? Wait, (10,11) is in column 11, row 10, which is on the right edge, not the bottom edge. Similarly, (11,10) is on the bottom edge. Let me see:\n\nWait, (10,11) is in column 11, row 10: that's on the right edge (column 11), not the bottom edge (row 11). Similarly, (11,10) is on the bottom edge (row 11), column 10.\n\nSo the bottom edge non-corner cells are columns 2-10 of row 11: 9 cells. Among these, which were fixed? (11,2) and (11,10). So that's two.\n\nSimilarly, the right edge non-corner cells are rows 2-10 of column 11: 9 cells. Among these, fixed are (2,11) and (10,11). So two fixed.\n\nTherefore, the count is correct: 8 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 29. Hmm, but we needed only 28. There must be an overlap somewhere.\n\nWait, maybe one of the fixed cells is counted in two categories? For example, is there a cell that's on both the top edge and right edge? No, because the corners are excluded, and the only overlap between edges is at the corners, which are excluded. So each non-corner perimeter cell is on exactly one edge.\n\nTherefore, the total should be 4 edges × 9 non-corner cells per edge = 36 non-corner perimeter cells. We fixed 8 cells (2 per hub × 4 hubs), so 36 - 8 = 28 remaining. But according to the above breakdown, it's 29. Therefore, there must be an error in the breakdown.\n\nWait, let's recast:\n\nTop edge non-corner cells: 9 cells (columns 2-10, row 1). Fixed: 1 cell (1,2). Remaining: 8.\n\nRight edge non-corner cells: 9 cells (rows 2-10, column 11). Fixed: 2 cells (2,11) and (10,11). Remaining: 7.\n\nBottom edge non-corner cells: 9 cells (rows 2-10, column 11? No: bottom edge is row 11, columns 2-10). So rows 2-10 of column 11 is the right edge. The bottom edge non-corner cells are row 11, columns 2-10: 9 cells. Fixed: (11,2) and (11,10). Remaining: 7.\n\nLeft edge non-corner cells: column 1, rows 2-10: 9 cells. Fixed: (2,1) and (10,1). Remaining: 7.\n\nTotal remaining: 8 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 29.\n\nAh, I see the problem: the bottom edge non-corner cells include (11,10), which is column 10, row 11, and the right edge non-corner cells include (10,11), which is row 10, column 11. These are distinct cells, so no overlap. The discrepancy must be because I miscalculated the fixed cells.\n\nWait, let's list all fixed perimeter cells:\n\nFrom hub (2,2): fixes (1,2) and (2,1).\n\nFrom hub (2,10): fixes (1,10) and (2,11).\n\nFrom hub (10,2): fixes (11,2) and (9,2).\n\nFrom hub (10,10): fixes (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nTherefore, the fixed perimeter cells are:\n\nTop edge: (1,2), (1,10),\n\nRight edge: (2,11), (10,11),\n\nBottom edge: (11,2), (11,10),\n\nLeft edge: (2,1), (9,2), (10,1).\n\nWait a second! Wait, the hub at (10,2) is adjacent to (11,2) [bottom edge] and (9,2) [left edge?].\n\nWait, cell (10,2) is in row 10, column 2.\n\nIts neighbors:\n\n- Above: (9,2) [interior],\n\n- Below: (11,2) [perimeter, bottom edge],\n\n- Left: (10,1) [perimeter, left edge],\n\n- Right: (10,3) [interior].\n\nTherefore, the hub (10,2) is adjacent to two perimeter cells: (11,2) and (10,1).\n\nSimilarly, hub (10,10) is in row 10, column 10:\n\n- Below: (11,10) [perimeter, bottom edge],\n\n- Right: (10,11) [perimeter, right edge],\n\n- Left: (10,9) [interior],\n\n- Above: (9,10) [interior].\n\nTherefore, the hub (10,10) is adjacent to (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nTherefore, the fixed perimeter cells from hub (10,2) are (11,2) and (10,1),\n\nand from hub (10,10) are (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nTherefore, the fixed perimeter cells are:\n\nTop edge: (1,2), (1,10),\n\nRight edge: (2,11), (10,11),\n\nBottom edge: (11,2), (11,10),\n\nLeft edge: (2,1), (10,1),\n\nand additionally from hub (10,2): (11,2) and (10,1) – wait, (11,2) is on the bottom edge and (10,1) is on the left edge.\n\nWait, so total fixed perimeter cells:\n\nFrom hub (2,2): (1,2), (2,1),\n\nFrom hub (2,10): (1,10), (2,11),\n\nFrom hub (10,2): (11,2), (10,1),\n\nFrom hub (10,10): (11,10), (10,11).\n\nTotal of 8 perimeter cells fixed, as before.\n\nNow, listing all fixed perimeter cells:\n\nTop edge: (1,2), (1,10),\n\nRight edge: (2,11), (10,11),\n\nBottom edge: (11,2), (11,10),\n\nLeft edge: (2,1), (10,1).\n\nThat's 8 cells.\n\nNow, the remaining non-corner perimeter cells are all the others:\n\nTop edge non-corner: columns 3-9 of row 1: cells (1,3) through (1,9): 7 cells,\n\nRight edge non-corner: rows 3-9 of column 11: cells (3,11) through (9,11): 7 cells,\n\nBottom edge non-corner: columns 3-9 of row 11: cells (11,3) through (11,9): 7 cells,\n\nLeft edge non-corner: rows 3-9 of column 1: cells (3,1) through (9,1): 7 cells,\n\nplus the cells (1,11) and (11,1), but those