is bad. Therefore, we must remove an edge to a perimeter cell.\n\nSpecifically, we can remove either the edge to (1,2) or the edge to (1,4). However, (1,2) is already a fixed perimeter cell; its degree is now 2 (even), so removing an edge from it would reduce its degree to 1, which is odd, which is bad. Therefore, we cannot remove the edge to (1,2) because that would ruin the fix we already did on (1,2).\n\nSimilarly, the cell (1,4) is also a perimeter cell that hasn't been fixed yet, so its degree is still 3. If we remove the edge between (1,3) and (1,4), then (1,4)'s degree would decrease by 1, from 3 to 2, which is good. But (1,3)'s degree would decrease by 1, from 3 to 2, which is also good. Therefore, this is a valid move: remove the edge between (1,3) and (1,4). This fixes both (1,3) and (1,4)'s degrees.\n\nWait a minute! If we remove the edge between two adjacent perimeter cells, both of which need to have their degrees reduced by 1, then this single edge removal fixes both of their degrees. Therefore, this is an efficient way to fix two perimeter cells at once.\n\nAh! This is the key insight I was missing earlier. Instead of trying to fix perimeter cells one by one by removing edges to interior cells (which is impossible due to interior cell degrees), we can remove edges between adjacent perimeter cells. Each such edge is shared between two perimeter cells, and removing it reduces both of their degrees by 1, which is exactly what they need (each was at 3, needs to go to 2). Therefore, by removing perimeter-perimeter edges between adjacent perimeter cells that both need their degrees reduced, we can fix two perimeter cells with a single edge removal.\n\nTherefore, the remaining 28 perimeter cells form a path along the perimeter? Or some connected structure where we can pair them up?\n\nLet me consider the remaining perimeter cells. After fixing the 8 perimeter cells adjacent to the hubs, the remaining 28 form four separate \"arcs\" on each side of the grid.\n