so 18 edges to remove), and the total edges are 220, leading to 202, I think the answer is 202.\n\nTherefore, after all that, I think the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 202, so the answer is \\boxed{202}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{202}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of finding the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse in an 11x11 grid of cells such that he starts and ends at the same cell without repeating any door, we can use concepts from graph theory related to Eulerian circuits and trail lengths.\n\n1. **Grid Representation**:\n   - The grid is represented as a graph where each cell is a vertex, and edges (doors) connect adjacent cells.\n   - The grid has 11 rows and 11 columns, resulting in 121 cells.\n   - The total number of doors (edges) in the grid is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Horizontal doors} = 11 \\times 10 = 110\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Vertical doors} = 10 \\times 11 = 110\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total doors} = 110 + 110 = 220\n     \\]\n\n2. **Degree Analysis**:\n   - Corner cells have degree 2 (even).\n   - Edge cells (non-corner) have degree 3 (odd).\n   - Interior cells have degree 4 (even).\n   - There are 4 corner cells and 36 edge cells (non-corner), resulting in 36 vertices with odd degrees.\n\n3. **Eulerian Circuit Requirements**:\n   - For an Eulerian circuit to exist, all vertices must have even degrees.\n   - The number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is half the number of vertices with odd degrees:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{36}{2} = 18\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification and Connectivity**:\n   - Removing 18 edges from the grid, specifically those between non-corner perimeter cells, ensures all remaining vertices have even degrees.\n   - The grid's high connectivity (being a 2-connected planar graph) ensures that removing 18 edges does not disconnect the graph.\n\n5. **Result Calculation**:\n   - The remaining number of edges (doors) after removing 18 edges is:\n     \\[\n     