t adjacent to each other; they are on the edges of the grid but separated by other cells. For example, take an edge cell on the top edge (not a corner), say (1,2). Its neighbors are (1,1) [a corner, degree 2], (1,3) [another edge cell, non-corner, degree 3], and (2,2) [an interior cell, degree 4]. So the edge cell (1,2) is adjacent to another edge cell (1,3) and an interior cell. Therefore, it is adjacent to one other edge cell (on the same edge) and one interior cell. Similarly, each edge cell (non-corner) on a side is adjacent to two other cells: one along the edge (another edge cell) and one interior cell, except for the corners.\n\nWait, actually, take an edge cell on the top edge, not a corner: let's say position (1, j) where 2 ≤ j ≤ 10. Then its neighbors are:\n\n- North: none (since it's on the top edge)\n\n- South: (2, j)\n\n- East: (1, j+1) if j < 11\n\n- West: (1, j-1) if j > 1\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge cell on the top edge has three neighbors: south, east, and west. Except for the corners, which have two neighbors.\n\nWait, for example, take cell (1,2):\n\n- North: nothing\n\n- South: (2,2)\n\n- East: (1,3)\n\n- West: (1,1) [which is a corner]\n\nTherefore, cell (1,2) is adjacent to three cells: (2,2), (1,3), and (1,1). Among these, (1,1) is a corner (degree 2), (1,3) is another edge cell (non-corner, degree 3), and (2,2) is an interior cell (degree 4). Therefore, the non-corner edge cell (1,2) is adjacent to one other non-corner edge cell (its eastern neighbor along the top edge) and an interior cell, and a corner cell.\n\nSimilarly, cell (1,10) on the top edge (second to last) would be adjacent to (1,9), (2,10), and (1,11) [the corner]. So again, adjacent to one other non-corner edge cell and a corner and an interior cell.\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge cell on a side is adjacent to exactly one other non-corner edge cell along that side, and one interior cell, and possibly a corner cell if it's near the corner.\n\nWait, for example, take the top-right non-corner edge cell (1,10): its neighbors are (1,9), (2,10), and (1,11) [the corner]. So, adjacent to (1,9) which is another non-corner edge cell, (2,10) which is interior, and (1,11) the corner.\n\nSimilarly, the cell (1,11) is the corner, which is adjacent to (1,10) and (2,11). So, the corner cell is adjacent to two non-corner edge cells? Wait, (1,11) is adjacent to (1,10) and (2,11). (2,11) is an edge cell on the rightmost column (since column 11 is the right edge), so (2,11) is a non-corner edge cell (since it's not a corner, as row 2, column 11). Therefore, the corner (1,11) is adjacent to two non-corner edge cells: (1,10) and (2,11). Wait, but (2,11) is on the right edge, so it's a non-corner edge cell? Yes, because it's on the right edge but not a corner (since row 2, column 11 is not a corner). Therefore, the corner cell (1,11) has two neighbors which are non-corner edge cells.\n\nTherefore, in general, each corner cell is adjacent to two non-corner edge cells. Each non-corner edge cell on a side is adjacent to one other non-corner edge cell along that side, one interior cell, and possibly a corner cell if it's at the end near the corner. Wait, actually, except for the cells adjacent to the corners, each non-corner edge cell is adjacent to one other non-corner edge cell along the same side, and then an interior cell and a corner cell? Wait, let me clarify with an example.\n\nTake the top edge non-corner cells from (1,2) to (1,10). Each of these cells except for (1,2) and (1,10) are adjacent to two other non-corner edge cells? Wait no:\n\nWait, cell (1,2) is adjacent to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [non-corner edge], and (2,2) [interior]. So only one other non-corner edge cell: (1,3).\n\nCell (1,3) is adjacent to (1,2), (1,4), (2,3). So two non-corner edge cells: (1,2) and (1,4).\n\nSimilarly, cell (1,4) is adjacent to (1,3), (1,5), (2,4). So two non-corner edge cells: (1,3) and (1,5).\n\nContinuing this way, cells from (1,3) through (1,9) are each adjacent to two non-corner edge cells on the top edge.\n\nThe cell (1,10) is adjacent to (1,9), (1,11) [corner], and (2,10). So only one non-corner edge cell: (1,9).\n\nTherefore, along the top edge, the non-corner edge cells form a linear chain from (1,2) to (1,10), each connected to their immediate neighbors. Therefore, except for the endpoints (1,2) and (1,10), each non-corner edge cell on the top edge is adjacent to two other non-corner edge cells. The endpoints (1,2) and (1,10) are each adjacent to one other non-corner edge cell and a corner cell.\n\nTherefore, in total, along each edge (top, bottom, left, right), the non-corner edge cells form a path graph of 9 nodes (since 11 - 2 corners = 9 non-corner edge cells per side). Each of these path graphs has two endpoints (which are the corners or adjacent to corners) and internal nodes.\n\nTherefore, in each side's non-corner edge cells, the two endpoints (like (1,2) and (1,10) on the top edge) are each adjacent to one non-corner edge cell and a corner cell. The internal non-corner edge cells on the side are each adjacent to two non-corner edge cells.\n\nTherefore, in terms of adjacency between non-corner edge cells: on each side, the non-corner edge cells form a path graph of 9 nodes, each connected to their immediate neighbors along the side. Therefore, each non-corner edge cell on a side is adjacent to either one or two other non-corner edge cells, depending on their position.\n\nTherefore, in the entire grid, the non-corner edge cells are arranged in four such path graphs (one per side), each of length 9 (nodes). Additionally, each corner cell is adjacent to two non-corner edge cells (one from each adjacent side). For example, the top-right corner (1,11) is adjacent to (1,10) on the top edge and (2,11) on the right edge.\n\nNow, returning to the problem of pairing the odd-degree vertices (the 36 non-corner edge cells) such that we can remove edges between them to fix their degrees.\n\nEach non-corner edge cell has degree 3 (odd). To make their degrees even, we need to reduce each by 1. To do this, we can remove an edge incident to each such vertex. However, each edge removal affects two vertices. Therefore, if we can pair up the 36 non-corner edge cells into 18 pairs, and for each pair, remove an edge that is incident to both, then we can fix both with one edge removal per pair.\n\nThe question is: can we pair up all 36 non-corner edge cells such that each pair is connected by an edge? If so, then we can remove those edges and fix all degrees with 18 edge removals.\n\nAlternatively, if not, perhaps we need a different strategy.\n\nLooking at the structure of the non-corner edge cells, arranged in four path graphs (each side's non-corner edges form a path of 9 nodes). In each of these path graphs, the nodes (non-corner edge cells) are connected in a line. In such a path graph, the endpoints have degree 1 within the path, and the internal nodes have degree 2 within the path. But in the overall grid graph, each non-corner edge cell also has connections to interior cells and possibly a corner cell.\n\nWait, but within the context of just the non-corner edge cells and their connections to other non-corner edge cells, each non-corner edge cell on a side is part of a path graph. So for example, on the top edge's non-corner cells: the path is (1,2) - (1,3) - ... - (1,10). Each of these is connected along the top edge. Additionally, each has connections to interior cells (south) and possibly a corner cell (for the endpoints). But in terms of their connections to other non-corner edge cells, along the top edge, they form a path.\n\nTherefore, in the subgraph induced by the non-corner edge cells and their connections to other non-corner edge cells along the same side, each side's non-corner edge cells form a path graph. Therefore, in each such path graph (of 9 nodes), the two endpoints have degree 1 within the path, and the internal nodes have degree 2 within the path. However, in the overall grid graph, each non-corner edge cell also has other edges (to interior cells and possibly a corner cell). Therefore, their total degree in the entire graph is 3, as we established earlier.\n\nBut in terms of pairing the non-corner edge cells to remove edges between them, perhaps we can pair adjacent non-corner edge cells along the sides, since they are connected by an edge. For example, on the top edge, we can pair (1,2) with (1,3), (1,4) with (1,5), etc., but this might not cover all.\n\nAlternatively, since each non-corner edge cell (except those at the very ends of the side) is connected to two other non-corner edge cells on the same side, we can form pairs along the path.\n\nWait, in a path graph with an odd number of nodes (9 nodes), you can pair them up as follows: the first with the second, third with the fourth, etc., leaving the last one unpaired if odd. But since 9 is odd, there will be one left over. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, in each side's path of 9 non-corner edge cells, you can pair the nodes in such a way that each pair is adjacent along the path. Since it's a path of 9 nodes, you can form 4 pairs and have one left over. Then, perhaps pair the leftover with someone from another side?\n\nBut this complicates things because the leftover from one side might not be adjacent to someone on another side.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can ignore the side constraints and just pair any two non-corner edge cells that are adjacent in the grid, regardless of which side they're on. Since the non-corner edge cells are arranged along the four sides, some are on adjacent sides near the corners.\n\nFor instance, the top-right corner's adjacent non-corner edge cells: (1,10) on the top edge and (2,11) on the right edge. These two are not adjacent to each other; they are diagonally adjacent? Wait, no. (1,10) is on the top row, column 10, and (2,11) is on the second row, column 11. They are not adjacent; they are separated by one cell diagonally. So they are not connec