0) as the singleton. Similarly for the left and right edges.\n\nTherefore, each side has one singleton leftover node. So total 4 singleton nodes. These are the \"end\" nodes of each side's path. For example:\n\n- Top edge's leftover: (1,10) or (1,2)? Wait, in my pairing above, the top edge's path from (1,2) to (1,10):\n\nIf we pair (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), then the leftover is (1,10). Alternatively, if we started pairing from the other end, we might have a different leftover. But regardless, each side has one leftover.\n\nThese leftover nodes are each adjacent only to one other non-corner edge cell along their side and possibly a corner cell. For example, (1,10) on the top edge is adjacent to (1,9) and (1,11) [the corner]. Similarly, (11,10) on the bottom edge is adjacent to (11,9) and (10,10) [interior? Wait, (11,10) is on the bottom edge, so its neighbors are (11,9), (11,11) [the corner], and (10,10) [interior]. So (11,10) is adjacent to (11,9) and (10,10), but (10,10) is interior, so not a non-corner edge cell.\n\nTherefore, each singleton leftover node is only adjacent to one non-corner edge cell (its neighbor along the side) and a corner cell and an interior cell. Therefore, they only have one connection to another non-corner edge cell.\n\nTherefore, these four singleton nodes cannot be paired with anyone else along their side, but perhaps they can be paired with nodes from other sides? However, as mentioned earlier, non-corner edge cells on different sides are not adjacent to each other except near the corners, but even then, they are not directly connected by an edge.\n\nFor instance, the top edge's leftover node (1,10) is adjacent to the corner (1,11), which is adjacent to the right edge's non-corner cell (2,11). But (1,10) and (2,11) are not adjacent; they are diagonally adjacent but not sharing an edge.\n\nTherefore, these four singleton nodes cannot be paired with any other non-corner edge cell via an edge in the grid graph. Therefore, we cannot pair them with anyone else using an existing edge. Therefore, for these four nodes, we cannot fix their degrees by removing an edge that affects both, because they only have one edge connecting them to another non-corner edge cell (along their own side). Therefore, to fix the degree of a singleton leftover node, we would have to remove its only edge connecting to another non-corner edge cell, but that would affect the paired node as well.\n\nWait, for example, take the top edge's leftover node (1,10). It is connected to (1,9) [a non-corner edge cell that was paired with someone else] and to the corner (1,11) and to (2,10) [interior]. If we remove the edge between (1,10) and (1,9), then we reduce the degree of (1,10) by 1 (from 3 to 2, which is even) and the degree of (1,9) by 1 (from 3 to 2, which is also even). However, (1,9) was previously paired with someone else, say (1,8). If we remove the edge between (1,9) and (1,10), then we also need to adjust the pairing for (1,9). Wait, this is getting complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of trying to pair along the side paths, we can consider that each non-corner edge cell has three edges: one along the side (to another non-corner edge cell or a corner), one to an interior cell, and one to another direction (like south for top edge cells). Therefore, each non-corner edge cell's three edges are:\n\n- For a top edge non-corner cell (1,j): edges to (1,j-1) [left], (1,j+1) [right], and (2,j) [south].\n\nSimilarly, a right edge non-corner cell (i,11): edges to (i-1,11) [north], (i+1,11) [south], and (i,10) [west].\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge cell has one edge along the side (connecting to another non-corner edge cell or a corner), one edge towards the interior of the grid, and one edge towards the adjacent row/column (depending on the side).\n\nTherefore, for the purpose of pairing non-corner edge cells to remove edges between them, we can focus on the edges along the sides. Each non-corner edge cell has one such edge along the side (except the endpoints which have only one side-edge connection). Wait, no: for example, a non-corner edge cell in the middle of the top edge has two side-edge connections: left and right along the top edge. Wait, actually, each non-corner edge cell (except those at the very ends of the side) has two side-edge connections (to the left and right along the edge) and one interior connection. The end non-corner edge cells (like (1,2) on the top edge) have one side-edge connection (to the right) and one to the corner and one interior.\n\nWait, let me clarify with an example:\n\nTake a non-corner edge cell on the top edge, not at the end, say (1,5):\n\n- North: nothing\n\n- South: (2,5) [interior]\n\n- East: (1,6) [non-corner edge cell]\n\n- West: (1,4) [non-corner edge cell]\n\nTherefore, this cell has two side-edge connections (east and west) along the top edge, one interior connection south, and no north connection.\n\nSimilarly, a non-corner edge cell at the end of the top edge, say (1,2):\n\n- North: nothing\n\n- South: (2,2) [interior]\n\n- East: (1,3) [non-corner edge cell]\n\n- West: (1,1) [corner]\n\nTherefore, this cell has one side-edge connection (east), one to the corner (west), and one interior (south).\n\nTherefore, in general, each non-corner edge cell (except those at the very ends of the side) has two side-edge connections (to adjacent non-corner edge cells on the same side), one interior connection, and one connection to a corner if it's at the end.\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge cell (except the four endpoints of the four sides) has two side-edge connections. The four endpoints (the ones at the very ends of each side, like (1,2) on the top, (1,11) is a corner, so the next one is (1,10)) have one side-edge connection.\n\nTherefore, in terms of edges along the sides:\n\nEach side's non-corner edge cells form a path graph where each internal node has degree 2 within the path (connected to left and right), and the two endpoints have degree 1 within the path.\n\nTherefore, along each side's path of 9 non-corner edge cells, there are 8 edges between them (since a path of n nodes has n-1 edges). So each side contributes 8 edges between its non-corner edge cells, and additionally, the endpoint nodes are connected to the corner cells (which are degree 2 vertices).\n\nTherefore, in total, the number of edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides is 4 sides * 8 edges = 32 edges.\n\nAdditionally, each non-corner edge cell has an edge to an interior cell. There are 36 non-corner edge cells, each connected to one interior cell (except maybe? Wait, no: every non-corner edge cell is on a side, and each has one edge going inward towards the interior of the grid). For example, a top-edge non-corner cell (1,j) has a south edge to (2,j), which is interior if j is not on the bottom row. Since all non-corner edge cells are on the edges, their inward edges go to the next row/column, which is interior. Therefore, each of the 36 non-corner edge cells has exactly one edge connecting to an interior cell. Therefore, there are 36 such interior connections.\n\nAdditionally, the corner cells each have two edges connecting to non-corner edge cells. There are four corners, each connected to two non-corner edge cells, so 4*2=8 edges, but these edges are already counted in the 32 edges between non-corner edge cells and corners? Wait, no. The edges from the corner cells to the non-corner edge cells are separate from the edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nThe edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides are the 32 edges (4 sides * 8 edges per side). Additionally, each corner cell is connected to two non-corner edge cells. For example, the top-right corner (1,11) is connected to (1,10) and (2,11). These edges (from corner to non-corner edge) are not counted in the 32 edges between non-corner edge cells, because the 32 edges were only between non-corner edge cells along the sides. The edges from the corner to the non-corner edge cells are separate.\n\nTherefore, in total, the edges related to non-corner edge cells are:\n\n- 32 edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides.\n\n- 36 edges from non-corner edge cells to interior cells.\n\n- 8 edges from corner cells to non-corner edge cells.\n\nAdditionally, the interior cells have their own connections among themselves, but we might not need to consider those for this problem.\n\nBut returning to the pairing idea: we need to pair up the 36 non-corner edge cells so that we can remove an edge between each pair, thereby reducing both their degrees by 1. To do this optimally, we want as many pairs as possible to be connected by an existing edge in the grid, so that removing that edge will fix both degrees.\n\nThe edges available for pairing are:\n\n1. The 32 edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides.\n\n2. The edges from non-corner edge cells to interior cells – but those edges connect to interior cells, which are not non-corner edge cells, so we can't pair non-corner edge cells via those edges.\n\n3. The edges from corner cells to non-corner edge cells – but those connect to corners, which are separate vertices, so again, not between two non-corner edge cells.\n\nTherefore, the only edges that connect two non-corner edge cells are the 32 edges along the sides.\n\nEach of these 32 edges connects two non-corner edge cells. Therefore, these 32 edges can be used to pair up 32 non-corner edge cells into 16 pairs. Each such pair, when we remove the connecting edge, will reduce the degree of both non-corner edge cells by 1, thereby making their degrees even (since they were originally 3, now 2). However, the remaining non-corner edge cells (36 - 32 = 4) cannot be paired via existing edges, since all edges between non-corner edge cells have already been used in these 16 pairs. Wait, no: each edge is between two non-corner edge cells, so each edge can only be used once. Since there are 32 edges, we can form 16 pairs, using all 32 edges. But we have 36 non-corner edge cells, so after pairing 32 of them (using 16 edges), we still have 4 non-corner edge cells left unpaired. These 4 must be handled somehow.\n\nWait, actually, each edge connects two non-corner edge cells, so each edge can be used to pair two non-corner edge cells. Therefore, with 32 edges, we can pair 32 non-corner edge cells into 16 pairs, leaving 36 - 32 = 4 non-corner edge cells unpaired. These 4 unpaired non-corner edge cells each have their degrees still at 3 (odd), which is problematic because we need all degrees to be even.\n\nTherefore, to fix those 4 unpaired non-corner edge cells, we need to reduce their degrees by 1 each. However, they can only be connected to:\n\n- For the unpaired non-corner edge cells: they are the ones that were not included in any of the 16 pairs. Since all edges between non-corner edge cells have been used in the pairs, the remaining unpaired non-corner edge cells must be the ones that were endpoints of the side paths, i.e., the ones that were not paired because they were at the ends of the path graphs.\n\nWait, in each side's path of 9 non-corner edge cells, we had 8 edges, allowing us to pair 16 non-corner edge cells (8 per side * 2 sides?), no, actually per side: 9 nodes, 8 edges. So per side, we can form 4 pairs (using 8 nodes) and leave 1 node unpaired. Therefore, over four sides, that's 4 unpaired nodes. So these four unpaired nodes are the \"end\" nodes of each side's path. Each of these four unpaired non-corner edge cells has only one edge connecting to another non-corner edge cell (the one before it along the path). The other edges of these unpaired nodes go to a corner or to an interior cell.\n\nTherefore, for each of these four unpaired non-corner edge cells, their only connection to another non-corner edge cell is along the side, but that connection has already been used in the pairing of the previous node. For example, take the top edge's unpaired node (1,10). It was paired with (1,9) via the edge between them? Wait no: in the path of the top edge, if we have nodes (1,2) through (1,10), and we paired them as (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), then the unpaired node is (1,10). The edge between (1,9) and (1,10) was not used in any pair; instead, (1,9) was paired with (1,8). Therefore, the edge between (1,9) and (1,10) is still present but wasn't used in a pair. Wait, no: actually, in the pairing, we only remove edges between the paired nodes. The unpaired nodes' edges to other non-corner edge cells are still present unless we choose to remove them.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm confusing the process. Let me rephrase:\n\nOur goal is to remove a set of edges such that every non-corner edge cell has its degree reduced by 1 (from 3 to 2), so that all degrees become even. To do this, for each non-corner edge cell, we need to remove exactly one edge incident to it. However, each edge removal affects two cells, so we have to choose edges to remove such that every non-corner edge cell has exactly one of its edges removed.\n\nThis is equivalent to finding a matching in the graph induced by the non-corner edge cells and their connections to each other (i.e., the edges along the sides) such that each non-corner edge cell is incident to exactly one edge in the matching. However, since the non-corner edge cells also have connections to interior cells and corners, but we are only allowed to remove edges that are between non-corner edge cells (since removing edges to interior or corner cells would affect other vertices' degrees as well, which we might not want to touch).\n\nWait, actually, we can remove any edge in the entire graph, but we are trying to minimize the number of edge removals while making all degrees even. However, the problem is that removing an edge between a non-corner edge cell and an interior cell would reduce the degree of the non-corner edge cell by 1 (good) but also reduce the degree of the interior cell by 1. Since interior cells have degree 4 (even), making their degree 3 (odd), which is bad. Therefore, we cannot remove edges that connect non-corner edge cells to interior cells, because that would create new odd-degree vertices (the interior cells). Similarly, removing an edge from a non-corner edge cell to a corner cell would reduce the corner's degree by 1 (from 2 to 1, which is odd), which is also bad because corners already had even degrees.\n\nTherefore, the only edges we can safely remove without introducing new odd-degree vertices are the edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides. Because:\n\n- Removing an edge between two non-corner edge cells reduces both their degrees by 1 (from 3 to 2), which is good.\n\n- Removing an edge between a non-corner edge cell and an interior cell would reduce the non-corner edge cell's degree by 1 (good) but the interior cell's degree by 1 (from 4 to 3, which is bad).\n\n- Removing an edge between a non-corner edge cell and a corner cell would reduce the non-corner edge cell's degree by 1 (good) but the corner's degree by 1 (from 2 to 1, which is bad).\n\nTherefore, the only edges we can remove without creating new odd-degree vertices are the 32 edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides. Thus, our only option is to use these 32 edges to form a matching that covers as many non-corner edge cells as possible, but we need to cover all 36 non-corner edge cells.\n\nBut since each edge can cover two non-corner edge cells, and there are 32 edges, we can cover 64 \"ends\" but we only have 36 non-corner edge cells. Wait, no: each edge covers two non-corner edge cells, so 32 edges can cover 64 non-corner edge cell slots, but since each cell is only one, we can cover at most 32*2 / 1 = 64, but since we have only 36 cells, the maximum coverage is 36. Therefore, we can cover all 36 non-corner edge cells with 18 edges (since 18 edges * 2 cells per edge = 36 cells). But we only have 32 edges available. Therefore, it's impossible to cover all 36 non-corner edge cells with the available 32 edges. Therefore, we are short by 4 edges.\n\nTherefore, we can only cover 32*2 = 64 cell-ends with 32 edges, but since each cell has one edge to remove, we need 36 edges. Therefore, we are short by 4 edges. Therefore, it's impossible to fix all 36 non-corner edge cells' degrees by only removing edges between them; we would need to remove 36 edges, but we only have 32 available without creating new odd-degree vertices. Therefore, we have a problem.\n\nThis suggests that we cannot make all degrees even by only removing edges between non-corner edge cells. Therefore, we must remove some edges that connect non-corner edge cells to other vertices (interior or corners), even though that might create new odd-degree vertices, and then compensate for those by removing additional edges elsewhere. This complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is more than 18, and we have to accept that some vertices will remain with odd degrees, but we are trying to minimize the number of edges to remove so that the remaining graph has as many even degrees as possible, allowing for the longest possible closed trail.\n\nWait, perhaps my initial approach was wrong. Maybe instead of trying to make the entire graph Eulerian, which might not be possible, we can instead find the maximum closed trail by considering that in any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, is that a thing?\n\nWait, let me recall that in any graph, the maximum number of edges in a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. I think this might be a theorem.\n\nWait, actually, according to some graph theory concepts, the maximum length of a closed trail in a graph is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Let me see if that makes sense.\n\nIn the case of the 3-node path graph with two odd-degree vertices (A and C), total edges = 2, number of odd-degree vertices = 2, so maximum closed trail length would be 2 - (2/2) = 1. But in reality, as we saw earlier, you can't have a closed trail of length 1 in that graph, since you can't form a cycle with a single edge. The maximum closed trail would actually be 0 (just staying put), but maybe they count the empty trail as 0. Alternatively, perhaps the formula counts something else.\n\nWait, maybe the formula is for the number of edges in an Eulerian trail, which requires the graph to be connected and have 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree. If it has 2 vertices of odd degree, then the Eulerian trail is open, and its length is total edges. If you want a closed trail, you need all degrees even, so if there are 2k odd-degree vertices, you need to remove k edges to make them even, and then the Eulerian circuit would have length total edges - k. So perhaps the formula is:\n\nIf the graph has m edges and n vertices with odd degree, then the maximum length of a closed trail is m - n/2.\n\nIn the 3-node path graph: m=2, n=2, so 2 - 1 = 1. But as we saw, you can't have a closed trail of length 1. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is m - (n - c)/2 where c is the number of connected components? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's m - (number of odd-degree vertices)/2, but only when the graph is connected. In the 3-node path graph, which is connected, it gives 2 - 1 = 1, but you can't have a closed trail of length 1. So maybe this formula is not correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the formula is that the maximum closed trail can have at most m - (number of odd-degree vertices)/2 edges, but you have to subtract more if the graph isn't connected or something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this is getting too convoluted, and I should look for another approach.\n\nLet me think about the grid itself. An 11x11 grid. Since Karthik is making a closed path that doesn't repeat any doors (edges), the problem is similar to finding the longest possible cycle in the grid graph where no edge is repeated.\n\nIn grid graphs, the longest cycle is generally related to the grid's dimensions. For an m x n grid, the longest cycle is known? Maybe it's the perimeter? But the perimeter would be 2*(m + n) - 4 for an m x n grid (since the four corners are counted twice). For an 11x11 grid, that would be 2*(11 + 11) - 4 = 44 - 4 = 40. But that's just the outer perimeter. However, we can have much longer cycles by going through the interior as well.\n\nAlternatively, the maximum cycle in a grid graph can be quite long. In fact, in a planar grid, the longest cycle can potentially traverse the entire grid in some fashion, but I don't recall the exact formula.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a grid graph, the maximum cycle length is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges needed to make all vertices even, which we were trying to compute earlier.\n\nWait, let's go back to the original idea that the maximum closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even. Even if that minimal number isn't exactly (number of odd-degree vertices)/2 due to graph structure, perhaps in practice for the grid graph, it's close enough?\n\nIn our case, total edges: 220.\n\nNumber of odd-degree vertices: 36 (the 36 non-corner edge cells).\n\nIf we suppose that the minimal number of edges to remove is 18 (36/2), then the maximum closed trail would be 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nBut earlier, we saw that we can't actually remove 18 edges to fix all 36 odd-degree vertices because there aren't enough edges between non-corner edge cells. We only have 32 edges between non-corner edge cells, so we can only fix 32*2=64 degree reductions, but we need 36. Wait, actually, each edge removal between two non-corner edge cells fixes two odd degrees, so each such edge removal reduces the total number of odd-degree vertices by 2. Starting with 36 odd-degree vertices, if we remove k edges between non-corner edge cells, we can reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by 2k. We need to get down to 0, so 2k = 36 => k = 18. But we only have 32 available edges between non-corner edge cells, so k can be at most 16 (since 32 edges can fix 32*2=64, but we only need 36). Wait, actually, no: each edge can only be used once. To fix 36 odd degrees, we need 18 edge removals (each fixing two). But since we only have 32 edges available for this purpose, we can do it in 18 removals, but we only need to use 18 edges out of the 32. The remaining 14 edges between non-corner edge cells can remain, but those would leave their two non-corner edge cells still with odd degrees? Wait no, if you remove an edge between two non-corner edge cells, both of their degrees are reduced by 1, so if they were odd (3), they become even (2). Therefore, every edge you remove between two non-corner edge cells fixes both of their degrees. Therefore, if you remove 18 edges between non-corner edge cells, you can fix 36 degree reductions (18 edges * 2 vertices per edge), thereby turning all 36 non-corner edge cells from odd degree to even degree. The only problem is that there are only 32 edges available between non-corner edge cells, so you can only remove up to 32 edges, which would fix 64 degrees, but we only need to fix 36. Wait, this seems contradictory.\n\nWait, let me think again: each non-corner edge cell has degree 3 (odd). To make it even, we need to remove one edge incident to it. Each edge removal that is between two non-corner edge cells will fix two non-corner edge cells' degrees. An edge removal that is between a non-corner edge cell and an interior cell would fix one non-corner edge cell's degree but mess up the interior cell's degree. Similarly for corner connections. Since we can't do the latter without introducing new odd-degree vertices, we must only use the 32 edges between non-corner edge cells. Each such edge removal fixes two non-corner edge cells. Therefore, to fix all 36 non-corner edge cells, we need to perform 18 edge removals (since 18*2 = 36). However, we have 32 edges available, so we can do this by removing 18 edges out of the 32, leaving 14 edges untouched. The untouched edges are still present, but their presence doesn't affect the degrees because we've already removed one edge from each non-corner edge cell involved in the removed edges.\n\nWait, actually, no: if we remove an edge between two non-corner edge cells, that reduces their degrees, but the other edges incident to them remain. For example, take a non-corner edge cell that is part of one removed edge and two other edges (to interior and another non-corner edge cell). After removing the one edge between it and another non-corner edge cell, its degree is now 3 - 1 = 2, which is even, so that's good. The other edges (to interior and the other non-corner edge cell) are still there, but their presence doesn't matter because we've already adjusted the degree. Wait, but the other edges are still present in the graph, but we are not traversing them in the trail? Wait no, the trail is a closed walk that doesn't repeat any edge. The degrees in the remaining graph after edge removals are what matter for the Eulerian circuit, but the trail is in the original graph with some edges removed.\n\nActually, perhaps I need to clarify: when we remove edges to make the graph Eulerian, the Eulerian circuit exists in the modified graph (with edges removed). The length of the Eulerian circuit is then the number of edges in the modified graph. So the maximum closed trail in the original graph without repeating edges is equal to the number of edges in the modified graph (after removing the minimal number of edges to make it Eulerian). Therefore, the formula would be:\n\nMaximum closed trail length = Total edges - (number of edges removed to make graph Eulerian)\n\nAnd the number of edges removed is equal to the minimal number of edges needed to make all degrees even, which in the case of our grid graph is 18 (since we have 36 odd-degree vertices, so 18 edge removals). But we only have 32 edges between non-corner edge cells, so we can perform 18 edge removals among those, and that would fix all 36 non-corner edge cells' degrees. The other edges (the 14 remaining between non-corner edge cells and the 36 edges to interior cells and 8 edges to corners) remain in the graph, but since we are only removing 18 edges (all between non-corner edge cells), the rest of the graph's edges are still there. However, the corners and interior cells will now have odd degrees?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nWhen we remove 18 edges between non-corner edge cells, we fix the 36 non-corner edge cells' degrees (each loses one edge, going from 3 to 2). But what about the corners and interior cells?\n\nEach corner cell originally had degree 2 (even). Each corner cell is connected to two non-corner edge cells. If we remove an edge from a corner cell to a non-corner edge cell, then the corner's degree would decrease by 1, making it odd. But in our removal process, we only removed edges between non-corner edge cells, not edges connecting to corners. Therefore, the corners' degrees remain at 2 (even). Similarly, interior cells: each interior cell originally has degree 4 (even). Each interior cell is connected to up to four edge cells. The edges we removed are only between non-corner edge cells, so interior cells are only affected if one of their edges was removed. But the edges we removed are only between non-corner edge cells, which are on the perimeter, so an interior cell's edges are to other interior cells or to perimeter non-corner edge cells. Wait, actually, each interior cell is connected to its four neighbors: up, down, left, right. Some of those neighbors might be non-corner edge cells if the interior cell is adjacent to the grid's edge.\n\nFor example, consider an interior cell adjacent to the top edge: say cell (2, j) where j is between 2 and 10. Then cell (2,j) is adjacent to the top-edge non-corner cell (1,j). If we remove the edge between (1,j) and its neighbor along the top edge, that doesn't affect the edge between (1,j) and (2,j). Therefore, the interior cell (2,j) still has its edge to (1,j) intact unless we specifically remove that edge. But in our edge removal process, we only removed edges along the top edge between non-corner edge cells, like between (1,j) and (1,j+1), not between (1,j) and (2,j). Therefore, the interior cells' degrees remain even, because none of the edges incident to them have been removed (except possibly if an interior cell is part of a removed edge, but interior cells aren't part of the non-corner edge cells; they are separate).\n\nWait, actually, interior cells are not non-corner edge cells; they are separate vertices. The edges we are removing are only between the non-corner edge cells along the sides. Therefore, the interior cells' degrees are unaffected, remaining at 4 (even). The corners' degrees are also unaffected, remaining at 2 (even). Therefore, by removing 18 edges between non-corner edge cells, we have transformed the original graph into a new graph where all vertices have even degrees: 0 odd-degree vertices. Therefore, this new graph is Eulerian, and thus has an Eulerian circuit, which is a closed trail that traverses every edge exactly once. Therefore, the length of this Eulerian circuit is equal to the number of edges in the new graph, which is 220 (original edges) minus 18 (edges removed) = 202 edges.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 202.\n\nBut wait, hold on, this seems to suggest that it's possible to have an Eulerian circuit in the modified graph with 202 edges. But earlier, we thought that in the original grid graph, the number of odd-degree vertices was 36, so we needed to remove 18 edges, and since we could do so by only removing edges between non-corner edge cells (which are available), then yes, this should work.\n\nBut let me verify with a smaller grid to see if this works.\n\nTake a 2x2 grid. That's 4 cells, forming a square. Each corner cell has degree 2, and there are no non-corner edge cells because in a 2x2 grid, all cells are corners. Wait, in a 2x2 grid, every cell is a corner, so all have degree 2. Therefore, it's already Eulerian, so the maximum closed trail is 4 edges, which is correct.\n\nAnother test: 3x3 grid.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, the corner cells have degree 2, the edge non-corner cells (there are 4 of them, on each side between the corners) have degree 3, and the center cell has degree 4.\n\nTotal non-corner edge cells: 4, each with degree 3 (odd). So number of odd-degree vertices: 4. Therefore, minimal edges to remove: 4/2 = 2 edges.\n\nTotal edges in 3x3 grid: horizontal edges: 3 rows * 2 edges per row = 6; vertical edges: 3 columns * 2 edges per column = 6; total edges = 12.\n\nTherefore, maximum closed trail length would be 12 - 2 = 10.\n\nIs that correct?\n\nLet me visualize a 3x3 grid. Let's label the cells from (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nThe non-corner edge cells are:\n\nTop edge: (1,2)\n\nBottom edge: (3,2)\n\nLeft edge: (2,1)\n\nRight edge: (2,3)\n\nEach of these has degree 3.\n\nCorners: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) have degree 2.\n\nCenter cell (2,2) has degree 4.\n\nTo make all degrees even, we need to remove 2 edges. Let's try removing two edges between the non-corner edge cells.\n\nFor example, remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (3,2) and (2,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2) now has degree 2 (since it loses the edge to (2,2)), but originally it had edges to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). Wait, no: (1,2) is on the top edge, so its neighbors are (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). If we remove the edge to (2,2), then its degree becomes 2 (still even). Similarly, (3,2) loses its edge to (2,2), going from 3 to 2. The center cell (2,2) loses two edges: to (1,2) and (3,2), so its degree goes from 4 to 2, which is even. The other non-corner edge cells: (2,1) and (2,3) still have degree 3? Wait, (2,1) is on the left edge: its neighbors are (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2). If we didn't remove any edge from (2,1) to (2,2), then (2,1) still has degree 3. Wait a second, so this approach didn't fix all non-corner edge cells.\n\nAh, right, because we only removed edges connected to the center cell, but the other non-corner edge cells like (2,1) and (2,3) are still connected to the center cell, so their degrees remain odd.\n\nTherefore, my mistake: removing edges from the center cell only affects its degree but not the others. To fix all four non-corner edge cells, we need to remove edges that are between the non-corner edge cells themselves.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the non-corner edge cells are each on the sides:\n\nTop: (1,2)\n\nBottom: (3,2)\n\nLeft: (2,1)\n\nRight: (2,3)\n\nEach of these is adjacent only to the center cell and one other non-corner edge cell? Wait:\n\nTop edge cell (1,2) is adjacent to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [corner], and (2,2) [center].\n\nSimilarly, bottom edge cell (3,2) is adjacent to (3,1) [corner], (3,3) [corner], and (2,2) [center].\n\nLeft edge cell (2,1) is adjacent to (1,1) [corner], (3,1) [corner], and (2,2) [center].\n\nRight edge cell (2,3) is adjacent to (1,3) [corner], (3,3) [corner], and (2,2) [center].\n\nTherefore, none of the non-corner edge cells are adjacent to each other; they are all only connected to the center and the corners. Therefore, there are no edges between non-corner edge cells in the 3x3 grid. Therefore, the only edges between non-corner edge cells would be zero. Therefore, we cannot remove any edges between non-corner edge cells because there are none. Therefore, to fix the four non-corner edge cells (each with odd degree), we have to remove edges that connect them to other vertices, but doing so would affect the degrees of those other vertices.\n\nFor example, if we remove the edge from (1,2) to (2,2), then (1,2) becomes degree 2 (good), but (2,2) becomes degree 3 (odd), which is bad. Similarly, if we remove the edge from (2,1) to (2,2), then (2,1) becomes degree 2 (good), but (2,2) becomes degree 3 (odd). To fix both (1,2) and (2,1), we would have to remove two edges connected to (2,2), but that would make (2,2)'s degree drop by 2, from 4 to 2, which is even. Wait:\n\nLet's try:\n\nRemove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (2,1) and (2,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2) degree: 2 (good)\n\n- (2,1) degree: 2 (good)\n\n- (2,2) degree: 4 - 2 = 2 (good)\n\n- (3,2) and (2,3) still have degree 3 (bad). So we still have two non-corner edge cells with odd degrees.\n\nThen we need to fix those as well. Remove edges from (3,2) to (2,2) and (2,3) to (2,2). Now:\n\n- (3,2) degree: 2 (good)\n\n- (2,3) degree: 2 (good)\n\n- (2,2) degree: 2 - 2 = 0 (even, good)\n\nNow all non-corner edge cells are fixed, but we removed four edges, each connected to the center cell. The center cell now has degree 0, which is even. The corners still have degree 2. So now the entire graph has all even degrees. Therefore, we needed to remove four edges, not two. But according to the formula, number of odd-degree vertices was 4, so minimal edges to remove should be 2. But in reality, we needed to remove four edges.\n\nThis discrepancy arises because in the 3x3 grid, the non-corner edge cells are not adjacent to each other, so we can't pair them via existing edges between non-corner edge cells. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is actually equal to the number of odd-degree vertices, not divided by two. Because each edge removal can only fix one odd-degree vertex if they are not adjacent.\n\nIn this case, since the four non-corner edge cells are each only connected to the center cell and corners, and not to each other, each requires its own edge removal to fix its degree, but each such edge removal affects another vertex (the center cell or a corner). However, in this case, removing edges from the non-corner edge cells to the center cell allows us to fix two non-corner edge cells at a time if we remove two edges from the center cell. Wait, in the above example, removing two edges from the center cell fixed two non-corner edge cells but also reduced the center cell's degree by two, which kept it even. Then removing another two edges fixed the other two non-corner edge cells without affecting the center cell's parity. Therefore, in total, four edges were removed, but they were all connected to the center cell. Since the center cell started with even degree (4), removing four edges (even number) brings it back to 0, which is even. Therefore, by removing four edges (all incident to the center cell), we fixed all four non-corner edge cells' degrees and kept all other vertices' degrees even. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is four, which is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices (four), not divided by two.\n\nTherefore, the formula that minimal edges to remove = number of odd-degree vertices / 2 only works when the odd-degree vertices can be paired via edges between them, allowing each edge removal to fix two vertices. In cases where they can't be paired (like in the 3x3 grid), you might need to remove more edges.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original 11x11 grid problem, we have 36 non-corner edge cells each with odd degree. If they can be paired such that each pair is connected by an edge, then we can fix them with 18 edge removals. However, in the 11x11 grid, the non-corner edge cells are arranged in four paths along the sides, each path having 9 nodes. In each such path, the nodes (non-corner edge cells) are connected in a line, so they can be paired along the path.\n\nFor example, on the top edge's path of 9 non-corner edge cells, we can pair them as (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), leaving (1,10) unpaired. Then on the next side, the bottom edge's path similarly can be paired, leaving its last node unpaired, and same for left and right edges. This gives us four unpaired nodes. However, these four unpaired nodes are each at the end of their respective side's path and are only connected to one other non-corner edge cell along the path. The remaining edges incident to them go to corners or interior cells.\n\nTo fix these four unpaired nodes, we would need to remove their only edge connecting them to another non-corner edge cell. For instance, take the top edge's unpaired node (1,10). It is connected to (1,9) via an edge along the top edge. If we remove that edge, then (1,10)'s degree becomes 2 (good), but (1,9)'s degree was originally 3 (odd), now becomes 2 (even). However, (1,9) was previously paired with (1,8), so removing the edge between (1,9) and (1,10) would also break the pairing for (1,9). This seems like a problem because we might have to adjust pairings.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of pairing along the path in a way that leaves one unpaired node per side, we can pair them differently to minimize the number of unpaired nodes. For example, in a path of 9 nodes, which is odd, you can't pair all nodes without one left over. Therefore, regardless of how you pair them, each side will have one unpaired node. Therefore, we have four unpaired nodes total.\n\nTo fix these four unpaired nodes, we need to remove their single edge connecting them to another non-corner edge cell. Each such removal will fix the unpaired node's degree but also affect the paired node's degree. However, the paired node was previously paired with another node, so removing one of its edges will unpair it, requiring another adjustment.\n\nThis suggests that we might need to form a different kind of pairing where some nodes are paired across different sides, but since non-corner edge cells on different sides aren't adjacent, this isn't possible via existing edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of trying to pair along the side paths, we can consider that each non-corner edge cell has three edges: one along the side, one to an interior cell, and one to a corner (for the end cells). To make all degrees even, we need to remove one edge from each non-corner edge cell. To do this without affecting other vertices' degrees, we can choose for each non-corner edge cell to remove the edge that connects it to an interior cell. This way, we don't affect the interior cells' degrees (since we're removing edges from non-corner edge cells to interiors, which would reduce the interior cells' degrees by 1, making them odd). But we can't do that because that would create new odd-degree vertices in the interior cells.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge that connects each non-corner edge cell to its corner. For example, for the top edge's end cell (1,10), remove its edge to the corner (1,11). This reduces its degree to 2 (good), and the corner's degree to 1 (bad). But the corner was originally degree 2, now it's 1, which is odd. Similarly, doing this for all four end non-corner edge cells connected to corners would reduce each corner's degree by 1 (making them odd) and fix the four unpaired non-corner edge cells. Then we would have four new odd-degree vertices (the corners) and 32 non-corner edge cells that were previously paired and now have even degrees? Wait, let's think carefully.\n\nSuppose we decide to remove the edge from each non-corner edge cell to its adjacent corner. For the four corners:\n\n- Each corner is connected to two non-corner edge cells. For example, the top-right corner (1,11) is connected to (1,10) and (2,11).\n\nIf we remove the edge from (1,10) to (1,11), then (1,10)'s degree becomes 2 (good), but (1,11)'s degree becomes 1 (bad).\n\nSimilarly, if we remove the edge from (2,11) to (1,11), then (2,11)'s degree becomes 2 (good), and (1,11)'s degree becomes 1 (bad). But we can only remove one edge per corner, since each corner has two edges to non-corner edge cells. To fix both non-corner edge cells adjacent to a corner, we would have to remove both edges connected to the corner, which would reduce the corner's degree by 2 (from 2 to 0, which is even). However, this would fix both non-corner edge cells adjacent to the corner but requires removing two edges per corner.\n\nFor example, take the top-right corner (1,11):\n\n- It is connected to (1,10) and (2,11).\n\nIf we remove both edges from (1,11) to (1,10) and to (2,11), then:\n\n- (1,10) loses one edge (to corner), so its degree goes from 3 to 2 (good).\n\n- (2,11) loses one edge (to corner), so its degree goes from 3 to 2 (good).\n\n- The corner (1,11) loses both edges, so its degree goes from 2 to 0 (even, good).\n\nTherefore, by removing two edges connected to each corner, we can fix the two non-corner edge cells adjacent to that corner without creating new odd-degree vertices. Since there are four corners, each requiring two edge removals, that's 4 corners * 2 edges = 8 edge removals.\n\nAfter doing this for all four corners, all non-corner edge cells that were adjacent to corners have their degrees fixed, and the corners are also fixed. However, the non-corner edge cells that are not adjacent to corners (the ones in the middle of the sides) still need to have one of their edges removed. Each of these middle non-corner edge cells has three edges: two along the side (to adjacent non-corner edge cells) and one to an interior cell.\n\nTo fix their degrees, we can choose to remove one of their side edges. For example, take a middle non-corner edge cell on the top edge, say (1,5). It is connected to (1,4), (1,6), and (2,5). To fix its degree, we can remove the edge to (1,4) or to (1,6), or to (2,5). Removing the edge to an interior cell (2,5) would affect the interior cell's degree, which we don't want. Removing an edge to another non-corner edge cell (along the side) doesn't affect any other vertex's degree negatively except the paired cell.\n\nSuppose we decide to remove edges between non-corner edge cells along the sides for the middle ones. For example, on the top edge's path of non-corner edge cells from (1,2) to (1,10), after removing the edges at the corners, we have the sequence:\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,3),\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) and (1,4),\n\n...\n\n(1,9) connected to (1,8) and (1,10).\n\nWait, but we already removed the edge from (1,10) to the corner, but not the edge between (1,9) and (1,10). To fix (1,9)'s degree, we need to remove one of its edges. If we remove the edge between (1,9) and (1,10), then (1,9)'s degree becomes 2 (since it was connected to (1,8), (1,10), and (2,9)), so removing one edge leaves it at 2. However, (1,10)'s degree was already fixed by removing its edge to the corner, so (1,10)'s degree is now 2 (from the three edges: to (1,9), (2,10), and the removed corner edge). Wait, no: (1,10)'s original edges were to (1,9), (1,11) [corner], and (2,10). We removed the edge to the corner, so now (1,10) has edges to (1,9) and (2,10), so degree 2, which is good. Therefore, removing the edge between (1,9) and (1,10) is okay because (1,10) is already fixed. But (1,9) was previously connected to (1,8) and (1,10), so removing the (1,9)-(1,10) edge leaves (1,9) with degree 2 (from (1,8) and (2,9)), which is good. Therefore, this removal doesn't create any new odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, for the middle non-corner edge cells, we can pair them along the side paths and remove the edges between them, fixing their degrees without affecting other vertices. Since each such removal fixes two non-corner edge cells' degrees, and there are 36 non-corner edge cells minus the 8 that were fixed by the corner edge removals (wait, no: each corner fixed two non-corner edge cells, and there are four corners, so 8 non-corner edge cells fixed by corner edge removals). Therefore, the remaining non-corner edge cells to fix are 36 - 8 = 28. These 28 can be paired along the side paths, forming 14 pairs, requiring 14 edge removals. Each pair removal fixes two non-corner edge cells.\n\nTherefore, total edge removals needed:\n\n- 8 edge removals at the corners (each corner required two edge removals, but actually each corner required removing two edges connected to it, which are edges to two non-corner edge cells, so that's two edges per corner, totaling 8 edges).\n\n- 14 edge removals along the side paths for the remaining non-corner edge cells.\n\nTotal edge removals: 8 + 14 = 22 edges.\n\nAfter these removals:\n\n- All non-corner edge cells have their degrees reduced by 1 (either via corner removals or side removals), so all are even (2).\n\n- The corners had their degrees reduced by 2 each (from 2 to 0), so they're even.\n\n- The interior cells: each interior cell had degree 4. Some of their edges may have been removed if they were connected to a non-corner edge cell whose edge was removed. For example, if we removed an edge between a non-corner edge cell and an interior cell, then the interior cell's degree would decrease by 1. But in our plan, we only removed edges between non-corner edge cells (along the sides) or between non-corner edge cells and corners. Therefore, the edges from non-corner edge cells to interior cells remain intact. Therefore, each interior cell still has all its original edges except possibly none, so their degrees remain 4 (even).\n\nTherefore, after removing 22 edges, the entire graph has all even degrees, and thus is Eulerian. Therefore, the maximum closed trail length is the total number of edges minus the edges removed: 220 - 22 = 198.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous calculation where I thought it was 202. Where did I go wrong?\n\nAh, in the initial approach, I assumed that we could remove 18 edges between non-corner edge cells and fix everything, but that didn't account for the fact that the corner-connected non-corner edge cells needed additional edge removals. By breaking it down, we see that it actually requires 22 edge removals, resulting in 198.\n\nBut let's verify this step-by-step:\n\n1. **Corner Handling:**\n   - Each corner is connected to two non-corner edge cells.\n   - To fix both non-corner edge cells adjacent to a corner without affecting the corner's parity, we must remove both edges from the corner to its two adjacent non-corner edge cells.\n   - This requires removing 2 edges per corner, so 4 corners * 2 edges = 8 edges removed.\n   - After this, the corner's degree is 0 (even), and the two adjacent non-corner edge cells each lose one edge, going from 3 to 2 (even).\n\n2. **Remaining Non-Corner Edge Cells:**\n   - Originally 36 non-corner edge cells.\n   - 8 were fixed by the corner edge removals, so 36 - 8 = 28 remain.\n   - These 28 are arranged along the four sides, each side now having its two end non-corner edge cells fixed, and the middle cells needing fixing.\n   - Each side originally had 9 non-corner edge cells. After fixing the two end cells (adjacent to the corner), each side has 9 - 2 = 7 non-corner edge cells remaining? Wait no:\n\nWait, let me think about one side, say the top edge:\n\n- The top edge has non-corner edge cells from (1,2) to (1,10), total of 9 cells.\n\n- The two end cells are (1,2) and (1,10).\n\n- Each end cell was connected to a corner. To fix their degrees, we removed their edges to the corners. Therefore, the two end cells now have degree 2 (good), but their connections along the side remain.\n\n- The remaining non-corner edge cells on the top edge are the middle 7 cells: (1,3) through (1,9).\n\n- Each of these middle cells has degree 3 (they are connected to two adjacent non-corner edge cells along the top edge and one interior cell below).\n\n- To fix their degrees, we need to remove one edge from each. Since they are in a path of 7 nodes, we can pair them into 3 pairs and have one left over? Wait, 7 is odd, so pairing would leave one unpaired.\n\nWait, perhaps my earlier assumption was incorrect. Let's consider one side after corner fixings:\n\nTake the top edge after fixing its two end cells:\n\nThe non-corner edge cells are still (1,2) through (1,10), but (1,2) and (1,10) are now degree 2. The middle cells (1,3) to (1,9) (7 cells) each have degree 3 and need to have one edge removed.\n\nTo fix these 7 cells, we can form 3 pairs and have one leftover. Each pair can be connected by an edge along the top edge, and we remove that edge, fixing both. The leftover cell would then need its degree fixed by removing another edge, but we can't pair it with anyone else along the side.\n\nAlternatively, since these middle cells form a path of 7 nodes (from (1,3) to (1,9)), we can pair them as follows:\n\n- (1,3)-(1,4), (1,5)-(1,6), (1,7)-(1,8), leaving (1,9) unpaired.\n\nThis uses 3 edge removals, fixing 6 cells, and leaves one cell (1,9) unpaired. To fix (1,9), we need to remove one of its edges. Its edges are to (1,8), (1,10), and (2,9). The edge to (1,10) is already fixed (since (1,10) is an end cell with degree 2), but removing the edge between (1,9) and (1,10) would reduce (1,9)'s degree to 2 (good), but (1,10) was already at degree 2, so its degree would go to 1 (bad). Therefore, we can't do that.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (1,9) and (2,9) (the interior cell). This would reduce (1,9)'s degree to 2 (good) but reduce the interior cell's degree by 1 (from 4 to 3, odd). Not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (1,9) and (1,8). This reduces (1,9)'s degree to 2 (good) and (1,8)'s degree from 3 to 2 (good). Wait, (1,8) was originally connected to (1,7), (1,9), and (2,8). If we remove the edge to (1,9), then (1,8)'s degree becomes 2 (good), and (1,9)'s degree becomes 2 (good). Therefore, this works! So by removing the edge between (1,8) and (1,9), we fix both (1,8) and (1,9)'s degrees.\n\nWait, but (1,8) was already part of a pair? Earlier, we paired (1,7)-(1,8). If we instead adjust the pairing to include (1,8)-(1,9), then we can fix both without leaving anyone unpaired.\n\nLet me re-examine the pairing on the top edge's middle 7 cells (positions 3 through 9):\n\nNodes: [1,3], [1,4], [1,5], [1,6], [1,7], [1,8], [1,9]\n\nTo pair them all:\n\n- Pair [1,3] with [1,4]\n\n- Pair [1,5] with [1,6]\n\n- Pair [1,7] with [1,8]\n\n- Leave [1,9] unpaired\n\nBut this leaves [1,9] unpaired. Alternatively:\n\n- Pair [1,3] with [1,4]\n\n- Pair [1,5] with [1,6]\n\n- Pair [1,7] with [1,8]\n\n- Then [1,9] is left. To fix [1,9], pair it with someone else? But it can only be paired with [1,8] or [1,10] or the interior. Since [1,8] is already paired, and [1,10] is fixed, the only option is to pair [1,9] with [1,8], breaking their previous pairing. Then:\n\n- Pair [1,7] with [1,8]\n\n- Then [1,9] is left. Remove the edge between [1,9] and [1,8], which would fix both [1,8] and [1,9]'s degrees, but [1,8] was already paired with [1,7]. This complicates.\n\nAlternatively, reconfigure pairings:\n\n- [1,3] with [1,4]\n\n- [1,5] with [1,6]\n\n- [1,7] with [1,9]\n\n- [1,8] remains unpaired? No, [1,7] paired with [1,9], leaving [1,8] unpaired.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Perhaps it's better to accept that with an odd number of nodes (7), you can't pair them all, so you have to have one leftover per side's middle section.\n\nTherefore, for the top edge's middle 7 cells, you can fix 6 of them with 3 edge removals, leaving one cell (e.g., [1,9]) needing an additional edge removal. To fix that last cell, you have to remove one of its edges, which may require another edge removal that affects another cell.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to not pair them strictly along the path and instead allow some flexibility. For example, for the top edge's middle 7 cells, instead of pairing consecutively, you can do:\n\n- [1,3] paired with [1,4]\n\n- [1,5] paired with [1,6]\n\n- [1,7] paired with [1,8]\n\n- [1,9] must be handled by removing its edge to [1,8], but that affects [1,8]'s pairing.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between [1,9] and [2,9] (the interior cell), but that affects the interior cell's degree.\n\nAlternatively, accept that we need an extra edge removal for the last cell, and account for it in the total count.\n\nIf on each side's middle section (after corner fixations) with an odd number of cells (7 in this case), we need one additional edge removal beyond the pairs, then per side:\n\n- For a side with 7 middle cells: requires 4 edge removals (3 pairs + 1 extra).\n\nWait, no: pairing 6 cells takes 3 edge removals, and the last cell requires 1 more edge removal (total 4 per side's middle section). But each edge removal in the middle section fixes two cells or one?\n\nActually, each edge removal in the middle section between two cells fixes both their degrees. For the last cell, if we remove an edge that it shares with another cell (even if that cell was previously paired), then both cells' degrees are adjusted.\n\nFor example, on the top edge's middle cells:\n\nAfter pairing [1,3]-[1,4], [1,5]-[1,6], [1,7]-[1,8], leaving [1,9]. To fix [1,9], remove the edge between [1,9] and [1,8]. This removes the edge that was previously part of the pair [1,7]-[1,8]. Now [1,8] loses an edge (from 3 to 2), and [1,9] loses an edge (from 3 to 2). However, [1,7] still has its original edges: connected to [1,6], [1,8], and (2,7). After removing the edge between [1,8] and [1,9], [1,8] now has degree 2 (from [1,7] and (2,8)), and [1,9] has degree 2 (from (2,9) and whatever else? Wait, [1,9] is connected to [1,8] (now removed), [1,10] (which is already fixed at degree 2), and (2,9). So [1,9]'s degree is 2 (from (2,9) and maybe another? Wait no:\n\nOriginal edges for [1,9]:\n\n- North: nothing\n\n- South: (2,9)\n\n- East: (1,10) [already fixed, degree 2]\n\n- West: (1,8)\n\nAfter removing the edge to (1,8), [1,9] has edges to (2,9) and (1,10). Since (1,10)'s degree is already 2 (from its other edges), this is okay. So [1,9]'s degree is now 2 (good), and [1,8]'s degree is now 2 (from [1,7] and (2,8)), so both are fixed. However, [1,7] still has its original connections: to [1,6], [1,8], and (2,7). After removing the edge between [1,8] and [1,9], [1,7] is still connected to [1,6] and (2,7), so its degree is still 3 (odd). Wait a minute! Oh no, this is a problem.\n\n[1,7] was part of the pair [1,7]-[1,8]. By removing the edge between [1,8] and [1,9], we didn't affect [1,7]'s edges. Therefore, [1,7] still has degree 3 (connected to [1,6], [1,8], and (2,7)). Therefore, this approach doesn't fix [1,7]'s degree.\n\nThis is getting really convoluted. Perhaps a better approach is needed.\n\nLet me step back and consider that in order to fix all non-corner edge cells' degrees without affecting other vertices' degrees, we must remove edges that are between non-corner edge cells. Each such removal fixes two non-corner edge cells but doesn't affect other vertices' degrees (since the other edges of those non-corner edge cells go to corners or interiors, which we are not touching). Therefore, the minimal number of edge removals required is equal to the number of non-corner edge cells divided by 2, rounded up? Wait, but they must be paired via existing edges.\n\nIn the entire grid, the non-corner edge cells form four separate paths along the sides, each of length 9. Each path of n nodes has (n - 1) edges between them. To pair all the non-corner edge cells along each path, we need to remove floor(n/2) edges per path, but since each edge removal fixes two nodes, the number of edge removals per path is ceil(n/2) - 1? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, for a path of n nodes, the number of edges between them is n-1. To make all nodes in the path have even degree (within the path), we need to remove edges such that each node has even degree in the path subgraph. Since originally each internal node has degree 2 in the path and the endpoints have degree 1. To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges so that the endpoints have degree 0 and internal nodes have degree 2. This requires removing the edges incident to the endpoints. For a path of n nodes, this would require removing the two endpoints' edges, but since they're connected to each other in the path, perhaps it's different.\n\nActually, in graph theory, to make all nodes in a path graph have even degree, you need to remove all edges incident to the endpoints. For a path of n nodes:\n\n- The two endpoints have degree 1.\n\n- To make them degree 0, remove their single edge.\n\n- The internal nodes then have degree 2, which is even.\n\nTherefore, for a path of n nodes, you need to remove 1 edge (the one connecting the two endpoints? No, wait, each endpoint has one edge in the path. To make both endpoints have degree 0 in the path, you need to remove both edges connected to the endpoints. However, the two endpoints are connected by the path's edges. For example, in a path A-B-C-D, the endpoints are A and D. To make A and D have degree 0 in the path, you need to remove the edge A-B and D-C. The internal nodes B and C will then have degree 1 (from A-B removed, so B only connected to C; and C only connected to B and D, but D-C is removed, so C only connected to B). Wait, that's not right.\n\nActually, in the path A-B-C-D:\n\n- Original degrees: A:1, B:2, C:2, D:1.\n\nTo make all degrees even:\n\n- Remove edge A-B: now A:0, B:1, C:2, D:1.\n\n- Remove edge D-C: now A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0.\n\nNow degrees: A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. Not all even.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges A-B and C-D:\n\n- Then degrees: A:0, B:1 (only connected to C), C:1 (connected to B and D), D:0. Still not all even.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges B-C:\n\n- Then degrees: A:1, B:1, C:1, D:1. All odd.\n\nHmm, so it's impossible to make all degrees even in a path graph by removing edges. Because the total degree is even (sum of degrees is 4 for 4 nodes), but you can't make all degrees even because you can only subtract even numbers from the total degree.\n\nWait, the sum of degrees in any graph is even. To have all degrees even, that's fine. But in a path graph with 4 nodes (n=4), the sum is 1+2+2+1=6, which is even. To make all degrees even, we need to reduce the two endpoints' degrees by 1 each, and leave the internal nodes as is. So remove the two edges at the ends: A-B and D-C. Then degrees become A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. The sum is 0+1+1+0=2, which is even, but not all degrees even. Alternatively, remove one internal edge: B-C. Then degrees become A:1, B:1, C:1, D:1. Sum 4, still not all even. Alternatively, remove two edges: A-B and B-C. Then degrees: A:0, B:0, C:1, D:1. Sum 2, still not all even.\n\nTherefore, it's impossible to make all degrees even in a path graph with an even number of nodes? Wait, but 4 nodes is even. Hmm, this suggests that for a path graph with even number of nodes, you can't make all degrees even by removing edges. Only if the number of nodes is odd?\n\nWait, let's try a path with 3 nodes: A-B-C.\n\nDegrees: A:1, B:2, C:1. Sum is 4.\n\nTo make all degrees even:\n\n- Remove edge A-B: degrees A:0, B:1, C:1. Sum 2.\n\n- Remove edge B-C: degrees A:1, B:0, C:0. Sum 1.\n\nNot possible.\n\nAlternatively, remove both edges: degrees all 0. Sum 0. That works. So by removing all edges, you make all degrees 0, which is even. But that's removing all edges, which is extreme.\n\nTherefore, the only way to make all degrees even in a path graph is to remove all edges, or remove edges in such a way that the remaining graph has all even degrees, which may require disconnecting parts of the graph.\n\nThis suggests that for a path of n nodes, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is ceil(n/2). For example:\n\n- n=3: remove 2 edges (leaves no edges, all degrees 0).\n\n- n=4: remove 2 edges (e.g., remove A-B and C-D, resulting in two isolated nodes B and C with degree 0? Wait no:\n\nIf you remove A-B and C-D from A-B-C-D, you get two disconnected components: A and D (each degree 0), and B-C (which is an edge, so B and C have degree 1). Not all even.\n\nAlternatively remove B-C and A-B: then you have A isolated, B isolated, and C-D as an edge. Still not all even.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even in a path of n nodes is floor(n/2).\n\nWait, for n=3: floor(3/2)=1, but we saw that removing 1 edge leaves two nodes with degree 1 and one with 0. Not all even. So that doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, the minimal number is n/2 when n even, but even then it may not work.\n\nThis indicates that for a path graph, you cannot achieve all even degrees by removing a minimal number of edges unless you remove all edges. Therefore, in our original problem, the four side paths of non-corner edge cells (each of length 9) cannot have their degrees made even without removing all edges along them, which is not feasible.\n\nThis suggests that my initial approach is fundamentally flawed because the non-corner edge cells are arranged in paths where it's impossible to make all their degrees even without affecting other parts of the graph in a way that creates new odd-degree vertices.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the confusion arising from the structure, perhaps I should look for another strategy or recall that in a grid graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2, even if the graph isn't connected. Wait, but the grid is connected.\n\nIn our case, total edges: 220\n\nNumber of odd-degree vertices: 36\n\nTherefore, according to the formula, maximum closed trail length would be 220 - 36/2 = 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nEven though my detailed analysis suggested complications, maybe the formula still holds because in the modified graph where we remove 18 edges between non-corner edge cells (even if some paths have leftover nodes), the degrees of the non-corner edge cells become even, and the corners and interiors remain even. The issue I encountered with the 3x3 grid might be because in that case, the non-corner edge cells couldn't be paired due to their isolation, but in the 11x11 grid, the non-corner edge cells are in long paths where most can be paired except for a few which can be handled by also removing edges connected to the corners.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is a standard result in graph theory for the maximum length of a closed trail in any graph: it's equal to the total number of edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2. This is because in any graph, you can always find a closed trail that covers all edges except for a set of edges whose removal makes all degrees even, and the minimal number of edges to remove is indeed (number of odd-degree vertices)/2. Even if the pairing isn't straightforward, the existence is guaranteed by some theorem.\n\nTherefore, trusting the formula, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nHowever, in the 3x3 grid example, this formula would give 12 - 4/2 = 10 edges, but we saw that you actually needed to remove 4 edges to achieve an Eulerian circuit, resulting in 8 edges. So there's a discrepancy there. In the 3x3 grid, the formula overestimates the possible trail length.\n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid:\n\nTotal edges: 12\n\nNumber of odd-degree vertices: 4 (the four non-corner edge cells)\n\nFormula: 12 - 4/2 = 10\n\nBut in reality, we had to remove 4 edges to get to an Eulerian circuit with 8 edges. So the formula overestimates by 2.\n\nTherefore, the formula might not always hold when the graph isn't connected or has certain structures.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the formula is for the maximum trail in a connected graph, but the 3x3 grid is connected, yet the discrepancy remains.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is for the maximum trail that is a circuit (i.e., closed) and doesn't require the graph to become Eulerian, but just that the trail doesn't repeat edges. In that case, maybe the maximum trail can sometimes be longer than the Eulerian circuit of the modified graph?\n\nWait, no. If you can form an Eulerian circuit in the modified graph, that would be the longest possible closed trail, since it includ