ulerian circuit exists iff the graph is connected and all vertices have even degree. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by two, provided that such a removal can be done without disconnecting the graph. Since our graph is connected and has 36 odd-degree vertices, so 18 edges need to be removed. So, if we can remove 18 edges in such a way that the graph remains connected and all degrees become even, then the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit, and the length of that circuit is 220 - 18 = 202 edges. Therefore, that would be the maximum possible.\n\nTherefore, the key question is: can we remove 18 edges from the 11x11 grid graph such that all vertices have even degree and the graph remains connected?\n\nSince the grid graph is 4-regular except on the boundary, which has degree 3 or 2, so it's a highly connected graph. Removing 18 edges should be possible without disconnecting it. For instance, we can remove edges in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph. For example, we can remove edges in pairs along some paths or in such a way that we're not cutting through critical connections.\n\nAlternatively, here's a more concrete approach: Let's consider the grid's boundary. The odd-degree vertices are all along the edges (non-corner). Each such vertex has three edges. To make their degree even, we can remove one edge from each such vertex. However, each edge removal affects two vertices. Therefore, if we can pair up these odd-degree vertices and remove one edge from each pair, then we can fix their parities.\n\nBut how do we pair them? Since they are all along the edges, perhaps we can pair adjacent odd-degree vertices along the edges and remove an edge between them? Wait, but adjacent odd-degree vertices along an edge are connected by an edge. For example, take a vertex on the top edge (not a corner). It has a left neighbor, a right neighbor, and a downward neighbor. The left and right neighbors are also on the top edge, so they are also odd-degree vertices (unless they are corners). Wait, actually, the vertices along the top edge (non-corner) are each connected to their left and right neighbors on the top edge, as well as the one below. So, for instance, take two adjacent vertices on the top edge: each has degree 3. If we remove the edge between them, then each of those two vertices would lose one degree, going from 3 to 2 (even), which is good. But then, the vertex below the first one would still have its degree reduced? Wait no, only the two vertices connected by the edge have their degrees reduced. So, if we remove an edge between two adjacent top-edge vertices, those two vertices become degree 2 (even), which is good, but the vertex below the first one still has its degree unchanged. Wait, actually, the vertex below the first top-edge vertex is not affected by that edge removal.\n\nWait, let me take an example. Let's label the top row as row 1, columns 1 to 11. Let's take vertex (1,2), which is on the top edge, not a corner. Its neighbors are (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). Similarly, vertex (1,3) has neighbors (1,2), (1,4), and (2,3). If we remove the edge between (1,2) and (1,3), then vertex (1,2) now has degree 2 (neighbors (1,1) and (2,2)), and vertex (1,3) now has degree 2 (neighbors (1,4) and (2,3)). Both of these were odd-degree vertices (degree 3) and now they are even (degree 2). Perfect! So by removing the edge between (1,2) and (1,3), we fixed two odd-degree vertices. Similarly, we can do this along the entire top edge. But the top edge has 11 vertices, so 10 edges between them. Each edge between two top-edge vertices can be removed to fix two odd-degree vertices. Since there are 10 edges on the top edge, removing all 10 edges on the top edge would fix all 10 non-corner top-edge vertices? Wait, no, the top edge has 11 vertices, so 10 edges between them. Each edge connects two vertices, so removing all 10 edges on the top edge would remove edges between (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3), ..., (1,10)-(1,11). But the corners (1,1) and (1,11) each have degree 2 already (they are corners), so their degrees are even. The other 9 non-corner top-edge vertices each have their degree reduced by 1 (from 3 to 2) by the removal of the edge to their right (except the last one). Wait, actually, each non-corner top-edge vertex is connected to two other top-edge vertices and one downward. So, if we remove the horizontal edge to their right (for the ones not at the end), then each of those vertices would lose one edge, going from 3 to 2. However, the vertex at (1,11) is a corner, so its only horizontal edge is to the left, which is between (1,10)-(1,11). Removing that edge would affect (1,10) and (1,11). But (1,11) is a corner with degree 2, so removing its only horizontal edge would reduce its degree to 1, which is odd! That's bad. Similarly, the vertex (1,1) is a corner with degree 2; its only horizontal edge is to the right, between (1,1)-(1,2). If we remove that edge, then (1,1) would have degree 1, which is odd. So, we cannot remove all the horizontal edges on the top edge because the two corners would end up with odd degree.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can remove all the horizontal edges on the top edge except the ones adjacent to the corners? Let's see: on the top edge, we have 11 vertices. The two corners (1,1) and (1,11) have degree 2. The other 9 vertices on the top edge have degree 3. To fix the 9 non-corner top-edge vertices, each needs to lose one degree. Each of these can be done by removing the horizontal edge to their right (for the ones not at the end). But if we remove the edge between (1,2)-(1,3), that affects (1,2) and (1,3). Similarly, remove (1,3)-(1,4), etc., up until (1,10)-(1,11). But as before, the last one would affect (1,10) and (1,11). Since (1,11) is a corner with degree 2, we can't reduce its degree. Therefore, instead of removing the edge (1,10)-(1,11), we can instead remove a vertical edge from (1,11). Wait, but (1,11) has edges: left to (1,10), down to (2,11), and that's it because it's a corner. So its degree is 2. To reduce its degree, we would have to remove one of its edges. If we remove the edge down to (2,11), then (1,11) would have degree 1, which is odd, which is bad. Alternatively, remove the edge to the left, which would make (1,11) have degree 1, which is also bad. Therefore, we cannot remove any edge incident to the corner (1,11) because it would make its degree odd. Therefore, we can only remove edges among the non-corner top-edge vertices. There are 9 non-corner top-edge vertices, each needing to lose one degree. Each horizontal edge between two non-corner top-edge vertices can fix two of them. Since there are 8 horizontal edges between the 9 non-corner top-edge vertices (from (1,2)-(1,3) up to (1,9)-(1,10)), so 8 edges. Each of these 8 edges, when removed, would fix two vertices. So, removing those 8 edges would fix 16 degree reductions, but we only need to fix 9 vertices. Wait, no, each edge removal fixes two vertices, so 8 edges would fix 16 vertex degrees, but we only have 9 vertices to fix. Wait, actually, each of the 9 non-corner top-edge vertices has degree 3, needs to become 2, so each needs one degree reduction. Therefore, total degree reductions needed: 9. Each edge removal can provide two degree reductions (one for each endpoint). So, to get 9 degree reductions, we need at least ceiling(9/2) = 5 edge removals? Wait, no. Wait, actually, the total number of degree reductions required is 9 (since each of the 9 vertices needs to lose 1 degree). Each edge removal provides two degree reductions (one for each vertex it connects). Therefore, the minimal number of edge removals required to achieve the 9 degree reductions is ceil(9/2) = 5? Wait, no, actually, if you have to cover 9 vertices each needing one reduction, and each edge can cover two vertices, then the minimal number of edges needed is the ceiling of 9/2, which is 5. But 5 edges can cover 10 degree reductions, which is one more than needed. Alternatively, maybe 5 edges can do it if overlapping appropriately.\n\nWait, but in the top edge, the non-corner vertices are in a line: (1,2) to (1,10). Each is connected horizontally to their immediate neighbors. To reduce each of these 9 vertices by 1 degree, we can remove the horizontal edges between them. However, each horizontal edge between two adjacent non-corner vertices will reduce both of their degrees by 1. So, if we remove the edge between (1,2)-(1,3), that reduces (1,2) and (1,3) each by 1. Similarly, removing (1,3)-(1,4) reduces (1,3) and (1,4), etc. If we remove edges (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), (1,10)-(1,11). Wait, but (1,10)-(1,11) is adjacent to the corner (1,11), which we can't touch. Wait, actually, if we remove (1,10)-(1,11), then (1,10) is okay (it was non-corner, degree 3, becomes 2), but (1,11) becomes degree 1, which is bad. Therefore, instead of removing that last edge, we can remove a vertical edge from (1,10) or (1,11). But (1,11) is a corner with only two edges: left and down. If we remove the vertical edge down from (1,11) to (2,11), then (1,11) would have degree 1 (left edge only), which is odd. Not good. If we remove the horizontal edge to the left from (1,11), but that's the same as removing (1,10)-(1,11), which we can't do. So perhaps instead of removing horizontal edges on the top edge, we can remove vertical edges from some of the top-edge vertices.\n\nAlternatively, for the last vertex (1,10), instead of removing its horizontal edge to (1,11), which is problematic, we can remove its vertical edge down to (2,10). Then, (1,10) would lose one degree (from 3 to 2), which is good, and (2,10) would lose one degree (from 4 to 3, since (2,10) is an interior vertex? Wait, (2,10) is on the second row, which is not the top or bottom edge, so it's an interior vertex? Wait, rows 2 through 10 are interior rows? Wait, the grid is 11x11, so rows 1 to 11. The top row is row 1, the bottom row is row 11. So, row 2 is adjacent to row 1, so it's on the second row. The vertex (2,10) is in row 2, column 10. Since column 10 is not on the rightmost edge (which is column 11), so column 10 is an interior column? Wait, columns 1 to 11: columns 1 and 11 are the left and right edges, so columns 2 through 10 are interior columns. Therefore, (2,10) is in an interior column, so its degree is 4 (up, down, left, right). If we remove the vertical edge from (1,10) down to (2,10), then (1,10) goes from degree 3 to 2 (good), and (2,10) goes from degree 4 to 3 (which is odd). So that would create a new odd-degree vertex at (2,10). Hmm, so that's not helpful because now we have another odd-degree vertex. So that's worse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to do some combination of horizontal and vertical edge removals on the top edge to fix the degrees without creating new odd-degree vertices elsewhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to not focus on just the top edge but consider the entire perimeter.\n\nThe entire perimeter of the grid consists of the outermost edges. The perimeter has 4 sides, each with 11 vertices, but the four corners are counted twice. So total perimeter vertices: 4*11 - 4 = 40 vertices? Wait, actually, each side has 11 vertices, but the four corners are shared by two sides. So total perimeter vertices: 4*(11) - 4 = 40. Wait, 4 sides each with 11 vertices, but subtract 4 duplicates for the corners. So 40 perimeter vertices. Of these, the 4 corners have degree 2, and the remaining 36 perimeter vertices (40 - 4) are the edge non-corner vertices, each with degree 3. So those 36 are the odd-degree vertices we need to fix.\n\nSo, all 36 odd-degree vertices are on the perimeter. So perhaps we can perform edge removals along the perimeter in such a way that we can fix all 36 odd-degree vertices without affecting the interior too much? Since the interior vertices all have degree 4 (even), so we don't need to touch them.\n\nIf we can remove edges only along the perimeter, then we can fix the degrees of the perimeter vertices without affecting the interior. Let me see.\n\nEach perimeter vertex (non-corner) has three edges: two along the perimeter and one leading inward. For example, a vertex on the top edge (non-corner) has left, right (both along the top edge), and down (inward). Similarly, a vertex on the right edge (non-corner) has up, down (along the right edge), and left (inward). The key is that each non-corner perimeter vertex has one inward edge and two perimeter edges.\n\nTo fix their degree from 3 to 2, we need to remove one edge from each. Since each such vertex has three edges, removing any one of them will reduce its degree by 1. However, if we remove a perimeter edge, that affects two adjacent perimeter vertices. If we remove an inward edge, that only affects one perimeter vertex (the one it's connected to) but also affects the interior vertex it's connected to, which might be okay as long as that interior vertex's degree remains even.\n\nWait, but the interior vertices all have degree 4, so if we remove an inward edge from a perimeter vertex, say from a top-edge non-corner vertex going down to the interior, then that interior vertex loses one degree, going from 4 to 3, which is odd. So that creates a new odd-degree vertex in the interior, which we didn't have before. That's bad because now we have more odd-degree vertices, so we have to fix those as well, which complicates things.\n\nTherefore, perhaps it's better to only remove perimeter edges, so that we don't create new odd-degree vertices in the interior. Since each perimeter edge is shared by two perimeter vertices, removing a perimeter edge will reduce the degree of two perimeter vertices by 1 each, which is exactly what we want: each of those two perimeter vertices needed to lose one degree to become even. Therefore, if we can pair up the perimeter vertices (the 36 non-corner ones) and remove the edge between them, that would fix both of their degrees.\n\nHowever, the perimeter vertices are arranged in a cycle: the top edge, right edge, bottom edge, left edge, connected at the corners. The non-corner perimeter vertices are each between two other perimeter vertices. For example, on the top edge, between (1,2) and (1,3) is an edge, and so on. So, the entire perimeter is a cycle of 40 vertices (including corners) but the 36 non-corner perimeter vertices form a cycle of 36? Wait no, the entire perimeter is a cycle of 40 vertices (including the 4 corners). The 36 non-corner perimeter vertices are part of this cycle, each separated by one edge. Wait, actually, the perimeter is a cycle graph of 40 nodes (the 4 corners plus 36 edge non-corner vertices). Each of the 36 non-corner perimeter vertices is part of this cycle and has two neighbors along the perimeter and one inward edge.\n\nTherefore, if we consider just the perimeter cycle, it's a cycle of 40 nodes. Each node in this cycle has degree 2 within the perimeter cycle, but in the entire graph, each has an additional inward edge, making their total degree 3 (for non-corner nodes) or 2 (for corners). Wait, actually, the corners are part of the perimeter cycle and have only two edges in the perimeter cycle, and no inward edges, so their degree remains 2.\n\nTherefore, if we consider the perimeter as a separate cycle graph, the non-corner perimeter vertices each have degree 3 in the whole graph, because they have two perimeter edges and one inward edge. So, to make all the non-corner perimeter vertices have even degree, we can either remove one of their perimeter edges or their inward edge. But as we saw earlier, removing an inward edge would affect an interior vertex, making it odd, which is bad. Therefore, the only safe way is to remove perimeter edges.\n\nEach perimeter edge is between two perimeter vertices (which are either both non-corner or one is a corner). However, the corners have degree 2, so we cannot remove an edge incident to a corner without reducing its degree below 2, which would make it odd. Therefore, we must only remove perimeter edges that are between two non-corner perimeter vertices.\n\nTherefore, along each side of the perimeter (top, right, bottom, left), between the non-corner vertices, there are edges that can be safely removed without affecting the corners. Let's analyze one side, say the top edge.\n\nOn the top edge, between the non-corner vertices (positions 2 through 10 on the top row), there are 9 non-corner vertices, so 8 edges between them. Each of these edges connects two non-corner perimeter vertices. If we remove all 8 edges on the top edge between the non-corner vertices, then each of those 9 vertices loses one perimeter edge, so their total degree becomes 3 - 1 = 2 (since they still have their inward edge). Wait, no: each non-corner perimeter vertex on the top edge has two perimeter edges (left and right) and one inward edge. If we remove one perimeter edge (say, the right edge for all except the last one), then each such vertex would lose one perimeter edge, so their degree becomes 2 (perimeter edges remaining) + 1 (inward edge) = 3? Wait, no, hold on.\n\nWait, let me take a specific example. Take a non-corner top-edge vertex, say (1,2). Its edges are: left to (1,1) [a corner], right to (1,3), and down to (2,2). If we remove the right edge to (1,3), then (1,2) now has edges left to (1,1) and down to (2,2), so degree 2. That's good. The vertex (1,3) now has its left edge removed (from (1,2)), so it loses one perimeter edge. Its edges are left to (1,2) [but that edge is removed], right to (1,4), and down to (2,3). So now (1,3) has degree 2 (right and down). So both (1,2) and (1,3) are fixed. Similarly, if we remove the edge between (1,3) and (1,4), then (1,3) and (1,4) each lose a perimeter edge, becoming degree 2. However, the vertex (1,4) still has its left edge to (1,3) [which is now removed], so it only has right and down edges. Wait, no: if we remove (1,3)-(1,4), then (1,4) loses the left edge to (1,3), so it now has right and down edges, degree 2. So that works.\n\nContinuing this way, if we remove all 8 edges on the top edge between the non-corner vertices, then each of the 9 non-corner top-edge vertices will have their degree reduced by 1 (from 3 to 2), and the two end vertices adjacent to the corners will also be okay? Let's see:\n\nTake the vertex (1,2): after removing the edge to (1,3), it's connected to (1,1) and (2,2). Its degree is 2.\n\nThe vertex (1,10): its right neighbor is (1,11), which is a corner. If we remove the edge between (1,9)-(1,10), then (1,10) is connected to (1,9) [which we just removed the edge from? Wait, no: if we are removing edges between non-corner vertices, then on the top edge, the edges to be removed are between (1,2)-(1,3), (1,3)-(1,4), ..., up to (1,9)-(1,10). The edge between (1,10) and (1,11) is not removed because (1,11) is a corner. Therefore, the vertex (1,10) is connected to (1,9) [still there?] Wait, no: if we remove the edge between (1,9)-(1,10), then (1,10) loses that edge, so it's only connected to (1,11) [the corner] and down to (2,10). Therefore, its degree is 2 (corner and down), which is good. The corner (1,11) still has its left edge to (1,10), so its degree remains 2. So that's okay.\n\nTherefore, on the top edge, by removing the 8 interior edges (between the non-corner vertices), we fix all 9 non-corner top-edge vertices' degrees to 2. Similarly, we can do the same for the right, bottom, and left edges.\n\nLet's check the right edge. The right edge consists of column 11, rows 1 to 11. The non-corner vertices on the right edge are rows 2 through 10 (since row 1 and 11 are corners). So there are 9 non-corner vertices on the right edge. Between these, there are 8 edges: between (2,11)-(3,11), (3,11)-(4,11), ..., (10,11)-(11,11). Wait, actually, rows 1 to 11 on the right edge: the non-corner vertices are rows 2 through 10 (9 vertices). The edges between them are from row 2-3 up to row 10-11? Wait, no. Wait, the right edge vertices are (1,11), (2,11), ..., (11,11). The non-corner ones are rows 2 through 10 (9 vertices). The edges between them are between (2,11)-(3,11), (3,11)-(4,11), ..., (10,11)-(11,11). Wait, but (10,11)-(11,11) is between a non-corner and a corner. So actually, there are 9 non-corner vertices on the right edge, but the edges between them are 8 edges that are between two non-corner vertices (from row 2-3 up to row 9-10), and one edge between (10,11) and the corner (11,11). So, similar to the top edge, on the right edge, we can remove the 8 edges between the non-corner vertices (rows 2-3 up to 9-10), which would fix the degrees of the 9 non-corner right-edge vertices. Each of those vertices loses one perimeter edge (their right/left along the edge), so their degree goes from 3 to 2, and the corner (11,11) remains at degree 2.\n\nSimilarly, on the bottom edge (row 11), the non-corner vertices are columns 2 through 10 (9 vertices). The edges between them can be removed similarly, and on the left edge (column 1), the non-corner vertices are rows 2 through 10 (9 vertices), and we can remove the edges between them.\n\nTherefore, for each of the four sides (top, right, bottom, left), we can remove 8 edges between the non-corner perimeter vertices, fixing the 9 non-corner vertices on that side. Each side has 9 non-corner vertices, and removing 8 edges per side fixes all 9 vertices on that side (since each edge removal fixes two vertices, but along a linear chain, removing each internal edge fixes the two vertices it connects, and the end vertices are fixed by the removal of the edge adjacent to the corner). Wait, actually, on each side, the 9 non-corner vertices form a linear chain with 8 edges between them, plus one edge connecting to the corner. So, removing the 8 internal edges between the non-corner vertices will leave each non-corner vertex with only one perimeter edge (either left or right, except the ends which are connected to the corner). Wait, let me see:\n\nTake the top edge again. After removing the 8 edges between the non-corner vertices:\n\n- The first non-corner vertex (1,2) is connected to the corner (1,1) on its left and has its right edge to (1,3) removed, so only connected to the corner and down. Degree 2.\n\n- The next vertex (1,3) was connected to (1,2) [still there?] Wait no: we removed the edge between (1,2) and (1,3). So (1,3) is now only connected to (1,4) [right], and down. Wait, no:\n\nWait, if we remove the edge between (1,2)-(1,3), then (1,3) loses that edge. Its remaining perimeter edges are to the right (to (1,4)) and to the left (to (1,2)), but the left edge is removed, so only the right edge remains. Wait, no: (1,3)'s left edge is to (1,2), but we removed the edge between (1,2) and (1,3), so (1,3) no longer has that edge. Therefore, (1,3) is only connected to (1,4) on the right and down. So degree 2.\n\nSimilarly, (1,4) had edges to (1,3) [removed], (1,5), and down. After removal, it's connected to (1,5) and down: degree 2.\n\nThis continues all the way to (1,10), which is connected to (1,11) [the corner] and down. Degree 2.\n\nSo, yes, by removing the 8 internal edges on each side, all 9 non-corner vertices on that side have their degrees reduced from 3 to 2, which is even. The corners remain at degree 2. The inward edges from the non-corner perimeter vertices are still present, so those inward edges are untouched. The interior vertices: each interior vertex (not on the perimeter) has degree 4. However, some of their edges may be connected to perimeter vertices whose inward edges have been removed? Wait no: the inward edges from the perimeter vertices are still there. Wait, no: we only removed perimeter edges, not inward edges.\n\nWait, the inward edges from the perimeter vertices are separate from the perimeter edges. The perimeter edges are the ones along the outside of the grid, while the inward edges go from the perimeter vertices into the interior. So, for example, the vertex (1,2) on the top edge has an inward edge going down to (2,2). We did not remove that edge; we only removed the horizontal edge to the right. Therefore, the inward edges remain intact. Therefore, the interior vertices connected by those inward edges still have their degrees intact.\n\nSpecifically, take an interior vertex like (2,2). It is connected to (1,2) [up], (3,2) [down], (2,1) [left], and (2,3) [right]. The edge to (1,2) is still there because we only removed perimeter edges on the top edge, not the downward edge from (1,2). Therefore, the degree of (2,2) remains 4. Similarly, all interior vertices remain at degree 4, which is even. The only vertices affected are the perimeter non-corner ones, which are now at degree 2, and the corners remain at 2. Therefore, after performing this operation on all four sides:\n\n- On each side (top, right, bottom, left), we removed 8 edges.\n\n- Total edges removed: 4 sides * 8 edges = 32 edges.\n\nWait a minute, hold on. But we only needed to remove 18 edges to fix the 36 odd-degree vertices. But here, by removing 32 edges, we've fixed all 36 odd-degree vertices (since each side had 9 vertices, 4 sides * 9 = 36). However, we removed 32 edges. But earlier, we thought we only needed to remove 18 edges. So there's a discrepancy here.\n\nAh! Because each edge removal on the perimeter affects two vertices, so to fix 36 vertices, you need 18 edge removals. But in my above approach, I was removing 8 edges per side, totaling 32, which is double the needed amount. Why is that?\n\nAh, because on each side, the 9 non-corner vertices each need to lose one degree, so 9 degree reductions per side. Since each edge removal on the perimeter can fix two vertices (the two endpoints of the edge), then per side, to fix 9 vertices, you need ceiling(9/2) = 5 edge removals per side? Wait, but 5 edge removals can fix up to 10 vertices. Since 9 is odd, you need 5 edge removals per side to fix 9 vertices (since 5 edges can fix 10 vertices, but we only need 9). Therefore, perhaps we can do it more efficiently.\n\nWait, actually, on a linear chain of n vertices, to make all degrees even, how many edges do you need to remove? Let's consider a path graph with n vertices (a straight line). Each internal vertex has degree 2, the two ends have degree 1. To make all degrees even, you need to remove edges so that all vertices have even degree. In a path graph, which is a straight line of vertices connected by edges, the degrees are 1 at the ends and 2 elsewhere. To make all degrees even, you can remove edges in pairs? Or perhaps remove edges from the middle?\n\nWait, in a path graph with n vertices (n-1 edges), the two end vertices have degree 1 (odd), and the internal vertices have degree 2 (even). To make all degrees even, you need to reduce the two end vertices' degrees to 0? But that would disconnect the graph. Alternatively, since we can only remove edges, not add them, and we need to make all degrees even, we can only do so if we can pair up the odd-degree vertices. In a path graph, there are two odd-degree vertices (the ends). To fix them, you can remove an edge adjacent to one end, but that would make that end have degree 0 and the next vertex have degree 1, creating a new odd vertex. Alternatively, remove the edge between the two middle vertices? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, in a path graph, the only way to make all degrees even is to remove all edges incident to the two end vertices? But that would leave the path as a single vertex in the middle, which has degree 0, and the rest disconnected. Not helpful.\n\nHmm, perhaps in a path graph, it's impossible to make all degrees even by removing edges without disconnecting the graph? Because you have two odd-degree vertices, and each edge removal can fix two vertices but might create new ones. For instance, if you remove an internal edge, you split the path into two paths, each with their own ends, which are now odd-degree vertices. So you end up with more odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, in a path graph, you cannot make all degrees even by removing edges without disconnecting the graph. Therefore, perhaps in our case, when dealing with the perimeter, which is a cycle, it's different.\n\nThe perimeter is a cycle of 40 vertices (including corners). Each non-corner perimeter vertex has degree 3 (two perimeter edges and one inward edge), and the corners have degree 2. To make all non-corner perimeter vertices have even degree, we can consider the perimeter cycle.\n\nIn a cycle graph, which is a closed path, all vertices have degree 2. If we add chords (the inward edges), then the non-corner perimeter vertices now have degree 3, and the corners remain at 2. To make all non-corner vertices have even degree, we can remove some perimeter edges (chords) such that each non-corner vertex loses one degree (from 3 to 2). Since the perimeter is a cycle, we can break the cycle into a path by removing one edge, but that would create two paths with odd degrees at the ends. Hmm, but we need to remove edges in such a way that every non-corner vertex loses exactly one perimeter edge.\n\nAlternatively, since the perimeter is a cycle of 40 vertices, and we need to remove edges so that each of the 36 non-corner vertices loses exactly one perimeter edge (thereby reducing their degree from 3 to 2), while the corners remain at degree 2.\n\nEach edge removed on the perimeter will affect two vertices. Since we have 36 vertices to reduce by 1 each, we need 18 edge removals (since each edge removal reduces two vertices' degrees by 1). Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove on the perimeter to fix the degrees is 18.\n\nBut the perimeter is a cycle of 40 vertices. To remove 18 edges from the cycle such that each non-corner vertex is incident to exactly one removed edge? Wait, no, because each removed edge affects two vertices. To have each of the 36 non-corner vertices have exactly one removed edge incident to them, we need the set of removed edges to form a matching that covers all 36 non-corner vertices, with each vertex in exactly one edge of the matching. However, 36 vertices would require 18 edges in a matching. But in a cycle of 40 vertices, can we find a matching of 18 edges that covers all 36 non-corner vertices?\n\nWait, the non-corner perimeter vertices are 36 in number, arranged in the cycle. The corners are 4 vertices. So, imagine the cycle as:\n\nCorners: C1, C2, C3, C4 (each connected to two non-corner vertices)\n\nNon-corner vertices: N1, N2, ..., N36 arranged in the cycle between the corners.\n\nTo cover all 36 non-corner vertices with a matching (i.e., a set of edges with no shared vertices), we need 18 edges. Since the non-corner vertices are in a cycle of 36 vertices (since the total cycle is 40, subtract 4 corners), but actually the non-corner vertices are interleaved with the corners. Wait, no, the entire perimeter is a single cycle of 40 vertices, with 4 corners and 36 non-corner vertices.\n\nTo cover all 36 non-corner vertices with a matching, we need to select 18 edges such that no two edges share a vertex, and every non-corner vertex is in exactly one edge. Since the non-corner vertices are 36, and each edge in the matching covers two non-corner vertices, 18 edges would cover all 36. However, the edges must not include any edges that connect a non-corner vertex to a corner, because the corners have degree 2 and we can't reduce their degree.\n\nWait, the edges we can remove are only the ones between non-corner vertices, because edges connecting a non-corner to a corner would, if removed, reduce the corner's degree below 2, which is not allowed. Therefore, the matching must be entirely within the non-corner vertices, i.e., a matching on the 36 non-corner vertices arranged in the cycle, excluding the corners.\n\nBut the non-corner vertices are arranged in the cycle with the corners between them? No, the non-corner vertices are between the corners. For example, starting at corner C1, then a non-corner vertex N1, then another non-corner N2, ..., up to N9, then corner C2, then N10, N11, ..., etc.\n\nWait, actually, in the perimeter cycle of 40 vertices, the 36 non-corner vertices are placed between the 4 corners. Each corner is between two non-corner vertices. Therefore, the non-corner vertices form a sequence interrupted by the corners. So the entire perimeter cycle can be thought of as:\n\nC1 - N1 - N2 - ... - N9 - C2 - N10 - N11 - ... - N18 - C3 - N19 - ... - N36 - C4 - N37? Wait, no, actually, since there are 36 non-corner vertices, and 4 corners, each corner is between two non-corner vertices, so the total number of non-corner vertices is 4*2 + (4 - 4)? Wait, no. Let's think step by step.\n\nImagine the perimeter as a cycle with 40 vertices: 4 corners and 36 non-corners. Each corner is between two non-corner vertices. Therefore, the 36 non-corner vertices are arranged in the cycle with the 4 corners separating them into 4 groups of 9 non-corner vertices between each pair of corners. Wait, no: between each pair of adjacent corners, there are 9 non-corner vertices? Let's see:\n\nTotal non-corner vertices: 36. There are 4 corners, so between each pair of corners, there are 36 / 4 = 9 non-corner vertices. Yes, exactly. So between corner C1 and C2, there are 9 non-corner vertices; between C2 and C3, another 9; between C3 and C4, another 9; and between C4 and C1, another 9. So each \"side\" of the square (between two corners) has 9 non-corner vertices. That matches our earlier analysis.\n\nTherefore, the perimeter cycle can be divided into four segments, each between two corners, each segment consisting of 9 non-corner vertices connected in a path. Each of these segments is a path of 9 vertices (non-corner) connected by edges, with the two ends of the path connected to the corners.\n\nTo form a matching that covers all 36 non-corner vertices, we need to select edges within these segments such that every non-corner vertex is in exactly one edge of the matching. Since each segment is a path of 9 vertices, which is an odd number, we cannot perfectly match all vertices in a single path with a matching (since a path with an odd number of vertices has one vertex left unmatched). Therefore, we have to handle each segment individually.\n\nWait a second, each segment is a path of 9 non-corner vertices (so 9 vertices, connected by 8 edges). To cover all 9 vertices with a matching, we need to select edges such that every vertex is in exactly one edge. But since 9 is odd, this is impossible because a matching can cover at most floor(9/2)*2 = 8 vertices, leaving one vertex uncovered. Therefore, we cannot cover all 9 vertices in a single path of 9 vertices with a matching. Therefore, we need to leave one vertex uncovered in each segment, but we can't because we need to cover all 36.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. Therefore, perhaps it's impossible to cover all 36 non-corner vertices with a matching on the perimeter edges without leaving some vertices uncovered. Therefore, my initial idea won't work. That means we cannot fix all 36 odd-degree vertices by only removing perimeter edges without creating some vertices with odd degrees elsewhere?\n\nWait, but maybe instead of trying to cover all 36 with a perfect matching, we can have some edges that are matched and some that are not, but then those unmatched vertices would still need to have their degrees adjusted by another method. But since we can't remove inward edges (as that would affect interior vertices), we are constrained to only remove perimeter edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can have a near-perfect matching, leaving two vertices uncovered per segment? Wait, but each segment has 9 vertices, so if we leave one vertex per segment uncovered, that would total 4 uncovered vertices, which is still a problem because those would remain with odd degree.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we have to accept that we cannot fix all 36 odd-degree vertices by only removing perimeter edges, and we have to remove some inward edges as well, even though that introduces new odd-degree vertices in the interior. Then, we would have to fix those as well, leading to a recursive problem.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Perhaps another approach is needed.\n\nLet me recall that in the original problem, we don't necessarily have to make the entire graph Eulerian; we just need a closed trail that doesn't repeat edges. The maximum length of such a trail would be equal to the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges that need to be removed so that the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit. However, as we saw, trying to do this by only removing perimeter edges causes us to run into the problem of not being able to cover all the odd-degree vertices without leaving some uncovered or introducing new odd-degree vertices in the interior.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is indeed 18, but we have to remove some perimeter edges and some inward edges in a way that balances things out.\n\nSuppose we remove 18 edges in total: some perimeter edges and some inward edges. Each perimeter edge removal fixes two perimeter vertices, and each inward edge removal fixes one perimeter vertex but introduces an odd-degree vertex in the interior. So, if we remove x perimeter edges and y inward edges, then:\n\n- The total degree reductions on perimeter vertices: 2x + y (since each inward edge removal reduces one perimeter vertex by 1, and each perimeter edge removal reduces two perimeter vertices by 1).\n\n- We need 2x + y = 36 (to fix all 36 perimeter odd-degree vertices).\n\n- Additionally, each inward edge removal also creates an odd-degree vertex in the interior, so we now have y new odd-degree vertices in the interior.\n\n- To fix those y interior odd-degree vertices, we need to remove additional edges, each of which can fix two interior vertices (by removing an edge between two interior vertices, for example), but this comp