 2 (connected to v3 and interior vertex) and v9 still connected to its neighbors. Wait, actually, the entire path from v2 to v9 is still a path graph with vertices v2 through v9, which is 8 vertices. Since 8 is even, this subpath can now be perfectly matched. For example, we can match v2-v3, v4-v5, v6-v7, and v8-v9. Each of these edges is within the path, and removing those edges would fix the degrees of those vertices.\n\nWait, but hold on, actually, in the subpath v2 to v9 (which is now a path of 8 vertices: v2, v3, v4, v5, v6, v7, v8, v9), each of these vertices has degree:\n\n- v2: connected to v3 and interior vertex (degree 2)\n\n- v3: connected to v2, v4, and interior vertex (degree 3)\n\nWait, no, hold on. Wait, actually, in the original grid graph, each vertex in the middle of the path (not an endpoint) has two edge non-corner neighbors and one interior neighbor. So, for example, v3 is connected to v2, v4, and an interior vertex. After removing the edge (v1, v2), v2 is now connected only to v3 and its interior vertex, so degree 2. But v3 is still connected to v2, v4, and its interior vertex, so degree 3. Similarly, v4 is connected to v3, v5, and interior vertex (degree 3), etc., up to v8, which is connected to v7, v9, and its interior vertex (degree 3). v9 is connected to v8 and its interior vertex (since it was originally connected to v8 and the corner; but wait, v9 is the endpoint adjacent to the corner, so originally connected to v8, the corner, and its interior vertex? Wait no:\n\nWait, let's clarify the connections for the endpoint v9 (the last non-corner on the top edge):\n\nv9 is adjacent to:\n\n- v8 (previous vertex along the top edge),\n\n- the top-right corner,\n\n- and an interior vertex below it.\n\nTherefore, its degree is 3.\n\nAfter removing the edge (v1, v2), we haven't touched v9 yet. So, in the subpath from v2 to v9:\n\n- v2 is connected to v3 and interior vertex (degree 2).\n\n- v3 is connected to v2, v4, and interior vertex (degree 3).\n\