t not v7. Hmm. So in this case, we still have v7 as odd.\n\nAlternatively, if we remove (v7,v8) instead of (v8,v9):\n\n- Removing (v7,v8): v7 and v8 become degree 2 (even). But then v9 remains connected to v8 (which is now degree 2) and the corner and interior vertex, so v9's degree is still 3 (odd). So v9 remains odd.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to remove an additional edge involving v9. For example, remove the edge between v9 and its interior vertex. But that would make v9's degree 2 (connected to v8 and corner), which is even, but the interior vertex loses a degree, becoming odd. Since the interior vertex was originally degree 4, now it's 3, which is odd. So we've created a new odd-degree vertex. Not good.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between v9 and the corner. But the corner is a degree 2 vertex; removing an edge from v9 to the corner would make the corner have degree 1 (odd) and v9 have degree 2 (even). But now the corner is odd, which we didn't want. So that's worse.\n\nHmm, so it seems that in this approach, we can only fix all but one vertex in each path? Or perhaps we need a different strategy.\n\nWait, let's try a different tactic for the entire path. Suppose in the path of 9 vertices (v1 to v9), we remove the edges (v1,v2), (v3,v4), (v5,v6), (v7,v8), and (v9, corner). Wait, but the corner isn't part of the edge non-corner vertices, so we can't remove an edge to the corner because that would involve the corner vertex, which is not an edge non-corner vertex. Alternatively, remove the edge between v9 and its interior vertex. But that affects the interior vertex.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's impossible to fix all vertices in a single path of 9 with edge removals within the path. Since 9 is odd, perhaps we can only fix 8 vertices, leaving one vertex with odd degree, and to fix that last one, we have to do something outside the path, but we can't because it's only connected within the path and to an interior vertex. And we can't involve the interior vertex because that would create a new odd vertex there.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in each path of 9 edge non-corner vertices, we can only fix 8 vertices with 4 edge removals, leaving one vertex with odd degree. Since there are four such paths, that would leave us with 4 vertices still odd. To fix those 4, we might need to do something, but we can't without affecting other vertices.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can accept that we can only fix 8 vertices per path, resulting in 4 leftover odd vertices, and then we have to remove additional edges that involve those 4 vertices and some other vertices in a way that doesn't create new odd vertices. But since those 4 leftover vertices are each only connected to one other edge non-corner vertex (their neighbor in the path) and an interior vertex, the only edges we can remove are either the edge to their neighbor or the edge to the interior vertex.\n\nIf we remove the edge between the leftover vertex and its neighbor, then both become even, but the neighbor was previously part of a matched pair? Wait, let's think with an example.\n\nSuppose in the top path, after trying to match as much as possible, we have one leftover vertex, say v1. To fix v1, we can remove the edge between v1 and v2. This makes v1 even and v2 even. However, v2 was previously matched with someone? If we didn't match v2, then removing the edge to v1 might free us to do something else.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the initial matching should be designed so that the leftover vertex is adjacent to someone who can be adjusted. This is getting too vague; perhaps we need a better mathematical approach.\n\nLet me recall that in any graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2, provided that the graph is connected. Wait, no, actually, in a connected graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, but only if the graph is connected. Wait, actually, according to the Handshaking Lemma, the number of odd-degree vertices must be even, which it is (36 here). And in a connected graph, you can always find a set of edges whose removal will reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by 2 for each edge removed, until all are even. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is indeed 18. But the question is whether such a set of edges exists in this graph.\n\nThe problem is that the graph may not be connected in terms of the odd-degree vertices. Wait, the entire graph is connected, but the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices (the 36 edge non-corner vertices) is disconnected into four separate paths. Each of these paths is a connected component in the induced subgraph. Since each path has an odd number of vertices, each component has an odd number of odd-degree vertices. To fix all odd degrees in a connected component, you need to have an even number of odd-degree vertices, but here each component has an odd number (9). Therefore, within each connected component (each path), it's impossible to fix all the odd degrees without leaving one vertex odd. Therefore, across the entire graph, since we have four components each with an odd number of odd-degree vertices, the total number of odd-degree vertices is 4×9=36, which is even, so in total it's possible to fix all, but because the components are separate, you can't fix them independently. You have to have some edges that go between components, but there are none.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that it's impossible to fix all 36 odd-degree vertices by only removing edges within the edge non-corner subgraph, because each connected component (path) has an odd number of odd-degree vertices, and you can't fix an odd number of odd-degree vertices in a connected component without leaving one behind. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is actually more than 18. Specifically, for each connected component with an odd number of odd-degree vertices, you have to leave one vertex odd, so total leftover odd vertices would be 4, and thus you need to remove edges in such a way that you fix 32 vertices and leave 4, but then you have to do something about those 4. Since you can't fix them without affecting other vertices, perhaps you have to remove additional edges involving those 4 vertices and some interior vertices, but that would create new odd-degree vertices elsewhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can connect the components by using interior vertices? For example, if we remove an edge from an edge non-corner vertex to an interior vertex, that would decrease the edge non-corner vertex's degree by 1 (making it even) and decrease the interior vertex's degree by 1 (making it odd). If we do this for four edge non-corner vertices (the four leftovers), each connected to an interior vertex, then we can fix the four edge non-corner vertices but create four new odd-degree interior vertices. However, those four new odd-degree vertices are now in the interior, which originally were all even. Now we have four new odd-degree vertices. But we can then try to fix those by removing edges between them? But interior vertices are connected to four neighbors (except edge ones), so maybe we can find a way to pair them up. But this is getting too convoluted and might not lead to a net gain.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is 18 + 4 = 22? Because we can fix 32 with 16 edges, and then fix the remaining 4 by removing 4 more edges that each fix two vertices but involve interior vertices, but this is not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is 220 - 18 = 202, and the problem assumes that you can ignore the parity issues and just go with the theoretical minimum, but that seems unlikely because the question is from a competition and probably expects an exact answer.\n\nWait, maybe I made a wrong assumption. Maybe the path doesn't have to be a single cycle but can visit edges multiple times as long as it doesn't traverse any door more than once. Wait no, the problem says \"he cannot go through any door more than once (not even in opposite directions)\". So it's a trail, a path without repeated edges, which is a closed trail, i.e., an Eulerian circuit in the multigraph where each door is an edge, but since doors are not repeated, it's just a simple cycle that doesn't repeat edges. So, it's a closed trail that is also a circuit, i.e., an Eulerian circuit in the graph after some edges are removed. Therefore, the maximum number of edges in such a trail is equal to the number of edges in the largest possible Eulerian circuit in the graph, which requires the graph to be Eulerian (all degrees even), so we have to remove the minimal number of edges to make it Eulerian, then the circuit will have E - f edges, where f is the number of edges removed.\n\nGiven that in our case, we can't make the graph Eulerian by removing only 18 edges due to the disconnectedness of the odd-degree components, perhaps we have to remove more edges.\n\nIn general, when you have a graph with multiple connected components each having an odd number of odd-degree vertices, you can't fix all the odd degrees without removing edges across components, but since there are no edges between components, you can't. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is actually equal to the total number of odd-degree vertices minus the number of connected components? Wait, not sure.\n\nWait, according to some references, in a graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2 if the graph is connected. But if the graph is disconnected, then for each connected component, if it has k odd-degree vertices, you need to remove at least k/2 edges within that component to make all degrees even in that component. However, if k is odd, you can't, so you have to leave one odd degree vertex in that component. Therefore, the total minimal number of edges to remove is the sum over all connected components of ceil(k_i / 2), where k_i is the number of odd-degree vertices in component i. Wait, but actually, since each edge removal can fix two odd degrees, in a connected component with k odd-degree vertices, you need at least ceil(k/2) edges to remove? Wait, no.\n\nWait, in a connected component with k odd-degree vertices, you can fix all but one by removing floor(k/2) edges? No, actually, in a connected component with k odd-degree vertices, you can fix all but one by removing (k - 1)/2 edges if k is odd. Because each edge removal fixes two vertices, so to fix k vertices, you need k/2 edges, but if k is odd, you can't, so you have to leave one.\n\nTherefore, for each connected component with k_i odd-degree vertices, you must remove at least (k_i - 1)/2 edges if k_i is odd, which would leave 1 odd-degree vertex in that component. But since we want all components to have even degrees, we have to instead remove enough edges so that each component has an even number of odd-degree vertices. Since each component initially has k_i odd-degree vertices, we can choose to leave an even number, so if k_i is even, leave 0; if k_i is odd, leave 2, 4, etc. But the minimal number of edges to remove would be to leave the minimal even number of odd vertices, i.e., leave 0 if possible, else 2, etc. Wait, actually, the minimal number of edges to remove in a connected component with k_i odd-degree vertices is ceil(k_i / 2) if we want to leave all but ceil(k_i / 2) vertices? I'm getting confused.\n\nWait, let's think of it this way: In any connected component, the number of odd-degree vertices must be even, because the sum of degrees is even. Therefore, if a connected component has an odd number of odd-degree vertices, that's impossible. Wait a second! Wait, hold on, the Handshaking Lemma says that the sum of degrees in any graph is even, so the number of odd-degree vertices must be even. Therefore, in any connected component, the number of odd-degree vertices must be even. Therefore, my earlier analysis was flawed.\n\nWait, this is critical. In any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree is even. Therefore, each connected component must also have an even number of odd-degree vertices. Therefore, my mistake earlier was in considering the four paths each having 9 (odd) edge non-corner vertices. But 9 is odd, so that can't be. Therefore, my initial count must be wrong.\n\nWait, let's recast this. Let me recalculate the number of odd-degree vertices in each connected component.\n\nEarlier, I thought that the edge non-corner vertices form four separate paths (top, right, bottom, left), each of length 9 (i.e., 9 vertices). However, each of those paths actually has 9 vertices, which is odd. But according to the Handshaking Lemma, each connected component must have an even number of odd-degree vertices. Therefore, this is a contradiction, meaning my earlier analysis of the connected components is wrong.\n\nTherefore, I must have made a mistake in determining the structure of the graph.\n\nLet me re-examine the degrees and connectivity.\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex has degree 3 (odd). There are 36 of them, which is even, so that's okay.\n\nNow, considering the connected components of the subgraph induced by the edge non-corner vertices:\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex is connected only to other edge non-corner vertices along its edge and to interior vertices. However, in the induced subgraph (only considering edges between edge non-corner vertices), each edge non-corner vertex is part of a path along its edge. For example, on the top edge, the non-corner vertices form a path graph of 9 vertices. Since 9 is odd, this path has an odd number of vertices, each of degree 3 in the original graph, but in the induced subgraph, their degrees are 1 or 2. However, in terms of the original graph's degrees, which are all 3 for edge non-corner vertices, the Handshaking Lemma still holds for the entire graph, but for the induced subgraph, it's a different story.\n\nWait, perhaps the error is that I considered the induced subgraph's degrees, but the Handshaking Lemma applies to the entire graph, not the induced subgraph. The induced subgraph can have any degree sequence as long as the original graph's degrees are maintained appropriately. However, the key point is that in the entire graph, the number of odd-degree vertices is even (36), so when we look at the connected components of the entire graph, each connected component must also have an even number of odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, my earlier mistake was thinking that the four paths (top, right, bottom, left) are separate connected components in the induced subgraph of edge non-corner vertices. But in reality, the entire graph is connected, so the induced subgraph of edge non-corner vertices is disconnected into four components, but the entire graph is connected through the interior vertices.\n\nHowever, when considering the connected components of the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices (the edge non-corner vertices), each of those four paths are separate components because there are no edges between them in that subgraph. However, in the entire graph, the edge non-corner vertices are connected through the interior vertices, but in the induced subgraph (only edge non-corner vertices), they are disconnected.\n\nBut when considering the entire graph's connectedness, the four paths are connected through the interior vertices. For example, a vertex on the top edge is connected via an interior vertex to a vertex on the right edge, etc. Therefore, the entire graph is connected, but the subgraph of just the edge non-corner vertices is disconnected.\n\nHowever, when we are trying to find the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even, we can consider the entire graph, not just the induced subgraph.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that I was trying to restrict edge removals only to edges between edge non-corner vertices, but actually, we can remove any edges in the entire graph to fix the degrees.\n\nAh! This is the key point I was missing. Earlier, I was only considering removing edges between edge non-corner vertices, but actually, to fix the odd degrees, we can also remove edges that connect an edge non-corner vertex to an interior vertex. Because when you remove an edge between an edge non-corner vertex and an interior vertex, you decrease the degree of the edge non-corner vertex by 1 (making it even) and decrease the degree of the interior vertex by 1 (making it odd). However, since the interior vertex was originally even, this would create a new odd-degree vertex there. But if we then also remove an edge incident to that new odd-degree interior vertex, we might fix it, but this could propagate.\n\nHowever, if we do this strategically, perhaps we can fix multiple odd-degree vertices at once without creating new ones. For example, if we have two edge non-corner vertices connected to the same interior vertex, removing both edges to that interior vertex would decrease each edge non-corner vertex's degree by 1 (making them even) and decrease the interior vertex's degree by 2 (keeping it even). That way, we can fix two edge non-corner vertices and not create any new odd-degree vertices.\n\nAh! That's a crucial observation. If an interior vertex is connected to multiple edge non-corner vertices, then we can remove edges from that interior vertex to multiple edge non-corner vertices, thereby fixing the edge non-corner vertices' parities without affecting the interior vertex's parity (since we remove an even number of edges from it).\n\nSpecifically, suppose an interior vertex has degree 4 (even). If we remove two edges from it to two different edge non-corner vertices, then the interior vertex's degree becomes 2 (even), and each of those two edge non-corner vertices have their degrees reduced by 1, turning them from odd to even. Perfect! So, this allows us to fix two edge non-corner vertices' parities by removing two edges incident to a common interior vertex.\n\nSimilarly, if an interior vertex is connected to an even number of edge non-corner vertices, we can pair them up and remove edges in pairs to fix those edge non-corner vertices without affecting the interior vertex's parity.\n\nTherefore, the strategy could be:\n\n1. Identify interior vertices that are connected to an even number of edge non-corner vertices.\n\n2. For each such interior vertex, pair up its incident edge non-corner vertices and remove the connecting edges. Each such pair removal fixes two edge non-corner vertices.\n\n3. For interior vertices connected to an odd number of edge non-corner vertices, we might need to handle them differently, perhaps by leaving one edge unremoved or combining with another vertex.\n\nHowever, let's analyze the connections between interior vertices and edge non-corner vertices.\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex is connected to exactly one interior vertex. Because, as we saw earlier, each edge non-corner vertex has three edges: two along the edge (to other edge non-corner vertices) and one inward to an interior vertex. Therefore, each edge non-corner vertex has exactly one edge connecting it to an interior vertex.\n\nTherefore, each interior vertex is connected to some number of edge non-corner vertices. Let's figure out how many edge non-corner vertices are connected to each interior vertex.\n\nConsider an interior vertex not on the edge. It has four neighbors: up, down, left, right. Depending on its position, some of these neighbors might be edge non-corner vertices or other interior vertices.\n\nFor example, consider an interior vertex that is one row in from the top edge. Its top neighbor is an edge non-corner vertex (on the top edge), its bottom neighbor is an interior vertex, its left and right neighbors are interior vertices (unless it's on the side, but we're considering interior vertices not on the edge). Wait, actually, let's be precise.\n\nLet me consider the grid as rows and columns from 1 to 11.\n\nAn interior vertex is any vertex not on the border, so rows 2 through 10 and columns 2 through 10. Wait, actually, in an 11x11 grid, the border vertices are those in row 1, row 11, column 1, or column 11. Therefore, interior vertices are those with 2 ≤ row ≤ 10 and 2 ≤ column ≤ 10. There are 9x9 = 81 interior vertices, which matches our earlier count.\n\nEach interior vertex has four neighbors: up, down, left, right.\n\nNow, for an interior vertex not on the border, its neighbors could be:\n\n- If it's in the second row (row 2), its top neighbor is a top edge non-corner vertex (since row 1 is the top edge).\n\n- Similarly, if it's in the second column (column 2), its left neighbor is a left edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- Similarly, if it's in the tenth row (row 10), its bottom neighbor is a bottom edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- If it's in the tenth column (column 10), its right neighbor is a right edge non-corner vertex.\n\nOther neighbors are interior vertices.\n\nTherefore, each interior vertex can be adjacent to 0, 1, 2, or possibly up to 4 edge non-corner vertices, but likely only up to two, depending on its position.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- A corner-adjacent interior vertex (e.g., in row 2, column 2) has two edge non-corner neighbors: the top neighbor (row 1, column 2) and the left neighbor (row 2, column 1). Wait, actually:\n\nWait, let's take an example interior vertex at position (2,2):\n\n- Up: (1,2), which is a top edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- Down: (3,2), interior.\n\n- Left: (2,1), which is a left edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- Right: (2,3), interior.\n\nTherefore, this interior vertex (2,2) is connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (1,2) and (2,1).\n\nSimilarly, an interior vertex at (2,3):\n\n- Up: (1,3), top edge non-corner.\n\n- Down: (3,3), interior.\n\n- Left: (2,2), interior.\n\n- Right: (2,4), interior.\n\nSo this one is only connected to one edge non-corner vertex: (1,3).\n\nAn interior vertex at (3,2):\n\n- Up: (2,2), interior.\n\n- Down: (4,2), interior.\n\n- Left: (3,1), left edge non-corner.\n\n- Right: (3,3), interior.\n\nSo connected to one edge non-corner vertex: (3,1).\n\nAn interior vertex at (10,10):\n\n- Up: (9,10), interior.\n\n- Down: (11,10), which is a bottom edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- Left: (10,9), interior.\n\n- Right: (10,11), which is a right edge non-corner vertex.\n\nTherefore, connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nAn interior vertex at (10,2):\n\n- Up: (9,2), interior.\n\n- Down: (11,2), bottom edge non-corner.\n\n- Left: (10,1), left edge non-corner.\n\n- Right: (10,3), interior.\n\nSo connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (11,2) and (10,1).\n\nAn interior vertex at (5,5):\n\n- All neighbors are interior vertices.\n\nSo connected to zero edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, the number of edge non-corner vertices connected to a given interior vertex can be 0, 1, or 2. Specifically:\n\n- Interior vertices on the \"corners\" of the interior, i.e., those adjacent to the original grid's edges, can have 1 or 2 connections to edge non-corner vertices.\n\n- Other interior vertices have 0.\n\nNow, let's count how many interior vertices have 0, 1, or 2 edge non-corner neighbors.\n\nFirst, let's count the number of interior vertices adjacent to the top edge:\n\nThese are the interior vertices in row 2 (since row 1 is the top edge). In row 2, columns 2 through 10 (since column 1 is the left edge, but column 1 in row 2 is actually an edge non-corner vertex). Wait, no:\n\nWait, row 2, column 1 is actually a left edge non-corner vertex, right? Because column 1, row 2 is on the left edge but not a corner. Similarly, row 2, column 11 is a right edge non-corner vertex.\n\nTherefore, in row 2:\n\n- Columns 1 and 11 are edge non-corner vertices.\n\n- Columns 2 through 10 are interior vertices in row 2, each of which has a top neighbor in the top