is is a critical mistake I made earlier.\n\nWait, actually, the edge non-corner vertices are those on the borders but not at the corners. Therefore:\n\n- On the top edge (row 1), the non-corner vertices are columns 2 through 10.\n\n- On the bottom edge (row 11), the non-corner vertices are columns 2 through 10.\n\n- On the left edge (column 1), the non-corner vertices are rows 2 through 10.\n\n- On the right edge (column 11), the non-corner vertices are rows 2 through 10.\n\nTherefore, the interior vertices are those not on any edge, i.e., rows 2 through 10 and columns 2 through 10. So the interior vertices are a 9x9 grid from (2,2) to (10,10).\n\nTherefore, my earlier mistake was considering that column 1, row 2 is an edge non-corner vertex, which it is, but when considering an interior vertex, they are strictly inside.\n\nTherefore, correcting that:\n\nAn interior vertex at (2,2):\n\n- Up: (1,2), which is a top edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- Down: (3,2), interior.\n\n- Left: (2,1), which is a left edge non-corner vertex.\n\n- Right: (2,3), interior.\n\nTherefore, connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (1,2) and (2,1).\n\nSimilarly, an interior vertex at (2,3):\n\n- Up: (1,3) (top edge non-corner),\n\n- Down: (3,3),\n\n- Left: (2,2) (interior),\n\n- Right: (2,4) (interior).\n\nSo only connected to one edge non-corner vertex: (1,3).\n\nSimilarly, an interior vertex at (3,2):\n\n- Up: (2,2) (interior),\n\n- Down: (4,2) (interior),\n\n- Left: (3,1) (left edge non-corner),\n\n- Right: (3,3) (interior).\n\nSo connected to one edge non-corner vertex: (3,1).\n\nAn interior vertex at (10,10):\n\n- Up: (9,10) (interior),\n\n- Down: (11,10) (bottom edge non-corner),\n\n- Left: (10,9) (interior),\n\n- Right: (10,11) (right edge non-corner).\n\nSo connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (11,10) and (10,11).\n\nAn interior vertex at (10,2):\n\n- Up: (9,2) (interior),\n\n- Down: (11,2) (bottom edge non-corner),\n\n- Left: (10,1) (left edge non-corner),\n\n- Right: (10,3) (interior).\n\nSo connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (11,2) and (10,1).\n\nAn interior vertex at (5,5):\n\n- All neighbors are interior.\n\nTherefore, in summary:\n\nFor each of the four \"border\" interior rows and columns:\n\n- The top row of interior vertices (row 2, columns 2-10):\n\n  - Columns 2 and 10: connected to two edge non-corner vertices each.\n\n  - Columns 3-9: connected to one edge non-corner vertex each.\n\n- The bottom row of interior vertices (row 10, columns 2-10):\n\n  - Columns 2 and 10: connected to two edge non-corner vertices each.\n\n  - Columns 3-9: connected to one edge non-corner vertex each.\n\n- The left column of interior vertices (column 2, rows 2-10):\n\n  - Rows 2 and 10: connected to two edge non-corner vertices each (top/bottom and left).\n\n  - Rows 3-9: connected to one edge non-corner vertex each (left).\n\n- The right column of interior vertices (column 10, rows 2-10):\n\n  - Rows 2 and 10: connected to two edge non-corner vertices each (top/bottom and right).\n\n  - Rows 3-9: connected to one edge non-corner vertex each (right).\n\nAdditionally, the four corner-adjacent interior vertices (row 2, column 2; row 2, column 10; row 10, column 2; row 10, column 10) are each connected to two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nNow, let's calculate the total number of edges between interior vertices and edge non-corner vertices.\n\nFirst, let's handle the top row interior vertices (row 2, columns 2-10):\n\n- Columns 2 and 10: each has two edge non-corner connections. So that's 2 vertices × 2 edges = 4 edges.\n\n- Columns 3-9: 7 vertices, each with 1 edge: 7 edges.\n\nTotal for top row: 4 + 7 = 11 edges.\n\nSimilarly, bottom row interior vertices (row 10, columns 2-10):\n\n- Columns 2 and 10: 2 vertices × 2 edges = 4 edges.\n\n- Columns 3-9: 7 vertices × 1 edge = 7 edges.\n\nTotal for bottom row: 11 edges.\n\nLeft column interior vertices (column 2, rows 2-10):\n\n- Rows 2 and 10: each has two edges (top/bottom and left). So 2 vertices × 2 edges = 4 edges.\n\n- Rows 3-9: 7 vertices × 1 edge = 7 edges.\n\nTotal for left column: 11 edges.\n\nRight column interior vertices (column 10, rows 2-10):\n\n- Rows 2 and 10: 2 vertices × 2 edges = 4 edges.\n\n- Rows 3-9: 7 vertices × 1 edge = 7 edges.\n\nTotal for right column: 11 edges.\n\nAdditionally, the four corner-adjacent interior vertices (row 2, col 2; row 2, col 10; row 10, col 2; row 10, col 10) are already counted in the above totals. For example, row 2, col 2 is part of the top row and left column calculations, so no double-counting.\n\nTherefore, total edges from interior vertices to edge non-corner vertices:\n\nTop: 11\n\nBottom: 11\n\nLeft: 11\n\nRight: 11\n\nTotal: 11 × 4 = 44 edges.\n\nAdditionally, we must check if there are any other edges connecting interior vertices to edge non-corner vertices. For example, the interior vertices on the very edges? Wait, no, we considered all the interior vertices adjacent to the grid's edges.\n\nTherefore, total of 44 edges connect interior vertices to edge non-corner vertices.\n\nEach of these edges, if removed, would decrease the degree of an edge non-corner vertex by 1 (turning it from 3 to 2, even) and decrease the degree of the interior vertex by 1 (from 4 to 3, odd). However, if we remove two such edges that are connected to the same interior vertex, then that interior vertex's degree decreases by 2 (to 2, even), and the two edge non-corner vertices each decrease by 1 (to 2, even). Therefore, by removing two edges incident to the same interior vertex, we can fix two edge non-corner vertices without creating a new odd-degree vertex.\n\nTherefore, the strategy is:\n\n1. Identify interior vertices that are connected to an even number of edge non-corner vertices. For each such interior vertex, we can pair up its incident edge non-corner vertices and remove the connecting edges in pairs. This will fix those edge non-corner vertices without affecting the interior vertex's parity.\n\n2. For interior vertices connected to an odd number of edge non-corner vertices (which are the ones connected to exactly one edge non-corner vertex), we can remove one edge to fix that single edge non-corner vertex, but this will create a new odd-degree vertex at the interior vertex. To fix that new odd-degree vertex, we might need to pair it with another, but since it's only connected to one edge non-corner vertex, this might not be possible. Alternatively, we can leave it as is, but then we have an extra odd-degree vertex.\n\nHowever, let's analyze the counts:\n\nFirst, let's count how many interior vertices have 0, 1, or 2 edge non-corner neighbors.\n\nFrom the above breakdown:\n\n- Each of the four \"corner-adjacent\" interior vertices (e.g., row 2, col 2) have 2 edge non-corner neighbors.\n\n- The other edge-adjacent interior vertices (on the borders but not the corners) have 1 edge non-corner neighbor.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- In the top row interior (row 2, columns 2-10):\n\n  - Columns 2 and 10: 2 edge non-corner neighbors each.\n\n  - Columns 3-9: 1 edge non-corner neighbor each. (7 vertices)\n\n- Similarly, bottom row interior (row 10, columns 2-10):\n\n  - Columns 2 and 10: 2 edge non-corner neighbors each.\n\n  - Columns 3-9: 1 edge non-corner neighbor each. (7 vertices)\n\n- Left column interior (column 2, rows 2-10):\n\n  - Rows 2 and 10: 2 edge non-corner neighbors each.\n\n  - Rows 3-9: 1 edge non-corner neighbor each. (7 vertices)\n\n- Right column interior (column 10, rows 2-10):\n\n  - Rows 2 and 10: 2 edge non-corner neighbors each.\n\n  - Rows 3-9: 1 edge non-corner neighbor each. (7 vertices)\n\nNow, let's count the number of interior vertices with 2, 1, or 0 edge non-corner neighbors:\n\n- Corners: The four corner-adjacent interior vertices (row 2,col2; row2,col10; row10,col2; row10,col10) each have 2 edge non-corner neighbors. So 4 vertices × 2 = 8 edges, but we are counting vertices here, so 4 vertices with 2 edges.\n\n- The remaining edge-adjacent interior vertices (not corners):\n\n  - Top row: columns 3-9: 7 vertices, each with 1 edge.\n\n  - Bottom row: columns 3-9: 7 vertices, each with 1 edge.\n\n  - Left column: rows 3-9: 7 vertices, each with 1 edge.\n\n  - Right column: rows 3-9: 7 vertices, each with 1 edge.\n\nTotal non-corner edge-adjacent interior vertices: 7×4 = 28 vertices, each with 1 edge.\n\n- The remaining interior vertices (those not on the border rows or columns) have 0 edge non-corner neighbors. These are the interior vertices in rows 3-9 and columns 3-9. There are (9-2) rows × (9-2) columns = 7×7 = 49 vertices, each with 0 edges to edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, summary:\n\n- 4 interior vertices with 2 edge non-corner neighbors.\n\n- 28 interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor.\n\n- 49 interior vertices with 0 edge non-corner neighbors.\n\nNow, to fix the edge non-corner vertices:\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex is connected to exactly one interior vertex. So there are 36 edge non-corner vertices, each connected to one interior vertex.\n\nThe total number of edges from edge non-corner vertices to interior vertices is 36 (since each edge non-corner has one such edge). However, according to our previous count using the interior vertices' perspectives, there are:\n\n- 4 interior vertices with 2 edges: contributing 4×2 = 8 edges.\n\n- 28 interior vertices with 1 edge: contributing 28×1 = 28 edges.\n\n- 49 interior vertices with 0 edges: contributing 0.\n\nTotal edges: 8 + 28 = 36, which matches. Good.\n\nNow, the plan is:\n\nFor each interior vertex with an even number of edge non-corner neighbors, we can pair up their incident edge non-corner vertices and remove the edges in pairs. For those with an odd number (1), we can remove one edge to fix that single edge non-corner vertex but this will create an odd-degree interior vertex, which we must then fix by pairing it with another.\n\nHowever, since the interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor are 28 in number, each connected to one edge non-corner vertex, we can handle them as follows:\n\nFor each interior vertex with 1 edge non-corner neighbor:\n\n- Remove the single edge connecting it to the edge non-corner vertex. This fixes the edge non-corner vertex (its degree becomes 2) but the interior vertex now has degree 3 (odd).\n\nTo fix the interior vertex's new odd degree, we need to remove another edge from it. But it only has that one edge left (since originally it had degree 4, now it's 3). Wait, no: the interior vertex had degree 4. After removing one edge, its degree is 3. To make it even, we need to remove one more edge, but it only has three edges left. Removing a second edge would make its degree 2, even, but which edge can we remove? It must be connected to another interior vertex.\n\nHowever, the interior vertex with 1 edge non-corner neighbor is connected to three interior vertices (since its original degree was 4: one edge non-corner and three interior). Therefore, after removing the edge to the edge non-corner vertex, it has three interior edges remaining. We can remove one of those, but then we'd have to fix that as well, creating a chain reaction.\n\nThis seems problematic. Alternatively, perhaps we should instead for each interior vertex with 1 edge non-corner neighbor:\n\n- Leave it as is, but then the edge non-corner vertex will remain odd, and the interior vertex will become odd. This is worse.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to not remove edges connected to these interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor, and instead accept that we can't fix them without affecting other vertices.\n\nAlternatively, realize that the total number of edge non-corner vertices is 36, each connected to one interior vertex. The interior vertices can absorb the \"extra\" oddness.\n\nSpecifically, the total number of edge non-corner vertices is 36, which is even. Each time we remove an edge from an interior vertex to an edge non-corner vertex, we fix one edge non-corner vertex but create an odd-degree interior vertex. Since we start with 36 edge non-corner vertices (all odd), and we want to turn all into even, we need to perform 18 such edge removals (each fixing one edge non-corner vertex but creating one odd interior vertex). However, each such removal also creates an odd interior vertex, so we would have 18 new odd-degree interior vertices. Since interior vertices were originally all even (degree 4), now we have 18 odd-degree interior vertices. To fix those, we need to perform another 9 edge removals (since 18/2 = 9), each removing an edge between two interior vertices, thus fixing two interior vertices' parities. However, this requires that the interior vertices are connected in such a way that we can pair them up.\n\nBut interior vertices are connected to each other in a grid, so likely this is possible. Let's formalize:\n\nStep 1: For each edge non-corner vertex, we can choose to remove its connecting edge to the interior vertex or not. To minimize the number of edge removals, we should pair up edge non-corner vertices connected to the same interior vertex and remove their edges in pairs where possible.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- For interior vertices with 2 edge non-corner neighbors: we can remove both edges in one go, fixing both edge non-corner vertices without affecting the interior vertex's parity (since it loses 2 edges, degree remains even). This requires 1 edge removal per such interior vertex (since removing two edges counts as two removals, but they are both from the same interior vertex). Wait, no: removing two edges from the same interior vertex requires two separate edge removals, but each removal affects that interior vertex's degree by 1. Wait, no, to remove two edges from an interior vertex connected to two edge non-corner vertices, you have to remove both edges, which are two separate edges. Each removal affects the interior vertex's degree by 1, so after two removals, its degree decreases by 2, maintaining evenness. The two edge non-corner vertices each lose one edge, going from 3 to 2 (even). Therefore, this requires two edge removals but fixes two edge non-corner vertices without creating new odd vertices.\n\n- For interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor: we have no choice but to remove that single edge, which fixes the edge non-corner vertex but creates an odd interior vertex. Each such removal requires one edge removal and creates one new odd interior vertex.\n\n- For interior vertices with 0 edge non-corner neighbors: we can ignore them.\n\nNow, let's compute how many edge non-corner vertices can be fixed without creating new odd interior vertices:\n\n- For each interior vertex with 2 edge non-corner neighbors: we can fix both edge non-corner vertices connected to it with 2 edge removals, without creating new odd vertices. There are 4 such interior vertices, each contributing 2 edge non-corner vertices fixed. Total fixed: 4×2 = 8 edge non-corner vertices. Edge removals used: 4×2 = 8.\n\n- For each interior vertex with 1 edge non-corner neighbor: we have to remove that edge, fixing 1 edge non-corner vertex but creating 1 new odd interior vertex. There are 28 such interior vertices, each contributing 1 edge non-corner vertex fixed. Total fixed: 28 edge non-corner vertices. Edge removals used: 28.\n\n- For interior vertices with 0: none.\n\nTotal edge non-corner vertices fixed: 8 + 28 = 36. Perfect! All of them.\n\nHowever, the cost is 8 + 28 = 36 edge removals, but these create 28 new odd-degree interior vertices (one per each of the 28 interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor). Since each of those 28 interior vertices had degree 4, now they have degree 3 (odd). So now we have 28 new odd-degree interior vertices.\n\nTo fix these 28 odd-degree interior vertices, we need to perform additional edge removals. Each edge removal between two interior vertices will fix two odd-degree vertices. Since 28 is even, this is possible. The number of edge removals needed is 28 / 2 = 14.\n\nTherefore, total edge removals:\n\n- First phase: 36 (to fix all edge non-corner vertices)\n\n- Second phase: 14 (to fix the new odd interior vertices)\n\nTotal: 36 + 14 = 50 edge removals.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is 50, so the maximal number of edges in the Eulerian circuit is total edges 220 minus 50, which is 170.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me verify this carefully.\n\nFirst Phase:\n\n- For the 4 interior vertices with 2 edge non-corner neighbors: each requires 2 edge removals to fix their two connected edge non-corner vertices. That's 4×2 = 8 edge removals, fixing 8 edge non-corner vertices.\n\n- For the 28 interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor: each requires 1 edge removal to fix their connected edge non-corner vertex, resulting in 28 edge removals and fixing 28 edge non-corner vertices. However, this creates 28 new odd-degree interior vertices.\n\nTotal after first phase:\n\n- All 36 edge non-corner vertices are even.\n\n- The 28 interior vertices that were connected to one edge non-corner vertex now have degree 3 (odd).\n\n- The other interior vertices (49 with 0, and 4 with 2) remain even.\n\nSecond Phase:\n\nWe have 28 odd-degree interior vertices. To fix them, we need to pair them up and remove edges between them. Each such removal will fix two odd-degree vertices.\n\nSince the interior vertices form a grid (7x7 grid from rows 3-9 and columns 3-9?), actually the interior vertices are a 9x9 grid? Wait, no:\n\nWait, the interior vertices are rows 2-10 and columns 2-10, but excluding the edge non-corner vertices. Wait no, actually, the interior vertices are strictly inside: rows 2-10 and columns 2-10, but excluding the ones on the borders? No, correction:\n\nWait, earlier we defined interior vertices as those not on the border, so rows 2-10 and columns 2-10, making a 9x9 grid (since from row 2 to 10 inclusive is 9 rows, same for columns). Within this 9x9 grid of interior vertices, some have connections to edge non-corner vertices, but the ones we're considering in the second phase are the 28 interior vertices that have one edge non-corner neighbor and are now odd.\n\nWait, actually, the 28 interior vertices with one edge non-corner neighbor are distributed along the borders of the interior 9x9 grid. Specifically:\n\n- The top row of the interior grid (row 2 in the original grid) has 8 interior vertices (columns 2-10 excluding the corners?), no:\n\nWait, in the interior vertices (rows 2-10, columns 2-10):\n\n- The \"top\" row of this interior grid is row 2 of the original grid, which has columns 2-10, but excluding the corners (columns 2 and 10 are part of the \"corner-adjacent\" category). Wait, no, in the interior vertices' own grid:\n\nActually, within the interior vertices (rows 2-10, columns 2-10), they form a 9x9 grid where each position (i,j) corresponds to original row i, column j. The edges between interior vertices are the standard grid edges.\n\nThe 28 interior vertices with one edge non-corner neighbor are those on the periphery of this 9x9 grid but not at the very corners of the original grid.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- The top row of the interior grid (row 2 original) has columns 2-10. Of these:\n\n  - Columns 2 and 10 are the \"corner-adjacent\" vertices with two edge non-corner neighbors.\n\n  - Columns 3-9 have one edge non-corner neighbor.\n\nSo in the interior grid's top row, excluding the two corners, there are 7 vertices with one edge non-corner neighbor.\n\nSimilarly, the bottom row (row 10 original) of the interior grid has 7 such vertices.\n\nThe left column (column 2 original) of the interior grid has rows 3-9 (since row 2 and 10 are already counted in the top and bottom rows), which is 7 vertices, each with one edge non-corner neighbor (left edge).\n\nThe right column (column 10 original) of the interior grid has rows 3-9, 7 vertices, each with one edge non-corner neighbor (right edge).\n\nAdditionally, the four \"corner-adjacent\" vertices (row 2,col2; row2,col10; row10,col2; row10,col10) are already accounted for in the first phase.\n\nTherefore, the 28 vertices with one edge non-corner neighbor are arranged on the perimeter of the interior 9x9 grid, excluding the four corners of the original grid's interior. This forms a sort of border around the interior grid.\n\nThese 28 vertices form a cycle? Or can they be paired up in the interior grid?\n\nThe key point is that these 28 interior vertices, which are now odd-degree, are interconnected through the interior grid's edges. Since they form a connected component (the interior grid is fully connected), we can find a set of edges whose removal will pair up all 28 odd-degree vertices.\n\nSpecifically, in any connected graph with an even number of odd-degree vertices, you can find a set of edges to remove to make all degrees even. Since the interior grid is connected and has 28 odd-degree vertices, we can perform such a correction with 14 edge removals (since 28/2 = 14).\n\nTherefore, the total number of edge removals needed is indeed 36 (first phase) + 14 (second phase) = 50.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is the total number of doors (edges) minus the number of edges removed: 220 - 50 = 170.\n\nBut wait, let me verify this with a smaller example to see if the logic holds.\n\nSuppose we have a 3x3 grid. Then it's a smaller version. Let's see if the same logic applies.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid:\n\n- Total cells: 9.\n\n- Edge non-corner vertices: each side has 1 non-corner vertex (since 3 cells per side, minus 2 corners). So 4 sides × 1 = 4 edge non-corner vertices, each with degree 3 (odd).\n\n- Interior vertices: 1 center cell, degree 4 (even).\n\nTotal edges: horizontal: 3 rows × 2 edges = 6; vertical: 3 columns × 2 edges = 6; total 12 edges.\n\nWe need to make all degrees even.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: 4, all odd.\n\nInterior vertex: even.\n\nSo total odd-degree vertices: 4, even number.\n\nUsing the same approach:\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex is connected to one interior vertex.\n\nThe interior vertex (center) is connected to all four edge non-corner vertices.\n\nSo in the interior vertex (degree 4), connected to four edge non-corner vertices.\n\nFirst phase:\n\n- The interior vertex has 4 edge non-corner neighbors. Since 4 is even, we can remove two pairs of edges. For example, remove two edges from the center to two edge non-corner vertices. This would fix two edge non-corner vertices (their degrees go from 3 to 2) but the center's degree goes from 4 to 2 (even). However, we can do this in two steps:\n\nWait, to fix all four edge non-corner vertices:\n\nSince the center is connected to all four, we can remove two pairs of edges. For instance, remove edges (center, A) and (center, B), then (center, C) and (center, D). Each removal of two edges from the center fixes two edge non-corner vertices without affecting the center's parity (since it loses 2 edges, stays even). Thus, four edge non-corner vertices fixed with four edge removals, and the center remains even.\n\nAlternatively, remove all four edges: but that would leave the center with degree 0, which is even, but then the edge non-corner vertices all have degree 2 (even). However, removing four edges might be more than necessary, but in this case, it's exactly needed.\n\nWait, actually, to fix all four edge non-corner vertices:\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex is connected only to the center and two edge non-corner neighbors along the edge.\n\nWait, in a 3x3 grid, each edge non-corner vertex is on a side, connected to two other edge non-corner vertices along the side and the center.\n\nFor example, the top edge non-corner vertex (top middle) is connected to the top-left corner, top-right corner, and the center.\n\nWait, no:\n\nIn a 3x3 grid:\n\nLet's label the cells from (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nThe edge non-corner vertices are:\n\n- Top edge non-corner: (1,2)\n\n- Bottom edge non-corner: (3,2)\n\n- Left edge non-corner: (2,1)\n\n- Right edge non-corner: (2,3)\n\nEach of these has degree 3:\n\n- (1,2) is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2).\n\n- Similarly for others.\n\nThe center (2,2) is connected to all four edge non-corner vertices and has degree 4.\n\nSo each edge non-corner vertex is connected to two edge non-corner neighbors along the edge and the center.\n\nTherefore, to fix the degrees:\n\nIf we remove the edges from the center to all four edge non-corner vertices, then:\n\n- Each edge non-corner vertex loses one connection (to the center), so their degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- The center loses four connections, degree becomes 0 (even).\n\nThus, this requires removing four edges, and results in all vertices having even degrees. Therefore, the maximum Eulerian circuit would have 12 - 4 = 8 edges.\n\nAlternatively, could we do it with fewer edge removals?\n\nSuppose we only remove two edges from the center:\n\n- Remove (center, top) and (center, bottom). Then:\n\n  - Top and bottom edge non-corner vertices have degree 2 (even).\n\n  - Center has degree 2 (even).\n\n  - Left and right edge non-corner vertices still have degree 3 (odd).\n\nThen we need to fix left and right:\n\nRemove edges from the center to left and right:\n\n- Now remove those two edges, total four.\n\nAlternatively, instead of removing center edges, remove edges along the perimeter.\n\nFor example, remove the edge between (1,1) and (1,2), and between (1,3) and (1,2), but those are edge non-corner vertices' connections to corners, which are degree 2 vertices. Removing those edges would affect the corner vertices (degree 2 to 1), making them odd, which is bad.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edge removals is indeed four, leading to 8 edges in the Eulerian circuit.\n\nUsing our previous formula:\n\nTotal edge non-corner vertices: 4 (all odd).\n\nEach is connected to the center (one interior vertex).\n\nThe center is connected to all four edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, in the first phase:\n\nThe center has four edge non-corner neighbors (even number). So we can pair them up and remove two edges from the center to two edge non-corner vertices, fixing those two, but the center's degree becomes 2 (even). Then we have two remaining edge non-corner vertices still odd.\n\nThen we need to remove two more edges from the center to the remaining two, totaling four edge removals. So same as above.\n\nAlternatively, according to the formula:\n\nNumber of edge non-corner vertices: 4.\n\nEach connected to the center (one interior vertex).\n\nThe center has degree 4 (even), connected to four edge non-corner vertices.\n\nFirst phase: since the center has even number of edge non-corner neighbors (4), we can fix all four by removing two pairs of edges (four edge removals), resulting in all edge non-corner vertices even and center even.\n\nThus, total edge removals: 4, leading to 12 - 4 = 8 edges in the circuit. Which matches.\n\nUsing our general formula:\n\nTotal edge non-corner vertices: 4.\n\nInterior vertices with connections to edge non-corner vertices: 1 (the center) with 4 connections (even). So first phase:\n\nFor the center (interior vertex with 4 connections), we can pair them all up, requiring 4/2 = 2 pairs, so 4 edge removals. Then no new odd vertices created. Thus total edge removals: 4.\n\nWhich matches.\n\nTherefore, the formula seems to hold for the 3x3 grid.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original 11x11 grid:\n\nTotal edge removals needed: 50, so maximum doors traversed: 220 - 50 = 170.\n\nBut wait, let me check the count again for the 11x11 grid.\n\nFirst phase:\n\n- 4 interior vertices with 2 edge non-corner neighbors: each requires 2 edge removals to fix their two connected edge non-corner vertices. Total for these: 4 vertices × 2 removals = 8 removals, fixing 8 edge non-corner vertices.\n\n- 28 interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor: each requires 1 removal to fix their connected edge non-corner vertex, creating 1 new odd interior vertex per removal. Total removals: 28, fixing 28 edge non-corner vertices, creating 28 new odd interior vertices.\n\nTotal edge non-corner fixed: 8 + 28 = 36. Good.\n\nSecond phase:\n\nWe have 28 new odd interior vertices. To fix them, need 28/2 = 14 edge removals between interior vertices.\n\nTotal edge removals: 8 + 28 + 14 = 50.\n\nThus, 220 - 50 = 170.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 170.\n\nBut wait, let me think if there's a possibility that in the second phase, the interior vertices can be paired up without needing to remove 14 edges. For example, if the 28 odd-degree interior vertices form a connected graph where a perfect matching exists, then yes, 14 edges can be removed to fix them all. Since the interior vertices form a 9x9 grid (which is highly connected), such a matching should exist.\n\nTherefore, the calculation seems consistent.\n\nHowever, I want to confirm with another small example. Let's take a 4x4 grid.\n\n4x4 grid:\n\n- Total cells: 16.\n\n- Edge non-corner vertices: each side has 2 non-corner vertices (since 4 cells per side, minus 2 corners). 4 sides × 2 = 8 edge non-corner vertices, each with degree 3 (odd).\n\n- Interior vertices: 4x4 grid's interior is 2x2 grid (rows 2-3, columns 2-3). Each interior vertex has degree 4 (even).\n\nTotal edges:\n\nHorizontal: 4 rows × 3 edges = 12.\n\nVertical: 4 columns × 3 edges = 12.\n\nTotal edges: 24.\n\nWe need to make all degrees even.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: 8, all odd.\n\nInterior vertices: 4, all even.\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex is connected to one interior vertex.\n\nThe interior vertices form a 2x2 grid. Each interior vertex is connected to four edge non-corner vertices? Wait no.\n\nLet's detail:\n\nIn a 4x4 grid:\n\nLabel cells (1,1) to (4,4).\n\nEdge non-corner vertices:\n\n- Top edge: (1,2), (1,3)\n\n- Bottom edge: (4,2), (4,3)\n\n- Left edge: (2,1), (3,1)\n\n- Right edge: (2,4), (3,4)\n\nEach of these has degree 3.\n\nInterior vertices: (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3), each with degree 4.\n\nConnections:\n\nTake top edge non-corner vertex (1,2):\n\n- Connected to (1,1) [corner, degree 2], (1,3) [edge non-corner], and (2,2) [interior].\n\nSimilarly, (1,3) is connected to (1,2), (1,4) [corner], and (2,3).\n\nThe interior vertex (2,2) is connected to:\n\n- (1,2), (2,1), (3,2), (2,3).\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nThus, each interior vertex is connected to four edge non-corner vertices? No:\n\nWait, (2,2) is connected to:\n\n- Up: (1,2) edge non-corner,\n\n- Down: (3,2) edge non-corner,\n\n- Left: (2,1) edge non-corner,\n\n- Right: (2,3) edge non-corner.\n\nWait, no: (2,3) is an edge non-corner vertex on the top edge? No, (2,3) is on the top edge? No, (2,3) is on the top edge only if it's in row 1. (2,3) is in row 2, column 3, which is on the right side? No, column 3 is not the edge unless it's column 4. Wait, in a 4x4 grid:\n\n- The right edge non-corner vertices are in column 4, rows 2 and 3: (2,4) and (3,4).\n\nTherefore, (2,3) is an interior vertex? No, (2,3) is in row 2, column 3. Since column 3 is not the edge (edges are column 1 and 4), so (2,3) is an interior vertex? Wait no:\n\nWait, in a 4x4 grid, the interior vertices are those not on the border, so rows 2-3 and columns 2-3. Therefore, (2,3) is on the border? No, column 3 is not the edge (edges are column 1 and 4). Therefore, (2,3) is an interior vertex? Wait, column 3 is not an edge column, so yes, (2,3) is an interior vertex. Wait but:\n\nWait, the interior vertices are rows 2-3 and columns 2-3, so (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3). So (2,3) is indeed an interior vertex. Its connections:\n\n- Up: (1,3) which is an edge non-corner vertex (top edge),\n\n- Down: (3,3) interior,\n\n- Left: (2,2) interior,\n\n- Right: (2,4) edge non-corner vertex (right edge).\n\nAh! So (2,3) is connected to two edge non-corner vertices: (1,3) above and (2,4) to the right.\n\nSimilarly, each interior vertex on the \"edge\" of the interior grid is connected to two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- The four interior vertices form a 2x2 grid.\n\n- Each corner interior vertex (like (2,2)) is connected to two edge non-corner vertices:\n\n  - (2,2): connected to (1,2) above and (2,1) left.\n\n- The edge interior vertices (like (2,3)) are connected to one edge non-corner vertex on one side and one on the other?\n\nWait, let's take (2,3):\n\n- Up: (1,3) edge non-corner,\n\n- Right: (2,4) edge non-corner,\n\n- Down: (3,3),\n\n- Left: (2,2).\n\nThus, (2,3) is connected to two edge non-corner vertices: up and right.\n\nSimilarly, (3,2) is connected to down (4,2) edge non-corner and left (3,1) edge non-corner.\n\n(3,3) is connected to down (4,3) edge non-corner and right (3,4) edge non-corner.\n\nTherefore, each of the four interior vertices is connected to two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, in the 4x4 grid:\n\n- Each interior vertex has two edge non-corner neighbors.\n\n- Each edge non-corner vertex is connected to one interior vertex and two edge non-corner neighbors along the edge.\n\nWait, let's check an edge non-corner vertex:\n\nTake (1,2) on the top edge:\n\n- Connected to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [edge non-corner], and (2,2) [interior].\n\nSo only one interior connection.\n\nSimilarly, (1,3) on the top edge:\n\n- Connected to (1,2), (1,4) [corner], and (2,3) [interior].\n\nThus, each edge non-corner vertex is connected to exactly one interior vertex.\n\nTherefore, the connections from interior vertices to edge non-corner vertices are:\n\n- Each interior vertex is connected to two edge non-corner vertices.\n\n- Each edge non-corner vertex is connected to one interior vertex.\n\nTherefore, total edge non-corner vertices: 8, each connected to one interior vertex.\n\nTotal interior vertices: 4, each connected to two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nThus, the total edges between interior and edge non-corner vertices: 4×2 = 8, and 8×1 = 8, consistent.\n\nNow, applying our method:\n\nFirst phase:\n\nFor each interior vertex with two edge non-corner neighbors (all four interior vertices):\n\n- Each can have their two connected edge non-corner vertices fixed by removing two edges per interior vertex.\n\nHowever, each interior vertex is connected to two edge non-corner vertices, so removing both edges from that interior vertex to its two edge non-corner neighbors would fix both edge non-corner vertices and keep the interior vertex's degree even (since it loses two edges, degree 4 - 2 = 2, still even).\n\nTherefore, for each interior vertex:\n\n- Remove two edges (to its two connected edge non-corner vertices).\n\nTotal edge removals per interior vertex: 2.\n\nFour interior vertices: 4 × 2 = 8 edge removals.\n\nThis fixes all 8 edge non-corner vertices (each losing one edge), and the interior vertices each lose two edges, remaining at degree 2 (even).\n\nTherefore, no new odd-degree vertices are created.\n\nThus, total edge removals: 8.\n\nTotal edges in the grid: 24.\n\nThus, maximum Eulerian circuit length: 24 - 8 = 16.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if that's correct.\n\nAfter removing the eight edges from the interior vertices to their connected edge non-corner vertices:\n\nEach interior vertex now has degree 2 (connected to its two adjacent interior vertices).\n\nEach edge non-corner vertex now has degree 2 (connected to its two edge non-corner neighbors along the edge and no longer to the interior).\n\nWait, let's take an example:\n\nTake interior vertex (2,2):\n\n- Originally connected to (1,2), (2,1), (3,2), (2,3).\n\nAfter removing edges to (1,2) and (2,1), it's connected to (3,2) and (2,3).\n\nSimilarly, edge non-corner vertex (1,2):\n\n- Originally connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2).\n\nAfter removing the edge to (2,2), it's connected to (1,1) and (1,3), degree 2.\n\nSimilarly for all others.\n\nThus, all vertices now have even degrees:\n\n- Corners: degree 2 (unchanged).\n\n- Edge non-corner vertices: degree 2.\n\n- Interior vertices: degree 2.\n\nTherefore, the entire graph now has all degrees even, and is connected (since the interior is still connected via their remaining edges). Therefore, an Eulerian circuit exists with 24 - 8 = 16 edges.\n\nThis matches our calculation. Therefore, the method works for the 4x4 grid.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem, the calculation of 50 edge removals leading to 170 doors traversed seems correct.\n\nHowever, I want to ensure that in the 11x11 grid, the second phase of removing edges between interior vertices to fix the 28 odd-degree vertices can indeed be done with 14 edge removals. Since the interior vertices form a 9x9 grid (which is a connected planar graph), and we have an even number of odd-degree vertices (28), it is indeed possible to find a set of 14 edges whose removal will make all those 28 vertices even. This is a standard result in graph theory: in any connected graph with an even number of odd-degree vertices, there exists a set of edges whose removal will make all degrees even. The minimal number of edges to remove is exactly half the number of odd-degree vertices, which is 14 here.\n\nTherefore, the total number of edges Karthik can traverse is 220 - 50 = 170.\n\nHowever, I recall that in some grid graphs, there might be constraints I haven't considered, but given the consistency in smaller grids and the logical breakdown, I think 170 is the right answer.\n\nBut wait, let me see if there is any other perspective.\n\nAnother way to think about it is that the original graph has 36 vertices of odd degree. To make all degrees even, you need to remove a number of edges equal to 36/2 = 18 if the graph were connected. But since the graph is disconnected in terms of the odd-degree vertices (they're in four separate components each with an odd number of odd vertices), you have to do more.\n\nEach connected component with an odd number of odd-degree vertices requires an additional edge removal to leave an even number. Wait, but each component already has an even number of odd-degree vertices because the Handshaking Lemma requires it.\n\n