graph's odd-degree vertices (the 36 edge non-corner vertices) form a single connected component in the graph.\n\nTherefore, the connected components of the odd-degree vertices is just one big component with 36 vertices, which is even, so it's okay.\n\nTherefore, my earlier analysis of the connected components was incorrect because I considered only the induced subgraph of edge non-corner vertices, but in the entire graph, they are connected via interior vertices.\n\nTherefore, the entire graph's odd-degree vertices form a single connected component with 36 vertices (even), so it is possible to find a set of 18 edges to remove to make all degrees even.\n\nWait, this is a game-changer.\n\nLet me clarify:\n\nThe Handshaking Lemma says that the entire graph has an even number of odd-degree vertices (36), which is fine.\n\nWhen considering the connected components of the graph, each connected component must also have an even number of odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, if the odd-degree vertices form a single connected component (which they do, because you can go from any edge non-corner vertex to any other via interior vertices), then that single component has 36 odd-degree vertices (even), so it is possible to find a set of 18 edges to remove within this component to make all degrees even.\n\nTherefore, my initial thought that it required 50 edge removals was incorrect because I incorrectly considered the disconnected induced subgraph of edge non-corner vertices, but in reality, they are all connected through the interior vertices.\n\nTherefore, the correct minimal number of edges to remove is 18, leading to an Eulerian circuit of 220 - 18 = 202 edges.\n\nThis is a critical realization. The key was recognizing that the edge non-corner vertices, although forming paths along the edges, are interconnected through the interior vertices, making the entire set of odd-degree vertices a single connected component. Therefore, we can apply the standard Eulerian circuit theorem: in a connected graph with all even degrees, an Eulerian circuit exists. To make all degrees even, we can remove 18 edges (since there are 36 odd-degree vertices), and the graph will become Eulerian.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nBut then why did my smaller example with the 4x4 grid give a different result?\n\nIn the 4x4 grid, we had 8 edge non-corner vertices (all odd), and by removing 8 edges (not 4), we achieved all even degrees. Wait, but according to this new logic, since all odd-degree vertices form a single connected component with 8 vertices (even), we should be able to fix them with 4 edge removals.\n\nIndeed, in the 4x4 grid:\n\n- The odd-degree vertices are the 8 edge non-corner vertices.\n\n- They form a single connected component through the interior vertices.\n\n- Therefore, the minimal number of edge removals should be 8 / 2 = 4.\n\nWhich matches the result we got manually: removing 8 edges (wait, no, in the 4x4 grid example, we removed 8 edges to fix all degrees, but according to this, it should be 4).\n\nWait, there is a discrepancy here.\n\nIn the 4x4 grid, we had 8 edge non-corner vertices (all odd). According to the new logic, since they form a single connected component with 8 odd-degree vertices, we should be able to fix them by removing 4 edges.\n\nBut in reality, when we tried to do it, we had to remove 8 edges.\n\nWhat's the issue here?\n\nAh! The confusion arises because the edges we are allowed to remove can be any edges in the graph, not just those between odd-degree vertices.\n\nIn the 4x4 grid example, the minimal number of edge removals required is indeed 4, but my manual approach removed 8 edges because I was removing all connections from the interior to the edge non-corner vertices, which is more than necessary.\n\nLet me redo the 4x4 grid with the new approach:\n\nGoal: fix 8 odd-degree vertices (edge non-corner) with minimal edge removals.\n\nSince they form a single connected component, we can find a set of 4 edges to remove such that each removal flips two odd-degree vertices' parities.\n\nFor example, pick four edges that form a matching within the odd-degree vertices' connections.\n\nIn the 4x4 grid, each edge non-corner vertex is connected to one interior vertex. The interior vertices are connected among themselves.\n\nSuppose we choose four edges that are part of a matching in the entire graph's odd-degree vertices.\n\nAlternatively, since each edge non-corner vertex is connected to one interior vertex, and each interior vertex is connected to two edge non-corner vertices, we can for each interior vertex, remove one edge connecting it to an edge non-corner vertex. This would fix two edge non-corner vertices per interior vertex removed? Wait:\n\nEach interior vertex has two edge non-corner neighbors.\n\nIf we remove one edge from an interior vertex to an edge non-corner vertex, that fixes that edge non-corner vertex (its degree becomes 2) but the interior vertex's degree becomes 3 (odd). To fix the interior vertex, we need to remove another edge from it, which would fix another edge non-corner vertex but leave the interior vertex at 2 (even). Thus, for each interior vertex, removing two edges can fix two edge non-corner vertices without affecting the interior vertex's parity.\n\nSince there are four interior vertices, each connected to two edge non-corner vertices:\n\n- For each interior vertex, remove two edges connecting it to its two edge non-corner neighbors.\n\n- This requires 4 interior vertices × 2 edges = 8 edge removals, which is what I did before.\n\nBut according to the new logic, since the total number of odd-degree vertices is even (8), we should be able to do it with 4 edge removals.\n\nAh! The key is that when you remove an edge between an interior vertex and an edge non-corner vertex, you are affecting both vertices. Each such removal flips the parity of both endpoints.\n\nTherefore, to fix all 8 odd-degree edge non-corner vertices, we can pair them up and remove the connecting edge to their common interior vertex.\n\nWait, but each edge non-corner vertex is only connected to one interior vertex. However, each interior vertex is connected to two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, for each interior vertex, which has two odd-degree edge non-corner neighbors, we can remove both edges from the interior vertex to those two edge non-corner vertices. This flips the parity of both edge non-corner vertices (from odd to even) and the interior vertex's degree by -2 (from 4 to 2, even). Thus, this operation fixes two edge non-corner vertices with two edge removals, and the interior vertex remains even.\n\nSince there are four interior vertices, each can be processed this way, requiring 4 × 2 = 8 edge removals, as before.\n\nAlternatively, if we instead remove one edge per interior vertex, but that would leave the interior vertex odd.\n\nAlternatively, is there a way to do it with fewer edge removals?\n\nSuppose we remove one edge from each of four interior vertices, each to one of their edge non-corner neighbors. This would fix four edge non-corner vertices but create four new odd-degree interior vertices. Then, we can remove four more edges between those new odd-degree interior vertices, fixing them. Total edge removals: 8.\n\nAlternatively, if the new odd-degree interior vertices can be paired with existing ones, but since they are spread out, it might not help.\n\nTherefore, it seems that for the 4x4 grid, the minimal number of edge removals required is indeed 8, not 4. This contradicts the earlier assertion that it should be half the number of odd-degree vertices (4). Where is the mistake?\n\nAh! The key point is that when you remove an edge between an interior vertex and an edge non-corner vertex, you are changing the parity of both endpoints. Therefore, each such edge removal can fix one odd-degree vertex (the edge non-corner) and create one new odd-degree vertex (the interior). Therefore, to fix all 8 edge non-corner vertices (all odd), you need to perform 8 edge removals, each fixing one edge non-corner vertex but creating one new odd interior vertex. However, this would result in 8 new odd interior vertices. To fix those, you need to remove 4 more edges (since 8/2=4), totaling 12 edge removals. But this is worse.\n\nAlternatively, if you remove two edges at a time from an interior vertex to its two edge non-corner neighbors, then each such pair of removals fixes two edge non-corner vertices and leaves the interior vertex even. Therefore, for each interior vertex, you need two edge removals to fix two edge non-corner vertices. With four interior vertices, this requires 8 edge removals, resulting in all edge non-corner vertices even and all interior vertices even. This works and requires 8 edge removals.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edge removals is indeed 8 for the 4x4 grid, which is equal to the number of edge non-corner vertices divided by 1 (since each edge non-corner vertex needs one edge removed), but actually, it's equal to the number of edge non-corner vertices because each requires one edge to be removed from it, but each interior vertex's two edges can be removed in pairs.\n\nWait, but in terms of the formula:\n\nTotal odd-degree vertices: 8 (all edge non-corner).\n\nEach edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices if they are connected by an edge. However, in this case, the odd-degree vertices (edge non-corner) are not connected to each other directly; they are connected through interior vertices. Therefore, the edges between the odd-degree vertices are not present; the only edges incident to them are to interior vertices or along the edge to other edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, the odd-degree vertices are not adjacent to each other, so we cannot pair them up directly. The only way to fix them is to go through the interior vertices.\n\nEach interior vertex connected to two odd-degree vertices can have both edges removed in a pair, fixing both without affecting the interior vertex's parity. Since there are four interior vertices each connected to two odd-degree vertices, this requires four × two = eight edge removals.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the minimal number of edge removals is indeed equal to the number of edge non-corner vertices, since each requires one edge to be removed from it, and each interior vertex handles two edge non-corner vertices with two edge removals.\n\nThus, the formula is:\n\nFor each interior vertex connected to k edge non-corner vertices, you need to remove k edges incident to it to fix all k edge non-corner vertices connected to it, but this requires k edge removals per interior vertex. However, if k is even, this keeps the interior vertex's degree even; if k is odd, it creates a problem.\n\nIn our 4x4 example, each interior vertex has k=2 (even), so we can do it with k/2 pairs per interior vertex.\n\nIn the original 11x11 grid:\n\nEach interior vertex connected to edge non-corner vertices has either 0, 1, or 2.\n\nFor those with 2 edge non-corner neighbors, we can remove both edges in pairs, fixing two edge non-corner vertices per two edge removals.\n\nFor those with 1 edge non-corner neighbor, we have to remove that single edge, fixing the edge non-corner vertex but creating an odd interior vertex.\n\nHowever, since the total number of edge non-corner vertices is 36 (even), and each interior vertex with 1 edge non-corner neighbor contributes 1 to the count, the number of such interior vertices must be even. Indeed, we had 28 interior vertices with 1 edge non-corner neighbor (even), and 4 with 2 (even). Therefore, we can handle the 28 with 28 edge removals, each fixing one edge non-corner vertex and creating one new odd interior vertex. The 28 new odd interior vertices can then be paired up and fixed with 14 edge removals between them.\n\nTotal edge removals:\n\n- For the 4 interior vertices with 2 edges: 4 × 2 = 8.\n\n- For the 28 interior vertices with 1 edge: 28 × 1 = 28.\n\n- For the new 28 odd interior vertices: 28 / 2 = 14.\n\nTotal: 8 + 28 + 14 = 50.\n\nBut according to the Handshaking Lemma, since the entire graph's odd-degree vertices are 36 (even), we should be able to do it with 18 edge removals. The discrepancy arises because when we remove edges to fix the edge non-corner vertices, we are also creating new odd-degree vertices (the interior ones), which then need to be addressed, leading to a higher total.\n\nThe key insight is that when you remove an edge from an interior vertex to an edge non-corner vertex, you are not just fixing one odd-degree vertex but potentially creating another. Therefore, the process is more involved and cannot be done in a straightforward way of just 18 edge removals because the removals affect multiple vertices.\n\nHowever, if we consider the entire graph and apply the theorem correctly: in any graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, provided the graph is connected. Since our graph is connected (the entire grid is connected), and has 36 odd-degree vertices, the minimal number of edges to remove is 18.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nThe earlier detailed analysis was overcomplicating by restricting edge removals to only those involving edge non-corner vertices or specific interior vertices, but in reality, you can remove any edges in the graph to achieve the parity fix.\n\nTherefore, the correct minimal edge removals are 18, leading to 202 doors.\n\nThe confusion arose because when trying to fix the odd-degree vertices by only removing edges connected to them, we inadvertently created new odd-degree vertices, necessitating additional removals. However, by strategically removing edges that affect pairs of odd-degree vertices wherever possible, without creating new ones, we can achieve the fix with just 18 edge removals.\n\nFor example, in the 4x4 grid, even though each edge non-corner vertex is only connected to one interior vertex, we can choose which edges to remove such that each removal fixes two odd-degree vertices at once. However, in the 4x4 grid, each interior vertex is connected to two edge non-corner vertices, so by removing both edges from an interior vertex to its two edge non-corner neighbors, we can fix both edge non-corner vertices with two edge removals, and since there are four such interior vertices, this totals 8 edge removals. But according to the theorem, it should be 4.\n\nWait, now I'm confused again.\n\nWait, in the 4x4 grid, there are 8 odd-degree vertices (edge non-corner). The minimal number of edges to remove should be 8 / 2 = 4.\n\nHow can this be achieved?\n\nSuppose we can find four edges whose removal will each fix two odd-degree vertices.\n\nFor instance, if we can find four edges that are between an interior vertex and an edge non-corner vertex, such that each interior vertex is involved in exactly one such edge removal, but this seems impossible since each interior vertex has two edge non-corner neighbors.\n\nAlternatively, if we remove four edges that form a matching in the odd-degree vertices' graph.\n\nBut the odd-degree vertices (edge non-corner) are not connected to each other; their only connections are to interior vertices or along the edge to other edge non-corner vertices.\n\nWait, the edge non-corner vertices along the same edge are connected to each other. For example, on the top edge, the two non-corner vertices are connected to each other.\n\nAh! Here's the key. The edge non-corner vertices along the same edge are connected to each other. For example, on the top edge of the 4x4 grid, the two non-corner vertices (1,2) and (1,3) are connected by an edge.\n\nTherefore, those two are adjacent and both have odd degree. So, we can remove the edge between them, which will reduce both their degrees by 1, turning them from 3 to 2 (even), and the edge between them is removed. This single edge removal fixes two odd-degree vertices.\n\nSimilarly, on each edge's non-corner vertices path, we can remove edges between adjacent non-corner vertices to fix pairs.\n\nFor the top edge non-corner vertices (1,2) and (1,3):\n\n- Remove the edge between them. This fixes both.\n\nSimilarly for the bottom edge's non-corner vertices (4,2) and (4,3).\n\nOn the left edge's non-corner vertices (2,1) and (3,1):\n\n- Remove the edge between them.\n\nOn the right edge's non-corner vertices (2,4) and (3,4):\n\n- Remove the edge between them.\n\nThis removes four edges, each fixing two odd-degree vertices, totaling eight edge non-corner vertices fixed with four edge removals.\n\nAdditionally, the interior vertices connected to these edge non-corner vertices now have their degrees reduced by one (since we removed the edge between the two non-corner vertices, which doesn't involve the interior vertices). Wait, no:\n\nWait, the edges between the edge non-corner vertices along the edge are separate from their connections to the interior vertices.\n\nFor example, the edge between (1,2) and (1,3)","num_tokens":32768,"correct":false,"formatted":false},{"predicted_answer":"120","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem about an 11x11 grid of cells. Each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door. Karthik wants to walk a path in this grid, starting in any cell, but he must end up in the same cell he started in. Also, he can't go through any door more than once—not even in opposite directions. The question is asking for the maximum number of doors he can go through in such a path. Hmm, interesting. Let me try to wrap my head around this step by step.\n\nFirst off, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. We have an 11x11 grid. Each cell is a square, and each edge between two adjacent cells (so that's up, down, left, right; diagonals aren't considered here because they're not edge-adjacent) is connected by a door. So, every pair of edge-adjacent cells shares a common door between them. So, each door is essentially an edge in a graph where the cells are the vertices. Therefore, this grid can be modeled as a graph with vertices corresponding to the cells and edges (or doors) corresponding to the adjacencies between cells.\n\nTherefore, the grid graph for an 11x11 grid has 121 vertices (since 11x11 = 121) and how many edges? Well, in an m x n grid graph, the number of horizontal edges is m*(n-1), and the number of vertical edges is (m-1)*n. Therefore, total edges are m*(n-1) + (m-1)*n. For an 11x11 grid, that would be 11*10 + 10*11 = 110 + 110 = 220 edges. So, there are 220 doors in total. Each door is an edge in this graph.\n\nNow, Karthik wants to walk a path that starts and ends at the same cell, so it's a closed walk, a cycle. However, he cannot go through any door more than once. In graph theory terms, this is equivalent to finding a closed walk that is also an Eulerian circuit, except that in an Eulerian circuit, you traverse every edge exactly once. However, here, the problem says he cannot go through any door more than once, so he can traverse each door at most once. So, actually, the problem is asking for the maximum length of a closed walk that does not repeat any edge (door), starting and ending at the same vertex. In graph theory terms, that would be a closed trail that is also a circuit (which it already is by being closed), but without repeating edges.\n\nWait, but an Eulerian circuit is a closed trail that traverses every edge exactly once. So, if such a circuit exists in the graph, then that would be the longest possible closed walk without repeating edges, since it uses all edges. However, an Eulerian circuit exists in a graph if and only if every vertex has even degree, and the graph is connected. So, first, is the grid graph connected? Yes, obviously, since you can get from any cell to any other cell by moving through adjacent cells. Second, does every vertex in the grid graph have even degree?\n\nLet me think about the degrees of the vertices in an 11x11 grid graph. Each vertex (cell) can have up to four neighbors: up, down, left, right. The corner cells will have degree 2 (since they only have two adjacent cells), the edge cells (but not corners) will have degree 3, and the inner cells (not on any edge) will have degree 4. Wait, hold on. Let me confirm that.\n\nTake a corner cell: in an 11x11 grid, the four corners each have two adjacent cells. For example, the top-left corner cell has a cell to the right and a cell below it, but no cells to the left or above it, so indeed degree 2. Then, an edge cell that is not a corner: let's say on the top edge, not in the corner. Then, such a cell has a cell to the left, a cell to the right, and a cell below it. So that's three neighbors, so degree 3. Similarly, a cell on the rightmost edge (but not a corner) would have a cell to the left, a cell above it, and a cell below it, so again degree 3. Similarly for the bottom edge. Then, for an inner cell, say somewhere in the middle, not on any edge, then it has a cell to the left, right, above, and below, so four neighbors, so degree 4.\n\nTherefore, in the grid graph of an 11x11 grid, all the corner cells have degree 2, the edge cells (but not corners) have degree 3, and all the inner cells have degree 4. Therefore, the degrees of the vertices are not all even. Specifically, the corner vertices have degree 2 (which is even), the edge vertices (non-corner) have degree 3 (which is odd), and the inner vertices have degree 4 (which is even). Therefore, since there are vertices of odd degree (the edge cells that are not corners), the graph does not have all vertices of even degree, so therefore, it does not have an Eulerian circuit.\n\nTherefore, we cannot have a closed walk that traverses every edge exactly once, because that requires all vertices to have even degree. Therefore, the maximum closed walk that doesn't repeat any edges must necessarily be shorter than the total number of edges, which is 220. So, the question is, how much shorter can it be? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can still have a closed walk that uses as many edges as possible without repeating any, but since there are vertices of odd degree, we can't have an Eulerian circuit. However, in such a case, the maximum trail (a trail is a walk with no repeated edges) that is closed must have some constraints based on the degrees of the vertices.\n\nWait, actually, maybe we can consider the concept of an Eulerian trail versus an Eulerian circuit. An Eulerian trail is a trail that starts and ends at different vertices and traverses every edge exactly once, which exists if and only if there are exactly two vertices of odd degree (and all others even), and the graph is connected. An Eulerian circuit is a special case of an Eulerian trail where the start and end vertices are the same, which requires all vertices to have even degree.\n\nBut in our case, we have a grid graph with four vertices of degree 2 (the corners), and the rest of the edge vertices (there are 11*4 - 4 = 40 edge vertices? Wait, let's count the number of edge vertices more carefully.\n\nWait, in an m x n grid, the number of corner vertices is 4, assuming m and n are at least 2, which they are here (11x11). The number of edge vertices (i.e., vertices on the border but not corners) can be calculated as follows:\n\nEach side of the grid has (number of vertices) minus the two corners. Since it's an 11x11 grid, each side has 11 vertices. Therefore, the top side has 11 vertices, of which two are corners, so 11 - 2 = 9 non-corner edge vertices on the top side. Similarly, the bottom side also has 9 non-corner edge vertices. The left side has 11 vertices, two of which are corners (the top-left and bottom-left corners), so 11 - 2 = 9 non-corner edge vertices on the left side. Similarly, the right side has 9 non-corner edge vertices. So total edge vertices (non-corner) are 4 sides * 9 = 36? Wait, hold on: 9 on top, 9 on bottom, 9 on left, 9 on right? Wait, but actually, the left and right sides include their own corners. Wait, perhaps my mistake.\n\nWait, perhaps better to think of it as: for each side of the grid, the number of non-corner vertices on that side is (length of side minus 2). Since each side has 11 vertices, so non-corner vertices on a side are 11 - 2 = 9. Since there are four sides, that would be 4*9 = 36 edge vertices. However, but wait, the four corners are counted in the sides, but we are excluding them here. Wait, actually, when I say \"edge vertices\" (vertices on the border), that includes the corners. Wait, actually, in graph theory, sometimes \"edge vertices\" isn't a standard term, but in this case, perhaps \"border vertices\" or \"vertices on the boundary\".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps better to count the number of vertices with degree 3. Since the vertices with degree 3 are exactly the ones that are on the border but not at the corners. Each border side (top, bottom, left, right) has 11 vertices, two of which are corners (degree 2), and the remaining 9 are edge vertices with degree 3. So, for each of the four sides: 9 non-corner vertices with degree 3, so total edge vertices (non-corner) with degree 3: 4 * 9 = 36. Then, the remaining vertices are the inner vertices, which are not on any edge, so their degree is 4. How many inner vertices are there?\n\nTotal vertices: 11x11 = 121. Subtract the border vertices: the border vertices consist of the four sides. But the four corners are counted twice if we just add up the sides. Wait, actually, the total number of border vertices is calculated as follows: each side has 11 vertices, but the four corners are shared between two sides. So, total border vertices = 4*11 - 4 = 44 - 4 = 40? Wait, 4 sides, each with 11 vertices, so 4*11 = 44, but the four corners are counted twice (once for each adjacent side), so we subtract 4 duplicates, so total border vertices: 44 - 4 = 40. Therefore, the inner vertices are total vertices minus border vertices: 121 - 40 = 81. So 81 inner vertices, each of degree 4. The remaining border vertices (excluding corners) are 40 total border vertices minus 4 corners = 36 non-corner border vertices, each of degree 3. And the four corners, each of degree 2.\n\nSo, in summary, in the grid graph of an 11x11 grid:\n\n- 4 vertices of degree 2 (the corners),\n\n- 36 vertices of degree 3 (the non-corner border vertices),\n\n- 81 vertices of degree 4 (the inner vertices).\n\nTherefore, the number of vertices with odd degree is 4 (corners) + 36 (degree 3) = 40 vertices of odd degree. Since an Eulerian circuit requires all vertices to have even degree, and since we have 40 vertices of odd degree here, which is a lot, so definitely no Eulerian circuit exists.\n\nNow, in graph theory, when you have a graph with multiple vertices of odd degree, the maximum length of a closed trail (a trail that starts and ends at the same vertex) is related to the number of odd degree vertices. Specifically, in any graph, the number of vertices of odd degree is even. Here, we have 40 vertices of odd degree, which is even, so that's okay. In fact, the maximum number of edges that can be included in a closed trail (which is called an Eulerian trail if it uses every edge, but here we can't because of the odd degrees) is limited by the number of odd degree vertices.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I should recall that in any graph, the maximum number of edges in a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of \"deficit\" edges needed to make all degrees even? Hmm, not sure. Wait, maybe I need to recall something called the \"Eulerian trail\" and the \"minimum number of edges to add to make the graph Eulerian\".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps another approach is needed here. Since we need a closed trail (a circuit) that doesn't repeat any edges, the maximum such trail would be as long as possible. The problem is similar to finding the longest possible closed trail in the grid graph without repeating edges. Since we can't have an Eulerian circuit because of the odd degrees, but perhaps we can have a trail that starts and ends at the same vertex but misses some edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum such trail can be constructed by pairing up the odd-degree vertices and creating paths between them, thereby \"using up\" the odd degrees, but since we need a closed trail, perhaps the trail must start and end at the same vertex, so maybe we need to have an even number of odd-degree vertices, which we do, but since it's closed, perhaps we need to have all degrees even? Wait, but in a closed trail, you can have vertices with odd degree as long as the number is even? Wait, actually, in any closed trail, the degrees of the vertices along the trail must satisfy certain conditions?\n\nWait, let me recall: in any graph, the number of vertices of odd degree is even. That's the handshaking lemma. So, in any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree is always even. So, that's a given.\n\nNow, in a closed trail, which is a trail that starts and ends at the same vertex, the degrees of the vertices in the trail can be considered in terms of the trail's path. Wait, actually, in a closed trail, each time you enter a vertex, you must leave it, except for the starting/ending vertex, which is entered and left the same number of times. Wait, actually, in a closed trail, every vertex is entered and exited the same number of times, so the degree of each vertex in the trail (counting the number of times the trail passes through it) must be even? Wait, no, perhaps not exactly.\n\nWait, actually, in a trail, a vertex can be visited multiple times, but the edges are not repeated. Each time you enter a vertex (except the start/end), you must leave via a different edge. So, in a closed trail, starting and ending at the same vertex, for each vertex except the start/end vertex, the number of times you enter equals the number of times you exit, so the