degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner: each side has 3 vertices, subtract 2 corners, so 1 edge non-corner per side, so 4 edges, each with degree 3.\n\nCenter vertex: degree 4.\n\nWait, in a 3x3 grid:\n\nEach corner has 2 edges.\n\nEach edge non-corner (there are 4 of them, one on each side) has degree 3.\n\nThe center has degree 4.\n\nTotal vertices: 9.\n\nTotal edges:\n\nHorizontal edges: 3 rows, each with 2 edges: 3*2 = 6.\n\nVertical edges: 3 columns, each with 2 edges: 3*2 = 6.\n\nTotal edges: 12.\n\nNow, the degrees:\n\nCorners: 4 vertices, each degree 2 (even).\n\nEdge non-corner: 4 vertices, each degree 3 (odd).\n\nCenter: 1 vertex, degree 4 (even).\n\nSo total odd-degree vertices: 4.\n\nTherefore, according to the previous reasoning, to make all degrees even, need to remove 4/2 = 2 edges.\n\nTotal edges in grid: 12, so maximum Eulerian subgraph would have 12 - 2 = 10 edges.\n\nIs that correct?\n\nLet me see if we can find a connected subgraph with 10 edges where all degrees are even.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to construct such a subgraph.\n\nStart with the entire grid, which has 12 edges. We need to remove 2 edges such that all degrees become even.\n\nThe original odd-degree vertices are the four edge non-corner vertices (each has degree 3). Each of these needs to have their degree reduced by 1 (to make it even). So, we need to remove one edge incident to each of these four vertices? But each edge removal affects two vertices. So, to reduce the degree of four vertices by 1 each, we can do this by removing two edges, each of which is incident to two of the odd-degree vertices.\n\nFor example, let's label the 3x3 grid as follows, with coordinates (row, column) from (1,1) to (3,3):\n\nCorners: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) – degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corners: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2) – each degree 3.\n\nCenter: (2,2) – degree 4.\n\nSuppose we remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (2,1) and (2,2). Then, let's see the effect:\n\nVertex (1,2): originally degree 3. It loses the edge to (2,2), so now degree 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,2): originally degree 4. It loses two edges: to (1,2) and to (2,1), so now degree 2 (even).\n\nVertex (2,1): originally degree 3. It loses the edge to (2,2), so now degree 2 (even).\n\nThe other odd-degree vertices: (2,3) and (3,2) are unaffected. Wait, but we only removed two edges, each affecting two vertices. Wait, actually, the two edges we removed are:\n\n1. Between (1,2) and (2,2).\n\n2. Between (2,1) and (2,2).\n\nSo, the vertices affected are:\n\n- (1,2): degree reduced by 1 (from 3 to 2).\n\n- (2,2): degree reduced by 2 (from 4 to 2).\n\n- (2,1): degree reduced by 1 (from 3 to 2).\n\nThe other two odd-degree vertices, (2,3) and (3,2), remain at degree 3 (odd). So that didn't fix all the odd degrees. Oops, so my mistake.\n\nI need to remove edges such that all four edge non-corner vertices have their degrees reduced by 1. Each edge can only reduce two vertices' degrees. Therefore, to reduce four vertices by 1 each, we need to remove two edges, each affecting two of the odd-degree vertices. But in the 3x3 grid, the four odd-degree vertices are arranged such that each is adjacent to the center vertex (2,2). So, each of the four edge non-corner vertices is connected to the center. Therefore, if we remove the four edges connecting the center to each of the four edge non-corner vertices, that would reduce each of those four vertices' degrees by 1 (from 3 to 2), and the center's degree by 4 (from 4 to 0). But that would remove four edges, which is more than the minimal 2 required. Alternatively, if we remove two edges that are not all incident to the center.\n\nAlternatively, maybe remove two edges that are between the edge non-corner vertices and other vertices. For instance, suppose we remove the edge between (1,2) and (1,1), and the edge between (2,1) and (3,1). Let's see:\n\nVertex (1,2): originally degree 3 (connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2)). If we remove the edge to (1,1), then