 to the odd-degree vertices are either to the center or to the corners.\n\nTherefore, the only edges that can be used to reduce the degree of two odd-degree vertices at once would be edges connecting two odd-degree vertices? But there are no such edges. Alternatively, edges connecting an odd-degree vertex to the center? Each odd-degree vertex is connected to the center. So if we remove an edge between an odd-degree vertex and the center, then we reduce that vertex's degree by 1 and the center's degree by 1. The center was originally even (degree 4), so it becomes odd. So that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, if we remove two edges from the center to two different odd-degree vertices:\n\nSuppose we remove the edge from center (2,2) to (1,2) and to (3,2). Then:\n\nVertex (1,2): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (3,2): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,2): degree 4 - 2 = 2 (good).\n\nBut the other two odd-degree vertices, (2,1) and (2,3), are still at degree 3 (odd). So we still have two odd-degree vertices left. Therefore, we need to remove two more edges. Hmm, so this approach isn't working.\n\nAlternatively, remove two edges that each connect an odd-degree vertex to a corner. For instance, remove the edge between (1,2) and (1,1), and the edge between (2,1) and (1,1). Then:\n\nVertex (1,2): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,1): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (1,1): degree 2 - 2 = 0 (even, but that's a problem because it's now isolated? Wait, no, it's still connected to other vertices? Wait, (1,1) was connected to (1,2) and (2,1). If both those edges are removed, then (1,1) has degree 0. But we don't care about the degrees of other vertices as long as they are even, but (1,1) now has degree 0, which is even, so that's okay? Wait, but the subgraph must remain connected. If we remove those two edges, then (1,1) is isolated, so the subgraph is disconnected. So that's bad.\n\nHmm. This is getting complicated. Maybe the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even while keeping the graph connected is more than 2 in the 3x3 grid?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the 3x3 grid, it's impossible to have a connected subgraph with all even degrees and 10 edges? Let's see.\n\nTotal edges in original: 12. If we can find a connected subgraph with 10 edges and all even degrees, then that would mean we removed 2 edges. But as we saw, trying to remove two edges and keep it connected while making all degrees even seems difficult.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the minimal number of edges to remove is 4? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we remove four edges: the four edges connecting the center to the four edge non-corner vertices. Then:\n\nEach of the four edge non-corner vertices now have degree 2 (even), and the center has degree 0 (even). The corners still have degree 2 (even). So all degrees are even. However, the center is now disconnected from the rest of the graph. So the subgraph consists of the four corners and the four edge non-corner vertices, but the center is isolated. Therefore, the subgraph is disconnected. So that's no good.\n\nAlternatively, remove three edges. Suppose we remove three edges: say, from the center to three of the four edge non-corner vertices. Then, those three edge non-corner vertices now have degree 2 (even), the center has degree 1 (odd), and the fourth edge non-corner vertex still has degree 3 (odd). So that doesn't fix all degrees. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, remove three edges in a way that affects two vertices each? For instance, remove three edges such that each removal fixes two odd vertices. But with three removals, you can fix six degrees, but we only have four odd degrees to fix. Wait, but each removal can fix two degrees. So three removals can fix six, but we only need four, so maybe overlapping? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, remove three edges: for example, remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), the edge between (2,1) and (2,2), and the edge between (2,3) and (2,2). Then:\n\nVertex (1,2): degree 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,1): degree 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,3): degree 2 (good).\n\nVertex (3,2): still degree 3 (bad).\n\nCenter (2,2): degree 4 - 3 = 1 (odd).\n\nSo now, we have two odd-degree vertices: (3,2) and (2,2). So still two odd degrees left. So need to remove another edge. So total four edges removed. Let's remove one more edge, say between (3,2) and (3,1). Then:\n\nVertex (3,2): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (3,1): degree 2 - 1 = 1 (odd). Now we have introduced a new odd degree at (3,1). Hmm, worse.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (3,2) and (3,3). Then:\n\nVertex (3,2): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (3,3): degree 2 - 1 = 1 (odd). Again, new odd degree.\n\nAlternatively, remove an edge connected to the center and (3,2): the edge between (3,2) and (2,2). But we already removed that? Wait no, in the previous step, we only removed edges to (1,2), (2,1), and (2,3). The edge between (3,2) and (2,2) is still there. So if we remove that edge as the fourth removal:\n\nVertex (3,2): degree 3 - 1 = 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,2): degree 1 - 1 = 0 (even).\n\nNow, all vertices except maybe (3,1) and (3,3) are even. Wait, (3,1) and (3,3) are still at degree 2? Let's see:\n\nOriginal degrees:\n\n(3,1): connected to (3,2) and (2,1). Originally degree 2. We removed the edge between (3,2) and (3,1)? Wait no, we only removed edges involving the center. Wait, no:\n\nWait, let me track this step by step:\n\nOriginal edges:\n\nAfter three removals:\n\nRemoved edges:\n\n1. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n2. (2,1)-(2,2)\n\n3. (2,3)-(2,2)\n\nSo remaining edges involving the center (2,2):\n\nOnly the edge to (3,2) is still present.\n\nOther edges:\n\nThe corner (3,1) is connected to (3,2) and (2,1). The edge (2,1)-(3,1) is still there because we only removed (2,1)-(2,2). Similarly, (3,3) is connected to (2,3) and (3,2). The edge (2,3)-(3,3) is still there because we only removed (2,3)-(2,2). The edge (3,2)-(3,3) is still there.\n\nSo after four removals: removing the fourth edge as (3,2)-(2,2):\n\nNow, the edges removed are:\n\n1. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n2. (2,1)-(2,2)\n\n3. (2,3)-(2,2)\n\n4. (3,2)-(2,2)\n\nNow, let's compute degrees:\n\nVertex (1,2): connected to (1,1), (1,3), and previously connected to (2,2), but that edge is removed. So degree 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,1): connected to (1,1), (3,1), and previously connected to (2,2), but that edge is removed. So degree 2 (good).\n\nVertex (2,3): connected to (1,3), (3,3), and previously connected to (2,2), but that edge is removed. So degree 2 (good).\n\nVertex (3,2): connected to (3,1), (3,3), and previously connected to (2,2), but that edge is removed. So degree 2 (good).\n\nCenter (2,2): originally degree 4, removed four edges, so degree 0 (even).\n\nCorners:\n\n(1,1): connected to (1,2) and (2,1). Both edges still present? The edge (1,2)-(1,1) is still there because we only removed (1,2)-(2,2). Similarly, (2,1)-(1,1) is still there. So (1,1) degree 2 (good).\n\n(1,3): connected to (1,2) and (2,3). Both edges still there? (1,3) connected to (1,2) [still there] and to (2,3) [still there]. So degree 2 (good).\n\n(3,1): connected to (2,1) and (3,2). Both edges still there? (2,1)-(3,1) is still there, and (3,2)-(3,1) is still there. So degree 2 (good).\n\n(3,3): connected to (2,3) and (3,2). Both edges still there? (2,3)-(3,3) is still there, and (3,2)-(3,3) is still there. So degree 2 (good).\n\nSo, after removing four edges, all vertices have even degrees (either 0, 2, or 2). However, the center (2,2) is now disconnected from the rest of the graph. The rest of the graph (the outer ring) is still connected, but the center is isolated. Therefore, the subgraph is disconnected. Therefore, to keep it connected, we might need to not disconnect the center?\n\nHmm. If we instead of removing the edge (3,2)-(2,2), remove a different edge that doesn't disconnect the center.\n\nSuppose instead of removing the fourth edge as (3,2)-(2,2), we remove an edge that is on the outer ring, say the edge between (1,1) and (1,2). 