2,3) connected to (1,3) and (3,3), etc. So all vertices have degree 2, which is even. Therefore, this subgraph is Eulerian, so it has an Eulerian circuit, which is exactly the trail we just described. So this trail has length 8 edges.\n\nIs there a longer trail? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we try to include the center. Let's see if we can make a trail that goes through the center.\n\nStart at (1,1), go right to (1,2), then down to (2,2), then right to (2,3), then down to (3,3), then left to (3,2), then up to (2,2), then left to (2,1), then up to (1,1). Wait, but let's count the edges here:\n\n1. (1,1)-(1,2)\n\n2. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n3. (2,2)-(2,3)\n\n4. (2,3)-(3,3)\n\n5. (3,3)-(3,2)\n\n6. (3,2)-(2,2)\n\n7. (2,2)-(2,1)\n\n8. (2,1)-(1,1)\n\nBut this uses the edge (2,2)-(2,1) and (2,2)-(2,3), etc. However, note that we traverse the edge (2,2)-(2,3) and then later come back from (3,2) to (2,2), which is the reverse direction, but since we can't traverse an edge more than once regardless of direction, that's okay as long as we don't traverse the same edge twice. However, in this path, we go from (2,2) to (2,3) and then later from (3,2) to (2,2). That's two different edges: (2,2)-(2,3) and (3,2)-(2,2). So those are distinct edges. However, when we go from (2,2) to (2,3) and then later from (3,2) to (2,2), that's allowed, as those are different edges.\n\nBut let's count the edges in this trail:\n\n1. (1,1)-(1,2)\n\n2. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n3. (2,2)-(2,3)\n\n4. (2,3)-(3,3)\n\n5. (3,3)-(3,2)\n\n6. (3,2)-(2,2)\n\n7. (2,2)-(2,1)\n\n8. (2,1)-(1,1)\n\nSo that's 8 edges again. However, in this trail, we used the center edges: (1,2)-(2,2), (2,2)-(2,3), (2,2)-(3,2), and (2,2)-(2,1). So that's four edges connected to the center. The other four edges are the perimeter edges except for the ones not connected to the center. Wait, actually, in this trail, we have included four edges from the center and four edges from the outer perimeter, totaling eight edges. However, in the previous perimeter-only trai