en, while keeping the graph connected, and thus the resulting subgraph has an Eulerian circuit of 220 - 18 = 202 edges.\n\nTherefore, I think this is the correct answer.\n\nHence, the maximum number of doors Karthik can go through is 202, so the answer is \\boxed{202}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{202}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of finding the maximum number of doors Karthik can go through in an 11x11 grid graph without repeating any door and returning to the starting cell, we need to consider the properties of Eulerian circuits and the structure of the grid.\n\n1. **Grid Structure and Degrees**:\n   - An 11x11 grid has 121 vertices.\n   - Corner vertices have degree 2.\n   - Edge non-corner vertices have degree 3.\n   - Internal vertices have degree 4.\n   - There are 4 corners, 36 edge non-corner vertices (each with degree 3), and 81 internal vertices (each with degree 4).\n\n2. **Eulerian Circuit Requirements**:\n   - An Eulerian circuit requires all vertices to have even degrees.\n   - The grid graph has 36 vertices with odd degrees (the edge non-corner vertices).\n\n3. **Edge Removal Calculation**:\n   - To make all vertices even-degree, we need to remove edges such that each odd-degree vertex's degree is reduced by 1.\n   - Each edge removal affects two vertices, so we need to remove \\( \\frac{36}{2} = 18 \\) edges.\n\n4. **Connectivity Consideration**:\n   - The grid is 2-connected, meaning it remains connected after removing any single vertex.\n   - Removing edges in pairs to fix odd degrees while maintaining connectivity is feasible due to the grid's structure.\n\n5. **Total Edges Calculation**:\n   - The total number of edges in an 11x11 grid is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Horizontal edges} = 11 \\times 10 = 110\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Vertical edges} = 11 \\times 10 = 110\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total edges} = 110 + 110 = 220\n     \\]\n\n6. **Resulting Subgraph**:\n   - Removing 18 edges from the total 220 edges result