dge non-corner vertex (on the bottom edge), and (11,2) is a corner. So that vertical edge connects an edge non-corner vertex to a corner.\n\nTherefore, vertical edges in the leftmost and rightmost columns (columns 1 and 11) will have similar behavior as the horizontal edges on the top and bottom rows. The vertical edges in the middle columns (columns 2 through 10) will connect either an edge non-corner vertex (on the top or bottom edge) to an interior vertex, or connect two interior vertices?\n\nWait, let's take a vertical edge in column 5, between rows 3 and 4. Both cells (3,5) and (4,5) are interior vertices (since they are not on the top, bottom, left, or right edges). So that vertical edge connects two interior vertices. Similarly, a vertical edge in column 5 between rows 1 and 2: cell (1,5) is an edge non-corner vertex (on the top edge) and cell (2,5) is an interior vertex. Similarly, between rows 10 and 11 in column 5: cell (10,5) is an edge non-corner (on the bottom edge), and cell (11,5) is a corner. So, in the middle columns (columns 2 through 10), vertical edges can be of two types: those connecting an edge non-corner vertex to an interior vertex, or those connecting two interior vertices. Similarly, horizontal edges in the middle rows (rows 2 through 10) will connect two interior vertices, except for the edges on the very left and right sides of those rows, which might connect to edge non-corner vertices?\n\nWait, actually, in a middle row (say row 5), the horizontal edges in that row would all connect interior vertices except for the ones at the very left and very right ends? Wait, no. Wait, in row 5, which is not the top or bottom row, all the cells in that row are interior except for the first and last cells? Wait, no. Wait, the first cell in row 5, column 1: that cell is on the left edge (since column 1 is the left edge), so it's an edge non-corner vertex. Similarly, the last cell in row 5, column 11, is on the right edge (column 11 is the right edge), so it's an edge non-corner vertex. Therefore, in row 5, the horizontal edges between columns 1-2, 2-3, ..., 10-11: the first horizontal edge (columns 1-2) connects an edge non-corner vertex (cell (5,1)) to an interior vertex (cell (5,2)), since (5,2) is not on the edge. Similarly, the last horizontal edge (columns 10-11) connects an interior vertex (cell (5,10)) to an edge non-corner vertex (cell (5,11)). The internal horizontal edges (columns 2-3, ..., 9-10) connect two interior vertices. Therefore, in a middle row, horizontal edges can connect edge non-corner to interior, or two interior vertices, depending on their position.\n\nThis is getting a bit complicated, but perhaps I can categorize all edges in the grid into three types:\n\n1. Edges connecting two edge non-corner vertices. These would be the internal edges along the top/bottom rows and left/right columns, excluding the corners. For example, in the top row, the horizontal edges between columns 2-3 up to 10-11 (but actually, from 2-3 to 10-11, but (1,11) is a corner, so actually from 2-3 to 10-11 is not quite... Wait, in the top row, the horizontal edges between columns 1-2 (corner to edge non-corner), columns 2-3 (edge non-corner to edge non-corner), ..., columns 10-11 (edge non-corner to corner). So the only horizontal edges in the top row that connect two edge non-corner vertices are from columns 2-3 up to columns 10-11? Wait no, columns 10-11 connects an edge non-corner (10) to a corner (11). So actually, only the horizontal edges from columns 2-3 up through columns 9-10 in the top row connect two edge non-corner vertices? Wait, let me count again.\n\nIn the top row (row 1):\n\n- Between columns 1 and 2: connects corner (1,1) to edge non-corner (1,2).\n\n- Between columns 2 and 3: connects edge non-corner (1,2) to edge non-corner (1,3).\n\n- ...\n\n- Between columns 10 and 11: connects edge non-corner (1,10) to corner (1,11).\n\nTherefore, in the top row, the horizontal edges that connect two edge non-corner vertices are those from columns 2-3 up to columns 9-10? Wait, actually from columns 2-3 to columns 9-10? Wait, columns 2-3, 3-4,...,9-10. That would be 8 edges? Let's see: from column 2 to column 3 is one edge, column 3 to 4 is another, ..., up to column 9 to 10. That is 8 edges? Wait, 10 - 2 = 8? Wait, columns 2 through 10 inclusive is 9 columns, so the number of edges between them is 8? Wait, actually, the number of edges between columns j and j+1 from column 2 to column 10 is 9 - 1 = 8 edges? Hmm, no: columns 2 to 11 have 10 columns, but starting at column 2 to column 11, that's 10 columns, so edges between them would be 9 edges. But in the top row, the first edge is between 1-2 (corner to edge), then edges 2-3 through 10-11 (edge to edge, edge to corner). Wait, actually columns 2-11 have 10 columns, so 9 edges. Out of those 9 edges in the top row:\n\n- The first edge (columns 1-2) is corner to edge non-corner.\n\n- The last edge (columns 10-11) is edge non-corner to corner.\n\n- The middle edges (columns 2-3 up to 9-10) connect edge non-corner to edge non-corner. Wait, columns 2-3: both are edge non-corner? (1,2) and (1,3) are both on the top edge, so yes, they are edge non-corner vertices. Similarly, columns 3-4: both edge non-corner, etc., up to columns 9-10: both edge non-corner. Then columns 10-11: (1,10) is edge non-corner, (1,11) is corner.\n\nTherefore, in the top row, there are 7 horizontal edges that connect two edge non-corner vertices? Wait, columns 2-3 up to columns 9-10: that's from column 2 to column 9, which is 8 columns? Wait, columns 2 through 10: that is 9 columns, so the edges between them are 8 edges. Wait, columns 2-3, 3-4,...,9-10: that's 8 edges? Wait, from 2 to 3 is first, 3 to 4 is second,..., 9 to 10 is eighth. Yes, so 8 edges in the top row that connect two edge non-corner vertices. Similarly, the bottom row (row 11) will have the same: 8 horizontal edges connecting two edge non-corner vertices. Similarly, for vertical edges on the leftmost and rightmost columns.\n\nLooking at the leftmost column (column 1):\n\n- Vertical edges between rows 1-2: connects corner (1,1) to edge non-corner (2,1).\n\n- Between rows 2-3: connects edge non-corner (2,1) to edge non-corner (3,1).\n\n- ...\n\n- Between rows 10-11: connects edge non-corner (10,1) to corner (11,1).\n\nSo, similar to the top row, in column 1 (the leftmost column), the vertical edges between rows 2-3 up to rows 9-10 connect two edge non-corner vertices. That's 8 edges. Similarly, the rightmost column (column 11) will have 8 vertical edges connecting two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nTherefore, for the horizontal edges on the top and bottom rows: each has 8 edges connecting two edge non-corner vertices. Similarly, vertical edges on the left and right columns: each has 8 edges connecting two edge non-corner vertices.\n\nAdditionally, are there any other edges that connect two edge non-corner vertices? For example, consider an edge non-corner vertex on the top edge and an edge non-corner vertex on the right edge? Wait, those are diagonally adjacent, but they aren't connected by an edge. Edges are only between orthogonally adjacent cells. So, an edge non-corner vertex on the top edge and one on the right edge are not adjacent; they are diagonal neighbors, so no edge between them. So, all edges connecting two edge non-corner vertices must be either along the same row (on the top or bottom rows) or same column (on the left or right columns), and not at the corners.\n\nTherefore, total number of edges that connect two edge non-corner vertices:\n\n- Top row: 8 horizontal edges.\n\n- Bottom row: 8 horizontal edges.\n\n- Left column: 8 vertical edges.\n\n- Right column: 8 vertical edges.\n\nTotal: 8*4 = 32 edges.\n\nEach of these 32 edges connects two edge non-corner vertices, both of which have odd degree (3). Therefore, if we remove these edges, each such removal would decrease the degree of two odd-degree vertices by 1, making them even. Since each such edge is between two odd-degree vertices, removing it reduces the total number of odd-degree vertices by 2.\n\nTherefore, if we remove all 32 of these edges, then each removal takes two odd-degree vertices and makes them even. However, we only have 36 odd-degree vertices to begin with. Removing 32 edges would reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by 64, but that can't be right because we only have 36. Wait, hold on, that doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, no. Each edge removal affects two vertices. If we remove an edge between two odd-degree vertices, each of those two vertices loses a degree, so their parity flips from odd to even. Therefore, each such removal reduces the total number of odd-degree vertices by 2. So, starting with 36 odd-degree vertices, if we remove k edges that each connect two odd-degree vertices, then the number of odd-degree vertices becomes 36 - 2k.\n\nWe want to get down to 0 or 2. So, to get to 0, we need 36 - 2k = 0 ⇒ k = 18. To get to 2, we need 36 - 2k = 2 ⇒ k = 17.\n\nTherefore, we only need to remove 18 edges that connect two odd-degree vertices to make all degrees even. However, we have 32 such edges available (the ones along the borders between edge non-corner vertices). So, we have more than enough edges to remove to achieve the required reduction.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges we need to remove is 18, which can be done by removing 18 of the 32 edges that connect two odd-degree vertices. After removing those 18 edges, the graph will have all vertices of even degree (since 36 - 2*18 = 0), so it will have an Eulerian circuit, which traverses every remaining edge exactly once. Therefore, the number of edges in the Eulerian circuit would be total edges minus 18, so 220 - 18 = 202 edges.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can go through is 202. Is that the answer? Wait, but hold on a second. Let me confirm that this is possible. Just because we can remove 18 edges that are between two odd-degree vertices doesn't automatically mean that the remaining graph is connected? Because if we remove edges, the graph might become disconnected, and an Eulerian circuit requires the graph to be connected.\n\nHmm, that's a good point. The grid graph is initially connected, but when we start removing edges, especially edges that are on the boundary, we might disconnect the graph. So, we have to ensure that after removing 18 edges, the graph remains connected. Otherwise, the Eulerian circuit wouldn't exist because it requires the graph to be connected.\n\nSo, perhaps the problem is more complicated because we have to not only remove edges but also ensure connectivity. Hmm, so maybe my previous reasoning is insufficient because it assumes that we can just remove any 18 edges between odd-degree vertices, but in reality, we have to remove them in such a way that the graph remains connected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the grid graph is 2-connected, and removing a limited number of edges won't disconnect it? Let me recall that a 2-connected graph remains connected after removal of any single vertex or edge. But we are removing edges, not vertices. A 2-edge-connected graph remains connected after removal of any single edge. But the grid graph is certainly more than 2-edge-connected. In fact, it's 4-edge-connected? Because you need to remove at least four edges to disconnect a grid graph? Not sure. But even if it's only 2-edge-connected, removing 18 edges might disconnect it, but perhaps not necessarily? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different approach. Instead of trying to make the entire graph Eulerian by removing edges, perhaps we can find a cycle that covers as many edges as possible without repeating any, which is an Eulerian trail but since it's closed, it has to be an Eulerian circuit. But if the graph isn't Eulerian, then the maximum trail would be less. Alternatively, maybe we can find a trail that uses as many edges as possible.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of the grid as a bipartite graph. The grid is a bipartite graph because you can color it like a chessboard, with alternating black and white squares. In such a coloring, every edge connects a black square to a white square. Therefore, any cycle in the grid graph must have even length, since it alternates between black and white. Therefore, the maximum possible cycle length in terms of edges would be even.\n\nBut how does that help us? Well, perhaps in terms of Eulerian trails, since in a bipartite graph, any Eulerian circuit must have an equal number of edges from each partition? Wait, in a bipartite graph with partitions A and B, an Eulerian circuit must have the same number of edges in each partition? Not exactly, but in a bipartite graph, the number of edges from A to B must be equal to the number of edges from B to A, but since it's undirected, it's just the total edges. Wait, actually in a bipartite graph, an Eulerian circuit requires that both partitions have the same number of vertices with odd degree? Wait, no, in any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree must be even, which is already satisfied here (36, which is even). But in a bipartite graph, the degrees on each partition must satisfy certain conditions.\n\nWait, perhaps in a bipartite graph, the necessary and sufficient condition for an Eulerian circuit is that both partitions have all vertices of even degree? Wait, no. Let me recall that in a bipartite graph, the degrees of the vertices in each partition must satisfy that the sum of degrees in partition A equals the sum of degrees in partition B, which is always true in a bipartite graph because every edge goes from A to B. But for an Eulerian circuit, all vertices must have even degree. So, in the bipartite grid graph, we have some vertices with odd degrees (the edge non-corner ones). To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges as before.\n\nBut perhaps another way to think about this is that in order to have an Eulerian circuit in a bipartite graph, you need all vertices to have even degree, which is the same as in any graph. So, going back, the problem is that to make the grid graph Eulerian, we need to remove 18 edges that connect two odd-degree vertices, but we have to make sure that the graph remains connected. Since the grid is so highly connected, maybe removing 18 edges won't disconnect it?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can choose which edges to remove in such a way that the graph remains connected. For example, if we remove edges that are all in one \"layer\" or something, but I need to think more carefully.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of trying to make the entire graph Eulerian, we can consider that the maximum trail will be equal to the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges needed to make the graph Eulerian, but only if those edges can be removed without disconnecting the graph. Since the grid is 4-connected (removing any single vertex doesn't disconnect it, and removing any single edge doesn't disconnect it), perhaps removing 18 edges in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph is possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the minimal number of edges to remove is actually less? Wait, no. The minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is 18, as we calculated. So regardless of connectivity, that's the minimal number. However, the problem is that when you remove edges, you might have to remove more edges if the graph becomes disconnected. Hmm, this is getting a bit too abstract.\n\nWait, maybe there's a different approach. Let me think of the grid as a graph and try to construct such a trail.\n\nSince Karthik has to start and end at the same cell, and can't traverse any door more than once, the path is a closed trail. The question is, what's the longest possible closed trail in the grid graph.\n\nIn graph theory, the maximum length of a closed trail in a graph is equal to the number of edges in the graph minus the size of the smallest edge cut that separates the graph into two components when those edges are removed. But I might be misremembering. Alternatively, perhaps it's related to the concept of the trail cover or something else.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that in any graph, the maximum trail length is bounded by the number of edges, and for a closed trail, it's at most the number of edges if the graph is Eulerian. Since our graph isn't Eulerian, the maximum closed trail is less. The difference between the total edges and the number of edges in the Eulerian circuit (if we could make it Eulerian) is the number of edges we have to remove, which is 18. Therefore, 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nBut to confirm that this is achievable, we need to ensure that after removing 18 edges between two odd-degree vertices, the graph remains connected. Let me try to imagine removing 18 edges in a way that doesn't disconnect the grid.\n\nSuppose we focus on the border edges. Since the grid is a rectangle, maybe we can remove edges along one side without disconnecting the rest. For example, if we remove 18 edges all from the top row, but the top row only has 11 cells, so only 10 horizontal edges. Wait, the top row has 10 horizontal edges, but only 8 of them connect two edge non-corner vertices. Wait, actually, in the top row, as we saw earlier, there are 9 horizontal edges, 8 of which connect two edge non-corner vertices (except the first and last which connect to corners). Wait, actually in the top row, the horizontal edges are 10 in total? Wait, no. Wait, in a row with 11 cells, there are 10 horizontal edges between them. So in the top row, there are 10 horizontal edges. Of those 10, the first connects a corner to an edge non-corner, the last connects an edge non-corner to a corner, and the middle 8 connect edge non-corner to edge non-corner. Therefore, only 8 of the 10 horizontal edges in the top row are between two edge non-corner vertices. Similarly for the bottom row.\n\nSo, if we decide to remove edges only from the internal parts of the bo