 separated by at least one unit in all directions. That is, there must be at least one grid line between them in every direction where they are adjacent? Wait, not necessarily in every direction, but just that they don't touch at all. So, for example, if one rectangle is entirely to the left of another, with a gap between them, then they don't overlap. Similarly, entirely above, below, etc. But if they are adjacent horizontally or vertically with no gap, then they share an edge, which is not allowed. Similarly, if their corners touch, like diagonally adjacent, then they share a vertex, which is also not allowed. Therefore, rectangles must be strictly separated by at least one grid unit in all directions where they are adjacent.\n\nTherefore, in order for two rectangles to not overlap, they must be placed such that there is at least one grid line between them horizontally and/or vertically, but actually, not necessarily in all directions. Wait, actually, if they are placed in such a way that they are in different rows and columns with no adjacency, that's okay. For instance, one rectangle could be in the top-left corner, and another in the bottom-right corner, as long as they don't share any edges or vertices. Wait, but actually, even if they are diagonal, they might still share a vertex if they are placed at the corners. For example, suppose you have a 1x1 rectangle at the top-left corner (covering the square from (1,1) to (1,1)), and another 1x1 rectangle at the bottom-right corner (covering the square from (5,5) to (5,5)). These two rectangles are diagonally opposite and do not share any edges or vertices, because the distance between them is more than one unit in both x and y directions. Wait, actually, in a 5x5 grid of squares, the coordinates of the squares can be considered as from (1,1) to (5,5). The squares are adjacent if they are next to each other. So the top-left square is (1,1), and the bottom-right square is (5,5). The distance between them is 4 squares in both directions, so they are separated by several grid lines, so they don't share any vertices. Wait, actually, the vertices of the grid are at integer coordinates. So the top-left square has vertices at (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2), etc. So the square at (1,1) has vertices at (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2). The square at (5,5) has vertices at (5,5), (5,6), (6,5), (6,6). So these two squares do not share any vertices, edges, or interiors. Therefore, those two rectangles (each being 1x1) are non-overlapping. However, if you have a rectangle that is 1x1 at (1,1) and another 1x1 at (1,2), then those two share an edge between (1,1) and (1,2), so they share an edge, which is not allowed. Similarly, a rectangle at (1,1) and another at (2,2) would share a vertex at (2,2)? Wait, let's see: the square (1,1) has vertices up to (2,2), and the square (2,2) has vertices starting at (2,2). So actually, the square (2,2) shares a vertex with the square (1,1) at (2,2). Therefore, those two squares share a vertex, so if you have rectangles (1x1) at those positions, they would share a vertex, so that is not allowed.\n\nTherefore, in order for two rectangles not to overlap, they must be separated by at least one grid line in every direction where they are adjacent? Not exactly, but in such a way that they don't come within one unit in any direction. Hmm. Maybe another way to think about it is that two rectangles are non-overlapping if and only if the distance between any of their edges is at least one unit? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the best way to compute the number of non-overlapping pairs is to consider that for any two rectangles, they can be placed in the grid such that their projections on the horizontal and vertical axes do not overlap? Wait, maybe not exactly. Let me recall that in one dimension, if you have intervals on a line, two intervals are non-overlapping if they don't share any points. In two dimensions, for rectangles, non-overlapping would mean that the projections on both the x and y axes do not overlap? Wait, actually, no. If the projections on the x-axis overlap and the projections on the y-axis also overlap, then the rectangles might still not overlap if they are arranged in a way that one is shifted appropriately? Wait, no, actually in two dimensions, two rectangles overlap if and only if their projections on the x-axis overlap and their projections on the y-axis overlap. Is that a theorem? Let me recall.\n\nYes, actually, in two dimensions, two rectangles overlap if and only if the projection of their x-intervals overlap and the projection of their y-intervals overlap. Because if their x-projections overlap and y-projections overlap, then there exists some point where the x and y coordinates are in both intervals, so the rectangles overlap at that point. Conversely, if they don't overlap, then either their x-projections don't overlap or their y-projections don't overlap. Therefore, the rectangles are non-overlapping if and only if either their x-projections don't overlap or their y-projections don't overlap.\n\nTherefore, perhaps this is a useful characterization.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute for two rectangles, whether their x-projections overlap and y-projections overlap, then we can determine if they overlap. Therefore, perhaps we can use this to compute the number of non-overlapping pairs.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the problem as follows: for each rectangle R, define its bounding box in terms of its left, right, top, and bottom coordinates. Then, another rectangle S must not overlap with R if either:\n\n1. The left edge of S is to the right of the right edge of R, or\n\n2. The right edge of S is to the left of the left edge of R, or\n\n3. The top edge of S is below the bottom edge of R, or\n\n4. The bottom edge of S is above the top edge of R.\n\nWait, actually, that's equivalent to saying that the rectangles are separated either horizontally or vertically. So in other words, if you have rectangle R with left = L_R, right = R_R, top = T_R, bottom = B_R, and rectangle S with left = L_S, right = R_S, top = T_S, bottom = B_S, then they do not overlap if:\n\nEither (R_R <= L_S) or (R_S <= L_R) or (B_R <= T_S) or (B_S <= T_R).\n\nWait, let me make sure.\n\nWait, the standard definition for non-overlapping rectangles:\n\nTwo rectangles do not overlap if one is to the left of the other, or one is to the right of the other, or one is above the other, or one is below the other. So in terms of coordinates:\n\nLet me use the following notation:\n\nLet rectangle R have:\n\n- left boundary: x_L\n\n- right boundary: x_R (so width = x_R - x_L)\n\n- top boundary: y_T\n\n- bottom boundary: y_B (so height = y_T - y_B)\n\nSimilarly for rectangle S.\n\nThen, the rectangles do not overlap if:\n\nEither (x_R <= x_S) or (x_S <= x_R) or (y_T <= y_S) or (y_S <= y_T)\n\nWait, actually, no. Let me think again.\n\nSuppose rectangle R is to the left of rectangle S. Then, the right edge of R is to the left of the left edge of S. So x_R <= x_S.\n\nSimilarly, if rectangle S is to the left of R, then x_S <= x_R.\n\nSimilarly, if R is above S, then the bottom edge of R is above the top edge of S. Wait, actually, maybe it's better to think in terms of vertical and horizontal separations.\n\nAlternatively, in terms of the standard rectangle separation:\n\nTwo rectangles do not overlap if:\n\n1. The right edge of R is to the left of the left edge of S, or\n\n2. The left edge of R is to the right of the right edge of S, or\n\n3. The top edge of R is below the bottom edge of S, or\n\n4. The bottom edge of R is above the top edge of S.\n\nWait, actually, in terms of coordinates, if we consider the vertical position:\n\nLet me use coordinates where the origin is at the bottom-left corner, but maybe that complicates. Alternatively, let's define:\n\nLet me define for rectangle R:\n\n- Its left boundary is at column L (a vertical line between columns),\n\n- Its right boundary is at column R (another vertical line to the right),\n\nso the width is R - L.\n\nSimilarly, its top boundary is at row T,\n\nand its bottom boundary is at row B,\n\nso the height is T - B.\n\nThen, for rectangle S, with left boundary l, right boundary r, top boundary t, bottom boundary b.\n\nThen, rectangles R and S do not overlap if:\n\nEither\n\n1. R is to the left of S: R < l (the right edge of R is left of the left edge of S),\n\nor\n\n2. S is to the left of R: l < R,\n\nWait, no, actually, if R is to the left of S, then R's right edge is less than S's left edge, so R < l.\n\nSimilarly, if S is to the left of R, then l < R.\n\nWait, but actually, in terms of inequalities:\n\nIf R is to the left of S: R < l,\n\nif S is to the left of R: l < R,\n\nSimilarly, if R is above S: B < b,\n\nif S is above R: b < B.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe in terms of rows and columns, the vertical positions (rows) and horizontal positions (columns):\n\nSuppose we have vertical coordinates (columns) from left to right as 1 to 6,\n\nand horizontal coordinates (rows) from bottom to top as 1 to 6.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I need to fix the coordinate system.\n\nLet me try to set up coordinates such that:\n\n- The vertical lines (columns) are numbered from left to right: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n\n- The horizontal lines (rows) are numbered from bottom to top: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n\nTherefore, a rectangle is defined by choosing two distinct vertical lines (left and right) and two distinct horizontal lines (bottom and top). Let me denote a rectangle by its left (L), right (R), bottom (B), and top (T), where L < R and B < T.\n\nTherefore, the width of the rectangle is (R - L), and the height is (T - B). The rectangle occupies the area from column L to column R-1 (since the vertical lines are between columns), and from row B to row T-1 (since the horizontal lines are between rows). Wait, actually, in terms of the squares: the rectangle from vertical lines L and R spans columns L through R-1, and from horizontal lines B and T spans rows B through T-1. So the rectangle is made up of (R - L) columns and (T - B) rows of squares.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the rectangle's boundaries are such that:\n\n- Left boundary: column L,\n\n- Right boundary: column R,\n\n- Bottom boundary: row B,\n\n- Top boundary: row T.\n\nTherefore, two rectangles R and S do not overlap if:\n\nEither\n\n1. The right boundary of R is to the left of the left boundary of S: R < L_S,\n\nor\n\n2. The left boundary of R is to the right of the right boundary of S: L_R > R_S,\n\nor\n\n3. The top boundary of R is below the bottom boundary of S: T_R < B_S,\n\nor\n\n4. The bottom boundary of R is above the top boundary of S: B_R > T_S.\n\nTherefore, in terms of the coordinates, non-overlapping occurs if any one of those four conditions is true.\n\nTherefore, given that, perhaps we can model the problem as follows: for each rectangle R, compute the number of rectangles S such that either R is to the left of S, S is to the left of R, R is above S, or S is above R. Wait, actually, the four conditions above correspond to R being entirely to the left of S, S entirely to the left of R, R entirely below S, or S entirely below R? Wait, let's see.\n\nWait, actually, condition 1: R is entirely to the left of S: R < L_S,\n\nCondition 2: S is entirely to the left of R: L_R > R_S,\n\nCondition 3: R is entirely below S: T_R < B_S,\n\nCondition 4: S is entirely below R: B_R > T_S.\n\nTherefore, the four conditions correspond to R and S being separated horizontally or vertically, with R on one side and S on the other.\n\nTherefore, the total number of non-overlapping rectangles with respect to R is equal to the number of rectangles entirely to the left of R, plus entirely to the right of R, plus entirely above R, plus entirely below R. But we have to be careful not to double-count rectangles that are both to the left and above, or something? Wait, actually, no, because if a rectangle is both to the left of R and above R, it would be counted in both the \"left\" category and the \"above\" category. Therefore, we might be overcounting. Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, actually, in the four conditions above, each condition is mutually exclusive? Let me see.\n\nSuppose a rectangle S is both to the left of R and above R. Then, it is counted in both the left category and the above category. Therefore, if we just add the four categories, we would be overcounting those rectangles that are in more than one category.\n\nTherefore, actually, the four conditions are not mutually exclusive, so we can't just add them up. Therefore, my initial thought was wrong.\n\nHmm, so perhaps inclusion-exclusion is needed here. That complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to model the grid and for a given rectangle R, compute the number of rectangles that lie entirely in the regions separated by R's boundaries.\n\nSo, if we have rectangle R with left L, right R, bottom B, top T, then the grid is divided into several regions:\n\n1. To the left of R: columns 1 to L-1,\n\n2. To the right of R: columns R+1 to 6,\n\n3. Below R: rows 1 to B-1,\n\n4. Above R: rows T+1 to 6,\n\n5. And the four \"corner\" regions:\n\n   a. To the left and below R: columns 1 to L-1 and rows 1 to B-1,\n\n   b. To the left and above R: columns 1 to L-1 and rows T+1 to 6,\n\n   c. To the right and below R: columns R+1 to 6 and rows 1 to B-1,\n\n   d. To the right and above R: columns R+1 to 6 and rows T+1 to 6,\n\nBut actually, the regions not overlapping with R are all rectangles that are entirely within these regions. However, the regions themselves are independent, so the total number of non-overlapping rectangles with R would be the number of rectangles in the left region plus the number in the right region plus the number in the below region plus the number in the above region minus the overlaps? Wait, no, actually, since the regions are separate, the rectangles in different regions don't interfere. Wait, actually, no, the regions are separate, so rectangles in different regions cannot overlap with R, but they can overlap with each other? Wait, but we are only concerned with rectangles that do not overlap with R. So rectangles that are in different regions can still overlap with each other, but that doesn't matter because we're only counting those that don't overlap with R. Therefore, actually, the total number of rectangles not overlapping with R is equal to the number of rectangles entirely to the left of R plus entirely to the right of R plus entirely below R plus entirely above R, regardless of whether those non-overlapping rectangles overlap with each other or not. Because the problem only requires that the two rectangles (R and the other one) do not overlap; overlapping between other rectangles is irrelevant.\n\nTherefore, actually, since we are counting for each R, the number of rectangles S such that S does not overlap with R, regardless of how S interacts with other rectangles. So in that case, the regions are separate, so the total number is indeed the sum over each of the four regions (left, right, below, above) of the number of rectangles in that region. Wait, but actually, the regions are not entirely separate? For example, the \"left\" region includes columns 1 to L-1, but spans all rows. Similarly, the \"below\" region includes all columns but rows 1 to B-1. So the intersection of left and below regions is the rectangle region columns 1 to L-1 and rows 1 to B-1, which is counted in both the left and below regions. However, when we count the number of rectangles entirely to the left of R, that includes all rectangles in columns 1 to L-1, regardless of their vertical position. Similarly, rectangles entirely below R are those in rows 1 to B-1, regardless of their horizontal position. Therefore, the overlap between left and below regions (the corner region) is included in both counts. Therefore, when we add the left, right, below, and above counts, we are overcounting the rectangles that lie in two or more of those regions. Therefore, we must use inclusion-exclusion here.\n\nTherefore, the correct formula would be:\n\nNumber of non-overlapping rectangles with R = (number in left + number in right + number in below + number in above) - (number in left-and-below + number in left-and-above + number in right-and-below + number in right-and-above) + (number in left-and-below-and-above + ... ) - ... etc.\n\nBut this seems complicated, but maybe manageable if we can compute the sizes of each region.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's simpler to note that the set of rectangles not overlapping with R is exactly the set of rectangles entirely contained within the union of the four regions: left, right, below, above. Since a rectangle not overlapping with R must be entirely within one of these four regions. However, rectangles in different regions can still overlap with each other, but since we are only concerned with not overlapping with R, that's okay.\n\nTherefore, the total number of rectangles not overlapping with R is equal to the number of rectangles entirely in the left region plus the number entirely in the right region plus the number entirely in the below region plus the number entirely in the above region, minus the overlaps between these regions? Wait, no, actually, because the regions overlap, so the total number is actually the sum over each region of the number of rectangles in that region, but since regions overlap, we have overcounted the intersections. Therefore, inclusion-exclusion is needed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of trying to compute the number of rectangles not overlapping with R by breaking into regions, perhaps we can compute the total number of rectangles minus the number of rectangles that do overlap with R.\n\nYes, that might be simpler. Let me consider that.\n\nTotal number of rectangles in the grid: 225.\n\nNumber of rectangles that overlap with R: ?\n\nTherefore, the number of rectangles not overlapping with R = Total rectangles - Number of rectangles overlapping with R.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute for a given R, the number of rectangles that overlap with it, then subtract that from 225, and that gives the number of non-overlapping rectangles with R.\n\nTherefore, perhaps this is a better approach.\n\nSo, let's try that.\n\nGiven a rectangle R with left L, right R, bottom B, top T (using the coordinate system where vertical lines are columns 1-6 and horizontal lines are rows 1-6, with R's left boundary at column L, right boundary at column R, bottom boundary at row B, top boundary at row T). Then, a rectangle S overlaps with R if and only if:\n\nTheir x-projections overlap AND their y-projections overlap.\n\nWhich, as per earlier, is equivalent to:\n\nThe horizontal intervals [L, R] and [l, r] overlap, and the vertical intervals [B, T] and [b, t] overlap.\n\nWhere S has left l, right r, bottom b, top t.\n\nTherefore, the number of rectangles S that overlap with R is equal to the number of rectangles whose horizontal interval overlaps with [L, R] AND whose vertical interval overlaps with [B, T].\n\nTherefore, to compute this, we can compute the number of rectangles with horizontal interval overlapping [L, R], multiplied by the number of rectangles with vertical interval overlapping [B, T], but actually, no, that would overcount because the horizontal and vertical overlaps are not independent? Wait, actually, no, the total number of rectangles that overlap in both x and y is equal to the number of rectangles whose horizontal interval overlaps [L, R] multiplied by the number of rectangles whose vertical interval overlaps [B, T], divided by the total? Wait, no, actually, that is not correct. Because the horizontal and vertical overlaps are independent? Wait, actually, no, the events are independent in the sense that a rectangle can have any horizontal interval and any vertical interval independently, so the total number of rectangles overlapping in both x and y is equal to the number of rectangles with horizontal overlap times the number with vertical overlap divided by total? Wait, no, actually, no.\n\nWait, actually, the total number of rectangles that overlap in the x-direction with R is:\n\nNumber of rectangles whose horizontal interval overlaps [L, R].\n\nSimilarly, the number of rectangles overlapping in the y-direction with R is the number with vertical interval overlapping [B, T].\n\nBut the rectangles that overlap in both x and y are not simply the product of those two numbers, because the horizontal and vertical overlaps are not independent. Wait, actually, they are independent in the sense that a rectangle's horizontal position and vertical position are independent. Therefore, the number of rectangles overlapping in both x and y is equal to the number of rectangles overlapping in x multiplied by the probability that a rectangle overlapping in x also overlaps in y? Hmm, no, actually, perhaps not.\n\nAlternatively, the total number is equal to the number of rectangles with horizontal overlap multiplied by the number of vertical overlaps, but adjusted by the overlap conditions. Wait, perhaps it's better to compute it as follows:\n\nFirst, compute the number of rectangles that have horizontal overlap with R's horizontal interval [L, R]. Let me denote H = [L, R] as the horizontal span of R.\n\nSimilarly, V = [B, T] as the vertical span of R.\n\nThen, the number of rectangles overlapping in the x-direction with H is equal to:\n\nTotal number of rectangles with horizontal interval overlapping H.\n\nSimilarly, the number overlapping in the y-direction with V is the number of rectangles with vertical interval overlapping V.\n\nHowever, the number of rectangles overlapping in both is not simply the product, because the horizontal and vertical intervals are independent.\n\nWait, actually, they are independent in terms of selection. Since a rectangle is defined by its horizontal interval and vertical interval independently. Therefore, the total number of rectangles overlapping in both x and y is equal to the number of horizontal intervals overlapping H multiplied by the number of vertical intervals overlapping V, divided by the total number of rectangles? No, that doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, actually, the total number of rectangles is the number of horizontal intervals times the number of vertical intervals, since each rectangle is a combination of a horizontal interval and a vertical interval. Wait, actually, the total number of rectangles is C(6,2) * C(6,2) = 15*15=225, as we had before.\n\nNow, the number of horizontal intervals that overlap with H: Let me compute that first.\n\nGiven H is [L, R], which is a horizontal interval from column L to column R (since horizontal lines are columns). Wait, actually, in terms of the horizontal lines, the horizontal interval [L, R] corresponds to the columns between vertical lines L and R, so the horizontal interval is from vertical line L to vertical line R, which spans columns L to R-1. Wait, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the horizontal line indices.\n\nWait, actually, in terms of the horizontal lines (which are the vertical grid lines for the columns), the horizontal interval [L, R] (where L and R are vertical line indices) corresponds to columns from L to R-1. But when considering the horizontal projection for a rectangle, the horizontal interval of a rectangle is the interval between its left and right vertical lines. So, for rectangle R, its horizontal interval is [L, R] (the vertical lines), so the length is R - L.\n\nSimilarly, for another rectangle S, its horizontal interval is [l, r], so to overlap with [L, R], the intervals [l, r] and [L, R] must overlap.\n\nThe condition for two intervals [a,b] and [c,d] to overlap is that a < d and c < b.\n\nTherefore, the number of horizontal intervals [l, r] that overlap with [L, R] is equal to:\n\nTotal number of horizontal intervals minus the number of horizontal intervals that are entirely to the left of [L, R] minus the number entirely to the right of [L, R].\n\nSimilarly, the number of horizontal intervals entirely to the left of [L, R] is the number of intervals [l, r] where r < L.\n\nSince the horizontal lines are from 1 to 6, the number of horizontal intervals entirely to the left of L is C(L, 2), because you choose two vertical lines from 1 to L-1, so C(L-1, 2). Wait, actually, the number of intervals entirely to the left of [L, R] would be the number of intervals with right endpoint less than L, i.e., intervals [l, r] with r < L. Since r must be at least l+1, so the number is C(L-1, 2).\n\nSimilarly, the number of horizontal intervals entirely to the right of [L, R] is the number of intervals with left endpoint greater than R, i.e., l > R. The number of such intervals is C(6 - R, 2), since the rightmost vertical line is 6, so the number of intervals starting after R is C((6 - R), 2). Wait, let me confirm:\n\nThe total number of horizontal intervals is C(6, 2) = 15.\n\nNumber of horizontal intervals entirely to the left of L: intervals [l, r] where r < L. Since r must be at least l+1, the number is the same as the number of ways to choose two vertical lines from 1 to L-1, which is C(L-1, 2).\n\nSimilarly, the number of horizontal intervals entirely to the right of R: intervals [l, r] where l > R. These are the intervals starting from vertical line R+1 to 6, so the number is C(6 - R, 2).\n\nTherefore, the number of horizontal intervals overlapping with [L, R] is:\n\nTotal horizontal intervals - left intervals - right intervals = C(6,2) - C(L-1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2).\n\nSimilarly, the number of vertical intervals overlapping with [B, T] (the vertical span of R) is computed analogously.\n\nLet me denote the vertical span of R as [B, T], so the vertical lines from B to T. The vertical intervals are between horizontal lines. So the vertical interval [B, T] corresponds to rows from B to T - 1.\n\nSimilarly, the number of vertical intervals overlapping with [B, T] is equal to:\n\nTotal vertical intervals - vertical intervals entirely below B - vertical intervals entirely above T.\n\nTotal vertical intervals: C(6, 2) = 15.\n\nNumber of vertical intervals entirely below B: intervals [b, t] with t < B. So these are intervals entirely within horizontal lines 1 to B-1, so C(B - 1, 2).\n\nNumber of vertical intervals entirely above T: intervals [b, t] with b > T. These are intervals entirely within horizontal lines T+1 to 6, so the number is C(6 - T, 2).\n\nTherefore, the number of vertical intervals overlapping with [B, T] is:\n\n15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2).\n\nTherefore, the number of rectangles overlapping with R in both x and y directions is equal to the number of horizontal intervals overlapping [L, R] multiplied by the number of vertical intervals overlapping [B, T].\n\nWait, is that correct?\n\nWait, since the horizontal and vertical intervals are independent, the total number of rectangles overlapping in both x and y is indeed (number of horizontal intervals overlapping [L, R]) multiplied by (number of vertical intervals overlapping [B, T]).\n\nBecause for each horizontal interval that overlaps [L, R], and for each vertical interval that overlaps [B, T], the combination forms a rectangle that overlaps in both x and y directions, hence overlapping with R.\n\nTherefore, yes, that should be correct.\n\nTherefore, the number of rectangles overlapping with R is:\n\nOverlap_R = [C(6,2) - C(L - 1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2)] * [C(6,2) - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2)]\n\nTherefore, once we have that, then the number of rectangles not overlapping with R is:\n\nNonOverlap_R = Total rectangles - Overlap_R = 225 - [ (15 - C(L-1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2)) * (15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2)) ]\n\nTherefore, to compute S, the sum over all rectangles R of NonOverlap_R, we need to compute for every rectangle R (defined by L, R, B, T), compute this expression and sum them all up. Then divide by 2 to get N.\n\nBut since there are 225 rectangles, this seems very tedious to do by hand. However, perhaps we can categorize the rectangles by their dimensions and positions, so that we can compute the sum over each category and multiply by the number of rectangles in that category.\n\nGiven that rectangles can be categorized by their width (w = R - L) and height (h = T - B), and their position (i.e., where their top-left corner is, or equivalently, their L, R, B, T coordinates), but since position affects the values of C(L - 1, 2), C(6 - R, 2), C(B - 1, 2), and C(6 - T, 2), it might be complicated. However, perhaps we can find a way to express the sum in terms of the dimensions and the position variables, and see if there's symmetry or a formula that allows us to compute it without enumerating all rectangles.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a smarter way. Let me consider the entire grid and think about the total over all pairs of non-overlapping rectangles.\n\nWait, another approach: Let me consider all possible pairs of rectangles and subtract those that overlap. The total number of pairs is C(225, 2) = 25,200 as computed earlier. Then, the number of overlapping pairs is equal to the total pairs minus the number of non-overlapping pairs. Wait, but actually we need the non-overlapping pairs, so if I can compute the overlapping pairs, then N = Total pairs - Overlapping pairs.\n\nBut how to compute overlapping pairs?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute the number of overlapping pairs by considering for each rectangle R, the number of rectangles that overlap with R, then sum over all R, and then divide by 2 (since each overlapping pair is counted twice). So similar to the earlier approach but for overlapping pairs.\n\nIndeed, the number of overlapping pairs is equal to (1/2) * sum_{R} (number of rectangles overlapping R). Because each overlapping pair (R, S) is counted once in R's count and once in S's count, so total sum is 2 * overlapping_pairs, so overlapping_pairs = (1/2) * sum_{R} Overlap_R.\n\nTherefore, since we have:\n\nNon-overlapping_pairs = Total_pairs - Overlapping_pairs = C(225, 2) - (1/2) * sum_{R} Overlap_R.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute sum_{R} Overlap_R, then I can compute Non-overlapping_pairs.\n\nBut to compute sum_{R} Overlap_R, we need to compute for each rectangle R, the number of rectangles overlapping with it, which we had earlier as:\n\nOverlap_R = [15 - C(L - 1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2)] * [15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2)]\n\nTherefore, the sum over all R of Overlap_R is equal to the sum over all rectangles R of [ (15 - C(L - 1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2)) * (15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2)) ]\n\nHmm, this still seems complicated, but perhaps we can find a way to compute this sum by considering the contributions from each term.\n\nLet me denote for a rectangle R with horizontal boundaries L and R, and vertical boundaries B and T:\n\nLet me define for horizontal boundaries L and R:\n\nLet A = 15 - C(L - 1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2)\n\nSimilarly, for vertical boundaries B and T:\n\nLet C = 15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2)\n\nThen, Overlap_R = A * C.\n\nTherefore, the total sum over all rectangles R is the sum over all possible L, R, B, T (with L < R, B < T) of [A * C].\n\nTherefore, we can separate the sum into the product of two separate sums? Wait, no, because A and C are dependent on different variables (L, R and B, T). Wait, actually, since L, R, B, T are all independent variables (the horizontal boundaries are independent of the vertical boundaries), then the double sum over all rectangles can be written as the product of the sum over all horizontal intervals [L, R] of A(L, R) multiplied by the sum over all vertical intervals [B, T] of C(B, T). Is that correct?\n\nWait, yes! Because each rectangle is uniquely determined by its horizontal interval [L, R] and its vertical interval [B, T], and since the horizontal and vertical intervals are independent, the total sum over all rectangles of A(L, R)*C(B, T) is equal to [sum_{horizontal intervals [L,R]} A(L,R)] multiplied by [sum_{vertical intervals [B,T]} C(B,T)].\n\nBecause for each horizontal interval [L,R], we have a term A(L,R), and for each vertical interval [B,T], we have a term C(B,T), and since the rectangle is the combination of a horizontal and vertical interval, the total sum is the product of the two sums.\n\nYes! That seems like a crucial insight. Therefore, the total sum over all rectangles R of Overlap_R is equal to [sum_{horizontal intervals [L,R]} A(L,R)] multiplied by [sum_{vertical intervals [B,T]} C(B,T)].\n\nTherefore, we can compute the horizontal sum and vertical sum separately, then multiply them together.\n\nLet me verify this with a simple example. Suppose we have a 2x2 grid of squares (so 3x3 grid lines). Then total rectangles would be C(3,2)*C(3,2) = 3*3=9. Suppose we compute sum_{R} Overlap_R. Let me list all rectangles in a 2x2 grid and compute Overlap_R for each, then sum them up, and see if it equals [sum_{horizontal} A] * [sum_{vertical} C].\n\nFirst, let me define for a 2x2 grid (so n=2 squares per side, so 3 grid lines). Let me denote the horizontal lines as 1,2,3 (top to bottom), and vertical lines as 1,2,3 (left to right). A rectangle is defined by L, R (horizontal lines) and B, T (vertical lines), with L < R and B < T.\n\nFirst, compute for each horizontal interval [L, R], the value A(L,R) = 15 - C(L-1,2) - C(6 - R, 2). Wait, but in the 2x2 grid, n=2 squares, so the grid lines are 3, so the formulas need to be adjusted for n=2.\n\nWait, actually, in the original problem, it's a 5x5 grid of squares, so grid lines are 6 in each direction. In my test case, I need to use a similar setup. Let me instead use a 1x1 grid of squares (so 2x2 grid lines). Let me see.\n\nWait, perhaps my test case is getting too convoluted. Let me just proceed with the original problem's parameters, assuming that the formulas hold.\n\nTherefore, proceeding with the original 5x5 grid (6x6 grid lines):\n\nFirst, compute the sum over all horizontal intervals [L, R] of A(L,R) where A(L,R) = 15 - C(L - 1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2).\n\nSimilarly, compute the sum over all vertical intervals [B, T] of C(B,T) where C(B,T) = 15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2).\n\nTherefore, let me first compute the horizontal sum S_h = sum_{L=1 to 5} sum_{R=L+1 to 6} [15 - C(L - 1, 2) - C(6 - R, 2)].\n\nSimilarly, the vertical sum S_v = sum_{B=1 to 5} sum_{T=B+1 to 6} [15 - C(B - 1, 2) - C(6 - T, 2)].\n\nNote that the vertical sum is exactly the same as the horizontal sum, since the grid is square (5x5 squares). Therefore, S_h = S_v, so the total sum over all rectangles of Overlap_R is S_h * S_h = (S_h)^2.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute S_h, then the total overlapping pairs is (S_h)^2 / 2, and then the non-overlapping pairs is C(225, 2) - (S_h)^2 / 2.\n\nWait, no, hold on: the total sum over all rectangles of Overlap_R is S_h * S_v = (S_h)^2, and then overlapping_pairs = (S_h)^2 / 2. Therefore, non-overlapping_pairs = C(225, 2) - (S_h)^2 / 2.\n\nTherefore, the key is to compute S_h.\n\nSo let's compute S_h.\n\nFirst, let me note that for horizontal intervals [L, R], where L ranges from 1 to 5, and R from L+1 to 6.\n\nFirst, let me precompute C(k, 2) for k from 0 to 5, since in the expressions we have terms like C(L - 1, 2) and C(6 - R, 2). Since L can be from 1 to 5, so L-1 can be from 0 to 4, and 6 - R can be from 1 to 5 (since R is from L+1 to 6, so when R=6, 6 - R = 0, but wait, actually when R=6, then 6 - R = 0, so C(0, 2) = 0.\n\nWait, let me make a table of C(k, 2) for k from 0 to 6:\n\nC(0,2) = 0\n\nC(1,2) = 0\n\nC(2,2) = 1\n\nC(3,2) = 3\n\nC(4,2) = 6\n\nC(5,2) = 10\n\nC(6,2) = 15\n\nBut in our case, for a horizontal interval [L, R], with L from 1 to 5 and R from L+1 to 6, the terms we need are:\n\nFor each [L, R]:\n\nTerm1 = C(L - 1, 2)\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - R, 2)\n\nTherefore, A(L,R) = 15 - Term1 - Term2\n\nTherefore, to compute S_h, we need to iterate over all possible L and R, compute A(L,R) for each, then sum them all.\n\nGiven that there are 15 horizontal intervals (since C(6,2) = 15), this is manageable with some organization.\n\nLet me proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, list all possible horizontal intervals [L, R], compute for each the value of Term1 = C(L - 1, 2) and Term2 = C(6 - R, 2), then compute A(L,R) = 15 - Term1 - Term2, then accumulate the sum.\n\nLet me create a table for L from 1 to 5 and R from L+1 to 6.\n\nStarting with L = 1:\n\nL = 1:\n\nR can be 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n\nFor each R:\n\nR = 2:\n\nTerm1 = C(1 - 1, 2) = C(0,2) = 0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - 2, 2) = C(4,2) = 6\n\nA = 15 - 0 - 6 = 9\n\nR = 3:\n\nTerm1 = C(0,2) = 0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - 3, 2) = C(3,2) = 3\n\nA = 15 - 0 - 3 = 12\n\nR = 4:\n\nTerm1 = 0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - 4, 2) = C(2,2) = 1\n\nA = 15 - 0 - 1 = 14\n\nR = 5:\n\nTerm1 = 0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - 5, 2) = C(1,2) = 0\n\nA = 15 - 0 - 0 = 15\n\nR = 6:\n\nTerm1 = C(0,2) = 0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - 6, 2) = C(0,2) = 0\n\nA = 15 - 0 - 0 = 15\n\nSo for L=1, the A values are: 9, 12, 14, 15, 15. Summing these: 9 + 12 = 21; 21 +14=35; 35+15=50; 50+15=65. So total for L=1 is 65.\n\nNext, L=2:\n\nL=2:\n\nR can be 3,4,5,6.\n\nR=3:\n\nTerm1 = C(2 - 1, 2) = C(1,2)=0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 - 3,2)=C(3,2)=3\n\nA = 15 - 0 - 3 = 12\n\nR=4:\n\nTerm1 = C(1,2)=0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 -4,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nA=15 -0 -1=14\n\nR=5:\n\nTerm1 = C(1,2)=0\n\nTerm2 = C(6 -5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nA=15 -0 -0=15\n\nR=6:\n\nTerm1 = C(1,2)=0\n\nTerm2 = C(0,2)=0\n\nA=15 -0 -0=15\n\nSo A values for L=2: 12,14,15,15. Sum: 12+14=26; 26+15=41; 41+15=56. Total for L=2: 56.\n\nL=3:\n\nR can be 4,5,6.\n\nR=4:\n\nTerm1 = C(3-1,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nTerm2 = C(6 -4,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nA=15 -1 -1=13\n\nR=5:\n\nTerm1 = C(2,2)=1\n\nTerm2 = C(6 -5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nA=15 -1 -0=14\n\nR=6:\n\nTerm1 = C(2,2)=1\n\nTerm2 = C(0,2)=0\n\nA=15 -1 -0=14\n\nThus, A values: 13,14,14. Sum: 13+14=27; 27+14=41. Total for L=3: 41.\n\nL=4:\n\nR can be 5,6.\n\nR=5:\n\nTerm1 = C(4-1,2)=C(3,2)=3\n\nTerm2 = C(6-5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nA=15 -3 -0=12\n\nR=6:\n\nTerm1 = C(3,2)=3\n\nTerm2 = C(0,2)=0\n\nA=15 -3 -0=12\n\nThus, A values:12,12. Sum: 24. Total for L=4:24.\n\nL=5:\n\nR can only be 6.\n\nR=6:\n\nTerm1 = C(5-1,2)=C(4,2)=6\n\nTerm2 = C(6-6,2)=0\n\nA=15 -6 -0=9\n\nThus, A=9. Total for L=5:9.\n\nNow, summing up all the S_h contributions from each L:\n\nL=1: 65\n\nL=2: 56\n\nL=3: 41\n\nL=4: 24\n\nL=5: 9\n\nTotal S_h = 65 + 56 + 41 + 24 + 9.\n\nLet me compute step by step:\n\n65 + 56 = 121\n\n121 + 41 = 162\n\n162 + 24 = 186\n\n186 + 9 = 195\n\nTherefore, S_h = 195.\n\nSince the grid is square, S_v = S_h = 195.\n\nTherefore, the total sum over all rectangles R of Overlap_R is S_h * S_v = 195 * 195.\n\nCompute that:\n\n195 * 195. Let me compute 200*200 = 40,000, subtract 5*200 + 5*200 - 5*5, wait maybe better to compute directly:\n\n195 * 195 = (200 - 5)^2 = 200^2 - 2*200*5 + 5^2 = 40,000 - 2,000 + 25 = 38,025.\n\nAlternatively, 195 * 195:\n\nFirst, 200*195 = 39,000\n\nThen subtract 5*195 = 975\n\nSo 39,000 - 975 = 38,025. Yes.\n\nTherefore, the total sum over all rectangles R of Overlap_R is 38,025.\n\nTherefore, the number of overlapping pairs is (38,025)/2 = 19,012.5? Wait, hold on, that can't be. Wait, the number of overlapping pairs must be an integer, but 38,025 divided by 2 is 19,012.5, which is not an integer. That suggests an error in my reasoning.\n\nWait, that's a problem. Where did I go wrong?\n\nLet me check the steps again.\n\nFirst, I had:\n\nsum_{R} Overlap_R = S_h * S_v = 195 * 195 = 38,025.\n\nThen, overlapping_pairs = (sum_{R} Overlap_R) / 2 = 38,025 / 2 = 19,012.5.\n\nBut the number of overlapping pairs must be an integer. Therefore, this suggests an error in my calculation.\n\nHmm. Let me check my calculation of S_h again.\n\nLet me recalculate S_h step by step.\n\nFirst, for L=1:\n\nR=2: A=9\n\nR=3:12\n\nR=4:14\n\nR=5:15\n\nR=6:15\n\nTotal for L=1: 9 +12=21; 21+14=35; 35+15=50; 50+15=65. That seems correct.\n\nL=2:\n\nR=3:12\n\nR=4:14\n\nR=5:15\n\nR=6:15\n\nTotal: 12+14=26; 26+15=41; 41+15=56. Correct.\n\nL=3:\n\nR=4:13\n\nR=5:14\n\nR=6:14\n\nTotal:13+14=27; 27+14=41. Correct.\n\nL=4:\n\nR=5:12\n\nR=6:12\n\nTotal:24. Correct.\n\nL=5:\n\nR=6:9. Correct.\n\nSo total S_h = 65 +56 +41 +24 +9.\n\n65 +56 = 121\n\n121 +41 = 162\n\n162 +24 = 186\n\n186 +9 = 195. That seems correct.\n\nHmm. So 195*195 is indeed 38,025, which is odd, so dividing by 2 gives a half-integer, which is impossible.\n\nTherefore, my assumption that sum_{R} Overlap_R = S_h * S_v must be wrong.\n\nWait, let me think again about the logic.\n\nI claimed that the total over all rectangles R of Overlap_R is equal to [sum_{horizontal intervals} A(L,R)] * [sum_{vertical intervals} C(B,T)].\n\nBut let me think of a simple case where the grid is 1x1 square (so 2x2 grid lines). Let's do a small example manually and see.\n\nLet me take a 1x1 grid of squares (so 2x2 grid lines). Total rectangles: C(2,2)*C(2,2)=1*1=1. Wait, only one rectangle, the entire grid.\n\nTherefore, in this case, sum_{R} Overlap_R would be Overlap of that single rectangle with itself? But since we are considering pairs of distinct rectangles, but in this case there's only one rectangle, so the sum over R of Overlap_R would be Overlap_R for that one rectangle, which is the number of rectangles overlapping with it, which is 1 (itself). But since we are considering pairs of distinct rectangles, the overlapping pairs would be zero, but the formula would give (sum Overlap_R)/2 = (1)/2 = 0.5, which is also wrong.\n\nHmm, so clearly the formula is invalid for small grids. Therefore, my earlier reasoning must have a flaw.\n\nWait, in the problem statement, when we consider pairs of rectangles, we are considering unordered pairs of distinct rectangles. Therefore, when we compute sum_{R} Overlap_R, we are counting for each rectangle R, the number of rectangles (including itself?) that overlap with R. Wait, in my earlier definition, Overlap_R includes all rectangles that overlap with R, including R itself?\n\nWait, hold on, in the problem, we are to pick two distinct rectangles, so when we compute the number of overlapping pairs, we should not include the case where the two rectangles are the same. Therefore, in the definition of Overlap_R, should we exclude R itself?\n\nYes, that must be the mistake.\n\nAh! Yes, absolutely. When I defined Overlap_R as the number of rectangles overlapping with R, that includes R itself. But in the context of counting overlapping pairs, we only want pairs of distinct rectangles. Therefore, for each R, the number of rectangles overlapping with R (including R) is Overlap_R, so the number of distinct rectangles overlapping with R (excluding R itself) is Overlap_R - 1.\n\nTherefore, the total sum over all R of (Overlap_R - 1) would be the total number of ordered pairs (R, S) where R ≠ S and they overlap. Therefore, the number of unordered overlapping pairs is then [sum_{R} (Overlap_R - 1)] / 2.\n\nTherefore, correcting my earlier mistake:\n\nTotal overlapping_pairs = [sum_{R} (Overlap_R - 1)] / 2 = [sum_{R} Overlap_R - sum_{R} 1] / 2 = [38,025 - 225] / 2 = (38,025 - 225)/2 = (37,800)/2 = 18,900.\n\nTherefore, overlapping_pairs = 18,900.\n\nTherefore, non-overlapping_pairs = Total_pairs - overlapping_pairs = C(225, 2) - 18,900.\n\nCompute C(225, 2):\n\n225 * 224 / 2 = (225 * 112) = let's compute 200*112=22,400 and 25*112=2,800, so total is 22,400 + 2,800 = 25,200. So Total_pairs = 25,200.\n\nTherefore, non-overlapping_pairs = 25,200 - 18,900 = 6,300.\n\nWait, so is the answer 6,300?\n\nBut let me verify this with the small grid example to see if the formula now works.\n\nTake the 1x1 grid (2x2 grid lines). Total rectangles: 1.\n\nTotal_pairs: C(1,2) = 0, since you can't choose two distinct rectangles.\n\nSum_{R} Overlap_R: For the only rectangle R, Overlap_R is the number of rectangles overlapping with R, which includes itself. Since there's only one rectangle, Overlap_R = 1.\n\nTherefore, sum_{R} Overlap_R = 1.\n\nThen, sum_{R} (Overlap_R - 1) = 1 - 1 = 0.\n\nTherefore, overlapping_pairs = 0 / 2 = 0. Which is correct, since there are no pairs to choose.\n\nnon-overlapping_pairs = 0 - 0 = 0. Which is also correct, since there are no pairs.\n\nAnother test case: 2x2 grid of squares (3x3 grid lines). Total rectangles: C(3,2)*C(3,2)= 3*3=9.\n\nCompute sum_{R} Overlap_R:\n\nFirst, for each rectangle in the 2x2 grid (which has 9 rectangles), compute Overlap_R (number of rectangles overlapping with R, including itself).\n\nThen, sum them all, subtract 9 (since each Overlap_R includes itself), then divide by 2 to get overlapping_pairs.\n\nThen non-overlapping_pairs = C(9,2) - overlapping_pairs.\n\nLet me try this.\n\nFirst, in a 2x2 grid (3x3 grid lines), let's list all rectangles and compute Overlap_R for each.\n\nFirst, label the grid lines horizontally as 1 (top), 2, 3 (bottom), and vertically as 1 (left), 2, 3 (right).\n\nEach rectangle is defined by choosing two horizontal lines (top and bottom) and two vertical lines (left and right). Let me list all 9 rectangles:\n\n1. Top-left 1x1: horizontal [1,2], vertical [1,2]\n\n2. Top-middle 1x1: horizontal [1,2], vertical [2,3]\n\n3. Top-right 1x1: horizontal [1,2], vertical [3,4] Wait, no, vertical lines only go up to 3. Wait, vertical lines are 1,2,3. So vertical intervals can be [1,2], [2,3], or [1,3].\n\nSimilarly, horizontal intervals are [1,2], [2,3], or [1,3].\n\nTherefore, the 9 rectangles are:\n\n1. [H=1-2, V=1-2] (the top-left 1x1 square)\n\n2. [H=1-2, V=1-3] (the top row, 1x2 rectangle)\n\n3. [H=1-2, V=2-3] (the middle column top, 1x1)\n\n4. [H=1-2, V=2-3] Wait, no, need to list all combinations:\n\nWait, better to systematically list them:\n\nHorizontal intervals (H) and vertical intervals (V):\n\nHorizontal intervals:\n\nH1: [1,2]\n\nH2: [2,3]\n\nH3: [1,3]\n\nVertical intervals:\n\nV1: [1,2]\n\nV2: [2,3]\n\nV3: [1,3]\n\nThus, all combinations:\n\n1. H1 & V1: [1,2]x[1,2] (1x1 top-left)\n\n2. H1 & V2: [1,2]x[2,3] (1x1 top-middle)\n\n3. H1 & V3: [1,2]x[1,3] (1x2 top row)\n\n4. H2 & V1: [2,3]x[1,2] (1x1 middle-left)\n\n5. H2 & V2: [2,3]x[2,3] (1x1 middle-center)\n\n6. H2 & V3: [2,3]x[1,3] (1x2 middle row)\n\n7. H3 & V1: [1,3]x[1,2] (2x1 left column)\n\n8. H3 & V2: [1,3]x[2,3] (2x1 middle column)\n\n9. H3 & V3: [1,3]x[1,3] (2x2 entire grid)\n\nNow, for each rectangle, compute Overlap_R, which is the number of rectangles that overlap with it (including itself).\n\nStarting with rectangle 1: [1,2]x[1,2]\n\nWhich rectangles overlap with it?\n\nAny rectangle that has horizontal overlap with [1,2] and vertical overlap with [1,2].\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [1,2]: all H intervals except those entirely to the right of 2 (i.e., H3). So H1, H2, H3? Wait:\n\nWait, horizontal interval [1,2] is from H=1 to H=2. Wait, the horizontal line indices are 1,2,3. So the horizontal interval [1,2] is between lines 1 and 2, so spans columns 1 to 1 (since vertical lines are columns). Wait, no, in terms of horizontal lines, the horizontal interval [H_start, H_end] corresponds to the rows between those lines.\n\nActually, in terms of the overlap condition for horizontal intervals:\n\nA horizontal interval [h1, h2] overlaps with [1,2] if h1 < 2 and h2 > 1.\n\nSo for horizontal intervals:\n\nH1: [1,2]: overlaps with [1,2] (yes)\n\nH2: [2,3]: overlaps? The overlap condition is h1 < 2 and h2 >1. For H2: h1=2, which is not < 2, so no overlap? Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, the overlap condition between two intervals [a,b] and [c,d] is that a < d and c < b.\n\nTherefore, for horizontal interval [1,2] and [2,3]:\n\na=1, b=2; c=2, d=3\n\nCheck if 1 < 3 (yes) and 2 < 2 (no). Therefore, they do not overlap. So H2 does not overlap with H1.\n\nSimilarly, H3: [1,3], which overlaps with [1,2] because 1 < 2 and 3 >1, so yes.\n\nTherefore, horizontal intervals overlapping [1,2] are H1 and H3.\n\nSimilarly for vertical intervals overlapping [1,2]:\n\nVertical intervals V1: [1,2] overlaps with [1,2] (yes)\n\nV2: [2,3]: check if 1 < 3 and 2 < 2? No, since 2 < 2 is false. So V2 does not overlap with [1,2].\n\nV3: [1,3]: overlaps because 1 < 2 and 3 >1, yes.\n\nTherefore vertical intervals overlapping [1,2] are V1 and V3.\n\nTherefore, rectangles overlapping with rectangle 1 are those with H in {H1, H3} and V in {V1, V3}.\n\nThus, the number of such rectangles is 2 (H choices) * 2 (V choices) = 4. However, this includes rectangle 1 itself (H1 & V1), so Overlap_R for rectangle 1 is 4.\n\nWait, but let me list them explicitly:\n\nThe rectangles that overlap with rectangle 1 ([1,2]x[1,2]) are:\n\n- H1 & V1: itself\n\n- H1 & V3: [1,2]x[1,3] (covers the top row, which includes rectangle1)\n\n- H3 & V1: [1,3]x[1,2] (covers the left column, which includes rectangle1)\n\n- H3 & V3: [1,3]x[1,3] (the entire grid, which includes rectangle1)\n\nTherefore, indeed 4 rectangles. So Overlap_R for rectangle1 is 4.\n\nSimilarly, proceeding to rectangle2: [1,2]x[2,3]\n\nThis is the top-middle 1x1 square.\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [1,2]: H1 and H3 (as before)\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [2,3]:\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [2,3] are those where V_start < 3 and V_end > 2.\n\nVertical intervals:\n\nV1: [1,2] does not overlap with [2,3] because 1 < 3 is true, but 2 < 2 is false. So no.\n\nV2: [2,3] overlaps with [2,3]: yes.\n\nV3: [1,3] overlaps with [2,3]: yes (since 1 < 3 and 3 > 2).\n\nThus vertical intervals overlapping [2,3] are V2 and V3.\n\nTherefore, rectangles overlapping with rectangle2 are H in {H1, H3} and V in {V2, V3}:\n\nH1 & V2: rectangle2 itself\n\nH1 & V3: rectangle3\n\nH3 & V2: rectangle6\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nTherefore, 4 rectangles. Thus Overlap_R for rectangle2 is 4.\n\nRectangle3: [1,2]x[1,3] (the top row, 1x2 rectangle)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [1,2]: H1 and H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [1,3]: all vertical intervals, since [1,3] overlaps with any vertical interval because any vertical interval is within [1,3] or spans it.\n\nVertical intervals are V1, V2, V3. All overlap with [1,3].\n\nTherefore, vertical intervals overlapping: all 3.\n\nThus rectangles overlapping with rectangle3 are H in {H1, H3} and V in {V1, V2, V3}:\n\nH1 & V1: rectangle1\n\nH1 & V2: rectangle2\n\nH1 & V3: rectangle3\n\nH3 & V1: rectangle7\n\nH3 & V2: rectangle8\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nTotal of 6 rectangles. Therefore Overlap_R for rectangle3 is 6.\n\nRectangle4: [2,3]x[1,2] (middle-left 1x1)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [2,3]: H2 and H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [1,2]: V1 and V3 (as before)\n\nThus, rectangles overlapping with rectangle4 are H in {H2, H3} and V in {V1, V3}:\n\nH2 & V1: rectangle4\n\nH2 & V3: rectangle6\n\nH3 & V1: rectangle7\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nWait, let's list them:\n\nH2 & V1: [2,3]x[1,2] → rectangle4\n\nH2 & V3: [2,3]x[1,3] → rectangle6\n\nH3 & V1: [1,3]x[1,2] → rectangle7\n\nH3 & V3: [1,3]x[1,3] → rectangle9\n\nThus, 4 rectangles. So Overlap_R=4.\n\nRectangle5: [2,3]x[2,3] (middle-center 1x1)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [2,3]: H2 and H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [2,3]: V2 and V3\n\nThus rectangles overlapping with rectangle5 are H in {H2, H3} and V in {V2, V3}:\n\nH2 & V2: rectangle5\n\nH2 & V3: rectangle6\n\nH3 & V2: rectangle8\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nTotal of 4 rectangles. So Overlap_R=4.\n\nRectangle6: [2,3]x[1,3] (middle row, 1x2)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [2,3]: H2 and H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [1,3]: all V1, V2, V3.\n\nThus, rectangles overlapping with rectangle6 are H in {H2, H3} and V in {V1, V2, V3}:\n\nH2 & V1: rectangle4\n\nH2 & V2: rectangle5\n\nH2 & V3: rectangle6\n\nH3 & V1: rectangle7\n\nH3 & V2: rectangle8\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nTotal of 6 rectangles. So Overlap_R=6.\n\nRectangle7: [1,3]x[1,2] (left column, 2x1)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [1,3]: all H1, H2, H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [1,2]: V1 and V3\n\nThus, rectangles overlapping with rectangle7 are H in {H1, H2, H3} and V in {V1, V3}:\n\nH1 & V1: rectangle1\n\nH1 & V3: rectangle3\n\nH2 & V1: rectangle4\n\nH2 & V3: rectangle6\n\nH3 & V1: rectangle7\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nTotal of 6 rectangles. So Overlap_R=6.\n\nRectangle8: [1,3]x[2,3] (middle column, 2x1)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [1,3]: all H1, H2, H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [2,3]: V2 and V3\n\nThus, rectangles overlapping with rectangle8 are H in {H1, H2, H3} and V in {V2, V3}:\n\nH1 & V2: rectangle2\n\nH1 & V3: rectangle3\n\nH2 & V2: rectangle5\n\nH2 & V3: rectangle6\n\nH3 & V2: rectangle8\n\nH3 & V3: rectangle9\n\nTotal of 6 rectangles. So Overlap_R=6.\n\nRectangle9: [1,3]x[1,3] (the entire grid)\n\nHorizontal intervals overlapping [1,3]: all H1, H2, H3\n\nVertical intervals overlapping [1,3]: all V1, V2, V3\n\nThus, all 9 rectangles overlap with rectangle9. Therefore, Overlap_R=9.\n\nNow, let's list all Overlap_R values:\n\n1:4\n\n2:4\n\n3:6\n\n4:4\n\n5:4\n\n6:6\n\n7:6\n\n8:6\n\n9:9\n\nNow, sum these up:\n\nFirst, list them:\n\n4, 4, 6, 4, 4, 6, 6, 6, 9.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 4 (first term)\n\n4 + 4 = 8\n\n8 + 6 = 14\n\n14 + 4 = 18\n\n18 + 4 = 22\n\n22 + 6 = 28\n\n28 + 6 = 34\n\n34 + 6 = 40\n\n40 + 9 = 49.\n\nSo total sum_{R} Overlap_R = 49.\n\nNow, applying the corrected formula:\n\noverlapping_pairs = [sum_{R} (Overlap_R - 1)] / 2 = [49 - 9]/2 = (40)/2 = 20.\n\nTherefore, overlapping_pairs = 20.\n\nTotal_pairs = C(9,2) = 36.\n\nnon-overlapping_pairs = 36 - 20 = 16.\n\nLet me manually verify this.\n\nIn a 2x2 grid of squares (so 3x3 grid lines), how many pairs of non-overlapping rectangles are there?\n\nFirst, list all rectangles and see which pairs don't overlap.\n\nThere are 9 rectangles. Let me list them again with labels 1-9 as above.\n\nWe need to count all unordered pairs {R, S} where R ≠ S and they don't overlap.\n\nAlternatively, since we have overlapping_pairs = 20, then non-overlapping_pairs = 36 - 20 = 16.\n\nLet me see if that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, let me count manually.\n\nFirst, the entire grid (rectangle9) overlaps with everyone else, so any pair involving rectangle9 is overlapping. There are 8 such pairs (9-1=8). So those 8 pairs are overlapping.\n\nNow, the other rectangles (1-8) may or may not overlap with each other.\n\nTotal overlapping_pairs = 20, so the remaining overlapping_pairs (20 - 8) = 12 must come from pairs among the first 8 rectangles.\n\nLet me see:\n\nLooking at rectangles 1-8:\n\nRectangle1: top-left 1x1\n\nRectangle2: top-middle 1x1\n\nRectangle3: top row 1x2\n\nRectangle4: middle-left 1x1\n\nRectangle5: middle-center 1x1\n\nRectangle6: middle row 1x2\n\nRectangle7: left column 2x1\n\nRectangle8: middle column 2x1\n\nNow, let's see overlaps among these:\n\nFirst, rectangle1 (1,1) overlaps with:\n\n- rectangle3 (top row), since it's part of it\n\n- rectangle7 (left column), since it's part of it\n\n- rectangle9 (entire grid)\n\nBut among the first 8, overlaps with rectangle3 and rectangle7.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle2 (top-middle) overlaps with rectangle3 (top row) and rectangle6 (middle row? Wait no, rectangle2 is top-middle 1x1, so it's in the top row, middle column. It overlaps with rectangle3 (top row spans columns 1-2, so rectangle2 is in column2, so yes, part of rectangle3). Also, rectangle2 overlaps with rectangle5 (middle-center) if they are adjacent? Wait rectangle2 is at [1,2]x[2,3], and rectangle5 is [2,3]x[2,3], so they share a corner at (2,3) but not an edge or interior. Wait, actually, rectangle2 is at row1-2, column2-3, and rectangle5 is row2-3, column2-3. So they share the point (2,3), which is a corner. Since overlapping at a vertex is not allowed, they do overlap (since the problem says no overlapping at vertices). Therefore, rectangle2 and rectangle5 share a vertex, so they overlap. Therefore, rectangle2 overlaps with rectangle5.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\nRectangle2: horizontal [1,2], vertical [2,3]\n\nRectangle5: horizontal [2,3], vertical [2,3]\n\nTheir horizontal intervals [1,2] and [2,3] overlap? The horizontal overlap is at point 2, but since intervals are [1,2] and [2,3], they only meet at the point 2, which is not an overlapping interval. Wait, the horizontal overlap condition: intervals [a,b] and [c,d] overlap if a < d and c < b.\n\nFor rectangle2's horizontal [1,2] and rectangle5's horizontal [2,3]:\n\na=1, b=2; c=2, d=3.\n\nCheck if 1 < 3 (yes) and 2 < 2 (no). Therefore, they do NOT overlap in the horizontal direction. Therefore, their horizontal intervals do not overlap, so the rectangles do not overlap. Wait, but their vertical intervals do overlap: vertical [2,3] and [2,3] overlap entirely.\n\nBut since their horizontal intervals do not overlap, the rectangles do not overlap. Wait, so rectangle2 and rectangle5 do NOT overlap?\n\nWait, rectangle2 is in rows 1-2, columns 2-3.\n\nRectangle5 is in rows 2-3, columns 2-3.\n\nTheir horizontal intervals are [1,2] and [2,3], which only meet at the line y=2, but since they don't overlap in the horizontal direction (the intervals don't have an overlap in the lines), their rectangles do not share any interior or edge points. The only common point is the corner at (2,3), but since that's a vertex, which is not allowed. Wait, but according to the problem statement, they cannot overlap at vertices either.\n\nWait, the problem states: \"the rectangles do not overlap at their interiors, edges, or vertices.\" So even sharing a vertex is not allowed. Therefore, if two rectangles share even a single vertex, they are considered overlapping and must be excluded.\n\nIn the case of rectangle2 and rectangle5:\n\nRectangle2 has its bottom-right corner at (2,3) [assuming coordinates where the bottom-left of the grid is (1,1), and increasing to the right and up]. Wait, actually, let me define coordinates precisely.\n\nLet me define the grid with rows numbered from bottom to top 1 to 3, and columns left to right 1 to 3.\n\nRectangle2 is defined by horizontal lines 1-2 (rows 1 to 2) and vertical lines 2-3 (columns 2 to 3). So its corners are at (1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3).\n\nRectangle5 is defined by horizontal lines 2-3 (rows 2 to 3) and vertical lines 2-3 (columns 2 to 3). Its corners are at (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3).\n\nTherefore, the point (2,3) is a corner of both rectangles. Therefore, they share a vertex, hence they overlap, so they cannot be paired.\n\nWait, but according to the overlap condition earlier, their horizontal intervals [1,2] and [2,3] do not overlap (since they only meet at 2, which is the endpoint), so their horizontal projections do not overlap, and their vertical projections do overlap. Therefore, according to the overlap condition (which requires both x and y projections to overlap), they do NOT overlap? Wait, but they share a vertex.\n\nHmm, this is a contradiction. Let me recall the earlier theorem: two rectangles overlap if and only if their projections on both axes overlap.\n\nIn this case, the horizontal projections: rectangle2's horizontal projection is columns 2-3, and rectangle5's horizontal projection is also columns 2-3. Wait, no, rectangle2's horizontal interval is vertical lines 2-3, which corresponds to columns 2 to 3-1=2? Wait, no, I'm getting confused between horizontal lines and columns.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nHorizontal lines are the rows. The horizontal interval [H_start, H_end] corresponds to rows from H_start to H_end - 1.\n\nSimilarly, vertical lines are the columns. The vertical interval [V_start, V_end] corresponds to columns from V_start to V_end - 1.\n\nTherefore, rectangle2 has horizontal lines 1 and 2, so spans rows 1 to 1 (since H_end - H_start = 2 -1 =1). Wait, no, hold on.\n\nWait, actually, the rectangle defined by horizontal lines H1 and H2 (where H1 < H2) spans rows from H1 to H2 - 1. Similarly, vertical lines V1 and V2 span columns from V1 to V2 - 1.\n\nTherefore, rectangle2 is defined by horizontal lines 1 and 2 (H_start=1, H_end=2), so it spans rows 1 to 1 (since H_end - H_start = 1). Wait, that can't be right because in a 2x2 grid, a 1x1 square should span one row and one column.\n\nWait, let me clarify with the 2x2 grid example:\n\nThe horizontal lines are numbered 1 (bottom), 2, 3 (top). A rectangle with horizontal lines 1 and 2 spans rows from 1 to 1 (since the next horizontal line is 2, so the rows are between 1 and 2, which is just row 1). Similarly, vertical lines 1 and 2 span columns 1 to 1 (column 1).\n\nTherefore, rectangle2 is defined by horizontal lines 1 and 2 (rows 1), and vertical lines 2 and 3 (columns 2 to 2). So it's a 1x1 square at row1, column2.\n\nRectangle5 is defined by horizontal lines 2 and 3 (rows 2 to 2), and vertical lines 2 and 3 (columns 2 to 2). So it's a 1x1 square at row2, column2.\n\nTherefore, these two rectangles are adjacent vertically, sharing a common edge between row1 and row2 at column2. Wait, no: rectangle2 is at row1, column2, and rectangle5 is at row2, column2. So they are stacked vertically, sharing a common edge along the vertical line between row1 and row2 at column2. Therefore, they share an edge, so they definitely overlap along that edge. Therefore, they should be considered overlapping.\n\nBut according to the projection overlap condition:\n\nHorizontal projections: rectangle2's horizontal interval is rows 1, so [1,1]. Rectangle5's horizontal interval is rows 2, so [2,2]. These do not overlap, so their horizontal projections don't overlap, so according to the theorem, they don't overlap. But they share an edge, which contradicts the theorem?\n\nWait, this is a problem. There must be a mistake in my understanding of the projection overlap condition.\n\nWait, let me rederive the theorem.\n\nTwo rectangles overlap if and only if their projections on the x-axis overlap AND their projections on the y-axis overlap.\n\nIn this case, rectangle2 is at row1, column2. Its horizontal projection (along the x-axis, which is columns) is column2. Its vertical projection (along the y-axis, which is rows) is row1.\n\nRectangle5 is at row2, column2. Its horizontal projection is column2, vertical projection is row2.\n\nTherefore, the horizontal projections (columns) both include column2, so they overlap in the x-axis.\n\nThe vertical projections (rows) are row1 and row2, which are adjacent but do not overlap (since row1 and row2 are distinct and adjacent but not overlapping). Therefore, their vertical projections do not overlap. Therefore, according to the theorem, they do NOT overlap. But in reality, they share an edge along the vertical line between row1 and row2 at column2. Wait, but the edge between row1 and row2 is a horizontal edge for rectangle2 and a horizontal edge for rectangle5? Wait no.\n\nWait, rectangle2 is at row1, so its top edge is the horizontal line between row1 and row2. Similarly, rectangle5 is at row2, so its bottom edge is the same horizontal line between row1 and row2. Therefore, they share that entire edge. Therefore, they do share an edge, hence they are overlapping. But according to the projection overlap condition, since their vertical projections (rows) do not overlap, they are not considered overlapping.\n\nThis is a contradiction. Therefore, my earlier theorem must be wrong.\n\nWait, let's re-examine the theorem.\n\nThe theorem states: Two rectangles overlap if and only if their projections on the x-axis overlap AND their projections on the y-axis overlap.\n\nIn this case, the vertical projections (along the y-axis) of the two rectangles are [1,1] and [2,2]. These do not overlap. Therefore, according to the theorem, they do not overlap. But in reality, they share an edge. So the theorem must be wrong?\n\nWait, but the shared edge is along the boundary between row1 and row2. However, the vertical projection of rectangle2 is [1,1], which is the row1, and the vertical projection of rectangle5 is [2,2], which is row2. Since they are adjacent but not overlapping in the vertical direction, the theorem says they don't overlap. However, geometrically, they share a common edge. So which is it?\n\nWait, according to the problem statement, rectangles must not overlap at edges or vertices. So even sharing an edge is not allowed. Therefore, they should be considered overlapping. Therefore, the theorem must have an error.\n\nAh! I think the issue is in how we define the projections. Perhaps the projections should be closed intervals?\n\nIn the definition of projection overlap, if we consider the projections as closed intervals, then the vertical projections of rectangle2 is [1,2) (from row1 to row2, not including row2), and rectangle5's vertical projection is (1,2] (from row2 to row3, not including row1). Therefore, they do not overlap. But if we consider the projections as closed intervals including the endpoints, then rectangle2's vertical projection is [1,2], and rectangle5's is [2,3], so they touch at 2 but do not overlap. Hence, they still don't overlap.\n\nHowever, in reality, the two rectangles share a common edge along the line between row1 and row2 at column2. Since that edge is part of both rectangles, they do share an edge, hence they are considered overlapping according to the problem's conditions.\n\nTherefore, there is a discrepancy between the geometric interpretation and the projection overlap theorem. Therefore, my initial theorem must be incorrect in this context.\n\nWait, let me think again. The standard theorem in geometry is that two axis-aligned rectangles overlap if and only if their projections on both axes overlap. However, in the case where they share a common boundary (edge), the projections on both axes would still overlap?\n\nWait, in the example above, rectangle2's vertical projection is rows [1,2) (assuming half-open intervals) and rectangle5's is [2,3). Then they don't overlap. But if we use closed intervals [1,2] and [2,3], they meet at 2 but do not overlap. Hence, according to the theorem, they do not overlap. But in reality, they share an edge, which is a line segment, so they do overlap along that edge.\n\nWait a second, the edge is part of both rectangles. In terms of the rectangle's interiors and boundaries, the edge is part of both rectangles, so they do overlap along that edge. Therefore, the theorem must require that the projections overlap in a way that the rectangles actually share interior points or edges? Hmm.\n\nActually, the standard theorem says that two rectangles overlap (i.e., their intersection is non-empty) if and only if their projections on both axes overlap. In the case of the two rectangles sharing an edge, their intersection is the shared edge, which is non-empty (it's a line segment), so they do overlap. Therefore, the projections must be considered as closed intervals, and in that case, the vertical projections of rectangle2 is [1,2] and rectangle5 is [2,3]. These do overlap at the point 2, so the vertical projections do overlap. Wait, but [1,2] and [2,3] overlap at the point 2? In terms of intervals on the real line, the overlap between [1,2] and [2,3] is just the single point 2. Since a single point has measure zero, but in terms of set inclusion, they do intersect at that point.\n\nTherefore, in the theorem, if we consider intervals as closed, then two intervals [a,b] and [c,d] overlap if a < d and c < b. In this case, for rectangle2's vertical projection [1,2] and rectangle5's [2,3], we have a=1, b=2, c=2, d=3. Then, a < d (1 < 3) and c < b (2 < 2) is false. Therefore, they do NOT overlap. Wait, but the point 2 is included in both intervals.\n\nHmm, the standard overlap condition for intervals is that they have a non-empty intersection, which requires that the start of one is less than the end of the other and vice versa. For closed intervals, [a,b] and [c,d] intersect if a < d and c < b. In this case, a=1 < d=3, which is true, and c=2 < b=2 is false. Therefore, the intervals do not overlap. Therefore, the theorem holds: their vertical projections do not overlap (since the overlap condition isn't met), so the rectangles do NOT overlap. But geometrically, they share an edge. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, the problem is arising because in the case of adjacent rectangles sharing an edge, their projections on the axes do not overlap (if we consider the projections as the ranges of their coordinates), but they do share an edge. This suggests that the theorem is considering only strict overlaps (interiors overlapping), but the problem here includes edges as overlapping.\n\nWait, the problem states: \"the rectangles do not overlap at their interiors, edges, or vertices.\" So even sharing an edge or a vertex is not allowed. Therefore, in the theorem, for the purpose of this problem, two rectangles are considered overlapping if their boundaries intersect, even if their interiors don't overlap. Therefore, the standard theorem which requires both projections to overlap (including touching at a point) is not sufficient for this problem's definition of overlapping.\n\nAh! So the issue is that in the problem's definition, rectangles are considered overlapping if they share any point, edge, or vertex. Whereas the standard theorem about axis-aligned rectangles overlapping only considers whether their interiors overlap. Therefore, my earlier approach using the projection overlap theorem is insufficient for this problem because it only accounts for interior overlaps, not boundary overlaps.\n\nTherefore, my entire previous calculation is flawed because I used the wrong condition for overlap. The problem's definition of overlapping includes sharing any point, edge, or vertex, which is a stricter condition than the standard rectangle overlap.\n\nTherefore, I need to rethink the entire approach.\n\nThis complicates things significantly. Because now, two rectangles are considered overlapping if they are adjacent in any way, even just touching at a corner or edge.\n\nTherefore, the condition for non-overlapping is that the rectangles must be separated by at least one unit in all directions—meaning there must be at least one grid line between them in every direction where they are adjacent. Wait, but in grid terms, if two rectangles are placed such that there is at least one grid line between them horizontally and vertically, then they don't share any points.\n\nAlternatively, to ensure that two rectangles do not share any points, edges, or vertices, they must be strictly separated by at least one grid unit in both the horizontal and vertical directions. That is, if one rectangle is to the left of the other, there must be at least one column between them; similarly for above, below, right, left.\n\nTherefore, the separation must be such that:\n\nIf rectangle R is to the left of rectangle S, then the right boundary of R is at least two columns to the left of the left boundary of S (i.e., R's right boundary < S's left boundary - 1).\n\nWait, let's formalize this.\n\nLet me use the coordinate system where horizontal lines are rows 1 (bottom) to 6 (top), and vertical lines are columns 1 (left) to 6 (right).\n\nA rectangle is defined by its left, right, bottom, top boundaries: L, R, B, T (with L < R, B < T).\n\nTwo rectangles R and S do not overlap if:\n\nEither\n\n1. R's right boundary < S's left boundary - 1, OR\n\n2. S's right boundary < R's left boundary - 1, OR\n\n3. R's top boundary < S's bottom boundary - 1, OR\n\n4. S's top boundary < R's bottom boundary - 1.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nSuppose rectangle R has right boundary at column R, and rectangle S has left boundary at column L_S. To have no overlap, we need that R < L_S - 1, meaning that there's at least one column between R and L_S. For example, if R is at column 3, then L_S must be at least 5 (so that column 4 is between them). Similarly for the other directions.\n\nAlternatively, in terms of the vertical lines (columns):\n\nThe rightmost vertical line of R is R, and the leftmost vertical line of S is L_S. To have no overlap, we need R < L_S - 1. Because if R = L_S - 1, then they are adjacent with no space in between, sharing a vertical line, hence sharing an edge or vertex.\n\nSimilarly, for the vertical (row) boundaries:\n\nThe top boundary of R is T, and the bottom boundary of S is B_S. To have no overlap vertically, we need T < B_S - 1.\n\nTherefore, the four conditions for non-overlapping are:\n\n1. R < L_S - 1 (R is far left of S with at least one column gap)\n\n2. L_R > R_S - 1 (S is far left of R with at least one column gap)\n\n3. T < B_S - 1 (R is far below S with at least one row gap)\n\n4. B_R > T_S - 1 (S is far below R with at least one row gap)\n\nTherefore, two rectangles are non-overlapping if any one of these four conditions holds.\n\nThis is a stricter condition than the projection overlap condition, because it requires separation by at least one unit in all directions where they are adjacent.\n\nTherefore, this complicates the counting because now we can't just use the projection overlap condition; we have to enforce this stricter separation.\n\nGiven this, my initial approach was incorrect because I used the wrong overlap condition. Therefore, I need to start over.\n\nGiven the time I've already invested and the complexity, perhaps I should look for another approach or see if there's a known formula for this.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can model the problem as placing two non-overlapping rectangles in the grid such that they are separated by at least one unit in all directions where they are adjacent. This seems complex, but maybe we can compute it using inclusion-exclusion or complementary counting with the corrected overlap condition.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to compute the total number of pairs of rectangles and subtract those that are overlapping (according to the problem's definition).\n\nTotal pairs: C(225, 2) = 25,200.\n\nNow, need to compute the number of overlapping pairs, where overlapping means sharing any point, edge, or vertex.\n\nComputing this directly seems difficult, but maybe we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion or some other combinatorial method.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute for each rectangle R, the number of rectangles that overlap with R (including those sharing a vertex or edge), then use that to compute the total overlapping pairs.\n\nGiven that, let's try to recast the Overlap_R calculation with the new definition.\n\nFor a given rectangle R with boundaries L, R, B, T (left, right, bottom, top), a rectangle S overlaps with R if:\n\nTheir projections on the x-axis (columns) overlap AND their projections on the y-axis (rows) overlap, OR they are adjacent in such a way that they share a point, edge, or vertex.\n\nWait, actually, according to the problem's definition, rectangles overlap if they share any point, edge, or vertex, regardless of projection overlap. Therefore, two rectangles are overlapping if their bounding boxes intersect in any way, including just touching at a corner or edge.\n\nTherefore, the condition for two rectangles to overlap is that the distance between their left/right boundaries is less than or equal to 1, and similarly for top/bottom.\n\nWait, more precisely:\n\nTwo rectangles overlap if:\n\nThe horizontal distance between their left and right boundaries is less than or equal to 1, and the vertical distance between their top and bottom boundaries is less than or equal to 1? No, that's not precise.\n\nActually, the condition for two rectangles to share any point, edge, or vertex is that their projections on both axes overlap when considering closed intervals.\n\nWait, if we model the rectangles as closed regions (including their boundaries), then two rectangles overlap if and only if their projections on both axes overlap (as closed intervals), because if the projections overlap on both axes, then the rectangles share at least a point (the intersection of the projections). Conversely, if they share any point, edge, or vertex, then their projections must overlap on both axes.\n\nWait, let's think:\n\nSuppose two rectangles share a common vertex. Then, their projections on both axes must include that vertex's coordinate, hence the projections must overlap on both axes.\n\nSimilarly, if they share an edge, then along that edge, both their x and y projections must overlap.\n\nTherefore, the condition that two rectangles share any point, edge, or vertex is equivalent to their projections on both axes overlapping (as closed intervals). Therefore, my initial theorem was correct for this problem's definition of overlapping.\n\nWait a second! In the earlier example with rectangle2 and rectangle5 in the 2x2 grid:\n\nRectangle2 has vertical projection [1,2] (rows) and horizontal projection [2,3] (columns).\n\nWait, no, let me re-express in closed intervals.\n\nRectangle2 is at rows 1 to 1 (since horizontal lines 1 and 2 correspond to row 1), and columns 2 to 2 (vertical lines 2 and 3 correspond to column 2). So its vertical (row) projection is [1,2] (from row1 to row2), and horizontal (column) projection is [2,3] (from column2 to column3). Wait, no, actually, if vertical lines are columns, then the horizontal projection (columns) for rectangle2 is [2,3) if we consider half-open intervals, but if we consider closed intervals, it's [2,2] because it spans column2 only. Wait, confusion again between open and closed intervals.\n\nLet me clarify with coordinates.\n\nLet me define the rectangle's boundaries as follows:\n\nA rectangle with left boundary L, right boundary R, bottom boundary B, top boundary T (all vertical and horizontal lines), with L < R and B < T.\n\nThe rectangle occupies:\n\n- Columns from L to R - 1 (since the vertical line R is the right boundary, so the last column is R - 1)\n\n- Rows from B to T - 1 (since the horizontal line T is the top boundary, so the last row is T - 1)\n\nTherefore, the projection on the x-axis (columns) is [L, R - 1], and on the y-axis (rows) is [B, T - 1].\n\nTherefore, the horizontal projection (columns) is the interval [L, R - 1], and the vertical projection (rows) is [B, T - 1].\n\nTherefore, two rectangles R and S overlap (according to the problem's definition) if and only if:\n\n1. Their column projections overlap: [L_R, R_R - 1] and [L_S, R_S - 1] overlap as intervals on the real line.\n\n2. Their row projections overlap: [B_R, T_R - 1] and [B_S, T_S - 1] overlap as intervals on the real line.\n\nBecause if both projections overlap, then the rectangles share at least a point (the intersection of the projections). If they share a point, edge, or vertex, that point must lie within both projections.\n\nTherefore, the initial theorem holds: two rectangles overlap (by the problem's definition) if and only if their column projections overlap AND their row projections overlap (as closed intervals).\n\nIn the earlier example of rectangle2 and rectangle5 in the 2x2 grid:\n\nRectangle2:\n\n- Columns: L=2, R=3 ⇒ columns [2, 2] (since R - 1 = 2)\n\n- Rows: B=1, T=2 ⇒ rows [1, 1]\n\nRectangle5:\n\n- Columns: L=2, R=3 ⇒ columns [2, 2]\n\n- Rows: B=2, T=3 ⇒ rows [2, 2]\n\nTherefore, column projections both are [2, 2], so they overlap.\n\nRow projections: [1,1] and [2,2], which do not overlap (since 1 < 2).\n\nTherefore, according to the theorem, they do NOT overlap, which contradicts the geometric intuition that they share an edge.\n\nWait, but in reality, rectangle2 is at row1, column2, and rectangle5 is at row2, column2. The point (column2, row2) is a corner of rectangle5 and the point (column2, row1) is a corner of rectangle2. The edge between them is along column2 from row1 to row2. Since the edge is part of both rectangles' boundaries, they share that edge, hence they should be considered overlapping.\n\nHowever, according to the projections:\n\n- Column projections: both [2,2], so overlap.\n\n- Row projections: [1,1] and [2,2], which do not overlap.\n\nTherefore, according to the theorem, they do NOT overlap, but geometrically they do share an edge.\n\nThis inconsistency arises because the edge between row1 and row2 at column2 is part of both rectangles' boundaries, but the row projections are [1,1] and [2,2], which do not overlap. The edge is along the boundary between these two intervals.\n\nAh! Here's the key: the row projection of rectangle2 is [1,1], which is the single row1, and the row projection of rectangle5 is [2,2], which is row2. The edge between them is along the line separating row1 and row2, which is not included in either projection's interval. Since the projections are defined as the actual rows occupied by the rectangle, which for rectangle2 is only row1, and for rectangle5 is only row2. The edge between them is not part of either rectangle's interior or boundary? Wait, no, the edge is part of both rectangles' boundaries.\n\nWait, the rectangle2 has its top boundary at row2 (the horizontal line between row1 and row2), and its bottom boundary at row1. Similarly, rectangle5 has its bottom boundary at row2 and top boundary at row3. Therefore, the horizontal line between row1 and row2 is the top boundary of rectangle2 and the bottom boundary of rectangle5. Therefore, that line is part of both rectangles' boundaries, hence they share that edge, so they should be considered overlapping.\n\nHowever, according to the projection overlap theorem, since their row projections do not overlap (they are [1,1] and [2,2]), they do not overlap. This is a contradiction.\n\nThe resolution is that the projections should include the boundaries in a way that accounts for the edges. Perhaps the projections should be considered as closed intervals including the boundary lines.\n\nWait, let me redefine the projections as follows:\n\nFor a rectangle with left boundary L, right boundary R, bottom boundary B, top boundary T:\n\n- The column projection is the interval [L, R], but since columns are between vertical lines, the actual columns covered are L to R-1. But when considering the projection for overlap, we should consider the vertical lines, not the columns between them. Wait, this is getting too confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct way to model this is to consider the rectangles as occupying the area from (L, B) to (R, T), where L, B, R, T are the vertical and horizontal lines. In this case, the rectangle's boundaries are the lines themselves, so the projection on the x-axis (columns) is the interval [L, R], and on the y-axis (rows) is [B, T].\n\nIn this model, the rectangle's area is the set of points (x, y) where L ≤ x ≤ R and B ≤ y ≤ T.\n\nThen, two rectangles R and S overlap if and only if their x-intervals [L_R, R_R] and [L_S, R_S] overlap AND their y-intervals [B_R, T_R] and [B_S, T_S] overlap.\n\nIn this case, for rectangle2 and rectangle5 in the 2x2 grid:\n\nRectangle2 has L=2, R=3, B=1, T=2.\n\nRectangle5 has L=2, R=3, B=2, T=3.\n\nTheir x-intervals [2,3] and [2,3] overlap completely.\n\nTheir y-intervals [1,2] and [2,3] overlap at the point y=2.\n\nSince the overlap on the y-axis is a single point (y=2), which is included in both intervals, the rectangles do overlap at that point (the corner at (2,2) is part of both rectangles' boundaries).\n\nTherefore, according to this model, they do overlap.\n\nThus, the correct condition is that the projections on both axes must overlap as closed intervals (including endpoints), and if they do, then the rectangles overlap (including at boundaries and vertices).\n\nTherefore, the initial theorem holds when considering closed intervals for the projections.\n\nTherefore, in the earlier example, the vertical projections of rectangle2 and rectangle5 are [1,2] and [2,3], which overlap at y=2, so they do overlap.\n\nSimilarly, their horizontal projections [2,3] and [2,3] overlap completely.\n\nTherefore, they do overlap, which aligns with the geometric intuition.\n\nTherefore, my mistake earlier was in incorrectly defining the projections as half-open intervals instead of closed intervals.\n\nTherefore, the correct overlap condition is:\n\nTwo rectangles overlap (by the problem's definition) if and only if their horizontal projections (as closed intervals [L, R]) and vertical projections (as closed intervals [B, T]) overlap on both axes.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem, the initial calculation was correct, except that in the small example, I had an error in defining the projections as half-open instead of closed.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem, the calculation that led to 6,300 non-overlapping pairs is correct, because it used the proper closed interval projections.\n\nIn the 2x2 grid example, let's recalculate using the correct overlap condition.\n\nFor rectangle2 and rectangle5:\n\nTheir horizontal projections [2,3] and [2,3] overlap.\n\nTheir vertical projections [1,2] and [2,3] overlap at y=2.\n\nTherefore, they do overlap, so they should be counted as overlapping pairs.\n\nIn the earlier manual count for the 2x2 grid:\n\nTotal pairs: 36.\n\nOverlapping_pairs computed via formula: 20.\n\nNon-overlapping_pairs: 16.\n\nLet me manually count overlapping_pairs:\n\nFirst, the entire grid (rectangle9) overlaps with everyone else: 8 overlaps.\n\nNow, among the other 8 rectangles:\n\nRectangle1 overlaps with:\n\n- rectangles3,7,9 (already counted 9), and also rectangle2? Wait, rectangle1 is [1,2]x[1,2].\n\nIts horizontal projection [1,2], vertical [1,2].\n\nOverlap with rectangle2: horizontal [1,2] vs [2,3] → overlap at 2? Yes, since [1,2] and [2,3] overlap at 2.\n\nVertical [1,2] vs [2,3] overlap at 2.\n\nThus, they do overlap.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle1 overlaps with rectangle3 (horizontal overlap [1,2] with [1,3], vertical [1,2] with [1,2]), so yes.\n\nRectangle1 also overlaps with rectangle7 (vertical overlap [1,2] with [1,2], horizontal overlap [1,2] with [1,2]).\n\nSo rectangle1 overlaps with rectangle2,3,7,9.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle2 overlaps with rectangle1,3,5,6,9?\n\nWait, this is getting too time-consuming, but given that the formula gave 20 overlapping_pairs and the total is 36, and the manual count should align if done correctly, I'll trust the formula now that the projection overlap is correctly applied with closed intervals.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem, with the 5x5 grid:\n\nWe computed non-overlapping_pairs = 6,300.\n\nBut let me confirm the steps once more to ensure no arithmetic errors.\n\nWe had:\n\nTotal_pairs = C(225, 2) = 225*224/2 = 25,200.\n\nSum_{R} Overlap_R = 195 * 195 = 38,025.\n\nThen, overlapping_pairs = (38,025 - 225)/2 = (37,800)/2 = 18,900.\n\nTherefore, non-overlapping_pairs = 25,200 - 18,900 = 6,300.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 6,300.\n\nHowever, given the complexity and the potential for arithmetic errors in such a lengthy calculation, I want to verify the computation of S_h again.\n\nEarlier, we computed S_h as the sum over all horizontal intervals [L, R] of A(L,R), where A(L,R) = 15 - C(L-1,2) - C(6 - R, 2).\n\nWe computed this sum as 195.\n\nLet me recompute S_h step by step for each L:\n\nFor L=1:\n\nR=2: A=15 - C(0,2) - C(4,2) = 15 - 0 - 6 = 9\n\nR=3: 15 - 0 - C(3,2)=15 - 0 -3=12\n\nR=4:15 -0 -C(2,2)=15-0-1=14\n\nR=5:15 -0 -C(1,2)=15-0-0=15\n\nR=6:15 -0 -C(0,2)=15-0-0=15\n\nSum for L=1: 9+12=21; 21+14=35; 35+15=50; 50+15=65. Correct.\n\nL=2:\n\nR=3:15 - C(1,2) - C(3,2)=15-0-3=12\n\nR=4:15 - C(1,2) - C(2,2)=15-0-1=14\n\nR=5:15 - C(1,2) - C(1,2)=15-0-0=15\n\nR=6:15 - C(1,2) - C(0,2)=15-0-0=15\n\nSum:12+14=26; 26+15=41; 41+15=56. Correct.\n\nL=3:\n\nR=4:15 - C(2,2) - C(2,2)=15-1-1=13\n\nR=5:15 - C(2,2) - C(1,2)=15-1-0=14\n\nR=6:15 - C(2,2) - C(0,2)=15-1-0=14\n\nSum:13+14=27; 27+14=41. Correct.\n\nL=4:\n\nR=5:15 - C(3,2) - C(1,2)=15-3-0=12\n\nR=6:15 - C(3,2) - C(0,2)=15-3-0=12\n\nSum:12+12=24. Correct.\n\nL=5:\n\nR=6:15 - C(4,2) - C(0,2)=15-6-0=9\n\nSum:9. Correct.\n\nTotal S_h = 65 +56 +41 +24 +9 = 195. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the computation is correct.\n\nThus, the final answer should be 6,300.\n\nBut to cross-verify, let me consider a simpler case, like a 1x1 grid (2x2 grid lines), and see if the formula works there.\n\nIn a 1x1 grid (2x2 grid lines), total rectangles: 1.\n\nTotal pairs: C(1,2)=0.\n\nSum_{R} Overlap_R: For the single rectangle, Overlap_R = 1 (itself).\n\nThen, overlapping_pairs = (1 - 1)/2 = 0. Correct.\n\nnon-overlapping_pairs = 0 - 0 = 0. Correct.\n\nAnother test: 2x2 grid (3x3 grid lines), total rectangles 9.\n\nAs above, with the correct overlap condition, the formula gives non-overlapping_pairs = 16.\n\nLet me see if that's plausible.\n\nIn a 2x2 grid of squares, how many non-overlapping rectangle pairs are there?\n\nFirst, list all rectangles and see which pairs don't overlap.\n\nThere are 9 rectangles. Let's consider them as labeled 1-9 as before.\n\nNon-overlapping pairs must have rectangles that are separated by at least one unit in all directions.\n\nGiven the grid is small, let's manually count.\n\nFirst, the entire grid (rectangle9) cannot be paired with any other rectangle without overlapping, so it forms 0 non-overlapping pairs.\n\nNow, consider the other 8 rectangles.\n\nLet's categorize them by size and position:\n\n1. 1x1 squares: rectangles1,2,4,5,7,8\n\n2. 1x2 rectangles:3,6\n\n3. 2x1 rectangles:7,8 (wait, rectangle7 and 8 are 2x1)\n\nWait, rectangle7 is [1,3]x[1,2], which is 2 columns wide (columns1-2) and 1 row tall, so 2x1.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle8 is 2x1.\n\nRectangle3 is [1,2]x[1,3], which is 1 column wide and 2 rows tall, so 1x2.\n\nRectangle6 is [2,3]x[1,3], same as rectangle3 but on the right.\n\nSo the 1x1 squares are 1,2,4,5,7,8? Wait:\n\nWait rectangle4 is [2,3]x[1,2], which is 1x1.\n\nRectangle5 is [2,3]x[2,3], 1x1.\n\nRectangle7 is [1,3]x[1,2], which is 2 columns (columns1-2) and 1 row, so 2x1.\n\nRectangle8 is [1,3]x[2,3], 2x1.\n\nWait, so actually the 1x1 squares are rectangles1,2,4,5.\n\nWait:\n\nRectangle1: [1,2]x[1,2] → 1x1\n\nRectangle2: [1,2]x[2,3] →1x1\n\nRectangle3: [1,2]x[1,3] →1x2\n\nRectangle4: [2,3]x[1,2] →1x1\n\nRectangle5: [2,3]x[2,3] →1x1\n\nRectangle6: [2,3]x[1,3] →1x2\n\nRectangle7: [1,3]x[1,2] →2x1\n\nRectangle8: [1,3]x[2,3] →2x1\n\nRectangle9: [1,3]x[1,3] →2x2\n\nSo the 1x1 squares are rectangles1,2,4,5 (4 of them).\n\nThe 1x2 rectangles are 3,6 (2 of them).\n\nThe 2x1 rectangles are7,8 (2 of them).\n\nThe 2x2 rectangle is9.\n\nNow, to find non-overlapping pairs among the 8 non-9 rectangles.\n\nA pair of rectangles is non-overlapping if they don't share any point, edge, or vertex.\n\nGiven the grid's small size, let's see:\n\nFirst, the 1x1 squares:\n\nCan two 1x1 squares be non-overlapping?\n\nFor example, rectangle1 (top-left) and rectangle5 (middle-center):\n\nRectangle1 is at (1,1), rectangle5 is at (2,2). They share a corner at (2,2) with rectangle1? Wait, rectangle1 is at row1, column1; rectangle5 is at row2, column2. The distance between them is one unit apart diagonally, so they don't share any point, edge, or vertex. Therefore, they are non-overlapping.\n\nWait, their coordinates:\n\nRectangle1: rows1, column1.\n\nRectangle5: rows2, column2.\n\nThe closest points are at (1,1) and (2,2), which are diagonally adjacent but not sharing any edge or vertex. The distance between their boundaries: horizontally, they are separated by one column (between column1 and column2), and vertically by one row (between row1 and row2). Therefore, they are separated by one unit in both directions, hence non-overlapping.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle1 and rectangle4: rectangle4 is at row1, column2. They are adjacent horizontally, sharing an edge? Rectangle1 is column1, rectangle4 is column2 in the same row. So they share an edge between column1 and column2 at row1. Therefore, they do overlap along that edge. Hence, they are overlapping.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle1 and rectangle2: they are in the same row but adjacent columns, sharing an edge between column1 and column2 at row1-2? Wait, rectangle1 is in columns1, row1; rectangle2 is in column2, row1. So they are adjacent horizontally in the same row, sharing an edge. Hence overlapping.\n\nRectangle1 and rectangle7: rectangle7 is the left column spanning two rows. Rectangle1 is at the top of the left column. They share the top edge of rectangle7 with rectangle1's bottom edge? Wait:\n\nRectangle7 spans rows1-2 and columns1-2.\n\nRectangle1 is rows1, column1.\n\nTherefore, rectangle7 includes rectangle1 entirely within it? No, rectangle7 is a 2x1 rectangle covering rows1-2 and columns1-2. Therefore, rectangle1 is entirely within rectangle7, so they overlap completely.\n\nTherefore, rectangle1 and rectangle7 overlap.\n\nRectangle1 and rectangle8: rectangle8 is rows2-3, columns1-2. So rectangle1 is at row1, column1. They are separated by one row. The vertical distance is one row (between row1 and row2), so they don't share any points. Therefore, they are non-overlapping.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle1 and rectangle5: non-overlapping as above.\n\nThis is getting complex, but let's try to count systematically.\n\nFirst, list all possible pairs among the 8 non-9 rectangles and see which are non-overlapping.\n\nThere are C(8,2)=28 pairs.\n\nWe need to count how many of these 28 pairs are non-overlapping.\n\nLet me categorize the rectangles:\n\nGroup A: 1x1 squares: 1,2,4,5\n\nGroup B: 1x2 rectangles:3,6\n\nGroup C: 2x1 rectangles:7,8\n\nGroup D: 2x2:9 (excluded)\n\nFirst, pairs within Group A (1x1 squares):\n\nThere are C(4,2)=6 pairs.\n\nCheck each:\n\n1 & 2: adjacent horizontally → overlap → exclude.\n\n1 & 4: adjacent horizontally → overlap → exclude.\n\n1 &5: diagonally adjacent → non-overlapping → count.\n\n1 & (others): rectangle1 with rectangle4: adjacent horizontally → overlap.\n\n1 & rectangle5: non-overlapping.\n\n1 & rectangle... Wait, Group A has 1,2,4,5.\n\n1 &2: adjacent → overlap\n\n1 &4: adjacent → overlap\n\n1 &5: diagonal → non-overlapping\n\n1 & rectangle? Wait, all four:\n\nPairs:\n\n1-2: overlap\n\n1-4: overlap\n\n1-5: non-overlap\n\n2-4: rectangle2 is at column2, row1; rectangle4 is at column2, row1? No, rectangle4 is [2,3]x[1,2], so column2, rows1-2. Wait:\n\nWait rectangle2 is [1,2]x[2,3], which is column2, row1.\n\nRectangle4 is [2,3]x[1,2], which is column2, rows1-2.\n\nTherefore, rectangle2 and rectangle4 share the cell at column2, row1? Wait:\n\nRectangle2 spans columns2-3? No, vertical lines 2-3 correspond to columns2 to 2 (since R - L =1). Wait, no:\n\nWait rectangle2 is defined by vertical lines L=2, R=3, so columns from 2 to 2 (since R - L =1). Similarly, rows from 1 to1 (since horizontal lines1-2).\n\nWait this is confusing again. Let me use the coordinate system where each rectangle's position is:\n\nRectangle1: (1,1) to (1,1) (1x1 top-left)\n\nRectangle2: (1,2) to (1,2) (1x1 top-middle)\n\nRectangle4: (2,1) to (2,2) (1x1 middle-left)\n\nRectangle5: (2,2) to (2,2) (1x1 middle-center)\n\nRectangle7: (1,1) to (2,2) (2x1 left column)\n\nRectangle8: (1,2) to (2,3) (2x1 middle column)\n\nRectangle3: (1,1) to (1,3) (1x2 top row)\n\nRectangle6: (2,1) to (2,3) (1x2 middle row)\n\nOkay, now with coordinates:\n\nRectangle1: (1,1)\n\nRectangle2: (1,2)\n\nRectangle4: (2,1)\n\nRectangle5: (2,2)\n\nRectangle7: covers (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2)\n\nRectangle8: covers (1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nRectangle3: covers (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\nRectangle6: covers (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nNow, pairs within Group A (1x1 squares):\n\n1 & 2: adjacent horizontally → overlap (share edge between (1,1)-(1,2))\n\n1 &4: adjacent vertically → overlap (share edge between (1,1)-(2,1))\n\n1 &5: diagonal from (1,1) to (2,2) → separated by one unit in both directions → non-overlapping\n\n1 & rectangle? Wait, only four members: 1,2,4,5.\n\nPairs:\n\n1-2: overlap\n\n1-4: overlap\n\n1-5: non-overlap\n\n2-4: rectangle2 at (1,2) and rectangle4 at (2,1): diagonally adjacent, no shared points → non-overlapping?\n\nThe distance between (1,2) and (2,1) is √2, so they don't share any points. So non-overlapping.\n\n2-5: rectangle2 (1,2) and rectangle5 (2,2): share a vertical edge between row1 and row2 at column2 → overlap.\n\n2-4: non-overlap\n\n2-5: overlap\n\n4-5: rectangle4 (2,1) and rectangle5 (2,2): adjacent horizontally → overlap\n\nTherefore, among Group A pairs:\n\nNon-overlapping pairs:\n\n1-5\n\n2-4\n\nTotal of 2 non-overlapping pairs.\n\nNow pairs between Group A and Group B (1x1 with 1x2):\n\nGroup B has rectangles3 and6.\n\nRectangle3 is the top row (1x2): covers (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\nRectangle6 is the middle row (1x2): covers (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nPairs:\n\n1 &3: rectangle1 (1,1) is inside rectangle3 → overlap.\n\n1 &6: rectangle1 (1,1) is above rectangle6, separated by one row → non-overlapping.\n\n2 &3: rectangle2 (1,2) is inside rectangle3 → overlap.\n\n2 &6: rectangle2 (1,2) is above rectangle6, separated by one row → non-overlapping.\n\n4 &3: rectangle4 (2,1) is inside rectangle3's left part? Rectangle3 is the entire top row, so rectangle4 is below it. Wait rectangle4 is at (2,1), which is in row2, column1. Rectangle3 is row1, so they are separated by one row → non-overlapping.\n\n4 &6: rectangle4 (2,1) is to the left of rectangle6 (which spans columns1-3 in row2). They share column1 in row2 → overlap at (2,1).\n\n5 &3: rectangle5 (2,2) is inside rectangle3's row? No, rectangle5 is at (2,2), which is in row2, while rectangle3 is row1. So separated by a row → non-overlapping.\n\n5 &6: rectangle5 (2,2) is inside rectangle6 → overlap.\n\nTherefore, non-overlapping pairs:\n\n1 &6\n\n2 &6\n\n4 &3\n\n5 &3\n\nTotal of 4 non-overlapping pairs.\n\nPairs between Group A and Group C (1x1 with 2x1):\n\nGroup C: rectangles7 and8.\n\nRectangle7: covers (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2)\n\nRectangle8: covers (1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nPairs:\n\n1 &7: rectangle1 is inside rectangle7 → overlap.\n\n1 &8: rectangle1 (1,1) and rectangle8 (1,2-3, 2-3). They are separated by one column → non-overlapping.\n\n2 &7: rectangle2 (1,2) is inside rectangle7 → overlap.\n\n2 &8: rectangle2 (1,2) is adjacent to rectangle8 along the edge between column2 and column3 in row1 → overlap.\n\n4 &7: rectangle4 (2,1) is inside rectangle7 → overlap.\n\n4 &8: rectangle4 (2,1) and rectangle8 (1,2-3, 2-3). They are diagonally separated by one unit → non-overlapping.\n\n5 &7: rectangle5 (2,2) is inside rectangle7 → overlap.\n\n5 &8: rectangle5 (2,2) is inside rectangle8 → overlap.\n\nNon-overlapping pairs:\n\n1 &8\n\n4 &8\n\nTotal of 2 non-overlapping pairs.\n\nPairs between Group B and Group C:\n\nGroup B:3,6\n\nGroup C:7,8\n\nRectangle3 (top row) with rectangle7 (left column):\n\nRectangle3 covers row1, columns1-3.\n\nRectangle7 covers columns1-2, rows1-2.\n\nThey overlap entirely in row1, columns1-2 → overlap.\n\nRectangle3 &8: rectangle3 is row1, rectangle8 is rows2-3, columns1-3 → separated by one row → non-overlapping.\n\nRectangle6 (middle row) with rectangle7: rectangle6 covers row2, columns1-3. Rectangle7 covers rows1-2, columns1-2. They overlap in row2, columns1-2 → overlap.\n\nRectangle6 &8: rectangle6 is row2, columns1-3; rectangle8 is rows2-3, columns1-3. They overlap in row2, columns1-3 → overlap.\n\nThus, non-overlapping pairs:\n\n3 &8\n\nTotal of1.\n\nPairs within Group B:\n\nGroup B has two rectangles:3 and6.\n\nThey are separated by one row (rectangle3 is row1, rectangle6 is row2). Their column projections overlap (columns1-3 for both), but their row projections [1,1] and [2,2] do not overlap. Therefore, according to the overlap condition, they do NOT overlap.\n\nWait, rectangle3's vertical projection is [1,1] (row1), and rectangle6's vertical projection is [2,2] (row2). Their vertical projections don't overlap, so they don't overlap. But geometrically, they are separated by one row, so no shared points. Therefore, non-overlapping.\n\nThus, pair3-6 is non-overlapping.\n\nSimilarly, pair3-6: non-overlapping.\n\nPair3 &6: non-overlapping.\n\nPairs within Group C:\n\nGroup C has two rectangles:7 and8.\n\nRectangle7 is left column, rows1-2; rectangle8 is middle column (columns2-3?), wait rectangle8 is columns1-2? Wait no:\n\nWait rectangle7: vertical lines1-3, so columns1-2; rows1-2.\n\nRectangle8: vertical lines1-3, rows2-3.\n\nTheir projections:\n\nHorizontal projections: both columns1-2 (for rectangle7) and columns1-2 (for rectangle8)? No, rectangle8 is vertical lines1-3 (columns1-2) and rows2-3. So horizontal projection is columns1-2.\n\nVertical projections: rows1-2 and rows2-3.\n\nTheir horizontal projections overlap (columns1-2), vertical projections overlap at row2.\n\nTherefore, their projections overlap on both axes, so they overlap along the edge between row2 and row3 in columns1-2.\n\nTherefore, rectangle7 and rectangle8 share an edge at row2, columns1-2 → overlap.\n\nThus, pair7-8: overlapping.\n\nThus, within Group C, no non-overlapping pairs.\n\nNow, pairs between Group B and others already counted.\n\nFinally, pairs within Group B and C already considered.\n\nNow, pairs between Group B and Group C:\n\nAlready considered.\n\nNow, total non-overlapping pairs so far:\n\nGroup A internal: 2\n\nGroup A-B:4\n\nGroup A-C:2\n\nGroup B-C:1\n\nGroup B internal:1 (3-6)\n\nTotal: 2+4+2+1+1=10.\n\nWait, let's add them step by step:\n\nGroup A internal:2\n\nGroup A-B:4 → total 6\n\nGroup A-C:2 → total 8\n\nGroup B-C:1 → total 9\n\nGroup B internal:1 → total 10\n\nGroup C internal:0 → remains 10\n\nAdditionally, pairs between Group B and others not yet considered? Wait, we considered all pairs:\n\nTotal pairs among 8 rectangles: C(8,2)=28.\n\nWe have counted 10 non-overlapping pairs so far. Need to see if there are more.\n\nWait, perhaps missed some:\n\nPairs between Group B (3,6) and others:\n\nWait rectangle3 (top row) with rectangle8 (middle column bottom):\n\nAlready counted as part of Group B-C: rectangle3 &8.\n\nPairs between rectangle6 and rectangle8: overlapping.\n\nPairs between rectangle6 and rectangle7: overlapping.\n\nPairs between rectangle3 and rectangle7: overlapping.\n\nPairs between rectangle6 and rectangle4? Wait rectangle4 is in Group A.\n\nWait rectangle4 is in Group A. We considered Group A with others.\n\nWait, perhaps missed pairs between Group B and Group C other than already counted?\n\nNo, seems covered.\n\nPairs between rectangle3 and rectangle7: overlapping.\n\nPairs between rectangle6 and rectangle4: rectangle4 is (2,1), rectangle6 is row2, columns1-3 → they overlap at (2,1).\n\nPairs between rectangle3 and rectangle4: rectangle3 is row1, rectangle4 is (2,1) → separated by a row → non-overlapping?\n\nWait rectangle3 is the top row (row1, columns1-3), and rectangle4 is at (2,1). The vertical distance is one row, so they don't overlap. Their projections:\n\nHorizontal projections: both cover columns1-3 (rectangle3) and column1 (rectangle4). The horizontal projections overlap at column1-3 and column1, so overlap. Vertical projections: rectangle3 is [1,1], rectangle4 is [2,2]. These do not overlap. Therefore, according to the overlap condition, they do not overlap.\n\nWait, rectangle4's vertical projection is [2,2] (row2), and rectangle3's is [1,1]. These don't overlap, so they don't overlap. But geometrically, they are separated by one row, so no shared points. Therefore, non-overlapping.\n\nWait, this pair (rectangle3 &4) wasn't counted yet.\n\nAh! Here's a mistake in earlier counting.\n\nGroup A includes rectangle4. When we considered pairs within Group A, we had:\n\nGroup A internal pairs:\n\n1 &4: overlapping (they are adjacent vertically)\n\nBut rectangle3 (Group B) and rectangle4 (Group A) form a pair not yet counted.\n\nWait, rectangle3 is in Group B, rectangle4 is in Group A.\n\nTheir pair: rectangle3 (top row) and rectangle4 (row2, column1).\n\nVertical projections: rectangle3 is [1,1], rectangle4 is [2,2]. No overlap.\n\nHorizontal projections: rectangle3 covers columns1-3, rectangle4 covers column1. Overlap in columns1-3 and column1 → overlap.\n\nTherefore, since vertical projections don't overlap, they do NOT overlap. Hence, non-overlapping pair.\n\nThis pair was missed in previous counting.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle3 and rectangle4: non-overlapping.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle3 and rectangle8: already counted as part of Group B-C.\n\nRectangle6 and rectangle4: rectangle6 is row2, columns1-3; rectangle4 is (2,1). They share the point (2,1), so overlap.\n\nRectangle3 and rectangle4: non-overlapping.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle3 and rectangle2: rectangle2 is (1,2), rectangle3 covers it → o