en, either they are separated in the x-direction (i.e., one is to the left of the other) or separated in the y-direction (one is above the other). Wait, actually, if they are not separated in the x-direction, then their x-projections overlap, and if they are not separated in the y-direction, their y-projections overlap, so if they are not separated in either direction, then their projections overlap in both axes, which would mean that the rectangles themselves must overlap. So, the negation is: rectangles overlap if and only if their x-projections overlap AND their y-projections overlap. Therefore, the rectangles do not overlap if either their x-projections do not overlap OR their y-projections do not overlap. Wait, so actually, the condition is that they are separated in at least one direction (x or y). So, two rectangles are disjoint if they are separated along the x-axis or along the y-axis. So, if they are not separated along the x-axis (i.e., their x-projections overlap) AND not separated along the y-axis (i.e., their y-projections overlap), then they do overlap. Therefore, to have non-overlapping rectangles, it suffices that they are separated along at least one axis.\n\nTherefore, in order to compute the number of pairs of rectangles that do not overlap, we can compute all pairs of rectangles and subtract those pairs that do overlap. Since overlapping is when their x-projections overlap AND their y-projections overlap.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the formula is:\n\nNumber of non-overlapping pairs = Total pairs - Number of overlapping pairs.\n\nTotal pairs is C(225, 2).\n\nNumber of overlapping pairs is the number of pairs of rectangles (R1, R2) such that the x-projections of R1 and R2 overlap AND the y-projections of R1 and R2 overlap.\n\nTherefore, to compute the number of overlapping pairs, we need to compute the number of such pairs where both the x-intervals and y-intervals overlap.\n\nHmm, this seems a bit involved. Let me see if there's a better way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute the number of non-overlapping pairs directly by considering the positions of the rectangles.\n\nBut perhaps first, let me think about how to compute the number of overlapping pairs.\n\nFirst, note that for two rectangles to overlap, their x-intervals must overlap and their y-intervals must overlap. So, let me consider the x-axis first.\n\nFor the x-axis projections: Each rectangle has an x-interval [x_left, x_right], where x_left and x_right are the x-coordinates of the left and right edges (which are among the vertical grid lines). Similarly, for the y-axis projections, each rectangle has a y-interval [y_bottom, y_top].\n\nTherefore, for two rectangles R1 and R2, with x-intervals [x1_l, x1_r] and [x2_l, x2_r], and y-intervals [y1_b, y1_t] and [y2_b, y2_t], the x-intervals overlap if and only if x1_r > x2_l and x2_r > x1_l. Similarly for the y-intervals.\n\nTherefore, to compute the number of overlapping pairs, we can think in terms of their x and y intervals.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as follows:\n\nFirst, for all pairs of rectangles, the probability that their x-intervals overlap and their y-intervals overlap can be calculated by considering the x and y coordinates independently? Wait, but since the rectangles are not independent in their x and y coordinates, that might not hold. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the number of overlapping pairs by first considering the x-axis and y-axis separately and then combining them? Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, here's an approach: For each rectangle R, we can compute the number of rectangles that overlap with R, and then sum over all R, and then divide by 2 (since each overlapping pair is counted twice). But this might be computationally intensive, but perhaps manageable given that the grid is only 5x5.\n\nWait, but given that the grid is 5x5, which is manageable? Let me see. The total number of rectangles is 225, so doing a double loop over all pairs would be 2