x2),\n\nsince right_v = number of vertical lines in right region,\n\nwhich is (5 - (x2 +2) +1)=4 -x2,\n\nso right_v = max(0,4 -x2),\n\nbottom_h = max(0, y1 -1),\n\nsince bottom region's horizontal lines are from y=0 to y1 -2,\n\nso number of horizontal lines is (y1 -2 -0 +1)=y1 -1,\n\nso bottom_h = max(0, y1 -1),\n\ntop_h = max(0,4 - y2),\n\nsince top region's horizontal lines are from y=y2+2 to5,\n\nnumber is 5 - (y2 +2) +1 =4 - y2,\n\nso top_h = max(0,4 - y2),\n\nTherefore,\n\nAvailable = C(left_v, 2)*15 + C(right_v, 2)*15 + C(bottom_h, 2)*15 + C(top_h, 2)*15,\n\nsince C(6,2)=15 for horizontal lines in the right, left, bottom, top regions (since those regions have the full y-range of 6 lines).\n\nWait, for the bottom and top regions,\n\nthe horizontal lines are restricted to their respective y-ranges,\n\nso for the bottom region, the number of horizontal lines is bottom_h = max(0, y1 -1),\n\nso the number of horizontal line pairs is C(bottom_h, 2),\n\nand similarly for top region,\n\nC(top_h, 2),\n\nbut the vertical lines in the bottom and top regions are still the full 6 vertical lines,\n\nso the number of rectangles in the bottom region is C(bottom_h, 2) × C(6, 2),\n\nand similarly for top region,\n\nC(top_h, 2) × C(6, 2),\n\nwhereas the left and right regions have full vertical lines but restricted horizontal lines (full 6 horizontal lines),\n\nso their contributions are C(left_v, 2) × C(6, 2) and C(right_v, 2) × C(6, 2),\n\nYes, that seems consistent.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together,\n\nAvailable(R) = 15 × [ C(left_v, 2) + C(right_v, 2) + C(bottom_h, 2) + C(top_h, 2) ]\n\nwhere\n\nleft_v = max(0, x1 - 1),\n\nright_v = max(0, 4 - x2),\n\nbottom_h = max(0, y1 - 1),\n\ntop_h = max(0, 4 - y2).\n\nBut note that left_v, right_v, bottom_h, top_h must be at least 2 for their combinations to be non-zero,\n\nbut the formula still works because C(n, 2) is zero when n <2.\n\nTherefore, this formula gives the number of rectangles S that can be placed without overlapping or touching R.\n\nHowever, we must also consider that the regions might be outside the grid, but since we used max(0, ...) that should be handled.\n\nTherefore, with this formula, we can compute Available(R) for any rectangle R.\n\nNow, the problem is that we need to compute the sum over all rectangles R of Available(R), then divide by 2 to get the number of unordered pairs.\n\nHowever, this requires iterating over all 225 rectangles, which is tedious by hand, but perhaps we can find a symmetry or categorize rectangles by their dimensions and positions.\n\nFirst, note that the grid is symmetric in all four directions, so we can categorize rectangles by their (w, h) dimensions and their position's \"distance\" from the edges.\n\nAlternatively, we can consider that for a rectangle of size w x h placed at position (i, j), the Available(R) depends on its distance from the borders.\n\nHowever, even this might be complex, but perhaps manageable by considering the contributions based on the rectangle's dimensions.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the Available(R) depends on x1, x2, y1, y2, which are determined by the rectangle's position and size.\n\nGiven a rectangle of width w and height h, its left edge x1 can range from 0 to 5 - w,\n\nand its bottom edge y1 can range from 0 to 5 - h,\n\nand for each such position (x1, y1), the right edge is x2 = x1 + w -1,\n\nand top edge is y2 = y1 + h -1.\n\nWait, actually, if a rectangle has width w (number of columns), then its right edge is x1 + w,\n\nbut since the vertical lines are at integer coordinates, the width in terms of grid squares is w, so the number of vertical lines spanned is w +1,\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nIf a rectangle has width w (number of squares horizontally), then its left edge is at x1,\n\nand its right edge is at x1 + w,\n\nso the width in terms of the grid lines is w,\n\nand the number of vertical lines it occupies is w +1,\n\ntherefore, the right edge coordinate x2 = x1 + w,\n\nSimilarly, height h corr