hat both include that edge is C(k_e, 2), where k_e is the number of rectangles using edge e.\n\nThen, sum over all edges, but then subtract the overcounts where pairs share multiple edges.\n\nThis is inclusion-exclusion again.\n\nSimilarly, for vertices, we can compute the number of pairs of rectangles that share a common vertex, then subtract those that also share an edge, etc.\n\nThis is getting too involved, but let's attempt it.\n\nFirst, compute the number of rectangle pairs sharing at least one common edge.\n\nLet me first compute the total number of edges in the grid.\n\nThe grid has 6 horizontal lines and 6 vertical lines,\n\nso total grid lines: 12 lines.\n\nEach grid line is divided into segments by the intersections with other grid lines.\n\nHowever, for our purposes, each edge of a rectangle is a segment of a grid line between two consecutive vertical or horizontal lines.\n\nWait, perhaps better to think of each edge as a side of a rectangle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of each internal grid line as being part of multiple rectangles.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the number of edges (as in, the sides of rectangles) is complex.\n\nAlternatively, for each vertical grid line x = c (for c from 0 to5),\n\nthe number of rectangles that have this line as their left or right edge.\n\nSimilarly for horizontal grid lines.\n\nLet me consider vertical edges first.\n\nEach vertical grid line x = c can be the left edge of some rectangles and the right edge of others.\n\nFor a vertical line x = c,\n\nthe number of rectangles that have this line as their left edge is equal to the number of rectangles starting at x = c and extending to the right.\n\nA rectangle with left edge at x = c can have width from 1 to (5 - c),\n\nso the number is sum_{w=1}^{5 - c} (number of heights for each width).\n\nWait, for a fixed left edge x = c,\n\nthe rectangle can have any right edge from x = c +1 to x =5,\n\nand for each such right edge x = d,\n\nthe rectangle's height can be from 1 to