 of S into regions as before, and count the number of rectangles that can be placed in the overlapping regions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute this as follows:\n\nLet me denote the column span of S as [c1, c2], where c1 = c and c2 = c + w - 1.\n\nSimilarly, the row span of S is [r1, r2], where r1 = r and r2 = r + h - 1.\n\nThen, the number of rectangles overlapping S in the x-direction is equal to the total number of rectangles whose column intervals overlap [c1, c2].\n\nSimilarly for the y-direction.\n\nHowever, the number of rectangles overlapping in both is not simply the product, because the x and y overlaps are not independent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the number of rectangles overlapping in x-direction as follows:\n\nThe column intervals of all rectangles must satisfy: their left edge ≤ c2 and their right edge ≥ c1.\n\nSimilarly for rows.\n\nTherefore, to count the number of rectangles R overlapping in both x and y:\n\nFirst, consider all possible rectangles R in the grid. For each such rectangle R, check if its column interval overlaps with [c1, c2] and its row interval overlaps with [r1, r2]. Count how many such R there are, then subtract 1 if we need to exclude S itself.\n\nWait, but in D(S), we are counting rectangles R ≠ S that overlap S in both x and y. So, total overlapping rectangles (including S) minus 1.\n\nTherefore, D(S) = [number of rectangles overlapping S in both x and y] - 1.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute the number of rectangles overlapping S in both x and y (including S itself), then subtract 1 to exclude S.\n\nTherefore, let me compute N(S) = number of rectangles overlapping S in both x and y (including S). Then D(S) = N(S) - 1.\n\nTherefore, how to compute N(S)?\n\nTo compute N(S), we can consider the grid divided by the boundaries of S into regions, and then count all rectangles that have column intervals overlapping [c1, c2] and row intervals overlapping [r1, r2].\n\nAlternatively, we can model this as follows:\