he number of columns of squares is 5 - (c + w) + 1 = 6 - c - w. Therefore, the number of vertical lines in that region is (6 - c - w) + 1 = 7 - c - w? Wait, no, the vertical lines are the lines between the columns. Wait, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the formula.\n\nThe number of rectangles entirely within the right region (columns c + w to 5) is equal to the number of rectangles in a grid with (6 - c - w) columns of squares and 5 rows. Therefore, the number of vertical lines is (6 - c - w) + 1 = 7 - c - w, so the number of vertical line choices is C(7 - c - w, 2). Similarly, the horizontal lines are still 6, so C(6, 2). Therefore, right_rectangles = C( (6 - c - w) + 1, 2 ) * C(6, 2) = C(7 - c - w, 2) * C(6, 2).\n\nWait, but hold on, let me confirm with an example. Suppose c = 1 and w = 1. Then the right region starts at column 2 to 5, which is 4 columns. The number of vertical lines in that region is 5 (since columns 2,3,4,5 have 4 columns of squares, so 5 vertical lines). Therefore, the number of vertical line choices is C(5, 2). According to the formula above: 7 - c - w = 7 -1 -1=5, so C(5,2). That matches. So the formula is correct.\n\nTherefore, in general:\n\nleft_rectangles = C(c, 2) * C(6, 2),\n\nright_rectangles = C( (7 - c - w), 2 ) * C(6, 2),\n\nWait, hold on: Wait, actually, the vertical lines for the right region are from column c + w to column 5, so the number of vertical lines is (5 - (c + w) + 1) = 6 - c - w + 1? Wait, no:\n\nWait, the number of vertical lines in the right region is the number of vertical lines from column c + w to column 5. Since each column of squares is between two vertical lines, the number of vertical lines in the right region is (number of columns of squares) + 1.\n\nThe number of columns of squares in the right region is (5 - (c + w - 1)) = 6 - c - w. Therefore, the number of vertical lines is (6 - c - w) + 1 = 7 - c - w. Therefore, the number of vertical line choices is C( (7 - c - w), 2 ). Therefore, right_rectangles = C(7 - c - w, 2) * C(6, 2).\n\nSimilarly, for the top and bottom regions:\n\n|not B| = above_rectangles + below_rectangles.\n\nabove_rectangles: rectangles entirely above S, i.e., in rows 1 to r - 1.\n\nThe number of rows of squares above S is (r - 1). Therefore, the number of horizontal lines above is (r - 1) + 1 = r. So the number of horizontal line choices for above is C(r, 2).\n\nThe vertical lines are still 6, so the number of vertical line choices is C(6, 2).\n\nTherefore, above_rectangles = C(r, 2) * C(6, 2).\n\nbelow_rectangles: rectangles entirely below S, in rows r + h to 5.\n\nNumber of rows of squares below S is 5 - (r + h - 1) = 6 - r - h.\n\nTherefore, the number of horizontal lines below is (6 - r - h) + 1 = 7 - r - h.\n\nThus, the number of horizontal line choices is C(7 - r - h, 2).\n\nTherefore, below_rectangles = C(7 - r - h, 2) * C(6, 2).\n\nNow, |not A ∩ not B| is the number of rectangles entirely in the regions:\n\n1. Left and above,\n\n2. Left and below,\n\n3. Right and above,\n\n4. Right and below.\n\nTherefore, we can compute each of these four regions and sum their rectangle counts.\n\nFirst, left and above: columns 1 to c - 1 and rows 1 to r - 1.\n\nThis is a grid of (c - 1) columns and (r - 1) rows.\n\nNumber of rectangles here is C((c - 1) + 1, 2) * C((r - 1) + 1, 2) = C(c, 2) * C(r, 2).\n\nSimilarly,\n\nleft and below: columns 1 to c - 1 and rows r + h to 5.\n\nNumber of columns: c - 1,\n\nnumber of rows: 5 - (r + h - 1) = 6 - r - h.\n\nTherefore, number of rectangles: C(c, 2) * C( (6 - r - h) + 1, 2 ) = C(c, 2) * C(7 - r - h, 2).\n\nright and above: columns c + w to 5 and rows 1 to r - 1.\n\nNumber of columns: 5 - (c + w - 1) = 6 - c - w,\n\nnumber of rows: r - 1.\n\nTherefore, number of rectangles: C( (6 - c - w) + 1, 2 ) * C(r, 2) = C(7 - c - w, 2) * C(r, 2).\n\nright and below: columns c + w to 5 and rows r + h to 5.\n\nNumber of columns: 6 - c - w,\n\nnumber of rows: 6 - r - h.\n\nThus, number of rectangles: C(7 - c - w, 2) * C(7 - r - h, 2).\n\nTherefore, the total |not A ∩ not B| is the sum of the four regions:\n\n|not A ∩ not B| = C(c, 2) * C(r, 2) +\n\nC(c, 2) * C(7 - r - h, 2) +\n\nC(7 - c - w, 2) * C(r, 2) +\n\nC(7 - c - w, 2) * C(7 - r - h, 2).\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\nN(S) = |A ∩ B| + 1 (including S itself). Wait no, actually:\n\nWait, recall that N(S) is the number of rectangles overlapping S in both x and y, including S. So:\n\nN(S) = Total - |not A| - |not B| + |not A ∩ not B|\n\nTherefore,\n\nN(S) = 225 - [left_rectangles + right_rectangles] - [above_rectangles + below_rectangles] + [left_above + left_below + right_above + right_below].\n\nTherefore, substituting the expressions we have:\n\nN(S) = 225 - [C(c,2)*C(6,2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(6,2)] - [C(r,2)*C(6,2) + C(7 - r - h, 2)*C(6,2)] + [C(c,2)*C(r,2) + C(c,2)*C(7 - r - h, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(r, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(7 - r - h, 2)].\n\nThis is quite a complicated expression, but perhaps we can factor out C(6,2) from some terms:\n\nLet me rewrite:\n\nLet’s denote C6 = C(6, 2) = 15.\n\nThen,\n\nN(S) = 225 - [C(c,2)*C6 + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C6] - [C(r,2)*C6 + C(7 - r - h, 2)*C6] + [C(c,2)*C(r,2) + C(c,2)*C(7 - r - h, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(r, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(7 - r - h, 2)].\n\nFactor C6 from the first two terms in the second bracket:\n\n= 225 - C6 [C(c,2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)] - C6 [C(r,2) + C(7 - r - h, 2)] + [C(c,2)*C(r,2) + C(c,2)*C(7 - r - h, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(r, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)*C(7 - r - h, 2)].\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s define:\n\nA = C(c, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)\n\nB = C(r, 2) + C(7 - r - h, 2)\n\nThen,\n\nN(S) = 225 - C6 * A - C6 * B + [C(c,2)C(r,2) + C(c,2)C(7 - r - h, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)C(r, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)C(7 - r - h, 2)]\n\n= 225 - C6(A + B) + [C(c,2)C(r,2) + C(c,2)C(7 - r - h, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)C(r, 2) + C(7 - c - w, 2)C(7 - r - h, 2)].\n\nHmm, not sure if this helps, but perhaps we can group terms:\n\nNotice that the terms inside the brackets can be grouped as:\n\nC(c,2)[C(r,2)