ernatively, let me think of a smaller grid to see if the method works.\n\nLet me try a 1×1 grid of squares, which has 2x2 grid points. So, how many rectangles are there? In a 1×1 grid of squares, which is 2x2 grid points, the number of rectangles is C(2,2)*C(2,2)=1*1=1 rectangle. So only one rectangle. Then, the number of pairs is C(1,2)=0, so trivial.\n\nNot helpful. Let me try a 2×2 grid of squares, which has 3x3 grid points. The number of rectangles is C(3,2)*C(3,2)=3*3=9 rectangles.\n\nLet me compute the number of non-overlapping pairs using the same method.\n\nFirst, total pairs: C(9,2)=36.\n\nNow, compute overlapping pairs.\n\nFirst, compute S for the 2x2 grid (i.e., 2x2 squares, which is 3x3 grid points). Let me see if my formula would work here.\n\nWait, in the 2x2 grid of squares, which has 3 horizontal and 3 vertical lines.\n\nFollowing the same steps:\n\nFirst, total rectangles: (3 choose 2)^2 = 9, as above.\n\nCompute S = S1 + S2 + S3 + S4.\n\nFirst, in this case, the grid is 2x2 squares, so the coordinates go from 0 to 2 in x and y.\n\nSo, for a rectangle R = (L, R, B, T), with 0 ≤ L < R ≤3? Wait, no, in a 2x2 grid of squares, the grid lines are from 0 to 3? Wait, no, a 2x2 grid of squares has 3 horizontal and 3 vertical lines. So the x and y coordinates go from 0 to 2, so the right coordinate R can be up to 3? Wait, no, sorry, in the 2x2 grid of squares, each square is between x=i and x=i+1 for i=0,1,2. So the vertical lines are at x=0,1,2,3? Wait, no, wait, in general, an m x n grid of squares has (m+1) horizontal lines and (n+1) vertical lines. So a 2x2 grid of squares has 3 horizontal lines and 3 vertical lines. Therefore, the coordinates go from 0 to 2 in both x and y.\n\nWait, so in this case, for a rectangle in a 2x2 grid of squares, its left x-coordinate L can be 0 or 1, right x-coordinate R can be L+1 or 2 or 3? Wait, no. Wait, in a 3x3 grid of points (since 2x2 squares), the x-coordinates go from 0 to 2, so the right edge R must satisfy L < R 