angement. Therefore, the total number of such cyclic arrangements is 26!.\n\nAdditionally, we can also have the blocks arranged in reverse order or with different phase shifts?\n\nWait, the base block can be any permutation of the 26 letters. The entire sequence is then that permutation repeated three times. Therefore, the number of such arrangements is 26!.\n\nBut in addition to this, we can also have the letters arranged in a \"shifted\" manner, where the base block is a cyclic permutation. For example, instead of starting with letter A at position 1, we could start with any letter, but since the base block is a permutation, that's already accounted for in the 26!.\n\nWait, actually, the base block permutation already includes all possible starting points because permuting the letters includes all possible orderings.\n\nTherefore, the number of such \"three-fold repetition\" arrangements is exactly 26!.\n\nAdditionally, we can have the blocks arranged in a different order, but since they are identical copies, that doesn't create new arrangements.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the blocks can be permuted among themselves? But since they are identical, swapping them doesn't change the sequence.\n\nTherefore, the only additional arrangements are the block arrangements where each letter's three copies are consecutive.\n\nIn the block arrangement, all three copies of a letter are consecutive. The number of such arrangements is 26! (the number of ways to arrange the 26 letter blocks in order).\n\nTherefore, combining both types of arrangements, the total number is 26! (block arrangements) + 26! (cyclic arrangements) = 2 × 26!.\n\nWait a minute, but in the two-letter case, this would give 2 × 2! = 4, which matches. In the three-letter case, it would give 2 × 6 = 12, but earlier we found at least one more arrangement beyond the blocks and cycles, but perhaps in the three-letter case, the three-fold repetition is different?\n\nWait, in the three-letter case with this method, th