25 characters long.\n\nNow, in this arrangement, since each block is the same permutation \\( \\sigma \\), the content of the first interval (positions \\( i + 1 \\) to \\( i + 25 \\)) is exactly the same as the content of the second interval (positions \\( i + 27 \\) to \\( i + 51 \\)), because the second interval is the same as the first interval shifted by 26 positions, which is the same as the first block again. Wait, let me make this precise.\n\nSuppose in the first block, the character at position \\( j \\) is \\( \\sigma(j) \\). Then in the second block, position \\( j + 26 \\) is also \\( \\sigma(j) \\), and in the third block, position \\( j + 52 \\) is \\( \\sigma(j) \\).\n\nTherefore, the interval between the first and second \\( x_1 \\)'s is positions \\( i + 1 \\) to \\( i + 25 \\). The characters in these positions are \\( \\sigma(i + 1) \\), \\( \\sigma(i + 2) \\), ..., \\( \\sigma(i + 25) \\). Similarly, the interval between the second and third \\( x_1 \\)'s is positions \\( i + 27 \\) to \\( i + 51 \\), which correspond to \\( \\sigma(i + 27) \\), \\( \\sigma(i + 28) \\), ..., \\( \\sigma(i + 51) \\). But note that \\( \\sigma(i + 27) \\) is the same as \\( \\sigma(i + 27 \\mod 26) \\), but wait, actually, the permutation is over the entire 26 letters, so each position in the block corresponds to a unique letter. Wait, no: the permutation \\( \\sigma \\) is a rearrangement of the 26 letters, so each position in the block has a unique letter, but the same letters are repeated in subsequent blocks. Wait, actually, no: in this arrangement, each block is a permutation of the 26 letters, meaning that in each block, every letter appears exactly once. Therefore, in the first block, each letter from 'a' to 'z' appears once. The second block is another permutation (possibly the same) of the letters, but actually, if we require that each letter's three occurrences are equally spaced, then each block must be the same permutation? Wait, no, actually, if we wan