ength is 78, but actually the positions can be anywhere as long as they are in order.\n\nWait, but for all letters to have their occurrences equally spaced with the same d, the entire sequence must be a repetition of a fundamental period. For example, if d is a divisor of 78, then perhaps we can have a periodic arrangement with period d + 1 or something. Wait, this is getting a bit vague.\n\nAlternatively, if we require that for every letter, the three occurrences are equally spaced with the same distance d between them, then the entire sequence must be a concatenation of these equally spaced triplets for each letter. But since there are 26 letters, each with their own triplet, this might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, if all letters have their three occurrences equally spaced with the same distance d, then the entire sequence must be a repetition of a base pattern of length d + 1 or something. For instance, if d = 25, then the three occurrences of a letter would be at positions p, p + 26, p + 52, as we considered earlier, which fits into the three-block structure. In that case, the entire sequence is three copies of the same permutation, which gives the 26! arrangements.\n\nBut in the two-letter case, we also had the two-blocks-of-three arrangements where all three of a letter are consecutive. In that case, the distance between the first and second occurrence is 1 (adjacent), and between the second and third is also 1, so d = 1. However, in that case, the other letter's occurrences are also consecutive, so their d is also 1. So that also satisfies the condition that for each letter, the spacing between its own occurrences is equal (d=1), and for the other letter, similarly.\n\nTherefore, in the two-letter case, there are two different possibilities for the spacing parameter d: either d=1 ( clustering all three of a letter together) or d=2 (alternating every two positions, which in the three-blocks-of-two case gives d=2 between occurrences). Wait, actuall