this arrangement also satisfies the condition.\n\nTherefore, in the two-letter case, we have two types of arrangements: those with all occurrences of a letter clustered together (leading to two arrangements: a's first or b's first), and those with the letters alternating (leading to two arrangements: a b a b a b or b a b a b a). So total of four.\n\nTherefore, in the general case with 26 letters, perhaps we have two distinct types of arrangements that satisfy the condition:\n\n1. **Clustered arrangements**: All three occurrences of each letter are consecutive. The entire sequence is a concatenation of blocks, each block being three of the same letter, and the blocks can be arranged in any order. Since there are 26 letters, each contributing one block of three identical letters, the number of such arrangements is the number of permutations of the 26 blocks, which is \\( 26! \\).\n\n2. **Periodic arrangements**: The sequence is composed of three identical copies of a permutation of the 26 letters. As in the earlier example, this gives \\( 26! \\) arrangements (since the permutation can be any of the 26! permutations, and then repeated three times).\n\nHowever, wait a second: in the two-letter case, the periodic arrangement (alternating) corresponds to a different structure than the clustered one. In the two-letter case, the periodic arrangement with period 2 (alternating) is actually equivalent to the three-blocks-of-two approach, whereas the clustered arrangements are two-blocks-of-three. Therefore, in the general case, these are two distinct types of arrangements, each giving \\( 26! \\) possibilities, so total of \\( 2 \\times 26! \\).\n\nBut hold on, in the two-letter case, the two types give \\( 2! \\times 2 = 4 \\), which matches our count. So perhaps in the general case, it's \\( 2 \\times 26! \\). However, we need to confirm whether there are more types of arrangements or if these are the only two.\n\nIs there another way to arrange the letters such that for e