ue class). So that's 26! arrangements. \n\nThe block arrangement is a separate construction where the letters are grouped into blocks of three, and the blocks are permuted, giving another 26! arrangements. \n\nTherefore, total arrangements are indeed 26! + 26! = 2×26!.\n\nBut wait, hold on, in the repetition of permutation approach, the permutation defines the order of the letters in each of the three blocks. However, in this case, the three blocks are identical permutations, so the entire sequence is just the permutation repeated three times. Therefore, permuting the blocks doesn't give new arrangements because the blocks are identical. Wait, no: in the repetition of permutation approach, the permutation defines the order of the letters in each block. Since all three blocks are the same permutation, the sequence is just three copies of the same permutation. Therefore, the entire sequence is determined uniquely by the permutation π, and permuting the blocks (i.e., changing the order of the blocks) would require a different permutation. \n\nWait, actually, no. Let me clarify:\n\nSuppose we have a permutation π, which is an ordering of the 26 letters. Then, the sequence is π₁ π₂ ... π₂₆ π₁ π₂ ... π₂₆ π₁ π₂ ... π₂₆. This is three copies of the permutation π. \n\nIf we want to have the blocks in a different order, say σ(π₁), σ(π₂), ..., σ(π₂₆), that would correspond to a different permutation σ∘π. Therefore, the set of all such sequences is exactly the set of sequences formed by repeating a permutation three times, which are in bijection with the set of permutations π. Hence, there are 26! such sequences. \n\nIn contrast, the block arrangement is formed by taking a permutation τ of the 26 letters, and then creating the sequence τ₁ τ₁ τ₁ τ₂ τ₂ τ₂ ... τ₂₆ τ₂₆ τ₂₆. This is different from the repetition of a permutation unless τ is a constant permutation, which it can't be because all letters must be used. \n\nTherefore, these are indeed two distinct sets of arrangements, e