ed together (either all A's first or all B's first) or interleaved perfectly with one letter between the others. So, in the interleaved case, each letter is separated by one of the other letter. So, in that case, the spacing between the occurrences of a letter is equal, specifically, the distance between the first and second occurrence is 2 (positions apart?), wait, in ABABAB, the first A is at position 1, second at 3, third at 5. So the distance between first and second is 2 positions (positions 2 in between?), actually, the number of letters between them is 1 (the B at position 2). Similarly, between second and third A, there's also one B at position 4. So that's consistent with the earlier calculation where the number of B's between the first and second A's is 1, and same between the second and third.\n\nSo, in the two-letter case, these four arrangements satisfy the condition. Now, moving on to the original problem with 26 letters each with three copies.\n\nHmm. So in the two-letter case, the key was that for each letter, the positions of their occurrences formed an arithmetic progression. Because in order for the number of the other letter between the first and second occurrence to equal the number between the second and third, the positions had to be equally spaced. Because if they weren't equally spaced, the counts between would differ.\n\nTherefore, in the general case with 26 letters, each letter must have its three occurrences equally spaced? Wait, but how can all 26 letters have their three occurrences equally spaced in the same sequence? That seems challenging because the spacings for different letters might conflict.\n\nWait, perhaps not exactly equally spaced, but in such a way that for any two letters, the spacing between their occurrences with respect to the other letter satisfies the condition. Hmm, maybe there is a more global structure here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the letters must be arranged in a very regular pattern. Maybe all the letters