bles is \\( \\frac{n}{n+1} \\).\n\nYes! The expected maximum of n i.i.d. uniform [0,1] variables is \\( \\frac{n}{n+1} \\).\n\nTherefore, for four variables, the expected maximum is \\( \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8 \\).\n\nTherefore, the uninfected position has an expected value of 0.75, while the infected positions have an expected value of 0.8 (the global maximum). Therefore, the uninfected position's value is less than the global maximum, but they are different in expectation.\n\nTherefore, in the four-element case after one step, the expected number of distinct values is 2: the global maximum (expected value 0.8) and the uninfected value (expected 0.75). Since these are different in expectation, the expected number of distinct values is 2.\n\nSimilarly, in the three-element case after one step, all positions become the global maximum, so the expected number of distinct values is 1.\n\nIn the four-element case after two steps, all positions become the global maximum, so the expected number is 1.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in general, after t steps, the expected number of distinct values is:\n\n- If the uninfected region has size m, then the expected number of distinct values is 1 (global maximum) plus the expected number of distinct values in the uninfected region.\n\nBut the uninfected region is a block of size m = n - (2t +1). If the uninfected region has size m ≥1, then its expected number of distinct values is ?\n\nIf the uninfected region is a single position (m=1), then its value is a random variable which is the maximum of its initial value and its neighbors from the previous step. But over time, this value will increase towards the global maximum.\n\nHowever, in expectation, after t steps, the value in the uninfected region is some value less than the global maximum, but how much less?\n\nAlternatively, if we consider that in each step, the uninfected region's values are pulled upwards by their infected neighbors, then after t steps, the expected value of the uninfected