\).\n- Since \\(2025 \\geq 4 \\cdot 100 + 2\\), the expected number of distinct values is \\(2025 - 4 \\cdot 100 = 1625\\).\n\nThus, the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds is \\(\\boxed{1625}\\).","num_tokens":30184,"correct":false,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"[no boxed]","full_generation":"Okay, so here's this problem about Albert writing 2025 numbers in a circle, each initially randomly chosen from the interval [0,1]. Then, every second, he simultaneously replaces each number \\( a_i \\) with the maximum of its immediate neighbors and itself, right? Specifically, for each \\( i \\), \\( a_i \\) becomes \\( \\max(a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}) \\), with the understanding that the indices wrap around because it's a circle. The question is asking for the expected number of distinct values remaining after 100 seconds. Alright, let me try to unpack this.\n\nFirst, since all the numbers are initially independently and uniformly random in [0,1], the starting configuration has each \\( a_i \\) uniformly distributed over [0,1], and all independent of each other. So the initial set \\( \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_{2025}\\} \\) is a collection of 2025 independent uniform random variables on [0,1].\n\nNow, every second, each \\( a_i \\) is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two immediate neighbors. Importantly, this replacement happens simultaneously for all \\( i \\) at the same time. So, even though the new value of \\( a_i \\) depends on the previous values of \\( a_{i-1} \\), \\( a_i \\), and \\( a_{i+1} \\), when computing the new values for all positions, we have to use the old values before any updates, right? Because it's simultaneous. So, for example, the new \\( a_1 \\) is computed based on the old \\( a_{2025} \\), old \\( a_1 \\), and old \\( a_2 \\), and similarly for all other \\( a_i \\).\n\nThe question is about the expected number of distinct values remaining after 100 seconds. So, after performing this update process 100 times, we look