\\( i \\).\n\nTherefore, for a value \\( v \\) to persist in the configuration, it must be that for every occurrence of \\( v \\) in the previous configuration, at least one of its neighbors (in the circular arrangement) is also \\( \\leq v \\), so that the maximum of the trio is \\( v \\), so that \\( v \\) stays at that position. Alternatively, if all neighbors of a \\( v \\) are less than or equal to \\( v \\), then the maximum will be \\( v \\), so \\( v \\) stays. But if any neighbor is greater than \\( v \\), then the maximum will be that higher value, so \\( v \\) will be replaced by that higher value, and hence \\( v \\) will not be present at that position in the next step.\n\nTherefore, the key is that a value \\( v \\) can survive to the next step only if, for every occurrence of \\( v \\), all its neighbors are \\( \\leq v \\). Otherwise, if any neighbor is greater than \\( v \\), then the value at that position will be increased to the neighbor's value, so \\( v \\) is removed from that position.\n\nTherefore, the set of values can only decrease over time because some values get \"pushed out\" by higher values in their neighborhoods, but new values cannot be introduced because all the new values are already present in the previous configuration.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values is a non-increasing sequence over time. So starting from the initial configuration, which has some number of distinct values, say \\( D_0 \\), which is a random variable since the initial values are random, then at each time step \\( t \\), \\( D_{t} \\leq D_{t-1} \\), and so after 100 time steps, \\( D_{100} \\) is some non-increasing random variable. The question is to compute \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_{100}] \\), the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds.\n\nHmm. So, given that the process can only decrease the number of distinct values over time, and we start with an expected number of distinct values in the initial configuration, which for \\( n \\) independent uniform random variables on [0,1], the expected number of distinct values is \\( n \\times (1 - 1/e) \\) approximately? Wait, actually, for continuous uniform variables, the expected number of distinct values among \\( n \\) i.i.d. samples is \\( n \\times (1 - e^{-1}) \\approx 0.632n \\). Wait, let me recall the formula.\n\nYes, for continuous distributions, the probability that two distinct samples are equal is zero, so actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values among \\( n \\) i.i.d. continuous random variables is \\( n \\times \\mathbb{P}(x_1 \\neq x_2) \\) for two variables, but actually, no, that's not quite right. Wait, actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values is \\( \\sum_{k=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(x_k \\text{ is the maximum}) \\times n \\), but actually, more precisely, for each element, the probability that it is the unique maximum among all elements is \\( \\frac{1}{n} \\), so the expected number of distinct values is \\( n \\times \\frac{1}{n} \\times 1 \\) (for being the unique maximum) plus... Wait, actually, perhaps not exactly.\n\nWait, actually, let me recall that for continuous independent variables, the expected number of distinct values among n samples is equal to \\( n \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{n}} \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} (1 - \\frac{k}{n})^n \\right) \\)? Hmm, no, perhaps I'm confusing with something else.\n\nWait, actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values can be calculated as follows: For each position \\( i \\), define an indicator random variable \\( I_i \\) which is 1 if \\( a_i \\) is distinct from all other \\( a_j \\) for \\( j \\neq i \\), and 0 otherwise. Then, the total number of distinct values \\( D \\) is equal to \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{2025} I_i \\). Therefore, \\( \\mathbb{E}[D] = \\sum_{i=1}^{2025} \\mathbb{P}(a_i \\text{ is unique}) \\).\n\nHowever, in the case of continuous distributions, the probability that any two variables are equal is zero, so actually, the expectation \\( \\mathbb{E}[D] \\) is equal to \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{2025} \\mathbb{P}(a_i \\text{ is the unique maximum among } \\{a_1, \\ldots, a_{2025}\\}) \\).\n\nWait, actually, that might be the case? Let me think. For continuous variables, the probability that two variables are equal is zero, so the only way for a value to be \"unique\" in the sense of being different from all others is if it is the unique maximum? Wait, no. Wait, actually, even if a value is not the maximum, it can still be unique in its value. For example, suppose you have values: 0.1, 0.3, 0.3, 0.5. Then, the value 0.1 is unique, the values 0.3 are not unique, and 0.5 is unique. So, the number of distinct values is 3, even though the maximum is unique. So, actually, the number of distinct values is equal to the number of unique values across all the samples, which can be thought of as the cardinality of the set \\( \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\} \\).\n\nTherefore, for continuous random variables, the expectation of the cardinality of such a set is known. Let me recall that for n independent uniform [0,1] random variables, the expected number of distinct values is \\( n \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^n} \\sum_{k=1}^n \\binom{n}{k} (1 - \\frac{k}{n})^n \\right) \\)? Wait, no, perhaps another way.\n\nAlternatively, the expectation can be calculated using the linearity of expectation and considering for each value \\( x \\in [0,1] \\), the probability that \\( x \\) is present in the sample. Then, the expected number of distinct values is the integral over [0,1] of the probability that \\( x \\) is present in the sample. Hmm, that might be a better approach.\n\nLet me recall that for continuous uniform variables, the expected number of distinct values is \\( \\int_{0}^{1} \\mathbb{P}(x \\text{ is present in } \\{a_1, \\ldots, a_n\\}) dx \\). Since for any \\( x \\in [0,1] \\), the probability that \\( x \\) is present in the sample is equal to \\( 1 - \\mathbb{P}(\\text{all } a_i < x \\text{ or all } a_i > x) \\). Wait, actually, more precisely, the probability that \\( x \\) is not present is the probability that all \\( a_i \\) are either less than \\( x \\) or greater than \\( x \\). Wait, no, actually, the probability that \\( x \\) is not present is the probability that all \\( a_i \\) are either strictly less than \\( x \\) or strictly greater than \\( x \\). Since the variables are continuous, the probability that any \\( a_i \\) equals \\( x \\) is zero, so the presence of \\( x \\) in the sample set is equivalent to there existing at least one \\( a_i \\) in [x, x + dx] for some infinitesimal interval around \\( x \\), but perhaps it's better to formalize it as follows:\n\nFor a fixed \\( x \\in [0,1] \\), the probability that \\( x \\) is not in the sample is equal to the probability that all \\( a_i \\) are either less than \\( x \\) or greater than \\( x \\). Since the variables are independent, this is equal to:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{P}(\\text{all } a_i < x \\text{ or all } a_i > x) \\)\n\nBut actually, since the events \"all \\( a_i < x \\)\" and \"all \\( a_i > x \\)\" are mutually exclusive (they can't all be less than \\( x \\) and all be greater than \\( x \\)), so we can write this as:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{P}(\\text{all } a_i < x) + \\mathbb{P}(\\text{all } a_i > x) \\)\n\nEach \\( a_i \\) is uniform on [0,1], so:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{P}(\\text{all } a_i < x) = x^n \\)\n\n\\( \\mathbb{P}(\\text{all } a_i > x) = (1 - x)^n \\)\n\nTherefore, the probability that \\( x \\) is not present in the sample is \\( x^n + (1 - x)^n \\). Therefore, the probability that \\( x \\) is present in the sample is \\( 1 - x^n - (1 - x)^n \\).\n\nTherefore, the expected number of distinct values \\( \\mathbb{E}[D] \\) is the integral from 0 to 1 of \\( 1 - x^n - (1 - x)^n \\) dx.\n\nSo, for our case, with \\( n = 2025 \\), the initial expected number of distinct values is:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{E}[D_0] = \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[1 - x^{2025} - (1 - x)^{2025}\\right] dx \\)\n\nHmm, okay, that's a concrete expression, but I don't know if we can simplify it further. However, perhaps we don't need the exact value of \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_0] \\), because the question is about \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_{100}] \\), the expected number after 100 steps.\n\nGiven that the number of distinct values is non-increasing over time, \\( D_t \\leq D_{t-1} \\), so \\( D_{100} \\leq D_0 \\). Therefore, \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_{100}] \\leq \\mathbb{E}[D_0] \\). But we need to find \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_{100}] \\).\n\nHmm. Since the process is non-increasing in the number of distinct values, perhaps after enough time steps, the system will reach a fixed point where no further changes occur? That is, when \\( D_t = D_{t+1} \\). So, perhaps after some time, the number of distinct values stops decreasing, and then stays constant? So, maybe after a certain number of steps, the system has reached a state where the maximum values have propagated in such a way that no new distinct values are being lost? Wait, but actually, since the values can only stay the same or increase, the process must eventually stabilize, right?\n\nWait, actually, in such a system where each value is the maximum of itself and its neighbors, perhaps the process will eventually reach a configuration where all the values are equal? Because if you keep taking the maximum of each element with its neighbors, then over time, the values can only increase, and since the maximum possible value is 1, and the minimum is 0, but actually, the values can't exceed 1 because they're all taken from [0,1].\n\nWait a second, actually, no. Wait, the values can only increase or stay the same, but they can't exceed 1 because all the initial values are in [0,1], and the maximum of three numbers in [0,1] is also in [0,1]. So the values are confined to [0,1], and they can't exceed 1. Similarly, they can't go below their current value. So, over time, the entire configuration can only approach some sort of equilibrium where the values are \"maximized\" in some way.\n\nWait, actually, in such a system with a circular arrangement, perhaps the process will eventually make all the values equal to the global maximum of the initial configuration? Because if you have a global maximum somewhere, then over time, that maximum will propagate to its neighbors, and then those neighbors will propagate it further, until eventually all the values become equal to the global maximum?\n\nWait, let me think of a small example. Let's say we have 3 numbers in a circle: \\( a_1, a_2, a_3 \\). Suppose initially they are \\( x, y, z \\), with \\( z \\) being the global maximum. Then, at the next step, each \\( a_i \\) becomes the maximum of itself and its neighbors. So:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) \\)\n\nSince \\( z \\) is the global maximum, then in particular, \\( a_3^{(0)} = z \\), so \\( a_1^{(1)} \\geq \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) \\geq z \\), but actually, \\( a_3^{(0)} = z \\), so \\( a_1^{(1)} \\geq z \\), but since \\( z \\) is already the maximum, \\( a_1^{(1)} = z \\), unless \\( a_1^{(0)} \\) or \\( a_2^{(0)} \\) is greater than \\( z \\), but since \\( z \\) is the global maximum, that can't be. So actually, \\( a_1^{(1)} = z \\), same with \\( a_2^{(1)} = z \\), and \\( a_3^{(1)} = z \\). So after one step, all values become \\( z \\). Therefore, in this case, after one step, all values are equal to the global maximum. Therefore, in this 3-element case, it stabilizes immediately.\n\nHmm, interesting. Let me try with 4 elements. Suppose we have four numbers in a circle: \\( a, b, c, d \\), with \\( d \\) being the global maximum. Then, at the next step:\n\n\\( a^{(1)} = \\max(d, a, b) \\)\n\n\\( b^{(1)} = \\max(a, b, c) \\)\n\n\\( c^{(1)} = \\max(b, c, d) \\)\n\n\\( d^{(1)} = \\max(c, d, a) \\)\n\nSince \\( d \\) is the global maximum, \\( \\max(d, a, b) = d \\), \\( \\max(b, c, d) = d \\), and \\( \\max(c, d, a) = d \\). However, \\( \\max(a, b, c) \\) might not be \\( d \\), unless one of \\( a, b, c \\) is \\( d \\), which it isn't, since \\( d \\) is the unique global maximum? Wait, hold on, actually in my example I assumed \\( d \\) is the global maximum, but maybe others could also be equal to \\( d \\). Let me assume that \\( d \\) is strictly greater than \\( a, b, c \\).\n\nThen, \\( a^{(1)} = d \\), \\( b^{(1)} = \\max(a, b, c) \\), which is less than \\( d \\), \\( c^{(1)} = d \\), and \\( d^{(1)} = \\max(c, d, a) = d \\). Therefore, after the first step, the values are: \\( d, \\max(a,b,c), d, d \\). So now, the second value is still less than \\( d \\), so in the next step:\n\n\\( a^{(2)} = \\max(d^{(1)}, a^{(1)}, b^{(1)}) = \\max(d, d, \\max(a,b,c)) = d \\)\n\n\\( b^{(2)} = \\max(a^{(1)}, b^{(1)}, c^{(1)}) = \\max(d, \\max(a,b,c), d) = d \\)\n\n\\( c^{(2)} = \\max(b^{(1)}, c^{(1)}, d^{(1)}) = \\max(\\max(a,b,c), d, d) = d \\)\n\n\\( d^{(2)} = \\max(c^{(1)}, d^{(1)}, a^{(1)}) = \\max(d, d, d) = d \\)\n\nTherefore, after the second step, all values become \\( d \\). So in the 4-element case, it takes two steps to reach the all-\\( d \\) configuration.\n\nHmm, so in the 3-element case, it took 1 step, in the 4-element case, it took 2 steps. Maybe in general, for an n-element circle, it takes n steps? Wait, let me test with n=2.\n\nWait, n=2: two elements in a circle: \\( a_1, a_2 \\). Then, each second, each is replaced by the maximum of itself and its neighbor. Since it's a circle, each has one neighbor. So:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) \\). Wait, hold on, actually, for n=2, each element has two neighbors: for \\( a_1 \\), neighbors are \\( a_2 \\) and \\( a_2 \\)? Wait, no, in a circle with two elements, each element has two neighbors, which are both the other element. Wait, actually, in a circle with two elements, each element has two neighbors, but since it's a circle of two, \\( a_1 \\)'s left neighbor is \\( a_2 \\), and its right neighbor is also \\( a_2 \\)? Wait, no, in a circle of two, each element has two neighbors: the next and the previous, which are both the other element. So, for \\( a_1 \\), neighbors are \\( a_2 \\) (previous) and \\( a_2 \\) (next)? Wait, actually, in a circle of two, \\( a_1 \\)'s left neighbor is \\( a_2 \\), and its right neighbor is \\( a_2 \\), because after \\( a_1 \\) comes \\( a_2 \\), and before \\( a_1 \\) is \\( a_2 \\). Therefore, in the update rule, for \\( a_1 \\), we have \\( \\max(a_{0}, a_1, a_{2}) \\), but with indices modulo 2, so \\( a_0 = a_2 \\), and \\( a_{2} = a_1 \\). Wait, actually, the problem statement says: \"for all \\( i=1,2,\\ldots,2025 \\) (where \\( a_0 = a_{2025} \\) and \\( a_{2026} = a_1 \\))\". So in the case of n=2, \\( a_0 = a_2 \\), and \\( a_{3} = a_1 \\). Therefore, for each \\( i \\), the three elements considered are \\( a_{i-1} \\), \\( a_i \\), \\( a_{i+1} \\). But in the case of n=2, for \\( i=1 \\), that would be \\( a_0 = a_2 \\), \\( a_1 \\), \\( a_2 \\). Similarly for \\( i=2 \\), it would be \\( a_1 \\), \\( a_2 \\), \\( a_3 = a_1 \\). Therefore, effectively, each \\( a_i \\) is replaced by the maximum of the other element and itself. Wait, so in effect, each \\( a_i^{(1)} = \\max(a_{i-1}^{(0)}, a_i^{(0)}, a_{i+1}^{(0)}) \\), but in the case of n=2, this reduces to \\( \\max(a_{2}^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) \\). Similarly for \\( a_2^{(1)} \\). Therefore, in the n=2 case, each element becomes the maximum of the two elements. Therefore, after one step, both elements become the maximum of the original two elements, so they both become equal to the global maximum, and then they stay that way forever. So, in n=2, it takes 1 step to reach the fixed point.\n\nSo for n=2: 1 step, n=3: 1 step, n=4: 2 steps. Wait, so maybe for n elements, the number of steps required to reach the fixed point is floor(n/2) or something? Wait, let me see n=5.\n\nTake n=5. Let's say we have 5 elements, with \\( a_5 \\) being the global maximum. Let me denote them as \\( a, b, c, d, e \\), with \\( e \\) being the global maximum. Then, step 1:\n\n\\( a^{(1)} = \\max(e, a, b) = e \\)\n\n\\( b^{(1)} = \\max(a, b, c) \\)\n\n\\( c^{(1)} = \\max(b, c, d) \\)\n\n\\( d^{(1)} = \\max(c, d, e) = e \\)\n\n\\( e^{(1)} = \\max(d, e, a) = e \\)\n\nSo after step 1, the values are: \\( e, \\max(a,b,c), \\max(b,c,d), e, e \\).\n\nNow, in step 2:\n\n\\( a^{(2)} = \\max(e^{(1)}, a^{(1)}, b^{(1)}) = \\max(e, e, \\max(a,b,c)) = e \\)\n\n\\( b^{(2)} = \\max(a^{(1)}, b^{(1)}, c^{(1)}) = \\max(e, \\max(a,b,c), \\max(b,c,d)) \\)\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me assign some numerical values to make it concrete. Suppose the initial values are \\( a = 0.1 \\), \\( b = 0.2 \\), \\( c = 0.3 \\), \\( d = 0.4 \\), \\( e = 1.0 \\). So e is the global maximum.\n\nFirst step:\n\n\\( a^{(1)} = \\max(e, a, b) = \\max(1.0, 0.1, 0.2) = 1.0 \\)\n\n\\( b^{(1)} = \\max(a, b, c) = \\max(0.1, 0.2, 0.3) = 0.3 \\)\n\n\\( c^{(1)} = \\max(b, c, d) = \\max(0.2, 0.3, 0.4) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( d^{(1)} = \\max(c, d, e) = \\max(0.3, 0.4, 1.0) = 1.0 \\)\n\n\\( e^{(1)} = \\max(d, e, a) = \\max(0.4, 1.0, 0.1) = 1.0 \\)\n\nSo after step 1, the values are: [1.0, 0.3, 0.4, 1.0, 1.0].\n\nNow step 2:\n\n\\( a^{(2)} = \\max(e^{(1)}, a^{(1)}, b^{(1)}) = \\max(1.0, 1.0, 0.3) = 1.0 \\)\n\n\\( b^{(2)} = \\max(a^{(1)}, b^{(1)}, c^{(1)}) = \\max(1.0, 0.3, 0.4) = 1.0 \\)\n\n\\( c^{(2)} = \\max(b^{(1)}, c^{(1)}, d^{(1)}) = \\max(0.3, 0.4, 1.0) = 1.0 \\)\n\n\\( d^{(2)} = \\max(c^{(1)}, d^{(1)}, e^{(1)}) = \\max(0.4, 1.0, 1.0) = 1.0 \\)\n\n\\( e^{(2)} = \\max(d^{(1)}, e^{(1)}, a^{(1)}) = \\max(1.0, 1.0, 1.0) = 1.0 \\)\n\nSo after step 2, all values are 1.0. So for n=5, it took 2 steps. Hmm, so n=2:1, n=3:1, n=4:2, n=5:2. Hmm, so perhaps the number of steps required is the ceiling of n/2 minus 1? Wait, n=2: ceiling(2/2)=1, which matches. n=3: ceiling(3/2)=2, but it took 1 step. Hmm, no, not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's the floor((n-1)/2). For n=2: floor(1/2)=0, nope. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe it's related to the maximum distance from the global maximum?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in general, for a circle of n nodes, the number of steps required for the maximum to propagate around the entire circle is n steps? But in the n=3 case, it took 1 step, which is n/3? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, in the n=3 case, the maximum is adjacent to itself after one step, but in n=4, it took two steps. Wait, in n=4, with the maximum at position 4, then after first step, positions 1 and 3 and 4 become the maximum, but position 2 remains lower. Then in the next step, position 2 can be updated based on its neighbors, which are now the maximum, so it gets updated to the maximum in the next step. So in the n=4 case, the distance from the initial maximum to the furthest point is 2 (since it's a circle, the maximum is at position 4, the furthest point is position 2, which is two steps away). So perhaps the number of steps needed is equal to the maximum distance from any initial maximum to any other point?\n\nWait, but in the n=4 case with the maximum at position 4, the furthest point is position 2, which is two steps away (going clockwise: 4 -> 1 -> 2). So that's two steps, and it took two steps to propagate the maximum to that point. Similarly, in the n=5 case, the furthest point from the initial maximum (position 5) would be position 3, which is two steps away (5 ->1 ->2 ->3? Wait, no, in a circle of 5, from position 5, going clockwise, the next is 1, then 2, then 3. So the distance from 5 to 3 is two steps? Wait, actually, the distance between 5 and 3: moving clockwise, it's 5 to 1 to 2 to 3, which is three steps? Wait, no, distance in a circle is usually the minimal number of steps in either direction. So from position 5 to position 3: clockwise it's 5 ->1->2->3 (three steps), counter-clockwise it's 5->4->3 (two steps). Therefore, the minimal distance is two steps. So the furthest point from the initial maximum is two steps away, and it took two steps to propagate the maximum there.\n\nIn the n=4 case, the furthest point from the initial maximum (position 4) is position 2, which is two steps away (either direction: clockwise: 4->1->2 (two steps), or counter-clockwise: 4->3->2 (two steps)). So that matches.\n\nIn the n=3 case, the furthest point from the initial maximum (say position 3) is position 1 or 2, which are one step away in either direction. Therefore, it took one step to propagate the maximum to those positions.\n\nSo perhaps in general, for a circle of n nodes, if you have a single global maximum, then the number of steps required for the maximum to propagate to all nodes is equal to the maximal minimal distance from any node to the initial maximum? Wait, but actually, in the process, the maximum can spread in both directions simultaneously, right?\n\nWait, in the n=4 case, with the maximum at position 4, after one step, positions 1, 3, and 4 become the maximum, but position 2 is still low. Then in the next step, position 2 is adjacent to position 1 (which is now maximum) and position 3 (which is also maximum), so in the next step, position 2 becomes the maximum as well. So the propagation can happen in both directions at the same time.\n\nTherefore, the time to propagate the maximum to all nodes is equal to the maximum distance from the initial maximum to any node, but since the propagation can happen in both directions simultaneously, the time needed is actually the ceiling of (n / 2) ?\n\nWait, for n=3, ceiling(3/2)=2, but it only took 1 step. Hmm, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, for a circle, the maximum distance from any node to the initial maximum is floor(n/2). So, for even n, it's n/2, for odd n, it's (n-1)/2. So the number of steps needed would be equal to that maximum distance. Let me check:\n\nFor n=3: maximum distance from any node to the initial maximum is 1 (since in a circle of 3, the furthest any node is from another is 1 step). So ceiling(n/2)=2, but the maximum distance is 1. Hmm, so perhaps it's the maximum distance, which for a circle is floor(n/2). For n=3, floor(3/2)=1, which matches. For n=4, floor(4/2)=2, which matches. For n=5, floor(5/2)=2, which also matches the example above where it took two steps. For n=2, floor(2/2)=1, which matches the one step needed. So that seems to hold.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in general, for a circle of n nodes with a single global maximum, the time required for the maximum to propagate to all nodes is floor(n/2). Therefore, after floor(n/2) steps, all nodes will have the global maximum, and then it remains constant thereafter.\n\nHowever, in our problem, we don't necessarily have a single global maximum; the initial configuration has 2025 numbers, each uniformly random in [0,1]. So there might be multiple local maxima, or the global maximum could be unique or there could be ties. But in the continuous case, the probability that two points are exactly equal is zero, so with probability 1, all the maxima are unique. Wait, actually, in continuous distributions, the probability that any two of the initial \\( a_i \\) are equal is zero, so almost surely, all the values are distinct, and there is a unique global maximum.\n\nTherefore, with probability 1, there is a unique global maximum in the initial configuration. Therefore, the process will proceed such that this global maximum will propagate outward in both directions around the circle, and the number of steps required to propagate it to all nodes is floor(n/2). Since n=2025 is odd, floor(2025/2) = 1012. So, floor(2025/2)=1012.5 floored is 1012. Wait, 2025 divided by 2 is 1012.5, so floor(2025/2)=1012. Therefore, after 1012 steps, the global maximum would have propagated to all nodes? Wait, but in my earlier small examples, for n=5 (which is odd), floor(5/2)=2, and it took 2 steps to propagate the maximum to all nodes, which worked. For n=3, floor(3/2)=1, which also worked. For n=4, floor(4/2)=2, which also worked. So yes, seems like the number of steps required for the global maximum to propagate to all nodes is floor(n/2). Therefore, for n=2025, floor(2025/2)=1012, so after 1012 steps, the entire circle should have the global maximum, and then it remains there forever.\n\nTherefore, after 1012 steps, the configuration becomes all equal to the global maximum, and remains constant thereafter. Since we are asked about 100 seconds, which is much less than 1012, so after 100 steps, the system has not yet reached the fixed point where all values are equal to the global maximum. Therefore, the number of distinct values is still decreasing, but hasn't reached the minimal possible value (which is 1, if all values become equal). Therefore, we need to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps.\n\nHmm, so this seems complicated. Maybe we can model the expected number of distinct values at time t as some function that decreases over time, but how exactly?\n\nAlternatively, maybe after t steps, the expected number of distinct values is equal to the expected number of initial values that are still \"active\" in some sense, meaning that their value has not been overtaken by a higher value in their neighborhood within t steps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps each value has some probability of surviving until time t, and the expected number of distinct values is the sum over all initial values of the probability that that value is still present at time t.\n\nWait, that might be a way to approach it. Let me think.\n\nSuppose we consider each initial value \\( a_i^{(0)} \\). The value \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) will be present in the configuration at time \\( t \\) if and only if, in the neighborhood of \\( i \\) at each step, the maximum never exceeded \\( a_i^{(0)} \\). Wait, actually, no. Wait, more precisely, for \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) to still be present at time \\( t \\), it must be that in the neighborhood of \\( i \\) at each step, the value at \\( i \\) was never forced to increase beyond \\( a_i^{(0)} \\). Wait, actually, since each \\( a_i^{(t)} \\geq a_i^{(t-1)} \\), so the value at position \\( i \\) can only increase or stay the same. Therefore, \\( a_i^{(t)} \\geq a_i^{(0)} \\). Therefore, the value \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) will only be present in the configuration at time \\( t \\) if \\( a_i^{(t)} = a_i^{(0)} \\), which requires that in all the updates from time 0 to time \\( t \\), the value at position \\( i \\) was never required to increase beyond \\( a_i^{(0)} \\). That is, for each step \\( s = 1, 2, \\ldots, t \\), when computing \\( a_i^{(s)} \\), the maximum of \\( a_{i-1}^{(s-1)}, a_i^{(s-1)}, a_{i+1}^{(s-1)} \\) was equal to \\( a_i^{(s-1)} \\), so that \\( a_i^{(s)} = a_i^{(s-1)} \\). Therefore, in other words, for \\( a_i^{(t)} = a_i^{(0)} \\), it must be that in all previous steps, the value at position \\( i \\) was the maximum of its neighbors at that step. Otherwise, if at any step \\( s \\), the maximum of the neighbors was greater than \\( a_i^{(s-1)} \\), then \\( a_i^{(s)} \\) would jump to that higher value, and \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) would no longer be present at position \\( i \\).\n\nTherefore, for each initial value \\( a_i^{(0)} \\), the probability that it survives until time \\( t \\) is equal to the probability that, for all \\( s = 1, 2, \\ldots, t \\), \\( a_i^{(s)} = \\max\\{a_{i-1}^{(s-1)}, a_i^{(s-1)}, a_{i+1}^{(s-1)}\\} \\).\n\nThis seems quite complex because the survival of \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) at position \\( i \\) depends on the values of its neighbors evolving over time, which in turn depend on their own neighbors, etc. This interdependency makes it difficult to compute directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the expected number of distinct values by considering that each value \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) has a certain probability of surviving until time \\( t \\), and then the expected number is the sum over all \\( i \\) of these survival probabilities. However, this would overcount because if two different initial values \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) and \\( a_j^{(0)} \\) both survive until time \\( t \\), they might actually be equal in value at time \\( t \\), so they wouldn't contribute to the distinct count. Therefore, this approach might not work because the events of different values surviving are not independent, and overlapping survival could lead to duplicates.\n\nHmm, this seems tricky. Maybe another approach is needed.\n\nWait, going back to the earlier observation that the number of distinct values is a non-increasing random variable, starting from some \\( D_0 \\), and we need \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_{100}] \\). Since the process can only decrease the number of distinct values over time, perhaps after a sufficient number of steps, the expected number of distinct values stabilizes or approaches some limit?\n\nAlternatively, maybe after many steps, the expected number of distinct values approaches 1, since eventually all values will converge to the global maximum almost surely? Because with probability 1, the global maximum will propagate to all positions in finite time (as we saw for n=2025, it would take 1012 steps), so after 1012 steps, all values are equal to the global maximum, and hence the number of distinct values is 1. Therefore, after 100 steps, which is much less than 1012, the expected number of distinct values is somewhere between 1 and the initial expected number \\( \\mathbb{E}[D_0] \\).\n\nBut how much exactly?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the \"radius\" of influence of each initial value. For a given initial value \\( a_i^{(0)} \\), how far can it \"influence\" the values in the circle over time? If its value is the maximum in some neighborhood, then it can spread outwards. However, since higher values can override it, the survival of \\( a_i^{(0)} \\) depends on it being the maximum in its neighborhood at each step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the problem as a Markov chain where the state is the current set of distinct values, but with 2025 elements, that's intractable.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that each position's value is non-decreasing, so the final value at each position is the maximum of the initial values in some window around that position?\n\nWait, that might be a key insight. Let me think: since each update sets \\( a_i^{(t)} \\) to the maximum of itself and its two neighbors from the previous step, then over time, the value at each position \\( i \\) at time \\( t \\) is the maximum of the initial values in some expanding window around \\( i \\).\n\nSpecifically, perhaps after \\( t \\) steps, the value at position \\( i \\) is the maximum of the initial values in the interval \\( [i - t, i + t] \\) modulo the circle? Wait, maybe.\n\nLet me try to formalize this idea. Suppose we have a circular arrangement of positions 1 through n. Let's consider the value at position \\( i \\) at time \\( t \\). At each step, the value at position \\( i \\) is the maximum of itself and its immediate neighbors. So, over time, the influence of each position spreads outwards.\n\nIn other words, the value at position \\( i \\) at time \\( t \\) is the maximum of all initial values in a neighborhood that has grown by one position on each side with each time step. So after \\( t \\) steps, the neighborhood around \\( i \\) would include positions \\( i - t, i - t + 1, \\ldots, i + t \\), modulo n. Therefore, the value at position \\( i \\) at time \\( t \\) is the maximum of the initial values in the interval of length \\( 2t + 1 \\) centered at \\( i \\). However, since the circle is finite, this interval wraps around.\n\nWait, let me test this idea with the small example of n=3, t=1.\n\nIn n=3, at time t=1, each position's value is the maximum of its immediate neighbors and itself. So for position 1, it's the max of positions 3, 1, 2. Which is the same as the maximum of all three positions, since it's a circle. Wait, but according to the window idea, after t=1 step, the window around position 1 would be positions 1 - 1 = 0 (which is 3), 1, and 1 + 1 = 2. So exactly the three positions. Therefore, the value at position 1 at t=1 is indeed the maximum of all three initial positions. Similarly for all positions, so after one step, all positions have the global maximum, which matches our earlier example.\n\nAnother example: n=4, t=1. For position 1, the window would be positions 4, 1, 2. So the max of those three. For position 2, it would be positions 1, 2, 3. For position 3: positions 2, 3, 4. For position 4: positions 3, 4, 1. Therefore, after one step, each position's value is the maximum of three consecutive initial positions. Then at time t=2, the window would be positions 4 - 2 = 2, 4 -1 = 3, 4, 4 +1 = 5 (which is 1), 4 +2 = 6 (which is 2). Wait, hold on, perhaps my initial intuition was oversimplified.\n\nWait, perhaps the window grows by one on each side each time? Wait, let me think again.\n\nActually, in the first step (t=1), each position's value is the maximum of itself and its two immediate neighbors, which corresponds to a window of size 3 centered at that position (on the circle). In the next step (t=2), each position's value is the maximum of its current value (which was the max of a window of size 3) and its new neighbors. Wait, but the neighbors have also been updated, so their values are now the maxima of their own windows. This complicates things because the windows are overlapping and the updates are interdependent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after t steps, the value at position i is the maximum over all initial values in the interval [i - t, i + t] modulo n. Let me see if that holds for n=4, t=2.\n\nTake n=4, positions 1,2,3,4. Let’s assume initial values are a,b,c,d in positions 1,2,3,4 respectively, with d being the global maximum.\n\nAt t=1:\n\nPosition 1: max(d, a, b)\n\nPosition 2: max(a, b, c)\n\nPosition 3: max(b, c, d)\n\nPosition 4: max(c, d, a)\n\nAt t=2:\n\nPosition 1: max( position1 at t=1, position4 at t=1, position2 at t=1 )\n\n= max( max(d,a,b), max(c,d,a), max(a,b,c) )\n\nSimilarly, position 2: max( position1 at t=1, position2 at t=1, position3 at t=1 )\n\n= max( max(d,a,b), max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d) )\n\nSimilarly, position3: max( position2 at t=1, position3 at t=1, position4 at t=1 )\n\n= max( max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d), max(c,d,a) )\n\nPosition4: max( position3 at t=1, position4 at t=1, position1 at t=1 )\n\n= max( max(b,c,d), max(c,d,a), max(d,a,b) )\n\nHmm, so at t=2, each position's value is the maximum of all the initial values except perhaps one? Let me see:\n\nLooking at position1's value at t=2: it's the maximum of the three values: max(d,a,b), max(c,d,a), and max(a,b,c). Each of these maxima includes at least some of the initial values. Specifically:\n\n- max(d,a,b) includes a,b,d\n\n- max(c,d,a) includes a,c,d\n\n- max(a,b,c) includes a,b,c\n\nTherefore, the maximum of these three would be the maximum of a,b,c,d. Because all four initial values are included in at least one of the three maxima. Similarly, for any position at t=2, the value is the maximum over all four initial values. Hence, after two steps in n=4, all positions have the global maximum.\n\nSo in this case, the value at each position after t=2 is indeed the global maximum, which is the maximum over all initial values. So in this case, after t=2 steps (which is floor(n/2)=2), all positions have the global maximum. So the window idea seems to hold here: after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum over an interval of length 2t+1 (or something like that) centered at the position. Wait, in n=4 and t=2, the window would need to cover all four positions, which is 2t+1=5, but since it's a circle of 4, that wraps around.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum of the initial values in the interval of length 2t+1 centered at that position, modulo n. In the n=4 case with t=2, that would be an interval of length 5, which modulo 4 is equivalent to covering all four positions. So that works.\n\nSimilarly, in n=3, t=1: interval length 3, which covers all three positions. So that works.\n\nIn the n=5 case with t=2: Let's see.\n\nSuppose n=5, positions 1-5, with initial values a,b,c,d,e (e is the global maximum). Let's compute the value at position 1 after t=2 steps.\n\nAt t=1:\n\nPosition1: max(e, a, b)\n\nPosition2: max(a, b, c)\n\nPosition3: max(b, c, d)\n\nPosition4: max(c, d, e)\n\nPosition5: max(d, e, a)\n\nAt t=2:\n\nPosition1: max( position1(t=1), position5(t=1), position2(t=1) )\n\n= max( max(e,a,b), max(d,e,a), max(a,b,c) )\n\nSimilarly, the three maxima here include:\n\n- max(e,a,b): covers a,b,e\n\n- max(d,e,a): covers a,d,e\n\n- max(a,b,c): covers a,b,c\n\nTherefore, the maximum over these three is the maximum of a,b,c,d,e, which is e. Similarly, every position at t=2 will have the global maximum e. So indeed, after t=2 steps, all positions have the global maximum, which is the maximum over all initial values. So the window idea holds here as well.\n\nTherefore, in general, after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum over the initial values in a window of size 2t+1 centered at that position (with wrap-around on the circle). Since the circle has n positions, after t steps, the window size is 2t+1, so if 2t+1 ≥ n, then the window covers the entire circle, so the maximum is the global maximum. Otherwise, if 2t+1 < n, then the window is smaller.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a circle of size n, after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum of the initial values in a window of size 2t+1 centered at that position. Therefore, the number of distinct values at time t is equal to the number of distinct maxima over all these sliding windows of size 2t+1.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: given n=2025 independent uniform [0,1] random variables, what is the expected number of distinct values among all the maxima of all windows of size w=2t+1, where t=100, so w=2*100 +1=201.\n\nTherefore, in our case, n=2025, window size w=201, so the expected number of distinct values is the expectation over all initial configurations of the number of distinct maxima over all windows of size 201 in a circular arrangement of 2025 uniform [0,1] variables.\n\nHmm, this seems like a more manageable problem. Let me formalize this.\n\nLet me denote w = 2t + 1, where t=100, so w=201.\n\nWe have a circular arrangement of n=2025 independent uniform [0,1] random variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n \\). For each position i, consider the window \\( W_i = \\{X_{i - w + 1}, X_{i - w + 2}, \\ldots, X_{i}, X_{i + 1}, \\ldots, X_{i + w - 1}\\} \\), where the indices are taken modulo n to handle the circularity. The value at position i at time t is \\( \\max(W_i) \\). Therefore, the number of distinct values at time t is the number of distinct \\( \\max(W_i) \\) over all i.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to computing \\( \\mathbb{E}\\left[ \\# \\{ \\max(W_i) \\mid i = 1, \\ldots, n \\} \\right] \\).\n\nTo compute this expectation, we can use the linearity of expectation by considering for each possible value x in [0,1], the probability that x is the maximum of some window \\( W_i \\), but adjusted for overlaps and dependencies. However, this might be complicated due to overlaps.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the formula for expectation of the number of distinct values, which can be written as:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{E}\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}_{\\{ \\max(W_i) \\neq \\max(W_j) \\text{ for all } j \\neq i \\}} \\right] \\)\n\nBut this seems difficult to compute directly.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the formula that for non-negative integer-valued random variables, the expectation of the number of distinct values is the sum over all possible values of the probability that the value occurs. However, in this case, the values are continuous, so we can write:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{E}[D_t] = \\int_{0}^{1} \\mathbb{P}(\\text{there exists some } W_i \\text{ with } \\max(W_i) \\in [x, x + dx)) dx \\)\n\nBut integrating over all x, this is equivalent to:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{E}[D_t] = \\int_{0}^{1} \\mathbb{P}\\left( \\bigcup_{i=1}^n \\{ \\max(W_i) \\in [x, x + dx) \\} \\right) dx \\)\n\nBut this still seems complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can approximate or find a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, notice that for a window size w, the expected number of distinct maxima can be approximated by n times the probability that a given window has a maximum in a particular interval, but I'm not sure.\n\nWait, let's think differently. For each window \\( W_i \\), the maximum \\( M_i = \\max(W_i) \\). The number of distinct \\( M_i \\) is what we need. The expectation of the number of distinct \\( M_i \\) is equal to the sum over all i of the probability that \\( M_i \\) is distinct from all other \\( M_j \\) for \\( j \\neq i \\). But due to dependencies between the \\( M_i \\), this is not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, using the inclusion-exclusion principle, but that also seems intractable for large n.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as follows: for large n and w much smaller than n, the maxima of the windows are roughly independent? Although they are not independent, since the windows overlap.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each window \\( W_i \\) is a set of w consecutive variables, and the maximum of each window is a random variable. The total number of distinct maxima would then be the number of distinct maxima among these n random variables. However, these random variables are not independent, but perhaps for large n and moderate w, the dependence is weak, so we might approximate the expectation as n times the probability that a single window has a maximum greater than a certain value, integrated appropriately?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the fact that for any two overlapping windows, their maxima are positively correlated, but it's still complicated.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each element \\( X_j \\) has a certain probability of being the maximum in its own window and in overlapping windows. But this might not directly help.\n\nWait, going back to the initial problem: we have n=2025, window size w=201, so the window is about 10% of the circle (since 201/2025 ≈ 0.1). Therefore, each window is relatively small compared to the whole circle. In such a case, perhaps the maxima of the windows are mostly independent? Since the windows are not too overlapping? Wait, the windows are consecutive and overlapping by w-1 elements. For w=201, each adjacent window shares 200 elements. Therefore, the windows are highly overlapping. Therefore, their maxima are strongly correlated.\n\nHmm, so this complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the process as each position's value at time t is the maximum of a window of size w=2t+1, so as t increases, the windows grow, and eventually, when w ≥ n, all maxima are the global maximum. But here, w=201, which is much smaller than n=2025.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can approximate the expected number of distinct maxima by considering that each window's maximum is a random variable, and the expected number of distinct maxima is roughly n times the probability that a given window has a maximum in a particular interval, but I need to think carefully.\n\nAlternatively, let me recall that for independent random variables, the expected number of distinct values can sometimes be computed using the formula involving the probability that a particular variable is the maximum. But in our case, the maxima of the windows are dependent variables.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use linearity of expectation in a clever way. Let me consider that for each position i, the value \\( M_i = \\max(W_i) \\), and we want \\( \\mathbb{E}[ \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}_{M_i \\text{ is unique}} ] \\). But uniqueness is hard to handle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can write the expectation as:\n\n\\( \\mathbb{E}[D_t] = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(M_i \\text{ is the unique maximum among all } M_j) \\)\n\nBut again, this requires knowing the probability that \\( M_i \\) is the unique maximum, which is difficult due to dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, maybe an approximate approach is warranted here, given the problem's context (a competition problem). Perhaps the expected number of distinct values after t steps is roughly n - 2t, or something linear in t? Wait, but in our small examples:\n\nFor n=3, t=1: D_1=1 (since all become the global maximum). So n - 2t = 3 - 2 = 1, which matches.\n\nFor n=4, t=1: Let's see. In n=4 with t=1, each window is size 3. Let's compute the expected number of distinct maxima.\n\nTake four independent uniform variables. Each window of 3 variables: there are 4 windows (each missing one variable). The maximum of each window is the maximum of three variables. The expected number of distinct maxima can be calculated.\n\nWait, let me compute it for n=4, w=3 (t=1). Let's compute E[D_1].\n\nEach window is missing one variable. For example, the first window is variables 1,2,3; the second is 2,3,4; the third is 3,4,1; the fourth is 4,1,2.\n\nEach window's maximum is the max of three independent uniforms. The four maxima are the maxima of four different triples. What is the expected number of distinct maxima among these four?\n\nHmm, this might be manageable for n=4.\n\nLet me denote the four variables as X, Y, Z, W, each iid uniform on [0,1].\n\nThe four windows are:\n\n1. X,Y,Z: max1 = max(X,Y,Z)\n\n2. Y,Z,W: max2 = max(Y,Z,W)\n\n3. Z,W,X: max3 = max(Z,W,X)\n\n4. W,X,Y: max4 = max(W,X,Y)\n\nWe need to compute E[ |{max1, max2, max3, max4}| ]\n\nThis seems doable, although tedious.\n\nFirst, note that all four maxima are maxima of three variables, so each is in [0,1]. The expectation of the number of distinct values is equal to 1 + sum_{i<j} P(max_i ≠ max_j).\n\nWait, no, actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values is equal to the sum over all i of P(max_i is the unique maximum), but it's complicated. Alternatively, using inclusion-exclusion:\n\nE[D] = sum_{i=1}^4 P(max_i exists) - sum_{i<j} P(max_i = max_j) + sum_{i<j<k} P(max_i = max_j = max_k) - ... + (-1)^{n+1} P(all equal).\n\nBut since the variables are continuous, the probability that any two maxima are equal is zero, because the maxima are continuous random variables. Wait, actually, the maxima are continuous random variables, so the probability that any two are equal is zero. Therefore, the expectation of the number of distinct maxima is equal to the sum over i=1 to 4 of P(max_i is the unique maximum). However, due to symmetry, each P(max_i is the unique maximum) is equal, so E[D] = 4 * P(max1 is the unique maximum).\n\nTherefore, compute P(max1 is the unique maximum) = P(max1 > max2, max1 > max3, max1 > max4).\n\nBecause if max1 is the unique maximum, then it must be greater than all the other maxima.\n\nSo, compute P(max1 > max2, max1 > max3, max1 > max4).\n\nLet me denote M1 = max(X,Y,Z), M2 = max(Y,Z,W), M3 = max(Z,W,X), M4 = max(W,X,Y).\n\nWe need P(M1 > M2, M1 > M3, M1 > M4).\n\nThis seems complicated, but perhaps we can compute it using symmetry and some probability theory.\n\nFirst, note that due to the symmetry of the problem (all variables are iid and the setup is rotationally symmetric), the probability that M1 is the unique maximum is the same as the probability that any specific M_i is the unique maximum. Therefore, since there are four variables, the expected number of unique maxima is 4 * P(M1 is unique max).\n\nTherefore, we can compute P(M1 is unique max) by considering the joint distribution of M1, M2, M3, M4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute it step by step.\n\nFirst, let me consider that for M1 to be the unique maximum, it must be greater than M2, M3, and M4.\n\nLet me first compute P(M1 > M2). Then, given that M1 > M2, compute P(M1 > M3 | M1 > M2), and so on. But this might be too involved.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this using order statistics.\n\nAlternatively, let me make a substitution: since all variables are iid uniform, perhaps we can consider the joint distribution.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to compute the probability numerically, but since this is a thought process, let me try to find a pattern.\n\nWait, in the n=4, t=1 case, what is the expected number of distinct maxima?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the case where all variables are independent, the expected number of distinct maxima among overlapping windows can be approximated by something?\n\nAlternatively, note that for each window, the probability that its maximum is greater than a certain value x is 1 - (1 - x)^3, since it's the max of three independent uniforms. Then, perhaps the expected number of distinct maxima can be approximated by integrating over x the probability that some window has maximum in [x, x+dx], which would be similar to the integral I mentioned earlier.\n\nBut this is essentially the same as the expectation of the number of distinct values, which can be written as:\n\nE[D] = ∫_{0}^{1} P(∃ at least one window with max in [x, x+dx)) dx\n\nWhich is equal to ∫_{0}^{1} [1 - P(∀ windows, max < x)] dx\n\nTherefore, for our n=4, t=1 case, with w=3,\n\nE[D] = ∫_{0}^{1} [1 - P(M1 < x, M2 < x, M3 < x, M4 < x)] dx\n\nBut this requires knowing the joint distribution of the four maxima, which is complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can approximate this expectation for large n and moderate w?\n\nWait, in our original problem, n=2025 and w=201, which is much smaller than n. Maybe we can approximate the expected number of distinct maxima by n times the probability that a single window's maximum is in a particular interval, integrated over the interval. Wait, but this is similar to the expectation of the number of distinct values in n independent samples, but they are not independent.\n\nAlternatively, if the windows were independent, which they are not, then the expected number of distinct maxima would be n * (1 - (1 - x)^w) integrated over x, but this is not the case.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct maxima is approximately n * (1 - e^{-w}) or something? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, recall that for a single window of size w, the expected value of its maximum is \\( 1 - (1 - 1/n)^w \\) approximately? Wait, no, for a single uniform variable, the expectation of the maximum of w independent uniforms is \\( \\frac{w}{w+1} \\). So for a window of size w, the expected maximum is \\( \\frac{w}{w+1} \\).\n\nBut we need the expected number of distinct maxima over all windows.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the probability that two adjacent windows have different maxima is high, leading to many distinct maxima, but since the windows overlap a lot, maybe the number of distinct maxima is roughly proportional to w?\n\nAlternatively, given that each window of size w=201 has a maximum that is the highest among 201 points, and the windows are shifted by one each time, the overlap is 200 points, so the maxima could vary slowly, but over 2025 positions, there could be many fluctuations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct maxima is roughly n - w, but this is a shot in the dark.\n\nAlternatively, think about the following: each position's value at time t is the maximum over a window of size w=2t+1. Therefore, the value at each position is determined by the maximum in its local neighborhood. The key observation might be that the expected number of distinct values is equal to the expected number of local maxima in the initial configuration, but I’m not sure.\n\nWait, actually, in the process, after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum of a window of size w=2t+1. Therefore, the final configuration consists of these local maxima. The number of distinct values in this final configuration is equal to the number of distinct local maxima over all windows of size w. So we need the expected number of distinct maxima over all windows of size w in a random circular 