ually, I must have made a mistake here.\n\nLet me think again. Let me denote \\( M(t) = \\max_{i} a_i^{(t)} \\). Then, for each \\( i \\), \\( a_i^{(t+1)} = \\max(a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, a_{i+1}^{(t)}) \\leq M(t) \\), since all the terms inside the max are at most \\( M(t) \\). Therefore, \\( M(t+1) \\leq M(t) \\). But also, each \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\geq a_i^{(t)} \\), so \\( M(t+1) \\geq M(t) \\), since \\( M(t+1) \\) is the max over all \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\), and each \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\geq a_i^{(t)} \\), so the maximum over the \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\) is at least the maximum over the \\( a_i^{(t)} \\). Wait, so we have both \\( M(t+1) \\geq M(t) \\) and \\( M(t+1) \\leq M(t) \\), so actually \\( M(t+1) = M(t) \\). Therefore, the maximum of the system is non-decreasing and non-increasing, so it must be constant. Therefore, the global maximum never changes after the first step? Wait, that can't be right.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to clarify this. Suppose at time \\( t \\), the maximum value is \\( M(t) \\). Then, at time \\( t+1 \\), each \\( a_i^{(t+1)} = \\max(a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, a_{i+1}^{(t)}) \\). Therefore, for any \\( i \\) such that \\( a_i^{(t)} = M(t) \\), then \\( a_i^{(t+1)} = \\max(a_{i-1}^{(t)}, M(t), a_{i+1}^{(t)}) = M(t) \\). However, for any \\( i \\) where \\( a_i^{(t)} < M(t) \\), then \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\) is the maximum of two numbers less than or equal to \\( M(t) \\), and itself less than \\( M(t) \\). Therefore, unless one of its neighbors is \\( M(t) \\), \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\) could be less than \\( M(t) \\). Wait, but actually, even if neither neighbor is \\( M(t) \\), then \\( a_i^{(t+1)} \\) could still be equal to \\( M(t) \\) if, for example, the neighbor that was previously \\( M(t) \\) was adjacent? Wait, no, if a position \\( i \\) has neighbors \\( i-1 \\) and \\( i+1 \\), but if neither of those neighbors was \\( M(t) \\), then \\( a_{i-1}^{(t)} < M(t) \\), \\( a_{i+1}^{(t)} < M(t) \\), and \\( a_i^{(t)} < M(t) \\), so then \\( a_i^{(t+1)}