l equalities have probability zero, the expectation of D is actually equal to the expected number of \"distinct\" values, but in continuous space, the expectation is actually infinite? Wait no, that can’t be. Wait, actually, in continuous space, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is 1? Wait no, actually, the probability that all n are distinct is 1? Wait, in continuous space, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is also 1? Wait, actually, no. Wait, the probability that all n variables are distinct is 1, because the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is the limit as n approaches infinity of the probability that all are distinct, but actually, in finite n, for continuous variables, the probability that all n are distinct is 1? Wait, actually, no.\n\nWait, actually, in continuous space, the probability that two variables are equal is zero, but the probability that all n are distinct is also 1? Let me see for n=2: the probability that two independent uniform variables are distinct is 1, since the probability they are equal is zero. For n=3, the probability that all three are distinct is 1 minus the probability that at least two are equal. Since the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all three are distinct is 1? Wait, no, actually, even though the probability that any two are equal is zero, the probability that all three are equal is zero, but the probability that exactly two are equal is also zero? Wait, in continuous space, the probability that any two are equal is zero, and the probability that all three are equal is also zero, but the probability that exactly two are equal is also zero? Because if you fix two variables to be equal, that has probability zero, and then the third is different, but since the equality is a set of measure zero, the probability that exactly two are equal is zero. Therefore, for continuous variables, the probability that all variables are distinct is 1? Wait, that can't be, because if you have three variables, you can have two equal and one different, but since the equality is a set of measure zero, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all three are distinct is 1? Hmm, actually, yes, in continuous space, the probability that any two variables are equal is zero, so the only possibilities are that all are distinct or some subset are equal with measure zero. Wait, but in reality, when you have three variables, the event that exactly two are equal is a union over all pairs of the events that those two are equal and the third is different, but each of those events has probability zero, so the total probability is zero. Therefore, the probability that all three are distinct is 1? Wait, but that seems counterintuitive. Wait, actually, in continuous space, yes, because you can have all three distinct with probability 1. For example, if you have three numbers chosen independently and uniformly, the chance they all land exactly on the same point is zero, and the chance that two land on the same point and the third is different is also zero, because the set of all such possibilities has measure zero. So in fact, for continuous distributions, the probability that all n variables are distinct is 1. Wait, but that can't be right, can it?\n\nWait, actually, no. Wait, suppose you have n variables, each in [0,1]. The probability that all n are distinct is equal to 1 minus the probability that at least two are equal. Since the probability that any two are equal is zero, then the probability that all n are distinct is 1? Hmm, actually, in measure-theoretic terms, yes. Because the set where any two are equal has measure zero. So, in continuous space, the probability that all n variables are distinct is 1. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values is n, but that contradicts intuition because if you have n variables, you might think that some are equal, but in continuous space, they almost surely are all distinct? Wait, that seems strange.\n\nWait, let me think of an example. Let’s take n=2. Then, yes, the two numbers are almost surely distinct, so the expected number of distinct values is 2? Wait, but in reality, for two numbers, you can have two distinct values with probability 1, so the expected number of distinct values is 2? But that can’t be, because the number of distinct values can only be 1 or 2. Since the probability that they are equal is zero, the expected number is 2 * 0 + 1 * 1 = 1? Wait, no. Wait, the expectation is the sum over all possible values of the probability that the value occurs times the count? Wait, actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values for n continuous uniform variables is n minus the sum over i < j of the probability that a_i = a_j. But since each P(a_i = a_j) = 0, then the expectation would be n? Wait, that can’t be, because the number of distinct values can’t exceed n, but in expectation, it would be n? Wait, but actually, in continuous space, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the expectation of the number of distinct values is equal to the sum over all i of P(a_i is distinct from all others). Wait, but how do you compute that?\n\nAlternatively, the expectation of the number of distinct values is equal to the sum over i=1 to n of P(a_i is the maximum). Wait, no, that would just give 1, since only one is the maximum. Hmm, that’s not helpful.\n\nWait, let me recall that for discrete variables, the expectation of the number of distinct values is sum_{k=1}^n P(at least k distinct values). But in continuous case, since the number of distinct values is almost surely an integer between 1 and n, but the probabilities for each exact number might be tricky.\n\nAlternatively, here's a better approach. Let me consider the expectation E[D] where D is the number of distinct values among n i.i.d. uniform [0,1] random variables.\n\nWe can write D = \\sum_{i=1}^n I_i where I_i is an indicator that the i-th variable is the maximum among all variables. Wait no, that would just give 1, since only one is the maximum. That's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps D = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n I_{i \\neq j} I_{a_i \\neq a_j} ?\n\nNo, that seems complicated.\n\nWait, actually, in continuous space, the expectation of the number of distinct values is n minus the expected number of collisions. But since each collision (i.e., equality between two variables) has probability zero, the expected number of collisions is zero, so the expectation of the number of distinct values is n? But that can’t be, because the number of distinct values can’t be n with probability 1. Wait, actually, in continuous space, the probability that all n variables are distinct is 1, so the number of distinct values is almost surely n? Wait, that must be the case. Because in continuous space, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is 1. Therefore, the number of distinct values is almost surely n. Wait, but that seems to contradict my intuition. Wait, for example, if you have two numbers, the probability they are equal is zero, so the number of distinct values is almost surely 2? But in reality, when you pick two numbers randomly, they are almost surely distinct? Yes, that's correct. For example, if you pick two real numbers uniformly at random, the probability they are equal is zero. Similarly, for three numbers, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all three are distinct is 1. Wait, so in that case, for any finite n, the number of distinct values among n i.i.d. uniform [0,1] random variables is almost surely n? That is, the expectation is n?\n\nWait, that must be the case. Because the probability that two are equal is zero, so the probability that at least two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is 1. Therefore, the number of distinct values is almost surely n, so its expectation is n. But that seems to contradict my earlier examples with N=3 and N=4 where after one or two steps, the number of distinct values becomes 1. Wait, but in those examples, after some steps, the process forces all values to become equal, so the number of distinct values becomes 1 with probability 1 after some finite time. But in the problem statement, we are to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds, starting from 2025 numbers.\n\nWait, but in the N=3 case, after one step, the number of distinct values becomes 1 with probability 1, so the expectation is 1. Similarly, for N=4, after two steps, it becomes 1 with probability 1, so the expectation is 1. Wait, but in the initial step for N=4, the number of distinct values is almost surely 4, but after one step, it becomes something else, but in expectation?\n\nWait a second, perhaps my confusion arises from mixing the initial configuration with the process over time. Let me parse the problem again.\n\nThe problem states: Albert writes 2025 numbers a₁,…,a_{2025} in a circle, each uniformly and independently sampled from [0,1]. Then, each second, he simultaneously replaces each a_i with max(a_{i−1}, a_i, a_{i+1}), with indices modulo 2025. Then we are to compute the expected value of the number of distinct values remaining after 100 seconds.\n\nAh! So the initial configuration has expected number of distinct values equal to 2025, since they are all independent uniform [0,1] variables, so almost surely all distinct, so the initial expected number of distinct values is 2025? Wait, but hold on. Wait, in continuous space, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is 1, so the number of distinct values is almost surely 2025 at time 0. But after one step, the process may cause some values to become equal, so the number of distinct values could drop.\n\nWait, so the key is that even though initially they are all distinct with probability 1, after one step, some of them become equal, so the number of distinct values may decrease. Then, over time, the number of distinct values might decrease further until it reaches 1? Or maybe it stays at some number?\n\nWait, in the N=3 case, after one step, all become equal, so the number of distinct values becomes 1. For N=4, after at most two steps, it becomes 1. So perhaps for general N, after a certain number of steps, the number of distinct values becomes 1 with probability 1? If that's the case, then after 100 steps, which is way more than enough for any N, the expected number of distinct values would be 1?\n\nBut that seems too straightforward. The problem is from an Olympiad, so maybe it's a trick question where the answer is 1? But let me verify with N=4. For N=4, after two steps, it becomes 1 with probability 1, so after 100 steps, it's 1. For N=3, after 1 step, it's 1. So for any N ≥ 3, after log N steps or something, it becomes 1? Wait, but how does it become 1?\n\nWait, let me think in general for N numbers arranged in a circle. Each step, every number is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. So this is a type of dynamic system where the values are \"spreading\" the maximum value around the circle. Since every number is being updated to the maximum of itself and its neighbors, over time, the maximum value in the entire circle should propagate to all positions. Because if there is a local maximum somewhere, then its neighbors will eventually become equal to it, and then the neighbors' neighbors, etc.\n\nIn other words, the global maximum of the initial configuration will eventually \"spread\" to the entire circle through this update rule, because each step allows the maximum value to influence its neighbors. Since the update is simultaneous, the maximum value will move outward by one position each step? Or maybe more?\n\nWait, let me consider the N=4 case again. Suppose we have a configuration where the maximum is at position 1. Then, in the next step, position 1's new value will be the max of its neighbors and itself, which are already at least as large as the current maximum? Wait, no. Wait, let me think with an example.\n\nSuppose in N=4, the initial configuration has a maximum at position 1: a₁ = M (the global maximum), and the others are less than M. Then, in the first step:\n\na₁_new = max(a₄, a₁, a₂). Since a₁ is M, and a₂ and a₄ are less than M, so a₁_new = M.\n\nSimilarly, a₂_new = max(a₁, a₂, a₃). Since a₁ is M, so a₂_new = M.\n\na₃_new = max(a₂, a₃, a₄). Since a₂ and a₄ are less than M, but a₃ might be less than M, so a₃_new would be max(a₂, a₃, a₄) which is less than M unless a₂ or a₄ is M, but they aren't. Wait, but a₂_new is now M, so in the next step, a₃'s neighbors will include a₂_new which is M.\n\nWait, let me do this step by step. Let me suppose the initial configuration is [M, x, y, z], where x, y, z < M.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁_new = max(z, M, x) = M\n\na₂_new = max(M, x, y) = M\n\na₃_new = max(x, y, z) = max(x, y, z) < M\n\na₄_new = max(y, z, M) = M\n\nSo after first step, the configuration is [M, M, max(x,y,z), M]\n\nSecond step:\n\nNow, compute the new values based on [M, M, m, M], where m = max(x,y,z) < M.\n\na₁_new = max(a₄_old, a₁_old, a₂_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na₂_new = max(a₁_old, a₂_old, a₃_old) = max(M, M, m) = M\n\na₃_new = max(a₂_old, a₃_old, a₄_old) = max(M, m, M) = M\n\na₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₁_old) = max(m, M, M) = M\n\nThus, after the second step, all become M. So even if the third element was lower, after two steps, it becomes M.\n\nTherefore, the maximum propagates to all positions in at most two steps for N=4. Wait, but in this case, the maximum was initially at position 1, and after one step, positions 1, 2, and 4 became M, and position 3 became m. Then, in the next step, position 3's neighbors (positions 2 and 4) are already M, so position 3 becomes M as well.\n\nTherefore, the maximum propagates to the entire circle in two steps. Similarly, in the N=3 case, the maximum propagates in one step to all positions.\n\nIn general, for a circle of N positions, if we have a single maximum value M somewhere, then in how many steps does it take for the entire circle to become M?\n\nIt seems like in the worst case, the maximum needs to travel from its initial position to all other positions. Since it's a circle, the maximum has to \"walk\" around the circle, updating its neighbors each step.\n\nWait, but in the N=4 example above, the maximum started at position 1, and after one step, positions 1, 2, and 4 became M, but position 3 was still m. Then, in the next step, position 3 was adjacent to positions 2 and 4, both of which were M, so it became M. So the maximum spread to the entire circle in two steps. Similarly, if the maximum was at position 1, then in step 1, it spreads to positions 2 and N (since it's a circle), and in step 2, it spreads to the next neighbors.\n\nWait, perhaps in general, for a circle of N nodes, the time to propagate the maximum to the entire circle is at most ceil(N/2) steps? Or maybe log N? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, in the N=4 case, it took 2 steps, which is N/2. For N=3, it took 1 step, which is less than N/2 (which would be 1.5). For N=5, maybe it would take 3 steps? Let me test with N=5.\n\nTake N=5, with initial configuration [M, x, y, z, w], where x,y,z,w < M.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁_new = max(w, M, x) = M\n\na₂_new = max(M, x, y) = M\n\na₃_new = max(x, y, z) = max(x,y,z) < M\n\na₄_new = max(y, z, w) = max(y,z,w) < M\n\na₅_new = max(z, w, M) = M\n\nSo after first step, the configuration is [M, M, m, n, M], where m = max(x,y,z), n = max(y,z,w).\n\nSecond step:\n\nCompute new values based on [M, M, m, n, M].\n\na₁_new = max(a₅_old, a₁_old, a₂_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na₂_new = max(a₁_old, a₂_old, a₃_old) = max(M, M, m) = M\n\na₃_new = max(a₂_old, a₃_old, a₄_old) = max(M, m, n) = M\n\na₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₅_old) = max(m, n, M) = M\n\na₅_new = max(a₄_old, a₅_old, a₁_old) = max(n, M, M) = M\n\nThus, after two steps, all become M. So for N=5, it only took two steps? Wait, that contradicts my earlier thought that it might take longer. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait in the first step for N=5:\n\nOriginal: [M, x, y, z, w]\n\nAfter first step:\n\na₁_new = max(w, M, x) = M\n\na₂_new = max(M, x, y) = M\n\na₃_new = max(x, y, z) = let's say that's some value m < M\n\na₄_new = max(y, z, w) = some value n < M\n\na₅_new = max(z, w, M) = M\n\nSo after first step: [M, M, m, n, M]\n\nThen, second step:\n\na₁_new = max(a₅_old, a₁_old, a₂_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na₂_new = max(a₁_old, a₂_old, a₃_old) = max(M, M, m) = M\n\na₃_new = max(a₂_old, a₃_old, a₄_old) = max(M, m, n) = M\n\na₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₅_old) = max(m, n, M) = M\n\na₅_new = max(a₄_old, a₅_old, a₁_old) = max(n, M, M) = M\n\nSo indeed, after two steps, all become M. So even for N=5, it only took two steps? Hmm, that seems inconsistent with my earlier thought that it might take longer. Wait, perhaps my intuition was wrong.\n\nWait, in the N=5 case, after the first step, positions 1, 2, and 5 become M, and positions 3 and 4 become m and n (which are less than M). Then, in the second step, position 3 is between position 2 (which is M) and position 4 (which is n < M). Therefore, a₃_new is the max of a₂_old (M), a₃_old (m), and a₄_old (n). Since a₂_old is M, so a₃_new becomes M. Similarly, position 4 is between a₃_old (m) and a₅_old (M). Therefore, a₄_new is the max of a₃_old (m), a₄_old (n), and a₅_old (M). Since a₅_old is M, so a₄_new becomes M. Therefore, all positions become M in two steps.\n\nHmm. So maybe for any N ≥ 3, the maximum propagates to the entire circle in two steps? Wait, let's check N=6.\n\nTake N=6, with initial configuration [M, x, y, z, w, v], all other values less than M.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁_new = max(v, M, x) = M\n\na₂_new = max(M, x, y) = M\n\na₃_new = max(x, y, z) = m1 < M\n\na₄_new = max(y, z, w) = m2 < M\n\na₅_new = max(z, w, v) = m3 < M\n\na₆_new = max(w, v, M) = M\n\nSo after first step: [M, M, m1, m2, m3, M]\n\nSecond step:\n\nCompute new values based on the above.\n\na₁_new = max(a₆_old, a₁_old, a₂_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na₂_new = max(a₁_old, a₂_old, a₃_old) = max(M, M, m1) = M\n\na₃_new = max(a₂_old, a₃_old, a₄_old) = max(M, m1, m2) = M\n\na₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₅_old) = max(m1, m2, m3) < M\n\na₅_new = max(a₄_old, a₅_old, a₆_old) = max(m2, m3, M) = M\n\na₆_new = max(a₅_old, a₆_old, a₁_old) = max(m3, M, M) = M\n\nSo after second step: [M, M, M, m4, M, M] where m4 = max(m1, m2, m3) < M.\n\nThird step:\n\nNow compute based on [M, M, M, m4, M, M]\n\na₁_new = max(a₆_old, a₁_old, a₂_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na₂_new = max(a₁_old, a₂_old, a₃_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na₃_new = max(a₂_old, a₃_old, a₄_old) = max(M, M, m4) = M\n\na₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₅_old) = max(M, m4, M) = M\n\na₅_new = max(a₄_old, a₅_old, a₆_old) = max(m4, M, M) = M\n\na₆_new = max(a₅_old, a₆_old, a₁_old) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\nThus, after third step, all become M. So for N=6, it took three steps?\n\nWait, in the first step, positions 1, 2, 6 become M. Positions 3,4,5 become m1, m2, m3. In the second step, positions 1,2,3,5,6 become M, leaving position 4 as m4. Then in the third step, position 4 is between positions 3 (M) and 5 (M), so it becomes M. So yes, three steps for N=6.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the number of steps required for the maximum to propagate around the circle is ceil(N/2)? Wait, for N=3: ceil(3/2)=2, but it took only 1 step. Hmm, that doesn't fit. Wait N=3: after one step, all became M. N=4: ceil(4/2)=2, which matched. N=5: ceil(5/2)=3, but it only took two steps. Hmm, inconsistency.\n\nWait maybe floor(N/2) +1? For N=3: floor(3/2)=1, so 2? No, didn't take 2 steps. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the number of steps needed is the minimal number such that 2^steps ≥ N? For N=3: 2 steps needed? But it only took 1. Hmm no.\n\nWait, perhaps it's related to the distance from the initial maximum to the farthest point. Since it's a circle, the maximum has to travel around the circle. The worst case is when the initial maximum is as far as possible from some other point. On a circle of N nodes, the maximum distance between two nodes is floor(N/2). So perhaps it takes floor(N/2) steps for the maximum to reach the opposite side?\n\nWait, in the N=6 case, the farthest distance is 3 (half the circle). It took 3 steps. For N=5, the farthest distance is 2 (since 5/2=2.5, floor is 2). But it took two steps. Wait, N=5: starting at position 1, the farthest point is position 4 (distance 3 clockwise or 2 counter-clockwise). Wait, on a circle of 5, the maximum distance between two nodes is 2 steps in one direction or 3 in the other, but the minimal distance is 2. So perhaps the number of steps needed is the minimal distance? Hmm.\n\nWait in N=6, starting at position 1, the farthest point is position 4 (distance 3). It took 3 steps to get there. For N=5, starting at position 1, the farthest point is position 3 (distance 2 in one direction, 3 in the other). But in our example, it only took two steps to cover the entire circle. Hmm, so perhaps my intuition is off.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the number of steps needed for the maximum to propagate to the entire circle is equal to the ceiling of log₂ N? For N=3, log₂3 ≈1.58, so ceiling is 2, but it only took 1 step. Hmm, not quite.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's the diameter of the graph under this update rule. Since each step allows the maximum to spread to adjacent nodes? Wait, actually, the update rule is that each node is updated to the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. So the influence of a node's new value depends on its neighbors. But the propagation of the global maximum is a bit different.\n\nWait, in the N=6 example, after first step, the maximum has spread to positions 1, 2, and 6. Then in the second step, it spreads to positions 3 and 5, but position 4 is still behind. Then in the third step, it spreads to position 4. So each step, the maximum can spread two steps further? Like in step 1: covers 1,2,6; step 2: covers 3,5; step 3: covers 4. Hmm, so maybe each step allows the maximum to expand by one or two positions?\n\nAlternatively, in terms of the distance from the initial maximum: in step 1, the maximum is at distance 0. Then in step 1, it can influence neighbors at distance 1, but also itself. Wait, actually, in the first step, all nodes adjacent to the original maximum become M as well, because their value is set to the max of their neighbors and themselves. Since one of their neighbors is the original maximum, so they become M. Then in the next step, the nodes adjacent to those nodes (which are now M) will also become M, because their neighbors now include an M. So the influence spreads outward by one node per step in each direction?\n\nWait in the N=6 example:\n\nStart with maximum at position 1.\n\nStep 1: positions 1, 2, and 6 become M. So the influence spreads to the immediate neighbors (positions 2 and 6) and stays at position 1.\n\nStep 2: positions adjacent to the newly M positions (positions 2 and 6) become M. Position 3 is adjacent to position 2 (now M), and position 5 is adjacent to position 6 (now M). So positions 3 and 5 become M. Also, position 1 is still M, and position 4 is adjacent to position 3 (which is now M) and position 5 (now M). Wait, in step 2, when computing position 4's new value, its neighbors are position 3 (which became M in step 2?) Wait no, in step 2, when computing the new values, we use the previous step's values. Let me clarify.\n\nIn step 1, after first update:\n\nPositions 1,2,6 are M; positions 3,4,5 are m1, m2, m3.\n\nIn step 2, when computing the new values for position 3:\n\na₃_new = max(a₂_old, a₃_old, a₄_old) = max(M, m1, m2). Since a₂_old is M, this becomes M.\n\nSimilarly, a₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₅_old) = max(m1, m2, m3). Since none of these are M, this remains as m4.\n\na₅_new = max(a₄_old, a₅_old, a₆_old) = max(m2, m3, M) = M.\n\nTherefore, after step 2:\n\nPositions 1,2,3,5,6 are M; position 4 is m4.\n\nThen in step 3, when computing a₄_new:\n\na₄_new = max(a₃_old, a₄_old, a₅_old) = max(M, m4, M) = M.\n\nSo position 4 becomes M in step 3.\n\nTherefore, each step allows the \"front\" of the M's to move one step further around the circle. So starting from position 1, the M front expands to the right (position 2), left (position 6), and stays at 1. Then in the next step, it expands further to positions 3 and 5, but position 4 is still behind. Then in the next step, it catches up to position 4. So the time to cover the entire circle is proportional to the distance from the initial maximum to the farthest point.\n\nIn the worst case, if the initial maximum is placed such that the farthest point is as far as possible, which on a circle of N nodes is floor(N/2) steps away. Therefore, it would take floor(N/2) steps to cover the entire circle? Wait in the N=6 case, the farthest point from position 1 is position 4, which is 3 steps away (distance 3). And it took 3 steps. For N=5, the farthest point from position 1 is position 3 (distance 2 in one direction or 3 in the other; minimal distance is 2). And it took 2 steps to cover the entire circle. For N=4, the farthest point is 2 steps away (distance 2), and it took 2 steps. For N=3, the farthest point is 1 step away (distance 1), and it took 1 step. So yes, it seems that the number of steps required for the maximum to propagate to the entire circle is equal to the minimal distance from the initial maximum position to the farthest node, which is floor(N/2). Wait, but in N=5, floor(N/2) is 2, which matched. In N=6, floor(N/2)=3, which matched. N=4: floor(4/2)=2, matched. N=3: floor(3/2)=1, matched. Therefore, in general, for a circle of N nodes, if the initial maximum is placed at some position, the time needed to propagate the maximum to the entire circle is floor(N/2) steps. Wait, but actually, in the N=5 example, after two steps, the entire circle became M, which is floor(5/2)=2. So that seems to fit.\n\nHowever, in our problem, the initial configuration has a single global maximum (with probability 1, since all are continuous), but the position of that maximum is random. Therefore, the time until the entire circle becomes M is a random variable depending on the initial position of the maximum. Since the maximum could be anywhere, the time to cover the circle would be up to floor(N/2) steps in the worst case, but on average?\n\nBut since we are to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds, and 100 is much larger than floor(N/2) for N=2025. Since floor(2025/2) = 1012.5, so floor is 1012. Therefore, 100 seconds is much less than 1012 steps needed in the worst case. Wait, so does that mean that the number of distinct values after 100 seconds is still greater than 1? Or does it stabilize at 1 after some steps?\n\nWait, no. Wait, actually, if the maximum takes up to 1012 steps to propagate around the circle, then after 100 steps, it might not have fully propagated yet. Therefore, the number of distinct values might still be greater than 1. Hmm, so my previous assumption that it becomes 1 after a small number of steps was only when the maximum was initially placed such that it needed few steps to propagate. But if the initial maximum is placed such that it needs 1012 steps to propagate, then after 100 steps, only part of the circle has become M, so there would still be some non-M values left, hence multiple distinct values.\n\nTherefore, my earlier analysis for small N was incorrect in assuming that it would become 1 quickly, but in reality, for large N like 2025, the time required to fully propagate the maximum is on the order of N/2, so after 100 steps, which is much smaller than N/2 (since N=2025, N/2 is ~1012.5), the system hasn't had time to fully synchronize all values to the maximum. Therefore, the number of distinct values after 100 seconds is still going to be large?\n\nWait, but perhaps not. Wait, even if the maximum hasn't propagated everywhere yet, maybe the values have stabilized into some pattern with a limited number of distinct values?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps regardless of the starting configuration, after t steps, the number of distinct values is at most something like O(1) or constant? Hmm, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the number of distinct values decreases by a constant factor each step? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the process converges to a state where all values are equal to the global maximum, but the time to do so depends on the initial configuration, and since we're taking expectation over all initial configurations, maybe the expectation of the number of distinct values after t steps tends to 1 as t approaches infinity, but for finite t, it's something else.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after t steps, the number of distinct values is equal to the number of \"active\" regions that have been influenced by the maximum. But this is vague.\n\nWait, let me think differently. Since all the updates are deterministic once the initial configuration is given, the process is a deterministic dynamical system on the space of [0,1]^N. The question is, for a random initial configuration, what is the expected number of distinct values after 100 iterations of this update rule.\n\nGiven that the update rule is such that each value is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two neighbors, this is a form of a \"majority\" or \"maximum\" diffusion process.\n\nIn such processes, often the system tends to converge to a uniform distribution or to a state where all values are equal, but the speed of convergence depends on the dimension and the update rule.\n\nIn our case, since we are dealing with a circular arrangement and the update is local (each node depends on its two neighbors and itself), this is similar to a cellular automaton with rule based on taking the maximum.\n\nIn such systems, the global maximum will spread outward at each step, as we saw in the small N examples. The time to fully synchronize all nodes to the global maximum is proportional to the diameter of the graph, which for a circle is linear in N. Therefore, for N=2025, it would take on the order of N steps to fully synchronize. Since we are only performing 100 steps, which is much less than N/2 (~1012), the system has not yet fully synchronized. Therefore, the number of distinct values after 100 steps is likely still substantial.\n\nHowever, perhaps there is some symmetry or property that causes the expected number of distinct values to decrease in a predictable way, even before full synchronization.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the expected number of distinct values stabilizes to a small constant after some time?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the values eventually become the global maximum, but the time needed is random, so the expectation is still 1? Wait, no, because even if with probability less than 1 the system hasn't synchronized yet, the expectation would be more than 1.\n\nWait, let me formalize this a bit. Let me denote by X_t the number of distinct values at time t. We need to compute E[X_{100}].\n\nAt time t=0, X_0 is almost surely 2025, since all initial values are distinct with probability 1 in continuous space.\n\nAt each subsequent time step, the values are updated based on the previous state. The key is to model how X_t evolves.\n\nNote that once a value becomes equal to the global maximum, it will stay equal to it forever, because in the next step, it will be the maximum of its neighbors (which may or may not be the global maximum yet) and itself. However, if a value is not the global maximum yet, it might become the maximum of its neighborhood in the next step, thus propagating the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the non-global-maximum values can only decrease or stay the same? Wait, no. Each value is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. Therefore, if a value is not the global maximum, but one of its neighbors is the global maximum, then it will become the global maximum in the next step. If neither of its neighbors is the global maximum, then its new value is the maximum of itself and its two neighbors, which are all less than the global maximum, so it remains less than the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the global maximum will spread to all nodes that are adjacent to a node that has already become the global maximum. Thus, the set of nodes that have become the global maximum grows by at least one node each step, until it covers the entire circle.\n\nWait, actually, in each step, the set of nodes that have become the global maximum can grow by up to two nodes: the two neighbors of an existing global maximum node. Wait, but since the update is simultaneous, when a node's neighbor becomes the global maximum in the previous step, then in the current step, that node will take the maximum of its neighbors (which includes the newly global-maximum neighbor) and itself. Therefore, the node will become the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the spread of the global maximum is such that in each step, any node adjacent to a global maximum node will also become a global maximum node in the next step. Therefore, the frontier of the global maximum region expands by one node in each direction each step? Wait, no, because it's a circle.\n\nActually, on a circle, suppose the current set of global maximum nodes is an interval. Then, in the next step, the nodes adjacent to this interval will also become global maximum nodes, thereby expanding the interval by one node on each end. Therefore, the length of the interval of global maximum nodes increases by two each step, until it wraps around the circle.\n\nWait, let's formalize this:\n\nSuppose at time t, the global maximum has spread to a contiguous arc of length L. Then, at time t+1, this arc will expand by one node on each end, so the new length is L + 2. However, once the arc wraps around the circle, it might start overlapping, so the expansion is modulo N.\n\nWait, actually, if the current global maximum region is an interval of length L, then in the next step, the nodes immediately adjacent to this interval (on both sides) will become global maxima, thereby extending the interval by one on each side, resulting in an interval of length L + 2. This continues until the interval wraps around the circle.\n\nTherefore, the time to cover the entire circle is roughly N/2 steps, since each step extends the interval by 2, so to cover N nodes, you need N/2 steps. This matches our earlier examples: for N=3, it took 1 step (which is 3/2 = 1.5 rounded down?), but actually, in N=3, starting with the maximum at one node, after one step it covers all three nodes. Hmm, so perhaps it's ceiling(N/2) steps?\n\nWait, for N=3: ceiling(3/2)=2, but it only took 1 step. Hmm, discrepancy. Wait, let me think again with N=3.\n\nIn N=3, suppose the initial maximum is at position 1. Then:\n\nStep 1:\n\na₁ becomes max(a₃, a₁, a₂) = M (since a₁ is M)\n\na₂ becomes max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = M (since a₁ is M)\n\na₃ becomes max(a₂, a₃, a₁) = M (since a₁ is M)\n\nSo after one step, all are M. So it took 1 step, which is less than ceiling(3/2)=2.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the formula is floor(N/2). For N=3: floor(3/2)=1, which matches. For N=4: floor(4/2)=2, which matches. N=5: floor(5/2)=2, but in our example with N=5 it took 2 steps to fully cover. Wait, in the N=5 example above, after two steps, everything became M. So floor(N/2) works here. For N=6: floor(6/2)=3, which matched the three steps needed. Therefore, the time required to fully synchronize the circle is floor(N/2) steps, assuming the initial maximum is placed such that it's in the middle? Wait no, actually, if the initial maximum is placed such that it's in a position that allows the spread to happen fastest, then it can take as few as 1 step for N=3, but if it's placed such that the spread has to go around the circle, then it can take up to floor(N/2) steps.\n\nWait, actually, the maximum time needed is floor(N/2) steps, because if the initial maximum is placed such that the farthest point is floor(N/2) steps away, then it would take that many steps to reach there. Since on a circle, the maximum distance between two nodes is floor(N/2). Therefore, the time to propagate the maximum to the farthest node is floor(N/2), hence the time to synchronize the entire circle is floor(N/2). Therefore, for N=2025, it would take floor(2025/2)=1012 steps to fully synchronize. Since we are only doing 100 steps, which is much less than 1012, the system has not yet synchronized.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values after 100 steps is going to be quite large, but we need to compute its expectation.\n\nHmm. So perhaps instead of trying to track the exact number, we can find a pattern or formula for the expected number of distinct values at time t.\n\nAlternatively, note that in each step, the set of global maximum nodes can only grow, and once a node becomes a global maximum, it stays that way. The non-global-maximum nodes can either stay the same or become global maxima in subsequent steps, depending on their neighbors.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct values decreases by a constant factor each step? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct values at time t is equal to the expected number of \"active\" intervals or something. Hmm, this is getting too vague.\n\nWait, let me think about the expectation. Since expectation is linear, even though the events of different values being present are dependent, perhaps we can compute the probability that a particular value is still present at time t, and then sum those probabilities? Wait, but the number of distinct values is the sum over all possible values of the indicator that the value is present at time t. However, since the values are continuous, the probability that any particular value is exactly present is zero, so this approach might not work.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the process in terms of the positions that have become the global maximum by time t, and the rest of the positions have some distribution of values below the global maximum.\n\nWait, let's consider that once a position becomes a global maximum, it remains so forever. The non-global-maximum positions are updated based on their neighbors, which may or may not be global maxima.\n\nTherefore, after t steps, the set of global maximum positions is some subset S_t of the circle, which grows over time. The values in S_t are all equal to the global maximum M, and the values outside S_t are less than M and follow some process.\n\nHowever, the exact evolution of the non-global-maximum values is complex because their updates depend on their neighbors, some of which may already be global maxima.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all non-global-maximum values are equal to the maximum of their current neighborhood, which includes some global maxima and some non-global maxima. Therefore, the non-global-maximum values can only increase over time, approaching M from below. However, their exact distribution might stabilize or follow some pattern.\n\nAlternatively, maybe after some time, all non-global-maximum values become equal to the same value less than M, so that the number of distinct values is 2: M and the lower value.\n\nWait, in the N=4 example, after one step, we had [M, M, m, M], then after the next step, everything became M. So in that case, it didn't stay at two distinct values.\n\nWait in the N=4 example with initial [0.1, 0.5, 0.1, 0.5], after one step everything became 0.5, so only one distinct value.\n\nHmm, but in another N=4 example with initial [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4], after two steps everything became 0.4. So in that case, it went from 4 distinct values to 1 in two steps.\n\nWait, but in the N=5 example, after two steps everything became M. So maybe for any N ≥ 3, after floor(N/2) steps, all values become M, so the number of distinct values becomes 1. But for t < floor(N/2), the number of distinct values is greater than 1.\n\nTherefore, if we are to compute E[X_t], the expected number of distinct values at time t, then for t < floor(N/2), it's greater than 1, and for t ≥ floor(N/2), it's 1.\n\nGiven that N=2025, floor(N/2)=1012. Since we are looking at t=100, which is much less than 1012, the expected number of distinct values is going to be greater than 1.\n\nBut how much greater?\n\nHmm. To compute E[X_t], perhaps we can note that each position has a certain probability of still being non-M (the global maximum) at time t. The number of distinct values would then be at least 1 (for M) plus the number of distinct values among the non-M positions.\n\nHowever, the non-M positions may have taken various values, some equal, some different. The expectation of the number of distinct values would then be 1 + E[D_{non-M}], where D_{non-M} is the number of distinct values among the non-M positions.\n\nBut calculating E[D_{non-M}] seems difficult.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the non-M positions have the same value after t steps? If that were the case, then the number of distinct values would be 2 (M and that common value). But in the N=4 example, after one step, the non-M positions were [m, m], so they were equal. Then in the next step, they became M. Wait, in the N=4 example with initial [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4], after first step we had [0.2, 0.3, 0.3, 0.3], so the non-M positions were [0.3, 0.3, 0.3], which are all equal. Then in the next step, they all became 0.3's neighbors which were already 0.3 and 0.4 (which became M), so they became M.\n\nWait, so in that case, the non-M values collapsed to a single value before becoming M. Hmm, interesting.\n\nWait in the N=5 example, after first step, the non-M positions were [m1, m2, m3], but then in the second step, those m's were updated based on their neighbors, which included M's, so they became M. So the non-M values didn't stay as a group; they were overwritten by M in the next step.\n\nHmm, so perhaps in general, the non-M positions, once their neighbors become M, will get updated to M in the next step, so the non-M values can only exist in a \"frontier\" region that is adjacent to the M region, and once that frontier expands, the non-M values in the new frontier get overwritten by M in the next step.\n\nTherefore, the set of non-M values at time t is exactly the set of nodes that are at a distance greater than t from the initial maximum position. Wait, perhaps?\n\nWait, let me formalize this.\n\nSuppose the initial global maximum is at position 0 (without loss of generality, due to rotational symmetry). Then, the set of nodes that have become M by time t is an interval of length 2t+1 centered at the initial maximum? Wait, no, on a circle it's a bit different.\n\nActually, on a circle, if the initial maximum is at position 0, then at each step, the M region expands by one node to the left and one node to the right. Therefore, after t steps, the M region is an arc of length 2t+1 (from -t to +t modulo N). However, once this arc wraps around the circle, it may start overlapping.\n\nWait, let me think in terms of linear distance. Let's \"unfold\" the circle into a line by cutting it at position 0, so positions 0, 1, 2, ..., N-1, with position N identified with 0.\n\nSuppose the initial maximum is at position 0. Then, at each step, the M region expands by one position to the left and one position to the right. However, on the circle, moving left from 0 would go to position N-1, and moving right would go to position 1.\n\nTherefore, after t steps, the M region is the interval [N - t, t] modulo N. Wait, perhaps more precisely, the M region is all positions within distance t from the initial position 0. The distance here is the minimal number of steps along the circle.\n\nTherefore, the number of non-M positions at time t is N - (2t + 1), provided that 2t + 1 ≤ N. If 2t + 1 > N, then the M region