rs are arranged as follows: 0.1, 0.9, 0.1, 0.9. So alternating 0.1 and 0.9. Let me see.\n\nFirst iteration:\n\na1' = max(d, a, b) = max(0.9, 0.1, 0.9) = 0.9\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(0.1, 0.9, 0.1) = 0.9\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0.9, 0.1, 0.9) = 0.9\n\na4' = max(c, d, a) = max(0.1, 0.9, 0.1) = 0.9\n\nSo after first iteration, all become 0.9. So again, one step.\n\nHmm, how about a more irregular configuration? Let's say a=0.5, b=0.6, c=0.4, d=0.7.\n\nFirst iteration:\n\na1' = max(d, a, b) = max(0.7, 0.5, 0.6) = 0.7\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(0.5, 0.6, 0.4) = 0.6\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0.6, 0.4, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na4' = max(c, d, a) = max(0.4, 0.7, 0.5) = 0.7\n\nSo after first iteration, we have [0.7, 0.6, 0.7, 0.7].\n\nSecond iteration:\n\na1'' = max(a4', a1', a2') = max(0.7, 0.7, 0.6) = 0.7\n\na2'' = max(a1', a2', a3') = max(0.7, 0.6, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na3'' = max(a2', a3', a4') = max(0.6, 0.7, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na4'' = max(a3', a4', a1') = max(0.7, 0.7, 0.7) = 0.7\n\nSo again, after two iterations, all become 0.7. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe if the maximum isn't present in the initial configuration? Wait, but every number is in [0,1], so the maximum over any three consecutive numbers will be at least the maximum of the entire circle? Wait, actually, no. Wait, the maximum over three consecutive numbers could be less than the global maximum if the global maximum is isolated?\n\nWait, actually, in a circle, every element is part of three different triples. Wait, actually, for N=4, each element is part of three triples? Wait, no. Wait, each element is part of three triples? Wait, in a circle of four elements, each element is in three triples? Let me see:\n\nWait, for N=4, each a_i is involved in the triples for a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1} (for the next iteration), but actually, in the first step, the triples are overlapping. Let me think again.\n\nWait, in the first step, each new a_i is the max of the previous a_{i-1}, current a_i, and next a_{i+1}. So each element is part of three different triples: as the left neighbor, the center, and the right neighbor for the next positions. Wait, actually, each original a_j is included in three new a_i's: namely, a_{j-1}, a_j, and a_{j+1} each of which will include a_j in their triple? Wait, no. Let me see:\n\nTake a_j. It is the current a_j, so it is included in the computation of a_{j+1}'s new value, because a_{j+1}' = max(a_j, a_{j+1}, a_{j+2}), so a_j is the left neighbor of a_{j+1}. Similarly, a_j is the right neighbor of a_{j-1}'s new value, which is a_{j-1}' = max(a_{j-2}, a_{j-1}, a_j). So a_j is the right neighbor of a_{j-1}'s computation. Also, a_j itself is the center in the computation of a_j's new value, which is a_j' = max(a_{j-1}, a_j, a_{j+1}).\n\nTherefore, each a_j is included in three new values: a_{j-1}'s new value (as the right neighbor), a_j's own new value (as the center), and a_{j+1}'s new value (as the left neighbor). Therefore, each original a_j is part of three triples in the first iteration. Therefore, the new values are all based on triples that include each original a_j in three different triples. Therefore, if the global maximum M is present in the original configuration, then in the first iteration, every new a_i will be at least M, because wherever M is located, it will be included in the triples for the neighboring positions. Wait, actually, if M is located at position k, then in the triples for positions k-1, k, and k+1, the triple for position k-1 includes a_{k-2}, a_{k-1}, a_k, which includes a_k = M. Similarly, the triple for position k includes a_{k-1}, a_k, a_{k+1}, which includes M, and the triple for position k+1 includes a_k, a_{k+1}, a_{k+2}, which includes M. So the new values at positions k-1, k, and k+1 will all be at least M. Then, in subsequent iterations, those positions with the new values equal to M will spread out? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if the global maximum M is present in the original configuration, then in the first iteration, all the neighbors of M will be at least M, and then in the next iteration, their neighbors will be at least M, etc. So perhaps the maximum propagates outward?\n\nWait, but in the N=4 case, once you have a maximum in the circle, in the first iteration, the three positions adjacent to the maximum will also become M, and then in the next iteration, the positions adjacent to those will also become M? Wait, but in N=4, if you have a single maximum, then in the first iteration, the three positions adjacent to it (which, in a circle of four, each position has two neighbors, so actually, each position has two neighbors, so each position is adjacent to two others. Wait a second, in a circle of four, each element has two neighbors, so when we take triples, each new a_i is the max of itself and its two immediate neighbors. Therefore, for each position, the triple includes itself and the two adjacent ones. Therefore, in the first iteration, if a position has the global maximum, then the three positions that are part of its triple (itself and its two neighbors) will all become at least the global maximum. But actually, since the global maximum is already one of the elements in the triple, the max of that triple is the global maximum. Wait, so actually, if any position has the global maximum, then all three positions in its triple (itself and its two neighbors) will have their new value set to the global maximum. Wait, so in the first iteration, any position that is adjacent to a global maximum (or is the global maximum itself) will become the global maximum in the next step?\n\nWait, let me formalize that.\n\nSuppose M is the global maximum of the original configuration. Let S be the set of positions where the original a_i = M. Then, in the first iteration, for each position i:\n\nIf i is in S, then the triple for i includes a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}, which includes M, so the new a_i' will be M.\n\nAdditionally, for positions adjacent to S, say position j where a_j is not M but one of its neighbors is M, then the triple for j includes a_{j-1}, a_j, a_{j+1}, which includes M (since, say, a_{j-1} is M or a_{j+1} is M), so the new a_j' will be M.\n\nTherefore, after the first iteration, all positions that are adjacent to any position in S will also become M. Therefore, the set S will expand to include all neighbors of its original positions.\n\nBut in a circle, the neighbors of neighbors can eventually cover the entire circle, depending on how the original S was arranged.\n\nWait, but in the case where the original S is a single point (only one position has the maximum M), then in the first iteration, that position and its two neighbors become M, so S expands to three positions. Then, in the next iteration, the neighbors of those three positions (which are all the other positions except the one opposite the original M?) Wait, in a circle of four, if you have three positions set to M after the first iteration, then the fourth position is adjacent to two of the M's. Let's see:\n\nSuppose original S was just position 1 (a1 = M, others less). Then after first iteration, positions 1, 2, and 4 become M (since position 1's triple includes itself, 4, and 2; position 2's triple includes 1, 2, 3; but position 3's triple is 2, 3, 4; and position 4's triple is 3, 4, 1). Wait, actually, in N=4:\n\nOriginal S = {1} (position 1 has M, others not). Then after first iteration:\n\na1' = max(a4, a1, a2) = max(..., M, ...) = M\n\na2' = max(a1, a2, a3) = max(M, ..., ...) = M\n\na3' = max(a2, a3, a4) = max(..., ..., ...) which might not be M, unless a2 or a4 were M? Wait, but in the original configuration, only a1 was M. So a2 and a4 are less than M, so the triple for a3' is max(a2, a3, a4). Since all three are less than M, so a3' < M.\n\nSimilarly, a4' = max(a3, a4, a1) = max(..., ..., M) = M.\n\nTherefore, after first iteration, positions 1, 2, and 4 are M, and position 3 is less than M.\n\nSo S after first iteration is {1,2,4}. Then in the second iteration:\n\na1'' = max(a4', a1', a2') = max(M, M, M) = M\n\na2'' = max(a1', a2', a3') = max(M, M, a3') = M (since a3' < M)\n\na3'' = max(a2', a3', a4') = max(M, a3', M) = M\n\na4'' = max(a3', a4', a1') = max(a3', M, M) = M\n\nSo after the second iteration, all positions become M. So in two steps, starting from a single maximum, it spreads to the entire circle. So in N=4, if you start with a single maximum, it takes two steps to cover the whole circle.\n\nAlternatively, if the original configuration has multiple maxima, then the expansion would be faster? For instance, if two adjacent positions have the maximum, then in the first iteration, their neighbors would also become maximum, but maybe overlapping regions.\n\nBut regardless, in N=4, it seems that regardless of the initial configuration, after at most two iterations, the entire circle becomes the global maximum. Therefore, the number of distinct values becomes 1 after at most two steps, and then stays 1 forever.\n\nWait, but hold on. Suppose that in the initial configuration, there are multiple local maxima that are not connected? Wait, but in a circle, all positions are connected through the triples. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, in a circle of N elements, every element is part of a triple that includes its neighbors, so if you have two separate clusters of high values, their triples might eventually merge?\n\nWait, let me try with N=5. Maybe with five elements, the dynamics are different?\n\nLet me try N=5 with some initial configuration.\n\nSuppose we have five numbers: a=0.1, b=0.5, c=0.3, d=0.7, e=0.2 arranged in a circle.\n\nFirst iteration:\n\nCompute each new value:\n\na1' = max(e, a, b) = max(0.2, 0.1, 0.5) = 0.5\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(0.1, 0.5, 0.3) = 0.5\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0.5, 0.3, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na4' = max(c, d, e) = max(0.3, 0.7, 0.2) = 0.7\n\na5' = max(d, e, a) = max(0.7, 0.2, 0.1) = 0.7\n\nSo after first iteration, we have [0.5, 0.5, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7].\n\nSecond iteration:\n\na1'' = max(a5', a1', a2') = max(0.7, 0.5, 0.5) = 0.7\n\na2'' = max(a1', a2', a3') = max(0.5, 0.5, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na3'' = max(a2', a3', a4') = max(0.5, 0.7, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na4'' = max(a3', a4', a5') = max(0.7, 0.7, 0.7) = 0.7\n\na5'' = max(a4', a5', a1') = max(0.7, 0.7, 0.5) = 0.7\n\nSo after the second iteration, all become 0.7. So again, two steps to reach a constant.\n\nHmm. What if I start with a configuration that has two separate high regions?\n\nSuppose in N=5: a=0.9, b=0.1, c=0.9, d=0.1, e=0.1.\n\nFirst iteration:\n\na1' = max(e, a, b) = max(0.1, 0.9, 0.1) = 0.9\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(0.9, 0.1, 0.9) = 0.9\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0.1, 0.9, 0.1) = 0.9\n\na4' = max(c, d, e) = max(0.9, 0.1, 0.1) = 0.9\n\na5' = max(d, e, a) = max(0.1, 0.1, 0.9) = 0.9\n\nSo after first iteration, all become 0.9. So that was quick.\n\nAnother example: suppose in N=5, we have two separate maxima that are not adjacent. Let's say a=1, b=0.5, c=0.5, d=1, e=0.5.\n\nFirst iteration:\n\na1' = max(e, a, b) = max(0.5, 1, 0.5) = 1\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(1, 0.5, 0.5) = 1\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0.5, 0.5, 1) = 1\n\na4' = max(c, d, e) = max(0.5, 1, 0.5) = 1\n\na5' = max(d, e, a) = max(1, 0.5, 1) = 1\n\nSo again, all become 1 in one step.\n\nHmm. Maybe in any case, for any N, after a certain number of steps, the entire circle becomes the global maximum? Then the number of distinct values becomes 1? But the problem is asking for the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds. If for N=3, N=4, N=5, it becomes 1 after 1 or 2 steps, then after 100 steps, it's definitely 1. But the problem is with N=2025, which is a large odd number. Wait, but maybe the same principle applies? That is, regardless of N, after a small number of steps, the entire circle becomes the global maximum, so after 100 steps, it's just 1? But that seems too straightforward. The problem says \"compute the expected value of the number of distinct values remaining after 100 seconds.\" If it's always 1, then the answer would be 1, but that seems maybe too easy?\n\nWait, but perhaps my reasoning is flawed because in some configurations, maybe the maximum doesn't spread as quickly? Wait, but in the examples above with N=3,4,5, it always did spread to the entire circle in a couple steps. Wait, but maybe in some configurations, there are multiple local maxima that don't merge? Let me try to think of an example where after some steps, there are still multiple distinct values remaining.\n\nWait, let me try N=5 with a different configuration. Let me try to have three separate peaks.\n\nSuppose in N=5: a=0.9, b=0.1, c=0.9, d=0.1, e=0.9. So alternating high and low, but since N is odd, it ends with a high at e.\n\nFirst iteration:\n\na1' = max(e, a, b) = max(0.9, 0.9, 0.1) = 0.9\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(0.9, 0.1, 0.9) = 0.9\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0.1, 0.9, 0.1) = 0.9\n\na4' = max(c, d, e) = max(0.9, 0.1, 0.9) = 0.9\n\na5' = max(d, e, a) = max(0.1, 0.9, 0.9) = 0.9\n\nSo again, all become 0.9 in one step.\n\nHmm, how about a configuration where the maximum isn't present in the initial configuration? Wait, but every number is in [0,1], so the maximum is always present somewhere. Wait, unless all are equal? If all initial numbers are equal, then obviously they stay equal forever. So in that case, the number of distinct values remains 1 from the start.\n\nWait, perhaps if the initial configuration has multiple local maxima that are not connected through the triples? Wait, but in a circle, every element is connected through the triples. Wait, actually, every element is part of a triple that includes its neighbors, so if you have two maxima separated by one element, like in N=5: a=1, b=0.5, c=0.5, d=1, e=0.5.\n\nWait, we did that earlier, and it became all 1s in one step.\n\nAlternatively, suppose in N=5, the initial configuration is [1, 0, 1, 0, 0]. Let's see:\n\nFirst iteration:\n\na1' = max(e, a, b) = max(0, 1, 0) = 1\n\na2' = max(a, b, c) = max(1, 0, 1) = 1\n\na3' = max(b, c, d) = max(0, 1, 0) = 1\n\na4' = max(c, d, e) = max(1, 0, 0) = 1\n\na5' = max(d, e, a) = max(0, 0, 1) = 1\n\nSo again, all become 1 in one step. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe it's impossible to have a non-constant configuration persist after one step? Because every position's new value is the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. So if there is at least one maximum in the entire circle, then in the next step, all positions adjacent to that maximum will become the maximum, and then in the next step, their neighbors will also become maximum, etc., so it propagates around the circle.\n\nTherefore, once the maximum value is present in the circle, it will eventually spread to the entire circle in at most floor(N/2) steps? Wait, but in N=3, it spread in one step. In N=4, two steps. Wait, maybe in N steps? Wait, no, in N=3, it took one step to spread to the entire circle. Wait, actually, in N=3, if you have a single maximum, then in the first step, all three positions become the maximum because each is part of a triple that includes the original maximum. Wait, in N=3, every position is part of a triple that includes the maximum. Because in N=3, each position is the center of its own triple, and the triples overlap. Wait, in N=3, all three triples are the entire circle. Wait, actually, in N=3, each new a_i' is the max of all three original a's. So actually, in N=3, the first step immediately sets all three to the global maximum. So regardless of where the maximum was, it becomes the maximum for all in one step.\n\nIn N=4, if you have a single maximum, then in the first iteration, the two neighbors of the maximum become the maximum, and then in the second iteration, their other neighbors (which are the remaining two positions) become the maximum as well. So in two steps, the entire circle is maximum.\n\nSimilarly, in N=5, starting with a single maximum, in the first iteration, the two neighbors of the maximum become maximum, then in the next iteration, the neighbors of those neighbors (i.e., the next two positions away) become