 a6, a1)=max(0,0,1)=1\n\nSo after first iteration: [1,1,0,0,0,1]\n\nSecond iteration:\n\na1'' = max(a6', a1', a2') = max(1,1,1)=1\n\na2'' = max(a1', a2', a3') = max(1,1,0)=1\n\na3'' = max(a2', a3', a4') = max(1,0,0)=1\n\na4'' = max(a3', a4', a5') = max(0,0,0)=0\n\na5'' = max(a4', a5', a6') = max(0,0,1)=1\n\na6'' = max(a5', a6', a1') = max(0,1,1)=1\n\nSo after second iteration: [1,1,1,0,1,1]\n\nThird iteration:\n\na1''' = max(a6'', a1'', a2'') = max(1,1,1)=1\n\na2''' = max(a1'', a2'', a3'') = max(1,1,1)=1\n\na3''' = max(a2'', a3'', a4'') = max(1,1,0)=1\n\na4''' = max(a3'', a4'', a5'') = max(1,0,1)=1\n\na5''' = max(a4'', a5'', a6'') = max(0,1,1)=1\n\na6''' = max(a5'', a6'', a1'') = max(1,1,1)=1\n\nSo after third iteration, all are 1. Hmm, so for N=6, starting with a single maximum, it took three iterations? Wait, but floor(N/2) = 3, so that matches. Wait, floor(6/2)=3. So floor(N/2) steps? Wait, but in N=3, floor(3/2)=1, which worked. For N=4, floor(4/2)=2, which worked. N=5, floor(5/2)=2, which worked. N=6, floor(6/2)=3, which worked. So maybe in general, starting from a single maximum, it takes floor(N/2) steps to propagate to the entire circle?\n\nWait, but in the N=5 case above, starting with a single maximum at position 1, after two iterations (floor(5/2)=2) it became all 1s. Wait, but in the N=5 example I did, it actually took two iterations to become all 1s, so that matches floor(N/2). Wait, but in N=6, it took three iterations, which is floor(6/2)=3. So perhaps that formula holds.\n\nTherefore, if you start with a single maximum, then after floor(N/2) steps, the entire circle becomes the maximum. Then, once it's all maximum, it stays that way forever.\n\nTherefore, in general, for any N, if you start with some initial configuration, the maximum value in the circle will spread to the entire circle in at most floor(N/2) steps, after which all values become equal to the global maximum, and then remain constant.\n\nTherefore, if that's the case, then after floor(N/2) steps, the number of distinct values becomes 1, and remains 1 thereafter. Therefore, for N=2025, floor(2025/2)=1012.5, so floor(2025/2)=1012. Therefore, after 1012 steps, the entire circle becomes the global maximum, and then stays there. Since we are asked for the expected number after 100 seconds, which is less than 1012, so we are before the stabilization time?\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, 100 seconds is less than floor(2025/2)=1012. Therefore, after 100 steps, the circle might not yet have stabilized to a single value. Therefore, my previous reasoning might be incomplete.\n\nHmm, so my mistake was assuming that starting from a single maximum takes floor(N/2) steps to spread, but in reality, the initial configuration could have multiple maxima or different structures, so maybe the spreading process is more complicated?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps regardless of the initial configuration, the number of distinct values decreases over time until it becomes 1? But maybe not necessarily?\n\nWait, let me think again. Suppose that in the initial configuration, there are several local maxima. Each local maximum will cause its neighbors to become at least as large as the local maximum in the next step, but if two local maxima are close enough, their influence regions might overlap, causing a merge?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps each step causes the set of local maxima to either stay the same or shrink? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the process is such that the number of distinct values can only stay the same or decrease over time? Let me see.\n\nSuppose we have a configuration with two distinct values, say A and B, with A > B. If in some positions, the triple includes A and two Bs, then the new value at that position will be A. If a position has two As and a B, then it will stay A. If a position has one A and two Bs, then it will become A. If a position has no A and all Bs, then it stays B. Therefore, the number of positions with A can only increase or stay the same each step, because any position adjacent to an A will become A in the next step, and positions with an A stay A. Similarly, the number of positions with B can decrease or stay the same, because positions adjacent to an A will become A, and positions not adjacent to any A will stay B if they were B.\n\nWait, actually, let me formalize this.\n\nSuppose in some step, there are some positions with value A (the maximum in the current configuration) and some with B < A. Then, in the next step:\n\n- Any position that is adjacent to an A will have a triple containing at least one A, so its new value will be A.\n\n- Positions not adjacent to any A will remain B, since their triples consist of only Bs (if all their neighbors are B). Wait, but if a position is not adjacent to any A, then all its neighbors are B, so its triple is all B, so it stays B.\n\nTherefore, the set of positions with A will grow by including all neighbors of existing A positions, and the set of positions with B will shrink by losing all neighbors of A positions.\n\nTherefore, the number of A positions can only increase or stay the same, and the number of B positions can only decrease or stay the same. Therefore, the number of distinct values can decrease or stay the same? Wait, but the values themselves are not necessarily staying as A and B. Because actually, when you take the maximum over three elements, if two are A and one is B, the new value is still A, so the A's persist. But if you have a position that was B but now becomes A, then the number of distinct values could stay the same or decrease? Wait, if you have a new A appearing where there was a B, then the number of distinct values could decrease if that B was the only instance of B, or stay the same if there were other Bs.\n\nWait, actually, the distinct values could potentially decrease if a B is replaced by an A, but the other values might still exist. Hmm, actually, the set of distinct values can only stay the same or decrease over time? Because whenever a value is replaced by a higher value, the number of distinct values can't increase. But if a lower value is replaced by a higher value, that might remove that lower value from the set, but could also introduce a new higher value if the triple had a higher value not previously present? Wait, no, because all the values are in [0,1], and we're taking maxima, so the values can only increase or stay the same? Wait, actually, no. Wait, the values are being replaced by their maximum with neighbors, so each new value is at least as large as the previous value at that position? Wait, no, actually, not necessarily. Wait, let me think.\n\nWait, for a given position i, the new value a_i' is the maximum of a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}. Therefore, a_i' is at least as large as a_i, because it's the maximum of a_i and its two neighbors. Therefore, each value is non-decreasing over time. Therefore, each individual value can only stay the same or increase. Therefore, the entire sequence of values is non-decreasing for each position. Therefore, the set of values can only increase or stay the same? Wait, no, actually, the set of distinct values can only increase or stay the same? Wait, no, since each value is non-decreasing, but the set of distinct values could decrease if some values that were unique are replaced by higher values that already exist.\n\nWait, for example, suppose you have two distinct values: A and B, with A > B. If in some step, a B is replaced by A, then the set of distinct values remains {A, B}, unless all Bs are replaced by A. If all Bs are replaced by A in some step, then the set becomes {A}, so the number of distinct values decreases.\n\nAlternatively, if a new value higher than the current maximum appears, but since all initial values are in [0,1], and we are taking maxima, the maximum can't exceed the global maximum of the initial configuration. Wait, actually, the global maximum of the initial configuration is the highest possible value, so all subsequent values can't exceed that. Therefore, the maximum value is non-decreasing over time, but it can only reach the initial global maximum after some steps.\n\nTherefore, the set of distinct values can only stay the same or decrease over time, because once a value increases, it might eliminate some lower values, but cannot introduce new values higher than the initial global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values is a non-increasing process over time, starting from the initial number (which is 2025, since all are independent uniform samples, so almost surely all distinct? Wait, actually, almost surely they are not all distinct, because there are 2025 numbers in [0,1], so by the birthday problem, the probability that two are equal is non-negligible, but the expected number of distinct values is less than 2025. But anyway, the key point is that the number of distinct values can only decrease or stay the same over time.\n\nTherefore, after 100 seconds, the number of distinct values is less than or equal to the initial number. However, we need the expectation.\n\nBut how much does it decrease? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after some time, the entire circle becomes the global maximum, so the number of distinct values becomes 1. However, as I thought earlier, for N=2025, starting from an arbitrary initial configuration, how many steps does it take for the global maximum to spread across the entire circle?\n\nEarlier, with smaller N, we saw that starting from a single maximum, it takes floor(N/2) steps to spread to the entire circle. Therefore, in general, for a circle of size N, the maximum will spread to the entire circle in at most floor(N/2) steps. Therefore, for N=2025, floor(2025/2)=1012. So after 1012 steps, the entire circle becomes the global maximum, so after that, it stays constant. Therefore, after 100 steps, which is less than 1012, the circle hasn't yet stabilized to a single value. Therefore, the number of distinct values is still greater than 1.\n\nTherefore, to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps, we need a better understanding of how the values evolve over time.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the process is such that after each step, the set of distinct values reduces in some predictable way, and we can model the expectation accordingly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe after each step, the number of distinct values decreases by a certain amount, but it's not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a Markov chain where the state is the current set of distinct values and their arrangement, but that seems intractable for N=2025.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a symmetry or linearity of expectation approach we can use.\n\nWait, perhaps instead of tracking the number of distinct values directly, we can compute the probability that a particular value survives (i.e., remains in the circle) after 100 steps, and then use linearity of expectation to compute the expected number of distinct values.\n\nHowever, the problem is that the events of different values surviving are not independent, so linearity of expectation still holds, but we have to be careful about dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute for each original value, the probability that it is still present in the circle after 100 steps, and then sum those probabilities? Wait, but that would overcount, since if a value is present in multiple positions, it would be counted multiple times, but we want the expected number of distinct values, not the expected number of \"active\" original values.\n\nHmm, tricky.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that once a value is replaced by a higher value in some step, it can never come back. Since each value is non-decreasing over time. Therefore, once a position's value increases past some value, it can't go back. Therefore, the set of distinct values is a subset of the initial values, but actually, no, because the new values are maxima of triples, so they can be equal to some existing value or a new value that's higher than some but not necessarily higher than all.\n\nWait, actually, since each new value is the maximum of three previous values, the new value is at least as large as each of those three. Therefore, the new value is at least as large as the previous value at that position, but could be equal or larger. However, the new value could be equal to one of the previous values in the triple, or larger than all three.\n\nBut importantly, once a position's value increases, it can never decrease afterward. Therefore, each position's value is a non-decreasing sequence over time. Therefore, the set of distinct values over time is a sequence of sets S_0 ⊇ S_1 ⊇ S_2 ⊇ ... ⊇ S_t, where S_t is the set of distinct values after t steps. Since each S_t is a subset of S_{t-1}, because you can only lose values when they are replaced by higher ones.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values is a non-increasing sequence, starting from the initial number of distinct values (which is a random variable) and decreasing over time until it reaches 1 (if the global maximum has spread throughout the circle) or some other fixed point?\n\nWait, but in the earlier examples with small N, it eventually reached 1, so perhaps for any N, regardless of starting configuration, the process will eventually reach a single value? That seems plausible.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values will decrease until it reaches 1. Therefore, the expected number after 100 steps is the expected value of S_{100}, where S_t is the set of distinct values at time t.\n\nTo compute E[|S_{100}|], perhaps we can model the process in terms of the survival of each initial value.\n\nAlternatively, note that for any value v that is present in the initial configuration, the probability that v survives (i.e., remains present in the circle) after t steps is the probability that there exists at least one position whose value is exactly v at time t, and that this v is not replaced by 