l local maxima from the previous step and their neighbors? Not sure.\n\nWait, perhaps another angle: since each a_i(t) is the maximum of a_{i-1}(t-1), a_i(t-1), and a_{i+1}(t-1)}, then the sequence for each a_i(t) is non-decreasing, and it is equal to the maximum of a_{i-1}(t-1), a_i(t-1), a_{i+1}(t-1)}.\n\nTherefore, each a_i(t) is the maximum of its three neighbors from the previous step. Therefore, this is similar to a cellular automaton where each cell takes the maximum of its three neighbors (including itself). Wait, actually, it's a bit different because it's the maximum of itself and its two immediate neighbors. So it's like a majority vote but with max instead of majority.\n\nIn such cellular automata, often the system tends towards uniformity or some stable pattern. In particular, if there is a single maximum, it tends to spread as we saw. If there are multiple maxima, they might merge.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after sufficient steps, all cells will have the global maximum, so the number of distinct values becomes 1. The question is, after 100 steps, how close are we to that?\n\nGiven that for N=2025, the time to fully propagate the maximum is about floor(N/2) = 1012 steps. Since 100 is much less than 1012, the propagation hasn't completed yet. Therefore, the number of distinct values is still relatively large.\n\nBut how to compute the expectation?\n\nPerhaps we can model the expected number of distinct values after t steps as a function of t, but I need a better approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe notice that in expectation, each position's value after t steps is the maximum of some random subset of the initial values? Not sure.\n\nWait, here's an idea. Since each step takes the maximum over triples, perhaps after t steps, each position's value is the maximum of some increasing number of overlapping triples from the initial configuration.\n\nIn other words, after t steps, the value at position i is the maximum of all the initial values in some window of size 2t+1 around position i? Wait, maybe?\n\nWait, let me think recursively. Let me denote by A^{(t)} the array at time t.\n\nThen, A^{(0)} is the initial array.\n\nThen, A^{(1)}[i] = max(A^{(0)}[i-1], A^{(0)}[i], A^{(0)}[i+1})\n\nSimilarly, A^{(2)}[i] = max(A^{(1)}[i-1], A^{(1)}[i], A^{(1)}[i+1})\n\nBut A^{(1)}[i-1], etc., themselves are maxima of triples from A^{(0)}. So perhaps after t steps, the value at position i is the maximum of some set of initial values within a certain distance?\n\nSpecifically, the influence of an initial value at position j on the value at position i after t steps might be limited to positions within a certain distance.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after t steps, the value at position i is the maximum of all initial values in the interval [i - t, i + t] modulo N? Not sure, but maybe something like that.\n\nAlternatively, considering that each step propagates the maximum one step further, then after t steps, the maximum from any initial position can influence a window of 2t+1 positions? Hmm, this might be the case.\n\nSuppose that an initial maximum at position k will propagate outward one step per iteration. Therefore, after t steps, it will have influenced a window of radius t around position k. Therefore, the value at any position i will be the maximum of all initial values within distance t from i?\n\nWait, but actually, the propagation might be more complex because it's a circle and the updates are simultaneous.\n\nAlternatively, in one dimension, if you have a line of cells where each cell takes the maximum of itself and its neighbors, then a single maximum would spread one cell per step to the left and right. On a circle, it would spread in both directions, so the time to cover the circle would be N/2 steps, as we thought earlier.\n\nTherefore, in the circle, after t steps, the maximum from an initial position k will have spread to all positions within distance t of k. Since it's a circle, the maximum will cover an arc of length 2t+1 (from k-t to k+t) after t steps.\n\nTherefore, after t steps, the value at any position i is the maximum of all initial values in the interval [i - t, i + t] modulo N.\n\nIf that is the case, then after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum over a sliding window of size 2t+1 around that position.\n\nIf that holds, then the value at each position is the maximum over a window of size 2t+1 of the initial values.\n\nIf that is true, then the number of distinct values after t steps would be the number of distinct window maxima over all windows of size 2t+1 in the initial circular arrangement.\n\nHowever, this might not be exactly accurate because the propagation isn't exactly a simple window maximum, but perhaps asymptotically or in expectation, it approximates that?\n\nAlternatively, maybe in expectation, due to the randomness of the initial values, the expected number of distinct values after t steps can be approximated by considering the probability that two nearby positions have overlapping windows that don't cause their maxima to be the same?\n\nThis seems complicated, but perhaps we can use linearity of expectation in some way.\n\nLet me think: the expected number of distinct values after t steps is equal to the sum over all possible values v of the probability that v is present in the configuration at time t.\n\nBut since the values are continuous (they are real numbers in [0,1]), this is tricky because there are uncountably many possible values. However, perhaps we can instead consider that for any two distinct initial values, the probability that they remain distinct in the final configuration can be considered, but this also seems difficult.\n\nAlternatively, note that the process is such that the only way a value v can disappear is if it is overtaken by a higher value in some neighborhood. Therefore, the survival probability of a particular value v is the probability that no higher value is present within a certain distance that can overwrite it before time t.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the key is that after t steps, each position's value is the maximum of a random subset of the initial values within a certain range, and due to the uniform independence and randomness, the expected number of distinct maxima over these subsets can be computed.\n\nAlternatively, since all initial values are independent uniform [0,1], perhaps after many steps, the expected number of distinct values tends to 1, but after 100 steps, it's still some function that we can approximate?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the process causes the values to \"smooth out\" such that after enough steps, the number of distinct values is roughly proportional to 1/t or something? Not sure.\n\nWait, perhaps an easier way is to note that for each position, its value after t steps is the maximum of some set of initial values. The expected value at each position after t steps is the expected maximum of a certain number of independent uniform [0,1] variables.\n\nWait, that might be a way to compute the expected value at each position, but the question is about the number of distinct values, not the expected value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct values after t steps is equal to the expected number of local maxima in the initial configuration after t steps of spreading. Since each local maximum will spread and potentially cover more area, but the number of distinct values might decrease as higher maxima overwrite lower ones.\n\nAlternatively, given the complexity, maybe the problem has a clever answer that I'm missing.\n\nWait, going back to the problem: 2025 numbers on a circle, each step replacing each number with the max of itself and its two neighbors. After 100 steps, expected number of distinct values.\n\nGiven that in the examples with small N, after a number of steps (like floor(N/2)), the entire circle becomes the global maximum. Since 100 is much less than floor(2025/2)=1012, perhaps after 100 steps, the number of distinct values