s inside the hexagon might not necessarily enter and exit through edges. For example, if both points are on the same side of a diameter, then the line might not cross any edges at all? Wait, no, actually, any line passing through two points inside the hexagon must cross the boundary of the hexagon an even number of times, right? Because it starts inside, exits through one edge, then enters again through another edge, and so on, ending up inside again. Wait, but actually, since it's a straight line, it can only cross the boundary an even number of times. Since a hexagon is a convex polygon, any straight line passing through two points inside the polygon will either stay entirely inside the polygon (if the two points are on the same side of a line that's tangent to the polygon?) or cross the boundary an even number of times? Wait, actually, in a convex polygon, any straight line passing through two interior points will either lie entirely inside the polygon or cross the boundary twice? Wait, actually, in a convex polygon, if you have two points inside, the line segment connecting them is entirely inside the polygon, right? Because convexity implies that the line segment between any two points is entirely contained in the polygon. Wait, hold on, that's true.\n\nAh, right! In a convex polygon, the line segment connecting any two points inside the polygon is entirely contained within the polygon. Therefore, the line extending beyond the segment might cross the boundary, but the segment itself is entirely inside. Wait, but the problem says \"a line passing through both of the points\". So, the line is infinitely long, but the two points are inside the hexagon, so the line passes through the two points, which are inside the hexagon. Then, the line could extend beyond the hexagon in both directions, but the portion of the line that is inside the hexagon is just the segment between the two points. Wait, but actually, the line itself passes through the two points, but the lin