rough opposite vertices or through the midpoints of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, perhaps if the line connecting the two points is parallel to one of those three lines that go through opposite edges, then it might be more likely to intersect those edges? Hmm, but actually, being parallel to such a symmetry axis might not necessarily mean it would intersect those edges. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the hexagon in coordinates so that we can parameterize the positions of the two points and then compute the condition for the line between them intersecting a pair of opposite edges.\n\nLet me try that approach.\n\nLet me set up a coordinate system for the regular hexagon. Let me choose coordinates such that the center of the hexagon is at the origin (0,0), and one of the vertices is at (1,0). Since it's regular, the vertices can be given in the complex plane as the sixth roots of unity, scaled appropriately. Let me recall that a regular hexagon can be inscribed in a circle of radius r. Let me assume the hexagon has a unit radius, so the distance from the center to any vertex is 1. Therefore, the vertices are at angles 0°, 60°, 120°, 180°, 240°, 300°, with coordinates (cos θ, sin θ) for θ = 0°, 60°, etc.\n\nAlternatively, if I want to make calculations easier, maybe it's better to have the hexagon with side length 1? Wait, but in terms of coordinates, using the unit circle might be easier because then coordinates are straightforward. However, the side length of a regular hexagon inscribed in a circle of radius r is equal to r. Wait, actually, in a regular hexagon, the side length is equal to the radius of the circumscribed circle. So, if we set the radius to 1, then each side length is 1. Alternatively, if we set the side length to 1, then the radius is also 1. Hmm, okay, so maybe using a unit circle radius for the hexagon is fine.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use a coordinate system where the hexagon is aligned with the x-axis and y-axis in such a way tha