where the line intersects each pair of opposite edges.\n\nGiven the hexagon's six-fold symmetry, each pair of opposite edges will have an angular sector of 60° where lines in those directions intersect that pair.\n\nSpecifically, for Pair 2 (top and bottom edges), the directions that intersect them are those between 60° and 120°, but let's verify.\n\nWait, let's consider the other quadrants.\n\nFor θ between 90° and 150°, the line would have a negative slope if we consider direction beyond 90°, but let's think in terms of standard angles from 0 to 360°.\n\nActually, to avoid confusion, let's consider the three pairs of opposite edges and their corresponding angular ranges.\n\nEach pair of opposite edges will have a 60° arc of directions where lines in those directions intersect that pair.\n\nFor Pair 2 (top and bottom horizontal edges):\n\nThe directions where the line intersects the top and bottom edges are those where the slope is between ±√3 (i.e., angles between 60° and 120° from the x-axis), but need to confirm.\n\nWait, let me re-express in terms of angles between 0° and 360°.\n\nFor Pair 2 (top and bottom edges):\n\nThe line intersects the top and bottom edges when its angle θ is such that the slope is steeper than the edges' slopes.\n\nThe top edge is horizontal, so a line steeper than the edges adjacent to it (which have slope ±√3) will intersect the top and bottom edges.\n\nWait, the edges adjacent to the top edge (Edge 1-2) are the right-top edge (Edge 0-1) and the left-top edge (Edge 2-3), both of which have slopes of -√3 and +√3 respectively.\n\nTherefore, a line with slope between -√3 and √3 will intersect the left and right edges, while a line with slope steeper than √3 or less than -√3 will intersect the top and bottom edges.\n\nWait, this is getting clearer.\n\nLet me consider the slope m of the line. The line through the origin has equation y = m x.\n\nIt will intersect the top edge y = √3/2 when x = (√3/2)/m, provided that this x is between -0.5 a