rsect the left and right edges (which are not part of the horizontal pair). For example, the line y = 0 intersects the left edge (edge FA or CD?) at (-1, 0) and the right edge (edge AB or DE?) at (1, 0). These are vertices on edges that are not part of the horizontal pair. So the horizontal line intersects two edges that are not a pair of opposites.\n\nTherefore, such a line does not intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nA line not parallel to any pair will intersect two edges that are not opposite, so it doesn't intersect any pair of opposite edges.\n\nA line parallel to one pair does not intersect that pair but intersects two other edges, which are also not opposite.\n\nWait a second, then does any line intersect a pair of opposite edges?\n\nWait, consider a line passing through the center with a slope of 0 (horizontal). It intersects the left and right edges, which are opposite edges (FA and CD?), but those are not a pair of opposite edges. The opposite of FA is CD? Let me recall:\n\nEdges:\n\nAB and DE are opposite.\n\nBC and EF are opposite.\n\nCD and FA are opposite.\n\nTherefore, edges FA and CD are opposite. So the horizontal line y=0 intersects edges FA and CD at their midpoints? Wait, at x=-1 and x=1, those are vertices A and D, which are on edges FA and CD respectively. Since FA and CD are opposite edges, then yes, the horizontal line does intersect a pair of opposite edges (FA and CD).\n\nWait, vertices A and D are on edges FA and CD, which are opposite edges. So the line y=0 intersects both FA and CD at vertices A and D, which are on opposite edges. Therefore, it does intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nSimilarly, the vertical line x=0 intersects BC and EF at their midpoints, which are on opposite edges.\n\nA line with slope √3 through the center intersects BC and EF at points B and E, which are on opposite edges (BC and EF are opposite).\n\nTherefore, lines through the center intersect a pair of opposite edges, but also lines like the horizontal and vertical lines do so as well.\n\nTherefore, all lines through the center intersect a pair of opposite edges, but also other lines do so.\n\nTherefore, the set of lines that intersect a pair of opposite edges includes all lines through the center, plus other lines.\n\nNow, the set of lines through the center is a one-dimensional subset of the two-dimensional space of lines, so it has measure zero. However, there are also other lines that intersect opposite edges not passing through the center.\n\nFor example, the line we considered earlier from (0.2, √3/2 - ε) to (0.2, -√3/2 + ε) is a nearly vertical line near x=0.2, which intersects BC and EF within their x-ranges, and does not pass through the center.\n\nTherefore, there are lines not through the center that intersect opposite edges, so the total measure is non-zero.\n\nHowever, calculating this measure requires integrating over the possible positions.\n\nPerhaps the probability is 1/2. Here's a heuristic argument: For any line not passing through the center, it either intersects a pair of opposite edges or not, and by symmetry, the probability might be 1/2. But this is just a guess.\n\nAlternatively, since the hexagon can be divided into three pairs of opposite edges, and for any line, it can be associated with one of the three pairs it could intersect, but due to symmetry, each pair is equally likely, so the probability for each pair is 1/3, totaling 1, but this contradicts because a line can only intersect one pair or none.\n\nWait, if a line intersects a pair, it can only intersect one pair, since the three pairs are mutually orthogonal in terms of their orientations. Therefore, the total probability is the sum over the three probabilities, each being P, so 3P = probability that the line intersects any pair. The question is asking for this total probability.\n\nIf we can argue that for any line, it either intersects a pair or doesn't, and by symmetry each pair is equally likely, then P = probability for one pair, and total probability is 3P. If the probability of intersecting any pair is equal to the probability of not intersecting any pair, then 3P = 1/2 ⇒ P = 1/6, so total probability 1/2. This is a common symmetry argument.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the probability is 1/2.\n\nGiven that in the square the probability is zero, but in the hexagon it's non-zero, and given the symmetry, I think the answer is 1/2.\n\nWait, but I need to verify this.\n\nImagine that for any line, if it doesn't pass through the center, it either intersects a pair of opposite edges or not. By symmetry, the set of lines through the center is measure zero, so the probability is determined by the other lines.\n\nSuppose that for any line not through the center, it intersects a pair of opposite edges with probability 1/2, then the total probability would be 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, here's an argument using a random rotation:\n\nSuppose we fix one point and consider the second point. The line through them will have a random angle. Due to the hexagon's symmetry, the probability distribution of angles is uniform over [0, π). For each angle θ, the line will intersect a pair of opposite edges if θ falls into a certain range.\n\nGiven the hexagon's symmetry every 60 degrees, the favorable angles for each pair are 60 degrees apart. Since there are three pairs, each pair corresponds to a 60-degree arc in the [0, π) interval.\n\nTherefore, the probability for each pair is 60/180 = 1/3, so the total probability is 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = 1, which is impossible.\n\nWait, this must be wrong because a line can't intersect three pairs.\n\nAlternatively, each pair's favorable angles are 60 degrees, but they overlap? No, because the three pairs are separated by 60 degrees each.\n\nWait, let me think of the angle θ as the angle of the line with respect to the x-axis. The three pairs of opposite edges have normals at angles 0°, 60°, and 120° from the x-axis.\n\nThe condition for the line to intersect a particular pair of opposite edges corresponds to the angle θ lying within a certain range around the normal direction of that pair.\n\nFor example, to intersect the horizontal pair (BC and EF), the line must be nearly horizontal, within a certain angular range around 0° or 180°.\n\nDue to the hexagon's symmetry, the range of angles where the line intersects a particular pair of opposite edges is 60 degrees. Therefore, the probability for each pair is 60/180 = 1/3, so the total probability is 1/3 * 3 = 1, which can't be, since a line can't intersect more than one pair.\n\nAh, here's the mistake: the angular ranges for the three pairs overlap. Each pair's favorable angle ranges are adjacent but not overlapping.\n\nActually, the three pairs are separated by 60 degrees each, so the total favorable angle coverage is 3 * 60 = 180 degrees, which is half the circle. Therefore, the probability would be 1/2.\n\nYes! That makes sense. If each pair corresponds to a 60-degree arc of angles where the line would intersect that pair, and the three arcs together cover 180 degrees, then the probability is 1/2.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Pair 1 (BC and EF) corresponds to angles between -30° and 30° (relative to the x-axis).\n\n- Pair 2 (another pair) corresponds to angles between 30° and 90°.\n\n- Pair 3 corresponds to angles between 90° and 150°, etc.\n\nWait, actually, let's think carefully.\n\nSuppose the three pairs of opposite edges have normals at angles 0°, 60°, and 120° from the x-axis. The angle θ of the line must be within 30° of each normal to intersect that pair's edges.\n\nTherefore, for each pair, the favorable angles are in a 60° arc centered on the normal direction.\n\nSince the three normals are spaced 60° apart, the three 60° arcs will cover the entire 180° of possible angles (since θ is defined modulo 180°). Specifically, each pair's favorable angles cover a 60° arc, and together they cover 180°, so the probability is 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a random line intersects any pair of opposite edges is 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1/2, so \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}.\n\nBut wait, let me verify this with an example.\n\nSuppose θ is the angle of the line, measured from the x-axis. The three pairs of opposite edges have normals at 0°, 60°, and 120°.\n\nFor the line to intersect the pair with normal 0° (horizontal), the line must be within 30° of horizontal, i.e., between -30° and 30°, which is a 60° arc.\n\nSimilarly, for the pair with normal 60°, the favorable angles are between 30° and 90°, another 60° arc.\n\nFor the pair with normal 120°, the favorable angles are between 90° and 150°, another 60° arc.\n\nWait, but 30° to 90° is 60°, and 90° to 150° is another 60°, and then 150° to 210° would be the next, but since angles are modulo 180°, 150° is equivalent to -30°, so actually the three arcs are:\n\n- From -30° to 30° (60°)\n\n- From 30° to 90° (60°)\n\n- From 90° to 150° (60°)\n\nBut 150° is equivalent to -30° modulo 180°, so the total covered is from -30° to 150°, which is 180°, hence exactly half the circle.\n\nTherefore, the probability is indeed 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a random line intersects a pair of opposite edges is 1/2.\n\nSince the problem asks for this probability, the answer should be 1/2.\n\nBut wait, in my earlier example with the square, this approach would give a probability of 1/2 as well, but we saw that in the square it's actually zero. There's a contradiction here.\n\nWait, in the square, the normals to the sides are at 0°, 90°, etc. So following the same logic, the favorable angles for each pair would be 45° around the normal, leading to 90° coverage, hence probability 1/2. But in reality, in the square, only lines aligned with the axes intersect opposite sides, which have measure zero. So the discrepancy arises because in the square, the 'favorable' angle ranges actually do not correspond to the physical possibility of intersecting opposite sides.\n\nTherefore, the above argument works for the hexagon but