sect the bottom edge y=-√3/2, we have x_bottom = (-√3/2 - c)/m\n\nFor both x_top and x_bottom to lie within [-0.5, 0.5], we need:\n\n-0.5 ≤ (√3/2 - c)/m ≤ 0.5,\n\nand\n\n-0.5 ≤ (-√3/2 - c)/m ≤ 0.5.\n\nAssuming m > 0 (since for m <0, the inequalities would flip signs appropriately).\n\nLet me suppose m >0 for simplicity.\n\nThen:\n\nFrom the first inequality:\n\n-0.5 ≤ (√3/2 - c)/m ≤0.5\n\nMultiply all parts by m:\n\n-0.5m ≤ √3/2 - c ≤0.5m\n\nRearranged:\n\n√3/2 -0.5m ≤ c ≤ √3/2 +0.5m\n\nFrom the second inequality:\n\n-0.5 ≤ (-√3/2 - c)/m ≤0.5\n\nMultiply by m:\n\n-0.5m ≤ -√3/2 - c ≤0.5m\n\nRearranged:\n\n-0.5m + √3/2 ≤ -c ≤0.5m - √3/2\n\nMultiply by -1 (reversing inequalities):\n\n0.5m - √3/2 ≥ c ≥ -0.5m - √3/2\n\nWait, this is getting too tangled. Perhaps it's better to consider the envelope of such lines.\n\nAlternatively, the set of lines intersecting both top and bottom edges can be seen as the set of lines whose y-intercepts c satisfy certain conditions relative to their slopes m.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for a line to intersect both top and bottom edges, its midpoint between the intersection points must lie along the vertical line x=0?\n\nNot necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the set of lines intersecting both top and bottom edges form a region in the space of lines, and the probability is the area of this region divided by the area of all possible lines.\n\nHowever, in geometric probability, the measure of lines in a convex body isn't straightforward because lines are one-dimensional.\n\nPerhaps a better approach is to use the concept of \"random chords\" in a convex polygon.\n\nIn convex polygons, the distribution of random chords can be complex, but for a regular polygon with high symmetry, there might be known results.\n\nI recall that in a square, the probability that a random chord is longer than the side length is 1/3, but this is for a specific type of random selection (e.g., random endpoints, random midpoint, etc.). The problem here 