rd between left and right edges won't cross left or right edges except at the edges.\n\nWait, actually, in a square, any chord that is not aligned with the sides will cross two adjacent sides. Wait, no. For example, a chord going from the midpoint of the left side to the midpoint of the right side is horizontal and crosses the top and bottom edges? No, it's horizontal, so it's parallel to the top and bottom edges, so it only intersects the left and right sides at its endpoints.\n\nWait a minute, in a square, a horizontal chord that is not at the top or bottom edge will only intersect the left and right sides if it's vertical? Wait, no. Wait, a horizontal line in a square will intersect the left and right sides only if it's vertical? No, confusion.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nIn a square with sides aligned with the axes, a horizontal line y = c (where -1 < c < 1) will intersect the left and right sides (x = ±1) at points (±1, c). Therefore, such a horizontal chord (if it's a line segment between two points on the square) would cross the left and right sides if it spans from left to right. But if the chord is just a line segment between two points inside the square, then a horizontal chord can be entirely within the square without crossing any sides, except at its endpoints if it's on the boundary.\n\nWait, actually, the chord is a line segment between two interior points. So, if the two points are on opposite sides, then the chord will cross the boundary between them. Wait, no, the chord is entirely within the square.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused between lines and line segments. The problem says \"a line passing through both of the points\", so it's the infinite line, not the line segment between the points. So in the square, the line passing through two random interior points will exit the square through two points on the boundary, and we want the probability that those two boundary points are on opposite sides.\n\nIn the square, the boundary consists of four sides. A line can exit through two opposite sides (left and right, or top and bottom) or two adjacent sides (e.g., top and right). The probability that it exits through opposite sides would be the probability that the line's slope is such that it goes from left to right or top to bottom.\n\nFor a square, due to symmetry, the probability that a random line exits through opposite sides is 1/2, since for any line not aligned with the axes, it has an equal chance to exit through left/right or top/bottom? Wait, actually, no.\n\nWait, let's consider a square from (0,0) to (1,1). Take a random line through two points in the square. The direction of the line is random. The probability that it crosses the left and right sides versus the top and bottom sides.\n\nDue to symmetry, the probability should be equal for crossing left-right or top-bottom, so each has probability 1/2. But actually, lines can also cross adjacent sides, like top and right. Wait, but in a square, any line that isn't aligned with the axes will cross two opposite sides or two adjacent sides?\n\nNo, actually, in a square, a line will cross exactly two sides, which can be either a pair of opposite sides or a pair of adjacent sides.\n\nThe set of lines that cross opposite sides are those that are not parallel to any pair of adjacent sides. Wait, no.\n\nActually, in a square, a line can cross two adjacent sides if it is nearly parallel to one of the sides but offset.\n\nFor example, a line with a very slight positive slope will cross the top and right sides, or the bottom and right sides, depending on its position.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a random line crosses a pair of opposite sides is less than 1/2.\n\nActually, in a square, the probability that a random line crosses two opposite sides is 1/2. Here's why: for any line not aligned with the axes, it has a 50% chance to cross left-right or top-bottom? No, actually, the probability is related to the angle.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the lines can be