ake X moves left to 9 and snake Y moves right to 12: then they are even further apart. Hmm.\n\nThe only way two snakes would end up on the same arc is if one snake moves into the arc that another snake is moving out of? Wait, no, because movements are simultaneous. So, for two snakes to end up on the same arc, one must move into the arc that the other is moving out of? Wait, actually, no. Let me think.\n\nSuppose snake A is on arc x and snake B is on arc y. If snake A moves to y and snake B moves to x, then after the move, snake A is on y and snake B is on x. So they just swapped positions. They don't end up on the same arc unless x = y, which they weren't initially. Alternatively, if snake A is on x and snake B is on x, then they were already on the same arc, but that's impossible initially because they start on distinct arcs. But in subsequent steps, if two snakes move into the same arc, that could happen. For example, suppose snake C is on arc z, and snake D is on arc w. If snake C moves to w and snake D moves to z, then they swap, so they are still on different arcs. But if two snakes both move to the same arc from different directions? For instance, snake E is on arc a and snake F is on arc b. If snake E moves right to b, and snake F moves right to c, then unless b = c, they don't collide. Wait, actually, to have two snakes end up on the same arc after moving, one snake must move into the arc that the other snake is moving into? Wait, no, because their movements are independent. So, suppose snake 1 is on arc x, and snake 2 is on arc y. If snake 1 moves to y, and snake 2 moves to y as well, then both would end up on y, causing a collision and a merge. So that's possible if both choose to move into the same target arc. Since their movements are independent, the probability that two specific snakes both move towards each other's current positions is... hmm, let's see.\n\nWait, in order for two snakes to end up on the same arc after moving, they must both choose to