 same arc.\n\nTherefore, the only way two snakes can end up on the same arc is if they both move towards the same target arc that is not their current position. For instance, snake 1 at position x and snake 2 at position y, with x ≠ y. If snake 1 moves towards y and snake 2 moves towards x, then they would swap, but not collide. However, if snake 1 moves towards some z and snake 2 also moves towards z, then they can collide at z. So for any two snakes, the probability that they both move towards the same target arc (other than their current positions) is equal to... Hmm, let's formalize this.\n\nSuppose we have two snakes, S and T, currently located at positions p and q respectively, with p ≠ q. Each snake has two choices: move left or move right. Since the circle is symmetric, we can assume without loss of generality that p < q (modulo 45). Let's fix the circle as a linear arrangement from 0 to 44 with 0 adjacent to 44.\n\nFor snake S at position p, moving left would take it to p - 1 (mod 45), and moving right would take it to p + 1 (mod 45). Similarly for snake T at position q: moving left to q - 1, moving right to q + 1.\n\nThe two snakes will collide (end up on the same arc) if and only if:\n\nEither:\n\n1. S moves right to p + 1 and T moves left to q - 1, and p + 1 = q - 1, i.e., q = p + 2. Then, if that is the case, then S moving right and T moving left would result in both moving to p + 1 = q - 1, so they would both be at p + 1 (which is also q - 1). Wait, but if q = p + 2, then moving S right to p + 1 and T left to q - 1 = (p + 2) - 1 = p + 1, so they both go to p + 1. Therefore, they collide at p + 1.\n\nAlternatively,\n\n2. S moves left to p - 1 and T moves right to q + 1, and p - 1 = q + 1 mod 45, which would require that p - 1 - (q + 1) ≡ 0 mod 45 ⇒ p - q - 2 ≡ 0 mod 45 ⇒ p - q ≡ 2 mod 45. Since we assumed p < q, this would mean that p - q is negative, so p - q ≡ 2 mod 45 ⇒ q - p ≡ -2 mod 45 ⇒ q - p ≡ 43 mod 45. Since the circle has 45 arcs, the distance between p and q can be measured in either direction, but since we fixed p < q, the clockwise distance from p to q is q - p, and the counterclockwise distance is 45 - (q - p). So, if the counterclockwise distance between p and q is 2, then q - p ≡ 43 mod 45. In that case, moving S left and T right would result in both going to p - 1 and q + 1, which are the same position because p - 1 ≡ q + 1 mod 45 ⇒ p - 1 - (q + 1) ≡ 0 mod 45 ⇒ same as above. So in that case, they would collide at p - 1.\n\nTherefore, in general, two snakes at positions p and q will collide if and only if the distance between them is exactly 2 arcs in one direction, and they move towards each other (i.e., S moves towards T and T moves towards S). In that case, they will collide at the midpoint. Alternatively, if they are two apart in the other direction, then moving in the opposite direction would also cause a collision.\n\nWait, actually, in the first case, if the clockwise distance from p to q is 2, then moving S right and T left causes collision at p + 1. In the second case, if the counterclockwise distance from p to q is 2 (i.e., clockwise distance is 43), then moving S left and T right causes collision at p - 1. So in either case, when two snakes are two arcs apart, moving towards each other will result in a collision. If they are more than two apart, then moving towards each other might not result in a collision? Wait, let's see.\n\nSuppose two snakes are three apart: p and q = p + 3. Then, if S moves right and T moves left, then S goes to p + 1 and T goes to q - 1 = p + 2. So they end up at p + 1 and p + 2, which are adjacent, but not the same arc, so no collision. Similarly, if S moves left and T moves right, S goes to p - 1 and T goes to q + 1 = p + 4, so they are even further apart. So no collision. Therefore, only when two snakes are exactly two apart can they collide by moving towards each other. If they are further apart, they can't collide in that way.\n\nAlternatively, if two snakes are closer than two apart, say one apart, then moving towards each other would result in them swapping positions, but not colliding. For instance, p and q = p + 1. If S moves right to q, and T moves left to p, then they swap, ending up on each other's original positions, so they are still adjacent but not on the same arc. So no collision.\n\nTherefore, in summary, two snakes will collide if and only if they are exactly two arcs apart and move towards each other. The probability of that happening for a given pair is: since each has a 1/2 chance to move left or right, the probability that S moves right and T moves left is (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4. Similarly, if they are two apart in the other direction (i.e., counterclockwise distance of 2), then the probability that S moves left and T moves right is also 1/4. However, in the circle, for any two distinct snakes, the distance between them can be measured in two directions, so we have to consider both possibilities? Wait, actually, for any two distinct snakes, the minimal distance between them is either d or 45 - d, where d is the clockwise distance. So, if the minimal distance is 2, then they can collide by moving towards each other. If the minimal distance is greater than 2, then they cannot collide by moving towards each other. If the minimal distance is 1, they can't collide by moving towards each other either.\n\nTherefore, for any pair of snakes, the probability that they will collide in a given time step is equal to the probability that they are exactly two apart and moving towards each other, which is 1/4 if they are two apart, and 0 otherwise.\n\nBut in our initial configuration, the snakes are spaced every three arcs. So initially, the minimal distance between any two snakes is 3 arcs. Since they are every third arc, so between snake i and snake i+1, there are two empty arcs, so the minimal distance is 3. Therefore, initially, no two snakes are two apart; they are three apart. Therefore, initially, no collisions can occur in the first step? Because all snakes are at least three apart, so the minimal distance is 3, so they can't collide in the first step.\n\nTherefore, the first possible collisions can only occur after some time steps when snakes have moved closer together.\n\nHmm, so this is going to be a complex process where the snakes are moving around the circle, potentially getting closer and merging when they are two apart and moving towards each other.\n\nWe need to compute the expected number of seconds until only one snake remains. This seems like a problem that might be approached using linearity of expectation and some sort of Markov chain analysis, but given the complexity of the system, it might be quite involved.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's some symmetry or clever way to model this.\n\nFirst, let's note that the circle has 45 arcs, and there are 15 snakes initially, each separated by two empty arcs. So the configuration is highly symmetric. Maybe we can exploit that symmetry.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model the problem in terms of the distances between the snakes? Since the circle is symmetric, the relative positions might be what matter. However, with 15 snakes, tracking all pairwise distances seems complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of this as a coalescing process, similar to the coalescent in population genetics, where particles (snakes) move and collide (coalesce) when they meet certain conditions. In the standard coalescent model, particles move towards each other and coalesce when they meet, but here the movement is deterministic in direction with probabilities, and the merging only occurs when they are two apart and moving towards each other? Hmm, not exactly the same.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of each snake as performing a random walk on the circle, with step left or right with probability 1/2 each, and when two snakes meet (i.e., are at the same position after moving), they merge into a single snake that then continues moving as a single entity. The merging process would then continue until only one snake remains. The question is the expected time until this happens.\n\nThis seems similar to a \"voter model\" or \"interacting particle system\" on a circle, with specific merging rules.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider the problem in terms of the number of snakes decreasing over time, and compute the expected time until the number reaches 1.\n\nLet me consider the expected number of time steps until the system reaches a single snake. Let me denote by E(n) the expected time remaining when there are n snakes. Then, we need to compute E(15). The base case is E(1) = 0, since if there's only one snake, no more time is needed.\n\nFor n ≥ 2, we can write a recursive equation: E(n) = 1 + sum_{possible transitions} P(transition) * E(n - k), where k is the number of snakes that merge in that transition. However, the transitions are not straightforward because the merging of snakes depends on their positions and movements, which are probabilistic and dependent on the current configuration.\n\nTherefore, this might be too complex because the transition probabilities depend on the current arrangement of the snakes, which is not easily characterized when n is large (like 15). So, perhaps we need a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the problem as each snake performing a random walk on the circle, and collisions cause merging, so the process continues until all have merged into one. In such systems, sometimes the expected time can be related to the total \"distance\" or some potential function that decreases with each collision.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a discrete-time Markov chain where the state is the current configuration of the snakes, and we need to compute the expected number of steps to reach the absorbing state of one snake. However, the state space is enormous because there are many possible configurations of 15 snakes on a circle of 45 positions. So this is not feasible directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is some invariant or quantity that decreases monotonically and can be used to compute the expectation via optional stopping theorem or similar.\n\nLet me think about the total \"energy\" or some other conserved quantity. Since it's a circle, perhaps the sum over all snakes of their positions modulo 45? But since the snakes are moving, their positions change over time, so that might not be conserved. Alternatively, maybe the sum of the positions squared or something?\n\nAlternatively, since all movements are symmetric, perhaps we can consider the configuration up to rotation. Since the circle is symmetric, maybe we can fix one snake's position and consider the relative positions of the others. However, even then, with 15 snakes, the number of relative configurations is still too large.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the problem in terms of the gaps between the snakes. Initially, the gaps between consecutive snakes are all 3 arcs (since they are every third arc). Each time two snakes merge, the gap structure changes. Maybe we can model the gaps as a collection of numbers, and the process is about the gaps closing when snakes move into them?\n\nWait, actually, when two snakes merge, they form a longer snake, which can then potentially close gaps with other snakes. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the problem in terms of the circle being divided into 45 positions, and each snake occupies one position. The movement of the snakes is like independent random walks on the circle, with the caveat that when two snakes land on the same position, they merge into a single snake that then behaves as a single entity moving from that position.\n\nThis is similar to the \"birth-death\" process on a circle, but with merging instead of births and deaths.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the expected time until all snakes have merged into one as similar to the expected time for all the random walkers to meet at a single point. But in this case, meetings can occur not just at a single point, but also when two walkers merge into a single walker which then can meet others.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of \"meeting times\" in random walks. In one dimension, for two particles starting at distance d apart, the expected time until they meet is d^2, but this is on a circle and with multiple particles, and with merging.\n\nWait, actually, in a circle with N positions, if you have M particles performing independent random walks, each moving left or right with probability 1/2 each step, then the expected time until they all meet at a single position... Hmm, I don't recall the exact formula for that, but perhaps we can derive it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that each snake's movement is a simple symmetric random walk on the circle, and the merging occurs when two snakes are at the same position. Then, the process continues until only one snake remains. The key point is that when two snakes merge, they form a single snake that then moves as a single entity. So the merged snake's movement is deterministic in the sense that it must move as a whole, but its direction is still random each time step.\n\nWait, actually, the merged snake still has to choose left or right with probability 1/2 each step. So it's like the merged snake is also performing a random walk, but with a larger \"step\" in the sense that it occupies multiple arcs. However, its movement is still just shifting left or right as a single block.\n\nHmm, perhaps we can model the system in terms of the gaps between the snakes. Let me try that approach.\n\nInitially, there are 15 snakes equally spaced around the circle, each separated by 2 empty arcs. So the circle is divided into 15 segments, each of length 3 (i.e., 2 empty arcs plus 1 occupied arc). Wait, actually, the distance between two consecutive snakes is 3 arcs (since they are every third arc). So the gap between each snake is 3 arcs. Since it's a circle, there are 15 gaps, each of length 3.\n\nNow, when a snake moves, it can either move left or right. If it moves towards another snake, it might reduce the gap between itself and that snake, potentially causing a collision if the gap becomes 2 and they move towards each other. Alternatively, if it moves away, it increases the gap.\n\nBut when two snakes merge, they form a single snake occupying two adjacent arcs (if they were two apart) or more? Wait, actually, as per earlier analysis, merging occurs when two snakes are two apart and move towards each other, resulting in them occupying the same arc? Wait no, actually, when two snakes are two apart and move towards each other, they end up on the same arc? Wait, let's go back.\n\nSuppose two snakes are two arcs apart. Let's say snake A is at position x, and snake B is at position x+2. If snake A moves right and snake B moves left, then snake A goes to x+1 and snake B goes to x+1. Therefore, they both end up at x+1, so they merge into a single snake at x+1. So the merged snake is of length 1? Wait, no, because they were each length 1 and merged into occupying the same arc. Wait, but the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". Since both were on x and x+2, and now they are on x+1? Wait, no, they both moved to x+1. So they were on x and x+2, then moved to x+1 and x+1, so merging into a single snake on x+1. Therefore, the merged snake is length 1? But that seems odd because they were two apart and now merged into a single arc. Hmm, but according to the problem statement, \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". The previous snakes were on x and x+2, but now they are both on x+1, so the merged snake is on x+1, which is only one arc. So it's as if they collapsed into one arc? That seems like a loss of information about where they were. Maybe that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when two snakes merge, they form a snake that spans the two original arcs? Wait, but if they were two apart and moved into the same arc, then they can't span two arcs anymore because they are now on the same arc. Hmm, maybe the problem is that when two snakes merge, they form a snake that occupies both arcs that the original snakes were on? Wait, no, in the example above, the original snakes were on x and x+2, but after moving, they are both on x+1, so the merged snake is only on x+1. That seems like the merged snake is just a single arc. Alternatively, maybe when two snakes merge, the merged snake occupies the union of the arcs that the two snakes were on before moving? But in that case, if they were on x and x+2, the merged snake would occupy x and x+2, but those are separated by an arc. So that would be a snake of length 2? But then how does it move? It would have to move as a single entity spanning two arcs. Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nWait, going back to the problem statement: \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nAh! The key is that the merged snake occupies the arcs of both previous snakes. So, if two snakes were on arcs A and B, then the merged snake is on arcs A and B. So regardless of whether they were adjacent or not, when they merge, the merged snake occupies both arcs that the original snakes were on. Therefore, in the case where two snakes were two apart (say on x and x+2), and they moved into each other's positions (i.e., snake A moved to x+2 and snake B moved to x), then they would now be on x and x+2 again? Wait, no, if they moved towards each other, they would have moved into the middle arc. Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, let's clarify with an example. Suppose snake A is on arc x, and snake B is on arc x+2. They decide to move towards each other: snake A moves right to x+1, and snake B moves left to x+1. Then, after moving, both snakes are on arc x+1. Therefore, the merged snake would occupy the arcs of both previous snakes, which were x and x+2. Wait, but they are now on x+1. That seems contradictory. Alternatively, maybe the merged snake occupies the arcs that the two original snakes were on before moving? That is, even though they moved, the merged snake is still considered to occupy the original positions? That doesn't make sense because the snakes have moved.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem means that when two snakes merge, the merged snake occupies the set of arcs that the two snakes currently occupy after moving. In the example above, after moving, both are on x+1, so the merged snake is just on x+1. If instead, snake A was on x and snake B on x+1, and they both move to x+1, then the merged snake is on x+1. But if snake A was on x and snake B on x+3, and they both move to x+1 and x+2 respectively, then they wouldn't merge. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\" means the arcs that the two snakes were on before merging. So in the case where snake A was on x and snake B was on x+2, and they moved to x+1 and x+1, then the merged snake occupies x and x+2? But they are no longer there. This is very confusing.\n\nGiven the problem's ambiguity, perhaps I should look for another way. Maybe the problem is intended to have snakes merge only when they are on adjacent arcs, and then the merged snake is of length 2, and so on. In that case, \"touching\" means being on adjacent arcs, and merging combines them into a length 2 snake. Then, the movement of the merged snake is as a single entity, shifting left or right as a block.\n\nAssuming that interpretation, let me proceed with that, since it might be the most straightforward, even if not perfectly matching the problem's exact wording.\n\nSo, assuming that \"touching\" means being on adjacent arcs, and merging results in a snake of length 2 occupying both arcs. Then, when a snake of length L moves, it shifts left or right as a block, so occupying L consecutive arcs. Then, the merged snake of length 2 can then potentially merge with another snake if it moves into an adjacent arc.\n\nIn this case, the process is similar to a system of particles on a circle, where each particle is a snake, and when two particles are adjacent, they merge into a single particle of double length, which then can continue moving and potentially merge with others.\n\nThis seems similar to the \"voter model\" or \"coalescing random walk\" on a circle, but with specific rules for merging.\n\nAlternatively, this is analogous to the \"abelian sandpile model\" or other cellular automata, but again, not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the expected time until all snakes have coalesced into one, considering that each snake is performing a random walk and merging with neighbors when they come close.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the expected time by considering the number of snakes and the \"distance\" between them.\n\nInitially, there are 15 snakes equally spaced every 3 arcs. The key is that the spacing between any two adjacent snakes is 3 arcs. Since the circle has 45 arcs, 45 / 15 = 3, so that checks out.\n\nEach time step, every snake independently decides to move left or right. If two snakes move towards each other and close the gap between them, they might merge.\n\nGiven the symmetry, perhaps we can consider the problem in terms of the gaps between snakes. Let me try to formalize this.\n\nLet me denote the gaps between consecutive snakes (in the circular arrangement) as g_1, g_2, ..., g_{15}, where each g_i is the number of empty arcs between snake i and snake i+1 (with snake 16 being snake 1). Initially, all gaps are 2 empty arcs plus the occupied arc of the next snake, so actually, wait, no: each snake occupies one arc, and the gaps between snakes are the number of unoccupied arcs between them. Since the snakes are every third arc, the number of unoccupied arcs between two consecutive snakes is 2. For example, between snake at position 0 and the next at position 3, there are arcs 1 and 2, so two unoccupied arcs. Therefore, each initial gap is 2.\n\nWait, actually, the distance between two consecutive snakes is 3 arcs: one occupied by the first snake, then two unoccupied, then the next snake. So the number of unoccupied arcs between two snakes is 2. Therefore, the gap size (in terms of unoccupied arcs) is 2 for all gaps initially.\n\nNow, when a snake moves, it can either move left or right, which will affect the gaps on either side of it.\n\nSuppose we focus on a single snake and its two neighboring gaps. Let's say we have a snake between two gaps of sizes g_left and g_right. If the snake moves left, then it will reduce the left gap by 1 (since the snake is leaving its current position, which was between the left gap and itself), and increase the right gap by 1 (since the snake is now occupying the position to the left, so the right gap is now one larger). Similarly, if the snake moves right, it will reduce the right gap by 1 and increase the left gap by 1.\n\nHowever, if the snake moves into a gap that is adjacent to another snake, then merging may occur. For instance, if a snake moves into a gap that has a neighboring snake on the other side, then they might merge.\n\nWait, this is getting a bit tangled. Maybe it's better to think in terms of the gaps between snakes. Each time a snake moves, it affects the gaps on either side. If two snakes are adjacent (i.e., the gap between them is 0), then they would have merged already, so gaps cannot be zero initially? Wait, actually, gaps are the number of unoccupied arcs between snakes, so if two snakes are adjacent (no unoccupied arcs between them), the gap would be 0. But initially, all gaps are 2.\n\nWhen a snake moves into a gap, it can reduce the gap size. For example, if a snake moves towards a gap of size g, it will decrease that gap by 1 (since the snake is now occupying the next arc towards that gap). However, if the gap it is moving into is adjacent to another snake, then upon moving, it might merge with that snake.\n\nWait, perhaps an example will help.\n\nConsider three consecutive snakes: Snake A at position x, Snake B at position x+3, and Snake C at position x+6. The gaps between them are 2 unoccupied arcs each: between A and B, there are arcs x+1 and x+2; between B and C, arcs x+4 and x+5; and between C and A (going the other way), arcs x+7 to x+44 and so on, but let's just focus on the forward direction.\n\nSuppose Snake A decides to move right. Then, it will move to position x+1. This reduces the gap between Snake A and Snake B from 2 to 1 (since now there's only one unoccupied arc between them: arc x+2). The gap between Snake A's new position and Snake B is now 1.\n\nHowever, Snake A moving to x+1 does not merge with Snake B because they are now separated by one unoccupied arc (arc x+2). To merge, they would need to be adjacent, i.e., with a gap of 0.\n\nSimilarly, if Snake A moves left, it goes to x-1 (modulo 45), which would increase the gap between Snake C and Snake A by 1 (since moving away from Snake C), and decrease the gap between Snake A and its previous left neighbor (if any) by 1.\n\nAlternatively, suppose Snake A and Snake B are separated by a gap of 2. If Snake A moves right and Snake B moves left, then they will each reduce their respective gaps by 1, and potentially approach each other.\n\nSpecifically, if Snake A is at position x with a gap of 2 to the right (towards Snake B), and Snake B is at position x+3 with a gap of 2 to the left (towards Snake A), then:\n\n- If Snake A moves right to x+1, the gap between A and B becomes 1 (since now between x+1 and x+3 there is arc x+2).\n\n- If Snake B moves left to x+2, the gap between B and A becomes 0 (since Snake B is now at x+2 and Snake A is at x+1, so they are adjacent with no gap).\n\nWait, no: if Snake B moves left from x+3 to x+2, then Snake A is at x+1 and Snake B is at x+2, so they are adjacent, separated by zero unoccupied arcs. Therefore, they are now adjacent, so they would merge into a single snake occupying arcs x+1 and x+2.\n\nAh! So in this case, if two snakes are separated by a gap of 2, and both move towards each other, then they reduce the gap to 0 and merge.\n\nTherefore, in this scenario, when two snakes are separated by a gap of 2, and they move towards each other, they merge. If they don't move towards each other, or if one moves away, the gap may increase or stay the same.\n\nTherefore, the merging can only occur when a pair of snakes are separated by a gap of 2 and move towards each other. The probability of that happening for any such pair is 1/4, as calculated earlier (each has a 1/2 chance to move towards the other, so 1/2 * 1/2 = 1/4).\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can model the system in terms of the gaps between snakes, where each gap is a number (number of unoccupied arcs between two snakes), and the merging process only occurs when a gap reduces to 0 due to two adjacent snakes moving towards each other.\n\nHowever, the problem is that the gaps are not independent; the movement of one snake affects multiple gaps. For example, a single snake's movement can affect the gap to its left and the gap to its right.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe we can consider that due to the high symmetry of the initial configuration, all gaps are identical and behave similarly, so we can model the system using the number of gaps of a certain size.\n\nInitially, all 15 gaps are size 2. Let me denote by G(k) the number of gaps of size k at any time. Since the total number of gaps is always 15 (because it's a circle), we have G(0) + G(1) + G(2) + ... = 15.\n\nHowever, gaps of size 0 would indicate that two snakes have merged into a single snake, so actually, when a gap reduces to 0, it means that two snakes have merged, and the merged snake effectively replaces the two original snakes and the gap between them. Wait, actually, when two snakes merge, they form a single snake, so the number of snakes decreases by 1, and the number of gaps also decreases by 1, because the two gaps adjacent to the merged snake are now adjacent to each other? Hmm, this complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the number of snakes and the configuration of gaps. But this seems too involved.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the concept of \"linear time\" or \"coalescing time\" in such systems. In the standard coalescing random walk on a circle, the expected time until all particles have met is known? I might need to look for a similar problem.\n\nWait, in the book \"Probability and Computing\" by Mitzenmacher and Upfal, there is a discussion of coalescing random walks, but I don't recall the exact results for the circular case with merging.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of each snake as performing a random walk on the circle, and the merging happens when two snakes occupy the same site. In that case, the problem reduces to the expected time until all 15 random walks have coalesced into a single walker. However, in standard coalescing random walks on a graph, when two particles meet, they merge into one, and the merged particle continues moving. The expected time until all particles have coalesced is known for certain graphs, but I don't recall the exact formula for a circle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can approximate or find a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each snake's movement is a symmetric random walk on the circle. The meeting time of two random walks on a circle can be calculated, but with 15 walkers, it's more complex.\n\nAlternatively, note that in such a system, the expected time until all have merged might be proportional to the square of the circumference divided by the number of snakes or something similar, but I need to think more carefully.\n\nWait, in one dimension, for two particles starting at distance d apart, the expected time until they meet is d². On a circle of N positions, if two particles start at distance d apart (the minimal distance), then the expected time until they meet might be similar?\n\nLet me consider two particles on a circle of N positions. Each moves left or right with probability 1/2 each step. What is the expected time until they meet?\n\nThis might be a known result. Let me try to derive it.\n\nLet me denote the positions of the two particles as X and Y, with X and Y being random variables on {0, 1, ..., N-1}. Since the circle is symmetric, we can fix one particle at position 0 without loss of generality, and let the other particle be at position D, where D is between 1 and floor(N/2). The expected time until they meet starting from distance D.\n\nLet E(D) be the expected time until meeting starting from distance D.\n\nAt each step, the particle at position 0 can move to 1 or N-1 (left or right), and the other particle can move to D-1 or D+1 (modulo N). However, since we've fixed one particle at 0, the other particle is at position D, so its movement is relative.\n\nWait, actually, if we fix one particle at position 0, the other is at position D (distance D clockwise from 0). Then, in the next step:\n\n- The fixed particle can move to 1 or N-1 (left or right).\n\n- The other particle can move to D-1 or D+1 (left or right).\n\nHowever, we need to consider their relative positions. Let's consider the distance between them. Let me define the distance as the minimal clockwise or counterclockwise distance between the two particles. Initially, the distance is D (clockwise) or N - D (counterclockwise). Let’s assume D ≤ N/2, so the minimal distance is D.\n\nAfter a step, the distance can change depending on their movements.\n\nCase 1: The fixed particle moves clockwise (to 1), and the other particle moves clockwise (to D+1). Then the new distance becomes (D + 1) - 1 = D (since the fixed particle moved +1 and the other moved +1, so the distance remains the same).\n\nWait, actually, let me think carefully. If the fixed particle is at 0 and moves to 1, and the other particle at D moves to D+1, then the distance between them (clockwise) is (D+1) - 1 = D. The counterclockwise distance would be N - (D+1 - 1) = N - D. So the minimal distance remains D.\n\nCase 2: Fixed particle moves clockwise (to 1), other particle moves counterclockwise (to D-1). Then, the distance becomes |1 - (D - 1)| = |2 - D|. Depending on D, this could be D - 2 or 2 - D. But since D ≥ 1, if D ≥ 2, then the new distance is D - 2. If D = 1, then the distance becomes 0, meaning they met.\n\nCase 3: Fixed particle moves counterclockwise (to N-1), other particle moves clockwise (to D+1). Then the clockwise distance from fixed particle (now at N-1) to the other particle (at D+1) is (D + 1) - (N - 1) mod N. Hmm, this is getting complicated because of the circle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider the distance as a number between 0 and N/2, and model it as a Markov chain.\n\nLet me define the distance as an integer d, where 0 ≤ d ≤ floor(N/2). The state is the current distance d. We start at distance D, and we want E(D).\n\nWhen in state d (distance d), the next step can lead to:\n\n- If the two particles move towards each other: probability 1/2 * 1/2 = 1/4, then the distance decreases by 2: new distance d - 2.\n\n- If they move in the same direction: probability 1/2 * 1/2 + 1/2 * 1/2 = 1/2, then the distance remains the same: new distance d.\n\n- If they move away from each other: probability 1/4, then the distance increases by 2: new distance d + 2. But since we are on a circle, if d + 2 exceeds N/2, it wraps around to N - (d + 2). Wait, no, actually, since we defined distance as the minimal distance, increasing the distance beyond N/2 would actually decrease it again. Therefore, the distance can never increase beyond N/2.\n\nWait, actually, if the distance is d, and they move in the same direction, then the distance remains d. If they move towards each other, distance decreases by 2. If they move away from each other, the distance increases by 2, but since the maximum possible minimal distance is floor(N/2), increasing beyond that would wrap around. Wait, but actually, if the distance is d, and they move away from each other, then the minimal distance becomes min(d + 2, N - (d + 2)).\n\nHmm, this complicates things because the distance isn't necessarily monotonic.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider the distance as a number from 0 to N-1, without taking the minimal, but then the dynamics are symmetric. However, this might not simplify things.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider small N to see if I can find a pattern.\n\nSuppose N = 3 (a triangle), and two particles start at distance 1 apart. What is the expected time until they meet?\n\nWait, N = 3, positions 0, 1, 2.\n\nIf two particles start at positions 0 and 1 (distance 1). Then, each step:\n\n- Particle A (at 0) can move to 2 or 1.\n\n- Particle B (at 1) can move to 0 or 2.\n\nPossible next states:\n\n- If A moves to 1 and B moves to 0: they swap, still distance 1.\n\n- If A moves to 1 and B moves to 2: distance becomes 1 (between 1 and 2).\n\n- If A moves to 2 and B moves to 0: they meet at position 0 and 2? Wait, no: if A moves to 2 and B moves to 0, then the particles are at positions 2 and 0. The distance between them is 1 (since it's a circle of 3). So still distance 1.\n\n- If A moves to 2 and B moves to 2: they both move to 2, so they meet at position 2. So with probability 1/4, they meet in one step.\n\nTherefore, the expected time E(1) satisfies:\n\nE(1) = 1 + (3/4) E(1) + (1/4)(0)\n\nSolving: E(1) = 1 + (3/4) E(1) ⇒ (1/4) E(1) = 1 ⇒ E(1) = 4.\n\nHmm, interesting. So for N=3, starting at distance 1, expected time to meet is 4.\n\nSimilarly, if they start at distance 2 (which is equivalent to distance 1 on a circle of 3), it's the same.\n\nAlternatively, if they start at the same position, E(0) = 0.\n\nAnother example: N = 4 (a square). Let’s compute E(1), the expected time starting at distance 1.\n\nPositions: 0,1,2,3.\n\nTwo particles start at 0 and 1 (distance 1).\n\nEach step:\n\nParticle A (at 0) can go to 3 or 1.\n\nParticle B (at 1) can go to 0 or 2.\n\nPossible next states:\n\n1. A moves to 1, B moves to 0: swap, distance remains 1.\n\n2. A moves to 1, B moves to 2: distance becomes 1 (between 1 and 2).\n\n3. A moves to 3, B moves to 0: now particles are at 3 and 0, distance 1 (since on a circle of 4, the distance between 0 and 3 is 1 clockwise or 3 counterclockwise, so minimal is 1).\n\n4. A moves to 3, B moves to 2: distance between 3 and 2 is 1 (clockwise: 3 to 0 to 1 to 2 is 3, counterclockwise: 3 to 2 is 1). So minimal distance 1.\n\nWait, actually in case 4, if A is at 3 and B is at 2, the distance is 1 (clockwise from 3 to 2 is 1 step counterclockwise? Wait, on a circle labeled 0,1,2,3 clockwise, the distance from 3 to 2 is 1 step counterclockwise, which is the same as 3 steps clockwise. So minimal distance is 1.\n\nTherefore, in all cases except when they move to the same position, the distance remains 1. The only way they can meet is if both move to the same position.\n\nWhen can they meet?\n\nThey meet if both move to the same position. That happens only if:\n\n- A moves to 1 and B moves to 1: but B can't move to 1 because it's already there? Wait no, B is at 1, moving to 0 or 2. So A moving to 1 and B moving to 1 is impossible because B can't stay put. Wait, actually, each particle must move to an adjacent position.\n\nTherefore, the only way they can meet is if:\n\n- A moves to position 1 and B moves to position 0, but then they swap.\n\nWait, actually in the four cases above, they never end up on the same position unless:\n\nWait, in case 1: A moves to 1 and B moves to 0: they swap, so A is at 1, B is at 0. Not the same.\n\nCase 2: A moves to 1, B moves to 2: A at 1, B at 2. Not the same.\n\nCase 3: A moves to 3, B moves to 0: A at 3, B at 0. Not the same.\n\nCase 4: A moves to 3, B moves to 2: A at 3, B at 2. Not the same.\n\nWait a second, in all cases, they don't meet? That can't be.\n\nWait, hold on: if both particles move to the same position. For that to happen, for example:\n\n- A moves to position 1 and B moves to position 1: but B can't move to 1 because it's already there? Wait no, B is at position 1, so moving left would take it to 0, moving right would take it to 2. So it can't stay at 1.\n\nSimilarly, A is at 0, moving left would take it to 3, moving right to 1.\n\nTherefore, the only way they can occupy the same position is if:\n\n- A moves to 1 and B moves to 1: impossible.\n\n- A moves to 3 and B moves to 3: but B can't move to 3 from 1 in one step.\n\n- A moves to 0 and B moves to 0: A can't stay at 0.\n\nWait, actually, in this setup, with N=4 and starting at distance 1, they can never meet? That can't be right.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nWait, starting at positions 0 and 1.\n\nAfter one step:\n\n- If A moves right to 1 and B moves left to 0: they swap, now at positions 1 and 0. So distance is still 1.\n\n- If A moves right to 1 and B moves right to 2: now at positions 1 and 2, distance 1.\n\n- If A moves left to 3 and B moves left to 0: now at 3 and 0, distance 1.\n\n- If A moves left to 3 and B moves right to 2: now at 3 and 2, distance 1.\n\nSo indeed, after one step, they are still at distance 1. So they can never meet? That seems odd.\n\nWait, but on a circle of 4, if two particles start at distance 1, is there a way for them to ever meet?\n\nSuppose they both move in the same direction every time. For example, both move clockwise:\n\n- Step 1: A to 1, B to 2. Distance now 1 (between 1 and 2).\n\n- Step 2: A to 2, B to 3. Distance 1 (between 2 and 3).\n\n- Step 3: A to 3, B to 0. Distance 1 (between 3 and 0).\n\n- Step 4: A to 0, B to 1. Back to start.\n\nSo they cycle every 4 steps without ever meeting.\n\nAlternatively, if they move in opposite directions:\n\nStep 1: A moves right to 1, B moves left to 0. Now at 1 and 0 (distance 1).\n\nStep 2: A moves left to 0, B moves right to 1. Back to start.\n\nSo they oscillate between positions 0/1 and 1/0 without ever meeting.\n\nAlternatively, if sometimes they move towards each other and sometimes away:\n\nSuppose in step 1, A moves right to 1, B moves left to 0: distance remains 1.\n\nIn step 2, A moves left to 0, B moves right to 1: back to start.\n\nAlternatively, step 1: A moves left to 3, B moves right to 2: distance remains 1.\n\nStep 2: A moves right to 0, B moves left to 1: distance 1.\n\nHmm, so it seems that on a circle of 4, two particles starting at distance 1 can never meet? That seems to be the case based on the above.\n\nBut that contradicts intuition because on an even-sized circle, maybe certain distances are impossible to close?\n\nWait, in a circle of even length, if two particles start at an odd distance apart, maybe they can never meet?\n\nIn this case, distance 1 is odd, and N=4 is even. Maybe that's why they can't meet.\n\nWait, let's try N=5 (a pentagon), starting at distance 1.\n\nPositions 0 and 1.\n\nStep 1:\n\nA moves to 1 or 4.\n\nB moves to 0 or 2.\n\nPossible outcomes:\n\n1. A→1, B→0: swap, now at 1 and 0 (distance 1).\n\n2. A→1, B→2: distance between 1 and 2 is 1.\n\n3. A→4, B→0: distance between 4 and 0 is 1 (since minimal distance on a pentagon: 4 to 0 is 1 step clockwise).\n\n4. A→4, B→2: distance between 4 and 2 is 2 (clockwise: 4→0→1→2 is 3 steps, counterclockwise: 4→3→2 is 2 steps; minimal is 2).\n\nAh, here in case 4, the distance becomes 2.\n\nSo now, in some cases, the distance can increase.\n\nContinuing from case 4: distance is now 2.\n\nThen, in the next step, from distance 2:\n\nPossible movements:\n\nParticle A (at 4) can move to 3 or 0.\n\nParticle B (at 2) can move to 1 or 3.\n\nPossible next states:\n\n1. A→3, B→1: distance between 3 and 1 is 2 (clockwise: 3→4→0→1 is 3 steps; counterclockwise: 3→2→1 is 2 steps).\n\nWait, minimal distance is 2.\n\n2. A→3, B→3: they meet at 3. Probability 1/4 (A moves left to 3, B moves right to 3). So they meet.\n\n3. A→0, B→1: distance between 0 and 1 is 1.\n\n4. A→0, B→3: distance between 0 and 3 is 3 (clockwise) or 2 (counterclockwise), so minimal is 2.\n\nTherefore, from distance 2, there is a 1/4 chance to meet, and 3/4 chance to go to distance 1 or 2.\n\nThis is getting complicated, but perhaps we can set up equations.\n\nLet E(d) be the expected time to meet from distance d on a circle of N positions.\n\nFor N=5, starting at d=1:\n\nE(1) = 1 + (probability transitions) * E(new distances)\n\nFrom d=1:\n\nWith probability 1/4, they move towards each other and reduce distance by 2: but d=1-2 = -1, which wraps to N -1 = 4? Wait no, minimal distance can't be negative.\n\nWait, actually, when reducing distance from d=1 by 2, it would become negative, which we take modulo N? Or just consider that they would have met?\n\nWait, if they are at distance 1 and move towards each other, they would meet, right? Because the distance would become 0.\n\nWait, in the N=5 example, if they are at distance 1 and move towards each other:\n\nSuppose they are at positions 0 and 1. If A moves to 1 and B moves to 0, they swap but don't meet. Wait, but to reduce the distance to 0, they need to move into the same position.\n\nAh! So in order to reduce the distance to 0, they must move into the same position. Which only happens if:\n\nFor two particles at distance d, moving towards each other, they will meet only if d is even? Or something?\n\nWait in the N=5 case, starting at distance 1:\n\nIf they move towards each other (A moves right to 1, B moves left to 0), they swap positions but don't meet. To meet, they have to both move into the same spot. Which requires that their movement vectors bring them to the same position.\n\nIn the N=5 example, starting at 0 and 1:\n\nTo meet at position 0: A would have to stay put (but can't), or move left to 4 and B moves left to 0. Wait:\n\nWait, to meet at position 0:\n\n- A must move left to 4, and B must move left to 0. Then A is at 4 and B is at 0: not the same.\n\nTo meet at position 1:\n\n- A moves right to 1, and B stays? No, B must move.\n\n- A moves right to 1, and B moves right to 2: no.\n\nWait, actually in N=5, starting at 0 and 1, there is no way for them to meet in one step. The only way they can meet is if their movements result in both being at the same position.\n\nWhich would require:\n\nA moves to position x, and B moves to position x, where x is adjacent to both their current positions.\n\nFrom 0 and 1:\n\nA can go to 4 or 1.\n\nB can go to 0 or 2.\n\nSo overlapping positions possible:\n\n- A goes to 1, B goes to 0: no overlap.\n\n- A goes to 1, B goes to 2: no overlap.\n\n- A goes to 4, B goes to 0: no overlap.\n\n- A goes to 4, B goes to 2: no overlap.\n\nTherefore, in N=5, starting at distance 1, they can never meet? That can't be.\n\nWait, but in my earlier analysis from distance 2, there was a chance to meet. So perhaps from distance 2, they can meet, but from distance 1 they cannot?\n\nWait, in N=5, if they start at distance 2 (positions 0 and 2):\n\nThen, possible movements:\n\nA (at 0) can go to 4 or 1.\n\nB (at 2) can go to 1 or 3.\n\nPossible overlaps:\n\n- A→1 and B→1: both move to 1. Probability 1/4 (A moves right, B moves left). Then they meet at 1.\n\n- A→4 and B→3: no overlap.\n\n- A→4 and B→1: no overlap.\n\n- A→1 and B→3: no overlap.\n\nTherefore, from distance 2, there is a 1/4 chance to meet in one step, and 3/4 chance to go to other distances.\n\nSo in this case, E(2) = 1 + (1/4)(0) + (3/4)(E(1) or E(3)).\n\nBut this is getting very involved. Maybe it's better to look for a general formula or see if the original problem can be approached differently.\n\nGiven that the original problem has 15 snakes initially, each separated by 3 arcs, and the circle has 45 arcs, which is 3 times 15, so the spacing is consistent.\n\nPerhaps the key insight is that the process is memoryless in some way, or that the expected time can be computed as the sum over all pairs of the expected time for each pair to coalesce, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, notice that each snake is performing a symmetric random walk on the circle, and the merging occurs when two snakes occupy the same position. The time until all have merged is the same as the time until all random walks have met at a single point. However, in the standard coalescing random walk on a circle, the expected time until all have met can be computed, but I'm not sure of the exact formula.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that each snake's movement is independent, so the time until any two meet is a certain value, and then recursively compute the expected time from there. But with 15 snakes, this would require a huge computation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem has a solution related to the number of snakes and the circumference.\n\nGiven that there are 15 snakes on a circle of 45 arcs, which is 3 times as many arcs as snakes, maybe the expected time is related to the square of the circumference divided by the number of snakes? For example, 45² / 15 = 135, but that seems arbitrary.\n\nAlternatively, think of each snake needing to traverse the entire circle to meet others, but with interactions.\n\nAlternatively, since each snake moves every second with 50% chance left or right, the expected time for a single snake to return to its starting point is infinite, but the meeting time is finite because of the interactions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time until all snakes have merged is equal to the expected time for the first collision multiplied by some factor accounting for the remaining snakes.\n\nBut this is vague.\n\nWait, perhaps we can think in terms of the number of possible collisions. Initially, there are no collisions possible because all snakes are spaced at least 3 apart. The first possible collision can occur only after some time when two snakes have closed the distance between them to 2 and then moved towards each other.\n\nGiven the high symmetry, maybe we can compute the expected time until the first collision, then after that collision, we have 14 snakes, and so on, so the total expected time would be the sum over k=15 down to 2 of the expected time between k and k-1 snakes.\n\nIf we can compute E(n), the expected time to go from n snakes to n-1 snakes, then the total time is the sum_{k=2}^{15} E(k).\n\nSo if we can find a formula for E(n), then we can compute the total.\n\nTo compute E(n), the expected time until the next collision given n snakes.\n\nBut how to compute E(n)?\n\nWell, in the case of n snakes, the system has certain configurations, but due to symmetry, we can consider that the snakes are equally spaced, so the gaps between them are all the same. Wait, initially they are equally spaced, but after some moves, they might not be.\n\nHowever, perhaps we can assume that due to symmetry, the gaps are all identical in distribution, so we can model the system based on the gap sizes.\n\nSuppose that at any time, the gaps between the snakes are all the same? No, that might not hold, but maybe in expectation?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the process is such that the gaps evolve in a way that preserves some symmetry.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that the expected time between collisions for n snakes is proportional to n squared or something.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the problem in terms of the expected time until the first collision.\n\nInitially, all snakes are spaced 3 apart. To have a collision, two snakes must move towards each other across a gap of 2. Since all gaps are initially 2, the probability that any specific pair of adjacent snakes will have a collision in the next step is 1/4 (as calculated earlier: each has 1/2 chance to move towards each other, so 1/4).\n\nThere are 15 such adjacent pairs (since it's a circle). So the probability that at least one collision occurs in the next step is 15*(1/4) - ... but we have to subtract the overlaps where two collisions happen simultaneously, etc. But for an approximate expectation, maybe we can use the linearity of expectation.\n\nThe expected number of collisions in the next step is 15*(1/4) = 15/4. However, a collision can only occur if two adjacent snakes are separated by a gap of 2 and move towards each other. Initially, all gaps are 2, so all adjacent pairs are at risk.\n\nHowever, if a collision occurs, it reduces the number of snakes by 1, and also changes the gap structure.\n\nBut since we are computing the expected time until the first collision, maybe we can approximate it as follows:\n\nLet T be the time until the first collision. Then,\n\nE[T] = 1 + P(no collision in first step) * E[T]\n\nSo,\n\nE[T] = 1 / (1 - P(no collision in first step))\n\nBut this is only accurate if the probability of no collision in each step is constant, which it isn't, because after a collision, the gap structure changes, affecting future collision probabilities.\n\nHowever, maybe for an initial approximation, if the probability of no collision is roughly constant, we can use this.\n\nThe probability that no collision occurs in the first step is 1 - 15/4? Wait, no, that can't be, because 15/4 is greater than 1, so that approach is flawed.\n\nActually, the expected number of collisions is 15/4, but the probability that at least one collision occurs is less than or equal to the expected number.\n\nActually, the probability that at least one collision occurs is ≤ 15/4, but since probabilities can't exceed 1, this suggests that my initial approach is invalid because multiple collisions can occur simultaneously.\n\nIndeed, if two non-adjacent pairs of snakes could collide simultaneously, but given the initial spacing, it's impossible for two collisions to occur in the first step because the gaps are all size 2, and to have two collisions, you would need two separate pairs of snakes each reducing their gap to zero, but that would require each pair to move towards each other, which are independent events.\n\nHowever, the probability that two specific adjacent pairs both collide in the same step is (1/4)*(1/4) = 1/16, but there are C(15,2) such pairs, so the probability of at least two collisions is complicated.\n\nThis is getting too involved for an exact calculation without a computer.\n\nPerhaps the problem has a elegant solution that I'm missing.\n\nWait, let's think differently. Since all snakes move independently, maybe the expected time until all snakes have met at a single point can be related to the expected time for a single snake to traverse the circle.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each snake is moving in a random walk on the circle, and the merging happens when two walks meet. The time until all walks have met can be related to the meeting time of the first two, then the next, etc.\n\nIn the standard coalescing random walk on the integers (a line), the expected time until n particles have coalesced is known to be O(n^2). But on a circle, it might be similar.\n\nAlternatively, in the book \"Probability on Trees and Networks\" by Lyons and Peres, there might be results on coalescing random walks on graphs, but I don't have the book handy.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to note that the process is equivalent to each snake performing a symmetric random walk on the circle, and the merging happens when two walks meet, which then form a single walk. The expected time until all walks have merged can be calculated using the formula for the expected time until all particles have met in the coalescing random walk.\n\nIn the coalescing random walk on the integers (a line), the expected time until n particles have met is known to be n(n-1). But on a circle, it might be different.\n\nAlternatively, for a circle with N positions and n particles, the expected time until all particles have met might be proportional to N^2 / n or something similar.\n\nWait, in our problem, N=45, n=15. If we suppose that the expected time is proportional to N² / n, then 45² / 15 = 135. But I need to verify.\n\nAlternatively, in the one-dimensional case with N positions and n particles, the expected time until all have met might be something like N² / (n(n-1)), but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each snake's walk is independent, and the probability that any two specific snakes meet by time t is roughly 1 - e^{-kt} for some k. Then, the expected time for all to meet would be like the maximum of n exponential variables, which is tricky.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the problem in terms of the total number of possible snake positions. Since there are 45 positions and 15 snakes, the probability that no two snakes occupy the same position at time t is roughly (45 - 15t)/45, but this is only valid for small t, and ignores the dynamic nature.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time is simply 45*15 = 675? That seems too large.\n\nAlternatively, since each snake has to traverse the circle, and there are 15 snakes, maybe the expected time is 45 * 15 = 675? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time is 45*14 = 630? Still a guess.\n\nWait, in the standard coalescing random walk on the integers (infinite line), the expected time until n particles have all met is n(n-1). So for n=15, that would be 15*14 = 210. But on a circle of finite size, maybe it's different.\n\nAlternatively, if we model the circle as a line by breaking it at a point, but the circularity complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that in order for all snakes to merge, each pair of snakes must eventually meet. The expected time for all pairs to have met might be the maximum of the meeting times of each pair, but expectation of maximum is hard to compute.\n\nAlternatively, the expected time until the first meeting is T₁, then given that, the expected time until the next meeting is T₂, etc., but this requires knowledge of the distribution of T₁, T₂, etc., which is non-trivial.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem has a answer of 45*14 = 630, but I need to think of a better way.\n\nWait, going back to the initial problem: there are 15 snakes, each starting at positions 0, 3, 6, ..., 42 (every third arc). Each second, every snake moves left or right with probability 1/2. When two snakes occupy the same arc, they merge into one snake. We need the expected time until only one snake remains.\n\nNotice that the merging process is similar to a graph where each snake is a node, and edges exist between nodes that can merge (i.e., are adjacent in position). The merging reduces the number of nodes by 1 each time, and the expected time would be the sum over the expected times between each coalescence.\n\nAssuming that each coalescence event reduces the number of snakes by 1 and that the expected time between each coalescence is constant, then the total time would be (15 - 1) times the expected time per coalescence. But this is only true if the expected time per coalescence is constant, which it isn't because the system becomes less \"active\" as fewer snakes are left.\n\nAlternatively, if we can find that the expected time between each coalescence is proportional to the current number of snakes, then the total expectation would be the sum from k=2 to 15 of k, which is (15)(16)/2 - 1 = 120 -1 = 119, but this is just a guess.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time until the first collision is T₁, then given that one collision has occurred, the expected additional time is T₂, etc., with T_k = something like k^2 or similar.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the problem in terms of the expected time until the first collision.\n\nInitially, there are 15 snakes, each separated by 2 empty arcs. For any two adjacent snakes, the probability they collide in the next step is 1/4, as computed before. There are 15 such adjacent pairs. So the expected number of collisions in the first step is 15*(1/4) = 15/4. However, the expected time until the first collision is not simply 1 divided by this expectation, because multiple collisions can occur and the process isn't memoryless in a simple way.\n\nAlternatively, the probability that no collision occurs in the first step is 1 - 15/4 + ... but this is an overcount because the events are not independent.\n\nActually, the probability that no collision occurs in the first step is equal to 1 minus the probability that at least one collision occurs.\n\nUsing the inclusion-exclusion principle, but this is complex.\n\nLet’s denote the probability that a specific adjacent pair collides in the first step as p = 1/4. There are 15 such pairs. Let the events A_i be that the i-th pair collides in the first step. Then,\n\nP(no collision) = 1 - P(⋃A_i) = 1 - ΣP(A_i) + ΣP(A_i ∩ A_j) - ΣP(A_i ∩ A_j ∩ A_k) + ... \n\nThe first term is ΣP(A_i) = 15*(1/4).\n\nThe second term is ΣP(A_i ∩ A_j). For pairs of events A_i and A_j, if the pairs are adjacent (i.e., share a common snake), then the events are dependent. If they are non-adjacent, then the events are independent?\n\nWait, actually, the pairs are arranged in a circle, so each A_i corresponds to an edge between two snakes. Two edges are adjacent if they share a common snake.\n\nFor two adjacent edges (sharing a common snake), the probability that both events occur (both pairs collide) is:\n\nSuppose we have three consecutive snakes: A, B, C. The pairs (A,B) and (B,C). For both pairs to collide, snake B must move into A's position and snake C must move into B's position? Not sure.\n\nWait, let's consider three consecutive snakes at positions x, x+3, x+6.\n\nThe first pair (x and x+3) colliding requires that snake x moves right and snake x+3 moves left, both towards each other, resulting in collision at x+1.5? Wait no, on the circle with discrete positions.\n\nWait, let's use specific positions.\n\nLet me take three consecutive snakes at positions 0, 3, and 6.\n\nThe first pair (0 and 3) can collide if snake 0 moves right and snake 3 moves left, resulting in both moving to position 1.5? Wait, no, positions are integers. Wait, in our problem, the arcs are discrete positions 0 through 44. So moving from position 0 right to 1, and from position 3 left to 2. Wait, no:\n\nWait, snake at 0 can move left to 44 or right to 1.\n\nSnake at 3 can move left to 2 or right to 4.\n\nSo for the pair (0 and 3) to collide, they must both move towards each other such that they end up on the same position.\n\nThe only way is if snake 0 moves right to 1 and snake 3 moves left to 2: no collision.\n\nOr snake 0 moves left to 44 and snake 3 moves right to 4: no collision.\n\nAlternatively, snake 0 moves right to 1 and snake 3 moves left to 2: they are now at 1 and 2, not the same.\n\nWait, actually, in this configuration, the pair (0,3) cannot collide in one step because they are three apart. Wait a second, earlier we were considering adjacent snakes with a gap of 2, but in this case, the gap between 0 and 3 is 3 arcs (positions 1, 2, and then 3). Wait, no: the distance between snake 0 and snake 3 is 3 arcs, meaning there are two empty arcs between them: positions 1 and 2. So the gap between them is 2 empty arcs. Therefore, they are two apart in terms of empty arcs.\n\nWait, the gap between snake 0 and snake 3 is two empty arcs (positions 1 and 2), so the minimal distance between them is 3 (positions), but in terms of the number of steps to move towards each other, they are two apart in terms of the number of empty arcs between them.\n\nAh! Earlier, we considered that two snakes separated by a gap of 2 empty arcs can collide by moving towards each other. So in this case, snake 0 and snake 3 are separated by a gap of 2 empty arcs, so they are at distance 3 in terms of positions but can collide in one step with probability 1/4.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nSnake 0 is at position 0, snake 3 is at position 3. The gap between them is positions 1 and 2, so two empty arcs. To collide, snake 0 must move right to 1, and snake 3 must move left to 2. Then, after moving, snake 0 is at 1 and snake 3 is at 2. They are now adjacent with a gap of 0? Wait, no, they are at positions 1 and 2, which are adjacent with no empty arcs between them. Wait, positions 1 and 2 are adjacent, so they are now adjacent snakes with a gap of 0 empty arcs between them. Therefore, they have merged? Or do they need to be on the same arc to merge?\n\nAh! Here's the crux: if two snakes are on adjacent arcs, do they merge immediately, or do they need to move into the same arc?\n\nAccording to the problem statement: \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\".\n\n\"Touching\" here likely means occupying adjacent arcs, since if they were on the same arc, they would have already merged before moving. Wait, but if they are on adjacent arcs, then after moving into those positions, they are now adjacent, so they touch and must merge.\n\nWait, in the example above, after moving, snake 0 is at 1 and snake 3 is at 2. These are adjacent arcs (positions 1 and 2), so they are now touching, hence must merge into a single snake occupying both arcs 1 and 2.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the pair (0 and 3) can indeed collide in one step with probability 1/4, by moving towards each other across the two empty arcs.\n\nTherefore, in the three-snake example with snakes at 0, 3, 6:\n\nThe pair (0,3) can collide with probability 1/4, resulting in a merged snake at positions 1 and 2, and the remaining snake at 6.\n\nSimilarly, the pair (3,6) can collide with probability 1/4, resulting in a merged snake at positions 4 and 5, and the snake at 0.\n\nThe pair (6,0) would also have a gap of 2 empty arcs (positions 7,8, etc., but since it's a circle, the gap from 6 to 0 is positions 7, 8, 9, ..., 44, which is more than two, but wait:\n\nWait, the gap between snake 6 and snake 0: since the snakes are at 0, 3, 6,..., the gap between 6 and 0 would be the arcs from 7 to 44, which is 38 arcs, but since it's a circle, the minimal gap is the other way: from 6 to 0 clockwise is 3 steps (positions 7, 8, 9), but actually, the minimal distance between 6 and 0 is min(|0 - 6|, 45 - |0 - 6|) = min(6, 39) = 6. So the minimal distance is 6, which is more than 2, so that pair cannot collide immediately.\n\nWait, this is confusing. In the three-snake example with snakes at 0, 3, 6 on a circle of 45 arcs, the gap between 6 and 0 is actually the long way around, but the minimal gap is the short way, which is 6 arcs? No, minimal gap is the smaller of the two directions. Between 6 and 0:\n\nClockwise: from 6 to 0: 0 - 6 = -6 mod 45 is 39, so 39 arcs.\n\nCounterclockwise: from 6 to 0 via 45: 45 - 6 = 39? Wait no, positions are 0 to 44. From 6 to 0 clockwise: positions 7,8,...,44,0: that's 39 arcs. Counterclockwise: from 6 to 5,4,...,0: that's 6 arcs. So the minimal gap is 6 arcs. Therefore, the gap between snake 6 and snake 0 is 6 arcs, which is larger than 2, so they cannot collide immediately.\n\nTherefore, in the three-snake example, only the two adjacent pairs (0,3) and (3,6) can potentially collide in the next step, each with probability 1/4. The pair (6,0) cannot collide because their gap is too large.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the probability that at least one collision occurs in the first step is 2*(1/4) - P(both (0,3) and (3,6) collide).\n\nThe probability that both (0,3) and (3,6) collide is (1/4)*(1/4) = 1/16, since their movements are independent.\n\nThus, P(no collision) = 1 - [2*(1/4) - 1/16] = 1 - [1/2 - 1/16] = 1 - 7/16 = 9/16.\n\nTherefore, the expected time until the first collision in this three-snake case is 1 / (1 - 9/16) = 16/7 ≈ 2.2857 steps.\n\nBut this is just for three snakes. Doing this for 15 snakes would be extremely tedious.\n\nHowever, noticing a pattern here: in the three-snake case with two possible collision sites, the expected time to first collision is 1/(1 - 2*(1/4) + ...). But it's not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe for the original problem with 15 snakes, the expected time until the first collision is 4 steps. Because initially, each adjacent pair has a 1/4 chance to collide, and with 15 pairs, the expected number of collisions is 15/4, so the expected time until the first collision is 1 divided by the probability of at least one collision per step. But this is not precise.\n\nAlternatively, in the first step, the probability that no collision occurs is 1 - 15/4 + ... but since 15/4 > 1, this approach is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps approximate the probability that no collision occurs in the first step as roughly (1 - 1/4)^15, assuming independence (which they are not), then E[T] ≈ 1 / [(3/4)^15]. But this is likely an underestimate because actual collisions are not independent.\n\nAlternatively, given the problem’s symmetry and the initial spacing, perhaps the expected time is simply 45*14 = 630, but I need a better approach.\n\nWait, here's another idea inspired by the linear case:\n\nIn the linear case with N positions and n particles, the expected time until all have merged can be calculated, but on a circle it's different. However, if we \"break\" the circle into a line by fixing one snake's position, then the problem becomes similar to a line with 45 positions and 15 snakes, but with the ends connected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time is proportional to the number of snakes multiplied by the circumference divided by the number of snakes or something.\n\nWait, the initial spacing between snakes is 3 arcs. Each time a snake moves, it can reduce the distance to an adjacent snake by 1 or increase it by 1. To merge, they need to reduce the distance to 0.\n\nThe expected time for two snakes separated by distance d to meet is d², as in the one-dimensional random walk meeting problem. If we consider that initially, each pair of adjacent snakes are separated by distance 3 (in terms of steps needed to meet?), but actually in terms of the number of empty arcs between them, which is 2.\n\nWait, in the one-dimensional meeting problem, two particles at distance d apart have expected meeting time d². If we have two particles on a circle of N positions, the expected meeting time might be similar but adjusted for circularity.\n\nIf we model the distance between two adjacent snakes as a random walk on the integers from 2 down to 0, with absorption at 0, then the expected time to absorption from distance d is d². So for d=2, it's 4. But in our case, the distance can both increase and decrease due to the other snake's movement.\n\nWait, actually in the one-dimensional case with two particles, the expected time until they meet starting from distance d is indeed d². Here, the distance can fluctuate because both particles are moving.\n\nWait, let me recall: for two independent symmetric random walks on the integers starting at positions 0 and d, the expected time until they meet is d². Yes, that's a classic result.\n\nIn our case, the two adjacent snakes are performing symmetric random walks on the circle, but their relative distance is what matters. However, because they are on a circle, the distance is modulo N, but if we consider the minimal distance, it's similar to the linear case until they meet.\n\nAssuming that the relative distance between two adjacent snakes performs a symmetric random walk on the integers from 0 to floor(N/2), with the expected time to reach 0 from distance d being d². Then, for our initial distance of 2 (since they are separated by two empty arcs), the expected time until they meet is 2² = 4 steps.\n\nHowever, in our problem, there are 15 such pairs of adjacent snakes, each with their own relative distance. But the movements of the snakes are not independent because the snakes are part of a larger system.\n\nHowever, if we assume that the expected time until any one of the 15 pairs meets is 4 steps, then the expected time until the first collision is 4 steps. Then, after that collision, we have 14 snakes, and the expected additional time would be similar but with the new configuration.\n\nIf we assume that after each collision, the system reduces to n-1 snakes with the same average gap size, then the expected time for each subsequent collision would be (current gap)^2, but the gaps have changed.\n\nAlternatively, if each collision reduces the number of snakes by 1 and the expected time between collisions is proportional to the current number of snakes squared, then the total expected time would be the sum_{k=1}^{14} k².\n\nSum_{k=1}^{n} k² = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6. For n=14, this would be 14*15*29/6 = (14*15*29)/6. Let me compute that:\n\n14/6 = 7/3, so 7/3 * 15 * 29 = 7 * 5 * 29 = 35 * 29 = 1015. That seems too large.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's the sum of the expected times for each pair to meet, which would be C(15,2) * 4, but that is also large.\n\nAlternatively, if each of the 15 snakes needs to traverse the entire circle on average, the expected time would be 45 * 2 = 90 steps per snake, so 15 * 90 = 1350, but this is likely overcounting.\n\nAlternatively, since each collision reduces the number of snakes by 1, and assuming that each collision takes on average 4 steps (from the two-particle meeting time), then total time would be 15 * 4 = 60. But this ignores the changing dynamics after each collision.\n\nAlternatively, if each collision takes 4 steps on average, and there are 14 collisions needed to go from 15 to 1, then 14*4=56.\n\nAlternatively, if the first collision takes 4 steps, then the next collision takes longer because the system is now less dense.\n\nSuppose after the first collision, we have 14 snakes. The new configuration would have 14 snakes with some new spacing. Initially, the gaps were all 2, but after a collision, two adjacent gaps are merged into a gap of 4 (since two gaps of 2 become one gap of 4? Wait, no.\n\nWait, when two adjacent snakes merge into a single snake occupying two adjacent arcs, the gap structure changes. Suppose we had a gap of 2 between snake A and snake B, and they merge into a snake occupying both arcs. Then, the gap that was between snake A and its previous left neighbor is now between the merged snake and that left neighbor. Similarly, the gap that was between snake B and its next right neighbor is now between the merged snake and that right neighbor.\n\nSpecifically, before merging, the gap to the left of snake A was g_left, and the gap to the right of snake B was g_right. After merging, the new gap to the left of the merged snake is g_left + 1 (because snake A moved into the previous gap), and the new gap to the right is g_right - 1 (because snake B moved into the previous gap). Wait, perhaps not exactly.\n\nActually, let's consider an example:\n\nSuppose we have three snakes: A at position 0, B at position 3, and C at position 6. The gaps between them are 2 (arcs 1,2) between A and B, and 2 (arcs 4,5) between B and C.\n\nIf A and B merge by moving towards each other (A moves right to 1, B moves left to 2), then the merged snake is at positions 1 and 2. Now, the new gaps are:\n\n- To the left of the merged snake (which is now at 1 and 2): the gap between the previous left neighbor (which would be the snake before A, but in this case, since it's a circle with only three snakes, the left neighbor of A is C (position 6). Wait, no, in this three-snake example, the left neighbor of A (position 0) is the snake before it in the circle, which would be snake C (position 6). The gap between C and A was originally 6 arcs (positions 7-44 and 0), but that's the long way. The minimal gap between C (6) and A (0) is actually 3 arcs (positions 7,8,9?), wait no, this is getting too tangled.\n\nPerhaps it's better to consider that when two adjacent snakes merge, the total number of gaps decreases by 1, and the sizes of the adjacent gaps are affected.\n\nSuppose before merging, between snake X and Y there was a gap of 2, and to the left of X was a gap of g, and to the right of Y was a gap of h. After merging X and Y into a single snake occupying the two arcs between them, the new gap to the left of the merged snake is g + 1 (since X moved into the previous gap), and the new gap to the right is h - 1 (since Y moved into the previous gap). However, the total number of gaps remains the same? Wait no, because merging two snakes reduces the number of snakes by 1, so the number of gaps should also reduce by 1.\n\nWait, initially, there are 15 gaps. After merging two snakes into one, there are now 14 snakes, hence 14 gaps. So one gap is removed.\n\nSpecifically, the gap between the merged snake and its left neighbor, and the gap between the merged snake and its right neighbor are now adjacent, so they form a single larger gap.\n\nFor example, suppose before merging, between snake A and snake B there was a gap of 2, and to the left of A there was a gap of g, and to the right of B there was a gap of h. After merging A and B into a single snake occupying the two arcs between them, the new gap to the left of the merged snake is g + 1 (since A moved into the previous gap), and the new gap to the right is h - 1 (since B moved into the previous gap). However, the gap between the left neighbor of A and the merged snake is now g + 1, and the gap between the merged snake and the right neighbor of B is now h - 1. But these two new gaps are now adjacent to each other, so they form a single gap of (g + 1) + (h - 1) = g + h. Therefore, the total number of gaps remains the same? Wait, no:\n\nWait, originally, there were three gaps: g (left of A), 2 (between A and B), and h (right of B). After merging A and B into a single snake, the gaps are:\n\n- To the left of the merged snake: g + 1\n\n- Between the merged snake and the right neighbor: h - 1\n\nBut these two gaps are now adjacent, so they form a single gap of (g + 1) + (h - 1) = g + h. Therefore, the total number of gaps decreases by 1 (from 3 to 2), which matches the reduction from 15 to 14 gaps when merging two snakes.\n\nTherefore, the new gap structure after a merge is that the two adjacent gaps (g and h) are replaced by a single gap of g + h, with an additional adjustment due to the movement.\n\nWait, actually, the movement of the snakes into the gap reduces the total number of gaps by 1. Specifically, the original gap between A and B (size 2) is eliminated, and the left and right gaps are each modified by ±1, but then merged into a single gap.\n\nThis is getting too detailed, but the key takeaway is that merging reduces the number of gaps by 1 and changes the sizes of the neighboring gaps.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps the problem has a known solution or there's a trick.\n\nWait, the problem says \"the circumference of a circle is divided into 45 arcs, each of length 1\". There are 15 snakes, each of length 1, occupying every third arc. So the initial configuration is highly symmetric.\n\nEvery second, each snake moves left or right with probability 1/2. When two snakes touch, they merge. The process continues until one snake remains.\n\nNotice that in such a symmetric setup, perhaps the expected time is simply 45*14 = 630? Because each snake has to traverse the entire circle on average, and there are 15 snakes, so 45*14 = 630? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since each snake has to cover the entire circumference to meet others, and there are 45 positions, the expected time for a single snake to traverse the circle is 45 steps (since it has a 1/2 chance each step, so expected time is 45/(1/2) = 90? Wait, no, for a symmetric random walk on a circle, the expected return time to the origin is infinite, but the expected time to meet another walker is different.\n\nAlternatively, the expected time until all snakes have met can be calculated as the sum over all pairs of the expected time for each pair to meet. Since there are C(15, 2) pairs, and each pair has an expected meeting time of 4 (from the two-particle meeting time d=2 gives 4), then total expected time would be C(15,2)*4 = (15*14/2)*4 = 105*4 = 420.\n\nBut this is likely an overcount because the meetings are not independent events.\n\nAlternatively, if we consider that each collision reduces the number of snakes by 1, and each collision takes on average 4 steps (from the two-particle meeting time), then with 14 collisions needed to go from 15 to 1, the total expected time would be 14 * 4 = 56.\n\nThis seems plausible, but in the three-snake example earlier, we saw that the expected time to first collision was 16/7 ≈ 2.2857, which is less than 4. So maybe the average time per collision decreases as the number of snakes decreases.\n\nIn the three-snake case:\n\n- First collision expected time: ~2.2857\n\n- Then, with two snakes left, the expected time to merge them would be, using the two-particle meeting time with distance initially reduced?\n\nAfter the first collision in the three-snake example, suppose we merged snakes A and B into a single snake occupying two adjacent arcs. The remaining snake C is now separated from the merged snake by some distance.\n\nIn the three-snake example, suppose after merging A and B, the new snake is between the original gap that was between A and C.\n\nOriginally, between B (position 3) and C (position 6) there was a gap of 2 (arcs 4 and 5). After merging A and B into positions 1 and 2, the distance between the merged snake (at 1-2) and C (at 6) is now:\n\nGoing clockwise from 2 to 6: arcs 3,4,5 → 3 arcs, so distance 3.\n\nGoing counterclockwise: from 2 to 1 (the merged snake) to 0, then to 6? No, counterclockwise from 2 to 6 would be longer. So minimal distance is 3.\n\nThus, the two snakes are now separated by a distance of 3 arcs, so their meeting time would be 3² = 9 steps? If we use the two-particle meeting time formula.\n\nThus, after the first collision at ~2.2857 steps, the expected additional time to merge the remaining two snakes would be 9 steps, totaling ~11.2857 steps. But this is just for three snakes.\n\nIf we extrapolate, for n snakes, the total expected time would be the sum over k=1 to n-1 of (k+1)^2, but this is speculative.\n\nAlternatively, in the three-snake case, the