t that would be a snake of length 2? Wait, but the problem says \"each of length 1\" initially, but after merging, does the merged snake have length 2? Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, let me check the problem again:\n\n\"Initially, there are 15 snakes, each of length 1, occupying every third arc. Every second, each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2. If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nHmm, so \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so if two snakes were on arcs A and B, then after merging, they occupy both arcs A and B. So the merged snake is now occupying two adjacent arcs? Wait, but in the initial setup, each snake is length 1, so occupies one arc. After merging two snakes that were on arcs separated by some distance, the merged snake occupies both arcs? But in the case where two snakes were on adjacent arcs, then merging them would result in a snake that occupies both adjacent arcs? So a snake of length 2? But then, when that snake moves, how does it move? The problem says \"the merged snake moves as one snake\". So presumably, the merged snake can move left or right as a single entity? Wait, but if it's occupying two arcs, then moving left would mean shifting the entire block of two arcs to the left by one arc? Or does each individual snake still move independently?\n\nWait, this is getting a bit confusing. Let me try to parse the problem again carefully.\n\n\"Initially, there are 15 snakes, each of length 1, occupying every third arc.\" So each snake is a single arc.\n\n\"Every second, each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2.\" So each individual snake (which is currently occupying one arc) moves to an adjacent arc, left or right, each with probability 1/2.\n\n\"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nSo \"touching\" must mean that after moving, two snakes are on the same arc. Because if they were on adjacent arcs and moved towards each other, they would swap places, but not occupy the same arc. But if they are on arcs separated by one or more arcs and move towards each other, they might end up on the same arc. Wait, actually, if two snakes are on arcs that are two apart, like position 0 and 2, then if both move towards each other (snake at 0 moves right, snake at 2 moves left), then they would end up on position 1 and position 1? Wait, no: the snake at 0 moving right goes to 1, and the snake at 2 moving left goes to 1. So both end up on position 1, hence merging into a snake of length 2? Because they occupy the same arc, so merging into a single snake on that arc. Wait, but the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so if they were on arcs 0 and 2, and merge into a snake occupying arcs 0 and 2? But that doesn't make sense because they moved to arc 1. Wait, maybe I misinterpret the merging.\n\nWait, hold on. Maybe when two snakes touch, meaning they are on adjacent arcs, then they merge into a single snake that occupies both arcs? So instead of moving to the same arc, if two snakes are on adjacent arcs, then they merge into a snake that spans both arcs? Hmm, that might make more sense. Then, in that case, the merged snake would be length 2, and when it moves, it can move as a single entity. So moving left would mean shifting the entire block of two arcs to the left by one, so the leftmost arc moves left, and the rightmost arc moves left as well? Or does the snake's movement work differently?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when two snakes merge, the merged snake's movement is determined by the movement of the original snakes? Hmm, the problem says \"the merged snake moves as one snake.\" Hmm. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe after merging, the merged snake