mm, maybe the problem assumes that when two snakes merge, they must have been adjacent? Because otherwise, if they are not adjacent, how do they merge? Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, in the initial setup, all snakes are spaced every three arcs apart. So initially, they are all separated by two arcs. So the minimal distance between any two snakes is two arcs. So in order for two snakes to merge, they have to move towards each other until they are on adjacent arcs, and then in the next step, if they both move towards each other, they would end up on the same arc? Wait, but according to the problem statement, merging occurs when they \"touch\", which may or may not require adjacency. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem defines \"touching\" as being on the same arc. So if two snakes end up on the same arc after moving, then they merge. If they are on adjacent arcs, they don't merge, but just stay adjacent. Then, in that case, merging only occurs when two snakes occupy the same arc. Therefore, the process would be that each snake moves left or right with 50% chance, and if any two snakes land on the same arc, they merge into a single snake on that arc, and then that merged snake continues moving in subsequent steps? But then, if a merged snake is on a single arc, how does it move? Because the merged snake is just occupying one arc? Wait, but if two snakes merged into one snake on a single arc, then that snake is effectively a single snake of length 1 again? Because it's occupying the same single arc as before? Wait, that can't be.\n\nWait, no, if two snakes merge into one snake occupying the arcs of both previous snakes, so if they were on arcs A and B, then the merged snake is on arcs A and B. Therefore, if they were on the same arc, then the merged snake is still just on that one arc, but now it's a merged snake? But that doesn't change anything? Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that when two snakes are on the same arc, they merge into a snake that