n\nIn that case, the merged snake of length 2 would move as a single unit, either left or right with probability 1/2 each, just like the original snakes, but now the snake has length 2.\n\nBut then, in this case, when two snakes merge, they form a snake of length 2, and that snake can then potentially merge with other snakes if it moves into another snake's position.\n\nAlternatively, if two snakes are on non-adjacent arcs, say separated by one arc, so like positions 0 and 2, and they both move towards each other, then they would end up on position 1? Wait, but position 1 is between them. Wait, if snake A is at 0 and moves right to 1, and snake B is at 2 and moves left to 1, then they both end up at position 1, so they would merge into a single snake at position 1? But the problem says they should occupy the arcs of both previous snakes. But in this case, the previous snakes were at 0 and 2, so the merged snake should occupy arcs 0 and 2? But they both moved to 1. Hmm, so maybe this is conflicting.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when two snakes move to the same arc, they merge into that arc, and the merged snake occupies only that single arc? But then the problem says \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", which in this case would be arcs 0 and 2, so that seems inconsistent.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. The problem's description is a bit ambiguous, and that's making it hard for me to proceed. Maybe I need to make an assumption here to move forward.\n\nPerhaps the key is that when two snakes occupy the same arc after moving, they merge into a single snake occupying that single arc, but now this snake is considered to have a \"length\" of 2? Or perhaps the length is irrelevant except for the merging condition? Wait, the problem says \"each of length 1\" initially, but after merging, the problem doesn't specify the length. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the length of the snake doesn't matter except for determining how it can merge with others. Since the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so the merged snake occupies both arcs that the two original snakes were on. Therefore, if two snakes were on arcs i and j, then the merged snake is on arcs i and j. Therefore, if the two original snakes were on adjacent arcs, then the merged snake is on those two adjacent arcs. If they were on non-adjacent arcs, then the merged snake is on both arcs, but separated by some distance? Wait, but then the snake would have a \"body\" that is not contiguous? That seems odd for a snake, but maybe that's how it is.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem assumes that when two snakes merge, they must have been adjacent, so that the merged snake is contiguous. Otherwise, merging non-adjacent snakes would result in a snake with a gap, which might complicate things. Since in the initial configuration, all snakes are spaced three apart, so they can only merge if they move closer over time.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"touching\" is defined as being on adjacent arcs, so when two snakes are on adjacent arcs, they merge into a single snake occupying both arcs. So in that case, merging only occurs when two snakes are on adjacent arcs, and then they form a snake of length 2. Then, in subsequent steps, that snake of length 2 can move left or right as a single entity, which would shift its entire body by one arc in the chosen direction.\n\nGiven that the problem mentions \"the merged snake moves as one snake\", it might make sense that the merged snake is treated as a single entity that can move left or right as a whole, just like the original snakes, but with a length. Since the problem doesn't specify the length, maybe the length is just an abstract property that affects how they can merge with others? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the length is irrelevant except that when two snakes occupy the same arc or adjacent arcs, they merge. But the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch\", so maybe \"touching\" is just being on the same arc, so merging happens when two snakes end up on the same arc, resulting in a snake that is now on that single arc, but perhaps with some combined properties? But then the merged snake would just be a single snake of length 1 again? That seems odd because merging two snakes into one would just result in the same as before.\n\nWait, perhaps the key is that when two snakes merge, they form a snake that is the combination of their previous positions, so if they were on arcs A and B, then the new snake is on both arcs A and B, so it's a snake of length 2 (if A and B are adjacent) or a snake that is disconnected? Hmm. Since the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so if the previous snakes were on non-adjacent arcs, then the merged snake would occupy both arcs, even if they are not adjacent. But then that snake would have two separate parts? That seems physically impossible for a snake. So maybe the problem assumes that merging only occurs when the two snakes were adjacent? Otherwise, they can't merge? Because otherwise, you could have a snake with two disconnected parts, which is not really a snake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is on a circular track where the snakes are point particles moving along the circumference, and when two particles meet at the same point, they merge into one particle at that point, and the merged particle continues moving. In that case, the \"snake\" is just a particle, and the length is a red herring? Wait, but the problem says \"each of length 1\", so maybe the length is important for collision detection? Hmm.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me try to think of the snakes as particles moving along the circle, each occupying a single arc. Each particle has a position (an arc), and a length of 1. When two particles end up on the same arc, they merge into a single particle that occupies that arc, and the length becomes... Well, the problem doesn't specify, but since it says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", perhaps if two particles were on arcs A and B, then the merged particle occupies both arcs A and B. But if they were on the same arc, then it's just that arc. But if they were on different arcs, then the merged particle is on both arcs? That would mean the particle has a length equal to the distance between the two arcs? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"length\" here just refers to the number of arcs they occupy. So initially, each snake has length 1 (occupies 1 arc). When two snakes merge, if they were on adjacent arcs, then the merged snake has length 2 (occupies 2 adjacent arcs). If they were on non-adjacent arcs, then the merged snake would have length equal to the distance between them? But that complicates things. Alternatively, perhaps only adjacent merges are possible? Because otherwise, you can't really merge non-adjacent snakes without first moving into adjacency?\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is intended to have snakes as point particles moving on the circle, with the only interaction being when two particles occupy the same arc, in which case they merge into one particle. The \"length\" of the snake might just be a misnomer, or perhaps it's indicating that each snake occupies one arc, and when they merge, they occupy the combined arcs? But in the case of merging non-adjacent snakes, that would create a snake that spans multiple arcs, but the problem might not care about the snake's length except for movement purposes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"length\" here is irrelevant, and all that matters is the position of the snake's \"head\" or something? Hmm, the problem is a bit ambiguous. Given that it's a competition-style problem, perhaps there is a standard interpretation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the snakes as particles on a circle, each occupying a single position (arc), moving left or right each second with probability 1/2, and when two particles occupy the same position, they merge into one particle at that position, and the merged particle then behaves like a single particle moving in subsequent steps. In this case, the \"length\" of the snake is just a distraction, and the problem is essentially a classic \"particle collision and merging\" problem on a circle, where we start with 15 particles equally spaced every 3 positions, and we want the expected time until only one particle remains.\n\nIf that is the case, then maybe we can model it as a Markov chain where the state is the number of particles remaining, but actually, the exact configuration might matter because the particles are on a circle with specific initial positions. However, given the symmetry, perhaps we can exploit rotational symmetry to reduce the state space.\n\nWait, the initial configuration has the 15 snakes equally spaced every 3 arcs. Since the circle has 45 arcs, spacing every 3 arcs gives 15 positions. Because 45 divided by 3 is 15. So the initial configuration is a regular 15-gon on the circle, with each vertex representing a snake's position.\n\nGiven the symmetry, perhaps the expected time to coalesce to a single snake depends only on the number of snakes remaining and their spacing? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, since all the snakes start equally spaced, maybe the problem has some kind of uniformity that allows us to compute the expectation without tracking individual positions.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the process is similar to a random walk where each snake is moving independently, and collisions cause mergers, which then act as a single entity moving in the next step. The key is that when two snakes collide, they form a single snake that then behaves like a single entity for the next step.\n\nThis seems similar to the \"coupon collector\" problem or some kind of coalescent process, but on a circle with movement.\n\nAlternatively, since all movements are symmetric and the circle is uniform, perhaps we can consider the problem on a line instead of a circle, but with the understanding that it's a circle with periodic boundary conditions. However, the circular nature might complicate things because the distance between snakes can wrap around.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can \"unfold\" the circle into a line by considering the positions modulo 45, but since it's a circle, the distance between two positions is the minimum of the clockwise and counterclockwise distances.\n\nHmm, this is getting quite complex. Maybe I need to think of the problem in terms of expected time until all snakes have merged into one, with each step involving independent movements and possible mergers.\n\nGiven that there are 15 snakes initially, each moving left or right with probability 1/2 each step, and when two meet, they merge into one. The process continues until only one snake remains.\n\nIn such a process, the expected time until only one snake remains would depend on the rates at which collisions occur and how mergers reduce the number of snakes.\n\nThis seems similar to a birth-death process where the \"birth\" rate increases the number of snakes (but actually, mergers decrease the number), but actually, in this case, mergers are the only way the number decreases. Each step, the number of snakes can decrease by some amount depending on how many collisions occur.\n\nHowever, since each snake moves independently, the probability of collision between any two snakes in a given time step is dependent on their relative positions and movements.\n\nGiven the complexity of tracking all possible collisions on a circle with moving snakes, perhaps we can model this using some form of linearity of expectation or consider the problem as a Markov chain with states representing the number of snakes, but even that might be difficult because the transitions depend on the specific configurations.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the problem in terms of the expected time until all snakes have collided with each other in some way, but I don't see an immediate way to compute that.\n\nWait, perhaps there is a clever way to model this as a random walk on the number of snakes. Let me consider that.\n\nLet me denote E(n) as the expected number of seconds until only one snake remains, starting from n snakes. We need to find E(15).\n\nWe know that E(1) = 0, since if there's only one snake left, we're done.\n\nFor n ≥ 2, we can try to write a recurrence relation for E(n).\n\nHowever, the problem is that the transitions from n snakes to n-1 snakes depend on the probability of at least one collision occurring in a given time step, but actually, it's possible that multiple collisions occur in a single time step, which could reduce the number of snakes by more than 1. For example, if three snakes all move into the same arc, then they would merge into a single snake, reducing the count by 2 in one step.\n\nTherefore, the number of snakes can decrease by any number from 1 up to n-1 in a single step, depending on how many collisions happen.\n\nThis complicates the recurrence because E(n) would depend not just on E(n-1), but also on the probabilities of transitioning to E(n-2), E(n-3), etc., which themselves depend on the probabilities of multiple collisions.\n\nTherefore, a simple recurrence relation might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can approximate or find some symmetry.\n\nGiven the initial configuration of the snakes, they are equally spaced every 3 arcs on a circle of 45 arcs. Since 45 is divisible by 3, this creates a regular 15-gon configuration. Due to the symmetry, perhaps the expected time until all snakes merge can be calculated by considering the expected time for each pair of snakes to collide, and then combining those expectations?\n\nBut in reality, the collisions are not independent events, and the merging of one pair affects the probabilities of other pairs colliding.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the problem as similar to the \"voter model\" or \"coalescent process\", where particles coalesce when they meet, and we can compute the expected time until all have coalesced.\n\nIn the coalescent process on a circle, the expected time until all particles have merged can sometimes be calculated using properties of the circle and the movement rules.\n\nHowever, in the standard coalescent process on a line or a circle, the particles typically move towards each other or away from each other with certain probabilities, leading to coalescence events.\n\nBut in our case, each particle moves left or right with equal probability each time step, independently. So their motion is like independent random walks on the circle.\n\nGiven that, two particles will meet (i.e., occupy the same position) if their relative motion causes them to converge. On a circle, the relative motion between two particles can be considered as a single particle moving on a circle with circumference equal to the distance between them, but since they are moving independently, their relative velocity is the difference of their individual velocities.\n\nWait, perhaps we can model the relative motion between two snakes.\n\nSuppose we fix one snake and consider the position of another snake relative to it. Since both are moving left or right with probability 1/2 each step, the relative movement between the two can be considered as a random walk on the circle.\n\nLet me formalize this. Let’s consider two snakes, A and B, initially separated by k arcs along the circumference. Since the circle has 45 arcs, the minimal distance between them is min(k, 45 - k). But due to the circular nature, their relative position can be considered modulo 45.\n\nLet’s suppose without loss of generality that we fix snake A at position 0 at time t=0, and snake B is initially at position d, where d is between 1 and 44. The distance between them is d, but since it's a circle, the actual minimal distance is min(d, 45 - d). However, since the initial configuration has all snakes spaced exactly 3 apart, the initial distance between any two snakes is a multiple of 3, specifically 3, 6, 9, ..., up to 42 (since 45 - 3 = 42, but 42 is equivalent to -3 mod 45). Wait, actually, in the initial configuration, each snake is 3 apart, so the minimal distance between any two adjacent snakes is 3 arcs. The distance between non-adjacent snakes would be multiples of 3 up to 21 arcs (since beyond that, it's shorter the other way). For example, the distance between snake 0 and snake 15 would be 15*3 = 45 arcs, which is the full circle, so minimal distance 0? Wait no, snake 15 would actually be at position 15*3 = 45, which is equivalent to 0, so actually, in the initial configuration, the snakes are every 3 positions, so the distance between snake i and snake j is 3*min(|i-j|, 15 - |i-j|). Wait, since there are 15 snakes equally spaced around the circle, the distance between any two snakes is a multiple of 3, specifically 3 times the minimal number of steps between them around the circle.\n\nTherefore, the initial minimal distance between any two snakes is 3 arcs, then 6, 9, ..., up to 21 arcs (since beyond 21, it's shorter the other way). For example, the distance between snake 0 and snake 8 would be 8*3 = 24 arcs clockwise, but counterclockwise it's (15 - 8)*3 = 21 arcs. So the minimal distance is 21.\n\nTherefore, the minimal distance between any two snakes initially is 3, 6, ..., up to 21.\n\nNow, considering two snakes with an initial minimal distance of d arcs (a multiple of 3), what is the expected time until they collide?\n\nIf we can compute the expected time until two specific snakes collide, then perhaps we can use linearity of expectation over all pairs? Although collisions are not independent events, maybe we can still get somewhere.\n\nAlternatively, since all snakes are symmetric and the process is memoryless except for the current positions, perhaps we can model the expected time until all snakes have collided with each other in some way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the problem as equivalent to a system of particles performing independent random walks on the circle, with the only interaction being that when two particles meet, they annihilate and produce a single particle (or merge into one), which then continues moving. The expected time until only one particle remains.\n\nThis is similar to the \"annihilation\" process on a circle with particles performing random walks.\n\nIn such a system, the expected time until all particles have annihilated (merged) into one can sometimes be calculated using potential theory or by solving difference equations, but I'm not sure of the exact method.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of \"meeting time\" for two particles and then extend it to more particles.\n\nLet me first consider the case of two particles on a circle of circumference N, starting at distance d apart (minimal distance). What is the expected time until they meet?\n\nLet me denote this expected time as T(d, N). Since in our problem, N=45 and d is a multiple of 3 initially, but during the process, the distance can change.\n\nWait, but in our problem, the two particles are moving independently left or right each step. So their relative motion can be modeled as a one-dimensional random walk on the circle, but with the circle having circumference N=45.\n\nWait, actually, since both particles are moving, the relative motion between them can be considered as a single particle performing a random walk on the circle. Let me think.\n\nSuppose we fix particle A at position 0 at time t=0, and particle B starts at position d. Then, at each time step, both particles move left or right with probability 1/2 each. The position of particle A at time t is the sum of its individual moves, and similarly for particle B.\n\nThe relative position of B with respect to A is (position_B - position_A) mod N. Let’s denote this relative position as X_t. Then, X_{t+1} = (X_t + (move_B - move_A)) mod N, where move_B is either +1 or -1 (clockwise or counterclockwise) with probability 1/2 each, and similarly move_A is either +1 or -1. Therefore, the change in the relative position is (move_B - move_A). Since move_B and move_A are independent, each ±1 with probability 1/2, so move_B - move_A can be:\n\n- If both move clockwise: +1 - (+1) = 0\n\n- If A moves clockwise and B moves counterclockwise: +1 - (-1) = +2\n\n- If A moves counterclockwise and B moves clockwise: -1 - (+1) = -2\n\n- If both move counterclockwise: -1 - (-1) = 0\n\nTherefore, the possible changes to the relative position are +2, 0, or -2, each with certain probabilities.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- Probability of +2: probability that A moves counterclockwise and B moves clockwise: (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4\n\n- Probability of -2: probability that A moves clockwise and B moves counterclockwise: (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4\n\n- Probability of 0: the remaining probability: 1 - 1/4 - 1/4 = 1/2\n\nTherefore, the relative position X_t performs a random walk on the circle with step sizes -2, 0, +2, each with probabilities 1/4, 1/2, 1/4 respectively.\n\nWe are interested in the expected time until X_t reaches 0 mod N (a collision), starting from X_0 = d mod N.\n\nWait, actually, the minimal distance is d, but on the circle, the relative position can be measured in either direction, but since we're taking mod N, the relative position is just an integer between 0 and N-1. However, the walk is on the circle, so the state space is the integers modulo N, but since the step sizes are multiples of 2, the parity of the position might matter.\n\nWait, actually, since each step changes the relative position by -2, 0, or +2, the relative position modulo 2 is invariant. Because starting from X_0 = d, which has some parity (even or odd). Since N=45 is odd, and d is a multiple of 3, but 3 is odd, so d is a multiple of an odd number, so d can be even or odd depending on how many times 3 is multiplied. Wait, actually, d is the minimal distance, which is a multiple of 3, so d = 3k where k is from 1 to 22 (since minimal distance can't exceed 22.5, but since it's integer, up to 22). Since 3 is odd, 3k is odd if k is odd, even if k is even. So d can be either even or odd.\n\nBut since N=45 is odd, the parity of the relative position modulo 2 will remain the same as the initial parity of d. Because each step changes the relative position by an even number (-2, 0, +2), so modulo 2, the relative position doesn't change. Therefore, if the initial relative distance d is even, then the walk stays even; if it's odd, stays odd. Since we start at distance d (a multiple of 3, so d = 3k), then d mod 2 is equal to k mod 2. Since 3 is odd, so 3k mod 2 = k mod 2. Therefore, the parity of d is the same as k mod 2.\n\nHowever, for a collision to occur, the relative position must reach 0 mod N. But 0 mod N is even (since N=45 is odd, so 0 mod 45 is 0, which is even? Wait, 0 mod 2 is 0, which is even, but 45 is odd. Hmm, actually, the parity of the relative position is modulo 2, and 0 mod N is 0 mod 2 only if N is even? Wait no, parity is just mod 2, independent of N.\n\nWait, perhaps my confusion arises because the walk is on a circle of circumference N=45, but the parity (evenness or oddness) of the relative position is a separate concept. Since each step changes the relative position by an even number, the parity remains constant. Therefore, if the initial relative distance d is even, then the relative position can only reach even positions, so it can never reach 0 mod N if 0 mod N is odd? Wait, but 0 mod N is 0, which is even (since 0 is even). Wait, 0 mod 2 is 0, so if N is odd, 0 mod N is still an even number in terms of mod 2. Wait, 0 is even regardless of N. Wait, actually, parity is just whether the number is even or odd, independent of the modulus. So 0 is even, and N mod 2 is 1 since N=45 is odd. Therefore, the parity of the relative position remains the same as the initial parity. Therefore, if the initial relative distance d is even, then the walk can reach 0 (which is even), but if d is odd, the walk can never reach 0 because 0 is even and they start at an odd parity. Wait a second, this is critical.\n\nWait, so if two particles start at a minimal distance d which is odd, then their relative position has an odd parity, and since all steps change the relative position by even numbers, they can never reach 0 mod N (which is even). Therefore, they will never collide? That can’t be right.\n\nWait, that must mean that my analysis is flawed.\n\nWait, let's think again. Suppose two particles start at positions 0 and 1 on a circle of circumference 3 (for simplicity). Then the minimal distance between them is 1 (since going the other way is 2, but minimal is 1). Wait, but in this case, N=3, which is odd. If their initial minimal distance is 1 (odd), then according to the above, they can never collide because their relative position parity is odd, and 0 is even. But in reality, on a circle of 3, starting at 0 and 1, each step they can move left or right.\n\nLet me simulate this small example.\n\nLet’s have two particles on a circle of 3 positions: 0, 1, 2.\n\nParticle A starts at 0, particle B starts at 1 (distance 1 apart).\n\nAt each time step:\n\nEach particle moves left or right with 50% chance.\n\nLet's track their positions over time.\n\nTime t=0: A=0, B=1.\n\nTime t=1:\n\nA can go to 2 or stay at 0? Wait, moving left from 0 would go to 2 (since it's a circle), and moving right goes to 1.\n\nSimilarly, B can move left to 0 or right to 2.\n\nSo possible moves:\n\nA: 2 or 0\n\nB: 0 or 2\n\nSo possible next positions:\n\nIf A moves left (to 2) and B moves left (to 0): then A=2, B=0. Now they are at distance 1 (since from 2 to 0 is 1 step clockwise, or 2 steps counterclockwise; minimal is 1). Wait, but they are at positions 2 and 0, which are adjacent? On a circle of 3, positions 0 and 2 are adjacent via the long way? Wait no, on a circle of 3, each position is adjacent to the next one. So 0 is adjacent to 1 and 2? Wait, no. On a circle of 3, each position has two neighbors: for position 0, neighbors are 1 and 2? Wait, no: in a circle of 3, the neighbors of 0 are 1 and 2? Wait, actually, in a circle, each position has two neighbors: the next one clockwise and the previous one counterclockwise. So position 0's clockwise neighbor is 1, and counterclockwise neighbor is 2 (since going counterclockwise from 0 would go to 2 in a 3-position circle). Wait, actually, in a circle of 3, moving left from 0 would go to 2, and moving right from 0 goes to 1. Similarly, moving left from 1 goes to 0, moving right from 1 goes to 2, etc.\n\nTherefore, positions 0 and 2 are adjacent via the counterclockwise direction from 0 to 2. Therefore, positions 0 and 2 are adjacent. Therefore, in the scenario where A is at 2 and B is at 0, they are adjacent, so their minimal distance is 1. Wait, but they are at adjacent positions, so their minimal distance is 1. But according to the earlier analysis, since their initial relative distance was 1 (odd), they should never collide? But in this case, they are now at adjacent positions, which is distance 1 apart, but they can still potentially collide in the next step.\n\nWait, in this case, at time t=1, they are at positions 2 and 0. Then at time t=2:\n\nA at 2 can move to 1 or 0 (left or right).\n\nB at 0 can move to 1 or 2 (left or right).\n\nSo possible next positions:\n\nIf A moves left (to 1) and B moves left (to 2):\n\nThen A=1, B=2: they are at adjacent positions again.\n\nIf A moves left (to 1) and B moves right (to 2):\n\nA=1, B=2: same as above.\n\nIf A moves right (to 0) and B moves left (to 2):\n\nA=0, B=2: adjacent again.\n\nIf A moves right (to 0) and B moves right (to 1):\n\nA=0, B=1: back to the original positions.\n\nWait a minute, so in this case, they can oscillate between positions with distance 1 and 0? Wait, but when do they actually collide?\n\nWait, in the above scenario, starting from t=0: positions 0 and 1.\n\nAt t=1, they can be at positions (2,0), (0,2), (1,2), or (0,1). Wait, actually, let me recast all possibilities:\n\nAt t=1, A can be at 2 or 0, and B can be at 0 or 2. So possible pairs:\n\n(A,B) = (2,0), (2,2), (0,0), (0,2).\n\nWait, hold on: if A is at 0 and B is at 0, then they collided and merged? Wait, but in this case, at t=1, if both A and B move to position 0, then they would be at the same position, so they merge. Similarly, if both move to position 2, they merge there.\n\nAh! So in the case where both particles move to the same position, they merge, and in subsequent steps, the merged particle behaves as a single particle.\n\nTherefore, in the example above, starting at (0,1):\n\nAt t=1:\n\n- With probability 1/4: A moves left to 2, B moves left to 0 → positions (2,0) → they are adjacent, not merged.\n\n- With probability 1/4: A moves left to 2, B moves right to 2 → both at 2 → merge into one particle at 2.\n\n- With probability 1/4: A moves right to 1, B moves left to 0 → positions (1,0) → which is the same as (0,1) rotated, so adjacent.\n\n- With probability 1/4: A moves right to 1, B moves right to 2 → positions (1,2) → adjacent.\n\nTherefore, there is a 1/4 chance at t=1 that they merge, and 3/4 chance they remain as two separate adjacent particles.\n\nIf they merge at t=1, then at t=2, we have a single particle at position 2. So the process would have terminated at t=1 with probability 1/4.\n\nIf they don’t merge at t=1, then at t=2, the two particles are at either (2,0), (1,0), or (1,2). Let's take the case (2,0):\n\nAt t=2, from (2,0):\n\nParticle A (originally A) is at 2, and particle B (originally B) is at 0.\n\nNow, each can move left or right:\n\nA at 2: left to 1, right to 0.\n\nB at 0: left to 2, right to 1.\n\nSo possible moves:\n\nA moves left (to 1) and B moves left (to 2): positions (1, 2) → adjacent.\n\nA moves left (to 1) and B moves right (to 1): both at 1 → merge.\n\nA moves right (to 0) and B moves left (to 2): positions (0,2) → adjacent.\n\nA moves right (to 0) and B moves right (to 1): positions (0,1) → original positions.\n\nSo again, there's a 1/4 chance to merge at t=2, and 3/4 chance to stay as two adjacent particles.\n\nSimilarly for other configurations. So in this case, the probability of merging at each step depends on the current distance.\n\nIn this small example, starting with distance 1 (odd), they can merge with probability 1/4 at each step, but they can also stay at distance 1 or swap positions.\n\nWait, so in this case, the expected time until merging can be calculated as a geometric distribution?\n\nWait, in the first step, probability 1/4 to merge, probability 3/4 to stay in a state where they are adjacent but not merged. Then, from the adjacent state, the next step again has 1/4 chance to merge, etc. So the expected time would be similar to a geometric distribution where each trial has success probability p=1/4, so the expectation is 1/p = 4.\n\nBut in reality, when they are in the adjacent state, they have the same probability 1/4 to merge each step, independent of previous steps? Wait, actually, no, because the system could potentially transition back to the original non-adjacent state? Wait in the above example, when starting at (0,1), after moving to (1,0), which is the same as (0,1) rotated, so it's symmetric. Similarly, moving to (1,2) is similar to (0,1) shifted.\n\nTherefore, once they are in an adjacent state, they remain in an adjacent state, with the same probabilities each step. Therefore, the process from the first step onward is: with probability 1/4, merge at time t; with probability 3/4, stay in the adjacent state and try again at t+1.\n\nTherefore, the expected time E to merge starting from adjacent is:\n\nE = 1 + (3/4) E\n\nSolving: E = 1 + (3/4) E ⇒ (1/4) E = 1 ⇒ E = 4.\n\nTherefore, the expected time to merge from an adjacent state is 4 seconds. Then, the total expected time starting from distance 1 is:\n\nAt t=0: distance 1 (non-merged), then with probability 1/4, merge at t=1; with probability 3/4, move to adjacent state, and then from there, expected additional time is 4. So total expectation:\n\nE = (1/4)(1) + (3/4)(1 + E_adj)\n\nBut E_adj is the expected time from the adjacent state, which is 4. Wait, actually, if you start at distance 1, then at t=1, with probability 1/4 you are done in 1 second, with probability 3/4 you are in the adjacent state and then need an additional E_adj seconds. But the adjacent state itself takes E_adj seconds on average. However, when you transition to the adjacent state at t=1, the expected time from there is E_adj, so total expectation is:\n\nE = (1/4)(1) + (3/4)(1 + E_adj)\n\nBut E_adj is the expected time from the adjacent state, which we found was 4. Wait, but actually, when you are in the adjacent state, the expected time to merge is E_adj = 4, so the total expectation starting from distance 1 is:\n\nE = (1/4)(1) + (3/4)(1 + 4) = (1/4)(1) + (3/4)(5) = 1/4 + 15/4 = 16/4 = 4.\n\nWait, that's interesting. So starting from distance 1 (odd), the expected time to merge is 4? But in the calculation above, we considered that from distance 1, you have a 1/4 chance to merge in the first step, and 3/4 chance to go to an adjacent state which then has an expected time of 4. So plugging in E_adj = 4:\n\nE = 1/4 * 1 + 3/4 * (1 + 4) = 1/4 + 3/4 * 5 = 1/4 + 15/4 = 16/4 = 4.\n\nAlternatively, if we think of it as a geometric distribution where each trial (each second) has a success probability p=1/4 of merging, then the expectation is 1/p = 4. So that matches.\n\nTherefore, in this small example, starting from distance 1 (odd), expected time to merge is 4.\n\nBut what if we start at an even distance?\n\nLet's try with distance 2 on the circle of 3.\n\nStarting positions: A at 0, B at 2 (distance 2, which is even).\n\nThen, similar analysis:\n\nAt t=0: positions (0,2).\n\nAt t=1:\n\nA can move to 1 or 0 (left or right).\n\nB can move to 1 or 0 (left or right).\n\nPossible next positions:\n\nA moves left to 1, B moves left to 1: both at 1 → merge.\n\nA moves left to 1, B moves right to 0: positions (1,0) → distance 1 (adjacent).\n\nA moves right to 0, B moves left to 1: positions (0,1) → distance 1.\n\nA moves right to 0, B moves right to 0: both at 0 → merge.\n\nSo the possible outcomes:\n\n- Merge with probability: when both move left or both move right: each has probability 1/4, so total 1/2.\n\n- Move to adjacent distance 1 with probability 1/2.\n\nTherefore, starting from distance 2 (even):\n\nE = (1/2)(1) + (1/2)(1 + E_adj)\n\nWhere E_adj is the expected time from distance 1 (which we already found is 4).\n\nTherefore:\n\nE = 1/2 * 1 + 1/2 * (1 + 4) = 1/2 + 1/2 * 5 = 1/2 + 5/2 = 6/2 = 3.\n\nSo starting from distance 2 (even), expected time is 3.\n\nSimilarly, if we started at distance 0, they are already merged, so E=0.\n\nSo in this small example, depending on the initial distance's parity, the expected time differs.\n\nBut in our original problem, the initial distances between snakes are multiples of 3, so let's see what their parities are.\n\nSince the circle has circumference 45 (odd), and the minimal distance between any two snakes is a multiple of 3 (since they are spaced every 3 arcs). Let’s denote the initial minimal distance between two snakes as d = 3k, where k is an integer from 1 to 22 (since beyond k=22, the distance would be shorter the other way). The parity of d is then the parity of 3k, which is the same as the parity of k, since 3 is odd. Therefore, d is even if k is even, and odd if k is odd.\n\nTherefore, for each pair of snakes, their initial minimal distance is either even or odd, depending on k.\n\nHowever, in our problem, we start with 15 snakes equally spaced every 3 arcs. Therefore, the distances between any two snakes are 3, 6, 9, ..., up to 21 arcs (since beyond that, it's shorter the other way). Let me list them:\n\nFor 15 equally spaced points on a circle of 45, the number of steps between adjacent snakes is 3, so the minimal distance between snake i and snake j is 3*min(|i-j