cent arcs, so that when two snakes are adjacent, they merge into a contiguous snake of length 2. If they are not adjacent, they just pass through each other or something? Wait, but in the problem, movement is simultaneous. So if two snakes are not adjacent and move towards each other, they could end up on adjacent arcs next time, but not overlapping. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is analogous to a cellular automaton where each snake is a cell that can merge with its neighbors under certain conditions. But I need to resolve this ambiguity to proceed.\n\nGiven that this is a math competition problem, perhaps \"touching\" is meant to be overlapping, i.e., being on the same arc. Because in that case, merging would only occur when two snakes end up on the same arc, and the merged snake would then occupy that single arc (so length 1, same as before?), but that seems odd. Alternatively, perhaps when two snakes merge, they form a snake that is the combination of their previous positions, so if they were on arcs i and j, the merged snake is on arcs i and j, so length 2 if they were on adjacent arcs, but length 1 if they were on the same arc? Wait, that might make sense. So if two snakes move to the same arc, then they merge into a snake that is on that single arc (so length 1, same as before). But if they move to adjacent arcs, they form a snake of length 2. So merging can both increase or not the length? That seems inconsistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe all merges result in a snake of length equal to the number of snakes that merged. So if two snakes merge, they form a snake of length 2, regardless of their positions. But in that case, how does the position work? If two snakes were on non-adjacent arcs, merging them would require the snake to occupy both arcs, but not connected? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is intended such that snakes are point particles moving along the circle, and when two particles meet at the same point, they annihilate or merge into a single particle. But the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so maybe it's like the merged snake occupies the union of the arcs previously occupied by the two snakes. So if two snakes were on arcs A and B, then after merging, they are on arcs A and B, so if A and B are adjacent, then it's a contiguous block of two arcs; if they are not adjacent, then it's two separate arcs. But then the snake would be non-contiguous? That complicates things because then the snake could potentially split again? Hmm, that seems too complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem assumes that all snakes are always contiguous. So when two snakes merge, they must be adjacent, so that the merged snake is contiguous. So merging can only occur between adjacent snakes. Then, \"touching\" would mean being adjacent. So in that case, two snakes are considered to touch if they are adjacent, and when they do, they merge into a single contiguous snake of length 2. Then, in subsequent steps, that merged snake can move as a single entity.\n\nThis seems plausible. So in this interpretation:\n\n- Each snake is a contiguous block of arcs.\n\n- Initially, each snake is a single arc.\n\n- When two adjacent snakes (i.e., their blocks are adjacent) merge, they form a single contiguous block of length 2.\n\n- The merged snake then moves as a single block, so in the next step, it can move left or right as a single unit, just like a snake of length 2.\n\n- If a snake of length 2 moves, it can move either left or right by one arc, so the entire block shifts one position.\n\nWait, but how does a snake of length L move? If it's a block of L contiguous arcs, then moving left would mean shifting the entire block one arc to the left, so each arc in the block moves left by one, with the leftmost arc wrapping around if necessary? Wait, but the circle has 45 arcs, so moving left from arc 0 would take you to arc 44.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when a snake moves, it can only move its \"head\" or something? Wait, the problem says \"each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2\". Hmm. Wait, this is critical. The problem says \"each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2\".\n\nSo each snake is moving as a whole? Or is each end of the snake moving? Wait, the problem says \"each snake... moves either one arc left or one arc right\". So probably the entire snake moves as a unit. So if a snake occupies a block of arcs, moving left would mean shifting the entire block one arc to the left, so each arc in the block moves left by one, with the leftmost arc wrapping around to the rightmost if it goes past 0.\n\nSimilarly, moving right would shift the entire block one arc to the right.\n\nTherefore, a snake of length L occupies L consecutive arcs, and when it moves left or right, it shifts its entire block by one arc in that direction.\n\nTherefore, in this interpretation, a snake is always a contiguous block of arcs, and when it moves, the entire block shifts left or right by one arc. Then, merging occurs when two adjacent blocks touch (i.e., are next to each other), and then they merge into a single block of length equal to the sum of the lengths of the two blocks.\n\nWait, but in the problem statement, it says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". So if two snakes are adjacent blocks, then their union is a single contiguous block, so merging them would just combine the two blocks into one. So the merged snake's length is the sum of the two original snakes' lengths.\n\nTherefore, in this interpretation, the snakes are always contiguous blocks, and when two adjacent blocks merge, they form a longer contiguous block.\n\nTherefore, the key is that the snakes are always contiguous, so when they move, they shift their entire block, and if two blocks become adjacent (i.e., their blocks are next to each other) after moving, then they merge into a single block. Wait, but the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge\". So perhaps whenever two snakes are in contact (i.e., their blocks are adjacent or overlapping), they merge immediately. But since they are moving as blocks, if two blocks are adjacent before moving, then they would have already merged in the previous step? Hmm, no, because movement happens all at once.\n\nWait, let me think again. Let me formalize this.\n\nEach snake is a contiguous block of 1 or more arcs. Initially, all snakes are single arcs, spaced every 3 arcs. Each second, every snake simultaneously chooses to move left or right with probability 1/2. When a snake moves left, its entire block shifts one arc to the left (with wrap-around if necessary). Similarly for right. After all snakes have moved, we check for any two snakes that are now adjacent (i.e., their blocks are touching or overlapping). If any two snakes are adjacent or overlapping, they merge into a single block that is the union of their blocks. Then, the merged snake continues moving in subsequent steps as a single block.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge... and the merged snake moves as one snake\". So perhaps the merging happens after all the movements have been executed for that time step, so first all snakes move, then any adjacent or overlapping snakes merge, and then the merged snakes continue.\n\nTherefore, the process is:\n\n1. All snakes move left or right with probability 1/2 each, independently.\n\n2. After all movements, check for any pairs of snakes that are now adjacent or overlapping.\n\n3. For each such pair, merge them into a single snake occupying the union of their blocks.\n\n4. Repeat until only one snake remains.\n\nBut the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge...\". So perhaps it's a continuous process where as soon as two snakes are in contact after a move, they merge immediately, and then that merged snake can participate in further mergers in the same time step? Hmm, but the problem says \"every second, each snake independently moves...\", so the movement is all simultaneous, then the merging happens after all movements. So probably step 1: move all snakes, step 2: merge all adjacent or overlapping snakes into single blocks, step 3: next second.\n\nTherefore, the key is that in each time step, movements happen, then merges happen based on the new positions, and then the next time step begins with the merged snakes.\n\nGiven that, and that each snake is a contiguous block, starting as single arcs, and merging when adjacent blocks touch, then we can model this as a system of blocks on a circle, where each block has a certain length and position, and each time step they all shift left or right, then adjacent blocks merge.\n\nThis seems manageable, but it's still a complex system. Since there are 15 snakes initially, each of length 1, spaced 3 apart. The circle has 45 arcs. So initially, the blocks are at positions 0, 3, 6, ..., 42, each of length 1.\n\nWe need to compute the expected time until all these blocks have merged into a single block.\n\nThis seems like a problem that might have some symmetry or be reducible to a Markov chain, but with 15 initial blocks, the state space is enormous. Maybe we can find some invariant or use linearity of expectation in some clever way?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem has a known solution or relates to some classic problem. Alternatively, maybe the expected time is just the time it takes for all the snakes to coalesce through random walks, with merging when they meet.\n\nAlternatively, since all snakes are moving randomly left or right, their relative positions are undergoing some kind of random walk, and the merging occurs when two snakes come together. Since they start equally spaced, maybe the expected time can be calculated based on the spacing between them and the probability of collisions?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model the distance between adjacent snakes and track the expected time until all distances collapse to zero?\n\nWait, let me think of the initial setup. The circle is divided into 45 arcs, with 15 snakes each starting on every third arc. So the initial spacing between consecutive snakes is 3 arcs. Since it's a circle, the spacing between the last snake and the first snake is also 3 arcs.\n\nEach snake moves left or right each second with probability 1/2. When two snakes move towards each other, their distance decreases; when they move away, their distance increases. Since they start 3 apart, maybe we can model the distance between adjacent snakes as a random walk?\n\nBut since all snakes are moving simultaneously, the interactions between them are not independent. For example, the movement of one snake affects the possible distances to its neighbors.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the system in terms of the gaps between the snakes. Since the snakes are initially equally spaced, maybe the gaps between them are all the same, and due to symmetry, the expected time until all gaps close can be calculated using some kind of formula.\n\nIn some problems with multiple particles performing random walks on a circle, the expected time until they all meet can be calculated by considering the meeting time of two particles and then extending, but with multiple particles, it's more complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of each pair of adjacent snakes as a separate entity, and model the time until they merge, then use some sort of union bound or expectation over the maximum?\n\nWait, but merging is not independent between different pairs. When two snakes merge, that reduces the number of snakes, which affects the interactions of the remaining snakes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the concept of coalescing random walks. In coalescing random walks, particles perform independent random walks and when two meet, they coalesce into one. The expected time until all particles coalesce is a classic problem. However, in our case, the particles are on a circle with periodic boundary conditions, and the merging happens when two adjacent particles meet. Also, the movement is such that each particle moves left or right with probability 1/2 each step, so their motion is like a simple symmetric random walk on the circle.\n\nWait, actually, in the coalescing random walk model, particles perform independent random walks, and when two particles are at the same position at the same time, they coalesce. In our problem, the merging is when two snakes are adjacent or overlapping? Wait, in our problem, merging happens when two snakes are adjacent (i.e., their blocks are touching), which in the coalescing model would correspond to them being at the same position? Hmm, not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, if we model each snake as a point particle moving on the circle, with the rule that if two particles are at distance 1 apart, they merge into a single particle occupying both positions? Wait, but in our problem, the merging happens when they are adjacent blocks, which would correspond to their point particles being distance 1 apart? Hmm, perhaps.\n\nAlternatively, if we model each snake as a point particle moving on the circle, with the circle having 45 positions. Each snake is a point that moves left or right each second with probability 1/2. If two points are at positions i and i+1 (mod 45), then they merge into a single point at, say, i or i+1? Or do they form a merged snake covering both positions?\n\nWait, in our problem, the merged snake occupies the arcs of both previous snakes, so if two snakes are at positions i and i+1, then after merging, the snake is at positions i and i+1, so length 2. So it's a block of two adjacent arcs.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the system can be modeled as blocks of length 1 or more, moving around the circle, with the rules:\n\n- Each block moves left or right with probability 1/2 each step.\n\n- After all movements, any two adjacent blocks (including those that have just become adjacent) merge into a single block of length equal to the sum of the two.\n\n- This process repeats until one block remains.\n\nThis seems like a variant of the \"voter model\" or \"coalescence\" model on a circle with movement.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe we can consider the problem in terms of the expected time until all the initial 15 blocks have merged into one.\n\nGiven the symmetry of the initial configuration, perhaps we can analyze the expected time for two adjacent blocks to merge and then use some recursive reasoning?\n\nAlternatively, since all the initial snakes are symmetrically placed, maybe we can consider the problem in terms of the gaps between the snakes and model the expected time for each gap to close.\n\nInitially, the gaps between consecutive snakes are all 3 arcs. Since the circle is divided into 45 arcs with 15 snakes, each snake occupies 1 arc, so the number of gaps between snakes is 15, each of length 3 arcs. Wait, actually, the total circumference is 45 arcs, and there are 15 snakes, so the number of gaps between snakes is also 15 (since it's a circle), each initially of length 3 arcs.\n\nEach time step, each snake moves left or right with probability 1/2. Therefore, each gap between two snakes can be thought of as being influenced by the movements of the two snakes at its ends.\n\nLet me consider two adjacent snakes, say Snake A and Snake B, separated by a gap of length g arcs. Let's model the evolution of this gap.\n\nWait, but actually, the movement of Snake A and Snake B will affect the gap between them. Suppose Snake A is to the left of Snake B, separated by a gap of g arcs. Each second, Snake A can move right or left, and Snake B can move left or right. The gap between them will change depending on their movements.\n\nWait, but since the snakes are blocks of length 1, moving left or right shifts their entire block. So if Snake A is at position x, moving right takes it to x+1, moving left takes it to x-1 (mod 45). Similarly for Snake B at position y = x + g (mod 45). So the gap between them is g arcs.\n\nIf Snake A moves right and Snake B moves left, then the gap between them decreases by 2: because Snake A is moving towards Snake B, and Snake B is moving towards Snake A. So the gap reduces by 2.\n\nIf Snake A moves right and Snake B moves right, then the gap increases by 2: because both are moving away from each other.\n\nIf Snake A moves left and Snake B moves left, then the gap increases by 2: both moving away from each other.\n\nIf Snake A moves left and Snake B moves right, then the gap decreases by 0: because Snake A is moving left (away from Snake B) and Snake B is moving right (away from Snake A), so the gap remains the same? Wait, no:\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nSuppose Snake A is at position x, and Snake B is at position y = x + g (mod 45). The gap between them is g arcs.\n\nIf Snake A moves right: it goes to x+1.\n\nIf Snake B moves left: it goes to y - 1 = x + g - 1.\n\nTherefore, the new positions are x+1 and x + g - 1, so the new gap is (x + g - 1) - (x + 1) = g - 2.\n\nSo the gap decreases by 2.\n\nIf Snake A moves right and Snake B moves right: Snake A goes to x+1, Snake B goes to y + 1 = x + g + 1. Then the gap becomes (x + g + 1) - (x + 1) = g. So the gap remains the same.\n\nWait, no: the positions are x+1 and x + g + 1, so the distance between them is (x + g + 1) - (x + 1) = g. So yes, the gap remains the same.\n\nIf Snake A moves left and Snake B moves left: Snake A goes to x - 1, Snake B goes to y - 1 = x + g - 1. Then the new positions are x - 1 and x + g - 1, so the distance between them is (x + g - 1) - (x - 1) = g. So the gap remains the same.\n\nIf Snake A moves left and Snake B moves right: Snake A goes to x - 1, Snake B goes to y + 1 = x + g + 1. Then the new positions are x - 1 and x + g + 1. The distance between them is (x + g + 1) - (x - 1) = g + 2. So the gap increases by 2.\n\nTherefore, summarizing:\n\n- With probability 1/4 (Snake A right, Snake B left): gap decreases by 2.\n\n- With probability 1/4 (Snake A left, Snake B right): gap increases by 2.\n\n- With probability 1/2 (both move in the same direction): gap remains the same.\n\nTherefore, for a gap of size g, the change in the gap is a random variable that can be -2, 0, or +2 with probabilities 1/4, 1/2, and 1/4 respectively.\n\nHowever, note that this is only for a single pair of adjacent snakes. But in reality, each snake is part of two gaps (since it's a circle), so the movement of a snake affects two adjacent gaps. Therefore, the gaps are not independent.\n\nThis complicates things because the gaps are not independent random variables; they are dependent on the movements of the snakes at their boundaries.\n\nGiven the circular symmetry and the initial uniform spacing, perhaps we can model the system as a Markov chain where the state is the multiset of gap sizes between the snakes. Since all gaps start at 3 and are identical, due to symmetry, perhaps we can consider the system in terms of the distribution of gap sizes and their expected times to coalesce.\n\nHowever, even with symmetry, the number of states would be enormous because the gaps can vary in size and interact in complex ways. For 15 gaps, each potentially of different sizes, the state space is too large.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can approximate or find some invariant. For instance, perhaps the expected time until all gaps close can be related to the initial gap sizes and some linear combination?\n\nAlternatively, since all gaps start at the same size, maybe we can consider the problem as a single gap that needs to close, and the other gaps are symmetrically identical, so their contributions can be averaged out?\n\nWait, in some coalescing random walk problems, when all particles are identical and symmetric, you can sometimes reduce the problem to considering a single particle's journey, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlterna