nakes were each occupying their own arcs. So if two snakes were at positions x and y, then the merged snake occupies both arcs x and y. But if they moved to the same position z, then the merged snake would occupy the arc z? Or both arcs leading to z?\n\nWait, this is critical. Let me re-read the problem statement:\n\n\"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nAh, so when two snakes touch, they merge into a snake that occupies the arcs that the two original snakes were occupying. Therefore, if two snakes were on arcs A and B, then the merged snake occupies both arcs A and B. Therefore, their positions before merging determine the merged snake's position.\n\nTherefore, in the movement step, if two snakes move into adjacent positions, they are considered to have \"touched\" during the movement? Or after the movement?\n\nWait, the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge\". So if during their movement, their paths cause them to occupy the same or adjacent arcs, then they merge. But according to the problem's exact wording: \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\".\n\nSo \"touching\" must mean that their current positions (after moving) are adjacent or overlapping? But overlapping would mean they are on the same arc, which would require that they moved to the same position. Since they can't occupy the same position unless they both moved there, which would cause a collision.\n\nWait, in the problem statement, it says \"each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2\". So if two snakes move to the same position, they would be on the same arc, hence touching, and thus merge into a single snake occupying that arc? But the problem says they merge into a snake occupying the arcs of both previous snakes. If they were both moving to the same arc, then the merged snake would occupy that single arc? Or both of their previous arcs?\n\nThis is ambiguous. Wait, let me read again:\n\n\"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"\n\nAh! So the merged snake occupies both arcs that the two original snakes were occupying before the merge. Therefore, the merge is based on their previous positions, not their current positions after moving. Wait, but that can't be, because they have moved.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"touching\" refers to their positions after moving. So if after moving, two snakes are on adjacent arcs or the same arc, then they merge, and the merged snake occupies both arcs that they were on after moving.\n\nBut if they moved to the same arc, then the merged snake would occupy that single arc? Or both arcs? The problem says \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", meaning the arcs they were on before moving?\n\nThis is very ambiguous. Given the problem's wording, I think it's more likely that \"touching\" refers to their positions after moving. So if after moving, two snakes are on the same arc or adjacent arcs, then they merge into a snake that occupies both of their current arcs. Wait, but if they are on the same arc, then merging would just occupy that single arc? The problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—the previous snakes' positions before merging? Or their current positions?\n\nThis is a critical ambiguity. Given that in the initial problem statement, the snakes are of length 1 and occupy every third arc, and when they merge, they form a snake of length 2 (occupying two arcs). Therefore, if two snakes merge, they must have been on adjacent arcs, and the merged snake occupies both of those arcs. Therefore, the merge happens when their current positions are adjacent, and the merged snake occupies both arcs.\n\nTherefore, in the movement step, if two snakes end up on adjacent arcs, they merge into a snake occupying both arcs. If they end up on the same arc, they also merge into a snake occupying that single arc? But the problem says \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so if they were on the same arc, then both previous snakes were on that arc, so the merged snake would occupy that arc. But since they were already on the same arc, they were not moving—they were already merged? Wait, no, they started on separate arcs and moved into the same one.\n\nThis is really confusing. Given the problem's example with merging into a length-2 snake when two length-1 snakes merge, I think the intended interpretation is that two snakes merge when they are adjacent (i.e., their current positions are adjacent), and the merged snake occupies both of those arcs, hence becoming length 2. If they were on the same arc, perhaps that is considered overlapping, and they also merge into a single snake of length 1? But that doesn't make sense because they were already on the same arc. Maybe overlapping is not considered \"touching\", only adjacency is?\n\nGiven the problem statement says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", and since merging two length-1 snakes results in a length-2 snake, it must be that \"touching\" means being on adjacent arcs, not the same arc. Therefore, if two snakes move to adjacent arcs, they merge into a length-2 snake occupying both arcs. If they move to the same arc, they do not merge? But that seems odd because they would be on the same arc. The problem might consider that as overlapping, but the merging only occurs when they are adjacent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe moving to the same arc causes an immediate merge into a single snake of length 1, but that contradicts the initial description that merging two length-1 snakes gives a length-2 snake.\n\nGiven the problem says \"each of length 1\" initially, and when they merge, it's \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", which were each of length 1, so the merged snake is length 2. Therefore, merging must occur when the two snakes are on adjacent arcs, not the same arc. Therefore, moving to the same arc does not cause a merge, but moving to adjacent arcs does.\n\nTherefore, in the smaller example with 3 snakes on 6 arcs:\n\nInitial positions: 0, 2, 4.\n\nEach has a gap of 1 empty arc between them.\n\nIn each time step, each snake moves left or right.\n\nLet's consider the possible movements for the three snakes:\n\nLet me label the snakes as A (0), B (2), C (4).\n\nEach has a 50% chance to move left or right.\n\nWe can enumerate all 2^3 = 8 possible movement combinations.\n\nFor each combination, we can compute the new positions and see if any merges occur.\n\nCase 1: All move left.\n\nA moves to 5 (since moving left from 0 goes to 5 on a 6-position circle).\n\nB moves to 1.\n\nC moves to 3.\n\nNew positions: A:5, B:1, C:3.\n\nNow, check adjacencies:\n\n- A at 5 and B at 1: distance between them is min(|5-1|, 6 - |5-1|) = min(4, 2) = 2 positions apart, so gap of 1 (since distance is 2 positions, so gap = distance - 1 = 1? Wait, confusion again.\n\nWait, positions are 0-5. Between A (5) and B (1): going clockwise, from 5 to 1 is 2 steps (positions 5 to 0 to 1), so distance is 2. The number of empty arcs between them is 1 (position 0). Similarly between B (1) and C (3): distance 2 (positions 1 to 2 to 3), so gap of 1 (position 2). Between C (3) and A (5): clockwise distance from 3 to 5 is 2 (positions 3-4-5), so gap of 1 (position 4). So all gaps remain size 1. No merges occurred.\n\nCase 2: A left, B left, C right.\n\nA:5, B:1, C:5 (since C was at 4 moving right goes to 5).\n\nNow, C moves to 5, same as A. So A and C are both at 5. Are they considered touching? If they are on the same arc, then according to our previous interpretation, they don't merge because merging requires adjacency, not overlapping. But they are on the same arc. The problem says \"if two snakes 