ere's a standard way to interpret it. Let me think of similar problems.\n\nIn some problems involving particles merging when they collide, the merging happens when two particles occupy the same site, and then they become a single particle (or a single entity) on that site. In this case, perhaps when two snakes move into the same arc, they merge into a single snake on that arc (so length 1 but now representing two snakes), but the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so maybe it's a snake that occupies both arcs? That would require that they were on adjacent arcs before moving?\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"touching\" means being adjacent, so whenever two snakes are adjacent (even without moving), they merge. But in that case, the initial configuration already has all snakes separated by two arcs, so no two are adjacent, so they don't merge initially. Then, as they move, they might become adjacent, and then merge.\n\nAssuming that \"touching\" means being adjacent, and merging occurs whenever two snakes are adjacent after moving, then the process is:\n\nAt each time step:\n\n1. All snakes move left or right with probability 1/2.\n\n2. Then, for every pair of adjacent snakes, merge them into a single snake occupying both arcs. This is done for all adjacent pairs simultaneously.\n\n3. The merged snakes (now of length 2) will, in the next time step, move as a single snake, choosing left or right with probability 1/2 each.\n\nThis seems plausible. So in this case, whenever two snakes end up on adjacent arcs after moving, they merge into a length-2 snake, which then in the next step can move as a single entity.\n\nUnder this interpretation, the merging only occurs for adjacent pairs after movement, and it's done all at once, so that merged snakes don't affect other merges in the same time step.\n\nGiven that, let's see if we can model the expected time until only one snake remains.\n\nFirst, note that the circle has 45 arcs, and we start with 15