es left, B moves right.\n\nNew positions: x-1 and (x + k) + 1.\n\nThe clockwise distance is [ (x + k + 1) - (x - 1) ] mod 45 = (k + 2) mod 45.\n\nCase 3: A moves right, B moves left.\n\nNew positions: x+1 and (x + k) - 1.\n\nThe clockwise distance is [ (x + k - 1) - (x + 1) ] mod 45 = (k - 2) mod 45.\n\nCase 4: A moves right, B moves right.\n\nNew positions: x+1 and (x + k) + 1.\n\nThe clockwise distance is [ (x + k + 1) - (x + 1) ] mod 45 = k mod 45.\n\nTherefore, depending on their movement, the distance between them can increase by 2, decrease by 2, or stay the same.\n\nThe probability of each case:\n\nEach snake moves independently, so:\n\n- Probability both move left: 1/2 * 1/2 = 1/4.\n\n- Probability A left, B right: 1/4.\n\n- Probability A right, B left: 1/4.\n\n- Probability both move right: 1/4.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- With probability 1/4, the distance remains k.\n\n- With probability 1/4, the distance increases by 2 (mod 45).\n\n- With probability 1/4, the distance decreases by 2 (mod 45).\n\nWait, but mod 45 complicates things because distances are between 1 and 22 (since beyond 22, the distance the other way is shorter). However, since we're dealing with expected time, perhaps we can consider the distance as a number from 1 to 22, and when it decreases below 1, it wraps around to 45 - (distance - 2), but this might complicate the analysis.\n\nAlternatively, since the circle is symmetric, perhaps we can consider the distance as a value between 1 and 22, and when the distance becomes 1, the snakes merge.\n\nWait, actually, when the distance between two snakes becomes 1, they are adjacent, so they merge. Therefore, the process ends for that pair when the distance reaches 1.\n\nHowever, in our two-snake example, if they start with distance k > 1, then each time step, the distance can decrease by 2 with probability 1/4, increase by 2 with probability 1/4, or stay the same with probability 1/2.\n\nWe can model this as a Markov chain where the state is the current 