ust a single snake on that arc, so its length is 1? But the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so if two snakes were on arcs A and B, and then move to arc C, then the merged snake is on arc C, but that's only one arc, so how does that occupy both arcs A and B? That seems inconsistent.\n\nHmm, maybe I need to look for similar problems or think of the problem in a different way. Alternatively, perhaps the problem is using \"touch\" to mean that their paths cross? But since they move simultaneously, maybe crossing paths doesn't count unless they end up on the same spot.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the key is that when two snakes are on adjacent arcs, they are considered to be \"touching\", and whenever any two snakes are touching (i.e., adjacent), they must merge immediately? But that would mean that as soon as any two snakes are adjacent, they merge, which might happen before the next time step? Hmm, but the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge\"—so perhaps whenever two snakes are on adjacent arcs at any time, they merge immediately, but the merging happens during the movement phase? This is really ambiguous.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"Every second, each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2. If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nSo the merging occurs as a result of the movement. So during the movement phase, if two snakes end up on the same arc or adjacent arcs? Or specifically the same arc? The problem says \"if two snakes ever touch\"—so perhaps \"touch\" is defined as ending up on the same arc after moving. Then merging happens when two or more snakes occupy the same arc, and then they merge into a single snake on that arc, so the length remains 1? But the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so if two snakes were on arcs 