wo-dimensional cave space, and these four points form the vertices of a square. Since the cave is two-dimensional, we can represent it as a coordinate system where the ceiling is along the line y = h (the height of the cave), and the floor is along the line y = 0. Then, each stalactite is a vertical segment from some point (x1, h) down to (x1, h - length_of_stalactite). Similarly, each stalagmite is a vertical segment from some point (x2, 0) up to (x2, height_of_stalagmite). Therefore, the tip of a stalactite will be at (x, h - L), where L is its length, and the tip of a stalagmite will be at (x, H), where H is its height.\n\nSince the four tips form a square, the four points must satisfy the properties of a square: all sides equal, all angles 90 degrees, and the diagonals are equal and bisect each other at 90 degrees. Since it's a square in a coordinate plane, perhaps aligned with the axes or rotated? Hmm, the problem doesn't specify that the square is axis-aligned, so it could be rotated. But given that all the structures are vertical, maybe the square is axis-aligned? Not sure yet. Let me think.\n\nFirst, let me assign coordinates. Let me suppose that the ceiling is along the line y = h, and the floor is along y = 0. Let me denote the two stalactites as Stalactite A with length 16 and Stalactite B with length 36. Similarly, Stalagmite X with height 25 and Stalagmite Y with height 49. The tips of the stalactites will be at (x_A, h - 16) and (x_B, h - 36). The tips of the stalagmites will be at (x_X, 25) and (x_Y, 49). Wait, hold on: if a stalagmite has a height of 25, then its tip is 25 units above the floor (y=0), so at y = 25, and similarly, the one with height 49 is at y = 49. But the ceiling is at y = h, so the tops of the stalagmites can't be above the ceiling? Wait, unless the height of the cave is at least 49? Because the tallest stalagmite is 49, so its tip is at y = 49, so h must be at least 49? Similarly, the lowest point of the stalactites are at y = h - 16 and y = h - 36, so since the floor is at y = 0, those points must be above the floor, so h - 16 ≥ 0 and h - 36 ≥ 0. Therefore, h must be at least 36. But since the ceiling must be above the tallest stalagmite's tip, h must be at least 49. So h ≥ 49. So the height of the cave is at least 49. But maybe exactly 49? Hmm, but maybe not necessarily. Wait, but actually, the problem says \"the tips of these four structures form the vertices of a square in some order.\" So, the four tips must lie in the plane of the cave, which is two-dimensional, so their coordinates must form a square. So if the tallest structure is a stalagmite with height 49, its tip is at (x, 49), but since the ceiling is at height h, then that point must be below or at the ceiling, so h must be at least 49. Similarly, the lowest point of the stalactites are at y = h - 36 and y = h - 16, so those have to be above the floor, so h must be at least 36. But since 49 is larger, h must be at least 49.\n\nHowever, the problem is asking for the height of the cave, so we need to figure out exactly what h is such that the four tips form a square. The square's vertices are the four points: the two stalactite tips and the two stalagmite tips. Let me note that the four tips are two points from the stalactites and two points from the stalagmites. The two stalactites have tips at (x_A, h - 16) and (x_B, h - 36). The two stalagmites have tips at (x_X, 25) and (x_Y, 49). Now, these four points must form a square. Since a square has four sides of equal length and four right angles, the positions of these four points must satisfy those geometric conditions.\n\nFirst, let me consider that the square can be in any orientation, not necessarily aligned with the coordinate axes. Therefore, we might need to use coordinate geometry to set up equations based on the distances between the points and the angles between them.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can assume that the square is axis-aligned? Let me see if that is possible. If the square is axis-aligned, then all the x-coordinates of the square's vertices would either be the same or form pairs of equal x-values, and the y-coordinates would also form pairs of equal y-values. However, since we have four different y-coordinates here: 25, 49, h - 16, and h - 36. So unless two of these y-values are equal, which they are not, since 25 and 49 are different, and h - 16 and h - 36 are also different (since 16 ≠ 36), then we have four distinct y-values, so the square cannot be axis-aligned. Because an axis-aligned square must have two distinct y-values for the horizontal sides and two distinct x-values for the vertical sides. Since here we have four different y-values, that would not form an axis-aligned square. Therefore, the square must be rotated relative to the coordinate system.\n\nTherefore, the square is a rotated square in the coordinate system. Therefore, we need to use the properties of squares in coordinate geometry where the sides are equal in length and the diagonals are equal and intersect at 90 degrees, but more systematically, perhaps we can assign coordinates to the four points and then set up equations based on the square conditions.\n\nLet me denote the four points as follows:\n\nLet me call the two stalactite tips as points P and Q:\n\n- Let P = (x1, h - 16)\n- Let Q = (x2, h - 36)\n\nAnd the two stalagmite tips as points R and S:\n\n- Let R = (x3, 25)\n- Let S = (x4, 49)\n\nThese four points P, Q, R, S form a square. Now, since it's a square, all sides must be equal, and the adjacent sides must be perpendicular. Also, the diagonals must be equal and of length side_length * sqrt(2). Additionally, the diagonals bisect each other at 90 degrees? Wait, actually in a square, the diagonals are equal in length and bisect each other at 90 degrees? Wait, actually, in a square, the diagonals bisect each other and are equal in length, and they bisect the angles, but they are not perpendicular unless it's a square? Wait, in a square, the diagonals are perpendicular. Wait, actually, yes, in a square, the diagonals are perpendicular and of equal length. Wait, actually, in a square, the diagonals are equal in length and bisect each other at 90 degrees. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to use vectors and coordinate differences. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can suppose that the four points form a square in some order, so we can assign labels such that P, Q, R, S are the four vertices in order around the square, but we don't know the order. So the problem says \"in some order\", so we don't know which point is connected to which. Therefore, we need to consider all possibilities of assigning the four points to the four vertices of a square, but that might be too many cases. Alternatively, perhaps we can use the general properties of squares and set up equations based on the distances between the points.\n\nLet me recall that in a square with vertices A, B, C, D, the following must hold:\n\n- The distance between A and B must equal the distance between B and C, and so on for all sides.\n- The distance between A and C must equal the distance between B and D (the diagonals).\n- The vectors AB and AD must be perpendicular and of equal length.\n\nAlternatively, using coordinates, suppose we have four points: let's say the four points are (a, b), (c, d), (e, f), (g, h). Then, for these to form a square, the squared distances between each pair must include six distances: four equal to the side length squared, two equal to the diagonal length squared, and the other distances (the ones between non-adjacent vertices that aren't diagonally opposite) would not be equal? Wait, actually in a square, each vertex is connected to two others by edges (sides) and one by a diagonal. So between four points, there are six distances: four side lengths and two diagonals. The two diagonals are equal, and the four sides are equal, and the diagonals are longer than the sides by a factor of sqrt(2). So, for four points to form a square, the six distances must consist of four equal shorter distances (the sides) and two longer equal distances (the diagonals), with the relationship that the longer distance squared equals 2 times the shorter distance squared.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can compute all pairwise distances between the four points and set up equations accordingly.\n\nBut since we don't know which points are adjacent or opposite in the square, this might be a bit involved, but maybe manageable.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that in a square, the coordinates of the four vertices can be expressed in terms of a center point and rotation. Let me recall that any square can be represented as a set of points obtained by rotating a point around the center by 90 degrees.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to set up variables for the coordinates of the four points and then write equations based on the square properties. Let me try that.\n\nLet me denote the four points as follows:\n\nLet me assign variables:\n\nLet me call the two stalactite tips as points A and B:\n\n- Point A: (a, h - 16)\n- Point B: (b, h - 36)\n\nAnd the two stalagmite tips as points C and D:\n\n- Point C: (c, 25)\n- Point D: (d, 49)\n\nThese four points A, B, C, D must form a square. Now, since we don't know the order of the square's vertices, we need to consider that any of the four points could be adjacent to any other, but perhaps we can fix an order and see if it works, or set up equations that are independent of the order.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to suppose that the square has sides of length s, and then write equations based on the coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can note that in a square, the coordinates of the four vertices can be written as (p, q), (p + t, q + u), (p + t - u, q + t + u), (p - u, q + t), or something like that, but that might not be straightforward. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to note that in the square, the midpoints of the diagonals must coincide. That is, the midpoint between two opposite vertices is the same as the midpoint between the other two opposite vertices. So, if we suppose that points A and C are opposite vertices, then the midpoint of AC must equal the midpoint of BD. Similarly, if A and B are adjacent, then the midpoints would still have to satisfy that the overall center is the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to consider the centroid of the square. The centroid (the intersection point of the diagonals) is the average of the coordinates of the four vertices. So, the centroid (Gx, Gy) is given by:\n\nGx = (a + b + c + d)/4\n\nGy = [(h - 16) + (h - 36) + 25 + 49]/4\n\nSimplify Gy:\n\nGy = [h - 16 + h - 36 + 25 + 49]/4\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nGy = [2h + (-16 - 36 + 25 + 49)] / 4\n\nCompute the constants:\n\n-16 -36 = -52\n\n25 + 49 = 74\n\nSo -52 + 74 = 22\n\nTherefore, Gy = (2h + 22)/4 = (h + 11)/2\n\nSo the y-coordinate of the centroid is (h + 11)/2.\n\nSimilarly, the x-coordinate of the centroid is (a + b + c + d)/4.\n\nNow, in a square, the centroid is also the intersection point of the diagonals, so it must lie at the midpoint between any pair of opposite vertices.\n\nTherefore, for instance, if we suppose that points A and C are opposite vertices, then the midpoint between A and C must be ( (a + c)/2, [(h - 16) + 25]/2 ) which must equal the centroid ( (a + b + c + d)/4, (h + 11)/2 )\n\nTherefore, setting the y-coordinates equal:\n\n[(h - 16) + 25]/2 = (h + 11)/2\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n(h - 16 + 25)/2 = (h + 9)/2\n\nSet equal to right side:\n\n(h + 9)/2 = (h + 11)/2\n\nSubtract (h + 9)/2 from both sides:\n\n0 = (h + 11)/2 - (h + 9)/2 = [ (h + 11) - (h + 9) ] / 2 = 2/2 = 1\n\nSo 0 = 1? That's a contradiction. Hmm, so that can't be. Therefore, our assumption that points A and C are opposite vertices must be wrong.\n\nAlternatively, maybe points A and D are opposite? Let me try that.\n\nSuppose points A and D are opposite vertices. Then the midpoint of A and D is:\n\nMidpoint AD: ( (a + d)/2, [ (h - 16) + 49 ] / 2 ) = ( (a + d)/2, (h + 33)/2 )\n\nThis must equal the centroid ( (a + b + c + d)/4, (h + 11)/2 )\n\nTherefore, equate the y-coordinates:\n\n(h + 33)/2 = (h + 11)/2\n\nAgain, subtract (h + 11)/2 from both sides:\n\n( (h + 33) - (h + 11) ) / 2 = 0 => 22/2 = 11 = 0? No, 11 ≠ 0. Contradiction again.\n\nHmm. Let's try points B and C as opposite vertices.\n\nMidpoint BC: ( (b + c)/2, [ (h - 36) + 25 ] / 2 ) = ( (b + c)/2, (h - 11)/2 )\n\nSet equal to centroid ( (a + b + c + d)/4, (h + 11)/2 )\n\nEquate y-coordinates:\n\n(h - 11)/2 = (h + 11)/2\n\nSubtract (h - 11)/2 from both sides:\n\n0 = [ (h + 11) - (h - 11) ] / 2 = (22)/2 = 11 ≠ 0. Again, contradiction.\n\nHmm, how about points B and D?\n\nMidpoint BD: ( (b + d)/2, [ (h - 36) + 49 ] / 2 ) = ( (b + d)/2, (h + 13)/2 )\n\nSet equal to centroid ( (a + b + c + d)/4, (h + 11)/2 )\n\nEquate y-coordinates:\n\n(h + 13)/2 = (h + 11)/2\n\nSubtract (h + 11)/2 from both sides:\n\n2/2 = 1 = 0? No, again a contradiction.\n\nHmm, so all possibilities for pairing A with C, A with D, B with C, B with D as opposite vertices lead to contradictions in the y-coordinate of the centroid? That can't be. Wait, perhaps I made a wrong assumption here.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that I assumed specific pairings of the points as opposite vertices, but maybe the square's vertices are arranged such that none of these pairings are actually opposite? Wait, no, in a square, any two vertices are either adjacent or opposite. So every pair of vertices must be either adjacent or opposite. Therefore, the midpoints of the diagonals must be the same point, the centroid. Therefore, regardless of how we pair the vertices into opposite pairs, the midpoints must all equal the centroid. Since we tried all four possible pairings (since there are four points, there are three ways to partition into two pairs of opposites, but actually four points have three possible pairings of opposite vertices: AB-CD, AC-BD, AD-BC). Wait, actually, in a square with four vertices, there are two diagonals, so the three pairings of opposite vertices are the two diagonals. Wait, actually, in a square, there are exactly two diagonals, so the three possible pairings of opposite vertices are the two diagonals and... no, actually, in a square, the opposite vertices are exactly the two endpoints of each diagonal. Since a square has two diagonals, each diagonal connects a pair of opposite vertices, so there are two pairs of opposite vertices. Therefore, when considering midpoints of diagonals, we have two midpoints, which must coincide. Therefore, if I pick any two diagonals, their midpoints must be the same.\n\nWait, in our case, the four points must form a square, so regardless of how we label them, there must be two diagonals whose midpoints coincide at the centroid. Therefore, my mistake earlier was trying to pair specific points as opposite vertices (A-C, A-D, etc.) but perhaps those pairings are not actually diagonals of the square. Therefore, perhaps instead of assuming specific pairings, I need to consider that for the four points, there exists some pairing of them into two pairs of opposite vertices such that the midpoints of those pairs are equal (i.e., the centroid). Therefore, perhaps the equations I set up earlier can still be used, but I need to not assume which points are paired as opposites, but instead set up equations that for some pairing, the midpoints are equal.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider all possible pairings and see which one gives a consistent equation.\n\nAlternatively, let's denote the four points as follows:\n\nLet me call the four points as follows: let’s say the four points are P1, P2, P3, P4, which are the four tips. Then, in some order, these four points form a square. Therefore, there must exist a permutation of these four points such that when connected in that order, they form a square. Therefore, perhaps we can assign variables to the coordinates and set up equations accordingly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that in a square, the vectors between the points must satisfy certain properties. For instance, if we consider three consecutive vertices, say A, B, C, then the vector from A to B rotated by 90 degrees should equal the vector from B to C.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to assign coordinates in a way that might simplify the problem.\n\nLet me suppose that the center of the square is at (Gx, Gy), which we already found to be ( (a + b + c + d)/4, (h + 11)/2 ). Since the centroid's y-coordinate is (h + 11)/2, and since the square's vertices have y-coordinates of 25, 49, h - 16, and h - 36, perhaps we can find relationships between these y-coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, note that in a square, the y-coordinates of the four vertices must be symmetric around the center's y-coordinate Gy. Similarly for the x-coordinates.\n\nSo, for the y-coordinates: the four y-values are 25, 49, h - 16, h - 36.\n\nThese four y-values must be symmetric around Gy = (h + 11)/2.\n\nTherefore, for each y-value y_i, there must be another y-value y_j such that (y_i + y_j)/2 = Gy.\n\nTherefore, pairing the y-values:\n\nTake 25 and 49: their average is (25 + 49)/2 = 74/2 = 37.\n\nTake h - 16 and h - 36: their average is ( (h - 16) + (h - 36) ) / 2 = (2h - 52)/2 = h - 26.\n\nThese two averages must both equal Gy, which is (h + 11)/2.\n\nTherefore, setting the two averages equal to Gy:\n\nFirst, 37 = (h + 11)/2,\n\nand\n\nSecond, h - 26 = (h + 11)/2.\n\nWait, so both of these equations must hold?\n\nWait, but that would require both:\n\nFrom the first pairing (25 and 49):\n\n37 = (h + 11)/2 ⇒ h + 11 = 74 ⇒ h = 63.\n\nFrom the second pairing (h - 16 and h - 36):\n\nh - 26 = (h + 11)/2 ⇒ Multiply both sides by 2:\n\n2h - 52 = h + 11 ⇒ 2h - h = 11 + 52 ⇒ h = 63.\n\nSo both equations give h = 63. Perfect! So that works out. Therefore, the height h must be 63.\n\nWait, so that seems to work? Let me check.\n\nIf h = 63, then:\n\nThe y-coordinates of the four points are:\n\nFor the stalactites:\n\n- First stalactite tip: y = h - 16 = 63 - 16 = 47\n\n- Second stalactite tip: y = h - 36 = 63 - 36 = 27\n\nFor the stalagmites:\n\n- First stalagmite tip: y = 25\n\n- Second stalagmite tip: y = 49\n\nTherefore, the four y-values are 47, 27, 25, and 49.\n\nNow, let's check their symmetry around the centroid's y-coordinate Gy = (h + 11)/2 = (63 + 11)/2 = 74/2 = 37.\n\nSo Gy is 37.\n\nNow, let's pair the y-values as (25 and 49) and (27 and 47). Let's check their averages:\n\n(25 + 49)/2 = 74/2 = 37,\n\nand (27 + 47)/2 = 74/2 = 37.\n\nPerfect! So that works. Therefore, the y-coordinates are symmetric around 37, which is the centroid's y-coordinate. So that's a good sign.\n\nNow, we need to check if the x-coordinates can also be symmetric around the centroid's x-coordinate Gx = (a + b + c + d)/4. But we don't know the x-coordinates yet. However, perhaps we can figure out the x-coordinates based on the square's geometry.\n\nSince we know the y-coordinates are symmetric, the x-coordinates must also be symmetric in some way. Let me think.\n\nIn a square, not only are the y-coordinates symmetric about the center's y-coordinate, but the x-coordinates must also be symmetric about the center's x-coordinate. That is, for each point (x, y), there must be another point (2Gx - x, 2Gy - y). So the reflection over the center point.\n\nTherefore, if we suppose that the four points form a square with centroid at (Gx, 37), then for each point (x, y), there must be another point (2Gx - x, 74 - y) because 2Gy - y = 2*37 - y = 74 - y.\n\nTherefore, let me see the y-values:\n\nTake the point with y = 25. Its paired y-coordinate should be 74 - 25 = 49, which is the y-coordinate of the other stalagmite tip. So that pairs the two stalagmite tips: (x3, 25) and (x4, 49). So those two must be symmetric across the center's y-coordinate.\n\nSimilarly, the point with y = 49 pairs with y = 25, and the points with y = 47 and y = 27 must pair with each other: 74 - 47 = 27 and 74 - 27 = 47. Therefore, the two stalactite tips (which are at y = 47 and y = 27) must be symmetric across the center's y-coordinate.\n\nTherefore, their x-coordinates must satisfy that if one is (a, 47), the other must be (2Gx - a, 27). Similarly, the two stalagmite tips (x3, 25) and (x4, 49) must satisfy that (x4, 49) is the reflection of (x3, 25) across the center, so x4 = 2Gx - x3, and 49 = 74 - 25, which checks out.\n\nTherefore, if we let’s denote the points as follows:\n\nLet me label the points as:\n\nPoint 1: (a, 47) [stalactite tip]\n\nPoint 2: (b, 27) [the other stalactite tip]\n\nPoint 3: (c, 25) [stalagmite tip]\n\nPoint 4: (d, 49) [the other stalagmite tip]\n\nNow, according to the reflection symmetry over the center (Gx, 37):\n\nPoint 1 (a, 47) must be paired with Point 2 (b, 27), such that:\n\nb = 2Gx - a,\n\nand\n\nSimilarly, Point 3 (c, 25) must be paired with Point 4 (d, 49), such that:\n\nd = 2Gx - c,\n\nand\n\nAlso, the center's x-coordinate Gx is given by:\n\nGx = (a + b + c + d)/4\n\nBut since b = 2Gx - a and d = 2Gx - c, we can substitute these into the expression for Gx:\n\nGx = (a + (2Gx - a) + c + (2Gx - c)) / 4\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\na + 2Gx - a + c + 2Gx - c = (a - a) + (c - c) + 2Gx + 2Gx = 4Gx\n\nTherefore, Gx = (4Gx)/4 = Gx, which is an identity. So that doesn't give us new information.\n\nTherefore, we need another equation to find Gx and the x-coordinates.\n\nNow, in a square, not only are the coordinates symmetric about the center, but the distances between the points must satisfy the square's properties. Let me consider the vectors between the points.\n\nLet me suppose that the four points are arranged in order around the square. Let me try to assign an order such that the points are connected in sequence. Since the square can be rotated, let me pick an arbitrary starting point and see if I can derive relationships.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider the vectors between the points. In a square, adjacent vertices are separated by a vector that is a 90-degree rotation of the previous side vector.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the distance between Point 1 (a, 47) and Point 3 (c, 25). Let me compute the squared distance between these two points:\n\nDistance squared between Point 1 and Point 3:\n\n(a - c)^2 + (47 - 25)^2 = (a - c)^2 + (22)^2\n\nSimilarly, the distance between Point 1 and Point 4 (d, 49):\n\n(a - d)^2 + (47 - 49)^2 = (a - d)^2 + (-2)^2 = (a - d)^2 + 4\n\nThe distance between Point 1 and Point 2 (b, 27):\n\n(a - b)^2 + (47 - 27)^2 = (a - b)^2 + (20)^2\n\nSimilarly, distance between Point 2 and Point 4:\n\n(b - d)^2 + (27 - 49)^2 = (b - d)^2 + (-22)^2 = (b - d)^2 + 484\n\nDistance between Point 2 and Point 3:\n\n(b - c)^2 + (27 - 25)^2 = (b - c)^2 + 4\n\nDistance between Point 3 and Point 4:\n\n(c - d)^2 + (25 - 49)^2 = (c - d)^2 + (-24)^2 = (c - d)^2 + 576\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated with so many variables. Maybe we can use the fact that in a square, all sides are equal and the diagonals are equal.\n\nSuppose that the side length is s, so the squared side length is s², and the squared diagonal length is 2s².\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that the distance between Point 1 and Point 3 should be a diagonal if they are opposite vertices, but we don't know which points are adjacent or opposite.\n\nAlternatively, since we know the y-coordinates, perhaps we can figure out possible pairings.\n\nWait, let me recall that the four y-coordinates are 25, 27, 47, 49. So the vertical distances between some of these points can give us some clues.\n\nFor instance, between the lowest point (25) and the next lowest (27), that's a vertical distance of 2 units. Between 27 and 47 is 20 units, and between 47 and 49 is 2 units. Similarly, between 25 and 49 is 24 units.\n\nIn a square, the vertical and horizontal distances must correspond to the side lengths and diagonals appropriately. However, since the square is rotated, the vertical and horizontal distances don't directly correspond to the sides, but the actual Euclidean distances do.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the vertical distance between two points can be related to the side length and the angle of rotation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can look for two points that are separated vertically by 2 units, and see if that could be a side or a diagonal.\n\nWait, the vertical distance between (c,25) and (d,49) is 24, which is quite large. The vertical distance between (a,47) and (b,27) is 20. The vertical distance between (a,47) and (c,25) is 22, and between (b,27) and (d,49) is 22. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to consider specific pairings. Let's suppose that the points are arranged such that the two stalactite tips are adjacent to each other and to the two stalagmite tips. Let me try to imagine the square.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider that in the square, the four points must have all their mutual distances either s (side) or s√2 (diagonal). So, let me compute all six pairwise distances squared between the four points (in terms of a, b, c, d, Gx) and set up equations that four of them are equal to s² and two of them equal to 2s².\n\nBut this might be too many variables. Alternatively, maybe we can assume some relations based on symmetry.\n\nGiven that the square is symmetric about its center (Gx, 37), then for any point (x, y), there is a point (2Gx - x, 74 - y). Therefore, the square is centrally symmetric with respect to (Gx, 37).\n\nTherefore, if we pick one point, say Point 1: (a, 47), then its opposite vertex (diagonal from it) must be Point 2: (b, 27), because 74 - 47 = 27. Wait, actually, the opposite vertex would be the one that is reflected over the center. Since the center is at (Gx, 37), the reflection of Point 1 (a, 47) over the center would be (2Gx - a, 2*37 - 47) = (2Gx - a, 74 - 47) = (2Gx - a, 27). Therefore, the opposite vertex to Point 1 is (2Gx - a, 27), which must be one of the other points: either Point 2 (b, 27) or Point 4 (d, 49). But since the y-coordinate is 27, it must be Point 2 (b, 27). Therefore, we can conclude that b = 2Gx - a.\n\nSimilarly, the reflection of Point 3 (c, 25) over the center is (2Gx - c, 74 - 25) = (2Gx - c, 49), which must be Point 4 (d, 49). Therefore, d = 2Gx - c.\n\nSo we have:\n\nb = 2Gx - a,\n\nd = 2Gx - c,\n\nand recall that Gx = (a + b + c + d)/4.\n\nSubstituting b and d in terms of a and c into the expression for Gx:\n\nGx = [a + (2Gx - a) + c + (2Gx - c)] / 4\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\na + 2Gx - a + c + 2Gx - c = (a - a) + (c - c) + 4Gx = 4Gx\n\nTherefore, Gx = (4Gx)/4 = Gx, which is just an identity, so no new information. So we still need more equations.\n\nPerhaps we can compute the distance between Point 1 and Point 3, and set it equal to the distance between Point 1 and Point 2, assuming they are adjacent vertices.\n\nWait, but we don't know the order of the vertices. Alternatively, let's suppose that the four points form a square with sides of length s. Let me pick two points and compute the distance between them, then see if that can be related to other distances.\n\nLet me compute the distance between Point 1 (a, 47) and Point 3 (c, 25):\n\nDistance squared: (a - c)^2 + (47 - 25)^2 = (a - c)^2 + 484.\n\nDistance between Point 1 and Point 2 (b, 27):\n\nDistance squared: (a - b)^2 + (47 - 27)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 400.\n\nDistance between Point 1 and Point 4 (d, 49):\n\nDistance squared: (a - d)^2 + (47 - 49)^2 = (a - d)^2 + 4.\n\nDistance between Point 2 (b, 27) and Point 3 (c, 25):\n\nDistance squared: (b - c)^2 + (27 - 25)^2 = (b - c)^2 + 4.\n\nDistance between Point 2 and Point 4 (d, 49):\n\nDistance squared: (b - d)^2 + (27 - 49)^2 = (b - d)^2 + 484.\n\nDistance between Point 3 (c, 25) and Point 4 (d, 49):\n\nDistance squared: (c - d)^2 + (25 - 49)^2 = (c - d)^2 + 576.\n\nNow, in a square, there are four sides of equal length and two diagonals of equal length (longer). So among these six distances squared, four should be equal to s² (the side squared) and two should be equal to 2s² (the diagonal squared).\n\nSo let's see if we can find such s² and assignments.\n\nLooking at the distances squared:\n\n1. (a - c)^2 + 484\n\n2. (a - b)^2 + 400\n\n3. (a - d)^2 + 4\n\n4. (b - c)^2 + 4\n\n5. (b - d)^2 + 484\n\n6. (c - d)^2 + 576\n\nHmm, these expressions look complex, but maybe some of them can be related. Let me see if any of these expressions can be equal to each other or to twice another.\n\nLooking at entries 3 and 4: both have a +4 term. Specifically, entry 3: (a - d)^2 + 4 and entry 4: (b - c)^2 + 4. If these are sides, then they should be equal to s², so perhaps (a - d)^2 = (b - c)^2, so |a - d| = |b - c|.\n\nAlternatively, maybe entries 3 and 4 are the same distance squared. Let me see if that could be.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps entry 3 and entry 4 are both sides, so equal to s², so:\n\n(a - d)^2 + 4 = (b - c)^2 + 4 ⇒ (a - d)^2 = (b - c)^2 ⇒ a - d = ±(b - c)\n\nSimilarly, perhaps.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if any of the other entries can be related.\n\nLooking at entries 1 and 5:\n\nEntry 1: (a - c)^2 + 484\n\nEntry 5: (b - d)^2 + 484\n\nIf these are diagonals, then they should both equal 2s², so:\n\n(a - c)^2 + 484 = 2s²,\n\nand\n\n(b - d)^2 + 484 = 2s²,\n\nso then (a - c)^2 = (b - d)^2 ⇒ a - c = ±(b - d)\n\nSimilarly, entries 2 and 6:\n\nEntry 2: (a - b)^2 + 400\n\nEntry 6: (c - d)^2 + 576\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps entries 1 and 6 are diagonals? Let me see:\n\nIf entry 1 and 6 are diagonals, then:\n\n(a - c)^2 + 484 = (c - d)^2 + 576,\n\nand similarly for others, but this might not hold.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to make an assumption to reduce variables.\n\nSince we have relationships between a, b, c, d via b = 2Gx - a and d = 2Gx - c, perhaps we can express everything in terms of a and c, and Gx.\n\nLet me set variables in terms of a and c.\n\nLet me let’s denote:\n\nLet’s let’s set variable substitutions:\n\nLet’s let’s set:\n\nLet’s denote:\n\nLet’s let’s define:\n\nLet’s let’s set b = 2Gx - a,\n\nand d = 2Gx - c.\n\nTherefore, we can write all variables in terms of a, c, and Gx.\n\nAlso, note that Gx = (a + b + c + d)/4,\n\nbut substituting b and d,\n\nGx = [a + (2Gx - a) + c + (2Gx - c)] /4,\n\nwhich simplifies to Gx = (4Gx)/4 = Gx, so no help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can express Gx in terms of a and c?\n\nWait, since b = 2Gx - a,\n\nthen a + b = a + (2Gx - a) = 2Gx,\n\nSimilarly, c + d = c + (2Gx - c) = 2Gx,\n\nTherefore, a + b = c + d = 2Gx,\n\nTherefore, the sum a + b + c + d = 4Gx,\n\nwhich we already knew.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can write expressions for the distances in terms of a, c, and Gx.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nFirst, note that b = 2Gx - a,\n\nand d = 2Gx - c.\n\nTherefore, let's rewrite all distances squared in terms of a, c, and Gx.\n\nStarting with distance squared between Point 1 (a,47) and Point 3 (c,25):\n\nDistance1: (a - c)^2 + (47 - 25)^2 = (a - c)^2 + 484.\n\nDistance between Point 1 and Point 2 (b,27):\n\nPoint 2 is (b,27) = (2Gx - a, 27),\n\nso distance squared is:\n\n(a - (2Gx - a))² + (47 - 27)^2\n\n= (2a - 2Gx)^2 + 400\n\n= 4(a - Gx)^2 + 400.\n\nSimilarly, distance between Point 1 and Point 4 (d,49):\n\nPoint 4 is (d,49) = (2Gx - c, 49),\n\nso distance squared is:\n\n(a - (2Gx - c))² + (47 - 49)^2\n\n= (a - 2Gx + c)^2 + 4\n\n= ( (a + c) - 2Gx )² + 4.\n\nDistance between Point 2 (2Gx - a, 27) and Point 3 (c,25):\n\nDistance squared is:\n\n( (2Gx - a) - c )² + (27 - 25)^2\n\n= (2Gx - a - c)^2 + 4.\n\nDistance between Point 2 and Point 4 (2Gx - c, 49):\n\nDistance squared is:\n\n( (2Gx - a) - (2Gx - c) )² + (27 - 49)^2\n\n= ( -a + c )² + (-22)^2\n\n= (c - a)^2 + 484.\n\nDistance between Point 3 (c,25) and Point 4 (2Gx - c,49):\n\nDistance squared is:\n\n(c - (2Gx - c))² + (25 - 49)^2\n\n= (2c - 2Gx)^2 + (-24)^2\n\n= 4(c - Gx)^2 + 576.\n\nSo now, let me list all the distances squared in terms of a, c, Gx:\n\n1. Distance1 (Point1-Point3): (a - c)^2 + 484\n\n2. Distance2 (Point1-Point2): 4(a - Gx)^2 + 400\n\n3. Distance3 (Point1-Point4): [ (a + c - 2Gx) ]^2 + 4\n\n4. Distance4 (Point2-Point3): (2Gx - a - c)^2 + 4\n\n5. Distance5 (Point2-Point4): (c - a)^2 + 484\n\n6. Distance6 (Point3-Point4): 4(c - Gx)^2 + 576\n\nHmm, okay. Let's see if we can find relationships here.\n\nFirst, notice that Distance1 and Distance5 are both (a - c)^2 + 484 and (c - a)^2 + 484, which are equal because (a - c)^2 = (c - a)^2. Therefore, Distance1 = Distance5.\n\nSimilarly, Distance3 and Distance4 both have terms involving (a + c - 2Gx)^2 and (2Gx - a - c)^2, which are the same because (a + c - 2Gx)^2 = ( - (2Gx - a - c) )^2 = (2Gx - a - c)^2. Therefore, Distance3 and Distance4 are equal.\n\nSo, we can group them:\n\nGroup1: Distances 1 and 5: both equal to (a - c)^2 + 484\n\nGroup2: Distances 3 and 4: both equal to (a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 4\n\nGroup3: Distance2: 4(a - Gx)^2 + 400\n\nGroup4: Distance6: 4(c - Gx)^2 + 576\n\nNow, in a square, there are four sides of equal length and two diagonals of equal length. Therefore, among these six distances squared, four should be equal (the sides) and two should be equal and larger (the diagonals). Alternatively, depending on how the distances are assigned.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two largest distances are the diagonals and the next two are the sides? Not sure. Let's see the numerical values.\n\nLooking at the expressions:\n\nGroup1: (a - c)^2 + 484\n\nGroup2: (a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 4\n\nGroup3: 4(a - Gx)^2 + 400\n\nGroup4: 4(c - Gx)^2 + 576\n\nHmm. Let me see if we can set some of these groups equal to each other.\n\nSuppose that the sides are from Group2 and Group3 and Group4? Not sure. Alternatively, perhaps the sides are the smaller distances and the diagonals are the larger ones.\n\nLooking at the constants added to the squared terms:\n\nGroup1 has +484,\n\nGroup2 has +4,\n\nGroup3 has +400,\n\nGroup4 has +576.\n\nSo Group2 has the smallest constant (4), Group3 has 400, Group1 has 484, and Group4 has 576. Therefore, perhaps the smallest distances squared are in Group2 (the +4), then Group3 (+400), then Group1 (+484), then Group4 (+576). Therefore, perhaps:\n\n- The sides have squared length equal to the smallest distances, which would be Group2 distances: (a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 4,\n\n- Then the next set could be the other sides? Wait, but there are four sides. Alternatively, perhaps two sides from Group2 and two sides from another group?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sides are the ones with the +4 and +400? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's suppose that the sides are the distances in Group2 and Group3, and the diagonals are Group1 and Group4. Let me try that.\n\nSuppose that the four sides squared are:\n\nTwo distances from Group2: (a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 4,\n\nand two distances from Group3: 4(a - Gx)^2 + 400,\n\nand the two diagonals squared are Group1: (a - c)^2 + 484,\n\nand Group4: 4(c - Gx)^2 + 576.\n\nBut in a square, the diagonals should be sqrt(2) times the sides. So if the sides squared are s², then diagonals squared should be 2s².\n\nTherefore, if we suppose that, say, the diagonals are Group1 and Group4, then:\n\nGroup1 = 2 * Group2,\n\nand Group4 = 2 * Group3,\n\nor some combination like that.\n\nLet me try that. Let's suppose that the diagonals are Group1 and Group4, and the sides are Group2 and Group3.\n\nTherefore:\n\nGroup1 = 2 * Group2,\n\nand Group4 = 2 * Group3,\n\nor\n\nGroup1 = 2 * Group3,\n\nand Group4 = 2 * Group2,\n\nor some other pairing.\n\nLet me try the first possibility:\n\nSuppose Group1 = 2 * Group2,\n\nand Group4 = 2 * Group3.\n\nSo,\n\n(a - c)^2 + 484 = 2 * [ (a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 4 ]\n\nand\n\n4(c - Gx)^2 + 576 = 2 * [4(a - Gx)^2 + 400 ]\n\nLet me write these equations out:\n\nFirst equation:\n\n(a - c)^2 + 484 = 2*(a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 8\n\nSecond equation:\n\n4(c - Gx)^2 + 576 = 2*(4(a - Gx)^2 + 400 )\n\nSimplify the second equation first:\n\nRight-hand side: 2*(4(a - Gx)^2 + 400) = 8(a - Gx)^2 + 800\n\nLeft-hand side: 4(c - Gx)^2 + 576\n\nTherefore, equation becomes:\n\n4(c - Gx)^2 + 576 = 8(a - Gx)^2 + 800\n\nDivide both sides by 4:\n\n(c - Gx)^2 + 144 = 2(a - Gx)^2 + 200\n\nBring variables to left and constants to right:\n\n(c - Gx)^2 - 2(a - Gx)^2 = 200 - 144 = 56\n\nEquation (A): (c - Gx)^2 - 2(a - Gx)^2 = 56\n\nNow, the first equation:\n\n(a - c)^2 + 484 = 2*(a + c - 2Gx)^2 + 8\n\nLet me expand the right-hand side:\n\nFirst, let me denote term inside the square: (a + c - 2Gx)\n\nLet me write the equation as:\n\n(a - c)^2 + 484 - 8 = 2*(a + c - 2Gx)^2\n\nSo,\n\n(a - c)^2 + 476 = 2*(a + c - 2Gx)^2\n\nLet me expand the right-hand side:\n\n2*(a + c - 2Gx)^2 = 2*[ (a + c)^2 - 4Gx(a + c) + 4Gx² ]\n\nTherefore,\n\nLeft side: (a - c)^2 + 476\n\nRight side: 2(a + c)^2 - 8Gx(a + c) + 8Gx²\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n(a - c)^2 + 476 - 2(a + c)^2 + 8Gx(a + c) - 8Gx² = 0\n\nExpand (a - c)^2 and (a + c)^2:\n\n(a² - 2ac + c²) + 476 - 2(a² + 2ac + c²) + 8Gx(a + c) - 8Gx² = 0\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nFirst term: a² - 2ac + c²\n\nSecond term: +476\n\nThird term: -2a² -4ac -2c²\n\nFourth term: +8Gx(a + c)\n\nFifth term: -8Gx²\n\nCombine like terms:\n\na² - 2ac + c² - 2a² -4ac -2c² + 476 + 8Gx(a + c) -8Gx² = 0\n\nCombine a² terms: (1 - 2)a² = -a²\n\nc² terms: (1 - 2)c² = -c²\n\nac terms: (-2ac -4ac) = -6ac\n\nConstants: +476\n\nThen the terms with Gx:\n\n+8Gx(a + c)\n\nAnd the term with Gx²: -8Gx²\n\nSo altogether:\n\n- a² - c² - 6ac + 476 + 8Gx(a + c) -8Gx² = 0\n\nLet me rearrange:\n\n- a² - c² - 6ac + 476 + 8Gx(a + c) -8Gx² = 0\n\nHmm, this is getting quite involved. Perhaps we need another equation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can find expressions for a and c in terms of Gx, or relate a and c.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that in the square, the vectors between the points should satisfy certain perpendicularity conditions. For example, the vector from Point1 to Point2 should be perpendicular to the vector from Point2 to Point3, and their lengths should be equal.\n\nLet me try this approach.\n\nLet me denote the points as follows for clarity:\n\nLet me name the points:\n\nP1: (a, 47) – Stalactite tip\n\nP2: (b, 27) – Stalactite tip (opposite vertex to P1)\n\nP3: (c, 25) – Stalagmite tip\n\nP4: (d, 49) – Stalagmite tip (opposite vertex to P3)\n\nAssuming the square is P1-P2-P4-P3-P1 or some order, but we need to figure out the connectivity.\n\nAlternatively, since we know the center is (Gx, 37), the order of the square's vertices can be determined by moving around the center.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider vectors from the center to each point.\n\nIn a square, the vectors from the center to each vertex are perpendicular and of equal length, rotated by 90 degrees.\n\nSpecifically, if we consider the vectors from the center (Gx, 37) to each vertex, these vectors should be rotations of each other by 90 degrees.\n\nLet me denote the vector from center to P1: (a - Gx, 47 - 37) = (a - Gx, 10)\n\nSimilarly, vector to P2: (b - Gx, 27 - 37) = (b - Gx, -10)\n\nVector to P3: (c - Gx, 25 - 37) = (c - Gx, -12)\n\nVector to P4: (d - Gx, 49 - 37) = (d - Gx, 12)\n\nIn a square, these vectors should be rotations of each other by 90 degrees. A 90-degree rotation of a vector (x, y) is (-y, x). So, for example, the vector to P2 should be a 90-degree rotation of the vector to P1, or some other rotation.\n\nLet me see:\n\nSuppose that the vector to P2 is the vector to P1 rotated 90 degrees clockwise. Then:\n\nVector to P2 = rotation of vector to P1 by -90 degrees (clockwise).\n\nRotation by -90 degrees: (x, y) → (y, -x)\n\nTherefore, if vector to P1 is (a - Gx, 10), then rotating it by -90 degrees gives (10, -(a - Gx)).\n\nBut the actual vector to P2 is (b - Gx, -10). Therefore:\n\n(10, -(a - Gx)) = (b - Gx, -10)\n\nTherefore, equating components:\n\n10 = b - Gx,\n\nand\n\n- (a - Gx) = -10 ⇒ a - Gx = 10 ⇒ a = Gx + 10\n\nFrom the first equation: b = Gx + 10\n\nBut also, since b = 2Gx - a (from earlier: b = 2Gx - a),\n\nSubstituting a = Gx + 10 into b = 2Gx - a:\n\nb = 2Gx - (Gx + 10) = Gx - 10\n\nBut from above, we also have b = Gx + 10,\n\nTherefore:\n\nGx - 10 = Gx + 10 ⇒ -10 = +10, which is a contradiction.\n\nHmm, that doesn't work. Let me try a different rotation.\n\nPerhaps the rotation is 90 degrees counterclockwise instead.\n\nA 90-degree counterclockwise rotation of a vector (x, y) is (-y, x).\n\nTherefore, if vector to P2 is a 90-degree counterclockwise rotation of vector to P1,\n\nthen:\n\nVector to P2 = rotation of vector to P1 by 90 CCW:\n\n( -10, a - Gx )\n\nBut the actual vector to P2 is (b - Gx, -10).\n\nTherefore:\n\n-10 = b - Gx,\n\nand\n\na - Gx = -10 ⇒ a = Gx -10\n\nFrom the first equation: b = Gx -10\n\nAgain, using the relation b = 2Gx - a,\n\nSubstitute a = Gx - 10 into b = 2Gx - a:\n\nb = 2Gx - (Gx - 10) = Gx + 10\n\nBut we also have from above that b = Gx -10,\n\nTherefore:\n\nGx + 10 = Gx -10 ⇒ 10 = -10, which is also a contradiction.\n\nHmm, so that didn't work either.\n\nMaybe the rotation is not from P1 to P2 but another pairing?\n\nPerhaps the rotation is from P1 to P3?\n\nLet me try that. Suppose the vector to P3 is a 90-degree rotation of the vector to P1.\n\nVector to P3 is (c - Gx, -12). Vector to P1 is (a - Gx, 10).\n\nIf rotating vector to P1 by 90 CCW gives vector to P3:\n\nRotation of (a - Gx, 10) by 90 CCW is (-10, a - Gx).\n\nSet equal to vector to P3: (c - Gx, -12)\n\nTherefore,\n\n-10 = c - Gx,\n\nand\n\na - Gx = -12 ⇒ a = Gx -12\n\nFrom first equation: c = Gx -10\n\nAlso, recall that d = 2Gx - c,\n\nso d = 2Gx - (Gx -10) = Gx +10\n\nAdditionally, we have the relation from vector to P4: (d - Gx, 12) = ( (Gx +10) - Gx, 12 ) = (10, 12)\n\nNow, let's see if this vector (10,12) relates to another vector via rotation.\n\nIf we rotate the vector to P3 by 90 CCW, we should get the vector to P4?\n\nVector to P3 is (c - Gx, -12) = (-10, -12) [since c = Gx -10]\n\nRotating (-10, -12) 90 CCW gives (12, -10)\n\nBut the vector to P4 is (10,12), which is not equal to (12, -10). Not matching.\n\nAlternatively, rotating vector to P1 by 90 CCW gives (-10, a - Gx) = (-10, -12), which is the vector to P3, which works.\n\nThen, rotating vector to P3 by 90 CCW should give the next vector, say to P4.\n\nVector to P3 is (-10, -12). Rotated 90 CCW is (12, -10). But the actual vector to P4 is (10,12). These are not the same.\n\nHmm, discrepancy here. The rotated vector is (12, -10), but the actual vector is (10,12). Not the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe scaling is involved? But in a square, the vectors from the center should all have the same length.\n\nLet me check the lengths:\n\nLength of vector to P1: sqrt( (a - Gx)^2 + 10^2 )\n\nSince a = Gx -12,\n\nthen (a - Gx)^2 = (-12)^2 = 144,\n\nso length is sqrt(144 + 100) = sqrt(244)\n\nVector to P3: sqrt( (-10)^2 + (-12)^2 ) = sqrt(100 + 144) = sqrt(244). Okay, same length.\n\nVector to P4: sqrt(10^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(100 + 144) = sqrt(244). Same.\n\nVector to P2: Let's compute vector to P2. From earlier, b = Gx -10,\n\nso vector to P2 is (b - Gx, -10) = (-10, -10). Wait, but earlier we had from the rotation approach:\n\nWait, vector to P2: (b - Gx, -10). Since b = Gx -10,\n\nthen (b - Gx) = -10,\n\nso vector to P2 is (-10, -10). Then its length is sqrt( (-10)^2 + (-10)^2 ) = sqrt(200) ≈ 14.14, which is different from sqrt(244) ≈ 15.62. So that's a problem. The vectors from the center should all have the same length in a square.\n\nTherefore, this approach leads to inconsistency because vector to P2 has a different length than the others.\n\nHmm, so that suggests that my assumption about the rotation path is incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the order of the vertices is different. Perhaps the square is arranged such that the vectors from the center are rotated by 90 degrees but in a different order.\n\nSuppose instead that the vector to P2 is obtained by rotating the vector to P3 by 90 degrees, or something like that. This is getting too convoluted; maybe I need a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's recall that in a square, the distance from the center to any vertex is the same, which is half the length of the diagonal divided by sqrt(2). Wait, actually, the distance from the center to a vertex is (side length) * sqrt(2)/2, which is (s/√2)*√2 = s/√2? Wait, let me recall:\n\nIn a square with side length s, the diagonal is s√2. The distance from the center to a vertex is half the diagonal, so (s√2)/2 = s/√2.\n\nTherefore, all vectors from the center to the vertices must have the same length.\n\nSo, in our case, the vectors from the center (Gx, 37) to each of the four points must all have the same length.\n\nCompute the length squared from center to P1: (a - Gx)^2 + (47 - 37)^2 = (a - Gx)^2 + 100\n\nTo P2: (b - Gx)^2 + (27 - 37)^2 = (b - Gx)^2 + 100\n\nTo P3: (c - Gx)^2 + (25 - 37)^2 = (c - Gx)^2 + 144\n\nTo P4: (d - Gx)^2 + (49 - 37)^2 = (d - Gx)^2 + 144\n\nTherefore, for these lengths to be equal, we must have:\n\n(a - Gx)^2 + 100 = (b - Gx)^2 + 100 ⇒ (a - Gx)^2 = (b - Gx)^2 ⇒ a - Gx = ±(b - Gx)\n\nSimilarly, (a - Gx)^2 + 100 = (c - Gx)^2 + 144 ⇒ (a - Gx)^2 - (c - Gx)^2 = 44\n\nAnd similarly, (a - Gx)^2 + 100 = (d - Gx)^2 + 144 ⇒ (a - Gx)^2 - (d - Gx)^2 = 44\n\nAlso, from the earlier relations:\n\nb = 2Gx - a,\n\nd = 2Gx - c,\n\nSo let's substitute b and d in terms of a and c.\n\nFirst, from (a - Gx)^2 = (b - Gx)^2,\n\nsubstituting b = 2Gx - a,\n\nwe have:\n\n(a - Gx)^2 = ( (2Gx - a) - Gx )^2 = (Gx - a)^2 = (a - Gx)^2,\n\nwhich is always true. So that equation gives no new information.\n\nNext, consider the equation (a - Gx)^2 - (c - Gx)^2 = 44.\n\nLet me write this as:\n\n[ (a - Gx) - (c - Gx) ] [ (a - Gx) + (c - Gx) ] = 44\n\nSimplify the terms inside the brackets:\n\nFirst factor: (a - Gx - c + Gx) = (a - c)\n\nSecond factor: (a - Gx + c - Gx) = (a + c - 2Gx)\n\nThus,\n\n(a - c)(a + c - 2Gx) = 44. Equation (B)\n\nSimilarly, the equation (a - Gx)^2 - (d - Gx)^2 = 44,\n\nBut d = 2Gx - c,\n\nso (d - Gx) = (2Gx - c) - Gx = Gx - c,\n\ntherefore,\n\n(a - Gx)^2 - (Gx - c)^2 = 44,\n\nwhich is the same as:\n\n[ (a - Gx) - (Gx - c) ] [ (a - Gx) + (Gx - c) ] = 44\n\nSimplify:\n\nFirst factor: (a - Gx - Gx + c) = (a + c - 2Gx)\n\nSecond factor: (a - Gx + Gx - c) = (a - c)\n\nTherefore, the product is again (a + c - 2Gx)(a - c) = 44, which is the same as Equation (B). So again, no new information.\n\nTherefore, we only have one equation: (a - c)(a + c - 2Gx) = 44. Equation (B)\n\nWe need another equation to relate these variables. Let's recall that the distance from center to P3 squared must equal distance to P1 squared:\n\n(a - Gx)^2 + 100 = (c - Gx)^2 + 144,\n\nwhich rearranged is:\n\n(a - Gx)^2 - (c - Gx)^2 = 44,\n\nwhich is the same as above, so no new info.\n\nHmm. Let's see if we can find another relation.\n\nWe also know that the vectors from the center must be perpendicular to each other in sequence. For example, the vector to P1 and the vector to P3 should be perpendicular if they are adjacent vertices.\n\nWait, in a square, adjacent vectors from the center are separated by 90 degrees, so their dot product should be zero.\n\nSuppose that the vectors to P1 and to P3 are adjacent, then their dot product should be zero.\n\nVector to P1: (a - Gx, 10)\n\nVector to P3: (c - Gx, -12)\n\nDot product: (a - Gx)(c - Gx) + (10)(-12) = 0\n\nSo,\n\n(a - Gx)(c - Gx) - 120 = 0 ⇒ (a - Gx)(c - Gx) = 120. Equation (C)\n\nSimilarly, if the vectors to P1 and to P2 are adjacent, their dot product should be zero:\n\nVector to P1: (a - Gx, 10)\n\nVector to P2: (b - Gx, -10)\n\nDot product: (a - Gx)(b - Gx) + (10)(-10) = 0 ⇒ (a - Gx)(b - Gx) - 100 = 0 ⇒ (a - Gx)(b - Gx) = 100. Equation (D)\n\nSimilarly, other dot products should also be zero, but perhaps starting with these.\n\nLet me try Equation (C) and Equation (D).\n\nFrom Equation (C): (a - Gx)(c - Gx) = 120\n\nFrom Equation (D): (a - Gx)(b - Gx) = 100\n\nRecall that b = 2Gx - a,\n\nso (b - Gx) = (2Gx - a) - Gx = Gx - a,\n\ntherefore Equation (D) becomes:\n\n(a - Gx)(Gx - a) = 100 ⇒ -(a - Gx)^2 = 100 ⇒ (a - Gx)^2 = -100,\n\nwhich is impossible because a real number squared cannot be negative.\n\nAh, contradiction. So that suggests that vectors P1 and P2 are not adjacent.\n\nTherefore, my assumption that vectors P1 and P3 are adjacent might be wrong.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps vectors P1 and P4 are adjacent?\n\nLet me try that.\n\nVector to P1: (a - Gx, 10)\n\nVector to P4: (d - Gx, 12)\n\nDot product: (a - Gx)(d - Gx) + (10)(12) = 0 ⇒ (a - Gx)(d - Gx) + 120 = 0 ⇒ (a - Gx)(d - Gx) = -120. Equation (E)\n\nSimilarly, vector to P4 and vector to P2 might be adjacent?\n\nVector to P4: (d - Gx, 12)\n\nVector to P2: (b - Gx, -10)\n\nDot product: (d - Gx)(b - Gx) + (12)(-10) = 0 ⇒ (d - Gx)(b - Gx) - 120 = 0 ⇒ (d - Gx)(b - Gx) = 120. Equation (F)\n\nHmm, this is getting too many variables. Maybe we need to express everything in terms of Gx and one other variable.\n\nLet me let’s set variable k = a - Gx,\n\nthen from Equation (C) or (D), but since we had a problem with Equation (D), perhaps proceed with Equation (C) and another equation.\n\nWait, let's see.\n\nLet me define:\n\nLet’s let’s set k = a - Gx,\n\nand m = c - Gx,\n\nthen from Equation (C):\n\nk * m = 120,\n\nand from the distance equality:\n\nFrom the vector lengths, we had:\n\n(a - Gx)^2 + 100 = (c - Gx)^2 + 144 ⇒ k² + 100 = m² + 144 ⇒ k² - m² = 44 ⇒ (k - m)(k + m) = 44. Equation (G)\n\nAlso, from Equation (B):\n\n(a - c)(a + c - 2Gx) = 44,\n\nbut a - c = (a - Gx) - (c - Gx) = k - m,\n\nand a + c - 2Gx = (a - Gx) + (c - Gx) = k + m,\n\nTherefore, Equation (B) is:\n\n(k - m)(k + m) = 44,\n\nwhich is exactly Equation (G). So again, no new info.\n\nTherefore, we have two equations:\n\n1. k * m = 120,\n\n2. (k - m)(k + m) = 44.\n\nLet me write these as:\n\nEquation 1: k m = 120,\n\nEquation 2: k² - m² = 44.\n\nWe can solve these equations for k and m.\n\nLet me let’s denote S = k + m,\n\nD = k - m,\n\nthen we have:\n\nk m = 120,\n\nand k² - m² = (k - m)(k + m) = D * S = 44,\n\nAlso, note that k² - m² = 44,\n\nand k m = 120.\n\nAdditionally, we can express (k + m)^2 = k² + 2 k m + m²,\n\nand (k - m)^2 = k² - 2 k m + m²,\n\nBut perhaps it's better to express in terms of S and D.\n\nWe have:\n\nS * D = 44,\n\nand we also know that k m = 120.\n\nAlso, note that:\n\n(k + m)^2 = S² = k² + 2 k m + m²,\n\n(k - m)^2 = D² = k² - 2 k m + m²,\n\nTherefore,\n\nS² - D² = 4 k m = 4*120 = 480,\n\nSo,\n\nS² - D² = 480,\n\nBut we also know that S * D = 44,\n\nLet me set variables:\n\nLet’s let’s let S * D = 44,\n\nand S² - D² = 480,\n\nLet me write S² - D² = (S - D)(S + D) = 480,\n\nBut not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let me solve for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFrom S * D = 44 ⇒ D = 44/S,\n\nSubstitute into S² - D² = 480:\n\nS² - (44/S)^2 = 480,\n\nMultiply both sides by S²:\n\nS^4 - 44² = 480 S²,\n\nThus,\n\nS^4 - 480 S² - 1936 = 0,\n\nLet me let’s set u = S²,\n\nthen equation becomes:\n\nu² - 480 u - 1936 = 0,\n\nSolving quadratic equation:\n\nu = [480 ± sqrt(480² + 4*1936)] / 2\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\nΔ = 480² + 4*1936 = 230400 + 7744 = 238,144\n\nCompute sqrt(238,144). Let me see:\n\n500² = 250,000, which is higher.\n\n488² = (500 - 12)^2 = 500² - 2*500*12 + 12² = 250,000 - 12,000 + 144 = 238,144\n\nWow! sqrt(238,144) = 488\n\nTherefore,\n\nu = [480 ± 488]/2\n\nWe have two solutions:\n\nu = (480 + 488)/2 = 968/2 = 484,\n\nor u = (480 - 488)/2 = (-8)/2 = -4,\n\nBut u = S² must be non-negative, so discard -4.\n\nThus, u = 484 ⇒ S² = 484 ⇒ S = sqrt(484) = 22 or S = -22.\n\nSince S = k + m, and k and m are real numbers, but we need to see the context.\n\nRecall that k = a - Gx,\n\nand m = c - Gx,\n\nso S = k + m = (a - Gx) + (c - Gx) = a + c - 2Gx,\n\nwhich from earlier was part of the expression in Equation (B). Not sure about the sign, but let's proceed.\n\nCase 1: S = 22,\n\nThen D = 44/S = 44/22 = 2,\n\nTherefore, D = 2,\n\nRecall D = k - m = 2,\n\nand S = k + m = 22,\n\nSo we have:\n\nk + m = 22,\n\nk - m = 2,\n\nAdding equations:\n\n2k = 24 ⇒ k = 12,\n\nthen m = 22 - k = 10,\n\nTherefore, k = 12, m = 10,\n\nThus,\n\nk = a - Gx = 12 ⇒ a = Gx + 12,\n\nm = c - Gx = 10 ⇒ c = Gx + 10,\n\nNow, recall that d = 2Gx - c = 2Gx - (Gx + 10) = Gx - 10,\n\nand b = 2Gx - a = 2Gx - (Gx + 12) = Gx - 12,\n\nNow, we can express all variables in terms of Gx:\n\na = Gx + 12,\n\nb = Gx - 12,\n\nc = Gx + 10,\n\nd = Gx - 10,\n\nNow, we can compute the distances to verify if they form a square.\n\nFirst, let's compute the vectors from the center (Gx, 37) to each point:\n\nVector to P1 (a,47): (a - Gx, 47 - 37) = (12, 10)\n\nVector to P2 (b,27): (b - Gx, 27 - 37) = (-12, -10)\n\nVector to P3 (c,25): (c - Gx, 25 - 37) = (10, -12)\n\nVector to P4 (d,49): (d - Gx, 49 - 37) = (-10, 12)\n\nNow, let's check the lengths of these vectors:\n\n|P1 vector| = sqrt(12² + 10²) = sqrt(144 + 100) = sqrt(244)\n\n|P2 vector| = sqrt((-12)^2 + (-10)^2) = sqrt(144 + 100) = sqrt(244)\n\n|P3 vector| = sqrt(10² + (-12)^2) = sqrt(100 + 144) = sqrt(244)\n\n|P4 vector| = sqrt((-10)^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(100 + 144) = sqrt(244)\n\nGood, all vectors have the same length, as required for a square.\n\nNow, check the angles between adjacent vectors to ensure they are 90 degrees.\n\nTake vectors to P1 and P3:\n\nP1 vector: (12, 10)\n\nP3 vector: (10, -12)\n\nDot product: 12*10 + 10*(-12) = 120 - 120 = 0. Perfect, perpendicular.\n\nSimilarly, P3 vector and P4 vector:\n\nP3: (10, -12)\n\nP4: (-10, 12)\n\nDot product: 10*(-10) + (-12)*12 = -100 - 144 = -244 ≠ 0. Wait, that's not zero. Wait, that's a problem.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, if the vectors are supposed to be adjacent vertices, then the angle between P3 and P4 vectors should be 90 degrees. But their dot product is -244, which is not zero. Hmm, contradiction.\n\nWait, perhaps the order of the vertices is different?\n\nLet me list the vectors again:\n\nP1: (12,10)\n\nP2: (-12, -10)\n\nP3: (10, -12)\n\nP4: (-10, 12)\n\nIf we consider the order P1, P3, P4, P2 around the square, let's see the angles between them.\n\nWait, perhaps the adjacency is P1 to P3, P3 to P4, P4 to P2, P2 to P1?\n\nWait, let me plot these vectors mentally.\n\nVector P1 is (12,10), which is in the first quadrant.\n\nVector P3 is (10, -12), which is in the fourth quadrant.\n\nVector P4 is (-10,12), which is in the second quadrant.\n\nVector P2 is (-12, -10), which is in the third quadrant.\n\nIf we connect them in the order P1 -> P3 -> P4 -> P2 -> P1, then the angle between P1 and P3 is 90 degrees (as we saw), but the angle between P3 and P4:\n\nVector P3 to P4: from (10, -12) to (-10,12), but the vectors from the center are P3 and P4.\n\nWait, actually, the angle between vectors P3 and P4 should be 90 degrees if they are adjacent.\n\nWait, the angle between vector P3 (10, -12) and vector P4 (-10, 12):\n\nDot product: (10)(-10) + (-12)(12) = -100 - 144 = -244,\n\nThe magnitudes are both sqrt(244), so the cosine of the angle is (-244)/(sqrt(244)*sqrt(244)) = -244/244 = -1,\n\nWait, that would mean the angle is 180 degrees, which is a straight line, not 90. That can’t be.\n\nHmm, something is wrong here. Wait, perhaps the order of the points around the square is different?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct order is P1, P2, P4, P3?\n\nLet me check the angle between P1 and P2 vectors:\n\nP1 vector (12,10) and P2 vector (-12, -10). Their dot product is 12*(-12) + 10*(-10) = -144 -100 = -244,\n\nWhich would imply angle of 180 degrees again, since the cosine is -1. Not good.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, the correct adjacency might be P1 to P4?\n\nVector P1 (12,10) and vector P4 (-10,12):\n\nDot product: 12*(-10) + 10*12 = -120 + 120 = 0. Ah, that's perpendicular!\n\nSimilarly, vector P4 (-10,12) and vector P3 (10, -12):\n\nDot product: (-10)(10) + 12*(-12) = -100 -144 = -244, which is not zero.\n\nWait, but if we go P1 -> P4 -> P3 -> P2 -> P1,\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nP1 to P4: vectors P1 and P4 are perpendicular (dot product zero).\n\nP4 to P3: vectors P4 and P3 have dot product -244, which is not perpendicular.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct order is P1 -> P3 -> P4 -> P2 -> P1?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nP1 to P3: perpendicular (dot product zero).\n\nP3 to P4: angle between P3 and P4 vectors is 180 degrees? That can't be.\n\nWait, this is confusing. Maybe the square is actually a rotated square where the vectors are not in the order I thought.\n\nWait, perhaps the points are ordered such that the vectors from the center are at 0°, 90°, 180°, 270°, but rotated somehow.\n\nWait, let me look at the vectors:\n\nP1: (12,10) – which is in the first quadrant,\n\nP3: (10, -12) – fourth quadrant,\n\nP4: (-10,12) – second quadrant,\n\nP2: (-12, -10) – third quadrant,\n\nIf we imagine rotating around the center, starting from P1 (12,10), the next vector should be a 90-degree rotation of P1.\n\nRotating P1 vector (12,10) 90 degrees counterclockwise would give (-10,12), which is exactly the P4 vector.\n\nThen rotating another 90 degrees would give (-12,-10), which is the P2 vector,\n\nThen another 90 would give (10,-12), which is P3,\n\nThen another 90 back to P1.\n\nTherefore, the order of the vertices around the square should be P1, P4, P2, P3, back to P1.\n\nTherefore, the connections are P1-P4-P2-P3-P1.\n\nLet me verify the angles between these vectors:\n\nBetween P1 and P4: already perpendicular (dot product zero),\n\nBetween P4 and P2: vectors P4 (-10,12) and P2 (-12,-10):\n\nDot product: (-10)(-12) + (12)(-10) = 120 - 120 = 0. Perfect, perpendicular.\n\nBetween P2 and P3: vectors P2 (-12,-10) and P3 (10, -12):\n\nDot product: (-12)(10) + (-10)(-12) = -120 + 120 = 0. Perpendicular.\n\nBetween P3 and P1: vectors P3 (10, -12) and P1 (12,10):\n\nDot product: 10*12 + (-12)*10 = 120 - 120 = 0. Perpendicular.\n\nPerfect! So the square is indeed formed by the points in the order P1, P4, P2, P3, with each adjacent pair's vectors from the center being perpendicular. Great!\n\nTherefore, with this configuration, the four points form a square with center at (Gx, 37), and all conditions are satisfied.\n\nNow, we just need to ensure that the side lengths are equal.\n\nCompute the distance between P1 and P4:\n\nP1 is (a,47) = (Gx + 12, 47),\n\nP4 is (d,49) = (Gx -10, 49),\n\nDistance squared between P1 and P4:\n\n(Gx +12 - (Gx -10))² + (47 - 49)^2 = (22)^2 + (-2)^2 = 484 + 4 = 488.\n\nWait, but in a square, the side length squared should be equal to the squared length of the vectors from the center times (something). Wait, the distance between two adjacent vertices (like P1 and P4) should be equal to the side length s.\n\nWait, the vectors from the center to the vertices have length sqrt(244). In a square, the distance between two adjacent vertices is s = sqrt(2) * (distance from center to vertex).\n\nWait, because the distance from center to vertex is (s/√2), so s = sqrt(2) * (distance from center to vertex).\n\nTherefore, s^2 = 2*(distance from center to vertex)^2 = 2*(244) = 488.\n\nWhich matches the computed distance squared between P1 and P4: 488. Good.\n\nSimilarly, distance between P4 and P2:\n\nP4: (Gx -10, 49),\n\nP2: (Gx -12, 27),\n\nDistance squared:\n\n(Gx -10 - (Gx -12))² + (49 - 27)^2 = (2)^2 + (22)^2 = 4 + 484 = 488. Same as above.\n\nDistance between P2 and P3:\n\nP2: (Gx -12, 27),\n\nP3: (Gx +10, 25),\n\nDistance squared:\n\n(Gx -12 - (Gx +10))² + (27 - 25)^2 = (-22)^2 + (2)^2 = 484 + 4 = 488.\n\nDistance between P3 and P1:\n\nP3: (Gx +10,25),\n\nP1: (Gx +12,47),\n\nDistance squared:\n\n(Gx +10 - (Gx +12))² + (25 - 47)^2 = (-2)^2 + (-22)^2 = 4 + 484 = 488.\n\nPerfect! All sides squared are 488, so side length squared is 488, so side length is sqrt(488).\n\nThe diagonals should be s*sqrt(2) = sqrt(488)*sqrt(2) = sqrt(976). Let's check the distance between P1 and P2, which should be a diagonal:\n\nP1: (Gx +12,47),\n\nP2: (Gx -12,27),\n\nDistance squared:\n\n(Gx +12 - (Gx -12))² + (47 - 27)^2 = (24)^2 + (20)^2 = 576 + 400 = 976,\n\nWhich is 976, which is indeed 2*488. So that checks out.\n\nGreat! So all conditions are satisfied with this configuration.\n\nNow, we just need to ensure that the x-coordinates are consistent, meaning that the points P1, P2, P3, P4 are indeed the tips of the stalactites and stalagmites as described.\n\nRecall that:\n\nPoint P1 is the tip of the first stalactite: (a, 47) = (Gx + 12, 47),\n\nwhich is a stalactite tip, so it must be one of the two stalactites with lengths 16 and 36.\n\nThe y-coordinate of a stalactite tip is h - length, so for P1, y = h - 16 or h - 36.\n\nGiven that P1 is at y = 47, then:\n\nIf P1 is the tip of the 16-length stalactite: h - 16 = 47 ⇒ h = 63,\n\nor if it's the 36-length one: h - 36 = 47 ⇒ h = 83.\n\nSimilarly, Point P2 is (b, 27) = (Gx -12, 27). Since P2 is also a stalactite tip, its y-coordinate is h - 16 or h - 36.\n\nSo for P2 at y = 27,\n\nif it's the 16-length: h - 16 = 27 ⇒ h = 43,\n\nor 36-length: h - 36 = 27 ⇒ h = 63.\n\nSimilarly, Point P3 is (c,25) = (Gx +10, 25), which is a stalagmite tip. Its y-coordinate is 25, which is the height of the first stalagmite (25), so that checks out. The height of the stalagmite is 25, so its tip is at y = 25, so that's consistent.\n\nPoint P4 is (d,49) = (Gx -10, 49), which is the tip of the second stalagmite with height 49, so that's consistent because its y-coordinate is 49, which is the height of that stalagmite.\n\nTherefore, the only possible h values come from the stalactites:\n\nFor P1 (y=47):\n\nIf P1 is the 16-length stalactite tip: h = 47 + 16 = 63,\n\nIf P1 is the 36-length stalactite tip: h = 47 + 36 = 83.\n\nFor P2 (y=27):\n\nIf P2 is the 16-length stalactite tip: h = 27 + 16 = 43,\n\nIf P2 is the 36-length stalactite tip: h = 27 + 36 = 63.\n\nNow, h must be consistent for both P1 and P2.\n\nLooking at the possibilities:\n\nIf h = 63,\n\nthen P1 is the 16-length stalactite (since 63 - 16 = 47),\n\nand P2 is the 36-length stalactite (since 63 - 36 = 27). That works.\n\nIf h = 83,\n\nthen P1 would be the 36-length stalactite (83 - 36 = 47),\n\nand P2 would have to be the 16-length (83 - 16 = 67), but P2 is at y=27, which would require h - 16 = 27 