+ (s/2)cosθ),\n\n(h - (s/2)cosθ - (s/2)sinθ, k - (s/2)sinθ + (s/2)cosθ),\n\n(h - (s/2)cosθ + (s/2)sinθ, k - (s/2)sinθ - (s/2)cosθ),\n\n(h + (s/2)cosθ + (s/2)sinθ, k + (s/2)sinθ - (s/2)cosθ).\n\nHmm, this might be a bit too involved, but perhaps manageable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of parameterizing the square, I can use the property that in a square, the coordinates of the four vertices satisfy certain equations.\n\nSuppose the four points are (x1, y1), (x2, y2), (x3, y3), (x4, y4). Then, for these to form a square, the following must hold:\n\n1. The distance between each pair of points must be either equal to the side length or the diagonal length.\n\n2. For any three points, the distances must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem (i.e., the sides must be perpendicular).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps more systematically:\n\nLet me consider all six pairwise distances between the four points. In a square, there are two distinct distances: the side length (let's say s) and the diagonal length (s√2). Each side occurs four times, and each diagonal occurs twice. Wait, actually, in a square, there are four sides and two diagonals, so six distances total: four sides (each of length s) and two diagonals (each of length s√2). Therefore, among the six distances between the four points, four should be equal to s, and two should be equal to s√2. So, if I can compute all six distances between the four points in terms of H and the unknown x-coordinates a, b, c, d, then I can set up equations that four of them are equal and the other two are equal and √2 times the first.\n\nBut since the x-coordinates a, b, c, d are unknown, this introduces more variables. Hmm, so maybe this is too many variables.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the horizontal positions (the x-coordinates) of the stalactites and stalagmites can be arranged such that the square is formed with sides not aligned with the x or y axes, but arranged in such a way that the vertical separations between the points correspond appropriately.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to notice that the vertical coordinates of the four points are H - 16, H - 36, 25, and 49. Let me note that these are four different y-values unless H is chosen such that some of them coincide, but given that H is the height of the cave, and the stalactites and stalagmites have lengths and heights given, we might need H to be such that these y-values can form a square's vertices.\n\nWait, perhaps the vertical coordinates of the four points must form a set of numbers that can be the y-coordinates of a square's vertices. For a square in the plane, the y-coordinates of the four vertices can vary, but they have to satisfy the square's geometry.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the vertical distances between the highest and lowest points must relate to the square's diagonal. Let me compute the possible vertical distances.\n\nThe highest y-coordinate among the four points is the maximum of {H - 16, H - 36, 25, 49}. The lowest y-coordinate is the minimum of those four. The vertical distance between the highest and lowest points is then:\n\nIf H - 16 is the highest: then highest y = H - 16, lowest y is min{H - 36, 25, 49}. Depending on H, this could be 25 or 49 or H - 36 (if H - 36 < 25 and H - 36 < 49). Hmm, complicated.\n\nAlternatively, if H - 36 is the highest, which would require H - 36 > H - 16, which is impossible because H - 36 is less than H - 16 by 20. Similarly, H - 36 can't be higher than H - 16.\n\nWait, actually, H - 16 is always higher than H - 36 because 16 < 36, so subtracting a smaller number from H gives a higher result. Therefore, the highest y-coordinate is always H - 16, and the next is H - 36, then 25 and 49, depending on H.\n\nSimilarly, the lowest y-coordinate is the minimum of {H - 16, H - 36, 25, 49}. Since H - 36 is less than H - 16, and 25 and 49 are fixed numbers. So, the lowest y-coordinate is the minimum between (H - 36) and 25 and 49. So, if H - 36 is less than 25, then the lowest y-coordinate is H - 36. If H - 36 is between 25 and 49, then the lowest y-coordinate is 25. If H - 36 is greater than 49, then the lowest y-coordinate is 49. But since H - 36 > 49 would mean H > 85, but then H - 16 would be greater than 69, so the highest y-coordinate would still be H - 16, but the lowest would be 49. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can suppose different cases based on the value of H and see which case allows the four points to form a square.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the vertical coordinates must form a square's y-coordinates. For a square, the y-coordinates can be arranged in such a way that they form an arithmetic sequence with a common difference related to the side length and the angle of rotation. Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the horizontal distances between the points must compensate for the vertical distances to form right angles and equal lengths. Since squares can be rotated, the horizontal and vertical differences between points must satisfy certain conditions.\n\nLet me try to think of coordinates.\n\nSuppose that the four points are the vertices of a square. Let me denote them as follows:\n\nLet’s call the four points:\n\nP: (a, H - 16)\n\nQ: (b, H - 36)\n\nR: (c, 25)\n\nS: (d, 49)\n\nWe need these four points to form a square. Let me consider the vectors between these points. In a square, the vectors representing the sides should be perpendicular and of equal length.\n\nSuppose we pick an order of the points, say P, Q, R, S going around the square. Then the vector from P to Q should be perpendicular to the vector from Q to R, and their lengths should be equal. But since we don’t know the order, this is tricky.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use complex numbers? Let me see.\n\nLet me assign complex numbers to the points:\n\nLet P = a + (H - 16)i\n\nQ = b + (H - 36)i\n\nR = c + 25i\n\nS = d + 49i\n\nIn the complex plane, a square can be represented such that if you take one vertex and add a complex number representing a side, you get the next vertex, and so on, with each turn being a 90-degree rotation (multiplication by i).\n\nBut without knowing the order, this might not help immediately.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that in a square, the midpoint of the diagonals is the same. So, if we can pair the points into two diagonally opposite pairs, then the midpoints of those pairs must coincide.\n\nTherefore, suppose that P and R are diagonal, and Q and S are diagonal. Then the midpoint of P and R must equal the midpoint of Q and S.\n\nMidpoint of P and R: [(a + c)/2, ( (H - 16) + 25 ) / 2 ]\n\nMidpoint of Q and S: [(b + d)/2, ( (H - 36) + 49 ) / 2 ]\n\nSetting these equal:\n\n(a + c)/2 = (b + d)/2 --> a + c = b + d\n\nand\n\n( (H - 16) + 25 ) / 2 = ( (H - 36) + 49 ) / 2\n\nSimplify the y-coordinate equation:\n\n(H - 16 + 25) = (H - 36 + 49)\n\nH + 9 = H + 13\n\nSubtract H from both sides: 9 = 13\n\nWait, that's a contradiction. 9 = 13? That can't be. Therefore, this pairing of diagonals (P-R and Q-S) is impossible.\n\nHmm, so that pairing doesn't work. Let's try another pairing.\n\nSuppose instead that P and Q are diagonal, and R and S are diagonal.\n\nThen midpoints:\n\nMidpoint of P and Q: [(a + b)/2, ( (H - 16) + (H - 36) ) / 2 ] = [(a + b)/2, (2H - 52)/2] = [(a + b)/2, H - 26]\n\nMidpoint of R and S: [(c + d)/2, (25 + 49)/2] = [(c + d)/2, 37]\n\nSetting equal:\n\n(a + b)/2 = (c + d)/2 --> a + b = c + d\n\nand\n\nH - 26 = 37 --> H = 63\n\nOkay, so if we pair P-Q as diagonals and R-S as diagonals, then we get H = 63. That seems promising. Let me note that H = 63 is a candidate.\n\nNow, we can check if this holds, and also check if the other conditions for a square are satisfied.\n\nSo if H = 63, then let's see the y-coordinates:\n\nP: y = 63 - 16 = 47\n\nQ: y = 63 - 36 = 27\n\nR: y = 25\n\nS: y = 49\n\nSo the four y-coordinates are 47, 27, 25, 49.\n\nWait, so the midpoints for the diagonals P-Q and R-S must both be at (something, 37), since H - 26 = 63 - 26 = 37, and the midpoint of R and S is 37 in y-coordinate. That works.\n\nSo, with H = 63, that condition is satisfied. Now, we need to check whether the other conditions for the square are satisfied, i.e., that the distances between the points correspond appropriately.\n\nSo, let's note that if the diagonals are P-Q and R-S, then the sides would be P-R, R-Q, Q-S, S-P or something? Wait, actually, in a square, the diagonals are the two lines connecting opposite vertices, so if P and Q are diagonal, then the other diagonal is R and S. Then the sides would be P to R, R to Q, Q to S, S to P? Wait, maybe not. Let me think.\n\nActually, in a square with diagonals PQ and RS intersecting at the center, the vertices are ordered such that moving around the square, you go from P to one adjacent vertex, then to the next, etc., with the diagonals being P to Q and R to S.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to compute all the distances and see if they can form a square.\n\nGiven H = 63, let's assign coordinates with variables for the x-coordinates:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nPoint P: (a, 47)\n\nPoint Q: (b, 27)\n\nPoint R: (c, 25)\n\nPoint S: (d, 49)\n\nWe already have the midpoints condition satisfied: a + b = c + d and the y-coordinate condition gave us H = 63.\n\nNow, to form a square, the distances between adjacent vertices must be equal, and the diagonals must be equal and of length sqrt(2) times the side length.\n\nBut since we don't know the order of the vertices around the square, we need to figure out which points are adjacent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the sides are between points that are closest in y-coordinate? Not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, let me suppose that the sides are between P and R, R and S, S and Q, Q and P? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can compute the distances between all pairs and see if they can form a square.\n\nLet me compute the distances squared between each pair, since dealing with squared distances might be easier and avoids square roots.\n\nFirst, list all six pairs:\n\n1. P to Q: distance squared = (a - b)^2 + (47 - 27)^2 = (a - b)^2 + (20)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 400\n\n2. P to R: distance squared = (a - c)^2 + (47 - 25)^2 = (a - c)^2 + (22)^2 = (a - c)^2 + 484\n\n3. P to S: distance squared = (a - d)^2 + (47 - 49)^2 = (a - d)^2 + (-2)^2 = (a - d)^2 + 4\n\n4. Q to R: distance squared = (b - c)^2 + (27 - 25)^2 = (b - c)^2 + (2)^2 = (b - c)^2 + 4\n\n5. Q to S: distance squared = (b - d)^2 + (27 - 49)^2 = (b - d)^2 + (-22)^2 = (b - d)^2 + 484\n\n6. R to S: distance squared = (c - d)^2 + (25 - 49)^2 = (c - d)^2 + (-24)^2 = (c - d)^2 + 576\n\nNow, in a square, there are four sides of equal length and two diagonals of equal length (longer by sqrt(2)). Therefore, among these six distances squared, four should be equal (the sides squared), and two should be equal and larger than the others (the diagonals squared), and the two diagonals squared should be equal to twice the side squared (since diagonal^2 = 2 * side^2).\n\nWait, actually, in a square, the diagonal squared is 2 times the side squared. Therefore, if the side length is s, then the diagonal is s√2, so diagonal squared is 2 s². Therefore, the two diagonals squared must each be 2 times the common side squared. However, in the six distances, there are four sides and two diagonals, so four distances squared should be equal to s², and two distances squared should be equal to 2 s². Therefore, the two largest distances squared should be equal and equal to 2 s², and the remaining four should be equal to s². Alternatively, depending on which are the diagonals.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two largest distances squared are the diagonals, and the next four are the sides? Wait, but in a square, the diagonals are longer than the sides, so yes.\n\nTherefore, in our case, the largest distance squared among the six should be the diagonal squared, and there should be two of them, and the next four should be the sides squared, equal to half of the largest distance squared.\n\nSo, let me see what the possible largest distances squared are here.\n\nLooking at the computed distances squared:\n\n1. P-Q: (a - b)^2 + 400\n\n2. P-R: (a - c)^2 + 484\n\n3. P-S: (a - d)^2 + 4\n\n4. Q-R: (b - c)^2 + 4\n\n5. Q-S: (b - d)^2 + 484\n\n6. R-S: (c - d)^2 + 576\n\nThe largest among these is likely R-S, which has 576, unless some of the others can be larger depending on the x-coordinates. Similarly, the next largest could be P-R or Q-S with 484, then P-Q with 400, etc. But since the x-coordinates (a, b, c, d) are variables, we can choose them such that some distances squared become larger.\n\nHowever, since we need the four side lengths squared to be equal and the two diagonals squared to be equal and twice that, we might need to set up equations such that two of the distances squared are equal to some value, say K, and the other four are equal to K/2, but actually no, wait: in a square, the sides squared are s², and the diagonals squared are 2 s². So if we let the side squared be S, then the diagonals squared are 2S. Therefore, the four sides squared must all equal S, and the two diagonals squared must equal 2S. Therefore, among the six distances squared, four must be equal to S and two must be equal to 2S. Therefore, we can set up equations where four of the expressions above equal S, and two equal 2S, and also S must be half of 2S, which is consistent.\n\nGiven that, perhaps we can set the two largest distances squared (which are candidates for diagonals) to be equal and equal to 2S, and the next four to be equal to S. But which ones are the diagonals?\n\nAlternatively, let me suppose that R-S is a diagonal, since its distance squared is 576 plus (c - d)^2. Wait, actually, R-S distance squared is (c - d)^2 + 576. Wait, no: Wait, point R is at (c,25) and point S is at (d,49). So the difference in x is (d - c), and difference in y is (49 - 25) = 24. So the distance squared is (d - c)^2 + (24)^2 = (d - c)^2 + 576. So that term is variable because (d - c) is variable.\n\nSimilarly, the distance between P and Q is (a - b)^2 + (47 - 27)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 400.\n\nDistance P-R is (a - c)^2 + (47 - 25)^2 = (a - c)^2 + 484.\n\nDistance P-S is (a - d)^2 + (47 - 49)^2 = (a - d)^2 + 4.\n\nDistance Q-R is (b - c)^2 + (27 - 25)^2 = (b - c)^2 + 4.\n\nDistance Q-S is (b - d)^2 + (27 - 49)^2 = (b - d)^2 + 484.\n\nSo, the distance squared between R and S is (d - c)^2 + 576, which can be made large or small depending on (d - c). Similarly, the distance squared between P and Q is (a - b)^2 + 400, which can be made large or small.\n\nGiven that we need two distances squared to be equal to 2S and four to be equal to S, we need to choose the variables a, b, c, d such that this holds. Since there are four variables, maybe we can set some variables in terms of others.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can make some of these distances equal by setting the differences in x-coordinates appropriately.\n\nAlternatively, notice that distances P-S and Q-R both have small added constants: 4 and 4 respectively. So their squared distances are (a - d)^2 + 4 and (b - c)^2 + 4. If we can set these to be equal to S, the side squared, and then the other distances can be arranged accordingly.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to see if I can set some of these expressions equal to each other.\n\nSuppose that the sides are the four smaller distances, and the diagonals are the two larger ones. Let's see:\n\nLooking at the expressions:\n\nThe smallest possible squared distance is 4 (from P-S and Q-R). The next smallest could be 400 (P-Q), then 484 (P-R and Q-S), then 576 + (d - c)^2 (R-S), and then others.\n\nBut if we want four distances squared to be equal to S and two to be equal to 2S, then perhaps S could be 400? Then 2S would be 800. Let's see if that works.\n\nIf S = 400, then the diagonals squared would need to be 800. Let's see if any of the expressions can equal 800.\n\nLooking at P-Q distance squared: (a - b)^2 + 400 = 800 --> (a - b)^2 = 400 --> a - b = ±20\n\nSimilarly, if we set P-Q as a diagonal, then its squared distance would be 2S, so 2*400 = 800, so that would work if (a - b)^2 + 400 = 800, so (a - b)^2 = 400, so a - b = ±20.\n\nThen, the sides squared would need to be 400. So other distances squared must equal 400.\n\nLooking at P-S: (a - d)^2 + 4 = 400 --> (a - d)^2 = 396 --> a - d = ±√396 ≈ ±19.899, which is not a whole number, but maybe okay.\n\nSimilarly, Q-R: (b - c)^2 + 4 = 400 --> (b - c)^2 = 396 --> same as above.\n\nThen, the other distances: P-R: (a - c)^2 + 484 = ?\n\nIf P-R is supposed to be a side, then that would have to equal 400, but (a - c)^2 + 484 = 400 --> (a - c)^2 = -84, which is impossible. So that can't be.\n\nAlternatively, maybe P-R is a diagonal? Then its squared distance would need to be 800. So (a - c)^2 + 484 = 800 --> (a - c)^2 = 316 --> a - c = ±√316 ≈ ±17.776, which is possible, but then the other diagonal, say Q-S: (b - d)^2 + 484 = 800 --> (b - d)^2 = 316, same as above.\n\nThen the remaining distances: P-Q is a diagonal (800), R-S: (d - c)^2 + 576. If R-S is a side, then that squared distance should be 400, so (d - c)^2 + 576 = 400 --> (d - c)^2 = -176, impossible. Alternatively, R-S is a diagonal, so squared distance should be 800: (d - c)^2 + 576 = 800 --> (d - c)^2 = 224 --> d - c = ±√224 ≈ ±14.966. Hmm.\n\nThen, the other distances: P-S and Q-R are supposed to be sides, so their squared distances are 400, which would require (a - d)^2 = 396 and (b - c)^2 = 396 as above. But then we have variables a, b, c, d with multiple equations.\n\nLet me see if this system is solvable.\n\nLet me denote variables:\n\nLet’s let’s set:\n\nFrom P-Q diagonal: (a - b)^2 = 400 ⇒ a - b = ±20. Let's suppose a - b = 20 for simplicity (the sign might not matter, or we can adjust later).\n\nFrom Q-R side: (b - c)^2 = 396 ⇒ b - c = ±√396. Let me write √396 as 2√99 ≈ 19.899, but let's keep it as √396 for exactness.\n\nFrom P-S side: (a - d)^2 = 396 ⇒ a - d = ±√396.\n\nFrom Q-S diagonal: (b - d)^2 = 316 ⇒ b - d = ±√316.\n\nFrom R-S diagonal: (d - c)^2 = 224 ⇒ d - c = ±√224.\n\nAdditionally, from the midpoint condition earlier: a + b = c + d (since we paired P-Q and R-S as diagonals, leading to H=63). Wait, actually in this scenario, we had earlier set H=63 from the midpoint condition when we paired P-Q and R-S as diagonals. Wait, actually in this case, we assumed that P-Q and R-S are diagonals, so the midpoint condition gave us H = 63, which we already used. So that condition is already satisfied, so we don't have an additional equation from that.\n\nSo now we have several equations:\n\n1. a - b = 20\n\n2. b - c = ±√396\n\n3. a - d = ±√396\n\n4. b - d = ±√316\n\n5. d - c = ±√224\n\nAlso, note that √396 = √(4*99) = 2√99 ≈ 19.899, √316 = √(4*79) = 2√79 ≈ 17.776, and √224 = √(16*14) = 4√14 ≈ 14.966.\n\nThis seems complicated with all these square roots, but maybe we can find relations between the variables.\n\nLet me try to express all variables in terms of, say, b.\n\nFrom equation 1: a = b + 20.\n\nFrom equation 2: c = b - √396 (assuming the positive root; we can check signs later).\n\nFrom equation 3: d = a - √396 = (b + 20) - √396.\n\nFrom equation 5: d - c = ±√224.\n\nSubstituting d and c from above:\n\nd - c = [ (b + 20) - √396 ] - [ b - √396 ] = (b + 20 - √396) - b + √396 = 20.\n\nTherefore, d - c = 20. But equation 5 says d - c = ±√224 ≈ ±14.966. However, we just found that d - c = 20. Contradiction.\n\nHmm, so that's a problem. So if we take the positive roots in equations 2, 3, 4, 5, we end up with d - c = 20, but equation 5 requires d - c = ±√224 ≈ ±14.966. Since 20 ≠ ±14.966, this is inconsistent.\n\nHmm, so maybe we need to take some negative roots?\n\nLet me try different signs. Let's see.\n\nLet me suppose in equation 2: b - c = -√396 ⇒ c = b + √396\n\nIn equation 3: a - d = -√396 ⇒ d = a + √396 = (b + 20) + √396\n\nThen, equation 5: d - c = [ (b + 20) + √396 ] - [ b + √396 ] = 20. Again, same result, 20, which still doesn't match equation 5's requirement of ±√224. So that still doesn't work.\n\nHmm, perhaps equation 4?\n\nEquation 4: b - d = ±√316.\n\nWith the previous substitutions:\n\nd = (b + 20) + √396 (if we took equation 3 as a - d = -√396, so d = a + √396, and a = b + 20)\n\nWait, let me recast with the negative in equation 3:\n\nIf equation 3 is a - d = -√396 ⇒ d = a + √396 = (b + 20) + √396\n\nThen equation 4: b - d = b - [ (b + 20) + √396 ] = -20 - √396\n\nThis is supposed to equal ±√316. But -20 - √396 ≈ -20 - 19.899 ≈ -39.899, which is not equal to ±√316 ≈ ±17.776. Not matching.\n\nAlternatively, maybe equation 4 has the negative sign: b - d = -√316 ⇒ d = b + √316.\n\nBut from equation 3, d = a + √396 = (b + 20) + √396.\n\nThus, setting equal:\n\nb + √316 = b + 20 + √396 ⇒ √316 = 20 + √396 ⇒ which is not true, since √316 ≈17.776 and 20 + √396≈ 20 + 19.899≈39.899. Nope.\n\nAlternatively, maybe equation 4 has the positive sign: b - d = √316 ⇒ d = b - √316.\n\nBut from equation 3: d = a + √396 = (b + 20) + √396\n\nThus,\n\nb - √316 = b + 20 + √396 ⇒ -√316 = 20 + √396 ⇒ which is also false.\n\nHmm, so regardless of the sign choices, this approach leads to inconsistency. That suggests that our initial assumption that S = 400 might be wrong, or that the pairing of diagonals as P-Q and R-S is incorrect, or that H = 63 is not the correct height?\n\nWait, but we derived H = 63 from the midpoint condition when we paired P-Q and R-S as diagonals, so if that pairing leads to inconsistency in the distances, maybe that pairing is invalid, and we need to try a different pairing of the diagonals.\n\nLet me try a different pairing of the diagonals.\n\nSuppose instead that the diagonals are P-R and Q-S. Then their midpoints must coincide.\n\nMidpoint of P-R: [(a + c)/2, (47 + 25)/2] = [(a + c)/2, 36]\n\nMidpoint of Q-S: [(b + d)/2, (27 + 49)/2] = [(b + d)/2, 38]\n\nSetting these equal:\n\n(a + c)/2 = (b + d)/2 ⇒ a + c = b + d\n\nand\n\n36 = 38 ⇒ 36 = 38, which is a contradiction. So that pairing is invalid.\n\nNext, try pairing P-S and Q-R as diagonals.\n\nMidpoint of P-S: [(a + d)/2, (47 + 49)/2] = [(a + d)/2, 48]\n\nMidpoint of Q-R: [(b + c)/2, (27 + 25)/2] = [(b + c)/2, 26]\n\nSetting equal:\n\n(a + d)/2 = (b + c)/2 ⇒ a + d = b + c\n\nand\n\n48 = 26 ⇒ 48 = 26, which is false. Not good.\n\nNext, pairing P-Q and R-S as diagonals we already did, which gave H=63 but led to inconsistency.\n\nNext, pairing P-R and Q-S we did, which gave a contradiction in y-coordinate.\n\nPairing P-S and Q-R gave a contradiction in y-coordinate.\n\nThe last possible pairing is P-Q and R-S, which we did first, leading to H=63 but inconsistency in distances.\n\nHmm. Maybe the only possible pairing that gave a consistent H was the first one (P-Q and R-S diagonals giving H=63), but the problem is that with H=63, the distances can't satisfy the square conditions unless the x-coordinates are chosen in a way that creates these square roots which conflict. Maybe we need to choose different signs or different pairings?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my assumption that the sides squared are 400 is wrong, and S is a different value.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the side squared is 484? Let's try that.\n\nSuppose that the sides squared are 484, so S = 484, then the diagonals squared would be 2*484 = 968.\n\nLooking at the distances squared:\n\nLooking for distances squared equal to 484 and 968.\n\nDistance P-R and Q-S both have 484 as a component. Let me see:\n\nDistance P-R squared: (a - c)^2 + 484. If this is to be a side, then (a - c)^2 + 484 = 484 ⇒ (a - c)^2 = 0 ⇒ a = c.\n\nSimilarly, distance Q-S squared: (b - d)^2 + 484. If that's a side, then (b - d)^2 + 484 = 484 ⇒ b = d.\n\nIf a = c and b = d, then let's see what happens.\n\nSet a = c and b = d.\n\nThen, the four points become:\n\nP: (a, 47)\n\nQ: (b, 27)\n\nR: (a, 25)\n\nS: (b, 49)\n\nNow, let's compute all the distances squared with a = c and b = d:\n\n1. P-Q: (a - b)^2 + (47 - 27)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 400\n\n2. P-R: (a - a)^2 + (47 - 25)^2 = 0 + (22)^2 = 484\n\n3. P-S: (a - b)^2 + (47 - 49)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 4\n\n4. Q-R: (b - a)^2 + (27 - 25)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 4\n\n5. Q-S: (b - b)^2 + (27 - 49)^2 = 0 + (-22)^2 = 484\n\n6. R-S: (a - b)^2 + (25 - 49)^2 = (a - b)^2 + (-24)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 576\n\nNow, with a = c and b = d, the distances squared are:\n\n1. (a - b)^2 + 400\n\n2. 484\n\n3. (a - b)^2 + 4\n\n4. (a - b)^2 + 4\n\n5. 484\n\n6. (a - b)^2 + 576\n\nSo, in this scenario, distances squared 2 and 5 are both 484, distances 3 and 4 are both (a - b)^2 + 4, distances 1, 6 are (a - b)^2 + 400 and (a - b)^2 + 576 respectively.\n\nIn a square, we need four sides of equal length and two diagonals of equal length. Here, we have two distances equal to 484, two distances equal to [(a - b)^2 + 4], and the remaining two distances are different unless (a - b)^2 + 400 equals something else.\n\nTo form a square, we need four sides equal and two diagonals equal. Let's see:\n\nSuppose that the sides are the distances 2, 5, 3, 4. That is, the four sides squared are 484, 484, [(a - b)^2 + 4], [(a - b)^2 + 4]. For these to be equal, we need 484 = (a - b)^2 + 4 ⇒ (a - b)^2 = 480 ⇒ a - b = ±√480 ≈ ±21.908.\n\nThen the sides squared would all be 484, and the other distances (1 and 6) would be:\n\nDistance 1 squared: 480 + 400 = 880\n\nDistance 6 squared: 480 + 576 = 1056\n\nNow, in a square, the diagonals should be equal and sqrt(2) times the side length. Here, the diagonals would need to be the larger distances, which are 1056 and 880. But 880 ≠ 1056, so that doesn't work. Therefore, this is not a square.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sides are the smaller distances: the two [(a - b)^2 + 4] and the two 484? But that would require [(a - b)^2 + 4] = 484 ⇒ (a - b)^2 = 480, as above, leading to the same problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sides are the 484 and the (a - b)^2 + 4, but then they need to be equal, which again requires (a - b)^2 + 4 = 484 ⇒ same as above.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the sides are the distances 1 and 6? No, those are longer.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this configuration cannot form a square unless we set a - b such that some of the distances become equal appropriately. Let's see.\n\nSuppose we want the four sides to be the four equal-length sides, say, the two [(a - b)^2 + 4] and the two [(a - b)^2 + 400]?\n\nWait, no, that would require [(a - b)^2 + 4] = [(a - b)^2 + 400], which implies 4 = 400, which is false.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the sides are the distances 3 and 4 (which are equal) and distances 1 and 6 (which are different). Doesn't help.\n\nHmm, this is getting too convoluted. Maybe this approach is not the best. Let me think differently.\n\nSince we have H = 63 from the first pairing, and even though the distance equations led to inconsistency, perhaps we can choose the x-coordinates such that some of the distances work out?\n\nLet me go back to H = 63, and see if I can assign x-coordinates such that the four points form a square.\n\nLet me consider that in a square, the sides are equal and the adjacent sides are perpendicular. So, perhaps we can set vectors between points to be perpendicular and of equal length.\n\nLet me suppose the four points are P(a, 47), Q(b, 27), R(c, 25), S(d, 49). We need to arrange these such that moving from one to the next involves a 90-degree turn and same length.\n\nAlternatively, pick one point as the origin temporarily and compute vectors.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to set coordinates such that point R is at the origin? Wait, but the coordinates are fixed in terms of H=63 and the y-coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps arrange the points so that two of them are on one horizontal line and two on another, but given their y-coordinates are all different except possibly if H is chosen differently.\n\nWait, but with H=63, the y-coordinates are 47, 27, 25, and 49. All distinct. So the square must be rotated.\n\nAlternatively, let me suppose that the square has its sides not aligned with the axes, and use rotation.\n\nLet me consider that the four points form a square, so the vectors between them must satisfy certain rotation conditions.\n\nSuppose we take point P(a, 47) and point Q(b, 27). The vector from P to Q is (b - a, 27 - 47) = (b - a, -20).\n\nThen, the next side of the square should be a 90-degree rotation of this vector. A 90-degree rotation of a vector (x, y) is (-y, x) or (y, -x), depending on clockwise or counterclockwise.\n\nAssuming the square is traversed in order P -> Q -> R -> S -> P, then the vector from Q to R should be the vector PQ rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise. Let's try that.\n\nRotating the vector PQ = (b - a, -20) 90 degrees counterclockwise gives (20, b - a). So the vector QR should be (20, b - a). Therefore, the coordinates of R would be Q + this vector: (b + 20, 27 + (b - a)).\n\nBut we know that point R has coordinates (c, 25). Therefore:\n\nc = b + 20\n\n25 = 27 + (b - a) ⇒ 25 = 27 + b - a ⇒ b - a = 25 - 27 = -2 ⇒ b = a - 2\n\nSo from this, we have:\n\nc = b + 20 = (a - 2) + 20 = a + 18\n\nAlso, from b = a - 2.\n\nNow, proceeding to the next side: the vector from R to S should be the vector QR rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise. The vector QR is (20, b - a) = (20, -2) [since b - a = -2].\n\nRotating (20, -2) 90 degrees counterclockwise gives (2, 20). Therefore, the vector RS should be (2, 20). Thus, point S is R + (2, 20) = (c + 2, 25 + 20) = (c + 2, 45). But point S is supposed to be at (d, 49). Therefore:\n\nd = c + 2\n\n49 = 45 ⇒ 49 = 45? That's a contradiction. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rotation was clockwise instead of counterclockwise. Let me try rotating the vector PQ 90 degrees clockwise.\n\nRotating vector PQ = (b - a, -20) 90 degrees clockwise gives ( -20, - (b - a) ). So the vector QR would be ( -20, - (b - a) ).\n\nThen, point R would be Q + this vector: (b - 20, 27 - (b - a)).\n\nBut point R is (c, 25), so:\n\nc = b - 20\n\n25 = 27 - (b - a) ⇒ 25 = 27 - b + a ⇒ a - b = 25 - 27 = -2 ⇒ a = b - 2\n\nThen, from c = b - 20, and a = b - 2.\n\nProceeding to the next side: vector RS should be the vector QR rotated 90 degrees clockwise.\n\nVector QR is (-20, - (b - a)) = (-20, - ( -2 )) = (-20, 2) [since b - a = -2]\n\nRotating this 90 degrees clockwise gives (2, 20). Wait, let's do the rotation properly.\n\nWait, rotating a vector (x, y) 90 degrees clockwise gives (x, y) → (x*cos(-90) - y*sin(-90), x*sin(-90) + y*cos(-90)) = (x*0 - y*(-1), x*(-1) + y*0) = (y, -x). So, rotating (-20, 2) 90 degrees clockwise would be (2, 20). Wait:\n\nWait, let me recall the rotation matrix:\n\nFor a 90-degree clockwise rotation matrix is:\n\n[ cos(-90)  -sin(-90) ]   = [ 0   1 ]\n[ sin(-90)   cos(-90) ]     [-1   0 ]\n\nSo, applying to vector (x, y):\n\nx' = x*0 + y*1 = y\n\ny' = x*(-1) + y*0 = -x\n\nTherefore, rotating (-20, 2) 90 degrees clockwise gives (2, 20).\n\nThus, vector RS is (2, 20). Therefore, point S is R + (2, 20) = (c + 2, 25 + 20) = (c + 2, 45). But point S is supposed to be at (d, 49). Therefore:\n\nd = c + 2\n\n49 = 45 ⇒ again, 49 = 45 is false. Same problem.\n\nHmm, so regardless of the rotation direction, we end up with a contradiction in the y-coordinate of point S. That suggests that assuming the order P -> Q -> R -> S is incorrect.\n\nPerhaps the order of the points around the square is different. Maybe it's P -> R -> Q -> S or some other permutation.\n\nLet me try a different order. Suppose the square is P -> R -> S -> Q -> P.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nStarting at P(a, 47). Let's define vector PR.\n\nVector PR is (c - a, 25 - 47) = (c - a, -22).\n\nThen, the next side from R to S should be a 90-degree rotation of PR.\n\nLet me try rotating PR 90 degrees counterclockwise to get the next vector RS.\n\nRotating PR = (c - a, -22) 90 degrees counterclockwise gives (22, c - a). So the vector RS would be (22, c - a). Therefore, point S is R + (22, c - a) = (c + 22, 25 + (c - a)).\n\nBut point S must be (d, 49). So:\n\nd = c + 22\n\n49 = 25 + (c - a) ⇒ c - a = 24 ⇒ c = a + 24\n\nAlso, from above, d = c + 22 = (a + 24) + 22 = a + 46\n\nNow, proceeding to the next side from S to Q. The vector SQ should be the previous vector RS rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n\nVector RS is (22, c - a) = (22, 24) [since c - a = 24]\n\nRotating RS 90 degrees counterclockwise gives (-24, 22). So vector SQ = (-24, 22). Therefore, point Q is S + (-24, 22) = (d - 24, 49 + 22) = (d - 24, 71). But point Q is supposed to be at (b, 27). Therefore:\n\nb = d - 24 = (a + 46) - 24 = a + 22\n\n27 = 71 ⇒ 27 = 71? That's a contradiction. Not good.\n\nAlternatively, rotating RS 90 degrees clockwise instead.\n\nRotating RS = (22, 24) 90 degrees clockwise gives (24, -22). So vector SQ = (24, -22). Then point Q is S + (24, -22) = (d + 24, 49 - 22) = (d + 24, 27). Which matches the y-coordinate of Q (27). So the x-coordinate must be b = d + 24.\n\nBut we had earlier:\n\nd = a + 46,\n\nso b = (a + 46) + 24 = a + 70\n\nAlso, from point Q's coordinates: Q is (b, 27), which is consistent with y-coordinate.\n\nNow, we also have from the vector SQ: the vector from S to Q is (24, -22), which should be equal to the rotation of RS. That part worked.\n\nNow, we need to close the loop back to P. The vector from Q to P should be the next rotation, but let's check.\n\nThe last side is from Q to P. Let's compute vector QP.\n\nPoint Q is (b, 27) = (a + 70, 27)\n\nPoint P is (a, 47)\n\nSo vector QP is (a - (a + 70), 47 - 27) = (-70, 20)\n\nThis vector should be the rotation of the previous vector SQ rotated 90 degrees. Let's see:\n\nVector SQ was (24, -22). Rotating that 90 degrees counterclockwise would give (22, 24). But vector QP is (-70, 20), which is not (22,24). Alternatively, rotating SQ 90 degrees clockwise would give (-22, -24). Not matching either.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rotation should be consistent around the square. Since we rotated each subsequent vector by 90 degrees clockwise, the last rotation should also be clockwise.\n\nWait, we rotated PR to get RS with a counterclockwise rotation? Wait, actually in this trial, I first rotated PR counterclockwise to get RS, but then when getting to SQ, I rotated RS clockwise. Maybe the rotation direction needs to be consistent.\n\nLet me retrace:\n\nIf we are going around the square in the order P -> R -> S -> Q -> P, then each turn should be a left turn (counterclockwise) or right turn (clockwise), but the rotation direction of the vectors should be consistent.\n\nLet me try again with consistent rotation direction.\n\nSuppose we traverse the square as P -> R -> S -> Q -> P, and at each step, we turn left (counterclockwise), so each subsequent vector is the previous one rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n\nStarting at P(a, 47):\n\nVector PR = (c - a, -22)\n\nRotated 90 CCW gives vector RS = (22, c - a) as before.\n\nThen vector RS is (22, c - a). Then next vector SQ should be RS rotated 90 CCW: which would be (-(c - a), 22). Wait:\n\nWait, rotating (22, c - a) 90 CCW: using the rotation formula:\n\nFor a vector (x, y), rotated 90 CCW is (-y, x). So:\n\nRS = (22, c - a) becomes (- (c - a), 22) for the next vector SQ.\n\nTherefore, vector SQ = (- (c - a), 22). Then, point Q is S + SQ.\n\nPoint S is at (c + 22, 25 + (c - a)) as before. Wait, actually let's recompute step by step.\n\nWait, let's re-express with consistent rotation.\n\nStarting at P(a,47):\n\nVector PR = (c - a, -22). Rotated 90 CCW gives vector RS = (22, c - a). Therefore, S = R + RS = (c + 22, 25 + (c - a)).\n\nThen vector RS is (22, c - a). Rotated 90 CCW gives vector SQ = (- (c - a), 22). Therefore, point Q = S + SQ = (c + 22 - (c - a), 25 + (c - a) + 22) = (c + 22 - c + a, 25 + c - a + 22) = (a + 22, c - a + 47).\n\nBut point Q must be (b, 27). Therefore:\n\nb = a + 22,\n\nand\n\n27 = c - a + 47 ⇒ c - a = 27 - 47 = -20 ⇒ c = a - 20\n\nAlso, from earlier, S's y-coordinate: 25 + (c - a) = 25 + (-20) = 5? Wait no:\n\nWait, S's y-coordinate is 25 + (c - a). Since c - a = -20, that gives 25 + (-20) = 5. But we were supposed to have point S at (d, 49). Wait, this is conflicting.\n\nHold on, there's a miscalculation here. Let me recast:\n\nPoint R is at (c, 25). Vector RS is (22, c - a). Therefore, the y-coordinate of S is 25 + (c - a). But we wanted S to be at (d, 49), so:\n\n25 + (c - a) = 49 ⇒ c - a = 24 ⇒ c = a + 24.\n\nWait, this contradicts the previous result from the Q y-coordinate.\n\nFrom the Q y-coordinate:\n\n27 = c - a + 47 ⇒ c - a = 27 - 47 = -20 ⇒ c = a - 20.\n\nBut from S's y-coordinate: c - a = 24 ⇒ c = a + 24.\n\nThese two equations:\n\nc = a - 20,\n\nc = a + 24,\n\nare contradictory. Therefore, this path also leads to inconsistency.\n\nHmm, this is frustrating. Maybe the order of the square's vertices is completely different?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the square is arranged such that P and S are opposite vertices, and Q and R are the other pair. Let me try that.\n\nLet me suppose the diagonally opposite pairs are P-S and Q-R.\n\nThen, the midpoints must coincide.\n\nMidpoint of P(a,47) and S(d,49) is ((a + d)/2, (47 + 49)/2) = ((a + d)/2, 48)\n\nMidpoint of Q(b,27) and R(c,25) is ((b + c)/2, (27 + 25)/2) = ((b + c)/2, 26)\n\nSetting these equal:\n\n(a + d)/2 = (b + c)/2 ⇒ a + d = b + c,\n\nand\n\n48 = 26 ⇒ 48 = 26, which is false. So that doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, diagonally opposite pairs P-Q and R-S we already tried.\n\nHmm.\n\nPerhaps the issue is that with H = 63, it's impossible to form a square with those y-coordinates, so maybe H is not 63? But we got H = 63 from the midpoint condition when pairing P-Q and R-S as diagonals. Maybe that pairing is invalid because it requires H=63 but then the distances can't form a square, so we have to try a different pairing that doesn't force H=63?\n\nWait, let's try another pairing where the midpoint condition doesn't force a contradiction.\n\nSuppose we pair P with R and Q with S as diagonals. Then their midpoints must coincide.\n\nMidpoint of P(a, H - 16) and R(c, 25): [(a + c)/2, (H - 16 + 25)/2] = [(a + c)/2, (H + 9)/2]\n\nMidpoint of Q(b, H - 36) and S(d, 49): [(b + d)/2, (H - 36 + 49)/2] = [(b + d)/2, (H + 13)/2]\n\nSetting these equal:\n\n(a + c)/2 = (b + d)/2 ⇒ a + c = b + d,\n\nand\n\n(H + 9)/2 = (H + 13)/2 ⇒ H + 9 = H + 13 ⇒ 9 = 13, which is impossible. So that pairing is invalid.\n\nNext, pairing P with S and Q with R as diagonals:\n\nMidpoint of P(a, H -16) and S(d, 49): [(a + d)/2, (H -16 + 49)/2] = [(a + d)/2, (H + 33)/2]\n\nMidpoint of Q(b, H - 36) and R(c, 25): [(b + c)/2, (H - 36 + 25)/2] = [(b + c)/2, (H - 11)/2]\n\nSetting equal:\n\n(a + d)/2 = (b + c)/2 ⇒ a + d = b + c,\n\nand\n\n(H + 33)/2 = (H - 11)/2 ⇒ H + 33 = H - 11 ⇒ 33 = -11, which is false.\n\nHmm. The only pairing that didn't give an immediate contradiction was the first one, pairing P-Q and R-S as diagonals, which gave H=63 but led to inconsistency in the distances. Since all other pairings give contradictions in the y-coordinate midpoints, perhaps that is the only possible pairing, and the inconsistency in distances must be resolved by choosing appropriate x-coordinates?\n\nEven though when I tried to enforce the rotation of vectors, I got contradictions, maybe there's a specific choice of x-coordinates that allows the square to exist?\n\nLet me try to set variables again with H=63 and see if I can find x-coordinates a, b, c, d that satisfy the square conditions.\n\nLet me denote the four points as:\n\nP: (a, 47)\n\nQ: (b, 27)\n\nR: (c, 25)\n\nS: (d, 49)\n\nWe need these four points to form a square. Let's consider all six pairwise distances and set up equations so that four distances are equal (the sides) and two are equal and larger (the diagonals), with the diagonal squared equal to twice the side squared.\n\nLet me denote the squared distances as follows:\n\nPQ² = (a - b)² + (47 - 27)² = (a - b)² + 400\n\nPR² = (a - c)² + (47 - 25)² = (a - c)² + 484\n\nPS² = (a - d)² + (47 - 49)² = (a - d)² + 4\n\nQR² = (b - c)² + (27 - 25)² = (b - c)² + 4\n\nQS² = (b - d)² + (27 - 49)² = (b - d)² + 484\n\nRS² = (c - d)² + (25 - 49)² = (c - d)² + 576\n\nWe need four of these to be equal to some value S², and two to be equal to 2S².\n\nLooking at the expressions, the smallest possible squared distance is 4 (from PS and QR), then 400 (PQ), then 484 (PR and QS), then 576 + ... (RS), and others.\n\nSuppose that the sides squared are 484, so S² = 484, then the diagonals squared must be 968.\n\nLooking at the distances:\n\nPR² and QS² are already 484, so if those are sides, then the other sides must also be 484. The other distances PQ², PS², QR², RS² need to be either 484 or 968.\n\nPS² = (a - d)^2 + 4 = 484 ⇒ (a - d)^2 = 480 ⇒ a - d = ±√480\n\nQR² = (b - c)^2 + 4 = 484 ⇒ (b - c)^2 = 480 ⇒ b - c = ±√480\n\nPQ² = (a - b)^2 + 400 = either 484 or 968\n\nIf PQ² = 484 ⇒ (a - b)^2 = 84 ⇒ a - b = ±√84\n\nOr if PQ² = 968 ⇒ (a - b)^2 = 568 ⇒ a - b = ±√568\n\nSimilarly, RS² = (c - d)^2 + 576 = either 484 or 968\n\nIf RS² = 484 ⇒ (c - d)^2 = -92 ⇒ impossible\n\nIf RS² = 968 ⇒ (c - d)^2 = 392 ⇒ c - d = ±√392\n\nNow, let's try to assume that PQ² and RS² are the diagonals (so 968), and the other four distances are sides (484).\n\nSo:\n\nSet PQ² = 968 ⇒ (a - b)^2 + 400 = 968 ⇒ (a - b)^2 = 568 ⇒ a - b = ±√568\n\nSet RS² = 968 ⇒ (c - d)^2 + 576 = 968 ⇒ (c - d)^2 = 392 ⇒ c - d = ±√392\n\nThe other distances:\n\nPR² = 484 (already sides)\n\nQS² = 484 (already sides)\n\nPS² = 484 ⇒ (a - d)^2 + 4 = 484 ⇒ (a - d)^2 = 480 ⇒ a - d = ±√480\n\nQR² = 484 ⇒ (b - c)^2 + 4 = 484 ⇒ (b - c)^2 = 480 ⇒ b - c = ±√480\n\nNow, we have several equations:\n\n1. a - b = ±√568\n\n2. c - d = ±√392\n\n3. a - d = ±√480\n\n4. b - c = ±√480\n\nAlso, we have the midpoint condition from earlier when we paired P-Q and R-S as diagonals, which gave H = 63, and we didn't use the x-coordinate relationships yet. Wait, actually, the midpoint condition was already used to get H = 63, but the x-coordinate conditions are separate. However, we might need to also satisfy the midpoint condition for the diagonals.\n\nWait, if we're considering P-Q and R-S as diagonals, then their midpoints must coincide. The midpoint of P(a, 47) and Q(b, 27) is ((a + b)/2, 37). The midpoint of R(c, 25) and S(d, 49) is ((c + d)/2, 37). Therefore, the x-coordinates must satisfy (a + b)/2 = (c + d)/2 ⇒ a + b = c + d.\n\nSo that's another equation:\n\n5. a + b = c + d\n\nNow, let's see if we can solve these equations.\n\nLet me denote some variables:\n\nLet me let’s set:\n\nLet’s define:\n\nLet’s let’s set a - b = √568 (positive for now; we can adjust signs later)\n\nSimilarly, c - d = √392\n\nAlso, a - d = √480\n\nAnd b - c = √480 ⇒ c = b - √480\n\nFrom equation 5: a + b = c + d\n\nSubstitute c = b - √480 and d = c - √392 = (b - √480) - √392\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, c - d = √392 ⇒ d = c - √392\n\nSo, substituting c = b - √480,\n\nd = (b - √480) - √392\n\nThen, plug into equation 5:\n\na + b = c + d = [b - √480] + [b - √480 - √392] = 2b - 2√480 - √392\n\nBut a = b + √568 (from a - b = √568)\n\nTherefore:\n\nLeft side: a + b = (b + √568) + b = 2b + √568\n\nRight side: 2b - 2√480 - √392\n\nSetting equal:\n\n2b + √568 = 2b - 2√480 - √392\n\nSubtract 2b from both sides:\n\n√568 = -2√480 - √392\n\nBut the left side is positive, and the right side is negative (since all terms are positive and there's a negative sign). Contradiction. So this sign choice is invalid.\n\nLet me try different sign combinations.\n\nSuppose:\n\na - b = √568 (positive)\n\nc - d = -√392 (so d = c + √392)\n\na - d = √480\n\nb - c = -√480 ⇒ c = b + √480\n\nThen, equation 5: a + b = c + d\n\nExpress c and d in terms of b:\n\nc = b + √480\n\nd = c + √392 = b + √480 + √392\n\nThen,\n\nLeft side: a + b = (b + √568) + b = 2b + √568\n\nRight side: c + d = [b + √480] + [b + √480 + √392] = 2b + 2√480 + √392\n\nSet equal:\n\n2b + √568 = 2b + 2√480 + √392\n\nSubtract 2b:\n\n√568 = 2√480 + √392\n\nLet me compute approximate values:\n\n√568 ≈ 23.832\n\n√480 ≈ 21.908\n\n2√480 ≈ 43.816\n\n√392 ≈ 19.798\n\nThen, 2√480 + √392 ≈ 43.816 + 19.798 ≈ 63.614\n\nBut √568 ≈ 23.832 ≈ not equal to 63.614. Not equal. So this is invalid.\n\nHmm, try other sign combinations.\n\nLet me try:\n\na - b = -√568 ⇒ a = b - √568\n\nc - d = √392 ⇒ d = c - √392\n\na - d = √480 ⇒ (b - √568) - d = √480 ⇒ d = b - √568 - √480\n\nBut d = c - √392 ⇒ c = d + √392 = [b - √568 - √480] + √392\n\nAlso, from QR²: b - c = ±√480. Let's see:\n\nc = b - √568 - √480 + √392\n\nThus,\n\nb - c = b - [b - √568 - √480 + √392] = √568 + √480 - √392\n\nSet this equal to ±√480:\n\nCase 1: b - c = √480 ⇒ √568 + √480 - √392 = √480 ⇒ √568 - √392 = 0 ⇒ √568 = √392 ⇒ 568 = 392 ⇒ false.\n\nCase 2: b - c = -√480 ⇒ √568 + √480 - √392 = -√480 ⇒ √568 - √392 = -2√480 ⇒ which is also false numerically:\n\n√568 ≈23.832, √392≈19.798, so LHS ≈23.832 - 19.798≈4.034\n\nRHS≈-2*21.908≈-43.816. Not equal.\n\nHmm. Another combination:\n\nLet me try a - b = √568,\n\nc - d = -√392 ⇒ d = c + √392,\n\na - d = -√480 ⇒ a = d - √480,\n\nand b - c = √480 ⇒ c = b - √480.\n\nThen equation 5: a + b = c + d.\n\nExpress a, c, d in terms of b:\n\nc = b - √480,\n\nd = c + √392 = b - √480 + √392,\n\na = d - √480 = [b - √480 + √392] - √480 = b - 2√480 + √392,\n\nThen,\n\nLeft side: a + b = [b - 2√480 + √392] + b = 2b - 2√480 + √392,\n\nRight side: c + d = [b - √480] + [b - √480 + √392] = 2b - 2√480 + √392,\n\nSo they are equal! Okay, this works.\n\nSo this combination works with the signs:\n\na - b = √568,\n\nc - d = -√392,\n\na - d = -√480,\n\nb - c = √480,\n\nand equation 5 is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, this is a valid set of equations.\n\nNow, let's see if we can find numerical values or at least verify consistency.\n\nLet me assign a variable to b, say let’s let b = 0 for simplicity (since we can shift all x-coordinates by a constant without affecting distances or the shape of the square). Wait, but actually, the positions are absolute in the cave, so shifting might not preserve the square's position, but since we're only concerned with the existence of such x-coordinates, we can set one variable to zero for simplicity.\n\nLet me set b = 0.\n\nThen:\n\nc = b - √480 = -√480 ≈ -21.908\n\nd = c + √392 = -√480 + √392 ≈ -21.908 + 19.798 ≈ -2.110\n\na = d - √480 = (-2.110) - 21.908 ≈ -24.018\n\nNow, let's compute all the points:\n\nP(a, 47) ≈ (-24.018, 47)\n\nQ(b, 27) = (0, 27)\n\nR(c, 25) ≈ (-21.908, 25)\n\nS(d, 49) ≈ (-2.110, 49)\n\nNow, let's compute the distances between these points to see if they form a square.\n\nFirst, compute PQ distance squared:\n\nP(-24.018,47) to Q(0,27):\n\nΔx = 24.018, Δy = -20\n\nDistance squared: (24.018)^2 + (-20)^2 ≈ (576.86) + 400 ≈ 976.86\n\nWhich should be 968 if we followed the earlier assumption, but it's approximately 976.86. Hmm, close but not exact, likely due to rounding errors in the approximate square roots. Let me compute more precisely.\n\nLet me use exact expressions.\n\nLet me keep variables symbolic for a moment.\n\nWe have:\n\na = b - √568,\n\nc = b - √480,\n\nd = c + √392 = b - √480 + √392,\n\nand a = d - √480 = (b - √480 + √392) - √480 = b - 2√480 + √392.\n\nBut since we set b = 0,\n\na = -2√480 + √392,\n\nc = -√480,\n\nd = -√480 + √392,\n\nNow, compute PQ squared:\n\nP(a,47) to Q(0,27):\n\nΔx = -a - 0 = -a = 2√480 - √392,\n\nΔy = 27 - 47 = -20,\n\nSo squared distance:\n\n(2√480 - √392)^2 + (-20)^2.\n\nExpand the first term:\n\n= [ (2√480)^2 - 2*(2√480)*(√392) + (√392)^2 ] + 400\n\n= [4*480 - 4√(480*392) + 392] + 400\n\nCompute each part:\n\n4*480 = 1920,\n\n4√(480*392): compute 480*392:\n\n480*392 = 480*(400 - 8) = 480*400 - 480*8 = 192,000 - 3,840 = 188,160,\n\nso √(188,160). Let me see:\n\n√(188,160) = √(16 * 11,760) = 4√11,760. Hmm, maybe factor more:\n\n11,760 = 16 * 735 = 16 * 49 * 15 = 16 * 49 * 15,\n\nso √(11,760) = 4 * 7 * √15 = 28√15,\n\nThus, √(188,160) = 4 * 28√15 = 112√15,\n\nTherefore,\n\n4√(480*392) = 4*(112√15) = 448√15,\n\nAnd (√392)^2 = 392.\n\nTherefore,\n\nThe first term becomes:\n\n1920 - 448√15 + 392 = (1920 + 392) - 448√15 = 2312 - 448√15,\n\nThen add the 400 from the y-component:\n\nTotal PQ squared distance = (2312 - 448√15) + 400 = 2712 - 448√15.\n\nHmm, not sure if this is exactly 968. Let me compute numerically:\n\n√15 ≈ 3.87298,\n\n448√15 ≈ 448 * 3.87298 ≈ 448 * 3.873 ≈ 448*3 + 448*0.873 ≈ 1344 + 391.344 ≈ 1735.344,\n\nThus,\n\n2712 - 1735.344 ≈ 2712 - 1735.344 ≈ 976.656,\n\nWhich is approximately 976.66, whereas 968 is the expected diagonal squared if sides were 484. But this is larger. Hmm.\n\nWait a second, perhaps my assumption that the sides are 484 is incorrect? Because in this case, PQ squared is approximately 976.66, which is the supposed diagonal squared (if sides were 484). Let's check another distance.\n\nCompute PR squared:\n\nP(a,47) to R(c,25):\n\nΔx = c - a = [ -√480 ] - [ -2√480 + √392 ] = (-√480) + 2√480 - √392 = √480 - √392,\n\nΔy = 25 - 47 = -22,\n\nSo squared distance:\n\n(√480 - √392)^2 + (-22)^2,\n\nExpand the first term:\n\n(√480)^2 - 2√480√392 + (√392)^2 + 484,\n\n= 480 - 2√(480*392) + 392 + 484,\n\n= (480 + 392 + 484) - 2√(188,160),\n\nCompute constants: 480 + 392 = 872; 872 + 484 = 1356,\n\nThus,\n\n1356 - 2*(448√15) [since √(480*392)=112√15, so 2* that is 224√15? Wait wait:\n\nWait, √(480*392) = √(188,160) = 112√15 as above,\n\nThus, 2*√(480*392) = 2*(112√15) = 224√15,\n\nTherefore,\n\nPR squared distance = 1356 - 224√15 + 484? Wait no:\n\nWait, wait, no:\n\nWait, the expansion was:\n\n(√480 - √392)^2 + (-22)^2 = [480 - 2√(480*392) + 392] + 484,\n\nSo that is 480 + 392 + 484 - 2√(480*392),\n\nWhich is indeed 1356 - 2*√(188,160) = 1356 - 2*(112√15) = 1356 - 224√15.\n\nNumerically:\n\n224√15 ≈ 224 * 3.87298 ≈ 224*3 + 224*0.87298 ≈ 672 + 195.5 ≈ 867.5,\n\nThus,\n\nPR squared ≈ 1356 - 867.5 ≈ 488.5,\n\nWhich is approximately 488.5, close to 484, but not exact. Hmm, due to the approximated values.\n\nSimilarly, let's check QS squared:\n\nQ(0,27) to S(d,49):\n\nΔx = d - 0 = d = -√480 + √392 ≈ -21.908 + 19.798 ≈ -2.11,\n\nΔy = 49 - 27 = 22,\n\nSo squared distance:\n\n(-√480 + √392)^2 + (22)^2,\n\nWhich is the same as PR squared, since it's symmetric:\n\n(√392 - √480)^2 + 22² = same as (√480 - √392)^2 + 484,\n\nThus, same as PR squared, so also ≈488.5.\n\nSimilarly, PS squared:\n\nP(a,47) to S(d,49):\n\nΔx = d - a = [ -√480 + √392 ] - [ -2√480 + √392 ] = (-√480 + √392) + 2√480 - √392 = √480,\n\nΔy = 49 - 47 = 2,\n\nSo squared distance: (√480)^2 + (2)^2 = 480 + 4 = 484. Exactly!\n\nAh! So PS squared is exactly 484.\n\nSimilarly, QR squared:\n\nQ(0,27) to R(c,25):\n\nΔx = c - 0 = c = -√480 ≈-21.908,\n\nΔy = 25 - 27 = -2,\n\nSquared distance: ( -√480 )² + (-2)^2 = 480 + 4 = 484. Exactly!\n\nSo PS and QR are exactly 484, while PR and QS are approximately 488.5, and PQ and RS are approximately 976.66.\n\nWait, but in this case, we have four sides of 484 (PS, QR, and then maybe others?), but PR and QS are not 484, they're longer. Hmm, but according to the earlier setup, we set PS and QR as sides (484), and PQ and RS as diagonals (which should be 2*484=968). But in reality, PQ squared is approximately 976.66, which is close to 968 but not exact, and PR squared is about 488.5, which is close to 484 but not exact. The discrepancies are due to rounding errors in the approximate values of the square roots.\n\nHowever, symbolically, let's see:\n\nWe set the sides to be PS and QR as 484, and the diagonals as PQ and RS.\n\nWait, but in our symbolic calculation, PS squared is exactly 484, QR squared is exactly 484,\n\nPR squared is 1356 - 224√15,\n\nand PQ squared is 2712 - 448√15,\n\nRS squared would be the same as PQ squared because of symmetry?\n\nWait, let me compute RS squared:\n\nR(c,25) to S(d,49):\n\nΔx = d - c = [ -√480 + √392 ] - [ -√480 ] = √392,\n\nΔy = 49 - 25 = 24,\n\nThus, squared distance:\n\n(√392)^2 + (24)^2 = 392 + 576 = 968. Exactly!\n\nAh! So RS squared is exactly 968,\n\nSimilarly, PQ squared was:\n\n(2√480 - √392)^2 + (-20)^2,\n\nBut let me compute it symbolically:\n\nLet me compute PQ squared:\n\nΔx = a - b = a - 0 = a = -2√480 + √392,\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, P is at (a,47), Q is at (0,27),\n\nSo Δx = 0 - a = -a = 2√480 - √392,\n\nΔy = 27 - 47 = -20,\n\nThus, squared distance:\n\n(2√480 - √392)^2 + (-20)^2,\n\nExpand the first term:\n\n(2√480)^2 - 2*(2√480)(√392) + (√392)^2 + 400,\n\nWhich is 4*480 - 4√(480*392) + 392 + 400,\n\nWhich is 1920 - 4√(188,160) + 792,\n\nWait, 392 + 400 = 792,\n\nThus total: 1920 + 792 - 4√(188,160) = 2712 - 4*(112√15) = 2712 - 448√15,\n\nWhich is the same as before.\n\nBut RS squared is exactly 968,\n\nSo RS squared is 968, which is supposed to be the diagonal squared (2*484). So that works.\n\nPQ squared is 2712 - 448√15. Let me see if this equals 2*484 = 968?\n\nWait, 2*484 is 968, but 2712 - 448√15 ≈ 2712 - 448*3.87298 ≈ 2712 - 1735 ≈ 976, which is close but not exactly 968. Hmm. Wait a second, perhaps I made a miscalculation here.\n\nWait, let me compute 448√15 precisely:\n\n√15 ≈ 3.872983346,\n\n448 * 3.872983346 ≈ 448 * 3.873 ≈ let's compute 400*3.873 = 1549.2, and 48*3.873 ≈ 185.904, so total ≈ 1549.2 + 185.904 ≈ 1735.104,\n\nThus,\n\n2712 - 1735.104 ≈ 976.896,\n\nSo approximately 976.9, which is not exactly 968. Hmm.\n\nWait, but RS squared is exactly 968. So PQ squared should also be 968 if it's the other diagonal. But it's not. What's going on?\n\nWait, perhaps my assumption that both diagonals are PQ and RS is incorrect? Let's see.\n\nWait, in this configuration, the four points are P, Q, R, S with the following distances:\n\nPS: 484 (side),\n\nQR: 484 (side),\n\nPR: approx 488.5,\n\nQS: approx 488.5,\n\nPQ: approx 976.9,\n\nRS: 968,\n\nWait, so RS is exactly 968, which is 2*484, so that's good. PQ is approx 976.9, which is more than 968. Hmm, so maybe RS and PQ are not both diagonals?\n\nWait, in a square, there are two diagonals of equal length. Here, RS is 968, and PQ is approx 977, which are nearly equal but not exactly. The discrepancy is due to the symbolic expressions not simplifying to exact values, but perhaps with exact values they are equal?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nWe have RS squared = 968 exactly,\n\nPQ squared = 2712 - 448√15,\n\nWe need to see if 2712 - 448√15 equals 968?\n\nLet me compute 2712 - 968 = 1744,\n\nSo, if 448√15 = 1744,\n\nthen √15 = 1744 / 448 ≈ 3.888...\n\nBut √15 ≈ 3.87298, so close but not exact. Therefore, it's not exactly equal. Therefore, this suggests that even with exact values, PQ squared is not exactly equal to RS squared, meaning that this configuration does not form a perfect square, only approximately.\n\nHmm, this is perplexing. Maybe the problem requires that the four points form a square regardless of the x-coordinate positions, meaning that such x-coordinates must exist, so the equations must have a solution, implying that the discrepancies I see are due to my approximations, and symbolically, with exact radicals, they might actually be equal?\n\nLet me see:\n\nWe have RS squared = 968,\n\nAnd PQ squared = 2712 - 448√15,\n\nWe want to see if 2712 - 448√15 equals 968?\n\nLet me compute 2712 - 968 = 1744,\n\nSo, is 448√15 = 1744?\n\nDivide both sides by 448:\n\n√15 = 1744 / 448 = 1744 ÷ 448.\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 16: 1744 ÷16=109, 448 ÷16=28,\n\nSo 109/28 ≈ 3.892857,\n\nBut √15 ≈3.87298, so not equal. Therefore, symbolically, they are not equal, meaning that with the chosen sign combinations, the figure is not a square. Hmm.\n\nPerhaps another set of sign choices?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the side length squared is not 484 but something else?\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that the four sides are PR, QS, PS, and QR, which are all approximately 488.5 or exactly 484 and 488.5. Hmm, not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the square is not aligned such that PS and QR are sides, but some other combination.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my entire approach is wrong, and I need to consider that the vertical distances must form part of the square's geometry.\n\nLet me think differently. Suppose the four points form a square. In a square, the vertical distances between the points must correspond to the vertical components of the sides and diagonals.\n\nLet me denote the four y-coordinates: 47, 27, 25, 49.\n\nThese must be arranged such that the differences in y between adjacent vertices correspond to the vertical components of the sides of the square.\n\nIn a square, the sides have the same length, say s, and the vertical component of each side can be s*sin(theta), where theta is the angle between the side and the horizontal axis.\n\nSimilarly, the vertical difference between two adjacent vertices would be s*sin(theta), and the horizontal difference would be s*cos(theta).\n\nSince the square can be rotated, the vertical differences between the points must satisfy that they are all either s*sin(theta) or -s*sin(theta), and similarly for horizontal differences.\n\nBut since we have four different y-coordinates (assuming H=63), the vertical differences between the points must be such that they can be expressed as multiples of sin(theta) times the side length.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the total vertical span of the square must be equal to the side length times sqrt(2) times sin(theta + 45°), but this might be too vague.\n\nAlternatively, the maximum and minimum y-coordinates in the square must differ by the diagonal's vertical component.\n\nThe maximum y-coordinate is 47 (from point P), and the minimum is 25 (from point R). So the total vertical span is 47 - 25 = 22.\n\nIn a square, the maximum vertical distance between any two vertices is the vertical component of the diagonal, which is s*sin(theta + 45°)*sqrt(2) ?\n\nWait, the vertical component of the diagonal of a square rotated by theta would be s*cos(theta) * sqrt(2), since the diagonal vector has components s*cos(theta) and s*sin(theta) rotated by 45 degrees?\n\nHmm, perhaps this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, the vertical span between the highest and lowest points in the square must be equal to s * |sin(theta + 45°)| * sqrt(2), but I might be overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, the vertical distance between the highest and lowest points in the square must be equal to the side length times sin(theta) * 2, if the square is oriented such that its sides are at an angle theta from the vertical.\n\nWait, perhaps the vertical distance between the highest and lowest points is equal to the side length times sin(theta), where theta is the angle between the side and the horizontal, multiplied by 2 (since it spans two sides). Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, in a square, the maximum vertical distance between any two vertices is the length of the vertical diagonal component, which would be s * sin(theta + 90°), depending on the rotation.\n\nThis is getting too vague. Maybe I should instead consider that the four y-coordinates must satisfy that the differences between them can form the necessary components of the square's sides and diagonals.\n\nThe four y-coordinates are 25, 27, 47, 49.\n\nThe differences between them are:\n\nBetween 25 and 27: 2\n\nBetween 25 and 47: 22\n\nBetween 25 and 49: 24\n\nBetween 27 and 47: 20\n\nBetween 27 and 49: 22\n\nBetween 47 and 49: 2\n\nSo the vertical differences are: 2, 22, 24, 20, 22, 2.\n\nIn a square, the vertical differences between adjacent vertices should be equal in magnitude but could alternate in sign, and the horizontal differences should complement them to form the side length.\n\nHowever, since we don't know the horizontal differences, this is still tricky.\n\nBut perhaps the vertical differences must consist of pairs that are equal and correspond to the vertical components of the sides and diagonals.\n\nIn a square, the vertical component of the sides should be equal in magnitude (since all sides are equal and the square is closed), and the vertical components of the diagonals should be equal to the sum or difference of the side components.\n\nWait, let me consider that in a square, if the sides have a vertical component of v and horizontal component of h, then the diagonals would have vertical components of v + h and v - h or something?\n\nActually, if a side has a vertical component of v and horizontal component of h, then the next side (perpendicular) would have a vertical component of -h and horizontal component of v, assuming a counterclockwise turn. Then the diagonal from the start to the end after two sides would have a vertical component of v - h and horizontal component of h + v.\n\nWait, perhaps better to use vectors.\n\nLet me suppose that the square has side vectors **u** and **v**, which are perpendicular and of equal length.\n\nLet **u** = (a, b),\n\nthen **v** = (-b, a) to be perpendicular and same length.\n\nThen the four vertices can be represented as **O**, **O**+**u**, **O**+**u**+**v**, **O**+**v**.\n\nThe vertical components (y-coordinates) of these points would be:\n\nStart: y0,\n\nAfter **u**: y0 + b,\n\nAfter **u** + **v**: y0 + b + a,\n\nAfter **v**: y0 + a,\n\nSo the vertical coordinates are y0, y0 + b, y0 + a + b, y0 + a.\n\nThe differences between these y-coordinates are:\n\nBetween first and second: b,\n\nBetween second and third: a,\n\nBetween third and fourth: -b,\n\nBetween fourth and first: a - b,\n\nWait, this is getting messy. Alternatively, the vertical distances between the highest and lowest points would be |(y0 + a + b) - y0| = |a + b| or something?\n\nAlternatively, the maximum vertical difference between any two points is |(y0 + a + b) - y0| = |a + b|,\n\nand the minimum is |(y0 + a) - (y0 + b)| = |a - b|,\n\nBut I need to relate this to our specific y-coordinates: 25, 27, 47, 49.\n\nThe total vertical span from 25 to 49 is 24. So if this is the maximum vertical difference, then |a + b| = 24,\n\nand the next largest difference is 22 (from 25 to 47, or 27 to 49). Maybe that's |a| or |b|?\n\nAlternatively, suppose that the vertical differences correspond to the components of the sides.\n\nSuppose that the side vectors have vertical components of 2 and 20, for example, but this is just guessing.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the vertical distances between the points include 2, 20, 22, 24. Maybe these can be paired as sides and diagonals.\n\