 coordinates of P be (x, y), then the areas of the four triangles are as follows:\n\nArea of triangle PAB = (1/2)*s*y,\n\nArea of triangle PBC = (1/2)*s*(s - x),\n\nArea of triangle PCD = (1/2)*s*(s - y),\n\nArea of triangle PDA = (1/2)*s*x.\n\nTherefore, if I denote these four areas as follows:\n\nLet’s say:\n\nLet’s define:\n\nArea_PAB = (1/2)s y,\n\nArea_PBC = (1/2)s (s - x),\n\nArea_PCD = (1/2)s (s - y),\n\nArea_PDA = (1/2)s x.\n\nNow, according to the problem, these four areas are 1, 2, 3, and 4 in some order. So, the four areas must be exactly the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, each appearing once. Therefore, the four expressions above must equal some permutation of 1, 2, 3, 4. So, if I let’s assign variables to make this clearer.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s let’s call:\n\nA1 = Area_PAB = (1/2)s y,\n\nA2 = Area_PBC = (1/2)s (s - x),\n\nA3 = Area_PCD = (1/2)s (s - y),\n\nA4 = Area_PDA = (1/2)s x.\n\nThen the problem states that {A1, A2, A3, A4} = {1, 2, 3, 4}, as sets. So, the four areas are 1, 2, 3, 4 in some order, but we don't know which is which. The question is to compute PA · PB · PC · PD.\n\nHmm, okay. So, first, perhaps we can find s, the side length of the square, and the coordinates (x, y) of point P? Because once we have coordinates (x, y) and s, we can compute the distances PA, PB, PC, PD, and then take their product.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can find PA · PB · PC · PD in terms of s, x, y, or in terms of the areas?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is some relation between the areas that allows us to solve for s, x, and y without knowing which area corresponds to which triangle?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can set up equations based on the areas and solve for x, y, and s?\n\nLet me try that. Let's consider that the four areas A1, A2, A3, A4 are 1, 2, 3, 4 in some order, so we can write equations:\n\n(1/2)s y = a,\n\n(1/2)s (s - x) = b,\n\n(1/2)s (s - y) = c,\n\n(1/2)s x = d,\n\nwhere {a, b, c, d} = {1, 2, 3, 4}.\n\nBut since we don't know which area corresponds 