till less than sqrt(10). Wait, sqrt(10) is about 3.16, so 8/sqrt(10) is approximately 2.53, which is okay. Wait, but let me compute it exactly:\n\nWait, sqrt(10) is irrational, but let me just see:\n\nWait, but if y = 8/sqrt(10), then let's check A3:\n\nA3 = (sqrt(10)/2)*(sqrt(10) - y) = (sqrt(10)/2)*(sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10)).\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10) = (10/sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10)) = 2/sqrt(10).\n\nTherefore, A3 = (sqrt(10)/2)*(2/sqrt(10)) = (sqrt(10)*2)/(2*sqrt(10)) = 1. Perfect, so that works.\n\nThen, moving on to A2 and A4:\n\nA2 = (sqrt(10)/2)*(sqrt(10) - x) = 2,\n\nSo same as before,\n\nsqrt(10) - x = (2 * 2)/sqrt(10) = 4/sqrt(10),\n\nso x = sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10) = 6/sqrt(10), same as before.\n\nThen A4 = (sqrt(10)/2)*x = (sqrt(10)/2)*(6/sqrt(10)) = 3, which is consistent with A4=3.\n\nSo in this case, coordinates of P are (x, y) = (6/sqrt(10), 8/sqrt(10)).\n\nWait a second, so in this case, the y-coordinate is 8/sqrt(10). Let's check if that is less than sqrt(10):\n\n8/sqrt(10) is approximately 8/3.16 ≈ 2.53, which is less than sqrt(10) ≈ 3.16, so that's okay. So this is also a valid point inside the square.\n\nHmm, so in this case, we have a different y-coordinate. So depending on how we assign the areas to the triangles, we can get different coordinates for P. Therefore, there are multiple possibilities? Wait, but the problem says \"Point P lies inside square ABCD such that the areas of triangles PAB, PBC, PCD, and PDA are 1, 2, 3, and 4, in some order.\" So it's possible that there are multiple points P satisfying the area conditions, but the problem is asking for PA · PB · PC · PD, which might be the same in all cases? Or perhaps only one case is possible? Hmm.\n\nWait, let's check another case. Let me try Case 3:\n\nCase 3:\n\nA1 = 1,\n\nA3 = 4,\n\nA2 = 3,\n\nA4 = 2.\n\nSo, similar to the first case but swapping A2 and A4.\n\nSo:\n\nA1 = (sqrt(10)/2)*y = 1 ⇒ y = 2/sqrt(10),\n\nA3 = (sqrt(10)/2)*(sqrt(10) - y) = 4 ⇒ same as before, leading to the same