 (s, 0) = (sqrt(10), 0),\n\nC: (sqrt(10), sqrt(10)),\n\nD: (0, sqrt(10)).\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the four vertices are as above.\n\nLet me first compute PA · PB · PC · PD for Case 1: P = (6/sqrt(10), 2/sqrt(10)).\n\nFirst, compute PA: distance from P to A (0,0):\n\nPA = sqrt( (6/sqrt(10) - 0)^2 + (2/sqrt(10) - 0)^2 )\n\n= sqrt( (36/10) + (4/10) ) = sqrt(40/10) = sqrt(4) = 2.\n\nWait, that's nice. Let me verify:\n\nCompute (6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\n(2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10,\n\nSum: 36/10 + 4/10 = 40/10 = 4,\n\nsqrt(4) = 2. Correct.\n\nThen PB: distance from P to B (sqrt(10), 0):\n\nPB = sqrt( (sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10))² + (0 - 2/sqrt(10))² )\n\nFirst, compute sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10):\n\nsqrt(10) = (10)/sqrt(10), so sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10) = (10 - 6)/sqrt(10) = 4/sqrt(10).\n\nTherefore, the x-component squared: (4/sqrt(10))² = 16/10.\n\nThe y-component squared: (-2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10.\n\nTherefore, PB = sqrt(16/10 + 4/10) = sqrt(20/10) = sqrt(2) ≈ 1.414.\n\nWait, sqrt(20/10) = sqrt(2). Hmm, okay.\n\nThen PC: distance from P to C (sqrt(10), sqrt(10)):\n\nPC = sqrt( (sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10) = same as above, 4/sqrt(10),\n\nsqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10) = (10/sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10)) = 8/sqrt(10),\n\nTherefore, squared terms:\n\n(4/sqrt(10))² = 16/10,\n\n(8/sqrt(10))² = 64/10,\n\nTotal: 16/10 + 64/10 = 80/10 = 8,\n\nThus PC = sqrt(8) = 2*sqrt(2).\n\nThen PD: distance from P to D (0, sqrt(10)):\n\nPD = sqrt( (0 - 6/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute x-component: (-6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\ny-component: (sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10)) = 8/sqrt(10) as above,\n\nso (8/sqrt(10))² = 64/10,\n\nTotal: 36/10 + 64/10 = 100/10 = 10,\n\nThus PD = sqrt(10).\n\nTherefore, the distances are PA = 2, PB = sqrt(2), PC = 2*sqrt(2), PD = sqrt(10).\n\nTherefore, the product PA · PB · PC · PD = 2 * sqrt(2) * 2*sqrt(2) * sqrt(10).\n\nLet me compute step by step:\n\nFirst, 2 * sqrt(2) = 2 sqrt(2),\n\nThen 2 sqrt(2) * 2 sqrt(2) = 4 * (sqrt(2))^2 = 4 * 2 = 8,\n\nThen 8 * sqrt(10) = 8 sqrt(10).\n\nHmm, so in Case 1, the product is 8 sqrt(10).\n\nWait, let me confirm the calculations again:\n\nPA = 2,\n\nPB = sqrt( (4/sqrt(10))² + ( -2/sqrt(10))² ) = sqrt(16/10 + 4/10) = sqrt(20/10) = sqrt(2),\n\nPC = sqrt( (4/sqrt(10))² + (8/sqrt(10))² ) = sqrt(16/10 + 64/10) = sqrt(80/10) = sqrt(8) = 2 sqrt(2),\n\nPD = sqrt( ( -6/sqrt(10))² + (8/sqrt(10))² ) = sqrt(36/10 + 64/10) = sqrt(100/10) = sqrt(10).\n\nTherefore, PA * PB * PC * PD = 2 * sqrt(2) * 2 sqrt(2) * sqrt(10).\n\nMultiplying the constants: 2 * 2 = 4,\n\nMultiplying the radicals: sqrt(2) * sqrt(2) = 2,\n\nSo then 4 * 2 = 8,\n\nThen 8 * sqrt(10) = 8 sqrt(10).\n\nOkay, so that's 8√10 for Case 1.\n\nNow let's check Case 2: P = (6/sqrt(10), 8/sqrt(10)).\n\nCompute PA, PB, PC, PD.\n\nPA: distance from (6/sqrt(10), 8/sqrt(10)) to A(0,0):\n\nsqrt( (6/sqrt(10))² + (8/sqrt(10))² ) = sqrt(36/10 + 64/10) = sqrt(100/10) = sqrt(10).\n\nPB: distance to B(sqrt(10), 0):\n\nsqrt( (sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10))² + (0 - 8/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10) = 4/sqrt(10),\n\nand the y-component: -8/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared terms:\n\n(4/sqrt(10))² = 16/10,\n\n(-8/sqrt(10))² = 64/10,\n\nTotal: 16/10 + 64/10 = 80/10 = 8,\n\nso PB = sqrt(8) = 2 sqrt(2).\n\nPC: distance to C(sqrt(10), sqrt(10)):\n\nsqrt( (sqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10))² )\n\nsqrt(10) - 6/sqrt(10) = 4/sqrt(10),\n\nsqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10) = (10 - 8)/sqrt(10) = 2/sqrt(10),\n\nTherefore squared terms:\n\n(4/sqrt(10))² = 16/10,\n\n(2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10,\n\nTotal: 20/10 = 2,\n\nso PC = sqrt(2).\n\nPD: distance to D(0, sqrt(10)):\n\nsqrt( (0 - 6/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute x-component: (-6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\ny-component: (sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10)) = 2/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared: (2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10,\n\nTotal: 36/10 + 4/10 = 40/10 = 4,\n\nso PD = sqrt(4) = 2.\n\nTherefore, the distances for Case 2 are PA = sqrt(10), PB = 2 sqrt(2), PC = sqrt(2), PD = 2.\n\nThus, the product PA · PB · PC · PD = sqrt(10) * 2 sqrt(2) * sqrt(2) * 2.\n\nLet me compute this step by step:\n\nFirst, sqrt(10) * 2 = 2 sqrt(10),\n\nThen, 2 sqrt(2) * sqrt(2) = 2*(sqrt(2))^2 = 2*2 = 4,\n\nThen, 4 * 2 = 8,\n\nThen, 8 * sqrt(10) = 8 sqrt(10). Wait, no, hold on, I think I messed up the order.\n\nWait, let me parenthesize properly:\n\nPA · PB · PC · PD = sqrt(10) * [2 sqrt(2)] * [sqrt(2)] * [2]\n\nSo group the constants and the radicals:\n\nConstants: sqrt(10) * 2 * 1 * 2 = sqrt(10) * 4,\n\nRadicals: sqrt(2) * sqrt(2) = 2,\n\nTherefore total: 4 sqrt(10) * 2 = 8 sqrt(10). Wait, same as before.\n\nWait, sqrt(10) * 2 sqrt(2) * sqrt(2) * 2:\n\nFirst, multiply the constants: 2 * 2 = 4,\n\nMultiply the radicals: sqrt(10) * sqrt(2) * sqrt(2) = sqrt(10) * (sqrt(2))^2 = sqrt(10)*2,\n\nThus total: 4 * 2 * sqrt(10) = 8 sqrt(10). Wait, that's the same as before. Wait, okay, so indeed, same product.\n\nWait, so in Case 2, the product is also 8 sqrt(10).\n\nHmm, interesting. Let me check Case 3.\n\nCase 3: P = (4/sqrt(10), 2/sqrt(10)).\n\nCompute PA, PB, PC, PD.\n\nPA: distance to A(0,0):\n\nsqrt( (4/sqrt(10))² + (2/sqrt(10))² ) = sqrt(16/10 + 4/10) = sqrt(20/10) = sqrt(2).\n\nPB: distance to B(sqrt(10), 0):\n\nsqrt( (sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10))² + (0 - 2/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10) = (10 - 4)/sqrt(10) = 6/sqrt(10),\n\nand the y-component is -2/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared terms:\n\n(6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\n(-2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10,\n\nTotal: 40/10 = 4,\n\nso PB = sqrt(4) = 2.\n\nPC: distance to C(sqrt(10), sqrt(10)):\n\nsqrt( (sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10) = 6/sqrt(10),\n\nsqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10) = 8/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared terms:\n\n(6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\n(8/sqrt(10))² = 64/10,\n\nTotal: 100/10 = 10,\n\nso PC = sqrt(10).\n\nPD: distance to D(0, sqrt(10)):\n\nsqrt( (0 - 4/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute x-component: (-4/sqrt(10))² = 16/10,\n\ny-component: (sqrt(10) - 2/sqrt(10)) = 8/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared: 64/10,\n\nTotal: 16/10 + 64/10 = 80/10 = 8,\n\nso PD = sqrt(8) = 2 sqrt(2).\n\nTherefore, the distances for Case 3 are PA = sqrt(2), PB = 2, PC = sqrt(10), PD = 2 sqrt(2).\n\nTherefore, the product PA · PB · PC · PD = sqrt(2) * 2 * sqrt(10) * 2 sqrt(2).\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nFirst, constants: 2 * 2 = 4,\n\nRadicals: sqrt(2) * sqrt(10) * sqrt(2) = sqrt(2)*sqrt(2)*sqrt(10) = 2 * sqrt(10),\n\nTherefore total: 4 * 2 * sqrt(10) = 8 sqrt(10). Again, same result!\n\nOkay, so that's 8 sqrt(10).\n\nNow, let's check Case 4: P = (4/sqrt(10), 8/sqrt(10)).\n\nCompute PA, PB, PC, PD.\n\nPA: distance to A(0,0):\n\nsqrt( (4/sqrt(10))² + (8/sqrt(10))² ) = sqrt(16/10 + 64/10) = sqrt(80/10) = sqrt(8) = 2 sqrt(2).\n\nPB: distance to B(sqrt(10), 0):\n\nsqrt( (sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10))² + (0 - 8/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10) = (10 - 4)/sqrt(10) = 6/sqrt(10),\n\ny-component: -8/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared terms:\n\n(6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\n(-8/sqrt(10))² = 64/10,\n\nTotal: 100/10 = 10,\n\nso PB = sqrt(10).\n\nPC: distance to C(sqrt(10), sqrt(10)):\n\nsqrt( (sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute sqrt(10) - 4/sqrt(10) = 6/sqrt(10),\n\nsqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10) = (10 - 8)/sqrt(10) = 2/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared terms:\n\n(6/sqrt(10))² = 36/10,\n\n(2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10,\n\nTotal: 40/10 = 4,\n\nso PC = sqrt(4) = 2.\n\nPD: distance to D(0, sqrt(10)):\n\nsqrt( (0 - 4/sqrt(10))² + (sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10))² )\n\nCompute x-component: (-4/sqrt(10))² = 16/10,\n\ny-component: (sqrt(10) - 8/sqrt(10)) = 2/sqrt(10),\n\nso squared: (2/sqrt(10))² = 4/10,\n\nTotal: 16/10 + 4/10 = 20/10 = 2,\n\nso PD = sqrt(2).\n\nTherefore, the distances for Case 4 are PA = 2 sqrt(2), PB = sqrt(10), PC = 2, PD = sqrt(2).\n\nThus, the product PA · PB · PC · PD = 2 sqrt(2) * sqrt(10) * 2 * sqrt(2).\n\nCompute this:\n\nFirst, constants: 2 * 2 = 4,\n\nRadicals: sqrt(2) * sqrt(10) * sqrt(2) = sqrt(2)*sqrt(2)*sqrt(10) = 2 * sqrt(10),\n\nTherefore total: 4 * 2 * sqrt(10) = 8 sqrt(10). Same as before