 axis equation is \\( (2c - 20)x + 2k y = c^2 \\). Let me write this in slope-intercept form.\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\n\\( 2k y = - (2c - 20)x + c^2 \\)\n\n\\( y = \\left( \\frac{ - (2c - 20) }{ 2k } \\right) x + \\frac{c^2}{2k} \\)\n\nTherefore, the slope \\( m_{PQ} = \\frac{ - (2c - 20) }{ 2k } = \\frac{ -2(c - 10) }{ 2k } = \\frac{ - (c - 10) }{ k } \\)\n\nSince the radical axis is perpendicular to the line connecting the centers, their slopes multiply to -1:\n\n\\( m_{O_3O_2} \\times m_{PQ} = \\left( \\frac{k}{c - 10} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{ - (c - 10) }{ k } \\right) = -1 \\)\n\nWhich checks out! So that's consistent. Good.\n\nTherefore, the configuration is valid.\n\nNow, returning to the problem: we need to compute \\( AC \\cdot CB \\), where \\( C \\) is the point of tangency on \\( AB \\), which is the x-axis between \\( A(0,0) \\) and \\( B(20,0) \\). Specifically, \\( C \\) is at \\( (c, 0) \\), so \\( AC = c - 0 = c \\), and \\( CB = 20 - c \\), so \\( AC \\cdot CB = c(20 - c) \\).\n\nTherefore, if we can find the value of \\( c \\), then we can compute \\( AC \\cdot CB = 20c - c^2 \\).\n\nSo our target is to find \\( c \\), given the constraints of the problem, which include the radius of \\( S_1 \\), which is 1, and the fact that \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) being parallel.\n\nSo far, we have equations involving \\( c \\) and \\( k \\), but we need another relation from the fact that \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with radius 1.\n\nLet me consider the configuration of \\( S_1 \\) inscribed in \\( S_2 \\). Similarly to how \\( S_2 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), meaning:\n\n- The diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is a chord of \\( S_2 \\).\n- The arc of \\( S_1 \\) is tangent to the diameter of \\( S_2 \\).\n\nGiven that the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is parallel to the diameter of \\( S_3 \\), which we've established is horizontal (since \\( S_