m O₃ perpendicular to DE, then the distance from O₃ to DE is exactly the distance between O₃ and O₂ along that perpendicular line. Wait, actually, the distance from O₃ to the chord DE is equal to the length of the vector from O₃ to O₂ minus the component along the direction perpendicular to DE? Hmm, maybe I need another approach.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula for the distance from the center of the circle to the chord: the distance d from O₃ to chord DE is equal to the distance between O₃ and O₂ minus the projection along the line perpendicular to DE? Wait, perhaps I need to use the formula for the length of the chord.\n\nThe length of chord DE in circle S₃ is 2r₂ (since DE is the diameter of S₂, which has radius r₂). Wait, no, DE is a chord of S₃, but the length of DE is equal to the diameter of S₂, which is 2r₂. Therefore, the chord DE in S₃ has length L = 2r₂, and the distance from O₃ to chord DE is d. Then, by the chord length formula:\n\nL = 2*sqrt(R² - d²)\n\nwhere R is the radius of S₃ (which is 10), and d is the distance from O₃ to chord DE.\n\nTherefore, 2r₂ = 2*sqrt(10² - d²), so r₂ = sqrt(100 - d²). \n\nBut also, the distance d from O₃ to chord DE is equal to the distance from O₃ to the line DE. Since the midpoint of DE is O₂ = (h, r₂), and the line O₃O₂ is perpendicular to DE, then the distance from O₃ to DE is exactly the distance between O₃ and O₂ along the line O₃O₂. Wait, actually, since O₂ is the midpoint of DE and the line O₃O₂ is perpendicular to DE, then the distance from O₃ to DE is the length of the segment O₃O₂ projected onto the direction perpendicular to DE, but since O₃O₂ is already along the perpendicular direction, the distance from O₃ to DE is just the length of O₃O₂. Wait, no. Wait, O₂ is the midpoint of DE, so the distance from O₃ to DE is the distance from O₃ to the line DE, which is equal to the length of the vector from O₃ to O₂ minus the component along DE? Wait, no, perhaps since O₃O₂ is perpendicular to DE, then the distanc