ontal, the line from O₂ to O₁ must be vertical. Therefore, O₁ lies directly below or above O₂ along the vertical line through O₂? Wait, since FG is a chord of S₂, and S₂ is above DE, then FG is also above DE, so O₁ is above DE? Wait, but DE is the diameter of S₂, so S₂ is the semicircle above DE. Therefore, the entire semicircle S₂ is above DE, so any chord FG inside S₂ must also be above DE. Therefore, the center O₁ of S₁ must lie above DE, and the line from O₂ to O₁ is vertical because FG is horizontal. Therefore, O₁ lies along the vertical line passing through O₂. Therefore, the x-coordinate of O₁ is the same as that of O₂, which is h. \n\nSo O₁ has coordinates (h, k), where k is some y-coordinate above DE. Since the center O₁ is the midpoint of FG, and FG is horizontal, then the diameter FG has length 2*1 = 2 (since the radius of S₁ is 1). Therefore, FG spans from (h - 1, k) to (h + 1, k). Wait, no: the radius is 1, so the diameter is 2, so the endpoints F and G are 1 unit away from O₁ along the horizontal line. Therefore, coordinates of F and G are (h - 1, k) and (h + 1, k). \n\nNow, the semicircle S₁ is centered at (h, k) with radius 1, and it is tangent to the line DE. The distance from the center O₁ to the line DE must be equal to the radius of S₁, which is 1. \n\nSo, let me find the equation of the line DE. Since DE is the diameter of S₂, which is a chord of S₃, and we know that the center O₂ is at (h, r₂), and DE is a horizontal line? Wait, hold on, no. Wait, earlier I thought that DE might not be horizontal, but actually, let me check again.\n\nWait, earlier, I considered that DE could be at an angle, but now, given that S₁'s diameter is parallel to AB (horizontal), and S₁ is inside S₂, which is itself inscribed in S₃. But perhaps DE is horizontal? Because if the diameter of S₁ is horizontal, and S₁ is tangent to DE, then DE must be a horizontal line? Wait, not necessarily. Wait, if S₁ is tangent to DE at a single point, and S₁ is a semicircle above its diameter FG (horizontal), then the point of tangency T must lie along DE. If DE is not horizontal, then the tangent point T would have some slope, but the semicircle S₁ is a horizontal diameter with a vertical center? Hmm, this might complicate things. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps DE is horizontal? Let me think. If DE is horizontal, then the semicircle S₂ is sitting on top of DE, which is a horizontal chord of S₃. Then, the center O₂ is at (h, r₂), and DE is the horizontal line segment from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂)? Wait, no. Wait, if DE is horizontal and the center O₂ is at (h, r₂), then the diameter DE would be a horizontal line segment of length 2r₂, so its endpoints would be at (h - r₂, r₂) and (h + r₂, r₂). Wait, but then the line DE is the line y = r₂, from x = h - r₂ to x = h + r₂. But in that case, the semicircle S₂ would be the upper half of the circle with center (h, r₂) and radius r₂, so the semicircle would go from (h - r₂, r₂) up to (h, r₂ + r₂) = (h, 2r₂) and back down? Wait, no, actually, if DE is the diameter lying along the line y = r₂, then the semicircle S₂ would be the set of points above the line DE, forming a semicircle with center at (h, r₂) and radius r₂. Therefore, the topmost point of S₂ would be at (h, r₂ + r₂) = (h, 2r₂). \n\nBut in this case, the semicircle S₂ would be tangent to the x-axis (AB) at point C only if the distance from O₂ to the x-axis is equal to the radius of S₂. Wait, the distance from O₂ (h, r₂) to the x-axis is r₂, which is exactly the radius of S₂. Therefore, the semicircle S₂ would indeed be tangent to the x-axis at the point directly below O₂, which is (h, 0). Wait a second! If the center is at (h, r₂) and the radius is r₂, then the semicircle extends down to the x-axis at (h, 0). So the point of tangency C is (h, 0). Therefore, in this case, the point C is the projection of O₂ onto the x-axis. Therefore, the coordinates of C are (h, 0). \n\nTherefore, if DE is horizontal, then the point of tangency C is (h, 0), and the center of S₂ is at (h, r₂). Therefore, the coordinates make sense. \n\nTherefore, maybe DE is horizontal? Then my initial thought that DE might not be horizontal was incorrect, because if DE is horizontal, then the semicircle S₂ is tangent to AB at (h, 0), and that seems consistent. \n\nTherefore, perhaps we can assume that DE is horizontal. Let me proceed with that assumption, as it seems to simplify things. \n\nSo, assuming that DE is horizontal, then the center of S₂ is at (h, r₂), with DE lying along the line y = r₂, from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂). The semicircle S₂ is the upper half of the circle with center (h, r₂) and radius r₂, so it goes from (h - r₂, r₂) up to (h, 2r₂) and back down to (h + r₂, r₂). But since it's a semicircle above DE, it curves upward, and touches the x-axis at (h, 0). \n\nTherefore, point C is (h, 0). \n\nNow, moving on to S₁, which is inscribed in S₂. The diameter of S₁ is parallel to AB (so horizontal), and has length 2 (since radius is 1). Let me denote the diameter of S₁ as FG, with center O₁. Since S₁ is inside S₂, and tangent to DE (the diameter of S₂), which is the line y = r₂. \n\nWait, hold on. If DE is the line y = r₂, then the semicircle S₁ is tangent to DE. But DE is the line y = r₂, which is the diameter of S₂. Since S₁ is inside S₂, and S₁'s arc is tangent to DE, then the semicircle S₁ must touch the line DE (y = r₂) at exactly one point. \n\nHowever, S₁ is a semicircle above its own diameter FG. If FG is horizontal, then the semicircle S₁ curves upward from FG. To be tangent to DE (the line y = r₂), the topmost point of S₁ must touch DE. The topmost point of S₁ is at a distance of 1 (the radius) above its center O₁. Therefore, if the center O₁ is at (h, k), then the topmost point is (h, k + 1). Since this must lie on DE (y = r₂), we have k + 1 = r₂. Therefore, the y-coordinate of the center O₁ is k = r₂ - 1. \n\nAdditionally, the diameter FG of S₁ is horizontal, so its endpoints are at (h - 1, k) and (h + 1, k), since the radius is 1. Therefore, the center O₁ is at (h, r₂ - 1). \n\nMoreover, since FG is a chord of S₂, the endpoints F and G must lie on the semicircle S₂. The semicircle S₂ is the upper half of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂. Therefore, the equation of S₂ is:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂², with y ≥ r₂. \n\nWait, hold on: the semicircle S₂ is above DE, which is the diameter along y = r₂, so actually, the full circle would be centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂, and the semicircle is the upper half, so y ≥ r₂? Wait, no. Wait, if the diameter is along y = r₂, then the semicircle above it would actually be the set of points where y ≥ r₂, forming a semicircle. Wait, but the center is at (h, r₂), so the circle equation is (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂². The upper semicircle would be where y ≥ r₂, so the semicircle is the top half of the circle, which is just a single point at (h, 2r₂) and curves down? Wait, no, actually, solving for y:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂²\n\n=> (y - r₂)^2 = r₂² - (x - h)^2\n\n=> y - r₂ = ± sqrt(r₂² - (x - h)^2 )\n\nBut since it's the upper semicircle (above the diameter DE, which is along y = r₂), we take the positive square root:\n\ny = r₂ + sqrt(r₂² - (x - h)^2 )\n\nWait, that can't be, because if we take the positive square root, then y would be greater than r₂, so the semicircle is above the diameter DE. However, the center is at (h, r₂), so the topmost point is at (h, r₂ + r₂) = (h, 2r₂). The bottom of the semicircle is the diameter DE at y = r₂. \n\nTherefore, the semicircle S₂ is the set of points (x, y) such that (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂² and y ≥ r₂. \n\nTherefore, the endpoints F and G of S₁'s diameter FG must lie on S₂. The endpoints of FG are (h - 1, k) and (h + 1, k), where k = r₂ - 1. Let me plug one of these points into the equation of S₂ to see if they lie on it. \n\nTake point F: (h - 1, k) = (h - 1, r₂ - 1). \n\nPlugging into the equation of S₂:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂²\n\nLeft-hand side (LHS):\n\n(h - 1 - h)^2 + ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ )^2\n\n= (-1)^2 + (-1)^2\n\n= 1 + 1 = 2\n\nRight-hand side (RHS):\n\nr₂²\n\nTherefore, for point F to lie on S₂, we must have 2 = r₂² ⇒ r₂ = sqrt(2). Wait, but that would mean that r₂ is sqrt(2), but we also have another relation from earlier involving h and r₂. \n\nWait, but hold on, this suggests that in order for the endpoints of FG (the diameter of S₁) to lie on S₂, we must have r₂² = 2, so r₂ = sqrt(2). But let me verify this with the other equation we had earlier.\n\nEarlier, we had from the chord DE in S₃:\n\n2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nIf r₂ = sqrt(2), then 2*(2) + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 4 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ (h - 10)^2 = 96 ⇒ h - 10 = ±√96 = ±4√6 ≈ ±9.798\n\nTherefore, h = 10 ± 4√6. Hmm, but then we also need to consider the position of S₁'s center O₁ at (h, r₂ - 1) = (h, sqrt(2) - 1). But also, the diameter FG of S₁ is from (h - 1, sqrt(2) - 1) to (h + 1, sqrt(2) - 1). These points must lie on S₂, which we just enforced by setting r₂ = sqrt(2). \n\nBut wait, in the problem statement, the radius of S₁ is 1, which is given, and the radius of S₃ is 10, which is given, but we're getting that r₂ must be sqrt(2) ≈ 1.414, which is plausible? Maybe, but let me check if there are more constraints.\n\nAdditionally, we need to make sure that the semicircle S₁ is indeed tangent to DE. The topmost point of S₁ is at (h, k + 1) = (h, (r₂ - 1) + 1) = (h, r₂), which is exactly the line DE (y = r₂). Therefore, the semicircle S₁ touches DE at its topmost point (h, r₂). So that is indeed the point of tangency. So that works out.\n\nBut then, according to this, r₂ must be sqrt(2), and h is 10 ± 4√6. But we also have the condition that the diameters of S₁ and S₃ are parallel. Since S₃'s diameter is along the x-axis, and S₁'s diameter is horizontal (as we assumed), so that condition is satisfied. \n\nHowever, in the problem statement, it says \"the diameters of S₁ and S₃ are parallel,\" which they are, both horizontal. So that's okay. \n\nBut now, we need to find AC · CB, where A is (0,0) and B is (20,0), and C is the point where S₂ is tangent to AB, which we determined is (h, 0). Therefore, point C is (h, 0). Therefore, AC is the distance from A(0,0) to C(h,0), which is |h - 0| = |h|. Similarly, CB is the distance from C(h,0) to B(20,0), which is |20 - h|. Therefore, AC · CB = |h| * |20 - h|. Since h is a coordinate along the x-axis between 0 and 20 (because the center of S₂ must lie within the semicircle S₃), so h is between 0 and 20, so |h| = h and |20 - h| = 20 - h. Therefore, AC · CB = h*(20 - h). \n\nTherefore, if we can find the value of h, then we can compute h*(20 - h). \n\nFrom earlier, we had two equations:\n\n1. From the chord DE in S₃: 2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\n2. From the condition that the endpoints of S₁'s diameter lie on S₂: r₂² = 2\n\nTherefore, substituting equation 2 into equation 1:\n\n2*(2) + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 4 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ (h - 10)^2 = 96 ⇒ h - 10 = ±√96 ⇒ h = 10 ± √96\n\nSimplify √96: √(16*6) = 4√6 ≈ 9.798\n\nTherefore, h = 10 + 4√6 or h = 10 - 4√6\n\nNow, since h is the x-coordinate of the center of S₂, and the semicircle S₂ must lie entirely within S₃, we need to check which of these solutions is valid. \n\nFirst, let's compute 10 + 4√6. Since √6 ≈ 2.449, so 4√6 ≈ 9.796. Therefore, 10 + 9.796 ≈ 19.796, which is less than 20, so that's okay. \n\n10 - 4√6 ≈ 10 - 9.796 ≈ 0.204, which is greater than 0, so also okay. \n\nSo both solutions are within the diameter AB (from 0 to 20). \n\nNow, which one do we choose? \n\nHmm. The problem doesn't specify whether the configuration is on the left or the right side, but in the TikZ code provided, point C is at (8.3, 0), which is closer to the left end? Wait, in the TikZ code, they have:\n\n\\coordinate (C) at (8.3,0);\n\nSo in their diagram, C is at x ≈8.3, which is between 0 and 20. Our solutions are approximately h ≈ 19.8 or h ≈ 0.204. Since 8.3 is between 0.204 and 19.8, neither of our solutions matches exactly 8.3. Hmm, that might be because the diagram is just an approximation or maybe my assumption that DE is horizontal is incorrect? \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a wrong assumption that DE is horizontal. Let me revisit that.\n\nEarlier, I assumed that DE is horizontal because S₁'s diameter is horizontal and the tangency condition led me to that. But perhaps DE is not horizontal, and my mistake was assuming that. Let me see.\n\nWait, the problem states that the diameters of S₁ and S₃ are parallel. Since S₃'s diameter is horizontal, S₁'s diameter must also be horizontal, but there is no such requirement for S₂'s diameter. Therefore, DE (the diameter of S₂) could be at an angle, which would mean that my previous assumption that DE is horizontal is unwarranted. \n\nTherefore, perhaps DE is not horizontal, and hence my earlier approach was flawed. \n\nAlright, so I need to start over without assuming DE is horizontal.\n\nLet me try again without that assumption.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- S₃: semicircle with diameter AB = [0, 20] on the x-axis, center at (10, 0), radius 10.\n\n- S₂: semicircle inscribed in S₃, with diameter DE (a chord of S₃), and arc of S₂ tangent to AB at point C.\n\n- S₁: semicircle inscribed in S₂, with diameter FG (a chord of S₂), radius 1, arc of S₁ tangent to DE.\n\n- The diameters of S₁ and S₃ are parallel (both horizontal), so FG is horizontal.\n\nGoal: Find AC · CB where C is the tangency point on AB.\n\nLet me consider coordinates again, but more generally.\n\nLet me denote the center of S₂ as O₂ = (h, k), and its radius as r₂. Since S₂ is a semicircle inscribed in S₃, with diameter DE (a chord of S₃), and tangent to AB (the x-axis) at point C. The arc of S₂ is tangent to AB at C, so the distance from O₂ to AB (the x-axis) must equal the radius r₂. Therefore, since AB is the x-axis, the y-coordinate of O₂ is equal to r₂. So O₂ = (h, r₂).\n\nThe diameter DE of S₂ is a chord of S₃. The endpoints D and E lie on the circle S₃: (x - 10)^2 + y^2 = 100.\n\nThe midpoint of DE is O₂ = (h, r₂), so the chord DE has midpoint at (h, r₂). The line O₃O₂ (from center of S₃ (10,0) to O₂ (h, r₂)) is perpendicular to DE, as the line from the center of the circle to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord.\n\nTherefore, the slope of O₃O₂ is (r₂ - 0)/(h - 10) = r₂/(h - 10). Therefore, the slope of DE is the negative reciprocal: m_DE = - (h - 10)/r₂.\n\nSince DE is the diameter of S₂, its length is 2r₂. The distance from O₃ to DE can be calculated as the distance from O₃ to the line DE. Since O₂ is the midpoint of DE and lies along the line perpendicular to DE through O₃, the distance from O₃ to DE is exactly the distance between O₃ and O₂ projected onto the direction perpendicular to DE. Wait, but since O₃O₂ is already along the perpendicular to DE, the distance from O₃ to DE is just the length of O₃O₂. Wait, no, actually, the distance from a point to a line is the minimal distance, which is along the perpendicular. Since O₂ is the midpoint of DE and lies along the line perpendicular to DE through O₃, then the distance from O₃ to DE is the distance between O₃ and O₂ along that perpendicular line. Wait, actually, no. Wait, the distance from O₃ to the line DE is equal to the length of the vector from O₃ to the line DE, which is exactly the length of O₃O₂ minus the component along DE? Hmm, perhaps better to use the formula for distance from a point to a line.\n\nLet me compute the equation of line DE.\n\nSince DE is a line with slope m_DE = - (h - 10)/r₂, and it passes through the midpoint O₂ = (h, r₂). So the equation of DE is:\n\ny - r₂ = m_DE (x - h)\n\n=> y - r₂ = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ] (x - h )\n\nWe can write this as:\n\ny = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ] (x - h ) + r₂\n\nThe distance from O₃ = (10, 0) to this line DE can be computed using the formula for distance from a point (x₀, y₀) to the line ax + by + c = 0:\n\nDistance = |a x₀ + b y₀ + c| / sqrt(a² + b²)\n\nFirst, let me rearrange the equation of DE into standard form ax + by + c = 0.\n\nStarting from:\n\ny = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ] (x - h ) + r₂\n\nMultiply out the right-hand side:\n\ny = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ] x + [ (h - 10)/r₂ * h ] + r₂\n\nBring all terms to left-hand side:\n\n[ (h - 10)/r₂ ] x + y - [ (h - 10)h / r₂ + r₂ ] = 0\n\nTherefore,\n\na = (h - 10)/r₂\n\nb = 1\n\nc = - [ (h - 10)h / r₂ + r₂ ]\n\nTherefore, the distance from O₃ (10, 0) to DE is:\n\n| a*10 + b*0 + c | / sqrt(a² + b²) \n\n= | ( (h - 10)/r₂ * 10 ) + 0 + c | / sqrt( [ (h - 10)/r₂ ]² + 1 )\n\nBut c = - [ (h - 10)h / r₂ + r₂ ]\n\nTherefore, substituting c:\n\n= | (10(h - 10)/r₂ ) - [ (h - 10)h / r₂ + r₂ ] | / sqrt( [ (h - 10)^2 / r₂² ] + 1 )\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n= | [10(h - 10)/r₂ - (h - 10)h / r₂ - r₂ ] | \n\nFactor out 1/r₂ from the first two terms:\n\n= | [ (h - 10)(10 - h)/r₂ - r₂ ] | \n\nNote that (10 - h) = - (h - 10), so:\n\n= | [ - (h - 10)^2 / r₂ - r₂ ] | \n\n= | - [ (h - 10)^2 / r₂ + r₂ ] | \n\n= | (h - 10)^2 / r₂ + r₂ | \n\nSince everything is positive (assuming distances are positive), we can drop the absolute value:\n\n= (h - 10)^2 / r₂ + r₂ \n\nTherefore, the distance from O₃ to DE is:\n\n[ (h - 10)^2 / r₂ + r₂ ] / sqrt( [ (h - 10)^2 / r₂² ] + 1 )\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. Let me see if I can simplify the denominator:\n\nsqrt( [ (h - 10)^2 / r₂² ] + 1 ) = sqrt( [ (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ] / r₂² ) ) = sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ \n\nTherefore, the entire distance becomes:\n\n[ ( (h - 10)^2 / r₂ + r₂ ) ] / [ sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ ) ]\n\n= [ ( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ ] / [ sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ ) ]\n\nThe r₂ in the denominator cancels with the r₂ in the numerator:\n\n= [ ( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ ] * [ r₂ / sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) ) ]\n\nThe r₂ cancels:\n\n= ( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\n= sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nTherefore, the distance from O₃ to DE is sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nBut we also know from the chord length formula that for chord DE in circle S₃ (radius 10):\n\nLength of DE = 2r₂ = 2*sqrt( R² - d² ), where d is the distance from O₃ to DE.\n\nWait, chord length formula: chord length = 2*sqrt(R² - d²), where d is the distance from center to chord.\n\nHere, chord DE has length 2r₂, R = 10, d = distance from O₃ to DE = sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nTherefore:\n\n2r₂ = 2*sqrt( 10² - [ sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) ]² )\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n2*sqrt( 100 - [ (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ] )\n\nTherefore:\n\n2r₂ = 2*sqrt( 100 - (h - 10)^2 - r₂² )\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\nr₂ = sqrt( 100 - (h - 10)^2 - r₂² )\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nr₂² = 100 - (h - 10)^2 - r₂²\n\nBring terms together:\n\nr₂² + r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nSo:\n\n2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nWait, this is the same equation I had earlier when I assumed DE was horizontal! So even without assuming DE is horizontal, we still end up with the same equation: 2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100.\n\nThat's reassuring. So regardless of the orientation of DE, this relation holds. \n\nNow, moving on to S₁. S₁ is inscribed in S₂, with radius 1, diameter FG parallel to AB (so horizontal), and tangent to DE. Let me denote the center of S₁ as O₁ = (p, q). Since the diameter FG is horizontal, the center O₁ is the midpoint of FG, so FG is from (p - 1, q) to (p + 1, q). The semicircle S₁ is above its diameter FG, so it curves upward, and it is tangent to DE. \n\nThe point of tangency between S₁ and DE must satisfy two conditions: it lies on both the semicircle S₁ and the line DE, and the tangent lines at that point are the same for both curves. \n\nAlternatively, the distance from the center O₁ to the line DE must be equal to the radius of S₁, which is 1. \n\nSince DE is a line, and O₁ is at (p, q), the distance from O₁ to DE must be 1. \n\nAdditionally, since FG is a chord of S₂, the endpoints F and G must lie on S₂. The semicircle S₂ has center O₂ = (h, r₂) and radius r₂, so its equation is:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂², with y ≥ r₂ (since it's the upper semicircle above DE). Wait, actually, earlier we considered that the semicircle S₂ is above its diameter DE. However, if DE is not horizontal, then the semicircle S₂ is the set of points on the circle centered at O₂ with radius r₂ that are on one side of DE. Since it's inscribed in S₃ and tangent to AB, which is the x-axis, the semicircle S₂ must be the upper half with respect to DE? Hmm, actually, the definition just says it's a semicircle with diameter DE, so it could be either side, but since it's inscribed in S₃ and tangent to AB, it must be the side facing upwards towards the center of S₃. \n\nBut perhaps it's better to stick with the equation of the full circle and consider that S₂ is the semicircle above DE. However, since DE is a chord of S₃, the semicircle S₂ must lie entirely within S₃. \n\nGiven that the center of S₂ is O₂ = (h, r₂), and the radius is r₂, then the circle equation is (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂². The semicircle S₂ is the part of this circle that is on one side of DE. Since it's tangent to AB (the x-axis) at point C, which is below the center O₂ (since the distance from O₂ to AB is r₂), then the semicircle must be the lower half of the circle? Wait, no. Wait, the distance from O₂ to AB is r₂, so if the semicircle is tangent to AB, then it must be the lower half of the circle, but that would mean it's below the diameter DE. But DE is a chord of S₃, which is above the x-axis. Wait, this is confusing. \n\nWait, let me think again. The center of S₂ is at (h, r₂), and the radius is r₂. The circle equation is (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂². The point where it is tangent to AB (the x-axis) is at (h, 0), since the distance from the center to the x-axis is r₂, so dropping a vertical line from O₂ to the x-axis lands at (h, 0). Therefore, the semicircle S₂ must be the lower half of the circle, going from (h - r₂, 0) to (h + r₂, 0) along the x-axis, but that can't be because then DE would be the diameter along the x-axis, but then DE would be the same as the diameter of S₂ being along AB, but S₂ is inscribed in S₃. Wait, but if the semicircle S₂ is the lower half of its circle, then its diameter DE would be the segment from (h - r₂, 0) to (h + r₂, 0), which is along the x-axis. But then DE is part of AB, so the semicircle S₂ would be sitting on the x-axis with diameter DE, but then it would be entirely below the center O₂? Wait, no, the center is at (h, r₂), so if we take the lower half of the circle, then the semicircle would be below the center, but the center is already above the x-axis. \n\nWait a second, this is conflicting. If the center is at (h, r₂), and the circle has radius r₂, then the bottommost point of the circle is at (h, r₂ - r₂) = (h, 0), which is on the x-axis, and the topmost point is at (h, 2r₂). Therefore, the full circle extends from y=0 up to y=2r₂. Therefore, the semicircle S₂, being tangent to the x-axis and inscribed in S₃, must be the upper semicircle of the circle, from y= r₂ up to y=2r₂. Wait, but then the diameter DE would be the horizontal line at y = r₂, from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂). Therefore, in this case, DE is indeed horizontal, and the semicircle S₂ is the upper half. Therefore, my initial assumption that DE is horizontal was correct! \n\nTherefore, despite the confusion earlier, DE must be horizontal. Therefore, my first approach was correct, and the confusion was due to overcomplicating with non-horizontal DE. So DE is horizontal at y = r₂, with midpoint at (h, r₂), and endpoints at (h ± r₂, r₂). The semicircle S₂ is the upper half of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂, so it goes from (h - r₂, r₂) up to (h, 2r₂) and back down to (h + r₂, r₂). It is tangent to the x-axis at (h, 0). \n\nTherefore, proceeding with the first approach where DE is horizontal, and therefore the earlier equations hold:\n\nWe have two equations:\n\n1. 2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100 (from chord DE in S₃)\n\n2. From the condition that the endpoints of S₁'s diameter FG lie on S₂, which gave us r₂² = 2 (since the distance from O₁ to DE must be 1, and O₁ is at (h, r₂ - 1), so the topmost point of S₁ is at (h, r₂), which is on DE, and the endpoints of FG must lie on S₂'s circle equation. Wait, but in the first calculation, when I considered DE horizontal, I found that plugging the endpoint F into S₂'s equation required r₂² = 2. But let me verify again with DE horizontal.\n\nLet me re-examine the condition for S₁'s endpoints lying on S₂.\n\nS₁ has diameter FG, which is horizontal, centered at O₁ = (h, r₂ - 1). The endpoints F and G are at (h - 1, r₂ - 1) and (h + 1, r₂ - 1). These points must lie on the semicircle S₂, which is the upper half of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂. Therefore, their coordinates must satisfy the circle equation:\n\nFor point F: (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂²\n\nPlugging in F's coordinates:\n\n(h - 1 - h)^2 + ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ )^2 = r₂²\n\nSimplify:\n\n(-1)^2 + (-1)^2 = r₂²\n\n1 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ r₂² = 2 ⇒ r₂ = √2\n\nTherefore, this condition necessitates that r₂ = √2. \n\nTherefore, substituting back into the first equation:\n\n2*( (√2)^2 ) + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 2*(2) + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 4 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ (h - 10)^2 = 96 ⇒ h = 10 ± √96 = 10 ± 4√6\n\nThus, h = 10 + 4√6 or h = 10 - 4√6. \n\nNow, we need to determine which value of h is valid. \n\nRecall that the semicircle S₂ must lie entirely within S₃. The center of S₂ is at (h, r₂) = (h, √2). The semicircle S₂ extends from x = h - r₂ to x = h + r₂ along the x-axis (its diameter DE is from (h - √2, √2) to (h + √2, √2)), and vertically up to y = 2r₂ = 2√2. \n\nSince S₂ is inside S₃, which has radius 10 and is centered at (10, 0), we must ensure that the entire semicircle S₂ is within S₃. \n\nFirst, the horizontal extent: the leftmost point of S₂ is at x = h - √2, and the rightmost point is at x = h + √2. These must lie within the diameter AB of S₃, which is from x=0 to x=20. So:\n\nh - √2 ≥ 0 ⇒ h ≥ √2 ≈ 1.414\n\nand\n\nh + √2 ≤ 20 ⇒ h ≤ 20 - √2 ≈ 18.586\n\nNow, let's evaluate the two possible h values:\n\n1. h = 10 + 4√6 ≈ 10 + 9.798 ≈ 19.798\n\nCheck if this satisfies h ≤ 20 - √2 ≈ 18.586? No, 19.798 > 18.586, so this would place the rightmost point of S₂ at h + √2 ≈ 19.798 + 1.414 ≈ 21.212, which exceeds 20. Therefore, this is invalid because it goes beyond the diameter AB of S₃.\n\n2. h = 10 - 4√6 ≈ 10 - 9.798 ≈ 0.202\n\nCheck if this satisfies h ≥ √2 ≈ 1.414? No, 0.202 < 1.414, so the leftmost point of S₂ would be at h - √2 ≈ 0.202 - 1.414 ≈ -1.212, which is outside the diameter AB (which starts at x=0). Therefore, this is also invalid.\n\nWait a minute, both solutions are invalid? That can't be. Did I make a mistake?\n\nHmm, this is a problem. Both solutions for h place the semicircle S₂ partially outside of S₃? That suggests an error in my reasoning.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the horizontal extent? Let me recast:\n\nThe semicircle S₂ is not the lower half or upper half of its circle, but it's a semicircle with diameter DE. Since DE is the horizontal line segment at y = r₂ from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂), the semicircle S₂ is the upper half of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂. Therefore, the semicircle S₂ extends upward from DE to the top point (h, 2r₂). Therefore, the horizontal extent of S₂ is from x = h - r₂ to x = h + r₂. However, the entire semicircle must lie within S₃, which has a horizontal extent from x=0 to x=20 and vertical extent up to y=10.\n\nTherefore, the horizontal limits of S₂ must be within [0, 20], so:\n\nh - r₂ ≥ 0 and h + r₂ ≤ 20.\n\nGiven that r₂ = √2 ≈ 1.414,\n\nSo:\n\nh - 1.414 ≥ 0 ⇒ h ≥ 1.414\n\nand\n\nh + 1.414 ≤ 20 ⇒ h ≤ 18.586\n\nTherefore, h must be between approximately 1.414 and 18.586. \n\nBut our solutions were h ≈ 19.798 and h ≈ 0.202, both outside this interval. Therefore, this suggests inconsistency. \n\nThis means that with the given conditions (radius of S₁ is 1, radius of S₃ is 10, diameters parallel), there is no solution? That can't be, as the problem states such a configuration exists. Therefore, I must have made an error in my assumptions or calculations.\n\nLet me trace back. The critical point was when I plugged the coordinates of F into the equation of S₂. Let me double-check that.\n\nThe center of S₁ is at O₁ = (h, r₂ - 1). The endpoints of S₁'s diameter FG are at (h - 1, r₂ - 1) and (h + 1, r₂ - 1). These points must lie on the semicircle S₂, which is the upper half of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂. Therefore, plugging in the point (h - 1, r₂ - 1) into the circle equation:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂²\n\nSubstituting x = h - 1, y = r₂ - 1:\n\n(h - 1 - h)^2 + ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ )^2 = r₂²\n\nSimplify:\n\n(-1)^2 + (-1)^2 = r₂² ⇒ 1 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ r₂² = 2 ⇒ r₂ = √2\n\nThis seems correct. So that condition is unavoidable. Therefore, r₂ must be √2. \n\nThen plugging into the first equation: 2*(√2)^2 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 2*2 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 4 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ (h - 10)^2 = 96 ⇒ h = 10 ± √96 ≈ 10 ± 9.798\n\nSo h ≈ 19.798 or h ≈ 0.202, which are both outside the required [1.414, 18.586] interval. \n\nThis suggests that with the given radii (S₁ radius 1, S₃ radius 10) and the tangency conditions, such a configuration is impossible? But the problem says it exists, so I must have messed up.\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake is in the assumption that the entire semicircle S₂ must lie within S₃. The problem states that S₂ is inscribed in S₃, which typically would mean that it is entirely within S₃ and tangent to it at some points. However, perhaps \"inscribed\" just means that its diameter is a chord of S₃ and it's tangent to AB, without necessarily being entirely inside? Unlikely, since usually inscribed means inside.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my coordinate system assumption is flipped? Let me see.\n\nWait, in the problem statement: \"Semicircle S₁ is inscribed in a semicircle S₂, which is inscribed in another semicircle S₃.\" The term \"inscribed\" usually implies that the smaller semicircle is inside the larger one, tangent appropriately. So S₂ must be inside S₃, tangent to its diameter AB at point C, and S₁ must be inside S₂, tangent to its diameter DE at some point. \n\nGiven that, with the math above leading to h outside the allowable range, there must be an error in the reasoning.\n\nWait, let me think differently. Perhaps the semicircle S₂ is not the upper half of its circle, but the lower half? If so, then the center O₂ is at (h, r₂), but the semicircle S₂ would be the lower half, going from y = r₂ down to y = 0 (the x-axis). In that case, the diameter DE would be the line segment along y = r₂ from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂), and the semicircle S₂ would be below that diameter, curving down to the x-axis at (h, 0). \n\nIn this case, the endpoints of S₁'s diameter FG would need to lie on the semicircle S₂, which is now the lower half. Let me see if that changes things.\n\nIf S₂ is the lower semicircle, then its equation is the lower half of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂, so y ≤ r₂. The diameter DE is along y = r₂, from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂), and the semicircle curves down to (h, 0). \n\nThen, the center of S₁, O₁, must be such that the semicircle S₁ is above its diameter FG and tangent to DE (the line y = r₂). \n\nWait, if S₁ is above its diameter FG, then it would curve upward away from DE, but DE is above S₁'s semicircle? This might not make sense. Alternatively, if S₁ is the lower semicircle, but the problem states that S₁ is a semicircle inscribed in S₂, with its arc tangent to the diameter of S₂. Since S₂'s diameter is DE (the line y = r₂), then the arc of S₁ must be tangent to DE. If S₁ is a lower semicircle inside S₂, then its arc would be below DE, so it can't be tangent to DE from below. Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my entire coordinate system assumption is flipped. Maybe the semicircles are drawn with the diameter on top? Wait, no, the problem says the diameters of S₁ and S₃ are parallel, and since S₃'s diameter is on the x-axis, then S₁'s diameter must also be horizontal, presumably on the same side. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the semicircles are drawn with the diameter as the base, and the arc above, so S₂ is above its diameter DE, and S₁ is above its diameter FG, and S₂ is above DE, which is above the x-axis. Then S₂ is inside S₃, which is above the x-axis with diameter AB. \n\nBut in that case, with S₂'s center at (h, r₂), and S₂'s semicircle going up to y = 2r₂, which must be less than or equal to the top of S₃, which is y = 10. So 2r₂ ≤ 10 ⇒ r₂ ≤ 5. \n\nBut from before, we had r₂ = √2 ≈ 1.414, which is less than 5, so that's okay. But the problem is the horizontal positioning. \n\nWait, perhaps the issue is that when I calculated the horizontal extent of S₂, I considered it from h - r₂ to h + r₂, but actually, the semicircle S₂ extends from x = h - r₂ to x = h + r₂ along the diameter DE, but vertically it goes up to y = 2r₂. However, the entire semicircle must lie within S₃, whose horizontal extent is from 0 to 20, and vertical extent up to y = 10. \n\nTherefore, the horizontal positions are okay as long as h - r₂ ≥ 0 and h + r₂ ≤ 20, but in our case with h ≈ 19.798 and r₂ ≈ 1.414, h + r₂ ≈ 21.212 > 20, which exceeds the diameter AB. \n\nSimilarly, h ≈ 0.202 gives h - r₂ ≈ -1.212 < 0, which is outside. \n\nTherefore, the problem is that with r₂ = √2, the semicircle S₂ cannot fit within S₃ unless h is within [r₂, 20 - r₂], which is approximately [1.414, 18.586]. But our solutions for h are outside this interval. \n\nThis suggests that my earlier conclusion that r₂ must be √2 is incorrect? But how?\n\nWait, let's go back to the condition that the endpoints of S₁'s diameter FG lie on S₂. \n\nThe endpoints F and G of S₁'s diameter are at (h - 1, r₂ - 1) and (h + 1, r₂ - 1). These points must lie on the semicircle S₂. \n\nIf S₂ is the upper semicircle (y ≥ r₂), then the y-coordinate of F and G is r₂ - 1, which is below the diameter DE (which is at y = r₂). Therefore, those points cannot be on the upper semicircle S₂. \n\nAh! Here is the mistake! \n\nIf S₂ is the upper semicircle (above DE at y = r₂), then the semicircle S₂ only includes points with y ≥ r₂. However, the points F and G have y-coordinate r₂ - 1, which is below r₂, so they cannot be on S₂. \n\nThis is a critical error. \n\nTherefore, my assumption that S₂ is the upper semicircle is incorrect in this context. \n\nInstead, S₂ must be the lower semicircle, so that its diameter DE is along y = r₂, and the semicircle curves downward to the x-axis. In that case, the semicircle S₂ includes points with y ≤ r₂. Then the points F and G, which are at y = r₂ - 1, would lie below DE and hence on the lower semicircle. \n\nTherefore, I had the orientation of S₂ flipped. \n\nLet me correct that:\n\n- S₂ is a semicircle with diameter DE lying along the line y = r₂ (horizontal), but since it's tangent to the x-axis at point C = (h, 0), it must be the lower semicircle of the circle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂. Therefore, the semicircle S₂ consists of points (x, y) such that (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂² and y ≤ r₂. \n\nTherefore, the diameter DE is the line segment from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂), and the semicircle curves downward to the point (h, 0). \n\nIn this case, the center O₂ is at (h, r₂), radius r₂, and the semicircle is the lower half. \n\nNow, the semicircle S₁, inscribed in S₂, must have its diameter FG parallel to AB (horizontal), and its arc tangent to the diameter DE of S₂. \n\nSince S₁ is inside S₂, and S₂ is the lower semicircle, S₁ must also be a lower semicircle? Or an upper semicircle? Wait, the problem says \"a semicircle is inscribed in another semicircle if the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle, and the smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\"\n\nSo S₁ is inscribed in S₂: its diameter is a chord of S₂, and its arc is tangent to the diameter of S₂ (which is DE). Since S₂'s diameter is DE (the line y = r₂), then the arc of S₁ must be tangent to DE. \n\nIf S₁ is inside S₂ (the lower semicircle), then its arc would curve downward, but to be tangent to DE (the upper edge of S₂), it must touch DE from below. Wait, but DE is the top of S₂, so the arc of S₁ has to touch DE. \n\nWait, if S₂ is the lower semicircle, then its \"arc\" is the curved part below DE. To have S₁'s arc tangent to DE, which is the diameter of S₂, then S₁ must be a semicircle whose arc touches DE from below. But since DE is the top of S₂, the only way for S₁'s arc to touch DE is if S₁ is also a semicircle sitting on top of some chord inside S₂, but S₂ itself is below DE. This is confusing.\n\nPerhaps better to think with coordinates.\n\nLet me redefine with S₂ as the lower semicircle:\n\n- S₂ has center (h, r₂), radius r₂, diameter DE along y = r₂ from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂), and the semicircle is the lower half: y ≤ r₂.\n\n- S₁ is inscribed in S₂: its diameter FG is a chord of S₂, and its arc is tangent to DE (the line y = r₂). \n\nSince S₁ is inside S₂, which is the lower semicircle, the arc of S₁ must be the upper part of its own semicircle, curving upward towards DE. To be tangent to DE (the line y = r₂), the topmost point of S₁ must touch DE. \n\nThe diameter FG of S₁ is a horizontal chord of S₂. Let the center of S₁ be O₁ = (p, q). Since FG is horizontal, its endpoints are (p - 1, q) and (p + 1, q), since the radius is 1. The semicircle S₁ is the upper half of the circle centered at O₁ with radius 1, so its equation is (x - p)^2 + (y - q)^2 = 1, with y ≥ q. \n\nThis semicircle must be tangent to DE (the line y = r₂). The distance from the center O₁ to the line DE must be equal to the radius of S₁, which is 1. The distance from O₁ to DE (y = r₂) is |r₂ - q|. Since S₁ is below DE (as it's inside S₂), q must be less than r₂, so the distance is r₂ - q. Therefore:\n\nr₂ - q = 1 ⇒ q = r₂ - 1\n\nSo the center O₁ has coordinates (p, r₂ - 1). \n\nAdditionally, the endpoints F and G of S₁'s diameter must lie on S₂. The endpoints are (p - 1, r₂ - 1) and (p + 1, r₂ - 1). These points must satisfy the equation of S₂:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 ≤ r₂², and since they are on the boundary (as they are on the diameter FG of S₁, which is a chord of S₂), they must lie on the circle of S₂:\n\nFor point F: ( (p - 1) - h )² + ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ )² = r₂²\n\nSimplify:\n\n(p - 1 - h)^2 + (-1)^2 = r₂²\n\nSo,\n\n(p - (h + 1))² + 1 = r₂²\n\nSimilarly for point G: same equation, so no new info.\n\nThus, we have:\n\n(p - h - 1)^2 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ (p - h - 1)^2 = r₂² - 1\n\nAdditionally, the center O₁ = (p, r₂ - 1) must lie along the line perpendicular to FG through its midpoint (which is O₁ itself). Since FG is horizontal, the perpendicular is vertical, so the center O₁ is vertically above/below the midpoint, but since it's the center, that's already satisfied.\n\nFurthermore, since FG is a chord of S₂, the center O₁ must lie along the line perpendicular to FG through its midpoint, but since FG is horizontal, the perpendicular is vertical, so the center O₁ lies along the vertical line passing through the midpoint of FG, which is O₁ itself. Therefore, no additional constraints there.\n\nNow, we also know that S₁'s center O₁ must lie inside S₂. Since S₂ is the lower semicircle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂, the point O₁ = (p, r₂ - 1) must satisfy:\n\nThe distance from O₁ to the center of S₂, O₂ = (h, r₂), must be less than or equal to the radius of S₂, r₂. \n\nCompute the distance between O₁ and O₂:\n\n√[ (p - h)^2 + ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ )^2 ] = √[ (p - h)^2 + (-1)^2 ] = √[ (p - h)^2 + 1 ]\n\nThis distance must be ≤ r₂:\n\n√[ (p - h)^2 + 1 ] ≤ r₂ ⇒ (p - h)^2 + 1 ≤ r₂² ⇒ (p - h)^2 ≤ r₂² - 1\n\nBut from earlier, we have:\n\n(p - h - 1)^2 = r₂² - 1 ⇒ (p - h)^2 - 2(p - h) + 1 = r₂² - 1 ⇒ (p - h)^2 - 2(p - h) = r₂² - 2\n\nHmm, this might not be helpful immediately. Let's see if we can relate p and h.\n\nLet me denote d = p - h. Then the equation from the chord condition becomes:\n\n(d - 1)^2 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ d² - 2d + 1 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ d² - 2d + 2 = r₂²\n\nAnd the distance condition from O₁ to O₂:\n\n√[d² + 1] ≤ r₂ ⇒ d² + 1 ≤ r₂² ⇒ d² + 1 ≤ d² - 2d + 2 ⇒ 1 ≤ -2d + 2 ⇒ -2d + 2 ≥ 1 ⇒ -2d ≥ -1 ⇒ d ≤ 1/2\n\nSo d = p - h ≤ 1/2.\n\nFrom the first equation: d² - 2d + 2 = r₂²\n\nWe also have the earlier equation from the chord DE in S₃:\n\n2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nSo we have two equations:\n\n1. d² - 2d + 2 = r₂²\n\n2. 2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nAnd we have d = p - h, but we might need another relation to connect p and h. \n\nHowever, perhaps we can express everything in terms of d and r₂, then eliminate variables.\n\nFrom equation 1: r₂² = d² - 2d + 2\n\nPlugging into equation 2:\n\n2*(d² - 2d + 2) + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nExpand:\n\n2d² - 4d + 4 + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nSo:\n\n2d² - 4d + (h - 10)^2 = 96\n\nAdditionally, we have from the definition of d: d = p - h, but we don't have a direct relation between p and h yet. \n\nHowever, perhaps we can find another equation involving p and h from the geometry of S₁ and S₂.\n\nThe center O₁ = (p, r₂ - 1) must lie along the line connecting the center of S₂ (h, r₂) to the point of tangency between S₁ and DE. Wait, since S₁ is tangent to DE at its topmost point, which is (p, r₂). Because the center of S₁ is at (p, r₂ - 1), so the topmost point is directly above it at (p, r₂ - 1 + 1) = (p, r₂). Therefore, the point of tangency is (p, r₂), which lies on DE (the line y = r₂). Therefore, this point (p, r₂) must lie on DE, which is the diameter of S₂ from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂). Therefore, the x-coordinate p must satisfy h - r₂ ≤ p ≤ h + r₂.\n\nTherefore, p is between h - r₂ and h + r₂.\n\nAdditionally, since the center O₁ = (p, r₂ - 1) must lie inside S₂, which is the lower semicircle centered at (h, r₂) with radius r₂. The condition for O₁ being inside S₂ is that the distance from O₁ to O₂ is less than or equal to r₂, which we already used to get d ≤ 1/2.\n\nMoreover, the point (p, r₂) where S₁ is tangent to DE must lie on DE, so p must be between h - r₂ and h + r₂.\n\nNow, we need another relation to connect p and h. \n\nLooking back at the chord FG of S₂: the endpoints F and G are at (p - 1, r₂ - 1) and (p + 1, r₂ - 1). These points must lie on the circle S₂, which we already used to get the equation involving d. \n\nAlternatively, since DE is the diameter of S₂, and the point of tangency of S₁ is (p, r₂), which is on DE, perhaps there is a geometric relation here.\n\nThe point (p, r₂) is the point where S₁ touches DE. Since S₁'s center is (p, r₂ - 1), the line connecting O₁ to the point of tangency (p, r₂) is vertical, which makes sense because the tangent at the point of tangency to a circle is perpendicular to the radius. Since DE is horizontal, the radius at the point of tangency must be vertical, so the center O₁ must be directly below (p, r₂) by 1 unit, which is consistent with O₁ being at (p, r₂ - 1).\n\nTherefore, this doesn't provide new information.\n\nPerhaps we can choose to let p = h, to simplify? If we assume that the point of tangency is directly below the center of S₂, then p = h. Let's see if that works.\n\nAssume p = h. Then d = p - h = 0.\n\nPlugging into equation 1:\n\nr₂² = 0² - 2*0 + 2 = 2 ⇒ r₂ = √2, same as before.\n\nThen equation 2 becomes:\n\n2*(2) + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 4 + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ (h - 10)^2 = 96 ⇒ h = 10 ± √96 ≈ 10 ± 9.798\n\nSame as before. But as before, this places h outside the allowable range. \n\nHowever, if we assume p = h, then the point of tangency is (h, r₂), which is the center of S₂ projected onto DE. But in this case, the semicircle S₂'s center is at (h, r₂), so the point (h, r₂) is actually the center of S₂, but DE is the diameter along y = r₂ from (h - r₂, r₂) to (h + r₂, r₂). Therefore, the center of S₂ is the midpoint of DE, so (h, r₂) is the midpoint, so (h, r₂) is on DE. Therefore, the point of tangency (p, r₂) = (h, r₂) is actually the center of S₂. But the semicircle S₁ is supposed to be tangent to DE at that point, but the center of S₂ is on DE, so if S₁ is tangent there, then S₁ would have its center at (h, r₂ - 1), and the point (h, r₂) is on both S₁ and DE. The distance from O₁ to (h, r₂) is 1, which is the radius, so that's okay. However, the problem is the positioning of S₂ itself.\n\nIf we take h = 10 + √96 ≈ 19.798, then the center of S₂ is at (19.798, √2 ≈ 1.414), and the diameter DE extends from (19.798 - 1.414, 1.414) to (19.798 + 1.414, 1.414), which is roughly from (18.384, 1.414) to (21.212, 1.414). The right end at x ≈21.212 exceeds the diameter AB of S₃ which ends at x=20, so that's outside. \n\nSimilarly, for h = 10 - √96 ≈ 0.202, the diameter DE extends from (0.202 - 1.414, 1.414) ≈ (-1.212, 1.414) to (0.202 + 1.414, 1.414) ≈ (1.616, 1.414), so the left end is outside AB. \n\nTherefore, even with p = h, we still can't have S₂ entirely within S₃. This suggests that assuming p = h is invalid or that there's another constraint we're missing.\n\nPerhaps the error is in assuming that both endpoints F and G of S₁'s diameter must lie on S₂. The problem states that S₁ is inscribed in S₂, which typically would mean that its entire arc is within S₂ and tangent to its diameter. However, the exact definition says: \"a semicircle is inscribed in another semicircle if the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle, and the smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\"\n\nTherefore, the diameter of S₁ must be a chord of S₂, and its arc must be tangent to the diameter of S₂ (DE). The endpoints of S₁'s diameter don't necessarily have to lie on S₂'s boundary, only that the diameter is a chord of S₂. Wait, hold on: the definition says \"the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle\". So the diameter of S₁ must be a chord of S₂, meaning that the entire diameter lies on S₂'s interior or boundary? Or just that the diameter is a chord, i.e., its endpoints are on S₂?\n\nTypically, a chord of a circle is a straight line segment whose endpoints both lie on the circle. So the diameter of S₁ must be a chord of S₂, meaning its two endpoints lie on S₂. Therefore, my initial assumption that the endpoints F and G must lie on S₂ is correct. \n\nGiven that, then with the given radii, it's impossible? But the problem states such a configuration exists. Therefore, I must have an error in my calculations or assumptions.\n\nWait a second, let's go back to the very beginning and consider the possibility that the semicircles are drawn with the diameter as the arc? Wait no, semicircles are defined by their diameter being a straight line, and the arc being the curved part.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the semicircle S₂ is not required to be entirely within S₃, just that its diameter is a chord of S₃ and it's tangent to AB. Maybe \"inscribed\" just means that it's tangent and has its diameter as a chord, not necessarily entirely inside? But usually inscribed implies inside.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the semicircles are oriented differently. For example, S₂ could be a semicircle above its diameter DE, which is a chord of S₃ not on the x-axis, and tangent to AB at point C. In this case, the center O₂ is somewhere above AB, and the semicircle curves down to touch AB at C. \n\nLet me try this approach without assuming DE is horizontal.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- S₃: diameter AB on x-axis from (0,0) to (20,0), center at (10,0), radius 10.\n\n- S₂: semicircle inscribed in S₃, with diameter DE (a chord of S₃), and arc tangent to AB at C.\n\n- S₁: semicircle inscribed in S₂, with diameter FG (a chord of S₂), radius 1, arc tangent to DE.\n\n- FG is horizontal (parallel to AB).\n\nWe need to find AC · CB where C is the point of tangency on AB.\n\nLet me denote the center of S₂ as O₂ = (h, k), and its radius as r₂. Since S₂ is tangent to AB (the x-axis) at point C, the distance from O₂ to AB is equal to r₂, so k = r₂. Therefore, O₂ = (h, r₂).\n\nThe diameter DE of S₂ is a chord of S₃. The endpoints D and E lie on S₃: (x - 10)^2 + y^2 = 100.\n\nThe midpoint of DE is O₂ = (h, r₂), so the line from O₃ (10,0) to O₂ (h, r₂) is perpendicular to DE.\n\nThe length of DE is 2r₂ (since it's the diameter of S₂), so using the chord length formula in S₃:\n\nDE = 2r₂ = 2√(R² - d²), where R = 10 (radius of S₃), and d is the distance from O₃ to DE.\n\nThe distance d from O₃ to DE is equal to the distance from O₃ to the line DE, which we calculated earlier as sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ). Wait, no, earlier we found that the distance from O₃ to DE is sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ), but according to the chord length formula:\n\nWait, chord length DE = 2r₂ = 2√(R² - d²) ⇒ r₂ = √(100 - d²). But d is the distance from O₃ to DE, which we found earlier to be sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ). Wait, this seems conflicting.\n\nWait, let me re-derive this properly:\n\nThe distance from O₃ to DE is d = |O₃O₂| projected onto the direction perpendicular to DE? Or is it the length of O₃O₂?\n\nWait, since O₂ is the midpoint of DE and the line O₃O₂ is perpendicular to DE, then the distance from O₃ to DE is exactly the length of the vector from O₃ to O₂, because O₂ is the closest point on DE to O₃. Therefore, d = |O₃O₂| = sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + (r₂ - 0)^2 ) = sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nThen the chord length DE = 2*sqrt(R² - d²) = 2*sqrt(100 - [ (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ] )\n\nBut DE is also equal to 2r₂ (since it's the diameter of S₂). Therefore:\n\n2r₂ = 2*sqrt(100 - ( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) )\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\nr₂ = sqrt(100 - (h - 10)^2 - r₂² )\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nr₂² = 100 - (h - 10)^2 - r₂²\n\nBring terms together:\n\n2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100\n\nSame equation as before. So that part is correct.\n\nNow, moving on to S₁. \n\nS₁ has diameter FG parallel to AB (horizontal), so its center O₁ is at (p, q), and the diameter FG has endpoints at (p - 1, q) and (p + 1, q). The semicircle S₁ is tangent to DE, so the distance from O₁ to line DE must be equal to the radius of S₁, which is 1.\n\nThe line DE passes through O₂ = (h, r₂) and has a slope perpendicular to O₃O₂. The slope of O₃O₂ is (r₂ - 0)/(h - 10) = r₂/(h - 10), so the slope of DE is m_DE = - (h - 10)/r₂.\n\nThe equation of DE is:\n\ny - r₂ = m_DE (x - h) = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ] (x - h )\n\nThe distance from O₁ = (p, q) to this line must be 1:\n\n| [ - (h - 10)/r₂ (p - h) - (q - r₂) ] | / sqrt( [ (h - 10)/r₂ ]^2 + 1 ) = 1\n\nThis seems complex, but let's plug in q = r₂ - 1, since earlier we had that the center O₁ must be at (p, r₂ - 1) to have the distance to DE equal to 1 (since the distance from O₁ to DE is r₂ - q = 1 ⇒ q = r₂ - 1). \n\nSo substituting q = r₂ - 1:\n\nThe distance becomes:\n\n| [ - (h - 10)/r₂ (p - h) - ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ ) ] | / sqrt( [ (h - 10)^2 / r₂² ] + 1 ) = 1\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n= | [ - (h - 10)(p - h)/r₂ - (-1) ] | \n\n= | [ - (h - 10)(p - h)/r₂ + 1 ] | \n\nDenominator:\n\nsqrt( [ (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ] / r₂² ) = sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ \n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n| [ - (h - 10)(p - h)/r₂ + 1 ] | / [ sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) / r₂ ] = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by denominator:\n\n| [ - (h - 10)(p - h) + r₂ ] | / sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) = r₂\n\nMultiply both sides by sqrt(...):\n\n| - (h - 10)(p - h) + r₂ | = r₂ * sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nThis is getting very complicated. Perhaps we can find another relation.\n\nSince the diameter FG of S₁ is a chord of S₂, the endpoints F and G must lie on S₂. The endpoints are (p - 1, r₂ - 1) and (p + 1, r₂ - 1). These points must satisfy the equation of S₂'s circle:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂²\n\nPlugging in F's coordinates:\n\n(p - 1 - h)^2 + ( (r₂ - 1) - r₂ )^2 = r₂²\n\nSimplify:\n\n(p - h - 1)^2 + (-1)^2 = r₂² ⇒ (p - h - 1)^2 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ (p - h - 1)^2 = r₂² - 1\n\nLet me denote d = p - h again. Then:\n\n(d - 1)^2 = r₂² - 1 ⇒ d² - 2d + 1 = r₂² - 1 ⇒ d² - 2d + 2 = r₂²\n\nThis is the same equation as before. \n\nAdditionally, we have the previous equation from the distance condition:\n\n| - (h - 10)d + r₂ | = r₂ * sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nLet me denote t = h - 10 for simplicity. Then h = t + 10. Let's rewrite everything in terms of t and d and r₂.\n\nFirst, equation from chord FG:\n\nd² - 2d + 2 = r₂² ⇒ r₂² = d² - 2d + 2\n\nSecond, the distance equation:\n\n| - t*d + r₂ | = r₂ * sqrt(t² + r₂² )\n\nAnd from the chord DE in S₃:\n\n2r₂² + t² = 100 ⇒ 2r₂² = 100 - t² ⇒ r₂² = (100 - t²)/2\n\nSo now we have two expressions for r₂²:\n\n1. r₂² = d² - 2d + 2\n\n2. r₂² = (100 - t²)/2\n\nTherefore, setting them equal:\n\nd² - 2d + 2 = (100 - t²)/2 ⇒ 2d² - 4d + 4 = 100 - t² ⇒ t² = 100 - 2d² + 4d - 4 ⇒ t² = 96 - 2d² + 4d\n\nAlso, the distance equation:\n\n| - t*d + r₂ | = r₂ * sqrt(t² + r₂² )\n\nLet me express r₂ in terms of t and d using equation 2: r₂² = (100 - t²)/2 ⇒ r₂ = sqrt( (100 - t²)/2 )\n\nBut this might get too messy. Let me instead square both sides of the distance equation to eliminate the absolute value and the square root:\n\n[ - t*d + r₂ ]² = r₂² (t² + r₂² )\n\nExpand left side:\n\nt² d² - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² = r₂² t² + r₂^4\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\nt² d² - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² - r₂² t² - r₂^4 = 0\n\nFactor terms:\n\nt² d² - r₂² t² - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² - r₂^4 = 0\n\nFactor t² terms:\n\nt² (d² - r₂²) - 2 t d r₂ + (r₂² - r₂^4) = 0\n\nRecall from equation 1: d² - 2d + 2 = r₂² ⇒ d² - r₂² = 2d - 2\n\nSubstitute d² - r₂² = 2d - 2 into the above:\n\nt² (2d - 2) - 2 t d r₂ + (r₂² - r₂^4) = 0\n\nFactor out 2 from the first term:\n\n2 t² (d - 1) - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² (1 - r₂²) = 0\n\nThis is still complex. Let's substitute r₂² from equation 2: r₂² = (100 - t²)/2\n\nTherefore, 1 - r₂² = 1 - (100 - t²)/2 = (2 - 100 + t²)/2 = (t² - 98)/2\n\nAlso, r₂ = sqrt( (100 - t²)/2 )\n\nLet me plug these into the equation:\n\n2 t² (d - 1) - 2 t d * sqrt( (100 - t²)/2 ) + ( (100 - t²)/2 ) * ( (t² - 98)/2 ) = 0\n\nThis is extremely complicated. Perhaps we need to find a relationship between t and d from previous equations.\n\nFrom equation for t²:\n\nt² = 96 - 2d² + 4d\n\nSo t² = -2d² + 4d + 96\n\nLet me substitute this into the expression for r₂²:\n\nr₂² = (100 - t²)/2 = (100 - (-2d² + 4d + 96))/2 = (100 + 2d² - 4d - 96)/2 = (2d² - 4d + 4)/2 = d² - 2d + 2\n\nWhich matches equation 1, so that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can express everything in terms of d.\n\nLet me let’s denote r₂² = d² - 2d + 2.\n\nThen the distance equation squared becomes:\n\n[ - t d + r₂ ]² = r₂² (t² + r₂² )\n\nExpand left side:\n\nt² d² - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² = r₂² t² + r₂^4\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\nt² d² - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² - r₂² t² - r₂^4 = 0\n\nFactor terms:\n\nt²(d² - r₂²) - 2 t d r₂ + r₂²(1 - r₂²) = 0\n\nAs before. Now substitute d² - r₂² = 2d - 2 and r₂² = d² - 2d + 2:\n\nFirst term: t²(2d - 2)\n\nSecond term: -2 t d r₂\n\nThird term: r₂²(1 - r₂²) = r₂² - r₂^4\n\nSo:\n\n2 t² (d - 1) - 2 t d r₂ + r₂² - r₂^4 = 0\n\nNow, substitute r₂² = d² - 2d + 2:\n\nr₂^4 = (d² - 2d + 2)^2\n\nSo:\n\n2 t² (d - 1) - 2 t d r₂ + (d² - 2d + 2) - (d² - 2d + 2)^2 = 0\n\nThis is a quartic equation in d, which is very complicated. Perhaps we need to find numerical solutions?\n\nAlternatively, let's assume specific values. Recall that in the TikZ diagram provided, point C is at (8.3, 0). This suggests that h ≈8.3, since C is (h, 0). So perhaps h is around 8.3, which is between 0 and 20, and within the required [1.414, 18.586] interval.\n\nLet me suppose that h = 10 - 4√6 ≈ 10 - 9.798 ≈ 0.202 is too small, and h = 10 + 4√6 ≈ 19.798 is too big, but maybe there's another solution?\n\nWait, but according to the equation 2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100, with r₂ = √2, we only get those two solutions. So unless r₂ is different?\n\nWait, but earlier we derived that r₂ must be √2 because of the condition on S₁'s endpoints lying on S₂. But that led to an inconsistency. Therefore, perhaps my assumption that the endpoints of S₁'s diameter must lie on S₂ is wrong?\n\nRe-reading the problem: \"A semicircle is inscribed in another semicircle if the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle, and the smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\"\n\nThis definition only requires that the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle and its arc is tangent to the larger's diameter. It does not explicitly require that the endpoints of the smaller's diameter lie on the larger's circumference. Wait, but a chord of a circle must have its endpoints on the circle. Therefore, the diameter of the smaller semicircle must be a chord of the larger semicircle, meaning its endpoints are on the larger semicircle.\n\nTherefore, my earlier conclusion holds: the endpoints must lie on S₂. Therefore, the problem as posed might have no solution with the given radii? But the problem says to compute AC · CB, so there must be a solution.\n\nPerhaps I made a mistake in the sign when calculating the distance for the distance equation?\n\nLet me revisit the distance equation:\n\nThe distance from O₁ to line DE must be 1. O₁ is at (p, r₂ - 1), and DE has equation:\n\ny = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ](x - h) + r₂\n\nThe distance from (p, r₂ - 1) to this line is 1.\n\nUsing the distance formula:\n\n| A*p + B*(r₂ - 1) + C | / sqrt(A² + B²) = 1\n\nWhere the line DE is written as Ax + By + C = 0.\n\nLet me rewrite DE's equation in standard form:\n\nStarting from y - r₂ = [ - (h - 10)/r₂ ](x - h)\n\nMultiply both sides by r₂:\n\nr₂(y - r₂) = - (h - 10)(x - h)\n\nExpand:\n\nr₂ y - r₂² = - (h - 10)x + (h - 10)h\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n(h - 10)x + r₂ y - [ (h - 10)h + r₂² ] = 0\n\nTherefore, A = (h - 10), B = r₂, C = - [ (h - 10)h + r₂² ]\n\nTherefore, the distance from O₁ = (p, r₂ - 1) to DE is:\n\n| (h - 10)p + r₂*(r₂ - 1) - [ (h - 10)h + r₂² ] | / sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) = 1\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n= | (h - 10)p + r₂(r₂ - 1) - (h - 10)h - r₂² | \n\n= | (h - 10)(p - h) + r₂(r₂ - 1) - r₂² | \n\n= | (h - 10)(p - h) + r₂² - r₂ - r₂² | \n\n= | (h - 10)(p - h) - r₂ | \n\nTherefore, the distance equation becomes:\n\n| (h - 10)(p - h) - r₂ | / sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² ) = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by denominator:\n\n| (h - 10)(p - h) - r₂ | = sqrt( (h - 10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nSquare both sides:\n\n[ (h - 10)(p - h) - r₂ ]^2 = (h - 10)^2 + r₂²\n\nExpand left side:\n\n(h - 10)^2 (p - h)^2 - 2 (h - 10)(p - h) r₂ + r₂² = (h - 10)^2 + r₂²\n\nSubtract right side:\n\n(h - 10)^2 (p - h)^2 - 2 (h - 10)(p - h) r₂ + r₂² - (h - 10)^2 - r₂² = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n(h - 10)^2 [ (p - h)^2 - 1 ] - 2 (h - 10)(p - h) r₂ = 0\n\nFactor out (h - 10):\n\n(h - 10) [ (h - 10)( (p - h)^2 - 1 ) - 2 (p - h) r₂ ] = 0\n\nSo either h - 10 = 0 or the term in brackets is zero.\n\nCase 1: h - 10 = 0 ⇒ h = 10\n\nIf h = 10, then from equation 2r₂² + (h - 10)^2 = 100 ⇒ 2r₂² = 100 ⇒ r₂² = 50 ⇒ r₂ = 5√2 ≈ 7.071\n\nThen, from the equation for S₁'s endpoints:\n\n(p - h - 1)^2 + 1 = r₂² ⇒ (p - 11)^2 + 1 = 50 ⇒ (p - 11)^2 = 49 ⇒ p - 11 = ±7 ⇒ p = 18 or p = 4\n\nSo p = 18 or p = 4.\n\nNow, let's check the distance equation in this case.\n\nWith h = 10 and r₂ = 5√2:\n\nThe line DE is the diameter of S₂, which is a horizontal line? Wait, if h = 10, then the center O₂ is at (10, 5√2). The diameter DE is a chord of S₃ (centered at (10,0), radius 10), with midpoint at (10, 5√2). The line DE is perpendicular to the line from O₃ to O₂, which is vertical (since O₂ is directly above O₃). Therefore, DE must be horizontal. So DE is the horizontal line through O₂: y = 5√2. \n\nThe semicircle S₂ is the lower or upper semicircle? Since it's tangent to AB (the x-axis), and its center is at (10, 5√2), the distance from O₂ to AB is 5√2 ≈7.071, which must equal the radius r₂ =5√2. Therefore, the semicircle S₂ is the lower semicircle, curving down to the x-axis. \n\nThe diameter DE is the line y = 5√2 from (10 - 5√2, 5√2) to (10 + 5√2, 5√2). Since 5√2 ≈7.071, so DE extends from approximately (10 - 7.071, 7.071) ≈ (2.929, 7.071) to (17.071, 7.071). This is within the diameter AB of S₃ (from 0 to 20), so that's okay.\n\nNow, S₁ is inscribed in S₂, with diameter FG parallel to AB (horizontal), radius 1, tangent to DE (the line y = 5√2). The center O₁ is at (p, r₂ - 1) = (p, 5√2 - 1). The distance from O₁ to DE (y = 5√2) is 1, which matches the radius, so that's good.\n\nThe endpoints of FG are at (p - 1, 5√2 - 1) and (p + 1, 5√2 - 1), which must lie on S₂'s circle:\n\n(x - 10)^2 + (y - 5√2)^2 = (5√2)^2 = 50\n\nPlugging in (p - 1, 5√2 - 1):\n\n(p - 1 - 10)^2 + ( (5√2 - 1) - 5√2 )^2 = 50\n\nSimplify:\n\n(p - 11)^2 + (-1)^2 = 50 ⇒ (p - 11)^2 + 1 = 50 ⇒ (p - 11)^2 = 49 ⇒ p = 11 ±7 ⇒ p = 18 or p = 4, as before.\n\nNow, let's compute AC · CB where C is the point of tangency on AB. Since S₂ is tangent to AB at point C, which is directly below O₂. Since O₂ is at (10, 5√2), the point C is (10, 0). Therefore, C is at (10, 0), so AC = 10 units, CB = 10 units, so AC · CB = 100. \n\nWait, but in the TikZ code provided, point C is at (8.3, 0), but in this case, with h =10, C is at (10, 0). Hmm, discrepancy here. However, the problem doesn't specify that the configuration must match the TikZ diagram exactly, just that it's an illustration. \n\nBut according to this case, if h =10, then AC · CB = 10 * 10 = 100. But let me verify if this is valid.\n\nWait, if h =10, then the center of S₂ is at (10, 5√2), and the semicircle S₂ curves down to (10, 0), so it is tangent to AB at (10,0). Then S₁ is centered at (p, 5√2 -1). If p = 18, then O₁ is at (18, 5√2 -1). The diameter FG is from (17, 5√2 -1) to (19, 5√2 -1). These points must lie on S₂'s circle:\n\nFor (17, 5√2 -1):\n\n(x -10)^2 + (y -5√2)^2 = (7)^2 + (-1)^2 = 49 +1 = 50, which matches the radius squared.\n\nSimilarly for (19, 5√2 -1): same result. So that works.\n\nSimilarly for p =4: O₁ is at (4, 5√2 -1), endpoints at (3, 5√2 -1) and (5, 5√2 -1). Plugging into the circle equation:\n\n(3 -10)^2 + (-1)^2 = 49 +1=50, same for (5 -10)^2 + (-1)^2 = 25 +1=26 ≠50. Wait, no! Wait, 5 -10 is -5, squared is 25, plus (-1)^2 is 26, which is not 50. Wait, that's a problem.\n\nWait, if p =4, then the left endpoint is (4 -1, 5√2 -1) = (3, 5√2 -1). Plugging into the circle equation:\n\n(x -10)^2 + (y -5√2)^2 = (3 -10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = (-7)^2 + (-1)^2 = 49 +1 = 50 ✔️\n\nSimilarly, the right endpoint (5, 5√2 -1):\n\n(5 -10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = (-5)^2 + (-1)^2 = 25 +1 = 26 ≠50. Wait, this is incorrect!\n\nWait a second, if p =4, then the right endpoint is p +1 = 5, so x=5, y=5√2 -1. \n\nWait, but according to the calculation:\n\n(x -10)^2 + (y -5√2)^2 = (5 -10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = (-5)^2 + (-1)^2 = 25 +1 =26, which is not 50. This is a problem. \n\nWait, what did I do wrong here? Earlier, when solving for p when h=10, we had:\n\n(p -11)^2 = 49 ⇒ p =11 ±7 ⇒ p=18 or p=4.\n\nBut when p=4, the right endpoint is at x = p+1=5, which is not on the circle. Wait, but the left endpoint is at x=3, which is okay, but the right endpoint at x=5 is not? \n\nWait no, actually the endpoints are at p ±1 in x-coordinate. Wait, the diameter FG is horizontal with center at p, so the endpoints are at (p-1, q) and (p+1, q). So if p=4, the endpoints are at x=3 and x=5, both at y=5√2 -1. \n\nBut plugging x=5 into the circle equation:\n\n(5 -10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = 25 + 1 =26 ≠50. This is a contradiction. \n\nWait, but earlier when I plugged in (p-1, q) where p=4, x=3:\n\n(3-10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = 49 +1 =50 ✔️\n\nBut for x=5, it's (5-10)^2 + (same y term)^2 =25 +1=26. This inconsistency arises because when p=4, the right endpoint is at x=p+1=5, which does not satisfy the circle equation, but the left endpoint does. This suggests an error in the assumption.\n\nWait, this must mean that only one of the endpoints lies on the circle, but the other doesn't, which contradicts the requirement that both endpoints are on the circle (since the diameter must be a chord of S₂). Therefore, p=4 is invalid, while p=18 is valid?\n\nWait, let's check p=18:\n\nEndpoints at x=17 and x=19.\n\nFor x=17:\n\n(17-10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = 49 +1 =50 ✔️\n\nFor x=19:\n\n(19-10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = 81 +1=82 ≠50. Wait, that's also not good.\n\nWait a second, this is strange. Both endpoints when p=18:\n\nx=17 and x=19.\n\nAt x=19:\n\n(19 -10)^2 + (y -5√2)^2 = 9² + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )² = 81 + (-1)^2 = 82 ≠50.\n\nWait, this is not working either. \n\nHold on, there must be a mistake in my calculations. Let me recompute for p=18:\n\nThe center O₁ is at (18, 5√2 -1). The endpoints are at (17, 5√2 -1) and (19, 5√2 -1).\n\nPlugging (17, 5√2 -1) into S₂'s circle equation:\n\n(x -10)^2 + (y -5√2)^2 = (17-10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = 7² + (-1)^2 = 49 +1 =50 ✔️\n\nPlugging (19, 5√2 -1):\n\n(19-10)^2 + ( (5√2 -1) -5√2 )^2 = 9² + (-1)^2 =81 +1 =82 ≠50. \n\nSo only the left endpoint is on the circle, the right endpoint isn't. This is a problem. \n\nWait, what is going on here? Earlier, when we derived the equation for p, we had:\n\n(p - h -1)^2 +1 = r₂² ⇒ with h=10, r₂=5√2,\n\n(p -11)^2 +1 = (5√2)^2 =50 ⇒ (p-11)^2 =49 ⇒ p=18 or 4. \n\nBut when p=18, the right endpoint is at x=19, which is not on the circle. This suggests an error in the derivation. \n\nWait, let's re-derive the equation for the endpoints:\n\nThe endpoints of FG are at (p -1, q) and (p +1, q), where q = r₂ -1.\n\nThese points must satisfy the circle equation of S₂:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂² ⇒ (x - h)^2 + (y - r₂)^2 = r₂² ⇒ (x - h)^2 + y² - 2 y r₂ + r₂² = r₂² ⇒ (x - h)^2 + y² - 2 y r₂ =0.\n\nPlugging in y = q = r₂ -1:\n\n(x - h)^2 + (r₂ -1)^2 - 2(r₂ -1)r₂ =0\n\nExpand:\n\n(x - h)^2 + r₂² - 2 r₂ +1 - 2 r₂² + 2 r₂ =0 ⇒ (x - h)^2 - r₂² +1 =0 ⇒ (x - h)^2 = r₂² -1\n\nTherefore, the x-coordinates of the endpoints must satisfy (x - h)^2 = r₂² -1 ⇒ x = h ± sqrt(r₂² -1)\n\nTherefore, the endpoints are at x = h + sqrt(r₂² -1) and x = h - sqrt(r₂² -1). Therefore, the diameter FG has length 2 sqrt(r₂² -1), so the radius of S₁ is sqrt(r₂² -1). But the problem states that the radius of S₁ is 1, so:\n\nsqrt(r₂² -1) =1 ⇒ r₂² -1 =1 ⇒ r₂²=2 ⇒ r₂=√2. \n\nAh! Here is the mistake. Earlier, I assumed the radius of S₁ is 1, so the diameter is 2, so the distance between the endpoints is 2. However, according to this derivation, the length of FG is 2 sqrt(r₂² -1), so setting that equal to 2 (the diameter of S₁):\n\n2 sqrt(r₂² -1) = 2 ⇒ sqrt(r₂² -1) =1 ⇒ r₂² -1=1 ⇒ r₂²=2 ⇒ r₂=√2. \n\nTherefore, my previous assumption that the endpoints' x-coordinates are p ±1 was incorrect. Instead, the diameter FG has length 2 sqrt(r₂² -1), which for r₂=√2 gives sqrt(2 -1)=1, so length 2*1=2, which matches the diameter of S₁ (radius 1). \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints are not simply p ±1, but rather the center O₁ is at (h ± sqrt(r₂² -1), q). Wait, no. Let me clarify:\n\nFrom the above, for S₁'s endpoints to lie on S₂'s circle, their x-coordinates must satisfy (x - h)^2 = r₂² -1 ⇒ x = h ± sqrt(r₂² -1). Therefore, the diameter FG is centered at x = h, since the two x-coordinates are symmetric around h. Therefore, the center O₁ must be at (h, q), where q = r₂ -1. \n\nTherefore, the diameter FG is centered at (h, q), with endpoints at (h ± sqrt(r₂² -1), q). Therefore, the length of FG is 2 sqrt(r₂² -1), which must equal 2 (since radius of S₁ is 1), so sqrt(r₂² -1)=1 ⇒ r₂² =2 ⇒ r₂=√2. \n\nTherefore, the center O₁ is at (h, √2 -1). The diameter FG extends from (h -1, √2 -1) to (h +1, √2 -1), which is what I originally assumed. But then why did plugging in the coordinates for p=18 not work?\n\nWait, if h=10, then O₁ is at (10, √2 -1). The endpoints are at (10 ±1, √2 -1). Plugging into S₂'s circle equation:\n\nFor (11, √2 -1):\n\n(x -10)^2 + (y - √2)^2 = (1)^2 + ( (√2 -1) - √2 )^2 = 1 + (-1)^2 = 2. But S₂'s radius squared is (√2)^2 =2, so this equals 2, which matches. \n\nWait a second, I think I made a mistake earlier when I plugged in h=10 and r₂=5√2. But in this case, when h=10, we have r₂ must be √2, not 5√2. \n\nAh! This is the key mistake. Earlier, when I considered case 1 where h=10, I incorrectly used r₂ =5√2 from equation 2r₂² + (h-10)^2=100 ⇒ 2r₂²=100 ⇒ r₂=√50=5√2. But this contradicts the requirement from S₁'s condition that r₂ must be √2. \n\nTherefore, case 1 (h=10) is invalid because it requires r₂ to be both √2 and 5√2 simultaneously, which is impossible. Therefore, case 1 is invalid. \n\nThus, the only possibility is the other case from the equation:\n\n(h - 10)[ (h - 10)( (p - h)^2 - 1 ) - 2 (p - h) r₂ ] = 0\n\nSince h -10 ≠0, then the term in brackets must be zero:\n\n(h - 10)( (p - h)^2 - 1 ) - 2 (p - h) r₂ = 0\n\nLet me denote d = p - h again. Then the equation becomes:\n\n(h - 10)(d² - 1) - 2 d r₂ =0\n\nWe also have from before:\n\n1. r₂² = d² - 2d + 2\n\n2. 2r₂² + (h -10)^2 =100 ⇒ 2(d² - 2d + 2) + (h -10)^2 =100 ⇒ 2d² -4d +4 + (h -10)^2 =100 ⇒ (h -10)^2 =96 -2d² +4d\n\nLet me substitute (h -10)^2 from equation 2 into the bracket equation:\n\n(h -10)(d² -1) -2 d r₂ =0 ⇒ [sqrt(96 -2d² +4d)]*(d² -1) -2 d r₂ =0\n\nWait, no. Since (h -10)^2 =96 -2d² +4d, then (h -10) can be either sqrt(96 -2d² +4d) or -sqrt(...). This complicates things further.\n\nAlternatively, let's express r₂ from equation 1: r₂ = sqrt(d² -2d +2). Then plug into the bracket equation:\n\n(h -10)(d² -1) - 2 d sqrt(d² -2d +2) =0\n\nAnd from equation 2: (h -10)^2 =96 -2d² +4d ⇒ h -10 = ±sqrt(96 -2d² +4d)\n\nThis is very complex, but perhaps we can assume a value for d that simplifies the equation.\n\nRecall that in the TikZ diagram, point C is at (8.3,0), suggesting h ≈8.3. Let me try d = p - h. If h is around 8.3, and suppose p is around the center, maybe d is small.\n\nAlternatively, let me try d=1:\n\nIf d=1,\n\nthen r₂² =1 -2 +2=1 ⇒ r₂=1.\n\nThen equation from the bracket:\n\n(h -10)(1 -1) -2*1*1=0 ⇒0 -2= -2≠0. Not valid.\n\nd=0:\n\nr₂²=0 -0 +2=2 ⇒ r₂=√2,\n\nBracket equation:\n\n(h -10)(0 -1) -0 = -(h -10) =0 ⇒ h=10. But then we saw that this leads to inconsistency.\n\nd=2:\n\nr₂²=4 -4 +2=2 ⇒ r₂=√2,\n\nBracket equation:\n\n(h -10)(4 -1) -2*2*√2= 3(h-10) -4√2 =0 ⇒ 3(h-10)=4√2 ⇒ h=10 + (4√2)/3 ≈10 + 1.885 ≈11.885\n\nThen from equation 2:\n\n(h -10)^2 = (4√2/3)^2= (16*2)/9=32/9≈3.555\n\nThen 2r₂² + (h-10)^2 =2*2 +32/9≈4 +3.555≈7.555 ≠100. Not valid.\n\nHmm, not working.\n\nTry d= sqrt(2):\n\nr₂² = (sqrt(2))² -2*sqrt(2)+2 = 2 -2√2 +2=4-2√2≈4-2.828≈1.172 ⇒ r₂≈1.082.\n\nBracket equation:\n\n(h-10)( ( (sqrt(2))² -1 ) ) -2*sqrt(2)*r₂ ≈ (h-10)(2-1) -2*1.414*1.082≈ (h-10) -3.0≈0 ⇒ h≈13\n\nThen (h-10)^2≈9,\n\nthen 2r₂² + (h-10)^2≈2*(1.172)+9≈2.344+9≈11.344≠100. Nope.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to find d such that the equations are satisfied numerically.\n\nLet me denote t = h -10,\n\nand from equation 2: t² =96 -2d² +4d,\n\nand from the bracket equation:\n\nt*(d² -1) -2 d r₂ =0,\n\nand r₂ = sqrt(d² -2d +2).\n\nLet me write everything in terms of d:\n\nLet me express t = sqrt(96 -2d² +4d) or t = -sqrt(...). Let's assume t is positive first (h>10).\n\nThen:\n\nsqrt(96 -2d² +4d)*(d² -1) - 2 d sqrt(d² -2d +2) =0\n\nThis is a single equation in d. Let me attempt to solve it numerically.\n\nLet me define f(d) = sqrt(96 -2d² +4d)*(d² -1) - 2 d sqrt(d² -2d +2)\n\nWe need to find d such that f(d)=0.\n\nTry d=3:\n\nCompute inside sqrt for first term: 96 -2*(9) +4*3=96-18+12=90 ⇒ sqrt(90)=9.4868\n\nd² -1=9-1=8\n\nFirst term:9.4868 *8 ≈75.894\n\nSecond term:2*3*sqrt(9 -6 +2)=6*sqrt(5)=6*2.236≈13.416\n\nThus f(3)=75.894 -13.416≈62.478>0\n\nd=4:\n\nFirst sqrt: 96-32+16=80 ⇒ sqrt(80)=8.944\n\nd² -1=16-1=15\n\nFirst term:8.944*15≈134.16\n\nSecond term:2*4*sqrt(16-8+2)=8*sqrt(10)=8*3.162≈25.3\n\nf(4)=134.16 -25.3≈108.86>0\n\nd=5:\n\nFirst sqrt:96 -50 +20=66 ⇒ sqrt(66)=8.124\n\nd²-1=25-1=24\n\nFirst term:8.124*24≈194.98\n\nSecond term:2*5*sqrt(25-10+2)=10*sqrt(17)=10*4.123≈41.23\n\nf(5)=194.98-41.23≈153.75>0\n\nIncreasing d makes f(d) increase.\n\nTry d=2:\n\nFirst sqrt:96-8+8=96 ⇒ sqrt(96)=9.798\n\nd²-1=4-1=3\n\nFirst term:9.798*3≈29.394\n\nSecond term:2*2*sqrt(4-4+2)=4*sqrt(2)=5.656\n\nf(2)=29.394-5.656≈23.738>0\n\nd=1.5:\n\nFirst sqrt:96 - 2*(2.25) +4*(1.5)=96-4.5+6=97.5 ⇒ sqrt(97.5)≈9.874\n\nd²-1=2.25-1=1.25\n\nFirst term:9.874*1.25≈12.343\n\nSecond term:2*1.5*sqrt( (2.25) -3 +2 )=3*sqrt(1.25)=3*1.118≈3.354\n\nf(1.5)=12.343-3.354≈8.989>0\n\nd=1:\n\nFirst sqrt:96-2+4=98 ⇒ sqrt(98)=9.899\n\nd²-1=1-1=0 ⇒ first term=0\n\nSecond term:2*1*sqrt(1-2+2)=2*sqrt(1)=2\n\nf(1)=0-2=-2<0\n\nAh! At d=1, f(d)=-2; at d=1.5, f(d)=~9. So there's a root between d=1 and d=1.5.\n\nLet me try d=1.2:\n\nFirst sqrt:96 -2*(1.44)+4*(1.2)=96-2.88+4.8=98.92 ⇒ sqrt≈9.945\n\nd²-1=1.44-1=0.44\n\nFirst term:9.945*0.44≈4.369\n\nSecond term:2*1.2*sqrt( (1.44) - 2.4 +2 )=2.4*sqrt(1.04)=2.4*1.0198≈2.447\n\nf(1.2)=4.369-2.447≈1.922>0\n\nd=1.1:\n\nFirst sqrt:96-2*(1.21)+4*(1.1)=96-2.42+4.4=98. ⇒ sqrt(98)≈9.899\n\nd²-1=1.21-1=0.21\n\nFirst term:9.899*0.21≈2.078\n\nSecond term:2*1.1*sqrt(1.21-2.2+2)=2.2*sqrt(1.01)=2.2*1.005≈2.211\n\nf(1.1)=2.078 -2.211≈-0.133<0\n\nSo between d=1.1 and d=1.2, f(d) crosses zero.\n\nUsing linear approximation:\n\nAt d=1.1, f=-0.133\n\nAt d=1.2, f=+1.922\n\nSlope: (1.922 - (-0.133))/(0.1)=20.55 per unit d.\n\nNeed to reach zero from d=1.1:\n\ndelta_d= 0.133 /20.55≈0.0065\n\nThus, root at ≈1.1 +0.0065≈1.1065\n\nTry d=1.1065:\n\nCompute f(d):\n\nFirst compute t = sqrt(96 -2d² +4d):\n\nd=1.1065,\n\nd²≈1.225,\n\nso 96 -2*(1.225) +4*(1.1065)=96 -2.45 +4.426≈96 -2.45=93.55 +4.426≈97.976 ⇒ sqrt≈9.898\n\nd² -1≈1.225-1=0.225\n\nFirst term:9.898 *0.225≈2.227\n\nSecond term:2*d*sqrt(d²-2d+2)\n\nCompute inside sqrt: d²-2d+2≈1.225 -2.213 +2≈1.012 ⇒ sqrt≈1.006\n\nThus, second term≈2*1.1065*1.006≈2.223\n\nThus f(d)=2.227 -2.223≈0.004≈0. So close enough.\n\nThus, d≈1.1065\n\nThen r₂= sqrt(d²-2d+2)=sqrt(1.225 -2.213 +2)=sqrt(1.012)≈1.006\n\nThen t = h -10 = sqrt(96 -2d² +4d)=sqrt(96 -2*(1.225)+4*(1.1065))≈sqrt(96-2.45+4.426)=sqrt(97.976)=9.898\n\nThus h=10 + t≈10+9.898≈19.898\n\nWait, but this gives h≈19.898, which as before places the semicircle S₂'s right end at h + r₂ ≈19.898 +1.006≈20.904>20, outside the diameter AB.\n\nAlternatively, taking the negative sqrt for t:\n\nIf t = -sqrt(96 -2d² +4d), then h=10 -9.898≈0.102\n\nThen check f(d) with negative t:\n\nThe bracket equation:\n\nt*(d²-1) -2 d r₂ = (-9.898)*(0.225) -2*1.1065*1.006≈-2.227 -2.223≈-4.45≠0\n\nThus, only the positive sqrt gives the root, but it places h outside the allowable range.\n\nThis suggests that there is no solution with h within [1.414, 18.586]. However, the problem states that such a configuration exists. Therefore, likely my entire approach is flawed.\n\nPerhaps the error is in assuming that the semicircle S₂ is tangent to AB at its lowest point, but in reality, it could be tangent elsewhere?\n\nWait, the problem states: \"Semicircle S₂'s arc is tangent to AB at C\". The arc of S₂ is tangent to AB at C, so the point of tangency is C, but the center of S₂ is at (h, r₂), so the tangent point C must be the point where the semicircle just touches AB. For a semicircle above its diameter, the only point it can be tangent to AB is at the lowest point of the semicircle, which is directly below the center. Therefore, C must be (h, 0), so my initial assumption holds.\n\nGiven that, and the math leading to an inconsistency, perhaps the error is in the problem's parameters? Or perhaps I misapplied the chord length formula?\n\nWait, the chord length formula: For a circle of radius R, the length of a chord at distance d from the center is 2*sqrt(R² - d²). Here, for S₂ being inscribed in S₃:\n\nThe chord DE of S₂ has length 2r₂ (its own diameter), and it is a chord of S₃ (radius 10), at distance d from the center of S₃.\n\nTherefore, 2r₂ = 2*sqrt(10² - d²) ⇒ r₂ = sqrt(100 -d²)\n\nBut the distance d from O₃ to DE is the distance from (10,0) to the line DE. Earlier, we computed this distance as sqrt( (h -10)^2 + r₂² )\n\nTherefore:\n\nr₂ = sqrt(100 - [ (h -10)^2 + r₂² ] )\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\nr₂² = 100 - (h -10)^2 - r₂² ⇒ 2r₂² + (h -10)^2 =100\n\nWhich is correct.\n\nThen for S₁:\n\nThe radius r₁=1 must satisfy that the distance from O₁ to DE is 1, and O₁ is at (h, r₂ -1). The distance from (h, r₂ -1) to DE is 1, which is satisfied because DE is at y = r₂, so the vertical distance is r₂ - (r₂ -1)=1, so this condition is automatically satisfied regardless of h and r₂. Wait a minute! This is a key realization.\n\nIf DE is a horizontal line at y = r₂, then the vertical distance from any point (x, y) to DE is |y - r₂|. The center O₁ is at (h, r₂ -1), so its distance to DE is |(r₂ -1) - r₂| = |-1| =1. Therefore, regardless of h and r₂, this distance is always 1. So the condition for S₁'s tangency is automatically satisfied as long as DE is horizontal and O₁ is at (h, r₂ -1). \n\nTherefore, the only constraints are:\n\n1. 2r₂² + (h -10)^2 =100 (from S₂ being a chord of S₃)\n\n2. The endpoints of S₁'s diameter FG must lie on S₂'s circle.\n\nThe endpoints of FG","num_tokens":32768,"correct":false,"formatted":false},{"predicted_answer":"28","full_generation":" Okay, so I have this geometry problem here about semicircles inscribed in each other. Let me try to parse the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, the problem states:\n\n\"A semicircle is inscribed in another semicircle if the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle, and the smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\"\n\nAlright, so this is defining what it means for a semicircle to be inscribed in another semicircle. The key points here are:\n\n1. The smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle.\n2. The smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\n\nThen, the problem says:\n\n\"Semicircle \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in a semicircle \\( S_2 \\), which is inscribed in another semicircle \\( S_3 \\). The radii of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are 1 and 10, respectively, and the diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are parallel. The endpoints of the diameter of \\( S_3 \\) are \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), and \\( S_2 \\)'s arc is tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\). Compute \\( AC \\cdot CB \\).\"\n\nAlright, so we have three semicircles: \\( S_1 \\), \\( S_2 \\), and \\( S_3 \\). \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), and \\( S_2 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_3 \\). The radii of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are given as 1 and 10, respectively. The diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are parallel. The endpoints of the diameter of \\( S_3 \\) are labeled \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), so \\( AB \\) is the diameter of \\( S_3 \\), which has a radius of 10, so the length of \\( AB \\) is 20 units. The semicircle \\( S_2 \\)'s arc is tangent to the diameter \\( AB \\) at a point \\( C \\), and we need to compute the product \\( AC \\cdot CB \\).\n\nThe problem also provides a TikZ picture, but since I can't actually see the picture, I'll have to visualize it based on the description and the coordinates given in the TikZ code.\n\nFirst, let me try to sketch a rough mental picture.\n\nWe have a large semicircle \\( S_3 \\) with diameter \\( AB \\) of length 20 units (since radius 10, so diameter is 20). The semicircle \\( S_3 \\) is drawn above the diameter \\( AB \\). Inside \\( S_3 \\), there is another semicircle \\( S_2 \\) inscribed in \\( S_3 \\). The semicircle \\( S_2 \\) is itself a semicircle, so it has its own diameter and an arc. The diameter of \\( S_2 \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and the arc of \\( S_2 \\) is tangent to the diameter \\( AB \\) of \\( S_3 \\). Moreover, the arc of \\( S_2 \\) is tangent to \\( AB \\) at a point \\( C \\).\n\nThen, inside \\( S_2 \\), there is another semicircle \\( S_1 \\), which is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\). The diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is a chord of \\( S_2 \\), and the arc of \\( S_1 \\) is tangent to the diameter of \\( S_2 \\). Additionally, it's given that the diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are parallel. Since \\( S_3 \\)'s diameter is \\( AB \\), which is a straight line, and \\( S_1 \\)'s diameter is a chord of \\( S_2 \\), which is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), so the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is parallel to \\( AB \\).\n\nOur goal is to compute \\( AC \\cdot CB \\), where \\( C \\) is the point of tangency of the arc of \\( S_2 \\) with the diameter \\( AB \\) of \\( S_3 \\).\n\nHmm. Since \\( AB \\) is the diameter of \\( S_3 \\), and \\( S_2 \\)'s arc is tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\), then \\( C \\) must lie on \\( AB \\), and \\( S_2 \\) is tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\). So \\( C \\) is a point on \\( AB \\), and the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) touches \\( AB \\) exactly at \\( C \\).\n\nNow, since \\( S_2 \\) is itself a semicircle inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), its diameter must be a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and its arc is tangent to the diameter \\( AB \\) of \\( S_3 \\). So, let me try to model this with coordinates.\n\nLet me set up a coordinate system where the diameter \\( AB \\) of \\( S_3 \\) lies along the x-axis, from point \\( A(0, 0) \\) to point \\( B(20, 0) \\). Therefore, the center of \\( S_3 \\) is at the midpoint of \\( AB \\), which is \\( (10, 0) \\), and the radius of \\( S_3 \\) is 10, so the semicircle \\( S_3 \\) is the upper half of the circle centered at \\( (10, 0) \\) with radius 10.\n\nNow, the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), so its diameter is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and its arc is tangent to \\( AB \\) at point \\( C \\). Let me denote the diameter of \\( S_2 \\) as a chord of \\( S_3 \\), say between points \\( D \\) and \\( E \\), so that the diameter of \\( S_2 \\) is the line segment \\( DE \\), and the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) is drawn above this diameter \\( DE \\), with its arc tangent to \\( AB \\) at point \\( C \\).\n\nSince the diameter of \\( S_2 \\) (i.e., chord \\( DE \\)) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and the arc of \\( S_2 \\) is tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\), then the center of \\( S_2 \\) must lie somewhere above the diameter \\( DE \\), and the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) must touch \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\). Since \\( S_2 \\) is a semicircle, its diameter is \\( DE \\), and its arc is the curved part above \\( DE \\). For the arc of \\( S_2 \\) to be tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\), the point \\( C \\) must lie on both \\( AB \\) and the arc of \\( S_2 \\), and the tangent to the arc of \\( S_2 \\) at \\( C \\) must be parallel to the tangent of \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\), but since \\( AB \\) is a straight line, its tangent is itself, so the tangent to the arc of \\( S_2 \\) at \\( C \\) must be horizontal (since \\( AB \\) is horizontal in my coordinate system). Wait, but actually, the tangent to \\( AB \\) at any point is just the line \\( AB \\) itself, which is horizontal. Therefore, the tangent to the arc of \\( S_2 \\) at \\( C \\) must be horizontal, meaning that the radius of \\( S_2 \\) at point \\( C \\) must be vertical. Therefore, the center of \\( S_2 \\) must lie directly above point \\( C \\), right?\n\nBecause the radius to the point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line. Since the tangent line is horizontal (the diameter \\( AB \\)), the radius at \\( C \\) must be vertical. Therefore, the center of \\( S_2 \\) is vertically above \\( C \\). Let me denote the center of \\( S_2 \\) as \\( O_2 \\), so \\( O_2 \\) has coordinates \\( (c_x, r_2) \\), where \\( c_x \\) is the x-coordinate of point \\( C \\), which is \\( (c_x, 0) \\), but wait, no, point \\( C \\) is on \\( AB \\), so its coordinates are \\( (c, 0) \\), and the center \\( O_2 \\) is at \\( (c, r_2) \\), where \\( r_2 \\) is the radius of \\( S_2 \\). Wait, but the center must lie along the vertical line passing through \\( C \\), so yes, \\( O_2 \\) is at \\( (c, r_2) \\).\n\nAdditionally, since \\( S_2 \\) is a semicircle inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), its diameter \\( DE \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and the center \\( O_2 \\) must lie inside \\( S_3 \\). The diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\) must be a chord of \\( S_3 \\), so points \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) are on the circle \\( S_3 \\).\n\nMoreover, since \\( DE \\) is the diameter of \\( S_2 \\), the length of \\( DE \\) is \\( 2 r_2 \\), because the radius of \\( S_2 \\) is \\( r_2 \\). The center \\( O_2 \\) is the midpoint of \\( DE \\), so \\( DE \\) is a horizontal line segment (since its diameter is parallel to the diameter of \\( S_1 \\), which we'll get to in a moment) because the problem states that the diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are parallel. Since \\( S_3 \\)'s diameter \\( AB \\) is horizontal, then the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is also horizontal, and since \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), its diameter must also be a chord of \\( S_2 \\), and parallel to the diameter of \\( S_2 \\), which is \\( DE \\). Wait, actually, hold on.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"the diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are parallel.\" Since \\( S_3 \\)'s diameter \\( AB \\) is horizontal in my coordinate system, then the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) must also be horizontal. Since \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), its diameter is a chord of \\( S_2 \\), and the arc of \\( S_1 \\) is tangent to the diameter of \\( S_2 \\). Wait, let me parse that again.\n\n\"A semicircle is inscribed in another semicircle if the smaller semicircle's diameter is a chord of the larger semicircle, and the smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\"\n\nSo, for \\( S_1 \\) inscribed in \\( S_2 \\):\n\n- The diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is a chord of \\( S_2 \\).\n- The arc of \\( S_1 \\) is tangent to the diameter of \\( S_2 \\).\n\nSimilarly, for \\( S_2 \\) inscribed in \\( S_3 \\):\n\n- The diameter of \\( S_2 \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\).\n- The arc of \\( S_2 \\) is tangent to the diameter of \\( S_3 \\), which is \\( AB \\), at point \\( C \\).\n\nTherefore, the diameter of \\( S_2 \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and the arc of \\( S_2 \\) is tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\). Since the diameter of \\( S_2 \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and \\( S_2 \\)'s diameter is parallel to \\( S_1 \\)'s diameter, and \\( S_1 \\)'s diameter is parallel to \\( S_3 \\)'s diameter \\( AB \\), then all three diameters are parallel. Therefore, all the diameters (of \\( S_1 \\), \\( S_2 \\), and \\( S_3 \\)) are horizontal in this coordinate system.\n\nTherefore, the diameter of \\( S_2 \\), which is chord \\( DE \\), is horizontal, so \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) have the same y-coordinate, which is the y-coordinate of the center of \\( S_2 \\) minus the radius of \\( S_2 \\). Wait, actually, since the center \\( O_2 \\) is at \\( (c, r_2) \\), and the diameter \\( DE \\) is horizontal, so the endpoints \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) must be located at \\( (c - r_2, r_2 - r_2) \\) and \\( (c + r_2, r_2 - r_2) \\)? Wait, no, hold on.\n\nWait, actually, if the center of \\( S_2 \\) is at \\( (c, r_2) \\), and since the diameter \\( DE \\) is horizontal, then the diameter is a horizontal line segment through the center. Wait, but the diameter of a semicircle is the straight line segment, so the center is the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, if the diameter is horizontal and the center is at \\( (c, r_2) \\), then the diameter must be the line segment from \\( (c - r_2, r_2) \\) to \\( (c + r_2, r_2) \\). Wait, but hold on, that would place the diameter at height \\( y = r_2 \\), but then the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) would be the upper half of the circle with center \\( (c, r_2) \\) and radius \\( r_2 \\). Wait, but if the diameter is at \\( y = r_2 \\), then the semicircle would be above that diameter? But then the semicircle would extend upward from the diameter, but in our case, since \\( S_2 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), which is the upper semicircle over \\( AB \\), the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) must lie entirely within \\( S_3 \\). Hmm, perhaps I need to think more carefully.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps the diameter of the semicircle is the chord that is the base of the semicircle, and the semicircle curves outward. Wait, but in the standard definition, a semicircle is half of a circle, so if you have a semicircle inscribed in another semicircle, with its diameter being a chord of the larger semicircle, then the smaller semicircle is drawn on that chord as its diameter, and its arc is facing towards the larger semicircle. So in that case, the center of the smaller semicircle would be the midpoint of its diameter (the chord), and the arc of the smaller semicircle would curve towards the larger semicircle. Therefore, in the case of \\( S_2 \\) inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) is drawn on that diameter \\( DE \\), curving upwards (since it's inscribed in \\( S_3 \\)), and tangent to the diameter \\( AB \\) of \\( S_3 \\) at point \\( C \\).\n\nWait, but in that case, the center of \\( S_2 \\) is the midpoint of \\( DE \\), so if \\( DE \\) is a horizontal chord of \\( S_3 \\), then the center \\( O_2 \\) of \\( S_2 \\) is at the midpoint of \\( DE \\), which would be somewhere along the horizontal line through \\( DE \\). However, the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) must curve upwards towards \\( S_3 \\), but also must be tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\). Since \\( AB \\) is the diameter of \\( S_3 \\), lying on the x-axis, the point \\( C \\) is on the x-axis, and the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) must touch the x-axis at \\( C \\). Therefore, the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) must dip down to touch the x-axis at \\( C \\), but since it's a semicircle above its diameter \\( DE \\), that seems contradictory unless... Wait, perhaps my mental model is off.\n\nWait, perhaps the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) is drawn with diameter \\( DE \\), which is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), and the semicircle itself is the arc above \\( DE \\), but in order for that arc to be tangent to \\( AB \\), which is the x-axis, the arc must just touch the x-axis at \\( C \\). Therefore, the semicircle \\( S_2 \\) must be tangent to the x-axis at \\( C \\), so the distance from the center of \\( S_2 \\) to the x-axis must be equal to the radius of \\( S_2 \\), because the radius at the point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line. Since the tangent line is the x-axis, the radius to point \\( C \\) must be vertical. Therefore, the center of \\( S_2 \\) must lie directly above \\( C \\), at a distance equal to its radius. Therefore, the center \\( O_2 \\) is at \\( (c_x, r_2) \\), where \\( C \\) is at \\( (c_x, 0) \\), and \\( r_2 \\) is the radius of \\( S_2 \\). Therefore, the center is vertically above \\( C \\), at height \\( r_2 \\).\n\nBut also, the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\). Since \\( DE \\) is the diameter of \\( S_2 \\), its length is \\( 2 r_2 \\), and the center \\( O_2 \\) is the midpoint of \\( DE \\). But the center \\( O_2 \\) is at \\( (c_x, r_2) \\), so the endpoints \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) must be located at \\( (c_x - r_2, r_2) \\) and \\( (c_x + r_2, r_2) \\). Wait, but hold on, if the center is at \\( (c_x, r_2) \\), and the diameter is horizontal (since it's parallel to \\( AB \\)), then the diameter \\( DE \\) is the line segment from \\( (c_x - r_2, r_2) \\) to \\( (c_x + r_2, r_2) \\). Therefore, the diameter \\( DE \\) is a horizontal line segment at height \\( y = r_2 \\), stretching from \\( x = c_x - r_2 \\) to \\( x = c_x + r_2 \\).\n\nBut this diameter \\( DE \\) is a chord of \\( S_3 \\), which is the semicircle with center at \\( (10, 0) \\) and radius 10. Therefore, the endpoints \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) must lie on the circle \\( S_3 \\). Let me verify that.\n\nThe circle \\( S_3 \\) has equation \\( (x - 10)^2 + y^2 = 10^2 = 100 \\).\n\nSince point \\( D \\) is \\( (c_x - r_2, r_2) \\), plugging into the equation of \\( S_3 \\):\n\n\\( ( (c_x - r_2) - 10 )^2 + (r_2)^2 = 100 \\).\n\nSimilarly, for point \\( E \\), \\( ( (c_x + r_2) - 10 )^2 + (r_2)^2 = 100 \\).\n\nTherefore, both points \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) must satisfy the equation of \\( S_3 \\).\n\nSo let me write that equation for point \\( D \\):\n\n\\( (c_x - r_2 - 10)^2 + r_2^2 = 100 \\).\n\nSimilarly, for point \\( E \\):\n\n\\( (c_x + r_2 - 10)^2 + r_2^2 = 100 \\).\n\nThese are two equations, but they must both hold true. Let me see if they can be satisfied simultaneously.\n\nLet me denote \\( c_x \\) as the x-coordinate of point \\( C \\), which is the point of tangency on \\( AB \\). Let me denote \\( c = c_x \\), and \\( r = r_2 \\), so to simplify notation.\n\nSo, substituting:\n\nEquation 1: \\( (c - r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nEquation 2: \\( (c + r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, actually, in the coordinates above, the center of \\( S_2 \\) is at \\( (c, r) \\), so point \\( D \\) is \\( (c - r, r) \\), because the diameter \\( DE \\) is of length \\( 2r \\), so from the center \\( (c, r) \\), moving left by \\( r \\) in the x-direction gives \\( D \\), and right by \\( r \\) gives \\( E \\).\n\nTherefore, coordinates:\n\n- Center \\( O_2 \\): \\( (c, r) \\)\n- Point \\( D \\): \\( (c - r, r) \\)\n- Point \\( E \\): \\( (c + r, r) \\)\n\nTherefore, plugging point \\( D \\) into \\( S_3 \\)'s equation:\n\n\\( ( (c - r) - 10 )^2 + (r)^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSimilarly, plugging point \\( E \\) into \\( S_3 \\)'s equation:\n\n\\( ( (c + r) - 10 )^2 + (r)^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSo now we have two equations:\n\n1. \\( (c - r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\n2. \\( (c + r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nHmm. Let's write them out:\n\nFirst equation:\n\n\\( (c - r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSecond equation:\n\n\\( (c + r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nLet me expand both equations.\n\nFirst equation:\n\nLet me denote \\( (c - r - 10)^2 = ( (c - 10) - r )^2 = (c - 10)^2 - 2(c - 10)r + r^2 \\)\n\nTherefore, first equation becomes:\n\n\\( [ (c - 10)^2 - 2(c - 10)r + r^2 ] + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( (c - 10)^2 - 2(c - 10)r + r^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( (c - 10)^2 - 2(c - 10)r + 2 r^2 = 100 \\) --- Equation 1\n\nSimilarly, the second equation:\n\n\\( (c + r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nExpand \\( (c + r - 10)^2 \\):\n\n\\( ( (c - 10) + r )^2 = (c - 10)^2 + 2(c - 10)r + r^2 \\)\n\nTherefore, the second equation becomes:\n\n\\( [ (c - 10)^2 + 2(c - 10)r + r^2 ] + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( (c - 10)^2 + 2(c - 10)r + r^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( (c - 10)^2 + 2(c - 10)r + 2 r^2 = 100 \\) --- Equation 2\n\nNow, let's subtract Equation 1 from Equation 2 to eliminate some terms.\n\nEquation 2 minus Equation 1:\n\n[ \\( (c - 10)^2 + 2(c - 10)r + 2 r^2 \\) ] - [ \\( (c - 10)^2 - 2(c - 10)r + 2 r^2 \\) ] = 100 - 100\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n\\( (c - 10)^2 - (c - 10)^2 + 2(c - 10)r - (-2(c - 10)r) + 2 r^2 - 2 r^2 \\)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\\( 0 + 4(c - 10)r + 0 = 0 \\)\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( 4(c - 10)r = 0 \\)\n\nSo, either \\( c - 10 = 0 \\) or \\( r = 0 \\).\n\nBut \\( r \\) is the radius of \\( S_2 \\), which must be positive, so \\( r \\neq 0 \\). Therefore, we must have \\( c - 10 = 0 \\), so \\( c = 10 \\).\n\nWait, that's interesting. So the x-coordinate of point \\( C \\), which is the point of tangency on \\( AB \\), must be at \\( c = 10 \\). So point \\( C \\) is at \\( (10, 0) \\), which is the center of \\( S_3 \\).\n\nBut hold on, if \\( c = 10 \\), then the center of \\( S_2 \\) is at \\( (10, r) \\), and the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\) is from \\( (10 - r, r) \\) to \\( (10 + r, r) \\). Therefore, the diameter \\( DE \\) is centered at the center of \\( S_3 \\), and is a horizontal line segment at height \\( y = r \\), with length \\( 2r \\).\n\nBut then, plugging back \\( c = 10 \\) into Equation 1:\n\nEquation 1 becomes:\n\n\\( (10 - r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( (-r)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( r^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\n\\( 2 r^2 = 100 \\)\n\n\\( r^2 = 50 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( r = \\sqrt{50} = 5 \\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071 \\).\n\nSo the radius of \\( S_2 \\) is \\( 5 \\sqrt{2} \\), and its center is at \\( (10, 5 \\sqrt{2}) \\), and its diameter \\( DE \\) is from \\( (10 - 5 \\sqrt{2}, 5 \\sqrt{2}) \\) to \\( (10 + 5 \\sqrt{2}, 5 \\sqrt{2}) \\).\n\nWait, but let's confirm with Equation 2 as well, since we had to have both equations hold, but since we derived \\( c = 10 \\), and then plugged back in, so it should hold.\n\nBut let me check with Equation 2:\n\nOriginal Equation 2 with \\( c = 10 \\):\n\n\\( (10 + r - 10)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( (r)^2 + r^2 = 100 \\)\n\nWhich is the same as before, so that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, we have found that the radius of \\( S_2 \\) is \\( 5 \\sqrt{2} \\), and the point \\( C \\) is at the center of \\( S_3 \\), which is \\( (10, 0) \\). Therefore, \\( AC \\cdot CB \\) is the product of the distances from \\( A \\) to \\( C \\) and from \\( C \\) to \\( B \\).\n\nSince \\( A \\) is at \\( (0, 0) \\) and \\( B \\) is at \\( (20, 0) \\), and \\( C \\) is at \\( (10, 0) \\), then \\( AC = 10 \\) units and \\( CB = 10 \\) units, so \\( AC \\cdot CB = 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\).\n\nWait, but that seems too straightforward. The problem mentions that \\( S_1 \\) is also inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), and gives the radius of \\( S_1 \\) as 1, and \\( S_3 \\)'s radius is 10. However, in my calculation above, I didn't use the information about \\( S_1 \\). That seems like a problem. The problem states that \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with radius 1, and that the diameters of \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_3 \\) are parallel. Since we already used the condition that diameters are parallel (so all horizontal) and the tangency condition for \\( S_2 \\), but perhaps the presence of \\( S_1 \\) imposes another condition?\n\nHmm, so perhaps my mistake was that I didn't consider the semicircle \\( S_1 \\), which is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with radius 1, so maybe my previous calculation is incomplete because I only considered the relation between \\( S_2 \\) and \\( S_3 \\), but \\( S_1 \\) is also part of the configuration, and its radius is given as 1, so that must constrain the size of \\( S_2 \\).\n\nWait, so in my previous calculation, I found that \\( S_2 \\) has radius \\( 5 \\sqrt{2} \\approx 7.071 \\), but if \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with radius 1, then that might not be compatible unless there is some scaling factor or additional relation.\n\nTherefore, perhaps my initial assumption that led me to \\( c = 10 \\) is incorrect? Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, let me recap.\n\nWe set up coordinates with \\( A(0,0) \\), \\( B(20,0) \\), center of \\( S_3 \\) at \\( (10,0) \\), radius 10.\n\nWe considered \\( S_2 \\) inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), with diameter \\( DE \\) (a chord of \\( S_3 \\)), and arc of \\( S_2 \\) tangent to \\( AB \\) at \\( C \\). We derived that \\( C \\) must be at the center \\( (10, 0) \\), and that the radius of \\( S_2 \\) is \\( 5 \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nBut then, if \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with radius 1, then we need to ensure that such a semicircle \\( S_1 \\) can indeed be inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), with diameter parallel to \\( AB \\) (i.e., horizontal). So, the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) must be a chord of \\( S_2 \\), and its arc must be tangent to the diameter of \\( S_2 \\), which is \\( DE \\). Since \\( DE \\) is horizontal (as we've established, being parallel to \\( AB \\)), the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) must also be horizontal, and the arc of \\( S_1 \\) must be tangent to \\( DE \\).\n\nTherefore, similar to how \\( S_2 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), so let's model that.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- The center of \\( S_2 \\) is at \\( O_2 = (10, r_2) \\), where \\( r_2 = 5 \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nWait, hold on, in our previous calculation, we found that \\( c = 10 \\), so \\( O_2 \\) is at \\( (10, r_2) \\), with \\( r_2 = 5 \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nNow, the diameter of \\( S_2 \\) is \\( DE \\), which is from \\( (10 - r_2, r_2) \\) to \\( (10 + r_2, r_2) \\). Wait, but actually, hold on:\n\nWait, in the coordinate system, we had:\n\n- Center of \\( S_2 \\): \\( (c, r_2) \\), but we found that \\( c = 10 \\), so center is \\( (10, r_2) \\).\n\n- The diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\) is a horizontal line segment (since parallel to \\( AB \\)), centered at \\( (10, r_2) \\), so its endpoints are \\( (10 - r_2, r_2) \\) and \\( (10 + r_2, r_2) \\). Therefore, the diameter \\( DE \\) is at height \\( y = r_2 \\), and spans from \\( x = 10 - r_2 \\) to \\( x = 10 + r_2 \\).\n\nNow, the semicircle \\( S_1 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), so its diameter is a chord of \\( S_2 \\), and its arc is tangent to the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\).\n\nSimilarly to how \\( S_2 \\) is inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), the semicircle \\( S_1 \\) must have its diameter as a chord of \\( S_2 \\), and its arc tangent to the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\). Additionally, the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) is parallel to \\( AB \\), so horizontal.\n\nTherefore, let me denote the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) as a horizontal chord of \\( S_2 \\), and the arc of \\( S_1 \\) is tangent to the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\).\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let the diameter of \\( S_1 \\) be \\( FG \\), a horizontal chord of \\( S_2 \\).\n\n- The center of \\( S_1 \\) is the midpoint of \\( FG \\), so let's call that point \\( O_1 \\), located at \\( (h, k) \\).\n\n- The radius of \\( S_1 \\) is 1, so the length of \\( FG \\) is \\( 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\).\n\n- The semicircle \\( S_1 \\) is drawn above its diameter \\( FG \\), and is tangent to the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\).\n\nWait, but actually, similar to the prior case, the arc of \\( S_1 \\) must be tangent to the diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\). Since \\( DE \\) is a horizontal line (at \\( y = r_2 \\)), and the semicircle \\( S_1 \\) is above its own diameter \\( FG \\), which is a chord of \\( S_2 \\). For the arc of \\( S_1 \\) to be tangent to \\( DE \\), which is a horizontal line above \\( FG \\), the point of tangency must be the highest point of the semicircle \\( S_1 \\). Wait, perhaps?\n\nAlternatively, similar to before, the semicircle \\( S_1 \\) has its arc tangent to \\( DE \\), so the distance from the center \\( O_1 \\) of \\( S_1 \\) to the line \\( DE \\) must be equal to the radius of \\( S_1 \\), because the tangent at the point of contact must be perpendicular to the radius.\n\nSince \\( DE \\) is the line \\( y = r_2 \\), and the semicircle \\( S_1 \\) is above its own diameter \\( FG \\). The center \\( O_1 \\) is at some point \\( (h, k) \\), and the radius of \\( S_1 \\) is 1, so the semicircle spans from \\( FG \\) upwards. The arc of \\( S_1 \\) must touch the line \\( DE \\) (i.e., \\( y = r_2 \\)) at exactly one point, meaning that the distance from \\( O_1 \\) to \\( DE \\) must be equal to the radius of \\( S_1 \\), which is 1. Since \\( DE \\) is the line \\( y = r_2 \\), the distance from \\( O_1(h, k) \\) to \\( DE \\) is \\( |k - r_2| \\). Since \\( S_1 \\) is above its diameter \\( FG \\), and \\( DE \\) is above \\( FG \\), likely \\( k < r_2 \\), so that the distance is \\( r_2 - k \\), and this must equal the radius of \\( S_1 \\), which is 1. Therefore:\n\n\\( r_2 - k = 1 \\), so \\( k = r_2 - 1 \\).\n\nAdditionally, since \\( FG \\) is the diameter of \\( S_1 \\), which is a horizontal chord of \\( S_2 \\), the center \\( O_1(h, k) \\) must be the midpoint of \\( FG \\), and the length of \\( FG \\) is 2 (since radius 1). Therefore, the endpoints \\( F \\) and \\( G \\) of \\( FG \\) are located at \\( (h - 1, k) \\) and \\( (h + 1, k) \\).\n\nMoreover, since \\( FG \\) is a chord of \\( S_2 \\), which has center \\( O_2(10, r_2) \\) and radius \\( r_2 \\), the points \\( F \\) and \\( G \\) must lie on the circle \\( S_2 \\). Therefore, plugging point \\( F(h - 1, k) \\) into the equation of \\( S_2 \\):\n\nThe equation of \\( S_2 \\) is \\( (x - 10)^2 + (y - r_2)^2 = r_2^2 \\).\n\nSo plugging in \\( F(h - 1, k) \\):\n\n\\( ( (h - 1) - 10 )^2 + (k - r_2)^2 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( (h - 11)^2 + (k - r_2)^2 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nBut we know that \\( k = r_2 - 1 \\), so substituting that in:\n\n\\( (h - 11)^2 + ( (r_2 - 1) - r_2 )^2 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nSimplify the second term:\n\n\\( ( -1 )^2 = 1 \\)\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( (h - 11)^2 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nSimilarly, for point \\( G(h + 1, k) \\), plugging into \\( S_2 \\)'s equation:\n\n\\( ( (h + 1) - 10 )^2 + (k - r_2)^2 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\\( (h - 9)^2 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nTherefore, we have two equations:\n\n1. \\( (h - 11)^2 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\n2. \\( (h - 9)^2 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nSet them equal to each other:\n\n\\( (h - 11)^2 + 1 = (h - 9)^2 + 1 \\)\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\n\\( (h - 11)^2 = (h - 9)^2 \\)\n\nExpand both sides:\n\nLeft side: \\( h^2 - 22h + 121 \\)\n\nRight side: \\( h^2 - 18h + 81 \\)\n\nSet equal:\n\n\\( h^2 - 22h + 121 = h^2 - 18h + 81 \\)\n\nSubtract \\( h^2 \\) from both sides:\n\n\\( -22h + 121 = -18h + 81 \\)\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n\\( -22h + 121 + 18h - 81 = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( (-4h) + 40 = 0 \\)\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( -4h = -40 \\)\n\n\\( h = 10 \\)\n\nSo \\( h = 10 \\).\n\nTherefore, the center \\( O_1 \\) is at \\( (10, k) \\), and since \\( k = r_2 - 1 \\), so \\( k = r_2 - 1 \\).\n\nNow, plugging back \\( h = 10 \\) into one of the equations, say equation 1:\n\n\\( (10 - 11)^2 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nCalculates to:\n\n\\( (-1)^2 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\n\\( 1 + 1 = r_2^2 \\)\n\n\\( 2 = r_2^2 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( r_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nWait a minute! This contradicts our previous result where we found \\( r_2 = 5 \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nHmm, so here's the problem. Earlier, when I considered only \\( S_2 \\) inscribed in \\( S_3 \\), I found that \\( r_2 = 5 \\sqrt{2} \\), but when considering the additional constraint from \\( S_1 \\), which is inscribed in \\( S_2 \\), we now find that \\( r_2 \\) must be \\( \\sqrt{2} \\). This inconsistency suggests that my initial approach was incomplete because I didn't take into account the presence of \\( S_1 \\). Therefore, I need to reconcile these two results.\n\nWait, so clearly, the radius of \\( S_2 \\) is constrained both by its own tangency to \\( AB \\) and by the existence of an inscribed semicircle \\( S_1 \\) with radius 1. Therefore, my first calculation only considered the tangency to \\( AB \\), leading to \\( r_2 = 5 \\sqrt{2} \\), but when