qrt{2}) \\) and radius \\( r_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\). The distance from \\( O_3(10, 0) \\) to \\( O_2(c, \\sqrt{2}) \\) must satisfy:\n\n\\( \\text{distance}(O_3, O_2) + r_2 \\leq R_3 \\)\n\nDistance \\( d = \\sqrt{(c - 10)^2 + (\\sqrt{2} - 0)^2} \\)\n\nThus:\n\n\\( \\sqrt{(c - 10)^2 + 2} + \\sqrt{2} \\leq 10 \\)\n\nWe need to find \\( c \\) such that this inequality holds, and also that points \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) (the endpoints of \\( S_2 \\)'s diameter) lie on \\( S_3 \\).\n\nThe diameter \\( DE \\) of \\( S_2 \\) is horizontal at \\( y = r_2 = \\sqrt{2} \\), centered at \\( (c, \\sqrt{2}) \\), so endpoints are \\( D(c - \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{2}) \\) and \\( E(c + \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{2}) \\).\n\nThese points must lie on \\( S_3 \\)'s circle:\n\nFor point \\( D \\):\n\n\\( ( (c - \\sqrt{2}) - 10 )^2 + ( \\sqrt{2} )^2 = 10^2 \\)\n\nExpand:\n\n\\( (c - \\sqrt{2} - 10)^2 + 2 = 100 \\)\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( (c - (10 + \\sqrt{2}))^2 = 98 \\)\n\nTake square roots:\n\n\\( c - (10 + \\sqrt{2}) = \\pm \\sqrt{98} = \\pm 7\\sqrt{2} \\)\n\nThus:\n\n\\( c = 10 + \\sqrt{2} \\pm 7\\sqrt{2} \\)\n\nSo:\n\n\\( c = 10 + \\sqrt{2} + 7\\sqrt{2} = 10 + 8\\sqrt{2} \\) or \\( c = 10 + \\sqrt{2} - 7\\sqrt{2} = 10 - 6\\sqrt{2} \\)\n\nTherefore, there are two possible solutions for \\( c \\):\n\n1. \\( c = 10 + 8\\sqrt{2} \\)\n2. \\( c = 10 - 6\\sqrt{2} \\)\n\nNow, we must check which of these satisfies the distance condition for \\( S_2 \\) to be inside \\( S_3 \\).\n\nFirst, compute \\( c \\) for both cases:\n\nCase 1: \\( c = 10 + 8\\sqrt{2} \\approx 10 + 11.31 = 21.31 \\). Since \\( AB \\) is from 0 to 20, this places \\( C \\) outside the diameter \\( AB \\), which is invalid because \\( C \\) must be on \\( AB \\).\n\nCase 2: \\( c = 10 - 6\\sqrt{2} \\approx 10 - 8.485 = 1.515 \\). This places \\( C \\) at approximately \\( x = 1.515 \\), which is within \\( AB \\) (from 0 to 20).\n\nTherefore, the valid solution is \\( c = 10 - 6\\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nNow, let's verify the distance condition for \\